window.SUBJECT_QBANK_DATA={"subjects":[{"subject":"Physiology","topics":[{"id":"physiology-1","subject":"Physiology","title":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","questionCount":21},{"id":"physiology-2","subject":"Physiology","title":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","questionCount":28},{"id":"physiology-3","subject":"Physiology","title":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","questionCount":16},{"id":"physiology-4","subject":"Physiology","title":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","questionCount":16},{"id":"physiology-5","subject":"Physiology","title":"Special Senses","questionCount":17},{"id":"physiology-6","subject":"Physiology","title":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","questionCount":11},{"id":"physiology-7","subject":"Physiology","title":"General Physiology","questionCount":30},{"id":"physiology-8","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":43},{"id":"physiology-9","subject":"Physiology","title":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","questionCount":26},{"id":"physiology-10","subject":"Physiology","title":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","questionCount":18},{"id":"physiology-11","subject":"Physiology","title":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","questionCount":20},{"id":"physiology-12","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":34},{"id":"physiology-13","subject":"Physiology","title":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","questionCount":18},{"id":"physiology-14","subject":"Physiology","title":"ECG","questionCount":11},{"id":"physiology-15","subject":"Physiology","title":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","questionCount":14},{"id":"physiology-16","subject":"Physiology","title":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","questionCount":17},{"id":"physiology-17","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":38},{"id":"physiology-18","subject":"Physiology","title":"Blood Physiology","questionCount":12},{"id":"physiology-19","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":15},{"id":"physiology-20","subject":"Physiology","title":"Renal Physiology","questionCount":26},{"id":"physiology-21","subject":"Physiology","title":"Tubular Transport","questionCount":20},{"id":"physiology-22","subject":"Physiology","title":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","questionCount":13},{"id":"physiology-23","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":50},{"id":"physiology-24","subject":"Physiology","title":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","questionCount":25},{"id":"physiology-25","subject":"Physiology","title":"Pulmonary Function Tests","questionCount":14},{"id":"physiology-26","subject":"Physiology","title":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","questionCount":24},{"id":"physiology-27","subject":"Physiology","title":"Regulation of respiration","questionCount":18},{"id":"physiology-28","subject":"Physiology","title":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","questionCount":20},{"id":"physiology-29","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":41},{"id":"physiology-30","subject":"Physiology","title":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","questionCount":23},{"id":"physiology-31","subject":"Physiology","title":"GIT Motility","questionCount":11},{"id":"physiology-32","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":26},{"id":"physiology-33","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":54},{"id":"physiology-34","subject":"Physiology","title":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","questionCount":22},{"id":"physiology-35","subject":"Physiology","title":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","questionCount":15},{"id":"physiology-36","subject":"Physiology","title":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","questionCount":21},{"id":"physiology-37","subject":"Physiology","title":"Previous Year Questions","questionCount":61},{"id":"physiology-38","subject":"Physiology","title":"Reproductive Physiology","questionCount":10}],"questions":[{"id":"physiology-1-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following best describes the mechanism of action of glutamate at its receptors in the central nervous system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It inhibits metabotropic receptors, activating a G-protein, thus increasing cAMP levels."},{"letter":"B","text":"It stimulates NMDA receptors, resulting in an influx of calcium ions."},{"letter":"C","text":"It inhibits AMPA receptors, preventing sodium influx."},{"letter":"D","text":"It stimulates kainate receptors, thus decreasing chloride ion permeability."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) It stimulates NMDA receptors, resulting in an influx of calcium ions.","explanation":"Glutamate is an amino acid that acts as a neuroexcitatory molecule by stimulating NMDA receptors in the CNS, which results in an influx of calcium ions. Type Subtype Features Functions Ionotrop AMPA Influx of Na+ and efflux of Produces fast excitatory ic recep (Alpha-amino-3-hydroxy-5- K+. postsynaptic potential. tors methylisoxazole-4-propion It has a low permeability ate) for Ca2+. (Option C) Kainate Influx of Na+ and Ca2+ Aids in excitatory receptor neurotransmission (Option D) NMDA Allows large influx of Involved in synaptic receptor Ca2+ and Na+ plasticity, memory, and (N-meth Unique Features: learning and can cause yl-D-asp Glycine Binding: Essential excitotoxicity in excess. artate) for receptor response to (Option glutamate. B) Mg²■ Block: At normal membrane potentials, the channel is blocked by extracellular Mg²■. This block is removed during depolarisation by adjacent AMPA and kainate receptors. EPSP: Slower than AMPA/kainate receptors-in duced EPSP. Metabot Presynaptic (mGluR2-4,6- Activation leads to Modulate synaptic ropic 8): changes in second plasticity and neurotransmi receptor Involved in limiting messengers: ssion indirectly through s glutamate release. IP3 and DAG: Increased second messengers. (Option intracellular levels. A) cAMP: Decreased intracellular levels. Postsyn aptic (mGluR 1,5): Involved in syna ptic plas ticity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 5-5; Solution Pages 14-15","sourcePage":5,"sourcePageEnd":5},{"id":"physiology-1-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":2,"question":"As illustrated below, in examining the effects of presynaptic inhibition and facilitation on action potentials, Ca²■ currents, and EPSPs (excitatory postsynaptic potential) ,which statement regarding the dashed line recordings is false? (solid lines for controls; dashed lines for inhibition/facilitation)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It can be produced by opening of voltage gated K+ channel"},{"letter":"B","text":"Produced due to enhanced Ca2+ entry in the presynaptic neuron."},{"letter":"C","text":"The effect is produced by GABA"},{"letter":"D","text":"Produced due to increases Cl− conductance"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Produced due to enhanced Ca2+ entry in the presynaptic neuron.","explanation":"Dashed lines the records obtained during inhibition. Presynaptic Inhibition occurs when a neuron reduces neurotransmitter release at the axon terminal. Key mechanisms include: • When K■ channels open, K■ exits the neuron, causing hyperpolarization. This makes it harder for the neuron to reach the threshold for action potentials, reducing overall activity. • During presynaptic inhibition, mediated by GABA (Option C) , hyperpolarization leads to decreased opening of voltage-gated Ca²■ channels during action potentials. This results in less Ca²■ entering the presynaptic neuron. • GABA binding opens Cl■ channels, allowing Cl■ to enter and further hyperpolarize the neuron, reinforcing the inhibitory effect. Where as Presynaptic Facilitation effectively boosts synaptic transmission by increasing Ca²■ levels in the presynaptic neuron, resulting in enhanced neurotransmitter release and a stronger response in the postsynaptic neuron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 5-5; Solution Pages 15-16","sourcePage":5,"sourcePageEnd":5},{"id":"physiology-1-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Feedforward Inhibition is seen in","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Renshaw cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cerebellum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Basal Ganglia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hippocampus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Cerebellum","explanation":"Feedforward inhibition is a mechanism where an excitatory neuron activates an inhibitory neuron, which then inhibits a downstream neuron to prevent excessive excitation. This is commonly observed in the cerebellum, which helps regulate motor control and coordination. Feat Feedback Inhibition Feedforward Inhibition ure Defi Inhibition where the output of a Inhibition where an excitatory nitio process inhibits its own production. neuron directly inhibits another n neuron in response to input. Mec The end product of a metabolic An excitatory neuron activates an hani pathway inhibits an enzyme inhibitory neuron and then inhibits a sm involved in the pathway. downstream neuron. Fun It helps maintain homeostasis by Anticipates and prevents excessive ction regulating the production of excitation in neural circuits. substances. Exa The inhibition of an enzyme in a In the cerebellum, excitatory inputs mpl metabolic pathway by its end inhibit output neurons to prevent e product (e.g., ATP inhibiting overexcitation. phosphofructokinase). Renshaw cells (Option A) are involved in feedback inhibition rather than feedforward inhibition. Basal ganglia (Option C) is involved in complex inhibitory circuits, but they are more commonly associated with feedback inhibition and disinhibition mechanisms rather than feedforward inhibition. Hippocampus (Option D) is more associated with memory and learning processes rather than direct feedforward inhibition","sourceRef":"Question Pages 6-6; Solution Pages 16-17","sourcePage":6,"sourcePageEnd":6},{"id":"physiology-1-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":4,"question":"In presynaptic inhibition, which neurotransmitter is most commonly responsible for inhibiting synaptic transmission at the presynaptic terminal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glutamate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Serotonin"},{"letter":"C","text":"GABA"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glycine"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) GABA","explanation":"GABA mediates presynaptic inhibition/Indirect Inhibition via a G-protein that causes an increase in K+ conductance. GABA is released onto the outer surfaces of presynaptic nerve terminals. Mechanism of action : • GABA opens anion channels, allowing chloride ions to diffuse into the terminal fibril. • The negative charges of chloride ions cancel out much of the excitatory effect of incoming sodium ions. Functions of presynaptic inhibition : • It occurs before the signal reaches the synapse • It's common in sensory pathways and helps minimise sideways spread and mixing of signals Features Indirect Inhibition Direct Inhibition Site of action Presynaptic terminal Postsynaptic neuron Primary mechanism Reduces neurotransmitter release. Hyperpolarises or shunts postsynaptic neurons.. Main neurotransmitte GABA (primarily) GABA, Glycine (Option D) r Receptor types Mainly GABA-B receptors GABA-A, Glycine receptors involved Spatial effect It can affect multiple synapses from Localized to specific synapses the same axon Onset and duration Can be longer-lasting Usually, rapid onset and offset Glutamate (Option A): It is typically an excitatory neurotransmitter that facilitates synaptic transmission rather than inhibiting it. Serotonin (Option B): It can modulate neurotransmitter release and has complex effects on synaptic transmission but is not the primary neurotransmitter for presynaptic inhibition. Glycine (Option D): It acts as an inhibitory neurotransmitter in the spinal cord rather than functioning through presynaptic inhibition in other areas of the central nervous system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 6-6; Solution Pages 17-18","sourcePage":6,"sourcePageEnd":6},{"id":"physiology-1-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 28-year-old woman has fluctuating muscle weakness that worsens throughout the day. Tests show a decremental response to repetitive nerve stimulation, improvement with edrophonium, and antibodies against neuromuscular junction proteins. What is the primary pathophysiological mechanism in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased acetylcholine release from motor nerve terminals"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reduced functional acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Antibodies blocking voltage-gated calcium channels on presynaptic terminals"},{"letter":"D","text":"It is a presynaptic syndrome"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Reduced functional acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane","explanation":"This scenario describes the classic presentation and diagnostic findings of myasthenia gravis in which functional acetylcholine receptors on the postsynaptic membrane are reduced. Disorders related to Neuromuscular junction: Feature Myasthenia Gravis Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome (LEMS) Primary autoantibod Acetylcholine receptors Voltage-gated calcium channels y target (Option C) Pathophysiology Reduced postsynaptic acetylcholine Decreased presynaptic receptors (Option B) acetylcholine release (Options A and D) Fatigue pattern Worsens with activity Improves with brief exercise. Reflexes Normal or mildly reduced Reduced or absent may improve after exercise. Repetitive nerve Decremental response. Incremental response. stimulation Response to Improves symptoms. Little to no improvement. edrophonium","sourceRef":"Question Pages 6-6; Solution Pages 18-19","sourcePage":6,"sourcePageEnd":6},{"id":"physiology-1-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is the correct sequence of events at the neuromuscular junction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ca2+ influx → ACh release → End-plate potential → Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"ACh release → Ca2+ influx → End-plate potential → Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"C","text":"End-plate potential → Ca2+ influx → ACh release → Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"D","text":"ACh release → End-plate potential → Ca2+ influx → Muscle contraction"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Ca2+ influx → ACh release → End-plate potential → Muscle contraction","explanation":"Mechanism of synapse at Neuromuscular junction:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 7-7; Solution Pages 19-19","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"physiology-1-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Slow Type inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) is generated by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Opening of K+ channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Closing of K+ channels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Opening of Cl- channels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Opening of Na+ channels"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Opening of K+ channels","explanation":"Slow inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) is mediated by the opening of K+ channels, which allow potassium ions to flow out of the cell, resulting in hyperpolarisation of the neuron, moving its membrane potential further from the threshold for firing an action potential. IPSP Type Channel Region Slow IPSP Opening of K+ Channel (Option A) Autonomic ganglia, smooth muscles, cardiac muscles. Fast IPSP Entry of Cl- Channel (Option C) All parts of the CNS. Closing of K+ channels (Option B) would typically cause depolarisation, not hyperpolarisation. Opening of Na+ channels (Option D) generates late Slow Excitatory postsynaptic potential","sourceRef":"Question Pages 7-7; Solution Pages 20-20","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"physiology-1-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the primary effect of an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP) on a neuron's excitability?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increases the rate of Neurotransmitter release"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreases membrane potential, closer to firing threshold"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increases membrane potential, farther from firing threshold"},{"letter":"D","text":"No effect on membrane potential"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increases membrane potential, farther from firing threshold","explanation":"IPSPs (Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potentials) are hyperpolarizations that occur when certain neurotransmitters activate Cl− channels on the postsynaptic membrane, allowing Cl− to enter the cell. • This causes the neuron to hyperpolarize, peaking 1–1.5 ms after the stimulus and then declining exponentially. • This hyperpolarisation shifts the membrane potential further from the firing threshold, reducing the neuron's excitability and necessitating greater excitatory input to reach the threshold (Option C) . • IPSPs can also occur through mechanisms such as: Opening of K+ channels, allowing K+ to exit the cell Closure of Na+ channels Closure of Ca2+ channels • Opening of K+ channels, allowing K+ to exit the cell • Closure of Na+ channels • Closure of Ca2+ channels • Opening of K+ channels, allowing K+ to exit the cell • Closure of Na+ channels • Closure of Ca2+ channels Increases the rate of neurotransmitter release (Option A): IPSPs do not influence neurotransmitter release; they affect the neuron's membrane potential. Decreases membrane potential, closer to firing threshold (Option B): IPSPs cause hyperpolarisation, which makes the membrane potential more negative and farther from the firing threshold. No effect on membrane potential (Option D): IPSPs affect the membrane potential by making it more negative, moving it further from the threshold.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 7-7; Solution Pages 20-21","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"physiology-1-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 65-year-old male patient presents with neurological symptoms following a Cerebrovascular accident. Imaging confirms ischemic damage in the brain. Elevated levels of glutamate are found in the extracellular of the brain. Which of the following statements about glutamate-induced cytotoxicity is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cleared primarily by K+dependent systems in neurons"},{"letter":"B","text":"Causes neuronal death by excessive Na+ influx"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cytotoxicity due to excessive Ca2+ influx"},{"letter":"D","text":"Elevated Na+ stimulates astrocytes to remove glutamate"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Cytotoxicity due to excessive Ca2+ influx","explanation":"Excessive glutamate acts on neuronal cell bodies, causing a massive influx of Ca2+, leading to neuronal cell death (Option B) . • Na+-dependent uptake systems in neurons and glia clear glutamate from the brain's extracellular fluid (Option A) . • Excessive levels of glutamate can occur in response to ischemia, anoxia, hypoglycemia, or trauma. • During a stroke Occlusion of a cerebral artery leads to cell death in the ischemic area. Surrounding partially ischemic cells may survive but lose their ability to maintain the transmembrane Na+ gradient. Elevated intracellular Na+ levels impair astrocytes' ability to remove glutamate from the extracellular fluid (Option D) . Glutamate accumulates, leading to excitotoxic damage and cell death in the penumbra, the region around the infarcted area. • Occlusion of a cerebral artery leads to cell death in the ischemic area. • Surrounding partially ischemic cells may survive but lose their ability to maintain the transmembrane Na+ gradient. • Elevated intracellular Na+ levels impair astrocytes' ability to remove glutamate from the extracellular fluid (Option D) . • Glutamate accumulates, leading to excitotoxic damage and cell death in the penumbra, the region around the infarcted area. • Occlusion of a cerebral artery leads to cell death in the ischemic area. • Surrounding partially ischemic cells may survive but lose their ability to maintain the transmembrane Na+ gradient. • Elevated intracellular Na+ levels impair astrocytes' ability to remove glutamate from the extracellular fluid (Option D) . • Glutamate accumulates, leading to excitotoxic damage and cell death in the penumbra, the region around the infarcted area.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 7-8; Solution Pages 21-22","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"physiology-1-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":10,"question":"During the generation of excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP), which of the following movements of ions occur at the postsynaptic membrane?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Efflux of sodium ions"},{"letter":"B","text":"Influx of calcium ions"},{"letter":"C","text":"Influx of potassium ions"},{"letter":"D","text":"Influx of chloride ions"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Influx of calcium ions","explanation":"During an EPSP, calcium ions (Ca2+) influx occurs when excitatory neurotransmitters bind to postsynaptic receptors, causing depolarisation and increased neuron excitability. Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential (EPSP): • Mechanism: Excitatory neurotransmitters bind to receptors on the postsynaptic membrane, causing the opening of Na2+ or Ca2+ channels. This results in the influx of Na2+ or Ca2+ into the postsynaptic neuron, leading to depolarization. • Effect: A single stimulus typically does not produce a propagated action potential in the postsynaptic neuron but results in a transient partial depolarisation called an EPSP. • Neuron Excitability: During an EPSP, the neuron's excitability to subsequent stimuli increases. • Propagation: The area of current flow is localised, meaning the EPSP only affects a small region of the membrane and does not depolarise the entire membrane. • Summation: Multiple EPSPs from various synaptic knobs can summate, leading to significant depolarisation and increased neuronal excitability. Efflux of sodium ions (Option A): During EPSP, sodium ions flow into the neuron, not out of it. This influx contributes to the depolarisation of the postsynaptic membrane. Influx of potassium ions (Option C): Potassium ions typically move out of the cell, not into the cell, during an excitatory postsynaptic potential. Influx of chloride ions (Option D): The influx of chloride ions typically results in hyperpolarisation, producing an inhibitory postsynaptic potential (IPSP), not an excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 8-8; Solution Pages 22-23","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"physiology-1-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which subtype of GABA receptors are associated with mediating fast inhibitory postsynaptic potentials through the entry of Cl- into neurons? GABA-A GABA-B GABA-C","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Receptor 1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Receptor 2 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"Receptor 1 and 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Receptor 1 and 3","explanation":"GABA-A and GABA-C are the two subtypes of GABA receptors associated with mediating fast inhibitory postsynaptic potential through the entry of Cl—ions into the neurons. Type Receptor subtype Subunit Dis Mechanism trib uti on Inotropic GABA- A Pentamers of CN Activation allows receptor various combination S Cl– entry into s: 6 α, 4 β, 4 γ, 1 δ, neurons, mediating 1ε fast inhibitory postsynapticpotentia ls (IPSP) GABA- C Pentamers of three Almost exclusively ρ subunits in in the retina. various combination s G-protein GABA- B No specific subunit CN Linked via G-protein coupled composition S s : Gi inhibits receptor adenylyl cyclase to open the K+ channel, Go inhibits or delays Ca2+ influx.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 8-8; Solution Pages 23-24","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"physiology-1-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 63-year-old male presents with a neurodegenerative condition involving impaired glutamate regulation. Which type of glial cell is primarily involved in glutamate regulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Schwann cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Astrocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ependymal cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oligodendrocytes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Astrocytes","explanation":"Astrocytes are glial cells that play an important role in regulating glutamate levels. They maintain glutamate levels by absorbing excess glutamate and preventing excitotoxicity. Types of Glial Cells: Oligodendrocytes (Option D) Form myelin sheath in CNS neurons. Astrocytes (Option B) Support and protect neurons, provide nutrition, and control levels of glutamate. It is of 2 types: Fibrous astrocytes (found in white matter) Protoplasmic astrocytes (found in grey matter) Schwann cells (Option A) Form myelin sheath in PNS neurons. Ependymal cells (Option C): These cells line the ventricles and the central canal of the Spinal cord and play a key role in the production and circulation of CSF.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 8-9; Solution Pages 24-24","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":9},{"id":"physiology-1-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Match the following neurotransmitters with their site of production in CNS. A. Acetylcholine (ACh) 1. Raphe nuclei B. Norepinephrine (NE) 2. Nucleus basalis of Meynert C. Dopamine 3. Locus ceruleus D. Serotonin 4. Substantia nigra","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acetylcholine (ACh) 1. Raphe nuclei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Norepinephrine (NE) 2. Nucleus basalis of Meynert"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dopamine 3. Locus ceruleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serotonin 4. Substantia nigra"},{"letter":"A","text":"A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-4, B-2, C-3, D-1"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","explanation":"The sites of production of neurotransmitters are as follows: Neurotransmitter Source Description s Acetylcholine Nucleus basalis of Associated with Muscle contraction, (ACh) Meynert Memory and REM sleep NT* for Sweat glands Norepinephrine Locus ceruleus Regulates wakefulness and (NE) alertness. Dopamine Substantia nigra Destruction causes Parkinson’s disease. NT* of reward and addiction. Serotonin Raphe nuclei Regulation of mood, sleep, and appetite. The highest concentration is in Platelets, enterochromaffin cells, and myenteric plexus. *NT: Neurotransmitter","sourceRef":"Question Pages 9-9; Solution Pages 25-25","sourcePage":9,"sourcePageEnd":9},{"id":"physiology-1-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which neurotransmitter is primarily responsible for inhibiting motor neuron activity at the level of the spinal cord to regulate muscle tone and reflexes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GABA"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glutamate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glycine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Acetylcholine"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Glycine","explanation":"At the spinal cord level: • Glycine acts as a primary inhibitory neurotransmitter. • It plays a crucial role in regulating motor neuron activity by hyperpolarising postsynaptic neurons, thereby reducing their excitability. • Dysfunction or deficiency in glycine can lead to increased motor neuron excitability, resulting in symptoms such as muscle spasms and exaggerated reflexes. In CNS: • Glycine has both inhibitory and excitatory action in the central nervous system. • A small amount of extracellular glycine is essential for the proper functioning of the NMDA channel. • Although glycine is an inhibitory neurotransmitter, it is necessary to activate the NMDA receptor, which plays a role in excitatory neurotransmission. GABA (Option A) is an inhibitory neurotransmitter primarily found in the brain and less involved in spinal motor neuron regulation than glycine. Glutamate (Option B) is an excitatory neurotransmitter in the brain that facilitates neuronal signalling. Acetylcholine (Option D) is involved in neuromuscular transmission at the neuromuscular junction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 9-9; Solution Pages 25-26","sourcePage":9,"sourcePageEnd":9},{"id":"physiology-1-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":15,"question":"25-year-old patient with generalized anxiety disorder presents with persistent insomnia. Which neurotransmitter is targeted to promote relaxation and reduce excitability?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glutamate and aspartate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dopamine and serotonin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endorphins and substance P"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA)","explanation":"Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA), an inhibitory neurotransmitter, plays a pivotal role in reducing neuronal excitability and promoting relaxation. Classification of Neurotransmitters based on Functions: Table rendering failed Both Excitatory and Inhibitory: • Acetylcholine (ACh) has excitatory effects in certain areas of the nervous system (e.g., neuromuscular junction) and inhibitory effects (e.g., cardiac autonomic neurons). • Glycine has Inhibitory effects in the spinal cord and facilitates NMDA receptors in the CNS, indirectly causing Excitatory effects. Classification Based on Chemical Structure: Class 1 Choline & Acetyl CoA: Acetylcholine (ACh) Class 2 Biogenic Amines: Norepinephrine, Dopamine, Serotonin, Histamine Class 3 Amino Acid Neurotransmitters: Glutamate, GABA, Aspartate, Glycine Class 4 Purines and Gases: ATP, NO, CO Peptide Neurotransmitters: Substance P, Neuropeptide Y","sourceRef":"Question Pages 10-10; Solution Pages 26-27","sourcePage":10,"sourcePageEnd":10},{"id":"physiology-1-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 40-year-old patient presents with severe muscle stiffness and spasms, including trismus and rigidity, after stepping on a rusty nail two weeks ago. Which toxin is responsible for these symptoms by interfering with neurotransmitter release at the neuromuscular junction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Botulinum toxin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tetanus toxin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Strychnine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Curare"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Tetanus toxin","explanation":"The clinical presentation of severe muscle stiffness, trismus, and muscle spasms following an injury with a rusty nail suggests tetanus, caused by the tetanus toxin (tetanospasmin). Tetanus toxin is produced by Clostridium tetani bacteria in soil and enters the body through wounds. Mechanism of Tetanus Toxin at the synapse: • Tetanus neurotoxin (TeNT) binds to the presynaptic membrane of the neuromuscular junction, is internalised, and then transported retroaxonally to the spinal cord. • It induces Spastic paralysis due to the blockade of neurotransmitter releases such as GABA and glycine from presynaptic terminals of spinal inhibitory interneurons, leading to unopposed excitation of motor neurons and muscle rigidity. The toxin breaks down synaptobrevin protein through its endopeptidase activity. Botulinum toxins (Option A): • These are potent neurotoxins produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. • There are several types, with types A, B, and E being toxic to humans. • Mechanism : Botulinum toxins A and E cleave SNAP-25 (synaptosome-associated protein-25), and B cleave Synaptobrevin, which are presynaptic membrane proteins essential for releasing acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction leading to Flaccid paralysis. Strychnine (Option C): • Strychnine is a naturally occurring alkaloid found in the seeds of plant Strychnos nux-vomica. • It acts by blocking glycine receptors in the spinal cord, resulting in exaggerated muscle contractions (spasms) and potentially severe muscle rigidity. Curare (Option D): • Curare is a neuromuscular blocking agent that competitively blocks acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, leading to muscle paralysis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 10-10; Solution Pages 27-28","sourcePage":10,"sourcePageEnd":10},{"id":"physiology-1-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which protein, essential for regulating neurotransmitter release at the neuromuscular junction, prevents the premature fusion of synaptic vesicles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Synaptobrevin"},{"letter":"B","text":"SNAP-25"},{"letter":"C","text":"Syntaxin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Complexin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Complexin","explanation":"In the presynaptic membrane mechanism, complexin binds to the SNARE complex, preventing premature fusion of synaptic vesicles with the presynaptic membrane until calcium influx triggers its dissociation. This regulatory function ensures that neurotransmitter release occurs at the appropriate time and in response to neuronal activity. Pre synaptic membrane Mechanism: • Docking stage : Synaptobrevin (V-SNARE) on synaptic vesicles binds to syntaxin and SNAP-25 (t-SNARE) on the presynaptic membrane. (Option A, B, C) • ATP and SM protein : ATP hydrolysis provides energy for protein coiling. SM proteins (Sec1/MUNC 18) regulate SNARE protein interactions. • Priming stages : Primary priming : Protein coils bring vesicles closer to the presynaptic membrane. Secondary priming : Complexin stabilises the SNARE complex, preventing premature fusion. • Primary priming : Protein coils bring vesicles closer to the presynaptic membrane. • Secondary priming : Complexin stabilises the SNARE complex, preventing premature fusion. • Primary priming : Protein coils bring vesicles closer to the presynaptic membrane. • Secondary priming : Complexin stabilises the SNARE complex, preventing premature fusion. • Calcium regulation : P/Q and N-type calcium channels open upon stimulation, allowing calcium influx, which removes complexin. • Fusion stage : Synaptobrevin and syntaxin further coil with SNAP-25, facilitating the fusion of vesicular and presynaptic membranes. • Release : Fusion leads to neurotransmitter release into the synaptic cleft.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 10-10; Solution Pages 28-29","sourcePage":10,"sourcePageEnd":10},{"id":"physiology-1-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following types of vesicle at the synaptic terminal contain glutamate neurotransmitter ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Small clear vesicles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Large dense-core vesicles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Small dense-core vesicles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endosomes"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Small clear vesicles","explanation":"Small clear vesicles contain glutamate along with other neurotransmitters. • The vesicles and proteins within them are mostly synthesised in the neuronal cell body and transported by fast axoplasmic transport. • Small vesicles are located near the synaptic cleft and discharge their contents into active zones. Types of vesicles Neurotransmitters Small clear vesicles Acetylcholine, GABA, glutamate Small dense core vesicles (Option C) Catecholamines(eg:dopamine,epinephrine and norepinephrine) Large dense core vesicles (Option B) Neuropeptides Endosomes (Option D): It plays an important role in endocytosis but has No role in neurotransmitter release.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 11-11; Solution Pages 29-30","sourcePage":11,"sourcePageEnd":11},{"id":"physiology-1-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A researcher studying the precision of synaptic connections between neurons focuses on how specific neurons target each other for communication. Which of the following concepts refers to the precise targeting of synaptic connections to ensure accurate neuronal communication?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Synaptic specificity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Active zones"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synaptobrevin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Postsynaptic density"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Synaptic specificity","explanation":"Synaptic specificity refers to the precise targeting of neurons to form connections with the appropriate partner neurons. • It involves mechanisms that ensure accurate and specific communication between neurons, which is crucial for proper neural network formation and function. • It is maintained by neurexins and neuroligins interaction, which stabilise synapses. Neurexins are presynaptic cell adhesion molecules, while neuroligins are located on the postsynaptic membrane. Active zones (Option B): These are specialised regions in the presynaptic terminal where neurotransmitter release occurs. They are crucial for the release process but do not directly involve the specific targeting or connection of neurons. Synaptobrevin (Option C): This protein facilitates the release of neurotransmitters from vesicles in the presynaptic terminal by aiding in vesicle fusion. It does not contribute to the specific targeting or connection of neurons. Postsynaptic density (Option D): This region on the postsynaptic membrane contains receptors and signalling proteins crucial for organising postsynaptic responses. It does not address neuronal targeting or synaptic connection precision.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 11-11; Solution Pages 30-31","sourcePage":11,"sourcePageEnd":11},{"id":"physiology-1-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A researcher studying synaptic transmission observes that communication at a particular synapse is both rapid and bidirectional. What is the most likely synapse?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chemical synapse"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axo Axonal synapse"},{"letter":"C","text":"Axodendritic synapse"},{"letter":"D","text":"Electrical synapses"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Electrical synapses","explanation":"Electrical synapses are rapid and bidirectional due to ‘Gap junctions’. Types of Synapses: Aspects Electrical synapse Chemical synapse ( Option A ) Occurrence Rare Most common Width of synaptic Narrow Wide cleft Neurotransmitters Absent Present Gap junctions Present Absent Synaptic transmis No time delay Time delay is present sion Transmission Bidirectional Unidirectional direction Component Pre-synaptic and Post-synaptic Pre-synaptic cleft and membranes (connected by gap post-synaptic cleft junctions) Types - 3 types: Axodendritic (Option C) Axoaxonal (Option B) Axosomatic","sourceRef":"Question Pages 11-11; Solution Pages 31-32","sourcePage":11,"sourcePageEnd":11},{"id":"physiology-1-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-1","chapter":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topic":"Synapse and Synaptic Inhibition","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 52-year-old male patient complains of visual disturbances and difficulty walking. On detailed examination, the patient is diagnosed with multiple sclerosis that occurs due to demyelination of neurons. Which of the following cells is involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Microglia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Oligodendrocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Schwann cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Astrocytes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Oligodendrocytes","explanation":"• Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a central nervous system (CNS) disorder characterised by the degeneration of oligodendrocytes, which are responsible for forming myelin sheaths around nerve fibres. • Loss of myelin leads to leakage of K+ through voltage-gated channels, which in turn leads to hyperpolarisation or abnormal depolarisation, impaired nerve signal conduction, and symptoms such as paraparesis, paresthesia, optic neuritis, dysarthria, and dysphagia. Myelination of CNS and PNS Characteristics Peripheral nervous system Central nervous system Cells involved in Myelination One Schwann cell for one neuron. One Oligodendrocyte for multiple neurons. Demyelinating disease Localised disease (e.g., Diffuse disease (e.g., Multiple Guillain-Barré Syndrome) Sclerosis) Covering of unmyelinated Surrounded by the Schwann cell Bare (no myelin sheath) neuron process. Microglia (Option A) are non-neural cells that act like scavenger cells and remove debris formed by infection, injury, and disease; they have no role in myelination. Astrocytes (Option D) are supporting cells that provide nutrition and maintain glutamate levels but don’t have a role in myelin sheath formation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 11-12; Solution Pages 32-33","sourcePage":11,"sourcePageEnd":12},{"id":"physiology-2-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 30-year-old patient reports that rubbing the injured area provides him temporary relief from pain. According to the gate-control theory of pain, what is the primary mechanism behind this relief?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased activation of nociceptive C fibers to reduce pain perception"},{"letter":"B","text":"Simultaneous activation of innocuous cutaneous mechanoreceptors"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aα and Aβ fibers inhibit pain signals from C fibers in the ventral horn of spinal cord"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inhibition of Aa and Aß fibers reduces the transmission of pain signals to the brain"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Simultaneous activation of innocuous cutaneous mechanoreceptors","explanation":"Rubbing or shaking an injured area decreases the pain due to the injury. This is due to simultaneous activation of innocuous cutaneous mechanoreceptors thereby reducing pain by affecting dorsal horn neuron responsiveness. This is called the gate-control mechanism of pain modulation. Gate control theory: Developed By Patrick David Wall and Ronald Melzack • It suggests that pain perception is influenced by the interaction of different nerve fibers in the spinal cord. • Large-diameter Aα and Aβ fibers inhibit pain signals from C fibers in the dorsal horn of spinal cord by activating inhibitory interneurons, effectively \"closing the gate\". ( Option C, D ruled out) • When small-diameter fibers are more active, they \"open the gate,\" allowing pain signals to reach the brain. Influencing Factors: • Psychological factors, including cognitive and emotional states affect gate control. • Depression can keep the gate open more often, increasing pain perception • Unhealthy lifestyles can also result in open gates. Application: • According to the gate-control theory, electrical stimulation of large nerve fibers should inhibit pain induced by C fibers alone. • Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation (TENS) uses electrodes to activate Aa and Aß fibers near the injury, thereby using the gate-control mechanism for pain relief. • Acupuncture. Analgesic systems: Table rendering failed Increased activation of nociceptive C fibres to reduce pain perception ( Option A) is not correct as increased activation of nociceptive C fibres would actually enhance pain perception, not reduce it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 34-34; Solution Pages 46-47","sourcePage":34,"sourcePageEnd":34},{"id":"physiology-2-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Action potentials in an afferent fiber from a mechanoreceptor of a single sensory unit is recorded in response to pressure stimulus. Which of the following principle is shown here:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Labelled line principle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Weber-Fechner Law"},{"letter":"C","text":"Frequency coding principle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Concept of specific nerve energy"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Frequency coding principle","explanation":"The principle shown here is the Frequency Coding Principle. • The Frequency Coding Principle states that the intensity of a stimulus is encoded by the frequency of action potentials generated in the afferent fibers. • As pressure increases, the firing rate of action potentials in the mechanoreceptor increases, conveying information about the strength of the stimulus • Action potentials in an afferent fiber from a mechanoreceptor of a single sensory unit increase in frequency as branches of the afferent neuron are stimulated by pressure of increasing magnitude. Labelled Line Principle (Option A) refers to the idea that specific pathways in the nervous system convey specific types of sensory information. Weber-Fechner Law (Option B) relates to the perception of changes in stimulus intensity and the relationship between the actual change and perceived change. Concept of Specific Nerve Energy (Option D) suggests that each type of sensory nerve fiber is dedicated to a specific type of sensory information, regardless of how it is activated.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 34-34; Solution Pages 47-48","sourcePage":34,"sourcePageEnd":34},{"id":"physiology-2-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The receptive fields (shown as red areas on fingertips) and firing rate of four different types of mechanoreceptors supplied by FA1, FA2, SA1 and SA2 afferent are shown here. The figure marked with B is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pacinian corpuscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meissner’s corpuscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Merkel’s disc"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ruffini’s ending"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Merkel’s disc","explanation":"The figure marked with B represents Merkel's disc The human hand contains four types of mechanoreceptors that work together to sense contact with objects: • Merkel cells and Ruffini endings: Activate for steady pressure. • Meissner and Pacinian corpuscles: Activate for tingling and vibratory sensations. Location of a stimulus is encoded by spatial distribution of the population of receptors activated. A receptor fires only when the skin close to its sensory terminals is touched. The receptive fields of mechanoreceptors (shown as red areas on fingertips) differ in size and response to touch. • Merkel cells and Meissner corpuscles provide the most precise localization as they have the smallest receptive fields and are most sensitive to pressure applied by a small probe. Stimulus intensity is signaled by firing rates of individual receptors; duration of stimulus is signaled by time course of firing. The spike trains indicate action potentials elicited by pressure from a small probe at the center of each receptive field. • Meissner and Pacinian corpuscles adapt rapidly, the others adapt slowly Accommodation: Refers to a gradual increase in threshold caused by prolonged slowly rising sub-threshold nerve depolarization, resulting from the inactivation of sodium channels. Adaptation: If a supra-threshold stimulus persists unchanged for several minutes without a change in position or amplitude, the neural response diminishes and sensation is lost, a condition called receptor adaptation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 34-35; Solution Pages 48-49","sourcePage":34,"sourcePageEnd":35},{"id":"physiology-2-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A patient with paraplegia uses thigh stimulation to manage bladder and bowel control, leading to involuntary evacuation. Which reflex is being utilized?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Righting reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mass reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hopping reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Placing reflex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Mass reflex","explanation":"Mass reflex is due to excessive activity in the spinal cord causing massive discharge in large portions of the cord. Mass reflex can sometimes be used to give patients with paraplegia a degree of bladder and bowel control. They can be trained to initiate urination and defecation by stroking or pinching their thighs, thus producing an intentional mass reflex. Refle Description Key Points x Mass Excessive activity in the spinal cord Results from activation of large Refl causing massive discharge in large numbers of reverberating circuits in ex portions of the cord. (Option B) the cord at once. Can last for minutes Stimulus: strong pain stimulus or excessive filling of a viscus (e.g., bladder or gut). Effects: Strong flexor spasm in skeletal muscles, evacuation of colon and bladder, increased arterial pressure, profuse sweating. Right Reflex involving uncoordinated Demonstrates complex reflexes ing R movements to raise the body to a associated with posture integrated eflex standing position when laid on the in the spinal cord. side. (Option A ruled out) Animals with a healed transected thoracic cord can right itself from lying position and even walk using both forelimbs and hindlimbs. Consists of: Labyrinthine Righting Reflex, Neck Righting Reflex, Body Righting Reflex, Limb Righting Reflex, Optical Righting Reflex. Hopp Short steps taken by an individual to Helps maintain balance and stability ing R balance the body when pushed after lateral push. eflex laterally. (Option C ruled out) Impaired following decortication. Indicates that motor cortex is the center for integration of hopping reactions. Placi Placing feet firmly on the ground to Ensures stability after the initial ng R attain and maintain a new stable balance provided by the hopping eflex position after lateral push. (Option D reaction. ruled out) Impaired following decortication. Indicates that motor cortex is the center for integration of placing reactions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 35-35; Solution Pages 49-50","sourcePage":35,"sourcePageEnd":35},{"id":"physiology-2-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 20 years old man is brought to the emergency room after stepping on a nail while walking barefoot. Upon examination, the following observations are made, Which of the following best describes the reflex pathways involved in this scenario? Right foot: Immediately withdraws upon being touched. Left foot: Planted firmly on the ground.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Monosynaptic stretch reflex Polysynaptic crossed extensor reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Polysynaptic flexor reflex Monosynaptic stretch reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Polysynaptic flexor reflex Polysynaptic crossed extensor reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Polysynaptic flexor reflex Monosynaptic stretch reflex"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Right - Polysynaptic flexor reflex; Left - Polysynaptic crossed extensor reflex.","explanation":"• Right foot withdrawal: Stepping on a nail triggers the withdrawal reflex, also known as the flexor reflex, which is polysynaptic. • Left foot response: The withdrawal reflex also involves a crossed extensor reflex which is polysynaptic reflex. Reflex Withdrawal Reflex Flexor Reflex Crossed Extensor Reflex Descri A protective reflex that A withdrawal reflex in A component of the ption causes withdrawal of a which the primary withdrawal reflex that limb or body part from a response is flexion of a occurs in the opposite stimulus. limb. limb. Rece Nociceptors (e.g., pain Nociceptors Nociceptors in the ptor receptors) contralateral limb are indirectly activated. Affere Group III and IV (pain) Group III and IV (pain) Signals are relayed from nt Fib and Group II (pressure) and Group II (pressure) the stimulated side. er Synap Polysynaptic (Options A, Polysynaptic Polysynaptic (Option B ses D ruled out) ruledout) Effere α-motor neurons to flexors α-motor neurons to flexors α-motor neurons to nt Fib extensors er Resp Flexion of ipsilateral limb Contraction of flexor Extension of the onse or body part to move it muscles and relaxation of contralateral limb. away from the stimulus. extensor muscles in the stimulated limb. Clinic It is a protective Part of the withdrawal Provides support and al Sig mechanism, absence or reflex and essential for balance during withdrawal nifica weakness of this can rapid response to painful of the opposite limb. nce indicate spinal cord injury stimuli. or dysfunction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 35-36; Solution Pages 50-51","sourcePage":35,"sourcePageEnd":36},{"id":"physiology-2-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 30 years old woman presents with a history of multiple sclerosis. Upon examination, she exhibits ankle clonus when the examiner briskly dorsiflexes her foot. What is the most likely cause of clonus in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoactive stretch reflexes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased GABAergic activity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyperactive muscle spindles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhanced Golgi tendon organ activation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Hyperactive muscle spindles","explanation":"• The patient's history of multiple sclerosis and the presence of ankle clonus strongly suggest damage to the descending motor pathways. • This damage leads to hyperactive muscle spindles and, consequently, clonus. • Clonus is characterized by rhythmic muscle contractions in response to sustained stretch. Table rendering failed Increased GABAergic activity (Option B) in the spinal cord would actually reduce clonus as GABA is an inhibitory neurotransmitter. Enhanced Golgi tendon organ activation (Option D): Golgi tendon organs (GTOs) are involved in the inverse stretch reflex, which causes muscle relaxation in response to increased tension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 36-36; Solution Pages 51-52","sourcePage":36,"sourcePageEnd":36},{"id":"physiology-2-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 35 year old weightlifter is lifting a heavier weight than usual and feels a sudden, intense muscle contraction in his biceps, the muscle suddenly relaxes, preventing a potential muscle tear. Which reflex is responsible for this protective muscle relaxation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inverse stretch reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Withdrawal reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stretch reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Crossed extensor reflex"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Inverse stretch reflex","explanation":"• Inverse Stretch Reflex is activated when there is excessive tension in a muscle, as detected by the Golgi tendon organ (GTO). • When the tension becomes too high, the GTO sends signals to the spinal cord, which in turn inhibits the alpha motor neurons innervating the same muscle, leading to muscle relaxation. Feature Stretch Reflex (Option C ruled out) Inverse Stretch Reflex Receptor Muscle Spindle Golgi Tendon Organ Stimulus Muscle Stretch Muscle Tension Response Muscle Contraction Muscle Relaxation Function Maintain Muscle Length, Posture Protect from Excessive Force Type of Reflex Monosynaptic Polysynaptic Withdrawal Reflex (Option B) is a protective mechanism that causes a body part to automatically move away from a source of pain or discomfort. Crossed extensor reflex (Option D) causes the opposite limb to extend when a withdrawal reflex is initiated in one limb.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 36-36; Solution Pages 52-53","sourcePage":36,"sourcePageEnd":36},{"id":"physiology-2-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45 year old weightlifter is attempting a deadlift. As he lifts the weight, he feels a sudden, intense pain in his hamstring muscle and immediately drops the weight. What is the role of the Golgi tendon organ in this scenario?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"To directly cause muscle contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"To prevent muscle damage by causing reflex inhibition of the contracting muscle."},{"letter":"C","text":"To increase the sensitivity of muscle spindles"},{"letter":"D","text":"To send information about muscle length to the brain"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) To prevent muscle damage by causing reflex inhibition of the contracting muscle.","explanation":"The Golgi tendon organ, when activated by excessive muscle tension, triggers a protective reflex that inhibits the contracting muscle, preventing tendon rupture or muscle tearing. Golgi tendon organ Location and Muscle tendons. Structure Encapsulated sensory receptors. Around 10-15 muscle fibers are connected. Stimulus Tension in the muscle, as reflected by tension in the tendon. Sudden, strong muscle contractions increase tension in the tendon Function Control and prevent excessive muscle tension. Operates as a negative feedback system. Reflex Pathway When stimulated, signals are transmitted to the spinal cord, triggering the inverse stretch reflex (also called autogenic inhibition). The signal activates an inhibitory interneuron in the spinal cord. The interneuron inhibits the anterior motor neuron of the respective muscle, causing relaxation, this is a bisynaptic pathway. This protects the muscle and tendon from excessive tension that might cause tearing. Afferent Fiber Golgi tendon organ to the spinal cord via large, fast-conducting type Ib nerve fibers. Vs Muscle Muscle spindle - muscle length. (Option D ruled out) spindle Goldi tendon organ - muscle tension. To directly cause muscle contraction (Option A) : Alpha motor neurons are responsible for muscle contraction To increase the sensitivity of muscle spindles (Option C): Gamma motor neurons are responsible for regulating the sensitivity of muscle spindles","sourceRef":"Question Pages 36-37; Solution Pages 53-54","sourcePage":36,"sourcePageEnd":37},{"id":"physiology-2-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 28 year old man presents with experience of prolonged episodes of tingling and numbness in his right arm. A reflex hammer test on the patient's patellar tendon noted a weaker than normal knee-jerk reflex and decision to use the Jendrassik maneuver to enhance the reflex response was taken. Which of the following is directly activated by the Jendrassik maneuver?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"α-motor neuron"},{"letter":"B","text":"γ-motor neuron"},{"letter":"C","text":"Golgi tendon organ"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extrafusal muscle fiber"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) γ-motor neuron","explanation":"• The Jendrassik maneuver, which can involve actions such as clenching the teeth or pulling on the hands, activates γ-motor neurons. • γ-motor neurons, also called gamma efferent fibers, innervate intrafusal muscle fibers. Muscle contractio n Feature Direct Indirect Definition Occurs when α-motor neurons Occurs when the muscle spindle is are directly stimulated. activated, leading to the activation of α-motor neurons via the stretch reflex pathway. Pathway Monosynaptic Monosynaptic Purpose Voluntary movements Involuntary, provides damping to smooth muscle contractions. Example Picking up an object. Maintaining posture, reacting to unexpected changes in load on a muscle (e.g., catching a ball). Jendrassik Maneuver: • Clinical technique used to enhance the sensitivity of the stretch reflex (and thus, the response of the muscle spindle). • The maneuver involves the patient performing an isometric contraction in a muscle group distant from the one being tested. Clenching the teeth Locking the fingers and pulling the hands apart Squeezing the knees together. • Clenching the teeth • Locking the fingers and pulling the hands apart • Squeezing the knees together. • The maneuver is thought to increase γ-motor neuron activity, making the muscle spindles more sensitive to stretch. • Clenching the teeth • Locking the fingers and pulling the hands apart • Squeezing the knees together. Alpha-Gamma Coactivation: • On voluntary muscle contraction, the brain often signals both α- and γ-motor neurons simultaneously. This is known as alpha-gamma coactivation. • Activating γ-motor neurons causes intrafusal fibers to contract, maintaining tension in the spindle ensuring that spindle remains sensitive to stretch even during voluntary contractions. • Example: When catching a ball, alpha-gamma coactivation keeps the muscle spindles responsive and allows for rapid adjustments to maintain grip and arm position as the weight of the ball impacts the hand. α-motor neuron (Option A): While the knee-jerk reflex does involve the activation of α-motor neurons to cause the quadriceps muscle to contract, the Jendrassik maneuver does not directly activate these neurons. Golgi tendon organ (Option C): the sensitivity of the Golgi tendon organ is not directly affected by the Jendrassik maneuver. Extrafusal muscle fibers (Option D) are not directly activated by the Jendrassik maneuver.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 37-37; Solution Pages 54-56","sourcePage":37,"sourcePageEnd":37},{"id":"physiology-2-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following best describes the sequence of events in the stretch reflex pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinal cord → Muscle spindle → α-motor neuron → Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscle spindle → Spinal cord → α-motor neuron → Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal cord→ α-motor neuron →Muscle spindle →Muscle contraction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Muscle spindle →α-motor neuron → Spinal cord → Muscle contraction"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Muscle spindle → Spinal cord → α-motor neuron → Muscle contraction","explanation":"Stretch reflex pathway: Regulates muscle length and contributes to postural control. It is initiated by the stretching of muscle, which activates specialised sensory receptors. • Muscle Spindles: Consists of intrafusal muscle fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers), Sensory nerve endings (group Ia and II afferent fibers), and Gamma motor neuron endings • Consists of intrafusal muscle fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers), • Sensory nerve endings (group Ia and II afferent fibers), and • Gamma motor neuron endings • Afferent Limb: When a muscle is stretched, the muscle spindles are also stretched, stimulating the sensory endings. This stretch-induced stimulation generates receptor potentials that trigger action potentials in the afferent fibers. • When a muscle is stretched, the muscle spindles are also stretched, stimulating the sensory endings. • This stretch-induced stimulation generates receptor potentials that trigger action potentials in the afferent fibers. • Integration Center: The Ia afferent fibers, have a large diameter and, conduct action potentials, transmit signals to the spinal cord through the dorsal roots. Within the spinal cord, these fibers form direct (monosynaptic) connections with alpha motor neurons located in the anterior horn. • The Ia afferent fibers, have a large diameter and, conduct action potentials, transmit signals to the spinal cord through the dorsal roots. • Within the spinal cord, these fibers form direct (monosynaptic) connections with alpha motor neurons located in the anterior horn. • Efferent Limb: The activated alpha motor neurons send signals through axons, which exit the spinal cord and travel to the extrafusal muscle fibers. • The activated alpha motor neurons send signals through axons, which exit the spinal cord and travel to the extrafusal muscle fibers. • Effector: The arrival of action potentials at the neuromuscular junctions causes the release of acetylcholine, initiating muscle fiber contraction. This counteracts the initial stretch by shortening the muscle, thus restoring its original length. • The arrival of action potentials at the neuromuscular junctions causes the release of acetylcholine, initiating muscle fiber contraction. • This counteracts the initial stretch by shortening the muscle, thus restoring its original length. • Consists of intrafusal muscle fibers (nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers), • Sensory nerve endings (group Ia and II afferent fibers), and • Gamma motor neuron endings • When a muscle is stretched, the muscle spindles are also stretched, stimulating the sensory endings. • This stretch-induced stimulation generates receptor potentials that trigger action potentials in the afferent fibers. • The Ia afferent fibers, have a large diameter and, conduct action potentials, transmit signals to the spinal cord through the dorsal roots. • Within the spinal cord, these fibers form direct (monosynaptic) connections with alpha motor neurons located in the anterior horn. • The activated alpha motor neurons send signals through axons, which exit the spinal cord and travel to the extrafusal muscle fibers. • The arrival of action potentials at the neuromuscular junctions causes the release of acetylcholine, initiating muscle fiber contraction. • This counteracts the initial stretch by shortening the muscle, thus restoring its original length.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 37-38; Solution Pages 56-58","sourcePage":37,"sourcePageEnd":38},{"id":"physiology-2-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45 year old man presents with complaints of muscle weakness and difficulty maintaining balance. Upon examination, the physician notices that the patient has diminished reflexes. Which of the following nerve fibers is responsible for transmitting information about the velocity and length of muscle stretch from the muscle spindle to the spinal cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type Ib fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type II fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aγ fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Type Ia fibers"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Type Ia fibers","explanation":"• Type Ia fibers are large, rapidly conducting sensory fibers that originate from the central portion of both nuclear bag and nuclear chain fibers within the muscle spindle. • These fibers are responsible for transmitting information about the velocity and length of muscle stretch to the spinal cord. Afferent (Sensor Efferent (Motor) y) Fi Type Ia fibers Type II fibers Aγ fibers γ-dynamic (γ-d) γ-static (γ-s) b fibers fibers e r T y p e D Large, rapidly Smaller sensory Smaller motor Excite nuclear Excite nuclear e conducting fibers that fibers that bag intrafusal chain intrafusal s fibers innervate innervate the fibers. fibers. cr nuclear chain intrafusal ip fibers. fibers. ti o n F Transmit informa Transmit informa Adjusts sensit Enhance the Enhance the u tion about tion about static ivity of the dynamic respon static response n velocity and length of the muscle spindl se (sensitivity to (sensitivity to ct length of muscle. e. the velocity of steady-state io muscle stretch. (Option B ruled (Option C muscle stretch). muscle length). n out) ruled out) O Annulospiral Secondary Gamma moto ri (primary) endings located r neurons in gi endings wrappe adjacent to the the anterior n d around the centers of static horns of the center of nuclear bag and spinal cord. dynamic and nuclear chain static nuclear fibers. bag fibers and nuclear chain fibers. Afferent and Efferent Fibres\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7851\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"367\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/fSsUUWJu7ODggEJfqSwc1744010595.png\" width=\"500\" /> Afferent Connections of the Muscle Spindle Efferent Connections of the Muscle Spindle Type Ib fibers (Option A) are large and rapidly conducting fibers, that originate from the Golgi tendon organs and transmit information about muscle tension, not muscle length.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 38-38; Solution Pages 58-59","sourcePage":38,"sourcePageEnd":38},{"id":"physiology-2-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 32 years old man came for an evaluation of muscle spasms and difficulty with fine motor tasks. Upon examination, the patient has hyperactive deep tendon reflexes and experiences a persistent tremor during voluntary movements. Which of the following types of intrafusal muscle fibers, would most likely cause this hyperactive deep tendon reflex?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dynamic nuclear bag fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Static nuclear bag fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Nuclear chain fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extrafusal fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Dynamic nuclear bag fibers","explanation":"• Dynamic nuclear bag fibers are highly sensitive to the velocity of muscle stretch. • When a muscle is stretched rapidly, these fibers fire strongly, leading to a robust activation of the stretch reflex, which causes exaggerated stretch reflexes. Types of intrafusal fibers Nuclear Bag Fiber Respond to changes in muscle length (stretch). Two subtypes: dynamic and static. Dynamic Nuclear Detects real-time shortening/lengthening of a muscle Bag Fiber (i.e., the velocity). Static Nuclear Bag Detects sustained/maintained length of a muscle but Fiber plays no role in detecting velocity. (Option B ruled out) Nuclear Chain Fiber Thinner and shorter than nuclear bag fibers and lack a definite bag. Static response of the muscle spindle, providing information about the steady-state muscle length. (Option C ruled out) Extrafusal fibers (Option D) are regular contractile fibers of skeletal muscle responsible for generating force and movement. They are not a part of the muscle spindle and do not directly contribute to the stretch reflex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 38-38; Solution Pages 59-60","sourcePage":38,"sourcePageEnd":38},{"id":"physiology-2-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The muscle spindle is primarily involved in detecting changes in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Muscle tension"},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscle length"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skin stretch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Joint pressure"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Muscle length","explanation":"• Muscle spindles are sensory receptors found within skeletal muscle. • The function of a muscle spindle is to detect changes in muscle length, also called stretch. Muscle spindle receptor: Muscle Spindle Receptor Location Encapsulated sensory receptors in skeletal muscle, attached to tendons. Components Each spindle contains 3 to 12 intrafusal muscle fibers. Sensory Endings Stimulated by stretching. Types of Sensory Primary Afferent Endings (Annulospiral) : Sensitive to Endings velocity of muscle length change (dynamic response). Secondary Afferent Endings (Flower-Spray) : Sensitive to steady-state muscle length (static response). Types of Gamma Motor Gamma-Dynamic (γ-d) : Excites nuclear bag intrafusal Neurons fibers. Gamma-Static (γ-s) : Excites nuclear chain intrafusal fibers. Functions Regulating Muscle Length : Provides feedback to regulate muscle length. Damping : Smoothens muscle contractions and prevents jerkiness. Stabilizing Body Position : Maintains posture and balance by informing the spinal cord and motor centers about muscle length. Higher Motor Control : Information is transmitted to the cerebellum and cerebral cortex. Muscle Spindle Receptor\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7857\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"307\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/lIwELzoimQh1yDiUZ2681744015307.png\" width=\"500\" /> Muscle Tension (Option A): Golgi tendon organs are the receptors responsible for detecting muscle tension. Skin stretch (Option C) is detected by cutaneous mechanoreceptors, such as Ruffini Corpuscles. Joint Pressure (Option D): Joint capsule receptors are responsible for detecting joint pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 38-39; Solution Pages 60-61","sourcePage":38,"sourcePageEnd":39},{"id":"physiology-2-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 35 y/o presented after a car accident and complained of severe pain. They administered morphine, knowing it acts on the descending pain modulation system. Which of the following brain regions and neurotransmitters are correctly paired and activated by morphine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Periaqueductal grey (PAG) neurons - endorphins in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord."},{"letter":"B","text":"Periventricular nucleus neurons - norepinephrine in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord."},{"letter":"C","text":"Periaqueductal grey (PAG) neurons - directly release serotonin in the nucleus raphe magnus."},{"letter":"D","text":"Periventricular nucleus neurons - enkephalins in the rostral ventromedial medulla."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Periaqueductal grey (PAG) neurons - endorphins in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord.","explanation":"• The periaqueductal grey (PAG) matter region in the midbrain plays a crucial role in the descending pain modulation pathway. • When stimulated by opioids like morphine, PAG neurons activate neurons in the brainstem, the nucleus raphe magnus (which releases serotonin) and the rostral ventromedial medulla (which releases norepinephrine). • These neurons project to the dorsal horn, where they release their respective neurotransmitters to inhibit the activity of pain-transmitting neurons. Brain Region Neurotransmitter Released Site Effect Periaqueductal Endorphins Dorsal horn of Inhibits pain transmission grey (PAG) the spinal cord by activating enkephalin-c ontaining interneurons. Nucleus raphe Activates neurons that magnus release serotonin. Rostral ventrom Activates neurons that edial medulla release norepinephrine. Periventricular Endorphins Raphe Magnus Activates neurons that region (PV) nucleus release serotonin. (Option B and D ruled out) Periventricular nucleus neurons - enkephalins in the rostral ventromedial medulla (Option C) is not true as PAG does not directly release serotonin; it activates serotonergic neurons in the nucleus raphe magnus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 39-39; Solution Pages 61-62","sourcePage":39,"sourcePageEnd":39},{"id":"physiology-2-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A researcher is studying the sensory perception abilities of different body parts in humans. She observes that some areas of the body can detect finer details and small differences in stimuli better than others. Which of the following statements best explains the reason behind the high sensitivity and discriminatory abilities in certain body areas?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Small receptive fields and low receptor density."},{"letter":"B","text":"Large receptive fields and high receptor density."},{"letter":"C","text":"Small receptive fields and high receptor density."},{"letter":"D","text":"Large receptive fields and low receptor density."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Small receptive fields, high receptor density.","explanation":"Small receptive fields coupled with a high receptor density allow for precise localisation and detailed discrimination of sensory stimuli, making these areas highly sensitive. Classification Function Type Specific Receptor of Sensory Receptors Mechanorecept Detect mechanical Skin Tactile Free nerve endings, ors compression or stretching Sensibilities Expanded tip endings of the receptor or adjacent (epidermis and (Merkel's discs) tissues dermis) Spray endings, Ruffini's endings, Encapsulated endings (Meissner's corpuscles, Krause's corpuscles) Hair end-organs Deep Tissue Free nerve endings, Sensibilities Expanded tip endings, Spray endings, Ruffini's endings, Encapsulated endings (Pacinian corpuscles), Muscle endings (Muscle spindles, Golgi tendon receptors) Hearing Sound receptors of cochlea Equilibrium Vestibular receptors Arterial Pressur Baroreceptors of carotid e sinuses and aorta Thermorecepto Detect changes in Cold Cold receptors rs temperature (cold or warmth) Warmth Warm receptors Nociceptors Detect physical or Pain Free nerve endings (pain receptors) chemical damage in the tissue Electromagneti Detect light on the retina Vision Rods c Receptors of the eye Cones Chemoreceptor Detect various chemical Taste Receptors of taste buds s changes including taste, smell, blood oxygen levels, osmolality, and CO2 concentration Smell Receptors of olfactory epithelium Arterial Oxygen Receptors of aortic and carotid bodies Osmolality Neurons in or near supraoptic nuclei Blood CO2 Receptors in/on the surface of the medulla, aortic and carotid bodies Blood Glucose, Receptors in hypothalamu Amino Acids, s Fatty Acids Location Receptive Field Size Characteristics Fingertips Small Fine two-point discrimination, high precision Back Large Poorer two-point discrimination, low precision Area Receptor Density Sensitivity and Discrimination Fingertips, Lips, Tongue High Greater sensitivity, fine detail discrimination Back, Forearm Low Less sensitivity, coarse discrimination","sourceRef":"Question Pages 39-39; Solution Pages 62-64","sourcePage":39,"sourcePageEnd":39},{"id":"physiology-2-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 55-year-old patient develops a persistent burning pain in the left arm following a thalamic stroke. Which of the following statements about thalamic pain syndrome is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Due to damage to the spinothalamic tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Can result from both ischemic and hemorrhagic strokes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Typically short-lived and resolves within a few months"},{"letter":"D","text":"Antidepressants and anticonvulsants are commonly used for treatment"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Typically short-lived and resolves within a few months","explanation":"Thalamic pain syndrome typically starts within the first six months post stroke, but can develop even after ten years. It is persistent and potentially life-long pain that does not resolve. Definition Thalamic pain syndrome or Dejerine Roussy syndrome or Central post-stroke pain is a rare central neuropathic pain syndrome that occurs after infarction of the ventro-posterolateral thalamus Aetiology Vascular lesions affecting the central somatosensory system, most commonly ischemic or hemorrhagic stroke (Option B ruled out) Pathophysiol Damage to the spinothalamic tract or thalamus disrupts ogy sensory processing, leading to abnormal pain perception (Option A ruled out) Epidemiology Prevalence ranges from 8% to 46% Onset Typically within the first six months post-stroke, but can develop after ten years also Symptoms Sharp, burning and stabbing pain with hyperalgesia and allodynia; Pain is described as pricking, aching, freezing, shooting, etc. Diagnosis Based on history, clinical examination, and imaging: CT/MRI to confirm stroke Treatment Pharmacological: Antidepressants, Anticonvulsants, (Option D ruled out) Opioids, NMDA receptor antagonists Non-Pharmacological: Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS), Repetitive transcranial magnetic stimulation (rTMS), Deep brain stimulation (DBS) Motor cortex stimulation Prognosis Poor Persistent and potentially life-long pain (Option C) Complication Reduces quality of life s Depression or anxiety Sleep disturbance Drug dependence Poor social interactions Increased suicide risk","sourceRef":"Question Pages 40-40; Solution Pages 64-65","sourcePage":40,"sourcePageEnd":40},{"id":"physiology-2-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which statement correctly explains the localization capability of the paleospinothalamic pathway for pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The pathway allows precise localization of pain to specific points on the body"},{"letter":"B","text":"The pathway localizes pain only to major body parts, such as an arm or leg, due to its diffuse connectivity."},{"letter":"C","text":"The pathway is responsible for localizing temperature changes in the body."},{"letter":"D","text":"The pathway can localize pain to exact muscle groups involved in movement"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The pathway localizes pain only to major body parts, such as an arm or leg, due to its diffuse connectivity.","explanation":"Slow-chronic pain can usually be localized only to a major part of the body, such as one arm or leg, but not to a specific point on the arm or leg. This phenomenon is due to the multisynaptic, diffuse connectivity of the Paleo-Spinothalamic pathway, which is why patients have difficulty localizing the source in some chronic types of pain. Paleo-Spinothalamic Pathway Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 40-40; Solution Pages 65-66","sourcePage":40,"sourcePageEnd":40},{"id":"physiology-2-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Identify the correct statement about the neospinothalamic tract for pain:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It primarily carries slow pain sensations"},{"letter":"B","text":"The first-order neurons terminate mainly in lamina II of the dorsal horn"},{"letter":"C","text":"The neurotransmitter released by the Aδ fiber is glutamate"},{"letter":"D","text":"All fibers terminate exclusively in the reticular formation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The neurotransmitter released by the Aδ fiber is glutamate","explanation":"Glutamate is the primary neurotransmitter at Aδ fiber endings. Neo-Spinothalamic Pathway : Aspect Description Fiber Type Aδ (A-delta) fibers Function Carries fast pain sensations, primarily mechanical and acute thermal pain (Option A ruled out) First-Order Neurons Terminate mainly in lamina I of the dorsal horn of the spinal cord (Option B ruled out) Second-Order Cross to the opposite side of the spinal cord through the Neurons anterior commissure. Ascend in the anterolateral pathway. The fibers terminate mainly in the ventrobasal complex, and a few fibers also terminate in the posterior nuclear complex of the thalamus. Sparse projections to midbrain reticular formation, periaqueductal grey and hypothalamus (Option D ruled out) Third-Order Originate from specific thalamic nuclei. Neurons The signals are then transmitted to other basal areas of the brain as well as somatosensory cortex Localization of Pain Fast pain is better localised than slow-chronic pain. Exact localisation requires simultaneous stimulation of tactile receptors Neurotransmitter Glutamate (primary neurotransmitter at Aδ fiber endings) (Option C) Topographic In spinal cord: Organization Lower body fibers placed laterally Upper body fibers placed medially Sacral segment fibers occupy most lateral part Cervical segment fibers occupy most medial part Special Features Pain perception persists despite removing somatic sensory areas of the cerebral cortex, indicating that the brain stem, thalamus, and lower centers handle this function. The cortex only helps in interpreting pain quality, not perception.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 40-40; Solution Pages 66-67","sourcePage":40,"sourcePageEnd":40},{"id":"physiology-2-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A patient reports severe pain when lightly touched with a cotton swab. This symptom is most consistent with?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Allodynia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyperalgesia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Analgesia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoalgesia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Allodynia","explanation":"Allodynia refers to an excessive response to mild or normally innocuous stimuli. Example: Painful sensation from a light touch which is never painful. Table rendering failed Analgesia (Option C) refers to the complete loss of pain sensation. It may not be associated with the loss of other sensations. Hypoalgesia (Option D) refers to decreased sensitivity and raised threshold to painful stimuli.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 40-41; Solution Pages 67-68","sourcePage":40,"sourcePageEnd":41},{"id":"physiology-2-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 40 years old woman comes to the clinic complaining of severe, burning pain in her right hand following a chemical spill causing tissue damage at her workplace. Which of the following substances is responsible for her pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dopamine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Serotonin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bradykinin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Bradykinin","explanation":"Based on the above scenario, the patient most likely has a chemical burn. Bradykinin is involved in mediating the chemical nociceptive stimuli experienced by the patient. Chemical Nociceptive stimuli • Both TRPA1 and TRPV1 detects chemical stimuli • Bradykinin (most painful chemical stimuli)- B1 & B2 receptors (Option D) • Bradykinin is a potent inflammatory mediator that is released during tissue injury and contributes significantly to the sensation of pain • Chemical burns can cause the release of bradykinin and other inflammatory mediators, which activate pain pathways. Other chemical mediators: • Serotonin- 5HT receptors • Histamine- H3/H4 receptors • Nerve endings also have Acid-Sensing Ion Channel (ASIC) receptors that detect H+ and cause acid induced pain. Dopamine (Option A) is involved in motor pathway and not sensory Acetylcholine (Option B) is primarily involved in neuromuscular transmission and autonomic nervous system function Serotonin (Option C) is involved in the pain pathway, but is not the primary mediator in chemical nociceptive stimuli","sourceRef":"Question Pages 41-41; Solution Pages 68-68","sourcePage":41,"sourcePageEnd":41},{"id":"physiology-2-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":21,"question":"The TRPA1 receptors are activated by all of the following nociceptive stimuli except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mechanical stimuli"},{"letter":"B","text":"Heat"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cold"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chemical stimuli"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Heat","explanation":"Transient Receptor Potential Ankyrin 1 (TRPA1) is activated by mechanical, cold and chemical stimuli. Types of nociceptive stimuli and their receptors: • Nociceptors can be activated by mechanical, chemical or thermal stimuli. Mechanical Nociceptors: Respond to strong pressure (eg: from sharp objects) The receptors for mechanical stimuli at free nerve ending include:Transient Receptor Potential Ankyrin 1 (TRPA1) (also for cold and chemical stimuli) (Option A, C & D ruled out) Mechanical stimuli can also release ATP, which acts on purinergic receptors such as P2X and P2Y and cause pain Thermal Nociceptors: Cold detection: TRP Ankyrin 1 - It is sensitive when the temperature is < 17°C and TRP Melastatin 8 - It is sensitive when the temperature is between 8-28°C. Heat detection: TRP Vanilloid 1(also detects acid and chemicals) (Option B ruled out) - Activated when temperature is > 43°C, H+ ions and capsaicin (the active ingredient of chili). TRPV2/V3/V4 - It is activated when temperature is > 52°C. Chemical Nociceptors: Both TRPA1 and TRPV1 detects chemical stimuli Nerve endings also have Acid-Sensing Ion Channel (ASIC) receptors that detect H+ and cause acid induced pain Bradykinin (most painful chemical stimuli) - B1 & B2 receptors Serotonin - 5HT receptors Histamine - H3/H4 receptors","sourceRef":"Question Pages 41-41; Solution Pages 69-69","sourcePage":41,"sourcePageEnd":41},{"id":"physiology-2-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Nociceptors have their cell bodies located in which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal root ganglion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral horn of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral geniculate nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sympathetic ganglion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Dorsal root ganglion","explanation":"• Nociceptors arise from cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion that send one axon to the periphery and another to the spinal cord or brainstem. • Nociceptors (pain receptors) are basically the free nerve endings that detect pain sensations. • Location of nociceptors: Arise from cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion that send one axon to the periphery and another to the spinal cord or brainstem (Option A) . • Nociceptors activate Transient receptor potential (TRP) channels on nerve endings, which causes the neurons to depolarize and generate action potentials. Ventral horn of the spinal cord (Option B) contains motor neuron cell bodies. Lateral geniculate nucleus (Option C) is part of the thalamus and is involved in visual processing. Sympathetic ganglion (Option D) is part of the autonomic nervous system and does not contain the cell bodies of nociceptors .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 41-42; Solution Pages 69-70","sourcePage":41,"sourcePageEnd":42},{"id":"physiology-2-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which neurotransmitter is primarily involved in fast pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Substance P"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glutamate"},{"letter":"C","text":"GABA"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serotonin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Glutamate","explanation":"Nociceptors: • These are responsible for detection of pain. • These receptors are basically free nerve endings. • There are two types of pain: Fast pain (First Pain) Slow pain (Second Pain) Fiber Type Aδ fiber C fiber Myelinated Unmyelinated Large diameter (2-5 µm) Smaller diameter (0.4 - 1.2 µm) Location Body surface Deeper tissues Conduction Very rapid (~12- 35 m/s) Very slow (~ 0.5 - 2 m/s) velocity Neurotrans Glutamate (Option B) Substance P (Option A Ruled out) mitter Description Rapid response Delayed, dull, intense, diffuse and Mediates the discriminative aspects unpleasant feeling associated with of pain (localisation and intensity) noxious stimulus","sourceRef":"Question Pages 42-42; Solution Pages 70-71","sourcePage":42,"sourcePageEnd":42},{"id":"physiology-2-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which type of receptor is known for its rapid adaptation to stimuli, making it particularly effective at detecting vibrations and rapid changes in pressure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Merkel cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meissner's corpuscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ruffini endings"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pacinian corpuscles"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Pacinian corpuscles","explanation":"Pacinian receptors are rapidly adapting receptors sensitive to vibrations and rapid pressure changes Adaptation of receptors: • One of the most important characteristics of sensory receptors is that they can adapt partially or completely to any constant stimulus after a period of time. • When a continuous stimulus is applied to a receptor, the initial response occurs at a high impulse rate, then at a progressively slower rate, which ultimately decreases to very few or none at all. Mechanism: • Adaptation occurs in 2 ways : 1 In Rapidly Adapting Receptors It is a faster mechanism. The receptors are viscoelastic in nature so that when a stimulus is applied to one side of the receptor, it is transmitted instantly by the viscous component to the same side of the central nerve fiber, eliciting a response Within a few seconds, the fluid in the receptors redistributes and the response is no longer elicited 2 In Slowly Adapting Receptors It is a slower mechanism called accommodation This occurs directly in the nerve fiber. If there is a continued stimulus, then the tip of the receptor becomes accommodated to the stimulus. This occurs from the progressive inactivation of sodium channels in the nerve fiber membrane. Rapidly adapting receptors Pacinian, Meissner’s and hearing organ Slow adapting receptors Merkel’s, Ruffini’s and free nerve endings (Option A & C ruled out) Meissner’s corpuscles (Option B) are also rapidly adapting receptors but are sensitive to light touch rather than vibrations.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 42-42; Solution Pages 71-72","sourcePage":42,"sourcePageEnd":42},{"id":"physiology-2-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A researcher is studying various sensory receptors and their responses to different stimuli. During an experiment with pacinian corpuscle, the researcher notes that the intensity of the initial electrical response in the receptor is proportionate with the mechanical pressure applied. What is the initial graded electrical response observed in sensory receptors upon stimulus application?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Action potential"},{"letter":"B","text":"Receptor potential"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synaptic potential"},{"letter":"D","text":"Resting potential"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Receptor potential","explanation":"Receptor potential is the graded depolarizing potential in a sensory receptor in response to a stimulus. Its magnitude varies with the intensity of the stimulus. Receptor Potentials • A receptor potential is a type of graded potential that occurs in sensory receptors. It is a local, non-propagated change in membrane potential that occurs in response to a stimulus. • Receptor potential is not an action potential; instead, it causes the sensory neuron to fire action potentials. • Once the receptor potential reaches a critical threshold, it triggers an action potential at the sensory nerve's first node of Ranvier. Receptor Potential\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7858\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"430\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/4dckPzu4YYs0VLd Sl2ED1744015597.png\" width=\"500\" /> Factors affecting receptor potential: • Proportional Response: Amplitude of receptor potential is generally proportional to stimulus intensity. • Nature: Non-propagated, depolarizing potential in sensory receptors. • Threshold: When it reaches a critical level, an action potential is generated. • Stimulus Intensity: Stronger stimuli lead to larger receptor potentials. Mechanism of receptor potential generation in Pacinian Corpuscle Table rendering failed sensory nerve fiber\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7859\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"358\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/RwU5xVtZreVvDR Usznng1744015663.png\" width=\"500\" /> Action potential (Option A) is an all-or-none electrical signal that occurs after the receptor potential reaches a threshold. Synaptic potential (Option C) occurs at synapses between neurons, not in sensory receptors. Resting potential (Option D) is the baseline electrical state of a cell when it is not stimulated.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 42-42; Solution Pages 72-73","sourcePage":42,"sourcePageEnd":42},{"id":"physiology-2-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which type of mechanoreceptor is primarily responsible for two-point discrimination and the ability to read Braille by detecting fine touch and texture details on the fingertips?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Free Nerve Ending"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hair End Organ"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iggo Dome Receptor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ruffini Corpuscle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Iggo Dome Receptor","explanation":"• Merkel discs are often grouped together in a receptor organ called touch domes, which project upward against the underside of the epithelium of the skin. • This upward projection causes the epithelium to protrude, forming a dome ( Iggo dome receptor, named after a scientist, Iggo) and creating an extremely sensitive receptor. • Blind individuals rely on the fine tactile acuity (two-point discrimination) of their fingertips to read Braille, which has dots spaced 2.5 mm apart. Rapidly Location Function Response Adapting Tactile Receptors Merkel’s Underneath the Detect continuous touch Transmit initially a strong Disc epithelium of the skin and pressure, response but partially (Option C Transmit strong initial adapting, then a ruled out) signal and weaker continuing weaker sustained signal, response that adapts Localise touch sensations slowly. (Two point discrimination), Extremely sensitive when Determine texture grouped in a receptor organ called touch domes Ruffini Deeper layers of the Important for signaling Very slow adapting, Corpuscles skin and deeper continuous states of providing continuous (Option D internal tissues, joint deformation of tissue such feedback about ruled out) capsules as heavy prolonged touch deformation and and pressure prolonged pressure. Help to signal the degree of joint rotation Free Nerve Found everywhere in Detect touch and pressure Can detect even light Ending skin contact (Option A ruled out) Hair End Organ (Option B) detects movement of objects on the body surface or initial contact with the body","sourceRef":"Question Pages 43-43; Solution Pages 73-74","sourcePage":43,"sourcePageEnd":43},{"id":"physiology-2-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Which type of receptor is most likely responsible for detecting the rapid changes in pressure and vibrations in the patient's fingertips?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Meissner Corpuscles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pacinian Corpuscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hair End Organ"},{"letter":"D","text":"Warmth Receptors"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Pacinian Corpuscles","explanation":"Pacinian Corpuscles are rapidly adapting receptors that are particularly sensitive to rapid changes in pressure and vibrations of tissues. Rapidly Structure Function Location Adaptation Adapting Tactile Receptors Pacinian Encapsulated nerve Detect tissue Immediately Adapts in a Corpuscle ending vibration or other beneath the skin few hundred (Option B) rapid changes in the and deep in the ths of a mechanical state of fascial tissues of the second tissues body Stimulated by rapid local compression of the tissue Meissner Elongated, encapsul Sensitive to Non-hairy skin, Adapts in a Corpuscle ated nerve ending movement of particularly fraction of a (Option A of a large myelinated objects over the abundant in fingertip second ruled out) sensory nerve fiber skin and low-frequen s and lips. cy vibrations crucial for spatial location discernment of touch sensations Hair End Nerve fiber Detects : Hairy skin Adapts readi Organ (Opt entwined at the Movement of ly ion C ruled base of each hair objects on the body out) surface or Initial contact with the body Pacinian Corpuscles\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7863\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"383\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/8UhmWxb4yJ5FYO yml55O1744016225.png\" width=\"500\" />","sourceRef":"Question Pages 43-43; Solution Pages 74-75","sourcePage":43,"sourcePageEnd":43},{"id":"physiology-2-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-2","chapter":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topic":"Somatosensory Receptors, Nociceptors, Proprioceptors, and Reflexes","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A patient reports an unusual sensitivity to temperature changes, and you suspect a disorder related to thermoreceptor function. Which of the following statements accurately describes the nerve fibers involved in transmitting signals from thermoreceptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cold receptors: myelinated type C fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Warmth receptors: myelinated type Aδ fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cold receptors: myelinated type Aδ fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Warmth receptors: myelinated type Aβ fibers"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Cold receptors: myelinated type Aδ fibers","explanation":"Cold receptors primarily use small, myelinated type Aδ fibers to transmit signals, which conduct at a speed of approximately 20 m/sec. Type Location Transmission Pathway Speed Cold Receptors Under the skin Type Aδ fibers ~20 m/sec (Option C) at spots (myelinated), some Type (Aδ fibers) C fibers (unmyelinated) (Option A ruled out) Warmth Recept Under the skin Unmyelinated Type C 0.4-2 m/sec ors at spots fibers (Option B&D; Ruled out) Type of Nerve Fiber Temperature Range Cold Receptors 10-40°C (peak ~24°C) Warmth Receptors >30°C (fade ~49°C) Cold Pain Fibers Very cold temperatures Heat Pain Fibers Start ~45°C • Transmitted in parallel pathways with pain signals. • Signals travel in the tract of Lissauer and terminate in laminae I, II, and III of dorsal horns. • Processed signals by cord neurons cross to the opposite anterolateral sensory tract and terminate in both brain stem's reticular areas and the thalamus's ventrobasal complex. • Removal of cortical postcentral gyrus reduces, but does not eliminate, temperature distinction ability.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 43-43; Solution Pages 76-76","sourcePage":43,"sourcePageEnd":43},{"id":"physiology-3-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which layer of the cerebral cortex is primarily responsible for receiving input sensory signals and sending them to other layers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Layer I"},{"letter":"B","text":"Layer III"},{"letter":"C","text":"Layer IV"},{"letter":"D","text":"Layer VI"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Layer IV","explanation":"Layer IV is the primary input layer where sensory signals first enter the cortex. It processes these signals and then distributes them to other layers. Cerebral Cortex Layers and Functions: Has six layers of neurons. Layer Function I- Molecular layer Receives nonspecific input signals from lower brain centers. Control the excitability of stimulated areas. II- External granular layer Receives nonspecific input and sends axons to the opposite cortex through the corpus callosum. III- External pyramidal Sends axons to related cortical areas on the opposite layer side through the corpus callosum. IV- Internal granular layer The primary layer for receiving input sensory signals, which then spread to the surface and deeper layers. V- Internal pyramidal Sends large axons to distant nervous system parts like layer basal ganglia, brain stem, and spinal cord. Controls signal transmission. VI- Fusiform/ polymorphic Sends axons to the thalamus. layer Regulates excitability of incoming sensory signals.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 78-78; Solution Pages 84-85","sourcePage":78,"sourcePageEnd":78},{"id":"physiology-3-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A patient presents to the clinic with difficulty feeling the precise location of a stimulus on their skin and reports problems distinguishing between different textures and shapes. Additionally, they are having trouble detecting rapid changes in vibrations. Which of the following somatosensory pathways is most likely affected in this patient, given their symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscal System"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral Spinothalamic Tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior Spinothalamic Tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corticospinal Tract"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscal System","explanation":"The Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscal System handles precise touch localization, texture, vibration, and proprioception. The patient’s difficulty with these sensations suggests an issue with this system. Somatosensory pathways: • Two primary somatosensory pathways that transmit sensory signals from the body to the brain: the dorsal column-medial lemniscal system and the anterolateral system. Pathway Sensory Modalities Dorsal Column-Medial Touch: Provides precise localization and fine gradations Lemniscal System of intensity. (Option A) Vibration: Detects rapid changes in stimulus through phasic sensations. Proprioception: Contributes to the sense of body position and movement. Pressure: Allows for the perception of fine degrees of pressure intensity. Anterolateral System Lateral Spinothalamic Tract: Transmits sensations of (Option B & C) pain and temperature (warmth and cold). Anterior Spinothalamic Tract: Carries crude touch and pressure for general skin contact, along with tickle, itch, and sexual sensations. Corticospinal Tract is involved in motor control, not sensory discrimination. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 78-78; Solution Pages 85-86","sourcePage":78,"sourcePageEnd":78},{"id":"physiology-3-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 45 Y/o man presents with numbness and loss of proprioception in his right leg and lower torso. Upon examination, the physician notes a loss of vibration sense and two-point discrimination in the affected areas. MRI shows a lesion in the medulla. Which of the following nucleus is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nucleus cuneatus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nucleus gracilis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Nucleus ambiguus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Nucleus gracilis","explanation":"Based on the above scenario, the patient is most likely having a lesion in the Nucleus gracilis Dorsal column-medial lemniscal pathway: Pathway Sensory receptors → Spinal cord (dorsal roots) → Brain (somatosensory cortex) Neurons First-order Originate in mechanoreceptors, and enter spinal cord neurons via dorsal roots Pseudo-unipolar neurons with cell bodies in the dorsal root ganglion Split into medial and lateral branches: Medial branch: Travels up dorsal column to brain Lateral branch: Synapses in dorsal horn, forming local reflexes, spinocerebellar tracts Fibers from lower thoracic, sacral, and lumbar regions (T7-T12, L, S) ascend to medulla without synapsing, forming the fasciculus gracilis Fibers from cervical and upper thoracic regions (C, T1-T6) ascend to medulla without synapsing, forming the fasciculus cuneatus Second-order Synapse in dorsal medulla (cuneate nucleus for upper neurons body, gracile nucleus for lower body) Cross to opposite brain stem side, travel to thalamus (medial lemniscus) Third-order Synapse in thalamus (ventrobasal complex) neurons Project to somatosensory cortex (postcentral gyrus, lateral parietal cortex) Nucleus cuneatus : Transmits sensory information from the upper body (arms and upper torso). The patient's symptoms do not involve these areas. (Option A) Ventral posterolateral nucleus (VPL) : Located in the thalamus, not the medulla. This option is incorrect because the lesion is in the medulla. (Option C) Nucleus ambiguus : Involved in motor control of the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles. It is not related to the dorsal column pathway or the sensory symptoms described. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 78-79; Solution Pages 86-87","sourcePage":78,"sourcePageEnd":79},{"id":"physiology-3-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A patient presents with a loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left side of the body, below the level of a spinal cord lesion. Upon examination, the patient also exhibits impaired sensation of crude touch and itch on the same side but fine touch and proprioception sensations are intact . Which sensory pathway is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscal System"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterolateral Pathway"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corticospinal Tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spinocerebellar Tract"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Anterolateral Pathway","explanation":"Based on the above scenario, the patient most likely has a lesion in the anterolateral pathway. . Anterolateral Pathway • Transmits sensory signals such as pain, temperature, crude touch, itch, sexual sensations, and crude pressure from the opposite side of the body. • Damage results in loss of pain and temperature sensation on the opposite side below the lesion. • Characteristics: Slower transmission, less precise spatial localization, reduced ability to transmit rapidly changing signals. Pathway: Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 79-79; Solution Pages 88-88","sourcePage":79,"sourcePageEnd":79},{"id":"physiology-3-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following best describes the sensory and motor deficits in Brown-Séquard Syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Contralateral loss of pain and temperature; ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration."},{"letter":"B","text":"Ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature; contralateral loss of proprioception and vibration."},{"letter":"C","text":"Ipsilateral loss of all sensations at the lesion; contralateral motor loss below the lesion."},{"letter":"D","text":"Contralateral loss of vibration and fine touch ; ipsilateral loss of temperature, and crude touch."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Contralateral loss of pain and temperature; ipsilateral loss of proprioception and vibration.","explanation":"Spinal cord syndromes: Condition Description Clinical Findings Brown-Séqu Ipsilateral loss of all sensation at the ard Syndro level of the lesion; ipsilateral LMN me signs (e.g., flaccid paralysis) at the level of the lesion; Ipsilateral UMN signs below the level of lesion (due to corticospinal tract damage); Ipsilateral loss of proprioception, vibration, and fine touch below the level of lesion (due to dorsal column damage); Contralateral loss of pain, Hemisection of the spinal cord. temperature, and crude touch below the level of lesion (due to spinothalamic tract damage); If a lesion occurs above T1, it may present with ipsilateral Horner syndrome due to damage of the oculosympathetic pathway. Anterior Presents with UMN signs below Spinal Arter lesion (corticospinal tract), y Occlusion LMN signs at the level of lesion (anterior horn), and Loss of pain and temperature sensation below the lesion (spinothalamic tract). Spinal cord infarction sparing dorsal horns and columns. Watershed area mid-thoracic; ... (content truncated) Tabes Dors Progressive sensory ataxia alis (impaired proprioception and poor coordination); Positive Romberg sign; Absent deep tendon reflexes (DTRs); Seen in the terminal stage of syphilis Associated with shooting pain, Argyll Robertson pupils, and Charcot joints. Degeneration/demyelination of dorsal columns and roots caused by Treponema pallidum (tertiary syphilis). Syringomyel “Cape-like” loss of pain and ia temperature sensation in bilateral upper extremities (due to damage of fibers crossing in anterior white commissure); LMN deficits if anterior horns are affected; Most lesions occur between C2 and T9. A fluid-filled, gliosis-lined cavity within the spinal cord, Often associated with Chiari I malformation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 79-79; Solution Pages 88-90","sourcePage":79,"sourcePageEnd":79},{"id":"physiology-3-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding removal of each somatosensory area is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Somatosensory Area I: complete loss of pain and temperature sensations."},{"letter":"B","text":"Somatosensory Area II: inability of precise localization of sensations"},{"letter":"C","text":"Somatosensory Association Areas: inability to recognize two sides of an object"},{"letter":"D","text":"Somatosensory Area I: enhance ability to judge the shape of an object"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Somatosensory Association Areas : inability to recognize two side of an object","explanation":"Feature Somatosensory Area I Somatosensory Area II Somatosensory (SSA 1) (SSA 2) Association Area Location Postcentral gyrus Superior wall of Sylvian Brodmann's areas 5 and (Brodmann's areas 3, 1, fissure 7, located in parietal and 2). cortex behind SSA I Represe High localization of body Poor localization (Option Combines information ntation parts. B) from the primary and loca Nose, lips, mouth and Face is represented somatosensory area, lisation face laterally; head, neck, anteriorly, arms centrally leading to complex shoulder and lower body and legs posteriorly sensations, such as part medially feeling an object like a knife or ball. Functio Primary area for somatic Less known functions; Decipher deeper n sensation Relies on input from SSA I meanings of sensory information from SSA Sensory Receive inputs from Signals enter from brain Receives signals from Informa thalamic and cortical areas stem, SSA I and from SSA I, thalamus,visual tion other sensory areas cortex and auditory cortex (visual and auditory) Effect B/L excision of leads to: Removal of parts of SSA U/L removal leads to: of Excisi Loss of precise II does not affect the Amorphosynthesis : on localization of sensations response of neurons in Difficulty recognizing Inability to judge pressure SSA I objects on one side. against the body, weight, (Option C) texture, or shape of the Hemineglect: Denial of object (astereognosis) the existence of the (Option D). opposite side of the body. Pain and temperature Constructional apraxia: senses preserved but Difficulty in constructing a poorly localized (Option A) picture.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 79-80; Solution Pages 90-91","sourcePage":79,"sourcePageEnd":80},{"id":"physiology-3-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the representation in motor and sensory homunculus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hand and digits have large cortical representation in the motor homunculus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Feet represented at the bottom of both the precentral gyrus and postcentral gyrus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trunk and back have large cortical representation in both homunculi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cortical representation is proportional to the size of the body part in both homunculi"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Hand and digits have large cortical representation in both homunculi","explanation":"Homunculus represents either the motor or the sensory distribution along the cerebral cortex of the brain. Feature Motor Homunculus Sensory Homunculus Location Precentral gyrus Postcentral gyrus Representation Feet at the top of the gyrus (Option Legs on top and head at the bottom B); face at the bottom of the gyrus (Option B) Cortical Represe Proportionate to the skill involved in Proportional to the number of ntation (Option fine, voluntary activities sensory receptors that sense D) muscle position and changes in muscle length Large Represent Hand, digits (Option A), lips, jaw, Face (especially lips), ation Areas and tongue (areas involved in Hand (especially digits) (Option A) (Option C) speech and hand movements) Small Represent Trunk and lower limb Trunk and lower limb ation Areas Specialization Large area for lips, jaw, and tongue Large area for face mainly lips) due due to specialization in speech to speech (vocalization of the cortex) Large area for hand(mainly digits) due to cutaneous sensations from areas involved in skilled activities Sensory Homunculus: Motor Homunculus:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 80-80; Solution Pages 91-92","sourcePage":80,"sourcePageEnd":80},{"id":"physiology-3-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old man recently had his right leg amputated below the knee. Weeks after surgery, he feels sensations and pain in the missing leg area, sometimes triggered by touching his thigh. Which statement best explains the patient's condition and suggests a treatment?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Brain reorganization of cortical brain area & Spinal cord stimulation may help."},{"letter":"B","text":"Infection at the amputation site causes pain & Antibiotics should be prescribed."},{"letter":"C","text":"Reorganization at spinal cord neuronal connection leading to pain."},{"letter":"D","text":"Pain is due to rejection of the prosthetic limb & Discontinue use immediately."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Brain reorganization of cortical brain area & Spinal cord stimulation may help.","explanation":"Cortical plasticity • Refers to the ability of neurons to modify their connections under specific circumstances. • Cortical structures are capable of reorganization (plasticity) after loss of a body part, neighboring areas take over the cortical area of the lost part. (Option A). • Cortical connections of sensory units become weak with disuse and strengthen with use. Phantom Limb Pain • Perceived due to cortical plasticity • In the phantom limb phenomenon, the limb actually does not exist (amputated), but the patient complains that he feels pain or itch in the limb • Based on the evidence, brain reorganization occurs when sensory input is cut off. For example: In leg amputees, the ventral posterior thalamic nucleus can perceive the signal that once received input from leg and foot now responds to stump (thigh) stimulation Management :","sourceRef":"Question Pages 80-80; Solution Pages 92-93","sourcePage":80,"sourcePageEnd":80},{"id":"physiology-3-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which law explains the phenomenon of phantom limb pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Muller's Doctrine of Specific Nerve Energy"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bell-Magendie Law"},{"letter":"C","text":"Law of Projection"},{"letter":"D","text":"Weber-Fechner Law"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Law of Projection","explanation":"The law of Projection forms the basis of phantom limb Laws of Sensory Physiology Bell-Magendie Law : • States that the dorsal root of the spinal cord is sensory and the ventral root of the spinal cord is motor in function. (Option B) Muller's Doctrine of Specific Nerve Energy : • States that sensation evoked by a stimulus that generates impulse in the specific sensory pathway depends on the precise area of the brain that is eventually activated by the stimulus. (Option A) Law of Projection : • States that no matter where a specific sensory pathway is stimulated along its course to the cortex, the sensation is referred to the location of the receptors. • Forms the basis of the phantom limb. (Option C) Weber-Fechner Law : • States that the magnitude of sensation felt is proportionate to the logarithm of the intensity of the stimulus. (Option D) Interpreted signal strength = Log (Stimulus) + Constant • Emphasizes that the greater the background sensory intensity, the greater an additional change must be for the individual to detect the change. Labelled Line Theory • States that each sensory receptor is specialized to detect specific stimuli and sends signals via dedicated nerve fibers to the corresponding area of the somatosensory cortex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 80-81; Solution Pages 93-94","sourcePage":80,"sourcePageEnd":81},{"id":"physiology-3-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45 Y/o right-handed woman presents with difficulty in performing coordinated movements and a lack of fluidity in her actions, particularly when performing tasks that require the integration of both hands, such as typing or playing the piano. Which of the following brain regions is most likely implicated in her condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary Motor Cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Premotor Cortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supplementary Motor Cortex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cortical Association Areas"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Supplementary Motor Cortex","explanation":"Based on the above scenario, the patient most likely has a lesion in the supplementary motor cortex, which is important for complex bimanual coordination. Cortical Motor areas: The motor cortex is located in the frontal lobe. Primary Motor Cortex: Corresponds to Brodmann Area 4 It lies in the first convolution of the frontal lobes anterior to the central sulcus Responsible for executing voluntary movements and controlling fine motor skills (Option A) Premotor Cortex: Corresponds to Brodmann area 6 Lies 1-3 cm anterior to the primary motor area Responsible for setting posture before movement (Option B) Supplementary Motor Cortex: Lies mainly in the longitudinal fissure Responsible for Bimanual coordination (Option C) Cortical Association Areas: Commands for voluntary movements originate in cortical association areas (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 81-81; Solution Pages 94-95","sourcePage":81,"sourcePageEnd":81},{"id":"physiology-3-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following is the major contributor to the corticospinal tract in terms of percentage of descending fibers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary motor cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Premotor cortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supplementary cortex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Somatosensory area"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Somatosensory area","explanation":"Corticospinal tract: • The corticospinal tract, also known as the pyramidal tract, is the most important descending tract from the motor cortex. • Contribution: • Primary motor cortex- 30% (Option A) • Premotor and supplementary cortex- 30% (Options B&C;) • Somatosensory area- 40% (Option D) • After leaving the cortex, the tract passes through the posterior limb of the internal capsule, then through the brainstem and into the medulla. • In the medulla, most of the fibers cross to the opposite side, forming the medullary pyramids. • These crossed fibers descend as the lateral corticospinal tract finally terminates in the spinal cord. • Some fibers which do not cross to the opposite side in the medulla pass ipsilaterally down to the spinal cord as the ventral corticospinal tract.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 81-81; Solution Pages 95-95","sourcePage":81,"sourcePageEnd":81},{"id":"physiology-3-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following is not true regarding extrapyramidal tracts?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They are descending tracts"},{"letter":"B","text":"The tectospinal tract functions through gamma motor neurons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pontine and medullary reticulospinal tracts are excitatory and inhibitory, respectively"},{"letter":"D","text":"The rubrospinal tract is involved in the lateral motor pathway"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The tectospinal tract functions through gamma motor neurons","explanation":"Extrapyramidal tracts: • These include the reticulospinal tract, vestibulospinal tract, tectospinal tract and rubrospinal tract. • All of them originate from the brain and terminate in the spinal cord. Hence, all are descending tracts (Option A) Tr Reticulospinal tract Vestibulospinal tract Tectospin Rubrospinal tract act al tract Ori Reticular activating Medial and inferior Superior Red nucleus gin system vestibular nuclei colliculus of the midbrain Ch Two types: It controls antigravity Controls Controls distal limb ara Pontine (excitatory) muscles, such as head and muscles cte and medullary proximal limb eye move The tract excites rist (inhibitory) tracts extensors, to control ments flexor motor ics (Option C) posture and balance neurons and inhibits They are involved in extensor motor maintaining posture neurons and modulating muscle tone via gamma motor neurons (Option B) Mo Medial Medial Medial Lateral (Option D) tor pat hw ay","sourceRef":"Question Pages 81-81; Solution Pages 96-97","sourcePage":81,"sourcePageEnd":81},{"id":"physiology-3-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following extra-pyramidal tracts contributes to the lateral motor pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vestibulospinal tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rubrospinal tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Reticulospinal tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tectospinal tract"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Rubrospinal tract","explanation":"Lateral motor pathway Medial motor pathway Comprise Corticospinal tract Reticulospinal tract s of: Rubrospinal tract Vestibulospinal tract Tectospinal tract Pathway Terminates in the dorsolateral area Terminates in ventromedial area of and contr of spinal gray matter and controls spinal gray matter and controls axial ol distal muscles and proximal muscles Function Fine, skilled, voluntary motor Maintaining erect posture movements","sourceRef":"Question Pages 82-82; Solution Pages 97-97","sourcePage":82,"sourcePageEnd":82},{"id":"physiology-3-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 60 y/o man presents with complaints of weakness and difficulty walking. Upon examination, he is found to have grade 1 power in right leg muscle and an exaggerated knee-jerk reflex. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower motor neuron lesion of the L4"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upper motor neuron lesion of the L4"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper motor neuron lesion of the right internal capsule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lower motor neuron lesion of the right internal capsule"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Upper motor neuron lesion of the right internal capsule Explanation : • The internal capsule contains corticospinal tract fibers, and a lesion in this location will cause an upper motor neuron lesion. Upper motor neurons (UMN): • Neurons in the cortex and brainstem that activate lower motor neurons. • The pyramidal tract and extrapyramidal tract are both considered UMNs because they both influence alpha motor neurons. • All neurons above the alpha motor neuron are upper motor neurons. Lower motor neurons (LMN) : • Spinal and cranial motor neurons that directly innervate skeletal muscles. • The alpha motor neuron is considered the lower motor neuron. • The lower motor neuron is the final common pathway for both upper motor neurons and the stretch reflex. • The corticospinal tract is excitatory to lower motor neurons while the extrapyramidal tract is inhibitory. Feature UMN Lesion LMN Lesion Atrophy None Severe (loss of trophic factor) Fasciculation None Present Tone Hypertonia (spasticity & rigidity) Hypotonia (Decreased) Deep tendon reflex Hyperactive Hypoactive or absent Muscle group affected Regional Segmental/distal Babinski sign Positive Absent An upper motor neuron lesion at the level of the L4 spinal nerve root would not affect the knee-jerk reflex. (Option B)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 82-82; Solution Pages 97-98","sourcePage":82,"sourcePageEnd":82},{"id":"physiology-3-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 65-year-old man complains of difficulty moving his arms and states that they feel \"stiff.\" Upon examination, both arms show increased resistance to passive movement. The resistance is present throughout the range of motion and does not change with the speed of movement. What is the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower motor neuron lesion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cerebellar lesion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lesion of the pyramidal tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lesion of the extrapyramidal tract"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Lesion of the extrapyramidal tract Explanation : • Rigidity is defined as an increased resistance to passive movement that is not velocity-dependent, meaning the resistance remains constant regardless of the speed of movement. • Rigidity is a feature of extrapyramidal lesions. Feature Spasticity Rigidity Definition Velocity-dependent resistance Velocity-independent resistance Mechanism Lesion of the pyramidal tract Lesion of the extrapyramidal tract (Option C) Clinical Resistance increases with speed Resistance is continuous and does Finding not vary with the speed of movement Example Clasp-knife spasticity Lead pipe rigidity, cogwheel rigidity Associated Weakness Hypertonia with Lower motor neuron lesions are characterized by hypotonia (decreased muscle tone), muscle wasting (atrophy), weakened or absent reflexes, and flaccid paralysis. (Option A) Cerebellar lesions can cause motor dysfunction, hypotonia, not rigidity. (Option B)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 82-82; Solution Pages 98-99","sourcePage":82,"sourcePageEnd":82},{"id":"physiology-3-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-3","chapter":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topic":"Ascending and Descending Tracts of Spinal Cords","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 17 y/o male is brought after being struck by a car. He is unconscious and exhibiting abnormal posturing. His arms are stiffly extended, his forearms are pronated, his wrists and fingers are flexed, and his legs are extended with his toes pointed inward. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decorticate rigidity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decerebrate rigidity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cerebellar ataxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spinal shock"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Decerebrate rigidity Explanation : The patient's posturing is characteristic of decerebrate rigidity. Featur Decerebrate Rigidity Decorticate Rigidity e Definiti A state of severe hypertonia A state in which the upper on characterized by extension of all extremities are flexed and the lower four limbs. extremities are extended. Cause Damage to the lower midbrain and Damage to the upper midbrain upper pons Mecha Loss of descending inhibition to the Rubrospinal tract excitation of flexor nism pontine reticular system causes muscles in the upper extremities overactivity of this system, which results in flexion due to damage excites antigravity muscles. above the red nucleus. Postur Arms adducted and extended Arms adducted and flexed e Forearms pronated Wrists and fingers flexed Wrists and fingers flexed Legs extended Legs extended Plantar flexion of the feet Feet plantar flexed (Option A) Clinical Severe brain stem damage; poor Better prognosis than decerebrate Signifi prognosis rigidity cance Cerebellar ataxia typically manifests as uncoordinated and inaccurate movements, often with intention tremor rather than the fixed, rigid posturing. (Option C) Spinal shock can occur after spinal cord injury and presents as a flaccid paralysis with loss of reflexes below the level of the lesion. (Option D)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 83-83; Solution Pages 99-100","sourcePage":83,"sourcePageEnd":83},{"id":"physiology-4-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements about the basal ganglia and their associated neurotransmitters is CORRECT?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The caudate nucleus primarily uses acetylcholine as its neurotransmitter."},{"letter":"B","text":"The globus pallidus internus (GPi) exerts an excitatory influence on the thalamus."},{"letter":"C","text":"The subthalamic nucleus (STN) uses glutamate as its principal neurotransmitter to excite the globus pallidus internus (GPi)."},{"letter":"D","text":"The substantia nigra pars reticulata (SNr) inhibits the thalamus using dopamine."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The subthalamic nucleus (STN) uses glutamate as its principal neurotransmitter to excite the globus pallidus internus (GPi).","explanation":"The subthalamic nucleus (STN) uses glutamate to excite the GPi. The basal ganglia are a group of subcortical nuclei in the brain involved in regulating movement and coordinating motor activity. Key parts include: • Striatum : Consists of the caudate nucleus and putamen. It's the main input center for the basal ganglia, receiving information from the cerebral cortex. • Globus Pallidus : Divided into the external (GPe) and internal (GPi) segments. It plays a role in the regulation of voluntary movement by processing signals from the striatum. • Substantia Nigra : Located in the midbrain and has two parts: the pars compacta (which produces dopamine) and the pars reticulata. It is involved in movement control and has a key role in the dopamine system. • Subthalamic Nucleus : Located below the thalamus, it contributes to motor control by influencing the output of the globus pallidus. The basal ganglia use several key neurotransmitters to regulate motor control: • The caudate nucleus primarily uses GABA as its neurotransmitter, not acetylcholine. (Option A) • The globus pallidus internus (GPi) exerts an inhibitory influence on the thalamus. (Option B) • The substantia nigra pars reticulata (SNr) inhibits the thalamus using GABA, not dopamine. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 101-101; Solution Pages 107-108","sourcePage":101,"sourcePageEnd":101},{"id":"physiology-4-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which statement correctly describes the effect of each basal ganglia pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The direct pathway decreases inhibitory output from the internal segment of the globus pallidus."},{"letter":"B","text":"The indirect pathway increases inhibitory output from the internal segment of the globus pallidus."},{"letter":"C","text":"The direct pathway increases inhibitory output from the external segment of the globus pallidus."},{"letter":"D","text":"The indirect pathway decreases inhibitory output from the external segment of the globus pallidus."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) The direct pathway decreases inhibitory output from the internal segment of the globus pallidus.","explanation":"The basal ganglia play a crucial role in regulating motor control, movement coordination, and certain aspects of cognition. Direct Pathway: • Promotes movement by reducing inhibitory output from the GPi to the thalamus. • Mechanism: The striatum inhibits the GPi, leading to less inhibition of the thalamus and increased motor activity. Indirect Pathway: • Inhibits movement by increasing inhibitory output from the GPe to the thalamus. • Mechanism: The striatum inhibits the GPe, resulting in less inhibition of the STN, which then excites the GPi. This increased activity of the GPi leads to more inhibition of the thalamus and decreased motor activity. indirect pathway\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"555\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"500\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/ShbNM6MM8gt JNqbjOtVH1736069514.png\" width=\"696\" /> Direct Pathway Indirect Pathway Function Facilitates motor activity Suppresses motor activity Input Excitatory input from the cerebral Excitatory input from the cerebral cortex cortex Target Striatum (caudate nucleus and Striatum (caudate nucleus and putamen) putamen) Striatum Output Inhibits internal segment of globus Inhibits external segment of globus pallidus (GPi) pallidus (GPe) GPi/SNr Effect Reduced inhibition of thalamus Increased inhibition of thalamus Thalamus Effect Increased thalamic activation Decreased thalamic activation Motor Cortex Effect Enhanced motor activity Reduced motor activity Subthalamic Nucleus Not directly involved Activated by reduced inhibition from (STN) GPe","sourceRef":"Question Pages 101-101; Solution Pages 108-109","sourcePage":101,"sourcePageEnd":101},{"id":"physiology-4-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with resting tremors, bradykinesia, rigidity, and postural instability. His symptoms have progressively worsened over the past year. Based on the clinical presentation, which of the following is the most likely underlying pathology?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Degeneration of cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain"},{"letter":"C","text":"Degeneration of GABAergic neurons in the striatum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Degeneration of serotonergic neurons in the raphe nuclei"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta","explanation":"• The patient's symptoms are characteristic of Parkinson's disease, which is primarily caused by the degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra pars compacta. • This leads to a decrease in dopamine levels in the striatum, affecting the basal ganglia circuitry and resulting in the hallmark motor symptoms of the disease. • Disorders affecting the basal ganglia can result in a range of motor symptoms, leading to either reduced (hypokinetic) or excessive (hyperkinetic) movement. Hypokinetic Diseases • Parkinsonism: Cause: Reduced dopamine in the substantia nigra. Symptoms: Akinesia, bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity, resting tremor, shuffling gait, stooped posture, reduced facial expression, small handwriting, and mood/cognitive issues. • Cause: Reduced dopamine in the substantia nigra. • Symptoms: Akinesia, bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity, resting tremor, shuffling gait, stooped posture, reduced facial expression, small handwriting, and mood/cognitive issues. • Cause: Reduced dopamine in the substantia nigra. • Symptoms: Akinesia, bradykinesia, cogwheel rigidity, resting tremor, shuffling gait, stooped posture, reduced facial expression, small handwriting, and mood/cognitive issues. Hyperkinetic Diseases • Hemiballismus : Damage to the subthalamic nucleus, leading to decreased inhibition of the thalamus and excessive movement. • Damage to the subthalamic nucleus, leading to decreased inhibition of the thalamus and excessive movement. • Huntington's Disease : Degeneration of the indirect pathway in the striatum, causing involuntary movements similar to hemiballismus. • Degeneration of the indirect pathway in the striatum, causing involuntary movements similar to hemiballismus. • Dystonia : Dysfunction in the direct pathway, resulting in sustained, twisting movements. • Dysfunction in the direct pathway, resulting in sustained, twisting movements. • Damage to the subthalamic nucleus, leading to decreased inhibition of the thalamus and excessive movement. • Degeneration of the indirect pathway in the striatum, causing involuntary movements similar to hemiballismus. • Dysfunction in the direct pathway, resulting in sustained, twisting movements. Degeneration of cholinergic neurons in the basal forebrain is associated with Alzheimer's disease, which primarily affects memory and cognitive functions rather than motor control. (Option B) GABAergic neurons in the striatum are involved in the motor control pathways, but their degeneration is more characteristic of Huntington’s disease which involves a different pattern of motor dysfunction, including chorea, which is not consistent with the symptoms described. (Option C) Degeneration of serotonergic neurons in the raphe nuclei is related to mood disorders and certain types of depression, rather than the motor symptoms seen in Parkinson’s disease. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 101-102; Solution Pages 109-111","sourcePage":101,"sourcePageEnd":102},{"id":"physiology-4-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which statement correctly describes the cerebellar structure and its nuclei?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior and posterior lobes regulate posture and balance"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flocculonodular lobe coordinates fine motor skills."},{"letter":"C","text":"Dentate nucleus is key in motor planning and execution."},{"letter":"D","text":"Granule cells are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Dentate nucleus is key in motor planning and execution","explanation":"Overview of the anatomy of the cerebellum, including its lobes, lobules, cortex, and nuclei: • Lobes: Anterior Lobe: Located superiorly and anteriorly, it is involved in the coordination of voluntary movements and posture. Posterior Lobe: Positioned between the primary fissure and the posterior cerebellar notch, it plays a crucial role in the planning and execution of complex movements. Flocculonodular Lobe: Located at the inferior part of the cerebellum, it is crucial for maintaining balance and coordinating eye movements. • Anterior Lobe: Located superiorly and anteriorly, it is involved in the coordination of voluntary movements and posture. • Posterior Lobe: Positioned between the primary fissure and the posterior cerebellar notch, it plays a crucial role in the planning and execution of complex movements. • Flocculonodular Lobe: Located at the inferior part of the cerebellum, it is crucial for maintaining balance and coordinating eye movements. • Lobules: The cerebellum is divided into lobules by fissures. Major lobules include: Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • The cerebellum is divided into lobules by fissures. Major lobules include: Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. • Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. • Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • Cortex: The cerebellar cortex is organized into three layers: Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • The cerebellar cortex is organized into three layers: Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. • Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. • Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • Nuclei: The deep cerebellar nuclei are the primary output nuclei of the cerebellum and include dentate nucleus, emboliform nucleus, globose nucleus, and fastigial nucleus . • The deep cerebellar nuclei are the primary output nuclei of the cerebellum and include dentate nucleus, emboliform nucleus, globose nucleus, and fastigial nucleus . • Anterior Lobe: Located superiorly and anteriorly, it is involved in the coordination of voluntary movements and posture. • Posterior Lobe: Positioned between the primary fissure and the posterior cerebellar notch, it plays a crucial role in the planning and execution of complex movements. • Flocculonodular Lobe: Located at the inferior part of the cerebellum, it is crucial for maintaining balance and coordinating eye movements. • The cerebellum is divided into lobules by fissures. Major lobules include: Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. • Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. • Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • Lobule I–V: Part of the anterior lobe. • Lobule VI–VII: Part of the posterior lobe. • Lobule VIII–IX: Located in the posterior lobe and flocculonodular lobe. • The cerebellar cortex is organized into three layers: Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. • Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. • Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • Molecular Layer: Contains the dendritic trees of Purkinje cells and interneurons like stellate and basket cells. • Purkinje Cell Layer: Contains Purkinje cells, which are the primary output neurons of the cerebellar cortex, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. • Granular Layer: Contains granule cells, which receive input from the mossy fibers and send excitatory projections to the Purkinje cells. • The deep cerebellar nuclei are the primary output nuclei of the cerebellum and include dentate nucleus, emboliform nucleus, globose nucleus, and fastigial nucleus . The anterior and posterior lobes of the cerebellum are primarily involved in the coordination of voluntary movements and fine motor control. Posture and balance are more closely associated with the flocculonodular lobe and its connections. (Option A) The cerebellum is actually divided into three main lobes: the anterior, posterior, and flocculonodular lobes. The flocculonodular lobe primarily contributes to balance and eye movements rather than fine motor skills. (Option B) Granule cells in the cerebellar cortex are not the primary output neurons. Instead, Purkinje cells are the main output neurons, sending inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. Granule cells send excitatory input to Purkinje cells. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 102-102; Solution Pages 111-113","sourcePage":102,"sourcePageEnd":102},{"id":"physiology-4-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the primary function of the mossy fibers in the internal circuitry of the cerebellum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Transmit motor commands to the cerebellum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Provide excitatory input to granule cells."},{"letter":"C","text":"Relay sensory information to the deep cerebellar nuclei."},{"letter":"D","text":"inhibit the activity of the deep cerebellar nuclei."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Provide excitatory input to granule cells","explanation":"• Mossy fibers provide excitatory input to the granule cells in the cerebellar cortex. Granule cells, in turn, project their axons as parallel fibers, which make excitatory synapses on the Purkinje cells. • The following components of the cerebellar internal circuit integrate sensory and motor information to fine-tune motor activities and support motor learning. • Mossy fibers do not directly transmit motor commands from the cerebral cortex. • They carry sensory and other inputs to the cerebellum, which then processes and integrates these inputs to help coordinate motor commands. (Option A) • Mossy fibers do not relay sensory information directly to the deep cerebellar nuclei. • They first synapse in the granule cell layer of the cerebellar cortex. From there, the processed information is relayed to the deep cerebellar nuclei via Purkinje cells. (Option C) • Inhibitory control of the deep cerebellar nuclei is primarily mediated by Purkinje cells, which send inhibitory signals to the deep cerebellar nuclei. • Mossy fibers provide excitatory input to the cerebellar cortex, which indirectly influences the activity of the deep nuclei. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 102-102; Solution Pages 114-114","sourcePage":102,"sourcePageEnd":102},{"id":"physiology-4-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements about the functional divisions of the cerebellum is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The vestibulocerebellum controls balance and eye movements."},{"letter":"B","text":"Spinocerebellum coordinates axial and proximal limb movements"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cerebrocerebellum handles movement planning and programming"},{"letter":"D","text":"The flocculonodular lobe coordinates fine motor movements."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Flocculonodular lobe coordinates fine motor movements","explanation":"The flocculonodular Lobe is a part of the vestibulocerebellum , which is important for balance and eye movements , not fine motor control. Function Function Description Location al Divisi on Spinoce Coordination of axial and Coordinates and Central portion of rebellum proximal limb movements smoothens voluntary the cerebellum (Option B) movements, particularly those involving the axial body and proximal limbs. Cerebro Planning and programmin Involved in motor planning Lateral parts of cerebell g of movements (Option and timing, especially for the cerebellar um C) complex, precise hemispheres movements. Vestibul Balance and eye Modulates motor Flocculonodular ocerebel movements commands to maintain lobe lum (Option A) posture and balance. Integrates information from the vestibular system to coordinate head and eye movements. Flocculo Balance and eye Part of the vestibulocerebe Flocculonodular nodular movements llum, responsible for lobe Lobe balance and equilibrium of the body.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 102-102; Solution Pages 114-115","sourcePage":102,"sourcePageEnd":102},{"id":"physiology-4-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 72-year-old man presents with uncoordinated movements and difficulty in performing rapid alternating tasks, speech difficulty, and experiences rapid, tremulous eye movements. Which of the following features is not typically associated with cerebellar disease?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypertonia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dysarthria"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ataxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rebound Phenomenon"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Hypertonia","explanation":"Hypotonia is a characteristic of cerebellar diseases, not hypertonia. It occurs on the same side as the cerebellar lesion. Clinical Features of Cerebellar Diseases Hypotonia Decreased muscle tone, often on the same side as the cerebellar lesion. Ataxia (Option C) Uncoordinated movements, often leading to a \"drunken\" gait. Dysdiadochokinesia Inability to perform rapid alternating movements, such as flipping the hands back and forth. Dysmetria (Past pointing) Movements that overshoot or undershoot the intended target. Intention tremor Tremors occurring when moving toward a target Dysarthria (Option B) Jumbled speech due to lack of coordination in the muscles involved in speech Nystagmus Rapid, tremulous eye movements Rebound Phenomenon Limb jerking back after resistance is suddenly removed (Option D) Decomposition of movement Can't move multiple joints simultaneously. No sensory deficit Not typically present in cerebellar diseases.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 103-103; Solution Pages 115-116","sourcePage":103,"sourcePageEnd":103},{"id":"physiology-4-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Match the parts of the Papez circuit with their correct functions: Part of Papez Circuit Function 1) Cingulate Gyrus A) Conversion of short-term memory to long-term memory 2) Parahippocampal Gyrus B) Controls heart rate, blood pressure, cognitive and emotional processing 3) Hippocampus C) Responsible for controlling recent memories of the brain 4) Mammillary Body and Anterior Nucleus of Thalamus D) Spatial memory, the memory of three-dimensional space Part of Papez Circuit Function","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Conversion of short-term memory to long-term 1) Cingulate Gyrus memory"},{"letter":"B","text":"Controls heart rate, blood pressure, cognitive and 2) Parahippocampal Gyrus emotional processing"},{"letter":"C","text":"Responsible for controlling recent memories of the 3) Hippocampus brain 4) Mammillary Body and Anterior Nucleus of D) Spatial memory, the memory of three-dimensional Thalamus space"},{"letter":"A","text":"1 - A, 2 - B, 3 - D, 4 - C"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 - B, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - C"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 - D, 2 - C, 3 - A, 4 - B"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 - C, 2 - A, 3 - B, 4 - D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 1 - B, 2 - D, 3 - A, 4 - C","explanation":"The Papez circuit is a neurocircuitry of the limbic system involved in learning and memory, emotion, and social behavior. It has the following components: Part of Papez Circuit Function Cingulate Gyrus Controls heart rate, blood pressure, cognitive and emotional processing. Parahippocampal Gyrus and Entorhinal Cortex Spatial memory, the memory of three-dimensional space. Hippocampus Responsible for the conversion of short-term memory to long-term memory. Mammillary Body and Anterior Nucleus of Responsible for controlling recent memories of the brain. Thalamus","sourceRef":"Question Pages 103-103; Solution Pages 116-117","sourcePage":103,"sourcePageEnd":103},{"id":"physiology-4-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements about the amygdala is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Controls emotions and motivations"},{"letter":"B","text":"Connected with the prefrontal cortex & hippocampus for emotional memory processing"},{"letter":"C","text":"Damage can result in a loss of sexual behaviour."},{"letter":"D","text":"The prefrontal cortex develops working memory"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Damage can result in a loss of sexual behaviour.","explanation":"• Damage to the amygdala typically results in hypersexuality, not a loss of sexual behavior, as seen in Kluver-Bucy syndrome. • Amygdala is part of limbic system central, controlling emotions and motivations. It is connected with the prefrontal cortex & hippocampus via amygdala circuitry for the acquisition, consolidation, and retrieval of memories associated with emotions. (Option A and B) Damage to the amygdala typically results in hypersexuality, not a loss of sexual behavior, as seen in Kluver-Bucy syndrome. Amygdala is part of limbic system central, controlling emotions and motivations. It is connected with the prefrontal cortex & hippocampus via amygdala circuitry for the acquisition, consolidation, and retrieval of memories associated with emotions. (Option A and B) Amygdala Circuit Part of the Amygdala Circuit Function Amygdala Controls emotions and motivations. Prefrontal Cortex Responsible for the development of working memory (Option D) Hippocampus Converts short-term memory to long-term memory","sourceRef":"Question Pages 104-104; Solution Pages 117-118","sourcePage":104,"sourcePageEnd":104},{"id":"physiology-4-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 24-year-old man is brought to the emergency room after a motor vehicle accident. He displays significant behavioral changes including placing non-food objects in his mouth, increased sexual advances towards staff, and appears to have no fear when approached by the medical team. Which of the following brain structures is MOST likely to be damaged in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cerebellum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Amygdala"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hippocampus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parietal lobe"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Amygdala","explanation":"The case presents a patient with classic symptoms of Klüver-Bucy syndrome , including a lack of fear, oral tendencies, and hypersexuality. • It is a rare behavioral disorder that can occur after damage to the anterior temporal lobes, including the amygdalae , which are almond-shaped groups of nuclei located deep within the temporal lobes. • The amygdala is responsible for processing emotions, especially fear and reward. The symptoms of Klüver-Bucy syndrome are varied but can include the following: • Lack of Fear : Individuals may show no fear of objects or situations that would normally elicit a fear response. • Excessive Curiosity : They may display an intense interest in exploring their surroundings, often in a non-directed and inappropriate manner. • Memory Impairment : Short-term memory is often significantly affected, leading to an inability to recall recent events. • Oral Tendencies : A tendency to put objects in their mouth, even those that are not edible. • Hypersexuality : An increased and often inappropriate sexual drive.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 104-104; Solution Pages 118-118","sourcePage":104,"sourcePageEnd":104},{"id":"physiology-4-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following nuclei has a function of thermoregulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial preoptic nuclei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral nuclei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Para-ventricle nuclei"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arcuate nuclei"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Medial preoptic nuclei","explanation":"Medial Preoptic Nucleus is primarily responsible for thermoregulation in response to heat. Anterior region of the Hypothalamus with functions:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 104-104; Solution Pages 119-119","sourcePage":104,"sourcePageEnd":104},{"id":"physiology-4-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents with significant behavioral changes following a traumatic brain injury. He experiences frequent unprovoked outbursts of anger and aggression, triggered by minimal stimuli, along with emotional blunting and decreased pleasure. Based on his presentation, which brain region is MOST likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hippocampus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Medial forebrain bundle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Central gray area"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventromedial hypothalamus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Ventromedial hypothalamus","explanation":"The ventromedial hypothalamus plays a crucial role in inhibiting rage responses generated by other parts of the limbic system, such as the periventricular zone and lateral hypothalamus. Damage to the ventromedial hypothalamus can lead to disinhibition of these rage centers, resulting in the aggressive behavior exhibited by the patient. Additionally, the ventromedial hypothalamus is involved in promoting feelings of satiety and tranquility, while damage to this area can lead to overeating and hyperactivity. Centres Location Function/Response Primary Medial forebrain bundle Lateral hypothalamus: Strong Reward Cent stimulation leads to rage; weaker ers Lateral and ventromedial nuclei of stimulation induces reward the hypothalamus Plays a dual role in both aggressive and rewarding behaviours Primary The central grey area surrounding Elicits potent aversive responses Punishment the aqueduct of Sylvius in the Centers mesencephalon Primary The periventricular zone of the Intense stimulation triggers rage Rage Center hypothalamus responses: Defensive posturing, s Lateral hypothalamus aggression, autonomic responses like piloerection and pupil dilation Inhibitory Ventromedial nuclei of the Suppress or inhibit rage responses Mechanisms hypothalamus Maintain emotional control and Hippocampi regulate aggressive behaviors Anterior limbic cortex: Anterior cingulate gyri and subcallosal gyri Hippocampus (Option A) – Primarily involved in memory and learning; damage causes memory deficits rather than aggression. Medial forebrain bundle (Option B) – Associated with reward and motivation, not directly responsible for aggressive outbursts. Central gray area (Option C) – Plays a role in pain modulation and defensive behaviors, but not in spontaneous, unprovoked aggression.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 105-105; Solution Pages 119-120","sourcePage":105,"sourcePageEnd":105},{"id":"physiology-4-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":13,"question":"In a patient experiencing severe hemorrhage resulting in isotonic fluid loss, which of the following accurately describes the role of osmoreceptors and their effect on the thirst mechanism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased blood osmolality stimulates the supra-optic nucleus to secrete ADH"},{"letter":"B","text":"Angiotensin II directly stimulates the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Osmoreceptors in the OVLT and MnPO are stimulated by decreased blood osmolality"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stimulated SFO osmoreceptors increase ANP secretion, which reduces ADH."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Angiotensin II directly stimulates the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus","explanation":"The hypothalamus primarily regulates the thirst mechanism, and the lateral nucleus houses the thirst centre. • The osmoreceptors in the hypothalamus These three osmoreceptors Median Preoptic Nucleus (MnPO), Organum Vasculosum Laminae Terminalis (OVLT), and Sub Fornical Nucleus (SFO) play key roles in detecting changes in blood osmolality or sodium ion concentration. An increase in blood osmolality or sodium ions stimulates the OVLT, which then activates MnPO through interconnected pathways. It does not directly stimulate the supra-optic nucleus (Option A) • These three osmoreceptors Median Preoptic Nucleus (MnPO), Organum Vasculosum Laminae Terminalis (OVLT), and Sub Fornical Nucleus (SFO) play key roles in detecting changes in blood osmolality or sodium ion concentration. • An increase in blood osmolality or sodium ions stimulates the OVLT, which then activates MnPO through interconnected pathways. It does not directly stimulate the supra-optic nucleus (Option A) • These three osmoreceptors Median Preoptic Nucleus (MnPO), Organum Vasculosum Laminae Terminalis (OVLT), and Sub Fornical Nucleus (SFO) play key roles in detecting changes in blood osmolality or sodium ion concentration. • An increase in blood osmolality or sodium ions stimulates the OVLT, which then activates MnPO through interconnected pathways. It does not directly stimulate the supra-optic nucleus (Option A) • Response to isotonic loss of fluid (e.g. severe haemorrhage) Severe hemorrhage results in isotonic fluid loss from the blood, triggering an increase in renin secretion. Renin secretion leads to elevated levels of angiotensin 2 (Ag II) , which acts as a stimulus for the SFO. Despite the blood osmolality remaining constant during haemorrhage, the SFO can still be stimulated by the presence of angiotensin 2. (Option B) • Severe hemorrhage results in isotonic fluid loss from the blood, triggering an increase in renin secretion. • Renin secretion leads to elevated levels of angiotensin 2 (Ag II) , which acts as a stimulus for the SFO. • Despite the blood osmolality remaining constant during haemorrhage, the SFO can still be stimulated by the presence of angiotensin 2. (Option B) • Severe hemorrhage results in isotonic fluid loss from the blood, triggering an increase in renin secretion. • Renin secretion leads to elevated levels of angiotensin 2 (Ag II) , which acts as a stimulus for the SFO. • Despite the blood osmolality remaining constant during haemorrhage, the SFO can still be stimulated by the presence of angiotensin 2. (Option B) • Pathways of signals Signals from stimulated osmoreceptors primarily target two brain regions: Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). ADH acts on the kidneys to reabsorb water, thereby reducing osmolality or sodium ion concentration in the body. • Signals from stimulated osmoreceptors primarily target two brain regions: Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). • Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. • Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). • ADH acts on the kidneys to reabsorb water, thereby reducing osmolality or sodium ion concentration in the body. • Signals from stimulated osmoreceptors primarily target two brain regions: Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). • Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. • Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). • ADH acts on the kidneys to reabsorb water, thereby reducing osmolality or sodium ion concentration in the body. • Lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, stimulating thirst and causing the person to feel thirsty. • Supra-optic nucleus of the hypothalamus, which produces ADH (antidiuretic hormone). Stimulated osmoreceptors in the SFO increase the secretion of ADH. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 105-105; Solution Pages 120-122","sourcePage":105,"sourcePageEnd":105},{"id":"physiology-4-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about the updated theory of control of feeding?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Both orexigenic and anorexigenic signals are present in the pre-ventricular nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Leptin, Peptide YY and cortisol are all anorexigenic signals"},{"letter":"C","text":"POMC Fragmentation produces alpha MSH promotes food intake"},{"letter":"D","text":"Orexin peptide produced from the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus plays an essential role in regulating sleep"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Orexin peptide produced from the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus plays an essential role in regulating sleep","explanation":"Orexin peptide produced from the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus plays an essential role in regulating sleep and also increases food intake. Theory of control of feeding: Arcuate Nucleus Overview: • Located in the hypothalamus, it integrates orexigenic and anorexigenic signals to regulate feeding behaviour. (Option A) Neuronal Types: • POMC Neurons: Produce alpha-MSH, an anorexigenic signal that reduces food intake. (Option C) • Produce alpha-MSH, an anorexigenic signal that reduces food intake. (Option C) • NPY and AGRP Neurons: Produce orexigenic signals that stimulate the hypothalamic pre-ventricular nucleus, increasing food intake. • Produce orexigenic signals that stimulate the hypothalamic pre-ventricular nucleus, increasing food intake. • Produce alpha-MSH, an anorexigenic signal that reduces food intake. (Option C) • Produce orexigenic signals that stimulate the hypothalamic pre-ventricular nucleus, increasing food intake. Peripheral Signals: • Anorexigenic Signals (reduce food intake): Insulin, leptin, CCK, and Peptide YY stimulate POMC neurons. (Option B) • Insulin, leptin, CCK, and Peptide YY stimulate POMC neurons. (Option B) • Orexigenic Signals (increase food intake): Ghrelin and cortisol stimulate NPY and AGRP neurons. • Ghrelin and cortisol stimulate NPY and AGRP neurons. • Insulin, leptin, CCK, and Peptide YY stimulate POMC neurons. (Option B) • Ghrelin and cortisol stimulate NPY and AGRP neurons. Leptin and Obesity: • Leptin, indicating fat cell mass, acts as an anorexigenic signal in the hypothalamus. • High leptin levels in obese individuals indicate a large fat cell mass, promoting reduced food intake. Role of Orexin: • Produced by the lateral nucleus of the hypothalamus, orexin regulates sleep and increases food intake.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 105-105; Solution Pages 123-124","sourcePage":105,"sourcePageEnd":105},{"id":"physiology-4-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which statement best describes the center-surround model of the basal ganglia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The direct pathway broadly inhibits motor actions, while the indirect pathway facilitates specific actions."},{"letter":"B","text":"The direct pathway facilitates specific motor actions, while the indirect pathway suppresses competing actions."},{"letter":"C","text":"The indirect pathway reduces inhibitory output from the GPi, while the direct pathway increases inhibitory output."},{"letter":"D","text":"Both pathways uniformly facilitate all motor actions"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The direct pathway facilitates specific motor actions, while the indirect pathway suppresses competing actions.","explanation":"Center-Surround Model: The centre-surround model describes how the basal ganglia circuitry provides focused activation (centre) while suppressing surrounding activity (surround) to enhance motor control and precision. • Centre: The direct pathway acts as a \"centre\" mechanism by facilitating specific motor actions and enhancing desired movements. It reduces inhibitory signals from the GPi/SNr to the thalamus, thereby increasing thalamic activity related to the motor cortex. • Surround: The indirect pathway acts as a \"surround\" mechanism by inhibiting unwanted movements and refining motor control. It increases inhibition in broader motor regions by enhancing the inhibition of the GPi/SNr, thus decreasing thalamic activation. • The direct pathway does not broadly inhibit motor actions; it facilitates specific actions by reducing inhibition from the GPi. The indirect pathway does not facilitate actions; it suppresses competing actions by increasing inhibition. (Option A) • The indirect pathway increases inhibitory output from the GPi, not reduces it. The direct pathway reduces inhibitory output from the GPi. (Option C) • The pathways do not uniformly facilitate all motor actions. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 106-106; Solution Pages 124-125","sourcePage":106,"sourcePageEnd":106},{"id":"physiology-4-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-4","chapter":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topic":"Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements about climbing and mossy fibers is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Climbing and mossy fibers provide the primary input to the cerebellar cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"The Vestibulocerebellar serves as the pathway for climbing fibers."},{"letter":"C","text":"Mossy fibers are the terminal endings of the olivocerebellar tracts."},{"letter":"D","text":"Mossy fibers use aspartate to provide excitatory signals to the Purkinje cells."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Climbing and mossy fibers provide the primary input to the cerebellar cortex. Explanation : Climbing fibers and mossy fibers are the main afferent pathways that bring excitatory input to the cerebellar cortex. Afferent Fibers to Cerebellar Cortex Source/Tracts Fiber Type Neurotransmit Function ter Olivocerebellar Climbing Aspartate Proprioceptive input from tracts Fibers the entire body Vestibulocerebell Mossy Fiber Glutamate Receives information from ar s the labyrinth. Spinocerebellar Mossy Fiber Glutamate Receives information from s the Muscle spindle, the Golgi Tendon Organ. Cuneocerebellar Mossy Fiber Glutamate Receives information from s the Muscle spindle, Golgi Tendon Organ. Tectocerebellar Mossy Fiber Glutamate Receives information from s visual & auditory. Pontocerebellar Mossy Fiber Glutamate Receives information from s the motor & other parts of the cerebral cortex.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 106-106; Solution Pages 125-126","sourcePage":106,"sourcePageEnd":106},{"id":"physiology-5-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following cells in the olfactory bulb lacks an axon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Periglomerular cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mitral cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tufted cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Granule cell"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: (D) Granule cell Explanation: Granule cells are inhibitory neurons present in the olfactory bulb. Their unique characteristic is the loss of axons, but have multiple branched-out dendrites. Olfactory bulb connection: The synapse between the bipolar neuron and the second-order neuron occurs within the olfactory bulb. • The second-order neuron belongs to the categories of mitral and tufted cells , which serve as projection neurons. • The specialised structure where these connections occur is termed glomeruli within the olfactory bulb. • Between the first-order (bipolar) and second-order (mitral and tufted) neurons, two additional cell types exist: Periglomerular cells function as inhibitory neurons. Granule cells are also inhibitory neurons ; they lack axons but possess dendrites, enabling dendrodendritic synapses. • Periglomerular cells function as inhibitory neurons. • Granule cells are also inhibitory neurons ; they lack axons but possess dendrites, enabling dendrodendritic synapses. • Both types of inhibitory neurons play roles in refining and concentrating olfactory signals. • Periglomerular cells function as inhibitory neurons. • Granule cells are also inhibitory neurons ; they lack axons but possess dendrites, enabling dendrodendritic synapses.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 127-127; Solution Pages 134-135","sourcePage":127,"sourcePageEnd":127},{"id":"physiology-5-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the pathway of taste perception from the tongue to the brain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Taste signals from the taste buds are first transmitted to the gustatory cortex via CN VII, IX & X"},{"letter":"B","text":"The taste receptors generate nerve impulses relayed to the nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla."},{"letter":"C","text":"From the nucleus of the solitary tract, taste signals travel to the ventral posterolateral nucleus of the thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"The gustatory cortex, responsible for taste perception, is located in the medulla oblongata,"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: (B) The taste receptors generate nerve impulses relayed to the nucleus of the solitary tract in the medulla. Explanation: • Taste Receptors on Tongue: The journey begins with the taste receptors on the tongue, responsible for detecting the five primary tastes: sour, salty, sweet, bitter, and umami. These receptors are located in taste buds. • Cranial Nerves: Taste signals are transmitted from the taste buds to the brain stem via three cranial nerves: the facial nerve (VII), the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), and the vagus nerve (X). • Nucleus of the Solitary Tract in the Medulla: These cranial nerves carry the taste information to a specific nucleus in the medulla oblongata called the nucleus of the solitary tract. (Option A) • Ventral Posterior Medial Nucleus of the Thalamus: From the nucleus of the solitary tract, the taste signals are relayed to the ventral posterior medial nucleus of the thalamus. (Option C) • Gustatory Cortex: Finally, the taste signals are transmitted from the thalamus to the gustatory cortex, the primary area responsible for processing taste perception in the brain. This region is located in the lower portion of the postcentral gyrus, extending into a part of the insular cortex. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 127-127; Solution Pages 135-136","sourcePage":127,"sourcePageEnd":127},{"id":"physiology-5-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following regions is responsible for the perception of smell?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Piriform cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Entorhinal cortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Amygdala"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior olfactory nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Answer: (A) Piriform cortex Explanation: Regions of the olfactory cortex are olfactory cortex\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"579\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"456\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/tNJnu5M5L1VD APiraNdx1736095211.png\" width=\"847\" /> • Anterior olfactory nucleus (Option D) • Olfactory tubercle. • Piriform cortex: Responsible for the perception of a smell. (Option A) • Amygdala: Mediated the emotional responses to olfactory stimuli. (Option C) • Entorhinal cortex: Concerned with olfactory memories . (Option B) Sequence of events in Olfactory cortex: • The second-order neuron travels to the cortex via the lateral olfactory tract. • The lateral olfactory tract originates from the olfactory bulb, specifically from the glomeruli, and extends into different areas of the olfactory cortex. • Some of these fibers project to the thalamus, which then relays signals to the orbitofrontal cortex. • The orbitofrontal cortex is involved in the conscious perception and discrimination of odors. • The pathway traverses the mediodorsal (dorsomedial) thalamic nucleus (MDT) and the orbitofrontal cortex, forming the new olfactory cortex together.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 127-127; Solution Pages 136-137","sourcePage":127,"sourcePageEnd":127},{"id":"physiology-5-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 38-year-old man presents to the ophthalmology clinic with complaints of sensitivity to light and occasional blurriness in his vision. During the physical examination,When light is shone into his right eye, both pupils constrict normally andWhen light is shone into his left eye, only the left pupil constricts. Given these observations, which of the following connections of the pathway is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ciliary ganglion and sphincter pupillae"},{"letter":"B","text":"Edinger-Westphal nucleus and ciliary ganglion"},{"letter":"C","text":"pretectal nucleus and Edinger-Westphal nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"optic tract and pretectal nucleus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"If there is a lesion between the pretectal nucleus and the contralateral Edinger-Westphal nucleus, the ability to coordinate the consensual light reflex between the two eyes will be impaired, resulting in failure of light reflex in the right eye when the light is shone on the left eye. • This would result in the left pupil constricting when light is shone into the left eye (direct reflex intact) but failing to cause the right pupil to constrict (impaired consensual reflex) as supposedly the connections are impaired between the right Edinger-Westphal nucleus and the pretectal nucleus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 128-128; Solution Pages 137-137","sourcePage":128,"sourcePageEnd":128},{"id":"physiology-5-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Identify the labelled structures A, B, C, and D in the auditory pathway diagram.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A: Inferior colliculus, B: Superior olivary nucleus, C: Dorsal cochlear nucleus, D: Medial geniculate body"},{"letter":"B","text":"A: Inferior Colliculus, B: Lateral lemniscus, C: Superior olivary nucleus, D: Ventral cochlear nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"A: Inferior Colliculus, B: Lateral Lemniscus, C: Superior olivary nucleus, D: Trapezoid Body"},{"letter":"D","text":"A: Primary auditory area, B: Lateral lemniscus, C: Superior olivary nucleus, D: Spiral ganglion"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The marked structures correspond to the auditory pathway, shown in the image below.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 128-129; Solution Pages 138-138","sourcePage":128,"sourcePageEnd":129},{"id":"physiology-5-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements about colour vision abnormalities is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Protanopia is the loss of L cones, resulting in green-blue vision only"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deuteranopia is the loss of M cones, resulting in red-green vision only."},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of L and M cones has X-linked inheritance, occurring in about 1% of males."},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of S cones is rare and has autosomal inheritance via chromosome 7."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Deuteranopia is the loss of M cones, resulting in loss of green colour perception and leading to red-blue vision only. Color Vision Type Cone Type affected Perceived vision Inheritance Protanopia L Cones Green-blue vision X-linked (Red defect) (Option A) Deuteranopia M Cones Red-blue vision X-linked (Green defect) (Option B) Tritanopia S Cones Red-green vision Autosomal (linked to (Blue defect) chromosome 7) (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 129-129; Solution Pages 139-139","sourcePage":129,"sourcePageEnd":129},{"id":"physiology-5-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the structure of the cochlea?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The cochlea is a part of the outer ear and contains the ossicles."},{"letter":"B","text":"The cochlea is divided into three fluid-filled compartments: the scala vestibuli, scala media, and scala tympani."},{"letter":"C","text":"The organ of Corti, which contains hair cells responsible for hearing, is located in the scala vestibuli."},{"letter":"D","text":"The basilar membrane separates the scala media from the scala tympani and is responsible for transmitting sound waves directly to the auditory nerve."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B. The cochlea is divided into three fluid-filled compartments: the scala vestibuli, scala media, and scala tympani. Explanation: • The cochlea is a spiral-shaped organ in the inner ear, essential for hearing. • It consists of three main compartments: Scala vestibuli: The upper chamber filled with perilymph. Scala media (cochlear duct): The middle chamber filled with endolymph and contains the organ of Corti. Scala tympani: The lower chamber filled with perilymph. • Scala vestibuli: The upper chamber filled with perilymph. • Scala media (cochlear duct): The middle chamber filled with endolymph and contains the organ of Corti. • Scala tympani: The lower chamber filled with perilymph. • The organ of Corti is located in the scala media and contains hair cells that detect sound vibrations and convert them into electrical signals sent to the brain. • Scala vestibuli: The upper chamber filled with perilymph. • Scala media (cochlear duct): The middle chamber filled with endolymph and contains the organ of Corti. • Scala tympani: The lower chamber filled with perilymph. Option A. The cochlea is a part of the outer ear and contains the ossicles. • The cochlea is part of the inner ear, not the outer ear. • The ossicles (malleus, incus, and stapes) are located in the middle ear, not in the cochlea. Option C. The organ of Corti, which contains hair cells responsible for hearing, is located in the scala vestibuli. • The organ of Corti is located in the scala media, not the scala vestibuli. • The scala vestibuli is one of the fluid-filled compartments but does not contain the organ of Corti. Option D. The basilar membrane separates the scala media from the scala tympani and is responsible for transmitting sound waves directly to the auditory nerve. • While the basilar membrane does separate the scala media from the scala tympani and plays a crucial role in sound wave propagation, it does not transmit sound waves directly to the auditory nerve. • The hair cells on the basilar membrane convert mechanical vibrations into electrical signals, which are then transmitted to the auditory nerve by synaptic connections.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 129-129; Solution Pages 139-140","sourcePage":129,"sourcePageEnd":129},{"id":"physiology-5-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Match the following types of taste with their respective mechanisms. Type of Taste Mechanism 1) Salt A) Mediated by H+ ions. 2) Sour B) Mediated by T1R2 and T1R3 GPCRs 3) Sweet C) Mediated by T1R1 and T1R3 GPCRs 4) Bitter D) Mediated by Na+-selective channels. 5) Umami E) Mediated by T2R family GPCRs. Type of Taste Mechanism 1) Salt A) Mediated by H+ ions. 2) Sour B) Mediated by T1R2 and T1R3 GPCRs 3) Sweet C) Mediated by T1R1 and T1R3 GPCRs 4) Bitter D) Mediated by Na+-selective channels. 5) Umami E) Mediated by T2R family GPCRs.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 - D, 2 - A, 3 - B, 4 - E, 5 - C"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 - D, 2 - A, 3 - E, 4 - B, 5 - C"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 - D, 2 - A, 3 - C, 4 - E, 5 - B"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 - D, 2 - E, 3 - B, 4 - C, 5 - A"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Humans have five established basic tastes: sweet, sour, bitter, salt, and umami. • All these tastes are recognized by binding of the taste chemical to a protein receptor molecule that lies on the taste receptor cell to initiate the receptor potential. • Each taste has a different mechanism for triggering receptor potential. Type of Taste Receptor Type Mechanism Salt Ionotropic Receptor (ENaC) Mediated by Na+-selective channels Sour Ionotropic Receptor (H+) Mediated by H+ ions. Sweet Metabotropic Receptor (GPCR) Mediated by T1R2 and T1R3 GPCRs. Bitter Metabotropic Receptor (GPCR) Mediated by T2R family GPCRs. Umami Metabotropic Receptor (GPCR) Mediated by T1R1 and T1R3 GPCRs","sourceRef":"Question Pages 129-130; Solution Pages 140-141","sourcePage":129,"sourcePageEnd":130},{"id":"physiology-5-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":9,"question":"What is the location and the number of taste buds approximately present in the image given below","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Up to 100, two lateral ends of the tongue"},{"letter":"B","text":"Up to 5, base of the tongue"},{"letter":"C","text":"Up to 100, tip of the tongue"},{"letter":"D","text":"Up to 25, base of the tongue"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Answer: (A) Up to 100, two lateral ends of the tongue Explanation: The image above describes a foliate papillae present the two lateral ends of the tongue and has upto 100 taste buds. Types of Papilla in tongue: Papilla in tongue\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"574\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"279\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/5KO5G0W9rF1 TfrwvbObl1736093721.png\" width=\"748\" /> Papilla in tongue-diagramatic\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"575\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"496\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/DlcUg0abI71Ju4T3Qx1w1736093760.png\" width=\"667\" />","sourceRef":"Question Pages 130-130; Solution Pages 141-141","sourcePage":130,"sourcePageEnd":130},{"id":"physiology-5-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":10,"question":"In the visual pathway, which of the following correctly identifies the first, second, and third-order neurons and their respective locations?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First-order: Bipolar cells in the retina; Second-order: Ganglion cells in the retina; Third-order: Neurons in the LGN of the thalamus."},{"letter":"B","text":"First-order: Photoreceptor cells in the retina; Second-order: Bipolar cells in the retina; Third-order: Ganglion cells in the retina."},{"letter":"C","text":"First-order: Bipolar cells in the retina; Second-order: Ganglion cells in the retina; Third-order: Neurons in the superior colliculus."},{"letter":"D","text":"First-order: Photoreceptor cells in the retina; Second-order: Ganglion cells in the retina; Third-order: Neurons in the LGN of the thalamus."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: D) First-order: Photoreceptor cells in the retina; Second-order: Ganglion cells in the retina; Third-order: Neurons in the LGN of the thalamus. Explanation: Neurons of Visual Pathway: • First-order neurons : Photoreceptor cells (rods and cones) in the retina. • Second-order neurons : Ganglion cells in the retina. Third-order neurons : Neurons in the thalamus's lateral geniculate nucleus (LGN). Neuronal Cell Types in the Retina: Function Photoreceptors Transmit signals to the outer plexiform layer. (Rods and They connect with bipolar and horizontal cells. cones) Horizontal Cells Transmit signals horizontally in the outer plexiform layer. Connect rods and cones to bipolar cells. Bipolar Cells Transmit signals vertically from rods, cones, and horizontal cells. Connect with ganglion cells and amacrine cells in the inner plexiform layer. Amacrine Cells Transmit signals in two ways: directly from bipolar cells to ganglion cells or horizontally within the inner plexiform layer. Connect axons of bipolar cells to dendrites of ganglion cells or other amacrine cells. Ganglion Cell Transmit output signals from the retina to the brain via the optic nerve. Interplexiform Transmit inhibitory signals retrogradely from the inner to Cells the outer plexiform layer. Control the lateral spread of visual signals by horizontal cells, potentially enhancing visual contrast.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 131-131; Solution Pages 142-143","sourcePage":131,"sourcePageEnd":131},{"id":"physiology-5-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":11,"question":"What is the primary mechanism by which an odorant molecule leads to the depolarisation of an olfactory cell?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"close sodium ion channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Triggers the breakdown of cyclic adenosine monophosphate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Initiating a signalling cascade that ultimately opens sodium ion channels."},{"letter":"D","text":"The odorant molecule is transported into the olfactory cell, where it directly depolarises"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: (C) Initiating a signaling cascade that ultimately opens sodium ion channels. Explanation: Mechanism of Olfactory signaling: Olfactory signaling\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"571\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"444\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/1y2b7mCXnmp CbEZQrbxW1736092465.png\" width=\"648\" /> • Single cilia (as shown in the picture) have olfactory receptors that are G protein-coupled receptors. • Odorants activate G protein, which increases cyclic AMP (cAMP) (Option B) as a second messenger. • cAMP opens non-specific cation channels in the cilium, allowing calcium and sodium ions to enter, causing depolarization. • Depolarization above a threshold (-30 mV) generates action potentials in bipolar sensory neurons. • Action potentials are transmitted to the olfactory bulb. • Calcium-gated chloride channels in the cilium further amplify depolarization. • Chloride ions move out due to increased calcium, reducing negative charge and enhancing positivity inside the cell. • This amplification process facilitates action potential generation and transmission to the olfactory bulb. (Option A) Odorant molecules don't close sodium channels but They initiate a signaling cascade for that lead to opening of Na channel (Option D) Odorant molecules don't need to enter the olfactory cell; binding to external receptors is sufficient to trigger the signaling cascade.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 131-131; Solution Pages 143-143","sourcePage":131,"sourcePageEnd":131},{"id":"physiology-5-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements about bipolar sensory neurons involved in the olfactory pathway is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Only first and second-order neurons are sufficient to reach the level of the cerebral cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bypasses the thalamus and directly enters the cortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Each bipolar sensory neuron has 6-12 cilia at the top of the olfactory epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Olfactory bulb is the site where first-order neurons synapse with bipolar sensory neurons"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: (D) Olfactory bulb is the site where first-order neurons synapse with bipolar sensory neurons. Explanation: Bipolar sensory neurons are the first order neurons that synapses with the second order neuron in the olfactory bulb. bipolar sensory neurons\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"570\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"451\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/zczrmM64cJ1yo7bV2OTw1736091649.png\" width=\"551\" /> • Bipolar sensory neurons have dendrites and axons. • Their axons extend through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone to the olfactory bulb. • At the olfactory bulb, bipolar neurons synapse with second-order neurons at structures called glomeruli. • Each bipolar neuron has 6-12 cilia containing olfactory receptors . (Option C) • When activated by odorants, bipolar cells generate action potentials transmitted through second-order neurons to the cerebral cortex. • Unlike other sensory pathways, the olfactory pathway requires only first and second-order neurons to reach the cerebral cortex directly, bypassing the thalamus. (Option A and B) Other cells of olfactory epithelium include: Cell type Description Bipolar sensory Approximately 50 million in number. neuron Supporting cells Secrete mucous in which the cilia of the bipolar sensory neurons are embedded. Chemicals encountered by the nose (smell) are first dissolved in the mucous before being detected by the receptors. Basal stem cells Responsible for regenerating bipolar sensory neurons.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 131-131; Solution Pages 144-144","sourcePage":131,"sourcePageEnd":131},{"id":"physiology-5-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Arrange in order the mechanism of sound transmission i) Depolarization of hair cells ii) Bending of stereocilia iii) Production of neurotransmitter glutamate iv) K & Ca enter via the channel","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"iv, iii, ii, i"},{"letter":"B","text":"iv, i, ii, iii"},{"letter":"C","text":"ii, i, iii, iv"},{"letter":"D","text":"ii, iv, i, iii"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: (D) ii, iv, i, iii Explanation: The organ of Corti, containing hair cells, are the sensory receptors for sound, required for the transmission of sound. When the basilar membrane vibrates, it causes the stereocilia (projections on the hair cells) to bend against the tectorial membrane. The bending of the stereocilia triggers a cascade of electrochemical events , ultimately generating nerve impulses in the auditory nerve fibers connected to the hair cells","sourceRef":"Question Pages 132-132; Solution Pages 145-145","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":132},{"id":"physiology-5-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the difference between outer and inner hair cells in the human cochlea?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Outer hair cells primarily send auditory signals to the brain, while inner hair cells are involved in amplifying sound."},{"letter":"B","text":"Inner hair cells are responsible for sending the majority of auditory information to the brain, whereas outer hair cells serve to amplify and fine-tune the sound vibrations."},{"letter":"C","text":"Both outer and inner hair cells have the same function of sending auditory signals to the brain."},{"letter":"D","text":"Outer hair cells are fewer in number compared to inner hair cells and play a critical role in sound transmission."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B. Inner hair cells are responsible for sending the majority of auditory information to the brain, whereas outer hair cells serve to amplify and fine-tune sound vibrations■■. Explanation: Outer hair cells (OHCs) and inner hair cells (IHCs). Differences between outer and inner hair cells: Feature Outer Hair Cells (OHCs) Inner Hair Cells (IHCs) Rows Three rows Arranged in a single row Structur Cylindrical shape; contains prestin, Flask-shaped; stereocilia arranged e a motor protein in a linear or slightly curved pattern Functio Amplify sound vibrations; enhance Convert sound vibrations into n sensitivity and frequency selectivity electrical signals sent to the brain Mechani Change length (contract and Stereocilia deflection opens ion sm elongate) in response to sound channels, causing depolarization (electromotility) and neurotransmitter release Innervat Predominantly efferent (motor) Predominantly afferent (sensory) ion nerve fibers nerve fibers Role in Act as mechanical amplifiers, Primary sensory receptors for Hearing increasing the amplitude of low-level hearing; transmit auditory sounds information to the brain Damage Loss leads to reduced hearing Damage results in significant Impact sensitivity and clarity, especially for hearing loss due to impaired signal soft sounds transmission","sourceRef":"Question Pages 132-132; Solution Pages 145-146","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":132},{"id":"physiology-5-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 25-year-old woman visits her ophthalmologist complaining of difficulty seeing in low-light conditions. She reports that her vision is fine during the day but deteriorates significantly at night. Based on your understanding of the visual pathway mechanisms in light versus dark conditions, which of the following best explains her symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased activity of rod cells in bright light."},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased neurotransmitter release from photoreceptors in the dark."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased depolarisation of photoreceptors in the presence of light."},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased cGMP levels in photoreceptors during darkness."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B. Decreased neurotransmitter release from photoreceptors in the dark. Explanation: In the dark: • Rods and cones are depolarised due to the high levels of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP). • cGMP binds to and opens cGMP-gated sodium (Na+) channels known as Funny Current Sodium Channels. • Sodium ions (Na+) flow into the photoreceptors, keeping them depolarised. • Depolarised photoreceptors continuously release the neurotransmitter glutamate at their synaptic terminals. • This glutamate release inhibits bipolar cells, preventing signal transmission to the ganglion cells and, subsequently, to the brain. In light: • Light photons are absorbed by photopigments (rhodopsin in rods, photopsins in cones). • This absorption activates a G-protein called transducin. • Transducin activates phosphodiesterase (PDE), an enzyme that breaks down cGMP. • The decrease in cGMP levels causes cGMP-gated sodium channels to close. • The closure of sodium channels leads to hyperpolarization of the photoreceptor membrane. • Hyperpolarized photoreceptors reduce the release of glutamate. • The decrease in glutamate release allows bipolar cells to become active, transmitting signals to ganglion cells and then to the brain, resulting in visual perception. Option A. Rod cells are not primarily active in bright light; they are more sensitive to low light. In bright light, rod cells are saturated and do not function effectively. Option C. Light exposure causes hyperpolarization of photoreceptors, not depolarization. Light reduces the intracellular cGMP levels, leading to the closure of sodium channels and hyperpolarization. Option D. In darkness, cGMP levels are high, keeping the sodium channels open and the photoreceptors depolarized.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 132-133; Solution Pages 146-148","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":133},{"id":"physiology-5-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly differentiates between the structure of rods and cones in the human retina?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rods contain the photopigment iodopsin, whereas cones contain rhodopsin."},{"letter":"B","text":"Rods are primarily responsible for colour vision, whereas cones are responsible for night vision."},{"letter":"C","text":"Rods have a longer outer segment with more disk-like structures, whereas cones have a shorter outer segment with fewer disk-like structures."},{"letter":"D","text":"Rods are concentrated in the fovea, whereas cones are distributed throughout the retina."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Rods have a longer outer segment with more disk-like structures, whereas cones have a shorter outer segment with fewer disk-like structures. Explanation: Structure and Function of Rods and Cones • Rods and cones are the two main types of photoreceptor cells in the retina, each with unique structural adaptations that allow them to perform their specific roles in vision. • Rods are optimised for night and peripheral vision, while cones enable detailed and colour vision in bright light. (Option B) • Both rods and cones share four major structural segments, as explained: Outer segment Inner segment Nucleus Synaptic body • Outer segment • Inner segment • Nucleus • Synaptic body Feature Rods Cones Outer Segment Shape Elongated and cylindrical Shorter and tapering (Option C) Disk Structures Numerous separate disks Fewer, continuous infoldings (Option C) Photopigment Rhodopsin Photopsins (iodopsins) (Option A) Light Sensitivity High (sensitive to low light) Lower (sensitive to bright light) Color Vision No (monochromatic) Yes (trichromatic) Vision Type Scotopic (night vision) Photopic (daylight and color vision) Distribution Throughout the retina, absent in the Concentrated in the fovea, less in (Option D) fovea the periphery Synaptic Terminal Spherule Pedicle Renewal of Disks Continuous shedding and renewal Continuous, slower renewal Connection to Bipolar Many rods to one bipolar cell One cone to one bipolar cell (in Cells fovea)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 133-133; Solution Pages 148-149","sourcePage":133,"sourcePageEnd":133},{"id":"physiology-5-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-5","chapter":"Special Senses","topic":"Special Senses","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following best explains why rods are more sensitive to low light than cones?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Individual connections to ganglion cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"High convergence onto ganglion cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lower density in the retina"},{"letter":"D","text":"Larger size of photoreceptors"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B. High convergence onto ganglion cells Explanation: Convergence in Rods: • Rods are highly convergent onto ganglion cells. This means that multiple rod cells synapse onto a single ganglion cell. • This high level of convergence allows for the pooling of signals from many rods. Consequently, even small amounts of light can trigger a response because the signals from multiple rods are combined and sent as a single, stronger signal to the brain. This increases the sensitivity of rods to low light conditions. • While convergence enhances sensitivity, it reduces spatial resolution. Since multiple rods converge onto a single ganglion cell, the specific location of light cannot be as accurately determined, leading to less detailed vision. Non-Convergence in Cones: • Cones generally have a more direct, one-to-one connection with ganglion cells, especially in the fovea. • As a result, they are less sensitive to low light compared to rods. Each cone needs a stronger light stimulus to activate the associated ganglion cell. The direct connection of cones to ganglion cells allows for high spatial resolution. Each cone contributes a distinct signal to the brain, enabling detailed and sharp vision. Individual connections to ganglion cells (Option A): This is true for cones, not rods. Cones connect individually to ganglion cells, especially in the fovea, which provides high spatial resolution but lower sensitivity. Lower density in the retina (Option C): Rods are more densely packed in the peripheral retina, which contributes to their sensitivity to low light, but the primary reason for their high sensitivity is their convergence onto ganglion cells. Larger size of photoreceptors (Option D): The size of the photoreceptors does not significantly impact their sensitivity to light.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 133-133; Solution Pages 149-150","sourcePage":133,"sourcePageEnd":133},{"id":"physiology-6-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements about EEG (Electroencephalogram) is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"EEG measures the summated version of EPSP and IPSP generated in the brain."},{"letter":"B","text":"EEG records the dendritic potentials of pyramidal neurons."},{"letter":"C","text":"Positive dendritic potentials are known as EPSP (Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential) and negative dendritic potentials are known as IPSP (Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potential)."},{"letter":"D","text":"D) Electrocorticography (ECOG) is the measurement of electrical activity specifically in the cerebral cortex by placing electrodes on the surface of scalp."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Electrocorticography (ECOG) involves placing electrodes directly on the cerebral cortex , not over the scalp. It is an invasive procedure requiring craniotomy. It is used to evaluate epileptogenic foci in patients with refractory epilepsy. Electroencephalogram - EEG • Electroencephalogram (EEG) is the record of spontaneous electrical activities generated in the cerebral cortex that are picked up from the brain's surface through electrodes placed on designated sites in the scalp. (Option B ruled out) • It detects the dendritic potentials of neurons, primarily pyramidal cells reaching the surface of the scalp. • Positive dendritic potential is known as EPSP (Excitatory Postsynaptic Potential), and negative dendritic potential is known as IPSP (Inhibitory postsynaptic potential). (Option C ruled out) • EEG captures the summated value of EPSP and IPSP . (Option A ruled out) • Also, EEG is the recording of local potential from the surface of the scalp, it is not the action potential .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 151-151; Solution Pages 156-157","sourcePage":151,"sourcePageEnd":151},{"id":"physiology-6-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Match the following EEG wave types with their respective frequency ranges and associated activities. Type of Wave Frequency Range (Hz) Activity During 1) Beta","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"8-13 i) Deep sleep 2) Alpha B) 14-30 ii) Relaxation, eyes closed 3) Theta C) 2-4 iii) Alert and active thinking 4) Delta D) 5-7 iv) Light sleep, emotional stress in adults Type of Wave Frequency Range (Hz) Activity During 1) Beta A) 8-13 i) Deep sleep 2) Alpha B) 14-30 ii) Relaxation, eyes closed 3) Theta C) 2-4 iii) Alert and active thinking iv) Light sleep, emotional stress in 4) Delta D) 5-7 adults"},{"letter":"A","text":"1 - B - iii, 2 - A - ii, 3 - D - iv, 4 - C - i"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 - C - i, 2 - B - iii, 3 - A - ii, 4 - D - iv"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 - B - iii, 2 - D - iv, 3 - A - ii, 4 - C - i"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 - A - ii, 2 - C - i, 3 - D - iv, 4 - B - iii"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Type of Wave Frequency Range (Hz) Activity During Beta 14-30 Alert and active thinking Alpha 8-13 Relaxation, eyes closed Theta 5-7 Light sleep, emotional stress in adults Delta 2-4 Deep sleep","sourceRef":"Question Pages 151-151; Solution Pages 157-157","sourcePage":151,"sourcePageEnd":151},{"id":"physiology-6-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What type of waves are going to be seen if the patient is evaluated on EEG during deep sleep?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sleep spindles"},{"letter":"B","text":"K-Complexes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Theta waves"},{"letter":"D","text":"Delta waves"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"The patient is likely to be in NREM Stage 4 sleep , known as the deep stage of sleep, characterized by delta waves (low frequency, high amplitude). Stage of Characteristics Sleep Stage N1 Light sleep, easy to wake up, characterised by theta Sleep waves. (Option C ruled out) Stage N2 Deeper than stage N1, the presence of sleep spindles Sleep and K-complexes, is still relatively easy to wake up. (Option A and B ruled out) Stage N3 Deepest sleep, Delta waves (low frequency and high Sleep amplitude), very difficult to wake up. REM Sleep Rapid eye movements, high brain activity, associated with dreaming, Beta wave pattern","sourceRef":"Question Pages 152-152; Solution Pages 158-158","sourcePage":152,"sourcePageEnd":152},{"id":"physiology-6-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements about REM and NREM sleep is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"During NREM sleep, there is an increase in parasympathetic outflow, leading to lower heart rate and blood pressure."},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscle tone is completely lost during REM sleep, and the body is effectively paralyzed."},{"letter":"C","text":"During deeper stages of NREM sleep, the subject is difficult to arouse by sensory stimuli."},{"letter":"D","text":"During REM sleep, the subject is easily aroused by sensory stimuli."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"NREM Sleep REM Sleep Autonomic Increased parasympathetic outflow, Increased sympathetic outflow Outflow leading to lower heart rate and blood pressure (Option A ruled out) Muscle Intact during NREM sleep Complete loss of muscle tone Tone and (muscle atonia) during REM sleep Reflexes (Option B ruled out) Arousability Difficult to arouse in deeper stages More difficult to arouse compared to (Stages 3 and 4) (Option C ruled NREM sleep by sensory stimuli, but out) may awaken spontaneously. Eye Movem Absence of rapid eye movement Presence of rapid eye movement ent (EOG) Brain Electr Not associated with PGO Associated with PGO spikes ical Activity (Ponto-Geniculo Occipital) spikes Dreaming Dreams uncertain; memorized if Vivid dreams; not memorized Activity dreams occur","sourceRef":"Question Pages 152-152; Solution Pages 158-159","sourcePage":152,"sourcePageEnd":152},{"id":"physiology-6-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 65-year-old woman presents with severe insomnia, reporting that she has been unable to sleep for several days. Neurological examination reveals a lesion in the basal ganglia and adjacent anterior hypothalamus. Which of the following statements about the mechanisms of sleep is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The wake-promoting area is located in the posterior nucleus of the hypothalamus and releases histamine."},{"letter":"B","text":"The sleep-promoting area is located in the anterior nucleus of the hypothalamus and releases GABA."},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased levels of adenosine at basal forebrain stimulate the sleep-promotion."},{"letter":"D","text":"The awake state has increased NE, serotonin, and reduced levels of acetylcholine, while the opposite is true for the state of sleep"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"There are 2 centres located in the hypothalamus, which are in constant mutual inhibition. • Wake-Promoting Area: Located in the posterior hypothalamus- Tuberomammillary nucleus (TMN), releasing histamine. (Option A ruled out) The wake-promoting area stimulates two important nuclei of the reticular activating system, i.e., Raphe nuclei (RN) , which produce serotonin , and Locus coeruleus (LC) , which produce non-epinephrine . These act at the level of the thalamus from which all the diffuse projections will reach the cortex and the active cortex means the person will remain awake. This active cortex indefinitely produces metabolites with awakeness, primarily Adenosine . An increase in adenosine acts at the level of the basal forebrain and stimulates the sleep-promoting area. • The wake-promoting area stimulates two important nuclei of the reticular activating system, i.e., Raphe nuclei (RN) , which produce serotonin , and Locus coeruleus (LC) , which produce non-epinephrine . • These act at the level of the thalamus from which all the diffuse projections will reach the cortex and the active cortex means the person will remain awake. • This active cortex indefinitely produces metabolites with awakeness, primarily Adenosine . • An increase in adenosine acts at the level of the basal forebrain and stimulates the sleep-promoting area. • Sleep-Promoting Area: Located in the anterior hypothalamus- Ventrolateral preoptic nucleus (VLPO), releasing GABA. (Option B ruled out) • The wake-promoting area stimulates two important nuclei of the reticular activating system, i.e., Raphe nuclei (RN) , which produce serotonin , and Locus coeruleus (LC) , which produce non-epinephrine . • These act at the level of the thalamus from which all the diffuse projections will reach the cortex and the active cortex means the person will remain awake. • This active cortex indefinitely produces metabolites with awakeness, primarily Adenosine . • An increase in adenosine acts at the level of the basal forebrain and stimulates the sleep-promoting area. Neurotransmitter Awake State REM State NREM Sleep Norepinephrine & High Low Low Serotonin Acetylcholine Low (Option D ruled out) High Low GABA & Histamine Low GABA, High High GABA, Low High GABA, Low Histamine Histamine Histamine","sourceRef":"Question Pages 152-153; Solution Pages 159-160","sourcePage":152,"sourcePageEnd":153},{"id":"physiology-6-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Match each sleep disorder with the correct stage of sleep in which it most commonly occurs Sleep Disorder Stage of Sleep a) Somnambulism 1) Stage 2 NREM b) Nocturnal enuresis 2) REM Sleep c) Narcolepsy 3) End of REM Sleep d) Sleep Paralysis 4) Stage 3 and 4 (NREM) Sleep Disorder Stage of Sleep a) Somnambulism 1) Stage 2 NREM b) Nocturnal enuresis 2) REM Sleep c) Narcolepsy 3) End of REM Sleep d) Sleep Paralysis 4) Stage 3 and 4 (NREM)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-4, b-2, c-1, d-3"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Answer B) a-4, b-1, c-2, d-3 Explanation: Stage of Sleep Sleep disorder Stage 3 and 4 NREM Somnambulism (Sleepwalking): Occurs during the deeper stages of NREM sleep, specifically stages 3 and 4. Nocturnal enuresis: Occurs in stages 3/4. Common in childhood, due to the failure of neural regulation of micturition. Stage 1 & 2 NREM Sleep Talking (Somniloquy): This can occur in any stage of sleep but is most common in stages 1 and 2 of NREM sleep. Bruxism (Teeth Grinding): Primarily occurs during stage 2 of NREM sleep. The transition from stage 3 to 4 NREM Night Terrors: Typically occur during the transition from stage 3 to stage 4 of NREM sleep. Unlike nightmares, individuals usually do not remember night terrors. REM Sleep Narcolepsy: Involves sudden onset of REM sleep. Individuals with narcolepsy often experience excessive daytime sleepiness, sudden muscle weakness (cataplexy), sleep paralysis, and hallucinations at the onset or end of sleep. The disorder is linked to a deficiency in the neurotransmitter orexin . Nightmares: Occur during REM sleep and involve vivid, disturbing dreams that can cause fear and anxiety. Unlike night terrors, nightmares are often remembered in detail upon waking. They can be triggered by stress, anxiety, or trauma.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 153-153; Solution Pages 160-161","sourcePage":153,"sourcePageEnd":153},{"id":"physiology-6-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 55-year-old patient presents with complaints of memory issues. He can recall general knowledge about the world, such as facts about historical events, but has difficulty remembering personal experiences, such as what he had for dinner last night or his activities from last weekend. Based on these symptoms, which of the following best describes the types of explicit memory affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Impaired semantic memory with intact episodic memory"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intact semantic memory with impaired episodic memory"},{"letter":"C","text":"Impaired procedural memory with intact working memory"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intact procedural memory with impaired working memory"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Intact semantic memory with impaired episodic memory","explanation":"The patient is able to recall general knowledge and facts about the world (intact semantic memory) but struggles to remember specific personal experiences such as what he had for dinner or activities from last weekend (impaired episodic memory). This distinction is consistent with the described symptoms and aligns with the known functions of semantic and episodic memory. Explicit memory , also known as declarative memory, is a type of long-term memory that involves conscious recollection of information. It is divided into two main categories: • This refers to the storage of general knowledge about the world. • It includes facts, concepts, and ideas that are not tied to specific personal experiences. • For instance, knowing that the capital of France is Paris, understanding the rules of mathematics, or recognizing historical dates and events are all parts of semantic memory. • The temporal lobes , particularly the anterior and lateral temporal cortex , are critically involved in the processing and storage of semantic memory. • This involves the recollection of personal experiences and specific events that have occurred at particular times and places. • It includes memories of one's own life, such as what you had for dinner last night, a vacation you took last summer, or a conversation you had with a friend. • The hippocampus and adjacent medial temporal lobe structures are essential for the formation and retrieval of episodic memories. • The patient’s difficulty in remembering what he had for dinner or his activities from the previous weekend suggests an impairment in episodic memory. (Option A ruled out) • Procedural memory is a type of implicit memory related to the performance of tasks, such as riding a bike or playing an instrument. • Working memory is a short-term memory system involved in the temporary storage and manipulation of information. The patient's symptoms do not relate to procedural memory or working memory issues but rather to long-term explicit memory. (Option C and Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 153-153; Solution Pages 161-162","sourcePage":153,"sourcePageEnd":153},{"id":"physiology-6-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 62-year-old woman who had a stroke several months ago reports that she is having difficulty with tasks she used to perform automatically. She struggles to remember how to ride her bicycle, a skill she previously performed with ease. She also mentions difficulty in learning new motor tasks. Based on these symptoms, which type of implicit memory is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Procedural memory"},{"letter":"B","text":"Priming"},{"letter":"C","text":"Associative learning"},{"letter":"D","text":"Procedural memory"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Procedural memory","explanation":"Implicit memory , also known as non-declarative memory, involves unconscious memory processes that influence thoughts and behaviors. Implicit memory is divided into following subcategories: • This type of implicit memory involves the learning and retention of motor skills and actions. • It enables individuals to perform tasks without conscious thought, such as riding a bicycle, typing on a keyboard, or playing a musical instrument. (Option A) • Priming refers to the unconscious influence that exposure to a stimulus has on a subsequent response to a different stimulus. • It helps with faster recognition or processing of similar stimuli without conscious awareness. • For example, seeing the word \"yellow\" might make it easier to recognize the word \"banana\" shortly afterward. (Option B ruled out) • Classical Conditioning : A process where a neutral stimulus becomes associated with a significant stimulus, resulting in a learned response. For example, Pavlov’s dogs learned to salivate at the sound of a bell because it was associated with food. • A process where a neutral stimulus becomes associated with a significant stimulus, resulting in a learned response. • For example, Pavlov’s dogs learned to salivate at the sound of a bell because it was associated with food. • Operant Conditioning : A type of learning where behavior is strengthened or weakened by rewards or punishments. For example, a rat learning to press a lever to receive food involves operant conditioning. • A type of learning where behavior is strengthened or weakened by rewards or punishments. • For example, a rat learning to press a lever to receive food involves operant conditioning. • Habituation : A decrease in response to a repeated, benign stimulus. For instance, becoming accustomed to a constant background noise and eventually ignoring it. • A decrease in response to a repeated, benign stimulus. • For instance, becoming accustomed to a constant background noise and eventually ignoring it. • Sensitisation : An increased response to a stimulus following a strong or noxious stimulus. For example, becoming more sensitive to a light touch after being exposed to a painful stimulus. • An increased response to a stimulus following a strong or noxious stimulus. • For example, becoming more sensitive to a light touch after being exposed to a painful stimulus. Semantic memory (Option D) is a type of explicit memory that involves the storage and recall of factual information and general knowledge, such as knowing the capital of a country or understanding historical events. The patient's difficulties with motor tasks and learning new skills are not related to semantic memory.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 154-154; Solution Pages 162-163","sourcePage":154,"sourcePageEnd":154},{"id":"physiology-6-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which type of learning involves the process by which humans and animals learn to behave in such a way as to obtain rewards and avoid punishments?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Classical conditioning"},{"letter":"B","text":"Operant conditioning"},{"letter":"C","text":"Observational learning"},{"letter":"D","text":"Habituation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Operant conditioning","explanation":"Operant conditioning involves learning through the consequences of behaviors . In this type of learning, behaviors are either reinforced (strengthened) or punished (weakened) based on their consequences. B.F. Skinner's experiments with rats in Skinner boxes demonstrated this concept. Skinner’s experiment: • In the experiment, a rat is placed in a chamber with an electrical grid on the floor and multiple switches on the wall. • The rat learns that pressing one switch results in an electric shock, while pressing another switch or lever results in food dropping into the box. • Over time, the rat associates pressing the food switch with receiving food, reinforcing this behavior. • Meanwhile, the association with the electric shock stimulus diminishes in the rat's memory. Thus, the rat associates with the stimulus to change the behaviour. Classical conditioning (Option A) is associative learning, where an organism learns to associate two stimuli. Observational learning (Option C): Observational learning, described by Albert Bandura, involves learning by observing others and their consequences. While observational learning can lead to behaviour modification, it focuses more on social learning and modelling rather than direct reinforcement or punishment. Habituation (Option D) is a form of non-associative learning where an organism decreases its response to a repeated stimulus that carries no positive or negative consequences. It does not involve learning through consequences or behavioural modification.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 154-154; Solution Pages 164-165","sourcePage":154,"sourcePageEnd":154},{"id":"physiology-6-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following parts of the brain is responsible for working memory?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prefrontal cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Neocortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventral hippocampus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Amygdala"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Prefrontal cortex","explanation":"Working memory is a type of short-term memory. • It typically lasts for about 18-20 seconds before information may fade without rehearsal. • The maximum capacity of working memory is around 7±2 'chunks' of information, such as items like fruits or numbers. • The prefrontal cortex is the brain region primarily responsible for working memory. (Option A) Short-term memory: • Short-term memory retains information for durations ranging from seconds to several hours. • The ventral hippocampus , specifically the CA1 region, is responsible for short-term memory. (Option C ruled out) • Through rehearsal or repetition, short-term memories can be consolidated into long-term memories. Long-term memory: • Long-term memory retains information for years or even a lifetime. • The neocortex serves as the centre for long-term memory storage. (Option B ruled out) Amygdala (Option D): The amygdala is primarily involved in emotional processing and the formation of emotional memories. Although it interacts with memory systems, it does not handle working memory, which is more closely linked to the prefrontal cortex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 154-154; Solution Pages 165-166","sourcePage":154,"sourcePageEnd":154},{"id":"physiology-6-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-6","chapter":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topic":"EEG & Sleep Learning and Memory","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following statements about synaptic plasticity and memory formation is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Long-term potentiation (LTP) primarily occurs at glutamatergic synapses in the hippocampus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Long-Term Depression (LTD) involves a significant influx of calcium into the postsynaptic neuron."},{"letter":"C","text":"LTP leads to the phosphorylation of AMPA receptors, enhancing their activity and strengthening the synapse."},{"letter":"D","text":"LTD is mediated by metabotropic glutamate receptors and involves dephosphorylation of AMPA receptors, leading to synaptic weakening."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Long-Term Depression (LTD) involves a significant influx of calcium into the postsynaptic neuron.","explanation":"A significant influx of calcium into the postsynaptic neuron is seen in the process of Long-term potentiation strengthening the synapse. Mechanism of memory formation: Memory formation relies on synaptic plasticity , which refers to the strengthening or weakening of synapses based on neuronal activity. Long-term potentiation: • Long-term potentiation (LTP), a mechanism for memory formation, involves the strengthening of synapses, particularly in the hippocampus (CA1) and cerebellum • It primarily occurs at glutamatergic synapses in the hippocampus. (Option A ruled out) • Two key glutamate receptors, AMPA and NMDA, are involved in LTP. • Glutamate binding to the AMPA receptor causes an influx of sodium ions (Na+) into the postsynaptic neuron, leading to depolarisation. • NMDA Receptor and Calcium Influx: The depolarization caused by AMPA receptor activation is crucial as it removes a magnesium ion (Mg2+) block from the NMDA receptor channel. This removal is key because Mg2+ normally prevents calcium ions (Ca2+) from entering the postsynaptic neuron. • Calcium's Role: This influx of Ca2+ initiates a cascade of events. Firstly, Ca2+ binds with calmodulin, forming a complex that activates kinase enzymes. • Kinase Activity and AMPA Receptor Phosphorylation: Kinases then phosphorylates various proteins, notably the AMPA receptor itself. Phosphorylation keeps the AMPA receptor open even after the initial stimulation ceases, leading to increased sensitivity and a strengthened synapse. (Option C ruled out) Long-term depression: • Long-Term Depression (LTD) is a process that weakens synapses • LTD involves a smaller influx of calcium ions (Ca2+) compared to LTP. • Metabotropic glutamate receptors are involved in LTD, unlike the NMDA receptors in LTP. These metabotropic receptors, when activated by glutamate, lead to a low influx of Ca2+ into the postsynaptic neuron. This smaller influx is crucial for initiating LTD. • Phosphatase enzymes are activated in LTD due to the low Ca2+ concentration. This contrasts with LTP, where a high Ca2+ concentration activates kinase enzymes. • Phosphatase activity results in the dephosphorylation of AMPA receptors, leading to their closure. (Option D ruled out) The closure of AMPA receptors inhibits the synapse, making it less responsive to stimuli. LTD, therefore, serves to weaken specific synapses, potentially filtering out irrelevant information while strengthening relevant ones through LTP.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 155-155; Solution Pages 166-167","sourcePage":155,"sourcePageEnd":155},{"id":"physiology-7-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient with metastatic breast cancer is prescribed paclitaxel. What effect does paclitaxel have on microtubules?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At low concentrations, paclitaxel promotes rapid polymerisation and mitotic spindle formation."},{"letter":"B","text":"At high concentrations, paclitaxel destabilizes microtubules, causing rapid disassembly and mitotic arrest."},{"letter":"C","text":"At high concentrations, paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules, increasing polymer mass and inducing bundle formation."},{"letter":"D","text":"Paclitaxel shifts equilibrium towards tubulin dimers, promoting microtubule dynamics and mitosis."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) At high concentrations, paclitaxel stabilizes microtubules, increasing polymer mass and inducing bundle formation.","explanation":"Paclitaxel primarily exerts anti-tumour effects by stabilising microtubules and altering their dynamics in a concentration-dependent manner. Paclitaxel: DEFINITION An anti-tumour agent which is effective against several cancers MECHANISM Binds to the β-subunit of tubulin in a 1:1 stoichiometry ↓ Causes stabilisation of microtubules (Options B and D) CONCENTRATION-DEPENDENT EFFECTS High Concentrations (Option C) Low Concentrations Microtubule polymer mass increases Suppresses microtubule dynamics without altering Induces microtubule bundle formation in polymer mass (Option A) interphase cells Blocks mitosis by stabilising spindle microtubules EFFECT ON CELLS Sensitive Cells Resistant Cells Mitotic Block Model 1: Contain \"hypo stable\" microtubules with Induces cell death equilibrium shifted towards tubulin dimers Model 2: Equilibrium is shifted towards highly dynamic microtubules","sourceRef":"Question Pages 168-168; Solution Pages 180-181","sourcePage":168,"sourcePageEnd":168},{"id":"physiology-7-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following is likely to be seen if a patient is infused with 3% NaCl?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Water shifts from ICF to ECF"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased plasma protein concentration"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased arterial blood pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All of the above","explanation":"3% NaCl is a hypertonic solution; the changes seen will be that of hypertonic volume expansion. Disorder Examples Effects Hyperosmatic Volume Excessive infusion of hypertonic Increase in ECF volume. Expansion saline. Increased plasma osmolality Water shifts from ICF to ECF Decreased plasma protein concentration Increased arterial blood pressure Hyposmotic Volume Expansion SIADH Increase in ECF volume. Increase in ICF volume. Decrease in plasma osmolarity. No change in hematocrit. Decreased plasma protein concentration. Isosmotic Volume Expansion Infusion of isotonic NaCl Increase in ECF volume. No change in ICF volume. No change in osmolarity. Decreased hematocrit. Decreased plasma protein concentration. Hypertonic Volume Expansion\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7848\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"262\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/nyCGzU1L7keWvGnol06d1744010158.png\" width=\"500\" /> Isotonic Volume Expansion\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7846\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"262\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/FukfpvgzNSUeF5TrVkMF1744009943.png\" width=\"500\" />","sourceRef":"Question Pages 168-168; Solution Pages 181-181","sourcePage":168,"sourcePageEnd":168},{"id":"physiology-7-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following does this Darrow-Yannet diagram (Solid line represent the controlled condition and broken red-line indicates new-steady state condition) represent?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Excessive NS infusion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Excessive infusion of hypertonic saline"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diarrhea"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diabetes insipidus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Diarrhea","explanation":"The Darrow-Yannet diagram shows isosmotic volume contraction; an example of that is diarrhoea. Darrow-Yannet Diagram: • It helps to understand the plasma osmolarity and volume alterations in various diseases. • The volume is plotted in x-axis and plasma osmolarity in y-axis. • Broken lines indicate the flow of water between compartments driven by osmotic gradients, reflecting passive movement in response to changes in solute concentrations. • A normal Darrow-Yannet Diagram is shown below: Disorder Examples Effects Isosmotic volume contraction Diarrhoea Decrease in ECF and plasma Vomiting volumes Haemorrhage Increased plasma protein Burns concentration Increased hematocrit Decreased arterial blood pressure Hyposmotic volume contraction Adrenocortical insufficiency Decreased plasma volume Gastric secretions loss Decreased ECF and plasma osmolality Water shifts from ECF to ICF Cellular swelling Decreased arterial blood pressure Hyperosmotic volume contraction Diabetes insipidus Decrease in ECF and plasma Excessive sweating volumes Alcoholism Increased ECF and plasma Fever osmolality Water shifts from ICF to ECF Increased hematocrit Decreased arterial blood pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 168-169; Solution Pages 182-183","sourcePage":168,"sourcePageEnd":169},{"id":"physiology-7-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Oxygen moves from the alveoli into the blood across the alveolar-capillary membrane. Which of the following best describes this process?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facilitated Diffusion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Simple Diffusion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Non-ionic diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Osmosis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Simple Diffusion","explanation":"Oxygen moves from an area of higher concentration in the alveoli to an area of lower concentration in the blood through simple diffusion, which follows Fick's law of diffusion. Types of diffusion: Simple diffusion Facilitated diffusion (Option A) Move from high to low solute concentration (downhill Movement of molecules across a membrane via carrier transport) protein. It does not require energy. No expenditure of energy. Not require carrier protein (channel protein may or For example, glucose is transported by all types of may not be present) GLUT. No saturation: more concentration leads to a higher Saturation is achieved after a certain point called the rate of transport. plateau. Fick's law: Rate (J) = D.A.∆C/x The maximum transport rate occurs at the plateau or D: Diffusion constant. saturation point, called maximum (Tm) A: Surface area of diffusion. X: Thickness of the membrane. ∆C: Concentration difference between membranes (most important determinant). Active transport requires energy to move substances against their concentration gradient, which is not the case for the passive movement of oxygen from the alveoli to the blood. (Option C) Osmosis refers to the movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration to an area of higher and does not describe the movement of gases like oxygen. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 169-169; Solution Pages 184-184","sourcePage":169,"sourcePageEnd":169},{"id":"physiology-7-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":5,"question":"While studying insulin secretion in pancreatic beta cells, a researcher scientist observes the fusion of the insulin-stored vesicles with the cell membrane to release insulin into the extracellular space in response to blood glucose levels. Which of the following statements about this process is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Exocytosis is a passive process that does not require energy."},{"letter":"B","text":"During exocytosis, vesicles fuse with the lysosome to release their contents."},{"letter":"C","text":"Exocytosis involves the transport of substances out of the cell via vesicle fusion with the cell membrane."},{"letter":"D","text":"Exocytosis is primarily used to uptake nutrients and molecules from the extracellular environment."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Exocytosis involves the transport of substances out of the cell via vesicle fusion with the cell membrane.","explanation":"Exocytosis can be constitutive or non-constitutive. Constitutive Non-constitutive Direct secretion of vesicles Vesicles that do not move directly outside the cell are outside the cell. moved to a particular portion of the cell, where they are temporarily stored. During their temporary storage, pre-processing of these vesicles happens. As a result, more refined protein vesicles are produced. This is known as non-constitutive exocytosis. Incorrect statements: Exocytosis is a passive process that does not require energy. • Exocytosis is an active process that requires energy in the form of ATP. • This energy is needed for the vesicle fusion with the cell membrane and the subsequent release of its contents. During exocytosis, vesicles fuse with the lysosome to release their contents. • This statement is incorrect because they fuse directly with the cell membrane, not with lysosomes. • Lysosomes are involved in intracellular digestion, not in the exocytotic release of substances. Exocytosis is primarily used to uptake nutrients and molecules from the extracellular environment. • Exocytosis expels substances from the cell, not to uptake them. • Endocytosis is the process used to take in nutrients and molecules from the extracellular environment.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 169-170; Solution Pages 185-185","sourcePage":169,"sourcePageEnd":170},{"id":"physiology-7-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Select the correct statements regarding endocytosis:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pinocytosis involves ingesting solid particles into the cell by budding small vesicles from the cell membrane."},{"letter":"B","text":"Phagocytosis is a type of endocytosis that involves the ingestion of liquid into the cell."},{"letter":"C","text":"Receptor-mediated endocytosis is a non-selective process where molecules are ingested into the cell without needing receptors.."},{"letter":"D","text":"All forms of endocytosis require ATP for the vesicle formation process"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All forms of endocytosis require ATP for the vesicle formation process.","explanation":"All forms of endocytosis require ATP for the vesicle formation process. Endocytosis: • Endocytosis is an active transport process that requires energy in the form of ATP to form and move vesicles within the cell. • Three types of endocytosis: Pinocytosis Referred to as \"cell drinking\". (Option: A) Uptake of extracellular fluids by budding small vesicles from the cell membrane. Phagocytosis Referred to as \"cell eating\". (Option: B) Engulfs large particles such as pathogens or cellular debris. into the cell. Receptor-mediated endocytosis (Option: C) ... (content truncated)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 170-170; Solution Pages 186-186","sourcePage":170,"sourcePageEnd":170},{"id":"physiology-7-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding facilitated diffusion is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It does not require ATP expenditure."},{"letter":"B","text":"Involved transport protein is stereospecific and follows saturation kinetics."},{"letter":"C","text":"Diffusion of ammonia in collecting ducts is a type of facilitated diffusion."},{"letter":"D","text":"Molecules move down the chemical gradient from high to low concentration."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Diffusion of ammonia in collecting ducts is a type of facilitated diffusion.","explanation":"Diffusion of ammonia in collecting ducts to excrete ammonium ions into urine is a type of non-ionic diffusion. It occurs at the level of GIT and kidney for the transportation of weak acids or bases. Facilitated diffusion does not require energy expenditure and uses transport (carrier) proteins that follow saturation kinetics, stereospecificity and competition. (Option A and B) Molecules move from areas of high to low concentration (down their chemical gradient), and cations move to negatively charged areas whereas anions move to positively charged areas (“down” their electrical gradient). (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 170-170; Solution Pages 186-187","sourcePage":170,"sourcePageEnd":170},{"id":"physiology-7-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Oxygen moves from the alveoli into the blood across the alveolar-capillary membrane. Which of the following best describes this process?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facilitated Diffusion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Simple Diffusion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Non-ionic diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Osmosis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Simple Diffusion","explanation":"Oxygen moves from an area of higher concentration in the alveoli to an area of lower concentration in the blood through simple diffusion, which follows Fick's law of diffusion. Types of diffusion: Simple diffusion Facilitated diffusion (Option A) Move from high to low solute concentration (downhill Movement of molecules across a membrane via carrier transport) protein. It does not require energy. No expenditure of energy. Not require carrier protein (channel protein may or For example, glucose is transported by all types of may not be present) GLUT. No saturation: more concentration leads to a higher Saturation is achieved after a certain point called the rate of transport. plateau. Fick's law: Rate (J) = D.A.∆C/x The maximum transport rate occurs at the plateau or D: Diffusion constant. saturation point, called maximum (Tm). A: Surface area of diffusion. X: Thickness of the membrane. ∆C: Concentration difference between membranes (most important determinant). Active transport requires energy to move substances against their concentration gradient, which is not the case for the passive movement of oxygen from the alveoli to the blood. (Option C) Osmosis refers to the movement of water across a semipermeable membrane from an area of lower solute concentration to an area of higher and does not describe the movement of gases like oxygen. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 170-170; Solution Pages 187-188","sourcePage":170,"sourcePageEnd":170},{"id":"physiology-7-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the statements below is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"An example of symport is the absorption of glucose into the small intestine."},{"letter":"B","text":"Antiport maintains sodium-potassium balance in cells."},{"letter":"C","text":"Secondary active transporter always depends on the primary active transporter in the symport."},{"letter":"D","text":"The symport mechanism in myocardial cells is blocked by digitalis, which increases contraction force."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) The symport mechanism in myocardial cells is blocked by digitalis, which increases contraction force.","explanation":"Digitalis blocks Antiport mechanism. Symport Antiport It involves the movement of two molecules in the same It involves the movement of two molecules in opposite direction directions Transport requires the binding of more than one Exchanges one substance for another substance to the transport protein. It is crucial for maintaining sodium-potassium balance in It is important in glucose absorption in the intestines. cells and basal metabolism. (Option B) (Option A) E.g. Sodium-Calcium exchanger Glucose and sodium move together, with sodium Calcium moves inside and outside the cell in uphill following the concentration gradient and glucose against transport (against the gradient); hence, sodium moves the concentration gradient. inside the cell along the concentration gradient. The secondary active transporter is always dependent Na-K-ATPase prevents intracellular sodium on the primary active transporter. (Option C) accumulation. For example, SGLT depends on the Na-K- ATPase Na-K-ATPase is blocked by Ouabain or pump to remove Na molecules from inside the cell. digitalis, increases calcium accumulation inside the myocardial cells and leads to increased force of contraction. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 171-171; Solution Pages 188-188","sourcePage":171,"sourcePageEnd":171},{"id":"physiology-7-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Match the following terms with their correct definitions: 1. Stereospecificity 2. Saturation Kinetics 3. Competition 4. Allosteric Regulation a. The phenomenon where increasing the concentration of a substrate leads to a point where enzyme activity plateaus because the enzyme is fully occupied. b. The selective interaction of an enzyme or receptor with only one stereoisomer of a chiral molecule, leading to a specific product. c. The inhibition or activation of enzyme activity through the binding of a molecule at a site other than the enzyme's active site, causing a conformational change. d. The scenario where two or more molecules with similar structures vie for the same active site on an enzyme, potentially inhibiting each other's binding and activity. 1. Stereospecificity 2. Saturation Kinetics 3. Competition 4. Allosteric Regulation a. The phenomenon where increasing the concentration of a substrate leads to a point where enzyme activity plateaus because the enzyme is fully occupied. b. The selective interaction of an enzyme or receptor with only one stereoisomer of a chiral molecule, leading to a specific product. c. The inhibition or activation of enzyme activity through the binding of a molecule at a site other than the enzyme's active site, causing a conformational change. d. The scenario where two or more molecules with similar structures vie for the same active site on an enzyme, potentially inhibiting each other's binding and activity.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3-a, 4-d"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 1-b, 2-a, 3-d, 4-c","explanation":"When any transportation occurs through carrier protein, it follows one of the three kinetics. Stereospecificity Saturation Kinetics Competition Property of enzymes or receptors to The phenomenon where the Multiple molecules with similar interact selectively with one enzyme's activity reaches a structures compete for the same stereoisomer of a chiral molecule, maximum rate at a certain high active site on an enzyme, potentially leading to the production of a substrate concentration and cannot blocking each other's binding and specific enantiomerically pure increase further because all active thus inhibiting each other's activity. product. sites are fully occupied, described by the Michaelis-Menten kinetics. E.g. D-glucose and L-glucose E.g. Glycine conjugation of salicylate E.g. Glucose and Galactose Allosteric Regulation - Regulation of an enzyme's activity through binding a regulatory molecule at a site other than the enzyme's active site induces a conformational change that alters the enzyme's activity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 171-172; Solution Pages 189-189","sourcePage":171,"sourcePageEnd":172},{"id":"physiology-7-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify the false statement regarding tight junctions.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tight junctions are involved in endothelial barrier function and endothelium-dependent vasodilation."},{"letter":"B","text":"Tight junctions allow the passage of small molecules and ions between cells through the paracellular pathway."},{"letter":"C","text":"Tight junctions are composed of proteins such as occludin, junctional adhesion molecules, and claudins."},{"letter":"D","text":"Tight junctions are primarily found in the apical margins of cells in connective tissue."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Tight junctions are primarily found in the apical margins of cells in connective tissue.","explanation":"Tight junction: Sealing strand composed of a long row of transmembrane adhesion proteins embedded in the two interacting plasma membranes. • The extracellular domains of these proteins join directly to one another to occlude the intercellular space. • Tight junctions are primarily found in apical margins of epithelia cells, not connective tissue (Option D) • Tight junctions actually limit the permeability of epithelial cells to ions and solutes, thus regulating the overall ion and solute flux rather than increasing it. • Tight junctions are important to endothelial barrier function and endothelium-dependent vasodilation. (Option A ruled out) • Tight junctions permit the passage of ions and small molecules between adjacent cells via the paracellular pathway. (Option B ruled out) • Occludin, junctional adhesion molecules, and claudins are the main families of transmembrane proteins that contribute to tight junctions. (Option C ruled out) Intercellular junctions:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 172-172; Solution Pages 189-190","sourcePage":172,"sourcePageEnd":172},{"id":"physiology-7-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements about the Na■-K■ ATPase is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is an electrogenic pump with a coupling ratio of 3Na+:2K+"},{"letter":"B","text":"The α subunit has intracellular Na■ and ATP-binding sites"},{"letter":"C","text":"The α subunit contains the phosphorylation site at Asp 376"},{"letter":"D","text":"Its activity is enhanced by ouabain and digitalis glycosides"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Its activity is enhanced by ouabain and digitalis glycosides Explanation : Na, K ATPase catalyses the hydrolysis of ATP to adenosine diphosphate(ADP) and uses the energy to extrude three Na+ from the cell and take two K+ into the cell for each molecule of ATP hydrolysed. Features: • Its activity is inhibited by ouabain and related digitalis glycosides used to treat heart failure. • It is an electrogenic pump with a coupling ratio of 3Na+:2K+ • It is a heterodimer made up of α subunit of weight 1,00,000 and β subunit of weight 55,000. • The α subunit has intracellular Na■ and ATP-binding sites. • The α subunit contains the phosphorylation site at Asp 376. • Both α and β subunits are heterogeneous, with multiple isoforms (α■, α■, α■, β■, β■, β■) present in different tissues. • Thyroid hormone, aldosterone, and aldosterone increase the pump activity whereas dopamine in the kidney inhibits the pump activity.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 172-172; Solution Pages 190-191","sourcePage":172,"sourcePageEnd":172},{"id":"physiology-7-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following is considered downhill transport?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Active transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"Passive transport"},{"letter":"C","text":"Primary active transport"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary active transport"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Passive transport","explanation":"Passive Transport Active Transport Primary Active Transport Secondary Active Transport Moves molecules down Moves molecules against It uses ATP directly to It uses energy from their gradient (downhill their gradient (uphill move molecules against the gradient created transport) from an area of transport) from an area of their gradient from an by primary active higher concentration to an lower concentration to an area of lower concentratio transport area of lower concentratio area of higher concentratio n to an area of higher Primary active n n. concentration. transport creates an ion gradient (e.g., Na+ gradient by the Na+/K+ pump) ↓ Na+/K+ pump uses ATP to pump Na+ out of the cell (against its gradient) ↓ A high concentration of Na+ outside the cell ↓ Uses the Na+ gradient to transport other molecules (e.g., glucose) into the cell It does not require energy Requires energy (ATP) Requires energy (ATP) Energy is indirectly required from the ATP used by the Na+/K+ pump Example: Diffusion of Example: Sodium-potassiu Example: Calcium pump Example: Glucose-s oxygen (O2) across a cell m pump (Na+/K+ pump) (Ca2+ pump) odium co-transport membrane (SGLT) Downhill transport Not downhill transport Not downhill transport Not downhill transport","sourceRef":"Question Pages 172-172; Solution Pages 191-192","sourcePage":172,"sourcePageEnd":172},{"id":"physiology-7-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following Connexin mutations is associated with cataracts?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Connexin 26"},{"letter":"B","text":"Connexin 30"},{"letter":"C","text":"Connexin 40"},{"letter":"D","text":"Connexin 50"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Connexin 50 Explanation : Connexin 46 and 50 mutations lead to cataracts. Connexons: • Structures that form the gap junction present in the membrane of each cell. • 1 Connexon = 6 connexins Connexins: • Protein subunits, which together form connexons. • They surround a channel. • When lined up with the channel in the corresponding connexon in the adjacent cell, it permits substances to pass between the cells without entering the ECF. Mutated Connexins and associated diseases: Mutated Connexin Disease Connexin 30 Clouston syndrome Connexin 30.3 and 31 Erythrokeratoderma Variabilis Connexin 26,30 and 31 Inherited deafness Connexin 36 Predisposition to Myoclonic Epilepsy Connexin 37 Predisposition to Arteriosclerosis Connexin 46 and 50 Cataract Connexin 40 Idiopathic Atrial fibrillation Connexin 32 Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 173-173; Solution Pages 192-193","sourcePage":173,"sourcePageEnd":173},{"id":"physiology-7-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following correctly matches a junction type with its key features?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tight Junctions - Provide mechanical strength and link cytoskeletons of adjacent cells."},{"letter":"B","text":"Adherens Junctions - Create a seal between adjacent cells and prevent the passage of molecules through intercellular space."},{"letter":"C","text":"Desmosomes - Form channels between cells, allowing direct passage of small molecules and ions."},{"letter":"D","text":"Gap Junctions - Enable rapid communication and coordination, forming channels between cells for electrical signal propagation."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Gap Junctions - Enable rapid communication and coordination, forming channels between cells for electrical signal propagation.","explanation":"Cell-to-Cell Interactions: Mechanism Key Components Physical Contact Tight junctions Adherens junctions Desmosomes Gap junctions Chemical Messengers Hormones Neurotransmitters growth factors ECM Interaction Proteins polysaccharides Cellular Junctions: Type Key Features Tight Junctions Create a seal between adjacent cells. Prevent the passage of molecules through intercellular space. It is essential for maintaining tissue barriers and regulating substance movement across epithelia. Adherens Junction Provide mechanical strength. s Link cytoskeletons of adjacent cells. Desmosomes Similar to adherens junctions in function. Provide additional mechanical stability. Gap Junctions Form channels between cells. Allow direct passage of small molecules and ions. Enable rapid communication and coordination (e.g.- electrical signals in cardiac muscle). Cell Adhesion Molecules (CAMs): Type Role Integrins Cell-to-cell and cell-to-ECM adhesion Involved in cell signaling, migration, and proliferation Immunoglobulins Cell-to-cell adhesion Immune recognition Cadherins Calcium-dependent Mediate cell-to-cell adhesion Crucial in tissue morphogenesis Selectins Transient cell-to-cell adhesion in the bloodstream Enable white blood cells to bind to endothelial cells and migrate to inflammation sites Extracellular Matrix (ECM): • Provides structural support, regulates cell behavior, and influences tissue development. Chemical Messengers: • Mechanism: Cells release and receive chemical messengers (hormones, neurotransmitters, growth factors). • Function: Bind to specific receptors on target cells, triggering intracellular signaling pathways to regulate cellular processes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 173-173; Solution Pages 193-194","sourcePage":173,"sourcePageEnd":173},{"id":"physiology-7-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following best describes the term \"milieu intérieur\"?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Intracellular fluid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extracellular fluid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transcellular fluid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intravascular fluid"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Extracellular fluid","explanation":"\"Milieu Intérieur\" - The stable internal environment within an organism, maintained despite external changes. Extracellular fluid (ECF): Definitio Fluid outside the cells n Constitutes about one-third of the total body water. Functio It functions as the body's internal environment, or milieu n intérieur since it contains the essential ions and nutrients necessary for cell survival. Types Interstitial Fluid: Fills the spaces between cells Lymph Tissue fluid Intravascular Fluid: The fluid within the blood vessels, mostly Plasma.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 173-173; Solution Pages 194-195","sourcePage":173,"sourcePageEnd":173},{"id":"physiology-7-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Match the following cell markers with their corresponding cells: Cell marker Cell i) Cytokeratin a) Astrocytes ii) Vimentin b) Skeletal muscle cell iii) Desmin c) Mesenchymal cell iv) Glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) d) Epithelial cell Cell marker Cell i) Cytokeratin a) Astrocytes ii) Vimentin b) Skeletal muscle cell iii) Desmin c) Mesenchymal cell iv) Glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) d) Epithelial cell","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"i-a ; ii-b ; iii-c ; iv-d"},{"letter":"B","text":"i-d ; ii-c ; iii-b ;iv-a"},{"letter":"C","text":"ii-a ; iii-d ; i-b ; iv-c"},{"letter":"D","text":"ii-d ; iv-b ; iii-a ; i-c"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) i-d ; ii-c ; iii-b ;iv-a","explanation":"Cell markers are used to identify different types of cells. Cell marker Cell Cytokeratin Epithelial cell Vimentin Mesenchymal cell. Desmin Skeletal muscle cell Glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP) Astrocytes","sourceRef":"Question Pages 174-174; Solution Pages 195-195","sourcePage":174,"sourcePageEnd":174},{"id":"physiology-7-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following statements about microtubules is true? i) Microtubules form the cytoskeleton of organelles such as cilia and nerve axons, providing structural integrity and rigidity. ii) Microtubules function as channels allowing the passage of small molecules and ions between cells. iii) Microtubules play a dynamic role in cell division, movement, and intracellular transport by acting as conveyor belts for vesicles, granules, and organelles like mitochondria.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"All are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"All are correct except (iii)"},{"letter":"C","text":"All are correct except (ii)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Only (i) is correct"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) All are correct except (ii)","explanation":"Microtubules: Role Explanation Key Points Cellular Scaffold Provides structural integrity as part Constructed from polymerised of the cell cytoskeleton. tubulin Provides rigidity and shape to cell parts Cytoskeleton of Forms cytoskeletons of cilia, nerve Important for cell division Organelles axons, and mitotic spindles. Maintains organisation and compartmentalisation Dynamic Roles Involved in cell division, movement, Acts as conveyor belts in Cells and intracellular transport. Facilitates movement of vesicles, granules, and organelles within the cell","sourceRef":"Question Pages 174-174; Solution Pages 196-196","sourcePage":174,"sourcePageEnd":174},{"id":"physiology-7-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which biological membrane has the highest protein-to-lipid ratio?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Plasma membrane"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mitochondrial inner membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mitochondrial outer membrane"},{"letter":"D","text":"All have the same ratio"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Mitochondrial inner membrane","explanation":"Biological membrane Protein (%) Lipid (%) Ratio Plasma membrane ~50 ~50 ~ 1.1 Mitochondrial inner ~75 ~25 ~ 3.1 membrane Myelin sheath ~15 to 30 ~70 to 85 ~ 0.17 to 0.4","sourceRef":"Question Pages 174-175; Solution Pages 196-196","sourcePage":174,"sourcePageEnd":175},{"id":"physiology-7-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which statements about the cell membrane are correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sphingolipids form channels for water and water-soluble substances, aiding diffusion."},{"letter":"B","text":"Cholesterol acts as a receptor for peptide hormones, triggering intracellular processes."},{"letter":"C","text":"Peripheral proteins penetrate the lipid bilayer and actively transport substances."},{"letter":"D","text":"Glycocalyx contributes to negative surface charge, cell adhesion, and immune reactions."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Glycocalyx contributes to negative surface charge, cell adhesion, and immune reactions.","explanation":"Components of cell membrane: Membrane Lipids: Compo Description Function nent Phosph Most abundant A barrier to water-soluble olipids Arranged in a bilayer with substances, allowing fat-soluble hydrophilic heads outward and substances to pass. hydrophobic tails inward. Sphing It is found in smaller amounts, Protection olipids especially in nerve cells. Signal transmission Adhesion sites for extracellular proteins Cholest Embedded in the lipid bilayer. Influences membrane permeability erol and fluidity Membrane Proteins: Type Description Function Integral Span the entire lipid bilayer, Transport: Form channels for water Proteins protruding on both sides. and ions. Selective Diffusion: Facilitate specific substance diffusion Carrier Proteins: Bind and transport substances, including active transport Enzymes: Catalyze reactions Receptors: Bind ligands to trigger intracellular processes Peripheral Attached to the inner or outer Function as enzymes or regulators Proteins surface, not spanning the lipid of substance transport. bilayer. Membrane Carbohydrates - Glycocalyx: Component Description Function Glycoprotei Proteins with carbohydrate chains Protection ns Cell recognition Signaling. Glycolipids Lipids with carbohydrate chains Cell recognition Communication. Proteoglyca Carbohydrate-rich compounds that Structural support ns are attached to the outer cell surface Interaction with the extracellular matrix.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 175-175; Solution Pages 197-198","sourcePage":175,"sourcePageEnd":175},{"id":"physiology-7-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 45-year-old male patient is admitted to the hospital after experiencing severe dehydration due to prolonged vomiting and diarrhoea. His symptoms include dry mouth, low blood pressure, and rapid heart rate. Which of the following fluids can be used in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Normal saline"},{"letter":"B","text":"5% sodium chloride"},{"letter":"C","text":"10% dextrose"},{"letter":"D","text":"Any of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Normal saline","explanation":"Among the given options, normal Saline is the only isotonic solution. Only isotonic solutions should be used in the case of dehydration. Isotonic solution: Definition Solution of equal concentration as that of red cell content & plasma. RBCs remain intact on using it. Examples 0.9%NaCl (Normal Saline). 5% glucose. 10% mannitol. 20% urea.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 175-175; Solution Pages 198-198","sourcePage":175,"sourcePageEnd":175},{"id":"physiology-7-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Match the following: Body fluids Indicators used to measure it 1. Extracellular fluid a. Inulin 2. Plasma b. Aminopyrine 3. Total body water c. Evans blue Body fluids Indicators used to measure it 1. Extracellular fluid a. Inulin 2. Plasma b. Aminopyrine 3. Total body water c. Evans blue","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-c, 3-b"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-c, 2-a, 3-b"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 1-a, 2-c, 3-b","explanation":"Indicators used to measure body fluids: ECF Plasma volume Interstitial fluid ICF Inulin Evans Blue Dye It cannot be measured It cannot be measured Radioactive Radio-iodinated Human directly. directly. Inulin Serum Albumin (RISA) It is calculated using ECF It is calculated using TBW Chlorine Radio-iodinated Gamma - Plasma volume. - ECF. Bromine Globulin Total body water (TBW) is Mannitol Fibrinogen measured by: Sucrose Radio-isotopes of Deuterium oxide (D 2 O). Sulphate Phosphorus Tritium oxide. Thiosulphat Iron Aminopyrine. e Chromium Thiocyanate Ferrocyanid e","sourceRef":"Question Pages 175-176; Solution Pages 198-199","sourcePage":175,"sourcePageEnd":176},{"id":"physiology-7-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following is an example of transcellular fluid?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cerebrospinal fluid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peritoneal fluid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitreous humor"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All of the above","explanation":"Transcellular Fluid: Definition Fluid in the lumen of structures lined by epithelium. Examples Fluid in the secretion of exocrine digestive glands Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) Pleural, peritoneal, synovial and pericardial fluids Intraocular (aqueous and vitreous humor) fluids Bile Luminal fluids of the gut Fluid in the cochlea and kidney tubules Volume 1.5% of body weight About 1L.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 176-176; Solution Pages 199-199","sourcePage":176,"sourcePageEnd":176},{"id":"physiology-7-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":24,"question":"The plasma volume of a person is 4 liters and the hematocrit is 20%, what is the blood volume?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"5 litres"},{"letter":"B","text":"4 litres"},{"letter":"C","text":"4.5 litres"},{"letter":"D","text":"6 litres"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 5 litres","explanation":"• Blood Volume = Plasma Volume/(1-Hematocrit) = 4/(1-20%) = 4/(1-0.2) = 4/0.8 = 5 litres","sourceRef":"Question Pages 176-176; Solution Pages 199-200","sourcePage":176,"sourcePageEnd":176},{"id":"physiology-7-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding body fluids is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ICF makes up 40% of body weight"},{"letter":"B","text":"ECF makes up 40% of body weight"},{"letter":"C","text":"ICF makes up 33% of body weight"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) ICF makes up 40% of body weight","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 176-177; Solution Pages 200-200","sourcePage":176,"sourcePageEnd":177},{"id":"physiology-7-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which of the following statements are examples of feed-forward control systems in physiology? The clotting cascade rapidly amplifies clot formation after vascular injury. Muscles adjust in advance for standing or moving. Increased heart rate before starting of physical activity. Sweating in response to increased body temperature. The smell of food triggers digestive enzyme secretion.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 2, 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"2, 3, 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"1, 3, 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"2, 4, 5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 2, 3, 5","explanation":"Feed-forward control system/anticipatory control system: • Predicts changes and initiates responses to prevent deviations from the desired state before they occur • Pre-empts disturbances, enhancing efficiency and stability by preparing in advance to maintain homeostasis Example Mechanism Physiological Benefit Anticipatory Secretion of insulin in response to Prevents a sharp spike in blood Regulation of the sight or smell of food glucose levels after eating. Blood Glucose Heart Rate Brain signals increase heart rate Ensures muscles receive adequate Adjustment during and blood flow to muscles before oxygen and nutrients from the start Exercise activity. of exercise. Digestive Enzyme The cephalic phase triggers the Prepares the digestive system to Secretion secretion of digestive enzymes and efficiently break down and absorb gastric juices. nutrients. Thermoregulation Anticipation of cold triggers Maintains core body temperature, vasoconstriction. Thermoregulation preventing a drop that could affect also has negative feedback enzymatic activities. component. Postural Control Muscles adjust in advance for Prevents falls and injuries by standing or moving. maintaining balance and coordination. Feed-Forward Granular cells excite basket cells, Ensures smooth, precise motor Inhibition by the which inhibit Purkinje cells, refining control by preventing excessive or Cerebellum motor commands before they occur. unwanted movements.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 177-177; Solution Pages 200-201","sourcePage":177,"sourcePageEnd":177},{"id":"physiology-7-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A person moved from his hometown to Delhi to appear for an exam. The initial respiratory rate was 12 breaths per minute. After arriving at the exam center, the respiratory rate increased to 20 breaths per minute. The respiratory center corrected this to 16 breaths per minute. Calculate the regulation factor for the respiratory rate.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0.25"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"0.5"},{"letter":"D","text":"6"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 0.5","explanation":"The regulation factor checks the accuracy of the control system. Regulation Factor = Change with control system / Change without control system • Initial Respiratory Rate: 12 breaths/minute • Post-Flight Respiratory Rate: 20 breaths/minute • Corrected Respiratory Rate: 16 breaths/minute Correction Achieved by the Respiratory Center = Change with control system = (20−16 = 4 breaths/minute) Total Correction Needed = Change without control system = (20−12 = 8 breaths/minute) • Regulation Factor = Change with control system / Change without control system = 4/8 = 0.5","sourceRef":"Question Pages 177-177; Solution Pages 201-202","sourcePage":177,"sourcePageEnd":177},{"id":"physiology-7-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A patient’s blood glucose level in a steady-state condition before a heavy lunch was 90 mg/dL. After lunch, the blood glucose level increased to 180 mg/dL. Insulin secretion from the pancreas reduced the blood glucose level to 120 mg/dL. Calculate the gain of the glucose regulatory mechanism.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"+2"},{"letter":"B","text":"-2"},{"letter":"C","text":"+3"},{"letter":"D","text":"-3"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) -2","explanation":"Correction achieved by the insulin secretion is: (180 mg/dL - 120 mg/dL = 60 mg/dL) Error compared to the initial state is: (120 mg/dL - 90 mg/dL = 30 mg/dL) Gain = Correction Achieved / Error Remaining Substituting the values: Gain = 60 mg/dL / 30 mg/dL = 2 Since the gain is negative in the context of this glucose regulatory mechanism (because an increase in blood glucose results in a decrease in gain): Gain = -2 The degree of effectiveness with which a control system maintains constant conditions is determined by the gain of negative feedback.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 177-178; Solution Pages 202-202","sourcePage":177,"sourcePageEnd":178},{"id":"physiology-7-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Which of the following operates based on a negative feedback mechanism? Thyroid hormone regulation Blood glucose regulation Blood clotting cascade Nerve Signal Propagation Temperature regulation","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 2, and 5"},{"letter":"B","text":"1, 3, and 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"2, 3, and 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"1, 2, and 4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 1, 2, and 5","explanation":"Thyroid hormone, blood glucose, and temperature regulation follow a negative feedback mechanism. Negative feedback mechanism: The body responds to a change by initiating actions that counteract the change, aiming to restore equilibrium. Examples of negative feedback mechanisms: System Stimulus Response Effect Body Temperature Temperature deviation Sweating/shivering, Restores normal vasodilation/constriction temperature Blood Glucose High/low blood Insulin/glucagon release Normalises blood glucose glucose levels Thyroid Hormones High/low thyroid TSH release adjustment Maintains hormone hormones balance Blood Pressure High/low blood Heart rate and vessel Restores normal blood pressure adjustment pressure Calcium Homeost High/low blood calcium PTH/calcitonin release Normalises blood calcium asis levels Menstrual Cycle Hormone level FSH/LH regulation Regulates menstrual cycle changes Respiratory Rate High CO 2 levels Increased breathing rate Normalizes CO 2 levels","sourceRef":"Question Pages 178-178; Solution Pages 202-203","sourcePage":178,"sourcePageEnd":178},{"id":"physiology-7-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-7","chapter":"General Physiology","topic":"General Physiology","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":30,"question":"A 3-week-old infant is being exclusively breastfed by her mother. During a recent pediatric visit, the mother expresses concern about her milk supply. The pediatrician explains that the infant's feeding patterns contribute to the regulation of milk production. Which physiological concept best describes how the mother’s body responds to the infant’s feeding cues to regulate milk production?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Negative feedback mechanism"},{"letter":"B","text":"Positive feedback mechanism"},{"letter":"C","text":"Autonomic reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intrinsic regulation mechanism"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Positive feedback mechanism","explanation":"Breastfeeding is an example of a positive feedback mechanism. The initial stimulus amplifies the positive feedback response. Breastfeeding: Examples of positive feedback mechanisms: Mechanism Description Outcome Labour and Stretching of cervix stimulates Amplified contractions leading to Childbirth oxytocin production. Oxytocin childbirth. release stimulates uterine contractions, which in turn stimulate more oxytocin release. Lactation Baby’s suckling stimulates prolactin Increased milk flow during and oxytocin release. Prolactin breastfeeding. causes enhancement of milk production and oxytocin causes ejection of milk. Blood Clottin Platelets release chemicals that Rapid formation of a blood clot g attract more platelets to the site of to seal the wound. injury. Nerve Signal Depolarisation triggers the opening Rapid propagation of the action Propagation of sodium channels, causing further potential along the nerve fibre. depolarisation and more sodium channel opening.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 178-178; Solution Pages 203-204","sourcePage":178,"sourcePageEnd":178},{"id":"physiology-8-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Dehydration is more severe in infants compared to adults. Which of the following is a true statement about infants?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extracellular fluid (ECF) is more than intracellular fluid (ICF)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intracellular fluid (ICF) is more than extracellular fluid (ECF)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Total body water in infants is more than in adults"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extracellular fluid equals intracellular fluid"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Total body water in infants is more than in adults","explanation":"Infants have a higher total body water (TBW) percentage compared to adults, making them more susceptible to dehydration . • Total body water (TBW) composition: Infants: ~ 75-80% of body weight Adults: ~ 55-60% of body weight • Infants: ~ 75-80% of body weight • Adults: ~ 55-60% of body weight • Higher water turnover: Infants lose water more rapidly due to higher metabolic rates, increased insensible losses, and immature renal function . • Infants: ~ 75-80% of body weight • Adults: ~ 55-60% of body weight Total Body Water (TBW) Composition: • 90% of body weight in early fetal life. • 75% of body weight at birth. • 60% of body weight by the end of the first year, remaining consistent until puberty. • After puberty, males have slightly more TBW compared to females. Preterm vs. Term Neonates: • Preterm neonates have a higher TBW compared to term neonates. TBW Distribution: • TBW is divided into: Extracellular Fluid (ECF) Intracellular Fluid (ICF) • Extracellular Fluid (ECF) • Intracellular Fluid (ICF) • The equation is: TBW = ECF + ICF ECF = plasma + interstitial fluid • ECF = plasma + interstitial fluid • Extracellular Fluid (ECF) • Intracellular Fluid (ICF) • ECF = plasma + interstitial fluid ECF and ICF Changes: • In fetuses and newborns, ECF > ICF volume. • After birth, ECF volume decreases and ICF volume increases. Extracellular fluid (ECF) is more than intracellular fluid (ICF) (Option A) is incorrect because, while infants have a higher ECF proportion at birth (~40%) , ICF eventually exceeds ECF as they grow. Intracellular fluid (ICF) is more than extracellular fluid (ECF) (Option B) is incorrect at birth , but becomes true after the first year . In neonates, ECF is initially greater than ICF . Extracellular fluid equals intracellular fluid (Option D) is incorrect because ECF and ICF are not equal at any stage ; there is always a shift, with ICF becoming dominant after infancy .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 205-205; Solution Pages 220-222","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"physiology-8-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":2,"question":"What is the mechanism of action of botulinum toxin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inhibits acetylcholine release from the presynaptic terminal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inhibits acetylcholine reuptake"},{"letter":"C","text":"Blocks the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inhibits acetylcholinesterase"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Inhibits acetylcholine release from the presynaptic terminal","explanation":"Botulinum toxin prevents acetylcholine (ACh) release at the neuromuscular junction , leading to flaccid paralysis . • Mechanism of Action: Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins (SNAP-25, synaptobrevin, syntaxin) , which are essential for vesicular fusion with the presynaptic membrane. This prevents ACh-containing vesicles from being released into the synaptic cleft. Result: Muscle paralysis due to the lack of ACh stimulation at the nicotinic receptors . • Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins (SNAP-25, synaptobrevin, syntaxin) , which are essential for vesicular fusion with the presynaptic membrane. • This prevents ACh-containing vesicles from being released into the synaptic cleft. • Result: Muscle paralysis due to the lack of ACh stimulation at the nicotinic receptors . • Clinical Uses: Botox (cosmetic use) : Reduces wrinkles by relaxing muscles. Dystonias (e.g., cervical dystonia, blepharospasm) Spasticity (e.g., cerebral palsy, stroke-related spasticity) Hyperhidrosis (excessive sweating) • Botox (cosmetic use) : Reduces wrinkles by relaxing muscles. • Dystonias (e.g., cervical dystonia, blepharospasm) • Spasticity (e.g., cerebral palsy, stroke-related spasticity) • Hyperhidrosis (excessive sweating) • Toxicity: Causes botulism , presenting with flaccid paralysis, ptosis, diplopia, dysphagia, and respiratory failure . Common sources: Improperly canned foods, honey (infant botulism) . • Causes botulism , presenting with flaccid paralysis, ptosis, diplopia, dysphagia, and respiratory failure . • Common sources: Improperly canned foods, honey (infant botulism) . • Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins (SNAP-25, synaptobrevin, syntaxin) , which are essential for vesicular fusion with the presynaptic membrane. • This prevents ACh-containing vesicles from being released into the synaptic cleft. • Result: Muscle paralysis due to the lack of ACh stimulation at the nicotinic receptors . • Botox (cosmetic use) : Reduces wrinkles by relaxing muscles. • Dystonias (e.g., cervical dystonia, blepharospasm) • Spasticity (e.g., cerebral palsy, stroke-related spasticity) • Hyperhidrosis (excessive sweating) • Causes botulism , presenting with flaccid paralysis, ptosis, diplopia, dysphagia, and respiratory failure . • Common sources: Improperly canned foods, honey (infant botulism) . Botulinum toxins: • These are potent neurotoxins produced by Clostridium botulinum bacteria. • There are several types, with types A, B, and E being toxic to humans. • Mechanism : Botulinum toxins A and E cleave SNAP-25 (synaptosome-associated protein-25), and B cleave Synaptobrevin, which are presynaptic membrane proteins essential for releasing acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction leading to Flaccid paralysis. Inhibits acetylcholine reuptake (Option B) is incorrect because ACh is not reuptaken ; it is degraded by acetylcholinesterase in the synapse. Blocks the postsynaptic acetylcholine receptor (Option C) is incorrect because this describes the action of curare and neuromuscular blockers (e.g., pancuronium, rocuronium) . Inhibits acetylcholinesterase (Option D) is incorrect because this describes the action of acetylcholinesterase inhibitors (e.g., neostigmine, organophosphates) , which increase ACh levels rather than decrease them.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 205-205; Solution Pages 222-223","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"physiology-8-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A patient presents with high blood pressure accompanied by a decrease in heart rate. What is the most likely physiological mechanism responsible for this response?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulation of baroreceptors"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inhibition of baroreceptors"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bezold-Jarisch reflex (J reflex)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stimulation of chemoreceptors"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Answer: A) Stimulation of baroreceptors Explanation: The combination of high blood pressure (hypertension) and bradycardia (low heart rate) suggests activation of the baroreceptor reflex , a homeostatic mechanism that regulates blood pressure. • Mechanism: Increased blood pressure stretches baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) . Increased baroreceptor firing signals the medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius) . Response: Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate • Increased blood pressure stretches baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) . • Increased baroreceptor firing signals the medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius) . • Response: Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate • Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction • Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate • Increased blood pressure stretches baroreceptors in the carotid sinus (CN IX) and aortic arch (CN X) . • Increased baroreceptor firing signals the medulla (nucleus tractus solitarius) . • Response: Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate • Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction • Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate • Inhibition of sympathetic outflow → ↓ heart rate (bradycardia) and ↓ vasoconstriction • Activation of parasympathetic (vagal) tone → further ↓ heart rate Aspect Details Mechanism Baroreceptors in the carotid sinus and the aortic arch detect BP changes Increased BP stretches receptors, sending signals to medulla via glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves Medulla inhibits vasoconstrictor center and stimulates vagal parasympathetic center Actions Vasodilation of blood vessels Decreased heart rate and contraction strength Reduced BP due to lower peripheral resistance and cardiac output Importance Maintains BP stability during activities like standing Fastest acting Tends to reset within 1-2 days for long-term regulation This helps counteract hypertension and maintain homeostasis. Inhibition of Baroreceptors (Option B) is incorrect because inhibition of baroreceptors occurs in hypotension , leading to tachycardia and vasoconstriction to restore blood pressure. Bezold-Jarisch Reflex (Option C) is incorrect because this reflex causes bradycardia and hypotension , typically triggered by ventricular ischemia or excessive vagal stimulation , not hypertension. Stimulation of Chemoreceptors (Option D) is incorrect because chemoreceptors (in the carotid bodies and medulla) primarily respond to hypoxia, hypercapnia, or acidosis , leading to increased respiratory drive and sympathetic activation (tachycardia), not bradycardia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 205-205; Solution Pages 223-225","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"physiology-8-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which electrolyte abnormality will lead to cardiac arrhythmia in patients with severe vomiting?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypokalemia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyponatremia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyperkalemia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypocalcemia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Hypokalemia","explanation":"Severe vomiting leads to hypokalaemia , which is a major risk factor for cardiac arrhythmias due to its effect on cardiac membrane potential and conduction . • Mechanism: Vomiting causes loss of gastric acid (HCl) → metabolic alkalosis Alkalosis shifts potassium into cells → worsens hypokalaemia Renal compensation increases potassium excretion (due to increased bicarbonate delivery to the distal tubule) Low serum potassium disrupts cardiac repolarisation , leading to arrhythmias • Vomiting causes loss of gastric acid (HCl) → metabolic alkalosis • Alkalosis shifts potassium into cells → worsens hypokalaemia • Renal compensation increases potassium excretion (due to increased bicarbonate delivery to the distal tubule) • Low serum potassium disrupts cardiac repolarisation , leading to arrhythmias • ECG changes in hypokalaemia: Flattened T waves U waves Prolonged QT interval → risk of ventricular arrhythmias (e.g., Torsades de Pointes, ventricular tachycardia) • Flattened T waves • U waves • Prolonged QT interval → risk of ventricular arrhythmias (e.g., Torsades de Pointes, ventricular tachycardia) • Vomiting causes loss of gastric acid (HCl) → metabolic alkalosis • Alkalosis shifts potassium into cells → worsens hypokalaemia • Renal compensation increases potassium excretion (due to increased bicarbonate delivery to the distal tubule) • Low serum potassium disrupts cardiac repolarisation , leading to arrhythmias • Flattened T waves • U waves • Prolonged QT interval → risk of ventricular arrhythmias (e.g., Torsades de Pointes, ventricular tachycardia) Hyponatraemia (Option B) is incorrect because although vomiting can lead to hyponatraemia (due to volume loss and ADH secretion), it is less likely to cause direct cardiac arrhythmias . Hyperkalaemia (Option C) is incorrect because vomiting typically leads to potassium loss , not hyperkalaemia. Hyperkalaemia is more commonly associated with renal failure, acidosis, or tissue breakdown . Hypocalcaemia (Option D) is incorrect because hypocalcaemia causes neuromuscular symptoms (tetany, Chvostek’s sign, Trousseau’s sign) and QT prolongation , but it is not the primary electrolyte abnormality seen in vomiting-induced arrhythmias.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 206-206; Solution Pages 225-226","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":206},{"id":"physiology-8-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which ion is most responsible for the resting membrane potential (RMP)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Potassium (K + )"},{"letter":"B","text":"Calcium (Ca 2+ )"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sodium (Na + )"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chloride (Cl - )"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct A - Potassium (K + ) : • Potassium ions are the primary contributors to the resting membrane potential. The RMP is mainly determined by the K + gradient across the cell membrane, as potassium ions move out of the cell through potassium leak channels. The loss of positively charged K + ions from the cell creates a negative charge inside the cell relative to the outside, establishing the RMP, which is typically around -70 mV in neurons. Incorrect Options: Option B - Calcium (Ca 2+ ) : Calcium ions play a crucial role in various cellular processes, including muscle contraction and neurotransmitter release. However, their influence on the RMP is relatively minimal compared to potassium. The primary role of calcium in relation to membrane potential is more related to action potentials and signal transduction rather than establishing the RMP. Option C - Sodium (Na + ) : Sodium ions also contribute to the membrane potential, but they are less influential than potassium in establishing the resting membrane potential. The sodium-potassium pump (Na + /K + ATPase) maintains the gradient by actively transporting 3 Na + ions out of the cell and 2 K + ions into the cell, which helps in stabilizing the RMP. However, the resting membrane potential is primarily determined by the K + gradient. Option D - Chloride (Cl-) : Chloride ions contribute to the membrane potential by balancing the charge, but they are not the primary determinant of the resting membrane potential. Chloride ions typically follow the electrochemical gradients established by other ions like potassium and sodium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 206-206; Solution Pages 226-227","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":206},{"id":"physiology-8-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following best explains the role of a second messenger in cellular signaling?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Act on extracellular receptors"},{"letter":"B","text":"Modulate extracellular hormone response"},{"letter":"C","text":"Modulate intracellular hormone response"},{"letter":"D","text":"Directly interact with the cell’s DNA"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Modulate intracellular hormone response: • Modulate intracellular hormone response: Second messengers are intracellular molecules that are generated in response to the binding of a hormone to its receptor on the cell surface. They help propagate and amplify the signal inside the cell, leading to various cellular responses, such as enzyme activation or gene expression. Incorrect Options: Option A - Act on extracellular receptors: Second messengers do not act directly on extracellular receptors. Instead, they work intracellularly after a receptor on the cell surface has been activated. Option B - Modulate extracellular hormone response: Second messengers do not affect the extracellular hormone response; their function is to relay and amplify the signal inside the cell after the hormone binds to its receptor. Option D - Directly interact with the cell’s DNA: Second messengers do not directly interact with DNA. Instead, they influence cellular processes through various pathways, such as activating kinases or other signaling molecules, which then affect DNA indirectly.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 206-206; Solution Pages 227-227","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":206},{"id":"physiology-8-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What physiological response is most likely to occur in a person exposed to high temperatures while exercising vigorously to maintain internal body temperature within a narrow range?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sweating and vasodilation in the skin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Shivering and vasoconstriction in the skin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased production of heat by the liver"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased heart rate (HR) and respiratory rate (RR)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Sweating and vasodilation in the skin: • When exercising vigorously in high temperatures, the body needs to dissipate heat to maintain a stable internal temperature. Sweating helps cool the body through evaporative cooling, while vasodilation (widening of blood vessels) in the skin increases blood flow to the surface, allowing more heat to be released into the environment. Incorrect Options: Option B - Shivering and vasoconstriction in the skin : Shivering and vasoconstriction are responses typically seen in cold environments. Shivering generates heat through muscle contractions, and vasoconstriction (narrowing of blood vessels) helps to retain heat. These mechanisms are not relevant in hot conditions. Option C - Increased production of heat by the liver: The liver produces heat as a byproduct of metabolic processes, but this is not a primary response to high temperatures during vigorous exercise. The body focuses on cooling mechanisms rather than increasing heat production in such conditions. Option D - Decreased heart rate (HR) and respiratory rate (RR): During vigorous exercise, both heart rate and respiratory rate typically increase to meet the higher oxygen demands of the muscles. A decrease in HR and RR would not be expected under these conditions; instead, they would rise to support increased physical activity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 206-207; Solution Pages 228-228","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":207},{"id":"physiology-8-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":8,"question":"In negative feedback, infinite gain is seen in which of the following?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Blood volume control by kidney"},{"letter":"B","text":"Temperature control in the hypothalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"BP control by baroreceptors"},{"letter":"D","text":"Infinite gain is not possible"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Blood volume control by kidney: • The degree of effectiveness with which a control system maintains constant conditions is determined by the gain of the negative feedback control system. Kidney is the organ with infinite gain meaning the residual error is zero and there is total correction of the change from the normal value. • The kidney is a very slow system for the regulation of blood volume. But given enough time, the kidney can maintain blood pressure, without residual error. The kidney will excrete excess water from the body and the blood pressure will return to back to normal value, which makes the error 0. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. There is residual error in these mechanisms.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 207-207; Solution Pages 228-229","sourcePage":207,"sourcePageEnd":207},{"id":"physiology-8-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":9,"question":"How do eukaryotes engage in intercellular communication?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lysosome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peroxisome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Exosome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Genome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C -Exosomes: • Intercellular communication in eukaryotes involves exchanging signaling molecules like proteins, nucleic acids, and lipids • Exosomes, small vesicles produced within cells, play a vital role in this process. They carry bioactive molecules, including proteins and RNA, transferring them between cells to regulate gene expression, immune responses, and other physiological processes. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lysosome: Lysosomes are organelles involved in the degradation and recycling of cellular waste and materials. Option B - Peroxisome: Peroxisomes are organelles involved in various metabolic processes, such as lipid metabolism and detoxification. Option D - Genome: The genome refers to the complete set of genetic material (DNA) present in an organism or a cell.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 207-207; Solution Pages 229-229","sourcePage":207,"sourcePageEnd":207},{"id":"physiology-8-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following curves represents gamma–globin chain production?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Green curve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Black curve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Blue curve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Orange curve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Orange curve: • Orange curve, which represents gamma-globin chain production. • In fetal development, the hemoglobin is formed by 2 gamma chains and 2 alpha chains, and gamma-globulin gradually decreases after birth as the production of other globin chains, such as alpha and beta, increases. The orange curve corresponds to the gamma chain, which is predominant during fetal life. Incorrect Options: Option A: Green curve: This represents beta-globin chain production, not gamma-globin chain. Option B: Black curve: This represents epsilon-globin chain production, not the gamma-globin chain. Option C: Blue curve: This represents delta-globin chain production, not a gamma-globin chain.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 207-208; Solution Pages 229-230","sourcePage":207,"sourcePageEnd":208},{"id":"physiology-8-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the options below does not exemplify a feed-forward control mechanism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Temperature regulation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cephalic phase of gastric secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cerebellar action in motor coordination"},{"letter":"D","text":"Exercise resulting in heart rate increase"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Exercise resulting in heart rate increase: • Exercise-induced heart rate increase, illustrates feedback, not feedforward control. The heart rate elevates in response to immediate exercise demands, rather than anticipatorily preparing for changes. Incorrect Options: Option A - Temperature regulation: This is an example of a feedforward control mechanism. For instance, when the body anticipates exposure to cold temperatures, it may initiate processes like vasoconstriction and shivering to maintain or raise body temperature before it actually drops significantly. Option B - Cephalic phase of gastric secretion: The cephalic phase of gastric secretion is indeed a feedforward control mechanism. It occurs before food reaches the stomach, and sensory stimuli such as the sight and smell of food can trigger the release of gastric juices in anticipation of digestion. Option C - Cerebellar action in motor coordination: The cerebellum plays a significant role in motor coordination and is involved in feedforward control. It helps anticipate and make adjustments to muscle activities to coordinate movements effectively.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 208-208; Solution Pages 230-230","sourcePage":208,"sourcePageEnd":208},{"id":"physiology-8-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":12,"question":"I-125 labeled albumin is used for measurement of:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Plasma volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Interstitial fluid volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"ICF"},{"letter":"D","text":"ECF"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Plasma volume: • I-125 labeled albumin is commonly used to measure plasma volume. I-125 is a iodine labeled albumin that is injected into the bloodstream. • It is impermeable to capillaries and does not dissociate into interstitial fluid and intracellular fluid. So, by measuring the dilution of the labeled albumin in the plasma, the total volume of plasma can be determined. • Evan’s blue is another substance that can be used for determining plasma volume. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. I-125 labeled iodine is used to measure the plasma volume.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 208-208; Solution Pages 230-231","sourcePage":208,"sourcePageEnd":208},{"id":"physiology-8-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":13,"question":"When calculating the quantity of IV fluids required for a patient, what amount of daily transient loss/insensible loss would you consider?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"150-200 ml"},{"letter":"B","text":"500-600 ml"},{"letter":"C","text":"200-350 ml 350-400 ml"},{"letter":"D","text":"350-400 ml"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 500-600 ml: • Constant water evaporation occurs in our body during respiration or directly from the skin's surface, which is normal, is referred to as insensible water loss. • Normally, 700 ml of water evaporates from our body daily. 350 ml of water is evaporated from the skin's surface and 350 ml from the respiratory tract. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 209-209; Solution Pages 231-231","sourcePage":209,"sourcePageEnd":209},{"id":"physiology-8-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Determine the extracellular fluid (ECF) volume in a participant who received an injection of 10 grams of mannitol into their venous blood. After reaching equilibrium, the urinary excretion of mannitol was observed to be 10%, while the plasma concentration of mannitol measured 65 mg per 100 ml.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"14L"},{"letter":"B","text":"10L"},{"letter":"C","text":"28L"},{"letter":"D","text":"20L"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 14L: • Mannitol is used to measure the extracellular fluid volume compartment because it is freely permeable to the capillary membrane and impermeable to cell membrane. • Given in the question: Amount of mannitol injected = 10 g Fraction excreted in urine = 10% = 1 gm. Plasma concentration of mannitol = 65 mg / 100 ml • Amount of mannitol injected = 10 g • Fraction excreted in urine = 10% = 1 gm. • Plasma concentration of mannitol = 65 mg / 100 ml • Using the formula of volume distribution (V) = (Amount injected - Amount excreted) / Concentration • Substituting the values into the formula: V = (10 g - 1 g) / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = (10 g - 1 g) / (65 mg / 100 ml) • Let's convert the units to be consistent: V = (10,000 mg - 1,000 mg) / (65 mg / 100 ml) V = 9,000 mg / (65 mg / 100 ml) V = (9,000 mg * 100 ml) / 65 mg V = 900,000 ml / 65 mg V ≈ 13,846 ml • V = (10,000 mg - 1,000 mg) / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = 9,000 mg / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = (9,000 mg * 100 ml) / 65 mg • V = 900,000 ml / 65 mg • V ≈ 13,846 ml • Finally, converting milliliters to liters V ≈ 13.846 L. • Therefore, the correct ECF volume is approximately 13.846 L, which is closest to option 14L. • Amount of mannitol injected = 10 g • Fraction excreted in urine = 10% = 1 gm. • Plasma concentration of mannitol = 65 mg / 100 ml • V = (10 g - 1 g) / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = (10,000 mg - 1,000 mg) / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = 9,000 mg / (65 mg / 100 ml) • V = (9,000 mg * 100 ml) / 65 mg • V = 900,000 ml / 65 mg • V ≈ 13,846 ml Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 209-209; Solution Pages 231-232","sourcePage":209,"sourcePageEnd":209},{"id":"physiology-8-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following types of amino acids are present in the transmembranous part of the membrane receptor?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amphipathic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hydrophobic"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hydrophilic"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B-Hydrophobic : The transmembrane region of membrane receptors anchors the receptor within the lipid bilayer and transmits signals across the membrane. It mainly consists of hydrophobic amino acids, which are suited for insertion into the hydrophobic interior of the lipid bilayer. Hydrophilic and amphipathic amino acids are less common in this region. Incorrect Options: Option A (Amphipathic): While some membrane receptors may have amphipathic amino acids in regions that interface with the lipid bilayer and the aqueous environment, they are less commonly found in the transmembrane region itself. Option C (Hydrophilic): Hydrophilic amino acids are more commonly found in regions of membrane receptors that are exposed to the aqueous environment rather than the transmembrane region. Option D none of the above is incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 209-209; Solution Pages 233-233","sourcePage":209,"sourcePageEnd":209},{"id":"physiology-8-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Gibbs – Donnan equilibrium is mainly due to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Phosphate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bicarbonate in the intracellular compartment"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcium in the extracellular compartment"},{"letter":"D","text":"Large intracellular proteins"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Large intracellular proteins: • The Gibbs-Donnan equilibrium, named after J. Willard Gibbs and Frederick G. Donnan, occurs across semipermeable membranes in the presence of large impermeable substances like proteins containing charge. • According to Donnan and Gibbs the presence of a non-diffusible ion, the diffusible ions distribute themselves so that at equilibrium, their concentration ratios are equal. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. These are diffusible ions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 210-210; Solution Pages 233-233","sourcePage":210,"sourcePageEnd":210},{"id":"physiology-8-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Lysosomal enzymes are present in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cytosol"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mitochondria"},{"letter":"C","text":"Endoplasmic reticulum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Nucleus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C-Endoplasmic Reticulum : • Lysosomal enzymes are made as inactive precursors in the endoplasmic reticulum, processed in the Golgi apparatus, and activated in lysosomes at acidic pH. Incorrect Options: Option A: Cytosol: Lysosomal enzymes are not present within cytosol. Option B: Mitochondria: Mitochondria are organelles involved in energy production and metabolism. They have their own distinct set of enzymes, such as those involved in oxidative phosphorylation and the Krebs cycle. Lysosomal enzymes are not typically found within mitochondria. Option C: Endoplasmic reticulum: The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is involved in protein synthesis, folding, and lipid metabolism. While the ER plays a role in the synthesis of lysosomal enzymes as inactive precursors, the active lysosomal enzymes are not present within the ER itself. Option D: Nucleus: The nucleus is the central organelle that houses the cell's genetic material and is involved in gene expression and regulation. Lysosomal enzymes are not found within the nucleus. However, the nucleus contains its own set of enzymes involved in DNA replication, transcription, and other nuclear processes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 210-210; Solution Pages 234-234","sourcePage":210,"sourcePageEnd":210},{"id":"physiology-8-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following options has the lowest concentration in intracellular fluid (ICF)compared to extracellular fluid?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Magnesium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Protein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Calcium: • Calcium is present in the least concentration in intracellular fluid (ICF). Inside the cell, calcium ions are tightly regulated to maintain proper cellular function. • The intracellular concentration of calcium is relatively low (0.0001 mEq/L) compared to the concentration in extracellular fluid.. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Their concentration is relatively higher in intracellular fluid compared to extracellular fluid.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 210-210; Solution Pages 234-234","sourcePage":210,"sourcePageEnd":210},{"id":"physiology-8-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following organelle has DNA?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"letter":"B","text":"Golgi complex"},{"letter":"C","text":"SER"},{"letter":"D","text":"RER"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option A-Mitochondria : • Mitochondria are organelles found in eukaryotic cells that have their own DNA. They are often referred to as the \"powerhouses\" of the cell because they are responsible for generating most of the cell's energy through a process called cellular respiration. Mitochondria have a double membrane structure, and within their inner membrane, they house their own DNA, known as mitochondrial DNA (mtDNA). • Mitochondrial DNA is separate from the nuclear DNA found in the cell's nucleus. It contains genes that encode for some of the proteins, RNA molecules, and other components needed for mitochondrial function and energy production. Incorrect Options: Option B. The Golgi complex, or Golgi apparatus, is involved in processing, modifying, and packaging proteins and lipids for transport to their specific destinations within the cell or for secretion. It does not have its own DNA. Option C . The SER is involved in various functions, including lipid metabolism, detoxification of drugs and toxins, and calcium storage. It does not contain DNA. Option D. The RER is responsible for the synthesis and processing of proteins. It is called \" rough\" because it is studded with ribosomes involved in protein synthesis. However, it does not contain its own DNA.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 210-211; Solution Pages 235-235","sourcePage":210,"sourcePageEnd":211},{"id":"physiology-8-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":20,"question":"What is the most accurate method to measure the temperature of a soldier brought to the ER from Siachen, in order to rule out hypothermia in a limited resource setting?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oral"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rectal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Infrared tympanic thermometer"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Rectal: • The rectal temperature is representative of the temperature at the core of the body and varies least with changes in environmental temperature. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Variations of temperature in these areas are usually associated with extreme environment.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 211-211; Solution Pages 235-236","sourcePage":211,"sourcePageEnd":211},{"id":"physiology-8-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which indicator is suitable for measuring the total amount of body water?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Evan’s blue"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tritium oxide"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mannitol"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sucrose"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Tritium: • Tritium oxide , also known as hydrogen-3, is a radioactive isotope of hydrogen. It can be used as an indicator to measure total body water because it is freely permeable to capillary and cell membrane. When tritiated water (water containing tritium) is ingested or injected into the body, it distributes uniformly throughout the total body water compartment. By measuring the concentration of tritium in body fluids, researchers can estimate the total body water content. • Other indicators used for measuring TBW deuterium oxide and aminopyrine, Incorrect Options: Option A - Evans blue: Evans blue is a dye that is commonly used to measure plasma volume. Option C and D: Mannitol and sucrose are used to measure extracellular fluid volume.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 211-211; Solution Pages 236-236","sourcePage":211,"sourcePageEnd":211},{"id":"physiology-8-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":22,"question":"What mechanism is observed in the baroreceptor reflex among the options listed below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Feedforward"},{"letter":"B","text":"Positive feedback"},{"letter":"C","text":"Negative feedback"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both A & B"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Negative feedback: • The baroreceptor reflex is a negative feedback mechanism that helps regulate blood pressure. It involves the baroreceptors, which are stretch receptors located in the walls of blood vessels, such as the carotid sinus and aortic arch, as well as the heart. These receptors detect changes in blood pressure and send signals to the brain, specifically the cardiovascular control center in the medulla oblongata, to initiate a response. • An increase in BP results in activation of the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) which activates the the CVLM (caudal ventrolateral medulla), and inhibits the RVLM (Rostral Ventrolateral Medulla) which is responsible for the inhibition of sympathetic stimulation resulting in decreased cardiac output and contractility. • If there is a decrease in BP it will not activate NTS and CVLM which in turn do not inhibit the RVLM and increases cardiac output by sympathetic supply. Incorrect Options: Option A - Feedforward: Feedforward is a mechanism in which the body anticipates changes and prepares for them before they occur. Regulation of body temperature is an example of a feedforward system. Option B - Positive feedback: Positive feedback is a mechanism in which a change in stimuli leads to an amplification of that change. For example secretion of oxytocin leading to uterine contractions, in turn will result in further secretion of oxytocin and further uterine contractions. Option D - Both A & B: It is an incorrect option.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 211-211; Solution Pages 236-237","sourcePage":211,"sourcePageEnd":211},{"id":"physiology-8-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following is not a dietary fiber?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cellulose"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inulin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pectin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gums"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D-Gums : • Gums like guar gum, locust bean gum, and xanthan gum are hydrocolloids derived from plants and used as food additives for thickening, stabilizing, and emulsifying. While they may contribute to dietary fiber content, they lack the same health benefits as natural dietary fibers. Incorrect Options: Option A - Cellulose: Insoluble fiber found in plant cell walls, adds bulk to stool. Option B - Inulin: Soluble fiber found in chicory root, acts as a prebiotic. Option C - Pectin: Soluble fiber found in fruits, used as a thickening agent, promotes satiety.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 212-212; Solution Pages 237-237","sourcePage":212,"sourcePageEnd":212},{"id":"physiology-8-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which of the following is the principal constituent of serum osmolality?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sodium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bicarbonate"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hydrogen ions"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Sodium: • The combined presence of sodium and chloride accounts for approximately 90% of plasma osmolality. Out of a total of 295 mOsm/kg H2O, sodium chloride contributes around 270 mOsm, while the remaining 20 mOsm is attributed to urea, glucose, protein, and other ions such as potassium and calcium. • Whenever Na+ moves from one compartment to the other, Cl– also moves to maintain electroneutrality, mainly contributing to osmolality. • Therefore, it is mainly the Na+ that contributes to osmolality. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 212-212; Solution Pages 237-238","sourcePage":212,"sourcePageEnd":212},{"id":"physiology-8-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A man was brought to the emergency after suddenly becoming unconscious while working in the field. On examination, his temperature was 105 degrees Fahrenheit, and his skin turgor was decreased. Which of the following would not be seen in the patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tachypnea"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypotension"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sweating"},{"letter":"D","text":"Red hot skin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Sweating: • The scenario described a case of heat stroke, a severe form of heat illness that occurs when the body's heat-regulating systems fail to respond to high temperatures adequately. • Classic signs of heat stroke include hyperthermia (body core temperature >105°F or 40.5°C), altered mental status, increased respiratory rate, and hot, dry skin. • Sweating is often absent in heat stroke as the body's thermoregulatory mechanisms are overwhelmed. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. These are seen in patients with heat stroke. Options A, B, and D are incorrect. These are seen in patients with heat stroke.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 212-212; Solution Pages 238-238","sourcePage":212,"sourcePageEnd":212},{"id":"physiology-8-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Identify the type of transport across the cell membrane given below.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Simple diffusion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Primary active transport"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary active transport"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Options B - Facilitated diffusion : • The image represents facilitated diffusion, a passive transport that moves substances down their concentration gradient without using cellular energy but requires the assistance of specific carrier proteins embedded in the cell membrane. • This process is essential for transporting large or polar molecules that cannot cross the cell membrane’s lipid bilayer by simple diffusion. Incorrect Options: Option A - Simple diffusion: This is a passive transport mechanism where substances move across the cell membrane along their concentration gradient directly through the lipid bilayer without the assistance of carrier proteins. Option C - Primary active transport: This active transport uses cellular energy (ATP) to transport substances against their concentration gradient. This process often involves specific carrier proteins, such as the Na+/K+ ATPase pump. Option D - Secondary active transport: This type of active transport uses the energy stored in the form of ion concentration gradients, established by primary active transport, to drive the transport of other substances against their concentration gradient.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 213-213; Solution Pages 238-239","sourcePage":213,"sourcePageEnd":213},{"id":"physiology-8-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":27,"question":"The signaling sequence in a type 2 membrane protein with N-terminal facing cytoplasm is located in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inside the membrane"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the C-terminal"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the N-terminal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both inside the membrane and at C-terminal"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Inside the membrane: • The signaling sequence in a type 2 membrane protein with N-terminal facing cytoplasm is located inside the membrane. This sequence is generally a hydrophobic alpha-helix that serves as a stop-transfer anchor, preventing the protein from being fully translocated across the membrane and causing it to become embedded in the membrane with the N-terminus facing the cytoplasm. • The N-terminus and C-terminus of a protein refer to the amino acid residues at the beginning and end of the protein, respectively. • Protein N-terminus: start; may hold signal for cell localization. • Protein C-terminus: end; can drive interactions or enzyme function. Incorrect Options: Option B - At the C-terminal: The C-terminal of a type 2 membrane protein is located on the extracellular side of the membrane and is not involved in the signaling sequence or the process of membrane insertion. Option C - At the N-terminal: The N-terminal of a type 2 membrane protein is located on the cytoplasmic side of the membrane and is involved in the signaling sequence that determines the orientation of the protein in the membrane. Option D - Both inside the membrane and at C-terminal : Only the inside of the membrane is involved in the signaling sequence of a type 2 membrane protein with N-terminal facing cytoplasm; the C-terminal is not involved in this process.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 213-213; Solution Pages 239-240","sourcePage":213,"sourcePageEnd":213},{"id":"physiology-8-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":28,"question":"What is the normal insensible water loss?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"100 mL/hour"},{"letter":"B","text":"150 mL/hour"},{"letter":"C","text":"50 mL/hour"},{"letter":"D","text":"200 mL/hour"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - 50 mL/hour: • Insensible water loss is the amount of water lost from the body through evaporation, such as through the skin or respiratory tract that cannot be easily measured. Normal insensible water loss is generally considered to be around 30-50 mL/hour in adults . Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 213-213; Solution Pages 240-240","sourcePage":213,"sourcePageEnd":213},{"id":"physiology-8-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Vessel A has a length of L and a diameter of D, and vessel B has 2L and 2D. Considering the flow is laminar through both cylinders, the flow through B is how many times as much as the flow through A?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4 times"},{"letter":"B","text":"8 times"},{"letter":"C","text":"16 times"},{"letter":"D","text":"32 times"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 8 times: • Flow is equal to the pressure difference divided by resistance. So, flow is inversely proportional to the resistance of the vessel. • According to the Hagen–Poiseuille equation, resistance can be directly measured by the following formula. • R = 8hL/pr4 Where, R = resistance. h = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. • Where, • R = resistance. • h = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to the fourth power of radius and directly proportional to the length of the vessel.. • So, if the radius and length are doubled, the original and other parameters remain constant, then the resistance will be: R = L/(r)4. R = 2/(2) 4. R = 2/16 = 8. • R = L/(r)4. • R = 2/(2) 4. • R = 2/16 = 8. • So, the resistance will decrease by 8 times, and flow will increase by 8 times. • Where, • R = resistance. • h = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • R = L/(r)4. • R = 2/(2) 4. • R = 2/16 = 8. Incorrect Options: • Option A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 214-214; Solution Pages 240-241","sourcePage":214,"sourcePageEnd":214},{"id":"physiology-8-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":30,"question":"What is the value of normal oncotic pressure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"12 mmHg"},{"letter":"B","text":"25 mmHg"},{"letter":"C","text":"32 mmHg"},{"letter":"D","text":"54 mmHg"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 25 mmHg: • The oncotic pressure , also known as colloid osmotic pressure , is the osmotic pressure exerted by proteins, notably albumin, in a blood vessel's plasma that usually pulls water into the circulatory system, i.e., it is a retaining force. The normal value of oncotic pressure in a healthy individual's plasma is approximately 25 mmHg. Incorrect Options: • Option A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 214-214; Solution Pages 241-242","sourcePage":214,"sourcePageEnd":214},{"id":"physiology-8-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Calculate the sodium deficit in a 30-year-old man weighing 70 kg with a sodium level of 120 mEq/L.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"280 mEq"},{"letter":"B","text":"480 mEq"},{"letter":"C","text":"840 mEq"},{"letter":"D","text":"1400 mEq"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C-840 mEq: • To calculate the sodium deficit, the following formula can be used: • Sodium Deficit (mEq) = (Desired Sodium - Current Sodium) x Total body water (TBW) • Total body water (TBW) = 60% of total body weight. • TBW = 0.6 x 70 = 42. • Desired sodium is 135 to 145 mEq/L, so taking an average value of 140 mEq/L. • Substituting the values in the formula: Sodium Deficit = (140 - 120) x 42. Sodium Deficit = 840 mEq • Sodium Deficit = (140 - 120) x 42. • Sodium Deficit = 840 mEq • Sodium Deficit = (140 - 120) x 42. • Sodium Deficit = 840 mEq Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 215-215; Solution Pages 242-242","sourcePage":215,"sourcePageEnd":215},{"id":"physiology-8-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":32,"question":"A body fluid sample is being studied with Na: 10 mEq/L, K: 140 mEq/L, Cl: 4 mEq/L. Identify the compartment from which the fluid has been obtained.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Interstitial"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intracellular fluid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extracellular fluid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Plasma"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B-Intracellular fluid : • Given the electrolyte concentrations, the sample likely originates from intracellular fluid. The low sodium (Na+) concentration (10 mEq/L) contrasts with typical extracellular levels (135-145 mEq/L), suggesting it's not extracellular. • The high potassium (K+) concentration (140 mEq/L) and low concentration of chloride (4 mEq/L) aligns with levels in the intracellular fluid compartment.Thus, the sample's composition suggests it's intracellular fluid. Incorrect Options : • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. These all are extracellular fluids and the composition of the ions are not consistent with its composition.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 215-215; Solution Pages 242-243","sourcePage":215,"sourcePageEnd":215},{"id":"physiology-8-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which of the following cell organelles is associated with destruction of bacteria and viruses?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Peroxisomes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lysosomes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Golgi complex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ribosomes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B-Lysosomes : • Lysosomes, membrane-bound organelles, destroy bacteria and pathogens within cells. They contain enzymes that break down biomolecules, aiding in the cell's defense. • By fusing with phagosomes, lysosomes form phagolysosomes, where enzymes degrade engulfed pathogens, killing them. Incorrect Options: Option A - Peroxisomes: Peroxisomes specialize in oxidative processes using molecular oxygen, producing hydrogen peroxide and utilizing catalase to break down fats.. Option C - Golgi complex: The Golgi complex, composed of membranes, prepares proteins and lipids for use both within and outside the cell. Option D - Ribosomes: Ribosomes, consisting of RNA and protein, synthesize proteins by translating mRNA sequences. They are not involved in the destruction of bacteria or pathogens.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 215-215; Solution Pages 243-243","sourcePage":215,"sourcePageEnd":215},{"id":"physiology-8-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Which of the following types of cell-to-cell signaling is depicted in the image below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paracrine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Autocrine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Endocrine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Merocrine"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Paracrine: • In Paracrine signaling, the signaling molecule is released by a cell and acts on nearby cells to trigger a response. • The signaling molecule can diffuse through the extracellular matrix and reach neighboring cells. • An example of paracrine signaling is the release of growth factors by cells during tissue repair. Incorrect Options: Option B - Autocrine signaling: • This mode of signaling is similar to paracrine signaling, but the signaling molecule acts on the same cell or cells of the same type that produced it. The beta-cell of the pancreas produces Insulin which inhibits the same cells for further release. Option C - Endocrine signaling: In this mode of signaling, hormones are released into the bloodstream by endocrine glands and travel throughout the body to reach their target cells. Examples of hormones include insulin, estrogen, and testosterone. Option D - Merocrine secretion: This mode of secretion involves the release of products by exocytosis, where the product is packaged in vesicles and released through the plasma membrane. Merocrine secretion is a mode of secretion in the salivary glands, sweat glands, and pancreas.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 215-216; Solution Pages 243-244","sourcePage":215,"sourcePageEnd":216},{"id":"physiology-8-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Glucose is absorbed in the intestine by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary active transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Simple diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary active transport"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D: Secondary active transport • Glucose is primarily absorbed in the intestine through SGLT 1, which transports the 2 molecules of Na+ with the gradient and 1 molecule of glucose against the gradient in the enterocyte, which is a secondary active transport. • It is dependent on Na+ K+ ATPase for the removal of excess Na+ from the enterocyte into the interstitial space when a large amount of glucose is absorbed from the cell for accommodation of the newer molecules. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 216-216; Solution Pages 244-245","sourcePage":216,"sourcePageEnd":216},{"id":"physiology-8-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which molecular motor protein results in spread of the rabies virus from the site of inoculation to the central nervous system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Kinesin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dynein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Actin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Myosin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Dynein: • Dynein is a motor protein that facilitates retrograde transport within cells. It moves towards the minus end of microtubules, i.e., retrograde transport which is typically oriented towards the center of the cell body of the nerve. • It transports viruses (rabies), toxins, and growth factors. Incorrect Options: Option A - Kinesin: Kinesin is an anterograde transport, i.e., positive end directed molecular motor protein that moves along microtubules, facilitating the transport of vesicles and mitochondria. Options C and D: Myosin is a bi-directional molecular motor protein involved in the transportation of the substance to and fro from the positive end of the microtubules to the axon terminal with the help of actin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 216-216; Solution Pages 245-245","sourcePage":216,"sourcePageEnd":216},{"id":"physiology-8-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":37,"question":"In ORS, sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Co-transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"Osmosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Primary active transport"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Co-transport : • Sodium is given along with glucose to facilitate co-transport. The Na+/glucose cotransporter SGLT1 is an active pump that stoichiometrically transports two sodium ions along the gradient and one glucose molecule against the gradient together across the cell membrane of the enterocyte. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 216-217; Solution Pages 246-246","sourcePage":216,"sourcePageEnd":217},{"id":"physiology-8-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":38,"question":"‘c’ wave in JVP is seen in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Iso-volumetric contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Slow filling at end of diastole"},{"letter":"C","text":"End of systole"},{"letter":"D","text":"Start of diastole"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Iso-volumetric contraction: • The 'c' wave in the JVP is typically seen during the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle. It occurs during early ventricular systole when the tricuspid valve bulges back into the right atrium due to the increased pressure in the right ventricle. This causes a slight rise in the JVP. • The isovolumetric contraction occurs immediately after the closure of the atrioventricular valves (mitral and tricuspid valves) and before the onset of ventricular ejection. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 217-217; Solution Pages 246-246","sourcePage":217,"sourcePageEnd":217},{"id":"physiology-8-39","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":39,"question":"What is the most effective stimulus for increasing aldosterone secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hyperkalemia"},{"letter":"B","text":"ACTH"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypernatremia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hyponatremia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Hyperkalemia: • Aldosterone secretion is maximally stimulated by hyperkalemia , which refers to an elevated level of potassium in the blood. • High levels of potassium act directly on the adrenal cortex, specifically the zona glomerulosa, to increase aldosterone production and release. • Aldosterone acts on the kidneys to promote potassium excretion and sodium reabsorption, helping to regulate electrolyte balance. Incorrect Options: Option B - ACTH: While ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) does stimulate the secretion of aldosterone to some extent, it is not the primary factor that maximally stimulates aldosterone secretion. ACTH primarily stimulates the secretion of cortisol from the adrenal cortex. Option C - Hypernatremia: Hypernatremia decreases the aldosterone secretion. Option D - Hyponatremia: It also increases the secretion of aldosterone, nut is not the most potent stimulus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 217-217; Solution Pages 246-247","sourcePage":217,"sourcePageEnd":217},{"id":"physiology-8-40","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":40,"question":"What is the transporter molecule responsible for transporting Fe2+ into the enterocytes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ferroportin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hephaestin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Divalent metal Transporter 1"},{"letter":"D","text":"Divalent Metal Transporter 2"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Divalent Metal Transporter 1 (DMT1): • Divalent metal transporter-1 (DMT1) is a transmembrane protein located in the apical membrane of enterocytes. It is responsible for transporting ferrous forms of iron from the intestinal lumen into the enterocyte. • DMT1 is expressed in the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine, where iron is absorbed from the diet. Incorrect Options: Option A - Ferroportin: Ferroportin is a transmembrane protein located on the basolateral membrane of enterocytes. It functions as the main exporter of intracellular iron, allowing iron to be released into the bloodstream for systemic distribution. Option B - Hephaestin: Hephaestin is a copper-dependent ferroxidase enzyme found on the basolateral membrane of enterocytes. It plays a role in oxidizing Fe2+ to Fe3+, which enables the transfer of iron to transferrin for transport in the bloodstream. Option D - Divalent Metal Transporter 2 (DMT2): DMT2 is a transporter primarily involved in the transport of manganese (Mn2+) rather than iron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 217-217; Solution Pages 247-248","sourcePage":217,"sourcePageEnd":217},{"id":"physiology-8-41","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Nuclear signalling pathways are all except","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caveolins"},{"letter":"B","text":"NF-kB"},{"letter":"C","text":"Importins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rat proteins"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Rat proteins: • These are not nuclear signalling pathways. • Recombinant rat IFN is a bioactive protein, it is used in cell culture applications. Incorrect Options: Option A - Caveolins: Caveolins are a family of proteins that are involved in the formation of caveolae, which are specialised lipid rafts in the plasma membrane. They are involved in receptor independent endocytosis. Option B - NF-kB: NF-kB (nuclear factor kappa-light-chain-enhancer of activated B cells) is a transcription factor that plays a central role in the regulation of immune and inflammatory responses. It is involved in various nuclear signaling pathways and is activated in response to diverse stimuli, such as cytokines, pathogens, and stress signals. Option C - Importins: Importins are proteins that facilitate the transport of molecules into the nucleus. They are responsible for importing various molecules, including transcription factors, into the nucleus to regulate gene expression and participate in nuclear signaling pathways.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 218-218; Solution Pages 248-248","sourcePage":218,"sourcePageEnd":218},{"id":"physiology-8-42","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":42,"question":"What are the mechanisms involved in the absorption of glucose in the intestine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary active transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"Secondary active transport"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diffusion"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Secondary active transport : • Glucose is primarily absorbed in the intestine through SGLT 1, which transports the 2 molecules of Na + with the gradient and 1 molecule of glucose against the gradient in the enterocyte, which is a secondary active transport. • It is dependent on Na + K + ATPase for the removal of excess Na + from the enterocyte into the interstitial space when a large amount of glucose is absorbed from the cell for accommodation of the newer molecules. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 218-218; Solution Pages 248-249","sourcePage":218,"sourcePageEnd":218},{"id":"physiology-8-43","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-8","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":43,"question":"What is the effect on blood flow when the radius of a vessel is doubled?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"8 times"},{"letter":"B","text":"16 times"},{"letter":"C","text":"32 times"},{"letter":"D","text":"256 times"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 16 times: • Flow is equal to the pressure difference divided by resistance. So, flow is inversely proportional to the resistance of the vessel. • According to the Hagen–Poiseuille equation, resistance can be directly measured by the following formula. • R = 8hL/pr4 Where, R = resistance. h = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. • Where, • R = resistance. • h = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to the fourth power of radius. • So, if the radius is doubled the original and other parameters remain constant, then the resistance will be: R = 1/(r)4. R = 1/(2) 4. R = 1/16 = 16. • R = 1/(r)4. • R = 1/(2) 4. • R = 1/16 = 16. • So, the resistance will decrease by 16 times, and flow will increase by 16 times. • Where, • R = resistance. • h = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • R = 1/(r)4. • R = 1/(2) 4. • R = 1/16 = 16. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 218-218; Solution Pages 249-250","sourcePage":218,"sourcePageEnd":218},{"id":"physiology-9-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following best describes the difference between temporal summation and spatial summation in neurons?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"There is no difference between Temporal summation and Spatial summation."},{"letter":"B","text":"Temporal summation requires signals to occur close in time, spatial summation requires simultaneous signals at different synapses."},{"letter":"C","text":"Temporal summation is not limited by the refractory period, spatial summation is."},{"letter":"D","text":"Temporal summation combines signals from different synapses, spatial summation combines signals from the same synapse."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Temporal summation requires signals to occur close in time, spatial summation requires simultaneous signals at different synapses.","explanation":"Temporal summation occurs when multiple excitatory signals at the same synapse happen close in time to reach the threshold and trigger an action potential. Spatial summation involves excitatory signals arriving simultaneously at different synapses, summating to reach the threshold. Feature Temporal Summation Spatial Summation Definition (Option B) Excitatory signals arriving at different synapses simultaneously Multiple subthreshold excitatory or within a short timeframe. signals at the same synapse occurring close in time. Threshold Summation of signals to reach the Combined effect of excitatory for Excitatio threshold potential for triggering an signals at multiple synapses to n action potential. reach the threshold. (Option D) Signal Sour Same synapse Different synapses ce (Option A) Current Local depolarizing current spreads Local depolarizing currents from Flow from the same synapse. different synapses overlap. Role in Can trigger an action potential if Can trigger an action potential if Action Pote signals are close enough in time to multiple signals from different ntials summate and reach threshold. locations summate to reach threshold. Impact of Limited by the refractory period; if Not directly limited by the refractory Refractory signals are too far apart, they won't period; depends on spatial proximity Period summate. of signals. (Option C) Examples Neurons receiving frequent, rapid Neurons receiving simultaneous signals at a single synapse. input from multiple presynaptic neurons. • The longer the time constant, the greater is the chance for two potentials to summate to induce an action potential (Temporal summation) • Longer the length constant, greater the chance of Spatial summation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 251-251; Solution Pages 263-264","sourcePage":251,"sourcePageEnd":251},{"id":"physiology-9-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Feedback control in voltage-gated ion channels in the membrane of neuron during action potential has been shown here. The phenomenon shown at point A is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Positive feedback"},{"letter":"B","text":"Negative feedback"},{"letter":"C","text":"Feedforward"},{"letter":"D","text":"Voltage dependent opening"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Negative feedback Explanation : K+ channels exert Negative feedback. • K■ channels provide negative feedback during an action potential by promoting repolarization. • Their delayed opening after depolarization allows K■ ions to exit the cell, reducing the positive membrane potential and stabilizing the neuron's electrical activity. • This feedback mechanism is essential for the proper functioning of neurons and for the propagation of action potentials. Positive Feedback (Option A) : Increases the activity or response, leading to a greater output. For example, during the rapid depolarization phase of an action potential, when sodium channels open and more sodium enters, it leads to further depolarization. Feedforward (Option C) : Anticipates changes and adjusts the response accordingly, but this is not the main focus in the context of ion channel behavior. Voltage-Dependent Opening (Option D) : Refers to the way ion channels open in response to changes in membrane potential, but does not describe the feedback mechanism.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 251-251; Solution Pages 264-265","sourcePage":251,"sourcePageEnd":251},{"id":"physiology-9-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Neurologists decide to assess the excitability of different tissues by plotting the strength-duration relationship parameters on a graph. Which of the following tissues show the highest excitability?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Heart"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Neuron","explanation":"Neuron exhibits the highest chronaxie value. Strength-Duration Relationship Parameters The strength-duration curve graphically depicts the relationship between the intensity of an electrical stimulus applied at a muscle's motor point and the duration required to produce a minimal muscle contraction. Rheobase: • Minimum electrical current (or voltage) needed to induce an action potential when applied indefinitely. • Typical Values: 2 to 18 mA (or 5 to 35 volts) for healthy muscle tissue. • Clinical Insight: Higher values in denervated muscles indicate reduced excitability. Chronaxie: • Minimum time required for a current that is double the rheobase intensity to stimulate the muscle. • Normal Value: Generally below 1 ms. • Clinical Insight: Longer chronaxie indicates lower excitability. Utilization Time: • Duration needed for a stimulus of rheobasic strength to excite the nerve and produce muscle contraction. • Clinical Insight: Crucial for understanding nerve activation dynamics and stimulus efficiency. Clinical Significance: • Assessment: Parameters assess nerve function and excitability. • The order of excitability among different tissues is generally: Neurons > skeletal muscles > myocardium > smooth muscles. Additionally, A fibers are the most excitable, followed by B and C fibers. • Diagnostic Use: Evaluate nerve damage, monitor recovery, and guide rehabilitation. • Strength-Duration Curve: Plots these parameters to reveal nerve injury extent and recovery potential.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 252-252; Solution Pages 265-267","sourcePage":252,"sourcePageEnd":252},{"id":"physiology-9-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 35-year-old woman presents to the neurology clinic with complaints of tingling and numbness in her hands and feet. On examination, the neurologist decides to conduct nerve conduction studies to assess the function of her peripheral nerves. The results show a compound action potential (CAP) with multiple peaks and a biphasic waveform. What does the presence of multiple peaks indicate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Summation of action potentials from a single nerve fiber"},{"letter":"B","text":"Different conduction velocities of various nerve fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Biphasic nature of the waveform"},{"letter":"D","text":"Single directional deflection"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Different conduction velocities of various nerve fibers.","explanation":"The multiple peaks in a compound action potential (CAP) indicate the summation of action potentials from different types of nerve fibers (e.g., Aα, Aβ, B, and C fibers), each with distinct conduction velocities. The different peaks reflect the sequential activation of these fibers. Action Potential - Monophasic/ Biphasic/ Compound Recording: One electrode inside the cell, the other outside. Monophasic Action Potential: Single directional deflection, recorded from a single fiber or small group. Characteristics: Single Peak: Indicates action potential of a single fiber or small group. Amplitude: Does not significantly increase with stimulation strength Follows All-or-nothing Principle. It states that once an action potential is initiated in a nerve fiber, it will travel the entire length of the fiber if the conditions are suitable, or it will not travel at all if the conditions are not right. Recording: Two electrodes of opposite polarity detect the wave of depolarization. Biphasic Action Potential: Two-phase action potential with positive and negative deflections. Characteristics: Waveform: Upward deflection followed by a downward deflection. Relation to CAP: Component of CAP, reflecting the activity of multiple fibers. Recording: Electrodes placed on the surface of the mixed nerve. Compound Action Potential (CAP): Summation of action potentials from different nerve fibers (Aα, Aβ, B, C) in a mixed nerve. Characteristics: Multiple Peaks: Correspond to different conduction velocities; Aα fibers first. Amplitude: Increases with stronger stimulation. Waveform: Biphasic , showing positive and negative deflections. Does not follow the All-or-nothing Principle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 252-252; Solution Pages 267-269","sourcePage":252,"sourcePageEnd":252},{"id":"physiology-9-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 35-year-old man undergoes a minor surgical procedure on his hand under local anesthesia. Which of the following sensory modalities would be the FIRST to be lost?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proprioception"},{"letter":"B","text":"Discriminative Touch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Motor function"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pain sensation"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Dull, aching pain.","explanation":"• Pain transmitted by A-d fibers that is most susceptible to blockade by local anesthetics like lidocaine. Spike potential is defined as a rapid, transient change in membrane potential, signifying an action potential. Spike duration is the length of time this spike potential lasts. Table rendering failed Susceptibility to Local Anesthesia Pressure Hypoxia Aγ and Aδ >> Aα and Aβ >> B >> C Aα > B > C B>A>C","sourceRef":"Question Pages 253-253; Solution Pages 269-269","sourcePage":253,"sourcePageEnd":253},{"id":"physiology-9-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Match the following nerve fibre type with its respective property. Fibre type Property 1. Aα a. Preganglionic autonomic 2. Aβ b. Touch 3. Aδ c. Temperature 4. B d. Proprioception Fibre type Property 1. Aα a. Preganglionic autonomic 2. Aβ b. Touch 3. Aδ c. Temperature 4. B d. Proprioception","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-d, 4-c"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-b, 2-a, 3-c, 4-d"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a","explanation":"P 7 1 P A A II T 3 5 P A A M 1 3 A A III P 1 2 B B P 3 < H C r 0 2 r β β o 0 - r γ γ ot 5 - δ δ ai 2 - r - 3 y ( o - - e u - 1 e o - 6 n - 5 e 1 p D p 1 2 s c 7 2 s r 3 (f 3 g 5 o o ri 2 0 s h, 0 s to 0 a 0 a xi r o 0 u p u m st) n a a c r r r u ,t gl - R e e e e s e io M o pt - s -I cl m ni o o io M s nt e p c st n, o u e s e a s s st r r pi r ut u o s e m n at o s m u e dl u n c at s di e r o e ic c at s e m pt m e e. (c ic ib ot pt ol le o ib d) r le Classificati on of nerve fibres - Erlanger and Gasser and Lloyd- Hunt Fiber Type Erlanger- Lloyd-H Myelinatio Function Conduct Diamete Sensitivity Gasser unt Cla n ion Velo r (µm) to Blockade Classificat ssificati city (m/s) ion on Aα Aα Ia, Ib Myelinate Propriocep 70-120 12-20 Pressure - d tion, somat Most susce ic motor ptible Aβ Aβ II Touch, 30-70 5-12 Pressure pressure - Interm ediate. Aγ Aγ Motor to 15-30 3-6 muscle spindles Aδ Aδ III Pain (fast), 12-30 2-5 temperat ure (cold) B B Pregangli 3-15 <3 Hypoxia onic auto - Most nomic suscepti ble C C IV Unmyelin Pain (slow), 0.5-2 0.4-1.2 Local anest (Dorsal ated temperatu hetics - Root) re (warm) Most susce ptible C (Sympath C Postgangl 0.7-2.3 0.3-1.3 etic) ionic sym pathetic","sourceRef":"Question Pages 253-253; Solution Pages 269-277","sourcePage":253,"sourcePageEnd":253},{"id":"physiology-9-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following would decrease the conduction velocity of an action potential along an axon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increasing the diameter of the axon."},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreasing the capacitance of the membrane."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increasing the axoplasmic resistance."},{"letter":"D","text":"Increasing the myelination of the axon."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increasing the axoplasmic resistance.","explanation":"Increasing this resistance would hinder the spread of Na+ ions, making it more difficult to reach the threshold for action potential initiation at adjacent nodes of Ranvier. Conduction velocity of the Action potential. Factors Influencing Myelination : Increases conduction velocity, enabling saltatory conduction. (Option D) Axon Diameter : Larger diameter axons exhibit faster conduction velocities. (Option A) Temperature : Higher temperatures increase ion channel opening and closing speeds affecting conduction velocity. Classification of Nerve Fibers Larger, myelinated fibers (like Aα fibers) - Faster conduction velocity. Smaller, unmyelinated fibers (like C fibers) - Slower conduction velocity. Clinical Significance Local anesthetics affect unmyelinated C fibers before myelinated A fibers. A decrease in chronaxie after nerve repair suggests increased excitability and potential regeneration. Stimulus Strength A stronger stimulus doesn't increase the speed of an action potential in a single nerve fiber, it might activate a greater number of fibers. A lower capacitance means that the membrane can depolarize more rapidly, leading to faster conduction. Myelination is a key factor in reducing membrane capacitance. (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 254-254; Solution Pages 277-278","sourcePage":254,"sourcePageEnd":254},{"id":"physiology-9-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements about the conduction of action potentials is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Speed of action potential conduction is inversely proportional to the degree of myelination of the axon."},{"letter":"B","text":"Saltatory conduction occurs in both myelinated and unmyelinated axons."},{"letter":"C","text":"The action potential jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next in myelinated axons."},{"letter":"D","text":"The myelin sheath increases the flow of ions across the axonal membrane."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The action potential jumps from one node of Ranvier to the next in myelinated axons.","explanation":"The myelin sheath prevents ion flow across the membrane, forcing the action potential to \"jump\" from one node of Ranvier to the next. Conduction of action potential Feature Unmyelinated Myelinated Conduction Slower (0.25 m/s) Faster (up to 100 m/s) Speed Mechanism Action potential flows continuously Action potential \"jumps\" between along the axon membrane. (Option nodes of Ranvier-saltatory B) conduction. Myelin Sheath Absent Present, decreases flow of ions, preventing ion leakage across the membrane. (Option D) Nodes of Absent Present, rapid action potential Ranvier regeneration. Energy Efficienc Less energy efficient. More energy efficient. y Fiber Diameter Smaller Larger • Factors that increase action potential conduction velocity include increased myelination and increased axon diameter. (Option A)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 254-254; Solution Pages 278-279","sourcePage":254,"sourcePageEnd":254},{"id":"physiology-9-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":9,"question":"What impact does hyperkalemia have on a neuron's resting membrane potential (RMP)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It causes depolarization, making the neuron more excitable."},{"letter":"B","text":"It causes hyperpolarization, making the neuron less excitable."},{"letter":"C","text":"It has no effect on the RMP."},{"letter":"D","text":"It increases the activity of the Na+/K+ -ATPase pump."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) It causes depolarization, making the neuron more excitable.","explanation":"• The increased extracellular potassium concentration decreases the concentration gradient for potassium to leave the cell, leading to depolarization. Condition Hyperkalemia Hypokalemia RMP Depolarized (Decreased RMP) Hyperpolarized (Increased RMP) (Option C) (Option B) Explanation Increased extracellular potassium In hypokalemia, cells lose more reduces the gradient for potassium potassium ions due to leaky to exit the cell, causingdepolarization. channels. The resting membrane potential The loss of positive charge becomes less negative than the increases the negative charge typical -70 mV. within the cell, causing hyperpolarization. Effect on Increased excitability can lead to Decreased excitability occurs as the Neuron spontaneous firing and even cell membrane potential is further from Excitability death. the threshold for action potential. Persistent depolarization inactivates Hypokalemia also inhibits the voltage-gated sodium channels, Na-K-ATPase pump. preventing action potential generation. Hypocalcemia: • When blood calcium is normal or high, calcium remains bound to the extracellular mouth of voltage-gated Na■ channels, stabilizing them. • Low blood Ca 2+ (hypocalcemia) causes calcium to detach from the channel, leading to easier sodium entry. This detachment increases Na■ channel sensitivity, enhancing excitability and action potential generation. Calcium and magnesium act as membrane stabilizers. Hyperkalemia can eventually lead to increased activity of the Na+/K+-ATPase pump as a compensatory mechanism, this is not a direct or immediate effect on the RMP. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 254-254; Solution Pages 279-281","sourcePage":254,"sourcePageEnd":254},{"id":"physiology-9-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":10,"question":"During an action potential, the voltage-gated sodium channel transitions through different states. Which of the following sequences accurately reflects the order of these states during a single action potential cycle starting from RMP?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Closed → Open → Inactive → Open"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inactive → Closed → Open → Inactive"},{"letter":"C","text":"Closed → Inactive → Open → Closed"},{"letter":"D","text":"Closed → Open → Inactive → Closed"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Closed → Open → Inactive → Closed","explanation":"Stage M H Description Gat Gat e e Clo Op This is the resting state of sed en the voltage-gated sodium channel, where the activation gate (M gate) is closed, preventing sodium ions from entering the cell. Closed Op Op When the membrane en en potential becomes less negative, the activation gate opens rapidly. This allows a large influx of sodium ions into the cell, leading to depolarization. Open Op Clo If the membrane potential en sed remains depolarized for a prolonged period, the inactivation gate will close. The closed inactivation gate prevents sodium ions Inactive from entering.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 254-255; Solution Pages 281-282","sourcePage":254,"sourcePageEnd":255},{"id":"physiology-9-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which statements accurately describe the refractory period on a neuron's signaling properties?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ensures action potentials move in one direction along an axon."},{"letter":"B","text":"Restricts the length of an action potential, stopping overly long depolarizations."},{"letter":"C","text":"Sets the intensity needed to start an action potential (threshold level)."},{"letter":"D","text":"Aids in the summation of local potentials, facilitating action potential generation."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Ensures action potentials move in one direction along an axon.","explanation":"• Action potentials are self-propagating impulses that travel down an axon with constant amplitude and velocity. • During the refractory period, the portion of the membrane that has just undergone depolarization is resistant to re-excitation. • This resistance ensures that the action potential moves forward along the axon rather than propagating backward to already stimulated areas. Refractory period: • Refractory period is a period of reduced excitability in a neuron following an action potential. • Role : Ensuring unidirectional propagation of action potentials. Modulating action potential frequency. • Ensuring unidirectional propagation of action potentials. • Modulating action potential frequency. • Ensuring unidirectional propagation of action potentials. • Modulating action potential frequency. Characteristi Absolute Refractory Period Relative Refractory Period c Definition The initial phase of the refractory Follows the absolute refractory period. period The period during which a second The period during which a stronger action potential cannot be elicited, than-normal stimulus can elicit a even with a strong stimulus. second action potential. Duration Sum of the total depolarization Last 2/3rd of the repolarization period and the first 1/3rd of the period of the action potential. repolarization period of the actionpotential. Excitability No stimulus, no matter how strong. Stronger than normal stimuli Voltage-Gate Inactive or open state. Transition from the inactive to the d Na+ Chann closed state. els Significance Limits the maximum frequency of Encoding of stimulus strength by action potentials a neuron can fire. modulating the frequency of action potentials. The refractory period mainly governs the neuron's ability to generate a second action potential, not the duration of an individual action potential. (Option B) While the refractory period influences a neuron's excitability, it doesn't directly determine the threshold intensity. (Option C) The refractory period actually makes it more difficult for local potentials to summate during its time course. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 255-255; Solution Pages 282-283","sourcePage":255,"sourcePageEnd":255},{"id":"physiology-9-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Based on the graph showing potassium ion permeability during an action potential, at which point does the permeability of potassium ions reach its peak?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Point A"},{"letter":"B","text":"Point C"},{"letter":"C","text":"Point D"},{"letter":"D","text":"Point F"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Point D","explanation":"• Point D signifies the early phase of repolarization. • At this stage, voltage-gated potassium channels are fully open, leading to the highest permeability to potassium ions. • Resting Membrane Potential ( Point A on graph): At rest, the neuron's membrane potential is around -70 mV. K+ permeability is higher than Na+ permeability due to the presence of K+ leak channels. However, the overall permeability to both ions is relatively low. • At rest, the neuron's membrane potential is around -70 mV. • K+ permeability is higher than Na+ permeability due to the presence of K+ leak channels. • However, the overall permeability to both ions is relatively low. • Depolarization Phase (Between Points A and C): Voltage-gated Na+ channels open faster than K+ channels. Rapid influx of Na+ ions, causing a rapid rise in membrane potential, causing a steep slope. • Voltage-gated Na+ channels open faster than K+ channels. • Rapid influx of Na+ ions, causing a rapid rise in membrane potential, causing a steep slope. • Peak of Action Potential (Point C) : Na+ permeability is at its maximum. K+ channels are also opening at this stage but have not reached their peak. • Na+ permeability is at its maximum. • K+ channels are also opening at this stage but have not reached their peak. • Early Repolarization Phase (Point D) : Early phase of repolarization. Voltage-gated K+ channels are fully open, resulting in the highest permeability. Na+ channels are rapidly inactivated during this phase. • Early phase of repolarization. • Voltage-gated K+ channels are fully open, resulting in the highest permeability. • Na+ channels are rapidly inactivated during this phase. • Late Repolarization and After-hyperpolarization (Points E and F): K+ permeability starts to decline as the membrane potential moves towards its resting state. Slow closure of K+ channels results in a period of hyperpolarization, where the membrane potential falls slightly below the resting potential (Point F) . • K+ permeability starts to decline as the membrane potential moves towards its resting state. • Slow closure of K+ channels results in a period of hyperpolarization, where the membrane potential falls slightly below the resting potential (Point F) . • At rest, the neuron's membrane potential is around -70 mV. • K+ permeability is higher than Na+ permeability due to the presence of K+ leak channels. • However, the overall permeability to both ions is relatively low. • Voltage-gated Na+ channels open faster than K+ channels. • Rapid influx of Na+ ions, causing a rapid rise in membrane potential, causing a steep slope. • Na+ permeability is at its maximum. • K+ channels are also opening at this stage but have not reached their peak. • Early phase of repolarization. • Voltage-gated K+ channels are fully open, resulting in the highest permeability. • Na+ channels are rapidly inactivated during this phase. • K+ permeability starts to decline as the membrane potential moves towards its resting state. • Slow closure of K+ channels results in a period of hyperpolarization, where the membrane potential falls slightly below the resting potential (Point F) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 255-255; Solution Pages 283-285","sourcePage":255,"sourcePageEnd":255},{"id":"physiology-9-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The graph below depicts an action potential. Which of the following statements correctly describes the state of ion channels at the point marked \"X\" on the graph?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na+ channels are closed; K+ channels are open."},{"letter":"B","text":"Na+ channels are completely inactivated; K+ channels are open."},{"letter":"C","text":"Na+ and K+ channels are open."},{"letter":"D","text":"Na+ and K+ channels are closed."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Na+ channels are inactivated; K+ channels are open.","explanation":"• At the peak of the action potential (point \"X\"), the neuron's membrane potential is briefly positive. • The voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated, at this same point, voltage-gated K+ channels are open, allowing K+ ions to flow out of the neuron. Phase Membrane Potential Na+ Channels K+ Channels Resting State Polarized (-70 mV) Closed Closed Depolarization (Rising Less negative, towards 0 Rapidly opening, then Slowly opens. Phase) mV. inactivating Overshoot (Peak) Briefly positive (+35 mV). Inactivated Open Repolarization (Falling Returning to negative Inactivated Open Phase) After-hyperpolarization More negative. Closed Slowly closing Return to Resting State Returning to -70 mV Closed Closed (Option D) At point \"X\", the peak of the action potential, voltage-gated Na+ channels are not merely closed; they are transitioning into their inactive state. (Option A) At the peak of the action potential, the voltage-gated Na+ channels are transitioning into their inactive state. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 256-256; Solution Pages 285-286","sourcePage":256,"sourcePageEnd":256},{"id":"physiology-9-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 75-year-old bedridden patient presents with severe pressure ulcers over the sacral region and complaints of muscle weakness and numbness in the lower limb. On examination, there is evidence of compromised blood flow and significant tissue damage. Which innermost layer of the peripheral nerve is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Perineurium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endoneurium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epineurium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mesoneurium"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Epineurium","explanation":"• The fasciculi are held together by a dense layer of connective tissue called the epineurium, which surrounds the entire nerve and contains nerve fibers. • In bedridden patients, loss of this protective fat layer can lead to pressure on nerve fibers, causing paralysis. Blood vessels travel through this connective tissue, so reduced blood supply can lead to ischemic neuritis and pain. Connective tissue layers of Peripheral Nerve : Endoneuri The endoneurium is a layer of connective tissue um covering each nerve fibre within its Schwann cell and (Option B) basal lamina in a peripheral nerve. Contains collagen, fibroblast,schwann cells, endothelial cells and macrophages. Endoneurium holds adjoining nerve fibres together and facilitates their aggregation to form fasciculi. Perineuriu A thick layer of connective tissue covering each fascicle m is called Perineurium. (Option A) It comprises layers of flattened cells separated by layers of collagen fibres. Controls diffusion of substances in and out of axons. Epineurium The fasciculi are held together by a fairly dense layer of (Option C) connective tissue that surrounds the entire nerve and is called Epineurium. Contribute to the tensile strength of the nerve. Loss of this layer in bedridden patients can lead to pressure on nerve fibres and paralysis. Mesoneurium (Option D) is a connective tissue sheath that suspends the nerve trunk within the soft tissue and is continuous with the underlying epineurium","sourceRef":"Question Pages 256-256; Solution Pages 286-287","sourcePage":256,"sourcePageEnd":256},{"id":"physiology-9-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following accurately compares local potentials and action potentials?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Local potentials are all-or-none; action potentials are graded and decremental."},{"letter":"B","text":"Local potentials can summate and are decremental; action potentials are all-or-nothing and non-decremental."},{"letter":"C","text":"Local potentials have constant amplitude; action potentials vary and diminish with distance."},{"letter":"D","text":"Both local and action potentials propagate without diminishing and can summate."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Local potentials can summate and are decremental; action potentials are all-or-nothing and non-decremental.","explanation":"Local Potentials: Graded, decremental, and can summate. Action Potentials: All-or-nothing, non-decremental, and do not summate. Feature Local Potentials Action Potentials Amplitude Graded; varies with stimulus All-or-nothing; amplitude remains (Vs Optio strength constant once threshold is reached n A&C;) Propagati Decremental; diminishes with Non-decremental; maintains on distance magnitude over long distances Summatio Can summate (spatial and temporal) Cannot summate; separated by n refractory periods (Option B) Function Initiate action potentials by reaching Transmit signals over long threshold at the axon hillock distances along nerve fibers Ionic Basi Involves ion movements, similar to Depolarization and repolarization s action potentials, but often localized driven by voltage-gated Na■ and K■ channels Excitatory Postsynaptic Potentials (EPSPs): Local, Graded Potentials • Depolarizing events that can trigger an action potential. Excitatory neurotransmitters bind to receptors, opening ion channels and allowing positively charged ions to enter the cell. • Action potential triggered if depolarization reaches around -55 mV. Action Potential Stages: Propagation of Action Potential: • Mechanism : Action potentials travel along the nerve fiber without diminishing. Depolarization at one site causes local current flow that triggers action potentials in adjacent regions. • All-or-Nothing Principle : An action potential occurs only if the threshold is reached, maintaining consistent magnitude regardless of stimulus strength. Refractory Periods: • Absolute Refractory Period : Na+ channels are inactivated, preventing another action potential. • Relative Refractory Period : Requires a stronger stimulus to trigger another action potential due to partial repolarization. Myelination and Saltatory Conduction: Myelin Sheath insulates nerve fibers, formed by Schwann cells. Action potentials \"jump\" between nodes of Ranvier, speeding up transmission and saving energy. Energy Dependence: Na■-K■ Pump restores ionic gradients after an action potential, consuming ATP. Factors Influencing Excitability: • Calcium Ions (Ca²■) : Low extracellular Ca²■ increases excitability by lowering the activation threshold for Na■ channels; high Ca²■ decreases excitability. • Local Anesthetics : Block voltage-gated Na■ channels, reducing excitability and preventing action potentials.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 257-257; Solution Pages 287-289","sourcePage":257,"sourcePageEnd":257},{"id":"physiology-9-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the changes in the resting membrane potential (RMP) of neuron during depolarization and hyperpolarization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Depolarization: RMP more negative; Hyperpolarization: RMP more positive."},{"letter":"B","text":"Depolarization: RMP more positive; Hyperpolarization: RMP more negative."},{"letter":"C","text":"Depolarization: RMP more negative; Hyperpolarization: RMP -65 mV."},{"letter":"D","text":"Depolarization: RMP more positive; Hyperpolarization: RMP less negative."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Depolarization: RMP more positive; Hyperpolarization: RMP more negative.","explanation":"Depolarization: RMP becomes more positive as Na■ enters the neuron. Hyperpolarization: RMP becomes more negative due to excessive K■ outflow or Cl■ influx. Resting Membrane Potential: • Typical Value: ~ -65 mV • Inside of the neuron is more negatively charged than the outside. • Primarily influenced by ion concentration gradients, especially potassium (K■). • Potassium Pump: Actively transports K■ ions into the neuron, maintaining higher internal concentration. • Creates a tendency for K■ ions to diffuse out, contributing to the negative resting potential. Depolarization: Sudden shift from negative to positive membrane potential. Mechanism: Increased permeability to Na■ ions allows rapid influx of Na■, neutralizing the negative potential. In large nerve fibers, Na■ influx can cause the membrane potential to briefly become positive (overshoot). Excitatory Postsynaptic Potentials (EPSPs): Depolarizing events that can trigger an action potential. Excitatory neurotransmitters bind to receptors, opening ion channels and allowing positively charged ions to enter. Action potential is triggered if depolarization reaches around -45 mV. Repolarization: Return of membrane potential to resting state (-65 mV) following depolarization. Na■ channels close, reducing Na■ influx. K+ channels open wider, allowing K■ ions to exit rapidly. Restores negative membrane potential. Hyperpolarizat Membrane potential becomes more negative than the ion: resting potential, below -65 mV. Inhibitory Neurotransmitters: Open channels allowing negatively charged ions (e.g., Cl■) to enter or positively charged (e.g., K■) to exit. Prolonged Potassium Channel Opening: Allows additional K■ outflow. Effect: Moves potential closer to the potassium Nernst potential, making the neuron less likely to fire immediately. Recovery: Gradual return to threshold potential for new action potential generation. Inhibitory Postsynaptic Potentials (IPSPs): Hyperpolarizing events that make it harder for the neuron to fire an action potential.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 257-257; Solution Pages 289-290","sourcePage":257,"sourcePageEnd":257},{"id":"physiology-9-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Match the ion with its equilibrium potential in a normal mammalian cell. Ion Equilibrium Potential (mV) 1. Sodium A. -70 2. Chloride B. +63 3. Potassium C. +132 4. Calcium D. -90 Ion Equilibrium Potential (mV) 1. Sodium A. -70 2. Chloride B. +63 3. Potassium C. +132 4. Calcium D. -90","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-C, 2-D, 3-A, 4-B"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","explanation":"Ion Equilibrium Potential Sodium +63 mV Chloride -70 mV Potassium -90 mV Calcium +132 mV","sourceRef":"Question Pages 257-258; Solution Pages 290-291","sourcePage":257,"sourcePageEnd":258},{"id":"physiology-9-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 25-year-old woman presents with symptoms of confusion and lethargy. Her blood test shows a serum sodium level of 120 mEq/L. The intracellular concentration of sodium ([Na■]■) is 12 mEq/L. what is the equilibrium potential for sodium (in mV)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"61 mV"},{"letter":"B","text":"72 mV"},{"letter":"C","text":"82 mV"},{"letter":"D","text":"92 mV"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 61 mV","explanation":"According to the question, [Na■]outside = 120 mEq/L [Na■]inside■ = 12 mEq/L Using the Nernst equation: 'z' is the electrical charge of the ion (+1 for K+). Therefore, Eq= 61 log (120/12) = 61 log (10) = 61*(1) = 61 mV • The Nernst equation calculates the electrical potential difference across a membrane (Nernst potential) that balances the diffusion of a specific ion, preventing further net movement despite a concentration gradient. • The Nernst equation calculates the Nernst potential (EMF) for a single, univalent ion (an ion with a +1 or -1 charge) at normal body temperature (37°C) • The Nernst potential magnitude depends on the ion concentration ratio across the membrane; larger differences result in higher potentials. • Nernst equation calculates the potential for a single ion, although cell membranes are permeable to multiple ions. Applications of the Nernst Equation: • Explains how ion concentration differences generate electrical potential across a selectively permeable membrane. • Basis for the Goldman-Hodgkin-Katz equation, which calculates membrane potential considering multiple ions and their permeabilities. Limitations: It works best for systems with only one permeant ion. In living cells, this is rarely the case, making the Goldman equation a more accurate representation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 258-258; Solution Pages 291-292","sourcePage":258,"sourcePageEnd":258},{"id":"physiology-9-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following is true regarding the leak channels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facilitate the rapid transmission of action potentials along the axon."},{"letter":"B","text":"Responsible for generation of synaptic potentials at the postsynaptic membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Help in maintaining resting membrane potential by allowing passive movement of ions"},{"letter":"D","text":"Play a crucial role in the repolarization phase of the action potential"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Help in maintaining resting membrane potential by allowing passive movement of ions","explanation":"Leak channels: • Leak channels are a subtype of ion channels that passively allow ions such as Na+/K+ to move across the membrane. • They are always open. • The most important leak channel is the K+ leak channel, which allows the passive transfer of K+ from inside the cell to outside. • This helps in creating the resting membrane potential of the neuron (Option C) • The resting membrane potential is crucial for neurons in generating and propagating action potentials. Transmission of action potentials along the axon is facilitated by voltage-gated Na+ channels (Option D) Generation of synaptic potentials at the postsynaptic membrane is facilitated by ligand-gated ion channels. (Option D) Repolarization phase of the action potential is facilitated by voltage gated K+ channel (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 258-258; Solution Pages 292-293","sourcePage":258,"sourcePageEnd":258},{"id":"physiology-9-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following best explains the phenomenon where the distribution of ion species across a semipermeable membrane is influenced by the presence of a non-permeable ion, resulting in an uneven distribution of permeable ions?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nernst Equation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bohr Effect"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gibbs-Donnan Effect"},{"letter":"D","text":"Van't Hoff Law"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Gibbs-Donnan Effect","explanation":"Gibbs-Donnan Effect: • It is described as the effect of an impermeable ion on the distribution of other ions to which the membrane is permeable (Option C). • The non permeable ion influences the distribution of permeable ions in such a way that there will be an unequal distribution of these ions on either side of the membrane. • This results in an electrochemical gradient, where the concentration of ions is balanced but not equal due to the presence of non-permeable ions. • It helps to create and maintain an uneven distribution of ions between ECF and ICF. Nernst Equation calculates the equilibrium potential for a particular ion across a membrane (Option A) Bohr effect describes how pH and CO 2 concentration affect the oxygen-binding affinity of hemoglobin (Option B) Van’t Hoff law relates to the effect of temperature on the equilibrium constant for a chemical reaction (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 258-259; Solution Pages 293-293","sourcePage":258,"sourcePageEnd":259},{"id":"physiology-9-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding resting membrane potential?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is created by diffusion of K+ from outside to inside the cell at rest"},{"letter":"B","text":"The RMP of cardiac muscle is -90 mV"},{"letter":"C","text":"K+ is the major determinant of RMP"},{"letter":"D","text":"The major contributor of RMP is through passive diffusion of ions"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) It is created by passive diffusion of K+ from outside to inside the cell at rest","explanation":"Resting membrane potential (RMP): • It is defined as the voltage difference between the exterior and interior of an excitable cell • In any cell, K+ is the major determinant of RMP (option C) • The major contribution of RMP is through passive diffusion of K+ ions followed by active diffusion through Na+-K+ ions (option D) Generation of RMP: RMP of different cells: Cell RMP Neuron -70mV Skeletal muscle -95mV Smooth muscle -60mV Cardiac muscle -90mV (option B) Astrocyte -90mV Erythrocyte -12mV Photoreceptor -40mV (dark) to -70mV (light)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 259-259; Solution Pages 294-294","sourcePage":259,"sourcePageEnd":259},{"id":"physiology-9-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the type, direction and mediators of axonal transport? 1 Anterograde Cell body to axon terminal Dynein 2 Anterograde Axon terminal to cell body Kinesin 3 Retrograde Axon terminal to cell body Dynein 4 Retrograde Cell body to axon terminal Kinesin 1 Anterograde Cell body to axon terminal Dynein 2 Anterograde Axon terminal to cell body Kinesin 3 Retrograde Axon terminal to cell body Dynein 4 Retrograde Cell body to axon terminal Kinesin","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 3","explanation":"Axonal transport: • It is a process in neurons which involves the transport of organelles, proteins and other cellular material along the axon • It can be abolished by colchicine, dinitrophenol, azide, cyanide and prolonged anoxia Type Mediate Characteristics d by Anterograde Kinesin It involves the transport of cellular transport material from soma to the axon terminal. It is responsible for the transport of proteins, synaptic vesicles necessary for synaptic transmission. Two types: Fast axoplasmic transport at a rate of 400 mm/day, mediated by kinesin. Slow axoplasmic transport at a rate of 0.5-2 mm/day, mediated by actin, neurofilaments and microtubules. Supplies material required for regeneration following nerve injury. Retrograde Dynein It involves the transport from axon transport terminals to soma at a rate of about 200 mm/day. It is important for transport of viruses, toxins, nerve growth factors and reuptake of synaptic neurotransmitters.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 259-259; Solution Pages 295-295","sourcePage":259,"sourcePageEnd":259},{"id":"physiology-9-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which type of neuron is primarily involved in the transmission of visual information from the retina to the brain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Unipolar neuron"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bipolar neuron"},{"letter":"C","text":"Multipolar neuron"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both multipolar and bipolar neuron"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Both multipolar and bipolar neuron","explanation":"Transmission of visual information from the retina to the brain is done by the Ganglion cell. • Ganglion cell is mostly multipolar but can be bipolar as well Types of neurons: Type Characteristic Unipolar neuron Seen in invertebrates Have one process with several (Option A) receptive and releasing terminals Bipolar neurons Seen in retina and olfactory epithelium (Option B) Have two specialized processes Dendrite carries information to the cell Axon carries information from the cell Pseudo-unipolar Seen in dorsal root ganglion. neurons The developing neuron has a single process that splits into two, both of which function as axons, one reaching up to the skin and the other to the spinal cord Multipolar neurons Most common (Option C) Seen in ganglion cell, hippocampal pyramidal cells, cerebellar Purkinje cells, motor neurons, etc Have one axon and multiple dendrites","sourceRef":"Question Pages 260-260; Solution Pages 296-296","sourcePage":260,"sourcePageEnd":260},{"id":"physiology-9-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 30-year-old man presents with progressive muscle weakness and fatigue. Electromyography (EMG) and nerve conduction studies indicate that the issue lies in the transmission of nerve impulses to his muscles, while central nervous system imaging shows no abnormalities. Which part of the neuron is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Initial Segment"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dendrite"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axon Terminal"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Axon Terminal","explanation":"Axon Terminal triggers the release of neurotransmitters into the synaptic cleft when action potential reaches it, facilitating communication with the next neuron or target cell. Neurons have four important functional zones: 1. Dendritic zone (Receptor zone) Receives information from other neurons and transmits impulses towards the cell body. Integrates local potentials from synaptic connections. 2. Axon Hillock and Initial segment Action potentials are generated here. (Action Potential Generation zone) In motor neurons, it occurs at the axon hillock; in sensory neurons, at the initial node of Ranvier. Summates local potentials from dendrites and generates action potentials if the threshold is reached. 3. Axon Long, slender process transmitting action potentials (Transmission zone) from the cell body to axon terminals. Ensures rapid and efficient propagation of nerve impulses over long distances. 4. Axon Terminal (Discharge zone) Contains synaptic vesicles with neurotransmitters. When an action potential reaches the axon terminal, it triggers neurotransmitter release into the synaptic cleft for communication with other neurons or target cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 260-260; Solution Pages 296-297","sourcePage":260,"sourcePageEnd":260},{"id":"physiology-9-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":25,"question":"In which part of the neuron are neurofibrillary tangles primarily found, which are associated with neurodegenerative diseases like Alzheimer's?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dendrites"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Soma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axon terminal"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Soma","explanation":"Cell body/Soma contain cytoskeletal proteins like neurofilaments, that form characteristic neurofibrillary tangles in Alzheimer’s. A neuron is the structural and functional unit of the nervous system, identified as an excitable cell due to its ability to generate action potentials when electrically stimulated. The neuron has three parts: Cell body, Dendrite and Axon that ends at the axon terminal. Table rendering failed Axons often acquire a myelin sheath, a protein-lipid complex wrapped around the axon. The myelin sheath envelops the axon except at the nodes of Ranvier, periodic 1 micron gaps where the axon is unmyelinated.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 260-260; Solution Pages 298-298","sourcePage":260,"sourcePageEnd":260},{"id":"physiology-9-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-9","chapter":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topic":"Neuron and nerve, Action Potential, RMP, Nerve fibre classification","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":26,"question":"A 55-year-old patient with brain tumor undergoes treatment with a novel chemotherapy drug targeting tumor cells. Despite the initial response, follow-up imaging shows minimal response and tumor progression. Which characteristic of the blood-brain barrier is responsible for this treatment challenge?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Impermeability to plasma proteins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Impermeability to lipid-soluble molecules"},{"letter":"C","text":"Presence of fenestrations in capillary walls"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lack of efflux transporters for drugs"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Impermeability to plasma proteins","explanation":"The blood-brain barrier (BBB) is impermeable to plasma proteins and most non-lipid soluble large organic molecules. This limits the effective concentration of therapeutic drugs, such as protein antibodies and non-lipid soluble drugs, in the CSF. Tight junctions between endothelial cells are responsible for the low permeability of the BBB. Blood-Brain Barrier (BBB) The BBB is the barrier between capillary endothelial cells in the brain and epithelial cells in the choroid plexus, limiting the exchange of substances into the brain. Anatomical Basis Formed by two special structures: Endothelial cells in brain capillaries are joined by tight junctions. Capillaries are surrounded by astrocyte foot processes. Permeability Easily Penetrated: Water, CO2, O2, lipid-soluble substances. Not Penetrated: Proteins, polypeptides. Circumventricular organs The BBB is deficient in certain brain regions known as circumventricular organs, which are outside the BBB: Organum vasculosum of lamina terminalis (OVLT) Subfornical Organ Area postrema in medulla oblongata Posterior pituitary and median eminence(ventral part) of hypothalamus Pineal gland Functions of BBB Maintain constancy of Na+, K+, Ca++, H+ and Mg++ in brain fluids Protect neurons from harmful toxins Prevents escape of neurotransmitters from brain into circulation Disruption of BBB helps in identifying the location and extent of lesions in the brain Clinical importance Kernicterus: Hyperbilirubinemia damages the basal ganglia in infants due to an underdeveloped BBB. Brain Tumor : BBB is disrupted at the tumor site. Infection and Injury: BBB breakdown occurs in infected or injured regions. Lipid-soluble substances permeate through BBB easily (Option B) BBB does not have fenestrations. (Option C) Efflux transporter in BBB limits the brain tissue exposure to various potential therapeutic agents. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 260-261; Solution Pages 298-300","sourcePage":260,"sourcePageEnd":261},{"id":"physiology-10-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":1,"question":"During muscle contraction, which best describes the role of the dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) and the ryanodine receptor (RyR) in skeletal muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"DHPR inhibits RyR to prevent calcium release."},{"letter":"B","text":"DHPR activates RyR to release calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"letter":"C","text":"RyR reuptakes calcium into the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"letter":"D","text":"DHPR releases calcium directly into the muscle cytoplasm."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) DHPR activates RyR to release calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.","explanation":"DHPR, located in the T-tubules, senses the action potential and undergoes a conformational change. This change directly activates RyR, the calcium release channel on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, leading to the release of calcium ions into the cytoplasm, which is crucial for muscle contraction. Sarcotubular System: • The Sarcotubular system is composed of the L tubule and T tubule . • The T-tubule contains a L-type voltage gated Calcium channel, known as the DHPR (dihydropyridine receptor channel). • The L-tubule contains a calcium channel known as RyR ryanodine receptor channel). DHPR activates, not inhibits, RyR to facilitate calcium release during muscle contraction. (Option A) The reuptake of calcium into the sarcoplasmic reticulum is performed by the SERCA pump, not by RyR. (Option C) DHPR does not release calcium directly; it activates RyR to release calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytoplasm. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 301-301; Solution Pages 309-310","sourcePage":301,"sourcePageEnd":301},{"id":"physiology-10-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Match the changes in the sarcomere during muscle contraction with their corresponding descriptions: 1. A band 2. H band 3. I band 4. M line Descriptions: A. Decreases or disappears during contraction B. Length remains the same during contraction C. Becomes more prominent during contraction D. Decreases in length during contraction","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-B, 2-A, 3-C, 4-D"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-A, 2-B, 3-D, 4-C"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C","explanation":"• A-band: The length of the A-band remains the same during muscle contraction because it corresponds to the length of the myosin filaments, which do not change. • H-band: The H-band decreases or disappears as the actin filaments slide over the myosin filaments during contraction, reducing the region where only myosin is present. • I-band: The length of the I-band decreases as the sarcomere shortens and the actin filaments overlap more with the myosin filaments. • M-line: The M-line becomes more prominent as it marks the central point of the sarcomere where the thick filaments are anchored.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 301-301; Solution Pages 310-311","sourcePage":301,"sourcePageEnd":301},{"id":"physiology-10-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which statement accurately describes the types of heat generated during muscle contraction and relaxation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Resting heat is produced during relaxation; activation heat during contraction."},{"letter":"B","text":"Activation heat is produced during relaxation; shortening heat during rest."},{"letter":"C","text":"Resting heat is produced at rest; activation, shortening, and relaxation heat during various phases of muscle activity."},{"letter":"D","text":"Shortening heat is produced during rest; relaxation heat during contraction."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Resting heat is produced at rest; activation, shortening, and relaxation heat during various phases of muscle activity.","explanation":"Types of heat generated during muscle contraction and relaxation: • Resting heat refers to the heat produced by the muscle at rest. • It is generated by the basal metabolic processes that occur in the muscle even when it is not actively contracting. • Activation heat is generated at the onset of muscle contraction. • This heat results from the initial metabolic processes that prepare the muscle fibers for contraction, including the breakdown of ATP and the activation of the contractile machinery. • Shortening heat is produced during the actual shortening of the muscle fibers. • This occurs as the muscle generates force and performs work, converting chemical energy into mechanical energy and heat. • Relaxation heat is generated as the muscle returns to its resting state after contraction. • This phase involves processes such as the re-synthesis of ATP, the re-establishment of ion gradients, and the removal of metabolic byproducts. Resting heat is not produced during relaxation. It is the heat generated by the muscle at rest, due to basal metabolic activities. Activation heat is correctly associated with the contraction phase. (Option A) Activation heat is not produced during relaxation; it occurs at the onset of contraction. Shortening heat is not generated during rest; it is produced when muscle fibers actively shorten during contraction. (Option B) Shortening heat is not produced during rest. Relaxation heat is not generated during contraction; it occurs during the relaxation phase as the muscle returns to its resting state. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 301-302; Solution Pages 311-312","sourcePage":301,"sourcePageEnd":302},{"id":"physiology-10-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of shortness of breath and fatigue during physical activity. Upon examination, the physician notes an elevated jugular venous pressure and an S3 heart sound. Echocardiography reveals dilated cardiac chambers with reduced ejection fraction. The physician explains that the heart is failing to pump efficiently, in part due to the length-tension relationship of the cardiac muscle. Which of the following best explains the difference between the length-tension relationship in skeletal muscle and cardiac muscle relevant to this patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The operating point for both skeletal and cardiac muscles is at the same point on the tension curve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skeletal muscle generates maximal tension at near resting length, while cardiac muscle generates maximal tension at slightly shorter lengths."},{"letter":"C","text":"Skeletal muscle maintains tension over a wider range of sarcomere lengths than cardiac muscle."},{"letter":"D","text":"Both muscles lose tension rapidly when the sarcomere length is significantly shortened or stretched beyond optimal length."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Skeletal muscle generates maximal tension at near resting length, while cardiac muscle generates maximal tension at slightly longer lengths.","explanation":"Skeletal muscle generates maximal tension when sarcomere length is near resting length (about 2.0 to 2.2 micrometers), while cardiac muscle generates maximal tension at slightly longer lengths due to the rising part of the active tension curve , which allows for greater force production as the heart fills with more blood. • In skeletal muscle, the operating point is at the plateau of the active tension curve , where maximal tension is achieved at resting length. In cardiac muscle, the operating point is on the rising part of the active tension curve , allowing room for additional stretching and increased force production. (Option A) • Skeletal muscle tension decreases sharply if the sarcomere length deviates significantly from its optimal range. Cardiac muscle, due to its basket-weave fiber arrangement and early starting passive tension curve, is adapted to maintain near-maximal tension over a range of sarcomere lengths, crucial for its function in varying blood volumes. (Option C) • Both muscle types lose tension if sarcomere lengths are significantly altered from their optimal range. However, cardiac muscle’s passive tension curve starts earlier, and its fibers’ basket-weave pattern allows it to recoil with significant force, enabling it to maintain function over a broader range of lengths compared to skeletal muscle. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 302-302; Solution Pages 312-313","sourcePage":302,"sourcePageEnd":302},{"id":"physiology-10-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which statement best describes the length-tension relationship in skeletal muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The maximum tension is generated when the sarcomere length is 1.6 µm."},{"letter":"B","text":"The number of cross-bridges and the tension generated decrease when the sarcomere length exceeds 2.2 µm."},{"letter":"C","text":"Passive tension contributes to muscle tension even when the sarcomere length is optimal."},{"letter":"D","text":"The sarcomere ruptures when the length exceeds 2.2 µm."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The number of cross-bridges and tension generated decrease when the sarcomere length exceeds 2.2 µm.","explanation":"The length-tension relationship in skeletal muscle: • Optimal Length: The sarcomere length that produces maximum tension is between 2.0 and 2.2 µm. (Option B ruled out) • Cross-Bridge Formation: Tension is greatest at this optimal length because cross-bridge formation between actin and myosin filaments is maximized. • Short Sarcomere Length: When sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, excessive overlap of actin filaments limits cross-bridge formation, reducing tension. • Long Sarcomere Length: When sarcomeres are longer than optimal, cross-bridge formation decreases as actin and myosin filaments overlap less, leading to reduced tension. • Maximum Stretch: At lengths around 3.6 µm, the sarcomere can rupture due to excessive stretching. (Option D ruled out) Passive Tension: Passive tension in muscle is the force generated by the stretching of the muscle's connective tissue structures. It arises due to the elasticity of the muscle's structural components, such as the titin protein, which resists excessive stretching. • Passive tension increases as the muscle is stretched beyond its optimal sarcomere length, contributing to the total tension experienced by the muscle. • While it is true that passive tension contributes to the total muscle tension at all lengths, option C is misleading because it implies that passive tension is a significant contributor even when the sarcomere length is optimal (2.0 to 2.2 µm). • The primary focus of the optimal length is on the maximal active tension generated by cross-bridge interactions, not the passive elastic properties of the muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 302-302; Solution Pages 313-314","sourcePage":302,"sourcePageEnd":302},{"id":"physiology-10-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Rigor mortis is a postmortem change resulting in the stiffening of muscles. Which of the following best explains the biochemical basis of rigor mortis in relation to the cross-bridge cycle in skeletal muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased calcium ion concentration in the sarcoplasm leads to continuous muscle contraction."},{"letter":"B","text":"Depletion of ATP prevents the detachment of myosin heads from actin filaments."},{"letter":"C","text":"Accumulation of lactic acid in muscles causes a permanent contraction."},{"letter":"D","text":"Degradation of muscle proteins leads to muscle stiffness."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Depletion of ATP prevents the detachment of myosin heads from actin filaments.","explanation":"• In the cross-bridge cycle, ATP is essential for the detachment of myosin heads from actin filaments after a power stroke. • In the absence of ATP, myosin heads remain bound to actin, leading to a state of continuous contraction, which is observed as muscle stiffness in rigor mortis. • This process begins several hours after death and continues until muscle proteins begin to decompose. Cross Bridge Formation: • Calcium ions play a crucial role in initiating muscle contraction by binding to troponin, which allows myosin to bind to actin. However, rigor mortis occurs due to the lack of ATP, which is necessary for the detachment of myosin from actin after contraction. (Option A) • Lactic acid accumulation in muscles is typically associated with anaerobic metabolism during vigorous exercise, leading to muscle fatigue and soreness. (Option C) • Degradation of muscle proteins leads to the eventual resolution of rigor mortis, not its onset. As the body decomposes, proteolytic enzymes break down muscle proteins, leading to the relaxation of the muscles and the end of rigor mortis. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 303-303; Solution Pages 314-315","sourcePage":303,"sourcePageEnd":303},{"id":"physiology-10-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Arrange the following steps in the correct sequence to describe the molecular mechanism of muscle contraction: Cross-bridge formation between myosin and actin Release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum Binding of calcium ions to troponin C Detachment of the myosin head from actin Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and Pi, re-cocking the myosin head Power stroke, moving the myosin head from 90 degrees to 45 degrees","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2, 3, 1, 6, 4, 5"},{"letter":"B","text":"2, 3, 4, 1, 6, 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"2, 3, 1, 4, 5, 6"},{"letter":"D","text":"2, 3, 1, 6, 5, 4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 2, 3, 1, 6, 4, 5 Explanation : Sequential events during muscle contraction Release of calcium ions from the An action potential triggers the release of calcium ions sarcoplasmic reticulum (Ca²■) from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the cytoplasm of the muscle cell. Binding of calcium ions to troponin C Calcium ions bind to troponin C on the thin filaments, causing a conformational change in the troponin complex. Cross-bridge formation between myosin The conformational change in the troponin complex and actin moves tropomyosin away from the myosin-binding sites on actin, allowing the myosin heads (with ADP and Pi bound) to attach to the exposed binding sites on actin. Power stroke, moving the myosin head The myosin head pivots from a 90-degree angle to a from 90 degrees to 45 degrees 45-degree angle, pulling the actin filament toward the center of the sarcomere. ADP and Pi are released during this movement. Detachment of the myosin head from actin A new ATP molecule binds to the myosin head, causing it to detach from the actin filament. Hydrolysis of ATP to ADP and Pi, The ATP is hydrolyzed to ADP and Pi, providing energy re-cocking the myosin head to re-cock the myosin head back to the 90-degree position, ready for another cycle of contraction.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 303-303; Solution Pages 315-316","sourcePage":303,"sourcePageEnd":303},{"id":"physiology-10-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements about the regulatory proteins of skeletal muscle is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tropomyosin prevents actin-myosin attachment in a resting muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"Tropomyosin is a filamentous protein, while troponin is a spherical protein consisting of 3 subunits."},{"letter":"C","text":"Troponin C can bind with up to 4 calcium ions at a time, which initiates a conformational change in the troponin complex."},{"letter":"D","text":"Troponin I attaches to tropomyosin, anchoring the troponin complex to the actin filament."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Troponin I attaches to tropomyosin, anchoring the troponin complex to the actin filament.","explanation":"Regulatory proteins play crucial roles in the contraction of skeletal muscles by controlling the interaction between actin and myosin filaments. Regulatory proteins are of 2 main types: • Tropomyosin • Troponin Complex ( Troponin C, Troponin T & Troponin I) Regulatory Function Protein Tropomyosin Spiral-shaped filamentous protein wrapped around actin filaments. (Option B ruled out) Blocks myosin-binding sites on actin in a resting muscle, preventing muscle contraction. (Option A ruled out) Known as the gatekeeper molecule of muscle contraction. Troponin T Attached to tropomyosin. Anchors the troponin complex to the actin filament. Troponin I Acts as the inhibitory subunit of troponin . Helps position tropomyosin over myosin-binding sites on actin, preventing actin-myosin interaction in a resting muscle. Troponin C Binds with calcium ions, up to 4 ions at a time (Option C) The binding of calcium ions causes a conformational change in the troponin complex, moving tropomyosin away from the myosin-binding sites on actin, allowing muscle contraction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 303-304; Solution Pages 316-316","sourcePage":303,"sourcePageEnd":304},{"id":"physiology-10-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the sliding filament mechanism of muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The sarcomere shortens as actin filaments slide over myosin filaments."},{"letter":"B","text":"Contraction is triggered by action potential causing the release of calcium ions from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"},{"letter":"C","text":"The I-band & H-zone shortens and can disappear during full contraction."},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcium ions bind to tropomyosin, allowing myosin to attach to actin."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Calcium ions bind to tropomyosin, allowing myosin to attach to actin.","explanation":"The sliding filament theory describes the process by which muscles contract to produce force. It involves the following key steps: I-band shortens during contraction as the actin filaments slide over the myosin filaments. H-zone is the region within the A-band where there is only myosin and no overlapping actin. Both disappear during full contraction. (Option C ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 304-304; Solution Pages 317-317","sourcePage":304,"sourcePageEnd":304},{"id":"physiology-10-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Match the labeled parts of the sarcomere (A, B, C, D, E) with their correct names. 1. Z-line 2. I-band 3. M-line 4. A-band 5. H-zone","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4, E-5"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-3, B-1, C-5, D-4, E-2"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4, E-5"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-3, B-1, C-5, D-4, E-2"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) A-3, B-1, C-5, D-4, E-2 Explanation : • A (M-line): The M-line is the center of the sarcomere and the H-zone, where the thick filaments are anchored. • B (Z-line): The Z-line marks the boundary between adjacent sarcomeres and anchors the thin filaments. • C (H-zone): The H-zone is the central region of the A-band where there are only thick filaments, with no thin filament overlap. • D (A-band): The A-band is the length of the thick filaments, which remains constant during contraction. • E (I-band): The I-band is the region of the sarcomere that contains only thin filaments.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 304-304; Solution Pages 317-317","sourcePage":304,"sourcePageEnd":304},{"id":"physiology-10-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 25-year-old athlete presents with muscle pain following intense exercise. A muscle biopsy is performed, and microscopic examination reveals the structural organization of muscle tissue. Which of the following correctly matches the muscle components with their descriptions?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fascicle - individual muscle cell; Fibre - bundle of myofibrils; Myofibril - contains contractile proteins; Contractile protein - forms entire muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"Fascicle - bundle of muscle fibres; Fibre - single muscle cell; Myofibril - contains sarcomeres; Contractile protein - actin and myosin."},{"letter":"C","text":"Fascicle - single muscle cell; Fibre - contains sarcomeres; Myofibril - bundle of fascicles; Contractile protein - forms myofibrils"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fascicle - bundle of muscle fibres; Fibre - contains sarcomeres; Myofibril - individual muscle cell; Contractile protein - actin and myosin."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Fascicle - bundle of muscle fibers; Fibre - single muscle cell; Myofibril - contains sarcomeres; Contractile protein - actin and myosin Explanation : Skeletal muscles are composed of multiple bundles called fascicles- which are made of individual muscle fibers (muscle cells)- muscle cells are made of multiple myofibrils- which are made up of contractile protein molecules- Actin and Myosin.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 305-305; Solution Pages 318-318","sourcePage":305,"sourcePageEnd":305},{"id":"physiology-10-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Examine the image of a sarcomere below. Which band is indicated by the X ? {{caption_text}}","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A band"},{"letter":"B","text":"I band"},{"letter":"C","text":"Z disk"},{"letter":"D","text":"H zone"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) A band","explanation":"• A band : The dark band, anisotropic to polarized light. It contains the entire length of the myosin filaments. The ends of the actin filaments also overlap with the myosin filaments in this band. • I band : The light band, isotropic to polarized light. It contains only actin filaments. (Option B ruled out) • Z disk : A structure composed of filamentous proteins (alpha-actinin) that bisects the I band. Actin filaments attach to the Z disk and extend from it in both directions to interdigitate with myosin filaments. Z disks also pass from myofibril to myofibril, attaching them together across the muscle fiber. (Option C ruled out) • H zone : Located at the center of the A band, this zone contains only myosin filaments and appears lighter than the rest of the A band. (Option D ruled out) • M line : A structure that runs down the center of the H zone, providing structural support for the myosin filaments.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 305-305; Solution Pages 318-319","sourcePage":305,"sourcePageEnd":305},{"id":"physiology-10-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding nerve-muscle physiology?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SERCA is a protein of skeletal muscle encoded by gene ATP2A1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Secondary active transport mechanism involving the NCX protein seen in skeletal muscle."},{"letter":"C","text":"Calsequestrin protein is present only in smooth muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calreticulin protein is present in cardiac muscle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) SERCA is a protein molecule encoded by the gene ATP2A1","explanation":"Sarcoplasmic reticulum Ca+ ATPase (SERCA) pump is a protein molecule encoded by gene ATP2A1. • SERCA is a calcium–adenosine triphosphatase (ATPase) pump that helps transport calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum from the muscle fiber, which leads to muscle relaxation. • It is present in both skeletal muscle and cardiac muscle. Sodium-Calcium Exchanger: Cardiac muscle also utilizes a sodium-calcium exchanger to remove calcium ions from the cell. • This exchanger allows sodium to enter the cell while simultaneously transporting calcium out. • The sodium that enters the cell during this process is then removed by the sodium-potassium ATPase pump • Sodium-calcium exchanger (NCX) is primarily involved in calcium handling in cardiac muscle, not skeletal muscle (Option B ruled out) Two proteins found within the sarcoplasmic reticulum include: • Calsequestrin protein: Found in skeletal muscle, myocardium, and smooth muscle . (Option C ruled out) • Calreticulin protein: Located specifically in smooth muscle tissue . (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 306-306; Solution Pages 319-320","sourcePage":306,"sourcePageEnd":306},{"id":"physiology-10-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 25-year-old man undergoing surgery for a fractured tibia experiences a sudden onset of muscle rigidity, a rapid heart rate, and a high fever shortly after receiving general anesthesia. Mutation in which of the following channels is involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"RyR1"},{"letter":"B","text":"RyR2"},{"letter":"C","text":"CaV"},{"letter":"D","text":"NaV"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) RyR1","explanation":"The symptoms of muscle rigidity, high fever, and rapid heart rate clinically represent Malignant Hyperthermia. A gain of mutation of the RyR1 channel causes excessive calcium release and causes muscle rigidity. RyR2 (Option B) – Mutations cause catecholaminergic polymorphic ventricular tachycardia (CPVT) , leading to life-threatening arrhythmias during stress or exercise. CaV (Option C) – Mutations in calcium voltage-gated channels cause hypokalemic periodic paralysis and certain forms of congenital myopathy . NaV (Option D) – Mutations in sodium voltage-gated channels cause paramyotonia congenita , hyperkalemic periodic paralysis , and some forms of epilepsy .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 306-306; Solution Pages 320-321","sourcePage":306,"sourcePageEnd":306},{"id":"physiology-10-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes a unique characteristic of excitation-contraction coupling in cardiac muscle compared to skeletal muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T-tubules are absent, and calcium influx solely relies on diffusion through the sarcolemma."},{"letter":"B","text":"Calcium-induced calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum is mediated by ryanodine receptors alone."},{"letter":"C","text":"Extracellular calcium entry into T-tubules contributes significantly to calcium influx during the plateau phase of the action potential."},{"letter":"D","text":"Relaxation of cardiac muscle is primarily initiated by potassium binding to troponin."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Extracellular calcium entry through T-tubule membrane contributes significantly to calcium influx during the plateau phase of the action potential.","explanation":"Unlike skeletal muscle, cardiac T-tubules are open to the extracellular space, allowing extracellular calcium influx during the plateau phase of the action potential. This influx is critical for sustaining calcium levels necessary for prolonged contraction in cardiac muscle. This phenomenon is stated as Calcium-induced calcium release. CONTRACTION IN CARDIAC MUSCLE T-tubules are present in both cardiac and skeletal muscle , and they are essential for transmitting the action potential into the interior of the muscle fiber (Option C) Calcium-induced calcium release in cardiac muscle involves both voltage-gated calcium channels in the T-tubules and ryanodine receptors on the SR membrane (Option C) Relaxation of cardiac muscle is primarily initiated by the removal of calcium ions from the cytoplasm, facilitated by the sarcoplasmic reticulum calcium ATPase (SERCA) , and not by potassium binding to troponin (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 306-306; Solution Pages 321-321","sourcePage":306,"sourcePageEnd":306},{"id":"physiology-10-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following events directly results in the exposure of myosin-binding sites on actin filaments during skeletal muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ATP binding to myosin heads"},{"letter":"B","text":"Depolarization of T-tubules"},{"letter":"C","text":"Binding of acetylcholine (ACh) to muscle fiber receptors"},{"letter":"D","text":"Conformational change in troponin complex"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Conformational change in troponin complex","explanation":"The conformational change in the troponin complex occurs in response to elevated cytoplasmic calcium ions (Ca²■), which bind to troponin. This causes tropomyosin to move away from the myosin-binding sites on actin filaments, allowing myosin heads to bind with Myosin binding sites of actin and initiate muscle contraction. CONTRACTION OF SKELETAL MUSCLE: ATP binding allows myosin heads to detach from actin filaments during muscle relaxation, not contraction. (Option A) Depolarization of T-tubules allows for the propagation of the action potential into the muscle fiber but does not directly expose myosin-binding sites on actin filaments. (Option B) Binding of acetylcholine (ACh) to muscle fiber receptors initiates muscle fiber depolarization but does not directly expose myosin-binding sites on actin filaments. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 307-307; Solution Pages 321-322","sourcePage":307,"sourcePageEnd":307},{"id":"physiology-10-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 10-year-old boy presents with progressive muscle weakness, difficulty walking, and frequent falls. His symptoms started around the age of 5 and have gradually worsened. Physical examination reveals calf hypertrophy and a positive Gower's sign. Laboratory tests show elevated serum creatine kinase levels. Genetic testing identifies a mutation in the dystrophin gene leading to the complete absence of dystrophin protein. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy"},{"letter":"B","text":"Becker Muscular Dystrophy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Emery-Dreifuss Muscular Dystrophy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facioscapulohumeral Muscular Dystrophy"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy","explanation":"The progressive muscle weakness, difficulty walking, and frequent falls starting at age 5 , along with calf hypertrophy, a positive Gower’s sign, and elevated creatine kinase , are classic findings of Duchenne muscular dystrophy (DMD) . Genetic testing confirms a dystrophin gene mutation leading to complete absence of dystrophin , which is a characteristic finding Becker Muscular Dystrophy (BMD) (Option B) also involves mutations in the dystrophin gene but usually presents later (adolescence or adulthood) with a slower progression and milder symptoms. Calf hypertrophy can be seen in both BMD and DMD, but the overall course and severity of BMD are milder. Emery-Dreifuss Muscular Dystrophy (EDMD) (Option C) often presents with early contractures, cardiac involvement (conduction defects, arrhythmias), and humeroperoneal muscle weakness. It does not typically involve calf hypertrophy and has a different pattern of muscle weakness compared to DMD. Facioscapulohumeral Muscular Dystrophy (FSHD) (Option D) primarily affects the muscles of the face, shoulders, and upper arms. It usually presents in adolescence or early adulthood with a distinct pattern of muscle weakness that spares the lower limbs until later stages.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 307-307; Solution Pages 322-323","sourcePage":307,"sourcePageEnd":307},{"id":"physiology-10-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-10","chapter":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topic":"Functional Anatomy of Skeletal Muscle","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 12-year-old boy presents to the OPD with complaints of difficulty climbing stairs, running, and lifting objects, and have noticed that his muscle weakness has gradually worsened. A muscle biopsy is performed, and immunohistochemical staining shows an absence of a key protein at the sarcolemma. Which of the following proteins is most likely absent, leading to this clinical presentation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dystrophin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Myosin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Actin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Troponin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Dystrophin is a key protein associated with the sarcolemma, the membrane surrounding muscle fibers. It plays a critical role in maintaining the structural integrity of muscle cells by linking the internal cytoskeleton to the extracellular matrix.The progressive muscle weakness, difficulty with climbing stairs and lifting objects, and absence of a key sarcolemmal protein on biopsy suggest Duchenne Muscular Dystrophy (DMD), which is caused by the absence of dystrophin . • Myosin (Option B) and Actin (Option C) are essential contractile proteins within the muscle fibers themselves, not specifically associated with the sarcolemma. • Troponin (Option D) is a regulatory protein complex involved in muscle contraction but is located within the muscle fiber, not at the sarcolemma.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 307-307; Solution Pages 323-323","sourcePage":307,"sourcePageEnd":307},{"id":"physiology-11-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Schematic diagram of sarcomere is given below. Red coloured structure is myosin with heads. Based on the length-tension relationship of muscle fibres, between which sarcomere length does the sarcomere allow optimal muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A to B"},{"letter":"B","text":"B to C"},{"letter":"C","text":"C to D"},{"letter":"D","text":"At any length"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) B to C","explanation":"When the sarcomere is at perfect length—about 2.2 micrometres—the actin and myosin overlap perfectly, allowing the muscle to contract optimally. The sarcomere maintains full tension as it shortens from 2.2 micrometres to 2 micrometres, i.e., from length B to C. • Sarcomeres are units in the muscles composed of actin and myosin filaments. • The amount of force or tension a muscle generates depends on the sarcomere's length. • Length A: Actin and myosin do not overlap; thus, muscle cannot generate tension. It occurs when the muscle is very stretched out. • Length B: Actin and myosin start to overlap, thus generating tension - maximum tension occurs when the overlap is just right - 2.2 micrometre • Length C: Sarcomere maintains full tension as it shortens from 2.2 micrometre to 2 micrometres • Length D: When the sarcomere length is shorter than 2 micrometres, the actin filaments overlap, thus reducing tension. • At about 1.65 micrometres, the sarcomere (Z disks) ends bump into the ends of the myosin filaments. • Further shortening crumples the myosin filaments, and tension drops to zero. • Thus, Length A to B: Muscle cannot generate any tension (Option A ruled out) Length B to C: Muscle starts generating tension, and the maximum is at 2.2 micrometres (Option B) Length C to D: Muscle can maintain full tension, and it allows optimum muscle contraction (Option C ruled out) • Length A to B: Muscle cannot generate any tension (Option A ruled out) • Length B to C: Muscle starts generating tension, and the maximum is at 2.2 micrometres (Option B) • Length C to D: Muscle can maintain full tension, and it allows optimum muscle contraction (Option C ruled out) • Length A to B: Muscle cannot generate any tension (Option A ruled out) • Length B to C: Muscle starts generating tension, and the maximum is at 2.2 micrometres (Option B) • Length C to D: Muscle can maintain full tension, and it allows optimum muscle contraction (Option C ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 325-325; Solution Pages 334-335","sourcePage":325,"sourcePageEnd":325},{"id":"physiology-11-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Effects of various agents on the membrane potential of intestinal smooth muscle is given below. Addition of which of the following substances produces the response labeled with B:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cold"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stretch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adrenaline"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Adrenaline","explanation":"The agent labeled as B in the effects on the membrane potential of intestinal smooth muscle is Adrenaline . The addition of Adrenaline to intestinal smooth muscle results in hyperpolarization of the membrane potential, decreased frequency of action potentials, and relaxation of the muscle. • Adrenaline (epinephrine) or norepinephrine acts as a neurotransmitter at noradrenergic nerve endings, inducing inhibitory potentials in smooth muscle. • As a result, the muscle becomes less active, and the overall contractile force is reduced. • In contrast, Acetylcholine (Option A ruled out) ( Labeled as A) has the opposite effect. When ACh is added to intestinal smooth muscle: The membrane potential becomes less negative (depolarization). This increase in depolarization leads to more frequent spikes and greater muscle activity, increasing tonic tension and rhythmic contractions. • The membrane potential becomes less negative (depolarization). • This increase in depolarization leads to more frequent spikes and greater muscle activity, increasing tonic tension and rhythmic contractions. • The membrane potential becomes less negative (depolarization). • This increase in depolarization leads to more frequent spikes and greater muscle activity, increasing tonic tension and rhythmic contractions. Cold (Option B): Typically reduces metabolic activity and may lead to decreased muscle contractions but does not have the specific hyperpolarizing effect of adrenaline. Stretch (Option C): Often activates stretch-sensitive channels, leading to depolarization and increased muscle activity, opposite to the effect of adrenaline.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 325-326; Solution Pages 335-336","sourcePage":325,"sourcePageEnd":326},{"id":"physiology-11-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Electrical activity of individual muscle cells in the guinea pig is recorded. Sinusoidal fluctuation of membrane potential with firing on the rising phase of each wave is identified from the recording. Identify the muscle type:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"cardiac muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"skeletal muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"smooth muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"none of the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Smooth muscle","explanation":"The recorded electrical activity shows sinusoidal fluctuations in membrane potential with firing on the rising phase, indicating Smooth Muscle . Left : Pacemaker-like activity with spikes firing at each peak. Right : Sinusoidal fluctuation of membrane potential with firing on the rising phase of each wave. Smooth Muscle Characteristics: • Instability of Membrane Potential: Lacks a true resting value, often fluctuating between -20 to -65 mV. • Divergent Electrical Activity: Exhibits slow sine wave-like fluctuations and spikes. Spikes may overshoot zero and last about 50 ms. • Continuous Contraction (Tonus): Smooth muscle can contract irregularly and independently of nerve supply. Cardiac Muscle (Option A): Exhibits rhythmic action potentials with a plateau phase, not sinusoidal fluctuations. Skeletal Muscle (Option B): Shows rapid, all-or-nothing action potentials without any sinusoidal pattern.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 326-327; Solution Pages 336-337","sourcePage":326,"sourcePageEnd":327},{"id":"physiology-11-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Graph below is showing simple muscle twitch in frog gastrocnemius muscle. Calculate tetanising frequency of this muscle.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"25"},{"letter":"B","text":"20"},{"letter":"C","text":"10"},{"letter":"D","text":"5"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 25Hz","explanation":"The Tetanising frequency of this muscle is 25Hz. • Each block corresponds to 10ms. • Therefore contraction period = 40ms = 40/1000 seconds Tetanising frequency = 1/ Contraction period Tetanising frequency = 1000/40 = 25Hz","sourceRef":"Question Pages 327-327; Solution Pages 337-338","sourcePage":327,"sourcePageEnd":327},{"id":"physiology-11-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following statements about the mechanism of smooth muscle contraction is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcium ions primarily come from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"},{"letter":"B","text":"The latent period before contraction is similar to that of skeletal muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcium ions diffuse into the cell from the extracellular fluid during contraction"},{"letter":"D","text":"The concentration of calcium ions is higher inside the smooth muscle cell"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Calcium ions diffuse into the cell from the extracellular fluid during contraction","explanation":"When stimulated by hormones, neurotransmitters, or stretch, calcium channels in smooth muscle open, allowing calcium ions to rapidly diffuse from the extracellular fluid into the cell and initiate contraction. • Smooth muscle can maintain contraction with less energy expenditure than skeletal muscle (Latch-bridge mechanism). • Relaxation: Occurs when Ca²■ is pumped out of the cell or back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, and myosin is dephosphorylated. Unlike skeletal muscle, the sarcoplasmic reticulum in smooth muscle is poorly developed and is not the primary source of calcium for contraction. (Option A) The latent period before smooth muscle contraction (200-300 milliseconds) is significantly longer than that of skeletal muscle, but it is not similar. (Option B) The concentration of calcium ions is much higher in the extracellular fluid (>10^-3 molar) compared to inside the smooth muscle cell (<10^-7 molar). (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 327-327; Solution Pages 338-339","sourcePage":327,"sourcePageEnd":327},{"id":"physiology-11-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a primary source/mechanism of calcium release for smooth muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extracellular fluid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sarcoplasmic reticulum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mitochondria"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcium-induced calcium release from intracellular stores"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Mitochondria","explanation":"While mitochondria play a role in cellular calcium homeostasis, they are not considered a primary source of calcium for smooth muscle contraction. Their main function is energy production, and although they can sequester calcium, they are not a significant source of the rapid calcium increase needed for contraction. The primary sources of calcium for smooth muscle contraction are: • Extracellular fluid: Calcium enters the cell through voltage-gated calcium channels in the cell membrane (Option A ruled out) • Sarcoplasmic reticulum: This intracellular organelle stores and releases calcium (Option B ruled out) • Calcium-induced calcium release amplifies the calcium signal by triggering further release from intracellular stores (Option D ruled out) • Voltage-gated calcium channels allow calcium influx from the extracellular space when the cell membrane is depolarised. • Most calcium ions for smooth muscle contraction come from the extracellular fluid, not internal stores.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 328-328; Solution Pages 339-340","sourcePage":328,"sourcePageEnd":328},{"id":"physiology-11-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following is a characteristic of multi-unit smooth muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fibers arranged in sheets or bundles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fibers contract as a single unit"},{"letter":"C","text":"Discrete, separate fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Connected by gap junctions"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Discrete, separate fibers","explanation":"Multi-unit smooth muscle is characterised by discrete, separate fibres. This structure allows for more precise control, with fibres able to contract independently. Options A, B and D are characteristics of unitary (single-unit) smooth muscle. There are 2 types of smooth muscles: Characteristic Multi-Unit Smooth Muscle Unitary (Single-Unit) Smooth Muscle Structure Discrete, separate fibres. Fibers arranged in sheets or (Option C) bundles (Option A ruled out) Innervation Often innervated by single Less dependent on nerve signals. nerve-ending. Contraction Fibers contract independently. Fibers contract as a single unit. (Option B ruled out) Cell Connecti Covered by a basement membrane, Connected by gap junctions (Option ons insulated D ruled out) Control Mainly by nerve signals. Mainly by non-nervous stimuli. Signal Propa Limited between fibres. Action potentials can spread gation between fibres. Examples Ciliary muscle, iris muscle, and The gastrointestinal tract, bile ducts, piloerector muscles. ureters, uterus, and many blood vessels. Also Known Syncytial smooth muscle, visceral As smooth muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 328-328; Solution Pages 340-341","sourcePage":328,"sourcePageEnd":328},{"id":"physiology-11-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A researcher investigating the electrical activity of smooth muscle cells comes across a specific group of cells in GIT similar to the cells in the S.A node of the heart that generates rhythmic activity without any stimulus. Which of the following will likely generate the above-mentioned activity in GIT cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spike potential"},{"letter":"B","text":"Action potential with plateau"},{"letter":"C","text":"Slow wave rhythm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Innervation of cells by ANS"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Slow wave rhythm","explanation":"The rhythmic contractile activity seen in a specific group of GIT cells is a slow wave rhythm generated by pacemaker cells. • Pacemaker cells of GIT are Interstitial cells of Cajal, similar to heart pacemaker cells. • Slow wave rhythms are due to an excitatory process, and these are not action potentials. • The exact cause of slow wave rhythms is not known, but two theories are suggested: • When slow waves are strong enough, they generate action potentials, which cause rhythmical contraction, also known as pacemaker waves. Spike Potential (Option A): Generated due to the action of hormones or neurotransmitters, these potentials are seen in the gastrointestinal tract. Action potential with plateau (Option B): Generated in single unit smooth muscle cells such as the uterus and ureter. This potential causes prolonged contraction. Innervation of cells by ANS (Option D): This is seen in multiunit smooth muscle cells such as those of the ciliary, iris, and piloerector muscles.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 328-328; Solution Pages 341-342","sourcePage":328,"sourcePageEnd":328},{"id":"physiology-11-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following combinations of features are characteristic of smooth muscle tissue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Z-line and troponin present"},{"letter":"B","text":"Caveolae and T-Tubule present"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calmodulin and titin present"},{"letter":"D","text":"Voltage-gated calcium channels and calmodulin present"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Voltage-gated calcium channels and calmodulin present","explanation":"Voltage-gated calcium channels and calmodulin are characteristics of smooth muscles, whereas Z-line, troponin and titin are features of Skeletal muscle. Characteristic features of Mmooth muscles and Skeletal muscles: Smooth muscles Skeletal muscles Spindle-shaped, single nucleated cells. Multiple nucleated cells. Neuromuscular junction is not present. Proper neuromuscular junction is present. Caveolae is present. (Option B ruled out) T-tubule is present. (Option B ruled out) Predominantly, calcium channels are present. Predominantly, sodium channels are present. Actin and myosin are not properly organised. Actin and myosin are properly organised. A dense body is present. The Z- line is present. (Option A ruled out) Calmodulin is present, and Titin is absent. (Option C Troponin (Option A ruled out) and Titin (Option C ruled ruled out) out) are present.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 328-329; Solution Pages 342-343","sourcePage":328,"sourcePageEnd":329},{"id":"physiology-11-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 33-year-old competitive cyclist is preparing himself for a training program. What type of muscle fibres are important to improve his cycling performance during various stages of his race","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Only type I"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only type II"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both type I and type II"},{"letter":"D","text":"Smooth muscles and type II"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Both type I and type II","explanation":"Competitive cycling requires both endurance and high-intensity effort, so both type I and type II fibres are required. Type I fibres provide prolonged strength of contraction over minutes to hours, and type II fibers provide maximal power for seconds to minutes. Type of fibres Slow fibres (Type I) Fast fibres (Type II) Size of fibres Slow fibres are small, providing Fast fibres are large and provide and their effect endurance and prolonged great strength of contraction for a on contraction contraction strength for longer very short period. (Option B, D ruled periods. (Option A ruled out) out) Innervation Innervated by small nerve fibres. By large nerve fibres. Sarcoplasmic Less extensive sarcoplasmic Extensive sarcoplasmic reticulum reticulum reticulum, slower calcium ion rapidly releases calcium ions release. Blood Supply An extensive blood vessel system Less extensive blood supply. supplies extra oxygen. Metabolic More number of mitochondria Large amounts of glycolytic pathway support a high level of oxidative enzymes rapidly release energy metabolism. through glycolysis. Myoglobin Present in large amounts, hence Less amounts of myoglobin, hence called red muscle. called white muscle. Smooth muscles and type II: Cycling requires activity in the lower leg muscles where smooth muscles are absent. (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 329-329; Solution Pages 343-344","sourcePage":329,"sourcePageEnd":329},{"id":"physiology-11-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 23-year-old pianist comes to the clinic complaining of weakness in her fingers. She says she is unable to play a challenging piece in a song. Which of the following is the innervation ratio of the group of muscles affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1:1000"},{"letter":"B","text":"1:2000"},{"letter":"C","text":"1:600"},{"letter":"D","text":"1:3"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 1:3","explanation":"This patient cannot play the piano, which suggests that the small muscles of the hands are affected. • The innervation ratio refers to the number of muscle fibres controlled by a single motor neuron. • The innervation ratio of small-sized motor units is 1:3 to 1:6. Difference between Small size motor unit vs Large size motor unit: Aspects Small-sized motor unit Large-sized motor unit Require Fine movements. Gross movements. d for Present Extraocular muscles (EOM) and the Lower limb muscles, particularly the in small muscles of our fingers. leg muscles, i.e., gastrocnemius and back muscles. Innervati 1:3 to 6, i.e. one single axon 1:600 to 2000, i.e., one single axon on ratio supplies 3-6 skeletal muscle fibres. supplies 600 - 2000 skeletal muscle (Option D) fibres. (Option A, B, C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 329-329; Solution Pages 344-344","sourcePage":329,"sourcePageEnd":329},{"id":"physiology-11-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A patient has been experiencing occasional involuntary movement of his facial muscles for the past three days. On examination of his face, minute fasciculation of his right side facial muscles was seen. Which of the following statements regarding muscle fasciculation is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Can occur due to late neuronal damage or irritation."},{"letter":"B","text":"Not visible to the naked eye and can only be detected by an EMG."},{"letter":"C","text":"Always indicative of neuromuscular disease."},{"letter":"D","text":"It can be irregular and brief or persistent."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) It can be irregular and brief or persistent","explanation":"Muscle fasciculation is spontaneous contractions of muscle fibres that are visible to the naked eye and can be irregular, brief, or persistent. Features Fasciculation Fibrillation Definition Spontaneous muscle contraction of Involuntary contraction of individual a group of muscle fibres. muscle fibres. Visibility Visible to the naked eye. Not visible to the naked eye Detected on EMG. (Option B) Cause Irritation or spontaneous activation Damage to muscle fibers, can occur of motor neurons. in late neuronal damage (Option A) Significance It is usually benign but can indicate Indicative of neuromuscular motor neuron disorders. disease. (Option C) Duration and Irregular and brief or persistent. Regular with a consistent pattern on pattern (Option D) EMG. Sensory functi Usually intact. Compromised if neuropathy is on present. EMG: Electromyography","sourceRef":"Question Pages 329-330; Solution Pages 345-345","sourcePage":329,"sourcePageEnd":330},{"id":"physiology-11-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with generalised muscle weakness. During an electromyography (EMG) study, you observe motor unit potentials (MUPs) with consistent amplitude, duration, and regular shape. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Early-stage amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Demyelinating polyneuropathy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Normal muscle function"},{"letter":"D","text":"Myopathy with fibre degeneration"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Normal muscle function","explanation":"EMG with consistent amplitude, duration, and regular shape MUPs is characteristic of normal muscle function despite muscle weakness, which in this case is not due to any primary muscle pathology. Motor unit potentials are the electrical activity recorded from a motor unit during muscle contraction. • It includes the contribution of all muscle fibres within a single motor unit. • Electromyography (EMG) is used to record Motor Unit Potentials(MUP). • The waveform on the EMG can help detect neuromuscular diseases. Duration of wave - time taken for the entire motor unit to fire Amplitude of wave - reflects the number and size of muscle fibres within the motor unit. Shape of wave - depends on the health of the motor unit. • Duration of wave - time taken for the entire motor unit to fire • Amplitude of wave - reflects the number and size of muscle fibres within the motor unit. • Shape of wave - depends on the health of the motor unit. • Normal wave = consistent amplitude, duration and regular shape. • Duration of wave - time taken for the entire motor unit to fire • Amplitude of wave - reflects the number and size of muscle fibres within the motor unit. • Shape of wave - depends on the health of the motor unit. In pathological conditions, such as early-stage amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) (Option A) , demyelinating polyneuropathy (Option B) , or myopathy with fibre degeneration (Option D) , EMG studies typically show abnormalities in the MUPs. • Polyphasic pattern and longer duration: Neurogenic abnormalities. • Polyphasic pattern and shorter duration: Myopathic abnormalities.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 330-330; Solution Pages 345-346","sourcePage":330,"sourcePageEnd":330},{"id":"physiology-11-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Based on the tetanisation of muscle fibres, which of the following statements is true regarding the tetanisation frequency?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is the frequency at which the muscle fibers show visible twitching"},{"letter":"B","text":"At tetanising frequency, successive contractions fuse into a smooth, continuous contraction."},{"letter":"C","text":"If the frequency exceeds the tetanising frequency, the strength of contraction increases."},{"letter":"D","text":"At this frequency, there are no calcium ions in the muscle sarcoplasm."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) At tetanising frequency, successive contractions fuse into a smooth, continuous contraction.","explanation":"At tetanising frequency, successive contractions fuse into a smooth, continuous contraction. • At low-frequency stimulation, individual contractions occur one after another, but as the frequency increases, new contractions begin before the previous one ends; thus, the second contraction partially adds to the first. • The total strength of contraction increases progressively. • At a critical tetanising frequency, the contraction fuses together, and the muscle contraction appears smooth and continuous - Tetanization. • Further, an increase in the frequency of stimulation does not increase contractile force. (Option C) • This occurs when sufficient calcium ions are maintained in the muscle sarcoplasm even between two action potentials (AP) (Option D) • Thus, a full contractile state is sustained without any relaxation between AP. Visible twitching occurs at lower frequencies when each stimulus produces a separate contraction. This is known as unfused tetanus. (Option A)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 330-330; Solution Pages 346-347","sourcePage":330,"sourcePageEnd":330},{"id":"physiology-11-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":15,"question":"During an experimental study, a researcher stimulates a skeletal muscle with increasing frequencies of electrical impulses. The tension recordings show successive contractions that do not fully relax between stimuli. This phenomenon is best described as?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Staircase phenomenon"},{"letter":"B","text":"Incomplete tetanus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Treppe phenomenon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Complete tetanus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Incomplete tetanus","explanation":"Incomplete tetanus is successful stimulation at increasing frequencies, resulting in sustained but oscillating tension with incomplete relaxation between contractions. Incomplete Tetanus Complete Tetanus Muscles are stimulated at an increased frequency, and Muscles are stimulated at a very high frequency, so each contraction begins before the previous one fully each new stimulus occurs before it can relax completely. relaxes. Sustained but oscillating tension output where the Sustained contraction where the muscle remains in a muscle shows partial relaxation between contractions. state of maximal tension without any relaxation. The tension curve shows successive peaks with The tension curve shows a sustained plateau with no incomplete relaxation periods in between, leading to a dips or relaxation periods, indicating continuous wave-like pattern. maximum contraction. Treppe / Staircase phenomenon refers to an incremental increase in muscle contraction height with repeated stimulations due to residual calcium accumulation. (Option A and C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 330-331; Solution Pages 347-348","sourcePage":330,"sourcePageEnd":331},{"id":"physiology-11-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":16,"question":"During a physiological study, a researcher observes incremental increases in muscle contraction height with repeated stimulations of a skeletal muscle. What is this phenomenon, also known as the Treppe phenomenon, primarily attributed to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased availability of ATP in the muscle fibres."},{"letter":"B","text":"Faster pumping of calcium ions into the sarcoplasmic reticulum."},{"letter":"C","text":"Enhanced sensitivity of the muscle fibres to acetylcholine."},{"letter":"D","text":"Accumulation of residual calcium ions in the cytoplasm"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Accumulation of residual calcium ions in the cytoplasm","explanation":"The Treppe phenomenon, or staircase phenomenon, occurs when the muscle contraction height gradually increases with repeated stimulations after reaching baseline. • This is primarily attributed to the accumulation of residual calcium ions in the cytoplasm that were released during the previous stimulation, as pumping Calcium ions back by the SERCA pump requires some time. • The height of the second contraction is more than that of the first contraction, known as the Beneficial effect. • After several contractions, there will be no further increase in height. Increased availability of ATP in the muscle fibres (Option A): • ATP is used in the cross-bridge cycling and relaxation processes, not in accumulating calcium ions. Faster pumping of calcium ions into the sarcoplasmic reticulum (Option B): • The SERCA pump pumps calcium ions back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, depleting calcium in the cytoplasm and aiding in Relaxation. Enhanced sensitivity of the muscle fibres to acetylcholine (Option C): • Acetylcholine initiates muscle contraction by binding to receptors on the muscle cell membrane. • While receptor sensitivity can affect muscle response, it does not explain the treppe phenomenon's mechanism of increase in the height of contraction..","sourceRef":"Question Pages 331-331; Solution Pages 348-349","sourcePage":331,"sourcePageEnd":331},{"id":"physiology-11-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":17,"question":"During a clinical examination, a neurologist stimulates the motor nerve supplying a patient's slow muscle fibre. Graphical recording of muscle tension shows distinct phases. Which of the following correctly identifies the sequence of phases and their durations?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Latency Period (10 ms), Contraction Period (40 ms), Relaxation Period (50 ms)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Latency Period (20 ms), Peak Contraction (60 ms), Recovery Period (30 ms)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Activation Stage (30 ms), Maximum Contraction (70 ms), Relaxation Interval (40 ms)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Initiation Phase (40 ms), Contraction Peak (50 ms), Relaxation Phase (10 ms)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Latency Period (10 ms), Contraction Period (40 ms), Relaxation Period (50 ms)","explanation":"Slow muscle fibres exhibit a characteristic contraction pattern with a relatively longer contraction and relaxation duration than fast muscle fibres. • A Simple Muscle Twitch occurs when a single action potential causes a brief contraction followed by relaxation. • After nerve stimulation, the muscle fibres go through the following phases: • Latent Period (LP) : Period before the onset of muscle contraction – 10 milliseconds • Contraction Period (CP): Duration from the onset to the peak of contraction – 40 ms • Relaxation Period (RP): Period that follows peak contraction period – 50 ms • These phases collectively make up a total duration of around 100 milliseconds. PS: Point of stimulus; PC: Point of onset of Contraction; PR: Point of end of Relaxation; PMC : Peak muscle tension","sourceRef":"Question Pages 331-331; Solution Pages 349-349","sourcePage":331,"sourcePageEnd":331},{"id":"physiology-11-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":18,"question":"During a physiology lab, students are studying the phases of a simple muscle twitch. They observe a brief delay between the application of a stimulus and the beginning of muscle contraction. What is this initial delay called?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Refractory period"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contraction period"},{"letter":"C","text":"Relaxation period"},{"letter":"D","text":"Latency period"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Latency period","explanation":"The latency period is the brief delay between the application of the stimulus and the onset of muscle contraction, during which the action potential travels along the muscle fibre and calcium ions are released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum. • Whenever the muscle contracts, it will produce some force known as increased tension generation by the muscle. • In skeletal muscle, the Simple Muscle Twitch (SMT) is a contraction followed by a relaxation. Latency Period (LP): The interval between the initial stimulation and the beginning of muscle contraction. Contraction Period (CP) (Option B) : The time from the onset of contraction to the peak, during which force generation increases. Relaxation Period (RP) (Option C) : The phase during which the muscle relaxes and force/tension decreases. • Latency Period (LP): The interval between the initial stimulation and the beginning of muscle contraction. • Contraction Period (CP) (Option B) : The time from the onset of contraction to the peak, during which force generation increases. • Relaxation Period (RP) (Option C) : The phase during which the muscle relaxes and force/tension decreases. • Latency Period (LP): The interval between the initial stimulation and the beginning of muscle contraction. • Contraction Period (CP) (Option B) : The time from the onset of contraction to the peak, during which force generation increases. • Relaxation Period (RP) (Option C) : The phase during which the muscle relaxes and force/tension decreases. Refractory period(Option A): The refractory period is the time following an action potential during which a muscle fibre cannot be re-excited, ensuring that action potentials are separated. This does not represent the initial latency period in a simple muscle twitch.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 331-332; Solution Pages 350-351","sourcePage":331,"sourcePageEnd":332},{"id":"physiology-11-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following best describes the process of tetanisation in muscle physiology?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Individual twitch contractions at low frequency."},{"letter":"B","text":"Overlapping muscle contractions increases total strength with higher frequency."},{"letter":"C","text":"Relaxation between action potentials."},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease in contraction strength with higher frequency."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Overlapping muscle contractions that increase total strength with higher frequency","explanation":"As the frequency of stimulation increases, contractions overlap, summing to increase total force, leading to smooth, continuous contractions known as tetanisation. Frequency Summation and Tetanization: • Low-frequency stimulation produces individual twitch contractions. (Option A) • As frequency increases, contractions overlap, enhancing total strength. • Contractions fuse into a smooth, continuous contraction called tetanisation at a critical frequency. • Further frequency increases maintain maximum contraction strength without additional force increase. (Option D ) • Tetany occurs because calcium ions remain in the muscle sarcoplasm between action potentials, sustaining full contraction without relaxation. Individual twitch contractions at low frequency (Option A) : It describes the initial phase before frequency summation and tetanisation. Relaxation between action potentials (Option C) During tetanisation, there is no relaxation between action potentials, resulting in sustained contraction. Maximum contraction strength with no further increase despite higher frequency (Option D): This describes the plateau after tetanisation is achieved, not the process itself.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 332-332; Solution Pages 351-352","sourcePage":332,"sourcePageEnd":332},{"id":"physiology-11-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-11","chapter":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topic":"Motor unit & Smooth muscle","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following best describes the order in which the motor units are recruited?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Large motor units are excited first"},{"letter":"B","text":"Small motor units are relaxed first"},{"letter":"C","text":"Large motor units are recruited first"},{"letter":"D","text":"Small motor units are excited first"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Small motor units are excited first","explanation":"During motor unit recruitment, the small motor units are excited first, followed by the larger motor units, which is known as the size principle. Recruitment: • The recruitment of motor units follows the size principle, where smaller motor units with fewer muscle fibres and lower force thresholds are activated first. • As the load increases, progressively larger motor units are recruited to generate the necessary force. • This allows for smooth and precise control of muscle contractions, starting with fine, low-force movements and progressing to stronger contractions as needed. Derecruitment: • When the task is completed, the motor units are relaxed, known as the Derecruitment of a motor unit. • The largest motor unit is derecruited first, followed by the smallest. Large motor units are excited first (Option A): Large motor units are recruited later in the process, as they are responsible for generating more force but lack precision. Small motor units are relaxed first (Option B): Motor units are not relaxed during the process of recruitment. Instead, they are sequentially activated. Large motor units are relaxed first (Option C): While large motor units might be derecruited first when the force demand decreases, the process of initial recruitment for increasing loads starts with smaller units.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 332-332; Solution Pages 352-353","sourcePage":332,"sourcePageEnd":332},{"id":"physiology-12-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following changes occurs during muscle contraction while exercising?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"M length increase"},{"letter":"B","text":"A length decrease"},{"letter":"C","text":"I length decrease"},{"letter":"D","text":"M length increase and I decrease"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) I length decrease Explanation: During muscle contraction , the sarcomere shortens, leading to specific changes in the myofilament structure : • A-band: The length of the A-band remains the same during muscle contraction because it corresponds to the length of the myosin filaments, which do not change. • H-band: The H-band decreases or disappears as the actin filaments slide over the myosin filaments during contraction, reducing the region where only myosin is present. • I-band: The length of the I-band decreases as the sarcomere shortens and the actin filaments overlap more with the myosin filaments. • M-line: The M-line becomes more prominent as it marks the central point of the sarcomere where the thick filaments are anchored. M length increase (Option A) is incorrect because the M line does not change in length during contraction. A length decrease (Option B) is incorrect because the A band remains constant (myosin filament length does not change). M length increase and I decrease (Option D) is incorrect because while I band decreases , the M line does not change in length .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 354-354; Solution Pages 367-368","sourcePage":354,"sourcePageEnd":354},{"id":"physiology-12-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Sequence the events in neuromuscular action potential conduction: Sodium channels open in the end plate Calcium enters at the nerve end plate Release of acetylcholine","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 → 2 → 3"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 → 3 → 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 → 2 → 1"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 → 3 → 1"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - 2 → 3 → 1: 2. Calcium enters at the nerve end plate : Calcium ions enter the nerve terminal when an action potential reaches the nerve end. This influx of calcium is crucial for the subsequent release of neurotransmitters. 3. Release of acetylcholine : The entry of calcium ions into the nerve terminal triggers the release of acetylcholine (ACh) from vesicles into the synaptic cleft. 1. Sodium channels open in the end plate : The binding of acetylcholine to receptors on the muscle end plate causes sodium channels to open, leading to an influx of sodium ions into the muscle cell and generating an action potential in the muscle. Incorrect Options: • Option A, B and C are incorrect as given above","sourceRef":"Question Pages 354-354; Solution Pages 368-368","sourcePage":354,"sourcePageEnd":354},{"id":"physiology-12-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":3,"question":"How does the muscle spindle detect muscle length during muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stretch receptor on the tendon"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stretch receptor in the muscle belly"},{"letter":"C","text":"Golgi tendon organ"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mechanoreceptor in the joint capsule"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Stretch receptor in the muscle belly: • The muscle spindle is a specialized sensory receptor located within the muscle belly. It detects changes in muscle length (stretch) and velocity to asses the joint's position. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D do not detect muscle length during muscle contraction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 354-354; Solution Pages 369-369","sourcePage":354,"sourcePageEnd":354},{"id":"physiology-12-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following components is absent in smooth muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Actin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Myosin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tropomyosin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Troponin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Troponin: • Troponin is absent in smooth muscle. In striated muscles, troponin is a calcium-binding protein that regulates muscle contraction. In smooth muscle, this function is performed by calmodulin, another calcium-binding protein. Calmodulin binds calcium and activates myosin light-chain kinase, which then phosphorylates myosin and facilitates contraction. Incorrect Options: Option A and B - Actin & Myosin: Both actin and myosin are present in smooth muscle. Unlike striated muscle, smooth muscle fibers do not have organized sarcomeres. Instead, the actin and myosin filaments are arranged in an oblique pattern. Actin filaments radiate from dense bodies, which function similarly to Z-lines in striated muscle. Option C - Tropomyosin: Tropomyosin is present in smooth muscle and plays a role in regulating the interaction between actin and myosin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 355-355; Solution Pages 369-369","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"physiology-12-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which protein does calcium bind to during smooth muscle contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calmodulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Troponin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Topoisomerase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Actin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Calmodulin: • In smooth muscle contraction, calcium binds to calmodulin, which then activates myosin light-chain kinase (MLCK). This activation is crucial for muscle contraction. Incorrect Options: Option B - Troponin: Troponin is a calcium-binding protein found in skeletal muscle, not smooth muscle. In skeletal muscle, calcium binds to troponin to initiate contraction. Option C - Topoisomerase: Topoisomerase is an enzyme that helps manage DNA supercoiling during replication and transcription. It does not interact with calcium during muscle contraction. Option D - Actin: Actin is a filamentous protein that forms the backbone of the thin filaments in muscle fibers. While actin interacts with myosin for muscle contraction, it does not bind directly with c alcium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 355-355; Solution Pages 369-370","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"physiology-12-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A man presents to the hospital with complaints of drooping eyelids (ptosis) and increasing eye irritation. He notes that his symptoms worsen as the day goes on, particularly during outdoor activities. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lambert-Eaton syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Myasthenia gravis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Botulism toxicity"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tetanus toxicity"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Myasthenia Gravis: • Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder that leads to weakness of the voluntary muscles, particularly affecting the eyes and face. The condition often worsens with activity and improves with rest. Symptoms commonly include ptosis, diplopia (double vision), and generalized muscle weakness. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lambert-Eaton Syndrome: Lambert-Eaton syndrome is an autoimmune disorder affecting the neuromuscular junction, characterized by muscle weakness that improves with activity. It typically presents with weakness in the proximal muscles and autonomic symptoms, rather than localized ptosis and worsening symptoms with exertion. Option C - Botulism Toxicity: Botulism is caused by botulinum toxin, which inhibits acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction. It results in muscle weakness, but symptoms typically include descending paralysis and can affect multiple muscle groups. While ptosis may occur, botulism generally presents with more widespread weakness and not specifically with worsening symptoms throughout the day. Option D - Tetanus Toxicity: Tetanus is caused by the toxin of Clostridium tetani, leading to severe muscle spasms and rigidity. Symptoms often include lockjaw, muscle stiffness, and generalized spasms, rather than ptosis or symptoms that worsen with the day.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 355-355; Solution Pages 370-370","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"physiology-12-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following is not a calcium-binding protein?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calbindin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Calmodulin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Troponin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Clathrin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Clathrin: • Clathrin is not a calcium-binding protein. It is a structural protein that plays a crucial role in the formation of coated vesicles during endocytosis, a process by which cells internalize molecules from the extracellular environment. Clathrin helps shape the vesicles by assembling them into a lattice-like structure around the invaginated membrane. Incorrect Options: Option A - Calbindin: Calbindin is a calcium-binding protein that regulates calcium homeostasis in cells. It is found in various tissues, including the brain, kidney, and intestines, and helps buffer and transport calcium ions. Option B - Calmodulin: Calmodulin is a highly conserved calcium-binding protein found in The smooth muscle cell. Option C - Troponin: Troponin is a complex of three subunits (troponin C, troponin I, and troponin T) found in skeletal muscle cells and is a calcium binding protein.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 355-356; Solution Pages 371-371","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"physiology-12-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the primary source of energy used by skeletal muscles to replenish ATP during a 100-meter dash?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Phosphocreatine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aerobic glycolysis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oxidation of Fatty acids"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oxidation of triglycerides"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Phosphocreatine: • During rapid strenuous activity like 100-meter dash the demand of ATP is covered by anaerobic glycolysis due to reduced oxygen availability. • Phosphocreatine (anaerobic glycolysis) is a high-energy phosphate compound that is stored in skeletal muscles. During high-intensity activities like sprinting, it can rapidly donate a phosphate group to ADP (adenosine diphosphate), regenerating ATP (adenosine triphosphate). This provides a quick source of energy without the need for oxygen or extensive metabolic pathways. Incorrect Options: Option B - Aerobic glycolysis: Aerobic glycolysis refers to the breakdown of glucose in the presence of oxygen to produce ATP. This process occurs in the cytoplasm of cells and involves a series of enzymatic reactions. It is an important energy pathway during prolonged endurance activities when oxygen is readily available. Option C and D: Fatty acids and tryglycerides are the primary fuel source for aerobic metabolism. During low-intensity and long-duration activities, such as distance running, the body relies on the oxidation of fatty acids to generate ATP.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 356-356; Solution Pages 371-372","sourcePage":356,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"physiology-12-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which mechanism is responsible for the detachment of the myosin head from actin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Binding of ATP"},{"letter":"B","text":"Entry of calcium into the sarcoplasmic reticulum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Change in troponin C configuration"},{"letter":"D","text":"Release of ADP and Pi"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Binding of ATP: • The detachment of the myosin head from actin occurs when ATP (adenosine triphosphate) binds to the myosin head. This binding causes a conformational change in the myosin molecule, leading to the release of myosin from actin. Incorrect Options: Option B - Entry of calcium into sarcoplasmic reticulum: The entry of calcium play a crucial role in initiating muscle contraction by binding to troponin, which leads to the exposure of myosin-binding sites on actin. Option C - Change in troponin C configuration: This process is responsible for the attachment of the actin-myosin during muscle contraction by uncoiling the troponin I from the tropomysoin. Option D - Release of ADP and Pi: The release of ADP (adenosine diphosphate) and inorganic phosphate (Pi) occurs after the myosin head has formed a cross-bridge with actin and undergone a power stroke.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 356-356; Solution Pages 372-372","sourcePage":356,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"physiology-12-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":10,"question":"What is the primary function of acetylcholine in the nervous system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inducing muscle contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inhibiting neurotransmission"},{"letter":"C","text":"Promoting bone growth"},{"letter":"D","text":"Regulating blood sugar levels"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option A - Inducing muscle contraction: • Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter that plays a crucial role in the nervous system, particularly in the transmission of signals between nerve cells and in the communication between nerves and muscles. One of its primary functions is to stimulate muscle contraction by transmitting signals across the neuromuscular junction. Upon release from nerve endings, acetylcholine binds to receptors on muscle cells, leading to the generation of an action potential and ultimately causing muscle contraction. • Therefore, option (a) \"Inducing muscle contraction\" is the correct choice. Incorrect Options: Option B - Inhibiting neurotransmission: Acetylcholine generally acts as an excitatory neurotransmitter in the nervous system, promoting the transmission of nerve impulses rather than inhibiting it. Option C - Promoting bone growth: While acetylcholine receptors are present in bone cells, there is no evidence to suggest that acetylcholine plays a significant role in promoting bone growth. Option D - Regulating blood sugar levels: Acetylcholine does not directly regulate blood sugar levels. Insulin and glucagon, produced by the pancreas, are the primary hormones involved in blood sugar regulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 356-356; Solution Pages 372-373","sourcePage":356,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"physiology-12-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Dynein protein is an example of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Transport protein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cell membrane protein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cell organelle adherent protein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intracellular junctional protein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Transport protein: • Dynein is a motor protein that facilitates retrograde transport within cells. It moves towards the minus end of microtubules, which is typically oriented towards the center of the cell body of the nerve. • By utilizing the energy derived from ATP hydrolysis, dynein generates mechanical force, allowing it to transport various cellular cargoes, including organelles, vesicles, and protein complexes, along microtubule tracks within the cytoplasm. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect: Refer to the explanation of Option A.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 357-357; Solution Pages 373-373","sourcePage":357,"sourcePageEnd":357},{"id":"physiology-12-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":12,"question":"What nerve fibers are responsible for the transmission of proprioception?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A alpha"},{"letter":"B","text":"A beta"},{"letter":"C","text":"A gamma"},{"letter":"D","text":"A delta"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - A alpha: • A alpha fibers are responsible for proprioception in the body. Incorrect Options: Option B - A beta: A beta fibers are responsible for pressure and touch. Option C - A gamma: A gamma fibers are efferents to muscle spindles. Option D - A delta: A delta fibers are responsible for pain and cold temperatures.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 357-357; Solution Pages 374-374","sourcePage":357,"sourcePageEnd":357},{"id":"physiology-12-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following A fiber subtypes is responsible for motor innervation of the ends of the muscle spindle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alpha"},{"letter":"B","text":"Beta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gamma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Delta"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C- Gamma: A Fiber sub-type Numerical Classification (By Lloyd Functions and Hunt) Alpha Ia Proprioception and somatic motor Ib Beta II Touch and pressure Gamma Efferent to muscle spindle Delta III Pain (fast) and temperature (cold) Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 357-357; Solution Pages 374-374","sourcePage":357,"sourcePageEnd":357},{"id":"physiology-12-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":14,"question":"The diagram displays nerve cell physiology. The event marked by 3 to 4 is due to _____?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na + entering the cell"},{"letter":"B","text":"Na + exiting the cell."},{"letter":"C","text":"K + exiting the cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"K + entering the cell"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - K+ exiting the cell: Skeletal muscle action potential: • 1 to 2: Stimulation of neurons leads to the generation of local potential towards the threshold voltage. • 2 to 3: It is followed by a rapid increase in the membrane potential due to influx of the Na + by opening of the fast type of voltage gated channel marked by depolarization phase. • 3 to 4: Next phase of the action potential is repolarization characterized by closing of the voltage gated Na + channel and opening of the slow type of voltage gated K + channel leading to efflux of the K + . • 4: The last phase is hyperpolarization, characterized by the presence of some already open K + channels and K + leak channels leading to continuous efflux of K+. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 357-358; Solution Pages 374-375","sourcePage":357,"sourcePageEnd":358},{"id":"physiology-12-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":15,"question":"What is the term used to describe the consistent amplitude of action potential when the stimulus is delivered at an intensity above the threshold?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"All or none law"},{"letter":"B","text":"Local potential"},{"letter":"C","text":"Absolute refractory period"},{"letter":"D","text":"Relative refractory period"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - All or none law: • The all-or-none law states that once a nerve or muscle cell is stimulated beyond a certain threshold, it will generate an action potential of consistent magnitude. • If the stimulus is strong enough, the resulting action potential will have a fixed amplitude and will propagate without loss of intensity. Below the threshold, no action potential occurs; above it, the response is maximal. Incorrect Options: Option B - Local potential: Local potential refers to the passive changes in membrane potential that occur during the propagation of electrical signals along a neuron. Option C - Absolute refractory period: Absolute refractory period refers to a brief period after an action potential when the neuron is unresponsive to further stimulation. During this period, another action potential cannot be generated, regardless of the strength of the stimulus. Option D - Relative refractory period: Relative refractory period refers to a period following the absolute refractory period during which a neuron can be stimulated to generate another action potential, but only with a stronger stimulus than the previous stimulus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 358-358; Solution Pages 375-375","sourcePage":358,"sourcePageEnd":358},{"id":"physiology-12-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Voltage gated calcium channel present on T-tubule is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SERCA pump"},{"letter":"B","text":"Troponin-myosin complex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dihydropyridine receptor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ryanodine receptor"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Dihydropyridine receptor: • The dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR), also called the L-type calcium channel, is found in the T-tubule membrane near the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) in muscle cells. • When an action potential travels along the T-tubule, DHPR channels open in response to voltage changes, allowing calcium ions to enter the sarcoplasm. ncorrect Options: Option A - SERCA pump (Sarcoplasmic/Endoplasmic Reticulum Calcium ATPase): • The SERCA pump, situated on the sarcoplasmic reticulum (SR) membrane in muscle cells, moves calcium ions (Ca2+) from the cell's cytoplasm into the SR lumen, against their concentration gradient. Option B - Troponin-myosin complex: • Troponin, a protein complex on muscle actin filaments, comprises three subunits: troponin C, troponin I, and troponin T. • This complex regulates muscle contraction and relaxation by modulating actin-myosin filament interaction in response to intracellular calcium changes. Option D - Ryanodine receptor: • Ryanodine receptors (RyRs) are intracellular calcium release channels located on the sarcoplasmic reticulum membrane or L tubule of muscle cells. They are activated by calcium-induced calcium release (CICR) and play a key role in releasing calcium ions from the SR into the cytoplasm in response to depolarization of the T-tubule membrane during excitation-contraction coupling.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 358-358; Solution Pages 376-376","sourcePage":358,"sourcePageEnd":358},{"id":"physiology-12-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following options has the slowest conduction velocity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Postganglionic sympathetic fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Somatic motor fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Preganglionic autonomic fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Touch and pressure fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Postganglionic sympathetic fibers: • Postganglionic sympathetic fibers have the least conduction velocity compared to the other options as these are unmyelinated fibers. These fibers are part of the autonomic nervous system and transmit signals from the autonomic ganglia to target organs, such as smooth muscles, cardiac muscles, and glands. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. These fibers have a higher conduction velocity than postganglionic sympathetic fibers of the autonomic nervous system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 359-359; Solution Pages 377-377","sourcePage":359,"sourcePageEnd":359},{"id":"physiology-12-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":18,"question":"What is the formula used to calculate Stroke Volume (SV) in the context of cardiac physiology?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SV = Heart Rate (HR) × Ejection Fraction (EF)"},{"letter":"B","text":"SV = End-Diastolic Volume (EDV) - End-Systolic Volume (ESV)"},{"letter":"C","text":"SV = Cardiac Output (CO) / Heart Rate (HR)"},{"letter":"D","text":"SV = Preload × Afterload"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - SV = End-Diastolic Volume (EDV) - End-Systolic Volume (ESV): • Stroke Volume (SV) refers to the amount of blood ejected by the left ventricle of the heart in a single contraction. • The formula to calculate Stroke Volume is based on the difference between the End-Diastolic Volume (EDV) and the End-Systolic Volume (ESV) and is around 70 ml. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 359-359; Solution Pages 377-377","sourcePage":359,"sourcePageEnd":359},{"id":"physiology-12-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which statement is accurate about the nerve action potential curve provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The threshold point is at A"},{"letter":"B","text":"At point E, the nerve is more excitable"},{"letter":"C","text":"Point C to D is due to the opening of Na+ and closure of K+ channels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Point B to D is a refractory period"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Point B to D is a refractory period: • The given graph represents an action potential curve of a nerve. • Point B to D represents the refractory period, a phase during which a second action potential is impossible or difficult to trigger. This period includes the absolute refractory period (impossible to trigger a second action potential) and the relative refractory period (asecond action potential can be triggered but requires a stronger stimulus). Incorrect Options: Option A - The threshold point is at A: Threshold point is typically represented by point B, where the depolarization is sufficient to open sodium channels and generate an action potential. Option B - At point E, the nerve is more excitable: the point E represents the hyperpolarization state of the action potential. Option C - Point C to D is due to the opening of Na+ and closure of K+ channels: Point C to D is the repolarization phase, primarily due to the opening of voltage-gated potassium channels and closure of sodium channels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 359-360; Solution Pages 377-378","sourcePage":359,"sourcePageEnd":360},{"id":"physiology-12-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A patient came to the hospital after a road traffic accident. He had severe muscle injury, and his serum K+ level was found to be 6.5 mEq/L. What will happen to the resting membrane potential in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"No change"},{"letter":"B","text":"Becomes more negative"},{"letter":"C","text":"Becomes more positive"},{"letter":"D","text":"First becomes more positive then negative"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Becomes more positive: • In hyperkalemia, where serum potassium levels are higher than normal (as in this patient with muscle injury), the concentration gradient of potassium ions across the cell membrane changes. • The larger extracellular potassium concentration diminishes the concentration gradient, which causes more potassium ions to stay inside the cell. • This results in the cell's resting membrane potential becoming more positive (less negative), leading to cell depolarization. Incorrect Options: Option A - No change: This would not be the case in hyperkalemia; changes in serum potassium levels directly affect the resting membrane potential. Option B - Becomes more negative: This could occur in hypokalemia (low serum potassium levels) but not hyperkalemia. Option D - First becomes more positive than negative: It is an incorrect option.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 360-360; Solution Pages 378-379","sourcePage":360,"sourcePageEnd":360},{"id":"physiology-12-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":21,"question":"The image shows changes in membrane potential during an action potential. The region marked between 4 to 5 is due to ______________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Influx of Na+"},{"letter":"B","text":"Efflux of Na+"},{"letter":"C","text":"Influx of K+"},{"letter":"D","text":"Efflux of K+"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Efflux of K+: • The region marked between 4 and 5 represents the repolarization phase of a nerve action potential. • It occurs due to closure of fast voltage gated sodium channels and opening of slow voltage gated potassium channels leading to the efflux of K+ out of the cell resulting in the restoration of the resting membrane potential. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 360-360; Solution Pages 379-379","sourcePage":360,"sourcePageEnd":360},{"id":"physiology-12-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":22,"question":"What causes the detachment of myosin from actin filaments?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Attachment of ATP to the myosin head"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cessation of calcium release from the sarcoplasm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Conformational change in Troponin T"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dissociation of ADP and Pi"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Attachment of ATP to the myosin head: • The detachment of myosin from actin filaments occurs when ATP binds to the myosin head. • After the power stroke (when myosin pulls on actin), ADP and inorganic phosphate (Pi) are still bound to the myosin head. When ATP binds to the myosin head, it causes the myosin head to detach from actin. This detachment allows the muscle to relax and prepares the myosin head for the next contraction cycle. Incorrect Options: Option B - Cessation of calcium release from the sarcoplasm: While calcium ions are crucial for initiating muscle contraction by binding to troponin and causing a conformational change that exposes myosin-binding sites on actin. Absence of calcium will result in the cessation of the cross bridge cycle at power stroke. Option C - Conformational change in Troponin T: Troponin T is involved in regulating the position of tropomyosin on actin, which in turn controls the binding of myosin to actin. Option D - Dissociation of ADP and Pi: Dissociation of ADP and Pi will result in the power stroke generation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 361-361; Solution Pages 379-380","sourcePage":361,"sourcePageEnd":361},{"id":"physiology-12-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A and B in the graph below represent which of the following ions, in terms of permeability for action potential generation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A is Na+, B is K+"},{"letter":"B","text":"A is Na+, B is Ca2+"},{"letter":"C","text":"A is K+, B is Ca2+"},{"letter":"D","text":"A is Ca2+, B is K+"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - A is Na+, B is K+: • In the resting state, the neuronal membrane is more permeable to K+ ions than to Na+ ions, maintaining a negative resting potential. • Upon receiving a stimulus, Na+ ions enter the cell, causing rapid depolarization seen as an upward slope on the membrane potential vs. time graph. • Subsequently, voltage-gated K+ channels open, leading to K+ efflux and membrane repolarization, shown as a downward slope. • Thus, it can be concluded that A- Na+, B- K+. Incorrect Options: • Option B, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 361-361; Solution Pages 380-380","sourcePage":361,"sourcePageEnd":361},{"id":"physiology-12-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Arrange the following parts of the sarcomere from the periphery to center. 1. Z line 2. M line 3. A band 4. H zone","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,3,4,2"},{"letter":"B","text":"4,2,3,1"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,3,4,1"},{"letter":"D","text":"3,1,4,2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1, 3, 4, 2: • Z line: The Z line is located at the periphery of the sarcomere. It forms the boundary of the sarcomere and anchors the thin filaments (actin) in place. • M line: The M line is located in the center of the sarcomere, running vertically through the middle. It provides structural support and helps to stabilize the position of the thick filaments (myosin). • A band: The A band is a dark-staining region in the center of the sarcomere. It represents the length of the thick filaments (myosin) and includes overlapping thick and thin filaments. • H zone: The H zone is located within the A band. It is a lighter region in the center of the sarcomere where only thick filaments (myosin) are present. The H zone gets shorter or disappears during muscle contraction. Incorrect Options: • Option B, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 361-362; Solution Pages 380-381","sourcePage":361,"sourcePageEnd":362},{"id":"physiology-12-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":25,"question":"During exercise, once a state of balance is achieved, it was observed that the body's oxygen consumption rate was 1500 ml/min. To determine the MET score, consider a baseline oxygen consumption rate of 250 ml/min.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"5"},{"letter":"B","text":"6"},{"letter":"C","text":"1/5"},{"letter":"D","text":"1/6"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 2: • MET (Metabolic Equivalent of Task) is a unit used to estimate energy expenditure during physical activity. It is defined as the ratio of the metabolic rate during the activity to the resting metabolic rate. • To calculate the MET score, we need to determine the ratio of oxygen consumption during exercise to resting oxygen consumption. • MET score = Oxygen consumption during exercise (ml/min) / Resting oxygen consumption (ml/min) • In this case, the oxygen consumption during exercise is given as 1500 ml/min, and the resting oxygen consumption is 250 ml/min. • MET score = 1500 ml/min divided by 250 ml/min • MET score = 6 Incorrect Options: • Option A, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 362-362; Solution Pages 381-382","sourcePage":362,"sourcePageEnd":362},{"id":"physiology-12-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":26,"question":"What are the chloride equilibrium potentials when the extracellular concentration is 100 mmol/L, and the intracellular concentration is 10 mmol/L?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"+ 10 mV"},{"letter":"B","text":"– 10 mV"},{"letter":"C","text":"– 60 mV"},{"letter":"D","text":"+ 60 mV"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - 60 mV: • The equilibrium potential of an ion can be calculated using the Nernst equation: E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) • E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) • Where: E = equilibrium potential R = gas constant T = temperature (in Kelvin) z = valency of the ion F = Faraday constant [Outside] = concentration of the ion outside the cell [Inside] = concentration of the ion inside the cell In this case, we have: [Cl-] outside = 100 mmol/L [Cl-] inside = 10 mmol/L z = -1 (since chloride has a negative charge) • E = equilibrium potential • R = gas constant • T = temperature (in Kelvin) • z = valency of the ion • F = Faraday constant • [Outside] = concentration of the ion outside the cell • [Inside] = concentration of the ion inside the cell • In this case, we have: • [Cl-] outside = 100 mmol/L • [Cl-] inside = 10 mmol/L • z = -1 (since chloride has a negative charge) • Substituting the values into the Nernst equation: E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) • E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) • E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) • E = equilibrium potential • R = gas constant • T = temperature (in Kelvin) • z = valency of the ion • F = Faraday constant • [Outside] = concentration of the ion outside the cell • [Inside] = concentration of the ion inside the cell • In this case, we have: • [Cl-] outside = 100 mmol/L • [Cl-] inside = 10 mmol/L • z = -1 (since chloride has a negative charge) • E = (RT/zF) * ln ([outside]/[inside]) = (8.314 * 310 / -1 * 96485) * ln (100/10) ≈ -61.54 mV • Therefore, the equilibrium potential of chloride (Cl-) is approximately -60 mV. Incorrect Options: • Option A, B & D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 362-362; Solution Pages 382-383","sourcePage":362,"sourcePageEnd":362},{"id":"physiology-12-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A person after sleeping overnight with the arm under his head now experiences paresis but no numbness in the morning. Which of the following is the best explanation for it?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"A fibers are more sensitive to hypoxia than B fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"A fibers are more susceptible to pressure changes than C fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"A fibers are more susceptible to hypoxia than C fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - fibers are more sensitive to pressure than C fibers: • The person experiences paresis (muscle weakness), but no numbness, indicating that motor function is affected, but the sensory function is preserved. In this scenario, pressure on the nerves during sleep likely affects the larger, myelinated motor neurons (A fibers) more than the smaller, unmyelinated sensory neurons (C fibers). A fibers carry motor signals to muscles and are more susceptible to pressure, leading to temporary muscle weakness without sensory deficits. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 362-363; Solution Pages 383-383","sourcePage":362,"sourcePageEnd":363},{"id":"physiology-12-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":28,"question":"The resting membrane potential of nerve fiber is close to the isoelectric potential of:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Potassium ions"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sodium ions"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chloride ions"},{"letter":"D","text":"Magnesium ions"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Chloride ions: • The resting membrane potential of a nerve fiber is equal to the equilibrium potential of chloride ion i.e., -70 mV. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 363-363; Solution Pages 383-384","sourcePage":363,"sourcePageEnd":363},{"id":"physiology-12-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":29,"question":"In skeletal muscle, dihydropyridine, and ryanodine receptors are coupled by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chemically"},{"letter":"B","text":"Electrically"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chemically and then electrically"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mechanically"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Mechanically: • In skeletal muscle, the DHPR (dihydropyridine receptor) and ryanodine receptors work together through electro-mechanical coupling. The DHPR, located on the cell membrane, acts as a voltage-gated calcium channel, detecting membrane depolarization during an action potential. Meanwhile, the ryanodine receptor, situated on the sarcoplasmic reticulum, releases calcium ions into the muscle cell's cytoplasm during contraction. • After the membrane depolarization, the DHPR channel will undergo conformation changes to extend one arm towards the RyR channel, which will lead to a mechanical interaction between the two channels. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 363-363; Solution Pages 384-384","sourcePage":363,"sourcePageEnd":363},{"id":"physiology-12-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":30,"question":"The image below shows a simple muscle twitch along with the time trace, recorded in a frog with the minimum tetanizing frequency. (The time tracer was made with a 100 Hz tuning fork)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"25 per second"},{"letter":"B","text":"15 per second"},{"letter":"C","text":"17 per second"},{"letter":"D","text":"20 per second"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - 20 per second: • The image displays a muscle twitch recorded with a 100 Hz tuning fork. Counting 5 muscle twitches in the trace, we calculate the frequency: 5 twitches / 100 Hz = 0.05 Hz. To express it as twitches per second, considering 20 twitches per cycle (100 Hz tuning fork), we get 0.05 Hz * 20 = 1 twitch per second. • So, the correct option is \"20 per second,\" indicating a frequency of 20 twitches per second. The image displays a muscle twitch recorded with a 100 Hz tuning fork. Counting 5 muscle twitches in the trace, we calculate the frequency: 5 twitches / 100 Hz = 0.05 Hz. To express it as twitches per second, considering 20 twitches per cycle (100 Hz tuning fork), we get 0.05 Hz * 20 = 1 twitch per second. So, the correct option is \"20 per second,\" indicating a frequency of 20 twitches per second. Incorrect Options: Option A - 25 per second: This option is not supported by the information given in the question. The time trace does not show 25 muscle twitches within the given time interval based on the 100 Hz tuning fork. Option B - 15 per second: This option is also not supported by the information given in the question. The time trace does not show 15 muscle twitches within the given time interval based on the 100 Hz tuning fork. Option C - 17 per second: Similarly, this option is not supported by the information given in the question. The time trace does not show 17 muscle twitches within the given time interval based on the 100 Hz tuning fork.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 363-364; Solution Pages 384-385","sourcePage":363,"sourcePageEnd":364},{"id":"physiology-12-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the mechanisms involved in depolarization, repolarization, and hyperpolarization during a skeletal muscle action potential? Depolarization due to influx of Na+ through voltage-gated channels Depolarization due to influx of Na+ through ligand-gated channels Repolarization due to efflux of K+ through a voltage-gated channel Repolarization due to efflux of K+ through a ligand-gated channel Hyperpolarisation due to efflux of K+","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2 and 4 are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,3 and 5 are correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,4 and 5 are correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"2,4 and 5 are correct"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 1,3 and 5 are correct: Skeletal muscle action potential: Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 364-364; Solution Pages 385-386","sourcePage":364,"sourcePageEnd":364},{"id":"physiology-12-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Identify the ions A and B based on the conductance graph of a neuron shown below:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na + , K +"},{"letter":"B","text":"K + , Na +"},{"letter":"C","text":"Na + , Ca ++"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ca ++ , Na +"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Na + , K + : • Sodium and Potassium are two essential ions involved in the generation and conduction of action potentials in neurons. Sodium plays a crucial role in depolarization, while potassium contributes to repolarization and restoring the resting membrane potential. • A - It is Na+ ion, characterized by rapid influx of Na+ through fast type of voltage gated Na+ channels leading to sudden rise in action potential. • B - It is K+ ions, characterized by slow and steady efflux of K+ ion through a slow type of voltage gated K+ channel leading to slow decrease in membrane potential. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 364-365; Solution Pages 386-386","sourcePage":364,"sourcePageEnd":365},{"id":"physiology-12-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which of the following is not a feature of an electrical synapse?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pre and postsynaptic neurons are connected through gap junctions"},{"letter":"B","text":"The direction of impulse is unidirectional"},{"letter":"C","text":"Connexons are involved in electrical synapse"},{"letter":"D","text":"The direction of impulse in bidirectional"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - The direction of impulse is unidirectional: • In an electrical synapse, however, the impulse can flow bidirectionally, allowing for direct electrical communication between the neurons in both directions. Charact Electrical Synapse Chemical Synapse eristic Illustrati on Occurre Rare Recreate this image nce Mostly present in the CNS Compon Presynaptic neuron and Presynaptic neuron and ents postsynaptic neuron postsynaptic neuron Synaptic Narrow (2-4 nm, average 4 nm) Wide (20-40 nm) Cleft Width Pre-syn No neurotransmitter-containing Contains neurotransmitter-containing aptic vesicles vesicles Termina ls Channel Gap junctions between presynaptic No gap junctions s and postsynaptic neuron Synaptic Action potential (Na + ) from the Release of neurotransmitters from Transm presynaptic membrane can easily the presynaptic neuron; the ission pass through the gap junctions to neurotransmitter gets attached to the postsynaptic membrane without the receptor on the post-synaptic any synaptic delay. membrane. This will lead to the release of either Ca ++ or Na + ions, leading to the generation of an action potential in the post-synaptic neuron. There will be an average synaptic delay of 0.5 milliseconds (0.3 to 5 milliseconds) due to multiple steps at the level of synaptic transmission. Transmi Bidirectional Unidirectional (from presynaptic to ssion postsynaptic) Directio n Location Mainly found in the Inferior Olivary Distributed throughout the CNS Nucleus (ION), Thalamus, Hypothalamus, Cortex Incorrect Options: • Options A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 365-365; Solution Pages 386-387","sourcePage":365,"sourcePageEnd":365},{"id":"physiology-12-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-12","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Integrin connects with:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cadherin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dynein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fibronectin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Selectin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Fibronectin: • Fibronectin: Fibronectin is a large glycoprotein present in the extracellular matrix. It forms a fibrous network and acts as a scaffold for cells. Fibronectin interacts with integrins, particularly integrin α5β1 and integrin αvβ3, among others. This interaction is important for cell adhesion, migration, and signaling. Fibronectin also plays a role in tissue development, wound healing, and cell-matrix interactions. Incorrect Options: Option A - Cadherins: Mediate calcium-dependent cell-cell adhesion, forming adherens junctions crucial for tissue organization and cell signaling. Option B - Vitronectin : Interacts with integrins, particularly αvβ3 and αvβ5, facilitating cell adhesion, migration, and signaling in various tissues. Option D - Selectins: Aid leukocyte recruitment during inflammation by mediating rolling adhesion on endothelial cells, facilitating interaction with inflammatory molecules.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 365-365; Solution Pages 388-388","sourcePage":365,"sourcePageEnd":365},{"id":"physiology-13-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following structures is repolarized last during ventricular repolarization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Apical epicardial surface"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterobasal portion of the left ventricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary conus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endocardial surface"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Endocardial surface","explanation":"Repolarization of ventricular muscle starts from the epicardial surface and ends in the endocardial surface. Depolarization Repolarization Atrial depolarization starts at the SA node Atrial repolarization starts near the SA node Starts from the middle portion of Starts from the apex of the heart, the septum on the left side Ventricular depolarization: Ventricular repolarization occurs in the reverse direction Starts at the left side of the interventricular septum Crosses to the right side through the interventricular septum Spreads down to the apex of the heart The endocardial surface depolarizes first and the The epicardial surface repolarizes first and the epicardial last endocardial last. Depolarized last: Repolarized last: Posterobasal portion of the left ventricle Septum Pulmonary conus Endocardial surface The uppermost portion of the septum","sourceRef":"Question Pages 389-389; Solution Pages 396-397","sourcePage":389,"sourcePageEnd":389},{"id":"physiology-13-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following best describes the effect of cyclic adenosine 3′,5′-monophosphate (cAMP) on heart rate regulation through nodal tissue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"cAMP decreases heart rate by increasing potassium (K + ) conductance in nodal tissue."},{"letter":"B","text":"cAMP increases heart rate by enhancing the opening of L-type calcium channels and \" funny channels\" in nodal tissue."},{"letter":"C","text":"cAMP does not affect the heart rate as it does not influence the activity of \"funny channels\" or calcium channels."},{"letter":"D","text":"cAMP decreases the heart rate by inhibiting the activity of L-type calcium channels and \" funny channels.\""}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) cAMP increases heart rate by enhancing the opening of L-type calcium channels and \"funny channels\" in nodal tissue.","explanation":"Elevated cAMP levels increase the opening of \"funny channels\" and L-type calcium channels, which speeds up depolarization and results in a higher heart rate. • Increased cAMP levels , often triggered by sympathetic stimulation via β1 receptors, directly facilitate the opening of L-type calcium channels. This leads to a more rapid influx of calcium ions (Ca 2+ ), speeding up the depolarization phase of the pacemaker potential and, consequently, increasing the heart rate. • Sympathetic stimulation increases cAMP levels in nodal cells. This increase in cAMP facilitates the opening of \"funny channels\" and subsequently L-type calcium channels. • The parasympathetic nervous system releases acetylcholine through the vagus nerve, which acts on the sinoatrial (SA) node. Acetylcholine binds to M2 muscarinic receptors on the SA nodal cells, leading to a reduction in cAMP levels . This decrease in cAMP slows the opening of L-type calcium channels, reducing the influx of calcium ions and slowing the depolarisation rate of the SA node. As a result, the heart rate decreases. Increased cAMP enhances heart rate by increasing the activity of \"funny channels\" and L-type calcium channels, rather than increasing potassium conductance (Option A). cAMP does influence the activity of \"funny channels\" and L-type calcium channels, thereby affecting heart rate (Option C). Increased cAMP enhances the activity of these channels, leading to an increased heart rate, not a decrease (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 389-389; Solution Pages 397-398","sourcePage":389,"sourcePageEnd":389},{"id":"physiology-13-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the unique properties of \"funny channels\" in the heart's nodal tissue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"\"Funny channels\" open during depolarization and are primarily permeable to calcium (Ca 2+ ) ions."},{"letter":"B","text":"\"Funny channels\" close upon hyperpolarization and restrict sodium (Na + ) influx."},{"letter":"C","text":"\"Funny channels\" open upon hyperpolarization, allowing both sodium (Na + ) and potassium (K + ) ions to pass through."},{"letter":"D","text":"\"Funny channels\" are activated by parasympathetic stimulation, speeding up the heart rate."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) \"Funny channels\" open upon hyperpolarization, allowing both sodium (Na + ) and potassium (K + ) ions to pass through.","explanation":"\"Funny channels,\" or hyperpolarization-activated cyclic nucleotide-gated (HCN) channels, are crucial for initiating the pacemaker potential in the heart's nodal tissue. They open upon hyperpolarization, allowing sodium and potassium ions to enter the cell , gradually depolarizing the membrane and contributing to the rhythmic electrical activity that controls the heart rate. This is incorrect because \"funny channels\" open during hyperpolarization and are not primarily permeable to calcium. They are mainly permeable to sodium and potassium ions (Option A). This statement is incorrect. \"Funny channels\" actually open upon hyperpolarization, allowing the influx of sodium and potassium ions (Option B). This statement is incorrect. Parasympathetic stimulation slows the heart rate by increasing potassium conductance, which hyperpolarizes the membrane and slows the rate of spontaneous depolarization (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 389-390; Solution Pages 398-399","sourcePage":389,"sourcePageEnd":390},{"id":"physiology-13-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 55 y/o male presents with episodes of dizziness and palpitations. He has been prescribed a new medication to help control his heart rate recently. His ECG reveals a resting heart rate of 45 bpm. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely contributing to his slower heart rate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased activity of L-type Ca 2+ channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased \"funny current\" (I h )"},{"letter":"C","text":"Enhanced sympathetic stimulation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Activation of T-type Ca 2+ channels"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Decreased \"funny current\" (I h )","explanation":"Decreased \"funny current\" (I h ): The \"funny current\" (I h ) is crucial for the pacemaker potential (Phase 4) in nodal tissue. It gradually depolarizes the membrane potential towards the threshold. A decrease in (I h ) would slow down the rate of spontaneous depolarization, leading to a lower heart rate, which aligns with the patient's bradycardia. The L-type Ca 2+ channels are responsible for the slow depolarization (Phase 0) in nodal cells. Increased activity of these channels would lead to a faster and stronger depolarizing impulse, which is not consistent with the patient's symptoms of a slow heart rate (Option A). Sympathetic stimulation increases the rate of spontaneous discharge by enhancing I h and making the depolarization phase more rapid. This would lead to an increased heart rate, which is the opposite of what is observed in the patient (Option C). T-type Ca 2+ channels contribute to the late Phase 4 depolarization by bringing the membrane potential closer to the threshold. Their activation would enhance the depolarization process, potentially increasing the heart rate, not decreasing it (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 390-390; Solution Pages 399-400","sourcePage":390,"sourcePageEnd":390},{"id":"physiology-13-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following mechanisms is primarily responsible for the relaxation of myocardial cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Opening of L-type calcium channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Activity of the SERCA2 pump"},{"letter":"C","text":"Blocking the sodium-calcium exchanger"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dephosphorylation of phospholamban"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Activity of the SERCA2 pump","explanation":"The activity of the SERCA2 pump is the primary mechanism responsible for myocardial cell relaxation. SERCA2 actively pumps Ca2+ from the cytoplasm back into the sarcoplasmic reticulum, reducing cytoplasmic Ca2+ concentration and facilitating muscle relaxation. This action would lead to an increase in Ca2+ influx into the myocardial cells, promoting muscle contraction rather than relaxation. For relaxation to occur, these channels must close (Option A). Blocking this exchanger would prevent the removal of Ca2+ from the cytoplasm, maintaining high cytoplasmic Ca2+ levels and preventing muscle relaxation (Option C). Phospholamban plays a key regulatory role in myocardial cell relaxation by modulating the activity of the SERCA2 pump. In its dephosphorylated state, it inhibits SERCA2, reducing calcium reuptake and prolonging contraction. When phosphorylated, this inhibition is relieved, allowing for increased calcium uptake and faster relaxation of the myocardial cells (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 390-390; Solution Pages 400-401","sourcePage":390,"sourcePageEnd":390},{"id":"physiology-13-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":6,"question":"During the process of calcium-induced calcium release (CICR) in cardiac muscle cells, what triggers the release of calcium from the sarcoplasmic reticulum","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Depolarization directly triggers Ca2+ release from SR"},{"letter":"B","text":"Influx of extracellular calcium through voltage-gated channels"},{"letter":"C","text":"The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger directly triggers Ca2+ release from SR"},{"letter":"D","text":"Troponin directly triggers Ca2+ release from SR"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Influx of extracellular calcium through voltage-gated channels","explanation":"The influx of extracellular calcium through the voltage-sensitive dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) or voltage-gated Ca2+ channels in the T system stimulates the ryanodine receptor (RyR2) channels at the sarcoplasmic reticulum, leading to calcium release. Depolarization initiates the process by opening voltage-gated Ca2+ channels, but it is not the direct trigger for Ca2+ release from SR (Option A). The Na+/Ca2+ exchanger helps in the recovery of cytosolic calcium concentrations but does not directly trigger the Ca2+ release from SR (Option C). Troponin interacts with calcium in the sarcoplasm to initiate muscle contraction, but it does not directly trigger the Ca2+ release from SR (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 390-391; Solution Pages 401-402","sourcePage":390,"sourcePageEnd":391},{"id":"physiology-13-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":7,"question":"In which phase in a cardiac muscle cell does the closure of the Ca2+ channels and a Slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Late rapid repolarization"},{"letter":"B","text":"Depolarisation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Plateau phase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Initial rapid repolarization"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Late rapid repolarization","explanation":"Late rapid repolarization is characterized by the closure of the Ca2+ channels and a Slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux which is Phase 3 of the action potential of cardiac myocytes. PHASES OPENING AND CLOSURE OF CHANNELS Phase 0- Depolarization (Option B) Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels Phase 1- Initial rapid repolarization (Option Closure of Na+ channels D) Opening of K+ channel Phase 2- Plateau phase (Option C) Slower but prolonged opening of L-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels Fast Potassium channels remain open Phase 3- Late rapid repolarization Closure of the Ca2+ channels Slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux Phase 4- Baseline Resting Membrane Potential About −80 to −90 millivolts.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 391-391; Solution Pages 402-403","sourcePage":391,"sourcePageEnd":391},{"id":"physiology-13-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Given below is the action potential of a cardiac muscle fiber. Which of the following gives rise to Phase 2 depicted in the image?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Closure of Na+ channels and opening of K+ channel"},{"letter":"B","text":"Closure of the Ca2+ channels and a slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux"},{"letter":"C","text":"Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels."},{"letter":"D","text":"Opening of slow L-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and efflux of K+ though rectifier channels."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Opening of slow L-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels and efflux of K+ though rectifier channels","explanation":"The given prolonged plateau (phase 2) is due to a slower but prolonged opening of voltage-gated L-type Ca2+ channels and efflux of Potassium through rectifier channels . PHASES OPENING AND CLOSURE OF CHANNELS Phase 0- Depolarization Opening of voltage-gated Na+ channels (Option C) Phase 1- Initial rapid repolarization Closure of Na+ channels Opening of K+ channel (Option A) Phase 2- Plateau phase Slower but prolonged opening of L-type voltage-gated Ca2+ channels K+ channel also remains open. Phase 3- Late rapid repolarization Closure of the Ca2+ channels Slow, delayed increase of K+ efflux (Option B) Phase 4- Baseline Resting Membrane Potential About −80 to −90 millivolts.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 391-391; Solution Pages 403-403","sourcePage":391,"sourcePageEnd":391},{"id":"physiology-13-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following correctly matches the resting membrane potential with the corresponding cardiac cell type in the given order: Purkinje cells, AV node, SA node, Ventricular myocytes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"-40, -60, -80, -90"},{"letter":"B","text":"-60, -60, -60, -90"},{"letter":"C","text":"-90, -60, -60, -90"},{"letter":"D","text":"-60, -90, -90, -60"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) -90, -60, -60, -90","explanation":"Cardiac Myocytes Resting Membrane Potential Purkinje fibers and atrial and ventricular myocytes −90 mV Sinoatrial (SA) and atrioventricular (AV) nodes −60 mV Cardiac Myocytes Resting Membrane Potential Purkinje fibers and atrial and ventricular myocytes −90 mV Sinoatrial (SA) and atrioventricular (AV) nodes −60 mV","sourceRef":"Question Pages 392-392; Solution Pages 404-404","sourcePage":392,"sourcePageEnd":392},{"id":"physiology-13-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly differentiates cardiac muscle from skeletal muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cardiac muscle cells are multinucleated, while skeletal muscle cells have a single, centrally located nucleus."},{"letter":"B","text":"Cardiac muscle forms a functional syncytium with intercalated discs; skeletal muscle lacks these structures."},{"letter":"C","text":"Cardiac muscle contraction is voluntary and regulated by the somatic nervous system; skeletal muscle contraction is involuntary."},{"letter":"D","text":"Cardiac muscle primarily utilizes glycolytic pathways for energy; skeletal muscle relies solely on oxidative metabolism."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Cardiac muscle forms a functional syncytium with intercalated discs; skeletal muscle lacks these structures. Explanation : Aspect Cardiac Muscle Skeletal Muscle Structure and Branched muscle fibers. Elongated, cylindrical fibers. Organization Interconnected by intercalated discs. Arranged in parallel bundles. Forms a functional syncytium. They lack branching and intercalated discs. Cellular Structure Single, centrally located nucleus. Multinucleated. (Option A) Nuclei located peripherally. Intercellular Intercalated discs with gap junctions. Do not possess gap junctions. Communication Enables rapid passage of ions and electrical signals. Regulation of Involuntary. Voluntary. Contraction (Option Regulated by the intrinsic Controlled by the somatic nervous C) conduction system and autonomic system. nervous system. Contraction Duration Longer duration. Generally, it has a shorter duration, varying with muscle fiber type. Energy Utilization Primarily oxidative metabolism of Utilizes both oxidative and glycolytic (Option D) fatty acids pathways. Depends on muscle fiber type and exercise intensity. Transverse Tubule Larger T tubules. Smaller T tubules. System Located at the Z line. Located at the A-I junction. Forms a diad with a single cistern. Forms a triad with two cisternae. Calcium Handling Relies on extracellular calcium influx Predominantly depends on calcium through T tubules. release from the sarcoplasmic Calcium release from the reticulum. sarcoplasmic reticulum","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 392-392; Solution Pages 404-405","sourcePage":392,"sourcePageEnd":392},{"id":"physiology-13-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following does NOT contribute to the high conduction velocity in Purkinje fibers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They are large diameter fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fast-type action potential"},{"letter":"C","text":"High density of gap junctions"},{"letter":"D","text":"High myofibril content"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) High myofibril content","explanation":"Purkinje fibers have very few myofibrils, meaning that they contract little or not at all during impulse transmission and hence have high conduction velocity. Conduction velocity in cardiac tissue Cardiac tissue Conduction velocity (m/s) SA node 0.05 Internodal pathways 1 AV node 0.02-0.05 His bundle 1 Purkinje fibers 4 Ventricular muscles 1 Purkinje fibers- fastest impulse conduction from apex to base of the heart AV node- slowest conduction Reasons for high conduction in Purkinje fibers • High level of permeability of the gap junctions at the intercalated discs between the successive cells that make up the Purkinje fibers (Option C) • They are large cells and can transmit potentials at a high velocity (Option A) • Very few myofibrils-minimal contractions during impulse transmission • Fast-type action potential (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 392-392; Solution Pages 405-406","sourcePage":392,"sourcePageEnd":392},{"id":"physiology-13-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":12,"question":"If the S-A node discharges at 0.00 seconds, when will the action potential normally arrive at the epicardial surface at the base of the left ventricle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0.22sec"},{"letter":"B","text":"0.18sec"},{"letter":"C","text":"0.12sec"},{"letter":"D","text":"0.09sec"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 0.22sec","explanation":"Action potential arriving at the epicardial surface at the base of the ventricle is the same as the total time taken for depolarization =0.22 sec The time interval for impulse conduction SA node to AV node 0.03 seconds AV node to bundle of His 0.10 seconds Bundle of his to interventricular septum 0.04 seconds Interventricular septum to the apex of the heart 0.03 seconds Apex to the base of the heart 0.02 seconds Total 0.22 seconds After the S-A node discharges, the action potential travels through the atria, the A-V bundle system, and finally to the ventricular septum and the ventricle. The last place that the impulse arrives is at the epicardial surface at the base of the left ventricle, which requires a transit time of 0.22 sec.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 393-393; Solution Pages 406-406","sourcePage":393,"sourcePageEnd":393},{"id":"physiology-13-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Arrange the following according to the conduction times (conduction delay) in the heart in ascending order (lowest time to highest time) a. SA node to AV node b. AV node to bundle of His c. Apex to the base of the heart d. Bundle of his to interventricular septum","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"c, b, a, d"},{"letter":"B","text":"c, a, d, b"},{"letter":"C","text":"b, a, d, c"},{"letter":"D","text":"d, a, b, c"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) c, a, d, b","explanation":"The time taken for the conduction of impulses in the heart in ascending order is as follows: Apex to the base of the heart (0.02sec)< SA node to AV node (0.03sec)< Bundle of his to interventricular septum(0.04sec)< Apex to the bundle of His (0.1sec). The time interval for impulse conduction Apex to the base of the heart 0.02 seconds SA node to AV node 0.03 seconds Interventricular septum to the apex of the heart 0.03 seconds Bundle of his to interventricular septum 0.04 seconds AV node to bundle of His 0.10 seconds AV nodal Delay: • Impulse travels through the AV node with a length of 5 mm and is very slow, known as AV nodal \"Delay\". • The time required for impulse conduction from the SA node to the AV node is 0.03 seconds. • The time taken for impulse conduction from the upper to the lower part of the AV node is 100milliseconds • Due to av nodal delay, Atrial depolarization is completed much before the beginning of ventricular depolarization. Therefore, when atrial systole occurs, the ventricle is in diastole. This helps the ventricular filling to occur. His Bundle Delay: • The time taken for impulse conduction from the upper to the lower part of His bundle is 40 milliseconds known as His bundle delay. • His bundle is in the uppermost portion of the interventricular septum. • When an impulse crosses the bundle of His, it causes depolarization of the septal muscle. The middle part of the interventricular septum is first depolarized. • When the SA node produces impulse at zero time point, it takes approximately 170 ms to start ventricular depolarization. An impulse travels through the septum depolarizing it from left to right and, reaching the heart's apex in 30 milliseconds. The total time taken to reach the apex of the heart is 200 milliseconds. The impulse depolarizes both ventricular muscles through Purkinje fibers. Depolarization occurs from the endocardium toward the epicardium and takes around 20 milliseconds The total time taken for complete depolarization of the heart is 220 ms.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 393-393; Solution Pages 406-408","sourcePage":393,"sourcePageEnd":393},{"id":"physiology-13-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following is the name of the middle internodal bundle between SA node and AV node in the atrium of the heart?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bachmann's bundle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Wenckebach's bundle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thorel's bundle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kent bundle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Wenckebach’s bundle","explanation":"The middle internodal bundle of the heart is called the Wenckebach’s bundle or the tract of Wenckebach. Internodal Pathways: • 3 bundles of atrial fibers that connect the SA node to the AV node • Conduction is more rapid in these bundles • These merge into the AV node Anterior Tract of Bachman (Option A) Middle Tract of Wenckebach Posterior Tract of Thorel (Option C) ‘ Bundle of his: • A small bundle of fibers arises from the AV node and terminates in the Purkinje system. • Situated below the AV node and passes towards the interventricular septum • Gives off 2 branches Right bundle branch Left bundle branch Conducts impulse to the right ventricle Conducts impulse to the left ventricle Exclusively innervates right ventricle Divides into Anterior fascicle: anterior portion of the left ventricle Posterior fascicle:posterolateral part of left ventricle Purkinje fibers • Network of small bundles of conducting fibers that are present throughout the sub-endocardial regions of the right and left ventricles • Purkinje cells are the largest cells in the heart • Gap junctions are present between the cells • The rate of impulse conduction is highest in the Purkinje cells d/t larger diameter and low impedance cell-to-cell connection. • Fast response type action potentials are generated Kent’s bundle: is an accessory conducting pathway between the atria and the ventricle of the heart. (Option D) .The bundle of Kent allows electrical activity to bypass the AV node, which can lead to an earlier activation of the ventricles. This can result in tachydysrhythmias","sourceRef":"Question Pages 393-393; Solution Pages 408-409","sourcePage":393,"sourcePageEnd":393},{"id":"physiology-13-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 65 y/o patient with a history of complete heart block presents for follow-up. The patient's ECG shows an idioventricular rhythm with a ventricular rate of 30 beats per minute. What is the most likely location of the pacemaker driving the patient's idioventricular rhythm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SA node"},{"letter":"B","text":"AV node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bundle of His"},{"letter":"D","text":"Purkinje Fibres"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Purkinje Fibres","explanation":"In a complete heart block with a very slow ventricular rate, the pacemaker is often located in the Purkinje fibers, resulting in rates around 30 beats per minute or slower. While the Bundle of His can be involved in conduction, Purkinje fibers are more likely at this low rate (Option C). • Role in Heart Block : Conduction Disruptions can occur between the SA and AV nodes or within the AV node, leading to heart block. Complete Heart Block : SA node signal doesn't reach ventricles; ventricles beat independently at a slower rate driven by a ventricular pacemaker. Ventricular Pacemaker Location : Determines idioventricular rhythm rate. • Conduction Disruptions can occur between the SA and AV nodes or within the AV node, leading to heart block. • Complete Heart Block : SA node signal doesn't reach ventricles; ventricles beat independently at a slower rate driven by a ventricular pacemaker. • Ventricular Pacemaker Location : Determines idioventricular rhythm rate. • Conduction Disruptions can occur between the SA and AV nodes or within the AV node, leading to heart block. • Complete Heart Block : SA node signal doesn't reach ventricles; ventricles beat independently at a slower rate driven by a ventricular pacemaker. • Ventricular Pacemaker Location : Determines idioventricular rhythm rate. Key Differences and Functions Aspect SA Node AV Node Intrinsic Rhythmicity 60-80 beats per minute(can go 40-60 beats per minute upto 100) (natural pacemaker of (Option B) the heart) (Option A) Location The upper wall of the right atrium The lower wall of the right atrium Function The primary pacemaker; initiates Delays impulses from the heart's rhythmic contractions atria; ensures coordinated contractions Action Potential Calcium-driven, slower conduction Prepotentials Present - Slow, gradual depolarizations observed Parasympathetic Modulation Acetylcholine decreases the firing rate Ach decreases the firing rate by hyperpolarizing nodal cells, increasing potassium conductance, slowing the \"funny\" current (Ih), and reducing cAMP levels. Sympathetic Modulation Norepinephrine increases the firing rate Norepinephrine: Increases firing rate by binding to β1 receptors, raising cAMP levels, and facilitating L-type calcium channel opening, accelerating depolarization. Role in Heart Block Fastest intrinsic rate, so normally It can be a site of unaffected conduction disruption leading to heart block","sourceRef":"Question Pages 394-394; Solution Pages 409-410","sourcePage":394,"sourcePageEnd":394},{"id":"physiology-13-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 68y/o male presents with episodes of dizziness and fainting. O/E, his heart rate is found to be irregular and bradycardic. ECG reveals complete heart block. Further evaluation shows that his ventricular rhythm is around 30 beats per minute. What is the most likely cause of his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Atrial fibrillation"},{"letter":"B","text":"AV nodal block"},{"letter":"C","text":"Infranodal block"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sick sinus syndrome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Infranodal block","explanation":"Infranodal block leads to a slower ventricular rate, often around 35 beats per minute or lower, consistent with the patient's rate of 30 beats per minute and symptoms of dizziness and fainting due to Stokes-Adams syndrome. Atrial fibrillation causes an irregularly irregular heart rhythm but does not typically cause complete heart block or an idioventricular rhythm (Option A). Sick sinus syndrome involves dysfunction of the sinoatrial node, leading to bradycardia or pauses, but does not account for the complete heart block observed in this patient (Option D). Complete Heart Block and Idioventricular Rhythm • Complete Heart Block : Electrical impulses from the atria fail to reach the ventricles due to a complete interruption in the conduction system. The interruption can occur at the AV node (AV nodal block) or below the AV node (infranodal block). • Electrical impulses from the atria fail to reach the ventricles due to a complete interruption in the conduction system. • The interruption can occur at the AV node (AV nodal block) or below the AV node (infranodal block). • Electrical impulses from the atria fail to reach the ventricles due to a complete interruption in the conduction system. • The interruption can occur at the AV node (AV nodal block) or below the AV node (infranodal block). • Independent Ventricular Rhythm : Ventricles develop an independent rhythm driven by a ventricular pacemaker due to the absence of pacing signals from the atria. • Ventricles develop an independent rhythm driven by a ventricular pacemaker due to the absence of pacing signals from the atria. • Location and Rate of Idioventricular Rhythm : AV Nodal Block : Remaining nodal tissue becomes pacemaker, resulting in a rate of ~45 beats per minute. (Option B) Infranodal Block : Pacemaker located more peripheral, resulting in a slower rate, averaging ~35 beats per minute, but can be as low as 15 beats per minute. • AV Nodal Block : Remaining nodal tissue becomes pacemaker, resulting in a rate of ~45 beats per minute. (Option B) • Infranodal Block : Pacemaker located more peripheral, resulting in a slower rate, averaging ~35 beats per minute, but can be as low as 15 beats per minute. • Ventricles develop an independent rhythm driven by a ventricular pacemaker due to the absence of pacing signals from the atria. • AV Nodal Block : Remaining nodal tissue becomes pacemaker, resulting in a rate of ~45 beats per minute. (Option B) • Infranodal Block : Pacemaker located more peripheral, resulting in a slower rate, averaging ~35 beats per minute, but can be as low as 15 beats per minute. • Occurs in individuals with complete heart block, especially infranodal block. • Characterized by episodes of dizziness and fainting (syncope). • Caused by periods of asystole, where ventricles fail to contract, leading to temporary cessation of blood circulation and decreased blood flow to the brain.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 394-394; Solution Pages 411-412","sourcePage":394,"sourcePageEnd":394},{"id":"physiology-13-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 55 y/o male heart transplant patient comes for a follow-up visit. He reports that he has been engaging in moderate exercise for about 30 minutes daily. Despite lacking sympathetic innervation to his transplanted heart, he notices an increase in his stamina and ability to exercise over time. Given the patient's condition, what physiological mechanism allows him to experience an increase in cardiac output during exercise?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased heart rate through parasympathetic withdrawal."},{"letter":"B","text":"An enhanced force of contraction via the Frank-Starling mechanism."},{"letter":"C","text":"Parasympathetic stimulation reduces heart rate variability."},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased heart rate through sympathetic stimulation to heart."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Enhanced force of contraction via the Frank-Starling mechanism","explanation":"The Frank-Starling mechanism allows the heart to increase stroke volume by enhancing the force of contraction in response to increased venous return during exercise, thereby increasing cardiac output. While parasympathetic withdrawal can lead to a slight increase in heart rate, it is not the primary mechanism by which cardiac output is increased in a heart transplant patient during exercise (Option A). Aspect Details Frank-Starling More heart muscle stretch during filling leads to stronger Mechanism contractions and higher stroke volume. Sympathetic Innervation Lost in heart transplant patients; cannot increase heart rate through the sympathetic nervous system. (Option D) Parasympathetic Lost in heart transplant patients; cannot decrease heart Innervation rate through the parasympathetic nervous system. (Option C) Stroke Volume Increase Achieved by increased force of contraction in response to increased venous return during exercise. Cardiac Output Increased primarily due to higher stroke volume, not heart rate. Comparison to Healthy Less increase in cardiac output, relying on stroke Individuals volume rather than both stroke volume and heart rate. So, in transplant patients,the increase in cardiac output during exercise is mainly due to an increase in stroke volume, not heart rate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 394-395; Solution Pages 412-413","sourcePage":394,"sourcePageEnd":395},{"id":"physiology-13-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-13","chapter":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topic":"Cardiac Conducting System and Action Potentials","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following characteristics primarily enables the SA node to act as the heart's primary pacemaker?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Presence of fast sodium channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Highest intrinsic firing rate among cardiac tissues"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lack of calcium channels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inactivation by parasympathetic stimulation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Highest intrinsic firing rate among cardiac tissues.","explanation":"The SA node's high intrinsic firing rate allows it to depolarize other cardiac tissues before they reach their own firing threshold, establishing its role as the primary pacemaker. Key Features of the SA Node and Its Role as the Heart's Primary Pacemaker: Automaticity and Unique Properties of SA Node Cells Intrinsic Leakiness Less negative resting membrane potential due to sodium and calcium ion leakiness. Funny Current (I h ) Inward sodium current drives slow depolarization toward the action potential threshold. Calcium Current (I Ca ) Voltage-gated calcium channels open at ~ -40 mV, causing rapid depolarization. (Option C) Fast Sodium Channels Mostly inactivated, leading to a slower depolarization rate compared to ventricular cells. (Option A) Influence of the Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic Stimulation Norepinephrine increases pacemaker potential slope, accelerating depolarization and heart rate. Parasympathetic Stimulation Acetylcholine decreases pacemaker potential slope, slowing depolarization and heart rate (Option D) Dominance A higher intrinsic firing rate depolarizes other cardiac tissues(AV nodes & Purkinje fibers) before they reach their own threshold.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 395-395; Solution Pages 413-414","sourcePage":395,"sourcePageEnd":395},{"id":"physiology-14-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which positive wave in a normal lead II ECG appears after the wave of atrial depolarization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"R wave"},{"letter":"B","text":"Q wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"P wave"},{"letter":"D","text":"T wave"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) R wave","explanation":"R wave is the first positive wave that appears after P wave (atrial depolarization) ECG: Trace of electrical conduction of the heart. Wave Key characteristics P wave First wave to appear due to atrial depolarization (option C) Q wave First negative wave after the P wave (option B) R wave First positive wave after the P wave (option A) S wave Immediately after R wave T wave After the QRS complex (option D) U wave Ventricular myocytes with long action potentials Yellow color denotes the areas that are depolarized. Interval Duration in seconds Events of the heart PR 0.12 to 0.20 Atrioventricular conduction QRS duration 0.08 Ventricular depolarization QT 0.40 Ventricular action potential ST 0.32 Plateau portion of the ventricular action potential","sourceRef":"Question Pages 415-415; Solution Pages 419-420","sourcePage":415,"sourcePageEnd":415},{"id":"physiology-14-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which segment on the ECG corresponds to the period during which the ventricles remain in a state of complete depolarization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PR interval"},{"letter":"B","text":"ST segment"},{"letter":"C","text":"QRS complex"},{"letter":"D","text":"QT interval"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) ST interval","explanation":"ST interval corresponds to the plateau portion of the ventricular action potential, where the action potential remains in a state of sustained depolarization. Action potential (two types in heart tissues): • Fast response action potential - In atrial and ventricular muscles and Purkinje fibers • Slow response action potential - In nodal tissues (SA and AV nodes) Fast response action potential in ventricular muscles: The phases of Corresponding ECG Ionic activity Key features in the action potential segment phase Phase 0 The initial part of the Opening of voltage■gated Rapid depolarization QRS complex sodium channels and overshoot. (Q wave) The rapid myocardial influx of sodium ions. Phase 1 The rest of the QRS Closure of sodium Initial rapid repolarization complex channels Cessation of sodium influx Phase 2 ST segment (option B) Slow and prolonged Plateau phase, opening of voltage■gated sustained depolarization calcium channel Slow calcium influx Efflux of potassium Phase 3 T wave Cessation of calcium Final repolarization. influx. Increased potassium efflux. Phase 4 No direct representatio Opening of inward Restoration of n on the ECG rectifying K+ channels membrane potential","sourceRef":"Question Pages 415-415; Solution Pages 420-421","sourcePage":415,"sourcePageEnd":415},{"id":"physiology-14-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements is false about the Class I antiarrhythmics on the cardiac action potential?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prolong the repolarization phase by blocking potassium channels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Class Ib decrease the QT interval"},{"letter":"C","text":"Class Ic are the most potent"},{"letter":"D","text":"Reduce the rate of depolarization during phase 0"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Prolong the repolarization phase by blocking potassium channels","explanation":"Class III antiarrhythmics are potassium channel blockers. Class 1 antiarrhythmics: Mechanism of action: Voltage-gated sodium channel blockers reduce the rate of depolarization during phase 0 . (option D) Class Ia Class Ib Class Ic Degree of block Moderate Mild Marked (option C) QT interval Prolong Decrease (option B) No effect Class III antiarrhythmics are potassium channel blockers, which decrease potassium efflux out of the cardiac cells and prolong the QTc interval.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 415-415; Solution Pages 422-422","sourcePage":415,"sourcePageEnd":415},{"id":"physiology-14-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which among the following options primarily contributes to QT prolongation on the ECG?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Opening of inward rectifying K+ channels in phase 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"Prolonged depolarization during Phase 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"Delayed repolarization during Phase 2 or 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased sodium influx during Phase 0"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Delayed repolarization during Phase 2 or 3","explanation":"QT prolongation on ECG is primarily caused by delayed repolarization during Phase 2 and 3 of the cardiac action potential. The Corresponding ECG Ionic activity Key features in the phase segment Phas The initial part of the QRS Opening of voltage■gated Rapid depolarization and e0 complex sodium channels overshoot. (Q wave) A rapid myocardial influx of sodium ions. (option D) Phas The rest of the QRS Closure of sodium Initial rapid repolarization e1 complex channels Cessation of sodium influx Phas ST segment (option C) Slow and prolonged Plateau phase, sustained e2 opening of voltage■gated depolarization calcium channel Slow calcium influx Efflux of potassium Phas T wave (option C) Cessation of calcium Final repolarization. e3 influx. Increased potassium efflux. Phas No direct representation Opening of inward Restoration of membrane e4 on the ECG rectifying K+ channels potential (option A) Class III antiarrhythmic drugs are potassium channel blockers that decrease potassium efflux out of the cell and prolong the QT interval.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 416-416; Solution Pages 422-423","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":416},{"id":"physiology-14-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":5,"question":"If the voltage in Lead II of the Einthoven triangle is 1.5 mV and the voltage in Lead III is 0.5 mV, what is the voltage in Lead I?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2.0 mV"},{"letter":"B","text":"1.0 mV"},{"letter":"C","text":"2.5 mV"},{"letter":"D","text":"1.5 mV"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 1.0 mV","explanation":"Voltages of lead I + lead III = lead II (Einthoven law) Hence, from the question, Lead I + 0.5 mV = 1.5 mV Lead I = 1.5 mV - 0.5 mV Lead II = 1.0 mV Einthoven triangle: • It is an equilateral triangle and each side represents the axis of one of the bipolar limb leads. • It is shaped like an inverted triangle, with the base between two shoulders and the apex pointing toward the groin. • The heart is considered to be at the center of the Einthoven triangle. • Each corner of the triangle is the location for an electrode for two leads to record ECG. Consequently, the three limbs of the triangle represent three leads. • Einthoven law: • Voltages of lead I + lead III = lead II • Voltage calculation: • Voltage recorded in each lead as follows • Lead I: left arm - right arm • Lead II: Left foot - right arm • Lead III: Left foot- left arm Einthoven law: Voltage calculation:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 416-416; Solution Pages 423-424","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":416},{"id":"physiology-14-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding ECG leads?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chest leads are bipolar limb leads- horizontal plane."},{"letter":"B","text":"Positive end of the voltmeter is connected to the Wilson terminal."},{"letter":"C","text":"In lead II the negative end is the right arm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Augmented limb lead is bipolar"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) In lead II the negative end is the right arm","explanation":"In lead II the negative end is right arm and the positive end is left foot. ECG Leads: An ECG lead is a pair of electrodes used to detect the potential differences of the heart. Leads in a standard 12-lead ECG: Leads Number of Leads Bipolar limb leads Lead I, Lead II, Lead III Augmented unipolar limb leads (option D) aVR, aVL, aVF Precordial/ chest unipolar leads V1, V2, V3, V4, V5 ,V6 Bipolar Unipolar Both the electrodes are active One electrode is the active or recording electrode and It is the difference between positive - the other one is the indifferent electrode. negative There are six unipolar chest leads and three unipolar Located on the frontal/ coronal plane. limb leads( augmented limb leads) (option A) Located on the frontal/ coronal plane .(option A) Bipolar limb leads: Leads Voltmeter connection Volage vector direction Negative end Positive end Lead I Right arm Left arm Negative to positive Lead II Right arm (option C) Left foot Negative to positive Lead III Left arm Left foot Positive to negative Chest leads: • Unipolar, located on the horizontal plane. • Exploring electrodes on the chest surface and indifferent electrodes are connected to the right arm, left arm and left leg through the high resistance, which is called Wilson’s terminal. (option B) Leads Position V1 4th intercostal space, right sternal border V2 4th intercostal space, left sternal border V3 Midway between V2 and V4 V4 5th intercostal space, midclavicular line V5 5th intercostal space, anterior axillary line V6 5th intercostal space, midaxillary line","sourceRef":"Question Pages 416-416; Solution Pages 424-426","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":416},{"id":"physiology-14-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":7,"question":"According to hexaxial reference system of ECG, the direction of voltage vector of lead aVR is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"+150°"},{"letter":"B","text":"-150°"},{"letter":"C","text":"+30°"},{"letter":"D","text":"-30°"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) -150 °","explanation":"The positive of lead aVR is at -150 ° and the negative of aVR is at +30 ° The axes of augmented limb leads are interspersed between bipolar limb leads as shown as in the picture. This is known as the Hexaxial system. Augmented limb leads: Lea Positive Electro Negative Electrodes View Clinical Use d de aV Right Arm (RA) Left Arm (LA) + Left From the right Right-sided heart R Leg (LL) shoulder abnormalities aVL Left Arm (LA) Right Arm (RA) + From the left Left-sided heart Left Leg (LL) shoulder abnormalities aVF Left Leg (LL) Right Arm (RA) + From the inferior Inferior heart Left Arm (LA) (foot) position abnormalities Vector of augmented limb lead = 3/2 vector of unaugmented limb lead","sourceRef":"Question Pages 416-417; Solution Pages 426-427","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":417},{"id":"physiology-14-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":8,"question":"In a patient presenting with ST elevation in leads V1 and V2 on an ECG, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis and the corresponding coronary artery involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior wall myocardial infarction due to Right Coronary Artery (RCA) occlusion."},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior wall myocardial infarction due to Left Anterior Descending (LAD) artery occlusion."},{"letter":"C","text":"Septal wall myocardial infarction due to Left Anterior Descending (LAD) artery occlusion."},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral wall myocardial infarction due to Left Circumflex (LCX) artery occlusion."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Septal wall myocardial infarction due to Left Anterior Descending (LAD) artery occlusion.","explanation":"Leads V1 and V2: ST elevation in these leads is indicative of damage to the septal wall of the left ventricle. Left Anterior Descending (LAD) Artery: The LAD artery supplies the anterior and septal walls of the left ventricle. Therefore, ST elevation in V1 and V2, which view the septal wall, is typically associated with LAD artery occlusion. ST Elevation Affected Cardiac Wall Coronary Artery Location I, aVL, V5, V6 Lateral Wall of the Left Ventricle Left Circumflex (LCX) or Left Anterior Descending (LAD) II, III, aVF Inferior Wall of the Left Ventricle Right Coronary Artery (RCA) V1, V2 Septal Wall of the Left Ventricle Left Anterior Descending (LAD) V3, V4 Anterior Wall of the Left Ventricle Left Anterior Descending (LAD) V1 to V4 Anterior Wall including Septal Left Anterior Descending (LAD) V7, V8, V9 Posterior Wall of the Left Right Coronary Artery (RCA) or Left Ventricle Circumflex (LCX) ST elevation in leads V1 and V2 is not associated with the anterior wall but with the septal wall. RCA occlusion is more likely to cause inferior wall damage ( Option A) Although LAD artery occlusion is involved, V1 and V2 are indicative of septal, not inferior, wall damage. Inferior wall damage is associated with leads II, III, and aVF ( Option B) Lateral wall myocardial infarction is associated with ST elevation in leads I, aVL, V5, and V6, not V1 and V2. LCX occlusion is involved in lateral wall damage ( Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 417-417; Solution Pages 427-428","sourcePage":417,"sourcePageEnd":417},{"id":"physiology-14-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":9,"question":"If a T wave spans 4 small boxes and 3 vertical boxes on an ECG recording paper moving at a standard speed of 25 mm/second,with standard voltage calibration, what is the duration and amplitude of the T wave?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duration: 0.16 seconds, Amplitude: 0.3 mV"},{"letter":"B","text":"Duration: 0.20 seconds, Amplitude: 0.4 mV"},{"letter":"C","text":"Duration: 0.16 seconds, Amplitude: 0.4 mV"},{"letter":"D","text":"Duration: 0.20 seconds, Amplitude: 0.3 mV"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Duration: 0.16 seconds, Amplitude: 0.3 mV","explanation":"Duration Calculation: • Each small box on the ECG paper represents 0.04 seconds. • If the T wave spans 4 small boxes, the duration of the T wave is: 4 x 0.04= 0.16 sec Amplitude Calculation: • Each small box represents 0.1 mV. • If the T wave spans 3 small boxes in height, the amplitude is: 3 x 0.1 = 0.3 mV Paper Speed: The standard paper speed is 25 mm/second. This means that the ECG paper moves at a rate of 25 millimeters per second. Measurement for Duration: The ECG paper is divided into small and large boxes. Each large box is 5 mm x 5 mm, and each small box within a large box is 1 mm x 1 mm. 1 Large Box: Represents 0.20 seconds (200 milliseconds). 1 Small Box: Represents 0.04 seconds (40 milliseconds) Amplitude: The amplitude of the ECG waveforms is measured in millivolts (mV). Each vertical small box represents 0.1 mV. Each large box represents 0.5 mV (since it contains 5 small boxes). Although the duration is correct, the amplitude given is for a different number of boxes ( Option B) The duration calculation is correct, but the amplitude is not accurate for the given number of boxes ( Option C) The amplitude is correct, but the duration provided is for a different number of boxes ( Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 417-417; Solution Pages 428-429","sourcePage":417,"sourcePageEnd":417},{"id":"physiology-14-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following can be considered as Left axis deviation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"+30°"},{"letter":"B","text":"-60°"},{"letter":"C","text":"+90°"},{"letter":"D","text":"-10°"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) -60°","explanation":"Left axis deviation falls between -90° to -30° The normal cardiac axis value- • Normal axis deviation: -30° to +110° • Left axis deviation: -90° to -30° • Right axis deviation: +110° to +180° Methods to calculate the mean cardiac axis- • Lead I (+) and aVF (+): Normal axis • Lead I (-) and aVF (+): Right axis deviation • Lead I (-) and aVF (-): Extreme right axis deviation • Lead I (+) and aVF (-): Left axis deviation Uses the net deflections in Leads I, II, and III to calculate the axis. • Calculate the net QRS deflection for each of the three limb leads. • Use vector addition to combine these deflections. • Calculate the resultant vector and determine its angle. Involves plotting the QRS vectors on a graph to find the axis. • Measure the net deflection of the QRS complex in at least two leads. • Plot these values on a coordinate system. • Use vector addition to determine the overall direction of the QRS vector. Identifies the isoelectric lead (lead with the smallest net QRS deflection). • Find the lead with the most isoelectric QRS complex. • The QRS axis is perpendicular to this lead. • Use adjacent leads to refine the exact axis using hexaxial system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 417-418; Solution Pages 429-429","sourcePage":417,"sourcePageEnd":418},{"id":"physiology-14-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-14","chapter":"ECG","topic":"ECG","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":11,"question":"In the His bundle electrogram (HBE), what does the H wave denote?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Atrial depolarization"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventricular depolarization"},{"letter":"C","text":"Depolarization of the His bundle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Repolarization of the ventricles"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Depolarization of the His bundle","explanation":"H wave in His bundle electrogram represents the depolarization of the His bundle His Bundle Electrogram (HBE): • Invasive ECG procedure • A catheter containing an electrode at its tip is inserted through an arm vein into the right atrium close to the tricuspid valve. The electrode records the HBE. Waveforms in the His Bundle Electrogram A Wave The A wave represents atrial (Option A) depolarization. H Wave The H wave represents (Option C) depolarization of the His bundle’s upper part. V Wave: The V wave represents ventricular (Option B) depolarization. Intervals in the His Bundle Electrogram P-A Interval The PA interval is the time from the onset of the P wave on the surface ECG to the onset of the A wave on the His bundle electrogram. The PA interval typically ranges from 25 to 55 milliseconds. A-H Interval The A-H interval is the time between the onset of the A wave and the onset of the H wave. Typically ranges from 50 to 120 milliseconds. This interval represents conduction time through the AV node. A prolonged A-H interval suggests AV nodal conduction delay. H-V Interval Typically ranges from 35 to 55 milliseconds. This interval represents conduction time through the His-Purkinje system. A prolonged H-V interval suggests an infranodal conduction delay or block.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 418-418; Solution Pages 430-431","sourcePage":418,"sourcePageEnd":418},{"id":"physiology-15-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A physiologist is studying blood flow in a network of four parallel blood vessels. The effective resistance of the network of vessels is 4.8 R units. The pressure at the proximal end of the network is 150 mmHg, and the pressure at the distal end is 90 mmHg. What is the total blood flow rate through the network of vessels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"8 L/s"},{"letter":"B","text":"12.5 L/s"},{"letter":"C","text":"10.5 L/s"},{"letter":"D","text":"20 L/s"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Effective Perfusion Pressure = 150 mmHg − 90 mmHg= 60 mmHg Using the blood flow analogue of Ohm's law, ■ total = 4.8R units Flow = Pressure / Resistance = 60/4.8 =12.5 L/s Factors Influencing Blood Flow within vessels: • Pressure differences • Resistance Flow, Pressure, and Resistance Relationship: Blood Flow Analog of Ohm’s law: Blood flow (F) in any part of the circulatory system is determined by the effective perfusion pressure (P) and the resistance (R) it encounters: F = P/R • The Poiseuille-Hagen formula describes the relationship between flow in a narrow tube, fluid viscosity, and tube radius. This formula is represented as: Where: • F = flow • PA − PB = pressure difference between the tube's two ends • η = viscosity • r = tube radius • L = tube length","sourceRef":"Question Pages 432-432; Solution Pages 437-438","sourcePage":432,"sourcePageEnd":432},{"id":"physiology-15-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capillaries provide maximum total peripheral resistance"},{"letter":"B","text":"Arteries contain the most blood volume in the systemic circulation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fenestrated capillaries has gaps size ranging from 300-600 nm"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-V anastomoses found in earlobes have thick, muscular walls"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) A-V anastomoses found in earlobes have thick, muscular walls","explanation":"Arteriovenous anastomoses (A-V anastomoses) , also known as shunts, are direct connections between arterioles and venules that bypass the capillary bed. They have thick, muscular walls and are abundantly innervated, likely by vasoconstrictor nerve fibers. These shunts are found in the fingers, palms, and earlobes. Other types of Blood vessels: Blood Vessel Type Blood Volum e Arteries 8% Carry oxygenated blood away from the heart. Arteries have elastic tissue in their walls, allowing them to stretch and recoil with the pumping of the heart. Arterioles 1% They are the primary site of resistance to blood flow Contain a high proportion of smooth muscle Capillaries 5% Tiny vessels with thin walls for the exchange of gases, nutrients, and waste products between blood and tissues. Veins 54% (Option Veins have thinner walls allowing them to distend easily B) and accommodate larger volumes of blood. Contain venous valves to prevent backflow of blood Types of capillaries: Capillaries, due to their large cross-sectional area, provide low resistance to blood flow. The arterioles actually provide the maximum total peripheral resistance ( Option A) Veins, not arteries, contain the most blood volume in the systemic circulation ( Option B) The typical pore size in fenestrated capillaries is around 60-80 nm( Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 432-432; Solution Pages 438-439","sourcePage":432,"sourcePageEnd":432},{"id":"physiology-15-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following best describes the characteristics of laminar blood flow?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Laminar blood flow is characterized by chaotic and irregular movements with significant mixing of fluid layers."},{"letter":"B","text":"Laminar blood flow occurs at high velocities and low fluid viscosity, leading to smooth and orderly movement."},{"letter":"C","text":"Laminar blood flow occurs at low velocities and high fluid viscosity, with fluid particles moving in parallel layers."},{"letter":"D","text":"Laminar blood flow is responsible for generating Korotkoff sounds during blood pressure measurement."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Laminar blood flow occurs at low velocities and high fluid viscosity, with fluid particles moving in parallel layers.","explanation":"Laminar blood flow is where the fluid moves in smooth, parallel layers with minimal mixing, typically occurring at low velocities and high viscosity. • Smooth, orderly flow of fluid in parallel layers with no disruption between the layers. • Characteristics : Fluid particles move in straight, parallel paths. Velocity of fluid is highest at the center and decreases towards the vessel walls. Occurs at lower velocities and with lower fluid density or higher fluid viscosity (Option B) . • Fluid particles move in straight, parallel paths. • Velocity of fluid is highest at the center and decreases towards the vessel walls. • Occurs at lower velocities and with lower fluid density or higher fluid viscosity (Option B) . • Fluid particles move in straight, parallel paths. • Velocity of fluid is highest at the center and decreases towards the vessel walls. • Occurs at lower velocities and with lower fluid density or higher fluid viscosity (Option B) . • Chaotic, irregular flow where fluid particles move in various directions, often forming eddies. • Characteristics : Disorganized movement with mixing of fluid layers (Option A) . Velocity varies unpredictably at any point in the fluid. Higher resistance compared to laminar flow. Occurs at higher velocities, with higher fluid density, or lower fluid viscosity. • Disorganized movement with mixing of fluid layers (Option A) . • Velocity varies unpredictably at any point in the fluid. • Higher resistance compared to laminar flow. • Occurs at higher velocities, with higher fluid density, or lower fluid viscosity. • Disorganized movement with mixing of fluid layers (Option A) . • Velocity varies unpredictably at any point in the fluid. • Higher resistance compared to laminar flow. • Occurs at higher velocities, with higher fluid density, or lower fluid viscosity. • Dimensionless numbers used to predict flow patterns in fluid dynamics. • Interpretation : Re < 2000 : Flow is typically laminar. Re > 4000 : Flow is typically turbulent. Re 2000-4000 : Transitional flow, where both laminar and turbulent flow may exist. • Re < 2000 : Flow is typically laminar. • Re > 4000 : Flow is typically turbulent. • Re 2000-4000 : Transitional flow, where both laminar and turbulent flow may exist. • Re < 2000 : Flow is typically laminar. • Re > 4000 : Flow is typically turbulent. • Re 2000-4000 : Transitional flow, where both laminar and turbulent flow may exist. • Sounds heard while measuring blood pressure using a sphygmomanometer and stethoscope. • Mechanism : Created by turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery when external pressure is between systolic and diastolic pressures. As cuff pressure drops below systolic pressure, blood begins to flow turbulently (Option D) , producing Korotkoff sounds. When cuff pressure drops below diastolic pressure, laminar flow is restored, and sounds disappear. • Created by turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery when external pressure is between systolic and diastolic pressures. • As cuff pressure drops below systolic pressure, blood begins to flow turbulently (Option D) , producing Korotkoff sounds. • When cuff pressure drops below diastolic pressure, laminar flow is restored, and sounds disappear. • Created by turbulent blood flow in the brachial artery when external pressure is between systolic and diastolic pressures. • As cuff pressure drops below systolic pressure, blood begins to flow turbulently (Option D) , producing Korotkoff sounds. • When cuff pressure drops below diastolic pressure, laminar flow is restored, and sounds disappear.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 432-433; Solution Pages 440-441","sourcePage":432,"sourcePageEnd":433},{"id":"physiology-15-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly applies the law of Laplace in cardiovascular physiology?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capillaries are prone to rupture due to high wall tension."},{"letter":"B","text":"Dilated hearts have to work harder due to increased myocardial tension."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased flow velocity in narrow vessels reduces distending pressure."},{"letter":"D","text":"The total energy in a tube or blood vessel remains constant."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Dilated hearts have to work harder due to increased myocardial tension.","explanation":"According to the law of Laplace, dilated hearts require greater myocardial tension to produce a given pressure, increasing cardiac workload. Princi Definition Applications ple Law of The tension in the wall of a cylinder Capillaries : Low tension in walls Lapla is equal to the product of transmural due to small radius, resistant to ce pressure and radius divided by wall rupture. (Option A) thickness. Dilated Hearts : Increased radius T= P.r/ 2w ( T= Tension, P= requires more tension, increasing Transmural pressure, r= Radius, w= cardiac workload.(Option B) Wall thickness) Berno The total energy in a tube or blood Pressure Measurement : Lower side ulli Pri vessel (kinetic energy + potential arm pressure in a T tube due to nciple energy) remains constant. (Option kinetic energy of flow. D) Atherosclerotic Plaques : Increased flow velocity in narrow vessels reduces distending pressure, maintaining vessel narrowing. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 433-433; Solution Pages 441-442","sourcePage":433,"sourcePageEnd":433},{"id":"physiology-15-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 45-y/o male patient presents with a blood pressure reading of 150/90 mmHg. Which of the following statements about his pulse pressure (PP) and mean arterial pressure (MAP) is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pulse pressure is 40 mmHg, and MAP is 110 mmHg."},{"letter":"B","text":"Pulse pressure is 60 mmHg, and MAP is 100 mmHg."},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulse pressure is 60 mmHg, and MAP is 110 mmHg."},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulse pressure is 40 mmHg, and MAP is 100 mmHg."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Pulse pressure is 60 mmHg, and MAP is 110 mmHg.","explanation":"Pulse Pressure (PP) : Pulse pressure is calculated as the difference between systolic blood pressure (SBP) and diastolic blood pressure (DBP). PP= SBP−DBP= 150−90= 60 mmHg Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) : Mean arterial pressure is calculated using the formula: MAP= DBP+1/3(SBP−DBP)= 90+1/3(150−90)=90+20= 110 mmHg Feature Pulse Pressure (PP) Mean Arterial Pressure (MAP) Definition Difference between systolic and Average pressure in arteries during diastolic blood pressure one cardiac cycle Calculation PP=SBP−DBP MAP=DBP+1/3(SBP−DBP) Normal Range 40-60 mmHg 70-100 mmHg Clinical Significance Indicates cardiovascular health and A better indicator of perfusion to heart function vital organs Implications of High Risk of cardiovascular events Can indicate a risk of hypertension Values and organ damage Implications of Low This may suggest poor heart This may indicate poor perfusion of Values function vital organs","sourceRef":"Question Pages 433-433; Solution Pages 442-442","sourcePage":433,"sourcePageEnd":433},{"id":"physiology-15-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is the fastest acting method of BP regulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capillary fluid shift"},{"letter":"B","text":"Baroreceptor reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Renin-angiotensin vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Renal body fluid mechanism"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Baroreceptor reflex","explanation":"Time Frame Mechanisms Functions Short-term (seconds to Autonomic nervous system Constrict veins to increase blood minutes) Baroreceptor reflex flow to the heart Central nervous system ischemic Increase heart rate and contractility response Constrict peripheral arterioles to Chemoreceptor mechanism elevate BP Intermediate-term Angiotensin mediated Renin triggers angiotensin II, (minutes to hours) vasoconstriction (Option C) constricting blood vessels and Stress relaxation increasing BP Capillary fluid shift (Option A) Sustained high BP leads to gradual pressure reduction Fluid absorption or loss in capillaries adjusts blood volume and BP Long-term (days to Renal-body fluid mechanism Regulate salt and water excretion to years) (Option D) maintain BP stability Aldosterone influence Aldosterone promotes salt and water retention when BP is low Aspect Details Mechanism Baroreceptors in carotid sinus and aortic arch detect BP changes Increased BP stretches receptors, sending signals to medulla via glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves Medulla inhibits vasoconstrictor center and stimulates vagal parasympathetic center Actions Vasodilation of blood vessels Decreased heart rate and contraction strength Reduced BP due to lower peripheral resistance and cardiac output Importance Maintains BP stability during activities like standing Fastest acting (Option B) Tends to reset within 1-2 days for long-term regulation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 433-433; Solution Pages 443-443","sourcePage":433,"sourcePageEnd":433},{"id":"physiology-15-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A patient undergoing PCI for myocardial infarction suddenly develops bradycardia, hypotension, and episodes of apnoea.Which of the following reflexes is most likely mediated by chemosensitive vagal C fibers in the cardiopulmonary region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cushing reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bainbridge reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bezold-Jarisch reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Baroreceptor reflex"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Bezold-Jarisch reflex","explanation":"Myocardial ischemia and reperfusion activates chemosensitive vagal C fibers in the cardiopulmonary region causing Bezold–Jarisch reflex leading to profound bradycardia, hypotension, and a brief period of apnea followed by rapid shallow breathing. Bezold-Jarisch reflex or coronary chemoreflex: • Activators : Capsaicin, serotonin, phenylbiguanide, veratridine and pathophysiologic conditions like reperfusion after myocardial ischemia ( increased production of oxygen radicals and agents used as radio contrast for coronary angiography). • Mechanism: Activation of chemosensitive vagal C fibers (Option C) in the cardiopulmonary region (juxtacapillary region of alveoli, ventricles, atria, great veins, and pulmonary artery). • Causing bradycardia, hypotension, and a brief apnea period followed by rapid shallow breathing. Cushing reflex (Option A): A combination of hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respiration is known as the triad of the Cushing reflex. Bainbridge reflex(Option B): Following vagotomy, the reflex is abolished as the responses are mediated by vagus nerves shown below. Baroreceptor reflex (Option D) or sino-aortic reflex: It quickly adjusts heart rate and vascular tone in response to changes in blood pressure to maintain cardiovascular stability.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 434-434; Solution Pages 444-445","sourcePage":434,"sourcePageEnd":434},{"id":"physiology-15-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A patient with massive blood loss is found to have increased sympathetic nervous system activity, resulting in elevated blood pressure. Which combination of factors primarily triggers the central nervous system (CNS) ischemic response?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxia and hypercapnia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoxia and hypocapnia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoglycemia and hypoxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglycemia and hypercapnia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Hypoxia and hypercapnia","explanation":"Decreased blood flow due to massive hemorrhage here causes significant drop in blood pressure leading to severe hypoxia and hypercapnia which activates Vasomotor center, increasing blood pressure and restoring cerebral perfusion. CNS ischemic response: • Vasomotor center (VMC) is very sensitive to local hypoxia and local hypercapnia causing strong sympathetic system activation as follows. (Option A) • Failure of this response leads to irreversible shock as this is the last physiological reflex to correct blood pressure. Hypoglycemia does not trigger the central nervous system (CNS) ischemic response.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 434-434; Solution Pages 445-446","sourcePage":434,"sourcePageEnd":434},{"id":"physiology-15-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":9,"question":"[Question stem not represented in the PDF text layer; source page contains a visual/text prompt.]","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulation of chemoreceptors leading to hypertension"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deactivation of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension"},{"letter":"C","text":"No response of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deactivation of chemoreceptors but no response on blood pressure"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Stimulation of chemoreceptors leading to hypertension","explanation":"In the scenario, severe hypoxemia and hypercapnia stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors and afferent signals stimulate cardio inhibitory center (CIC) to produce bradycardia and vasomotor center (VMC) to produce vasoconstriction as a part of the primary effect. Chemoreceptor reflex and BP: • Afferent from the carotid body: IX cranial nerve • Afferent from aortic body: X cranial nerve","sourceRef":"Question Pages 434-434; Solution Pages 446-446","sourcePage":434,"sourcePageEnd":434},{"id":"physiology-15-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 55-year-old patient with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) presents with acute exacerbation. ABG shows severe hypoxemia and hypercapnia. How do peripheral chemoreceptors respond to these changes, and its effect on blood pressure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulation of chemoreceptors leading to hypertension"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deactivation of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension"},{"letter":"C","text":"No response of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deactivation of chemoreceptors but no response on blood pressure"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Stimulation of chemoreceptors leading to hypertension","explanation":"In the scenario, severe hypoxemia and hypercapnia, stimulates peripheral chemoreceptors and afferent signals stimulate cardio inhibitory center (CIC) to produce bradycardia and vasomotor center (VMC) to produce vasoconstriction as apart of primary effect. Chemoreceptor reflex and BP: • Afferent from carotid body: IX cranial nerve • Afferent from aortic body: X cranial nerve Deactivation of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension (Option B) – Severe hypoxemia and hypercapnia stimulate, not deactivate, chemoreceptors. No response of chemoreceptors leading to hypotension (Option C) – Peripheral chemoreceptors actively respond to hypoxemia by increasing sympathetic output. Deactivation of chemoreceptors but no response on blood pressure (Option D) – Chemoreceptors are stimulated, not deactivated, and they influence blood pressure via vasoconstriction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 435-435; Solution Pages 447-447","sourcePage":435,"sourcePageEnd":435},{"id":"physiology-15-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 55 y/o man is undergoing a treadmill stress test. Which of the following is true about coronary perfusion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primarily occurs during left ventricle systole"},{"letter":"B","text":"It is the difference between aortic systolic pressure and diastolic pressure."},{"letter":"C","text":"Right ventricle perfusion decreases during systole than diastole."},{"letter":"D","text":"Regulation of coronary blood flow involves local metabolites"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Regulation of coronary blood flow involves local metabolites","explanation":"Regulation of coronary blood flow involves primarily local metabolites like adenosine, hydrogen ions, lactic acid, potassium ions and certain prostaglandins by mechanism known as metabolite mediated vasodilatation. Coronary perfusion: Key points Explanation Coronary circulation Supplies heart muscles Major coronary arteries Left and right coronary arteries Rate of blood flow in myocardium 250ml/minute Percentage of blood flow in heart 5% of cardiac output • The right and left coronary arteries originate from the coronary sinuses at the aortic root. • These arteries extend along the epicardium, and then branch through the myocardium to form subendocardial plexuses to perfuse the myocardium. • Myocardial contraction during systole compresses arterial branches and prevents perfusion. • Coronary perfusion occurs during diastole rather than systole from epicardial to endocardial regions. Coronary perfusion pressure: (Option B) • Coronary Perfusion Pressure (CPP) = Aortic Diastolic Pressure – Left Ventricular end-diastolic Pressure (LVEDP). • Aortic systolic pressure; 120 mmHg • Aortic diastolic pressure: 80 mmHg • Blood flow to the left ventricle occurs only during the early stage of diastole (isovolumetric relaxation) (Option A) • Blood flow in the right ventricle occurs during systole and diastole. Blood flow increases in systole than in diastole. (Option C) Regulation of coronary perfusion: Chemical Factors: • Regulation of blood flow occurs primarily through vasodilation caused by local metabolites (Option D) which are formed due to 70-80% oxygen extraction of myocardium from the coronary arteries. • Metabolites are CO2 , H+, K+, lactate, prostaglandins, adenine nucleotides, and adenosine. Neural factors: • α-adrenergic receptors on coronary arterioles: vasoconstriction • β-adrenergic receptors on coronary arterioles: vasodilation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 435-435; Solution Pages 448-449","sourcePage":435,"sourcePageEnd":435},{"id":"physiology-15-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements about myocardial muscle tension is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Preload primarily affects the velocity of muscle shortening"},{"letter":"B","text":"Afterload does not affect the duration of systole"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increasing preload leads to decreased muscle tension"},{"letter":"D","text":"The force-velocity relationship in cardiac muscle is inverse and hyperbolic"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) The force-velocity relationship in cardiac muscle is inverse and hyperbolic","explanation":"The force-velocity relationship in cardiac muscle is inverse and hyperbolic. This fundamental property of cardiac muscle mechanics describes how the velocity of muscle shortening is related to the force (or tension) developed by the muscle. • As the load (force) against which the muscle contracts increases, the velocity of shortening decreases. • When the muscle contracts against a lighter load, it can shorten more quickly. • This relationship is not linear but follows a hyperbolic curve. • At zero load, the muscle shortens at its maximum velocity (Vmax), while at maximum isometric force, the shortening velocity is zero. • This is how the heart can maintain adequate cardiac output over a wide range of afterloads by adjusting its contractile properties. Preload primarily affects the force of contraction, not the velocity of muscle shortening. This is based on the Frank-Starling mechanism (Option A) Afterload does affect the duration of systole. Increased afterload can prolong the duration of systole as the ventricle requires more time to generate sufficient pressure to open the aortic valve and eject blood (Option B) More EDV( which is the best determinant of preload) leads to increased tension in the cardiac muscle. ↑ EDV→ ↑ ventricular muscle length → ↑ muscle contraction force (contractility of the heart). This is known as the Frank-Starling law of the heart (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 435-435; Solution Pages 449-450","sourcePage":435,"sourcePageEnd":435},{"id":"physiology-15-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":13,"question":"According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which of the following statements is incorrect regarding the intracranial pressure (ICP) homeostasis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"An increase in cerebrospinal fluid volume can be compensated by a decrease in brain tissue volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"An increase in blood volume can be offset by a decrease in cerebrospinal fluid volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"The sum of volumes of brain tissue, blood, and cerebrospinal fluid remains constant"},{"letter":"D","text":"A significant increase in one intracranial component always leads to a proportional increase in ICP"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) An increase in cerebrospinal fluid volume can be compensated by a decrease in brain tissue volume","explanation":"The Monro-Kellie doctrine states that the cranial compartment is incompressible, and the volume inside the cranium is fixed hence Option A in the incorrect statement.The doctrine describes the relationship between the volumes of brain tissue, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), and blood within the cranium. • According to this principle, an increase in one component must be offset by a decrease in one or both of the other components to maintain a constant intracranial pressure. • Brain tissue is not easily compressible, and its volume does not acutely decrease to compensate for increases in other components . • Therefore, compensatory mechanisms primarily involve changes in CSF and blood volumes. During a rise in intracranial pressure, CSF can be displaced into the spinal subarachnoid space or its production can be reduced, and/ or venous blood can be displaced from the cranial cavity. • During a rise in intracranial pressure, CSF can be displaced into the spinal subarachnoid space or its production can be reduced, and/ or venous blood can be displaced from the cranial cavity. • During a rise in intracranial pressure, CSF can be displaced into the spinal subarachnoid space or its production can be reduced, and/ or venous blood can be displaced from the cranial cavity. An increase in blood volume can be compensated by a decrease in CSF volume (Option B) The Principle of the Monro-Kellie doctrine is that the sum of the volumes of brain tissue, blood, and CSF must remain constant within the rigid confines of the skull (Option C) Small increases in the volume of one component can often be compensated without a significant change in ICP, due to the compensatory mechanisms described earlier. However, once these compensatory mechanisms are exhausted, even small volume increases can lead to dramatic rises in ICP (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 435-436; Solution Pages 450-451","sourcePage":435,"sourcePageEnd":436},{"id":"physiology-15-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-15","chapter":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topic":"Hemodynamics, BP Regulation, and Regional Circulation","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":14,"question":"In a scientific research, large group of non-hypertensive individuals (systolic blood pressures < 120 mm Hg) were studied every 2 years throughout their adult lives. Effects of age and sex on arterial pressure components (systolic and diastolic BP) in the study are given below. Fall of diastolic pressure after the middle age is due to:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase stiffness of arteries"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreases stiffness of arteries"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decrease in contractile force of heart"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease in total peripheral resistance"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increase stiffness of arteries","explanation":"Fall of diastolic pressure after the middle age is due to increase in stiffness of arteries Blood Pressure Changes with Age: • Systolic Pressure : Increases steadily throughout life, even in individuals with normal initial levels (<120 mm Hg at ages 50–60). • Diastolic Pressure : Rises with age but starts to decline in middle age due to increased arterial stiffness. • Pulse Pressure : Increases as the gap between systolic and diastolic pressure widens. • Gender Differences : Women have lower blood pressures than men until around ages 55–65. This may contribute to their longer lifespan, as lower blood pressure is linked to a reduced risk of heart attacks and strokes. Decrease in Stiffness of Arteries (Option B) : Aging typically increases arterial stiffness. Decrease in Contractile Force of Heart (Option C) : Diastolic pressure changes are more related to arterial stiffness than heart contractility. Decrease in Total Peripheral Resistance (Option D) : Aging often increases peripheral resistance, not decreases it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 436-436; Solution Pages 451-452","sourcePage":436,"sourcePageEnd":436},{"id":"physiology-16-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":1,"question":"In the jugular venous pressure (JVP) waveform, which of the following statements accurately describes the \"c wave\"?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The \"c wave\" is caused by atrial systole"},{"letter":"B","text":"The \"c wave\" arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric ventricular contraction"},{"letter":"C","text":"The \"c wave\" corresponds to the rise in atrial pressure as blood fills the atria"},{"letter":"D","text":"The \"c wave\" is influenced by the fluctuations in venous pressure caused by respiration"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The \"c wave\" arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric ventricular contraction, transmitted through the closed tricuspid valve.","explanation":"The \"c wave\" arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric ventricular contraction, which is transmitted through the closed tricuspid valve. a wave Caused by atrial systole. (Option A ruled out) Atrial contraction results in a temporary halt in venous inflow. Some blood flows back into the veins, causing a rise in venous pressure. c wave Arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric ventricular contraction. (Option B) Pressure is transmitted through the closed and bulging tricuspid valve. x descent Ventricular systole pulls the tricuspid valve down v wave Corresponds to the rise in atrial pressure as blood fills the atria. (Option C ruled out) Occurs during diastole while the tricuspid valve is still closed. y descent Opening of tricuspid valve causing emptying of right atrium into ventricle Respiration Influence : • Venous pressure decreases during inspiration due to negative intrathoracic pressure. (Option D ruled pout) • Venous pressure rises during expiration.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 453-453; Solution Pages 460-461","sourcePage":453,"sourcePageEnd":453},{"id":"physiology-16-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the differentiation pathway of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) in the production of granulocytes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"HSCs differentiate directly into granulocytes without intermediate stages."},{"letter":"B","text":"HSCs differentiate into common lymphoid progenitors, which then produce granulocytes."},{"letter":"C","text":"HSCs differentiate into common myeloid progenitors, which then give rise to granulocyte-monocyte progenitors."},{"letter":"D","text":"HSCs differentiate into megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors, which then produce granulocytes."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) HSCs differentiate into common myeloid progenitors, which then give rise to granulocyte-monocyte progenitors.","explanation":"HSCs first differentiate into common myeloid progenitors (CMPs). CMPs then differentiate into granulocyte-monocyte progenitors (GMPs), which give rise to granulocytes (neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils). Hematopoiesis- • Definition : Hematopoiesis is the process of blood cell formation and development from hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) in the bone marrow. • Location : Primarily occurs in the bone marrow, but also in the liver and spleen during fetal development. • Hematopoietic Stem Cells (HSCs) : Multipotent stem cells that can differentiate into all types of blood cells. • Stages : Involves differentiation from HSCs to progenitor cells, which further differentiate into various mature blood cells. • Lineages : Myeloid Lineage : Produces red blood cells, platelets, monocytes, neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. Lymphoid Lineage : Produces T cells, B cells, and natural killer (NK) cells. • Myeloid Lineage : Produces red blood cells, platelets, monocytes, neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. • Lymphoid Lineage : Produces T cells, B cells, and natural killer (NK) cells. • Myeloid Lineage : Produces red blood cells, platelets, monocytes, neutrophils, eosinophils, and basophils. • Lymphoid Lineage : Produces T cells, B cells, and natural killer (NK) cells. HSCs differentiate directly into granulocytes without intermediate stages. ( Option A) Hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) do not differentiate directly into granulocytes; they go through several intermediate stages. HSCs differentiate into common lymphoid progenitors, which then produce granulocytes. (Option B) Common lymphoid progenitors produce lymphocytes (T cells, B cells, and NK cells), not granulocytes. HSCs differentiate into megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors, which then produce granulocytes (Option D) Megakaryocyte-erythroid progenitors (MEPs) give rise to megakaryocytes (which produce platelets) and erythrocytes (red blood cells), not granulocytes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 453-453; Solution Pages 461-463","sourcePage":453,"sourcePageEnd":453},{"id":"physiology-16-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 65-year-old male presents to the emergency department with severe chest pain and shortness of breath. His blood pressure is 80/50 mmHg, heart rate is 110 bpm, and has cold, clammy skin and elevated JVP. ECG shows ST-segment elevations in lead II, III and aVF. The patient is also oliguric. Which of the following is the most likely underlying cause of this patient's symptoms and signs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Myocardial infarction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sepsis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adrenal insufficiency"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cardiac tamponade"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Myocardial infarction","explanation":"ST segment elevation in leads II, III and aVF corresponds to inferior wall Myocardial infarction, which leads to cardiogenic shock as in this scenario. Shocks: Types Pathophysiology Causes Cardia of shoc c outp k ut Cardio Inadequate pumping of Acute myocardial ↓ genic the heart due to infarction (Option myocardial function. Heart failure A) Cardiac arrhythmias Hypovo Decreased amount of fluid Acute hemorrhage ↓ lemic in the vascular system Burn Metabolic: adrenal insufficiency (Option C ruled out) , diabetic ketoacidosis Severe diarrhea or vomiting. Distribu Sudden increase in the Neurogenic shock ↓ tive vascular compartment Anaphylactic shock capacity due to acute Septic shock (Option B marked vasodilation ruled out) Obstru Obstruction to the ejection Cardiac tamponade ↓ ctive of blood from the ventricle (Option D ruled out) Constrictive pericarditis Cardiac tumor Tension pneumothorax","sourceRef":"Question Pages 453-454; Solution Pages 463-463","sourcePage":453,"sourcePageEnd":454},{"id":"physiology-16-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which physiological mechanism best describes the Frank-Starling law of the heart?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The heart's stroke volume decreases as myocardial fiber length increases."},{"letter":"B","text":"The heart's stroke volume is regulated primarily by sympathetic nervous system stimulation."},{"letter":"C","text":"The heart's stroke volume increases in response to increased end-diastolic volume due to the length-tension relationship of cardiac muscle fibers."},{"letter":"D","text":"The heart's stroke volume remains unchanged regardless of changes in venous return, due to intrinsic pacemaker activity"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The heart's stroke volume increases in response to increased end-diastolic volume due to the length-tension relationship of cardiac muscle fibers.","explanation":"This option correctly describes the Frank-Starling law. It states that as the end-diastolic volume (the volume of blood filling the heart) increases, the myocardial fibers stretch more, which enhances their contraction strength due to the optimal length-tension relationship, thereby increasing stroke volume. The Frank-Starling law of the heart describes the relationship between the volume of blood filling the heart (end-diastolic volume) and the force of contraction during the next heartbeat (stroke volume) . Mechanism: • Preload: Increased venous return leads to increased end-diastolic volume. • Myocardial Stretch: Greater end-diastolic volume stretches the myocardial fibers. • Force of Contraction: The stretched fibers generate a more forceful contraction due to the optimal overlap of actin and myosin filaments (length-tension relationship). The heart's stroke volume decreases as myocardial fiber length increases beyond the optimal length (Option A) It is incorrect because the Frank-Starling law indicates that stroke volume increases with increased myocardial fiber length, up to an optimal point, rather than decreasing. The heart's stroke volume is regulated primarily by sympathetic nervous system stimulation. (Option B) This option is incorrect because, while sympathetic nervous system stimulation does affect heart function, the Frank-Starling law specifically refers to the intrinsic ability of the heart to adjust stroke volume based on changes in end-diastolic volume, not primarily on external neural influences. The heart's stroke volume remains unchanged regardless of changes in venous return, due to intrinsic pacemaker activity. (Option D) This option is incorrect because the Frank-Starling law explains that stroke volume changes in response to venous return and end-diastolic volume, not that it remains unchanged.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 454-454; Solution Pages 464-464","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":454},{"id":"physiology-16-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 50-year-old patient with heart failure is evaluated for changes in myocardial contractility. Which of the following statements best describes the impact of decreased contractility on the Frank-Starling curve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Shifts the curve upward and to the left, resulting in a greater stroke volume at any given preload."},{"letter":"B","text":"Shifts the curve downward, resulting in a decreased stroke volume at any given preload."},{"letter":"C","text":"Does not affect the Frank-Starling curve but increases the heart rate."},{"letter":"D","text":"Shifts the curve upward and to the right, resulting in a decreased stroke volume at any given preload."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Shifts the curve downward and to the right, resulting in a decreased stroke volume at any given preload.","explanation":"Decreased contractility shifts the Frank-Starling curve downward and to the right, leading to a reduced stroke volume for any given preload- Negative Inotropic Effect. Myocardial Contractility • Definition: The intrinsic ability of the myocardium to contract and generate force, independent of preload (end-diastolic volume) and afterload (resistance against which the heart pumps). Changes in contractility shift the Frank-Starling curve (relationship between stroke volume and end-diastolic volume). • The intrinsic ability of the myocardium to contract and generate force, independent of preload (end-diastolic volume) and afterload (resistance against which the heart pumps). • Changes in contractility shift the Frank-Starling curve (relationship between stroke volume and end-diastolic volume). • Impact on Frank-Starling Curve: Increased Contractility (Positive Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve upward and to the left. Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. Decreased Contractility (Negative Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve downward and to the right. • Increased Contractility (Positive Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve upward and to the left. Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. • Shifts the curve upward and to the left. • Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. • Decreased Contractility (Negative Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve downward and to the right. • Shifts the curve downward and to the right. • The intrinsic ability of the myocardium to contract and generate force, independent of preload (end-diastolic volume) and afterload (resistance against which the heart pumps). • Changes in contractility shift the Frank-Starling curve (relationship between stroke volume and end-diastolic volume). • Increased Contractility (Positive Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve upward and to the left. Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. • Shifts the curve upward and to the left. • Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. • Decreased Contractility (Negative Inotropic Effects): Shifts the curve downward and to the right. • Shifts the curve downward and to the right. • Shifts the curve upward and to the left. • Results in a greater stroke volume at any given preload. • Shifts the curve downward and to the right. The Bowditch Effect Key Points • Alternative Names: Treppe phenomenon, staircase phenomenon, frequency-dependent activation • Definition: An increase in heart rate enhances the force of contraction of myocardial cells with each heartbeat, independent of other influences. • An increase in heart rate enhances the force of contraction of myocardial cells with each heartbeat, independent of other influences. • Mechanism: Related to calcium ion handling and mishandling in cardiac cells. Involves proteins crucial for excitation-contraction coupling, such as sarcoplasmic endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA). • Related to calcium ion handling and mishandling in cardiac cells. • Involves proteins crucial for excitation-contraction coupling, such as sarcoplasmic endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA). • An increase in heart rate enhances the force of contraction of myocardial cells with each heartbeat, independent of other influences. • Related to calcium ion handling and mishandling in cardiac cells. • Involves proteins crucial for excitation-contraction coupling, such as sarcoplasmic endoplasmic reticulum calcium-ATPase (SERCA). Aspect Details Positive Bowditch Effect Seen in healthy hearts, related to SER Ca load alterations. Negative Bowditch Pathological, due to diminished SERCA protein Effect expression, hallmark of heart failure. Clinical Significance Shift from increasing heart rate to beta-blockers for improved cardiac function in heart failure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 454-454; Solution Pages 465-466","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":454},{"id":"physiology-16-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 55-year-old patient undergoes a blood transfusion, which results in a significant increase in blood volume. Which of the following best describes the physiological response to activation of the Bainbridge reflex due to an increase in blood volume?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased heart rate and decreased contractility"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increase in heart rate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased heart rate and increased contractility"},{"letter":"D","text":"No change in heart rate or contractility"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Increase in heart rate","explanation":"The Bainbridge reflex, also known as the atrial reflex, is triggered by the stretching of the atrial walls due to increased venous return, resulting in an increased heart rate. • Intrinsic Regulation: Frank-Starling Mechanism Describes how increased stretching of the cardiac muscle (within physiological limits) due to more incoming blood results in a stronger contraction. • Describes how increased stretching of the cardiac muscle (within physiological limits) due to more incoming blood results in a stronger contraction. • Describes how increased stretching of the cardiac muscle (within physiological limits) due to more incoming blood results in a stronger contraction. • Extrinsic Regulation: Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic Stimulation- Increases both heart rate and contractility. Parasympathetic Stimulation- Decreases heart rate and has a minimal effect on contractility. • Sympathetic Stimulation- Increases both heart rate and contractility. • Parasympathetic Stimulation- Decreases heart rate and has a minimal effect on contractility. • Temperature changes impact contractility • Sympathetic Stimulation- Increases both heart rate and contractility. • Parasympathetic Stimulation- Decreases heart rate and has a minimal effect on contractility. • Overall Regulation: combines intrinsic mechanisms, neural control, and environmental factors to adjust cardiac output. Bainbridge Reflex (Atrial Reflex) • Trigger: Stretching of the atrial walls due to increased venous return (higher blood volume). • Mechanism: Receptors: Low-pressure stretch receptors (atrial receptors) are activated by the increased blood volume. Signal Transmission: These receptors send signals to the medulla oblongata via the vagus nerve. Response: The medulla sends efferent signals through both vagal and sympathetic nerves. Outcome: Increased heart rate and often enhanced contraction strength, which helps to eject excess blood from the atria and prevent its accumulation. • Receptors: Low-pressure stretch receptors (atrial receptors) are activated by the increased blood volume. • Signal Transmission: These receptors send signals to the medulla oblongata via the vagus nerve. • Response: The medulla sends efferent signals through both vagal and sympathetic nerves. • Outcome: Increased heart rate and often enhanced contraction strength, which helps to eject excess blood from the atria and prevent its accumulation. • Receptors: Low-pressure stretch receptors (atrial receptors) are activated by the increased blood volume. • Signal Transmission: These receptors send signals to the medulla oblongata via the vagus nerve. • Response: The medulla sends efferent signals through both vagal and sympathetic nerves. • Outcome: Increased heart rate and often enhanced contraction strength, which helps to eject excess blood from the atria and prevent its accumulation. • Interaction with Baroreceptors: Baroreceptor Reflex: Sensitive to changes in arterial pressure, it works to counteract the effects of the Bainbridge reflex by slowing the heart rate. • Baroreceptor Reflex: Sensitive to changes in arterial pressure, it works to counteract the effects of the Bainbridge reflex by slowing the heart rate. • Baroreceptor Reflex: Sensitive to changes in arterial pressure, it works to counteract the effects of the Bainbridge reflex by slowing the heart rate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 454-455; Solution Pages 467-468","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":455},{"id":"physiology-16-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":7,"question":"According to the Fick principle, which of the following is essential for calculating cardiac output?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Measurement of the patient’s blood pressure before and after exercise"},{"letter":"B","text":"Determination of the oxygen uptake by the lungs and the arteriovenous oxygen content difference"},{"letter":"C","text":"Assessment of the patient’s heart rate and electrocardiographic findings"},{"letter":"D","text":"Injection of a contrast dye and measurement of its concentration in the blood over time"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Determination of the oxygen uptake by the lungs and the arteriovenous oxygen content difference","explanation":"The Fick principle calculates cardiac output by measuring the total oxygen uptake by the lungs and relating it to the difference in oxygen content between arterial and mixed venous blood. This method is based on the principle that the total uptake of a substance (oxygen in this case) by an organ is equivalent to the product of blood flow to that organ and the arteriovenous concentration difference of the substance. Cardiac output is volume of blood ejected by the heart into the aorta per minute and it is measured by: Method Principle Measurement Key Considerations Basis Fick Principle Oxygen uptake and Oxygen content in Accurate in steady states arteriovenous difference blood Indicator Dilution Method Indicator concentration Injected dye or Requires precise indicator dilution tracer handling Echocardiography Ultrasound visualization Stroke volume Non-invasive, real-time and flow velocity estimation data Thoracic Electrical Electrical impedance Impedance fluctuati Sensitive to external Bioimpedance changes ons factors Measurement of the patient’s blood pressure before and after exercise ( Option A ) It is not related to the Fick principle. It provides information about blood pressure changes but does not calculate cardiac output based on oxygen uptake. Determination of the oxygen uptake by the lungs and the arteriovenous oxygen content difference ( Option C ) Assessment of heart rate and electrocardiographic findings does not directly relate to the Fick principle. Cardiac output measurement via the Fick principle does not rely on these parameters. Injection of a contrast dye and measurement of its concentration in the blood over time ( Option D ) This describes the Indicator Dilution Method, which involves injecting a contrast dye and measuring its concentration to determine cardiac output, not the Fick principle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 455-455; Solution Pages 468-469","sourcePage":455,"sourcePageEnd":455},{"id":"physiology-16-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 42-year-old patient undergoes cardiac evaluation, and the following values are obtained: end-diastolic volume (EDV) of 140 mL, and ejection fraction of 48%. Which of the following best describes the patient's cardiac parameters?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The patient's ejection fraction is below the normal range, indicating reduced ventricular function."},{"letter":"B","text":"The stroke volume is higher than normal, suggesting improved cardiac output."},{"letter":"C","text":"The cardiac output is higher than average due to the increased EDV."},{"letter":"D","text":"The cardiac output can be calculated accurately from the given values alone."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) The patient's ejection fraction is below the normal range, indicating reduced ventricular function.","explanation":"An ejection fraction of 48% is below the normal range (50%-70%), which may suggest reduced ventricular function or potential heart failure. Parameter Description Typical Value Cardiac Output The total volume of blood pumped Varies with by the heart per minute, reflecting demand its ability to meet the body's oxygen and nutrient demands. Stroke Volume The volume of blood ejected from ~70-90 mL each ventricle per heartbeat. at rest End-Diastolic Volume of blood in the ventricle at ~130 mL at Volume (EDV) the end of diastole, just before rest contraction. Cardiac Index Cardiac output adjusted for body ~3.2 L/min/ size, expressed as liters per minute m² per square meter of body surface area, allowing for comparison across different body sizes. Ejection Fraction Percentage of EDV ejected with 50%-70% each heartbeat, reflecting ventricular function and efficiency. Additional Factors: Sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous system activity, hormonal influences, and mechanical factors can affect these parameters. The stroke volume is higher than normal, suggesting improved cardiac output (Option B) –S troke volume of 70 mL is within the normal range (70-90 mL) and does not indicate improved function. The cardiac output is higher than average due to the increased EDV (Option C) – H igher EDV does not necessarily mean higher cardiac output if ejection fraction is reduced. The cardiac output can be calculated accurately from the given values alone (Option D) – H eart rate is also required to determine cardiac output (CO = SV × HR).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 455-455; Solution Pages 469-470","sourcePage":455,"sourcePageEnd":455},{"id":"physiology-16-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 50-year-old patient is undergoing evaluation of cardiac function, and a pressure-volume (PV) loop of the left ventricle is analyzed. From the PV loop, the following measurements are obtained: end-diastolic volume (EDV) is 140 mL and end-systolic volume (ESV) is 60 mL. What is the ejection fraction (EF) for this patient, and how is it calculated?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"30%, calculated as (EDV - ESV)/EDV"},{"letter":"B","text":"43%, calculated as (SV/EDV) * 100"},{"letter":"C","text":"57%, calculated as (EDV - ESV)/EDV"},{"letter":"D","text":"57%, calculated as (SV/EDV) * 100"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 57%, calculated as (SV/EDV) * 100","explanation":"The stroke volume (SV) is calculated as EDV - ESV = 140 mL - 60 mL = 80 mL. The ejection fraction (EF) is then EF = (SV/EDV) x 100% = (80/140) x 100% = 57%. PV Loop • A graphical representation of pressure and volume changes in the left ventricle during the cardiac cycle. • Used to derive stroke volume and ejection fraction. Key Phases: • Diastole: The period when the left ventricle fills with blood. End-Diastolic Volume (EDV): Volume of blood in the left ventricle at the end of diastole. • End-Diastolic Volume (EDV): Volume of blood in the left ventricle at the end of diastole. • Systole: The period when the left ventricle contracts and ejects blood. End-Systolic Volume (ESV): Volume of blood remaining in the left ventricle after contraction. • End-Systolic Volume (ESV): Volume of blood remaining in the left ventricle after contraction. • End-Diastolic Volume (EDV): Volume of blood in the left ventricle at the end of diastole. • End-Systolic Volume (ESV): Volume of blood remaining in the left ventricle after contraction. Stroke Volume (SV) • Represents the blood ejected from the left ventricle per heartbeat. • Determined by the horizontal distance between end-diastolic volume (EDV) and end-systolic volume (ESV). • Formula: SV = EDV - ESV. Ejection Fraction (EF) • Indicates the percentage of blood ejected from the left ventricle relative to the end-diastolic volume. • Formula: EF = (SV/EDV) x 100%. • Normal value is approximately 65%. Normal PV Loop of Left Ventricle: a to b: Isovolumetric Contraction. ; b to c: Period of ejection. c to d: Isovolumetric Relaxation. ; d to a: Period of filling.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 456-456; Solution Pages 470-471","sourcePage":456,"sourcePageEnd":456},{"id":"physiology-16-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":10,"question":"What is the duration of the cardiac cycle for a heart rate of 180 beats per minute?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0.5 seconds"},{"letter":"B","text":"0.75 seconds"},{"letter":"C","text":"0.33 seconds"},{"letter":"D","text":"0.66 seconds"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 0.33 seconds","explanation":"Duration of cardiac cycle can be calculated by 60 (sec)/ heart rate per min So, 60/ 180 =0.33 sec Duration of a normal cardiac cycle is 0.8 sec (for 72 beats per minute) Ventricular Systole = 0.3 sec Ventricular Diastole = 0.5 sec Phases of cardiac cycle - Phase Sub-Phase Key Events Systol Isovolumetric Ventricles begin to contract e Contraction Ranges from closure of AV valves (mitral and tricuspid) till opening of semilunar valves. First heart sound appears. No blood volume change in ventricles Ventricular Ejection Has 2 phases- Rapid ejection phase and Reduced ejection phase. Ventricular pressure exceeds aortic and pulmonary pressure Aortic and pulmonary valves open Blood ejected into aorta and pulmonary artery Ventricular volume decreases Ends with the closure of the aortic valve. Diastol Isovolumetric Ventricles relax e Relaxation Ventricular pressure drops Aortic and pulmonary valves close No blood volume change in ventricles Second heart sounds appears. Ends with the opening of AV valves. Rapid Filling AV valves (mitral and tricuspid) open Blood flows rapidly from atria to ventricles Ventricular volume increases rapidly Third heart sound appears. Diastasis Blood flow from atria to ventricles slows No change in the atrial and ventricular pressure. Ventricular volume increases slowly Atrial Systole Atria contract Additional blood pushed into ventricles Ventricular volume increases slightly End-diastolic volume (EDV) reached Fourth heart sound is produced.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 456-456; Solution Pages 472-473","sourcePage":456,"sourcePageEnd":456},{"id":"physiology-16-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which factor primarily contributes to an increased pulse pressure in the aortic pressure curve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased stroke volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased arterial compliance"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased stroke volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased heart rate"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increased stroke volume","explanation":"Increased stroke volume leads to a more pronounced increase in systolic pressure during ejection and a more substantial decrease during diastole, thereby increasing pulse pressure. Phase Description Left Ventricle Contraction Ventricular pressure increases until aortic valve opens Period of Ejection Left ventricular pressure > aortic pressure; blood ejected 80-120 mmHg Isovolumic Relaxation Aortic pressure forces blood back, closing aortic valve Incisura Notch on the curve due to aortic valve closure Diastolic Decline 80mmHg diastolic Aortic pressure declines as blood flows into systemic circulation Factors Influencing Aortic Pressure Stroke Volume Arterial Compliance Transmission of Pressure Pulses Pressure Wave Propagation- generated by ventricular contraction travels through the arterial system Dampening Effect- pulsations diminish in smaller arteries, becoming negligible in the capillaries. Stroke Volume: • Increase in Stroke Volume: When stroke volume increases, more blood is pumped into the arteries, leading to a higher systolic pressure. If diastolic pressure remains unchanged, this results in an increased pulse pressure. • Decrease in Stroke Volume: Conversely, if stroke volume decreases, less blood is ejected into the arteries, leading to a lower systolic pressure. If diastolic pressure stays the same, this results in a decreased pulse pressure (Option A) Arterial Compliance: • Increased Arterial Compliance: Compliance refers to the ability of arteries to expand and accommodate the blood ejected with each heartbeat. Higher arterial compliance means arteries can stretch more easily. This reduces the rise in systolic pressure and dampens the pulse pressure, as the arteries absorb more of the pressure variation (Option B) • Decreased Arterial Compliance: Lower arterial compliance means arteries are stiffer and less able to expand. This results in a greater increase in systolic pressure with each heartbeat, leading to a higher pulse pressure, as the arteries cannot absorb the pressure as effectively. So, an increase in stroke volume and a decrease in arterial compliance typically raises pulse pressure. Heart Rate (Option D) - lower heart rate allows more time for diastolic runoff, leading to a lower diastolic pressure, which may slightly increase pulse pressure, but stroke volume remains the primary determinant.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 456-456; Solution Pages 473-474","sourcePage":456,"sourcePageEnd":456},{"id":"physiology-16-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 30-year-old healthy volunteer undergoes invasive hemodynamic monitoring. At which point in the cardiac cycle is the pressure gradient between the left atrium and left ventricle greatest, and what event does this gradient trigger?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"End of atrial systole; opening of mitral valve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mid-ventricular systole; closure of aortic valve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Early diastole; rapid ventricular filling"},{"letter":"D","text":"End of isovolumic relaxation; opening of mitral valve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) End of isovolumic relaxation; opening of mitral valve","explanation":"The greatest pressure gradient between the left atrium and left ventricle occurs at the end of isovolumic relaxation. At this point, ventricular pressure has fallen below atrial pressure, creating a significant pressure gradient. This gradient triggers the opening of the mitral valve and initiates rapid ventricular filling. End of atrial systole; opening of mitral valve (Option A): At the end of atrial systole, the pressure gradient is actually minimal, as atrial contraction has pushed blood into the ventricle, equalizing pressures. Mid-ventricular systole; closure of aortic valve (Option B): During mid-ventricular systole, ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure. The aortic valve closes at the end of systole, not mid-systole. Early diastole; rapid ventricular filling (Option C): While rapid ventricular filling occurs in early diastole, it is the result of, not the cause of, the greatest pressure gradient.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 457-457; Solution Pages 475-475","sourcePage":457,"sourcePageEnd":457},{"id":"physiology-16-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 40-year-old patient with suspected heart failure undergoes a comprehensive cardiac evaluation. During auscultation, both S3 and S4 are heard. Regarding the correlation of these extra heart sounds with the ECG, which statement is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S3 occurs during the P wave; S4 coincides with the QRS complex"},{"letter":"B","text":"S3 is heard after the T wave & before next P wave; S4 occurs immediately after the P wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"S3 coincides with the U wave; S4 is heard during the PR interval"},{"letter":"D","text":"S3 occurs during the ST segment; S4 is heard at the peak of the R wave"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) S3 is heard just after the T wave; S4 occurs immediately after the P wave","explanation":"S3 is typically heard just after the T wave on the ECG, during early diastole. S4 occurs immediately after the P wave, during late diastole, just before the QRS complex. This timing corresponds to the physiological events causing these sounds. Phas Hear Timing on ECG Cause Clinical Significa Key Points e t Sou nce nd Early S3 Early diastole, Rapid ventricular Normal in childre Rapid ventricula Diast after T wave filling n and athletes; r filling phase ole in adults may AV valves open indicate heart Ventricles relax failure Late S4 Late diastole, Atrial contraction Always abnorma Atrial systole Diast just before P against a stiff l; may indicate Completes ole wave ventricle hypertension, ventricular filling ischemia, or Precedes next cardiomyopathy ventricular systole S3 occurs during the P wave; S4 coincides with the QRS complex ( Option A): This option switches the timing of S3 and S4. S3 occurs in early diastole, after the T wave, not during the P wave. S4 occurs in late diastole, after the P wave, not during the QRS complex. S3 coincides with the U wave; S4 is heard during the PR interval (Option C): While S3 may sometimes coincide with the U wave (if present), it's not a consistent relationship. S4 occurs after the P wave, not during the PR interval. S3 occurs during the ST segment; S4 is heard at the peak of the R wave (Option D): S3 occurs in diastole, after the T wave, not during the ST segment which is part of systole. S4 occurs after the P wave, not at the peak of the R wave.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 457-457; Solution Pages 475-476","sourcePage":457,"sourcePageEnd":457},{"id":"physiology-16-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":14,"question":"During a cardiac examination, a 35-year-old patient's simultaneous ECG and phonocardiography are recorded. Which of the following statements most accurately describes the relationship between the second heart sound (S2) and the ECG?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S2 occurs at the peak of the T wave in all cardiac cycles"},{"letter":"B","text":"S2 is heard just before the T wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"S2 coincides with the onset of the U wave when present"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2 typically occurs at the end of T wave and the subsequent P wave"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) S2 typically occurs between the T wave and the subsequent P wave","explanation":"The second heart sound (S2) typically occurs between the T wave and the subsequent P wave on the ECG. This timing corresponds to the end of ventricular systole and the beginning of ventricular diastole when the semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonary) close. Ph Heart Timing on Cause Clinical Significa Key Points as Sound ECG nce e Sy S2 At the end of Closure of Normal finding; Ventricular sto the T-wave aortic and Split S2 may ejection ends le pulmonic valves indicate delayed Marks beginnin pulmonic valve g of diastole closure Aortic valve closes S2 does not occur at the peak of the T wave. The T wave represents ventricular repolarization, which occurs before the end of mechanical systole and valve closure ( Option A). While S2 does occur after the T wave, it's not consistently 0.02-0.04 seconds after the end of the T wave. The timing can vary based on heart rate and other factors (Option B). The U wave, when present, typically occurs after S2, not coinciding with it. The U wave is thought to represent the final repolarization of the ventricles or Purkinje fibers (Option C).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 457-457; Solution Pages 476-477","sourcePage":457,"sourcePageEnd":457},{"id":"physiology-16-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Regarding the timing and correlation of the Phonocardiogram's first heart sound (S1) with the ECG, which of the following statements is CORRECT?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S1 occurs immediately after the P wave and coincides with atrial systole"},{"letter":"B","text":"S1 begins during the ST segment and ends before the T wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"S1 starts 0.02-0.04 seconds after the onset of the QRS complex"},{"letter":"D","text":"S1 is heard simultaneously with the peak of the R wave in all lead"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) S1 starts 0.02-0.04 seconds after the onset of the QRS complex Explanation : The first heart sound (S1) typically starts 0.02-0.04 seconds after the onset of the QRS complex. This slight delay represents the time between the electrical activation of the ventricles and the mechanical closure of the AV(atrioventricular) valves, which produces S1. Ph Heart Timing on Cause Clinical Significa Key Points ase Sound ECG nce Sy S1 Just after Closure of Normal finding; Ventricular stol QRS complex mitral and Split S1 may contraction e tricuspid valves indicate bundle begins branch block Isovolumetric contraction phase Aortic valve opens S1 does not occur immediately after the P wave. The P wave represents atrial depolarization, while S1 is associated with the onset of ventricular systole and closure of the AV valves (Option A). S1 occurs much earlier in the cardiac cycle, not during the ST segment. The ST segment represents ventricular repolarization, which happens after ventricular systole has begun (Option B) . While S1 often occurs near the peak of the R wave, it doesn't always coincide exactly with the R wave peak, especially not in all ECG leads. The timing can vary slightly depending on conduction and mechanical factors (Option D) .","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 458-458; Solution Pages 477-478","sourcePage":458,"sourcePageEnd":458},{"id":"physiology-16-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 30-year-old patient undergoes cardiac catheterization with simultaneous ECG recording. During the isovolumic relaxation phase, which combination of events is most accurate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T wave ends; ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure; mitral valve opens"},{"letter":"B","text":"U wave begins; ventricular pressure equals aortic pressure; all valves closed"},{"letter":"C","text":"P wave starts; ventricular volume increases; tricuspid valve opens"},{"letter":"D","text":"Second heart sound occurs; ventricular pressure drops rapidly; aortic valve closes"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Second heart sound occurs; ventricular pressure drops rapidly; aortic valve closes Explanation : During the isovolumic relaxation phase: • The second heart sound (S2) occurs at the beginning of this phase • Ventricular pressure drops rapidly • The aortic valve closes at the start of this phase • Ventricular volume remains constant as all valves are closed The T wave typically ends before the isovolumic relaxation phase. Ventricular pressure falling below atrial pressure and mitral valve opening marks the end of this phase and the beginning of rapid ventricular filling (Option A) . The U wave, when present, usually occurs after the T wave and before the next P wave. Ventricular pressure is falling during this phase and is below aortic pressure. While all valves are indeed closed, this is not a defining characteristic unique to this phase (Option B) . The P wave occurs later in diastole, during the late ventricular filling phase. Ventricular volume remains constant during isovolumic relaxation, and the tricuspid valve remains closed throughout this phase (Option C)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 458-458; Solution Pages 478-479","sourcePage":458,"sourcePageEnd":458},{"id":"physiology-16-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-16","chapter":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topic":"Cardiac Cycle and Cardiac Output","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":17,"question":"During the isovolumic contraction phase, which of the following combinations of events is most accurate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Second heart sound occurs; ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure; aortic valve opens"},{"letter":"B","text":"QRS complex ends; ventricular pressure rises but remains below aortic pressure; all valves closed"},{"letter":"C","text":"T wave begins; ventricular volume decreases slightly; mitral valve closes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Second heart sound occurs; ventricular pressure equals aortic pressure; pulmonary valve closes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) QRS complex ends; ventricular pressure rises but remains below aortic pressure; all valves closed Explanation : During the isovolumic contraction phase: • The QRS complex typically ends just as this phase begins • Ventricular pressure rises rapidly but remains below aortic pressure • All valves (mitral, tricuspid, aortic, and pulmonary) remain closed The first heart sound occurs at the beginning of systole, just before isovolumic contraction. While ventricular pressure does exceed atrial pressure, the aortic valve doesn't open during this phase (Option A). The T wave typically occurs later in systole, during the ejection phase. Ventricular volume remains constant during isovolumic contraction. The mitral valve closes just before this phase begins (Option C) . The second heart sound occurs at the end of systole, during isovolumic relaxation. Ventricular pressure doesn't equal aortic pressure during isovolumic contraction, and the pulmonary valve remains closed throughout this phase (Option D) .","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 458-458; Solution Pages 479-479","sourcePage":458,"sourcePageEnd":458},{"id":"physiology-17-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":1,"question":"In the case of reduced contractility of the heart, which of the following changes would be observed? a) ESV (End-systolic volume) b) EDV (End-diastolic volume)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased end-diastolic volume and pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Shift of the end-systolic volume and pressure curves to the right"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased stroke volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhanced response to sympathetic stimulation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Option A. Decreased end-diastolic volume and pressure • Incorrect. Reduced contractility often leads to impaired filling during diastole, causing an increase in end-diastolic volume and pressure as the heart struggles to fill properly. Option B. Shift of the end-systolic volume and pressure curves to the right • Correct. Reduced contractility shifts the end-systolic volume and pressure curves to the right on the pressure-volume loop, indicating that the heart is ejecting less blood per contraction. Option C. Increased stroke volume • Incorrect. Reduced contractility typically results in a decreased stroke volume because the heart's ability to pump blood effectively is compromised. Option D. Enhanced response to sympathetic stimulation • Incorrect. Reduced contractility often indicates a weakened response to sympathetic stimulation, as the heart muscles are not contracting as forcefully as they should. • So, the correct answer is Option B. Shift of the end-systolic volume and pressure curves to the right in the case of reduced contractility of the heart. So, the correct answer is Option B. Shift of the end-systolic volume and pressure curves to the right in the case of reduced contractility of the heart.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 480-480; Solution Pages 493-494","sourcePage":480,"sourcePageEnd":480},{"id":"physiology-17-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":2,"question":"What is the most effective trigger for the secretion of vasopressin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypotonicity of extracellular fluid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypertension"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypotension"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypo-osmolality of extracellular fluid"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Option A. Hypotonicity of extracellular fluid Explanation: Vasopressin, also known as antidiuretic hormone (ADH), is a hormone produced by the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary gland. Its primary function is to regulate water balance in the body by controlling the reabsorption of water in the kidneys, thereby influencing urine concentration. The most effective trigger for the secretion of vasopressin is the hypotonicity of extracellular fluid (option a) . When the extracellular fluid becomes hypotonic (meaning it has a lower concentration of solutes), the body detects this decrease in osmolality. In response, vasopressin is released, which leads to increased water reabsorption in the kidneys, conserving water and reducing urine output. This mechanism helps the body maintain proper fluid balance. Incorrect Options: Option B. Hypertension: High blood pressure is not a direct trigger for vasopressin secretion. Vasopressin primarily regulates water balance rather than blood pressure. Option C. Hypotension: Low blood pressure is not the primary trigger for vasopressin secretion. While severe hypotension can stimulate vasopressin release as a compensatory mechanism to increase blood pressure, it is not the most effective trigger. Option D. Hypo-osmolality of extracellular fluid: This option is incorrect because it repeats the concept of hypotonicity, which is the decreased concentration of solutes in the extracellular fluid, leading to vasopressin secretion. Therefore, A . Hypotonicity of extracellular fluid is the correct option.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 480-480; Solution Pages 494-495","sourcePage":480,"sourcePageEnd":480},{"id":"physiology-17-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Please arrange the following items in the order they occur after the \"p wave\" in an ECG. A- ‘a’ wave B – 1st heart sound C – Rapid filling of ventricles D – ‘t’ wave","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ABCD"},{"letter":"B","text":"ABDC"},{"letter":"C","text":"CABD"},{"letter":"D","text":"ACBD"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: The correct sequence after the P wave is as follows: A. 'a' wave: The 'a' wave represents atrial contraction. It occurs during atrial systole when the atria contract to push blood into the ventricles. B. 1st heart sound: The 1st heart sound, also known as S1, is the closure of the atrioventricular (AV) valves (mitral and tricuspid valves) at the beginning of ventricular systole. It marks the onset of ventricular contraction. D. 't' wave: The 't' wave is a repolarization phase of the ventricles. It represents the relaxation and resetting of the ventricles after contraction. C. Rapid filling of ventricles: After the 't' wave, the ventricles enter the diastole phase, during which they passively fill with blood from the atria. This is known as rapid ventricular filling. Therefore, the correct sequence is ABDC: 'a' wave, 1st heart sound, rapid filling of ventricles, and 't' wave. Incorrect Options: Option A: ACBD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D). Option C: CABD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A) and the 1st heart sound (B). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D). Option D: ACBD: This option incorrectly places the rapid filling of ventricles (C) before the 'a' wave (A). The correct sequence is 'a' wave (A), 1st heart sound (B), rapid filling of ventricles (C), and 't' wave (D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 480-480; Solution Pages 495-495","sourcePage":480,"sourcePageEnd":480},{"id":"physiology-17-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which organelle is not present in the designated region of the cardiac muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lysosome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Golgi bodies"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endoplasmic reticulum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: • Lysosomes are responsible for intracellular digestion and waste removal. They contain enzymes that break down cellular waste materials and cellular components that are no longer functional. • The area marked is devoid of nuclei, and lysosomes are usually seen in perinuclear areas. Incorrect Options: Option A . Mitochondria are abundant in cardiac muscle cells due to their high energy demands. They are responsible for generating ATP through oxidative phosphorylation, which is necessary for the contraction and relaxation of the cardiac muscle. Option C . Golgi bodies are involved in processing and packaging proteins and lipids synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum. They modify and sort these molecules and direct them to their specific destinations within the cell or for secretion outside the cell. Option D. The endoplasmic reticulum (ER) is a complex network of membranes involved in protein synthesis, folding, and transport. It is responsible for the synthesis of proteins that are destined for secretion, as well as the synthesis of membrane lipids.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 481-481; Solution Pages 496-496","sourcePage":481,"sourcePageEnd":481},{"id":"physiology-17-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which reflex is responsible for tachycardia when the right atrium is distended?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"J reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cushing reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bezoid jarisch reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bainbridge reflex"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct option is D: Bainbridge reflex. • The Bainbridge reflex is responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension. When the right atrium becomes stretched or distended due to an increased volume of blood, specialized stretch receptors in the atrial wall called atrial mechanoreceptors are activated. These mechanoreceptors send signals to the cardiovascular control centers in the brain, resulting in an increase in heart rate through the Bainbridge reflex. This reflex helps to regulate cardiac output by increasing heart rate to accommodate the increased venous return to the heart . Incorrect options: Option A: J reflex : The J reflex is a pulmonary reflex that is involved in regulating airway resistance and breathing. It is not directly related to tachycardia during right atrial distension. Option B: Cushing reflex: The Cushing reflex refers to a physiological response that occurs in the brain in response to increased intracranial pressure. It leads to increased blood pressure and decreased heart rate, which is the opposite of tachycardia. Option C: Bezold-Jarisch reflex: The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is a reflex that can be triggered by stimulation of specialized sensory receptors in the heart, typically in response to excessive ventricular filling or ischemia. It leads to a decrease in heart rate, vasodilation, and hypotension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 481-481; Solution Pages 496-497","sourcePage":481,"sourcePageEnd":481},{"id":"physiology-17-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What is the cause of dicrotic notch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Passive filling of blood in ventricles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rapid ejection phase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Peripheral resistance"},{"letter":"D","text":"Isovolumic contraction"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Peripheral resistance: • The dicrotic notch is primarily caused by the closure of the aortic valve during ventricular diastole. This closure, in the presence of peripheral resistance, creates a momentary increase in pressure, leading to the notch in the arterial pressure waveform. Incorrect Options: Option A - Passive filling of blood in ventricles: The dicrotic notch is not directly related to the passive filling of blood in the ventricles. Option B - Rapid ejection phase: The dicrotic notch occurs during the diastolic phase, not the rapid ejection phase. Option D - Isovolumic contraction: The isovolumic contraction is a phase of the cardiac cycle where ventricular pressure increases without changes in volume. It is not directly associated with the dicrotic notch.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 481-481; Solution Pages 497-497","sourcePage":481,"sourcePageEnd":481},{"id":"physiology-17-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following are properties of ventricular cardiac muscle? All or none law Length tension relationship Long refractory period Can be tetanized","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 only"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 only"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,2,3"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct statements Statement 1: All or None Law This is a fundamental property of ventricular cardiac muscle, which states that once a threshold is reached, the cardiac muscle fibers contract maximally. There is no partial contraction in response to a stimulus; the response is either full or none. Statement 2: Length-Tension Relationship Ventricular cardiac muscle follows the length-tension relationship, where the force of contraction is dependent on the initial length of the cardiac muscle fibers. This relationship is crucial for the heart's function, allowing it to pump efficiently by adjusting the force of contraction based on the volume of blood filling the heart (preload). Statement 3: Long Refractory Period Cardiac muscle cells have a long refractory period, which is significantly longer than that of skeletal muscle cells. This long refractory period is essential to prevent tetanization (sustained contraction) and to ensure proper rhythmic contractions of the heart. Inorrect statements Statement 4: Can be Tetanized Unlike skeletal muscle, ventricular cardiac muscle cannot be tetanized due to its long refractory period. This characteristic is vital to maintaining the rhythmic and cyclical nature of heartbeats, preventing continuous contraction that would impede normal heart function.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 482-482; Solution Pages 497-498","sourcePage":482,"sourcePageEnd":482},{"id":"physiology-17-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A patient is brought to the Emergency Room following a road traffic accident (RTA) with thoracic and abdominal injuries. The patient is found to be in hypovolemic shock. What is the most probable cause of the decreased cardiac output in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decrease in preload"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decrease in contractility of the heart"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase in preload"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increase in afterload"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Decrease in preload: • In hypovolemic shock, there is a significant loss of blood or fluid volume. This results in a reduced volume of blood returning to the heart (preload). Since preload is a key determinant of stroke volume and cardiac output, a decrease in preload will lead to a decreased cardiac output. Incorrect Options: Option B - Decrease in contractility of the heart: While decreased contractility can impact cardiac output, in this scenario of hypovolemic shock due to fluid loss, the primary issue is reduced preload rather than contractility. Option C - Increase in preload: This is incorrect because in hypovolemic shock, preload is actually decreased due to fluid loss, not increased. Option D - Increase in afterload: Although increased afterload can affect cardiac output, in the context of hypovolemic shock, the immediate cause of decreased cardiac output is the reduction in preload due to the decreased blood volume.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 482-482; Solution Pages 498-499","sourcePage":482,"sourcePageEnd":482},{"id":"physiology-17-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A man presents with an irregularly irregular pulse and dyspnoea. What is the expected finding in the jugular venous pressure (JVP)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Absent a wave"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cannon a wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"Prominent X descent"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prominent Y descent"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Absent a wave: • In the JVP waveform, the 'a' wave represents atrial contraction. An absent 'a' wave is typically seen in atrial fibrillation, where the atria do not contract effectively due to irregular impulses. Incorrect Options: Option B - Cannon a wave : Cannon 'a' waves are large, prominent waves in the JVP that occur when atrial contraction is against a closed tricuspid valve, often due to arrhythmias like complete heart block. This is not typically associated with an irregularly irregular pulse. Option C - Prominent X descent : The X descent in the JVP represents atrial relaxation and descent of the tricuspid valve during ventricular systole. Prominent X descent is generally seen in conditions like tricuspid regurgitation or right ventricular failure but not specifically in atrial fibrillation. Option D - Prominent Y descent : The Y descent reflects the rapid emptying of the right atrium into the right ventricle. Prominent Y descent can be seen in conditions such as constrictive pericarditis or right heart failure but is not a characteristic finding in atrial fibrillation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 482-482; Solution Pages 499-499","sourcePage":482,"sourcePageEnd":482},{"id":"physiology-17-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":10,"question":"In the jugular venous pressure (JVP) waveform, the \"a\" wave corresponds to:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right Atrial contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tricuspid valve bulging into Right atria"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right Atrial relaxation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right atrial filling"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Right Atrial contraction","explanation":"The \"a\" wave in the jugular venous pressure (JVP) waveform corresponds to right atrial contraction . • Mechanism: During the right atrial systole , blood is pushed into the right ventricle , causing a transient increase in right atrial pressure , which is reflected as the \"a\" wave in the JVP. This occurs just before the first heart sound (S1) and the QRS complex on ECG. • During the right atrial systole , blood is pushed into the right ventricle , causing a transient increase in right atrial pressure , which is reflected as the \"a\" wave in the JVP. • This occurs just before the first heart sound (S1) and the QRS complex on ECG. • During the right atrial systole , blood is pushed into the right ventricle , causing a transient increase in right atrial pressure , which is reflected as the \"a\" wave in the JVP. • This occurs just before the first heart sound (S1) and the QRS complex on ECG. Jugular Venous Pressure (JVP) Waveform: a wave Caused by atrial systole. Atrial contraction results in a temporary halt in venous inflow. Some blood flows back into the veins, causing a rise in venous pressure. c wave Arises from the increase in atrial pressure during isovolumetric ventricular contraction. Pressure is transmitted through the closed and bulging tricuspid valve. x descent Ventricular systole pulls the tricuspid valve down v wave Corresponds to the rise in atrial pressure as blood fills the atria. Occurs during diastole while the tricuspid valve is still closed. y descent Opening of tricuspid valve causing emptying of right atrium into ventricle Respiration Influence : • Venous pressure decreases during inspiration due to negative intrathoracic pressure. • Venous pressure rises during expiration. Tricuspid valve bulging into the right atria (Option B) is incorrect because this corresponds to the \" c\" wave , which occurs when the tricuspid valve bulges into the atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction . Right atrial relaxation (Option C) is incorrect because this occurs after the \"a\" wave and corresponds to the \"x\" descent in the JVP waveform. Right atrial filling (Option D) is incorrect because this corresponds to the \"v\" wave , which represents venous return filling the right atrium against a closed tricuspid valve .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 483-483; Solution Pages 499-501","sourcePage":483,"sourcePageEnd":483},{"id":"physiology-17-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Please arrange the following steps of ventricular action potential in the correct order. A. Activation of calcium channels regulated by changes in voltage. B. Repolarizing K+ currents C. Opening of voltage – gated sodium channels D. Transient outward flow of potassium","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"B, A, C, D"},{"letter":"B","text":"D, C, B, A"},{"letter":"C","text":"C, D, A, B"},{"letter":"D","text":"A, B, C, D"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - C, D, A, B: • Steps of fast type ventricular action potential in the correct order are: Phase 0 - Opening of voltage-gated sodium channels: This step involves the rapid influx of sodium ions into the heart muscle cells, leading to cell membrane depolarization. Phase 1 - Transient outward flow of potassium: This step involves the transient (brief) outward flow of potassium ions through KT.O channel from the heart muscle cells, leading to partial cell membrane repolarization. Phase 2 - Opening of voltage-gated calcium channels: This step involves the influx of calcium ions through L-type of calcium channels into the heart muscle cells along with efflux of the potassium channels balancing the charge flow and resulting in the plateau phase. Phase 3 - Repolarizing K+ currents: This step involves the outward flow of potassium ions through delayed rectifier channels from the heart muscle cells, repolarizing the cell membrane and restoring its resting state. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B & D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 483-483; Solution Pages 501-502","sourcePage":483,"sourcePageEnd":483},{"id":"physiology-17-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":12,"question":"If radius of a vessel is doubled then the blood flow is increased by:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"32 times"},{"letter":"B","text":"16 times"},{"letter":"C","text":"256 times"},{"letter":"D","text":"8 times"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Options B - 16 times: • Flow is equal to pressure difference divided by resistance. So, flow is inversely proportional to the resistance of the vessel. • According to the Hagen–Poiseuille equation, resistance can be directly measured by the following formula. R = 8ηL/pr 4 Where, R = resistance. η = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to its r 4 (radius). If other parameters remain constant. So, if the radius is doubled of the original, then the resistance will be: R = 1/(2) 4 . R = 1/16. • R = 8ηL/pr 4 • Where, R = resistance. η = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. • R = resistance. • η = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to its r 4 (radius). • If other parameters remain constant. • So, if the radius is doubled of the original, then the resistance will be: • R = 1/(2) 4 . • R = 1/16. • So, the resistance will decrease by 16 times. • So, the flow will increase by 16 times. • R = 8ηL/pr 4 • Where, R = resistance. η = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. • R = resistance. • η = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to its r 4 (radius). • If other parameters remain constant. • So, if the radius is doubled of the original, then the resistance will be: • R = 1/(2) 4 . • R = 1/16. • R = resistance. • η = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 483-483; Solution Pages 502-503","sourcePage":483,"sourcePageEnd":483},{"id":"physiology-17-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following is correct about isovolumetric relaxation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Semilunar valves open"},{"letter":"B","text":"C Wave of JVP"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corresponds to QT interval"},{"letter":"D","text":"AV Valves are closed"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - AV Valves are closed: • During isovolumetric relaxation, the ventricles are relaxing, and the AV valves are closed. Both the tricuspid and mitral valves prevent blood from flowing back into the atria. Incorrect Options: Option A - Semilunar valves open: Isovolumetric relaxation is a phase where both the AV valves (tricuspid and mitral valves) and the semilunar valves (aortic and pulmonary valves) are closed. Option B - C Wave of JVP: The C wave of the jugular venous pulse (JVP) is associated with the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular contraction, not with isovolumetric relaxation. Option C - Corresponds to QT interval: The QT interval on an electrocardiogram represents the total time for ventricular depolarization and repolarization, not specifically isovolumetric relaxation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 483-484; Solution Pages 503-503","sourcePage":483,"sourcePageEnd":484},{"id":"physiology-17-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Baroreceptor reflex is a type of __________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Negative feedback"},{"letter":"B","text":"Positive feedback"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both negative and positive feedback"},{"letter":"D","text":"Feedforward control"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Negative feedback : • The baroreceptor reflex is a negative feedback mechanism that helps regulate blood pressure. It involves stretch receptors called baroreceptors, which are located in the walls of blood vessels such as the carotid sinus and aortic arch, as well as the heart. These receptors detect changes in blood pressure and send signals to the brain, specifically the cardiovascular control center in the medulla oblongata, to initiate a response. • When blood pressure increases, the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) is activated, which in turn activates the caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM) and inhibits the rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM). This results in decreased sympathetic stimulation, leading to decreased cardiac output and contractility. • Conversely, if blood pressure decreases, the NTS and CVLM are not activated, which means they do not inhibit the RVLM. This leads to an increase in sympathetic supply, resulting in increased cardiac output. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 484-484; Solution Pages 504-504","sourcePage":484,"sourcePageEnd":484},{"id":"physiology-17-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 65-year-old patient visits his family practitioner for a yearly examination. On examination, his BP was 190/100 mm Hg. His pulse pressure was 90 mm Hg. What would have decreased resulting in this increase in pulse pressure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Myocardial contractility"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stroke volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"Arterial wall compliance"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cardiac output"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Arterial wall compliance: • Pulse pressure is the difference between systolic blood pressure and diastolic blood pressure. Ratio of stroke volume to the compliance of the aorta is also known as pulse pressure. So, pulse pressure is inversely proportional to the compliance of the aorta. • Aorta wall compliance refers to the elasticity and distensibility of the arterial walls. When aorta wall compliance decreases, the arteries become stiffer and less expansible with each cardiac cycle. This results in an increase in pulse pressure, as the pressure difference between systole and diastole becomes greater. Incorrect Options: Option A - Myocardial contractility: Myocardial contractility can affect stroke volume and indirectly affects the pulse pressure. Option B - Stroke volume: Stroke volume is directly proportional to the pulse pressure, i.e., with decrease in stroke volume pulse pressure ill also increase.the amount of blood ejected by the heart with each contraction. While changes in stroke volume can influence pulse pressure, they are not the primary cause of the increased pulse pressure in this case. Option D - Cardiac output: Cardiac output is the volume of blood pumped by the heart per unit of time i.e., stroke volume multiplied with heart rate and indirectly affects the pulse pressure..","sourceRef":"Question Pages 484-484; Solution Pages 504-505","sourcePage":484,"sourcePageEnd":484},{"id":"physiology-17-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following contributes to the zero phase of the action potential in the SA node?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sodium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcium and sodium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Calcium: • The action potential in SA node is a Slow-type of action potential with only 3 phases that are phase 4, 0, and 3. • Phase 0: A large number of L-type Ca++ channels, which are slow in nature, open and result in the influx of calcium, leading to depolarization, and the membrane voltage slowly rises to +10 mV. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 484-485; Solution Pages 505-505","sourcePage":484,"sourcePageEnd":485},{"id":"physiology-17-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":17,"question":"‘c’ wave in JVP is seen in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Iso-volumetric contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Slow filling at end of diastole"},{"letter":"C","text":"End of systole"},{"letter":"D","text":"Start of diastole"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Iso-volumetric contraction: • The 'c' wave in the JVP is typically seen during the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle. It occurs during early ventricular systole when the tricuspid valve bulges back into the right atrium due to the increased pressure in the right ventricle. This causes a slight rise in the JVP. • The isovolumetric contraction occurs immediately after the closure of the atrioventricular valves (mitral and tricuspid valves) and before the onset of ventricular ejection. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 485-485; Solution Pages 505-506","sourcePage":485,"sourcePageEnd":485},{"id":"physiology-17-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":18,"question":"In the cardiac muscle, the inhibition of Na+ K+ ATPase results in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hyperpolarization and then depolarization"},{"letter":"B","text":"Depolarization"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyperpolarization"},{"letter":"D","text":"Repolarization"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Depolarization: • In the cardiac muscle, the Na + K + ATPase is an essential membrane-bound enzyme that maintains the resting membrane potential of the cell membrane. It actively transports sodium ions (Na + ) out of the cell and potassium ions (K + ) into the cell against their concentration gradients, utilizing ATP as an energy source. • When the Na + K + ATPase is inhibited, the efflux of Na + and influx of K + is reduced, which in turn disrupts a secondary active transporter, i.e., NCX (Sodium calcium exchanger) responsible for the efflux of the calcium out of the cell and influx of Na + . • This disruption of the Na + K + ATPase results in an increase of the positive charge inside the cell leading to depolarization. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 485-485; Solution Pages 506-506","sourcePage":485,"sourcePageEnd":485},{"id":"physiology-17-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":19,"question":"In the following LV pressure-volume loop diagram, point C denotes –","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mitral valve closes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aortic valve opens"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aortic valve closes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mitral valve opens"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Aortic Valve Opens: • In a left ventricular (LV) pressure-volume loop, point C represents the transition from the filling phase to the ejection phase of the cardiac cycle. During this transition, the ventricular volume reaches its maximum, and the ventricular pressure continues to rise leading to opening of the aortic valve and marks the beginning of the ejection phase.. Incorrect Options: Option A - Mitral valve closes: It is represented by the point B. Option C - Aortic valve closes: Closure of aortic valve is represented by point D. Option D - Mitral valve opens : It is represented by point A.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 485-486; Solution Pages 506-507","sourcePage":485,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"physiology-17-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following events occur immediately when a person changes his/ her posture from supine to standing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decrease in firing from baroreceptor"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vagal output to cardiac fibers increase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesenteric Vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"D","text":"decreased cardiac contractility"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Decrease in firing from baroreceptor: • When a person transitions from supine to standing, the effect of gravity causes blood to pool in the lower extremities. This decreases the venous return to the heart and subsequently reduces cardiac output. In response, the baroreceptors detect the decrease in blood pressure and initiate the baroreceptor reflex. • The baroreceptor reflex involves a decrease in firing from the baroreceptors, which leads to a subsequent increase in sympathetic nervous system activity and a decrease in parasympathetic (vagal) output. • This results in vasoconstriction of peripheral blood vessels, increased heart rate, and increased cardiac contractility, and increased venous return, and maintains the blood pressure. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 486-486; Solution Pages 507-507","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"physiology-17-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":21,"question":"The cardiac event that occurs at the end of the isometric relaxation phase is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Atrioventricular valves open"},{"letter":"B","text":"Atrioventricular valves close"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corresponds to the peak of C wave in JVP"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corresponds to T wave in ECG"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Atrioventricular valves open: • The cardiac event that occurs after the isometric relaxation phase is the opening of the atrioventricular valves followed by the first rapid filling phase of the ventricles. Incorrect Options: Option B - Atrioventricular valves close: Closure of atrioventricular valves marks the isovolumetric contraction phase. Option C - Corresponds to the peak of the C wave in JVP: Isovolumic relaxation phase is marked by ‘v’ wave. The C wave represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during ventricular contraction. Option D - Corresponds to the T wave in the ECG: The T wave in the ECG represents ventricular repolarization, which occurs before the isometric relaxation phase.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 486-486; Solution Pages 507-508","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"physiology-17-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":22,"question":"C wave of JVP represents?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contraction of right atrium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Passive filling of right atrium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Passive emptying of right atrium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Tricuspid bulge into right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction: • The ‘c’ wave of the jugular venous pulse (JVP) represents the bulging of the tricuspid valve into the right atrium during isovolumetric ventricular contraction. Incorrect Options: Option B - Contraction of right atrium: Contraction of the right atrium is represented by the ‘a’ wave. Option C - Passive filling of right atrium: Passive filling of the right atrium is represented by the ‘v’ wave . Option D - Passive emptying of right atrium: Passive emptying of the right atrium is not represented by the ‘y descent’ wave.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 486-486; Solution Pages 508-508","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"physiology-17-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":23,"question":"The first heart sound coincides with which phase of cardiac cycle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rapid atrial filling"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aortic ejection"},{"letter":"C","text":"Isovolumetric contraction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Isovolumetric relaxation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Isovolumetric contraction: • The first heart sound, also known as S1, is a low-pitched sound that occurs due to the closure of the atrioventricular valve during the beginning of isovolumetric contraction. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 487-487; Solution Pages 508-508","sourcePage":487,"sourcePageEnd":487},{"id":"physiology-17-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":24,"question":"The presence of a prominent 'v' wave of jugular venous pulse is suggestive of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tricuspid regurgitation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cardiomyopathy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tricuspid stenosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Atrial fibrillation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Tricuspid Regurgitation: • In a jugular venous examination, the presence of a prominent 'V' wave in the jugular venous pulse can be indicative of tricuspid regurgitation. Tricuspid regurgitation is a valvular heart condition where the tricuspid valve does not close properly, allowing blood to flow backward from the right ventricle to the right atrium during systole. This regurgitant flow leads to an increased 'V' wave in the jugular venous pulse. Incorrect Options: Option B - Cardiomyopathy: It is characterized by prominent ‘a’ waves. Option C - Tricuspid Stenosis: It is characterized by prominent ‘a’ waves. Option D - Atrial Fibrillation: It is characterized by absent ‘a’ waves.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 487-487; Solution Pages 509-509","sourcePage":487,"sourcePageEnd":487},{"id":"physiology-17-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Identify the true statement regarding the segment marked in red on the myocardial action potential curve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Referred to as the pacemaker potential"},{"letter":"B","text":"Plateau phase of the fast-type action potential"},{"letter":"C","text":"Membrane potential is returning to the resting membrane potential"},{"letter":"D","text":"Represents the resting membrane potential"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Plateau phase of the fast-type action potential : • The point marked on the myocardial action potential curve represents phase 2, the plateau phase. • This phase is characterized by the slow but prolonged opening of L-type calcium channels, aka dihydropyridine receptor (DHPR) designated as CaV 1.2, which allows the influx of calcium ions into the cell. • An outward potassium current balances this inward current through the slow, delayed rectifier potassium channels. • The net effect is a plateau phase in the action potential, making the cardiac muscle action potential unique compared to other types of action potentials. Incorrect Options: Option A: Seen in slow type Slow Type of Action Potential Options B: Represents phase 3 Options C:Represents phase 4","sourceRef":"Question Pages 487-488; Solution Pages 509-510","sourcePage":487,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"physiology-17-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":26,"question":"What options can be employed to determine cardiac output? A. ECHO B. Ventilation – perfusion scan C. Thermodilution D. Fick’s principle","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A, B, C, D"},{"letter":"B","text":"B, A, D"},{"letter":"C","text":"B, C, D"},{"letter":"D","text":"A, C, D"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - A, C, D: Methods used for the measurement of cardiac output are: • ECHO : The stroke volume is measured by assessing blood velocity through the arch of the aorta and multiplying the stroke volume by the heart rate. • Thermodilution: The thermodilution method is done by inserting a catheter at the level of pulmonary artery, hence known as the pulmonary artery catheter method. The thermodilution method is the gold-standard method. It works best for a high cardiac output state of the body. • Fick’s Principle: Fick's principle is a method used to calculate cardiac output based on the oxygen consumption of the body. By measuring the oxygen content of arterial and venous blood, along with the oxygen consumption, cardiac output can be calculated using the formula: • Cardiac Output = Oxygen Consumption / (Arterial Oxygen Content - Venous Oxygen Content) Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 488-488; Solution Pages 510-510","sourcePage":488,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"physiology-17-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":27,"question":"What is the cause of fetal heart rate acceleration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Carotid baroreceptor"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aortic baroreceptor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Autonomic nervous system"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chemoreceptors"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Autonomic nervous system: • The autonomic nervous system plays a crucial role in regulating various involuntary functions in the body, including heart rate. It controls the balance between the sympathetic and parasympathetic nervous systems, which are responsible for accelerating or decelerating heart rate, respectively. Fetal heart rate acceleration is primarily mediated by the autonomic nervous system in response to stimuli like fetal movement. Incorrect Options: Option A - Carotid baroreceptor: These receptors are involved in regulating blood pressure by sensing changes in arterial pressure. Option B - Aortic baroreceptor: Similar to carotid baroreceptors, they also play a role in regulating blood pressure. Option D - Chemoreceptors: Chemoreceptors are sensory receptors that detect changes in chemical concentrations, such as oxygen and carbon dioxide levels, in the blood.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 488-488; Solution Pages 511-511","sourcePage":488,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"physiology-17-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":28,"question":"Windkessel effect is not shown by which of the following vessels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pulmonary artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abdominal aorta"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Radial artery: • The Windkessel effect is due to arterial elasticity, which facilitates the movement of blood in arteries in forward direction during diastole. • The radial artery is a muscular artery rather than an elastic artery, and it does not exhibit the same level of elasticity and compliance as large elastic arteries like the aorta. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. These arteries show the Windkessel effect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 488-488; Solution Pages 511-511","sourcePage":488,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"physiology-17-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Blood pressure changes in the radial artery were measured while performing a valsalva maneuver. Which of the following is the reason for the initial rise in blood pressure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase in left ventricular volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decrease in left ventricular pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase in aortic pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease in aortic pressure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Increase in aortic pressure: • The Valsalva maneuver is a technique used to assess autonomic nervous system function and cardiovascular response. • Valsalva maneuver has four phases: Phase I (onset of strain): In this phase, there is a transient increase in blood pressure that lasts for a few seconds. This occurs due to increased intrathoracic pressure adding to the aortic pressure. Phase II (phase of straining): In this phase, venous return initially decreases, lowering cardiac output and blood pressure for 4 seconds. Later, blood pressure returns to normal due to increased peripheral resistance from sympathetic vasoconstriction. Heart rate steadily increases due to vagal withdrawal and sympathetic activation. Phase III: This phase occurs following the release of strain characterized by a transient decrease in blood pressure lasting for a few seconds. This is caused by the mechanical displacement of blood to the pulmonary vascular bed, which was under increased intrathoracic pressure. There is little change in heart rate. Phase IV: Occurs after further release of strain; blood pressure slowly increases, and heart rate decreases for about a minute. This is due to an increase in venous return, stroke volume, and cardiac output. • Phase I (onset of strain): In this phase, there is a transient increase in blood pressure that lasts for a few seconds. This occurs due to increased intrathoracic pressure adding to the aortic pressure. • Phase II (phase of straining): In this phase, venous return initially decreases, lowering cardiac output and blood pressure for 4 seconds. Later, blood pressure returns to normal due to increased peripheral resistance from sympathetic vasoconstriction. Heart rate steadily increases due to vagal withdrawal and sympathetic activation. • Phase III: This phase occurs following the release of strain characterized by a transient decrease in blood pressure lasting for a few seconds. This is caused by the mechanical displacement of blood to the pulmonary vascular bed, which was under increasedintrathoracic pressure. There is little change in heart rate. • Phase IV: Occurs after further release of strain; blood pressure slowly increases, and heart rate decreases for about a minute. This is due to an increase in venous return, stroke volume, and cardiac output. • Phase I (onset of strain): In this phase, there is a transient increase in blood pressure that lasts for a few seconds. This occurs due to increased intrathoracic pressure adding to the aortic pressure. • Phase II (phase of straining): In this phase, venous return initially decreases, lowering cardiac output and blood pressure for 4 seconds. Later, blood pressure returns to normal due to increased peripheral resistance from sympathetic vasoconstriction. Heart rate steadily increases due to vagal withdrawal and sympathetic activation. • Phase III: This phase occurs following the release of strain characterized by a transient decrease in blood pressure lasting for a few seconds. This is caused by the mechanical displacement of blood to the pulmonary vascular bed, which was under increased intrathoracic pressure. There is little change in heart rate. • Phase IV: Occurs after further release of strain; blood pressure slowly increases, and heart rate decreases for about a minute. This is due to an increase in venous return, stroke volume, and cardiac output. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 489-489; Solution Pages 512-513","sourcePage":489,"sourcePageEnd":489},{"id":"physiology-17-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":30,"question":"PR interval in ECG denotes which of the following?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Conduction through AV nodes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventricular depolarization"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventricular repolarization"},{"letter":"D","text":"Atrial depolarization"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Conduction through AV nodes: • The PR interval represents the time it takes for the electrical impulse to travel from the atria, through the atrioventricular (AV) node, and into the ventricles of the heart. It is measured from the beginning of the P wave (which represents atrial depolarization) to the beginning of the QRS complex (which represents ventricular depolarization). • The AV node acts as a gateway between the atria and the ventricles, slowing down the electrical impulse to allow for proper coordination and synchronization of atrial contraction before ventricular contraction. The PR interval, therefore, reflects the time it takes for the electrical signal to travel through the AV node. Incorrect Options: Option B - Ventricular depolarization: It is marked by the R wave of the ECG. Option C - Ventricular repolarization: Ventricular repolarization is represented by the T wave on an electrocardiogram (ECG). Option D - Atrial depolarization: It is represented by the P wave of the ECG.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 489-489; Solution Pages 513-514","sourcePage":489,"sourcePageEnd":489},{"id":"physiology-17-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Where do electrical impulses originate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SA node"},{"letter":"B","text":"AV node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bundle of his"},{"letter":"D","text":"Purkinje fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option. A - SA node: • The SA node, also known as the pacemaker of the heart, is a specialized group of cells located in the right atrium. It is responsible for initiating the electrical impulses that regulate the heart's rhythm. The SA node generates electrical impulses spontaneously, causing the atria to contract and initiating the electrical conduction system of the heart. Incorrect Options: • Options B ,C, and D are incorrect. These structures are latent pacemakers of the heart and help in the conduction of electrical impulse.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 489-489; Solution Pages 514-514","sourcePage":489,"sourcePageEnd":489},{"id":"physiology-17-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":32,"question":"The stroke volume depends on end-diastolic volume. This is best represented as?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Length–weight relationship"},{"letter":"B","text":"Length–velocity relationship"},{"letter":"C","text":"Length-tension relationship"},{"letter":"D","text":"Length-viscosity relationship"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Length-tension relationship: • Accurately represents the relationship between the stretching of the cardiac muscle fibers (due to EDV) and the force of contraction (stroke volume). When the cardiac muscle fibers are stretched to an optimal length , they can generate a stronger contraction, leading to a larger stroke volume. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 490-490; Solution Pages 514-514","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":490},{"id":"physiology-17-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which of the following jugular venous pressure (JVP) waves corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A wave"},{"letter":"B","text":"C wave"},{"letter":"C","text":"X descent"},{"letter":"D","text":"Y descent"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - C wave: • The jugular venous pressure (JVP) reflects the pressure in the right atrium, corresponding to different cardiac cycle phases. • The C wave in the JVP corresponds to the isovolumetric contraction phase of the cardiac cycle. It occurs during early ventricular systole when the tricuspid valve bulges back into the right atrium due to the increased pressure in the right ventricle. This causes a slight rise in the JVP. Incorrect Options: Option A - A wave: The A wave in the JVP represents atrial contraction. Option C - X descent: The X descent in the JVP represents the atrial relaxation phase. Option D - Y descent: The Y descent in the JVP represents the rapid filling phase of the right ventricle. It occurs during early diastole when the tricuspid valve opens, and blood flows from the right atrium to the right ventricle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 490-490; Solution Pages 515-515","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":490},{"id":"physiology-17-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Reflex responsible for tachycardia during right atrial distension is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bezold-Jarisch reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bain bridge reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cushing reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Baroreceptor reflex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Bain bridge reflex: • The Bainbridge reflex is a cardiovascular reflex that increases heart rate in response to increased blood volume in the right atrium. • Special pressure sensors called baroreceptors (or veno-atrial stretch receptors) located in the right atrium of the heart detect increases in the volume and pressure of blood returned to the heart. • These receptors transmit information along the vagus nerve (10th cranial nerve) to the central nervous system. • This response activates sympathetic nerve pathways that increase the strength of contraction of the heart muscle and heart rate (tachycardia). Incorrect Options: Option A - Bezold-Jarisch reflex: • The Bezold-Jarisch reflex is an inhibitory reflex usually denoted as a cardioinhibitory reflex defined as bradycardia, vasodilation, and hypotension resulting from the stimulation of cardiac receptors. • The Bezold-Jarisch reflex originates from inhibitory mechanoreceptors in the left ventricle (particularly the infero-posterior wall). Option C - Cushing reflex: • The Cushing reflex is a physiological nervous system response to acute elevations of intracranial pressure (ICP), resulting in Cushing’s triad of widened pulse pressure (increasing systolic, decreasing diastolic), hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations. Option D - Baroreceptor reflex: • Baroreceptors are present in the carotid sinus, arch of aorta, and adventitial layer of blood vessels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 490-490; Solution Pages 515-516","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":490},{"id":"physiology-17-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure increases in which of the following?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypovolemic shock"},{"letter":"B","text":"Septic shock"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cardiogenic shock"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anaphylactic shock"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Cardiogenic shock: Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) increases in the following condition: • Pulmonary capillary wedge pressure measures the left atrium pressure. Cardiogenic shock refers to a state of inadequate cardiac output due to severe dysfunction or failure of the heart. In this condition, PCWP typically increases. Cardiogenic shock can result from conditions such as myocardial infarction (heart attack), severe heart failure, or arrhythmias, leading to decreased cardiac output and elevated pressures within the heart chambers, including the left atrium. • The elevated PCWP reflects the increased pressure in the left atrium, which is transmitted to the pulmonary capillaries. Incorrect Options: Option A - Hypovolemic shock: Hypovolemic shock occurs due to a significant loss of blood volume, such as from hemorrhage or fluid loss. In hypovolemic shock, the PCWP may be decreased. Option B - Septic shock: Septic shock is a severe systemic infection characterized by inflammatory response causing vasodilation and increased capillary permeability, which can lead to decreased systemic vascular resistance and lower filling pressures. In septic shock, the PCWP may be normal or decreased.. Option D - Anaphylactic shock: In anaphylactic shock, the PCWP may be normal or decreased. The systemic vasodilation and increased capillary permeability associated with anaphylaxis can result in decreased filling pressures, including PCWP.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 490-491; Solution Pages 516-517","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"physiology-17-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":36,"question":"What is the cause of dicrotic notch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Passive filling of blood in ventricles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rapid ejection phase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Closure of semilunar valves"},{"letter":"D","text":"Isovolumic contraction"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Closure of semilunar valves: • Closure of semilunar valves: The dicrotic notch is a small downward deflection that occurs in the aortic pressure waveform after the c losure of the aortic valve (semilunar valve) . Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Dicrotic notch is formed due to closure of aortic valve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 491-491; Solution Pages 517-517","sourcePage":491,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"physiology-17-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes Isovolumic relaxation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"AV valves are closed"},{"letter":"B","text":"Corresponds to QT interval"},{"letter":"C","text":"'C' wave of JVP"},{"letter":"D","text":"Semilunar valves open"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Potassium ions: • Isovolumic relaxation is marked by closure of the semilunar valves and already closed atrioventricular valves. The duration of this phase is 0.06 sec. • It corresponds to the ‘v’ wave of jugular venous pulse. Incorrect Options: Option B - Corresponds to QT interval: Ventricular depolarization and repolarization corresponds to the QT interval. Options C and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 491-491; Solution Pages 517-518","sourcePage":491,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"physiology-17-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-17","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":38,"question":"What is the increase in resistance of an artery if its radius is reduced to one-third?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"9 times"},{"letter":"B","text":"81 times"},{"letter":"C","text":"27 times"},{"letter":"D","text":"18 times"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 81 times: • According to the Hagen–Poiseuille equation, resistance can be directly measured by the following formula. • R = 8ηL/pr 4 • Where, R = resistance. η = viscosity. L = length. r = radius of the vessel. • R = resistance. • η = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • From this equation, the resistance of a vessel is inversely proportional to its r 4 (radius). If other parameters remain constant. • So, if the radius is reduced by 1/3rd of the original, then resistance will be: R = 1/ (1/3) 4 . R = 81. • R = 1/ (1/3) 4 . • R = 81. • So, the resistance will increase by 81 times. • R = resistance. • η = viscosity. • L = length. • r = radius of the vessel. • R = 1/ (1/3) 4 . • R = 81. Incorrect Options: Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 491-491; Solution Pages 518-518","sourcePage":491,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"physiology-18-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following conditions is associated with a right shift in Arneth count?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Active tuberculosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Acute progressive infections"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bone marrow suppression"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bone marrow stimulation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Answer : C) Bone marrow suppression Explanation: The presence of older neutrophils (N4 and N5) indicates suppression of bone marrow with a shift to the right in Arneth count. The Arneth count is a method used to classify neutrophils as N1 to N5 based on the number of lobes in their nuclei. Class I : Myelocytes and neutrophils with indented or horseshoe-shaped nuclei. (5%) Class II : Neutrophils with bilobed nuclei. (35%) Class III : Neutrophils with tri-lobed nuclei. (41%) Class IV : Neutrophils with four-lobed nuclei. (17%) Classification of Neutrophil Class V : Neutrophils with five or more lobes in their nuclei. (2%) Toxic Conditions Fewer cells in Classes IV and V indicate a left-sided shift Active tuberculosis often shows a left shift. (Option A) Other infections showing a left shift may have superimposed tuberculosis Extrinsic Factors Influencing Certain drugs, tissue extracts, and hormones. Arneth Count Trauma , various forms of radiation. Induction of anesthesia. Infections and Arneth Count Acute progressive infections: Notable left shift. (Option B) As infection subsides: Less marked left shift, may shift to the right. Chronic infections: Other features like lymphocytosis come into range. Normal Neutrophil Distribution N1 and N3 constitute most neutrophils in peripheral blood. Bone Marrow Stimulation Presence of younger neutrophils (N1 and N2) indicates stimulation of bone marrow (shift to the left). (Option D) Bone Marrow Suppression Presence of older cells (N4 and N5) indicates suppression of bone marrow (shift to right) (Option C) Hypersegmented Neutrophil Seen in megaloblastic anemia due to folate and vitamin B12 deficiency (shift to right)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 520-520; Solution Pages 525-526","sourcePage":520,"sourcePageEnd":520},{"id":"physiology-18-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements about platelets are true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alpha granules contain clotting factors and platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Splenectomy causes a decrease in the platelet count"},{"letter":"C","text":"Interleukins IL-2, IL-4, IL-5, and IL-7 are involved in stimulating platelet production"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thrombopoietin is produced exclusively by megakaryocytes and regulates platelet production"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Alpha granules contain clotting factors and platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF)","explanation":"Alpha granules are abundant in platelets, about 50-80 granules per platelet. They contain secreted proteins like clotting factors and platelet-derived growth factor(PDGF). Platelets General Description Small, granulated, non-nucleated discs important for hemostasis Size 1-4 µm in diameter Concentration in Blood 150,000 - 450,000/µL Half-life ~4 days Origin Formed from megakaryocytes in the bone marrow Distribution 60-75% in circulation; the remainder mainly in the spleen Splenectomy Effect Causes an increase in platelet count (thrombocytosis) (Option B) Thrombopoietin Produced by liver and kidneys; regulates platelet production (Option D) Membrane Receptors For collagen, ADP, von Willebrand factor and fibrinogen Cytoplasmic Components Actin, myosin, glycogen, lysosomes and granules Granules Alpha granules: Soluble coagulation proteins (vWF, Factor V, II ), adhesion molecule, growth factor (IGF, PDGF , TGF-β, platelet factor 4), Integrin, Cytokines (Option A) Dense granules: ADP, ATP, Calcium, Serotonin Response to Injury Adhere to exposed collagen and von Willebrand factor; release granule contents upon activation Deficiency Effect Thrombocytopenic purpura (easy bruising, multiple subcutaneous hemorrhages) Interleukins IL-1, IL-3, IL-6, IL-11 stimulate platelet production (Option C) Factors increasing Inflammation, thrombopoietin and IL-1, IL-3, IL-6, IL-11 platelet synthesis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 520-520; Solution Pages 526-527","sourcePage":520,"sourcePageEnd":520},{"id":"physiology-18-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following coagulation factors is crucial for initiating the extrinsic pathway of blood coagulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Factor II"},{"letter":"B","text":"Factor III"},{"letter":"C","text":"Factor V"},{"letter":"D","text":"Factor VII"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Factor III","explanation":"Factor III, also known as Tissue thromboplastin , is responsible for the initiation of the extrinsic pathway of coagulation. Clotting Factors: Clotting Factor Synonyms Function Half lif e I Fibrinogen Clot formation 3-5 da ys II (Option A) Prothrombin Converts fibrinogen to 60 hou fibrin rs Activates platelets Also has anti clotting activity Has growth factor and cytokine like activities III (Option B) Tissue thromboplastin Initiator of extrinsic - pathway of coagulation Activates factor VII IV Calcium Convert X to Xa - Convert prothrombin to thrombin V (Option C) Proaccelerin; labile factor; Activate prothrombin to 12 hou accelerator globulin (Ac-G) thrombin rs Participates in activation of X VII (Option D) Serum prothrombin Activates factor IX and X 3.5 ho conversion accelerator urs (SPCA); proconvertin; stable factor VIII Antihemophilic Activates factor X 8 - 12 factor(AHF); antihemophili hours c globulin(AHG); antihemophilic factor A IX Plasma thromboplastin Activates factor X 18 - 24 component (PTC); hours antihemophilic factor B ; christmas factor X Stuart factor; Stuart-Prowe Conversion of 34 - 40 r factor prothrombin to thrombin hours XI Plasma thromboplastin Activates factor IX 45 hou antecedent (PTA); rs antihemophilic factor C XII Hageman factor; glass Activates factor XI - factor XIII Fibrin- stabilizing factor; Crosslinks fibrin 10 day Laki-Lorand factor s Prekallikrein Fletcher factor Contact activation of 35 hou blood coagulation rs High molecular weight Fitzgerald factor Non enzymatic cofactor 150 ho kininogen (HMW-K) in contact activation urs Act as thiol protease inhibitor Platelets phospholipid PL Temporary hemostasis 4 days Synthesis factor V and XI Deficiency Clinical Syndrome Cause of Factor I Afibrinogenemia Depletion during pregnancy with premature separation of placenta; also congenital (rare) II Hypoprothrombinemia Decreased hepatic synthesis, often (hemorrhagic tendency in liver due to vitamin K deficiency disease) V Parahemophilia Congenital VII Hypoconvertinemia Congenital VIII Hemophilia A Congenital defect in the gene on the (classic hemophilia) X chromosome coding for factor VIII; inherited as a sex-linked characteristic. IX Hemophilia B Congenital (Christmas disease) X Stuart-Prower factor deficiency Congenital XI PTA deficiency Congenital XII Hageman trait Congenital","sourceRef":"Question Pages 520-520; Solution Pages 527-529","sourcePage":520,"sourcePageEnd":520},{"id":"physiology-18-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Identify the incorrect statement:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Platelet concentrates have a shelf life of up to 8 days at +22°C ± 2°C"},{"letter":"B","text":"Red cell concentrates stored with CPDA-1 have a shelf life of 35 days"},{"letter":"C","text":"Plasma stored at temperatures colder than –25°C has a shelf life of up to 3 years"},{"letter":"D","text":"Red cell concentrates stored with CPD have a shelf life of 28 days"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A)Platelet concentrates have a shelf life of up to 8 days at +22°C ± 2°C","explanation":"Platelets can be kept for as long as 8–13 days , but blood banks are only allowed to keep them for 5 days because of bacterial contamination. Blood constituents Preservative/ Uses Temp Shelf life eratur e Red cells Citrate-citric acid- glucose Blood transfusion +4°C (ACD) / 21 days ± 2°C Citrate phosphate dextrose (CPD) / 28 days (Option D) CPDA-1(CPD with adenine) / 35 days (Option B) CPD+ Additive solution / 42 days Platelets Plasma / 5-7 days Platelet therapy +22° (5 days preferred due to C ± 2° bacterial risk) (Option A) C Plasma CPD,ACD, Buffered Stable clotting factors (FII, +4°C Citrate/ 3 years if frozen/ FVII, FIX, FX, fibrinogen) ± 2°C 3 months Labile clotting factors (FV, 3 years if colder than FVIII) -25°C (Option C) / 3months if stored at -18°C to -25°C","sourceRef":"Question Pages 521-521; Solution Pages 529-530","sourcePage":521,"sourcePageEnd":521},{"id":"physiology-18-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 45-year-old patient needs an urgent blood transfusion. The image below shows the results of the patient’s blood group typing test. Based on the agglutination patterns observed, identify the patient's blood group?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"O positive(O+ve)"},{"letter":"B","text":"A positive (A-ve)"},{"letter":"C","text":"AB positive (AB+ve)"},{"letter":"D","text":"B positive (B+ve)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) AB positive (AB+ve)","explanation":"In the above clinical scenario, the image shows agglutination with anti-A and anti-B and positive anti-D (Rh) sera, indicating the presence of A antigen, B antigen and positive Rh factor. Therefore, the blood group is AB positive (AB+ve). A-B-O System: Blood type Antigens on RBC Antibodies in Genotypes Frequency Plasma O - Anti-A and Anti-B OO 47% A A Anti-B OA or AA 41% B B Anti-A OB or BB 9% AB A and B - AB 3% Landsteiner's Law states that if a particular agglutinogen is present on the red cell membrane of an individual, the corresponding agglutinin must be absent in his plasma and vice versa. Agglutination test results: H Antigen: • The H gene codes for fucose transferase, forming the H antigen, found in all blood types . • In addition, Type A adds N-acetylgalactosamine, Type B adds galactose, Type AB has both transferases, and Type O has neither, leaving the H antigen unchanged . • Blood group O with H antigen in plasma or saliva is called the OH group or Bombay (Oh : if H is not dominant). Bombay Blood Group : • Phenotype: Oh , lacking the H gene and unable to form A or B antigens. • Identified as group O in routine tests, with serum containing anti-A, anti-B, and anti-H antibodies. Therefore transfusion can be done only with Bombay blood group. Rh Factor : Common Rh antigen C, D, E, c, d and e s Detection Only in red cells D antigen Most antigenic, defines Rh positive No D antigen Rh negative ; Forms anti-D agglutinins upon exposure to Rh+ cells (Option B) Prevalence 85% of whites Rh+ve ,15% Rh-ve Over 99% of Asians Rh+ve Anti-D antibody Requires exposure (transfusion or feto-maternal blood formation transfer) to D-positive red cells Transfusion Requires massive exposure to Rh antigen to produce reaction onset enough agglutinins for significant transfusion reaction to develop","sourceRef":"Question Pages 521-521; Solution Pages 530-532","sourcePage":521,"sourcePageEnd":521},{"id":"physiology-18-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A pediatrician is reviewing the development of hematopoiesis in a fetus at various stages of gestation. They are particularly interested in understanding the shifts in hematopoietic activity locations as the fetus develops. At which gestational age does the bone marrow become the primary site of hematopoiesis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3-6 weeks"},{"letter":"B","text":"6-8 weeks"},{"letter":"C","text":"Around 20 weeks"},{"letter":"D","text":"After birth"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Around 20 weeks","explanation":"Around 20 weeks of gestation, hematopoiesis becomes centered in the bone marrow, which remains the major site for the remainder of fetal development and throughout adult life. Stage Location Key Points Mesobl Yolk sac, PSP, AGM Yolk sac: Multilineage differentiating astic capabilities (starts functioning by the 2nd week of gestation). Non-yolk sac regions: Para Aortic splanchnopleura (PSP) and aorta-gonad mesonephros (AGM) regions. PSP: Gives rise to B-cell progenitors. AGM: Contains pluripotent stem cells. Hepati Liver, spleen 2nd trimester of pregnancy. c Liver: Hematopoiesis starts at the 5th week, peaks at the 5th month, and ceases at birth. Spleen: Hematopoiesis mainly in the 2nd trimester, less than liver. Postnatal: Normally does not occur in liver and spleen unless demand is high (extramedullary hemopoiesis). Medull Bone marrow (medullary Marrow cavities appear by the 5th ary hemopoiesis). month (around 20 weeks) of fetal life, and become hemopoietic. (Option C) Initial phase: Bone marrow exclusive for granulocytic and megakaryocytic proliferation. Erythropoiesis starts in bone marrow towards the end of the 3rd trimester, and continues throughout life. Postnatal: Hemopoiesis confined to bone marrow, extramedullary hemopoiesis abnormal.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 521-522; Solution Pages 532-533","sourcePage":521,"sourcePageEnd":522},{"id":"physiology-18-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A researcher is studying the behavior of hematopoietic stem cells (HSCs) and wants to understand the mechanisms that regulate their self-renewal during tissue repair. Which type of stem cell division increases the stem cell pool during tissue repair?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Asymmetric division"},{"letter":"B","text":"Symmetric division"},{"letter":"C","text":"Terminal differentiation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral inhibition"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Symmetric division","explanation":"Symmetric division results in both daughter cells retaining stem cell properties, thereby increasing the stem cell pool, which is crucial during tissue repair. Pathwa Definition Mechanisms / Factors Signaling y Pathways Self-Re Produces at least one Symmetric and Wnt, Notch, newal daughter cell with stem Asymmetric Division TGF-β cell properties Differen Produces specialized cell Transcription Factors, Wnt, Notch, tiation types Microenvironmental TGF-β Signals Both Pa Balance between Interplay of intrinsic and thways self-renewal and extrinsic factors differentiation to maintain stem cell pool and tissue function Stem Cell Type Description Example Totipotent Stem Can generate the entire organism, Single cell from the 8-cell embryo Cells including the placenta. stage. Pluripotent Can generate the entire organism Cells from the inner cell mass of the Stem Cells except the placenta. blastocyst. Multipotent Can form only one lineage of cells. Hematopoietic stem cells that give Stem Cells rise to various blood cell types. Steps in hematopoiesis: Multipotent Stem Cell ➜ Progenitor Cell ➜ Precursor Cell ➜ Mature Cell Mature Cell: Differentiated cell with decreased self-renewal and proliferation properties. • Hematopoietic Stem Cells: Convert to multipotent stem cells, giving rise to two progenitor types. • Memory B and T Cells are mature cells but have the ability to self-renew and proliferate. Asymmetric division (Option A) – Maintains homeostasis by generating one stem cell and one differentiated cell , rather than expanding the pool. Terminal differentiation (Option C) – Leads to fully specialized cells with no further division Lateral inhibition (Option D) – A process in which a cell inhibits its neighboring cells from adopting the same fate, playing a role in cell differentiation rather than expanding the stem cell pool.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 522-522; Solution Pages 533-534","sourcePage":522,"sourcePageEnd":522},{"id":"physiology-18-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements best describes the Burst Forming Unit-Erythroid (BFU-E) in the process of erythropoiesis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a mature red blood cell that participates in oxygen transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"It is a type of stem cell that can differentiate into any blood cell type"},{"letter":"C","text":"It is an early progenitor cell in erythropoiesis that gives rise to CFU-E"},{"letter":"D","text":"It is a precursor cell in erythropoiesis that directly produces reticulocytes"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) It is an early progenitor cell in erythropoiesis that gives rise to CFU-E","explanation":"Erythropoiesis: Since hemoglobin is not present in BFU-E, it can not transport oxygen. (Option A) Multipotent hematopoietic stem cell gives rise to all the cells circulating in blood. (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 522-522; Solution Pages 535-535","sourcePage":522,"sourcePageEnd":522},{"id":"physiology-18-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding erythropoietin is not true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is majorly produced by the liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Major stimulus for its production is hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"It acts on JAK-STAT pathway"},{"letter":"D","text":"The maximum erythropoietin receptor density is seen on CFU-E"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) It is majorly produced by the liver","explanation":"Erythropoietin: • It is the major regulator of erythropoiesis, which increases the number of RBCs in blood by acting on the JAK STAT pathway (Option C) . • Erythropoietin is synthesized from: Kidney (around 90%) (Option A) Liver Brain • Kidney (around 90%) (Option A) • Liver • Brain • The major stimulus for erythropoietin production is Low oxygen levels or tissue hypoxia (Option B) . • When there is tissue hypoxia, erythropoietin acts on Burst forming unit-Erythroid (BFU-E), converting them into colony forming unit-Erythroid (CFU-E), which has the maximum erythropoietin receptor density (Option D) . • CFU-E on the continued action of erythropoietin converts into proerythrocytes and following. • Kidney (around 90%) (Option A) • Liver • Brain","sourceRef":"Question Pages 522-523; Solution Pages 535-536","sourcePage":522,"sourcePageEnd":523},{"id":"physiology-18-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":10,"question":"How many oxygen atoms can one molecule of hemoglobin bind?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"6"},{"letter":"D","text":"8"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 8","explanation":"Hemoglobin: • Hb synthesis begins in the polychromatophil erythroblasts. • The most common form of hemoglobin found in adults is HbA, which is a combination of 2 alpha chains and 2 beta chains. • The heme group in one Hb molecule has 4 atoms of iron, which binds to 4 molecules of oxygen or 8 atoms of oxygen (option D) • Therefore, each Hb molecule carries 4 oxygen molecules to the tissues.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 523-523; Solution Pages 536-536","sourcePage":523,"sourcePageEnd":523},{"id":"physiology-18-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following is the primary protein responsible for binding and transporting free heme in the bloodstream?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Haptoglobin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Albumin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hemopexin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transferrin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Hemopexin","explanation":"• Hemopexin is a primary protein that is responsible for binding and transporting free heme in the bloodstream. • When RBC undergoes hemolysis, heme is released into the blood. • Free heme is potentially toxic due to its ability to generate reactive oxygen species and cause oxidative damage. • Hemopexin binds to free heme with high affinity, facilitating safe transport of the liver for degradation and prevention of toxicity (option D). Haptoglobin binds free hemoglobin, but not free heme (Option A) Albumin maintains osmotic pressure and serves as a carrier protein for various substances, including hormones, fatty acids, and drugs, but is not a carrier of heme (Option B) Transferrin is responsible for transfer of iron in the bloodstream. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 523-523; Solution Pages 536-537","sourcePage":523,"sourcePageEnd":523},{"id":"physiology-18-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-18","chapter":"Blood Physiology","topic":"Blood Physiology","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 45-year-old female with asthma presents with persistent coughing, wheezing, and shortness of breath. Which interleukin is most directly involved in the activation of eosinophils in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"IL-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"IL-4"},{"letter":"C","text":"IL-5"},{"letter":"D","text":"IL-10"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) IL-5","explanation":"In an allergic reaction, the leukocytes most involved are eosinophils. The interleukin involved in eosinophilia is IL-5. Leukopoiesis: Factors involved in leukopoiesis: • Colony-stimulating factors such as G-CSF and GM-CSF • IL-5 is required for differentiation of leukocytes into eosinophils (option C) • IL-3 & 4 are required for the development of basophils (option B) IL-1 is responsible for the activation of T cells and macrophages and the promotion of inflammation (Option A) IL-10 is an anti-inflammatory cytokine that regulates immune reactions. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 523-523; Solution Pages 537-538","sourcePage":523,"sourcePageEnd":523},{"id":"physiology-19-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":1,"question":"What is the underlying cause of hypoxia in a newly arrived resident in a high-altitude region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Low hemoglobin levels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Low partial pressure of O 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Low blood lactate levels"},{"letter":"D","text":"High partial pressure of CO 2"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: • Low partial pressure of O 2 cause hypoxia in high altitude. • In the given scenario, the individual has recently moved to a high-altitude area with lower atmospheric pressure than at sea level. • The partial pressure of oxygen in the air is directly proportional to the atmospheric pressure. • At high altitudes, the atmospheric pressure decreases, and consequently, the partial pressure of oxygen decreases. • This results in a reduced amount of oxygen being available for inhalation, leading to hypoxia. Incorrect Options: Option A : Low hemoglobin levels can lead to a condition called anaemia. Anaemia can cause hypoxia, but it is not the causative factor in this scenario. Option C: Blood lactate levels are not related to the development of hypoxia. increased lactic acid levels cause the fatigue. Option D: High levels of carbon dioxide (CO2) in the blood can lead to a condition called hypercapnia. Hypercapnia can cause hypoxia, but it is not the causative factor in this scenario.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 539-539; Solution Pages 544-545","sourcePage":539,"sourcePageEnd":539},{"id":"physiology-19-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":2,"question":"In the extrinsic pathway of coagulation, which clotting factor takes part?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Factor VIII"},{"letter":"B","text":"Factor VII"},{"letter":"C","text":"Factor XI"},{"letter":"D","text":"Factor IX"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: • The extrinsic pathway of coagulation is initiated by tissue factor (TF), which is released from damaged tissues. • Factor VII (FVII) is a vitamin K-dependent clotting factor that is activated by binding to TF. • Once activated, the FVII-TF complex activates factor X (FX), which leads to the formation of thrombin and ultimately the formation of a blood clot. Incorrect Options: Option A: Factor VIII is a clotting factor that participates in the intrinsic pathway of coagulation, which is activated by damage to the endothelial lining of blood vessels. Option C: Factor XI is also a clotting factor that participates in the intrinsic pathway of coagulation. Option D: Factor IX is a clotting factor that participates in the intrinsic pathway of coagulation, and is activated by factor XI.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 539-539; Solution Pages 545-545","sourcePage":539,"sourcePageEnd":539},{"id":"physiology-19-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Hemoglobin with iron in ferric form is","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Methemoglobin"},{"letter":"B","text":"HbA"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fetal hemoglobin"},{"letter":"D","text":"HbS"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: • Methemoglobin is a form of hemoglobin where the iron in heme is oxidized to the ferric state (Fe3+). • This alters the shape of the heme group, making it unable to bind oxygen effectively. • Methemoglobinemia is a condition characterized by increased methemoglobin in the blood, which can result in decreased oxygen delivery to the tissues. • Hemoglobin is a protein found in red blood cells that is responsible for carrying oxygen from the lungs to the tissues of the body. • The iron in hemoglobin is in the ferrous (Fe2+) form, which allows it to bind to oxygen. • However, under certain conditions, the iron in hemoglobin can be oxidized to the ferric (Fe3+) form, forming methemoglobin. Incorrect Options: Option B: Hemoglobin A (HbA) is the most common form of hemoglobin in adults. It is composed of two alpha and two beta globin chains, each containing a heme group with a ferrous (Fe2+) ion. Option C: Fetal hemoglobin (HbF) is the primary form of hemoglobin found in fetuses and newborn infants. It has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin, which allows for efficient transfer of oxygen from the mother's blood to the fetus. HbF has two alpha and two gamma globin chains, with each chain containing a heme group with a ferrous (Fe2+) ion. Option D: Hemoglobin S (HbS) is a variant of hemoglobin that results from a mutation in the beta globin gene. It is responsible for sickle cell disease, a condition in which red blood cells become stiff and sickle-shaped, leading to decreased oxygen delivery and various complications. HbS contains two alpha and two mutant beta globin chains, each with a heme group containing a ferrous (Fe2+) ion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 539-539; Solution Pages 546-546","sourcePage":539,"sourcePageEnd":539},{"id":"physiology-19-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Please match the following molecules with their corresponding carriers in plasma. TRANSPORTER ORGANIC SUBSTANCE 1. Thyroxine A. Hemopexin 2. Fatty acid B. Prealbumin 3. Hemoglobin C. Albumin 4. Heme D. Haptoglobin TRANSPORTER ORGANIC SUBSTANCE 1. Thyroxine A. Hemopexin 2. Fatty acid B. Prealbumin 3. Hemoglobin C. Albumin 4. Heme D. Haptoglobin","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-A, 2-D, 3-B, 4-C"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-B, 2-C, 3-A, 4-D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1-B, 2-C, 3-D, 4-A: 1. Thyroxine • Correct Match: Thyroxine is transported by Albumin (Option C) . Albumin is the most abundant plasma protein and carries various substances, including thyroxine. 2. Fatty acid • Correct Match: Fatty acids are transported by Prealbumin (also known as Transthyretin) (Option B) . Prealbumin binds to thyroxine as well as fatty acids. 3. Hemoglobin • Correct Match: Hemoglobin is transported by Haptoglobin (Option D) . Haptoglobin binds to free hemoglobin, preventing its loss through the kidneys and potential kidney damage. 4. Heme • Correct Match: Heme is transported by Hemopexin (Option A) . Hemopexin binds to heme and prevents its oxidative effects and potential tissue damage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 540-540; Solution Pages 546-547","sourcePage":540,"sourcePageEnd":540},{"id":"physiology-19-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In patients undergoing warfarin therapy, which clotting factor would display a decrease in gamma carboxyglutamate residue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Factor 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Factor 11"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tissue factor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Factor 5"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: Factor 2 • The clotting factor that would have a decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residuevitamin in patients on warfarin therapy is Factor 2, also known as prothrombin. • By inhibiting the regeneration of reduced vitamin K, warfarin decreases the availability of active vitamin K in the body. This results in impaired carboxylation of clotting factors, leading to decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residues. Among the clotting factors affected by warfarin therapy, Factor 2 (prothrombin) is one of them. Therefore, patients on warfarin therapy would have decreased gamma carboxyglutamate residues in Factor 2. Incorrect Options: Option B, C & D: Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant medication that inhibits the activity of vitamin K epoxide reductase, an enzyme required for the regeneration of reduced vitamin K. Vitamin K is essential for the carboxylation of certain clotting factors, including Factors 2, 7, 9, and 10, and proteins C and S. The carboxylation process adds gamma carboxyglutamate residues to these factors, allowing them to bind calcium ions and participate in the coagulation cascade.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 540-540; Solution Pages 547-547","sourcePage":540,"sourcePageEnd":540},{"id":"physiology-19-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What makes CPDA superior than ACD for blood storage, except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The fall in 2,3 DPG is less"},{"letter":"B","text":"Improved Viability of Red Blood Cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased Potassium Load"},{"letter":"D","text":"It is less acidic"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Option C. Increased Potassium Load: Incorrect . CPDA does not cause an increased potassium load compared to ACD. Both CPDA and ACD experience a rise in potassium levels during blood storage as red blood cells leak potassium over time. However, CPDA generally preserves the structural integrity of RBCs better than ACD, which helps reduce potassium leakage. Therefore, CPDA does not result in an increased potassium load and is in fact preferable for reducing the risk of hyperkalemia in sensitive patients. Incorrect options Option A. The fall in 2,3 DPG is less: Correct. CPDA helps preserve 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3 DPG), an important compound for oxygen delivery. Higher levels of 2,3 DPG in stored blood ensure that oxygen is released more readily to tissues when transfused. Option B. Improved Viability of Red Blood Cells: Correct. CPDA helps maintain the viability and structural integrity of red blood cells, ensuring they function effectively after transfusion. Higher viability means the transfused cells can properly carry oxygen to the body's tissues. Option D. It is less acidic: Correct. CPDA has a higher pH compared to ACD, making it less acidic. This less acidic environment helps preserve the structural integrity of red blood cells during storage. Acidic conditions can cause hemolysis, damaging the cells and affecting their functionality.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 540-540; Solution Pages 548-548","sourcePage":540,"sourcePageEnd":540},{"id":"physiology-19-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What would be the duration for preserving red blood cells (RBCs) using Acid Citrate Dextrose (ACD) for storage, when phosphate is added alone and when adenine and phosphate are added together to ACD?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"35 days and 42 days"},{"letter":"B","text":"21 days and 35 days"},{"letter":"C","text":"28 days and 45 days"},{"letter":"D","text":"35 days and 28 days"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"The correct answer is: Option B. 21 days and 35 days Explanation: • Red Blood Cells (RBCs) Preservation using ACD (Acid Citrate Dextrose) : ACD is an anticoagulant solution used for the preservation of blood. When RBCs are preserved using ACD alone, the duration of storage is approximately 21 days . • Addition of Adenine and Phosphate to ACD : Adenine and phosphate are often added to the ACD solution to enhance the preservation of RBCs. With the addition of adenine and phosphate to ACD, the duration of storage is extended to approximately 35 days . Red Blood Cells (RBCs) Preservation using ACD (Acid Citrate Dextrose) : ACD is an anticoagulant solution used for the preservation of blood. When RBCs are preserved using ACD alone, the duration of storage is approximately 21 days . Addition of Adenine and Phosphate to ACD : Adenine and phosphate are often added to the ACD solution to enhance the preservation of RBCs. With the addition of adenine and phosphate to ACD, the duration of storage is extended to approximately 35 days . These additives provide necessary nutrients and enhance the storage conditions, allowing for a longer preservation period of red blood cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 541-541; Solution Pages 548-549","sourcePage":541,"sourcePageEnd":541},{"id":"physiology-19-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the blood group determined by the agglutination test depicted below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"B positive"},{"letter":"B","text":"A positive"},{"letter":"C","text":"O positive"},{"letter":"D","text":"AB positive"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct option is C: O positive. • O positive : In this case, there is no agglutination in either A or B antigens, indicating the absence of ABO antigens on the red blood cells. Blood group O is known as the universal donor because individuals with blood group O do not have A or B antigens on their red blood cells. Incorrect options: Option A : B positive : Since there is no agglutination in the B antigen, it is not indicative of blood group B. Therefore, option a) is incorrect. Option B: A positive : Similarly, since there is no agglutination in the A antigen, it is not indicative of blood group A. Therefore, option b) is incorrect. Option D: AB positive : Agglutination in both A and B antigens would indicate the presence of both A and B antigens on the red blood cells, which corresponds to blood group AB. However, since no agglutination is observed in the A or B antigens in this case, blood group AB is not the correct option. Therefore, option d) is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 541-541; Solution Pages 549-549","sourcePage":541,"sourcePageEnd":541},{"id":"physiology-19-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is not synthesized by the liver?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Albumin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Coagulation factors"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acute phase proteins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Immunoglobulin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct option is D: Immunoglobulin. • Immunoglobulins , also known as antibodies, are primarily produced by plasma cells, which are a type of white blood cell derived from B lymphocytes . While the liver is involved in immune-related functions , such as phagocytosis by Kupffer cells, it does not synthesize immunoglobulins. Incorrect options Option A : Albumin is a protein synthesized by the liver. It is the most abundant protein in the blood and plays a crucial role in maintaining oncotic pressure, transporting substances such as hormones and drugs, and regulating fluid balance. Option B: Coagulation factors, such as fibrinogen, prothrombin, and factors VII, VIII, IX, X, are produced by the liver. These factors are essential for blood clotting and are involved in the coagulation cascade. Option C : Acute phase proteins, including C-reactive protein, haptoglobin, and serum amyloid A, are synthesized by the liver. They are part of the immune response and play a role in inflammation and tissue repair.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 541-542; Solution Pages 550-550","sourcePage":541,"sourcePageEnd":542},{"id":"physiology-19-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":10,"question":"cGMP is the second messenger of","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Somatostatin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Angiotensin II"},{"letter":"C","text":"ADH"},{"letter":"D","text":"NO (nitric oxide)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D: NO (nitric oxide) • Nitric oxide (NO) activates the enzyme guanylate cyclase, which converts guanosine triphosphate (GTP) into cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) . • cGMP then serves as the second messenger, mediating various cellular responses. • NO is a key signaling molecule involved in several physiological processes, including v asodilation, neurotransmission, and immune regulation. Incorrect Options: Option A: Somatostatin - Somatostatin acts through G-protein-coupled receptors, but its second messenger system involves inhibitory G-proteins and intracellular pathways such as the inhibition of adenylate cyclase and decrease in cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) levels . It does not involve cGMP as the second messenger. Option B: Angiotensin II- This option is incorrect. Angiotensin II primarily acts through the angiotensin II type 1 receptor (AT1 receptor) and utilizes the inositol trisphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG) signaling pathway, leading to an increase in intracellular calcium levels. It does not involve cGMP as the second messenger. Option C: ADH (antidiuretic hormone, also known as vasopressin)- ADH acts through the V2 receptor and utilizes the cAMP signaling pathway, activating protein kinase A (PKA) and regulating water reabsorption in the kidneys. It does not involve cGMP as the second messenger.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 542-542; Solution Pages 550-551","sourcePage":542,"sourcePageEnd":542},{"id":"physiology-19-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":11,"question":"What is required for the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Magnesium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sodium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: Calcium • Calcium: Calcium plays a crucial role in the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin during the coagulation cascade. Calcium ions are necessary for the activation of several coagulation factors, including factor X, which leads to the conversion of prothrombin (factor II) into thrombin (factor IIa). Calcium ions act as cofactors, helping to stabilize the formation of the enzyme complex necessary for the conversion process. Incorrect options Option B: Magnesium: Magnesium is not directly involved in the prothrombin-to-thrombin conversion. However, magnesium ions are required for several other enzymatic reactions in the body and play a role in maintaining normal blood clotting function. Option C: Potassium: Potassium does not directly participate in the prothrombin-to-thrombin conversion. Potassium is primarily involved in maintaining the cell membrane potential and plays a vital role in nerve and muscle function. Option D: Sodium: Sodium is not directly involved in the conversion of prothrombin to thrombin. Sodium ions are primarily involved in maintaining fluid balance, nerve function, and muscle contraction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 542-542; Solution Pages 551-551","sourcePage":542,"sourcePageEnd":542},{"id":"physiology-19-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Erythropoietin is secreted by which of the following organs? A. Liver B. Kidney","options":[{"letter":"C","text":"Muscle D. Heart\""},{"letter":"A","text":"AB"},{"letter":"B","text":"BC"},{"letter":"C","text":"ABC"},{"letter":"D","text":"Only B"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A – AB: • Erythropoietin (EPO) is produced from peritubular epithelium cells of the kidney and perivenous hepatocytes of the liver. Incorrect Options: • Option B, C & D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 542-542; Solution Pages 552-552","sourcePage":542,"sourcePageEnd":542},{"id":"physiology-19-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following describes the chloride ion exchange in red blood cells, where bicarbonate ions are exchanged for chloride ions to maintain electrical neutrality?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Haldane effect"},{"letter":"B","text":"Root effect"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bohr effect"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chloride shift"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: D) Chloride shift Explanation: The chloride shift , also known as the Hamburger effect , is the process by which bicarbonate (HCO■■) exits red blood cells (RBCs) in exchange for chloride (Cl■) ions , maintaining electrical neutrality . • Mechanism: In peripheral tissues , CO■ diffuses into RBCs and reacts with water, catalysed by carbonic anhydrase , forming HCO■■ and H■ . HCO■■ is transported out of the RBC into the plasma via the band 3 protein in exchange for Cl■ . This shift maintains ionic balance and facilitates CO■ transport in the blood . In the lungs , the process reverses , allowing CO■ exhalation. • In peripheral tissues , CO■ diffuses into RBCs and reacts with water, catalysed by carbonic anhydrase , forming HCO■■ and H■ . • HCO■■ is transported out of the RBC into the plasma via the band 3 protein in exchange for Cl■ . • This shift maintains ionic balance and facilitates CO■ transport in the blood . • In the lungs , the process reverses , allowing CO■ exhalation. • In peripheral tissues , CO■ diffuses into RBCs and reacts with water, catalysed by carbonic anhydrase , forming HCO■■ and H■ . • HCO■■ is transported out of the RBC into the plasma via the band 3 protein in exchange for Cl■ . • This shift maintains ionic balance and facilitates CO■ transport in the blood . • In the lungs , the process reverses , allowing CO■ exhalation. Chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) vs Reverse chloride shift: Feature Chloride shift Reverse chloride shift Location Tissues Lungs Process CO■ enters RBCs, forming H■CO■ CO■ exits RBCs. HCO■■ re-enters → H■ + HCO■■. HCO■■ exits in RBCs in exchange for Cl■ exchange for Cl■ Effect on Chloride influx causes water influx, Chloride efflux balances HCO■■ RBCs swelling RBCs re-entry, CO■ is released Impact on Increases hematocrit due to RBC Primarily facilitates CO■ release, haematocrit swelling indirectly affecting hematocrit Haldane Effect (Option A) is incorrect because it describes increased CO■ binding to haemoglobin in deoxygenated blood , enhancing CO■ transport . Root Effect (Option B): Incorrect because it refers to a reduction in hemoglobin’s O■ carrying capacity in acidic conditions. Bohr Effect (Option C) is incorrect because it refers to the effect of CO■ and H■ on oxygen affinity , where increased CO■ lowers haemoglobin’s oxygen affinity , promoting O■ unloading in tissues .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 543-543; Solution Pages 552-553","sourcePage":543,"sourcePageEnd":543},{"id":"physiology-19-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following factors causes a rightward shift in the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase in CO■"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increase in O■"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decrease in CO■"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease in temperature"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increase in CO■","explanation":"A rightward shift in the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve means reduced oxygen affinity , facilitating oxygen unloading to tissues . This occurs in conditions where tissues require more oxygen, such as exercise or metabolic activity . • Factors causing a rightward shift (Bohr effect): ↑ CO■: Increases carbonic acid (H■CO■) formation , lowering pH and promoting oxygen release. ↑ H■ (↓ pH): Acidic conditions reduce haemoglobin's oxygen affinity. ↑ Temperature: Higher metabolic activity increasesoxygen demand. ↑ 2,3-BPG (DPG): Enhances O■ unloading by stabilising the deoxygenatedform of haemoglobin. • ↑ CO■: Increases carbonic acid (H■CO■) formation , lowering pH and promoting oxygen release. • ↑ H■ (↓ pH): Acidic conditions reduce haemoglobin's oxygen affinity. • ↑ Temperature: Higher metabolic activity increases oxygen demand. • ↑ 2,3-BPG (DPG): Enhances O■ unloading by stabilising the deoxygenated form of haemoglobin. • ↑ CO■: Increases carbonic acid (H■CO■) formation , lowering pH and promoting oxygen release. • ↑ H■ (↓ pH): Acidic conditions reduce haemoglobin's oxygen affinity. • ↑ Temperature: Higher metabolic activity increases oxygen demand. • ↑ 2,3-BPG (DPG): Enhances O■ unloading by stabilising the deoxygenated form of haemoglobin. Table rendering failed The Double Bohr Effect is a physiological phenomenon observed in the placenta.This effect involves two distinct Bohr effects occurring simultaneously—one on the fetal side and one on the maternal side . Double Bohr effect Details Location Placenta, where the maternal and fetal circulation meet. Fetal Blood Flow Umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood with high CO 2 from fetus to placenta CO 2 Diffusion CO 2 diffuses from fetal to maternal blood Effect on Fetal Blood CO 2 decreases, blood becomes alkaline Oxygen dissociation curve shifts left Effect on Maternal Blood CO 2 increases, blood becomes acidic Oxygen dissociation curve shifts right Clinical Significance Enhances oxygen transfer from mother to fetus, improving fetal oxygenation Increase in O■ (Option B) is incorrect because higher O■ levels promote oxygen binding rather than a rightward shift. Decrease in CO■ (Option C) is incorrect because low CO■ leads to higher pH , causing a leftward shift (increased O■ affinity, reduced unloading). Decrease in Temperature (Option D) is incorrect because low temperature stabilises haemoglobin’s oxygen-binding state , shifting the curve leftward .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 543-543; Solution Pages 553-555","sourcePage":543,"sourcePageEnd":543},{"id":"physiology-19-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-19","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":15,"question":"At what stage does erythropoietin first demonstrate effectiveness in the formation of red blood cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Burst forming units"},{"letter":"B","text":"CFU-GM"},{"letter":"C","text":"Late erythroblast stage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Normoblast stage"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Burst forming units: • BFUs are the earliest stage at which erythropoietin is effective in red blood cell formation. BFUs are pluripotent stem cells that give rise to erythroid colony-forming units (CFUs). Erythropoietin acts on BFUs to promote their differentiation into erythroid progenitor cells. Incorrect Options: Option B - CFU-GM: Colony forming unit GM are the progenitor cells for the monocytes and granulocytes. Option C - Late erythroblast stage: The late erythroblast stage refers to the later stages of erythropoiesis when the cells have progressed beyond the BFU/CFU stage. Option D - Normoblast stage: The normoblast stage is another term for the late erythroblast stage, referring to the stage where the erythroblasts have reached a normal size and shape.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 543-543; Solution Pages 555-555","sourcePage":543,"sourcePageEnd":543},{"id":"physiology-20-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following combinations correctly describes the effect on Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) given the status of the afferent and efferent arterioles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Afferent arteriole constriction- GFR increased"},{"letter":"B","text":"Efferent arteriole dilation - GFR increased"},{"letter":"C","text":"Afferent arteriole dilation - GFR decreased"},{"letter":"D","text":"Efferent arteriole mild constriction - GFR increased"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D ) Efferent arteriole mild constriction - GFR increased","explanation":"Arteriole Change Effect Effect on Glomerula Effect on GFR on Resis r Hydrostatic tance Pressure Afferent Arteriole Increase Decreases Decreases Constriction s Afferent Arteriole Decreas Increases Increases Dilation es Efferent Arteriole Increase Increases Slight increase Constriction- Mild s Efferent Arteriole Increase Increases Initially increases GFR Constriction- s then ultimately decreases. Severe Severe efferent arteriole constriction reduces renal blood flow and increases filtration fraction, concentrating plasma proteins in the glomerular capillaries and elevating glomerular colloid osmotic pressure. This counteracts the effect of increased hydrostatic pressure, ultimately reducing GFR Efferent Arteriole Decreas Decreases Decreases Dilation es","sourceRef":"Question Pages 556-556; Solution Pages 567-567","sourcePage":556,"sourcePageEnd":556},{"id":"physiology-20-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A physiologist is studying the effect of food additives on GFR in order to determine what additives should be avoided in individuals with CKD. Caffeine was found to affect urine output through an effect on GFR. What is the effect and the mechanism involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase urine output; afferent arteriole vasodilation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increase urine output; efferent arteriole vasodilation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase urine output; afferent arteriole vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease urine output; afferent arteriole vasodilation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increase urine output; afferent arteriole vasodilation","explanation":"Caffeine's primary effect is to increase urine output via afferent arteriole vasodilation . • Caffeine causes relaxation of the smooth muscle in the afferent arterioles • This vasodilation increases blood flow into the glomeruli. • The increase in blood flow raises the hydrostatic pressure within the glomeruli, leading to an increased GFR. • A higher GFR means that more plasma is filtered into the renal tubules. • With a higher GFR, more filtrate is produced, which ultimately results in an increase in urine volume. Increase urine output; efferent arteriole vasodilation (Option B) : • This would decrease glomerular pressure and GFR, leading to lower urine output. Increase urine output; afferent arteriole vasoconstriction (Option C) : • Vasoconstriction would reduce blood flow into the glomeruli, decreasing GFR and urine output. Decrease urine output; afferent arteriole vasodilation (Option D) : • Afferent arteriole vasodilation increases GFR and urine output, not decreases it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 556-556; Solution Pages 568-568","sourcePage":556,"sourcePageEnd":556},{"id":"physiology-20-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 56-year-old man is admitted to the ED with an acute myocardial infarction. The man was transferred to the CCU and was placed on 24 h intake and output. A few days later his 24 hr urinary output was decreased. An increase in which of the following contributes to the reduced urine flow in a patient with congestive heart failure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ANP"},{"letter":"B","text":"Urodilatin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Renal perfusion pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Renal sympathetic nerve activity"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Renal sympathetic nerve activity","explanation":"In a patient with congestive heart failure, reduced urine flow is primarily due to an increase in renal sympathetic nerve activity. Renal Sympathetic Nerve Activity: Promotes a decrease in sodium and water excretion by: • Decreasing glomerular filtration rate (GFR). • Increasing renin secretion, leading to further fluid retention via the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. • Enhancing tubular sodium reabsorption. ANP (Atrial Natriuretic Peptide) (Option A) : Typically promotes diuresis and sodium excretion; its levels are often lower in heart failure due to decreased atrial stretch. Urodilatin (Option B) : A natriuretic peptide isolated from human urine, synthesized in kidney tubular cells. • It is a 32-amino-acid peptide processed from the same precursor as ANP. • Acts as a paracrine regulator of sodium and water homeostasis in the distal nephron. • Increases sodium and water excretion by interacting with luminal receptors in the distal and connecting tubules, but its action may be blunted in heart failure. Renal Perfusion Pressure (Option C) : While decreased due to heart failure, this does not directly increase urine flow; it typically leads to retention.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 556-557; Solution Pages 568-569","sourcePage":556,"sourcePageEnd":557},{"id":"physiology-20-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following best describes what happens to the PAH excretion rate when the plasma concentration of PAH exceeds the transport maximum (Tm)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The excretion rate continues to increase linearly with increasing plasma concentration upto a certain level."},{"letter":"B","text":"The excretion rate reaches a plateau and remains constant despite further increases in plasma concentration beyond a level.."},{"letter":"C","text":"The excretion rate decreases with increasing plasma concentration."},{"letter":"D","text":"The excretion rate first increases linearly, then decreases after reaching the transport maximum."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The excretion rate reaches a plateau and remains constant despite further increases in plasma concentration.","explanation":"Transport Maximum - The highest rate at which a substance can be reabsorbed or secreted by the renal tubules of the kidneys. Significance: • Diagnostic Tool: It aids in diagnosing conditions like diabetes mellitus (glycosuria) and renal tubular disorders. • Homeostasis: Tm ensures efficient essential nutrients and electrolytes reabsorption, maintaining body homeostasis. • Drug Excretion: It influences the excretion of drugs and toxins, impacting pharmacokinetics and dosing regimens. • Pathophysiology: T m provides insights into kidney function and the pathophysiology of diseases affecting renal transport mechanisms. • Clinical Monitoring: It assists in monitoring the progression and treatment of kidney diseases and other metabolic conditions. When the plasma concentration of PAH exceeds the transport maximum (T m ), the renal tubules are saturated and cannot secrete PAH any faster. As a result, the excretion rate reaches a plateau and does not increase further despite increasing plasma concentrations of PAH. PAH Plasma conc. Vs clearance rate The excretion rate does not continues to increase linearly with increasing plasma concentration as the transport mechanisms in the kidneys have a maximum capacity (Tm), beyond which they cannot increase the rate of PAH excretion, causing the rate to plateau. (Option A) The excretion rate should remain at the maximum capacity of the transport mechanisms (T m ) and not decrease. (Option C) While it correctly states that the excretion rate initially increases linearly, it inaccurately suggests that the rate decreases after reaching the transport maximum. The excretion rate plateaus and remains constant after reaching Tm, without decreasing. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 557-557; Solution Pages 569-570","sourcePage":557,"sourcePageEnd":557},{"id":"physiology-20-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the juxtaglomerular (JG) apparatus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Macula densa"},{"letter":"B","text":"Juxtaglomerular cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extraglomerular mesangial cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loop of Henle"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Loop of Henle","explanation":"Loop of Henle: • A segment of the nephron in the kidney's medulla. • It is involved in concentrating urine and reabsorbing water and solutes. • However, it is not part of the juxtaglomerular apparatus. JuxtaGlomerular Apparatus(JG) : Crucial for regulating kidney function and blood pressure Significance: • Renin Release: Juxtaglomerular cells secrete renin in response to low blood pressure, low sodium chloride concentration, or sympathetic nervous system stimulation. • Renin-Angiotensin-Aldosterone System (RAAS): Renin initiates the RAAS, leading to the production of angiotensin II, which increases blood pressure by constricting blood vessels and stimulating aldosterone release to retain sodium and water. • Blood Pressure Regulation: JGA plays a vital role in maintaining systemic blood pressure and fluid balance. • Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) Regulation: Macula densa cells sense changes in sodium chloride and help adjust the GFR by influencing afferent arteriole constriction or dilation. • Homeostasis: Essential for maintaining electrolyte balance, blood volume, and overall homeostasis. Consists of 3 components: • Macula densa • Juxtaglomerular cells/Granular cells • Extraglomerular mesangial cells/Lacis cells Macula densa: • A specialised group of epithelial cells located in the distal convoluted tubule adjacent to the glomerulus. • They detect sodium concentration in the filtrate and help regulate blood pressure and filtration rate. Juxtaglomerular cells/Granular cells: • Specialised smooth muscle cells in the walls of the afferent arterioles. • They produce and secrete renin, crucial in regulating blood pressure and electrolyte balance. Extraglomerular mesangial cells/Lacis cells: • It is located between the afferent and efferent arterioles and the distal convoluted tubule. • They are thought to assist in signalling between the macula densa and juxtaglomerular cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 557-557; Solution Pages 570-572","sourcePage":557,"sourcePageEnd":557},{"id":"physiology-20-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following best explains the relationship between creatinine clearance (CrCl) and glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Creatinine clearance is a direct measure of GFR."},{"letter":"B","text":"Creatinine clearance is an indirect measure of GFR and typically overestimates GFR."},{"letter":"C","text":"Creatinine clearance underestimates GFR because it accounts for tubular secretion of creatinine."},{"letter":"D","text":"Normal creatinine clearance is 50mL/min."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Creatinine clearance is an indirect measure of GFR and typically overestimates GFR.","explanation":"Creatinine Clearance measures how effectively the kidneys filter creatinine from the blood. • It estimates the glomerular filtration rate (GFR), reflecting overall kidney function. • The measurement involves collecting urine over 24 hours and taking a blood sample to determine the creatinine concentration in both urine and blood. • Normal creatinine clearance is 140 mL/min. Creatinine clearance is an indirect measure of GFR and typically overestimates GFR. (Option B) • Creatinine clearance is often used to estimate GFR. • However, it tends to overestimate GFR because creatinine is filtered by the glomeruli and secreted in small amounts by the renal tubules. Creatinine clearance is not a direct measure of GFR. (Option A) • It is the amount of blood filtered by the glomeruli per minute, and creatinine clearance approximates this value by measuring the clearance of creatinine, a waste product, from the blood. Creatinine clearance does not underestimate GFR. (Option C) • Tubular secretion of creatinine would cause creatinine clearance to be overestimated, not underestimated, in the GFR. • The additional creatinine secreted by the tubules leads to a higher clearance rate than the actual filtration rate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 557-558; Solution Pages 572-573","sourcePage":557,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"physiology-20-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements about \"free water clearance\" (CH2O) is correct? Free water clearance = CH2O Urine osmolality = Uosm Plasma osmolality = Posm Urine flow rate = V■","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Free water clearance is positive when the urine is hypotonic and negative when the urine is hypertonic."},{"letter":"B","text":"Free water clearance is the same as urine flow rate (V■)."},{"letter":"C","text":"Free water clearance is calculated using C H2O = U osm (V■)/ P osm."},{"letter":"D","text":"Free water clearance is positive when the urine is hypertonic and negative when the urine is hypotonic."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Free water clearance is positive when the urine is hypotonic and negative when the urine is hypertonic.","explanation":"Free Water Clearance: • Measure of the kidney's ability to excrete or conserve free water, independent of solute excretion. • Free water clearance is positive when the urine is hypotonic and negative when the urine is hypertonic. • Free water clearance reflects the ability of the kidneys to excrete or conserve water. When urine is hypotonic (less concentrated than plasma), the kidneys excrete more free water, making C H2O positive. When urine is hypertonic (more concentrated than plasma), the kidneys conserve water, making C H2O negative. • When urine is hypotonic (less concentrated than plasma), the kidneys excrete more free water, making C H2O positive. • When urine is hypertonic (more concentrated than plasma), the kidneys conserve water, making C H2O negative. • When urine is hypotonic (less concentrated than plasma), the kidneys excrete more free water, making C H2O positive. • When urine is hypertonic (more concentrated than plasma), the kidneys conserve water, making C H2O negative. Free water clearance is not the same as urine flow rate. (Option B) It is derived from the difference between the urine flow rate and the osmolar clearance , i ndicating that it adjusts the urine flow rate by the osmotic concentration of the urine compared to plasma. Free water clearance is calculated using the formula: (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 558-558; Solution Pages 573-574","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"physiology-20-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A patient's hematocrit (the fraction of blood volume occupied by red blood cells) is 40%. The patient's renal plasma flow (RPF) is measured to be 600 mL/min. What is this patient's renal blood flow (RBF)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"360 mL/min"},{"letter":"B","text":"720 mL/min"},{"letter":"C","text":"1000 mL/min"},{"letter":"D","text":"1500 mL/min"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 1000 mL/min","explanation":"Renal Blood Flow (RBF): • The volume of blood delivered to the kidneys per unit of time. • Typically, RBF is about 1.0 to 1.2 liters per minute in a healthy adult, accounting for approximately 20-25% of the cardiac output. Renal Plasma Flow (RPF): • Volume of plasma (the liquid part of blood without cells) that passes through the kidneys per unit of time. • It reflects the effective blood flow that is available for filtration in the kidneys. • RPF is usually about 55-60% of the total renal blood flow. Renal blood flow (RBF) can be calculated using the formula: RBF = RPF/1- Hematocrit Given: Renal Plasma Flow (RPF) = 600 mL/min Hematocrit = 0.40 RBF = 600/1-0.40 = 600/0.60 = 1000 mL/min.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 558-558; Solution Pages 574-574","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"physiology-20-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Why is inulin clearance the gold standard for measuring glomerular filtration rate (GFR)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inulin is readily available and naturally produced in the body"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, providing an accurate measurement of GFR."},{"letter":"C","text":"Inulin has a high plasma concentration that enhances measurement accuracy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inulin is rapidly metabolised by the kidneys, making it easier to measure"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Inulin is freely filtered and neither reabsorbed nor secreted, providing an accurate measurement of GFR. Explanation Inulin clearance is a method for estimating glomerular filtration rate (GFR), a key indicator of kidney function. This method utilises inulin, a plant-derived polysaccharide, due to its unique properties: • Freely Filtered: Inulin, similar to water, passes through the glomerular capillaries without restriction. • Not Reabsorbed or Secreted: Inulin remains unchanged in the tubules, neither reabsorbed into the bloodstream nor secreted into the tubules. This ensures that the excretion rate of inulin accurately reflects the filtration rate by the glomeruli. • Inulin remains unchanged in the tubules, neither reabsorbed into the bloodstream nor secreted into the tubules. • This ensures that the excretion rate of inulin accurately reflects the filtration rate by the glomeruli. • Inulin remains unchanged in the tubules, neither reabsorbed into the bloodstream nor secreted into the tubules. • This ensures that the excretion rate of inulin accurately reflects the filtration rate by the glomeruli. To measure inulin clearance: Clearance Formula : ( Cs) = (Us x V) / Ps Us : Urine concentration of the substance V : Urine flow rate Ps : Plasma concentration of the substance Despite its accuracy, inulin clearance is less practical for routine use due to the need for intravenous administration. Therefore, other methods like creatinine clearance are more commonly used for estimating GFR in clinical settings.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 558-559; Solution Pages 575-575","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"physiology-20-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A patient has a urine concentration of a substance of 10 mg/mL, a urine flow rate of 2 mL/min, and a plasma concentration of that substance of 5 mg/mL. What is the clearance rate of this substance?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4 mL/min"},{"letter":"B","text":"5 mL/min"},{"letter":"C","text":"10 mL/min"},{"letter":"D","text":"20 mL/min"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 4 mL/min","explanation":"Clearance measures the volume of plasma completely cleared of a substance by the kidneys per minute (mL/min), reflecting renal excretory efficiency. It is essential for evaluating kidney function, including filtration, reabsorption, and secretion processes. To calculate the clearance rate (Cs) of the substance, use the formula: Clearance Formula : ( Cs) = (Us x V) / Ps Us : Urine concentration of the substance V : Urine flow rate Ps : Plasma concentration of the substance Plugging in the values: Cs = (10 ×2)/5 = 4 ml /min So, the clearance rate of the substance is 4 mL/min . Types of Clearance Measurements: Inulin Clearance Used to measure Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) as it is freely filtered but not reabsorbed or secreted. Not ideal for routine use due to intravenous administration. Creatinine Estimates GFR using creatinine, which is primarily Clearance filtered by the glomeruli. Slightly inaccurate due to tubular secretion, but practical for clinical settings. PAH Clearance Estimates Renal Plasma Flow (RPF). PAH is cleared through both glomerular filtration and tubular secretion. Correction for PAH extraction ratio improves accuracy.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 559-559; Solution Pages 576-576","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"physiology-20-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 65-year-old patient with severe renal artery stenosis is admitted for hypertension management. The physician decides to prescribe a medication to control the blood pressure but must avoid drugs that could adversely affect kidney function due to the existing renal impairment. Which drug is contraindicated in this patient due to the risk of worsening renal function?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Beta-blocker"},{"letter":"B","text":"Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcium channel blocker"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diuretic"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor Explanation Renal artery stenosis reduces blood flow to the affected kidney, decreasing renal perfusion pressure. This decrease is perceived as threatening blood pressure homeostasis, triggering a cascade of events within the renin-angiotensin system. Increased angiotensin II levels help maintain GFR by constricting the efferent arteriole, preserving glomerular pressure despite reduced renal blood flow. ACE Inhibitors block the conversion of Angiotensin I to Angiotensin II, disrupting the compensatory mechanism and potentially causing a significant drop in GFR. ACE inhibitors can lead to acute renal failure in renal artery stenosis by reducing protective efferent arteriole constriction and worsening GFR. Beta-blockers (Option A) reduce blood pressure by decreasing heart rate and cardiac output. They do not directly affect renal perfusion or glomerular filtration rate. Calcium channel blockers (Option C) reduce blood pressure by relaxing blood vessels, including the arterioles. They do not significantly impair renal perfusion and are generally safe in patients with renal artery stenosis. Diuretics (Option D) help reduce blood pressure by increasing urine production and reducing fluid volume. While they must be used cautiously to avoid dehydration, they do not directly impair renal perfusion or GFR like ACE inhibitors.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 559-559; Solution Pages 577-577","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"physiology-20-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following is NOT an effect of angiotensin II?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Constriction of efferent arterioles in the kidneys"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased sodium reabsorption in the renal tubules"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stimulation of aldosterone release from the adrenal cortex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased thirst and vasopressin (ADH) secretion"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Decreased sodium reabsorption in the renal tubules","explanation":"Effects of Angiotensin II: 1 Renal Tubular Directly increases sodium Reabsorption reabsorption in proximal tubules, loops of Henle, distal tubules, and collecting tubules. (Option B) 2 Vasoconstriction Angiotensin II constricts arterioles, especially efferent arterioles in the kidneys, increasing glomerular pressure and maintaining GFR even at low blood pressure. (Option A) 3 Aldosterone Secretion Stimulates adrenal cortex to release aldosterone, enhancing sodium and water retention in the kidneys. (Option C) 4 Baroreflex Sensitivity Reduces baroreflex sensitivity in the brain, boosting its effects on blood pressure. 5 Thirst and Hormone Increases thirst, stimulates Secretion vasopressin (ADH) release for water reabsorption, and triggers ACTH secretion. (Option D) 6 Mesangial Cells Causes contraction of mesangial cells, potentially reducing GFR by decreasing filtration surface area 7 System Interactions Effects are influenced by sodium intake, blood volume, and other hormones. Active during low sodium or volume depletion to maintain GFR and fluid balance.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 559-560; Solution Pages 577-578","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":560},{"id":"physiology-20-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":13,"question":"All of the following increase GFR except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bradykinin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nitric Oxide"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sympathetic Nerves"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Sympathetic Nerves","explanation":"Mediator Effect on Additional Notes GFR Prostaglandins (PGE2, Increase Cause vasodilation; especially PGI2) (Option D) important in counteracting vasoconstriction from sympathetic nerves or angiotensin II Bradykinin (Option A) Increase Functions similarly to prostaglandins by causing vasodilation Nitric Oxide (Option B) Increases Important for maintaining normal renal excretion of sodium and water; decreased production linked to hypertension Angiotensin II Biphasic Constricts efferent arterioles, effect on increasing glomerular pressure; GFR severe constriction decreases GFR Sympathetic Nerves Decrease Cause vasoconstriction, especially (Option C) under stress or volume depletion Norepinephrine, Decreases Vasoconstriction Epinephrine, Endothelin","sourceRef":"Question Pages 560-560; Solution Pages 578-579","sourcePage":560,"sourcePageEnd":560},{"id":"physiology-20-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements about nephrons is correct? Total renal blood flow rate is 1000 to 1260 ml/minute Tubular reabsorption is the first step in urine production Chronic kidney disease occurs when there is a 75% reduction in the original number of nephrons. The cortical region of the kidney is most vulnerable to ischemia. Nephrons can regenerate after age 40. Maintains Acid-Base balance by secreting hydrogen ions and reabsorbing bicarbonate.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Statements 1, 2, and 5."},{"letter":"B","text":"Statements 1, 3, and 6."},{"letter":"C","text":"Statements 2, 4, and 6"},{"letter":"D","text":"Statements 3, 4, and 5."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Statements 1, 3, and 6 .","explanation":"Nephrons Structural and functional units of the kidney Number per Kidney 0.8 to 1.2 million (average of 1 million) Length 4.5 to 6.5 cm (average of 5 cm) Renal Blood Flow Total Blood Flow Rate: 1000 to 1260 ml/minute (20 to 25% of cardiac output) Blood Flow Distribution: Cortex: 90%, Medulla: 10% Vulnerability to Ischemia: The Medulla is prone to ischemia; the Corticomedullary junction is more vulnerable Plasma Flow Rate: 625 to 700 ml/minute through both kidneys combined Nephron Function Glomerular Filtration: First step in urine formation; filters water, ions, and small molecules from blood into Bowman's capsule Tubular Reabsorption: Process where the nephron removes water and solutes from the filtrate and returns them to the blood Tubular Secretion: Transfer of materials from blood into the tubular fluid Excretion: Final elimination of urine from the body Additional Functions Regulation of Blood Pressure: By controlling blood volume through reabsorption and secretion mechanisms Acid-Base Balance: By secreting hydrogen ions and reabsorbing bicarbonate Electrolyte Balance: By reabsorbing and secreting electrolytes such as sodium, potassium, calcium, and magnesium Erythropoietin Production: A hormone that stimulates red blood cell production in the bone marrow, especially in response to low oxygen levels in the blood Age-Specific Dynamics Nephrons cannot regenerate. of Nephron Nephron count decreases by 10% every 10 year after age 40. By age 80, about 40% of nephrons remain functional. Chronic kidney disease occurs when there is a 70 to 75% reduction in the original number of nephrons.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 560-560; Solution Pages 579-580","sourcePage":560,"sourcePageEnd":560},{"id":"physiology-20-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following mechanisms is responsible for Myogenic Autoregulation of renal blood flow?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcium influx"},{"letter":"B","text":"Activation of sympathetic fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Angiotensin release"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dopamine release"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Calcium influx","explanation":"Myogenic Autoregulation: It maintains relatively constant renal blood flow and GFR and may protect the kidney from hypertension-induced injury.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 560-561; Solution Pages 580-580","sourcePage":560,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"physiology-20-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Calculate Renal Plasma Flow (RPF) if the GFR is 125 ml/min and the Filtration Fraction (FF) is 0.2.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"625"},{"letter":"B","text":"500"},{"letter":"C","text":"600"},{"letter":"D","text":"610"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 625","explanation":"Filtration fraction is the fraction of renal plasma flow that is filtered. Filtration fraction = GFR/Renal Plasma Flow 0.2 = 125/Renal Plasma Flow Renal Plasma Flow = 125/0.2=625","sourceRef":"Question Pages 561-561; Solution Pages 580-581","sourcePage":561,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"physiology-20-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Calculate the Net Filtration Pressure from the values given below: Glomerular Hydrostatic Pressure = 60 mmHg Bowman’s Capsule Pressure = 18 mmHg Glomerular Colloid Osmotic Pressure = 32 mmHg","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"10 mmHg"},{"letter":"B","text":"20 mmHg"},{"letter":"C","text":"30 mmHg"},{"letter":"D","text":"15 mmHg"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 10 mmHg","explanation":"Net filtration pressure = Glomerular hydrostatic pressure - Bowman’s capsule pressure - Glomerular colloid osmotic pressure = 60 - 18 - 32 =10. Forces Favoring Filtration: • Promotes filtration by pushing fluid out of the glomerular capillaries into Bowman's capsule. Forces Opposing Filtration: • Opposes filtration by pushing fluid back into the glomerular capillaries. 2. Glomerular Capillary Colloid Osmotic Pressure: 32 mm Hg • The presence of plasma proteins prevents filtration by attracting water back into the glomerular capillaries. Net Filtration Pressure: Forces favouring filtration-Forces opposing filtration = 60 mm Hg - (18 mm Hg + 32 mm Hg) = 10 mm Hg Thus, healthy humans have a net filtration pressure of approximately 10 mm Hg.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 561-561; Solution Pages 581-582","sourcePage":561,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"physiology-20-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":18,"question":"If the molecular radius of a substance is 3.0 nm, it is known as:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Freely filtrable substance"},{"letter":"B","text":"Selectively permeable substance"},{"letter":"C","text":"Impermeable substance"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Selectively permeable substance","explanation":"The permeability of substances at the molecular filtration barrier is affected by their molecular diameter. Substances with 1.8-4.2 nm molecular radius are selectively permeable; their charges decide their permeability across the glomerular filtration barrier(order of permeability: positively charged substances > neutral substances > negatively charged substances). Type of substance Molecular radius Explanation Examples Freely filtrable <1.8 nm Easily pass through the Sodium barrier Potassium Water Urea Creatinine Inulin PAH Bicarbonate Selectively permeable 1.8-4.2 nm The charge on them Albumin determines filtration Haemoglobin Myoglobin Bence Jones proteins Impermeable >4.2 nm It cannot be filtered","sourceRef":"Question Pages 561-561; Solution Pages 582-582","sourcePage":561,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"physiology-20-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following diseases can be caused by the defects in proteins of the slit diaphragm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Congenital Nephrotic Syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Steroid Resistant Nephrotic Syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All of the above","explanation":"• The foot processes of podocytes form the 3rd and innermost layer of the glomerular filtration barrier • The gaps between these foot processes are called slit pores or filtration slits • A porous filtration slit diaphragm bridges the filtration slits • Proteins in the slit diaphragm and diseases caused by the mutations of the genes coding for it: Protein Disease Nephrin Congenital Nephrotic Syndrome of Finnish type Podocin Steroid-Resistant Nephrotic Syndrome ■-Actinin Focal Segmental Glomerulosclerosis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 562-562; Solution Pages 582-583","sourcePage":562,"sourcePageEnd":562},{"id":"physiology-20-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the Glomerular Basement Membrane (GBM) is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is not a part of the glomerular filtration barrier"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contains type 3 collagen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Excludes substances larger than 8 nm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Charges of substances do not affect their permeability through GBM"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Excludes substances larger than 8 nm","explanation":"The Glomerular Basement Membrane is the layer of the glomerular filtration barrier. Layers of glomerular filtration barrier: Glomerular Basement Membrane: Aspect Description Structure A specialized membrane forming a sheath shared by neighboring capillaries Contents Fine fibrils in a gel-like matrix The matrix contains type 4 collagen, laminin, fibronectin, and other negatively charged proteins Permeability Permits free passage of neutral substances up to 4 nm in diameter Excludes substances larger than 8 nm Molecular Charge Charge on molecules affects their passage into Influence Bowman's capsule.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 562-562; Solution Pages 583-583","sourcePage":562,"sourcePageEnd":562},{"id":"physiology-20-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following is the primary site of action of acetazolamide in the nephron?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Proximal convoluted tubule","explanation":"Acetazolamide primarily acts on the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of the nephron. It works by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase, specifically types II and IV. Carbonic anhydrase plays a crucial role in the reabsorption of bicarbonate (HCO■■) in this segment. By inhibiting this enzyme, acetazolamide reduces the reabsorption of bicarbonate, leading to increased bicarbonate excretion and diuresis. The Loop of Henle (Option B) is primarily affected by loop diuretics, such as furosemide, which inhibit the Na-K-2Cl symporter in the thick ascending limb. The distal convoluted tubule (Option C) is the site of action for thiazide diuretics, which inhibit the Na-Cl symporter. Potassium-sparing diuretics, such as spironolactone and amiloride, act on the collecting duct. Acetazolamide does not have its primary action in the collecting duct. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 562-562; Solution Pages 584-584","sourcePage":562,"sourcePageEnd":562},{"id":"physiology-20-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following characteristics correctly differentiates the Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT) from the Collecting Duct (CT)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paracellular transport present in CT and not in PCT"},{"letter":"B","text":"Brush border of epithelium present in PCT, not in CT"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tight junctions are tight in PCT and leaky in CT"},{"letter":"D","text":"Carbonic anhydrase type 4 absent in PCT, but present in CT"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Brush border of epithelium present in PCT, not in CT","explanation":"Feature Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT) Collecting Duct (CT) Epithelium Type Simple cuboidal with brush border Simple cuboidal to columnar without brush border Brush Border Present Absent Microvilli Dense microvilli (forming brush Absent microvilli border) Paracellular Transport Significant due to leaky tight Minimal due to tight tight junctions junctions Tight Junctions Leaky Tight Carbonic Anhydrase Type Present in microvilli Generally absent 4 Mitochondrial Density High Lower than PCT Transport Mechanisms Active and passive Primarily passive Reabsorption of Glucose Almost complete Minimal","sourceRef":"Question Pages 563-563; Solution Pages 584-585","sourcePage":563,"sourcePageEnd":563},{"id":"physiology-20-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A 58-year-old patient with chronic renal failure presents with symptoms of fatigue, fluid retention, and low serum potassium levels. On examination, the patient shows signs of hypertension and oedema. Which of the following types of cells being defective in the renal tubules is responsible for the following condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Principal Cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alpha Intercalated Cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Beta Intercalated Cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Juxtaglomerular Cells"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Principal Cells","explanation":"Cell Type Functions Characteristics Principal Cells Sodium reabsorption Responsive to aldosterone (Option A) Potassium secretion ADH receptors Water reabsorption Columnar epithelium Alpha Intercalated Cells Secrete H■ ions Apical H■-ATPases (Option B) Reabsorb bicarbonate Cuboidal epithelium Beta Intercalated Cells Secrete bicarbonate Apical bicarbonate transporters (Option C) Reabsorb H■ ions Cuboidal epithelium Juxtaglomerular Cells (Option D): • They are located in the juxtaglomerular apparatus and are responsible for the secretion of renin, which plays a role in the regulation of blood pressure and the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. • They are not directly involved in sodium reabsorption, potassium secretion, or water reabsorption.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 563-563; Solution Pages 585-585","sourcePage":563,"sourcePageEnd":563},{"id":"physiology-20-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which part of the proximal tubule is the most vulnerable and prone to ischemia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S1"},{"letter":"B","text":"S2"},{"letter":"C","text":"S3"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2 and S3"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) S3","explanation":"The proximal tubule is divided into three segments: S1, S2, and S3. Among these, S3 is the most vulnerable to ischemia. This increased susceptibility is due to the following reasons: • High Metabolic Activity: S3 has the highest metabolic rate and density of mitochondria, making it more dependent on oxygen. • Proximity to Renal Medulla: Located closer to the renal medulla, which has lower oxygen levels. • Fewer Collateral Blood Vessels: S3 has less collateral circulation, reducing its ability to compensate for decreased blood flow. S1 S2 S3 Location Proximal to the Between S1 and S3. Near the loop of Henle. glomerulus. Function Reabsorption of nutrients, Reabsorption of water, Reabsorption of sodium, ions, and water. ions, and bicarbonate. bicarbonate, and nutrients. Features High brush border density. A brush border is present High mitochondrial but less extensive than S1. density. Risk of Least prone Intermediate Most prone ischaemia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 563-563; Solution Pages 586-586","sourcePage":563,"sourcePageEnd":563},{"id":"physiology-20-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Match each component of the countercurrent system with its respective function: Components Functions 1. Ascending Limb of Loop of Henle a. Blood loses water and gains solutes as it passes through the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium. 2. Descending Limb of Loop of Henle b. Blood gains water and loses solutes as it passes through the less concentrated interstitium. 3. Ascending Vasa Recta c. Actively transports sodium and chloride into the interstitium, diluting the filtrate. 4. Descending Vasa Recta d. Permeable to water; water is reabsorbed into the interstitium, concentrating the filtrate. Components Functions 1. Ascending Limb of Loop of a. Blood loses water and gains solutes as it passes Henle through the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium. 2. Descending Limb of Loop b. Blood gains water and loses solutes as it passes of Henle through the less concentrated interstitium. c. Actively transports sodium and chloride into the 3. Ascending Vasa Recta interstitium, diluting the filtrate. d. Permeable to water; water is reabsorbed into the 4. Descending Vasa Recta interstitium, concentrating the filtrate.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a, 4-d"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-d, 2-a, 3-c, 4-b"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a","explanation":"The countercurrent system is essential for maintaining the body's fluid and electrolyte balance, particularly through urine concentration. This system involves two main components in the kidneys: • Countercurrent multiplier system in the loop of henle. • Countercurrent exchanger system in the vasa recta. Countercurrent Multiplier System Countercurrent Exchanger System Location: Loop of Henle Location: Vasa Recta Function: Creates an osmotic gradient in the renal Function: Maintains the osmotic gradient established by medulla to concentrate urine the countercurrent multiplier system and supplies nutrients to the medulla Descending Limb: Permeable to water; water is Descending Vasa Recta: Blood loses water, and gains reabsorbed into the interstitium, concentrating the filtrate solute as it descends through the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium Ascending Limb: Impermeable to water; actively Ascending Vasa Recta: Blood gains water and loses transports sodium and chloride out into the interstitium, solutes as it ascends through the less concentrated diluting the filtrate interstitium. Mechanism: Countercurrent flow between descending Mechanism: Countercurrent exchange of water and and ascending limbs multiplies the osmotic gradient, solutes between blood in the vasa recta and the enabling the concentration of urine surrounding interstitium preserves the osmotic gradient","sourceRef":"Question Pages 564-564; Solution Pages 586-587","sourcePage":564,"sourcePageEnd":564},{"id":"physiology-20-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-20","chapter":"Renal Physiology","topic":"Renal Physiology","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly differentiates between cortical and juxtamedullary nephrons?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cortical nephrons have longer loops of Henle."},{"letter":"B","text":"Juxtamedullary nephrons constitute approximately 85% in the kidney."},{"letter":"C","text":"Cortical nephrons usually have a higher filtration rate."},{"letter":"D","text":"Juxtamedullary nephrons are located primarily in the renal cortex, whereas cortical nephrons are in the renal medulla."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Cortical nephrons usually have a higher filtration rate.","explanation":"Cortical nephrons and Juxtamedullary nephrons: Feature Cortical Nephrons Juxtamedullary Nephrons Location Renal cortex. Near the boundary between the renal cortex and medulla. Percentage of Approximately 85% Approximately 15% Total Nephrons Loop of Henle Short and extends only into the Long and extends deep into the outer medulla. inner medulla. Vasa Recta Short and less extensive. Long vasa recta, which follows the loop of Henle into the medulla. Glomerular Has a larger surface area relative to Smaller surface area relative to the Capsule the length of the loop of Henle. length of the loop of Henle. Filtration Rate High Lower filtration rate but more specialised in concentration. Presence of Typically not present. These nephrons are closely Macula Densa associated with the juxtaglomerular apparatus. Function Primarily involved in regulating Concentration of urine due to the blood composition and volume longer loops and extensive vasa through reabsorption and secretion. recta Contributing to the medullary osmotic gradient.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 564-565; Solution Pages 587-588","sourcePage":564,"sourcePageEnd":565},{"id":"physiology-21-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with a 6-month history of muscle weakness, fatigue, and frequent urination. Her blood pressure is 160/95 mmHg. Investigations: Serum K+: 2.8 mEq/L, Serum Na+: 148 mEq/L, Plasma aldosterone: 35 ng/dL, Plasma renin activity: 0.2 ng/mL/h. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bartter's syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gitelman's syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Conn's syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Liddle's syndrome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Conn’s syndrome","explanation":"The likely diagnosis from the given scenario is Conn's syndrome, also known as primary hyperaldosteronism, which is due to autonomous overproduction of aldosterone, usually from an adrenal adenoma. • It is characterised by resistant hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. • Causes: Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn's adenoma) - Most common. Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. • Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn's adenoma) - Most common. • Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. • Aldosterone-producing adenoma (Conn's adenoma) - Most common. • Bilateral adrenal hyperplasia. • Symptoms often include muscle weakness, fatigue, headaches, and frequent urination. • Laboratory findings: Elevated plasma aldosterone levels Suppressed plasma renin activity High aldosterone-to-renin ratio • Elevated plasma aldosterone levels • Suppressed plasma renin activity • High aldosterone-to-renin ratio • Treatment involves surgical removal of the adenoma and the use of mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists, such as spironolactone and eplerenone. • Elevated plasma aldosterone levels • Suppressed plasma renin activity • High aldosterone-to-renin ratio Role of Aldosterone : It acts on the collecting duct to – • Increase sodium reabsorption via epithelial sodium channels (ENaC). • Enhance potassium secretion through potassium channels (ROMK). Bartter's syndrome(Option A) • It is a genetic disorder,childhood-onset, affecting the kidneys, leading to abnormalities in electrolyte reabsorption. • Typically presents with low blood pressure, hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, elevated renin levels, and high urinary calcium excretion (hypercalciuria). Gitelman's syndrome :( Option B) • A genetic disorder affects the distal convoluted tubule of the kidney, leading to hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia, and metabolic alkalosis. • Typically presents with low or normal blood pressure and low urinary calcium excretion (hypocalciuria). Liddle's syndrome(Option D) • It is a genetic disorder caused by the increased activity of epithelial sodium channels (ENaC), leading to sodium retention and potassium excretion. • It has low aldosterone and renin levels and typically presents with hypertension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 589-589; Solution Pages 597-598","sourcePage":589,"sourcePageEnd":589},{"id":"physiology-21-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":2,"question":"When a nephrologist studying kidney reabsorption encounters the effects of Anti-diuretic hormone (ADH) on water reabsorption, what is the most likely influence of ADH on water reabsorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facultative water reabsorption involves glucose transporters"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facultative reabsorption dependent on ADH"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obligatory reabsorption dependent of ADH"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facultative reabsorption occurs in proximal tubules"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Facultative reabsorption independent of ADH","explanation":"Facultative Reabsorption of water occurs in the in the distal convoluted tubules and collecting ducts under the influence of ADH. Facultative reabsorption of water: • When ADH binds to receptors on tubule cell membranes, it triggers the insertion of aquaporin channels. • This increases water reabsorption into the bloodstream, helping to conserve water and produce concentrated urine, especially during dehydration or high plasma osmolality. Obligatory reabsorption of water: • This occurs primarily in the proximal convoluted tubules and is driven by osmotic gradients and other factors, not directly influenced by ADH. • It involves the reabsorption of a significant amount of water and solutes regardless of the body's hydration status.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 589-589; Solution Pages 598-598","sourcePage":589,"sourcePageEnd":589},{"id":"physiology-21-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 52-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes is being monitored for her renal glucose handling. During the monitoring, the clinician noticed that glucose appeared in her urine even at low plasma glucose concentrations. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Explained by Splay effect"},{"letter":"B","text":"High Renal threshold for glucose"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased Transport maximum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased Glomerular filtration"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Splay effect","explanation":"The splay effect describes the phenomenon where glucose begins to appear in the urine before reaching the expected renal threshold for glucose reabsorption. This occurs due to variability in the glucose transport capacity across different nephrons and differences in the efficiency of glucose reabsorption. Splay effect: • It is the difference between renal threshold and transport maximum value. • It arises because some neurons don’t function properly, and few neurons don’t function equally. • Due to improper functioning of neurons, the actual renal threshold is less than the predicted threshold. • Splay is inversely proportional to avidity, with which the transport mechanism binds the substance it transports. Renal threshold for glucose (Option B) It is the plasma glucose concentration at which glucose starts to appear in the urine, but it does not explain the appearance of glucose in the urine at lower concentrations. Transport maximum (Option C) The transport maximum (Tm) refers to the maximum rate at which the renal tubules can reabsorb glucose. Only if plasma glucose concentration exceeds this maximum, glucose will appear in the urine. Glomerular filtration (Option D) This process involves the filtering of blood by the kidneys. While important, it does not specifically account for glucose appearing in the urine at lower plasma levels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 589-590; Solution Pages 598-599","sourcePage":589,"sourcePageEnd":590},{"id":"physiology-21-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 63-year-old male complains of increased micturition, tingling numbness, and a non-healing ulcer. On examination, his FBS is 260 mg/dl, and he has glucose in his urine. What is the most likely cause of glucose in urine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased tubular secretion of glucose."},{"letter":"B","text":"Exceeded renal threshold of glucose."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased insulin sensitivity."},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased tubular reabsorption of glucose."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Exceeded renal threshold of glucose.","explanation":"The presence of glucose in urine in a patient with diabetes is due to exceeding the renal threshold of glucose. • When plasma glucose is normal, all the filtered glucose is reabsorbed, but when plasma glucose levels ↑are high, filtered glucose leads to the tubular load and appears in the urine. • For example, when the plasma glucose concentration is 100 mg/dL, the filtered glucose load is at the normal level of 125 mg/min, and no glucose is present in the urine. • However, when the plasma glucose concentration increases to 200 mg/dL, the filtered load rises to approximately 250 mg/min, and a small amount of glucose starts to appear in the urine. Decreased tubular secretion of glucose- Glucose is reabsorbed but not secreted into the tubules.( Option A) Increased insulin sensitivity leads to decreased blood glucose levels but does not affect glucose levels in urine. ( Option C) Increased tubular reabsorption of glucose leads to decreased glucose excretion. ( Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 590-590; Solution Pages 600-600","sourcePage":590,"sourcePageEnd":590},{"id":"physiology-21-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 47-year-old diabetic woman is being treated with medication that inhibits the reabsorption of glucose by the kidneys. What is the likely transporter inhibited by oral hyperglycemic medication, and what is its likely absorption mechanism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SGLT-2, Secondary active transport"},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT-2, Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"C","text":"SGLT-2, Facilitated diffusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"GLUT-2, Secondary active transport"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) SGLT-2, Secondary active transport","explanation":"• In this scenario, the likely transporter inhibited by oral hyperglycemic medication is SGLT-2 (Sodium-Glucose Co-Transporter 2). • Secondary active transport is the mechanism of glucose reabsorption inhibited by this medication. • Examples of SGLT-2 inhibitors include canagliflozin, dapagliflozin, and empagliflozin. Type of glucose transporters in the Kidney: Aspects Luminal transporters Basolateral transport ers SGLT-1 SGLT-2 GLUT-1 GLU T-2 Location In the distal part (S3 In the later part S3 s S1 s in PCT segment) egm egm ent ent Inhibition No Yes No No by medicati on Transport Transport of Transport of mechanis glucose from the glucose from the m lumen to the cytoplasm to blood cytoplasm by by facilitated secondary active diffusion. transporter. PCT: Proximal Convoluted Tubule Mechanism of Glucose absorption:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 590-590; Solution Pages 600-601","sourcePage":590,"sourcePageEnd":590},{"id":"physiology-21-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 35-year-old female complains of muscle weakness, metabolic alkalosis and hypertension. Genetic analysis reveals a mutation of ENaC. What is the most likely condition that occurs due to a mutation of ENaC?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gordon Syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Barttin syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gitelman’s syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Liddle's syndrome"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Liddle's syndrome","explanation":"• Liddle's syndrome is characterised by hypertension and metabolic alkalosis, which are due to a mutation of ENaC (Epithelial Sodium Channel) present in the distal and collecting tubules. • This rare autosomal dominant disorder causes excessive ENaC activity, resulting in increased sodium and water reabsorption. • Patients experience sodium retention, decreased renin secretion, and reduced levels of Angiotensin II, which subsequently decreases aldosterone secretion. • Treatment: Amiloride (Blocks excessive ENaC activity). Option A: Gordon’s syndrome: This condition is caused by mutations in the WNK1 and WNK4 genes and typically presents with hyperkalemia and metabolic acidosis. Option B: Barttin syndrome: • This syndrome results from mutations affecting the sodium-potassium-chloride cotransporter, potassium channels in the luminal membrane, and chloride channels in the basolateral membrane of the thick ascending loop of Henle. • Patients present with hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. Option C: Gitelman’s syndrome: It arises from a mutation in the thiazide-sensitive sodium-chloride cotransporter in the distal tubule. Clinical manifestations include hypokalemia, hypocalciuria, and metabolic alkalosis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 590-591; Solution Pages 602-603","sourcePage":590,"sourcePageEnd":591},{"id":"physiology-21-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which channels are not typically found on the luminal side of principal cells in the DCT?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epithelial Sodium Channel (ENaC)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Renal Outer Medullary Potassium (ROMK) channel"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aquaporin-2 (AQP2) water channel"},{"letter":"D","text":"Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2)","explanation":"The Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2) is not typically found on the luminal membrane of principal cells in the distal convoluted tubule. It is primarily located in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle. Channel/Transporter Location Role Epithelial Sodium Channel Luminal membrane Sodium reabsorption from the (ENaC) tubular lumen into the cell. Renal Outer Medullary Potassium secretion from the cell Potassium (ROMK) channel into the tubular lumen. Aquaporin-2 (AQP2) water Water reabsorption under the channel influence of ADH. Aquaporin - Type 3/4 Basolateral membrane Water reabsorption from cell cytoplasm to blood. Na+-K+ ATPase pump Active transport of sodium out of the cell into blood and potassium into the cell.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 591-591; Solution Pages 603-603","sourcePage":591,"sourcePageEnd":591},{"id":"physiology-21-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements about the cells in the distal tubule and cortical collecting tubule is NOT correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Principal cells are responsible for sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion."},{"letter":"B","text":"Type A intercalated cells secrete hydrogen ions and reabsorb bicarbonate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Amiloride and triamterene act as potassium-sparing diuretics by blocking sodium channels in principal cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spironolactone and eplerenone increase sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in principal cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Spironolactone and eplerenone increase sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in principal cells.","explanation":"Spironolactone and eplerenone decrease sodium reabsorption and potassium secretion in principal cells. Cells of Collecting Duct: Characteristic Principal Cells Intercalated Cells Main Function Sodium reabsorption and potassium Acid-base regulation secretion (Option A) Proportion 60-70% of cells in collecting tubules 30-40% of cells in collecting tubules and ducts. and ducts. Types - Type A Type B Key Processes Na+ reabsorption via Na+ channels Type A: (Option B) K+ secretion via K+ channels H+ secretion Water reabsorption (regulated by HCO3- reabsorption ADH). Type B: HCO3- secretion H+ reabsorption Key Transporters Na+-K+ ATPase pump in Type A basolateral membrane. H+-ATPase (apical H+-K+-ATPase (apical) Cl--HCO3- exchanger (basolateral) Type B: H+-ATPase (basolateral) Pendrin (Cl--HCO3- exchanger, apical) Hormonal Regulation Responsive to aldosterone Not primarily regulated by aldosterone. Diuretic Targets Primary targets for Not primary targets for most potassium-sparing diuretics diuretics. Specific Diuretics Spironolactone and eplerenone Not specifically targeted by common (aldosterone antagonists) diuretics. Amiloride and triamterene (Na+ channel blockers) (Option C) Effect of K+-sparing Decrease Na+ reabsorption and K+ Minimal direct effect. Diuretics secretion","sourceRef":"Question Pages 591-591; Solution Pages 604-604","sourcePage":591,"sourcePageEnd":591},{"id":"physiology-21-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of Gitelman syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hyperkalemia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypercalciuria"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypomagnesemia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Metabolic acidosis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Hypomagnesemia","explanation":"Gitelman syndrome is characterised by several electrolyte abnormalities, with hypomagnesemia being one of its hallmark features. • It's caused by mutations in the SLC12A3 gene, which encodes the thiazide-sensitive sodium-chloride cotransporter in the distal convoluted tubule. • It is an autosomal-recessive disorder with characteristics similar to Bartter syndrome but usually less severe: Salt and water loss. Mild water volume depletion. Activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. • Salt and water loss. • Mild water volume depletion. • Activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. • It typically presents with Hypokalemia(Option A is ruled out) Hypocalciuria (Option B is ruled out) Metabolic alkalosis (Option D is ruled out) • Hypokalemia(Option A is ruled out) • Hypocalciuria (Option B is ruled out) • Metabolic alkalosis (Option D is ruled out) • Treatment focuses on replacing losses of sodium chloride and potassium and correcting hypokalemia. • Potential therapeutic approaches: Blockade of prostaglandin synthesis with NSAIDs Administration of aldosterone antagonists like spironolactone • Blockade of prostaglandin synthesis with NSAIDs • Administration of aldosterone antagonists like spironolactone • Salt and water loss. • Mild water volume depletion. • Activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. • Hypokalemia(Option A is ruled out) • Hypocalciuria (Option B is ruled out) • Metabolic alkalosis (Option D is ruled out) • Blockade of prostaglandin synthesis with NSAIDs • Administration of aldosterone antagonists like spironolactone Gitelman syndrome is often described as a milder variant of Bartter syndrome but with distinct features such as hypomagnesemia and hypocalciuria.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 591-591; Solution Pages 605-606","sourcePage":591,"sourcePageEnd":591},{"id":"physiology-21-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding Bartter's syndrome is NOT true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caused by mutations in the sodium-chloride-potassium co-transporter in the loop of Henle."},{"letter":"B","text":"Common features include hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis."},{"letter":"C","text":"Typically inherited in an autosomal dominant manner."},{"letter":"D","text":"Leads to the activation of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Typically inherited in an autosomal dominant manner.","explanation":"Bartter's syndrome is typically inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, not autosomal dominant • It is a rare group of kidney disorders caused by genetic mutations affecting: Sodium-chloride-potassium co-transporter in the loop of Henle. Potassium channels in the luminal membrane. Chloride channels in the basolateral membrane of the thick ascending loop of Henle. • Sodium-chloride-potassium co-transporter in the loop of Henle. • Potassium channels in the luminal membrane. • Chloride channels in the basolateral membrane of the thick ascending loop of Henle. • It is characterised by increased renal excretion of water, sodium, chloride, potassium, and calcium. • It activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS), resulting in hypokalemia (low potassium levels) and metabolic alkalosis. • The mechanism involves increased aldosterone and high distal tubular flow, stimulating potassium and hydrogen secretion in collecting tubules. • Subtypes: Based on loss-of-function mutations in the gene Type 1: Na–K–2Cl cotransporter Type 2: ROMK K+ channel Type 3: ClC–Kb Cl− channel Type 4: Barttin protein • Type 1: Na–K–2Cl cotransporter • Type 2: ROMK K+ channel • Type 3: ClC–Kb Cl− channel • Type 4: Barttin protein • Sodium-chloride-potassium co-transporter in the loop of Henle. • Potassium channels in the luminal membrane. • Chloride channels in the basolateral membrane of the thick ascending loop of Henle. • Type 1: Na–K–2Cl cotransporter • Type 2: ROMK K+ channel • Type 3: ClC–Kb Cl− channel • Type 4: Barttin protein","sourceRef":"Question Pages 592-592; Solution Pages 607-607","sourcePage":592,"sourcePageEnd":592},{"id":"physiology-21-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":11,"question":"While flowing through which of the following segments does the filtrate become progressively hypertonic compared to plasma?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"D"},{"letter":"B","text":"A"},{"letter":"C","text":"B"},{"letter":"D","text":"C"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) B","explanation":"The segment of the nephron where the filtrate becomes progressively hypertonic compared to plasma is the Descending limb of the loop of Henle (Segment B). Descending limb of Loop of Henle: • In the descending limb, the permeability to water allows for the passive reabsorption of water into the hypertonic medullary interstitium. • This segment is impermeable to solutes such as sodium and chloride but permeable to water. • As the filtrate descends into the medulla, where the interstitial osmolality is very high (up to 1200-1400 mOsm/kg), water moves out of the descending limb due to osmosis. • This results in the filtrate becoming more concentrated (hypertonic) compared to plasma as it progresses through the descending limb. Thick Ascending limb of LOH (Segment C)(Option C) The ascending limb actively transports sodium, potassium, and chloride ions out of the filtrate into the interstitium, making the filtrate progressively hypotonic as ions are reabsorbed without water. Early Distal convoluted tubule (Segment D)(Option A) The early distal tubule has properties similar to those of the thick ascending loop of Henle, so further dilution of the tubular fluid occurs as solutes are reabsorbed while water remains in the tubule. Glomerulus (Segment A)(Option B) It’s a filtration process. No active transport or reabsorption occurs in the glomerulus itself that would change the osmolality of the filtrate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 592-592; Solution Pages 608-609","sourcePage":592,"sourcePageEnd":592},{"id":"physiology-21-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the roles of principal cells and type A intercalated cells in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and cortical collecting ducts?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type B intercalated cells reabsorb bicarbonate ions and secrete hydrogen ions."},{"letter":"B","text":"Type B intercalated cells reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium ions"},{"letter":"C","text":"Principal cells reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium ions into the tubular lumen."},{"letter":"D","text":"Type A intercalated cells reabsorb potassium ions and secrete bicarbonate."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Principal cells reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium ions into the tubular lumen.","explanation":"Principal cells reabsorb sodium and secrete potassium in the DCT. Cell Channel / Side Function Pump Principal Cells Na+-K+ Basolateral membrane Exchanges sodium with (Option C) ATPase potassium in renal pump interstitial fluid. Sodium channels Luminal Reabsorb sodium from membrane the lumen. Potassium channels Luminal Secrete potassium into membrane the lumen.. Type A Intercalated H+-ATPase Luminal membrane Secrete hydrogen ions Cells pump into the lumen. (Option D) H+-K+ ATPase pump Luminal Reabsorb potassium ions membrane from the lumen. Chloride-bicarbonate Basolateral Exchanges Chloride with exchanger membrane bicarbonate into the interstitial fluid. Type B Intercalated H+-ATPase Basolateral membrane Reabsorb hydrogen ions. Cells pump (Option A and B) Chloride-bicarbonate Apical Secrete bicarbonate into exchanger (Pendrin) membrane the lumen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 592-593; Solution Pages 609-609","sourcePage":592,"sourcePageEnd":593},{"id":"physiology-21-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 65-year-old man presents with muscle cramps and tetany for the past 2 days. Investigations reveal hypocalcemia and elevated phosphate levels. If the hypocalcemia is caused by damage to the part of the nephron responsible for the maximum reabsorption of calcium, which part of the nephron is likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Proximal tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting duct"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Proximal tubule","explanation":"Almost 65% of filtered calcium is reabsorbed in the PCT, mostly via the paracellular (passive) pathway. 99% of the filtered calcium is reabsorbed, and only 1% is excreted. Sites of reabsorption • PCT: 65% of filtered calcium is reabsorbed via a paracellular/passive pathway, and it parallels sodium and water reabsorption (Option C) • Loop of Henle (Option A): Thick ascending limb reabsorbs 25-30% of filtered calcium. 50% via the paracellular route due to a positive lumen charge. 50% via the transcellular pathway, stimulated by PTH. • 50% via the paracellular route due to a positive lumen charge. • 50% via the transcellular pathway, stimulated by PTH. • Distal and Collecting Tubules: (Option B and D) Reabsorbs 4-9% of filtered calcium. Almost entirely by active transport. Calcium enters cells via apical channels and exits via basolateral calcium-ATPase pumps and sodium-calcium exchangers. • Reabsorbs 4-9% of filtered calcium. • Almost entirely by active transport. • Calcium enters cells via apical channels and exits via basolateral calcium-ATPase pumps and sodium-calcium exchangers. • 50% via the paracellular route due to a positive lumen charge. • 50% via the transcellular pathway, stimulated by PTH. • Reabsorbs 4-9% of filtered calcium. • Almost entirely by active transport. • Calcium enters cells via apical channels and exits via basolateral calcium-ATPase pumps and sodium-calcium exchangers. Hormonal Regulation of calcium excretion • Parathyroid Hormone (PTH): Primary regulator. Increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubules. Reduces urinary calcium excretion. • Primary regulator. • Increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubules. • Reduces urinary calcium excretion. • Primary regulator. • Increases calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubules. • Reduces urinary calcium excretion. • Vitamin D (Calcitriol): Enhances calcium reabsorption in the distal tubule and thick ascending limb. • Calcitonin: Stimulates calcium reabsorption in the thick ascending limb and distal tubule. Calcium is passively absorbed in PCT and TAL (50% is passive), and magnesium is also absorbed similarly.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 593-593; Solution Pages 610-611","sourcePage":593,"sourcePageEnd":593},{"id":"physiology-21-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding potassium homeostasis by the renal tubule is NOT True?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Maximum K+ reabsorption occurs in the PCT."},{"letter":"B","text":"Principal cells are the major site for K+ secretion."},{"letter":"C","text":"Reabsorption of K+ in the PCT depends on the daily changes in potassium intake."},{"letter":"D","text":"Type B intercalated cells secrete potassium into the tubule when there is excess K+."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Reabsorption of K+ in the PCT depends on the daily changes in potassium intake.","explanation":"Reabsorption of K+ in the PCT is relatively constant and not influenced by daily changes in potassium intake. Mechanism of Potassium reabsorption and secretion at different levels: Site Potassium reabsorption and secretion PCT Maximum potassium reabsorption (65%). Does not depend on daily K+ intake. Loop of About 30% of K+ is reabsorbed. Henle Potassium is cotransported with sodium and chloride. DCT and Principal cells Major site for potassium secretion Collecting Na+-K+ ATPase pump moves K+ duct into the cell from the interstitium. Passive diffusion of K+ from the cell into the tubular lumen through ROMK and BK channels. Type A Reabsorbs potassium during intercalate potassium depletion. d cells. Mechanism: Hydrogen-potassium ATPase transporter in the luminal membrane exchanges hydrogenions for potassium Type B Secrete potassium when there is an intercalate excess. d cells Mechanism: Hydrogen-potassium ATPase transporter on the basolateral membrane pumps potassium into the cell, which then diffuses into the tubular lumen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 593-593; Solution Pages 611-612","sourcePage":593,"sourcePageEnd":593},{"id":"physiology-21-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":15,"question":"At which part of the renal tubule does the maximum reabsorption of sodium occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thick Ascending loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Proximal convoluted tubule","explanation":"• About 65-70% of sodium reabsorption occurs in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) • Sodium is only filtered and reabsorbed by the renal tubules, and no secretion occurs. • Only the thin descending segment of the nephron cannot reabsorb sodium as it is impermeable to all solutes, but all the other parts can reabsorb sodium. • All segments of the nephron play a role in active and passive sodium reabsorption except for the ascending thin segment (near the JG apparatus ), where only passive reabsorption occurs. • Reabsorption of sodium at respective levels are Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT): 65-70 % Thick Ascending loop of Henle (TAL): 25-30 % (Option B) Distal Convoluted tubule (DCT): 5-7 % (Option C) Collecting Duct (CD): 2-3 % (Option D) • Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT): 65-70 % • Thick Ascending loop of Henle (TAL): 25-30 % (Option B) • Distal Convoluted tubule (DCT): 5-7 % (Option C) • Collecting Duct (CD): 2-3 % (Option D) • Proximal convoluted tubule (PCT): 65-70 % • Thick Ascending loop of Henle (TAL): 25-30 % (Option B) • Distal Convoluted tubule (DCT): 5-7 % (Option C) • Collecting Duct (CD): 2-3 % (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 593-594; Solution Pages 612-613","sourcePage":593,"sourcePageEnd":594},{"id":"physiology-21-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Where is the sodium-glucose-linked transporter (SGLT) located in the renal tubule?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Early part of the Proximal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Later part of the Proximal convoluted tubule"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Early Proximal convoluted tubule","explanation":"Sodium-glucose linked transporter (SGLT1 and 2), both are located on the brush border of the proximal convoluted tubule (Early PCT). • It helps carry glucose against a concentration gradient. • SGLT2 resorbs 90% of filtered glucose in the early proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), while SGLT1 reabsorbs 10% in the latter half. • The reabsorbed glucose exits the cell via the basolateral membrane through GLUT2 and GLUT1 transporters, respectively. Types of transporters in the different parts of the tubule: PART of TUBULE TRANSPORTERS Proximal tubule Early PCT (Option A) Sodium-Glucose Co-transporter (SGLT) Sodium-Amino Acid Cotransporter Sodium-Hydrogen Exchanger (NHE) Na+/K+ ATPase Pump Late PCT (Option D) Sodium-Chloride Cotransporter Chloride Channels Thin descending loop of Henle Freely permeable to water and some solutes such as urea and sodium No specific transporter Thick ascending loop of Henle Na+-K+-2Cl− Co-transporter (NKCC2) (Option B) Sodium-Hydrogen Exchanger Na+/K+ ATPase Pump Distal convoluted tubule (Option C) Early DCT Sodium-chloride cotransporter (NCC) Na+/K+ ATPase Pump Chloride Channels Late DCT Principal Cells : Epithelial Sodium Channels (ENaC) Potassium Channels Na+/K+ ATPase Pump Type A Intercalated Cells: Hydrogen-ATPase Hydrogen-Potassium ATPase Chloride-Bicarbonate Exchanger Type B Intercalated Cells: Hydrogen-ATPase (Basolateral) Chloride-Bicarbonate Exchanger (Pendrin)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 594-594; Solution Pages 613-615","sourcePage":594,"sourcePageEnd":594},{"id":"physiology-21-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 60-year-old male with chronic kidney disease presents with electrolyte abnormalities. He undergoes renal function tests revealing impaired reabsorption in his proximal convoluted tubule (PCT). All the following ions are reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amino Acids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Phosphate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bicarbonate"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hydrogen Ions"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Hydrogen Ions Hydrogen ions (H+) are actively secreted into the tubular lumen in the PCT (Not Reabsorbed) Proximal Convoluted Tubule: PCT plays a crucial role in renal function by reabsorbing approximately 65% of filtered sodium, chloride, potassium and bicarbonate, along with almost all filtered glucose and amino acids. • Isotonic Absorption • Passive reabsorption of Calcium (65%) Sodium Resorption: Occurs through various Carrier Proteins • ■■■■■■■ ■■■■■■■ Antiporter. • It provides H+ ions and aids in the absorption of Bicarbonate • Na/K ATPase is present on the Basolateral side, which creates the gradient for Sodium. • Sodium Bi-Carbonate Co-transporter (NBC): Helps in Na reabsorption in the basolateral membrane. Proximal Tubule has two parts: • Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT): It is a little impermeable for chloride reabsorption, leading to a higher concentration of chloride in the Proximal convoluted tubule. • Proximal Straight Tubule (PST): Na+ is reabsorbed along with chloride.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 594-594; Solution Pages 615-616","sourcePage":594,"sourcePageEnd":594},{"id":"physiology-21-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 45-year-old male patient with chronic hypertension undergoes renal function tests. His glomerular filtration rate (GFR) fluctuates due to his condition, but the percentage of sodium reabsorption at the proximal tubule remains constant. This renal phenomenon ensures stable sodium reabsorption relative to GFR changes and is primarily mediated by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Renal Autoregulation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Modulating GFR based on feedback from sodium concentrations."},{"letter":"C","text":"Glomerulo-tubular balance"},{"letter":"D","text":"Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) regulating sodium reabsorption"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Glomerulo-tubular balance Glomerulo-tubular balance is the process by which the proximal tubule adjusts sodium reabsorption in response to changes in glomerular filtration rate (GFR). This ensures a constant percentage of sodium is reabsorbed despite variations in GFR. Peritubular capillaries play a crucial role in reabsorbing substances from the renal tubules back into the bloodstream. These capillaries will proportionately reabsorb whatever is reabsorbed at the proximal tubule, thus depleting the Sodium in the interstitium. This process is governed by Starling forces, which involve hydrostatic pressure (P) and oncotic pressure (π). Renal Autoregulation(Option A) refers to the kidney’s ability to maintain stable renal blood flow and GFR despite changes in systemic blood pressure. Modulating GFR based on feedback from sodium concentrations(Option B) Tubuloglomerular feedback is a mechanism where the macula densa cells in the distal tubule monitor sodium chloride levels and regulate afferent arteriolar resistance to control GFR. Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) regulating sodium reabsorption(Option D) The RAAS system is vital for regulating sodium and water balance in response to changes in blood pressure and blood volume by acting on distal nephron segments (e.g., distal convoluted tubule, collecting duct) to enhance sodium reabsorption.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 594-595; Solution Pages 616-616","sourcePage":594,"sourcePageEnd":595},{"id":"physiology-21-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with complaints of excessive thirst and frequent urination. Laboratory tests reveal hypernatremia (elevated serum sodium levels) and low urine osmolality. Further investigation shows undetectable levels of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). What is the location of minimum urine tonicity expected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Distal convoluted tubule (DCT)"},{"letter":"B","text":"End of the collecting duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Descending thin limb of the loop of Henle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ascending limb of the loop of Henle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) End of the collecting duct","explanation":"Without ADH, the collecting ducts cannot effectively reabsorb water, resulting in diluted urine with low osmolality despite high plasma osmolality. Therefore, the minimum urine tonicity is at the end of the collecting duct, where water reabsorption is impaired due to the lack of ADH. The Graph explains the osmolarity of Tubular Fluid with respect to plasma in the presence and absence of ADH: RED: Presence of ADH GREEN: Absence of ADH Condition Maximum Urine Tonicity Minimum Urine Tonicity In the Presence of End of the Collecting Duct(CD) / Tip Distal Convoluted Tubule ADH of Loop of Henle (LOH) (DCT) In the Absence of Tip of Loop of Henle (LOH) End of Collecting Duct (CD) ADH","sourceRef":"Question Pages 595-595; Solution Pages 617-617","sourcePage":595,"sourcePageEnd":595},{"id":"physiology-21-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-21","chapter":"Tubular Transport","topic":"Tubular Transport","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 40-year-old woman presents to the clinic with complaints of excessive thirst and polyuria. Further investigation reveals a diagnosis of central diabetes insipidus. What is the approximate tubular fluid-to-plasma osmolality ratio expected in this patient's collecting duct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Less than 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Equal to 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater than 1"},{"letter":"D","text":"Between 1 and 2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Less than 1 In central diabetes insipidus, antidiuretic hormone (ADH) is deficient, which leads to impaired water reabsorption in the kidneys' collecting ducts. As a result, urine osmolality is typically much lower than plasma osmolality. Tubular Fluid Plasma Ratio: The graph below plots the length of the nephron and the ratio where TF is the tubular fluid osmolality and P is the plasma osmolality. • 1 on the y-axis indicates the isotonic nature of urine • The highest point of the graph is at the tip of the Loop of Henle (LOH) • The red curve goes upwards in the presence of ADH due to Antidiuresis.TF/P >1 • The Blue curve goes downwards in the absence of ADH, causing water diuresis TF/P <1","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 595-595; Solution Pages 617-618","sourcePage":595,"sourcePageEnd":595},{"id":"physiology-22-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":1,"question":"What is the approximate daily production of non-volatile (metabolic) acids in an adult human body?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"12,000 to 24,000 mmol"},{"letter":"B","text":"70 to 100 mmol"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,000 to 1,500 mmol"},{"letter":"D","text":"450 to 550 mmol"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) 70 to 100 mmol Explanation: • Non-volatile (Metabolic) or fixed acids are produced from incomplete metabolism of carbohydrates (e.g. lactate), fats (e.g. ketones), and proteins (e.g. sulfate, phosphate). • In a typical diet, the body excretes about 40% of nonvolatile acids (around 30 mEq/day) through phosphate systems and 60% (approximately 50 mEq/day) through NH4+ excretion. • The net production of these fixed acids is about 70 to 100 mmol of H+ per day in an adult. This excludes lactate, as most of the lactate produced is metabolized and not excreted 12,000 to 24,000 mmol: Volatile (Respiratory) Acids(option A) • The main volatile acid is carbonic acid (H2CO3), which is more correctly referred to as carbon dioxide (CO2). CO2 is the end-product of the complete oxidation of carbohydrates and fatty acids. • Basal CO2 production is typically 12,000 to 13,000 mmol/day. However, actual daily CO2 production is usually higher, often in the range of 12,000 to 24,000 mmol/day, depending on metabolic activity levels. Options C) and D) are incorrect values","sourceRef":"Question Pages 619-619; Solution Pages 624-625","sourcePage":619,"sourcePageEnd":619},{"id":"physiology-22-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":2,"question":"How does the excretion of ammonium (NH4+) contribute to bicarbonate (HCO3■) regulation in the kidneys?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It directly increases the concentration of HCO3■ in the urine."},{"letter":"B","text":"It generates HCO3■ , which is released into the bloodstream."},{"letter":"C","text":"It decreases the overall HCO3■ reabsorption in the kidneys."},{"letter":"D","text":"It prevents the secretion of H+ in the renal tubules."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) It generates HCO3■ , which is released into the bloodstream.","explanation":"• The kidneys filter bicarbonate (HCO3■) from the blood. Approximately 85% to 90% of bicarbonate is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule, while only about 10% is reabsorbed in the distal tubules and collecting ducts. • When NH4+ is excreted, it is produced from the deamination of amino acids. For each NH4+ ion excreted, one HCO3■ ion is generated and released into the bloodstream, helping to maintain acid-base balance. It directly increases the concentration of HCO3 ■ in the urine: While NH4+ is excreted in the urine, it does not directly increase the concentration of HCO3- in the urine. Instead, NH4+ excretion is linked to HCO3■ generation in the bloodstream (Option A). It decreases the overall HCO3 ■ reabsorption in the kidneys: The excretion of NH4+ does not decrease HCO3■ reabsorption; rather, it is part of the mechanism that helps maintain HCO3■ levels in the blood (Option C). It prevents the secretion of H+ in the renal tubules: The presence of NH4+ does not prevent the secretion of H+ ions. In fact, H+ ions are secreted into the tubular fluid, which is critical for HCO3■ reabsorption (Option D). Factors Influencing Bicarbonate Excretion","sourceRef":"Question Pages 619-619; Solution Pages 625-625","sourcePage":619,"sourcePageEnd":619},{"id":"physiology-22-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which transport mechanism is primarily responsible for bicarbonate (HCO3■) reabsorption in the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na ■ -K ■ ATPase pump"},{"letter":"B","text":"Na ■ -H ■ antiporter"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cl■-HCO3■ antiporter"},{"letter":"D","text":"H ■ -ATPase pump"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Na■-H■ antiporter","explanation":"Mechanism of Bicarbonate Reabsorption Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT): • Na■-H■ Antiporter: The primary mechanism for bicarbonate reabsorption involves the Na ■ -H ■ antiporter located on the apical surface of epithelial cells. • Carbonic Anhydrase: CA-4 catalyzes the hydration of CO2 to form carbonic acid (H2CO3), which dissociates into H ■ and HCO3-.This reaction occurs at the brush border of proximal tubular cells. CA Traps CO2 in the tubular fluid, preventing its complete removal. Facilitates the secretion of H ■ into the tubular lumen while retaining bicarbonate within the cell. • CA Traps CO2 in the tubular fluid, preventing its complete removal. • Facilitates the secretion of H ■ into the tubular lumen while retaining bicarbonate within the cell. • Higher permeability to H ■ and HCO3■ leads to a tubular fluid pH of approximately 6.5. • Bicarbonate ions diffuse into the interstitium, similar to K ■ ions. • Na+ and K+ Exchange: Na ■ -K ■ ATPase pump is located in the basolateral membrane. Na ■ enters the interstitium in exchange for K ■ via the Na ■ -K ■ ATPase, maintaining K ■ concentration while decreasing Na ■ concentration. (Option A) • CA Traps CO2 in the tubular fluid, preventing its complete removal. • Facilitates the secretion of H ■ into the tubular lumen while retaining bicarbonate within the cell. Regulation of Acid-Base Balance • Urine acidification in the PCT is dependent on Na ■ concentration. • Parathyroid Hormone (PTH): PTH inhibits the Na ■ -H ■ exchanger in the PCT, affecting urine pH. • Carbonic Anhydrase Inhibitors: Drugs that inhibit carbonic anhydrase decrease urine acidification by impairing bicarbonate reabsorption. Cl■-HCO3■ antiporter, also known as Band 3 protein is a Chloride-bicarbonate exchanger at the basolateral membrane of the distal tubules and collecting ducts aids bicarbonate retention and urine acidification (Option C). The H■-ATPase pump is located at the apical surface of the tubular cells of distal tubules and collecting ducts and facilitates ATP-driven transport of H■ into the tubular lumen (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 619-619; Solution Pages 626-627","sourcePage":619,"sourcePageEnd":619},{"id":"physiology-22-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":4,"question":"What effect does aldosterone have on acid secretion in the distal convoluted tubule (DCT) and collecting duct (CD)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreases the activity of H ■ -ATPase."},{"letter":"B","text":"Inhibits the secretion of H ■ ."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increases the activity of H ■ -ATPase"},{"letter":"D","text":"No effect on acid secretion."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Acid Secretion at the DCT and CD • Mechanism: Intercalated Cells: Produce H ■ and HCO3- from CO2, facilitated by carbonic anhydrase. H+ Secretion: Via H ■ -ATPase or K ■ -H ■ ATPase into tubular fluid. HCO3- Transport: Into interstitium via Cl■-HCO3■ antiporter. • Intercalated Cells: Produce H ■ and HCO3- from CO2, facilitated by carbonic anhydrase. • H+ Secretion: Via H ■ -ATPase or K ■ -H ■ ATPase into tubular fluid. • HCO3- Transport: Into interstitium via Cl■-HCO3■ antiporter. • Acidification of Tubular Fluid: Low H+ permeability results in more acidic fluid (pH ~4.5), crucial for urinary buffer excretion. H+ secretion is independent of Na+ concentration. • Low H+ permeability results in more acidic fluid (pH ~4.5), crucial for urinary buffer excretion. • H+ secretion is independent of Na+ concentration. • Role of Aldosterone: Enhances acid secretion by increasing proton pump activity. (Option C) • Enhances acid secretion by increasing proton pump activity. (Option C) • Band 3 Protein (Anion Exchanger 1 or AE1): Chloride-bicarbonate exchanger at basolateral membrane aids bicarbonate retention and urine acidification. • Chloride-bicarbonate exchanger at basolateral membrane aids bicarbonate retention and urine acidification. • NH4+ Excretion: H ■ combines with NH3 to form NH4 ■ , which is excreted, and essential for acid-base balance. • H ■ combines with NH3 to form NH4 ■ , which is excreted, and essential for acid-base balance. • Clinical Implications: Reduced H ■ secretion decreases net acid excretion and affects HCO3■ buffering of non-volatile acids. • Reduced H ■ secretion decreases net acid excretion and affects HCO3■ buffering of non-volatile acids. • Intercalated Cells: Produce H ■ and HCO3- from CO2, facilitated by carbonic anhydrase. • H+ Secretion: Via H ■ -ATPase or K ■ -H ■ ATPase into tubular fluid. • HCO3- Transport: Into interstitium via Cl■-HCO3■ antiporter. • Low H+ permeability results in more acidic fluid (pH ~4.5), crucial for urinary buffer excretion. • H+ secretion is independent of Na+ concentration. • Enhances acid secretion by increasing proton pump activity. (Option C) • Chloride-bicarbonate exchanger at basolateral membrane aids bicarbonate retention and urine acidification. • H ■ combines with NH3 to form NH4 ■ , which is excreted, and essential for acid-base balance. • Reduced H ■ secretion decreases net acid excretion and affects HCO3■ buffering of non-volatile acids. A) Decreases the activity of H■-ATPase: Aldosterone increases the activity of H ■ -ATPase but does not decrease it. B) Inhibits the secretion of H■: Aldosterone enhances the secretion of H ■ , not inhibits it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 620-620; Solution Pages 627-628","sourcePage":620,"sourcePageEnd":620},{"id":"physiology-22-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What triggers the formation of ammonium ions (NH4■) and bicarbonate (HCO3■) in the ammonia buffer system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased sodium concentration"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alkalosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acidosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dehydration"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Acidosis stimulates the formation of ammonium ions (NH4 ■ ) and bicarbonate (HCO3■) in the ammonia buffer system. This process helps the body excrete excess acid and maintain acid-base balance. BUFFERS DESCRIPTION Bicarbonate Most powerful extracellular buffer system. Buffer Composed of H2CO3 and NaHCO3 Buffers strong acids by forming H2CO3 and strong bases by reacting with H+ to form H2O and CO2. Effective due to regulation by kidneys and lungs. Phosphate Buffer Composed of H2PO4■ and HPO4. pK of 6.8, effective in slightly acidic urine. Phosphate concentration increases in tubular fluid due to water reabsorption. Ammonia Buffer Composed of ammonia (NH3) and ammonium ion (NH4 ■ ). In the liver, Glutamine metabolized to 2NH4 ■ and 2HCO3−. NH4 ■ is secreted into the tubular lumen HCO3− reabsorbed into blood. Acidosis stimulates NH4 ■ and HCO3■ formation; alkalosis decreases their formation. The rate of NH4 ■ excretion can increase significantly with chronic acidosis. Others Urate Buffer (less significant) Citrate Buffer A) Increased sodium concentration: Sodium concentration does not directly trigger the formation of ammonium ions (NH4■) and bicarbonate (HCO3■) in the ammonia buffer system. Instead, sodium balance is primarily related to the regulation of fluid volume and blood pressure. B) Alkalosis: In contrast, alkalosis decreases the formation of NH4■ and HCO3■. D) Dehydration: Dehydration primarily affects fluid balance and can lead to an increase in blood osmolarity. While severe dehydration can lead to metabolic disturbances, it does not specifically trigger the formation of NH4■ and HCO3■ in the ammonia buffer system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 620-620; Solution Pages 629-629","sourcePage":620,"sourcePageEnd":620},{"id":"physiology-22-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 40 y/o man presents with muscle weakness, bone pain, and polyuria. Lab tests show a normal anion gap metabolic acidosis, hypophosphatemia, hypokalemia, and glycosuria despite normal blood glucose levels. His urine pH is less than 5.5. Which of the following conditions is most likely responsible for his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Distal Renal Tubular Acidosis (Type 1)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Proximal Renal Tubular Acidosis (Type 2)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyperkalemic Renal Tubular Acidosis (Type 4)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Proximal RTA results from a defect in bicarbonate reabsorption in the proximal tubule. The patient’s symptoms and laboratory findings match those of proximal RTA, making it the most likely diagnosis. RTA Features Proximal Renal Tubular Caused by a defect in bicarbonate reabsorption in the Acidosis (Type 2) proximal tubule. Associated with generalized proximal tubular dysfunction. Symptoms include low plasma bicarbonate, glycosuria, aminoaciduria, phosphaturia, hypophosphatemia, and hypokalemia The urine pH may be acidic when plasma bicarbonate is low. Distal Renal Tubular Acidosis Results from an inability to acidify the urine in the distal (Type 1) tubule. Characterized by persistently high urine pH (>5.5), hypokalemia, and a non-anion gap metabolic acidosis. Often associated with nephrolithiasis and nephrocalcinosis. (Option A) Hyperkalemic Renal Tubular Characterized by hyperkalemia and a mild non-anion Acidosis (Type 4) gap metabolic acidosis. Common in conditions such as diabetes mellitus and chronic kidney disease. (Option C) D) While CKD can lead to metabolic acidosis, it typically presents with features such as elevated serum creatinine and urea levels and a mixed acid-base disturbance that may include both metabolic acidosis and changes in electrolyte balance. The patient's specific findings are more indicative of proximal RTA rather than CKD alone.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 620-620; Solution Pages 630-630","sourcePage":620,"sourcePageEnd":620},{"id":"physiology-22-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following is not a prerequisite for a functional counter-current mechanism in the body?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parallel and opposite flow of fluids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Close proximity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Active transport of solutes across all segments"},{"letter":"D","text":"Selective permeability of pathways"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Active transport of solutes across all segments","explanation":"This is NOT a prerequisite for a functional counter-current mechanism. While active transport is essential in some segments, such as the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, which actively transports Na+ and Cl−, it is not required across all segments of the system. Passive transport and selective permeability are critical components. Parallel and opposite flow of fluids : This is a fundamental requirement for a counter-current mechanism. In the kidneys, the descending and ascending limbs of the loop of Henle and the vasa recta have fluid flows in opposite directions, which maximizes the efficiency of exchange processes (O ption A) Close proximity and selective permeability of pathways: This selective permeability is crucial for the system's function. In the loop of Henle, the descending limb is permeable to water but not solutes, while the ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports solutes (Option B) A concentration gradient between inflow and outflow pathways : In the kidneys, the active transport of Na+ and Cl− from the ascending limb of the loop of Henle into the medullary interstitium creates the osmotic gradient that is essential for the counter-current mechanism to function effectively. The vasa recta helps maintain this gradient (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 621-621; Solution Pages 630-631","sourcePage":621,"sourcePageEnd":621},{"id":"physiology-22-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the primary aim of the counter-current mechanism in the kidneys?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"To dilute urine for better excretion of waste products"},{"letter":"B","text":"To conserve sodium and chloride ions in the body"},{"letter":"C","text":"To concentrate urine and maintain fluid and electrolyte balance"},{"letter":"D","text":"To increase blood flow to the renal cortex"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The counter-current mechanism concentrates urine by creating a hyperosmotic environment in the renal medulla . This allows the kidneys to excrete waste products with minimal water loss, conserving water and maintaining fluid and electrolyte balance. A) To dilute urine for better excretion of waste products: The counter-current mechanism aims to concentrate, not dilute, the urine. Dilution would result in a loss of excess water, which could lead to dehydration. B) To conserve sodium and chloride ions in the body: While sodium and chloride reabsorption occurs during the process, the primary goal is not to conserve these ions but to create an osmotic gradient that facilitates water reabsorption. D) To increase blood flow to the renal cortex: The counter-current mechanism primarily operates in the renal medulla and focuses on concentrating urine rather than regulating blood flow to the renal cortex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 621-621; Solution Pages 631-632","sourcePage":621,"sourcePageEnd":621},{"id":"physiology-22-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a component of the counter-current multiplier system in the kidneys?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cortical Collecting Duct (CCD)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thick Ascending Limb (TAL) of the Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thin Ascending Segment (ATS) of the Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medullary Collecting Duct (MCD)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"The CCD is involved in the reabsorption of water and electrolytes under the influence of hormones such as aldosterone and antidiuretic hormone (ADH). However, it is not directly part of the counter-current multiplier system. The counter-current multiplier specifically refers to the mechanisms occurring within the loop of Henle and associated structures in the medulla, not the cortical regions. COUNTERCURRENT MULTIPLIER Site Function Proximal Tubule Delivers an isosmotic filtrate to the loop of Henle, with approximately 65% of filtered electrolytes and water reabsorbed. The osmolarity here is around 300 mOsm/L, similar to plasma. Thin Descending Limb of the Highly permeable to water due to the presence of Loop of Henle aquaporin-1 (AQP-1) channels and mildly permeable to solutes like urea and sodium chloride. As the filtrate moves deeper into the hyperosmotic medulla, water exits by osmosis, concentrating the tubular fluid. Thin Ascending Limb of the Impermeable to water but permeable to solutes. Loop of Henle NaCl passively diffuses out, and some urea enters, contributing to medullary hyperosmolarity. (Option C) Thick Ascending Limb of the Impermeable to water and urea, actively transports Loop of Henle (TAL) NaCl, K■ out of the tubule into the interstitium using the Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter. This action increases interstitial osmolarity and delivers hypotonic fluid to the distal tubule. (Option B) Medullary Collecting Duct (MCD) Under the influence of ADH, becomes permeable to water. Water is reabsorbed from the MCD into the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, concentrating the urine. The MCD operates within the hyperosmotic environment established by the counter-current multiplier. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 621-621; Solution Pages 632-633","sourcePage":621,"sourcePageEnd":621},{"id":"physiology-22-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45 y/o patient presents with polyuria and polydipsia. Lab tests reveal a low urine osmolarity and a high plasma osmolarity. After administration of desmopressin (a synthetic analog of ADH), the patient's urine osmolarity increases significantly. Which of the following best explains the mechanism by which ADH improved the patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ADH increases the permeability of the ascending limb of the loop of Henle to water."},{"letter":"B","text":"ADH decreases the active transport of sodium chloride in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle."},{"letter":"C","text":"ADH increases the permeability of the collecting ducts to water."},{"letter":"D","text":"ADH causes the osmolarity of the medullary interstitium to decrease rapidly."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"ADH increases the permeability of the collecting ducts to water by promoting the insertion of aquaporin-2 water channels into the apical membranes of collecting duct cells. This allows water to be reabsorbed from the tubular fluid into the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, concentrating the urine and reducing urine volume. A) The ascending limb of the loop of Henle is impermeable to water, a characteristic that is essential for the creation of a hyperosmotic medullary interstitium. ADH does not alter this permeability. B) ADH does not affect the active transport of sodium chloride in the thick ascending limb. The active transport in this segment is necessary for establishing the osmotic gradient but is not regulated by desmopressin. D) ADH does not decrease the osmolarity of the medullary interstitium. Instead, it facilitates water reabsorption in the collecting ducts, leveraging the existing high osmolarity of the medullary interstitium to concentrate the urine.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 621-622; Solution Pages 633-633","sourcePage":621,"sourcePageEnd":622},{"id":"physiology-22-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following statements about urea transport in the kidneys is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"UT-A1 and UT-A3 in the cortical collecting duct are regulated by aldosterone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Urea helps establish the osmotic gradient in the medullary pyramids but is not involved in urine concentration."},{"letter":"C","text":"A high-protein diet reduces the kidneys' ability to concentrate urine."},{"letter":"D","text":"UT-B is found in erythrocytes and the descending limbs of the vasa recta."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"UT-B is indeed found in erythrocytes and the descending limbs of the vasa recta. Role of Urea in Kidney Function: Functions: • Osmotic Gradient: Urea helps establish the osmotic gradient in the medullary pyramids. • Concentrated Urine: Contributes to forming concentrated urine in the collecting ducts. (Option B) Urea Transport: • Mediated by: Urea transporters (UT-A isoforms). • Types: UT-A1 to UT-A4: Found in kidneys. UT-B: Found in erythrocytes and descending limbs of the vasa recta. • UT-A1 to UT-A4: Found in kidneys. • UT-B: Found in erythrocytes and descending limbs of the vasa recta. • Key Transporters in Collecting Duct: UT-A1 and UT-A3: Found in the medullary collecting ducts. Regulated by vasopressin. (Option A) • UT-A1 and UT-A3: Found in the medullary collecting ducts. Regulated by vasopressin. (Option A) • UT-A1 to UT-A4: Found in kidneys. • UT-B: Found in erythrocytes and descending limbs of the vasa recta. • UT-A1 and UT-A3: Found in the medullary collecting ducts. Regulated by vasopressin. (Option A) Regulation: • Vasopressin (ADH): Increases during antidiuresis, raising urea levels in the medullary interstitium, and enhancing the kidney’s urine concentrating capacity. Impact of Diet: • High-Protein Diet: Increases urea filtration, enhancing urine concentration ability. (Option C) • Low-Protein Diet: Decreases urea filtration, reducing urine concentration ability.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 622-622; Solution Pages 633-634","sourcePage":622,"sourcePageEnd":622},{"id":"physiology-22-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following substances plays the most crucial role in establishing and maintaining the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sodium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Urea"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chloride"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Urea is the most important substance for establishing and maintaining the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium. It significantly contributes to the osmotic gradient and urine concentration by: • Urea recycling: Urea is reabsorbed in the collecting ducts and secreted into the loop of Henle. This recycling helps to maintain a high concentration of urea in the medulla. • Osmotic Gradient: The high concentration of urea in the medullary interstitium creates an osmotic gradient. This gradient allows for the reabsorption of water from the collecting ducts, concentrating the urine. • Water Reabsorption: The osmotic gradient established by urea, along with sodium and other solutes, helps in reabsorbing water from the filtrate, making the medullary interstitium hyperosmotic. A) Sodium is involved in creating the osmotic gradient but is not the most crucial substance for maintaining it. B) Potassium plays a role in ionic transport esp. In the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, but is not the primary substance for the hyperosmotic gradient. D) Chloride is involved in the osmotic gradient but is not as critical as urea for maintaining the hyperosmotic medullary interstitium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 622-622; Solution Pages 634-635","sourcePage":622,"sourcePageEnd":622},{"id":"physiology-22-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-22","chapter":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topic":"Acid Secretion and Counter-Current Mechanism of the Kidney","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following structures is responsible for maintaining the counter-current exchange mechanism in the kidney?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loops of Henle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Collecting Ducts"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vasa Recta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glomerulus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The vasa recta maintains the osmotic gradient through countercurrent exchange. A) The loops of Henle are responsible for creating the osmotic gradient (countercurrent multiplier system) but not for maintaining it. B) The collecting ducts use the osmotic gradient to concentrate urine but do not maintain the gradient. D) The glomerulus is involved in the filtration of blood to form urine but does not maintain the osmotic gradient in the medulla. Counter-Current Exchange Mechanism in the Vasa Recta Aspect of Vasa Details Recta Function Maintains the osmotic gradient established by the loops of Henle, crucial for concentrated urine production. Structure Hairpin-loop blood vessels that descend and ascend alongside the loops of Henle. Descending Limb Solutes (NaCl and urea) diffuse from the interstitium into the vasa recta. Water moves out of the vasa recta into the interstitium due to the osmotic gradient. Result: Blood becomes more concentrated as it descends. Ascending Limb Solutes diffuse from the vasa recta into the interstitium. Water moves from the interstitium into the vasa recta. The ascending vasa recta are fenestrated to facilitate this exchange. Net Effect Role: Prevents dissipation of the osmotic gradient. Outcome: Maintains the hypertonicity of the medulla, essential for water reabsorption and concentrated urine production. Conclusion: Works with the loops of Henle to establish and maintain medullary osmotic gradient","sourceRef":"Question Pages 622-623; Solution Pages 635-636","sourcePage":622,"sourcePageEnd":623},{"id":"physiology-23-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":1,"question":"What is the typical Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR) in a healthy adult?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"125 ml/min"},{"letter":"B","text":"200 ml/min"},{"letter":"C","text":"100 ml/min"},{"letter":"D","text":"150 ml/min"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 125 ml/min: • This is the typical GFR for a healthy adult. GFR measures how well the kidneys are filtering blood, and 125 ml/min is considered the normal rate, reflecting optimal kidney function. Incorrect Options: Option B - 200 ml/min: This value is higher than the normal range and could indicate an overestimation of kidney function or an error in measurement. Such a high rate is not typical for healthy adults. Option C - 100 ml/min: This value is slightly below the normal range. A GFR of 100 ml/min may suggest mild kidney impairment, although it can still be considered acceptable in some contexts. Option D - 150 ml/min: This value is above the normal range, which could indicate an overestimation or abnormal kidney function. It is not a typical GFR for a healthy adult.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 637-637; Solution Pages 656-656","sourcePage":637,"sourcePageEnd":637},{"id":"physiology-23-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":2,"question":"How does the kidney react to shock?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increasing renal blood flow"},{"letter":"B","text":"Constricting afferent arteriole"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increasing GFR"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both B and C"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: Constricting afferent arteriole • During shock, the kidney responds by constricting the afferent arteriole, which is the blood vessel that supplies blood to the glomerulus. • this constriction helps to maintain the glomerular filtration pressure and prevent a drastic decrease in the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). • By constricting the afferent arteriole, the kidney can redirect blood flow to vital organs and ensure adequate perfusion. Incorrect Options: Option A: Increasing renal blood flow- In response to shock, the kidney actually decreases renal blood flow. The constriction of the afferent arteriole reduces the amount of blood entering the glomerulus, resulting in reduced renal blood flow. Option C: Increasing GFR- During shock, the kidney aims to maintain a stable GFR rather than increasing it. The constriction of the afferent arteriole helps to preserve the glomerular filtration pressure and prevent a significant decline in the GFR. This response is important for maintaining renal function in conditions of reduced blood volume or blood pressure. Option D: Both B and C- This statement is incorrect because option (c) is incorrect. While the kidney does respond to shock by increasing the tone of the afferent arteriole (option B), it does not increase the GFR (option C).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 637-637; Solution Pages 656-657","sourcePage":637,"sourcePageEnd":637},{"id":"physiology-23-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":3,"question":"In the renal arterial circulation, the glomerulus is supplied with blood through an afferent arteriole. Unlike other capillary beds, the glomerular capillaries do not lead to venules but instead to efferent arterioles. The resistance in the efferent arterioles creates enough hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus to facilitate ultrafiltration. Which of the subsequent molecules will be found in the glomerular ultrafiltrate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Polycationic large radius molecules"},{"letter":"B","text":"Polycationic small radius molecules"},{"letter":"C","text":"Polyanionic small radius molecules"},{"letter":"D","text":"Polyanionic large radius molecules"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: The glomerular ultrafiltrate primarily consists of small molecules and ions that can pass through the filtration barrier of the glomerulus. The filtration barrier consists of three layers: the fenestrated endothelium of the glomerular capillaries, the basement membrane, and the filtration slits formed by podocyte foot processes. This barrier allows the passage of small molecules, such as water, electrolytes, glucose, and small proteins, while preventing the filtration of larger molecules like red blood cells and most plasma proteins. The hydrostatic pressure within the glomerulus, generated by the resistance of the efferent arteriole, is responsible for the ultrafiltration process. This pressure forces water and small solutes through the filtration barrier into the glomerular capsule, forming the glomerular ultrafiltrate. Polycationic small radius molecules, which are positively charged and have a small size, are capable of passing through the filtration barrier and would therefore be present in the glomerular ultrafiltrate. Incorrect Options: Option A. Polycationic large radius molecules: Large molecules, regardless of their charge, generally cannot pass through the filtration barrier of the glomerulus. Therefore, polycationic large radius molecules would not be present in the glomerular ultrafiltrate. Option C. Polyanionic small radius molecules: Polyanionic small radius molecules, which are negatively charged and have a small size, may pass through the filtration barrier of the glomerulus. However, the question specifically mentions polycationic molecules, so polyanionic molecules are not relevant to the answer. Option D. Polyanionic large radius molecules: Similar to option A, large molecules, regardless of their charge, would be hindered from passing through the filtration barrier of the glomerulus. Therefore, polyanionic large radius molecules would not be present in the glomerular ultrafiltrate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 637-637; Solution Pages 657-658","sourcePage":637,"sourcePageEnd":637},{"id":"physiology-23-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":4,"question":"As filtrate flows through PCT, the concentration of all of the following decreases except: (or) Except for which of the following, the concentration of all the following decreases as the filtrate passes through the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aminoacid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucose"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chloride"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bicarbonate"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C: • As the filtrate flows through the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT) of the nephron in the kidney, various substances are reabsorbed back into the bloodstream. This reabsorption process helps to maintain the balance of electrolytes and other solutes in the body. • In the PCT, glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate are actively reabsorbed from the filtrate back into the bloodstream. This reabsorption is essential for maintaining the appropriate levels of these substances in the body. Therefore, the concentrations of glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate decrease as they are being reabsorbed. • However, chloride (Cl-) is primarily passively reabsorbed in the PCT. This means that it follows the movement of other solutes, particularly sodium (Na+), through the paracellular pathway. As sodium is actively reabsorbed in the PCT, chloride ions also passively move along, resulting in their reabsorption. Therefore, the concentration of chloride does not decrease significantly as the filtrate flows through the PCT. Incorrect Options: Option A: Amino acid: The concentration of amino acids decreases as they are actively reabsorbed in the PCT. Option B: Glucose: The glucose concentration decreases as it is actively reabsorbed in the PCT. Option D: Bicarbonate: The concentration of bicarbonate decreases as it is actively reabsorbed in the PCT.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 638-638; Solution Pages 658-658","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":638},{"id":"physiology-23-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In a normal physiological state, what is the cause of hemoglobin being unable to pass through the glomerulus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a large molecule bound with another protein"},{"letter":"B","text":"It gets attracted to the negative charge of the glomerular filtration barrier"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both A and B"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A. It is a large molecule bound with another protein The correct reason that hemoglobin cannot pass through the glomerulus in a normal physiological state is because it is a large molecule. Hemoglobin is a complex protein with a molecular weight of about 64,500 Daltons. The glomerular filtration barrier in the kidneys allows the passage of small molecules and ions but restricts the movement of large proteins and cells. Hemoglobin is too large to pass through the filtration barrier, so it is retained within the bloodstream and does not get filtered into the renal tubules. Incorrect Options: Option B. It gets attracted to the negative charge of the glomerular filtration barrier This statement is not accurate. The glomerular filtration barrier does have negative charges due to the presence of glycoproteins in the glomerular basement membrane. These negative charges repel negatively charged molecules, preventing them from being filtered. However, hemoglobin is not positively charged; its inability to pass through the glomerulus is primarily due to its large size, not its charge. Option C. Both A and B Option C is incorrect because hemoglobin's inability to pass through the glomerulus is primarily due to its large size. Option D. None of the above Option D is incorrect based on the explanation provided above. Hemoglobin's large size is the main reason it cannot pass through the glomerulus in a normal physiological state.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 638-638; Solution Pages 659-659","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":638},{"id":"physiology-23-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following leads to a decrease in GFR?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased RBF"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased Glomerular hydrostatic pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased Bowman's oncotic pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased Glomerular oncotic pressure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Decreased Bowman's oncotic pressure: Net filtration pressure = P C - P B + π B - π C • P C - Hydrostatic pressure of glomerular capillaries • P B - Hydrostatic pressure of Bowman's capsule • π B - Oncotic pressure of Bowman's capsule • π C - Oncotic pressure of glomerular capillaries Hence, a decrease in Bowman's oncotic pressure causes a decrease in GFR rather than an increase. Incorrect Options : Option A - Increased RBF: Increased blood flow to the kidney enhances blood delivery to the glomerulus, facilitating filtration, while decreased flow hampers filtration. Option B - Increased Glomerular hydrostatic pressure: An increase in Glomerular hydrostatic pressure increases the net forces hence increasing the GFR. Option D - Decreased Glomerular oncotic pressure: Glomerular capillary osmotic pressure, from plasma proteins, opposes filtration. So, low protein levels increase GFR, while dehydration reduces GFR and urine production.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 638-638; Solution Pages 659-660","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":638},{"id":"physiology-23-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which part of renal tubules reabsorbs maximum water in the presence of ADH?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PCT"},{"letter":"B","text":"DCT"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting duct"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Collecting duct: • Antidiuretic Hormone (ADH) primarily acts on the collecting ducts of the kidneys. In the presence of ADH, the collecting ducts become more permeable to water by increasing the expression of aquaporin-2 channels on the tubular cells. This allows more water to be reabsorbed from the filtrate back into the bloodstream, helping to concentrate urine and conserve water in the body. Incorrect Options • A. Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT) : The PCT does reabsorb a large amount of water, but this process is independent of ADH. • B. Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT) : The DCT reabsorbs a smaller portion of water, but the major site of ADH action is the collecting duct. • C. Loop of Henle : The ascending limb of the Loop of Henle is impermeable to water, while the descending limb reabsorbs water passively, but this is not directly influenced by ADH.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 639-639; Solution Pages 660-660","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"physiology-23-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Where does the majority of water absorption occur within the renal tubules?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loop of Henle (LOH)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting Duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Proximal Convoluted Tubule (PCT): • Approximately 65-70% of water reabsorption occurs in the PCT. The PCT plays a crucial role in reabsorbing both sodium and water from the filtrate. • This process begins with the filtration of blood at the glomerulus, which produces a filtrate rich in sodium and other solutes. The PCT cells, connected by tight junctions, actively transport these substances from the lumen of the nephron into the surrounding peritubular capillary blood. • The approximate contribution to sodium reabsorption in the PCT is: SGLT: 5% Co-transporters: 45% NHE: 50% • SGLT: 5% • Co-transporters: 45% • NHE: 50% • SGLT: 5% • Co-transporters: 45% • NHE: 50% Incorrect Options: Option B - Distal Convoluted Tubule (DCT): The DCT is responsible for fine-tuning the reabsorption of sodium and water, but it does not account for the majority of water reabsorption. It primarily adjusts the concentration of urine in response to hormonal signals. Option C - Loop of Henle (LOH): The LOH plays a significant role in establishing the medullary osmotic gradient, crucial for water reabsorption in the collecting duct, but does not absorb the majority of water. Option D - Collecting Duct: While the collecting duct is responsible for the final adjustments in water reabsorption under the influence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH), the majority of water absorption has already occurred by this point in the PCT.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 639-639; Solution Pages 661-661","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"physiology-23-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is true regarding an increase in Glomerular Filtration Rate (GFR)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase in the resistance of the afferent arteriole"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decrease in the resistance of the efferent arteriole"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase in Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increase in renal perfusion"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct option D - Increase in renal perfusion: • Increasing renal perfusion (blood flow to the kidneys) enhances the amount of blood reaching the glomeruli, which typically increases the GFR. Incorrect Options: Option A - Increase in the resistance of the afferent arteriole: Increasing the resistance of the afferent arteriole reduces the blood flow into the glomerulus, which decreases the GFR. Option B - Decrease in the resistance of the efferent arteriole: Decreasing the resistance of the efferent arteriole would lead to increased outflow from the glomerulus, which typically reduces the GFR, not increases it. Option C - Increase in Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure: An increase in the hydrostatic pressure within Bowman’s capsule would oppose filtration and thus decrease the GFR.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 639-639; Solution Pages 661-662","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"physiology-23-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A patient arrives at the emergency department unconscious. An arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis reveals the following results: pH: 7.1 PCO2: 23 mmHg PO2: 98 mmHg HCO3: 16 mEq/L What do these ABG results indicate about the patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Respiratory alkalosis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Metabolic Acidosis: To determine the type of acid-base disturbance, let's break down the ABG results: • pH: The pH of 7.1 indicates acidemia (a lower-than-normal pH). • HCO3 (Bicarbonate): The bicarbonate level is 16 mEq/L, which is lower than the normal range (22-26 mEq/L). Low bicarbonate levels typically indicate a primary metabolic problem. • PCO2 (Partial Pressure of Carbon Dioxide): The PCO2 level is 23 mmHg, which is lower than the normal range (35-45 mmHg). This indicates that the body is compensating for the primary metabolic disturbance by increasing respiratory rate to blow off CO2. • Metabolic Acidosis (Option A): The low pH (acidosis) combined with low HCO3 suggests a primary metabolic acidosis. The decreased HCO3 is consistent with metabolic acidosis. The low PCO2 is a compensatory response to the metabolic acidosis, where the body is trying to reduce CO2 to help counteract the acidosis. Incorrect Options: Option B - Metabolic Alkalosis: This would be characterized by a high pH and high HCO3. This is not the case here, as the pH is low and HCO3 is low. Option C - Respiratory Acidosis: This would be characterized by a low pH and high PCO2, not a low PCO2. The bicarbonate level would not be as low in a primary respiratory acidosis. Option D - Respiratory Alkalosis: This would show a high pH and low PCO2, but the bicarbonate level would be normal or compensatory high. In this case, the pH is low, and HCO3 is also low.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 639-640; Solution Pages 662-663","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"physiology-23-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":11,"question":"What renal function is represented by the formula UV/P ■?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Filtration"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tubular Secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tubular Reabsorption"},{"letter":"D","text":"Clearance"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Clearance: • The formula UV/P, where U is the concentration of a substance in the urine, V is the urine flow rate, and P is the concentration of the substance in the plasma, represents the renal clearance of that substance. It quantifies the volume of plasma from which the substance is completely removed per unit time. C = ml/min = volume/time Incorrect Options: Option A - Filtration: This process refers to the initial filtration of blood through the glomerulus to form filtrate. The formula ■ does not measure this process directly. Option B - Tubular Secretion: This is the process by which substances are actively transported from the blood into the renal tubules. The formula does not specifically measure tubular secretion. Option C - Tubular Reabsorption: This refers to the process of reabsorbing substances from the renal tubules back into the blood. The formula ■ does not measure reabsorption directly.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 640-640; Solution Pages 663-663","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"physiology-23-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which segment of the nephron has the lowest osmolality under the influence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Early distal tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Descending limb of the loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Collecting duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Late distal tubule"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Early distal tubule","explanation":"The early distal tubule has the lowest osmolality under the influence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) . • Mechanism: ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting duct and late distal tubule , concentrating the urine. The descending limb of the loop of Henle is highly permeable to water but not solutes , leading to an increase in osmolality . In contrast, the early distal tubule is impermeable to water , but actively reabsorbs NaCl , making the tubular fluid hypotonic. As a result, the lowest osmolality is found in the early distal tubule (~100mOsm/kg), even in the presence of ADH. • ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting duct and late distal tubule , concentrating the urine. • The descending limb of the loop of Henle is highly permeable to water but not solutes , leading to an increase in osmolality . • In contrast, the early distal tubule is impermeable to water , but actively reabsorbs NaCl , making the tubular fluid hypotonic. • As a result, the lowest osmolality is found in the early distal tubule (~100mOsm/kg), even in the presence of ADH. • ADH increases water reabsorption in the collecting duct and late distal tubule , concentrating the urine. • The descending limb of the loop of Henle is highly permeable to water but not solutes , leading to an increase in osmolality . • In contrast, the early distal tubule is impermeable to water , but actively reabsorbs NaCl , making the tubular fluid hypotonic. • As a result, the lowest osmolality is found in the early distal tubule (~100mOsm/kg), even in the presence of ADH. Descending limb of the loop of Henle (Option B) is incorrect because it is highly permeable to water and allows water to leave, increasing osmolality (~1200 mOsm/kg in the medullary region). Collecting duct (Option C) is incorrect because under ADH influence, the collecting duct becomes highly water-permeable , leading to urine concentration (osmolality can reach >1200 mOsm/kg ). Late distal tubule (Option D) is incorrect because it is more permeable to water in the presence of ADH, leading to an increase in osmolality compared to the early distal tubule.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 640-640; Solution Pages 663-664","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"physiology-23-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":13,"question":"In which of the body's parts is the countercurrent mechanism found? A. Eye B. Testes C. Kidney D. Gut E. Lungs","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A and B are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"A, B, C are correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"A, B, C, D, E are correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"B, C, D, E are correct"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"The countercurrent mechanism is primarily found in the kidneys . It plays a crucial role in the concentration of urine. The correct answer is: Correct Option D - B, C , D, E: • B. Testes: Countercurrent exchange is present in the testicular blood vessels, which helps regulate the temperature within the testes, crucial for sperm production. • C. Kidney: Countercurrent mechanisms, such as those in the loop of Henle, are fundamental in the kidney's ability to concentrate urine and regulate water and electrolyte balance. • D and E: The countercurrent mechanism is a significant feature in the lungs or the gut. B. Testes: Countercurrent exchange is present in the testicular blood vessels, which helps regulate the temperature within the testes, crucial for sperm production. C. Kidney: Countercurrent mechanisms, such as those in the loop of Henle, are fundamental in the kidney's ability to concentrate urine and regulate water and electrolyte balance. D and E: The countercurrent mechanism is a significant feature in the lungs or the gut.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 640-641; Solution Pages 665-665","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":641},{"id":"physiology-23-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A renal biopsy image is shown. Identify the cells marked in the histological section.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Macula densa"},{"letter":"B","text":"Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lacis cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Podocytes"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Lacis cells Explanation: Lacis cells, also called extraglomerular mesangial cells or Goormaghtigh cells , are found within the juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) of the kidney. • These cells are located between the afferent and efferent arterioles and the macula densa . • They play a role in signal transmission between the macula densa and juxtaglomerular cells, influencing renin secretion . • They are part of the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) and contribute to blood pressure regulation. Juxtaglomerular Apparatus (JGA) and its Components Component Location Function Macula densa Distal tubule near afferent Senses sodium concentration & arteriole regulates renin secretion Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells Afferent arteriole Secrete renin in response to low BP Lacis cells (Extraglomerular Between the afferent & Facilitate communication between mesangial cells) efferent arterioles macula densa and JG cells Podocytes Visceral layer of Bowman's Filtration barrier in glomerulus capsule Macula densa (Option A): Incorrect because macula densa cells are found in the distal tubule and sense sodium concentration. Juxtaglomerular (JG) cells (Option B): Incorrect because JG cells are located in the afferent arteriole and primarily secrete renin. Podocytes (Option D): Incorrect because podocytes are specialized epithelial cells covering the glomerular capillaries.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 641-641; Solution Pages 665-666","sourcePage":641,"sourcePageEnd":641},{"id":"physiology-23-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":15,"question":"This is the graph depicting the filterability of dextran through the kidney, with 1 representing complete filtration and 0 meaning no filtration. Choose the substance A, B, and C respectively.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A - Polycationic, B - Neutral, C - Polyanionic"},{"letter":"B","text":"A - Polyanionic, B - Neutral, C - Polycationic"},{"letter":"C","text":"A - Neutral, B - Polycationic, C - Polyanionic"},{"letter":"D","text":"A - Polyanionic, B - Polycationic, C - Neutral"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: A - Polycationic, B - Neutral, C - Polyanionic: • The Glomerular Filtration Barrier is a negatively charged barrier. • Depending on the nature of filtration, there are 3 different substances at the level of the barrier. • Freely Filtered Substances: If the molecular radius < 1.8 nm. They can easily pass through irrespective of charge. E.g., Na + , K + , H 2 O, Urea, Creatinine, Inulin, PAH, Bicarbonate, etc. • Selective Permeable Substances: If the molecular radius is 1.8 nm-4.2 nm. The filtration of the substances is determined by the charge present on them. The filtration scale is Positively charged >> Neutral charge >> Negatively charged. • The filtration scale is Positively charged >> Neutral charge >> Negatively charged. • Impermeable substances: If the molecular radius > 4.2 nm. • So, in graph A represents a positively charged molecule, B represents a neutral charged molecule, and C represents the negatively charged molecule. • The filtration scale is Positively charged >> Neutral charge >> Negatively charged. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 641-642; Solution Pages 666-667","sourcePage":641,"sourcePageEnd":642},{"id":"physiology-23-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Renin is secreted by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lacis cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Macula densa"},{"letter":"C","text":"JG cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intraglomerular mesangial cells"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - JG cells: • Juxtaglomerular cells are the modified smooth muscle cells of the afferent arteriole with granules containing renin. • JG cells contain GPCR (G S/I type), which generates a secondary messenger cyclic AMP (cAMP) and another GPCR (GQ type) generates calcium ions that are a secondary messenger. • Increased intracellular cAMP causes degranulation of JG cells, thus stimulating renin secretion. • Increased Calcium concentration inhibits degranulation and restricts renin secretion. • Factors increasing renin secretion: Intrarenal hypotension (< 80 mm Hg - most potent stimulator), increased sympathetic stimulation, and prostaglandins (E2 and I2). • Factors decreasing renin secretion: Increased levels of angiotensin II, ATP, and adenosine. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 642-642; Solution Pages 667-668","sourcePage":642,"sourcePageEnd":642},{"id":"physiology-23-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following is the site of maximum water reabsorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proximal tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collecting duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Proximal tubule: • The proximal tubule is the first segment of the renal tubule after the glomerulus. It is responsible for the reabsorption of water, electrolytes, and other essential substances from the glomerular filtrate back into the bloodstream. • In this segment, water reabsorption occurs through aquaporin 1 and osmosis, driven by the concentration gradient created by active transport of solutes out of the tubule. 60 - 70% of water is reabsorbed at the proximal tubule. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Maximum water reabsorption occurs at proximal tubule of the nephron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 642-642; Solution Pages 668-668","sourcePage":642,"sourcePageEnd":642},{"id":"physiology-23-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following is the diluting segment of the kidney?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PCT"},{"letter":"B","text":"Collecting duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ascending thick loop of Henle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Descending loop of Henle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Ascending thick loop of Henle: • The Ascending Thick Loop of Henle is the segment of the nephron responsible for diluting the urine. It actively transports ions, such as sodium, chloride, and potassium, but is impermeable to water and urea. Hence, referred to as the diluting segment because it also dilutes the tubular fluid. Incorrect Options: Option A - PCT (Proximal Convoluted Tubule): The Proximal Convoluted Tubule is the first segment of the nephron following the glomerulus. It is involved in both solute and water reabsorption, but it is not specifically associated with urine dilution. Option B - Collecting Duct: It is involved in reabsorbing water and concentrating the urine under the influence of antidiuretic hormone (ADH). Option D - Descending Loop of Henle: It is permeable to water but not to solutes, resulting in the concentration of the tubular fluid as it descends deeper into the medulla.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 643-643; Solution Pages 668-669","sourcePage":643,"sourcePageEnd":643},{"id":"physiology-23-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Glucose is absorbed by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Upper part of proximal tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending limb of LOH"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cortical Collecting duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medullary collecting duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Upper part of proximal tubule: • 100% glucose is absorbed in the proximal tubules. Proximal tubules are divided into two parts as follows. Tubular Segment Glucose Luminal Transporter(Lume Basolateral Transporter Reabsorption n to cytoplasm) (Cytoplasm to blood) (%) Proximal Convoluted 90% SGLT type II (Secondary GLUT-2 (Facilitated Tubules (PCT) active Transporter) diffusion transporter) 1 molecule of sodium and 1 molecule of glucose from the lumen to the cell. Proximal Straight 10% SGLT type I (Secondary GLUT-1 (Facilitated Tubules (PST) active Transporter) diffusion transporter) 2 molecules of sodium and 1 molecule of glucose from the lumen to the cell. Incorrect options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 643-643; Solution Pages 669-669","sourcePage":643,"sourcePageEnd":643},{"id":"physiology-23-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Mark the TRUE statements:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Efferent arteriole dilation decreases RBF"},{"letter":"B","text":"Afferent arteriole constriction increases GFR"},{"letter":"C","text":"Efferent arteriolar constriction decreases renal blood flow"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mild contraction of efferent arteriole decreases GFR"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Efferent arteriolar constriction decreases renal blood flow: • This statement is true. Constriction of the efferent arteriole leads to a decrease in renal blood flow. This constriction increases the resistance to blood flow, causing a reduction in blood flow to the kidneys. Incorrect Option: Option A - Efferent arteriole dilation decreases RBF: This statement is incorrect. Efferent arteriole dilation will result in increased renal plasma flow. Option B - Afferent arteriole constriction increases GFR: This statement is incorrect. Afferent arteriole constriction leads to decrease in plasma flow resulting in decreased capillary hydrostatic pressure and reduced GFR. Option D - Mild constriction of the efferent arteriole decreases GFR: This statement is incorrect. Mild constriction leads to less reduction in RPF. It may decrease to 500 ml. Due to the mild constriction, the plasma cannot exit the capillary easily, leading to accumulation of plasma when the second 500 ml arrives in the next minute. The subsequent 500 ml pushes the first 500 ml. Thus, in steady-state conditions, the total volume of plasma present at the capillary is approximately 1000 ml, i.e., higher than normal. This leads to a high hydrostatic pressure, which in turn leads to increased GFR.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 643-643; Solution Pages 669-670","sourcePage":643,"sourcePageEnd":643},{"id":"physiology-23-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":21,"question":"What happens to urine after passing the thin descending loop of Henle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypotonic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Normal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypertonic"},{"letter":"D","text":"Remains unchanged"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Hypertonic: • The descending thin segment of the loop of henle is permeable to water and impermeable to the solutes. • The tubular fluid will be hypertonic after passing through the descending thin segment of the Loop of Henle. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 643-644; Solution Pages 670-670","sourcePage":643,"sourcePageEnd":644},{"id":"physiology-23-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Glucose is reabsorbed in which part?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Proximal straight tubule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Collecting duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Proximal convoluted tubule: • 100% glucose is absorbed in the proximal tubules. Proximal tubules are divided into two parts as follows. Tubular Segment Glucose Luminal Transporter Basolateral Transporter Reabsorption (Lumen to cytoplasm) (Cytoplasm to blood) (%) Proximal Convoluted 90% SGLT type II (Secondary GLUT-2 (Facilitated Tubules (PCT) active Transporter) diffusion transporter) 1 molecule of sodium and 1 molecule of glucose from the lumen to the cell. Proximal Straight 10% SGLT type I (Secondary GLUT-1 (Facilitated Tubules (PST) active Transporter) diffusion transporter) 2 molecules of sodium and 1 molecule of glucose from the lumen to the cell. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 644-644; Solution Pages 671-671","sourcePage":644,"sourcePageEnd":644},{"id":"physiology-23-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":23,"question":"The cause of edema in nephrotic syndrome is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased colloid osmotic pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased hydrostatic pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased colloid osmotic pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased hydrostatic pressure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Decreased colloid osmotic pressure: • In nephrotic syndrome, the kidneys lose large amounts of protein, particularly albumin, through the urine. • Since albumin helps maintain colloid osmotic pressure in the blood vessels, decreased levels of albumin result in decreased colloid osmotic pressure. Osmotic pressure is a retaining force. • This reduction in colloid osmotic pressure leads to fluid shifting from the blood vessels into the surrounding tissues, causing edema. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 644-644; Solution Pages 671-671","sourcePage":644,"sourcePageEnd":644},{"id":"physiology-23-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Normal anion gap value is____ mEq/L?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4-6"},{"letter":"B","text":"6-12"},{"letter":"C","text":"12-24"},{"letter":"D","text":"24-50"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 6-12: • The normal anion gap is between 7-16 mEq/L and is used to assess the presence of unmeasured cations and anion ions in the blood and helps in diagnosing the different causes of metabolic acidosis. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 644-644; Solution Pages 672-672","sourcePage":644,"sourcePageEnd":644},{"id":"physiology-23-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Why is sugar added to ORS?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enhance acceptability"},{"letter":"B","text":"Enhance shelf life"},{"letter":"C","text":"Enhance salt absorption"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhance taste"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Enhance salt absorption: • In diarrhea, only salt is lost but sugar is added as it helps to absorb salt and sugar by process of symport. The transporter in the gut is SGLT-1 and kidney is SGLT-2. • Coupling ratio: SGLT 1: 2:1 as 2 molecules of Na are transported for 1 molecule of glucose. SGLT 2: 1:1 as 1 molecule of Na is transported for 1 molecule of glucose. • SGLT 1: 2:1 as 2 molecules of Na are transported for 1 molecule of glucose. • SGLT 2: 1:1 as 1 molecule of Na is transported for 1 molecule of glucose. • SGLT 1: 2:1 as 2 molecules of Na are transported for 1 molecule of glucose. • SGLT 2: 1:1 as 1 molecule of Na is transported for 1 molecule of glucose. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 645-645; Solution Pages 672-672","sourcePage":645,"sourcePageEnd":645},{"id":"physiology-23-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":26,"question":"In the adjacent graph, plateau phase represents which one of the following properties?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Elasticity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Plasticity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Contractility"},{"letter":"D","text":"Automaticity"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Plasticity: • The given graph is cystometrogram (CMG). • A CMG is a test that measures the pressure inside the bladder as it fills with fluid. It helps assess the bladder’s function and its ability to store and release urine. • Plateau Phase Explanation : The plateau phase of the cystometrogram, where the pressure remains relatively constant despite increasing volume, can be explained by the bladder's ability to stretch and accommodate increasing volumes of urine. This is a result of the unique properties of the bladder's smooth muscle. • Law of Laplace : According to the Law of Laplace, for a spherical viscus (like the bladder), the pressure (P) inside the bladder is related to the wall tension (T) and the radius (r) of the bladder. The relationship is given by P=2T/r■. As the bladder fills, the radius increases, which helps keep the pressure relatively low despite the increased volume. • Plasticity : Plasticity refers to the ability of the bladder's smooth muscle to undergo prolonged changes in length (stretch) without a significant increase in pressure. This allows the bladder to store larger volumes of urine without a significant rise in pressure. A CMG is a test that measures the pressure inside the bladder as it fills with fluid. It helps assess the bladder’s function and its ability to store and release urine. Plateau Phase Explanation : The plateau phase of the cystometrogram, where the pressure remains relatively constant despite increasing volume, can be explained by the bladder's ability to stretch and accommodate increasing volumes of urine. This is a result of the unique properties of the bladder's smooth muscle. Law of Laplace : According to the Law of Laplace, for a spherical viscus (like the bladder), the pressure (P) inside the bladder is related to the wall tension (T) and the radius (r) of the bladder. The relationship is given by P=2T/r■. As the bladder fills, the radius increases, which helps keep the pressure relatively low despite the increased volume. Plasticity : Plasticity refers to the ability of the bladder's smooth muscle to undergo prolonged changes in length (stretch) without a significant increase in pressure. This allows the bladder to store larger volumes of urine without a significant rise in pressure. Incorrect Options: Option A - Elasticity: Elasticity is the property of a material to return to its original shape after being stretched or deformed. Option C - Contractility: Contractility is the fundamental property of muscle tissue that enables it to contract when stimulated. Option D - Automaticity: Automaticity refers to the ability of certain cells (such as cardiac pacemaker cells) to spontaneously generate electrical impulses without external stimulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 645-645; Solution Pages 673-674","sourcePage":645,"sourcePageEnd":645},{"id":"physiology-23-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Which of the following statements is accurate regarding creatinine clearance?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Equal to inulin clearance"},{"letter":"B","text":"Less than inulin clearance"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater than inulin clearance"},{"letter":"D","text":"Equal to renal plasma flow"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Greater than inulin clearance: • Creatinine clearance is an indirect measure of GFR that is calculated from the amount of creatinine that is filtered by the glomeruli and then excreted in the urine over a given period. • Creatinine is not as accurate as inulin in measuring GFR because creatinine is partially secreted and never reabsorbed by the renal tubules, which can result in an overestimation of GFR. • Inulin is a freely filterable substance, neither endogenously secreted by the body nor reabsorbed by the nephron. • The clearance of creatinine is slightly higher than that of inulin due to the tubular secretion of creatinine. • Order of clearance of different substances across the kidney is: C PAH >>C Cr >>C inulin (125ml/min (GFR)>>C Urea >>C Na+ >>C Glucose/AA • C PAH >>C Cr >>C inulin (125ml/min (GFR)>>C Urea >>C Na+ >>C Glucose/AA • C PAH >>C Cr >>C inulin (125ml/min (GFR)>>C Urea >>C Na+ >>C Glucose/AA C PAH >>C Cr >>C inulin (125ml/min (GFR)>>C Urea >>C Na+ >>C Glucose/AA Incorrect Options: Options A and B are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation. Option D - Equal to renal plasma flow: Renal plasma flow is a measure of para-amino hippuric acid clearance which is filtered and completely secreted in the nephron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 645-646; Solution Pages 674-674","sourcePage":645,"sourcePageEnd":646},{"id":"physiology-23-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":28,"question":"What is the role of tubuloglomerular feedback?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Regulate GFR"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decrease plasma sodium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase plasma volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease tubular secretion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Regulate GFR: • Tubuloglomerular feedback is responsible for the autoregulation of GFR. . The juxtaglomerular apparatus, which consists of macula densa cells, has an NKCC channel in the luminal membrane which absorbs Na + , K + , and 2 Cl - . The transport of Na + across the basolateral membrane requires Na + K + ATPase leading to increased adenosine formation. • The lacis cells have A1 receptor for the adenosine leading to influx of Ca 2+ into lacis cells and then diffuses into smooth muscles of afferent arteriole leading to constriction and juxtaglomerular cells leading to decrease in renin release. Incorrect Options: Option B: Decrease plasma sodium : The regulation of plasma sodium concentration primarily Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 646-646; Solution Pages 674-675","sourcePage":646,"sourcePageEnd":646},{"id":"physiology-23-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Identify the correctly matched pair of substances with their renal clearance from the graph below.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-Glucose, B-PAH, C- Bicarbonate and D - Inulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-Glucose, B- Bicarbonate, C-Inulin and D - PAH"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-PAH, B-Inulin, C - Glucose and D - Bicarbonate"},{"letter":"D","text":"A - Inulin, B - Glucose, C - Bicarbonate and D - PAH"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - A-Glucose, B- Bicarbonate, C-Inulin and D - PAH: The given graph shows renal clearance of different substances. • Substance A, with a clearance of zero, is glucose. Normally, glucose is completely reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule, and none is excreted in the urine. • Substance B is bicarbonate, with a clearance value less than the glomerular filtration rate (GFR). The kidneys reabsorb most filtered bicarbonate, resulting in a clearance lower than the GFR. • Substance C is inulin, with a clearance value equal to the GFR. Inulin is filtered but neither reabsorbed nor secreted, making it a good measure of the GFR. • Substance D, with a clearance value higher than the GFR, is para-amino hippuric acid (PAH). PAH is filtered and secreted, resulting in a clearance higher than the GFR. also can be creatinine. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 646-646; Solution Pages 675-675","sourcePage":646,"sourcePageEnd":646},{"id":"physiology-23-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which among the following organs has the least arteriovenous oxygen difference?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Brain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Kidney"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Liver"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Kidney: • The kidneys have the least arteriovenous oxygen difference, meaning that they extract a relatively small amount of oxygen from the blood as it passes through. This is because the kidneys do not have a high metabolic demand and primarily function to filter waste products from the blood rather than perform energy-intensive processes. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 646-647; Solution Pages 676-676","sourcePage":646,"sourcePageEnd":647},{"id":"physiology-23-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Where is macula densa located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Junction of thick descending limb and DCT"},{"letter":"B","text":"Junction of thick descending limb and PCT"},{"letter":"C","text":"Junction of thick ascending limb and DCT"},{"letter":"D","text":"Junction of DCT and collecting duct"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Junction of thick ascending limb and DCT: • The macula densa is located at the junction of the thick ascending limb and DCT. The macula densa is a specialized group of cells in the wall of the DCT that are involved in regulating renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate (GFR). The cells of the macula densa sense changes in the concentration of sodium and chloride ions in the filtrate and signal the juxtaglomerular cells of the afferent arteriole to adjust renal blood flow and GFR accordingly. Incorrect Options: • Option A, B and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 647-647; Solution Pages 676-676","sourcePage":647,"sourcePageEnd":647},{"id":"physiology-23-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Hyperkalemia refers to the serum potassium concentration of _____.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4.5 mEq/L"},{"letter":"B","text":"6.0 mEq/L"},{"letter":"C","text":"4.0 mEq/L"},{"letter":"D","text":"5 meq/L"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 6.0 mEq/L: • Hyperkalemia refers to an elevated level of serum potassium more than 5.5 mEq/L. • Normal value of serum potassium is 3.5 to 5.5 mEq/l. Incorrect options Options A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 647-647; Solution Pages 676-677","sourcePage":647,"sourcePageEnd":647},{"id":"physiology-23-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":33,"question":"The protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for charge-dependent filtration is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fibronectin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Heparan sulfate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Collagen Type IV"},{"letter":"D","text":"Entactin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Heparan sulfate: • The protein in the glomerular basement membrane responsible for charge-dependent filtration is Heparan sulfate. • Heparan sulfate is a type of glycosaminoglycan (GAG) that is a key component of the glomerular basement membrane (GBM) in the kidney. • Heparan sulfate has negative charges that repel negatively charged proteins, contributing to the selective filtration of molecules based on their charge. Incorrect Options: • Option A, C & D are incorrect. Heparan sulfate is the charged protein present in the glomerular basement membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 647-647; Solution Pages 677-677","sourcePage":647,"sourcePageEnd":647},{"id":"physiology-23-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":34,"question":"The formula for urea clearance is U×V/P, where U is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Urine volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Serum urea in mg/100 ml"},{"letter":"C","text":"Urine concentration of urea in mg/100ml"},{"letter":"D","text":"Urine concentration of urea in mg/ml"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D:Urine urea in mg/ml • The formula for urea clearance is U × V / P, • Where U represents the concentration of urea in the urine in mg/ml, V represents the urine flow rate or volume in ml/min, P represents the concentration of urea in the plasma in mg/ml. • U represents the concentration of urea in the urine in mg/ml, • V represents the urine flow rate or volume in ml/min, • P represents the concentration of urea in the plasma in mg/ml. • U represents the concentration of urea in the urine in mg/ml, • V represents the urine flow rate or volume in ml/min, • P represents the concentration of urea in the plasma in mg/ml. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 648-648; Solution Pages 677-678","sourcePage":648,"sourcePageEnd":648},{"id":"physiology-23-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":35,"question":"All of the following ions have decreased concentration on the luminal side of the proximal convoluted tubule except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amino acids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucose"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chloride"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bicarbonate"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Chloride: • In the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), various substances are reabsorbed to maintain electrolyte and fluid balance in the body. Normally, glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate are reabsorbed from the tubular lumen into the renal interstitium, leading to decreased concentrations of these substances in the luminal side of the PCT. • Chloride, on the other hand, is primarily reabsorbed paracellularly in the PCT. It follows the movement of sodium ions, which are actively reabsorbed across the luminal membrane. Therefore, chloride concentration in the luminal side of the PCT remains relatively high, as it is not actively reabsorbed. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 648-648; Solution Pages 678-678","sourcePage":648,"sourcePageEnd":648},{"id":"physiology-23-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which one of the following is not associated with renal auto-regulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tubuloglomerular feedback"},{"letter":"B","text":"Myogenic reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Renal sympathetic system fibers"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Renal sympathetic system fibers: • Renal auto-regulation refers to the ability of the kidneys to maintain a relatively constant glomerular filtration rate (GFR) and renal blood flow (RBF) despite changes in systemic blood pressure. • It involves the following mechanisms that work together to adjust the diameter of the renal arterioles and maintain adequate renal perfusion. Tubuloglomerular feedback mediated by adenosine. Myogenic reflex mediated by calcium. • Tubuloglomerular feedback mediated by adenosine. • Myogenic reflex mediated by calcium. • Renal sympathetic system fibers are not involved in the autoregulation of the GFR. • Tubuloglomerular feedback mediated by adenosine. • Myogenic reflex mediated by calcium. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 648-648; Solution Pages 678-679","sourcePage":648,"sourcePageEnd":648},{"id":"physiology-23-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Which of the following ions in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle is responsible for the reabsorption of divalent ions?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"H +"},{"letter":"B","text":"HCO 3 -"},{"letter":"C","text":"K +"},{"letter":"D","text":"Na +"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - • In the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, the primary ion responsible for the reabsorption of divalent ions, such as calcium (Ca 2+ ) and magnesium (Mg 2+ ), is potassium (K+). The thick ascending limb is impermeable to water but actively transports ions, including sodium (Na + ), potassium (K + ), and 2 chloride (Cl - ), out of the tubular lumen through the action of the apical membrane sodium-potassium-2 chloride cotransporter (NKCC2) into the thick ascending limb (TAL). • The another channel in the luminal side of the TAL is ROMK (Renal Outer-Medullary Potassium) channel which pushes the absorbed K + , out into the lumen leaving a positive charge on the lumen called lumen positive charge. • Because of this, the positive ions such as Ca ++ and Mg ++ are repulsed into a paracellular pathway (Passive reabsorption) through the tight junction and reabsorbed into capillary blood. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 648-649; Solution Pages 679-680","sourcePage":648,"sourcePageEnd":649},{"id":"physiology-23-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Which sensory receptors are accountable for tactile discrimination among the options provided? Merkel disc Ruffini end organ Pacinian corpuscle Meissner’s corpuscle","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 4 are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 is correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 is correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"None are correct."},{"letter":"E","text":"Both A & D"}],"correctOption":null,"correctAnswerText":"Not provided in source.","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1 and 4 are correct (Merkel disc and Meissner’s corpuscle): • Tactile discrimination, which refers to the ability to discern fine details, textures, and shapes through touch, relies on superficial sensory receptors in the skin. • Both Merkel discs and Meissner’s corpuscles are involved in tactile discrimination, allowing individuals to sense and distinguish intricate textures and shapes. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Pacinian and ruffini end organs are deep sensory receptors stimulated by pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 649-649; Solution Pages 680-680","sourcePage":649,"sourcePageEnd":649},{"id":"physiology-23-39","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":39,"question":"According to myogenic hypothesis of renal autoregulation the afferent arterioles contract in response?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"NO"},{"letter":"B","text":"Noradrenaline"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adenosine"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Calcium: • According to the myogenic hypothesis, it suggests that when the afferent arterioles experience an increase in blood pressure or stretch, it triggers the opening of calcium channels in the smooth muscle cells of the arterioles. • The influx of calcium ions leads to vasoconstriction, reducing the blood flow and preventing excessive pressure within the glomerulus. This response helps maintain a relatively constant blood flow and glomerular filtration rate despite changes in systemic blood pressure. Incorrect Options: Options A and B: These are not involved in the autoregulation of GFR. Option D - Adenosine: Adenosine is a mediator of tubuloglomerular feedback mechanism.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 649-649; Solution Pages 680-680","sourcePage":649,"sourcePageEnd":649},{"id":"physiology-23-40","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":40,"question":"Which part of the nephron forms the macula densa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PCT"},{"letter":"B","text":"DCT"},{"letter":"C","text":"Afferent arteriole"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bowman’s capsule"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - DCT: • Macula densa cells are the columnar epithelial cells in contact with afferent and efferent arterioles, involved in the regulation of renal blood flow and glomerular filtration rate. • These cells are located abundantly in the uppermost part of the thick ascending limb (TAL) of the loop of Henle and few in the distal convoluted tubule. • It is the intra-renal chemoreceptor cells that sense the presence of NaCl load inside the lumen of the tubule. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Macula densa cells are not located in these parts of the nephron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 649-650; Solution Pages 681-681","sourcePage":649,"sourcePageEnd":650},{"id":"physiology-23-41","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Through which aquaporin channel does ADH act?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option is B - 2: • Vasopressin acts on Aquaporin 2 (AQP2) channels present in the collecting duct. When vasopressin binds to its V2 receptor on the basolateral membrane of the collecting duct cells, it triggers increased cAMP levels promoting the translocation and insertion of AQP2 channels into the apical membrane. This insertion allows for increased water reabsorption from the urine, leading to concentrated urine and water conservation. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. These are ADH independent hormones.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 650-650; Solution Pages 681-681","sourcePage":650,"sourcePageEnd":650},{"id":"physiology-23-42","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":42,"question":"As filtrate flows through PCT, the concentration of all of the following decreases except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amino-acid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucose"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chloride"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bicarbonate"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Chloride: • In the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT), various substances are reabsorbed to maintain electrolyte and fluid balance in the body. Normally, glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate are reabsorbed from the tubular lumen into the renal interstitium, leading to decreased concentrations of these substances in the luminal side of the PCT. • Chloride, on the other hand, is primarily reabsorbed paracellularly in the PCT. It follows the movement of sodium ions, which are actively reabsorbed across the luminal membrane. Therefore, chloride concentration in the luminal side of the PCT remains relatively high, as it is not actively reabsorbed. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 650-650; Solution Pages 682-682","sourcePage":650,"sourcePageEnd":650},{"id":"physiology-23-43","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":43,"question":"Which of the following components is not responsible for the countercurrent multiplier mechanism in the kidney?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sodium outflow in thick ascending limb"},{"letter":"B","text":"Water outflow in thin descending limb"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sodium outflow in thin ascending limb"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flow of tubular fluid from PCT to DCT"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Flow of tubular fluid from PCT to DCT: • This component is not involved in the countercurrent multiplier mechanism of the kidney. • The countercurrent multiplier involves the following: The high medullary tonicity is maintained by the following: Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. This outflow of solutes into the medullary interstitium will result in hypertonicity which will result in the outflow of the water from isotonic fluid flowing through the thin descending limb of the loop of the henle. • The high medullary tonicity is maintained by the following: Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. • Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. • Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. • Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. • This outflow of solutes into the medullary interstitium will result in hypertonicity which will result in the outflow of the water from isotonic fluid flowing through the thin descending limb of the loop of the henle. • The high medullary tonicity is maintained by the following: Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. • Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. • Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. • Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. • This outflow of solutes into the medullary interstitium will result in hypertonicity which will result in the outflow of the water from isotonic fluid flowing through the thin descending limb of the loop of the henle. • Sodium and chloride outflow in thick ascending limb. • Sodium and chloride outflow in the thin ascending limb. • Reabsorption of urea from medullary collecting duct into the interstitium. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 650-650; Solution Pages 682-683","sourcePage":650,"sourcePageEnd":650},{"id":"physiology-23-44","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":44,"question":"Which of the following is true about renal tubular transport?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Maximum reabsorption in the distal tubule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Urea is absorbed 100% in PCT"},{"letter":"C","text":"H+ is absorbed in the proximal tubule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Amino acid is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Amino acid is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule: • Co-transporters present in the luminal membrane of the proximal tubule reabsorb sodium along with other substances like amino acids (sodium-amino acid cotransporter), phosphate (sodium-phosphate cotransporter), citric acid (sodium-citric acid cotransporter). Incorrect Options: Option A - Maximum reabsorption in the distal tubule: Maximum reabsorption occurs in PCT. Option B - Urea is absorbed 100% in PCT: Urea is not 100% absorbed in PCT. Option C - H+ is absorbed in the proximal tubule: NHE (Sodium-Acid exchanger) reabsorbs sodium and secretes one molecule of H+ ion into the lumen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 651-651; Solution Pages 683-683","sourcePage":651,"sourcePageEnd":651},{"id":"physiology-23-45","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":45,"question":"All of the following are true regarding the actions of endothelin-1 except_________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bronchodilation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased GFR"},{"letter":"D","text":"Has inotropic effect"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Options A - Bronchodilation: • Endothelin 1 is the most potent vasoconstrictor known so far. Veins are more sensitive to endothelin 1 than arteries. Endothelin-1 can cause a decrease in glomerular filtration rate (GFR) by constricting the afferent arterioles of the kidney. • It is also a potent positive chronotropic and inotropic agent (increases heart rate and myocardial contractility). Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 651-651; Solution Pages 684-684","sourcePage":651,"sourcePageEnd":651},{"id":"physiology-23-46","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":46,"question":"GFR is increased by all except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased renal blood flow"},{"letter":"B","text":"Efferent arteriolar constriction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ureteric stone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased oncotic pressure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Ureteric stone: • Ureteric stone: A ureteric stone refers to a stone that forms in the ureter, the tube that connects the kidneys to the bladder. Ureteric stones can cause obstruction and block the flow of urine, which can result in decreased urine flow from the affected kidney. • The obstruction can lead to increased pressure in the renal pelvis and the affected kidney, potentially impairing the filtration process and reducing the GFR. Therefore, a ureteric stone is expected to decrease GFR. Incorrect Options: Option A - Increased renal blood flow: When there is a higher volume of blood flowing into the kidneys, it results in increased glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which promotes filtration and thus increases GFR. Option B - Efferent arteriolar constriction: Constriction of the efferent arteriole causes an increase in glomerular hydrostatic pressure and promotes filtration. Option D - Decreased oncotic pressure: Oncotic pressure is a retaining force and it tends to retain water in the renal capillaries. Decreased oncotic pressure will result in reduced Glomerular filtration rate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 651-651; Solution Pages 684-685","sourcePage":651,"sourcePageEnd":651},{"id":"physiology-23-47","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":47,"question":"Calculate the net filtration pressure across the GBM for a patient with Glomerular ultrafiltration coefficient = 6, Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure = 45, Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure = 20, Bowman’s capsule hydrostatic pressure = 10, Bowman’s capsule oncotic pressure = 0.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"90 mmHg"},{"letter":"B","text":"60 mmHg"},{"letter":"C","text":"30 mmHg"},{"letter":"D","text":"15 mmHg"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option: D • According to Starling law, the net filtration pressure at the level of glomerular capillary is the combination of hydrostatic pressure and oncotic pressure. • Net Force = {(P C - P B ) + (π B - π C )} where, P C = Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure P B = Bowman capsule hydrostatic pressure π C = Capillary oncotic pressure π B = Bowman capsule oncotic pressure • Substituting the values, NFP = (45 - 10) + (0 - 20) ■ 45 - 30 ■ 15 mmHg • NFP = (45 - 10) + (0 - 20) ■ 45 - 30 ■ 15 mmHg • Therefore, the net filtration pressure, in this case, is 15 mmHg. • NFP = (45 - 10) + (0 - 20) ■ 45 - 30 ■ 15 mmHg Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 651-652; Solution Pages 685-685","sourcePage":651,"sourcePageEnd":652},{"id":"physiology-23-48","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":48,"question":"Normal anion gap is_____mEq/L?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4-6"},{"letter":"B","text":"6-12"},{"letter":"C","text":"12-24"},{"letter":"D","text":"24-50"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 6-12: • Anion gap is used to assess the presence of unmeasured cations and anions in the blood and can help in the diagnosing the different causes of metabolic acidosis. • The normal anion gap ranges within the 7-16 mEq/L. Incorrect options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 652-652; Solution Pages 686-686","sourcePage":652,"sourcePageEnd":652},{"id":"physiology-23-49","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":49,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the given cystometrogram?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Segment Ia is due to residual urine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Segment lb is due to Laplace law"},{"letter":"C","text":"Micturition fails to happen in segment II"},{"letter":"D","text":"Micturition occurs in segment Ib"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Segment lb is due to Laplace law: • Segment Ib is due to Laplace law where volume is increased but there is no rise in pressure. • This law states that the pressure in a spherical viscus is equal to twice the wall tension divided by the radius. • In the case of the bladder, the tension increases as the organ fills, but so does the radius. Therefore, the pressure increase is slight until the organ is relatively full. Incorrect Options: Option A - Segment la is due to residual urine: Segment Ia represents the filling phase of the bladder. Option C - Micturition fails to happen in segment II: Segment II represents the tremendous increase in pressure due to large volume of urine (400 ml) and results in micturition. Option D - Micturition occurs in segment Ib: It occurs in segment II.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 652-652; Solution Pages 686-686","sourcePage":652,"sourcePageEnd":652},{"id":"physiology-23-50","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-23","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":50,"question":"Which of the following factors is not necessary for determining creatinine clearance?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Urine creatinine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Serum creatinine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Urine flow per unit time"},{"letter":"D","text":"The volume of serum passing through the kidney in 24 hours"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - The volume of serum passing through the kidney in 24 hours: • To calculate creatinine clearance, the following formula is used: Clearance = U×V/P Where, U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. • Clearance = U×V/P • Where, U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. • U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. • V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. • P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. • Clearance = U×V/P • Where, U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. • U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. • V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. • P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. • U - concentration of creatinine in the urine in mg/ml. • V - urine flow rate or volume in ml/min. • P - concentration of creatinine in the plasma in mg/ml. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. These parameters are used for the calculation of creatinine clearance.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 653-653; Solution Pages 687-687","sourcePage":653,"sourcePageEnd":653},{"id":"physiology-24-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents with a persistent dry cough and shortness of breath. A CT scan of the chest reveals localized airway obstruction at the level of the terminal bronchioles. Based on Weibel's classification of the respiratory system, which generation of airways is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Generation 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"Generation 8"},{"letter":"C","text":"Generation 16"},{"letter":"D","text":"Generation 20"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Generation 16","explanation":"Weibel's classification of the respiratory system organizes the airways into 23 generations, starting from the trachea (Generation 0) down to the alveolar sacs (Generation 23). • The terminal bronchioles are found at generation/division 16, marking the end of the conducting zone and the beginning of the respiratory zone. This level is critical as it is the last portion of the airways involved in conducting air before gas exchange begins in the respiratory bronchioles. • This level is critical as it is the last portion of the airways involved in conducting air before gas exchange begins in the respiratory bronchioles. • Division number 0 to division number 16 is known as the conducting zone of the airway . Division number 1 to 10 are known as Bronchi. Division numbers 11 to 16 are known as terminal bronchioles. • Division number 1 to 10 are known as Bronchi. • Division numbers 11 to 16 are known as terminal bronchioles. • Division number 17 to division number 23 are responsible for gas exchange and are known as the exchange zone of alveoli. • This level is critical as it is the last portion of the airways involved in conducting air before gas exchange begins in the respiratory bronchioles. • Division number 1 to 10 are known as Bronchi. • Division numbers 11 to 16 are known as terminal bronchioles.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 688-688; Solution Pages 700-700","sourcePage":688,"sourcePageEnd":688},{"id":"physiology-24-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements about intra-alveolar pressure changes during normal breathing is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The maximum and minimum intra-alveolar pressure is at start of expiration and start of inspiration respectively"},{"letter":"B","text":"Minimum Intra-alveolar pressure is approximately -1 mm Hg during inspiration."},{"letter":"C","text":"Maximum Intra-alveolar pressure is approximately +1 mm Hg during expiration."},{"letter":"D","text":"At the end of both inspiration and expiration, intra-alveolar pressure is 0 mm Hg."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) The maximum and minimum intra-alveolar pressure is at the start of expiration and start of inspiration respectively.","explanation":"The maximum and minimum intra-alveolar pressure are during mid-expiration and mid-inspiration respectively. Changes in Intra-alveolar pressure during respiration Phase of Respiration Intra-Alveolar Pressure (IAP) Resting Condition 0 mm Hg Start of Inspiration 0 mm Hg Middle of Inspiration -1 mm Hg End of Inspiration 0 mm Hg Start of Expiration 0 mm Hg Middle of Expiration +1 mm Hg End of Expiration 0 mm Hg","sourceRef":"Question Pages 688-688; Solution Pages 700-701","sourcePage":688,"sourcePageEnd":688},{"id":"physiology-24-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Uptake of various substances during their transit through a pulmonary capillary is shown here. Curve B represents:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"N2O"},{"letter":"B","text":"O2"},{"letter":"C","text":"CO2"},{"letter":"D","text":"CO"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) O2","explanation":"Curve B represents O2 capillary \" data-author=\"na\" data-hash=\"536\" data-license=\"na\" data-source=\"na\" data-tags=\"January2025\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/PulREAui57Hk9Pff6Oyv1735 921405.png\" /> Substances moving from alveoli to capillary blood can reach equilibrium based on their interactions with blood components during the 0.75 seconds blood spends in the pulmonary capillaries: • Nitrous Oxide (Option A) :Reaches equilibrium quickly (about 0.1 seconds) as it does not react with blood components. Its uptake is flow-limited because diffusion is not the limiting factor. • Carbon Monoxide (Option D) : Binds very strongly to hemoglobin, keeping its partial pressure in the capillaries very low. As a result, equilibrium is not reached in the 0.75 seconds blood spends in the capillaries, making its transfer diffusion-limited . • Oxygen (Option B) : Intermediate between N2O and CO. Binds to hemoglobin, but not as avidly as CO. It reaches equilibrium in about 0.3 seconds, which makes its transfer primarily perfusion-limited","sourceRef":"Question Pages 688-689; Solution Pages 701-702","sourcePage":688,"sourcePageEnd":689},{"id":"physiology-24-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 30-year-old patient with a history of asthma presents with complaints of shortness of breath and wheezing. Pulmonary function tests are performed and indicate that the patient's dynamic compliance is significantly reducd, while static compliance remains relatively unchanged. What is the most likely explanation for these findings?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Surfactant deficiency leading to increased alveolar surface tension"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reduced chest wall compliance due to muscle weakness"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased airway resistance due to bronchospasm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Presence of fibrotic tissue in the lungs, restricting lung expansion"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Increased airway resistance due to bronchospasm Explanation: Static Compliance : • This refers to lung compliance measured when there is no airflow in the lungs. It provides insights into the condition of the lung parenchyma, alveoli, and the chest wall. • Surfactant deficiency primarily affects static compliance (Option A) Dynamic Compliance : • This refers to lung compliance measured during continuous airflow, not when breathing is paused. It reflects the additional force needed to overcome airway resistance during breathing. • Dynamic compliance considers both airway resistance and the factors influencing static compliance. • It is always lower than static compliance due to the added resistance of airflow. • Conditions like bronchospasm can reduce dynamic compliance by increasing airway resistance, even when static compliance remains normal. • In restrictive lung diseases like fibrosis, both dynamic and static compliance are lower due to increased lung stiffness. (Option D) Specific Compliance : • This measure is calculated by dividing static compliance (C) by the functional residual capacity (FRC). • It is particularly useful for comparing lung compliance between individuals of different sizes, as lung size influences overall compliance Reduced chest wall compliance due to muscle weakness can decrease both static and dynamic compliance. (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 689-689; Solution Pages 702-703","sourcePage":689,"sourcePageEnd":689},{"id":"physiology-24-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following correctly depicts A ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Emphysema"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lung fibrosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Myasthenia Gravis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kyphoscoliosis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Answer: A) Emphysema Explanation: In emphysema, the walls of the alveoli are damaged and lose their elasticity , making it harder for the lungs to recoil during exhalation. This would cause increased lung compliance. • A compliance curve shown above, with transpulmonary pressure plotted on the Xaxis and lung volume on the Y-axis. • The normal curve depicted above is the expiratory curve. • The compliance is calculated from this curve by plotting two perpendiculars to the exponential part of the curve from the X-axis and Y-axis. • The difference between the two points is ∆V on the Y-axis and ∆P on the X-axis. • Also compliance can be compared among various lung conditions in this graph. Options (B, C, D): All these conditions have a reduced lung compliance and hence have an expiratory graph below the normal curve as shown.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 689-690; Solution Pages 703-704","sourcePage":689,"sourcePageEnd":690},{"id":"physiology-24-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following denotes the compliance curve depicting both inspiratory and expiratory phase? (Y-axis denotes volume and x-axis denotes pressure) A.","options":[{"letter":"B","text":"{{caption_text}} C. D."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D)","explanation":"HYSTERESIS LOOP: Inhalation : • During inhalation, the inspiratory muscles (primarily the diaphragm) contract, expanding the thoracic cavity. This expansion reduces pleural pressure, creating a more negative pressure compared to the alveolar pressure. • The difference between alveolar pressure and pleural pressure, known as transpulmonary pressure, increases, causing the lungs to expand. • The inspiratory compliance curve on the compliance diagram illustrates this relationship, showing how lung volume increases as pleural pressure decreases (becomes more negative). • The shape of the inspiratory compliance curve is influenced by the elastic properties of the lungs, with steeper curves indicating greater compliance (easier lung expansion). Exhalation : • During exhalation, the inspiratory muscles relax, and the elastic recoil of the lungs and chest wall causes the thoracic cavity to decrease in volume. This decrease in volume increases the pleural pressure, making it less negative. • As pleural pressure rises, the transpulmonary pressure decreases, leading to lung deflation. • The expiratory compliance curve on the compliance diagram depicts this relationship, showing lung volume decreasing as pleural pressure increases (becomes less negative).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 690-690; Solution Pages 704-705","sourcePage":690,"sourcePageEnd":690},{"id":"physiology-24-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":7,"question":"In the alveoli, surfactant primarily reduces surface tension by:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increasing the viscosity of the alveolar fluid, thereby reducing the tendency to collapse"},{"letter":"B","text":"Interfering with the diffusion of water molecules across the alveolar lining."},{"letter":"C","text":"Spreading phospholipids and proteins uniformly across the alveolar surface."},{"letter":"D","text":"Forming a film that alters the behavior of water molecules at the air-liquid interface"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: D) Forming a film that alters the behavior of water molecules at the air-liquid interface Explanation: Surface tension is a force that arises at the interface of air and liquid , causing the liquid surface to contract. • In the lungs, this interface exists between the alveolar fluid and the air inside the alveoli. Surfactant is a surface-active agent in water that significantly reduces the surface tension of water. • Surfactant is produced by type II alveolar epithelial cells, which comprise about 10% of the alveolar surface area. Type II alveolar epithelial cells are granular and contain lipid inclusions that are secreted as surfactant into the alveoli. • Surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids, proteins, and ions. • Dipalmitoyl phosphatidylcholine , surfactant apoproteins, and calcium ions are the most important components. are primarily responsible for reducing surface tension by partially dissolving in the fluid lining the alveolar surface while the rest spreads over the water's surface in the alveoli. Importance of Surfactant • Surfactant reduces the surface tension in the alveoli, preventing their collapse. • The pressure generated by surface tension in an alveolus is inversely proportional to its radius – smaller alveoli experience greater pressure. • The absence of surfactant in premature infants with small alveoli can lead to respiratory distress syndrome of the newborn, a potentially fatal condition Increasing the viscosity of the alveolar fluid, thereby reducing the tendency to collapse (Option A): Surfactant reduces surface tension , but it does not increase viscosity of alveolar fluid. Interfering with the diffusion of water molecules across the alveolar lining (Option B): Surfactant does not interfere with water diffusion ; it acts at the air-liquid interface to reduce surface tension. Spreading phospholipids and proteins uniformly across the alveolar surface. (Option C): Surfactant is not uniformly spread ; it dynamically adjusts its distribution to stabilize alveoli of different sizes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 690-690; Solution Pages 705-706","sourcePage":690,"sourcePageEnd":690},{"id":"physiology-24-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Old-age lung has high compliance"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elastic tissue recoil and surface tension oppose each other during expansion of alveoli"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyaline membrane disease has low compliance and high surface tension"},{"letter":"D","text":"Low compliance of the lungs is seen in Atelectasis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Elastic tissue recoil and surface tension oppose each other during expansion","explanation":"Elastic tissue recoil and surface tension are the two opposing forces to lung compliance and are not opposing forces to each other. Two primary factors influence lung compliance: • Elastic forces of the lung tissue : These forces are primarily determined by the presence of elastin and collagen fibers within the lung parenchyma (the functional tissue of the lungs). In a deflated lung, these fibers are contracted and kinked. As the lung expands during inspiration, these fibers stretch and unkink, generating an elastic force that opposes further expansion. • These forces are primarily determined by the presence of elastin and collagen fibers within the lung parenchyma (the functional tissue of the lungs). • In a deflated lung, these fibers are contracted and kinked. As the lung expands during inspiration, these fibers stretch and unkink, generating an elastic force that opposes further expansion. • Surface tension : A thin layer of fluid lines the inside of the alveoli. At the air-fluid interface within the alveoli, surface tension arises from the stronger attraction between water molecules compared to the attraction between water molecules and air molecules. This surface tension creates an inward pull, attempting to minimize the surface area of the fluid lining the alveoli. Consequently, this inward pull contributes to the elastic recoil of the lungs, making them tend to collapse • A thin layer of fluid lines the inside of the alveoli. At the air-fluid interface within the alveoli, surface tension arises from the stronger attraction between water molecules compared to the attraction between water molecules and air molecules. • This surface tension creates an inward pull, attempting to minimize the surface area of the fluid lining the alveoli. Consequently, this inward pull contributes to the elastic recoil of the lungs, making them tend to collapse • These forces are primarily determined by the presence of elastin and collagen fibers within the lung parenchyma (the functional tissue of the lungs). • In a deflated lung, these fibers are contracted and kinked. As the lung expands during inspiration, these fibers stretch and unkink, generating an elastic force that opposes further expansion. • A thin layer of fluid lines the inside of the alveoli. At the air-fluid interface within the alveoli, surface tension arises from the stronger attraction between water molecules compared to the attraction between water molecules and air molecules. • This surface tension creates an inward pull, attempting to minimize the surface area of the fluid lining the alveoli. Consequently, this inward pull contributes to the elastic recoil of the lungs, making them tend to collapse Option A) Old-age lung has loss of elastic tissue and hence have reduced elastic recoil force, causing easy expansion of lung, i.e high compliance Option C) Hyaline membrane disease, normally seen in premature babies has low compliance and high surface tension due to reduced production of surfactants. Option D) Low compliance is seen in atelectasis due to a decrease in lung volume and requires higher pressure to inflate the alveoli.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 691-691; Solution Pages 706-707","sourcePage":691,"sourcePageEnd":691},{"id":"physiology-24-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A patient's lung volume increases by 500 ml when their transpulmonary pressure increases from 3 cm H2O to 5.5 cm H2O. What is the compliance of the patient's lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"100 ml/cm"},{"letter":"B","text":"150 ml/cm"},{"letter":"C","text":"200 ml/cm"},{"letter":"D","text":"250 ml/cm"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 200 ml/cm","explanation":"Lung compliance refers to the lungs' ability to stretch and expand in response to changes in transpulmonary pressure. • Compliance = Change in Lung Volume / Change in Transpulmonary Pressure • In this scenario, the change in lung volume is 500 ml, and the change in transpulmonary pressure is 2.5 cm H2O (5.5 cm H2O - 3 cm H2O) • Therefore , Compliance = 500 ml / 2.5 cm H2O = 200 ml/cm H2O","sourceRef":"Question Pages 691-691; Solution Pages 708-708","sourcePage":691,"sourcePageEnd":691},{"id":"physiology-24-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Match the following zones of the lungs with their corresponding pressure relationships and blood flow characteristics. Zone Pressure Relationship Blood Flow Characteristic 1 A) Pulmonary artery pressure > Pulmonary venous pressure > Alveolar pressure i) Continuous blood flow 2 B) Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary artery pressure > Pulmonary venous pressure ii) No blood flow 3 C) Pulmonary artery pressure > Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary venous pressure iii) Intermittent blood flow (only during systole) Zon Pressure Relationship Blood Flow Characteristic e","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pulmonary artery pressure > 1 Pulmonary venous pressure > i) Continuous blood flow Alveolar pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary 2 artery pressure > Pulmonary venous ii) No blood flow pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary artery pressure > iii) Intermittent blood flow (only 3 Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary during systole) venous pressure"},{"letter":"A","text":"1 - A - i, 2 - B - ii, 3 - C - iii"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 - B - ii, 2 - C - iii, 3 - A - i"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 - C - iii, 2 - B - ii, 3 - A - i"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 - B - ii, 2 - A - i, 3 - C - iii"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 1 - B - ii, 2 - C - iii, 3 - A - i","explanation":"Zo Pressure Relationship Blood Flow Characteristic ne 1 Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary No blood flow, as alveolar pressure artery pressure > Pulmonary venous occludes the capillaries. pressure 2 Pulmonary artery pressure > Intermittent blood flow occurs during Alveolar pressure > Pulmonary systole. venous pressure 3 Pulmonary artery pressure > Continuous blood flow. Pulmonary venous pressure > Alveolar pressure Note that the zones discussed are not the anatomical zones of the lungs.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 691-692; Solution Pages 708-709","sourcePage":691,"sourcePageEnd":692},{"id":"physiology-24-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Two separate patients are being considered for respiratory distress. Imaging reveals an obstruction in the right bronchus in one patient and an embolism in the pulmonary arteriole in another patient. Based on these findings, what are the expected changes in the ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio and the partial pressures of oxygen (PO2) and carbon dioxide (PCO2) in the affected alveoli?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In the obstructed bronchus, the V/Q ratio is infinite, and the partial pressures are PO2■■ = 149 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 0.3 mm Hg."},{"letter":"B","text":"In the embolized vessel, the V/Q ratio is zero, and the partial pressures are PO2■■ = 40 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 46 mm Hg."},{"letter":"C","text":"In the obstructed bronchus, the V/Q ratio is zero, and the partial pressures are PO2■■ = 40 mmHg and PCO2 = 46 mm Hg."},{"letter":"D","text":"In the embolized vessel, the V/Q ratio is infinite, and the partial pressures are PO2■■ = 40 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 46 mm Hg."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) In the obstructed bronchus, the V/Q ratio is zero, and the partial pressures are PO2■■ = 40 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 46 mm Hg.","explanation":"Obstruction: • V/Q Ratio: Zero • Partial Pressures: The alveoli receive no ventilation, but perfusion continues. Consequently, the partial pressures of gases in the alveoli equilibrate with the venous blood, resulting in PO2■■ = 40 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 46 mmHg. Embolism: • V/Q Ratio: Infinite • Partial Pressures: The alveoli receive ventilation, but no perfusion. The partial pressures of gases in the alveoli match those of the inspired air, resulting in PO2■■ = 149 mmHg and PCO2■■ = 0.3 mmHg.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 692-692; Solution Pages 709-709","sourcePage":692,"sourcePageEnd":692},{"id":"physiology-24-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 65-year-old male patient with a history of shortness of breath on exertion presents to the OPD. During the examination, it is noted that his ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratio varies significantly in different regions of the lungs. What are the expected partial pressures of oxygen (pO2) and carbon dioxide (pCO2■■) at the apex, middle, and base of the lungs in the upright position?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the apex: pO2 is lowest and pCO2■■ is highest."},{"letter":"B","text":"At the base: pO2■■ is highest and pCO2 is lowest."},{"letter":"C","text":"At the middle: pO2■■ is highest and pCO2■■ is lowest."},{"letter":"D","text":"At the base: pO2■■ is lowest and pCO2 is highest."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) At the base: pO2■■ is lowest and pCO2■■ is highest.","explanation":"• The base of the lungs has a low V/Q ratio and ventilation is drastically less in comparison to perfusion, so less oxygen is going to enter the alveoli and will be easily delivered to the venous blood. Thus, remaining oxygen at the level of basal alveoli is the lowest (PO2= 89 mm Hg). • Also, carbon dioxide is very high at the level of basal alveoli because of the huge amount of venous blood and the exit of carbon dioxide into alveoli is incomplete. Therefore, the PCO2 is the highest at the base (PCO2= 42 mm Hg). Region of Lungs V/Q Ratio PO2 (mm Hg) PCO2 (mm Hg) pH of blood Apex Highest (3.3) Highest (132) Lowest (28) 7.51 (slightly alkaline) Middle Moderate (0.8) 100-104 40 7.4 Base Lowest (0.6) Lowest (89) Highest (42) 7.39 (slightly acidic)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 692-693; Solution Pages 709-710","sourcePage":692,"sourcePageEnd":693},{"id":"physiology-24-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 45-year-old female patient is evaluated for differences in ventilation-perfusion (V/Q) ratios in various positions. In the upright position, the V/Q ratios at the apex, middle, and base of the lungs differ due to gravitational effects. Which of the following statements correctly describes the V/Q ratio at these lung regions in the upright vs. supine position?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In the upright position, the V/Q ratio is highest at the base of the lungs."},{"letter":"B","text":"In the upright position, the V/Q ratio is highest at the apex of the lungs."},{"letter":"C","text":"In the supine position, the V/Q ratio difference between the apex and base is more pronounced than in the upright position."},{"letter":"D","text":"In the supine position, the V/Q ratio is highest at the middle of the lungs."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) In the upright position, the V/Q ratio is highest at the apex of the lungs.","explanation":"• Both ventilation (V) and perfusion (Q) are higher at the base than at the apex , in upright position. • However, the relative change in blood flow from the apex to the base is greater than the relative change in ventilation, so the ventilation/perfusion (V/Q) ratio is low at the base and high at the apex . • In supine position, the apex to base differences in V and Q are negligible .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 693-693; Solution Pages 710-710","sourcePage":693,"sourcePageEnd":693},{"id":"physiology-24-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 55-year-old male patient presents with shortness of breath and fatigue. His respiratory rate is 16 breaths per minute, and his tidal volume is 400 mL. The dead space is estimated to be 150 mL. Which of the following statements about ventilation and perfusion is/are correct? 1) Pulmonary ventilation refers to the amount of air entering the lungs in a minute, while alveolar ventilation is the volume of air reaching the alveolar zone for gas exchange. 2) Pulmonary ventilation for this patient is approximately 6.4L/min. 3) Alveolar ventilation for this patient is approximately 4L/min. 4) Perfusion is the blood flow at the level of the alveoli which is approximately equal to cardiac output from the right ventricle.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"1, 2 and 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"1, 2, 3 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"1, 3 and 4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) 1, 2, 3 and 4 Explanation: Statement Des Explanation cript ion 1) Pulmonary ventilation refers to Corr Pulmonary ventilation = TV x RR; the amount of air entering the lungs ect Alveolar ventilation = (TV - Dead in a minute, while alveolar Space) x RR. ventilation is the volume of air reaching the alveolar zone for gas exchange. 2) Pulmonary ventilation for this Corr Pulmonary ventilation = 400 mL x patient is approximately 6.4 L/min. ect 16 = 6.4 L/min. 3) Alveolar ventilation for this patient Corr Alveolar ventilation = (400 mL - 150 is approximately 4 L/min. ect mL) x 16 = 4.0 L/min. 4) Perfusion is the blood flow at the Corr Perfusion is typically equal to the level of the alveoli which is ect cardiac output of the right ventricle, approximately equal to cardiac approximately 5.4 L/min. output from the right ventricle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 693-694; Solution Pages 710-711","sourcePage":693,"sourcePageEnd":694},{"id":"physiology-24-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which statement correctly describes normal inspiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Diaphragm relaxation leads to decreased intrapleural pressure, allowing air to enter the lungs."},{"letter":"B","text":"Diaphragm contraction creates negative intrapleural pressure, causing air to enter the lungs."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased intra-alveolar pressure from diaphragm contraction draws air into the lungs."},{"letter":"D","text":"External intercostal muscle relaxation decreases intrapleural pressure, expanding the alveoli."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Diaphragm contraction creates negative intrapleural pressure, causing air to enter the lungs.","explanation":"Mechanism of Normal Respiration: Inspiration • The diaphragm, which contracts during inspiration and contributes to 75% of the inspiratory effort, and the external intercostal muscles, account for 25% of the inspiratory effort. • During normal inspiration, the diaphragm contracts and moves downward. • Inspiration results in an increase in intrapleural volume, leading to a decrease in intrapleural pressure from -2.5 mm Hg to -6 mm Hg. This negative intrapleural pressure is the force that expands the alveoli. Greater negative intrapleural pressure causes the alveoli to stretch. • As alveolar volume increases, intra-alveolar pressure drops from 0 to -1 mm Hg. • This pressure drop below atmospheric levels draws air into the lungs until intra-alveolar pressure reaches equilibrium at 0 mm Hg which marks the end of air entry, concluding inspiration. • At the end of inspiration, the alveoli are expanded, and intra-alveolar pressure is 0 mm Hg. Expiration • Expiration is a passive process that occurs when the muscles contracted during inspiration relax. • As the diaphragm relaxes, it moves upward, compressing the alveolar space. • This compression reduces the volume of the alveoli. • As a result, intra-alveolar pressure increases to +1 mmHg, which is higher than atmospheric pressure, pushing air out of the lungs. • By the end of expiration, intra-alveolar pressure returns to equilibrium at 0 mmHg. Option A) Diaphragm relaxation would cause an increase in intra-alveolar pressure, not a decrease in intrapleural pressure. Option C) Diaphragm contraction decreases intra-alveolar pressure (making it negative relative to atmospheric pressure), which allows air to flow into the lungs. Option D) During normal inspiration, the external intercostal muscles contract, not relax. Their contraction along with diaphragm contraction, helps decrease intrapleural pressure and expand the alveoli.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 694-694; Solution Pages 711-712","sourcePage":694,"sourcePageEnd":694},{"id":"physiology-24-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 35-year-old male presents with severe shortness of breath and chest pain after being involved in a motor vehicle accident. On examination, he is tachycardic and hypotensive, with decreased breath sounds on the right side and tracheal deviation to the left. Which of the following pressure changes most accurately explains the underlying mechanism of his condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased intrapulmonary pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased intrapleural pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased intrapleural pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased intrapulmonary pressure"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Increased intrapleural pressure","explanation":"Based on the provided scenario, the patient most likely has tension pneumothorax , which occurs following a laceration to the lung. Intrapleural Pressure/Intrathoracic Pressure • The intrapleural pressure is typically -2.5 mmHg or -5 cm H■O, which helps keep the alveoli open. • Alveoli tend to collapse unless maintained by a stretching force from inside or negative pressure from outside. • Intrapleural fluid is secreted by capillaries in the parietal pleura and absorbed by lymphatics in the visceral pleura. The net force pushing fluid out of capillaries is 9 cm H■O, and the absorption pressure by lymphatics is 10 cm H■O. This creates a net negative pressure in the intrapleural space, maintained by lymphatic drainage. • The pressure in the alveoli at rest is equal to atmospheric pressure (760 mmHg), and this intra-alveolar pressure is considered 0 mmHg relative to atmospheric pressure. • Intrapleural pressure is -2.5 mmHg relative to atmospheric pressure, meaning it is below the atmospheric pressure by 2.5 mmHg. Changes in Tension Pneumothorax: • Some injuries create a one-way valve effect at the site of the damaged visceral pleura. • During inspiration, air enters the pleural space, but the valve closes during expiration, trapping the air inside. This repetitive trapping of air with each breath leads to tension pneumothorax. • As air continues to accumulate in the pleural space, t he intrapleural pressure increases , becoming positive relative to atmospheric pressure (greater than 760 mmHg). This increased pressure compresses the alveoli, which are already prone to collapse. • The result is a significant collapse of the lung and a shift of the mediastinum. In contrast, a simple pneumothorax involves air entering the pleural space without the one-way valve effect, leading to a collapse of the lung without the progressive increase in intrapleural pressure or mediastinal shift seen in tension pneumothorax. Option A) Increased intrapulmonary pressure generally refers to pressure within the airways and alveoli during conditions like forced expiration or positive-pressure ventilation. This pressure would lead to barotrauma or air trapping. Option C) Decreased intrapleural pressure does not cause the symptoms observed in tension pneumothorax. Option D) Tension pneumothorax is characterized by increased intrapleural pressure due to trapped air, not decreased intrapulmonary pressure or fluid accumulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 694-694; Solution Pages 712-713","sourcePage":694,"sourcePageEnd":694},{"id":"physiology-24-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 35-year-old male holds his breath at the end of normal expiration. Which of the following accurately describes the condition of his lungs at this moment?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The alveolar pressure is higher than the atmospheric pressure."},{"letter":"B","text":"The intrapleural pressure is higher than the atmospheric pressure."},{"letter":"C","text":"The functional residual capacity is at its minimum."},{"letter":"D","text":"The elastic recoil forces of the lungs and chest wall are in equilibrium."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) The elastic recoil forces of the lungs and chest wall are in equilibrium.","explanation":"Resting Condition of the Lungs: • At the end of a normal exhalation, the lungs are in a state called Functional Residual Capacity (FRC). • FRC comprises the Residual Volume (RV) and the Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV). In this state, the respiratory muscles are relaxed. • The elastic recoil forces of the lungs, which pull inward, and the chest wall, which pulls outward, are balanced. • This equilibrium creates a negative intrapleural pressure, helping to keep the lungs partially inflated and preventing the alveoli from collapsing. At the end of normal expiration, alveolar pressure is equal to atmospheric pressure, not higher. This equilibrium allows the air inside the lungs to stay at rest without any flow in or out. Option A) The intrapleural pressure is actually lower than the atmospheric pressure. This negative pressure is crucial for keeping the lungs partially inflated and preventing collapse. ( Option B) Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) is not at its minimum at the end of normal expiration. FRC is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a normal exhalation and represents a resting volume where no additional effort is required by the respiratory muscles. ( Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 694-695; Solution Pages 714-714","sourcePage":694,"sourcePageEnd":695},{"id":"physiology-24-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 55-year-old woman with a history of asthma presents to the emergency department with severe dyspnea. On examination, she is using accessory muscles to breathe and her oxygen saturation is reduced. Arterial blood gas analysis shows a high level of carbon dioxide (PaCO■) despite increased ventilation. A bedside pulmonary function test reveals that her physiological dead space has increased compared to her last visit. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for the increase in physiological dead space in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased alveolar dead space due to poorly ventilated but well-perfused alveoli, leading to reduced effective gas exchange."},{"letter":"B","text":"Anatomical dead space has increased due to an obstruction in the conducting airways, causing less air to reach the alveoli."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased alveolar dead space due to poorly perfused but well-ventilated alveoli, reducing the amount of air available for gas exchange."},{"letter":"D","text":"A reduction in anatomical dead space due to inflammation and narrowing of the airways, leading to a decrease in overall physiological dead space"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increased alveolar dead space due to poorly perfused but well-ventilated alveoli, reducing the amount of air available for gas exchange.","explanation":"This option best explains the increase in physiological dead space by noting that elevated alveolar dead space (poor perfusion of ventilated alveoli) would lead to ineffective gas exchange, thereby increasing the physiological dead space. Alveolar Dead Space • Definition : Alveolar dead space is the portion of the air in the alveoli that is ventilated but not perfused with blood, meaning it does not participate in gas exchange. • This occurs when certain alveoli receive air but lack adequate blood flow. Conditions such as pulmonary embolism can cause this imbalance. • The presence of alveolar dead space results in inefficient gas exchange, as some of the air that reaches the alveoli cannot be used to oxygenate blood or remove carbon dioxide. Physiological Dead Space • Definition : Physiological dead space is the total volume of air in the respiratory system that does not participate in gas exchange. It includes both anatomical and alveolar dead space. • Components : Anatomical Dead Space : It is the air in the conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, etc.) where no gas exchange occurs. It is typically about 150 mL in a healthy adult. Alveolar Dead Space : This is the air in alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused. • Anatomical Dead Space : It is the air in the conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, etc.) where no gas exchange occurs. It is typically about 150 mL in a healthy adult. • Alveolar Dead Space : This is the air in alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused. • Significance : An increase in physiological dead space means that a greater portion of the inhaled air is not being used effectively for gas exchange. This can result from increases in either anatomical or alveolar dead space. • Anatomical Dead Space : It is the air in the conducting airways (trachea, bronchi, etc.) where no gas exchange occurs. It is typically about 150 mL in a healthy adult. • Alveolar Dead Space : This is the air in alveoli that are ventilated but not perfused. In this case, alveolar dead space would not be increased; instead, the problem would be related to low ventilation-perfusion ratio. The scenario described actually involves increased alveolar dead space due to well-ventilated but poorly perfused alveoli. ( Option A) An increase in anatomical dead space would result from obstructions or increased airway size, not from the alveolar dead space problem described. In this patient, the issue is with the alveoli themselves, not the conducting airways. ( Option B) The patient’s problem is with an increase in physiological dead space, which is primarily due to an increase in alveolar dead space rather than anatomical dead space. Inflammation and narrowing of airways are not consistent with the scenario of increased physiological dead space. ( Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 695-695; Solution Pages 714-715","sourcePage":695,"sourcePageEnd":695},{"id":"physiology-24-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":19,"question":"In the Nitrogen Washout Method for measuring lung volumes, what does the phase III observed in the graph represent?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The volume of air in the conducting zone."},{"letter":"B","text":"The mixed air from the conducting zone and alveoli."},{"letter":"C","text":"The pure alveolar air coming from the alveolar region."},{"letter":"D","text":"The closing volume of the lungs."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The pure alveolar air coming from the alveolar region.","explanation":"Nitrogen washout method is used to calculate the volume of anatomical dead space and for measuring the closing volume or closing capacity of the lung. The procedure of Nitrogen Washout Method: • Inhalation Phase: The subject inhales pure oxygen from a cylinder, which replaces the normal atmospheric air in the conducting zone and enters the alveoli. By the end of inhalation, the conducting zone is filled with pure oxygen, while the alveoli have a mix of pure oxygen and residual alveolar air. • The subject inhales pure oxygen from a cylinder, which replaces the normal atmospheric air in the conducting zone and enters the alveoli. • By the end of inhalation, the conducting zone is filled with pure oxygen, while the alveoli have a mix of pure oxygen and residual alveolar air. • Expiration Phase: As the subject exhales, the air passing through the nitrogen meter initially contains pure oxygen from the conducting zone, resulting in a zero nitrogen concentration reading. As exhalation progresses, air from the alveolar region—containing a constant nitrogen concentration—begins to mix with the air from the conducting zone. • As the subject exhales, the air passing through the nitrogen meter initially contains pure oxygen from the conducting zone, resulting in a zero nitrogen concentration reading. • As exhalation progresses, air from the alveolar region—containing a constant nitrogen concentration—begins to mix with the air from the conducting zone. • Graph of Nitrogen washout method: The resulting graph of nitrogen concentration versus lung volume will show: An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . This is followed by a sudden increase in nitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D). • The resulting graph of nitrogen concentration versus lung volume will show: An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . This is followed by a sudden increase in nitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D). • An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) • A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) • A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . • This is followed by a sudden increase in nitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D). • The subject inhales pure oxygen from a cylinder, which replaces the normal atmospheric air in the conducting zone and enters the alveoli. • By the end of inhalation, the conducting zone is filled with pure oxygen, while the alveoli have a mix of pure oxygen and residual alveolar air. • As the subject exhales, the air passing through the nitrogen meter initially contains pure oxygen from the conducting zone, resulting in a zero nitrogen concentration reading. • As exhalation progresses, air from the alveolar region—containing a constant nitrogen concentration—begins to mix with the air from the conducting zone. • The resulting graph of nitrogen concentration versus lung volume will show: An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . This is followed by a sudden increase innitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D). • An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) • A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) • A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . • This is followed by a sudden increase in nitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D). • An initial zero reading (representing anatomical dead space) (Option A) • A gradual increase as alveolar air mixes with the conducting zone air (Option B) • A plateau at a fixed nitrogen concentration, which indicates the pure alveolar air (Option C) . • This is followed by a sudden increase in nitrogen concentration known as the Closing Volume of the Lungs (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 695-696; Solution Pages 716-718","sourcePage":695,"sourcePageEnd":696},{"id":"physiology-24-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 45-year-old woman undergoes elective surgery under general anesthesia. During the procedure, her respiratory parameters are closely monitored. Which of the following statements best explains how general anesthesia typically affects anatomical dead space?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"General anesthesia increases alveolar ventilation by reducing dead space."},{"letter":"B","text":"General anesthesia decreases anatomical dead space by promoting bronchoconstriction."},{"letter":"C","text":"General anesthesia does not affect anatomical dead space but increases alveolar dead space."},{"letter":"D","text":"General anesthesia increases anatomical dead space due to the loss of bronchial tone, leading to bronchial dilation."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) General anesthesia increases anatomical dead space due to the loss of bronchial tone, leading to bronchial dilation.","explanation":"Anatomical Dead Space Volume is the volume of air that occupies the conducting zone of the airways and does not participate in the gaseous exchange. Key Points: • Any kind of bronchodilation experienced by a person will increase the anatomical dead space volume because bronchus and bronchioles will be dilated, which will entrap more dead volume. • If general anesthesia is given to a person, there will be a loss of bronchial tone, and dilation will be more, which will be more anatomical dead space. (Option A). • In inspiration the length of the anatomical dead space zone is increasing, hence dead space volume will also increase. • Extension of the neck and protrusion of the jaw, all done during anesthesia will result in increased anatomical dead space.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 696-696; Solution Pages 718-719","sourcePage":696,"sourcePageEnd":696},{"id":"physiology-24-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 60-year-old man with a history of smoking presents with a persistent cough and occasional wheezing. During a physical examination, you notice that the patient has increased breath sounds over the central airways. In which part of the respiratory system is the velocity of airflow is high, leading to turbulent airflow in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alveoli"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trachea"},{"letter":"C","text":"Terminal bronchioles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Respiratory bronchioles"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Trachea","explanation":"• Velocity is inversely proportional to cross-sectional area. In the respiratory system, consider the cross-sectional area of di■erent segments like a cross-section of the trachea, both the bronchus and bronchioles, etc, • The total cross-sectional area is minimum at division number 3. Hence velocity is highest. • Velocity will be maximum at division number 3 and then velocity progressively decreases towards the alveoli, and minimum velocity is seen at the level of alveoli. Alveoli(Option A): The alveoli have the largest total cross-sectional area , leading to minimal airflow velocity and predominantly laminar flow. Terminal bronchioles(Option C): Terminal bronchioles have a larger cumulative cross-sectional area than the trachea, leading to lower airflow velocity and smoother flow Respiratory bronchioles (Option D): Respiratory bronchioles, being part of the gas exchange zone, have an even greater cross-sectional area , causing minimal velocity and mostly laminar flow.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 696-697; Solution Pages 719-720","sourcePage":696,"sourcePageEnd":697},{"id":"physiology-24-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 55-year-old male with a history of chronic bronchitis presents with increased difficulty in breathing. Based on Poiseuille's law and the distribution of airway resistance, which part of the respiratory system contributes the most to the total resistance to airflow?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alveolar ducts"},{"letter":"B","text":"Small airways (bronchioles)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medium to large-size bronchi (lobar and segmental bronchi)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Upper airways (pharynx and larynx)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Answer : C) Medium to large-size bronchi (lobar and segmental bronchi) Explanation: The medium to large-size bronchi, specifically the lobar and segmental bronchi, are the site of maximum resistance to airflow. This is because these bronchi have a relatively larger radius but are fewer in number, resulting in higher individual resistance. • According to Poiseuille's law, resistance to airflow (R) is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the radius (r) of the airway. • Resistance: R = 8ηL/πr 4 • Therefore, small changes in the radius result in significant changes in resistance. Despite the smaller radius of the bronchioles, their parallel arrangement results in a lower total resistance. The upper airways (pharynx and larynx) also contribute significantly to airflow resistance but less than medium to large-sized bronchi.( Option D) Only about 20% of the resistance occurs at the level of the small airways. ( Option B) Alveoli have a large cross-sectional area which significantly reduces the resistance according to Poiseuille’s law. Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 697-697; Solution Pages 720-720","sourcePage":697,"sourcePageEnd":697},{"id":"physiology-24-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A premature infant presents with severe respiratory distress shortly after birth. The neonate is diagnosed with neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS). Which type of alveolar cell is most likely deficient in this condition, leading to inadequate surfactant production?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type I pneumocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type II pneumocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Alveolar macrophages"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endothelial cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Type II pneumocytes","explanation":"Neonatal respiratory distress syndrome (NRDS) is primarily caused by a deficiency in surfactant, a substance crucial for reducing surface tension in the alveoli and preventing their collapse during exhalation. Surfactant is produced by Type II pneumocytes. • Type I pneumocytes (Option A) are thin, flat cells that cover the majority of the alveolar surface and are involved in gas exchange. • Alveolar macrophages (Option C) are immune cells that ingest and remove debris and pathogens. • Endothelial cells (Option D) line the capillaries surrounding the alveoli and are involved in the exchange of gases between the alveoli and blood.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 697-697; Solution Pages 720-721","sourcePage":697,"sourcePageEnd":697},{"id":"physiology-24-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":24,"question":"All of the following statements about Clara cells of the respiratory tract are true except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They are found in the bronchioles."},{"letter":"B","text":"They stabilise surfactant proteins"},{"letter":"C","text":"They have cilia."},{"letter":"D","text":"They detoxify harmful substances."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) They have cilia.","explanation":"• Clara cells, also known as club cells, are non-ciliated, dome-shaped cells found in the bronchioles of the respiratory tract. (Option A ruled out) • They play a crucial role in maintaining the integrity of the bronchiolar epithelium by stabilising surfactant proteins that reduce surface tension. (Option B ruled out) • They are involved in detoxifying harmful substances through their enzymatic activity. (Option D ruled out) • They are not ciliated, differentiating them from other epithelial cells in the respiratory tract that do have cilia, such as the pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelial cells found in the trachea and larger bronchi.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 698-698; Solution Pages 721-721","sourcePage":698,"sourcePageEnd":698},{"id":"physiology-24-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-24","chapter":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topic":"Functional Anatomy, Mechanics, Ventilation-Perfusion, Compliance of RS","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A biopsy from a segment of the respiratory tree reveals pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells. From which part of the respiratory tract was this sample most likely taken?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alveoli"},{"letter":"B","text":"Terminal bronchioles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trachea"},{"letter":"D","text":"Respiratory bronchioles"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Trachea Explanation: The epithelial lining of trachea and large bronchi are composed of pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. • Ciliated epithelium is present at the level of the trachea, bronchus, and bronchioles. • Cartilaginous rings are present up to the bronchi. • Smooth muscles are present up to respiratory bronchioles (bronchial tone). (Option D ruled out) • Epithelial lining Trachea and Large Bronchi: Pseudostrati■ed columnar ciliated epithelium. Bronchioles: Cuboidal-ciliated epithelium. (Option B ruled out) Alveoli: Simple squamous epithelium. (Option A ruled out) • Trachea and Large Bronchi: Pseudostrati■ed columnar ciliated epithelium. • Bronchioles: Cuboidal-ciliated epithelium. (Option B ruled out) • Alveoli: Simple squamous epithelium. (Option A ruled out) • Trachea and Large Bronchi: Pseudostrati■ed columnar ciliated epithelium. • Bronchioles: Cuboidal-ciliated epithelium. (Option B ruled out) • Alveoli: Simple squamous epithelium. (Option A ruled out)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 698-698; Solution Pages 721-722","sourcePage":698,"sourcePageEnd":698},{"id":"physiology-25-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following parameters belong to static lung volumes and capacities? Maximum Voluntary Ventilation Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) Dead space Residual Volume (RV) Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second (FEV1)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 3, and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"2, 3, and 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"1, 2, and 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"2, 4, and 5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 2, 3, and 4 are correct","explanation":"Aspect Static Lung Volumes & Capacities Dynamic Lung Volumes & Capacities Measurem Lung volume without the recording Lung volume to time ent Focus of time Units of Measured in millilitres (ml) or litres Measured in millilitres per second Measurem (ml/second) ent Examples Volume: Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) Tidal Volume (TV) Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV) second (FEV1) Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) FEV1/FVC Ratio Residual Volume (RV) Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR) Anatomical Dead Space Inspiratory Flow Rate Capacity: Expiratory Flow Rate Total Lung Capacity (TLC) Vital Capacity (VC) Inspiratory Capacity (IC) Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) Clinical Assess the maximum air volume the Assess the rate of airflow and lung Significanc lungs can hold and the different function over time. e capacities during various breathing Diagnosis of obstructive lung phases. diseases such as asthma and Diagnosis of restrictive lung COPD diseases such as fibrosis and sarcoidosis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 723-723; Solution Pages 729-730","sourcePage":723,"sourcePageEnd":723},{"id":"physiology-25-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following parameters cannot be measured by spirometry?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vital capacity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Residual volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inspiratory reserve volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tidal volume"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Residual volume","explanation":"Respiratory parameters that cannot be measured by spirometry: Parameter Method for Measurement Residual Volume (RV) Body Plethysmography (Best). Gas Dilution (Helium dilution Method). Nitrogen Washout Functional Residual Capacity (FRC) Total Lung Capacity (TLC) Body Plethysmography. Gas Dilution (Helium Dilution Method) Alveolar Dead Space Single-Breath Tests. Imaging Studies. Diffusing Capacity (DLCO) DLCO Test (Carbon Monoxide Diffusion Test)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 723-723; Solution Pages 730-731","sourcePage":723,"sourcePageEnd":723},{"id":"physiology-25-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following formulas correctly represents the Vital Capacity (VC) of the lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"TV + IRV + ERV + RV"},{"letter":"B","text":"TV + IRV + ERV"},{"letter":"C","text":"TV + IRV"},{"letter":"D","text":"ERV + RV"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) TV + IRV + ERV","explanation":"Vital Capacity (VC) is the maximum amount of air that can be exhaled after a maximum inhalation and is calculated by summing Tidal Volume (TV), Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV), and Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV). VC = TV + IRV + ERV Lung Volume/Capacity Description Typical Volum Formula e (mL) Tidal Volume (TV) Amount of air inhaled or 500 - exhaled during normal breath Inspiratory Reserve Additional air that can be 3000 - Volume (IRV) inhaled after a normal inhalation Expiratory Reserve Additional air that can be 1200 - Volume (ERV) exhaled after a normal exhalation Residual Volume (RV) Air remaining in the lungs 1200 - after a maximal exhalation Anatomical Dead Space Volume of air in 150 - conducting airways where no gas exchange occurs Total Lung Capacity Maximum amount of air 6000 TLC = TV + IRV + (TLC) the lungs can hold ERV + RV Vital Capacity (VC) Maximum air exhaled 4800 VC = TV + IRV + after a maximal inhalation ERV Inspiratory Capacity (IC) Maximum air inhaled after 3500 IC = TV + IRV a normal exhalation Functional Residual Air remaining in the lungs 2400 FRC = ERV + RV Capacity (FRC) after a normal exhalation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 723-723; Solution Pages 731-731","sourcePage":723,"sourcePageEnd":723},{"id":"physiology-25-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":4,"question":"What is the true statement regarding Closing Volume (CV)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"CV is the lung volume at which large airways begin to close during inspiration."},{"letter":"B","text":"CV measurement is typically performed using a Multi-Breath Helium Dilution Test."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased CV is seen in conditions such as ageing, obstructive lung diseases, obesity, and smoking."},{"letter":"D","text":"A normal CV indicates small airway dysfunction and abnormal lung mechanics."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increased CV is seen in conditions such as ageing, obstructive lung diseases, obesity, and smoking.","explanation":"Closing Volume (CV) Definition: • Closing Volume (CV) is the lung volume at which small airways begin to close during expiration, particularly in the dependent parts of the lungs. (Option A ruled out) • CV = Closing capacity - Residual volume. Mechanism: • During expiration, as lung volume decreases, pleural pressure becomes less negative. • At the closing volume, airway pressure drops below pleural pressure, causing small airways to close. Measurement: • Single-Breath Nitrogen Washout Test: This test measures CV by observing the washout of nitrogen from the lungs during a single breath. Clinical Implications: Increased CV: Indicates small airway dysfunction and can be seen in: (Option D ruled out) • Ageing: Decreased elastic recoil and increased lung compliance. • Obstructive Lung Diseases: COPD and asthma. • Obesity and Pregnancy: Reduced lung volumes and diaphragmatic changes. • Smoking: Indicates early small airway dysfunction. Closing Capacity (CC): • Closing Capacity (CC) is the lung volume at which the basal alveoli begin to close off during expiration. • It is the sum of the closing and residual volumes (CC = CV + RV). • During forceful expiration, the volume at which only the basal alveoli are closed off is known as closing capacity. • Further expiration will eventually lead to all alveoli being closed off, reaching the residual volume. • Ageing: Functional residual capacity (FRC) is greater than CC in a normal adult. By around 66 years of age, FRC is nearly equal to CC, indicating that CC increases gradually with age. • Pathological Implications: If CC significantly exceeds FRC, it indicates highly collapsible lung diseases, such as hyaline membrane disease. Measurement : Single-Breath Nitrogen Washout Method (Fowler's) measures CC and anatomical dead space by observing nitrogen washout during a single breath. (Option B ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 724-724; Solution Pages 732-733","sourcePage":724,"sourcePageEnd":724},{"id":"physiology-25-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 60-year-old man with a history of smoking presents with shortness of breath. During spirometry, dynamic compression of the airways is observed during forceful expiration. Which of the following statements best describes the point at which dynamic compression occurs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Early expiration, high lung volume."},{"letter":"B","text":"Mid expiration, FRC reached."},{"letter":"C","text":"Late expiration, pleural pressure > airway pressure."},{"letter":"D","text":"End expiration, near RV."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Dynamic compression of the airways occurs primarily during the late phase of forceful expiration when intrathoracic pressure (pleural pressure) exceeds airway pressure. This leads to airway narrowing or collapse, particularly in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), with increased resistance and decreased elastic recoil. Mechanism: • During forceful expiration, elastic tissues in the alveoli help in passive expiration by recoiling inward. As air moves through the bronchi and bronchioles, airway resistance causes the pressure to drop. • An external force from the chest wall also acts on the bronchi and bronchioles. At the end of expiration, the pressure inside the bronchioles equals the external pressure, known as the equal pressure point. Dynamic compression does not occur during the early expiration phase when the lung volume is still relatively high; the surrounding lung tissue generally supports the airways well. (Option A) Dynamic compression is not associated with functional residual capacity (FRC) but occurs later when airway and pleural pressures interact. (Option B) When reaching the residual volume (RV) by the end of expiration, dynamic compression is less of a concern, as the airflow has largely ceased. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 724-724; Solution Pages 733-734","sourcePage":724,"sourcePageEnd":724},{"id":"physiology-25-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A healthy 35-year-old male undergoes pulmonary function tests as part of a routine health check-up. Which of the following findings is most likely to be observed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tidal volume 400 ml"},{"letter":"B","text":"FEV 1 /FVC 0.65"},{"letter":"C","text":"Residual volume 1200 ml"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vital capacity 3500 ml"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Residual volume 1200 ml","explanation":"A residual volume of 1200 ml is normal for a healthy 35-year-old male. Residual volume remains stable unless there is a pathological condition affecting lung function, and it helps differentiate between obstructive and restrictive lung diseases: • Obstructive lung diseases (e.g., COPD) increase residual volume due to air trapping. • Restrictive lung diseases (e.g., pulmonary fibrosis) decrease residual volume due to reduced lung compliance. Additionally, residual volume can differentiate between types of restrictive lung diseases: • Parenchymal restrictive lung disease (e.g., pulmonary fibrosis) shows highly reduced residual volume due to stiff and scarred lung tissue. • Extra-parenchymal restrictive lung disease (e.g., neuromuscular diseases, chest wall deformities) may show normal or slightly reduced residual volume, as the issue is outside the lung tissue, affecting breathing mechanics rather than the lung parenchyma. Key parameters of pulmonary function tests: Spirometry Forced Vital Capacity (FVC) Total air exhaled forcefully after deep breath. Approximately 4-6 liters (depending on age, sex, height, and weight) Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 Second (FEV 1 ) Air exhaled in the first second of the FVC manoeuvre. Approximately 80% of FVC FEV 1 /FVC Ratio Percentage of FVC exhaled in the first second. Approximately 70-80% Peak Expiratory Flow (PEF) Maximum speed of expiration. Varies by age, sex, and height (e.g., 400-600 L/min for adults) Lung Volumes Total Lung Capacity (TLC) The total volume of air in the lungs after maximal inhalation Approximately 6-8 liters Residual Volume (RV) Air remaining in the lungs after maximal exhalation Approximately 1.2-1.5 litres Vital Capacity (VC) Air that can be exhaled after maximal inhalation (FVC minus RV) Approximately 4-5 liters Inspiratory Reserve Volume (IRV) Additional air inhaled after normal inhalation Approximately 2-3 liters Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) Additional air exhaled after normal exhalation Approximately 1-1.5 liters Diffusing Capacity (DLCO) Lung’s ability to transfer gases (e.g., CO) Approximately 20-30 mL/min/mmHg The other options (A, B, and D) are also within normal ranges for healthy adults but are less specific to routine findings in pulmonary function tests compared to residual volume.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 724-724; Solution Pages 734-735","sourcePage":724,"sourcePageEnd":724},{"id":"physiology-25-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 65-year-old male, lifelong smoker, presents with chronic shortness of breath and a persistent cough. Imaging studies confirm hyperinflation and destruction of alveolar walls. He was diagnosed with emphysema. What are the expected results of his Pulmonary Function Test?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased Residual Volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased Total Lung Capacity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased FEV/FVC"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both A and B"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Both A and B","explanation":"Emphysema is an obstructive lung disease. Increased Residual Volume and Total Lung Capacity are the findings in obstructive lung disease. Features of Obstructive Diseases: Emphysema: Examples of obstructive lung disease: • Asthma • COPD (Chronic bronchitis and emphysema) • Bronchiectasis • Bronchiolitis • Cystic fibrosis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 725-725; Solution Pages 735-736","sourcePage":725,"sourcePageEnd":725},{"id":"physiology-25-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following is false on the findings of restrictive lung disease?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased TLC"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased VC"},{"letter":"C","text":"Preservation of Forced Expiratory Flow Rates"},{"letter":"D","text":"All are true"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All are true","explanation":"Definition Lung disorders are characterised by a reduced distensibility of the lungs, compromising lung expansion, and, in turn, reduced lung volumes Features Decreased TLC (Total Lung Capacity) Decreased VC (Vital Capacity) Decreased RV (Residual Volume) Preservation of Forced Expiratory Flow Rates. Pathology 1. Intrinsic Conditions: Destruction of Distal Lung Parenchyma Inflammatory Changes in the Alveolar Interstitium Interstitial Lung Diseases Alterations from Within the Alveolus(oedema or haemorrhage) 2. Extrinsic Conditions: Limitations in Neuromuscular Function Chest Wall Movement Impairments (pleural disorders, obesity, costosternal or costovertebral fusion, thoracic vertebral fusion or deviation) Parenchymal Restrictive Pulmonary fibrosis Dysfunction Pneumoconiosis Radiation-induced interstitial lung disease Extra-parenchymal 1. Neuromuscular Diseases: Restrictive Dysfunction Myasthenia gravis Paralysis of diaphragm Muscular dystrophy 2. Chest Wall Diseases Kyphoscoliosis Obesity Ankylosing spondylitis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 725-725; Solution Pages 736-737","sourcePage":725,"sourcePageEnd":725},{"id":"physiology-25-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is false about Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Maximum velocity of flow in litres per minute with air being forced out of the lungs"},{"letter":"B","text":"Recorded by using the peak-flow meter"},{"letter":"C","text":"The normal value is 400 to 600 L/min"},{"letter":"D","text":"All are true"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All are true","explanation":"• Peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR) is the maximum flow velocity in litres per minute with air being forced out of the lungs.(Option A) • PEFR is recorded by using the peak-flow meter.(Option B) • The normal value of PEFR is 400 to 600 L/min or 6 to 10 L/s.(Option C) • PEFR is an index of patency of airways.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 725-725; Solution Pages 737-737","sourcePage":725,"sourcePageEnd":725},{"id":"physiology-25-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is the most sensitive indicator of small airway diseases?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PEFR"},{"letter":"B","text":"FEV1"},{"letter":"C","text":"FEV1/FVC"},{"letter":"D","text":"MMEFR"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) MMEFR","explanation":"Maximum Mid-expiratory Flow Rate (MMEFR): • Maximum flow achieved during the middle third of the total expired volume. • Forced expiratory flow at 25% to 75% of the lung volume. • Indicates the patency of small airways. • More sensitive marker of airway disease (particularly for the obstruction of smaller-sized airways) than traditional parameters like FEV1, FEV1/FVC ratio, or PEFR. PEFR (Option A) – Less sensitive as it reflects large airway function and can remain normal in early small airway disease. FEV1 (Option B) – Commonly used but detects moderate-to-severe airway obstruction , making it less sensitive for early disease. FEV1/FVC Ratio (Option C) – Helps diagnose obstructive lung diseases , but may not detect early small airway dysfunction as well as MMEFR.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 725-726; Solution Pages 737-738","sourcePage":725,"sourcePageEnd":726},{"id":"physiology-25-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":11,"question":"In the image given below what does the yellow curve represent?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Normal lung function"},{"letter":"B","text":"Obstructive lung disease"},{"letter":"C","text":"Restrictive lung disease"},{"letter":"D","text":"Respiratory failure"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Obstructive lung disease","explanation":"This is a Peak Expiratory Air Flow-Lung Volume Curve. Condition Curve Characteristics Restrictive diseases Curve is smaller and shifted to the right (Option C ruled (Constricted lung) out) Peak flow is lower, and the volume is less due to reduced TLC and RV Obstructive diseases Curve is smaller and shifted to the left Lower peak flow Scooped-out appearance during expiration Normal lung function (Option A) – Normal curve would have a smooth, symmetrical peak and descent without a scooped-out appearance or shift in volume. Respiratory failure (Option D) – Respiratory failure is a clinical condition rather than a distinct spirometric pattern, and it can involve both obstructive and restrictive features depending on the cause","sourceRef":"Question Pages 726-726; Solution Pages 738-739","sourcePage":726,"sourcePageEnd":726},{"id":"physiology-25-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A patient underwent pulmonary function testing, and the flow-volume loop is shown below. What does it show?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fixed upper airway obstruction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Variable intra-thoracic obstruction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Normal lung functioning"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Fixed upper airway obstruction","explanation":"Given is a flow-volume loop of a fixed upper airway obstruction. Fixed upper airway obstruction: • Both inspiration and expiration affected. • Examples: Foreign body Scarring Enlarged thyroid gland • Foreign body • Scarring • Enlarged thyroid gland • Foreign body • Scarring • Enlarged thyroid gland The flow-volume loops in variable extrathoracic, variable intrathoracic, and fixed upper airway obstruction:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 726-727; Solution Pages 739-739","sourcePage":726,"sourcePageEnd":727},{"id":"physiology-25-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Analyse the following flow volume loop diagram. Which of the following statements is NOT true regarding this flow volume loop?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The loop demonstrates flatness in the expiratory portion"},{"letter":"B","text":"This loop occurs in variable intrathoracic obstruction"},{"letter":"C","text":"The \"box pattern\" indicates fixed upper airway obstruction."},{"letter":"D","text":"The loop shows a reduction in peak expiratory flow (PEF) due to the obstruction."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The \"box pattern\" indicates fixed airway obstruction.","explanation":"A \"box pattern\" on the flow volume loop, characterised by flattening both inspiratory and expiratory portions, can occur in both fixed and variable intrathoracic obstructions and is not specific to one. Feature Variable Intrathoracic Obstruction Variable Extrathoracic Obstruction Location Within the thoracic cavity (trachea, Outside the thoracic cavity (larynx, bronchi) pharynx) Causes Tracheobronchomalacia, mainstem Laryngeal oedema, vocal cord bronchial obstruction, lower airway dysfunction, tumours in the upper obstructions airway Flow-Volume Flattened expiratory portion (Option Flattened inspiratory portion; Loop Characteris A ruled out) ; reduced peak expiration may remain relatively tics expiratory flow (PEF) (Option D unaffected ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 727-727; Solution Pages 740-740","sourcePage":727,"sourcePageEnd":727},{"id":"physiology-25-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-25","chapter":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topic":"Pulmonary Function Tests","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":14,"question":"The volume of gas expired by a normal adult man during a forced expiration, demonstrating the FEV1 and the FVC. Calculate the FEV1/FVC of this person.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"60%"},{"letter":"B","text":"70%"},{"letter":"C","text":"80%"},{"letter":"D","text":"90%"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 80%","explanation":"FEV1 (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second): Volume of air that can be forcibly exhaled in the first second of expiration. FVC (Forced Vital Capacity): Total volume of air that can be forcibly exhaled after taking a deep inhalation. Here, • FEV1 = 4L • FVC = 5L = ■ x 100 = 80% Interpretation of Values: • Normal Ratio (70%-80% or higher): Indicates normal lung function. • Low Ratio (<70%): Suggests obstructive lung disease. • Normal or High Ratio with Reduced Volumes : Suggests restrictive lung disease. Normal Ratio (70%-80% or higher): Indicates normal lung function. Low Ratio (<70%): Suggests obstructive lung disease.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 728-728; Solution Pages 741-741","sourcePage":728,"sourcePageEnd":728},{"id":"physiology-26-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following formulas gives the oxygen extraction ratio (OER) of a substance?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"(Arterial Concentration+Venous Concentration) / Arterial Concentration"},{"letter":"B","text":"(Arterial Concentration−Venous Concentration) / Arterial Concentration"},{"letter":"C","text":"Venous Concentration / Arterial Concentration"},{"letter":"D","text":"(Venous Concentration−Arterial Concentration) / Venous Concentration"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) (Arterial Concentration−Venous Concentration) / Arterial Concentration","explanation":"The Oxygen Extraction Ratio (OER) is defined as the ratio of oxygen consumption (VO■) to oxygen delivery (DO■), expressed mathematically as: Oxygen Extraction Ratio (OER) = oxygen consumption (VO■) / oxygen delivery (DO■) VO 2 = Arterial Concentration−Venous Concentration DO 2 = Arterial Concentration In a healthy adult, OER is about 25%. During intense physical activity, OER can rise to 70-80%. Factors affecting OER: Factor Key Influences Impact on OER Oxygen Delivery (DO 2 ) Cardiac output, Hemoglobin, SpO■ Decrease leads to increased OER Oxygen Consumption Metabolic demands (exercise, Increase without DO■ increase (VO 2 ) sepsis) elevates OER Blood Flow Vascular diseases, Shock states Altered blood flow dynamics affect OER Physiological Hypoxia, Anemia, Hypercapnia Hypoxia increases OER Conditions Dialysis Hemodynamic changes during Higher OER linked to intradialytic dialysis hypotension Age and Health Status Age-related cardiovascular/respirator Reduced cardiac output and y changes hemoglobin levels impact OER Medications/ Vasodilators, Inotropes, Mechanical Influence blood flow and oxygen Treatments ventilation delivery, affecting OER","sourceRef":"Question Pages 742-742; Solution Pages 752-752","sourcePage":742,"sourcePageEnd":742},{"id":"physiology-26-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following contributes to the higher hematocrit in venous blood than arterial blood?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower Plasma Volume in venous blood"},{"letter":"B","text":"Chloride shift causing red blood cell swelling"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater carbon dioxide content in venous blood"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All of the above","explanation":"Higher hematocrit in venous blood compared to arterial blood can be attributed to several factors: • Fluid Shifting: A small amount of fluid from arterial blood returns to circulation via the lymphatic system, reducing plasma volume in veins and increasing hematocrit. • CO 2 and Bicarbonate: Venous blood has higher CO■ levels, which result in increased bicarbonate formation within red blood cells. The chloride shift facilitates this process, contributing to red blood cell swelling and higher hematocrit. • Venous blood has higher CO■ levels, which result in increased bicarbonate formation within red blood cells. • The chloride shift facilitates this process, contributing to red blood cell swelling and higher hematocrit. • Venous blood has higher CO■ levels, which result in increased bicarbonate formation within red blood cells. • The chloride shift facilitates this process, contributing to red blood cell swelling and higher hematocrit. Chloride shift (Hamburger phenomenon) vs Reverse chloride shift: Feature Chloride shift Reverse chloride shift Location Tissues Lungs Process CO■ enters RBCs, forming H■CO■ CO■ exits RBCs. HCO■■ re-enters → H■ + HCO■■. HCO■■ exits in RBCs in exchange for Cl■ exchange for Cl■ Effect on Chloride influx causes water influx, Chloride efflux balances HCO■■ RBCs swelling RBCs re-entry, CO■ is released Impact on Increases hematocrit due to RBC Primarily facilitates CO■ release, haematocrit swelling indirectly affecting hematocrit","sourceRef":"Question Pages 742-742; Solution Pages 753-753","sourcePage":742,"sourcePageEnd":742},{"id":"physiology-26-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What is the significance of the Haldane effect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oxygenation promotes CO■ release in the lungs."},{"letter":"B","text":"Deoxygenation enhances oxygen uptake in tissues."},{"letter":"C","text":"CO■ binding boosts oxygen release in tissues."},{"letter":"D","text":"Haemoglobin buffers blood pH by binding hydrogen ions."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Oxygenation promotes CO■ release in the lungs.","explanation":"Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 742-742; Solution Pages 753-754","sourcePage":742,"sourcePageEnd":742},{"id":"physiology-26-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which phenomenon explains the release of oxygen from haemoglobin in response to high carbon dioxide concentrations at the tissue level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Haldane Effect"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bohr Effect"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chloride Shift"},{"letter":"D","text":"Root Effect"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Bohr Effect","explanation":"Table rendering failed The Double Bohr Effect is a physiological phenomenon observed in the placenta. This effect involves two distinct Bohr effects occurring simultaneously—one on the fetal side and one on the maternal side . Double bohr effect Details Location Placenta where maternal and fetal circulation meet. Fetal Blood Flow Umbilical arteries carry deoxygenated blood with high CO 2 from the fetus to placenta CO 2 Diffusion CO 2 diffuses from fetal to maternal blood Effect on Fetal Blood CO 2 decreases, blood becomes alkaline Oxygen dissociation curve shifts left Effect on Maternal Blood CO 2 increases, blood becomes acidic Oxygen dissociation curve shifts right Clinical Significance Enhances oxygen transfer from mother to fetus, improving fetal oxygenation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 743-743; Solution Pages 754-755","sourcePage":743,"sourcePageEnd":743},{"id":"physiology-26-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In which form is carbon dioxide predominantly transported in the blood?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dissolved CO 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Carbaminohemoglobin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bicarbonate ions (HCO 3 ■)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Protein bound"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Bicarbonate ions (HCO 3 ■)","explanation":"• Bicarbonate Ions (HCO 3 ■) (70%): CO■ reacts with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates into H■ and HCO■■. HCO■■ moves to plasma, and Cl■ enters red blood cells. This is the main CO■ transport method contributing 70%. • Dissolved CO■ (7%)(Option A ruled out) : A small percentage of CO■ dissolves directly into the plasma. About 2.7 ml/dl of CO■ dissolves in plasma at 45 mmHg, making up 7% of CO■ transport. • Carbaminohemoglobin (23%)(Option B ruled out) : CO■ binds to hemoglobin to form carbaminohemoglobin which is a reversible process, releasing CO■ in the alveoli. • Protein Bound (Option D ruled out): CO■ binds to plasma proteins (mainly albumin). Similar to carbamino formation with hemoglobin. Minor contribution to overall CO■ transport","sourceRef":"Question Pages 743-743; Solution Pages 755-755","sourcePage":743,"sourcePageEnd":743},{"id":"physiology-26-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following solutions is considered best for blood storage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ACD (Acid Citrate Dextrose)"},{"letter":"B","text":"CPDA (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine)"},{"letter":"C","text":"CPD (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose)"},{"letter":"D","text":"EDTA (Ethylenediaminetetraacetic Acid)"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) CPDA (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine)","explanation":"CPDA (Citrate Phosphate Dextrose Adenine) is best for blood storage for the following reasons: Preservation Properties: CPDA-1 is commonly used to store whole blood and red blood cells due to its superior preservation properties: Benefits: The combination of these components in CPDA-1 allows it to preserve blood for up to 35 days, which is longer than other anticoagulants and preservative solutions like ACD, EDTA, and CPD. This makes CPDA-1 an ideal solution for storing blood for medical use.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 743-743; Solution Pages 755-756","sourcePage":743,"sourcePageEnd":743},{"id":"physiology-26-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What is the shape of the oxygen dissociation curve for myoglobin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rectangular Hyperbolic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sigmoidal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Linear"},{"letter":"D","text":"Exponential"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Rectangular Hyperbolic","explanation":"Myoglobin-O2 dissociation curve: dissociation curve\" data-author=\"na\" data-hash=\"551\" data-license=\"na\" data-source=\"na\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"600\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/4MwulaJsCTJ7 LckFdZxW1735978703.png\" width=\"600\" /> • The oxygen dissociation curve for myoglobin is rectangular hyperbolic . • This is because, in the case of myoglobin, one molecule of haemoglobin binds with one molecule of oxygen with high affinity, and complete saturation will occur. • Unlike haemoglobin, which has a sigmoidal curve due to cooperative binding among its four subunits, myoglobin's curve does not exhibit cooperativity. • This means myoglobin can efficiently bind and release oxygen at varying partial pressures, making it well-suited for oxygen storage in muscle tissues.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 743-744; Solution Pages 756-756","sourcePage":743,"sourcePageEnd":744},{"id":"physiology-26-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the Luebering-Rapoport pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It reduces the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen by producing 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG)."},{"letter":"B","text":"It generates NADPH as a key product and decreases the affinity of hemoglobin for oxygen"},{"letter":"C","text":"It functions to increase the ATP yield of glycolysis in red blood cells."},{"letter":"D","text":"It involves the enzyme hexokinase to initiate the pathway."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) It reduces the affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen by producing 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate (2,3-BPG). Explanations: Luebering- Rapoport Pathway • The Luebering-Rapoport pathway produces 2,3-BPG, which binds to haemoglobin and decreases its affinity for oxygen, facilitating oxygen release to tissues. (Option B ruled out) • Bisphosphoglycerate mutase enzyme initiates the Luebering-Rapoport pathway (Option D ruled out) • The balance between bisphosphoglycerate mutase and bisphosphoglycerate phosphatase enzyme determines the amount of 2,3-BPG in blood. • 2,3-BPG causes the right shifting of OHDC by stabilising the lower affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen. • One molecule of haemoglobin binds with one molecule of 2,3-BPG which is a byproduct of the glycolytic pathway • Chronic acidosis maintained for a long time inhibits the bisphosphoglycerate mutase enzyme, which decreases the blood's 2,3-BPG content, leading to left shifting of OHDC. • The Luebering-Rapoport pathway diverts from the ATP-producing steps of glycolysis, resulting in a net decrease in ATP yield because it consumes ATP. (Option C ruled out)","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 744-744; Solution Pages 756-757","sourcePage":744,"sourcePageEnd":744},{"id":"physiology-26-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A patient has a haemoglobin concentration of 15 g/dL. Calculate the oxygen-carrying capacity of the patient's blood.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"20.1 mL O2/g"},{"letter":"B","text":"17.5 mL O2/g"},{"letter":"C","text":"19.8 mL O2/g"},{"letter":"D","text":"15.0 mL O2/g"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) 20.1 mL O2/g","explanation":"Oxygen-carrying capacity = Hemoglobin concentration * Oxygen bound per gram of hemoglobin Given the hemoglobin concentration of 15 g/dL and knowing that each gram of hemoglobin can bind 1.34 mL of oxygen, we can calculate: Oxygen-carrying capacity = 15 g/dL *1.34 mL O 2 /g hemoglobin Oxygen-carrying capacity = 20.1mL O 2 /g Hb","sourceRef":"Question Pages 744-744; Solution Pages 757-758","sourcePage":744,"sourcePageEnd":744},{"id":"physiology-26-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":10,"question":"What is the cause of this shift in the hemoglobin-oxygen (Hb-O2) dissociation curve given below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased pH"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased temperature"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased metabolic rate"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased carbon dioxide levels"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increased pH","explanation":"Given image in the question is a left shift in the Hb-O 2 dissociation curve. Hb-O 2 dissociation curve shift Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 744-745; Solution Pages 758-758","sourcePage":744,"sourcePageEnd":745},{"id":"physiology-26-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Select the correct statements from the following The binding of one oxygen molecule to Hb decreases the affinity of the remaining heme groups for oxygen. Hb shifts from the Tense (T) state to the Relaxed (R) state upon oxygen binding, increasing oxygen affinity. At low PO2, small changes in PO2 lead to large changes in oxygen saturation. At moderate PO2, the dissociation curve is steep, meaning small increases in PO2 results in large increase in oxygen saturation.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"3 and 4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 2 and 4","explanation":"Statements 1 and 3 are wrong as binding of one oxygen molecule to Hb increases the affinity of the remaining heme groups for oxygen. At low PO2, small PO2 changes lead to small changes in oxygen saturation. Cooperative Binding of Oxygen to Haemoglobin (Hb): • Cooperative Process: The binding of one oxygen molecule to Hb increases the affinity of the remaining heme groups for oxygen, reflected in the sigmoid shape of the oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve. • Conformational Change: Tense (T) State: Low affinity for oxygen. Relaxed (R) State: High affinity for oxygen. Oxygen binding to the first heme group shifts Hb from the T state to the R state, exposing more oxygen-binding sites. • Tense (T) State: Low affinity for oxygen. • Relaxed (R) State: High affinity for oxygen. • Oxygen binding to the first heme group shifts Hb from the T state to the R state, exposing more oxygen-binding sites. • As the partial pressure of oxygen (PO 2 ) increases, more oxygen molecules are available to bind to Hb, resulting in a higher percentage of oxygen saturation • Tense (T) State: Low affinity for oxygen. • Relaxed (R) State: High affinity for oxygen. • Oxygen binding to the first heme group shifts Hb from the T state to the R state, exposing more oxygen-binding sites. PO2 Levels Oxygen Saturation Affinity for Oxygen Low Slow increase Low (T state) Moderate Steep increase High (cooperative) High Plateau Approaching full saturation • Low PO 2 : Small PO 2 changes lead to small changes in oxygen saturation. • Steep Curve (Moderate PO 2 ): Small PO 2 increases lead to large oxygen saturation increases. • High PO 2 : Hb nearing full saturation; large PO 2 increases result in small saturation changes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 745-745; Solution Pages 758-759","sourcePage":745,"sourcePageEnd":745},{"id":"physiology-26-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":12,"question":"All of the following are true regarding the oxygen-hemoglobin (O2-Hb) dissociation curve except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a sigmoid-shaped curve."},{"letter":"B","text":"The plateau phase ranges from 10-60 mm Hg."},{"letter":"C","text":"The steep phase ensures efficient oxygen delivery to tissues even with small drops in PO 2 ."},{"letter":"D","text":"P 50 assesses the binding affinity of haemoglobin for oxygen."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The plateau phase ranges from 10-60 mm Hg.","explanation":"The plateau phase ranges from 60-100 mm Hg. Oxygen-Haemoglobin Disassociation Curve: The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve represents the relationship between the partial pressure of oxygen (PO 2 ) and the percentage of haemoglobin saturated with oxygen (SaO 2 ). Key Points: • Sigmoid Shape: Reflects varying Hb affinity for oxygen at different PO 2 levels. (Option A ruled out) • Steep Phase: Rapid increase in Hb saturation with PO 2 . • Plateau Phase: Minimal change in Hb saturation despite large changes in PO 2 . Phases: Steep phase Plateau phase Range 10-60 mm Hg 60-100 mm Hg Chara A rapid increase in Hb saturation. Curve flattens. cteristi Hb ~90% saturated at PO 2 of 60 Small increase in Hb saturation cs mm Hg. despite PO 2 rise. Signifi The steep phase (10-60 mm Hg It provides a safety buffer. cance PO■) ensures efficient oxygen It maintains high Hb saturation unloading, where small PO■ drops (~90%) despite moderate PO 2 cause significant O■ release, decreases. crucial for active muscles and It ensures stable oxygen delivery hypoxic conditions (Option C ruled under varying conditions. out) P 50 : P 50 is the level of PO 2 at which 50% of haemoglobin is saturated with oxygen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 745-746; Solution Pages 759-760","sourcePage":745,"sourcePageEnd":746},{"id":"physiology-26-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following is not a component of the respiratory unit?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Respiratory bronchiole"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alveolar duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Terminal bronchiole"},{"letter":"D","text":"Alveolus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Terminal bronchiole","explanation":"Terminal bronchiole (Option C) is a component of the respiratory system's conducting zone and does not participate directly in gas exchange. It leads to the respiratory bronchioles but is not considered part of the respiratory unit. respiratory unit\" data-author=\"na\" data-hash=\"547\" data-license=\"na\" data-source=\"na\" data-tags=\"January2025\" height=\"459\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/ZrpMZPrmti2Jp EgYgCKY1735977823.png\" width=\"500\" /> The respiratory unit, also known as the acinus, is the functional unit of the lung where gas exchange occurs. It consists of several structures: • Respiratory bronchiole (Option A) : These are the airways where gas exchange begins and leads to alveolar ducts. • Alveolar duct (Option B) : These passages lead from respiratory bronchioles to the alveolar sacs and provide the pathway for air to reach the alveoli. • Alveolus (Option D) : The alveoli are the tiny air sacs where gas exchange occurs. They are the final part of the respiratory unit and essential for respiration.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 746-746; Solution Pages 761-761","sourcePage":746,"sourcePageEnd":746},{"id":"physiology-26-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":14,"question":"What is the major mode of transport for oxygen in the blood?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dissolved directly in plasma"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bound to haemoglobin in red blood cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"As bicarbonate ions in plasma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bound to plasma proteins"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Bound to haemoglobin in red blood cells","explanation":"Oxygen Transport from Lungs to Body Tissues: Hemoglobin Transport (97%): • Binding: Oxygen binds reversibly to the iron atom in the heme portion of haemoglobin molecules within the red blood cells. • Capacity: Each haemoglobin molecule can bind up to four oxygen molecules. • Significance of Haemoglobin in Oxygen Transport: Oxygen Buffer: Haemoglobin acts as an oxygen buffer, ensuring that tissue PO2 remains stable (between 15-40 mm Hg) despite fluctuations in environmental oxygen levels. Exercise Adaptation: During exercise, increased levels of CO 2 , higher temperatures, and lower pH (Bohr effect) cause a rightward shift inthe dissociation curve, which enhances the release of oxygen to active muscles, ensuringadequate oxygen delivery. • Oxygen Buffer: Haemoglobin acts as an oxygen buffer, ensuring that tissue PO2 remains stable (between 15-40 mm Hg) despite fluctuations in environmental oxygen levels. • Exercise Adaptation: During exercise, increased levels of CO 2 , higher temperatures, and lower pH (Bohr effect) cause a rightward shift in the dissociation curve, which enhances the release of oxygen to active muscles, ensuring adequate oxygen delivery. • Oxygen Buffer: Haemoglobin acts as an oxygen buffer, ensuring that tissue PO2 remains stable (between 15-40 mm Hg) despite fluctuations in environmental oxygen levels. • Exercise Adaptation: During exercise, increased levels of CO 2 , higher temperatures, and lower pH (Bohr effect) cause a rightward shift in the dissociation curve, which enhances the release of oxygen to active muscles, ensuring adequate oxygen delivery. Dissolved Oxygen Transport (3%): • Mechanism: A small amount of oxygen is dissolved in the water component of plasma and blood cells. • Amounts: At a PO 2 of 95 mm Hg, approximately 0.29 ml of oxygen is dissolved per 100 ml of blood. At a PO 2 of 40 mm Hg, approximately 0.12 ml of oxygen is dissolved. • At a PO 2 of 95 mm Hg, approximately 0.29 ml of oxygen is dissolved per 100 ml of blood. • At a PO 2 of 40 mm Hg, approximately 0.12 ml of oxygen is dissolved. • While the amount of dissolved oxygen is minor under normal conditions, it becomes more significant at high alveolar PO 2 levels, such as during hyperbaric oxygen therapy. • However, this mechanism alone cannot meet the body's oxygen demands. • At a PO 2 of 95 mm Hg, approximately 0.29 ml of oxygen is dissolved per 100 ml of blood. • At a PO 2 of 40 mm Hg, approximately 0.12 ml of oxygen is dissolved.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 746-746; Solution Pages 761-762","sourcePage":746,"sourcePageEnd":746},{"id":"physiology-26-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following describes Henry's law?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure at constant temperature."},{"letter":"B","text":"A gas's effusion rate is inversely proportional to the square root of its molar mass."},{"letter":"C","text":"The solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas above the liquid at a constant temperature."},{"letter":"D","text":"The volume of a gas is directly proportional to its temperature at constant pressure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The solubility of a gas in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of the gas above the liquid at a constant temperature.","explanation":"Henry's law states that the amount of gas that dissolves in a liquid is directly proportional to the partial pressure of that gas in contact with the liquid. Partial Pressure = Concentration of Dissolved Gas■ / Solubility Coefficient Solubility Coefficients for Respiratory Gases at Normal Body Temperature: Gas Solubility Coefficient Oxygen 0.024 Carbon Dioxide 0.57 Carbon Monoxide 0.018 Nitrogen 0.012 Helium 0.008 • Carbon Dioxide (CO2) has a higher solubility coefficient than oxygen, meaning more CO2 can dissolve in water at the same partial pressure as oxygen. • The difference in solubility coefficients plays a crucial role in transporting respiratory gases within the body. Boyle's law states that the volume of a gas is inversely proportional to its pressure at constant temperature(PV = nRT) (Option A) Graham's law states that a gas's diffusion rate is inversely proportional to molecular weight. (Option B) Charles's law states that at a constant pressure, the volume occupied by a fixed mass of gas is proportional to temperature (V/T = constant). (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 746-746; Solution Pages 762-763","sourcePage":746,"sourcePageEnd":746},{"id":"physiology-26-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the effect of high altitude on gas diffusion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Diffusion capacity increases"},{"letter":"B","text":"Diffusion capacity decreases"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diffusion capacity remains constant"},{"letter":"D","text":"Any of the above, depending on the type of gas"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Diffusion capacity increases","explanation":"Pulmonary diffusion of gases increases in high altitudes. Respiratory changes in high altitude:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 747-747; Solution Pages 763-763","sourcePage":747,"sourcePageEnd":747},{"id":"physiology-26-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 55-year-old female patient with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is admitted to the hospital with severe shortness of breath. Arterial blood gas analysis shows an oxygen content of 16 ml/dl, while the mixed venous blood sample shows an oxygen content of 12 ml/dl. The pulmonary capillary oxygen content is determined to be 20 ml/dl. Calculate the percentage of cardiac output shunt for this patient.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"25%"},{"letter":"B","text":"10%"},{"letter":"C","text":"15%"},{"letter":"D","text":"50%"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 50%","explanation":"Shunt Fraction: • The proportion of cardiac output bypassing the alveoli and not participating in gas exchange. • Frequently calculated in critically ill patients to monitor pulmonary oxygenation effectiveness. Shunt fraction = (CcO 2 - CaO 2 ) / (CcO 2 - CvO 2 ) CcO 2 : Oxygen content of pulmonary capillary blood = 20 ml/dl CaO 2 : Arterial oxygen content = 16 ml/dl CvO 2 : Mixed venous oxygen content = 12 ml/dl Shunt fraction = (20-16)/(20-12) = 4/8 = 1/2 = 50%","sourceRef":"Question Pages 747-747; Solution Pages 764-764","sourcePage":747,"sourcePageEnd":747},{"id":"physiology-26-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Nitrous oxide is classified as a perfusion-limited gas. What does this imply about its diffusion properties in the lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"N 2 O uptake into pulmonary capillaries is dependent on the amount of blood flowing through it"},{"letter":"B","text":"N 2 O uptake into pulmonary capillaries is dependent on temperature"},{"letter":"C","text":"N 2 O uptake into pulmonary capillaries is dependent on atmospheric pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) N 2 O uptake into pulmonary capillaries is dependent on the amount of blood flowing through it","explanation":"N 2 O is a prefusion-limited gas. Its uptake is limited by the amount of blood flowing through the pulmonary capillaries. Gas Type Explanation Examp le Diffusion-Limited The diffusion process limits the CO Gas transfer Diffusing capacity for CO (DLCO) is used to measure the diffusing capacity of the lungs Perfusion-Limited The amount of gas taken up is N2O Gas dependent on blood flow (perfusion) through pulmonary capillaries Intermediate Case Uptake is primarily perfusion-limited O2 under normal conditions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 747-748; Solution Pages 765-765","sourcePage":747,"sourcePageEnd":748},{"id":"physiology-26-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Calculate the alveolar oxygen tension (PAO2) for a patient with a barometric pressure (Patm) of 760 mmHg, a water vapour pressure (PH2O) of 47 mmHg, an inspired oxygen fraction (FiO2) of 0.21, and an arterial carbon dioxide tension (PACO2) of 40 mmHg. Assume the respiratory quotient (R.Q) is 0.8.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"80 mmHg"},{"letter":"B","text":"90 mmHg"},{"letter":"C","text":"100 mmHg"},{"letter":"D","text":"70 mmHg"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 100 mmHg","explanation":"Alveolar gas equation: PAO 2 = (P atm - P H2O ) x FiO2 - PaCO 2 /RQ = (760-47) x 0.21 - (40/0.8) = 713x0.21-50 = 99.73 ≈ 100 mm Hg","sourceRef":"Question Pages 748-748; Solution Pages 765-765","sourcePage":748,"sourcePageEnd":748},{"id":"physiology-26-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":20,"question":"During a pulmonary function test, it is noted that the diffusion equilibrium for oxygen is reached at ■ rd of the transit time in the pulmonary capillary. Based on the information provided in the diagram, at what point during the red blood cell's transit through the pulmonary capillary is diffusion equilibrium for oxygen reached? {{caption_text}}","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0.75 second"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 second"},{"letter":"C","text":"0.25 second"},{"letter":"D","text":"0.1 second"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 0.25 second","explanation":"Diffusion equilibrium for oxygen and carbon dioxide will be reached at 0.25 seconds. • Transit time: The total time the RBC takes to travel the whole pulmonary capillary = 0.75 seconds. • Air inspiration is diffused from the alveolus to the pulmonary capillary. • Diffusion equilibrium of oxygen and carbon dioxide will be reached when RBC has reached ■ rd of transit time = ■ x 0.75 = 0.25 seconds. • No diffusion occurs in the remaining ■ rd of the transit time.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 748-749; Solution Pages 766-766","sourcePage":748,"sourcePageEnd":749},{"id":"physiology-26-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Carbon dioxide (CO2) diffuses more rapidly than oxygen (O2) primarily due to which of the following properties?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Higher molecular weight"},{"letter":"B","text":"Higher solubility in water"},{"letter":"C","text":"Smaller molecular size"},{"letter":"D","text":"Higher partial pressure in the alveoli"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Higher solubility in water","explanation":"CO■ is about 20 times more soluble in water (and thus in blood) than O■. This higher solubility allows CO■ to diffuse more rapidly across the alveolar and capillary membranes. Higher molecular weight (Option A) : Although CO 2 has a higher molecular weight (44 g/mol) compared to O 2 (32 g/mol), diffusion rate is influenced more by solubility and other factors. Smaller molecular size (Option C): O 2 is smaller in molecular size than CO 2 . However, molecular size is not the primary factor determining diffusion rates in this context. Higher partial pressure in the alveoli (Option D) : While the partial pressures of gases affect their diffusion, O■ generally has a higher partial pressure gradient in the alveoli than CO■.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 749-749; Solution Pages 766-766","sourcePage":749,"sourcePageEnd":749},{"id":"physiology-26-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 65-year-old male has had progressive difficulty breathing over the past year. He reports a persistent dry cough and fatigue. He has no history of smoking or exposure to occupational hazards. On examination, he has fine crackles at the lung bases and clubbing of the fingers. PFT reveals a restrictive pattern. An HRCT scan of the chest shows honeycombing and reticular opacities, predominantly in the lower lobes. This condition primarily affects which aspect of the respiratory membrane?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alveolar epithelium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Capillary endothelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Interstitial space"},{"letter":"D","text":"Surfactant layer"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Interstitial space","explanation":"• The clinical vignette suggests pulmonary fibrosis (restrictive lung disease). In pulmonary fibrosis, the interstitial space of the lungs becomes thickened and scarred due to abnormal fibrotic processes. This disrupts the normal lung architecture, impairing gas exchange and restrictive lung disease. • The thickening of the interstitium increases the diffusion distance across the respiratory membrane, hindering the transfer of oxygen and carbon dioxide. Consequently, this increased diffusion distance results in hypoxemia and contributes to the patient's difficulty in breathing. Alveolar epithelium (Option A) can be affected in pulmonary fibrosis, the primary pathological changes occur in the interstitial space, leading to thickening and scarring. Capillary endothelium (Option B) may be secondarily involved, but the primary issue in pulmonary fibrosis is the interstitial space, where fibrosis and scarring occur. Surfactant layer (Option D) reduces surface tension within the alveoli, and is not the primary site affected by pulmonary fibrosis. The main problem lies in the interstitial space.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 749-749; Solution Pages 766-767","sourcePage":749,"sourcePageEnd":749},{"id":"physiology-26-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A 65-year-old male with a 40-year history of heavy smoking presents with progressive shortness of breath and a chronic cough. On examination, he has a barrel-shaped chest and decreased breath sounds bilaterally. Pulmonary function tests show an FEV1/FVC ratio of 0.55 and a significantly decreased DLCO. Which of the following is the most likely reason for the decreased DLCO in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of alveolar surface area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased pulmonary blood flow"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hyperinflation of the lungs"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Loss of alveolar surface area.","explanation":"In emphysema, the destruction of alveolar walls leads to a significant reduction in the surface area available for gas exchange. This is the primary reason for the decreased DLCO in patients with emphysema, as the reduced alveolar surface area limits the ability of gases, such as carbon monoxide, to diffuse across the alveolar-capillary membrane. DL CO (Diffusing Capacity of the Lungs for Carbon Monoxide) • It measures carbon monoxide (CO) transfer from the alveolar gas to the pulmonary capillary blood. • Carbon monoxide is used because it has a high affinity for hemoglobin, and its uptake is limited primarily by the rate of diffusion rather than by perfusion. Procedure Inhale gas mixture (CO and helium) Hold breath for ~10 seconds Exhale and measure CO concentration Factors Affecting DL CO Lung Volume: Lower volumes decrease DL CO (e.g., restrictive lung diseases) Hemoglobin Level: Anemia lowers DL CO ; polycythemia increases DL CO Carboxyhemoglobin Levels: Elevated in smokers, reducing DL CO Alveolar Surface Area: Reduced in emphysema, decreasing DL CO Pulmonary Blood Flow: Reduced flow (e.g., pulmonary hypertension) decreases DL CO Clinical Interpretation Reduced DL CO : Interstitial lung disease, emphysema, pulmonary fibrosis, pulmonary vascular disease, anemia Increased DL CO : Polycythemia, asthma, left-to-right cardiac shunts Significance in Clinical Differentiate obstructive vs restrictive lung diseases Practice Assess disease extent in idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis Increased thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane can decrease DLCO, it is more characteristic of interstitial lung diseases such as pulmonary fibrosis, where fibrosis thickens the membrane. (Option B) Decreased pulmonary blood flow can contribute to a reduced DLCO, it is not the main mechanism in emphysema. In emphysema, the primary issue is the loss of alveolar structures. (Option C) Hyperinflation of the lungs is a common feature of emphysema, it does not directly cause a decreased DLCO. Hyperinflation results from the loss of elastic recoil and air trapping, contributing to airflow obstruction but not directly to the decrease in diffusing capacity. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 749-750; Solution Pages 767-768","sourcePage":749,"sourcePageEnd":750},{"id":"physiology-26-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-26","chapter":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topic":"Diffusion of Gases, O2 and CO2 Transport","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which of the following factors does NOT affect the rate of diffusion in the lung according to Fick's Law? Thickness of the alveolar-capillary membrane Diameter of the bronchiole Surface area of the alveolar-capillary membrane Partial pressure gradient of the gases Temperature Solubility of the Gas","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"4 and 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 only"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 2 only","explanation":"Fick's Law of diffusion states that the rate of gas diffusion is directly proportional to the surface area of the membrane, the partial pressure gradient, and the gas's diffusion coefficient and inversely proportional to the membrane's thickness. Diffusion Capacity (D L ) : It measures how effectively gases like oxygen (O 2 ) and carbon dioxide (CO 2 ) move from the alveoli into the blood. Diffusion Capacity Formula: Fick's Law describes the rate of diffusion of a gas across a membrane. For gases diffusing across the alveolar-capillary membrane in the lungs, the law can be expressed as ∆P: Partial pressure. A: Total surface area. K: Constant (Diffusion Constant of gases) d: Diffusion distance or the thickness of the membrane. D L : Total diffusion capacity of the lung. Factors Affecting the Rate of Diffusion: Surface Area of the The greater the surface area available for diffusion, the Membrane higher the rate of gas exchange. In diseases like emphysema, where alveolar surface area is reduced, diffusion capacity decreases. Concentration Gradient A steeper gradient (difference in partial pressures between the alveoli and the blood) increases the diffusion rate. For example, if the alveolar partial pressure of oxygen is high compared to the blood, oxygen will diffuse more rapidly. Thickness of the The thinner the membrane (alveolar-capillary Membrane membrane), the faster the diffusion rate. Conditions like pulmonary fibrosis increase membrane thickness and reduce diffusion capacity. Diffusion Coefficient of Different gases diffuse at different rates. the Gas For example, CO 2 diffuses more readily than O 2 due to its higher diffusion coefficient. Temperature and Higher temperatures increase the diffusion rate, and Pressure barometric pressure changes can also influence diffusion rates. Solubility of the Gas Gases more soluble in blood (like CO 2 ) will diffuse more readily than gases with lower solubility (like O 2 ).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 750-750; Solution Pages 768-770","sourcePage":750,"sourcePageEnd":750},{"id":"physiology-27-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":1,"question":"The true statement regarding central chemoreceptors is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Detects H+ in brain interstitial fluid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gets stimulated to decrease respiration"},{"letter":"C","text":"Located above the surface of the ventrolateral medulla"},{"letter":"D","text":"All are true"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Detects H+ in brain interstitial fluid","explanation":"The central chemoreceptors detect H+ in brain interstitial fluid. Central chemoreceptors: Details Location Bilaterally below (Option C ruled out) the surface of the ventrolateral medulla, immediately caudal to the pontomedullary junction. Synonyms Medullary chemoreceptors. Chemosensitive areas. Mechanism of Arterial PCO 2 increases Stimulation ↓ H + ions in the brain interstitial fluid increases. ↓ Central chemoreceptors get stimulated to increase respiration (Option B ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 771-771; Solution Pages 777-778","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":771},{"id":"physiology-27-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":2,"question":"All of the following are true regarding the J receptor reflex except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They are located near the pulmonary capillaries."},{"letter":"B","text":"Activation of J receptors causes rapid breathing."},{"letter":"C","text":"They are stimulated by hyperinflation of the lungs."},{"letter":"D","text":"They are exclusively involved in regulating airway smooth muscle tone."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) They are exclusively involved in regulating airway smooth muscle tone","explanation":"J-receptor reflex: • The pulmonary C fibres are located adjacent to the alveoli and are accessible from the pulmonary capillary circulation (Option A ruled out) • They are also called juxtapulmonary capillary or J receptors (described by Indian scientist Dr A. S. Paintal). • The pulmonary C fibres are more sensitive to mechanical processes like edema, congestion, embolism, and intravenous or intracardiac administration of chemicals like capsaicin P. • Effect: On stimulation, they cause rapid shallow breathing, bronchoconstriction, increased airway secretion, and cardiovascular depression. (Option B ruled out) • Acute stimulation results in apnea and a marked hypotension. • Skeletal muscle tone is also abruptly reduced.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 771-771; Solution Pages 778-779","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":771},{"id":"physiology-27-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 45-year-old patient with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) is undergoing a respiratory assessment in the clinic. The patient is asked to take a deep breath and hold it for an extended period. During this maneuver, the clinician observes that the patient’s inspiratory phase extends rather than transitioning to expiration. Which reflex is most likely responsible for this observation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hering-Breuer reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Head's paradoxical reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cough reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sneeze reflex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Head's paradoxical reflex","explanation":"In this scenario, the observation of prolonged inspiration extending rather than transitioning to expiration suggests the involvement of Head’s paradoxical reflex. Head's paradoxical reflex: Aspect Details Receptors Rapidly adapting pulmonary stretch receptors (RARs) Response Brief burst of action potentials in response to lung inflation Feedback Mechanis Enhances inspiration, especially during vagus nerve m recovery Clinical Significance Initiates first breath in newborns , generates augmented breaths, prevents lung collapse Implications Relevant for respiratory physiology, neonatal resuscitation, and respiratory control mechanisms Cough reflex (Option C) is triggered by irritation in the airways. It involves a sudden, forceful expulsion of air from the lungs to clear the airways of irritants or obstructions. Sneeze reflex (Option D) rapidly expulses air from the lungs to clear nasal passages. It is triggered by irritation in the nasal passages and involves a deep inspiration followed by a forceful exhalation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 771-771; Solution Pages 779-779","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":771},{"id":"physiology-27-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following is the receptor for Herring-Breur reflexes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chemoreceptors"},{"letter":"B","text":"Baroreceptors"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary stretch receptors"},{"letter":"D","text":"Proprioceptors"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Pulmonary stretch receptors","explanation":"• Hering and Breuer in 1868 found that lung inflation decreases tidal volume and increases respiratory frequency. • There are two Hering- Breuer reflexes: Hering-Breuer inflation & deflation reflexes. Hering-Breuer inflation reflex: • Steady increase in lung volume (lung inflation) results in an increase in the duration of expiration. • Receptors: Slowly adapting • Marked inflation of the lungs with sustained pressure may even abruptly terminate inspiration in progress, prolonging expiration. Hering-Breuer deflation reflex: • Marked deflation of the lung results in a decrease in expiration duration. • As described above, the receptors are slowly adapting stretch receptors in the lung parenchyma and airways, and the vagus nerve provides the afferent and efferent pathways. Significance of Hering-Breuer Reflex: • It protects the lung from overinflation while marinating normal alveolar ventilation. • It is more useful in infants than in adults. • It controls tidal volume during eupnea (normal breathing) in infants. • This reflex does not operate in adults unless the tidal volume is much greater than the normal inspiration. • Especially in the awake state in adults, this reflex is overwhelmed by more prominent central control.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 772-772; Solution Pages 779-780","sourcePage":772,"sourcePageEnd":772},{"id":"physiology-27-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What happens if a person inhales a gas mixture containing 5% carbon dioxide (CO2) during voluntary hyperventilation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The person will experience a significant drop in arterial PCO 2 , leading to apnea."},{"letter":"B","text":"The person will maintain a normal arterial PCO■ and prevent respiratory alkalosis."},{"letter":"C","text":"The person will experience increased alveolar and arterial PCO 2 , preventing apnea."},{"letter":"D","text":"The person will experience decreased ventilation due to the stimulation of medullary chemoreceptors."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The person will experience increased alveolar and arterial PCO■, preventing apnea.","explanation":"Physiological Description Change Increase in Inhalation of 5% CO■ increases the alveoli's partial Alveolar CO■ pressure of CO■ (PCO■). Elevated alveolar PCO■ leads to an increase in arterial PCO■. Stimulation of A rise in arterial PCO■ stimulates the medullary Ventilation chemoreceptors. This results in an increase in ventilation (hyperventilation continues) to eliminate excess CO■. Avoidance of Apnea Without additional CO■, hyperventilation-induced hypocapnia can lead to apnea (temporary cessation of breathing). Inhaling 5% CO■ prevents a significant drop in arterial PCO■, avoiding apnea. Maintaining Blood Inhaled CO■ helps maintain blood pH closer to normal pH by preventing respiratory alkalosis. This balance is crucial to avoid adverse effects on cellular and enzymatic functions. New Equilibrium A new equilibrium is established with slightly elevated alveolar and arterial PCO■. The person continues to hyperventilate as long as CO■ is inhaled.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 772-772; Solution Pages 780-781","sourcePage":772,"sourcePageEnd":772},{"id":"physiology-27-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":6,"question":"In which of the following conditions does the sensitivity of chemoreceptors decrease?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Emphysema"},{"letter":"B","text":"Asthma"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acute bronchitis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulmonary fibrosis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Emphysema","explanation":"Emphysema impairs chemoreceptor function, affecting respiratory drive, increasing dyspnea, and causing potential complications. Impaired Chemoreceptor Function: • ↓ Detection of gas exchange abnormalities • ↓ Response to CO■ levels Consequence Description Complications Dyspnea Difficulty breathing due to impaired Occurs even with non-critically chemoreceptor function abnormal blood gas levels Acidosis and Mental Respiratory acidosis from Mental status changes, need for Status Changes inadequate CO■ expulsion ventilatory support Ventilation- Mismatch due to structural lung Complicates breathing regulation Perfusion Mismatch changes reduced compensatory mechanism Asthma (Option B) generally increases chemoreceptor sensitivity due to intermittent hypoxia and hypercapnia. Acute bronchitis (Option C) is often associated with increased respiratory irritability and sensitivity. Pulmonary fibrosis (Option D) can lead to hypoxia and increased sensitivity of chemoreceptors, though the overall impact on sensitivity can vary.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 772-772; Solution Pages 781-782","sourcePage":772,"sourcePageEnd":772},{"id":"physiology-27-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Arrange the following in the correct sequence regarding the mechanism of action of a glomus cell: Depolarisation Calcium influx Reduced K+ conductance Increased ventilation Dopamine release","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 3, 2, 5, 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"3, 1, 2, 5, 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"2, 1, 3, 4, 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"4, 3, 1, 2, 5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) 3, 1, 2, 5, 4","explanation":"• Under hypoxic conditions, the reduced oxygen levels lead to decreased conductance of potassium (K+) channels in the glomus cell membrane. • This is due to the hypoxia causing the K+ channels to close or become less active. • The reduced K+ conductance decreases K+ efflux, resulting in the depolarisation of the glomus cell membrane. • This change in membrane potential makes the inside of the cell less negative. • Depolarisation opens voltage-gated L-type calcium (Ca 2+ ) channels, allowing calcium ions to flow into the cell. • The influx of Ca 2+ further depolarises the cell and initiates intracellular signalling. • The increased intracellular Ca 2+ triggers the release of neurotransmitters, such as dopamine, from the glomus cell. • Dopamine is released into the synaptic cleft of the afferent nerve endings. • Dopamine acts on the central nervous system by binding to D2 receptors in the medulla oblongata. • This signalling increases respiratory drive and ventilation (breathing rate and depth).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 773-773; Solution Pages 782-783","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"physiology-27-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Decreased arterial blood pressure leads to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased ventilation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased ventilation"},{"letter":"C","text":"No effect at all"},{"letter":"D","text":"Depends on the temperature"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increased ventilation","explanation":"Decreased arterial blood pressure leads to increased ventilation by central and peripheral chemoreceptors. Peripheral Chemoreceptors Central Chemoreceptors P Decreased arterial pressure → Decreased arterial pressure → at decreased perfusion of peripheral reduced removal of carbon dioxide h tissues, including the carotid and (CO 2 ) → CO 2 retention → w aortic bodies → hypoxia (decreased increased arterial P CO2 → ay oxygen levels) → stimulation of increased concentration of peripheral chemoreceptors. hydrogen ions (H + ) in cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) due to the conversion of CO 2 to HCO 3 - and H + → stimulation of central chemoreceptors in the medulla. R The peripheral chemoreceptors The central chemoreceptors also es send signals to the respiratory stimulate the respiratory centres to ult centres in the brainstem to increase enhance ventilation, thereby the rate and depth of breathing to increasing the elimination of CO 2 improve oxygen delivery and restore and improving blood gas levels. homeostasis. This dual mechanism ensures that the body responds promptly to a drop in arterial blood pressure by increasing ventilation, thereby improving oxygenation and CO 2 clearance to maintain physiological balance. Decreased ventilation (Option B) – Incorrect, as hypoxia and hypotension trigger compensatory mechanisms to increase, not decrease, ventilation. No effect at all (Option C) – Incorrect, as the body responds to hypotension with increased respiratory drive via chemoreceptor activation. Depends on the temperature (Option D) – Incorrect, as ventilation regulation is primarily controlled by blood gases and pH, not temperature.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 773-773; Solution Pages 783-784","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"physiology-27-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 65-year-old patient, Mr Kumar, is admitted to the hospital with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) exacerbation. He appears short of breath and has a low oxygen saturation of 88% on room air. Arterial blood gas (ABG) analysis reveals: pH: 7.32 PaCO2: 55 mm Hg PaO2: 55 mm Hg HCO3-: 24 mmol/L What will most likely activate Mr. Kumar's peripheral chemoreceptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"pH: 7.32"},{"letter":"B","text":"PaCO 2 : 55 mm Hg"},{"letter":"C","text":"PaO 2 : 55 mm Hg"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) All of the above","explanation":"Peripheral chemoreceptors respond to hypoxia (PaO 2 <60 mm Hg), hypercapnia (PaCO 2 >45 mm Hg), and acidosis (pH <7.35). Peripheral chemoreceptors: Topic Details Location Carotid bodies: At bifurcations of common carotid arteries Aortic bodies: Along the ascending aorta and arch of the aorta Function Send signals to the dorsal respiratory group (DRG) in the medulla to increase ventilation Carotid Bodies Type I (glomus) cells and Type II (sustentacular) cells Type I Cells (Glomus Release dopamine in response to hypoxia, triggering an Cells) action potential in the carotid sinus nerve Aortic Bodies Contain glomus cells similar to carotid bodies but are less effective in regulating respiration Responses Hypoxia: Increased firing rate of afferent nerves when PaO2 < 60 mm Hg Hypercapnia: Linear increase in chemoreceptor discharge rate with increased PaCO2 Acidosis: Stimulates ventilation independently of hypoxia and hypercapnia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 773-773; Solution Pages 784-784","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"physiology-27-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Where is the pneumotaxic centre, which regulates respiratory rate, located in the brain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medulla oblongata"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Midbrain"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cerebellum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Pons","explanation":"The pneumotaxic centre is part of the brain's respiratory control system and is located in the upper part of the pons. It maintains the rate of breathing. The primary function of the pneumotaxic centre is to limit the duration of inspiration, thereby controlling the respiratory rate. Sending inhibitory signals to the inspiratory centre in the medulla oblongata helps prevent the lungs from overinflating. Mechanism:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 774-774; Solution Pages 785-785","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"physiology-27-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":11,"question":"The apneustic centre is inhibited by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal respiratory group"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral respiratory group"},{"letter":"C","text":"Central pattern generator"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pneumotaxic center"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Pneumotaxic center","explanation":"Apneustic center: • Located in the caudal pons • Stimulation results in prolonged inspiratory effort interrupted by brief expiration (apneusis) • Strongly activates the medullary inspiratory centre • Tonically inhibited by the pneumotaxic centre. It ensures that the inspiratory drive originating in the apneustic centre is regulated and checked.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 774-774; Solution Pages 785-786","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"physiology-27-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which group of neurons is involved in controlling forceful expiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal respiratory group of neurons"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral respiratory group of neurons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both A and B"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Ventral respiratory group of neurons","explanation":"The Ventral Respiratory Group of neurons (VRG) in the medulla controls forceful expiration. Ventral Respiratory Group of neurons (VRG): • When the need for increased pulmonary ventilation arises, signals spill over into the VRG neurons. • It contributes to the extra respiratory drive which is required. • Electrical stimulation of some neurons causes inspiration, while stimulation of others causes expiration. • It is crucial for providing powerful expiratory signals to the abdominal muscles during forceful expiration. • This is important during activities requiring high levels of pulmonary ventilation (heavy exercise).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 774-774; Solution Pages 786-786","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"physiology-27-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":13,"question":"What is the structure responsible for the voluntary control of respiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cerebral cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Medulla"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cerebellum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Cerebral cortex","explanation":"• The cerebral cortex is the outermost layer of the brain, involved in various higher-order brain functions such as sensation, voluntary muscle movement, thought, reasoning, and memory. • The primary motor cortex, located in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, plays a key role in voluntary movements, including the voluntary control of breathing. • The voluntary control of respiration originates in the motor cortex and involves the corticospinal tract. This pathway directly sends signals from the cerebral cortex to the spinal cord, bypassing the brainstem respiratory centres. • Through this pathway, individuals can consciously control their breathing, such as holding their breath, taking a deep breath, or altering the breathing pattern during speech and singing. • While the brainstem (specifically the medulla and pons) is primarily responsible for the automatic, rhythmic breathing pattern, the cerebral cortex can override these automatic signals when exercising voluntary control. • This voluntary control is critical during activities requiring conscious modulation of breathing, such as talking, singing, playing wind instruments, or deliberate breath-holding.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 774-775; Solution Pages 786-787","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"physiology-27-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 45-year-old male was involved in a severe motor vehicle accident resulting in multiple injuries, including a traumatic brain injury. Imaging studies revealed a complete transection of the brain stem between the pons and medulla. Given this anatomical disruption, what would be the most likely effect on the patient's respiratory activity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cessation of spontaneous breathing"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyperventilation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Apneustic breathing"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cheyne-Stokes respiration"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Spontaneous but irregular respiration","explanation":"Effects of various lesions and brainstem transections: Level of transection Vagus intact Vagus cut A Complete transection Regular breathing Regular breathing above the pons continues continues; the depth of inspiration increases. B Mid-pontine level section Apneusis C Transection midway Irregular and rhythmic between the pons and the respiration medulla D Complete transection Cessation of spontaneou below the medulla s respiration Hyperventilation (Option B) is typically associated with lesions in the pons, particularly affecting the pneumotaxic centre, which helps regulate the rate and pattern of breathing. Apneustic breathing (Option C) is characterised by prolonged inspiratory phases, is typically caused by damage to the apneustic centre in the lower pons. Since the transection is below the pons, it is unlikely to result in apneustic breathing. Cheyne-Stokes respiration (Option D) involves periodic breathing with cycles of increasing and decreasing tidal volumes followed by periods of apnea. This pattern is usually associated with bilateral cortical or diencephalic damage, congestive heart failure, or high altitude.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 775-775; Solution Pages 787-788","sourcePage":775,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"physiology-27-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Ondine's curse, or Ondine's syndrome, is a rare disorder of the respiratory control centre. Which of the following is true about Ondine's curse?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of voluntary, but intact autonomic control"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loss of autonomic, but intact voluntary control"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of both autonomic and voluntary control"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of reflective control only"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Loss of autonomic, but intact voluntary control","explanation":"Ondine's curse, also known as congenital central hypoventilation syndrome (CCHS), involves a failure of the autonomic regulation of breathing, leading to episodes of hypoventilation or hypoxia and requiring the individual to remember to breathe consciously. Cause: Failure in autonomic regulation of breathing, often congenital or acquired due to brainstem damage. Symptoms: Difficulty breathing during sleep, leading to sleep apnea or hypoventilation. Symptoms may not be present while awake. Diagnosis: Sleep studies (polysomnography) to monitor and identify disruptions in breathing during sleep. Treatment: Positive pressure ventilation devices like CPAP or BiPAP to maintain adequate ventilation during sleep.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 775-775; Solution Pages 788-789","sourcePage":775,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"physiology-27-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following is considered the primary pacemaker for respiration in the brainstem?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rostral Ventral Respiratory Group (VRG)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Botzinger Complex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pre-Bötzinger Complex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Caudal Ventral Respiratory Group (VRG)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Pre-Bötzinger Complex","explanation":"The Ventral Respiratory Group consist of 4 clear zones. Zones Location Function Pre-Bötzinger Complex Ventrolateral A primary pacemaker for respiration medulla that generates the basic rhythmic pattern of breathing. Bötzinger Complex Medulla oblon Modulates respiratory rhythm; (Option B ruled out) gata, dorsal involved in expiratory phase regulation and inhibition of inspiration. Rostral Ventral Medulla oblon It contributes to rhythm generation Respiratory Group gata, rostral and integrates respiratory signals; it (VRG) is involved in the inspiratory and (Option A ruled out) expiratory phases. Caudal Ventral Medulla oblon Primarily involved in controlling the Respiratory Group gata, caudal expiratory phase of respiration. (VRG) (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 775-775; Solution Pages 789-789","sourcePage":775,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"physiology-27-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the functions of the dorsal respiratory group (DRG) of neurons in the medulla? The DRG primarily regulates the rhythmic pattern of breathing. The DRG receives sensory input from peripheral chemoreceptors and baroreceptors. The DRG mainly influences the expiratory phase of respiration. The DRG integrates information from the vagus nerve to adjust breathing depth. The DRG controls voluntary breathing movements during speech.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Statements 1 and 2 are correct."},{"letter":"B","text":"Statements 2 and 4 are correct."},{"letter":"C","text":"Statements 3 and 5 are correct."},{"letter":"D","text":"Statements 1 and 4 are correct."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Statements 2 and 4 are correct","explanation":"Feature Dorsal Respiratory Group (DRG) Ventral Respiratory Group (VRG) Location Medulla oblongata, primarily in the Medulla oblongata, ventrolateral nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) region, including nucleus ambiguus and nucleus retroambiguus Primary Controls basic rhythmic breathing; Modulates rhythm and force of Function initiates and regulates respiration breathing; involved in complex breathing patterns Breathing Generates and maintains rhythmic Modifies breathing patterns; active Pattern breathing; primarily active during during both inspiration and expiration inspiration Connection Integrates sensory input from Coordinates with pneumotaxic and with Other peripheral chemoreceptors and apneustic centres; adjust breathing Centers baroreceptors to adjust breathing for varying demands Sensory Receives input from lung stretch Receives input from higher brain Input receptors and chemoreceptors centres detecting CO■ and O■ levels and integrates information from the vagus nerve to adjust breathing depth Effect on Establishes and regulates basic Fine-tune breathing patterns and Breathing breathing rhythm; adjusts rate and force; adjust breathing during depth different activities Statement 1 (Incorrect): The DRG primarily regulates the rhythmic breathing. • This function is primarily associated with the ventral respiratory group (VRG). Statement 3 (Incorrect): The DRG mainly influences the expiratory phase of respiration. • The DRG is more involved in the inspiratory than the expiratory phase. Statement 5 (incorrect): The DRG controls voluntary breathing movements during speech. • Voluntary breathing movements, such as those involved in speech, are primarily controlled by higher brain centres.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 776-776; Solution Pages 789-790","sourcePage":776,"sourcePageEnd":776},{"id":"physiology-27-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-27","chapter":"Regulation of respiration","topic":"Regulation of respiration","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following controls the depth of breathing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pneumotaxic center"},{"letter":"B","text":"Apneustic center"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal respiratory group"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventral respiratory group"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Apneustic center","explanation":"The apneustic center, located in the lower part of the pons, sends signals to the dorsal respiratory group to promote deep, prolonged inspirations, thus influencing the depth of each breath. Mechanism:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 776-776; Solution Pages 790-791","sourcePage":776,"sourcePageEnd":776},{"id":"physiology-28-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 32 year old woman presents with severe headache and dizziness after using a faulty gas heater. Blood tests reveal normal arterial PO2 but significantly elevated carboxyhemoglobin levels, indicating impaired oxygen transport. Which of the following types of hypoxia does not stimulate chemoreceptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxic hypoxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anemic hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stagnant hypoxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic hypoxia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Anemic hypoxia","explanation":"Anemic hypoxia has normal PaO2 levels and does not stimulate chemoreceptors. Peripheral chemoreceptors: • Peripheral chemoreceptors are located in the carotid and aortic bodies • The carotid bodies are very sensitive and can detect even minor changes in PO2, PCO2, and pH of the blood. • Hypoxia is the major stimulus for the activation of peripheral chemoreceptors. • They have 2 types of cells: Type 1 -Glomus cells- the chemosensitive cells Type 2 - Sustentacular cells • Type 1 -Glomus cells- the chemosensitive cells • Type 2 - Sustentacular cells • The response of peripheral chemoreceptors to oxygen depends on PaO2 • Type 1 -Glomus cells- the chemosensitive cells • Type 2 - Sustentacular cells Type of Features Chemoreceptor stimulation Hypoxia Hypoxic Decreased PO2 Chemoreceptor stimulated (+) hypoxia Dissolved O2 is low (Option A) Anemic hypo PO2 normal Chemoreceptor is not stimulated xia Dissolved O2 is normal Stagnant Stagnation of flow (Low blood flow) Chemoreceptor stimulated (++) hypoxia to glomus cells (Option C) Histotoxic Tissue unable to extract oxygen Chemoreceptor stimulated (++) due hypoxia to impaired tissue oxygen extraction (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 792-792; Solution Pages 801-801","sourcePage":792,"sourcePageEnd":792},{"id":"physiology-28-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 35 year old female hiker ascends to 5,000m in 2 days. Within 24 hours, she develops severe headaches, nausea, coordination difficulties, confusion, and occasional hallucinations. On examination, she is ataxic, with a GCS score of 10 and signs of papilledema. What is the next best step in managing this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Immediate descent to a lower altitude and start dexamethasone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Start oxygen therapy and monitor for improvement"},{"letter":"C","text":"Provide hydration, antiemetics, and continue the ascent"},{"letter":"D","text":"Administer acetazolamide and maintain current altitude for the next 24 hours at least."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Immediate descent to a lower altitude and start dexamethasone","explanation":"The patient’s signs and symptoms are suggestive of a condition known as High-altitude cerebral edema (HACE). • Immediate descent to a lower altitude is the most crucial step in the management of HACE to reduce hypoxic stress. • Starting dexamethasone can help reduce cerebral edema and inflammation, complementing the descent. This combined approach is most effective for severe HACE. Oxygen therapy can provide temporary symptomatic relief, but it does not address the need for reducing cerebral hypoxia and edema. Immediate descent is required for effective treatment (Option B) . While hydration and antiemetics can provide symptomatic relief, continuing the ascent is highly dangerous in the context of severe HACE. The priority is to descend to a lower altitude (Option C) . Acetazolamide is useful for preventing and treating mild to moderate altitude sickness, but it is not sufficient as a standalone treatment for severe HACE. Maintaining the current altitude can worsen the patient’s condition (Option D) . HIGH-ALTITUDE CEREBRAL EDEMA (HACE) Category Details Cause Capillary leakage leads to brain swelling, often due to rapid ascent to high altitudes. Symptoms Severe headache, nausea, vomiting, ataxia (lack of coordination), disorientation, confusion, hallucinations, papilledema, and potentially coma due to herniation of the brain through tentorium. Mechanism Increased capillary pressure is caused by hypoxia-induced local vasodilation of cerebral blood vessels, leading to fluid leakage into brain tissues. Chemical factors like vascular endothelial growth factor and inflammatory cytokines may also increase endothelial permeability. Onset Typically within 8-24 hours after rapid ascent to high altitude; symptoms can progress quickly over the next 4-8 days if untreated. Treatment First Step - Immediate descent to a lower altitude Administration of oxygen High-dose dexamethasone to reduce cerebral edema and inflammation Acetazolamide to promote acclimatization and reduce CSF formation. Portable Hyperbaric Chamber: Symptoms will return when the patient leaves the chamber, but they may improve briefly, allowing the patient to help with evacuation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 792-792; Solution Pages 801-803","sourcePage":792,"sourcePageEnd":792},{"id":"physiology-28-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 30 year old hiker arrives at a clinic complaining of severe headache, nausea, and fatigue 12 hours after a rapid ascent to 3,500 meters. Physical examination reveals signs of mild confusion. Based on these symptoms, what is the most likely underlying mechanism contributing to his condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased cerebral blood flow leading to cerebral edema"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased capillary pressure in the brain due to hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased oxygen levels in the brain causing vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased blood-brain barrier permeability due to hypoxia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Increased cerebral blood flow leading to cerebral edema","explanation":"The symptoms point to Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS). In AMS, the body's response to reduced oxygen includes increased cerebral blood flow to enhance oxygen delivery. This response can lead to elevated capillary pressure in the brain, resulting in fluid leakage and cerebral edema. Acute Mountain Sickness Symptoms Headache Nausea Fatigue Insomnia Appear hours to days after rapid ascent Pathophysiology Associated with cerebral edema, caused by increased capillary pressure in the brain due to dilation of blood vessels in response to low oxygen levels. The increased permeability of the blood-brain barrier contributes to fluid leakage into brain tissue. Treatment Descending to a lower altitude Supplemental oxygen, and Acetazolamide to facilitate acclimatization. In severe cases, corticosteroids may be used to reduce cerebral edema. Hypoxia typically causes increased, not decreased, capillary pressure in the brain due to the dilation of blood vessels, which can contribute to cerebral edema (Option B) . At high altitudes, oxygen levels are low, not high. The primary issue is increased blood vessel dilation, not constriction, which leads to cerebral edema (Option C) . Hypoxia generally increases, rather than decreases, the permeability of the blood-brain barrier, contributing to fluid leakage and cerebral edema (Option D) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 792-793; Solution Pages 803-804","sourcePage":792,"sourcePageEnd":793},{"id":"physiology-28-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 55 year old man who recently relocated to a high-altitude region presents with worsening breathlessness and signs of right-sided heart failure. His examination reveals elevated pulmonary arterial pressure and symptoms of pulmonary edema. Given these findings, which vascular response to hypoxia is most likely responsible for his condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Systemic vasoconstriction causing increased blood pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pulmonary vasoconstriction leading to elevated pulmonary arterial pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Systemic vasodilation resulting in decreased oxygen delivery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased pulmonary blood flow due to systemic vasoconstriction"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Pulmonary vasoconstriction leading to elevated pulmonary arterial pressure","explanation":"At high altitudes, widespread pulmonary vasoconstriction due to hypoxia increases pulmonary arterial pressure, which can strain the right side of the heart and contribute to symptoms like breathlessness and pulmonary edema. Hypoxia causes systemic vasodilation which increases the blood flow and oxygen supply to the tissues. Elevated systemic blood pressure is less likely to be the primary cause of the symptoms described (Options A and D) . While hypoxia does lead to systemic vasodilation, this response is intended to enhance oxygen delivery to tissues, not decrease it. The symptoms described are more related to pulmonary rather than systemic changes (Option C) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 793-793; Solution Pages 804-804","sourcePage":793,"sourcePageEnd":793},{"id":"physiology-28-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which adaptation is associated with long-term high-altitude exposure and helps to enhance oxygen delivery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Elevated systemic blood pressure and reduced capillary surface area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased production of erythropoietin and enhanced hemoglobin levels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased number of red blood cells and lower 2,3-DPG concentration"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased pulmonary blood flow and decreased oxygen affinity of hemoglobin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Increased production of erythropoietin and enhanced hemoglobin levels","explanation":"Long-term exposure to high altitude leads to increased erythropoietin production, stimulating red blood cell production and thus enhancing hemoglobin levels. This adaptation improves the blood’s oxygen-carrying capacity. Changes to Hypoxia Cardiovascular Pulmonary vasoconstriction Systemic vasodilation Increased Heart Rate, Cardiac Output, Blood Pressure Angiogenesis RBC Increased Production (EPO, RBC) Increased Hematocrit and Hemoglobin Increased 2,3 - DPG Note: Significant Polycythemia→ increased blood viscosity→ increased workload of heart→ cardiac hypertrophy. While systemic blood pressure may initially rise, long-term exposure leads to increased capillary surface area (angiogenesis), not reduced (Option A) . Hypoxia causes an increase in red blood cells and 2,3-DPG levels, to enhance oxygen release in tissues, not a decrease (Option C) . While hypoxia can lead to increased pulmonary blood flow initially, long-term adaptation involves increased hemoglobin affinity due to higher 2,3-DPG, which facilitates oxygen release in tissues rather than decreasing oxygen affinity (Option D) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 793-793; Solution Pages 805-805","sourcePage":793,"sourcePageEnd":793},{"id":"physiology-28-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What primary physiological change sustains increased ventilation over several days of high-altitude exposure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Initial rapid increase in breathing rate."},{"letter":"B","text":"Compensatory renal excretion of bicarbonate."},{"letter":"C","text":"Decrease in cerebrospinal fluid pH."},{"letter":"D","text":"Decrease in respiratory rate over time."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Compensatory renal excretion of bicarbonate.","explanation":"As acclimatization continues, the kidneys adjust to the respiratory alkalosis induced by hyperventilation. They do so by excreting bicarbonate , which helps to counteract the elevated blood pH (alkalosis) caused by the increased rate and depth of breathing. This renal compensation helps to normalize the blood pH and allows for a sustained increase in ventilation over several days. When first exposed to high altitude, the body responds to the lower oxygen levels by increasing the breathing rate rapidly (Option A) . • This initial response is driven by peripheral chemoreceptors sensing low oxygen levels. • However, this initial increase is not sufficient to sustain increased ventilation over several days because excessive breathing leads to a decrease in CO2 levels (hypocapnia), which can counteract the drive to breathe more. Initially, increased breathing lowers CO2 levels, making the CSF more alkaline. Over time, CSF pH normalizes with gradual bicarbonate adjustment. However, sustained ventilation isn't solely maintained by CSF pH changes alone; renal compensation plays a crucial role alongside other adjustments (Option C) . With acclimatization, the respiratory rate stays elevated initially and stabilizes at a higher level than before acclimatization. This sustained increase in ventilation is supported by adaptations like renal compensation, rather than a decrease in respiratory rate over time (Option D) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 793-794; Solution Pages 805-806","sourcePage":793,"sourcePageEnd":794},{"id":"physiology-28-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":7,"question":"When a person ascends to high altitudes, their body undergoes several adaptive changes to cope with lower oxygen levels, a process known as acclimatization. Which of the following correctly describes these physiological changes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased pulmonary ventilation with respiratory acidosis, elevated EPO levels, higher cardiac output, and angiogenesis in skeletal muscles."},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased pulmonary ventilation with respiratory alkalosis, increased RBC production, higher cardiac output, and increased mitochondrial content in skeletal muscles."},{"letter":"C","text":"Decreased pulmonary ventilation with respiratory acidosis, decreased RBC production, lower cardiac output, and decreased capillary density in skeletal muscles."},{"letter":"D","text":"Unchanged pulmonary ventilation, no changes in blood pH, constant RBC production, and unchanged skeletal muscle characteristics."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Increased pulmonary ventilation with respiratory alkalosis, increased RBC production, higher cardiac output, and increased mitochondrial content in skeletal muscles.","explanation":"Changes during Acclimatization Respiratory At high altitudes, lower oxygen levels stimulate peripheral chemoreceptors, increasing pulmonary ventilation. This leads to enhanced CO2 removal, causing respiratory alkalosis. Kidneys respond by excreting more bicarbonate to stabilize blood pH, causing a sustained increase in ventilation and supporting prolonged acclimatization. Hematological At high altitudes, lower PO2 boosts erythropoietin production, increasing red blood cell count. This also raises 2,3-diphosphoglycerate (2,3-DPG) levels in red blood cells. Initially, alkalosis shifts the oxyhemoglobin dissociation curve to the left but increased 2,3-DPG shifts it to the right, improving oxygen release to tissues. Cardiovascular The heart rate, cardiac output, and blood pressure increase due to the activation of the sympathoadrenal axis by hypoxia. Increased cardiac output leads to an increase in blood flow, and hypoxia causes vasodilation in the systemic circulation, further contributing to increased blood flow and oxygen supply to the tissues. Tissue In acclimatization, skeletal muscles undergo several changes, including an increase in the number of capillaries and mitochondria and an increase in myoglobin content. Further, chemicals released from the hypoxic tissues stimulate angiogenesis. • There is an occurrence of respiratory alkalosis, not respiratory acidosis (Option A) . • There is an increase in pulmonary ventilation, RBC production, Cardiac output, and new capillary formation (Options C and D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 794-794; Solution Pages 806-807","sourcePage":794,"sourcePageEnd":794},{"id":"physiology-28-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 28-year-old climber at 4,500 meters (14,764 feet) develops severe headache, nausea, and breathlessness after 36 hours. Even with supplemental oxygen, his symptoms worsen with confusion and coordination loss, prompting his team to descend.What likely caused his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acute Mountain Sickness (AMS)."},{"letter":"B","text":"High-Altitude Pulmonary Edema (HAPE)."},{"letter":"C","text":"High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE)."},{"letter":"D","text":"An increase in alveolar PCO2."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE)","explanation":"The climber is experiencing High-Altitude Cerebral Edema (HACE). Severe headache, confusion, loss of coordination, and persistence of symptoms despite supplemental oxygen indicate HACE. At high altitudes, ventilation increases, reducing alveolar PCO2 (Option D) . The issue is low oxygen and potential cerebral edema. Common problems of High Altitude: • Due to extreme cold (hypothermia, frostbite, trench foot, chilblains). • Effects due to low oxygen concentration (pulmonary edema, cerebral edema). • Due to extreme cold (hypothermia, frost bites, trench foot, chilblains). • Due to exposure to snow (snow blindness). • Due to solar radiation (sunburn). • Psychological problems. High Altitude Physiology • Decrease in barometric pressure with elevation. • Hypoxic hypoxia occurs: As altitude increases, alveolar (lung) PO2 decreases, hence the PaO2 of blood is reduced, leading to decreased O2 delivery to the tissues. Symptoms and Effects of High Altitude Condition Description Symptoms Acute Mountain A common condition, developing Headache, irritability, insomnia, Sickness (AMS) within 8-24 hours of ascent, lasting breathlessness, nausea, vomiting. (Option A) 4-8 days. High-Altitude Develops within 1-3 days. Ataxia, disorientation, potential Cerebral Edema Progression of AMS with significant coma, death. (HACE) brain swelling due to capillary leakage. High-Altitude Patchy edema in lungs from Fluid accumulation, and increased Pulmonary increased pulmonary arterial capillary pressure- causing cough, Edema (HAPE) pressure. dyspnea, and chest pain. (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 794-794; Solution Pages 807-808","sourcePage":794,"sourcePageEnd":794},{"id":"physiology-28-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":9,"question":"On a hike, a healthy 35 year old mountaineer starts feeling severe dizziness, confusion, and shortness of breath at a high altitude (12,000 meters or 39,370 feet) despite using supplemental oxygen. His team measures his alveolar oxygen levels and finds them significantly low. They choose to descend to a lower altitude promptly. What is the likely reason for his symptoms and the team's decision to descend?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The barometric pressure at 12,000meters is too low to maintain adequate PO2 even with supplemental oxygen."},{"letter":"B","text":"The barometric pressure at 12,000 meters causes body fluids to boil."},{"letter":"C","text":"The composition of the air changes drastically at high altitudes."},{"letter":"D","text":"The mountaineer's alveolar PCO2 increases due to increased ventilation."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) The barometric pressure at 12,000 meters is too low to maintain adequate PO2 even with supplemental oxygen.","explanation":"At extreme altitudes, the barometric pressure is so low that even breathing 100% oxygen cannot maintain a normal alveolar PO2, leading to severe hypoxia. As you ascend altitude, the barometric pressure decreases because the weight of the atmosphere above you lessens. Although the composition of the air remains consistent, the partial pressure of oxygen (PO2) decreases proportionally with the decrease in barometric pressure. (Option C) 1) Decrease in Barometric Pressure and PO2: 2) Hypoxic Stimulation and Ventilation: 3) Breathing 100% Oxygen: 4) Limits of Oxygen Breathing at Higher Altitudes:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 794-795; Solution Pages 808-809","sourcePage":794,"sourcePageEnd":795},{"id":"physiology-28-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 50 year old man is found unconscious after a fire in a closed space. Upon investigation of the scene, it was discovered that there was a cyanide gas attack and the patient is severely hypoxic The doctor suspects that he is suffering from histotoxic hypoxia. Which of the following is NOT seen in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inhibition of cellular metabolism"},{"letter":"B","text":"High venous PO2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Normal arterial PO2"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cyanosis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Cyanosis","explanation":"Cyanosis is not typically seen in histotoxic hypoxia because the arterial blood oxygen levels and hemoglobin saturation with oxygen remain normal. Cyanosis: • Bluish discoloration of skin. • Cause: Excessive amounts of deoxygenated hemoglobin in the capillaries. • Deoxygenated hemoglobin has an intense dark blue-purple color that is transmitted through the skin • Cyanosis occurs whenever the arterial blood contains more than 5 grams of deoxygenated hemoglobin in each 100 ml of blood Type of Hypoxia Features Cyanosis Hypoxic hypoxia Low O2 saturation in arterial and Cyanosis ++ venous blood Anemic hypoxia Anemia Cyanosis seen rarely Methemoglobinemia Cyanosis-like appearance Stagnant hypoxia Reduced O2 saturation in venous Cyanosis ++ blood Histotoxic hypoxia O2 saturation is normal in arterial Cyanosis never seen blood and high in venous blood The most sensitive areas of the Brain in case of hypoxia are: • Hippocampus • The Purkinje cell of the cerebellum • Striatal region of the basal ganglia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 795-795; Solution Pages 809-810","sourcePage":795,"sourcePageEnd":795},{"id":"physiology-28-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 50-year-old man experiences severe shortness of breath and dizziness while climbing a mountain. Despite receiving supplemental oxygen, he continues to show signs of hypoxia. Which of the following is the most common form of hypoxia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxic hypoxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stagnant hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anemic hypoxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic hypoxia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Hypoxic hypoxia","explanation":"Hypoxic hypoxia is the most common form of hypoxia because of ventilation-perfusion mismatch Hypoxia Deficiency of O2 at the tissue level due to decreased O2 supply or failure to utilize available O2. Types of Hypoxia Hypoxic Hypoxia • Definition : Reduced PO2 of arterial blood. • Mechanism : Decreased PO2 in arterial blood reduces O2 delivery to tissues. Causes: • Low PO2 in Inspired Air High altitude (e.g., 3000m/10,000ft, 5000m/15,000ft). • Hypoventilation Airway obstruction, respiratory muscle paralysis, respiratory center depression, scoliosis. • Diffusion Defect Pulmonary edema, pulmonary fibrosis, lung collapse. • Ventilation-Perfusion Mismatch Large A-V shunts, atelectasis, lung collapse, cyanotic congenital heart disease. Impact: • Partial pressure is a determinant of the saturation of hemoglobin in the arterial blood. • Reduced partial pressure, will also lead to a proportional decrease of the SaO2 as well. • In the case of hypoxic hypoxia, both the dissolved oxygen and hemoglobin-bound oxygen will be low. • Ultimately resulting in a low supply of oxygen to the tissue resulting in hypoxia. Stagnant hypoxia/ischemic hypoxia (Option B) occurs when blood flow is restricted, leading to inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues. It is frequently seen in conditions like heart failure or shock but is not as broadly encountered as hypoxic hypoxia. Anemic hypoxia (Option C) arises from reduced hemoglobin levels or impaired hemoglobin function, limiting the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity. It is less common compared to hypoxic hypoxia and is not associated with altitude changes. Histotoxic hypoxia (Option D) occurs when tissues are unable to utilize oxygen effectively, often due to toxins like cyanide. It is less common in general populations compared to hypoxic hypoxia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 795-795; Solution Pages 810-811","sourcePage":795,"sourcePageEnd":795},{"id":"physiology-28-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stagnant hypoxia is where tissues cannot utilize O2 despite a normal supply"},{"letter":"B","text":"Heart failure causes histotoxic hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stagnant hypoxia is due to the inhibition of tissue oxidative processes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Artery venous oxygen difference increases in stagnant hypoxia"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Artery venous oxygen difference increases in stagnant hypoxia.","explanation":"The arterial and venous blood oxygen difference will increase in stagnant hypoxia because the tissue will keep extracting more and more oxygen from the same blood leading to a low oxygen level in the venous blood. Aspect Stagnant Hypoxia Histotoxic hypoxia Definition Hypoxia due to decreased blood Hypoxia where tissues cannot utilize flow to tissues O2 despite normal O2 supply (Option A) Mechanism Normal arterial O2 content, hypoxia Inhibition of tissue oxidative due to stagnation of blood circulation processes (Option C) . Conditions Heart failure (decreased cardiac Cyanide poisoning - Cyanide associated output) (Option B) inhibits cytochrome oxidase. Tissue Shock oxidation is paralyzed. Vascular obstruction (decreased Severe diphtheria- blood flow) Diphtheria toxin inhibits cytochrome synthesis. Consequenc Local acidosis from lactate Inhibition of cellular metabolism es accumulation Treatment Address underlying cause (e.g., Cyanide poisoning: Methylene blue improve cardiac output) or nitrites, Hyperbaric O2 therapy Arterial and With no adequate blood supply, the Tissues cannot extract oxygen from Venous bloo tissue will keep extracting more and the blood hence level of venous d oxygen more oxygen from the same blood blood oxygen will be higher than leading to a low oxygen level in the normal. venous blood. The artery venous oxygen The arterial and venous blood difference will decrease. oxygen difference will increase.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 796-796; Solution Pages 811-812","sourcePage":796,"sourcePageEnd":796},{"id":"physiology-28-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A diver experiences joint pain, dizziness, and motor weakness after rapidly ascending from a deep dive. Which of the following best explains the pathophysiology of his symptoms and outlines the immediate management?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nitrogen bubbles formed during rapid ascent obstruct small blood vessels; treatment is high-flow oxygen therapy"},{"letter":"B","text":"High atmospheric pressure increases nitrogen dissolution in tissues; immediate recompression followed by slow decompression is essential."},{"letter":"C","text":"Oxygen toxicity due to high partial pressures; reducing oxygen concentration and administering IV fluids is required."},{"letter":"D","text":"Dehydration from prolonged diving; rehydration and analgesics for joint pain are the primary treatments"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) High atmospheric pressure increases nitrogen dissolution in tissues; immediate recompression followed by slow decompression is essential.","explanation":"Decompression sickness due to rapid ascent after prolonged exposure to high pressure leads to nitrogen bubble formation in tissues . The immediate and correct management is recompression therapy in a hyperbaric chamber, followed by slow decompression to safely eliminate the bubbles and prevent further damage. Decompression sickness Aspect Description Definition Occurs when subjects exposed to high atmospheric pressures are suddenly brought to low atmospheric pressures Include deep-sea divers or workers in caissons returning to the surface rapidly or rapid ascent in an unpressurized airplane cabin. Pressure Sea Level: 1 atmosphere. Changes Depth: Pressure increases by 1 atmosphere for every 10 meters Nitrogen at Sea Level: 1 liter dissolved in the body (half in water, half in fat). Mechanism Nitrogen Dissolution : Divers breathe 80% nitrogen from gas mixtures. Nitrogen dissolves in body fluids and lipid structures of tissues, proportional to the depth of descent. Slow Ascent : Gases diffuse into the blood and are eliminated by the lungs. Rapid Ascent : Rapid escape of gases forms bubbles in tissues and blood, causing damage. Symptoms Onset : Symptoms usually appear within half an hour of surfacing. Mild : Severe pain in muscles and joints (decompression sickness or caisson disease)- bends Severe : Bubbles decrease blood flow to the brain and spinal cord causing sensory and motor deficits (paresthesia, motor weakness, paralysis, respiratory failure). Bubbles in pulmonary vessels cause dyspnea and respiratory compression.- chokes Bubbles in coronary vessels cause myocardial ischemia Treatment Immediate recompression in a pressurized cabin followed by slow decompression. Hyperbaric oxygen therapy is helpful. Recovery is usually complete, though some residual neural deficits may persist. • Slow Ascent : Prevents decompression sickness by allowing nitrogen to be eliminated via the lungs. • Decompression Tables : Detailed procedures for safe decompression by the US Navy. Example: Schedule for 190 feet depth for 60 minutes: Decompression Time Depth 10 minutes 50ft 17 minutes 40ft 19 minutes 30ft 50 minutes 20ft 84 minutes 10ft High-flow oxygen therapy alone is not sufficient as immediate treatment. The correct treatment involves immediate recompression in a hyperbaric chamber followed by slow decompression to safely eliminate the nitrogen bubbles (Option A) . Symptoms are due to nitrogen bubbles, not oxygen toxicity . Therefore, reducing oxygen concentration and administering IV fluids are not the appropriate immediate treatments (Option B) . The critical issue here is decompression sickness, not dehydration . The immediate treatment should focus on recompression therapy to address the nitrogen bubbles causing the severe symptoms (Option D) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 796-796; Solution Pages 812-813","sourcePage":796,"sourcePageEnd":796},{"id":"physiology-28-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A diver at 150 feet depth begins to exhibit signs of drowsiness and impaired coordination, likely experiencing nitrogen narcosis. Which of the following statements is incorrect about nitrogen narcosis at this depth?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nitrogen narcosis can cause euphoria at depths around 120 feet."},{"letter":"B","text":"Symptoms of nitrogen narcosis worsen progressively with increasing depth."},{"letter":"C","text":"The effects of nitrogen narcosis are irreversible even after ascending to a shallower depth."},{"letter":"D","text":"Nitrogen narcosis occurs due to nitrogen dissolving in fatty neuronal membranes."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) The effects of nitrogen narcosis are irreversible even after ascending to a shallower depth.","explanation":"The effects of nitrogen narcosis are reversible and can reverse within a few minutes upon ascending to a shallower depth. Nitrogen Toxicity • About four-fifths of air is nitrogen. • Nitrogen has no significant effect on bodily function at sea level pressure. • At high pressures, nitrogen can cause varying degrees of narcosis. Nitrogen Narcosis • Symptoms: The first symptoms of mild narcosis appear at about 120 feet depth after 1 hour or more underwater, causing euphoria (similar to mild alcohol intoxication) (Option A) . At 150 to 250 feet: the Diver becomes drowsy. Significant loss of strength and clumsiness, hindering work performance. Beyond 250 feet: hallucinations, stupor, coma, and even death (Option B) . • At 150 to 250 feet: the Diver becomes drowsy. Significant loss of strength and clumsiness, hindering work performance. • Beyond 250 feet: hallucinations, stupor, coma, and even death (Option B) . • Mechanism: Similar to alcohol intoxication and called \"raptures of the depths\"; believed to dissolve in fatty neuronal membranes, altering ionic conductance and reducing neuronal excitability (Option D) . • Reversal: Narcosis reverses within a few minutes upon ascent to a shallower depth, with no known long-term effects if the ascent is not too rapid. • At 150 to 250 feet: the Diver becomes drowsy. Significant loss of strength and clumsiness, hindering work performance. • Beyond 250 feet: hallucinations, stupor, coma, and even death (Option B) .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 796-797; Solution Pages 814-814","sourcePage":796,"sourcePageEnd":797},{"id":"physiology-28-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 40 year old deep-sea diver experiences shortness of breath and dizziness after prolonged use of a rebreathing apparatus. His symptoms worsen despite increased breathing efforts, indicating possible CO2 buildup in his equipment. What is the tolerable level up to which alveolar CO2 pressure (Pco2) can go for a diver?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"40mm Hg"},{"letter":"B","text":"60mm Hg"},{"letter":"C","text":"80mm Hg"},{"letter":"D","text":"100mm Hg"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 80mm Hg","explanation":"Divers can tolerate up to an alveolar pressure of 80mm Hg by increasing the respiratory volume 8-11 fold Carbon dioxide toxicity in deep-sea diving • Proper Gear prevents CO2 toxicity as depth alone doesn't increase alveolar CO2 pressure. Normal breathing maintains CO2 levels. • Certain diving helmets and rebreathing apparatus can cause CO2 buildup, leading to rebreathing of CO2. • Up to 80 mm Hg alveolar CO2 (twice normal), divers can tolerate it by increasing respiratory volume 8- to 11-fold. • Above 80 mm Hg, CO2 depresses the respiratory center, causing the respiratory failure-critical threshold • High CO2 levels lead to severe respiratory acidosis, lethargy, narcosis, and eventually anesthesia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 797-797; Solution Pages 815-815","sourcePage":797,"sourcePageEnd":797},{"id":"physiology-28-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 38-year-old diver, after a deep dive with a partial pressure of oxygen of 5 atm for 50 minutes, develops sudden muscle twitching, visual disturbances, and seizures upon surfacing, necessitating urgent medical attention. What is the most likely mechanism responsible for the diver’s symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Excessive production of oxygen-free radicals leading to cellular damage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Direct damage to lung tissue from high partial pressure of oxygen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoxia caused by inadequate oxygen supply to the brain"},{"letter":"D","text":"Barotrauma due to pressure changes affecting air-filled spaces"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Excessive production of oxygen-free radicals leading to cellular damage","explanation":"At elevated partial pressures of oxygen, the production of oxygen free radicals increases significantly. • These radicals can overwhelm the body's antioxidant defenses, causing oxidative damage to cells, especially in the nervous system. • This mechanism explains symptoms like muscle twitching, visual disturbances, and seizures observed in the diver. Direct damage to lung tissue from high partial pressure of oxygen (Option B): Direct lung damage typically manifests as pulmonary symptoms, such as coughing and shortness of breath, rather than neurological symptoms like seizures and muscle twitching. The diver's symptoms are more indicative of nervous system involvement due to high levels of oxygen-free radicals. Hypoxia caused by inadequate oxygen supply to the brain (Option C): Hypoxia can lead to symptoms like confusion, dizziness, and shortness of breath, but not typically to seizures or muscle twitching. In the scenario, the diver is exposed to a high partial pressure of oxygen, not a lack of it. Barotrauma due to pressure changes affecting air-filled spaces (Option D): Barotrauma results from pressure changes that damage air-filled spaces in the body, such as the lungs or ears, and usually causes symptoms related to physical injury rather than neurological symptoms like those described. Aspect Acute Oxygen Toxicity Chronic Oxygen Toxicity Pressure Breathing O2 at ■4 atm Exposure to 1 atm pressure of O2 for about 12 hours Symptom Seizures, coma, nausea, muscle Lung congestion, pulmonary s twitching, dizziness, visual edema, atelectasis disturbances, irritability, disorientation Mechanis Overproduction of oxygen free Direct exposure of lung tissues to m radicals (superoxide, hydrogen high O2 pressure damages bronchi peroxide) overwhelms enzyme and alveoli linings systems Affected Nervous system (high lipid content) Lungs (direct exposure) Tissues Onset Symptoms appear within 30 to 60 Develops after prolonged exposure minutes, often without warning (around 12 hours) Severity Exercise increases the severity and Exercise does not significantly affect with Exer earlier onset of symptoms the development of symptoms cise Treatmen In gas mixtures intended for t inhalation at high pressures, the oxygen concentration is reduced to approximately 20% to prevent oxygen toxicity","sourceRef":"Question Pages 797-797; Solution Pages 815-816","sourcePage":797,"sourcePageEnd":797},{"id":"physiology-28-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":17,"question":"How does the SCUBA demand system ensure that air is supplied to the diver only when needed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The air is continuously supplied to the mask at a constant pressure."},{"letter":"B","text":"The first-stage reducing valve maintains a constant high pressure on the mask."},{"letter":"C","text":"Air is supplied to the mask only during inhalation through a demand valve."},{"letter":"D","text":"Exhaled air is redirected back into the tank for reuse."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Air is supplied to the mask only during inhalation through a demand valve.","explanation":"The SCUBA demand system works by having the demand valve open during inhalation. When the diver inhales, the slight negative pressure pulls the valve open, allowing air from the tank to enter the mask and lungs. This ensures air is supplied only when needed. The air is continuously supplied to the mask at a constant pressure (Option A): In the SCUBA demand system, air is NOT continuously supplied to the mask. Instead, air is delivered only when the diver inhales, thanks to the demand valve. The first-stage reducing valve maintains a constant high pressure on the mask (Option B): The first-stage reducing valve lowers the high pressure from the tanks to a lower pressure suitable for breathing, but it does not maintain a constant high pressure on the mask. Exhaled air is redirected back into the tank for reuse (Option D): Exhaled air is expelled into the water and is not redirected back into the tank. This prevents the buildup of CO2 in the diver’s lungs. Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 797-798; Solution Pages 817-817","sourcePage":797,"sourcePageEnd":798},{"id":"physiology-28-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A diver descends to a depth of 300 feet in the sea. If the atmospheric pressure at sea level is 760 mmHg (1 atm), what is the total pressure experienced by the diver at this depth?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"31 atm"},{"letter":"B","text":"30 atm"},{"letter":"C","text":"11 atm"},{"letter":"D","text":"10 atm"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) 10 atm","explanation":"• At sea level, the atmospheric pressure is 760 mmHg (1 atmosphere) and the pressure in the lungs is equal to this pressure • The pressure increases by 1 atmosphere for every 33 feet of descent. • For 300 feet, the pressure increase is calculated as follows: Number of atmospheres=300 feet/ 33 feet/atmosphere = 9.09 atmospheres • Number of atmospheres=300 feet/ 33 feet/atmosphere = 9.09 atmospheres • Number of atmospheres=300 feet/ 33 feet/atmosphere = 9.09 atmospheres • Total pressure = 1 atmosphere (sea level) + 9.09 atmospheres (increase due to depth) = 10.09 atmospheres. = 10 atmospheres approx.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 798-798; Solution Pages 817-818","sourcePage":798,"sourcePageEnd":798},{"id":"physiology-28-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 45 year old female climber, who reached 5,200m in 36 hours, presents with severe dyspnea, persistent cough with pink, frothy sputum, fatigue, cyanosis, and oxygen saturation of 75% on room air. Despite supplemental oxygen, her condition does not improve. The team is considering interventions. Which treatment option is least effective for severe HAPE in this scenario?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Immediate descent to a lower altitude and administration of supplemental oxygen."},{"letter":"B","text":"Use of a portable hyperbaric chamber and provide high-flow oxygen."},{"letter":"C","text":"Providing aggressive hydration and using diuretics to reduce pulmonary edema."},{"letter":"D","text":"Starting nifedipine and preparing for emergency evacuation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Providing aggressive hydration and using diuretics to reduce pulmonary edema.","explanation":"Aggressive hydration and diuretics are not effective treatments for HAPE. Diuretics do not address the underlying hypoxia and pulmonary edema adequately and may worsen the condition by increasing fluid loss. HIGH-ALTITUDE PULMONARY EDEMA (HAPE) Category Details Cause Marked pulmonary hypertension that develops at high altitude due to severe hypoxia causing uneven vasoconstriction of pulmonary arterioles. Symptoms Patchy edema of the lungs, Severe pulmonary dysfunction, Cough Dyspnea Chest pain Mechanism Severe hypoxia causes powerful constriction of pulmonary arterioles, but the constriction is uneven, leading to increased blood flow and high capillary pressure in unconstricted areas, resulting in capillary wall disruption (stress failure). Onset From a few hours to up to 5 days after rapid ascent to high altitude. Prophylaxis Only started for people with a previous history of HAPE, specifically with recurrence. Gradual Ascent. Extended-release Nifedipine, 30 mg every 12 hours, starting the day before ascent and continuing for 4-7 days after reaching the target elevation or until descent. Treatment Immediate descent to a lower altitude (Option A) Administration of oxygen Use of a hyperbaric chamber, if available (Option B) Nifedipine (a Ca2+ channel blocker that lowers pulmonary artery pressure) (Option D) Diuretics and Acetazolamide are NOT RECOMMENDED for HAPE. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 798-798; Solution Pages 818-819","sourcePage":798,"sourcePageEnd":798},{"id":"physiology-28-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-28","chapter":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topic":"Hypoxia, Altitude, and Deep sea pressures","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 45 year old woman presents with fatigue and dyspnea. Her blood tests reveal a low hemoglobin concentration and elevated carboxyhemoglobin levels despite normal arterial oxygen saturation. Which type of hypoxia is most consistent with patients' findings?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxic hypoxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stagnant hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anemic hypoxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic hypoxia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Anemic hypoxia","explanation":"Anemic hypoxia results from insufficient or impaired hemoglobin thus leading to decreased oxygen transport and causing hypoxia Anemic Hypoxia Definition: Normal PO2 of arterial blood but inadequate hemoglobin (Hb) to carry O2 . Causes: • Anemia (decreased Hb concentration) • Carbon monoxide (CO) poisoning • Altered Hb (e.g., Methemoglobin) Mechanism: • Normal PO2: PO2 in inspired air and arterial blood is normal. • Reduced O2 Transport: Inadequate Hb reduces O2 supply to tissues. • CO Poisoning: • CO binds to Hb with 210 times greater affinity than O2, forming carboxyhemoglobin (COHb). • Slow release of CO from COHb prevents O2 binding. • Normal Hb content, but Hb is unavailable for O2 transport. • Hb-dissociation curve shifts left, decreasing O2 release to tissues. Hypoxic hypoxia (Option A) is caused by low arterial PO2 due to factors like high altitude or respiratory disease, not related to hemoglobin levels Stagnant hypoxia (Option B) occurs due to reduced blood flow or cardiac output, causing inadequate tissue perfusion despite normal arterial PO2 Histotoxic hypoxia (Option D) Involves impaired cellular utilization of oxygen, often due to toxins, despite normal oxygen supply and delivery","sourceRef":"Question Pages 798-799; Solution Pages 819-820","sourcePage":798,"sourcePageEnd":799},{"id":"physiology-29-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient presents with respiratory difficulty and a history of chronic smoking. Pulmonary function testing shows a decrease in FEV1%. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pulmonary Fibrosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Interstitial Lung Disease"},{"letter":"D","text":"Asthma"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD)","explanation":"A history of chronic smoking and decreased FEV■% (forced expiratory volume in 1 second as a percentage of forced vital capacity) are hallmarks of obstructive lung disease, with COPD being the most probable diagnosis. • Key Features of COPD: Chronic smoking history is the most significant risk factor. Obstructive pattern on spirometry: ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction Progressive dyspnoea, chronic cough, sputum production Irreversible airflow limitation • Chronic smoking history is the most significant risk factor. • Obstructive pattern on spirometry: ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction • ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) • ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction • Progressive dyspnoea, chronic cough, sputum production • Irreversible airflow limitation • Chronic smoking history is the most significant risk factor. • Obstructive pattern on spirometry: ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction • ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) • ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction • Progressive dyspnoea, chronic cough, sputum production • Irreversible airflow limitation • ↓ FEV■ (forced expiratory volume in 1 sec) • ↓ FEV■/FVC ratio (<70%) → hallmark of airflow obstruction Features of Obstructive Diseases: Emphysema: Chronic infection ↓ Impaired mucus clearance + Excess mucus secretion + Inflammatory edema Bronchiolar obstruction Air trapping and Alveolar damage Elevated RV and TLC Examples of obstructive lung disease: • Asthma • COPD (Chronic bronchitis and emphysema) • Bronchiectasis • Bronchiolitis • Cystic fibrosis Pulmonary Fibrosis (Option B) is incorrect because pulmonary fibrosis is a restrictive lung disease, which shows decreased FVC but normal or increased FEV■/FVC ratio. Interstitial Lung Disease (Option C) is incorrect because interstitial lung diseases (ILDs) cause a restrictive pattern, with normal or increased FEV■/FVC rather than a decreased ratio. Asthma (Option D) is incorrect because while asthma also causes obstructive airway disease with a low FEV■%, it is typically reversible with bronchodilators, unlike COPD, which is progressive and largely irreversible.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 821-821; Solution Pages 836-838","sourcePage":821,"sourcePageEnd":821},{"id":"physiology-29-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following secretes surfactant?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type I pneumocyte"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type Il pneumocyte"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sertoli cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"Leydig cell"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: Type Il pneumocyte • Type II pneumocytes: Type II pneumocytes are specialized cells in the alveoli that produce and secrete surfactant. Surfactant is a mixture of lipids and proteins that lines the inner surface of the alveoli. It reduces the surface tension within the alveoli, preventing their collapse during expiration and promoting efficient gas exchange. Incorrect Options: Option A: Type I pneumocyte: Type I pneumocytes are responsible for the structure and integrity of the alveoli. They are thin and flat cells that form the majority of the alveolar surface area for gas exchange. However, they do not secrete surfactants Option C: Sertoli cell: Sertoli cells are found in the seminiferous tubules of the testes and play a crucial role in supporting and nourishing developing sperm cells. They are not involved in the secretion of surfactants. Option D: Leydig cell: Leydig cells are found in the interstitial tissue of the testes and are responsible for the production and secretion of testosterone. They are not involved in the secretion of surfactants.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 821-821; Solution Pages 838-838","sourcePage":821,"sourcePageEnd":821},{"id":"physiology-29-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What would be the resulting lung volume if the chest wall is excluded and the lung is allowed to undergo elastic recoil?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Minimum volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Zero"},{"letter":"C","text":"Functional residual capacity"},{"letter":"D","text":"Residual volume"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option: Option A. • Minimum volume: The minimum volume of the lung refers to the volume of the lung when it is allowed to undergo maximum elastic recoil without any opposing forces from the chest wall. It is the smallest volume the lung can reach during expiration. Incorrect options Option B. Zero: If the lung undergoes elastic recoil without the chest wall, it does not mean that the lung volume would reach zero. The lungs still have residual air and do not collapse completely. Option C. Functional residual capacity (FRC): FRC is the volume of air remaining in the lungs after a normal expiration, when the forces of lung recoil and chest wall expansion are in equilibrium. It is the volume at which the lung and chest wall exert equal but opposite forces. Option D. Residual volume: The residual volume is the volume of air that remains in the lungs after a maximal expiration. It represents the air that cannot be expelled from the lungs and serves to keep the alveoli open.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 821-821; Solution Pages 839-839","sourcePage":821,"sourcePageEnd":821},{"id":"physiology-29-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":4,"question":"The diagram below shows the pressure–volume curves for 3 different lungs. Which of the following is the best representation of compliance between the three lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A<B<C"},{"letter":"B","text":"B>A>C"},{"letter":"C","text":"B>C>A"},{"letter":"D","text":"C>A>B"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: • The pressure-volume curve of the lungs represents the relationship between the pressure applied to the lungs and the corresponding volume of air in the lungs. This curve helps us understand the behavior of the lungs during inspiration and expiration. • The pressure-volume curve of the lungs typically exhibits two phases: inflation and deflation. During the inflation phase: • Initially, as the lung volume increases, the compliance of the lungs is high, meaning that the lungs easily expand with minimal changes in pressure. • As the lung volume continues to increase, the compliance gradually decreases. This is due to the stretching of the lung tissue and the increasing resistance to expansion. • At a certain point, the compliance reaches its lowest value, which is known as the lower inflection point. Beyond this point, the compliance increases again, indicating easier expansion of the lungs. During the deflation phase: • Initially, as the lung volume decreases, the compliance remains relatively high, allowing the lungs to recoil passively. • As the lung volume further decreases, the compliance starts to decrease again, indicating increasing resistance to deflation. • At a certain point, the compliance reaches its highest value, which is known as the upper inflection point. Beyond this point, the compliance decreases again, indicating increasing resistance to deflation. • The pressure-volume curve of the lungs can provide valuable information about lung mechanics and can be used to assess lung diseases or evaluate the effects of therapeutic interventions. Changes in the shape and position of the curve can indicate alterations in lung compliance and resistance.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 822-822; Solution Pages 839-840","sourcePage":822,"sourcePageEnd":822},{"id":"physiology-29-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In which of the subsequent situations does oxygen therapy prove to be ineffective?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Asthma"},{"letter":"B","text":"Acute myocardial infarction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary edema"},{"letter":"D","text":"Central respiratory depression"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D: Central respiratory depression • In cases of central respiratory depression, oxygen therapy may not be beneficial or effective. • Central respiratory depression refers to a decrease or cessation of breathing due to a suppressed respiratory drive in the central nervous system. In such cases, providing oxygen alone may not stimulate the respiratory centers enough to overcome the underlying cause of respiratory depression. Incorrect Options: Option A: Asthma- Oxygen therapy can be beneficial in asthma exacerbations. In asthma, there is bronchoconstriction and inflammation of the airways, which can lead to reduced oxygen levels. Supplemental oxygen can help improve oxygenation and alleviate symptoms in individuals with asthma. Option B: Acute myocardial infarction- Oxygen therapy is commonly used in the management of acute myocardial infarction (heart attack). It helps to increase oxygen supply to the heart muscle and improve oxygenation in the body. Providing supplemental oxygen during an acute myocardial infarction can help reduce myocardial damage and improve outcomes. Option C: Pulmonary edema- This option is incorrect. Oxygen therapy is typically used in themanagement of pulmonary edema. Pulmonary edema is characterized by fluid accumulation inthe lungs, leading to impaired gas exchange. Supplemental oxygen can help improveoxygenation and relieve respiratory distress in individuals with pulmonary edema.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 822-822; Solution Pages 840-841","sourcePage":822,"sourcePageEnd":822},{"id":"physiology-29-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":6,"question":"In peripheral oxygen sensing chemoreceptors, which ion is implicated?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Calcium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sodium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chlorine"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option is A: Potassium. • Potassium ions (K+) are involved in peripheral oxygen-sensing chemoreceptors. These chemoreceptors, known as peripheral chemoreceptors or arterial chemoreceptors, are primarily located in the carotid bodies and aortic bodies. They detect changes in oxygen levels in the blood and play a crucial role in regulating respiratory and cardiovascular responses. Incorrect options Option B: Calcium ions (Ca2+) are not directly involved in peripheral oxygen-sensing chemoreceptors. However, calcium ions are important for various cellular processes and signaling pathways within the body. Option C: Sodium ions (Na+) are not directly involved in peripheral oxygen-sensing chemoreceptors. Sodium ions play a critical role in maintaining the body's fluid balance, nerve impulse transmission, and muscle contraction. Option D: Chlorine ions (Cl-) are not directly involved in peripheral oxygen-sensing chemoreceptors. Chlorine ions primarily function as an anion to maintain electrical neutrality and are involved in maintaining the osmotic balance and acid-base balance in the body.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 822-823; Solution Pages 841-841","sourcePage":822,"sourcePageEnd":823},{"id":"physiology-29-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":7,"question":"In which of the following situations is the equation V/Q = infinity accurate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Foreign body obstruction in the bronchus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Equal to dead space"},{"letter":"C","text":"When O2 and CO2 ratio is equal"},{"letter":"D","text":"The PO2 of alveolar air is 159 mmHg and PCO2 is 40 mmHg"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: Equal to dead space Incorrect Options: Option A: Foreign body obstruction in the bronchus - V/Q is 0 here Option C: When O2 and CO2 ratio is equal V/Q is not Infinite in this case. Option D: The PO2 of alveolar air is 159 mmHg and PCO2 is 40 mm Hg V/Q is not Infinite in this case.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 823-823; Solution Pages 841-842","sourcePage":823,"sourcePageEnd":823},{"id":"physiology-29-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Hypoxic Hypoxia is seen in which of the following? (or) Hypoxic Hypoxia is seen in which of the following?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"CO poisoning"},{"letter":"B","text":"HCN poisoning"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ischemia"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-V shunt"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct option: Option D. A-V shunt : An A-V (arteriovenous) shunt refers to an abnormal connection between an artery and a vein, bypassing the capillary network. It is associated with hypoxic hypoxia Incorrect options: Option A. CO poisoning: Carbon monoxide (CO) can lead to anemic hypoxia. CO has a strong affinity for hemoglobin, binding to it more readily than oxygen. This reduces the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, resulting in decreased oxygen delivery to the tissues. Option B. HCN poisoning: Hydrogen cyanide (HCN) poisoning can cause Histotoxic hypoxia. HCN inhibits cellular respiration by interfering with the electron transport chain, impairing the utilization of oxygen by the cells. Option C. Ischemia: Ischemia refers to inadequate blood flow to tissues, which can result in stagnant hypoxia. Reduced blood flow means less oxygen is delivered to the affected tissues, leading to hypoxia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 823-823; Solution Pages 842-842","sourcePage":823,"sourcePageEnd":823},{"id":"physiology-29-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Comment on the breathing pattern of a patient with prolonged inspiratory spasms that resemble breath holding?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cheyne stokes breathing"},{"letter":"B","text":"Kussmaul breathing"},{"letter":"C","text":"Apneustic breathing"},{"letter":"D","text":"Biot breathing"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Apneustic breathing: • Apneustic breathing is marked by prolonged inspiratory spasms, often resembling breath holding. It results from damage to the pons in the brainstem, disrupting normal respiratory control. Incorrect Options: Option A - Cheyne-Stokes breathing: This breathing pattern involves alternating periods of deep, rapid breathing followed by brief periods of apnea. It doesn't match the prolonged inspiratory spasms described. Option B - Kussmaul breathing: Kussmaul breathing is characterized by deep, rapid, and labored breathing often associated with metabolic acidosis. It doesn't involve prolonged inspiratory spasms resembling breath holding. Option D - Biot breathing: Biot breathing is characterized by irregular breathing with periods of apnea and hyperpnea. It doesn't specifically involve prolonged inspiratory spasms resembling breath holding.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 823-824; Solution Pages 842-843","sourcePage":823,"sourcePageEnd":824},{"id":"physiology-29-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":10,"question":"If the pH is reduced from 7.4 to 7.3 with the PO2 being constant at 100 mmHg, which line would PaCO2 follow?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1: • The reduction in pH from 7.4 to 7.3 would trigger a compensatory response by the respiratory system to increase the excretion of carbon dioxide, which would lead to a decrease in PaCO2 (partial pressure of carbon dioxide in arterial blood). This response is known as respiratory compensation. • On a minute ventilation and PaCO2 graph, the line representing PaCO2 would follow a downward trend, indicating a decrease in PaCO2. The exact slope and magnitude of the change would depend on various factors such as baseline minute ventilation, respiratory rate, and dead space ventilation. • Note that the relationship between minute ventilation and PaCO2 is not linear, and many factors can affect this relationship, including metabolic rate, alveolar ventilation, and pulmonary perfusion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 824-824; Solution Pages 843-843","sourcePage":824,"sourcePageEnd":824},{"id":"physiology-29-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which type of ion channel is affected by mutations in the CFTR gene?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chloride"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sodium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Potassium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Chloride: • The CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator) gene codes for a chloride ion channel. Mutations in this gene lead to a defective CFTR protein, which disrupts chloride ion transport across cell membranes, contributing to the symptoms of cystic fibrosis. Incorrect Options: Option B - Sodium: Sodium ion channels are not directly affected by CFTR mutations. However, changes in chloride transport can indirectly impact sodium transport and balance in the body. Option C - Potassium: Potassium ion channels are unrelated to the CFTR gene. Mutations in the CFTR gene do not affect potassium transport. Option D - Calcium: Calcium ion channels are not connected to CFTR gene mutations. The CFTR gene specifically affects chloride ion channels, not calcium ones.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 824-824; Solution Pages 843-844","sourcePage":824,"sourcePageEnd":824},{"id":"physiology-29-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Adequate oxygen delivery at cellular level is present in which type of hypoxia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anemic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stagnant"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoxic"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Histotoxic: • Histotoxic hypoxia occurs when there is a disruption in the utilization of oxygen at the level of the tissue by the cells, despite adequate oxygen delivery. • It can be caused by cyanide poisoning and beriberi that interfere with the cellular metabolic processes, preventing the cells from effectively utilizing the oxygen delivered to them. Incorrect Options: Option A - Anemic: Anemic hypoxia results from a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood due to reduced hemoglobin levels or abnormal hemoglobin function. Option B - Stagnant: Stagnant hypoxia arises from the impaired circulation of blood, leading to inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues, as seen in hemorrhage and heart failure. Option C - Hypoxic: Hypoxic hypoxia is a condition where the partial pressure of oxygen in the arterial blood is too low.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 825-825; Solution Pages 844-844","sourcePage":825,"sourcePageEnd":825},{"id":"physiology-29-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Determine the respiratory quotient (RQ) of a patient weighing 50 kg who exhales 200 ml/min of CO2 and consumed 250 ml/min O2.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0.8"},{"letter":"B","text":"1"},{"letter":"C","text":"1.25"},{"letter":"D","text":"1.5"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 0.8: • To calculate the respiratory quotient (RQ), we need to determine the ratio of carbon dioxide production (VCO2) to oxygen consumption (VO2). The formula for RQ is: RQ = VCO2 / VO2 Given the values: CO2 exhaled (VCO2) = 200 ml/min O2 consumed (VO2) = 250 ml/min Substituting these values into the formula: RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 • RQ = VCO2 / VO2 • Given the values: CO2 exhaled (VCO2) = 200 ml/min O2 consumed (VO2) = 250 ml/min • Substituting these values into the formula: RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 • RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 • RQ = VCO2 / VO2 • Given the values: CO2 exhaled (VCO2) = 200 ml/min O2 consumed (VO2) = 250 ml/min • Substituting these values into the formula: RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 • RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 • RQ = 200 ml/min / 250 ml/min RQ = 0.8 Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 825-825; Solution Pages 845-845","sourcePage":825,"sourcePageEnd":825},{"id":"physiology-29-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which among the following statements best describes the appropriate mechanism involved in coughing? Forceful expiration against a closed glottis Expiration against an open glottis Normal inspiration Removing harmful substances.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,4"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,2,3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,4"},{"letter":"D","text":"2,3"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1 and 4 are correct statements: • Forceful expiration against a closed glottis: This describes the initial phase of a cough. The cough reflex begins with a deep inhalation, followed by the closure of the glottis (the opening between the vocal cords). The expiratory muscles then contract forcefully, increasing the pressure in the chest. When the glottis suddenly opens, the built-up pressure is released, producing a forceful expulsion of air and any irritants in the airway. • This describes the initial phase of a cough. The cough reflex begins with a deep inhalation, followed by the closure of the glottis (the opening between the vocal cords). The expiratory muscles then contract forcefully, increasing the pressure in the chest. When the glottis suddenly opens, the built-up pressure is released, producing a forceful expulsion of air andany irritants in the airway. • Removing harmful substances: Coughing serves to clear the respiratory tract of irritants, such as mucus, dust, smoke, or other harmful substances. This is a protective reflex mechanism aimed at keeping the airways clear and preventing the aspiration of foreign materials. • Coughing serves to clear the respiratory tract of irritants, such as mucus, dust, smoke, or other harmful substances. This is a protective reflex mechanism aimed at keeping the airways clear and preventing the aspiration of foreign materials. Forceful expiration against a closed glottis: • This describes the initial phase of a cough. The cough reflex begins with a deep inhalation, followed by the closure of the glottis (the opening between the vocal cords). The expiratory muscles then contract forcefully, increasing the pressure in the chest. When the glottis suddenly opens, the built-up pressure is released, producing a forceful expulsion of air and any irritants in the airway. Removing harmful substances: • Coughing serves to clear the respiratory tract of irritants, such as mucus, dust, smoke, or other harmful substances. This is a protective reflex mechanism aimed at keeping the airways clear and preventing the aspiration of foreign materials. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 825-825; Solution Pages 845-846","sourcePage":825,"sourcePageEnd":825},{"id":"physiology-29-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":15,"question":"What would be the least likely condition to happen if a group of colleagues took an excursion to a high altitude-mountain region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increase PaO2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increase in RBC & Hb"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased mitochndria"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hyperventilation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Increase PaO2: • PaO2 is the least likely condition to happen during an excursion to a high-altitude mountain region. At higher altitudes, the partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) decreases due to the lower atmospheric pressure. This can lead to a hypoxia, where there's a reduced availability of oxygen for the body's cells and tissues. Incorrect Options: Option B - Increase in RBC & Hb: When exposed to lower oxygen levels, the body can adapt by producing more red blood cells and hemoglobin to enhance oxygen-carrying capacity, improving the overall oxygen delivery to tissues. Option C - Increased mitochondria: To extract the increased amount of oxygen by the tissue, the number of mitochondria and cytochrome oxidase enzymes are increased. Option D - Hyperventilation: It's the body's attempt to compensate for lower oxygen levels by increasing the respiratory rate to take in more oxygen. This can help temporarily alleviate the effects of hypoxia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 825-826; Solution Pages 846-847","sourcePage":825,"sourcePageEnd":826},{"id":"physiology-29-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":16,"question":"In the presented spirometry image, what term corresponds to the point labeled F?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vital capacity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Total lung capacity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Expiratory reserve volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inspiratory capacity"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Vital capacity: • ‘F’ represents the vital capacity which is the maximum amount of air a person can expel from the lungs after a forced inspiration (≈4600 ml). • VC = ERV + TV +IRV. • Where, ERV - Expiratory reserve volume. TV - Tidal volume. IRV - Inspiratory reserve volume. • ERV - Expiratory reserve volume. • TV - Tidal volume. • IRV - Inspiratory reserve volume. • ERV - Expiratory reserve volume. • TV - Tidal volume. • IRV - Inspiratory reserve volume. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 826-826; Solution Pages 847-848","sourcePage":826,"sourcePageEnd":826},{"id":"physiology-29-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":17,"question":"In the pressure-volume curve, what does a reading of zero pressure imply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Residual volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Functional residual capacity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tidal volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inspiratory reserve volume"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Functional residual capacity: • The zero-pressure point on the pressure-volume curve represents the Functional residual capacity (FRC), which is the point at which the lung's tendency to collapse is balanced by the chest wall's tendency to expand. • FRC is the volume of air that remains in the lungs after a normal expiration. It is the balance point between the elastic recoil of the lungs and the chest wall. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 826-826; Solution Pages 848-848","sourcePage":826,"sourcePageEnd":826},{"id":"physiology-29-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Single breath nitrogen method is used for measuring _____________","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Physiological dead space volume"},{"letter":"B","text":"Residual volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tidal volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anatomical dead space volume"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Anatomical dead space volume: • The single breath nitrogen test, aka fowler’s method is used to estimate anatomical dead space. It involves inhaling pure oxygen and then exhaling slowly and steadily. • The alveolar oxygen is mixed with the nitrogen whereas the air in the anatomical dead space is free of nitrogen containing only oxygen thus measuring the anatomical dead space. Incorrect Options: Option A - Physiological dead space volume: Bohr’s method is used to measure physiological dead space volume. Option B - Residual Volume: It is calculated by multiple breath nitrogen washout method. Option C - Tidal volume: It can be calculated by using a spirometer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 827-827; Solution Pages 848-848","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":827},{"id":"physiology-29-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Pacemaker generating the rhythm for breathing is ______________","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pneumotaxic centre"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dorsal group of neurons in the medulla"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pre-Botziger complex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Apneustic centre"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Pre-Botziger complex: • The Pre-Botzinger complex, located in the medulla oblongata, is considered the primary pacemaker for spontaneous breathing. It consists of I (inspiratory) and E (Expiratory) neurons that spontaneously generate rhythmic signals, initiating the pattern of breathing. Incorrect Options: Option A - Pneumotaxic centre: The pneumotaxic center, located in the pons of the brainstem, helps regulate the duration and depth of each breath. It controls the switching off of the inspiratory phase, allowing for smoother transitions between inhalation and exhalation. Option B - Botzinger complex: It contains the expiratory group of neurons, and their main role is to control the expiration. Option D - Apneustic center: The apneustic center, located in the lower pons of the brainstem, is involved in regulating the intensity and duration of inhalation through inhalatory neuron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 827-827; Solution Pages 849-849","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":827},{"id":"physiology-29-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":20,"question":"During forceful expiration, which type of neurons primarily exhibit increased firing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal group of neurons"},{"letter":"B","text":"Caudal Ventral group of neurons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pneumotaxic center"},{"letter":"D","text":"Apneustic center"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Caudal Ventral group of neurons: • Caudal ventral respiratory group neurons mainly contain the expiratory group of neurons, which is dominantly discharged during forceful expiration. • During forceful expiration, one takes the help of the accessory muscles of respiration, for example, the abdominal muscles and internal intercostal muscles. • These are all contracted during forceful expiration. This control is exerted by the caudal ventral respiratory group neurons. Incorrect Options: Option A - Dorsal group of neurons: The dorsal group of neurons, also known as the dorsal respiratory group (DRG), is responsible for integrating sensory signals from the peripherals, such as the lungs or the muscles. Option C - Pneumotaxic center: The pneumotaxic center, also known as the pontine respiratory group, is a part of the brainstem that regulates the rate of breathing. Option D - Apneustic center: Apneustic center is also a pontine respiratory group that regulates the depth of breathing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 827-827; Solution Pages 849-850","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":827},{"id":"physiology-29-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following methods is used for calculation of anatomical dead space ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Multiple breath nitrogen test"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bohr’s method"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spirometry"},{"letter":"D","text":"Single breath nitrogen test"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Single breath nitrogen test: • The single breath nitrogen test is a method used to estimate anatomical dead space. It involves inhaling pure oxygen and then exhaling slowly and steadily. • The alveolar oxygen is mixed with the nitrogen whereas the air in the anatomical dead space is free of nitrogen containing only oxygen thus measuring the anatomical dead space Incorrect Options: Option A - Multiple breath nitrogen test: It is used for measuring the residual volume. Option B - Bohr’s method: it is used for measuring the physiological dead space. Option C - Spirometry: Spirometry is a commonly used test to assess lung function and measure various parameters such as forced vital capacity (FVC), forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV1), and peak expiratory flow rate (PEFR).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 827-828; Solution Pages 850-850","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":828},{"id":"physiology-29-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Carbon monoxide poisoning causes which type of hypoxia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anemic hypoxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoxic hypoxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stagnant hypoxia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic hypoxia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Anemic hypoxia: CO poisoning causes: • Anemic hypoxia occurs due to a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood, which can happen in conditions such as anemia and CO poisoning. • In the case of carbon monoxide poisoning, CO binds strongly to hemoglobin, forming carboxyhemoglobin. This reduces the availability of hemoglobin for oxygen binding, resulting in a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. • Carboxyhemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen compared to normal hemoglobin, which impairs the release of oxygen to the tissues. As a result, despite the presence of oxygen in the bloodstream, there is a reduced delivery of oxygen to the tissues. This reduction in oxygen supply to the tissues is characteristic of anemic hypoxia. Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Anemic hypoxia is caused by carbon monoxide poisoning.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 828-828; Solution Pages 850-851","sourcePage":828,"sourcePageEnd":828},{"id":"physiology-29-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Adequate O2 oxygen delivery at cellular level occurs in which type of hypoxia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anaemic"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stagnant"},{"letter":"D","text":"Histotoxic"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Histotoxic: • Histotoxic hypoxia occurs when there is a disruption in the utilization of oxygen at the level of the tissue, despite adequate oxygen delivery. • It can be caused by cyanide poisoning and beriberi that interfere with the cellular metabolic processes, preventing the cells from effectively utilizing the oxygen delivered to them. In histotoxic hypoxia, the oxygen delivery to tissues is adequate, but the cells are unable to utilize it properly. Incorrect Options: Option A - Hypoxic hypoxia: Hypoxic hypoxia occurs when there is a decrease in the oxygen tension (partial pressure of oxygen) in the arterial blood. Option B - Anemic hypoxia: Anemic hypoxia results from a decrease in the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood due to reduced hemoglobin levels or abnormal hemoglobin function. Option C - Stagnant hypoxia: Stagnant hypoxia arises from the impaired circulation of blood, leading to inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 828-828; Solution Pages 851-851","sourcePage":828,"sourcePageEnd":828},{"id":"physiology-29-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Respiratory distress syndrome is due to the deficiency of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dipalmitoyl Phosphatidylcholine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Phosphatidylserine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Phosphatidylethanolamine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Phosphatidylinositol"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Dipalmitoyl Phosphatidylcholine: • Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) is caused by the deficiency of surfactant in the lungs which helps in reducing the surface tension. • Surfactant is composed of the following constituents: Lipids (90%) - Mainly dipalmitoyl choline (60%), phosphatidylglycerol (6%), and other phospholipids (10%). Proteins (8%) - Albumin and immunoglobulin A. Carbohydrates (2%). • Lipids (90%) - Mainly dipalmitoyl choline (60%), phosphatidylglycerol (6%), and other phospholipids (10%). • Proteins (8%) - Albumin and immunoglobulin A. • Carbohydrates (2%). • So, among the given options the deficiency of dipalmitoyl choline , a primary constituent of surfactant will result in respiratory deficiency. • Lipids (90%) - Mainly dipalmitoyl choline (60%), phosphatidylglycerol (6%), and other phospholipids (10%). • Proteins (8%) - Albumin and immunoglobulin A. • Carbohydrates (2%). Incorrect Options: • Options B, C & D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 828-828; Solution Pages 851-852","sourcePage":828,"sourcePageEnd":828},{"id":"physiology-29-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Identify the marked region in the given spirometry.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Functional residual capacity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tidal volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"Expiratory reserve volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inspiratory reserve volume"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Functional residual capacity: • Spirometry measures airflow in and out of the lungs, which is useful in diagnosing and monitoring lung diseases. • The marked region in the given spirometry represents the Functional Residual Capacity (FRC). • FRC is the lung air volume after a normal, passive exhalation. It is calculated as the sum of the Expiratory Reserve Volume (ERV) and the Residual Volume (RV) and is around 2.3 L. Incorrect Options: Option B - Tidal volume: This is the air that moves in and out of the lungs with each regular breath, normal value is 500 to 750ml.. Option C - Expiratory reserve volume: This is the maximum volume of air that can be voluntarily exhaled after a normal exhalation. It is around 1.1 L. Option D - Inspiratory reserve volume: This is the maximum volume of air that can be inhaled after a normal inhalation. It is around 2L.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 829-829; Solution Pages 852-853","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":829},{"id":"physiology-29-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":26,"question":"A preterm baby who was delivered at 28 weeks developed respiratory distress syndrome. Which of the following is true about surface tension and compliance in this baby?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Surface tension - decreased; Compliance - increased"},{"letter":"B","text":"Surface tension - increased; Compliance - decreased"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both surface tension and compliance decreased"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both surface tension and compliance increased"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Surface tension - increased; Compliance - decreased: • This statement is accurate regarding surface tension and compliance in a preterm baby with respiratory distress syndrome (RDS). • Surface tension: RDS is characterized by a deficiency in pulmonary surfactant, a substance produced by type II pneumocytes in the alveoli due to premature delivery. Surfactant reduces surface tension in the alveoli, preventing their collapse during expiration. In RDS, the lack of surfactant leads to increased surface tension, making it difficult for the alveoli to remain open and causing them to collapse. • Compliance: Compliance refers to the ease with which the lungs can expand. In the absence of surfactant, the alveoli become stiff and less compliant, requiring greater effort for inspiration. Therefore, compliance is decreased in RDS due to the decreased ability of the lungs to expand easily. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 829-829; Solution Pages 853-853","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":829},{"id":"physiology-29-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Which physiological adaptation does not happen at high altitudes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alkaline urine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Polycythemia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Respiratory acidosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoxia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Respiratory acidosis: • The body undergoes respiratory alkalosis instead of respiratory acidosis at high altitudes. This is because the respiratory rate and depth increases to compensate for the reduced oxygen pressure in the air, resulting in depletion of CO2 levels in the bloodstream, causing respiratory alkalosis rather than acidosis. Incorrect Options: Option A - Alkaline urine: To correct respiratory alkalosis, the kidney secrete HCO3- in the urine, thus urine is alkaline in nature. Option B - Polycythemia - Hypoxia stimulates the production of erythropoietin resulting in polycythemia to increase the oxygen-carrying capacity of the blood. Option D - Hypoxia- Hypoxia is a physiological adaptation that occurs at high altitudes due to the decrease in oxygen levels in the air. Hypoxia can lead to a range of symptoms, including shortness of breath, fatigue, and dizziness.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 829-830; Solution Pages 853-854","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":830},{"id":"physiology-29-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":28,"question":"What is indicated by the curve labeled as 'A' in the given oxygen dissociation curves graph?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Myoglobin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Carboxyhemoglobinemia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Methemoglobin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fetal hemoglobin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Myoglobin: • Myoglobin is a protein found in muscle cells that is responsible for storing and delivering oxygen to the muscle tissue. The oxygen dissociation curve for myoglobin is hyperbolic, because in the case of myoglobin, one molecule of hemoglobin binds with one molecule of oxygen, and complete saturation will occur. Therefore, a sudden increase in saturation can be seen in a myoglobin curve. • Thus, A in the graph indicates myoglobin. Incorrect Options: Option B - Carboxyhemoglobinemia: Carboxyhemoglobinemia shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the left and markedly reduces the oxygen dissociation. Option C - Methemoglobin: Methemoglobin is a form of hemoglobin that has been oxidized and can no longer bind to oxygen effectively due to ferric state or iron. Option D - Fetal hemoglobin: Fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin. This shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the left as marked by B.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 830-830; Solution Pages 854-854","sourcePage":830,"sourcePageEnd":830},{"id":"physiology-29-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":29,"question":"2, 3-BPG causes the unloading of oxygen to tissues by ___________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increasing ATP production"},{"letter":"B","text":"Binding to the beta subunit of Hb"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inhibiting taut structure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreasing ATP production"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Binding to the beta subunit of Hb: • 2, 3-BPG is a small molecule that can bind to a pocket in the beta subunit of hemoglobin. • 2,3-DPG carries approximately 3.5 negative charges that interact with eight positively charged amino acids in the central cavity. This interaction destabilizes the binding of Hb with oxygen. • As a result, when 2,3-DPG binds with Hb, it causes the Oxy-Hb dissociation curve to shift to the right, promoting the release of oxygen from red blood cells into the tissues. Incorrect Options: • Option A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 830-830; Solution Pages 855-855","sourcePage":830,"sourcePageEnd":830},{"id":"physiology-29-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which channels are affected by the defect caused by cystic fibrosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na+"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ca2+"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cl-"},{"letter":"D","text":"K+"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Cl- (Chloride): • Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive monogenic disorder, in which the major defect is in the chloride channel. • The disease occurs due to mutation in the CFTR gene (cystic fibrosis transmembrane-conductance regulator gene), located on chromosome 7. • The lung is the most common organ affected, and decreased nasociliary clearance is the common pathology. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 831-831; Solution Pages 855-855","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":831},{"id":"physiology-29-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":31,"question":"A person develops headache and breathlessness on a trekking expedition following a rapid ascent to over 3000 meters above the sea level. Which of the following is not used in the treatment of this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"IV digoxin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Immediate descent"},{"letter":"C","text":"Administration of oxygen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tablet acetazolamide"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - IV digoxin: • IV digoxin is not used in the treatment of the condition described. Digoxin is a medication commonly used for heart conditions such as heart failure and atrial fibrillation. • Altitude sickness is managed by Immediate descent greatly improves the symptoms. Diuretics: Acetazolamide is the preferred diuretic. It inhibits carbonic anhydrase and increases bicarbonate excretion in urine, thus decreasing alkali load. Steroids: Glucocorticoid decreases cerebral edema. Oxygen therapy: Hyperbaric oxygen is very useful if pulmonary edema is present. Nifedipine: Calcium channel blockers like nifedipine help to reduce pulmonary arterial pressure. • Immediate descent greatly improves the symptoms. • Diuretics: Acetazolamide is the preferred diuretic. It inhibits carbonic anhydrase and increases bicarbonate excretion in urine, thus decreasing alkali load. • Steroids: Glucocorticoid decreases cerebral edema. • Oxygen therapy: Hyperbaric oxygen is very useful if pulmonary edema is present. • Nifedipine: Calcium channel blockers like nifedipine help to reduce pulmonary arterial pressure. • Immediate descent greatly improves the symptoms. • Diuretics: Acetazolamide is the preferred diuretic. It inhibits carbonic anhydrase and increases bicarbonate excretion in urine, thus decreasing alkali load. • Steroids: Glucocorticoid decreases cerebral edema. • Oxygen therapy: Hyperbaric oxygen is very useful if pulmonary edema is present. • Nifedipine: Calcium channel blockers like nifedipine help to reduce pulmonary arterial pressure. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 831-831; Solution Pages 855-856","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":831},{"id":"physiology-29-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Respiratory distress syndrome is caused due to defective?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type 1 pneumocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type 2 pneumocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Alveolar capillary endothelial cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bronchial mucosal epithelial cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Type 2 pneumocytes: • Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), also known as neonatal respiratory distress syndrome or infant respiratory distress syndrome, is primarily due to a defect in Type 2 pneumocytes. • Type 2 pneumocytes, also called surfactant-producing cells, play a crucial role in the production and secretion of pulmonary surfactant. Pulmonary surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids, proteins, and other substances that coats the alveoli within the lungs. It reduces surface tension, preventing the alveoli from collapsing during exhalation and promoting efficient gas exchange. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. These cells are not responsible for the production of the surfactant.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 831-831; Solution Pages 856-857","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":831},{"id":"physiology-29-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which of the subsequent factors experiences an elevation in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)? FRC TLC VC FEV1/FVC","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 only"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 and 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 and 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 and 2"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D. 1 and 2 FRC (Functional Residual Capacity): FRC is increased in Chronic Obstructive Pulmonary Disease (COPD). In COPD, there is difficulty exhaling air due to airway obstruction and decreased lung elasticity, leading to air trapping in the lungs during expiration. This increased residual volume results in an elevated FRC. TLC (Total Lung Capacity): TLC is often increased in COPD. This increase is primarily due to the elevated residual volume and FRC. In COPD like emphysema, the lungs are hyperinflated, leading to an increase in TLC. VC (Vital Capacity): VC is reduced in COPD. The vital capacity, which is the maximum amount of air a person can exhale after a maximal inhalation, is reduced due to the airway obstruction and decreased lung elasticity in COPD. FEV1/FVC (Forced Expiratory Volume in 1 second/Forced Vital Capacity ratio): This ratio is reduced in COPD. The reduced FEV1/FVC ratio indicates the presence of airflow obstruction. In COPD, the airflow limitation results in a decreased FEV1/FVC ratio.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 831-832; Solution Pages 857-857","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":832},{"id":"physiology-29-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Which of the following conditions will shift the oxygen-Hb dissociation curve to the left?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypercarbia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Alkalosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increase in 2,3-DPG"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Alkalosis: • Conditions associated with shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve to the left are: Decreased temperature Increased pH Decreased PCO2 Decreased 2,3-DPG Fetal Hb Carbon monoxide • Decreased temperature • Increased pH • Decreased PCO2 • Decreased 2,3-DPG • Fetal Hb • Carbon monoxide • Therefore among the given options alkalosis will result in left shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve. • Decreased temperature • Increased pH • Decreased PCO2 • Decreased 2,3-DPG • Fetal Hb • Carbon monoxide Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. These conditions will result in the right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 832-832; Solution Pages 857-858","sourcePage":832,"sourcePageEnd":832},{"id":"physiology-29-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Which among the following has the highest airway resistance?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Alveolar duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bronchi"},{"letter":"C","text":"Respiratory bronchioles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Small bronchioles"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Bronchi: • The main site of airway resistance is the medium-sized bronchi and bronchi down to approximately the seventh generation. • According to Poiseuille’s law, airway resistance increases with the fourth power of airway radius. However, despite this, small airways contribute only about 10 to 15% to the total airway resistance. This is due to the fact that most small airways are aligned in parallel, and the total surface area of small airways is greater. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 832-832; Solution Pages 858-859","sourcePage":832,"sourcePageEnd":832},{"id":"physiology-29-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which of the following does not cause a right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased HbF"},{"letter":"B","text":"Raised 2,3 DPG"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acidosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased CO2."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Increased HbF: • Factors causing a right shift of the oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve are: Increased temperature Decreased pH Increased PCO2 Increased 2,3-DPG Hypoxia • Increased temperature • Decreased pH • Increased PCO2 • Increased 2,3-DPG • Hypoxia • Instead, increased HbF causes a left shift of the oxygen hemoglobin dissociation curve. • Increased temperature • Decreased pH • Increased PCO2 • Increased 2,3-DPG • Hypoxia Incorrect Options : • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 832-832; Solution Pages 859-859","sourcePage":832,"sourcePageEnd":832},{"id":"physiology-29-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":37,"question":"In the lungs, which type of cells are responsible for the secretion of surfactant?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type 1 pneumocyte"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alveolar macrophage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dust cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Type 2 pneumocyte"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Type 2 pneumocyte: • Type 2 pneumocytes, also called surfactant-producing cells, play a crucial role in the production and secretion of pulmonary surfactant. • Pulmonary surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids, proteins, and other substances that coat the alveoli within the lungs. It reduces surface tension, preventing the alveoli from collapsing during exhalation and promoting efficient gas exchange. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 833-833; Solution Pages 859-860","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":833},{"id":"physiology-29-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Functional residual capacity is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Volume expired after normal inspiration"},{"letter":"B","text":"Volume that remains after forced expiration"},{"letter":"C","text":"Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Expiratory reserve volume + residual volume: • Functional residual capacity (FRC) is the volume of air that remains in the lungs after a normal tidal expiration. • It is calculated by adding the expiratory reserve volume (the amount of air that can still be exhaled after a normal tidal expiration) to the residual volume and is equal to the 2.3L. Incorrect Options: Option A - Volume expired after normal inspiration: The volume expired after a normal inspiration is referred to as the tidal volume and is around 500 ml.. Option B - Volume that remains after forced expiration: The volume that remains after a forced expiration is the residual volume and is around 1.2 L. Option D - Tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume: The sum of the tidal volume and the inspiratory reserve volume represents inspiratory capacity and is around 3.5L.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 833-833; Solution Pages 860-860","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":833},{"id":"physiology-29-39","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":39,"question":"[Question stem not represented in the PDF text layer; source page contains a visual/text prompt.]","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Partial pressure of O2 becomes zero"},{"letter":"B","text":"No exchange of O2 & CO2"},{"letter":"C","text":"CO2 alone equilibrates with the venous blood"},{"letter":"D","text":"Partial pressure of both CO2 and O2 remain normal"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - No exchange of O2 & CO2: • When the ventilation-perfusion ratio (V/Q) is infinite, it means that there is adequate ventilation and no perfusion due to an emboli leading to no exchange of oxygen (O2) and carbon dioxide (CO2) occurring in the lungs. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 833-833; Solution Pages 860-861","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":833},{"id":"physiology-29-40","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":40,"question":"What is the affinity of carbon monoxide to hemoglobin in comparison to oxygen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"100"},{"letter":"B","text":"4"},{"letter":"C","text":"250"},{"letter":"D","text":"150"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - 250: • The affinity of carbon monoxide for hemoglobin is approximately 250 times greater than the affinity of oxygen for hemoglobin. This means that when both gases are present, carbon monoxide will bind to hemoglobin preferentially, reducing the amount of oxygen that can bind and be transported to the body's tissues. This can lead to hypoxia (low oxygen levels) and can be life-threatening. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 833-834; Solution Pages 861-861","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":834},{"id":"physiology-29-41","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-29","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Which of the following breathing patterns have prolonged inspiratory spasms that resemble breath-holding?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Biot breathing"},{"letter":"B","text":"Apneustic breathing"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cheyne stokes breathing"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kussmaul breathing"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Apneustic breathing: • Apneustic breathing is characterized by prolonged inspiratory spasms or gasping breaths that resemble breath-holding with a brief expiration. • The apneustic center results in prolonged inspiration by activating inspiratory neurons leading to deeper inspiration. Incorrect Options: Option A - Biot breathing: Biot breathing is characterized by normal 3 to 4 cycles of breathing followed by abrupt pauses or periods of apnea. Option C - Cheyne-Stokes breathing: Cheyne-Stokes breathing is characterized by a cyclical pattern of apnea and shallow breathing due to an underlying pathology like congestive heart failure. Option D - Kussmaul breathing: Kussmaul breathing is a deep and labored breathing pattern often seen in individuals with diabeticketoacidosis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 834-834; Solution Pages 861-862","sourcePage":834,"sourcePageEnd":834},{"id":"physiology-30-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following secretions is NOT isotonic?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Salivary secretion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastric secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pancreatic secretion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intestinal secretion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Salivary secretion","explanation":"Saliva is a hypotonic secretion, not an isotonic one. • Saliva contains high concentrations of potassium (K+) and bicarbonate (HCO3-) ions and lower concentrations of sodium (Na+) and chloride (Cl-) ions, making it a hypotonic solution. • Except for saliva, all other gastrointestinal secretions, such as Gastric (Option B), Pancreatic (Option C) and intestinal secretions (Option D), have osmolality similar to osmolality plasma, i.e., isotonic. • Saliva is secreted by acinar cells of salivary glands.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 863-863; Solution Pages 872-872","sourcePage":863,"sourcePageEnd":863},{"id":"physiology-30-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements about iron absorption is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Iron absorption starts in the jejunum."},{"letter":"B","text":"Phytic acid in cereals enhances iron absorption."},{"letter":"C","text":"Ferrous (Fe²) form of iron is absorbed more readily than the ferric (Fe³) form."},{"letter":"D","text":"Hepcidin directly binds to transferrin to regulate iron levels."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Ferrous (Fe²■) form of iron is absorbed more readily than the ferric (Fe³■) form.","explanation":"Most dietary iron is in the ferric (Fe³■) form and must be reduced to the ferrous (Fe²■) form by gastric secretions and reductase enzymes in the brush borders of enterocytes. Absorption of Iron: • Most dietary iron is ferric (Fe³■), and the absorbed form is ferrous (Fe²■). • Fe³■ is reduced to Fe²■ by gastric secretions and reductase activity in enterocytes. • Iron absorption occurs mainly in the duodenum via divalent metal transporter 1 (DMT1). • Some Fe²■ is stored in ferritin within enterocytes, and the remaining is transported out by ferroportin 1. • Hephaestin (Hp) aids basolateral transport. • In plasma, Fe²■ converts to Fe³■ and binds to transferrin, a transport protein that delivers iron to various tissues. Iron is stored in the body primarily as: • Ferritin: A soluble protein complex that stores iron safely in the liver, spleen, bone marrow, and intestinal mucosa. • Hemosiderin: A less soluble and more stable iron-storage complex, mainly found in macrophages, especially under iron overload conditions. Iron is primarily absorbed in the jejunum (Option A): Most iron absorption occurs in the duodenum, not the jejunum. The duodenum is the first part of the small intestine immediately beyond the stomach. The acidic environment helps in the reduction of Fe³■ to Fe²■, which is then absorbed. Phytic acid in cereals enhances iron absorption (Option B): Phytic acid in cereals inhibits iron absorption. It binds to iron and forms insoluble complexes that the intestines cannot absorb. Oxalates and phosphates also inhibit iron absorption. Hepcidin directly binds to transferrin to regulate iron levels (Option C): Hepcidin regulates iron levels by binding to ferroportin, causing its degradation. This decreases iron transport from enterocytes into the bloodstream.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 863-863; Solution Pages 872-873","sourcePage":863,"sourcePageEnd":863},{"id":"physiology-30-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which vitamin is primarily absorbed in the ileum of the small intestine and requires an intrinsic factor for its absorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vitamin D"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitamin C"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitamin B12"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitamin K"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Vitamin B12","explanation":"Vitamin B12 is primarily absorbed in the ileum of the small intestine and requires an intrinsic factor for its absorption. Absorption of Vitamins: Type Vitamin Primary Absorption Site Mechanism of Absorption Fat-Solu A, D, E, K Small intestine Require bile salts for ble (Options A and D) (Duodenum, mainly emulsification. jejunum). Incorporated into mixed micelles. Absorbed by passive diffusion into enterocytes. Packaged into chylomicron s for transport. Water-S B Vitamins Jejunum and Ileum. Various mechanisms oluble (B12 specifically in ileum) include: Active transport Passive diffusion Carrier-mediated transport Sodium-dependent transport B12 requires an intrinsic factor C (Optio Sodium-dependent active n B) transport at low concentrations. Passive diffusion at high concentrations.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 863-863; Solution Pages 874-874","sourcePage":863,"sourcePageEnd":863},{"id":"physiology-30-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following is true regarding the process of protein digestion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pepsin initiates the digestion of proteins in the small intestine."},{"letter":"B","text":"Pepsin accounts for 90% of total protein digestion."},{"letter":"C","text":"Collagen digestion starts in the small intestine."},{"letter":"D","text":"The final step of protein digestion occurs inside the cytosol of Enterocytes."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) The final step of protein digestion occurs inside the cytosol of Enterocytes.","explanation":"The final step of protein digestion takes place inside the cytosol of Enterocytes, where they break down remaining dipeptides and tripeptides into amino acids. Process of Protein Digestion: 1. In Stomach: • Enzyme: Pepsin (Optimal pH: 2.0 to 3.0) • Function: Pepsin initiates protein digestion (Option A) by breaking proteins into proteoses, peptones, and smaller polypeptides. • Collagen Breakdown: Pepsin is crucial for digesting collagen in meats (Option C), allowing other digestive enzymes to access and break down meat proteins. • Contribution: Pepsin accounts for 10-20% of total protein digestion. (Option B). 2. In Small Intestine: (Duodenum and Jejunum) • Most of the Protein Digestion occurs in SI. • Enzymes: Pancreatic proteolytic enzymes (trypsin, chymotrypsin, carboxypolypeptidase, elastase) • Function: Trypsin and Chymotrypsin: Split proteins into small polypeptides. Carboxypolypeptidase: Cleaves individual amino acids from polypeptides. Elastase: Digests elastin fibres in meats. • Trypsin and Chymotrypsin: Split proteins into small polypeptides. • Carboxypolypeptidase: Cleaves individual amino acids from polypeptides. • Elastase: Digests elastin fibres in meats. • Outcome: Proteins are mostly reduced to dipeptides and tripeptides. • Most amino acids are absorbed by sodium through a co-transporter molecule, which is part of secondary active transport. They can also be absorbed in the form of di or tripeptides by the PepT1 Transporter. • Trypsin and Chymotrypsin: Split proteins into small polypeptides. • Carboxypolypeptidase: Cleaves individual amino acids from polypeptides. • Elastase: Digests elastin fibres in meats. 3. Final Digestion and Absorption: (Brush border of enterocytes in the small intestine villi) • Enzymes: Aminopolypeptidase and dipeptidases • Function: Brush Border Peptidases Intracellular Peptidases • Brush Border Peptidases • Intracellular Peptidases • Brush Border Peptidases • Intracellular Peptidases Break down remaining dipeptides and tripeptides into amino acids within the enterocyte cytosol. (Final Step) • Absorption: Over 99% of absorbed protein digestion products are individual amino acids. Peptides and whole protein molecules are rarely absorbed, and they can cause allergic reactions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 863-864; Solution Pages 874-876","sourcePage":863,"sourcePageEnd":864},{"id":"physiology-30-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A newborn presents with severe diarrhoea and dehydration shortly after birth. Stool analysis reveals undigested and unabsorbed carbohydrates. Which of the following mechanisms is most likely impaired in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SGLT1-mediated absorption of glucose and galactose"},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT5-mediated absorption of fructose"},{"letter":"C","text":"Peptidase-mediated breakdown"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lipase mediated breakdown"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) SGLT1-mediated absorption of glucose and galactose","explanation":"Glucose Galactose Malabsorption (GGM) is a rare genetic disorder caused by mutations in the SGLT1 gene, which encodes for the sodium-glucose cotransporter 1 (SGLT-1). When glucose and galactose are not absorbed, they remain in the intestinal lumen, which draws water due to their osmotic properties resulting in osmotic diarrhoea and dehydration, as seen in this newborn. Mechanism of Carbohydrate Absorption: Transporters involved in the Mechanism of absorption of Carbohydrates: • Glucose and Galactose Absorption (SGLT1): SGLT1 is a transporter found on the surface of intestinal cells. It actively transports glucose and galactose from the intestine into these cells using energy derived from sodium ions moving down their concentration gradient. (Secondary active transport). • SGLT1 is a transporter found on the surface of intestinal cells. • It actively transports glucose and galactose from the intestine into these cells using energy derived from sodium ions moving down their concentration gradient. (Secondary active transport). • SGLT1 is a transporter found on the surface of intestinal cells. • It actively transports glucose and galactose from the intestine into these cells using energy derived from sodium ions moving down their concentration gradient. (Secondary active transport). • Fructose Absorption (GLUT5): GLUT5 (Option B) is another transporter located on the surface of intestinal cells. It facilitates the diffusion of fructose from the intestine into these cells without requiring energy (Facilitated diffusion). Fructose is transported independently of sodium. • GLUT5 (Option B) is another transporter located on the surface of intestinal cells. • It facilitates the diffusion of fructose from the intestine into these cells without requiring energy (Facilitated diffusion). • Fructose is transported independently of sodium. • GLUT5 (Option B) is another transporter located on the surface of intestinal cells. • It facilitates the diffusion of fructose from the intestine into these cells without requiring energy (Facilitated diffusion). • Fructose is transported independently of sodium. • GLUT 2: It transports glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane of intestinal cells into the bloodstream. This ensures that absorbed glucose, galactose, and fructose enter the bloodstream for cell use throughout the body. • It transports glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane of intestinal cells into the bloodstream. • This ensures that absorbed glucose, galactose, and fructose enter the bloodstream for cell use throughout the body. • It transports glucose, galactose, and fructose across the basolateral membrane of intestinal cells into the bloodstream. • This ensures that absorbed glucose, galactose, and fructose enter the bloodstream for cell use throughout the body. Peptidases (Option C): Peptidases are enzymes that break down proteins into peptides and amino acids, not carbohydrates. Lipases (Option D): Lipases are associated with the breakdown of Fats. An impairment in lipase activity would lead to fat malabsorption and steatorrhea (fatty stools), not undigested carbohydrates.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 864-864; Solution Pages 876-878","sourcePage":864,"sourcePageEnd":864},{"id":"physiology-30-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following intestinal cells are likely to have a high zinc concentration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Goblet cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Enteroendocrine cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Enterocytes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Paneth cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Paneth cells","explanation":"Zinc plays a critical role in immune function and is particularly concentrated in Paneth cells within the small intestine. • Paneth cells are specialised immune cells found in the intestinal crypts of Lieberkuhn, primarily in the small intestine. • They are known for secreting antimicrobial peptides, such as defensins and lysozymes, which help protect the gut against infections. Goblet cells (Option A): • Goblet cells are specialised epithelial cells throughout the intestines that secrete mucus. • This mucus forms a protective layer over the epithelial surface, safeguarding the intestinal lining from mechanical and chemical damage. Enteroendocrine cells (Option B): • Enteroendocrine cells are scattered throughout the gastrointestinal tract and produce hormones like serotonin, gastrin, and cholecystokinin. These hormones are involved in digestion, appetite regulation, and gut motility. Enterocytes (Option C): • Enterocytes are the absorptive cells lining the intestinal villi and are responsible for absorbing nutrients such as carbohydrates, proteins, fats, vitamins, and minerals.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 864-864; Solution Pages 878-878","sourcePage":864,"sourcePageEnd":864},{"id":"physiology-30-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 50-year-old patient with obstructive jaundice from a common bile duct stone, confirmed by elevated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase, underwent successful stone extraction via ERCP. Post-procedure, the patient received education on bile flow regulation. Which of the following is not associated with bile synthesis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cholecystokinin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cholesterol"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bile salts"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Cholecystokinin","explanation":"CCK primarily stimulates the contraction of the gallbladder, which releases stored bile into the duodenum to aid in fat digestion. It also enhances pancreatic enzyme secretion. However, CCK does not directly regulate the synthesis of bile in the liver. Regulation of Bile: Hormonal Secretin (Option B): Stimulates the secretion of a Regulation watery, bicarbonate-rich solution by epithelial cells in the bile ducts. Cholecystokinin (CCK): Induces gallbladder contraction and relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi, facilitating the release of concentrated bile into the duodenum in response to fatty foods. Neural Regulatio Vagal Stimulation: Promotes weak gallbladder n contraction. Enteric Nervous System: Nerve fibres in the intestine stimulate gallbladder motility and secretion. Feedback It is influenced by the presence of fats and acids in the Regulation duodenum. Bile salts are the most potent regulators of Bile synthesis, which is regulated by the feedback mechanism. (Option D) Cholesterol (Option C): Cholesterol is a precursor for bile acids. In the liver, it is converted into primary bile acids (such as cholic acid and chenodeoxycholic acid), which are essential components of bile and are thus involved in bile synthesis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 864-865; Solution Pages 879-879","sourcePage":864,"sourcePageEnd":865},{"id":"physiology-30-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which hormone is primarily responsible for stimulating the secretion of pancreatic digestive enzymes in response to the presence of peptides and fatty acids in the small intestine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cholecystokinin (CCK)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gastrin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Cholecystokinin (CCK)","explanation":"When peptides and fatty acids enter the small intestine, Cholecystokinin (CCK) is the hormone primarily responsible for stimulating the secretion of pancreatic digestive enzymes. Pancreatic secretion is regulated by three key stimuli: • Acetylcholine, mainly released from vagal nerve endings in the enteric nervous system, plays a minor role in stimulating both acinar and duct cells. • Cholecystokinin (CCK), secreted by the duodenal and upper jejunal mucosa in response to amino acids and fatty acids, is the primary stimulus for acinar cells to produce enzymes. • Secretin, secreted by the duodenal and jejunal mucosa in response to acidic food in the small intestine, is the most important stimulus for duct cells to produce bicarbonate. Pancreatic Digestive Enzymes: Pancreatic secretion contains multiple enzymes for digesting all of the three major types of food (proteins, carbohydrates, fats) • Proteins: Trypsin, chymotrypsin, carboxypolypeptidase • Carbohydrates: Pancreatic Amylase • Fats: Pancreatic Lipase, Cholesterol Esterase, Phospholipase • Acidity Regulation: Bicarbonate","sourceRef":"Question Pages 865-865; Solution Pages 879-880","sourcePage":865,"sourcePageEnd":865},{"id":"physiology-30-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 45-year-old male patient complains of frequent heartburn and bloating after meals, which worsens with large or spicy foods. He experiences relief with antacids, indicating excess gastric acid secretion. Which phase of gastric secretion is likely responsible for these symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cephalic Phase"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastric Phase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intestinal Phase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Basal Phase"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Gastric Phase","explanation":"This patient's symptoms of heartburn and bloating after meals, exacerbated by large or spicy foods, suggest excessive gastric acid production. The Gastric phase of secretion accounts for about 60% of total gastric secretion. Phases of Gastric secretion: Cephalic Trigger: Initiated by sensory stimuli such as sight, smell, Phase thought, or taste of food. Origin: Neurogenic signals originate in the cerebral cortex, amygdala, and hypothalamus and are transmitted via vagus nerves to stimulate gastric secretion. Contribution: Accounts for approximately 30% of gastric secretion Associated with meal anticipation and preparation. Gastric Trigger: Begins when food enters the stomach. Phase Mechanisms: Long vasovagal reflexes between the stomach and brain. Local enteric reflexes within the stomach. Gastrin release, which stimulates gastric juice secretion. Duration: It lasts several hours while food remains in the stomach. Contribution: Accounts for about 60% of total gastric secretion. Intestinal Trigger: Initiated by food reaching the duodenum of the phase small intestine. Mechanism: Small amounts of gastrin released by duodenal mucosa continue to stimulate gastric juice secretion. Contribution: Contributes to approximately 10% of the total acid response to a meal, ensuring continued digestive support as food progresses through the digestive tract.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 865-865; Solution Pages 880-881","sourcePage":865,"sourcePageEnd":865},{"id":"physiology-30-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following factors is associated with increased gastric acid secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Somatostatin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Histamine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcitonin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Histamine","explanation":"Histamine increases gastric acid secretion by acting on H2 receptors in the parietal cells of the stomach. Regulation of gastric acid secretion: 1. Histamine: • It is released from the stomach's enterochromaffin-like (ECL) cells and binds to H2 receptors on parietal cells. • This activates the Gs protein, raising cAMP levels and activating protein kinase A (PKA), which enhances the proton pump activity and increases gastric acid secretion. 2. Acetylcholine: • It is released from the vagus nerve and acts on M3 receptors of parietal cells. • This interaction activates the Gq protein, leading to an increase in intracellular calcium. • The rise in calcium activates protein kinase C (PKC) and calmodulin, which stimulate the proton pump, increasing acid secretion. 3. Gastrin: • It is produced by G cells in the stomach's antrum and binds to CCK-B2 receptors on parietal and Enterochromaffin (ECL) cells. • On parietal cells, this activates the Gq protein, raising intracellular calcium levels and activating protein kinase C (PKC), which stimulates the proton pump, increasing acid secretion. • Gastrin stimulates ECL cells to release histamine, further enhancing acid secretion. Factors influencing gastric acid secretion: Factors increasing gastric acid Factors inhibiting gastric acid secretion secretion Luminal Peptides and amino acids. Acidic pH Stomach distention Somatostatin (Option A) Neural Increased vagal discharge via GRP – (Gastrin-Releasing Peptide) Blood bur Calcium Secretin (Option B) n Epinephrine GIP (Gastric Inhibitory Peptide) VIP (Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide) Glucagon Calcitonin (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 865-866; Solution Pages 881-882","sourcePage":865,"sourcePageEnd":866},{"id":"physiology-30-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following is true regarding the mechanism of gastric acid secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Release of K+ into the lumen by proton pump."},{"letter":"B","text":"Secretion by the parietal cells in the stomach lining."},{"letter":"C","text":"Intake of H+ by proton pump."},{"letter":"D","text":"H+ and Cl- are released together into the lumen."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Secretion by the parietal cells in the stomach lining","explanation":"Parietal cells or oxyntic cells, which are present in the stomach mucosa, primarily secrete gastric acid, i.e., Hydrochloric acid (HCl). • HCl helps kill bacteria and digest proteins. • Stomach mucosa has two significant types of tubular glands Mechanism of Gastric acid secretion: After meals, H+ and Chloride ion secretion is enhanced, and bicarbonate molecules enter the blood, making it alkaline. This is known as postprandial alkaline tide.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 866-866; Solution Pages 882-883","sourcePage":866,"sourcePageEnd":866},{"id":"physiology-30-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 35-year-old female complains of excessive salivation irrespective of any sight or smell of food. Which of the following is true considering the autonomic regulation of salivary secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parasympathetic increases secretion, and Sympathetic decreases secretion."},{"letter":"B","text":"Parasympathetic decreases secretion, and Sympathetic increases secretion."},{"letter":"C","text":"Both Sympathetic and Parasympathetic increase secretion."},{"letter":"D","text":"Both Sympathetic and Parasympathetic decrease secretion."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Both Sympathetic and Parasympathetic increase secretion.","explanation":"Both Sympathetic and Parasympathetic increase salivary secretion in different pathways. Autonomic regulation of salivary secretion Aspect Sympathetic System Parasympathetic System Salivary Secretion Produces less saliva Produces a larger volume of saliva. Saliva Characteristics Mucus-rich and thick. Watery and less viscous. Effect on Blood No significant effect. Dilates blood vessels, increasing Vessels gland nutrition Saliva Composition Less bicarbonate and K+ Higher concentrations of K+ and bicarbonate Saliva Tonicity Generally more viscous and less Hypotonic hypotonic. Mechanism of Salivary secretion:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 866-866; Solution Pages 883-884","sourcePage":866,"sourcePageEnd":866},{"id":"physiology-30-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which cells in the duodenum and jejunum secrete the hormone that stimulates bile release and pancreatic enzyme secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"G cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"I cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"S cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"K cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) I cells","explanation":"Hormone Secreting Location Function Cells CCK (Cholecystokinin) I cells Duodenum Stimulates: (Option B) Jejunum Pancreatic enzyme secretion Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion Gallbladder contraction (Release of bile) Growth of exocrine pancreas Inhibits: Gastric emptying Gastrin G cells Stomach (antrum) Stimulates: (Option A) Duodenum Gastric acid secretion Mucosal growth Secretin S cells Duodenum Stimulates: (Option C) Pepsin secretion Pancreatic bicarbonate secretion Biliary bicarbonate secretion Growth of exocrine pancreas Inhibits: Gastrin release and gastric acid secretion GIP (Glucose-dependent K cells Duodenum Stimulates: Insulinotropic Peptide) (Option D) Jejunum Insulin release from pancreatic β-cells β-cell proliferation and survival Glucose uptake in adipose tissue Inhibits: Gastric acid secretion Gastric motility","sourceRef":"Question Pages 866-866; Solution Pages 884-885","sourcePage":866,"sourcePageEnd":866},{"id":"physiology-30-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 55-year-old woman presents with symptoms of acid reflux and indigestion due to high acid content in her stomach. Which of the following is the most alkaline secretion in the GIT that can neutralise the acid content in her stomach?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Small intestinal juice"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreatic juice"},{"letter":"C","text":"Saliva"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gastric juice"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Pancreatic juice","explanation":"Among the given options, Pancreatic juice is more alkaline as its secretions contain high concentrations of bicarbonate ions. But in general, Brunner’s gland secretion is more alkaline than pancreatic juice. Type of secretion pH Saliva 6.0-7.0 (Option C) Gastric secretion 1.0-3.5 (Option D) Pancreatic secretion 8.0-8.3 (Option B) Bile 7.8 Small intestine secretion 7.5-8.0 (Option A) Brunner’s gland secretion 8.0-8.9 Large intestinal secretion 7.5-8.0","sourceRef":"Question Pages 867-867; Solution Pages 885-886","sourcePage":867,"sourcePageEnd":867},{"id":"physiology-30-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 25-year-old man presents with acute diarrhoea after returning from a trip from southeast Asia. Laboratory tests confirm an infection with enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC). Which of the following peptides is most likely influenced by this bacterial toxin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Motilin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Guanylin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcitonin gene receptor peptide (CGRP)"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Guanylin","explanation":"Guanylin is the peptide hormone that is influenced by Enterotoxigenic Escherichia coli (ETEC). • Guanylin promotes fluid absorption in the small and large intestines. • Serum ProGuanylin levels indicate intestinal guanylin levels and reflect intestinal fluid balance. • Heat-Stable Enterotoxin of E. coli: Activates guanylyl cyclase enzyme, thus increasing cGMP levels. It reduces intestinal guanylin levels. This results in excessive water and electrolyte secretion into the GIT lumen, leading to secretory diarrhoea. • Activates guanylyl cyclase enzyme, thus increasing cGMP levels. • It reduces intestinal guanylin levels. • This results in excessive water and electrolyte secretion into the GIT lumen, leading to secretory diarrhoea. • Activates guanylyl cyclase enzyme, thus increasing cGMP levels. • It reduces intestinal guanylin levels. • This results in excessive water and electrolyte secretion into the GIT lumen, leading to secretory diarrhoea. Motilin (Option A): This is involved in stimulating gastric and small intestine motility during fasting. It helps initiate and regulate migratory motor complexes and is not typically influenced by E. coli. Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) (Option C): VIP can induce intestinal water and electrolyte secretion, but it is not specifically targeted by E. coli enterotoxins. VIP-induced diarrhoea is seen in VIPomas. Calcitonin gene receptor peptide (CGRP) (Option D): CGRP is involved in somatostatin release, which is an inhibitor of GIT secretion; it also causes smooth muscle contraction","sourceRef":"Question Pages 867-867; Solution Pages 886-887","sourcePage":867,"sourcePageEnd":867},{"id":"physiology-30-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following GI hormones is not responsible for insulin secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GLP-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"GIP"},{"letter":"C","text":"VIP"},{"letter":"D","text":"CCK"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) VIP","explanation":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide is not responsible for insulin secretion from the pancreas. GI Hormones associated with the secretion of insulin from the pancreas: HORMONE STIMULI CELLS GIP (Gastric inhibitory peptide) Protein, Fats, Carbohydrates. K cells of duodenum and jejunum. (A.k.a Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide) Glucagon-like peptide L cells in the small intestine. CCK Protein, Fats, Acid I cells in duodenum and jejunum. Gastrin Protein, Gastric distension, Vagal G cells in gastric antrum, duodenum stimulation and jejunum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 867-867; Solution Pages 887-887","sourcePage":867,"sourcePageEnd":867},{"id":"physiology-30-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 52-year-old patient presents with symptoms of indigestion and steatorrhea. Laboratory tests suggest decreased bicarbonate secretion from the pancreas. Which Gastrointestinal hormone is most likely deficient in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cholecystokinin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Motilin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Secretin","explanation":"• Secretin is released from the S cells in response to acid and fat in the duodenum and is responsible for bicarbonate secretion in the GIT. • Functions: Pepsin secretion - helps in protein digestion (patient is suffering from indigestion because of deficiency of this function) Pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion - neutralises the acid in the duodenum Stimulates hepatic ductal secretion Growth of exocrine pancreas Inhibition of gastrin release and direct gastric acid secretion It also inhibits gastric emptying and small intestinal motility. • Pepsin secretion - helps in protein digestion (patient is suffering from indigestion because of deficiency of this function) • Pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion - neutralises the acid in the duodenum • Stimulates hepatic ductal secretion • Growth of exocrine pancreas • Inhibition of gastrin release and direct gastric acid secretion • It also inhibits gastric emptying and small intestinal motility. • Pepsin secretion - helps in protein digestion (patient is suffering from indigestion because of deficiency of this function) • Pancreatic and biliary bicarbonate secretion - neutralises the acid in the duodenum • Stimulates hepatic ductal secretion • Growth of exocrine pancreas • Inhibition of gastrin release and direct gastric acid secretion • It also inhibits gastric emptying and small intestinal motility.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 868-868; Solution Pages 887-888","sourcePage":868,"sourcePageEnd":868},{"id":"physiology-30-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding the effects of Cholecystokinin on GI motility?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inhibition of gastric motility"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contraction of sphincter of Oddi"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stimulation of intestinal peristalsis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Contraction of pyloric sphincter"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Contraction of sphincter of Oddi","explanation":"Cholecystokinin is the major stimulus for gallbladder emptying and pancreatic enzyme secretion; thus, it causes relaxation of the sphincter of Oddi. Effects of Cholecystokinin on GI motility: • Inhibition of gastric motility (Option A). • Contraction of pyloric sphincter (Option D) . This allows adequate time for the fatty food in the upper SI to get emulsified and digested. • This allows adequate time for the fatty food in the upper SI to get emulsified and digested. • Stimulation of gallbladder emptying. This expels bile into the small intestine, helping to emulsify fat and aid in fat digestion. • This expels bile into the small intestine, helping to emulsify fat and aid in fat digestion. • Relaxation of sphincter of Oddi. • Stimulation of intestinal peristalsis (Option C) . • This allows adequate time for the fatty food in the upper SI to get emulsified and digested. • This expels bile into the small intestine, helping to emulsify fat and aid in fat digestion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 868-868; Solution Pages 888-889","sourcePage":868,"sourcePageEnd":868},{"id":"physiology-30-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a gastrointestinal hormone that acts on the pancreatic acinar cells to release all of the following except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bicarbonate ions"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreatic lipase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trypsinogen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chymotrypsinogen"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Bicarbonate ions","explanation":"Cholecystokinin causes the release of pancreatic enzymes and not electrolytes, i.e. bicarbonate. Pancreatic secretion of bicarbonate is stimulated by Secretin and not by CCK. Pancreatic secretion is regulated by 3 basic stimuli: • Acetylcholine: Released from the vagus nerve and other cholinergic nerves in the enteric system. • Cholecystokinin: Released from the duodenum and upper jejunal mucosa when food enters the duodenum. • Secretin: Released by duodenal and upper jejunal mucosa when acidic food enters the duodenum. Actions of CCK: • Stimulation of Pancreatic enzyme secretion-Amylase, Lipase and Trypsinogen (Options B, C & D) Growth of exocrine pancreas. Gallbladder contraction. • Pancreatic enzyme secretion-Amylase, Lipase and Trypsinogen (Options B, C & D) • Growth of exocrine pancreas. • Gallbladder contraction. • Relaxation of sphincter of Oddi. • Inhibition of gastric emptying. • Pancreatic enzyme secretion-Amylase, Lipase and Trypsinogen (Options B, C & D) • Growth of exocrine pancreas. • Gallbladder contraction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 868-868; Solution Pages 889-890","sourcePage":868,"sourcePageEnd":868},{"id":"physiology-30-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following is the primary stimulus for secretin release?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fatty acids in the small intestine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Amino acids in the small intestine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acidic chyme entering the duodenum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Distention of the stomach"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Acidic chyme entering the duodenum","explanation":"Secretin's release is primarily triggered by the low pH (high acidity) of the chyme. Acidic chyme (pH < 4.5) enters the duodenum → S cells in duodenal mucosa detect low pH → Secretin is released into the bloodstream. Stimulus for Secretin and CCK: Hormones Stimuli Secretin Primary: Acidic chyme entering the duodenum (pH < 4.5) Secondary: Fatty acids in the duodenum Peptides Cholecystokinin Partially digested proteins (peptides) (CCK) Fatty acids in the small intestine. Amino acids in the small intestine.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 869-869; Solution Pages 890-890","sourcePage":869,"sourcePageEnd":869},{"id":"physiology-30-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 65-year-old woman presents with fatigue, shortness of breath, and a sore tongue. Blood tests reveal macrocytic anaemia with low vitamin B12 levels. Further testing shows the presence of anti-intrinsic factor antibodies. What would you expect regarding this patient's serum gastrin levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Normal gastrin levels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fluctuating gastrin levels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Elevated gastrin levels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Low gastrin levels"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Elevated gastrin levels","explanation":"Pernicious anaemia is an autoimmune condition in which the body produces antibodies against parietal cells. These cells in the stomach produce intrinsic factors and hydrochloric acid. In pernicious anaemia, gastrin levels are typically severely elevated, often 10-20 times the normal range. Clinical implications: Severely elevated gastrin levels cause hyperplasia of enterochromaffin-like cells in the stomach, which can potentially progress to gastric carcinoid tumours in some cases.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 869-869; Solution Pages 891-891","sourcePage":869,"sourcePageEnd":869},{"id":"physiology-30-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a primary function of a hormone responsible for gastric acid production?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulation of gastric acid secretion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trophic action on gastric mucosa"},{"letter":"C","text":"Relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stimulation of gastric motility"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter","explanation":"Gastrin causes the contraction (not relaxation) of the lower oesophageal sphincter, which helps prevent the reflux of stomach contents into the oesophagus. Functions of Gastrin: Gastric Secretion Stimulates gastric acid secretion from parietal cells. Promotes pepsinogen secretion from chief cells. Gastric Motility Enhances gastric motility and Promotes gastric emptying. Trophic Effects Stimulates growth and proliferation of gastric mucosa. Sphincter Control It causes contraction of the lower oesophagal sphincter (LES) and prevents reflux.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 869-869; Solution Pages 891-892","sourcePage":869,"sourcePageEnd":869},{"id":"physiology-30-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-30","chapter":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topic":"GIT Hormones, Secretions, and Absorption Mechanisms","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following does NOT stimulate the secretion of Gastrin hormone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Presence of proteins in the stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Parasympathetic stimuli"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distension of the stomach"},{"letter":"D","text":"Low pH in the stomach"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Low pH in the stomach","explanation":"Increased acidity (Low pH) doesn’t stimulate the secretion of Gastrin; rather, it inhibits the secretion through a negative feedback mechanism. Stimuli for Gastrin Release Chemical Stimuli Proteins and amino acids in the stomach (Option A) Peptides Calcium ions Mechanical Stimuli Distension of the stomach (Option C) Neural Stimuli Vagal nerve stimulation Parasympathetic nervous system activation (Option B) Other Factors Caffeine Alcohol Certain medications (e.g., antacids) Absence of Inhibitory Factors Decreased acidity in the stomach (high pH)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 869-870; Solution Pages 892-892","sourcePage":869,"sourcePageEnd":870},{"id":"physiology-31-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":1,"question":"All the factors inhibit gastric emptying except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"B","text":"A large volume of solid meals"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypertonic solution"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypotonic solution"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Hypotonic solution in duodenum","explanation":"Hypotonic solutions do not inhibit gastric emptying. In fact, they may even promote faster gastric emptying due to their lower osmolarity. Factors Promoting Gastric Emptying: Factor Mechanism Vagal stimulation Stimulates antral contraction, promoting gastric emptying Liquid meal Faster gastric emptying due to lower viscosity and faster transit through the stomach Motilin Stimulates antral contraction and gastric emptying, especially during the interdigestive state Factors Inhibiting Gastric Emptying: Factor Mechanism Acid in the duodenum Releases secretin, inhibiting antral contraction and stimulating pyloric sphincter contraction Products of fat digestion Releases CCK and GIP, inhibiting gastric emptying Hypertonic solutions in the Releases hormones inhibiting gastric emptying duodenum Products of protein digestion Releases gastrin, CCK, and GIP, inhibiting gastric emptying Large meal volume (solids) Prolongs gastric emptying time Stretching of duodenum Initiates enterogastric reflex, inhibiting gastric emptying Sympathetic stimulation Decreased gastric motility (by norepinephrine) and increased pyloric sphincter tone. Hormones (CCK, GIP, secretin) -","sourceRef":"Question Pages 893-893; Solution Pages 897-898","sourcePage":893,"sourcePageEnd":893},{"id":"physiology-31-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the roles of the myenteric (Auerbach's) plexus and the submucosal (Meissner's) plexus in the enteric nervous system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Myenteric: enzyme secretion; Submucosal: motility"},{"letter":"B","text":"Myenteric: motility; Submucosal: enzyme secretion and blood flow"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both: motility"},{"letter":"D","text":"Submucosal: rhythmic contractions; Myenteric: mucosal surface area"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) Myenteric: motility; Submucosal: enzyme secretion and blood flow Explanation: The enteric nervous system is composed of two well-organised neural plexus:- Feature Myenteric (Auerbach’s) Plexus Submucosal (Meissner’s) Plexus Location Between the longitudinal and Between the circular muscle and circular muscle layers luminal mucosa Primary Function Regulates gastrointestinal motility Controls enzyme secretion & blood flow Control Over Peristalsis & muscle contractions Secretion, absorption, and blood flow Involvement in the digestive Movement of food through the GI Regulation of the internal Process tract environment of the GI tract The image below shows the layers of the GI tract. 1. Mucosa: Epithelium 6. Duct of gland outside 11. Vein 16. Auerbach’s tract myenteric plexus 2. Mucosa: Lamina 7. Gland in mucosa 12. Muscularis: Circular 17. Nerve propria muscle 3. Mucosa: Muscularis 8. Submucosa 13. Muscularis: 18. Artery mucosae Longitudinal muscle 4. Lumen 9. Glands in submucosa 14. Serosa: Areola 19. Mesentry connective tissue 5. Lymphatic tissue 10. Meissner’s 15. Serosa: Epithelium submucosal plexus","sourceRef":"Question Pages 893-893; Solution Pages 898-899","sourcePage":893,"sourcePageEnd":893},{"id":"physiology-31-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 3-day-old newborn has not passed meconium since birth. The baby has a distended abdomen and frequent vomiting, which sometimes contains bile. Upon physical examination, there was a lack of stool in the rectal vault. An abdominal X-ray reveals a dilated colon with a narrowed segment near the rectum. What is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Meconium ileus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased enteric ganglion cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intestinal atresia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endothelial B receptor gene mutation"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Endothelial B receptor gene mutation","explanation":"Hirschsprung’s disease (Aganglionic megacolon): It is due to congenital absence of enteric ganglion cells in both myentric and meissner’s plexus in segment of distal colon due to failure of cranial to caudal migration of neural crest cells during development. Causes: • Endothelial B receptor gene mutation. • Neural crest cell migration defect. • RET proto-oncogene mutation. Symptoms: • Failure to pass meconium within the first 48 hours of life. • Abdominal distention. • Bilious vomiting. • Constipation. • Failure to thrive. Clinical Features: • Lack of stool in the rectal vault on digital rectal examination. • Explosive release of gas and stool upon withdrawal of the examining finger (squirt sign). • Chronic constipation in older children.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 893-893; Solution Pages 899-900","sourcePage":893,"sourcePageEnd":893},{"id":"physiology-31-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following is responsible for the basal electrical rhythm in the gastrointestinal tract (GIT)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parietal cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Chief cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Interstitial cells of Cajal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enteroendocrine cells"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Interstitial cells of Cajal","explanation":"Basal Electrical Rhythm: • Except in the oesophagus and proximal portion of the stomach, the smooth muscle of the GIT has spontaneous rhythmic fluctuations in membrane potential between -65 and -45 mV. • The interstitial cells of Cajal are stellate mesenchymal pacemaker cells with smooth muscle-like features that send long, multiply branched processes into the intestinal smooth muscle, generating the basal electrical rhythm (BER). • The function of BER is to coordinate peristaltic and other motor activity, such as setting the rhythm of segmentation and contraction only during the depolarising part of the wave. • Spike potentials superimposed on the most depolarising portions of the BER waves do increase muscle tension (figure below). • The depolarising portion of each spike is due to calcium influx, and the repolarising portion is due to potassium efflux. • Acetylcholine increases the number of spikes and tension of the smooth muscle, whereas Epinephrine decreases them. • The rate of BER: Stomach: 4/min. Distal ileum: 8/min. Duodenum: 12/min. • Stomach: 4/min. • Distal ileum: 8/min. • Duodenum: 12/min. • The BER rate in the colon is about 2/min at the cecum and about 6/min at the sigmoid colon. • Stomach: 4/min. • Distal ileum: 8/min. • Duodenum: 12/min.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 894-894; Solution Pages 900-901","sourcePage":894,"sourcePageEnd":894},{"id":"physiology-31-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What is the movement responsible for the movement of food bolus in the gastrointestinal tract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Segmentation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peristalsis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tonic contractions"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mass movement"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Peristalsis","explanation":"Peristalsis: • Reflex response is initiated by the stretching of the gut wall due to the contents of the lumen. Location: • It occurs throughout the gastrointestinal tract, from the esophagus to the rectum. Mechanism: • Stretching triggers circular contraction behind the stimulus and relaxation in front of it. • The wave of contraction moves in an oral-to-caudal direction. • Propels the contents of the lumen forward at rates of 2 to 25 cm/s. Regulation: • Peristaltic activity is influenced by autonomic input but occurs independently of extrinsic innervation. • Local stretch releases serotonin, activating sensory neurons that stimulate the myenteric plexus. • Cholinergic neurons in the myenteric plexus: In retrograde direction: release substance P and acetylcholine, causing contraction behind the bolus. In anterograde direction: activate the neurons releasing nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP), causing relaxation ahead of the bolus (Figure below). • In retrograde direction: release substance P and acetylcholine, causing contraction behind the bolus. • In anterograde direction: activate the neurons releasing nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP), causing relaxation ahead of the bolus (Figure below). • In retrograde direction: release substance P and acetylcholine, causing contraction behind the bolus. • In anterograde direction: activate the neurons releasing nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal polypeptide (VIP), causing relaxation ahead of the bolus (Figure below). Functional Integration: • Peristalsis is an example of the integrated activity of the enteric nervous system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 894-894; Solution Pages 901-902","sourcePage":894,"sourcePageEnd":894},{"id":"physiology-31-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which reflex is responsible for initiating mass movements in the colon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enterogastric reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrocolic reflex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Rectocolic reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Colorectal reflex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Gastrocolic reflex","explanation":"Mass Movement in Colon: Coordinated contractions of the colon that propel faecal material towards the rectum. Gastrocolic reflex: The gastrocolic reflex is initiated when food accumulates in the stomach. • This reflex increases the motility of the colon in response to the stretching of the stomach walls after food intake. • Therefore, the urge for defecation is usually enhanced after eating a large meal. • The gastrocolic reflex can be more active in children, often leading to a more immediate urge to defecate after eating. • While gastrin, a hormone secreted by the stomach in response to gastric distension, plays a role, neural factors also contribute to the reflex. • The involvement of the enteric and autonomic nervous systems is significant in mediating this reflex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 894-894; Solution Pages 902-903","sourcePage":894,"sourcePageEnd":894},{"id":"physiology-31-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What type of motility retards the movement of intestinal contents to provide time for digestion and absorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Peristalsis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Segmentation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mass movement"},{"letter":"D","text":"Propulsion"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Segmentation","explanation":"Segmentation: The enteric nervous system promotes a motility pattern that is related to peristalsis but retards the movement of intestinal contents along the length of the intestinal tract to provide time for digestion and absorption. This is the most common movement of the small intestine. • It is characterised by closely spaced contractions of the circular muscle layer. These contractions divide the small intestine into many segments. • These contractions divide the small intestine into many segments. • The rate of segmental contraction is the same as the frequency of slow waves. Duodenum: 18/min. Jejunum: 15/min. Ileum: 12/min. • Duodenum: 18/min. • Jejunum: 15/min. • Ileum: 12/min. • Segmentation contractions effectively mix chyme with pancreatic and intestinal secretions. • Two nearby propulsive segments force the chyme toward each other into the receiving segment, which efficiently helps in mixing. • They also bring the fresh chyme into contact with the mucosal surface. • Therefore, segmentation movements are also called mixing movements. They help in the digestion and absorption of nutrients. • These contractions divide the small intestine into many segments. • Duodenum: 18/min. • Jejunum: 15/min. • Ileum: 12/min.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 895-895; Solution Pages 903-904","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"physiology-31-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following statements about the migrating motor complex (MMC) is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The MMC occurs during periods of digestion to facilitate nutrient absorption."},{"letter":"B","text":"The MMC is initiated by motilin and consists of three phases."},{"letter":"C","text":"The MMC contractions migrate orally at about 10 cm/min."},{"letter":"D","text":"The MMC occurs at intervals of approximately 50 minutes in the interdigestive state."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) The MMC is initiated by motilin and consists of three phases.","explanation":"Migrating Motor Complex (MMC): • Cyclic, recurring motility pattern occurs in the stomach and small intestine during fasting between meals. • The MMC occurs during fasting periods between digestion, not during active digestion. • Its primary role is to clear the stomach and small intestine of luminal contents in preparation for the next meal rather than facilitating nutrient absorption. • The hormone motilin initiates the MMC. • It consists of three distinct phases. Quiescent period (phase I) Period of irregular electrical and mechanical activity (phase II) Burst of regular activity (phase III). • Quiescent period (phase I) • Period of irregular electrical and mechanical activity (phase II) • Burst of regular activity (phase III). • The MMC contractions migrate aborally (away from the mouth, towards the rectum) at about 5 cm/min, not orally. • The MMC occurs at intervals of approximately 90-100 minutes in the interdigestive state. • Gastric secretion, bile flow and pancreatic secretion increased during each MMC. • When a meal is ingested, motilin secretion is suppressed, and MMC is abolished until digestion and absorption are complete. • Quiescent period (phase I) • Period of irregular electrical and mechanical activity (phase II) • Burst of regular activity (phase III).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 895-895; Solution Pages 905-905","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"physiology-31-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following increases intestinal motility?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dehydration"},{"letter":"B","text":"High fibre diet"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acid reflex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Opioids"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) High fibre diet","explanation":"Factors Promoting vs Inhibiting Intestinal Motility: Factor Promoting intestinal motility Inhibiting intestinal mobility Dietary High fibre diet Low fibre diet Hydration Adequate hydration Dehydration Hormones Gastrin and motilin Secretin and somatostatin Neural Parasympathetic Sympathetic Physical activity Regular exercise Sedentary lifestyle Medications Prokinetics Opioids and anticholinergics GI reflexes Gastrocolic reflex Enterogastric reflex Food intake Ingestion Fasting Electrolyte balance Adequate potassium levels Electrolyte imbalances Health conditions Infections (e.g. gastroenteritis) Intestinal obstructions and post-surgical states","sourceRef":"Question Pages 895-895; Solution Pages 906-906","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"physiology-31-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which segment of the gastrointestinal tract has the longest transit time?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Jejunum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Colon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ileum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Colon","explanation":"The large intestine (colon) has the longest transit time in the gastrointestinal tract. It is responsible for absorbing water and electrolytes from the remaining indigestible food matter and compacting it into feces. Water absorption and stool formation take longer than other gastrointestinal tract segments. Transit Time in Small Intestine and Colon: • The First part of the meal reaches the cecum in about 4 hours. • All undigested portions have entered the colon in 8 or 9 hours. • First remnants of the meal traverse the first third of the colon in 6 hours. • The second third of the colon in 9 hours and reach the terminal part of the colon (Sigmoid colon) in 12 hours. • From the sigmoid colon to the anus, transport is much slower. • 70% of the meal is recovered in stool in 72 hours, but total recovery requires more than a week. • Transit time can be observed by monitoring the progress of a small pill that contains sensors and a miniature radio transmitter.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 895-896; Solution Pages 906-907","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":896},{"id":"physiology-31-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-31","chapter":"GIT Motility","topic":"GIT Motility","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following mediates the receptive relaxation of the stomach?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sympathetic nervous system"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Enteric nervous system"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hormonal control"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Vagus nerve","explanation":"Receptive relaxation is a physiological process in which the stomach, specifically the fundus and upper portion of the body, relaxes and accommodates food without significantly increasing internal pressure. Mechanism: • This process is vagally mediated and triggered by the movement of the pharynx and oesophagus. Neural Pathway: • The brain responds by sending signals back through vagal efferent fibres, releasing neurotransmitters like nitric oxide (NO) and vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP). • They relax the stomach muscles, allowing them to expand with minimal pressure increase and aiding digestion. Subsequent Events: • Peristalsis begins in the lower portion of the stomach, mixing and grinding the food before it passes through the pylorus and enters the duodenum. • Contraction of the distal stomach caused by each wave is called antral systole and can last up to 10 seconds. Waves occur 3-4 times per minute. • Contraction of the antrum is followed by sequential contraction of the pyloric region and duodenum. • In the antrum, partial contraction ahead of the advancing gastric content prevents solid masses from entering the duodenum. • Regurgitation from the duodenum does not normally occur because the contraction of the pyloric segment tends to persist slightly longer than that of the duodenum. Adaptive relaxation: • The dilation of the stomach accommodates the food particles that have already entered the stomach. • The local vagovagal reflex regulates it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 896-896; Solution Pages 907-908","sourcePage":896,"sourcePageEnd":896},{"id":"physiology-32-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient who has undergone gastrectomy (surgical removal of part or all of the stomach) is at risk of deficiencies in certain nutrients. Which of the following vitamins is most likely to require supplementation in such a patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vitamin C"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitamin D"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitamin B12"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitamin A"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Vitamin B12: • A patient who has undergone gastrectomy is at risk of vitamin B12 deficiency due to the reduced production of intrinsic factor, which is necessary for vitamin B12 absorption. Therefore, vitamin B12 supplementation is typically required to prevent deficiency in such patients. Incorrect Options: Option A - Vitamin C: Although vitamin C is important for immune function and skin health, deficiency is not typically a direct result of gastrectomy. Patients may get enough vitamin C through their diet or supplements without specific concern related to gastrectomy. Option B - Vitamin D: Vitamin D is crucial for bone health and calcium absorption. While vitamin D deficiency can occur in various conditions, it is not specifically linked to gastrectomy. Patients may need to monitor vitamin D levels, but it is not the primary concern post-gastrectomy. Option D - Vitamin A: Vitamin A is important for vision, skin health, and immune function. Deficiency in vitamin A is less commonly associated with gastrectomy compared to vitamin B12.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 909-909; Solution Pages 919-919","sourcePage":909,"sourcePageEnd":909},{"id":"physiology-32-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which substance inhibits the iron transporter on the basal side of the enterocyte?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hepcidin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ferritin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transferrin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ferroportin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Hepcidin: • This is a peptide hormone produced by the liver that regulates iron homeostasis. It inhibits ferroportin, which is the iron transporter on the basal side of the enterocyte, thereby decreasing iron absorption and release into the bloodstream. Incorrect Options: Option B - Ferritin: This is a protein that stores iron within cells, including enterocytes. It does not inhibit iron transport but rather stores excess iron. Option C - Transferrin: This is a blood plasma protein that binds and transports iron throughout the bloodstream. It does not have a direct role in inhibiting iron transport at the enterocyte level. Option D - Ferroportin: This is the iron transporter located on the basal side of the enterocyte that releases iron into the bloodstream. Hepcidin acts to inhibit this transporter, not the other way around.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 909-909; Solution Pages 919-920","sourcePage":909,"sourcePageEnd":909},{"id":"physiology-32-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Least frequency of slow waves is seen in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ileum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Jejunum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stomach"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Stomach: • Slow waves are rhythmic electrical oscillations in the smooth muscle cells of the GI tract. • The stomach has the lowest frequency (3-5 waves/min) to regulate gastric emptying among the given options. • Frequency of slow waves in other parts of the gastrointestinal tract are as follows. GI Tract Region Frequency of Slow Waves (per minute) Stomach 3-5 Duodenum 12-20 Jejunum 8-12 Ileum 6-9 Colon 6 -10 Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 909-909; Solution Pages 920-920","sourcePage":909,"sourcePageEnd":909},{"id":"physiology-32-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following functions is NOT attributed to the intestinal epithelium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Absorption of the ions"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bile salts secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mucous secretion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secretion of digestive enzyme"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Bile salts secretion: • Bile salts are produced in the liver and stored in the gallbladder. They are released into the small intestine when needed to aid in the digestion and absorption of fats. Bile salts emulsify large fat molecules, breaking them down into smaller droplets, which increases the efficiency of fat digestion by enzymes. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. All these functions are carried out by intestinal epithelium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 910-910; Solution Pages 921-921","sourcePage":910,"sourcePageEnd":910},{"id":"physiology-32-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which co-transporter is primarily responsible for facilitating glucose absorption across the basolateral membrane of an enterocytein this scenario?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SGLT1"},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT2"},{"letter":"C","text":"GLUT5"},{"letter":"D","text":"SGLT2"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - GLUT2: • Glucose Transporter Type 2 (GLUT2) is a protein that helps with glucose transport across cell membranes. In the small intestine, GLUT2 is found on the basolateral membrane of enterocytes (intestinal cells), where it facilitates the transport of glucose from these cells into the bloodstream. Incorrect Options: Option A - SGLT1: Sodium-Glucose Transport Protein 1 (SGLT1) is a protein that aids in sodium and glucose co-transport in the apical membrane of the enterocytes. Option C - GLUT5: Glucose Transporter Type 5 (GLUT5) facilitates the fructose absorption by facilitated diffusion from interstitium to enterocytes. Option D - SGLT2: Sodium-Glucose Transport Protein 2 (SGLT2) is another sodium glucose co-transporter that aids in glucose absorption in the kidney.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 910-910; Solution Pages 921-921","sourcePage":910,"sourcePageEnd":910},{"id":"physiology-32-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following cells is responsible for destroying bacterial foreign bodies in the liver sinusoids?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hepatocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ito cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sinusoidal endothelial cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kupffer cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Kupffer cells: • Kupffer cells are scattered flat cells belonging to the reticuloendothelial system and function as macrophages in the liver. • They are located within the liver sinusoids and prevent liver injury or infection by breaking down and engulfing toxic substances from the blood. • They also help in the regulation of iron metabolism within the liver. • Kupffer cells play a major role in maintaining the structural integrity of the liver by releasing certain growth factors and enzymes that build the extracellular matrix of the liver. Incorrect Options: Option A - Hepatocytes: • Hepatocytes are the functional cells of the liver that help in the secretion and excretion of bile, detoxification of harmful substances like drugs or alcohol, metabolism of lipids, proteins, and carbohydrates, and the manufacture of important plasma proteins like albumin, fibrinogen, and prothrombin. Option B - Ito cells: • Ito cells are fat-storing cells (containing vitamin A) located within the space of Disse, which is the space between hepatocytes and sinusoidal lining endothelial cells. Option C - Sinusoidal endothelial cells: • They are specialized cells that line the sinusoids. • They have small pores called fenestrations that help in the exchange of nutrients and other various small molecules between the liver and the blood. • They also release cytokines that help regulate the liver's immune system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 910-910; Solution Pages 921-922","sourcePage":910,"sourcePageEnd":910},{"id":"physiology-32-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Cells lining the isthmus of the gastric pits:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chief cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stem cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parietal cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mucous cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Stem cells: • The cells lining the isthmus of the gastric pits are known as stem cells. • Stem cells proliferate and differentiate to form goblet and Paneth cells. • These stem cells also ascend upward to form epithelium cells of GIT. Incorrect Options: Option A - Chief cells: Chief cells are found in the base of the gastric glands and are responsible for the production and secretion of pepsinogen, an inactive form of the enzyme pepsin. Option C - Parietal cells: Parietal cells are found in the upper region of the gastric glands and are responsible for the production and secretion of hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor. Option D - Mucous cells: Mucous cells are found in the neck and surface regions of the gastric pits and are responsible for producing and secreting mucus, which helps protect the gastric mucosa from the acidic environment of the stomach.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 910-911; Solution Pages 922-923","sourcePage":910,"sourcePageEnd":911},{"id":"physiology-32-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which hormone among the following does not exert its effects on the pancreas?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Secretin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cholecystokinin"},{"letter":"D","text":"GIP"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Gastrin: • This option suggests that gastrin does not act on the pancreas. However, gastrin does have an indirect effect on the pancreas. Gastrin is released by the stomach in response to the presence of food and stimulates the secretion of gastric acid. The release of gastric acid can indirectly affect pancreatic function by modulating the release of other hormones involved in pancreatic secretion. Incorrect Options: Option A - Secretin: Secretin is a hormone that acts on the pancreas. It is released by the duodenum in response to low pH in the stomach. Secretin stimulates the pancreas to secrete bicarbonate-rich pancreatic juice, which helps neutralize the acidic chyme entering the small intestine. Option C - Cholecystokinin: Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a hormone that acts on the pancreas. It is released by the duodenum in response to the presence of fatty acids and amino acids. CCK stimulates the secretion of pancreatic enzymes, such as lipase and amylase, to aid in the digestion of fats and carbohydrates. Option D - GIP (Glucose-dependent insulinotropic peptide): GIP is a hormone that acts on the pancreas. It is released by the small intestine in response to the ingestion of glucose and fatty acids. GIP stimulates the release of insulin from pancreatic beta cells, which helps regulate blood glucose levels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 911-911; Solution Pages 923-924","sourcePage":911,"sourcePageEnd":911},{"id":"physiology-32-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":9,"question":"On which receptor does Hepcidin act to hinder the absorption of iron?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"DMT-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ferroportin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hephaestin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transferrin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Ferroportin: • Ferroportin is a transmembrane protein facilitating iron release from cells like enterocytes and macrophages. • Hepcidin, produced by the liver, regulates iron levels by binding to ferroportin, triggering its internalization and degradation, thereby reducing iron release into the bloodstream. This inhibition decreases iron absorption from the intestines and iron release from macrophages, resulting in decreased iron levels in circulation. Incorrect Options: Option A - DMT-1: DMT-1 (divalent metal transporter 1) is a transmembrane protein responsible for transporting iron into cells. Option C - Hephaestin: Hephaestin is an enzyme involved in iron metabolism, particularly in the release of iron from enterocytes into the bloodstream. Option D - Transferrin: Transferrin is a protein that binds to iron in the bloodstream and transports it to cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 911-911; Solution Pages 924-924","sourcePage":911,"sourcePageEnd":911},{"id":"physiology-32-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":10,"question":"The following Darrow Yannet diagram is suggestive of which condition? Note: The solid line is the original fluid compartment and the color shaded compartment with the dotted change caused by the condition.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Excessive sweating"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adrenal insufficiency"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diarrhea"},{"letter":"D","text":"SIADH"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Adrenal insufficiency: • Adrenal insufficiency, or Addison's disease, results from insufficient cortisol production by the adrenal glands. Symptoms include fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, and electrolyte imbalances. • In the Darrow Yannet diagram, it leads to decreased extracellular fluid (ECF) and increased intracellular fluid (ICF) due to hormonal imbalances. Incorrect Options: Option A - Excessive sweating, or hyperhidrosis, involves uncontrollable sweating in specific body areas. However, it's not typically represented in a Darrow Yannet diagram. Option C - Diarrhea causes fluid and electrolyte losses, leading to a decrease in the extracellular fluid compartment. However, without more context, it's unclear if the diagram specifically represents diarrhea. Option D -: Syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH) results in excess ADH production, leading to water retention and expansion of the extracellular fluid compartment, as shown in the Darrow Yannet diagram.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 911-912; Solution Pages 924-925","sourcePage":911,"sourcePageEnd":912},{"id":"physiology-32-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify this movement that takes place in the intestine.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Segmented movement"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peristalsis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intersegmental contraction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diffusion"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Peristalsis: • Peristalsis is a coordinated muscular movement that occurs in the gastrointestinal tract, including the intestines. It involves rhythmic contractions and relaxations of the smooth muscle to propel the contents of the intestine forward. • During peristalsis, there is a wave-like movement of contraction that begins in one section of the intestine and progresses along its length. The contraction of the circular muscles behind the food bolus (oral side) pushes the contents forward, while the relaxation of the circular muscles in front of the bolus ( aboral side) allows for the forward movement. Incorrect Options: Option A - Segmented movement: Segmented movement refers to the localized contractions and relaxations that occur in various sections of the intestine. It helps mix the intestinal contents but does not involve the overall forward movement of the contents as seen in peristalsis. Option C - Intersegmental contraction: Intersegmental contraction is not a specific movement that occurs in the intestine. Option D - Diffusion: Diffusion is a process of passive movement of molecules from an area of higher concentration to an area of lower concentration.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 912-912; Solution Pages 925-926","sourcePage":912,"sourcePageEnd":912},{"id":"physiology-32-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following factors is responsible for increasing gastric motility?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Presence of fatty food in the stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Distension of stomach"},{"letter":"C","text":"Presence of tryptophan in the stomach"},{"letter":"D","text":"The presence of acid in the stomach"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Distension of stomach: • Stomach distension, caused by food or fluids, triggers gastric motility by activating stretch receptors in the gastric smooth muscle. • This stimulation prompts the release of hormone serotonin which activates the myenteric plexus. The activation of myenteric plexus results in activation of cholinergic neurons in the anterograde and retrograde direction of the peristalsis. • In anterograde direction neurons secrete VIP, NO and ATP resulting in the relaxation of the smooth muscles. • In the retrograde direction (i.e., behind the food bolus), acetylcholine (ACh) and substance P are released, which cause contraction of the smooth muscles, pushing the contents forward. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 913-913; Solution Pages 926-926","sourcePage":913,"sourcePageEnd":913},{"id":"physiology-32-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Enteropeptidase enzyme is secreted by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Jejunum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ileum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Duodenum: • Enteropeptidase, also known as enterokinase, is an enzyme that is primarily secreted by the duodenum. It is produced by the epithelial cells lining the duodenal mucosa. • Enteropeptidase plays a crucial role in the digestion and activation of pancreatic enzymes. It converts trypsinogen, an inactive form of the enzyme trypsin, into its active form trypsin. Trypsin then activates other pancreatic enzymes involved in the digestion of proteins. Enteropeptidase, also known as enterokinase, is an enzyme that is primarily secreted by the duodenum. It is produced by the epithelial cells lining the duodenal mucosa. Enteropeptidase plays a crucial role in the digestion and activation of pancreatic enzymes. It converts trypsinogen, an inactive form of the enzyme trypsin, into its active form trypsin. Trypsin then activates other pancreatic enzymes involved in the digestion of proteins. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation. Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option B for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 913-913; Solution Pages 926-927","sourcePage":913,"sourcePageEnd":913},{"id":"physiology-32-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following acts as a secondary messenger in the mechanism of gastric acid secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"cGMP"},{"letter":"B","text":"cAMP"},{"letter":"C","text":"CCK-B receptor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ach"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - cAMP: • cAMP and calcium act as secondary messengers for the hormones acting on the H+ K+ ATPase in the parietal cell. • cAMP and calcium activates protein kinase A, which phosphorylates the H+/K+ ATPase pump, increasing acid secretion into the lumen. • Hormones acting through cAMP as a secondary messenger are prostaglandin E2 and histamine. • Hormones acting through calcium as a secondary messenger are gastrin and acetylcholine. Incorrect Options: Option A - cGMP: While cGMP is involved in the regulation of some gastrointestinal functions, such as smooth muscle relaxation. Option C - CCK-B receptor: The CCK-B receptor is involved in the regulation of pancreatic enzyme secretion and gallbladder contraction. Option D - Ach: While Ach is involved in the activation of parietal cells, it is not a secondary messenger in the mechanism of gastric acid secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 913-913; Solution Pages 927-927","sourcePage":913,"sourcePageEnd":913},{"id":"physiology-32-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which part of the liver stores vitamin A?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ito cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hepatocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Endothelial cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kupffer cells"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ito cells: • Ito cells, also known as hepatic stellate cells, are responsible for storing vitamin A in the liver. These cells are found in the space of Disse, which is located between the hepatocytes and endothelial cells in the liver sinusoids. Ito cells store vitamin A as lipid droplets within their cytoplasm. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 913-914; Solution Pages 928-928","sourcePage":913,"sourcePageEnd":914},{"id":"physiology-32-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":16,"question":"All of the following increases gastric acid secretion except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gastrin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Histamine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serotonin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Serotonin: • Serotonin (5-hydroxytryptamine or 5-HT) plays a role in the gastrointestinal motility. • During peristalsis the stretching of the intestinal wall leads to the release of serotonin which activates myenteric plexus and governs the peristalsis. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Gastrin, acetylcholine, and histamine increases the HCl secretion. Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Gastrin, acetylcholine, and histamine increases the HCl secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 914-914; Solution Pages 928-928","sourcePage":914,"sourcePageEnd":914},{"id":"physiology-32-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":17,"question":"What is the cause of the relaxation of the intestinal segment following the segment containing the food bolus during peristalsis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"VIP"},{"letter":"B","text":"Substances P"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dopamine from adrenergic fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Acetylcholine from myenteric fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - VIP: • Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide (VIP) is a neurotransmitter released when the cholinergic neuron from the myenteric plexus moves in the anterograde direction of the peristalsis.. • It acts as a relaxant on smooth muscles in the proximal segment of the segment containing chyme, resulting in relaxation of the proximal segment of the chyme and movement of the content towards the rectum. Incorrect Options: Option B - Substance P: Substance P is a neurotransmitter that is released when cholinergic neuron from the myenteric plexus moves in the retrograde direction of the peristalsis and results in the contraction of the smooth muscle. Option C- Dopamine from adrenergic fibers: Dopamine is a neurotransmitter that can have both inhibitory and excitatory effects, depending on the receptors it acts on. It is not released during the peristalsis. Option D - Acetylcholine from myenteric fibers: Acetylcholine is the primary neurotransmitter released by cholinergic neurons in the retrograde direction of the peristalsis and results in the contraction of the smooth muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 914-914; Solution Pages 928-929","sourcePage":914,"sourcePageEnd":914},{"id":"physiology-32-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":18,"question":"What is the most probable outcome for a patient who has undergone complete ileal and partial jejunal resection?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Folic acid deficiency"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gastric ulcer"},{"letter":"D","text":"Constipation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Vitamin B12 deficiency: • Massive ileal resection can lead to vitamin B12 deficiency due to impaired absorption in the ileum, the main absorption site for vitamin B12. This results in symptoms such as diarrhea, fatigue, headaches, and cognitive changes. Additionally, resection may cause increased defecation frequency and loose stools due to decreased absorption of fluid and jejunal secretions. Incorrect Options: Option A - Folic acid deficiency may occur due to resection of the distal small intestine, affecting absorption, but dietary intake and compensation from the remaining intestine mitigate severe deficiency. Option C - Gastric ulcers, including duodenal ulcers, are not directly affected by ileum and jejunal resection, as it doesn't impact stomach acid production, thus reducing the risk. Option D - Ileum and partial jejunal resection can lead to constipation due to slowed peristalsis and excessive water absorption in the colon, but it's not the most common symptom.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 914-914; Solution Pages 929-930","sourcePage":914,"sourcePageEnd":914},{"id":"physiology-32-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":19,"question":"In which of the following reflexes, gastric emptying is delayed when acidic content enters the duodenum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gastrocolic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intestino intestinal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gastroileal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enterogastric"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Enterogastric: • The Enterogastric reflex delays gastric emptying when acidic content enters the duodenum by inhibiting the release of gastrin from G cells in the antrum of the stomach inhibiting gastric emptying and further secretion of the acid in the stomach.. The Enterogastric reflex delays gastric emptying when acidic content enters the duodenum by inhibiting the release of gastrin from G cells in the antrum of the stomach inhibiting gastric emptying and further secretion of the acid in the stomach.. Incorrect Options: Option A - Gastrocolic: The Gastrocolic reflex is a reflex that stimulates colonic motility and promotes the movement of feces from the colon to the rectum when food enters in the stomach leading to distension. Option B - Intestino intestinal: When a part of the intestine is over-distended, the rest of the intestine relaxes. This is called intestine intestinal reflex. Option C - Gastroilea: The Gastroileal reflex is a reflex that stimulates the emptying of the ileum, the final portion of the small intestine, by relaxing the ileocecal valve. It is not directly related to gastric emptying or the entry of acidic content into the duodenum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 915-915; Solution Pages 930-930","sourcePage":915,"sourcePageEnd":915},{"id":"physiology-32-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Following complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is most likely to develop?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Folic acid deficiency"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitamin B12 deficiency"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iron deficiency"},{"letter":"D","text":"Reduced absorption of carbohydrates"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Vitamin B12 deficiency: • Following complete ileal and partial jejunal resection, the patient is most likely to have vitamin B12 deficiency. • The maximum absorption of vitamin B12 takes place in the ileum. Incorrect Options: Option A - Folic acid deficiency: The maximum absorption of folic acid takes place in the duodenum and to some extent in the proximal part of the jejunum. Option C - Iron deficiency: Iron is also absorbed maximally in the duodenum and to some extent in the proximal part of the jejunum. Option D - Reduced absorption of carbohydrates: Carbohydrates are absorbed maximally in the duodenum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 915-915; Solution Pages 930-931","sourcePage":915,"sourcePageEnd":915},{"id":"physiology-32-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":21,"question":"What are the effects of dietary fibers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Reduces intestinal transit time"},{"letter":"B","text":"No role in metabolism"},{"letter":"C","text":"Causes constipation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased blood Glucose"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Reduces intestinal transit time: • Dietary fibers are known to increase stool bulk and promote regular bowel movements, thus reducing intestinal transit time. They add bulk to the stool, making it easier to pass through the digestive system and preventing constipation. Incorrect Options: Option B - No role in metabolism: • This is incorrect because dietary fibers play a significant role in metabolism. They are not digested by human enzymes, but they are fermented by gut bacteria in the colon, producing short-chain fatty acids that can be utilized as an energy source. Option C - Causes constipation: • This is incorrect because dietary fibers actually help prevent constipation. They add bulk to the stool, soften it, and promote regular bowel movements. Option D - Increased blood Glucose: • This is incorrect because dietary fibers have the opposite effect on blood glucose levels. Soluble fibers, in particular, can slow down the absorption of glucose, leading to a more gradual and controlled rise in blood glucose levels after a meal.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 915-915; Solution Pages 931-931","sourcePage":915,"sourcePageEnd":915},{"id":"physiology-32-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following has the maximum thermogenic effect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fat"},{"letter":"B","text":"Protein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Carbohydrates"},{"letter":"D","text":"All have same effect"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Protein: • Protein has the highest thermogenic effect among macronutrients, estimated at 20-30% of its consumed energy. Incorrect Options: Option A - Fat: Fat has the lowest thermogenic effect among macronutrients, estimated at 0-3% of its consumed energy. Option C - Carbohydrates: Carbohydrates have a moderate thermogenic effect. The thermogenic effect of carbohydrates is estimated to be around 5-10% of the energy obtained from their consumption. Option D - All have same effect: This option is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 915-916; Solution Pages 932-932","sourcePage":915,"sourcePageEnd":916},{"id":"physiology-32-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following is an incorrect match?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gastrin - G cells from stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Motilin - Mo cells from duodenum and jejunum"},{"letter":"C","text":"CCK - D cells of the duodenum and pancreas"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secretin - S cells of duodenum and jejunum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - CCK- D cells of duodenum and pancreas: • Cholecystokinin (CCK) is a hormone involved in the regulation of digestion and appetite. It is primarily produced by specialized cells called I cells, which are located in the duodenum. • D or delta cells secrete somatostatin. Incorrect Options: Option A - Gastrin- G cells from stomach: • Gastrin, produced by G cells primarily in the stomach's antrum, regulates gastric acid secretion and motility in response to food and various signals. Option B - Motilin- M cells from duodenum and jejunum: • This statement is correct. Motilin is primarily produced by Mo cells and enterochromaffin cells of all parts of GIT except esophagus and rectum. Option D - Secretin- S cells of duodenum and jejunum: • Secretin is a hormone that regulates the pH and digestive functions of the gastrointestinal tract. It is primarily produced by specialized cells called S cells, which are located in the duodenum and jejunum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 916-916; Solution Pages 932-933","sourcePage":916,"sourcePageEnd":916},{"id":"physiology-32-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":24,"question":"The second phase of saliva secretion involves:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, passive K+ secretion, active secretion of HCO3"},{"letter":"B","text":"Passive Na+ reabsorption, active Cl- reabsorption, passive K+ secretion, active secretion of HCO3-"},{"letter":"C","text":"Passive Na+ secretion, active Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-"},{"letter":"D","text":"Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Active Na+ reabsorption, passive Cl- reabsorption, active K+ secretion, passive secretion of HCO3-: The second phase of saliva secretion, i.e., secretion in ducts, following events take place: • Active Na+ reabsorption occurs. • Passive Cl- reabsorption follows. • Active K+ secretion takes place. • Passive HCO3- secretion neutralizes saliva acidity. Incorrect Options: Option A, B and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 916-916; Solution Pages 933-933","sourcePage":916,"sourcePageEnd":916},{"id":"physiology-32-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Transport of iron into enterocytes is done by which of the following proteins:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Transferrin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ferroportin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hepcidin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Divalent metal transporter – 1"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Divalent metal transporter – 1: • Divalent metal transporter-1 (DMT1) is a transmembrane protein located in the apical membrane of enterocytes. It is responsible for transporting ferrous forms of iron from the intestinal lumen into the enterocyte. • DMT1 is expressed in the duodenum, the first part of the small intestine where iron is absorbed from the diet. Incorrect Options: Option A, B and C are not directly involved in the transport of iron into the enterocytes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 916-917; Solution Pages 933-933","sourcePage":916,"sourcePageEnd":917},{"id":"physiology-32-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-32","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":26,"question":"The correct sequence of events during vomiting are? Relaxation of lower esophageal sphincter Strong contractions in the stomach Inspiration against a closed glottis Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter Relaxation of upper esophageal sphincter Reverse peristalsis in the small intestine","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"6,4,2,5,1,3"},{"letter":"B","text":"6,4,3,2,1,5"},{"letter":"C","text":"4,6,2,1,3,5"},{"letter":"D","text":"4,6,2,5,3,1"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - 6,4,3,2,1,5: The sequence of events during vomiting can be explained as follows: Step 1: Reverse peristalsis in the small intestine - The small intestine undergoes reverse peristalsis, pushing the intestinal contents back into the stomach. Step 2: Relaxation of the pyloric sphincter: The pyloric sphincter, the muscular valve at the lower end of the stomach, relaxes to allow the content from the intestine to enter in the stomach. Step 3: Inspiration against a closed glottis - Forced inspiration occurs against the closed glottis. Lowering of the diaphragm increases intra-abdominal pressure. Step 4: Strong contractions in the stomach - Vigorous contraction of abdominal muscles increases intra-abdominal pressure sharply, which forces the gastric contents to enter the esophagus. Step 5: Relaxation of LES - Relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter allows gastric content to enter the esophagus. Step 6: Relaxation of UES - With a sharp rise in intra abdominal pressure, the upper esophageal sphincter relaxes, resulting in the passage of chyme through the mouth.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 917-917; Solution Pages 934-934","sourcePage":917,"sourcePageEnd":917},{"id":"physiology-33-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Caffeine impairs with sleep by which of the following:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Blocks adenosine action and wakefulness"},{"letter":"B","text":"Activate locus coeruleus and wakefulness"},{"letter":"C","text":"No role in maintaining wakefulness"},{"letter":"D","text":"Activates histamine release and prevent sleep."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct A - Blocks adenosine action and wakefulness: • Caffeine works by blocking adenosine receptors. Adenosine is a neurotransmitter that promotes sleep by slowing down nerve activity. By blocking adenosine, caffeine prevents the brain from sensing sleepiness, thereby promoting wakefulness. Incorrect Options: Option B - Activates locus coeruleus and wakefulness: • The locus coeruleus is a nucleus in the brainstem involved in arousal and wakefulness. While it does play a role in promoting wakefulness, caffeine’s primary mechanism of action is not through direct activation of the locus coeruleus. Option C - No role in maintaining wakefulness: • Caffeine is well-known for its role in maintaining wakefulness by counteracting sleep-promoting mechanisms in the brain. Option D - Activates histamine release and prevents sleep: • Histamine is involved in promoting wakefulness, but caffeine does not directly cause histamine release. Its effect on sleep is primarily through adenosine receptor blockade rather than histamine pathways.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 935-935; Solution Pages 954-954","sourcePage":935,"sourcePageEnd":935},{"id":"physiology-33-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Where do the afferent fibers from baroreceptors, responsible for blood pressure regulation, go to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nucleus ambiguous"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rostral-ventro lateral medulla"},{"letter":"C","text":"Nucleus Tractus Solitariu"},{"letter":"D","text":"Caudal ventrolateral medulla"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option: Option C. Nucleus Tractus Solitarius: The Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (NTS) is a nucleus in the medulla oblongata that serves as the primary site for receiving afferent signals from baroreceptors. Baroreceptors, located in the walls of the carotid sinus and aortic arch, sense changes in blood pressure. Afferent fibers from these baroreceptors transmit signals to the NTS, which plays a vital role in regulating blood pressure by modulating autonomic outputs. Incorrect Option: Option A. Nucleus Ambiguous: The Nucleus Ambiguus is a motor nucleus in the medulla oblongata that primarily innervates the muscles involved in swallowing and phonation. It is not directly involved in receiving afferent signals from baroreceptors for blood pressure control. Option B. Rostral-Ventro Lateral Medulla: The Rostral-Ventro Lateral Medulla (RVLM) is a region in the medulla oblongata that plays a crucial role in the regulation of sympathetic outflow and blood pressure. It is involved in the efferent pathway for blood pressure control, but it does not directly receive afferent signals from baroreceptors. Option D. Caudal Ventrolateral Medulla: The Caudal Ventrolateral Medulla (CVLM) is a region in the medulla oblongata involved in the regulation of sympathetic outflow and blood pressure. It primarily serves as an inhibitory center, tonically suppressing sympathetic activity. It is not directly involved in receiving afferent signals from baroreceptors.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 935-935; Solution Pages 954-955","sourcePage":935,"sourcePageEnd":935},{"id":"physiology-33-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What is the term used to describe increased pain in response to a minor stimulus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypersensitivity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Causalgia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hyperalgesia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Allodynia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct option. C • In case a small stimulus causes more pain, it is called \"Hyperalgesia.\" • Hyperalgesia is a condition where there is an increased sensitivity to painful stimuli, resulting in an exaggerated pain response. It can occur in response to various factors, including tissue injury, inflammation, nerve damage, or certain medical conditions. • Hyperalgesia can be categorized into two types: Primary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in the area surrounding the site of tissue injury or inflammation. The affected area becomes more sensitive to painful stimuli, even in response to mild or non-painful stimuli. Secondary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in areas surrounding the primary hyperalgesic zone. It is characterized by an expanded area of increased pain sensitivity beyond the immediate injury site. • Primary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in the area surrounding the site of tissue injury or inflammation. The affected area becomes more sensitive to painful stimuli, even in response to mild or non-painful stimuli. • Secondary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in areas surrounding the primary hyperalgesic zone. It is characterized by an expanded area of increased pain sensitivity beyond the immediate injury site. • Hyperalgesia can manifest as an increased intensity or prolonged duration of pain in response to stimuli that would normally be perceived as mildly painful or non-painful. It is believed to involve changes in the peripheral and central nervous systems, including altered neuronal excitability and enhanced pain transmission. • Primary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in the area surrounding the site of tissue injury or inflammation. The affected area becomes more sensitive to painful stimuli, even in response to mild or non-painful stimuli. • Secondary Hyperalgesia: This occurs in areas surrounding the primary hyperalgesic zone. It is characterized by an expanded area of increased pain sensitivity beyond the immediate injury site. Incorrect options: Option A: Hypersensitivity refers to an exaggerated or abnormal response of the immune system to a normally harmless substance, resulting in an allergic or autoimmune reaction. Option C: Causalgia is a type of chronic pain syndrome characterized by severe burning pain, typically following a peripheral nerve injury. Option D: Allodynia refers to the perception of pain in response to a stimulus that is not normally painful, such as light touch or a gentle stroke.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 935-935; Solution Pages 955-956","sourcePage":935,"sourcePageEnd":935},{"id":"physiology-33-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":4,"question":"What specific region of the thalamus is associated with the regulation of motor control?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ventrolateral thalamus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral posteromedial"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventral posterolateral"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior and inferior colliculi"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option. A • The ventrolateral thalamus (VL) is a region of the thalamus that is closely associated with motor control. It is involved in the transmission of motor signals from the basal ganglia and cerebellum to the motor cortex. • The ventrolateral thalamus receives input from the basal ganglia, which is responsible for motor planning and coordination, as well as the cerebellum, which plays a crucial role in motor learning and fine-tuning of movements. The information processed in the VL thalamus is then relayed to the motor cortex, where it contributes to the execution of voluntary movements. Incorrect options: Option B. Ventral posteromedial (VPM) and option (c) Ventral posterolateral (VPL) are regions of the thalamus that are primarily involved in somatosensory processing. They receive sensory information from the body, including touch, temperature, and pain, and relay it to the somatosensory cortex for further processing. Option D. Superior and inferior colliculi are structures located in the midbrain and are involved in visual and auditory processing, respectively. They play important roles in orienting responses to visual and auditory stimuli but are not directly related to motor control.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 936-936; Solution Pages 956-957","sourcePage":936,"sourcePageEnd":936},{"id":"physiology-33-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What mediates Inverse stretch reflex?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Golgi tendon"},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscle spindle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Unmyelinated C fibres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dorsal Column"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option. A • The inverse stretch reflex, also known as the inverse myotatic reflex, is mediated by the Golgi tendon organs (GTOs). Golgi tendon organs are specialized sensory receptors located in the tendons near the junction of muscles and tendons. • When the muscle contracts and applies tension to the tendon, the GTOs are activated. They sense changes in tension and send inhibitory signals to the spinal cord, which in turn inhibits the motor neurons innervating the muscle. This reflex helps prevent excessive tension and potential damage to the muscle and tendon by inhibiting further contraction. Incorrect option: Option B Muscle spindle is incorrect in this context. The muscle spindle is responsible for the stretch reflex, also known as the myotatic reflex, which detects changes in muscle length and initiates a reflex contraction of the muscle. Option C Unmyelinated C fibers are associated with pain perception and are not specifically involved in the inverse stretch reflex. Option D Dorsal Column refers to a pathway in the spinal cord involved in transmitting sensory information related to touch, proprioception, and vibration. It is not directly involved in mediating the inverse stretch reflex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 936-936; Solution Pages 957-958","sourcePage":936,"sourcePageEnd":936},{"id":"physiology-33-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Reward centre in the brain is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amygdala"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hippocampus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventral tegmental area"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct option. D • The reward center in the brain is primarily associated with the ventral tegmental area (VTA). The VTA is a region located in the midbrain, specifically in the mesolimbic pathway. It is known for its role in the brain's reward system, which is involved in the experience of pleasure, motivation, and reinforcement of certain behaviors. • The VTA contains dopamine-producing neurons that project to various regions of the brain, including the nucleus accumbens, prefrontal cortex, and amygdala. Dopamine is a neurotransmitter closely associated with reward and pleasure. When the VTA neurons release dopamine in response to rewarding stimuli, it can reinforce behaviors and create a sense of pleasure and motivation. Incorrect options: Option A. Amygdala: The amygdala is a key structure in the brain involved in emotional processing, particularly in the formation and regulation of fear responses. While it plays a role in processing reward-related stimuli, it is not considered the primary reward center in the brain. The amygdala is interconnected with the reward system and can influence emotional responses to rewarding stimuli, but it does not serve as the central hub for reward processing. Option B. Thalamus: The thalamus is a critical relay station in the brain that processes and relays sensory information to different areas of the cerebral cortex. It serves as a gateway for sensory inputs but is not specifically associated with the reward system or the primary reward center in the brain. Option C. Hippocampus: The hippocampus is primarily involved in memory formation, spatial navigation, and learning. While it is interconnected with other brain regions involved in reward processing, such as the VTA and nucleus accumbens, it is not considered the primary reward center itself. The hippocampus is crucial for memory and contextual associations but is not primarily responsible for the experience of pleasure or motivation related to rewards.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 936-936; Solution Pages 958-958","sourcePage":936,"sourcePageEnd":936},{"id":"physiology-33-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What is the connecting structure between Broca's area and Wernicke's area?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Arcuate fasciculus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior commissure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corpus callosum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fornix"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option: A • The arcuate fasciculus is a white matter tract that connects two important language areas of the brain: Broca's area and Wernicke's area. Broca's area, located in the frontal lobe of the dominant hemisphere (usually the left hemisphere in right-handed individuals), is involved in the production of speech and language expression. Wernicke's area, located in the posterior part of the superior temporal gyrus, is involved in language comprehension. • The arcuate fasciculus serves as a major pathway for communication between these two language areas. It allows for the coordination of language production and comprehension. Damage or disruption to the arcuate fasciculus can result in language deficits, such as conduction aphasia, where there is difficulty repeating words or phrases.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 936-937; Solution Pages 959-959","sourcePage":936,"sourcePageEnd":937},{"id":"physiology-33-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the term used to describe the opposition to passive stretching?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spasticity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Rigidity"},{"letter":"D","text":"Paratonia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct option: B. Spasticity • Explanation: Spasticity is characterized by increased muscle tone that is velocity-dependent, meaning the resistance to passive movement increases with higher speed or sudden stretching. This directly describes the opposition to passive stretching, as spastic muscles resist being stretched quickly. Incorrect option: Option A. Tone • Explanation: Tone refers to the general tension or resistance in a muscle at rest. It can be increased (hypertonic) or decreased (hypotonic) depending on the condition. Tone itself does not specifically describe the opposition to passive stretching but rather the overall state of muscle tension. Option C. Rigidity: • Rigidity involves increased resistance to passive movement that is independent of velocity. It is a constant, uniform resistance and is not specifically related to the speed or force of stretching, thus not accurately describing opposition to passive stretching. Option D. Paratonia: • Paratonia is characterized by variable resistance to passive movement, which fluctuates between increased and decreased muscle tone. It involves an impaired ability to modulate muscle tone but does not specifically refer to opposition to passive stretching in a consistent manner.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 937-937; Solution Pages 959-960","sourcePage":937,"sourcePageEnd":937},{"id":"physiology-33-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Maximum area in homunculus signifies","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Low control and skilled movements"},{"letter":"B","text":"More muscle fibers are present"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased preciseness of movements"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C. • Increased preciseness of movements: This option is correct. The maximum area in the homunculus signifies increased preciseness of movements. The larger the area dedicated to a specific body part in the homunculus, the more neural resources are allocated to that body part, indicating a higher level of precision and fine motor control. Incorrect Options: Option A. Low control and skilled movements: This option is incorrect. The maximum area in the homunculus does not signify low control and skilled movements. In fact, the maximum area in the homunculus represents areas of the body that require more precise and skilled movements. Option B. More muscle fibers are present: This option is incorrect. The size of the area in the homunculus does not indicate the number of muscle fibers present. The homunculus is a visual representation of the somatosensory and motor cortices in the brain, where the size of each body part in the homunculus corresponds to the amount of neural representation dedicated to that body part, not the number of muscle fibers. Option D. None of the above: This option is incorrect, as option C (Increased preciseness of movements) is the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 937-937; Solution Pages 960-960","sourcePage":937,"sourcePageEnd":937},{"id":"physiology-33-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A person is having increased sexual activity ,increased appetite, anger and no fear , where is structure injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amygdala"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cingulate gyrus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypothalamus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"The correct answer is 4. Hypothalamus. Explanation of Options: Amygdala: The amygdala plays a role in processing emotions, particularly fear and aggression. If there were an injury to the amygdala, it might result in altered emotional responses, including decreased fear and potentially increased aggression, but it would not likely be the primary cause of increased sexual activity and appetite. Thalamus: The thalamus serves as a relay center for sensory and motor signals. It is not directly responsible for regulating emotions, sexual activity, appetite, or fear responses. Cingulate Gyrus: The cingulate gyrus is involved in emotional processing and regulation, but it is not the primary structure responsible for increased sexual activity, increased appetite, anger, and decreased fear. Hypothalamus: This is the correct answer. The hypothalamus is a key structure in the brain responsible for regulating a variety of physiological functions, including hunger, thirst, sexual behavior, and emotions. It plays a vital role in maintaining homeostasis and orchestrating the body's responses to various stimuli. An injury to the hypothalamus could lead to the described changes in behavior, including increased sexual activity, appetite, and altered emotional responses.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 937-937; Solution Pages 960-961","sourcePage":937,"sourcePageEnd":937},{"id":"physiology-33-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents with progressive weakness and spasticity in his right leg. He also notices increased muscle tone, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign on the same side. His left leg and upper limbs appear unaffected. MRI of the brain reveals a lesion involving the left motor cortex. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parkinson's disease"},{"letter":"B","text":"Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Multiple sclerosis (MS)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right-sided upper motor neuron lesion"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"The correct answer is D) Right-sided upper motor neuron lesion. In this clinical scenario, the patient presents with progressive weakness, spasticity, increased muscle tone, hyperreflexia, and a positive Babinski sign on the right side of the body. These clinical findings suggest an upper motor neuron lesion affecting the right side of the body. The lesion is localized to the left motor cortex, leading to disruption of the descending motor pathways that control the right side of the body. Options A, B, and C are not the correct diagnoses because they do not match the clinical presentation described. Parkinson's disease typically presents with resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia, and a shuffling gait. ALS is characterized by both upper and lower motor neuron signs and can affect multiple limbs, not just one side. Multiple sclerosis (MS) is associated with multifocal demyelination, leading to various neurological symptoms that are not limited to one side of the body.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 938-938; Solution Pages 961-961","sourcePage":938,"sourcePageEnd":938},{"id":"physiology-33-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 40-year-old patient presented to the hospital with a complaint of chronic headache. Upon further investigation, a tumor was identified in the hypothalamus region, which was exerting pressure on the supraoptic nucleus. This structural alteration was found to be affecting hormonal regulation. Which hormone imbalance is most likely associated with this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ACTH"},{"letter":"B","text":"GH"},{"letter":"C","text":"ADH"},{"letter":"D","text":"TSH"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - ADH (Antidiuretic Hormone): • ADH, also known as vasopressin, is produced in the supraoptic nucleus of the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary gland. It plays a key role in regulating water balance by controlling water reabsorption in the kidneys. A tumor in the hypothalamus region that exerts pressure on the supraoptic nucleus can disrupt the production and release of ADH, leading to a condition called diabetes insipidus. In diabetes insipidus, there is an inability to concentrate urine, resulting in excessive thirst and urination. Incorrect Options: Option A - ACTH (Adrenocorticotropic Hormone): ACTH is produced by the anterior pituitary gland and regulates the release of cortisol from the adrenal cortex. It plays a role in the body's response to stress and helps maintain proper immune and metabolic functions. A tumor in the hypothalamus region affecting the supraoptic nucleus is unlikely to directly influence ACTH secretion. Option B - GH (Growth Hormone): GH is also produced by the anterior pituitary gland and is responsible for promoting growth and cell reproduction. While hypothalamic tumors can sometimes cause hormonal imbalances, a tumor affecting the supraoptic nucleus is not likely to have a direct impact on GH secretion. Option D - TSH (Thyroid Stimulating Hormone): TSH is produced by the anterior pituitary gland and controls the release of thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) from the thyroid gland. It is responsible for regulating the body's metabolism. A tumor in the hypothalamus region affecting the supraoptic nucleus is unlikely to directly influence TSH secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 938-938; Solution Pages 962-962","sourcePage":938,"sourcePageEnd":938},{"id":"physiology-33-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":13,"question":"In a young patient who experienced a head injury from a road traffic accident, his family reports a change in his behavior. They describe him as being excessively curious and constantly touching objects. He also exhibits rapid forgetfulness and attempts to put various things in his mouth, including plates. Additionally, they have observed him squeezing his genitals. Based on these symptoms, which structure is most likely to be affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gray matter around the aqueduct of Sylvius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Amygdala"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral nuclei of the hypothalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior cingulate gyri"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B • The amygdala is an almond-shaped structure that lies in the temporal lobe, lying just beneath the uncus. The amygdala is diverse and complex in structure and comprises approximately 13 nuclei. They further subdivide into extensive internuclear and intranuclearconnections. These nuclei functionally sort into five major groups: basolateral nuclei,cortical-like nuclei, central nuclei, other amygdaloid nuclei, and extended amygdala. • Amygdala is one of the components of the limbic system, which is responsible for the control of emotions and behavior besides memory formation. It is involved in processing emotions, including fear, anger, and pleasure. Damage to the amygdala can lead to changes in behavior, including increased aggression, impulsivity, and poor decision-making. Amygdala manages the processing of information between the prefrontal-temporal association cortices and the hypothalamus. • Amygdala has neural circuits to carry out its different functions with two major output pathways; the Dorsal route via stria terminalis that projects to the septal area and hypothalamus, and the ventral route via the ventral amygdalofugal pathway, which terminates in the septal area, hypothalamus, and the medial dorsal thalamic nucleus. The patient's behavior is most consistent with damage to the amygdala. This is because the amygdala is involved in processing emotions, and the patient's behavior is characterized by changes in emotion. Incorrect Choices: Option A. Gray matter around the aqueduct of Sylvius: Periaqueductal gray (PAG) matter is a column of gray matter that surrounds the cerebral aqueduct of Sylvius in the midbrain. The periaqueductal gray matter extends from the superior border of the midbrain (which forms the posterior aspect of the floor of the third ventricle), inferiorly towards the fourth ventricle, and the superior medullary velum. The gray matter around the aqueduct of Sylvius is involved in the control of movement. Damage to this area can lead to paralysis or weakness on one side of the body. Option C. Lateral nuclei of the hypothalamus: In the hypothalamus's lateral posterior part, the lateral tuberal nucleus is a circumscribed cell mass that houses approximately 60000 neurons. Many researchers have investigated how the lateral hypothalamus (LH), and more especially the hypothalamus, controls pain and inflammation. There are connections between the parasympathetic part of the LH and all of the main brain areas. • It governs eating behavior, waking cycles, and the autonomic nervous system (ANS). It is a component of the reward system. It also contains a variety of neurons, the most significant of which are orexin neurons. Despite being small in number, these neurons are crucial for blocking the transmission of pain and have an impact on the reward system, food patterns, and the hypothalamus-pituitary axis (HPA), among other things. • The horizontal cores of the nerve center are associated with managing internal heat levels, thirst, and hunger. These functions may be affected by damage to these nuclei, but it is unlikely that the symptoms described in the case will occur. Option D. Anterior cingulate gyri: The frontal portion of the cingulate cortex is called the anterior cingulate cortex (ACC). The corpus callosum, which transmits brain information between the right and left hemispheres, is encircled by the ACC. The ACC is crucial for cognitive processes, including anticipating rewards, making decisions, empathetic behavior, and emotion. It also appears to have a role in a wide range of autonomic processes, such as controlling blood pressure and heart rate. Numerous bidirectional connections exist between the cingulate cortex and the frontal, temporal, and occipital cortex in both cerebral hemispheres.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 938-939; Solution Pages 962-964","sourcePage":938,"sourcePageEnd":939},{"id":"physiology-33-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with a peculiar speech. On further evaluation, a defect in the Wernicke's area of his brain is identified. Which of the following condition is associated with the given defect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"He can make the decision what he speaks"},{"letter":"B","text":"Senseless continuous speaking"},{"letter":"C","text":"He is able to understand words and speak properly"},{"letter":"D","text":"He is understanding language but unable to speak"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Senseless continuous speaking: • A defect in the Wernicke's area, which is located in the left hemisphere of the brain's temporal lobe, can result in a condition known as Wernicke's aphasia. Wernicke's aphasia is characterized by fluent speech that lacks meaningful content. The person may produce sentences that are grammatically correct but don't make much sense. This is often referred to as \"word salad\" and is a hallmark of damage to the Wernicke's area. Incorrect Options: Option A - He can make the decision what he speaks: This option refers to intact executive function and conscious decision-making in speech. If this were the case, the person would have control over their speech and would be able to choose their words appropriately. This is not associated with a defect in the Wernicke's area. Option C - He is able to understand words and speak properly: This option describes intact language comprehension and production abilities. If this were the case, the individual would be able to understand words and their meanings, as well as communicate their thoughts effectively. Wernicke's area is involved in language comprehension, so a defect in this area would likely impact the ability to understand words properly. Option D - He is understanding language but unable to speak: This option describes a condition known as Broca's aphasia. Broca's aphasia is characterized by difficulty in producing speech despite relatively preserved language comprehension. Individuals with Broca's aphasia often struggle to form grammatically correct sentences and may speak in short, telegraphic phrases. This condition is associated with damage to the Broca's area, which is located in the left frontal lobe of the brain, typically in the left hemisphere.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 939-939; Solution Pages 964-964","sourcePage":939,"sourcePageEnd":939},{"id":"physiology-33-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding GFR is accurate? GFR stands for Glomerular Filtration Rate, while RPF refers to Renal Plasma Flow.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"An increase in sympathetic function increases GFR"},{"letter":"B","text":"Afferent arteriolar constriction increases GFR"},{"letter":"C","text":"An increase in RPF increases GFR"},{"letter":"D","text":"A decrease in RPF increases GFR"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C. An increase in RPF increases GFR: • Correct. An increase in renal plasma flow (RPF) generally increases the glomerular hydrostatic pressure, enhancing the filtration process and thus increasing GFR. When more plasma flows into the kidneys, it elevates the pressure in the glomerular capillaries, thereby increasing the filtration rate. Option A. An increase in sympathetic function increases GFR: • Incorrect. Increased sympathetic function generally causes vasoconstriction of the renal arterioles, particularly the afferent arterioles, which reduces renal blood flow and decreases GFR. Option B. Afferent arteriolar constriction increases GFR: • Incorrect. Constriction of the afferent arteriole reduces the blood flow into the glomerulus, decreasing the glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which in turn decreases GFR. Option D. A decrease in RPF increases GFR: • Incorrect. A decrease in renal plasma flow reduces the amount of blood reaching the glomeruli, leading to a lower glomerular hydrostatic pressure, which decreases GFR.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 939-939; Solution Pages 965-965","sourcePage":939,"sourcePageEnd":939},{"id":"physiology-33-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A patient is experiencing phantom limb pain after amputation of right limb . What is observed on a PET scan in a patient with phantom limb pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Expansion of right somatosensory fibers for the hand into neighboring areas"},{"letter":"B","text":"Neighboring fibers of the right somatosensory area extending into the right-hand area"},{"letter":"C","text":"Expansion of right sensory cortex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Expansion of Left sensory cortex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Neighboring fibers of the right somatosensory area extending into the right-hand area: • After amputation, the brain undergoes cortical reorganization. In this case, the neighboring somatosensory fibers that previously represented adjacent body parts extend into the region that formerly represented the right hand. This reorganization is associated with the perception of phantom limb pain. Incorrect Options: Option A - Expansion of right somatosensory fibers for the hand into neighboring areas: • This option suggests that the somatosensory fibers specifically responsible for the right hand expand into neighboring regions. However, it is not the fibers themselves that expand; rather, it is the cortical representation that changes. Option C - Expansion of right sensory cortex: • This option implies a general enlargement of the right sensory cortex. However, the expansion is not global; rather, it involves the neighboring cortical areas invading the region corresponding to the amputated limb. Option D - Expansion of left sensory cortex: • The left sensory cortex is responsible for processing sensory input from the right side of the body, but this option inaccurately describes the phenomenon. The reorganization occurs in the sensory cortex corresponding to the amputated limb (in this case, the right cortex, which processes left-sided sensations).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 939-939; Solution Pages 965-966","sourcePage":939,"sourcePageEnd":939},{"id":"physiology-33-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following fibers reach the Purkinje cell directly?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior olivary nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Raphe nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vestibular nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Locus coeruleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Inferior olivary nucleus Incorrect Options: Option B - Raphe nucleus:: • The Raphe nucleus in the brainstem synthesizes and releases serotonin, regulating mood.Although it connects with various brain regions, including the cerebellum, it doesn't directly reach Purkinje cells. Option C - Vestibular nucleus:: • The Vestibular nucleus, in the brainstem, processes balance and spatial orientation signals from the vestibular system. • It coordinates motor responses but doesn't directly connect to Purkinje cells, though it may have connections with the cerebellum. Option D - Locus coeruleus:: • The Locus coeruleus in the brainstem releases norepinephrine, regulating attention, arousal, and stress responses. • It has extensive brain connections but doesn't directly reach Purkinje cells in the cerebellum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 940-940; Solution Pages 966-967","sourcePage":940,"sourcePageEnd":940},{"id":"physiology-33-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following oxygen-sensitive channels is present in peripheral chemoreceptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"K+"},{"letter":"B","text":"Na+"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ca++"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cl-"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - K+: • Potassium channels are oxygen-sensitive ion channels located in the peripheral chemoreceptors. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are not oxygen-sensitive.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 940-940; Solution Pages 967-967","sourcePage":940,"sourcePageEnd":940},{"id":"physiology-33-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":19,"question":"In response to increased blood pressure which of the following is released at the level SA node?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epinephrine"},{"letter":"B","text":"NE"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dopamine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ach"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D – Ach: • When there is an increase in blood pressure it stimulates the baroreceptor reflex which then stimulates the nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) which in turn stimulates the Cardiac inhibitory center (CIC), the center for parasympathetic activity. This causes release Acetycholine at the level of nodal tissue. Incorrect Options: Options A, B & C are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 940-940; Solution Pages 967-967","sourcePage":940,"sourcePageEnd":940},{"id":"physiology-33-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":20,"question":"What is the length of time for which short-term memory lasts?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3-6 Hours"},{"letter":"B","text":"12-18 hours"},{"letter":"C","text":"30-300 seconds"},{"letter":"D","text":"3-4 seconds"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"• Short-term memory, also known as working memory, refers to the temporary storage and manipulation of information for a brief period. It is responsible for holding and processing information needed for immediate tasks. The duration of short-term memory is limited andtypically lasts from 30 to 300 seconds (or 0.5 to 5 minutes). Incorrect Choices: • Option a. 3-6 hours: This duration falls into the range of intermediate-term memory, which can retain information for several hours to a few days. • Option b. 12-18 hours: This duration falls into the range of long-term memory, which can retain information for an extended period, ranging from hours to years or even a lifetime. • Option d. 3-4 seconds: This duration is too short for short-term memory and is more in line with sensory memory, which holds sensory information for a brief moment, usually less than a second.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 940-941; Solution Pages 967-968","sourcePage":940,"sourcePageEnd":941},{"id":"physiology-33-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":21,"question":"What are the consequences of experimental muscle spindle sectioning in frogs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Voluntary Axn & stretch reflex present"},{"letter":"B","text":"Voluntary Axn lost but stretch reflex present"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both are lost"},{"letter":"D","text":"Voluntary Axn present, stretch reflex is lost."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct option: Option C: Both are lost: This option is correct. Cutting the muscle spindle disrupts the sensory input from the spindle, leading to the loss of both voluntary Axn and the stretch reflex. Incorrect Option : Option A: Voluntary Axn (Action) and stretch reflex present: This option is incorrect. When the muscle spindle is cut, the sensory input from the spindle is disrupted, leading to the loss of the stretch reflex. As a result, the stretch reflex would not be present. Option B: Voluntary Axn lost but stretch reflex present: This option is incorrect. Cutting the muscle spindle interrupts the sensory input to the spinal cord, resulting in the loss of both voluntary Axn and the stretch reflex. Therefore, neither the voluntary Axn nor the stretch reflex would be present. Option D: Both voluntary Axn present, stretch reflex is lost: This option is incorrect. When the muscle spindle is cut, the sensory input necessary for the stretch reflex is disrupted, resulting in the loss of the stretch reflex. Therefore, the stretch reflex would not be present.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 941-941; Solution Pages 968-968","sourcePage":941,"sourcePageEnd":941},{"id":"physiology-33-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Spinal nucleus of trigeminal is associated with which sensation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vibration"},{"letter":"B","text":"Conscious proprioception"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pain"},{"letter":"D","text":"Two point discrimination"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C – Pain: • The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve is primarily associated with the sensation of pain and temperature. Incorrect Options: Option A - Vibration: Vibration sensation is primarily transmitted through the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway, not the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve. Option B - Conscious proprioception: Conscious proprioception is also transmitted through the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway, not the trigeminal nerve. Option D - Two-point discrimination: Two-point discrimination is mediated by the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway and the dorsal root ganglia, not the trigeminal nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 941-941; Solution Pages 969-969","sourcePage":941,"sourcePageEnd":941},{"id":"physiology-33-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":23,"question":"The function of the Golgi tendon organ is to ___________","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sense changes in muscle direction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sense changes in muscle tension"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sense changes in position of the muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sense changes in length of the muscl"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Sense Changes in Muscle Tension: • The Golgi tendon organ, located within the muscle tendon, senses changes in muscle tension or force generated during muscle contraction. It provides information to the central nervous system about the level of tension in the muscle. Incorrect Options: • Option A, C, and D: They are not the main role of the Golgi tendon organ.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 941-941; Solution Pages 969-969","sourcePage":941,"sourcePageEnd":941},{"id":"physiology-33-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which type of nerve fiber is most likely to be affected in a 35-year-old female who experienced a tingling sensation over her arm after watching TV for an extended period of time with her hands placed under her head?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sympathetic"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"The tingling sensation experienced by the woman suggests compression or irritation of a nerve, which can lead to symptoms like tingling, numbness, or paresthesia (abnormal sensations). In this case, the most likely nerve fiber type affected is: Option A. Aβ fibers Explanation: Aβ fibers are large, myelinated nerve fibers that are responsible for transmitting signals related to touch, pressure, and proprioception (sense of body position) from the skin and joints to the central nervous system. When these fibers are compressed or irritated, they can cause sensations like tingling or numbness. The scenario described, with the woman placing her hands under her head while watching TV for an extended period of time, likely compressed the nerves in her arms. Prolonged pressure can lead to temporary compression of Aβ fibers, resulting in the tingling sensation experienced. Options B (B fibers) and C (C fibers) are not the primary fibers responsible for transmitting sensations related to touch and pressure. B fibers are associated with autonomic nervous system functions, and C fibers are associated with pain and temperature sensations. Option D (Sympathetic fibers) is not a specific type of nerve fiber. Sympathetic fibers can be A, B, or C fibers and are part of the autonomic nervous system, controlling involuntary bodily functions such as heart rate and blood pressure. Therefore, the correct answer is Option A. Aβ fibers.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 942-942; Solution Pages 969-970","sourcePage":942,"sourcePageEnd":942},{"id":"physiology-33-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which of the following are typically associated with Lower Motor Neuron (LMN) lesions? Select all that apply. Atrophy Fasciculations DTR increased Rigidity","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"1, 2 and 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"2, 3 and 4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1 and 2: • When lower motor neurons are disrupted, the muscle loses its innervation, resulting in significant muscle atrophy due to the cessation of nerve growth factor secretion and loss of muscle function. Denervation potentials such as fibrillation or fascicular twitches are observed in EMG recordings as a result of muscle denervation. Incorrect Options: Options B, C & D:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 942-942; Solution Pages 970-970","sourcePage":942,"sourcePageEnd":942},{"id":"physiology-33-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Weber-Fechner law relates the magnitude of sensation to ___________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Strength of muscle contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intensity of stimulus"},{"letter":"C","text":"The amplitude of action potential"},{"letter":"D","text":"Duration of stimulus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Intensity of stimulus: • Weber-Fechner’s law states that the magnitude of sensation felt is proportionate to the log of the intensity of the stimulus. • I = K log (S/So) • Where: So: Threshold amplitude K: Constant S: Stimulus strength I: Intensity of sensation • So: Threshold amplitude • K: Constant • S: Stimulus strength • I: Intensity of sensation • So: Threshold amplitude • K: Constant • S: Stimulus strength • I: Intensity of sensation Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 942-942; Solution Pages 971-971","sourcePage":942,"sourcePageEnd":942},{"id":"physiology-33-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A patient presented to the ER with a history of a road traffic accident. On examination his vitals were stable, but he could not speak. He had normal handwriting skills, and could understand what was being asked of him to which he responded by nodding. Where could be the lesion for this clinical condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Broca’s area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Wernicke’s area"},{"letter":"C","text":"Primary motor cortex"},{"letter":"D","text":"Occlusion of left internal carotid artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Broca’s area: • Broca's area is a region located in the frontal lobe of the dominant hemisphere (usually the left hemisphere) of the brain. It is responsible for the production of speech and the coordination of the muscles involved in speech production. Lesions or damage to Broca's area can result in Broca's aphasia or expressive aphasia. • In Broca’s aphasia, the primary deficit is in language output or speech production, with relative preservation of comprehension. Incorrect Options: Option B - Wernicke's area: Lesion at wernicke’s area results in fluent aphasia or receptive aphasia. It is characterized by impairment of comprehension of speech and relatively fluent but paraphasic speech (use of malformed and inappropriate words). Option C - Primary motor cortex: Lesions in this area can cause motor deficits, such as weakness or paralysis in specific body parts. Option D - Occlusion of left internal carotid artery: It will result in global aphasia characterized by features of both broca’s and wernicke’s aphasia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 943-943; Solution Pages 971-972","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":943},{"id":"physiology-33-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":28,"question":"In the homunculus, which of the following body parts exhibits the highest degree of representation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Low control and skilled movements"},{"letter":"B","text":"Crude touch"},{"letter":"C","text":"The precision of movements"},{"letter":"D","text":"The intensity of perception"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C -The precision of movements: • Representation of body image in homunculus is based on the fact that the size of the cortical receiving area for impulses from a particular part of the body is proportionate to the use of the part. The more the area of a body part, the more the receptors for carrying out function carried out by those body parts. The homunculus is mainly covered by hand, face, and tongue, which carries out precise movements. • Therefore, the part of the body responsible for precise movements occupies the maximum space in the homunculus. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 943-943; Solution Pages 972-972","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":943},{"id":"physiology-33-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":29,"question":"A neuron receiving 400 inputs from 150 sensory neurons can be best described as?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Divergence"},{"letter":"B","text":"Convergence"},{"letter":"C","text":"Feedback inhibition"},{"letter":"D","text":"Feedforward System"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Convergence: • Convergence occurs when multiple neurons synapse onto a single neuron. In this case, the 150 sensory neurons are converging their signals onto a single neuron, resulting in 400 inputs. • Each sensory neuron can transmit information from different stimuli or locations, and when their signals converge onto a single neuron, it allows for the integration of multiple inputs. Incorrect Options: Option A - Divergence: Divergence is the opposite of convergence, where a single neuron sends signals to multiple downstream neurons. Option C - Feedback inhibition: Feedback inhibition refers to a regulatory mechanism where the output of a process inhibits or reduces the activity of the process itself. Option D - Feedforward system: Feedforward system is an anticipatory system.When the control system predicts that some change is about to happen and corrective measures are being taken before the change occurs.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 943-943; Solution Pages 972-973","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":943},{"id":"physiology-33-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which of the following regulate body temperature?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypothalamus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basal ganglia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Limbic system"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Hypothalamus: The hypothalamus plays a central role in regulating body temperature through a complex mechanism involving: • Sensing temperature changes via thermoreceptors in the skin, deep tissue, spinal cord, and hypothalamus. • Integrating areas are: Anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area), response to heat. Posterior hypothalamus, response to cold. • Anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area), response to heat. • Posterior hypothalamus, response to cold. • Initiating effector responses, such as Activation of sudomotor sympathetic nerves to promote sweating, which helps dissipate heat through evaporative cooling. Activation of skin sympathetic nerves to induce cutaneous vasodilation, leading to increased blood flow to the skin's surface and heat loss through radiation and convection. • Activation of sudomotor sympathetic nerves to promote sweating, which helps dissipate heat through evaporative cooling. • Activation of skin sympathetic nerves to induce cutaneous vasodilation, leading to increased blood flow to the skin's surface and heat loss through radiation and convection. • The combined effect of the cooling or warming mechanisms allows for the body temperature to return to the normal range (around 98.6°F/37°C). Once the body temperature stabilizes, the activity of thermoreceptors decreases, and the hypothalamus reduces its signaling accordingly. • Anterior hypothalamus (preoptic area), response to heat. • Posterior hypothalamus, response to cold. • Activation of sudomotor sympathetic nerves to promote sweating, which helps dissipate heat through evaporative cooling. • Activation of skin sympathetic nerves to induce cutaneous vasodilation, leading to increased blood flow to the skin's surface and heat loss through radiation and convection. Incorrect Options: Option B - Basal ganglia: • The basal ganglia are primarily involved in the control of voluntary movements, motor coordination, and procedural learning, rather than temperature regulation. Option C - Thalamus: • The thalamus serves as a relay center for sensory information, transmitting signals to different regions of the brain. While it plays a role in processing sensory inputs related to temperature, it does not directly regulate body temperature. Option D - Limbic system: • The limbic system is involved in emotions, memory, and behavior. While certain components of the limbic system, such as the amygdala and hypothalamus (which is part of the limbic system), may influence physiological responses to emotions and stressors that can affect body temperature, the limbic system itself does not directly regulate body temperature.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 943-944; Solution Pages 973-974","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"physiology-33-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":31,"question":"How is cerebral perfusion pressure calculated?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Systolic blood pressure + Intracranial pressure"},{"letter":"B","text":"Systolic blood pressure – intracranial pressure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mean arterial pressure/intracranial pressure."},{"letter":"D","text":"Mean arterial pressure – intracranial pressure."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Mean arterial pressure – intracranial pressure: • Cerebral perfusion pressure is defined as the pressure gradient that drives blood flow to the brain. It is calculated by subtracting the intracranial pressure (ICP) from the mean arterial pressure (MAP). This formula accurately measures the pressure gradient that drives cerebral blood flow and is an essential parameter to consider in managing patients with neurological conditions. Incorrect Options: Option A, B and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 944-944; Solution Pages 974-975","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"physiology-33-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which of the following is responsible for relieving pain when pressure is applied to the painful area.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Raphe nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Corticospinal tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial leminiscus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medial longitudinal fasciculus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Raphe nucleus: • The Raphe nucleus present in the medulla is responsible for profound analgesia. These nuclei are serotonergic and end in the dorsal horn in the spinal cord. • The Pain-carrying fiber (Adelta/C) creates a synapse with a second-order neuron, which brings the sensation up to the level of the thalamus and perceives pain. • Serotonin activates enkephalin-producing interneurons and inhibits the pain pathway, acting at the levels of presynaptic (1st order) and postsynaptic neurons (2nd order). • Other areas responsible for analgesics are RVLM (Rostral Ventrolateral Medullary Region) and Locus coeruleus region secretes norepinephrine. Incorrect Options: Option B - Corticospinal tract: The corticospinal tract is primarily involved in voluntary motor control and does not play a direct role in pain relief. Option C - Medial lemniscus: The medial lemniscus is a pathway in the central nervous system that carries sensory information related to touch, vibration, and proprioception. Option D - Medial longitudinal fasciculus: The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a neural pathway involved in coordinating eye movements and connecting various brainstem nuclei.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 944-944; Solution Pages 975-975","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"physiology-33-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which sensory receptors are accountable for tactile discrimination among the options provided? Merkel disc Ruffini end organ Pacinian corpuscle Meissner’s corpuscle","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 4 are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 is correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 is correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"None are correct."},{"letter":"E","text":"Both A & D"}],"correctOption":null,"correctAnswerText":"Not provided in source.","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1 and 4 are correct (Merkel disc and Meissner’s corpuscle): • Tactile discrimination, which refers to the ability to discern fine details, textures, and shapes through touch, relies on superficial sensory receptors in the skin. • Both Merkel discs and Meissner’s corpuscles are involved in tactile discrimination, allowing individuals to sense and distinguish intricate textures and shapes. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Pacinian and ruffini end organs are deep sensory receptors stimulated by pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 944-945; Solution Pages 976-976","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"physiology-33-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":34,"question":"What is the underlying mechanism of action for pain reduction by transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Central inhibition"},{"letter":"B","text":"Desensitization"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gate control theory"},{"letter":"D","text":"Descending inhibition"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Gate Control Theory: • TENS works based on the gate control theory of pain occurring at the level of spinal cord, which suggests that non-painful sensory input (pressure) can block pain signals from reaching the brain. • By stimulating large-diameter nerve fibers (Aβ fibers) via pressure or vibrations that stimulate the interneuron, i.e.,G neuron where Aβ, Aδ, and C fibers synapse. • Stimulation of Aβ fibers inhibits interneuron and stops the transmission from Aδ, and C fibers carrying pain perception. This is called gate control theory. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B and D: Central inhibition, desensitization, and descending inhibition are not the primary mechanisms of action for TENS. Options A, B and D: Central inhibition, desensitization, and descending inhibition are not the primary mechanisms of action for TENS.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 945-945; Solution Pages 976-976","sourcePage":945,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"physiology-33-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Which receptors are responsible for mediating the stretch reflex among the options provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Golgi tendon organ"},{"letter":"B","text":"Merkel's disc"},{"letter":"C","text":"Muscle spindle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Meissner's corpuscles"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Muscle Spindle: • Muscle spindles are responsible for mediating the stretch reflex. They detect changes in muscle length and initiate a reflex response that leads to muscle contraction. Incorrect Options: Option A - Golgi tendon organs: Golgi tendon organs detect muscle tension. Option B - Merkel's discs: Merkel's discs are involved in light touch sensation in the skin. Option D - Meissner's corpuscles: Meissner's corpuscles perceive tactile discrimination.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 945-945; Solution Pages 977-977","sourcePage":945,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"physiology-33-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of decerebrate rigidity according to the Herrington classification?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increase in the rate of discharge of the γ efferent neuron"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased excitability of the motor neuron pool"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mid Collicular lesion can lead to decerebrate rigidity"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Rigidity occurs in all the muscles of the body: • Decerebrate rigidity primarily affects the extensor muscles of the limbs, leading to the characteristic posture of rigid extension. It does not affect all the muscles of the body equally. • According to the Herrington classification, decerebrate rigidity is a form of abnormal muscle tone that occurs due to damage or dysfunction in the medulla resulting in increased activity of motoneurons that facilitate stretch reflexes. • Facilitation of stretch reflexes occurs due to two mechanisms: Increased excitability of the motor neuron pool in general. Increased rate of γ motor neuron discharge. • Increased excitability of the motor neuron pool in general. • Increased rate of γ motor neuron discharge. • Producing a mid-collicular lesion in an experimental animal results in decerebration. • Increased excitability of the motor neuron pool in general. • Increased rate of γ motor neuron discharge. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 945-945; Solution Pages 977-977","sourcePage":945,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"physiology-33-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":37,"question":"In a patient who experienced a stroke 2 days ago, the current symptoms include uncontrollable, forceful, and jerking motions of the limbs on one side. Where is the probable location of the lesion in this individual?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Subthalamic nuclei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Substantia nigra"},{"letter":"C","text":"Putamen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Caudate nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Subthalamic nuclei: • The clinical presentation of uncontrollable, forceful, and jerking motions of the limbs on one side and a history of stroke suggest the diagnosis of hemiballismus, a movement disorder characterized by involuntary and violent flinging movements of the extremities on one side of the body. • Hemiballismus is typically caused by damage to the subthalamic nucleus, a part of the basal ganglia involved in regulating movement. Incorrect Options: Option B - Substantia nigra: Lesions of the substantia nigra lead to extremely severe disease of rigidity, akinesia, and tremors known as Parkinson’s disease Option C - Putamen: Lesion in putamen will result in flicking movements in the hands, face, and other parts of the body, called chorea. Option D - Caudate nucleus: Damage to the caudate nucleus can also lead to deficit in performance and chorea.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 946-946; Solution Pages 978-978","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"physiology-33-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":38,"question":"What findings would be observed in a patient who has suffered a cervical spine injury due to a road traffic accident, whereby the fracture fragment has penetrated the lateral aspect of the dorsal column tract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Absence of ipsilateral lower limb proprioception"},{"letter":"B","text":"Absence of fine motor movement of fingers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Absence of ipsilateral arm proprioception"},{"letter":"D","text":"Absence of contralateral lower limb proprioception"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Absence of ipsilateral arm proprioception: • The dorsal column tract of the spinal cord is responsible for proprioception, vibration sensation, and fine touch. • The tract is divided into two parts: the fasciculus gracilis, which carries information from the lower body (sacral, lumbar, and lower 6 thoracic regions T7 to T12), and the fasciculus cuneatus, which carries information from the upper body (cervical and upper 6 thoracic region T1 to T6) and upper extremities. The tract of cuneatus lies lateral to the tract of gracilis. • In both tracts, the sensory fibers ascend ipsilaterally (on the same side) in the spinal cord. Therefore, an injury to the lateral aspect of the dorsal column tract would affect the fasciculus cuneatus, leading to loss of proprioception in the ipsilateral arm. Incorrect Options: Option A - Absence of ipsilateral lower limb proprioception: This would occur if the medial aspect of the dorsal column tract, which contains the fasciculus gracilis, were injured. Option B - Absence of fine motor movement of fingers: Fine motor movements are governed by the corticospinal tract, not the dorsal column tract. Option D - Absence of contralateral lower limb proprioception: Proprioception ascends ipsilaterally in the spinal cord, so an injury would not affect the contralateral side.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 946-946; Solution Pages 978-979","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"physiology-33-39","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":39,"question":"Which of the following substances causes a bitter taste that deters us from consuming toxic substances?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aldehyde"},{"letter":"B","text":"Amino acids"},{"letter":"C","text":"Alkaloids"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hydrogen ions"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Alkaloids: • Alkaloids are a class of naturally occurring organic compounds that often have bitter tastes. They are produced by various plants as a defense mechanism against herbivores and insects. Many alkaloids are toxic or have pharmacological effects on the human body. Some examples of alkaloids include caffeine, nicotine, morphine, and quinine. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 946-946; Solution Pages 979-979","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"physiology-33-40","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":40,"question":"Which cells are impacted in multiple sclerosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Microglia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Oligodendrocytes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Astrocytes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ependymal cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Oligodendrocytes: • In multiple sclerosis (MS), the cells primarily affected are oligodendrocytes. Oligodendrocytes are a type of glial cell in the central nervous system (CNS) that play a crucial role in producing and maintaining the protective myelin sheath around nerve fibers. The myelin sheath acts as an insulating layer, allowing for efficient transmission of electricalsignals between nerve cells. • In MS, the immune system mistakenly attacks and damages the myelin sheath, leading to demyelination. This demyelination disrupts the normal transmission of nerve impulses, resulting in a wide range of neurological symptoms such as motor weakness, nuchal rigidity, swallowing difficulties, and impaired daily activities. Incorrect Options: Option A - Microglia: Microglia are immune cells in the CNS that act as the resident macrophages, responsible for immune surveillance and response in the brain. Option C - Astrocytes: Astrocytes are another type of glial cell found in the CNS. They have various functions, including providing structural support, maintaining the blood-brain barrier, and regulating neurotransmitter levels. Option D - Ependymal cells: Ependymal cells line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord. They play a role in the production and circulation of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 947-947; Solution Pages 979-980","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":947},{"id":"physiology-33-41","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Which fibre is responsible for High-frequency vibration, pressure sensation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pacinian corpuscles"},{"letter":"B","text":"Free nerve endings"},{"letter":"C","text":"Meissner corpuscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Merkel discs"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Pacinian corpuscles: • Pacinian Corpuscles are the largest and most sensitive tactile receptor present in hairy and non-hairy skin. • It is responsible for high-frequency vibration detection of 200 Hz or more. Incorrect Options: Option B - Free nerve endings: Free nerve endings are slowly adapting receptor. It is responsible for itching and tickling kind of sensations. Option C - Meissner corpuscles: Meissner corpuscles are fast adapting receptor. It is responsible for the vibration detection with a frequency lower than 50 Hz. Option D - Merkel discs: Merkel’s disc are slowly adapting receptor responsible for low-frequency (5 Hz) vibrations detection and sharp edge or corner points stimulus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 947-947; Solution Pages 980-980","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":947},{"id":"physiology-33-42","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":42,"question":"The patient exhibits symptoms depicted in the image provided below. Shows the site of the lesion Experiences decreased proprioception, vibration, and discriminative touch on the affected side. Has loss of pain and temperature beginning one or two segments below the side of the lesion on the contralateral side. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Weber’s syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dejerine syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Brown Sequrad syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Wallenberg syndrome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Brown Sequrad syndrome: • Brown Sequard syndrome occurs due to the hemisection of the spinal cord. • It is characterized by: On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. • On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. • On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. • On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. • On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. Incorrect Options: Option A: Weber’s syndrome - Weber syndrome, classically described as a midbrain stroke syndrome characterized by drooping of the eyelid, diplopia associated with contralateral partial or complete weakness of upper limb and lower limb. Option B: Dejerine syndrome - Central post-stroke pain, also known as Dejerine Roussy syndrome or thalamic pain syndrome, is a rare central neuropathic pain syndrome that occurs after infarction of the ventroposterolateral thalamus. Option D: Wallenberg syndrome - Wallenberg's syndrome is caused by a stroke in the brain stem, specifically in one of the arteries that provide blood to the cerebellum. It is characterized by dizziness with vertigo, loss of balance with gait instability, hoarseness of voice, and difficulty swallowing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 947-948; Solution Pages 981-981","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"physiology-33-43","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":43,"question":"What causes the stimulation of the Pacinian corpuscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cold Temperature"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hot temperature"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pressure"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Pressure: • Pacinian corpuscles are sensory receptors found in the skin and deeper tissues of the body. They are specialized to detect mechanical stimuli, particularly vibrations and pressure changes. When pressure is applied to the Pacinian corpuscle, it undergoes deformation, which triggers the generation of electrical signals and stimulates the associated sensory nerve fibers. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Pacinian corpuscles are stimulated by pressure. Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Pacinian corpuscles are stimulated by pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 948-948; Solution Pages 982-982","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"physiology-33-44","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":44,"question":"Which lobe is affected by a lesion that results in a deficit in hand-eye coordination?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parietal lobe"},{"letter":"B","text":"Frontal lobe"},{"letter":"C","text":"Temporal lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Occipital lobe"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Parietal lobe: • Impairment of hand-eye coordination is commonly associated with lesions in the brain’s parietal lobe especially in the brodmann area 5 and 7. • The parietal lobe integrates visual input from the occipital lobe and motor output from the frontal lobe and acts as an association cortex.. It helps process and integrate sensory inputs to guide motor movements and coordinate hand-eye interactions and is the most important area. Incorrect Options: Option B - Frontal lobe: The frontal lobe is responsible for motor output and is involved in the hand-eye movements, but the parietal lobe is the most important. Option C - Temporal lobe: The temporal lobe is mainly associated with auditory processing, memory, and language functions, and lesions in this lobe typically do not cause significant impairments in hand-eye coordination. Option D - Occipital lobe: The occipital lobe is primarily responsible for visual processing and is involved in the hand-eye movements, but the parietal lobe is the most important.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 948-948; Solution Pages 982-983","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"physiology-33-45","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":45,"question":"Corticospinal tract crosses at?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Midbrain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper medulla"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lower medulla"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Lower medulla: • 80% of the corticospinal tract after crossing the pyramid of medulla oblongata present in the lower medulla decussate to the other side forming lateral spinothalamic tract. • Rest of the 20% of fibres continues as anterior spinothalamic tract ipsilaterally and decussate at the level of the spinal cord. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 948-948; Solution Pages 983-983","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"physiology-33-46","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":46,"question":"What changes do not occur in stage A as compared to stage B when the hypothalamic thermostat in a patient is reset from point A to point C, as depicted below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Shivering"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vasoconstriction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sweating"},{"letter":"D","text":"Epinephrine secretion"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Sweating: • The hypothalamic thermostat controls the body’s core temperature by initiating either heat production or heat loss mechanisms to maintain a set point. When the set point is changed, the body responds by adjusting its temperature through various mechanisms. • When thermostat pressure is changed from point A to point C it increases the thermostat pressure all the mechanisms for raising the body temperature are brought into play, that are: Vasoconstriction. Piloerection. Epinephrine release. Shivering. • Vasoconstriction. • Piloerection. • Epinephrine release. • Shivering. • Sweating and vasodilation is observed when thermostat temperature is set to a low temperature. • Vasoconstriction. • Piloerection. • Epinephrine release. • Shivering. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 949-949; Solution Pages 983-984","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"physiology-33-47","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":47,"question":"Which of the options accurately describes decorticate rigidity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is produced by the removal of cerebral cortex and basal ganglia."},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexion of lower limbs and extension of upper limbs occurs."},{"letter":"C","text":"Rigidity is pronounced."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - It is characterized by flexion of upper limbs and extension of lower limbs: • Injury to the cerebral cortex results in decorticate rigidity, characterized by upper extremity flexion at the elbow and increased extension in the lower extremities. • The flexion of the upper extremities is explained by rubrospinal excitation of flexor muscles, while the hyperextension of the lower extremities is due to dysfunction in the medulla, resulting in increased activity of motoneurons that facilitate stretch reflexes. • Dysfunction in the descending pathways that control muscle tone and movement. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 949-949; Solution Pages 984-984","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"physiology-33-48","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":48,"question":"What is the effect of a lesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of the pons?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pain and temperature loss in the same side"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pain and temperature loss in the opposite side"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of tactile and proprioception on the same side"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Loss of tactile and proprioception on the opposite side: • A lesion in the medial lemniscus at the level of the pons would lead to damage in the dorsal column pathway as the second order neurons pass from it to reach thalamus. • The second order neurons arise from nucleus gracilis and cuneatus of the contralateral side.. These are responsible for the transmitting the following sensations: Fine touch Vibration Proprioception Tactile localization Tactile discrimination Stereognosis • Fine touch • Vibration • Proprioception • Tactile localization • Tactile discrimination • Stereognosis • So, damage to the dorsal column pathway at medial lemniscus will result in loss of proprioception and tactile on the opposite side. • Fine touch • Vibration • Proprioception • Tactile localization • Tactile discrimination • Stereognosis Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to Option D for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 949-950; Solution Pages 984-985","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":950},{"id":"physiology-33-49","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":49,"question":"Which specific neurotransmitter is accountable for causing dilation of the small intestine in a forward direction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"VIP"},{"letter":"B","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Substance P"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serotonin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - VIP (Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide): • Vasoactive Intestinal Peptide (VIP) is a neurotransmitter released when the cholinergic neuron from the myenteric plexus moves in the anterograde direction of the peristalsis. • It acts as a relaxant on smooth muscles in the proximal segment of the segment containing chyme, resulting in relaxation of the proximal segment of the chyme and movement of the content towards the rectum. Incorrect Options: Option B - Acetylcholine: Acetylcholine is the primary neurotransmitter released by cholinergic neurons in the retrograde direction of the peristalsis and results in the contraction of the smooth muscle. Option C - Substance P: Substance P is a neurotransmitter that is released when cholinergic neuron from the myenteric plexus moves in the retrograde direction of the peristalsis and results in the contraction of the smooth muscle. Option D - Serotonin: Serotonin is a neurotransmitter that is secreted by the gastric epithelial cells in response to stretch due to food. It is responsible for the activation of myenteric plexus resulting in coordinated movements in peristalsis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 950-950; Solution Pages 985-986","sourcePage":950,"sourcePageEnd":950},{"id":"physiology-33-50","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":50,"question":"What is the defining characteristic of Brown-Séquard syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fine touch lost on the opposite side"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pain and temperature lost on the same side"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side"},{"letter":"D","text":"Only pain lost on the same side"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Pain and temperature loss on the opposite side: • Brown Sequard syndrome occurs due to the hemisection of the spinal cord. • It is characterized by: On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. • On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. • On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. • On the side of the lesion: Loss of touch, proprioception, vibration, and tactile discrimination are lost due to damage to the dorsal column pathway. • On the opposite side: Pain and thermal sensations are lost due to damage to the spinothalamic tract. Incorrect Option: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option C for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 950-950; Solution Pages 986-986","sourcePage":950,"sourcePageEnd":950},{"id":"physiology-33-51","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":51,"question":"What is Prosopagnosia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Impairment of consciousness"},{"letter":"B","text":"Being unaware of one’s problems"},{"letter":"C","text":"Difficulty in identifying known faces"},{"letter":"D","text":"Failure to identify objects"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Difficulty in identifying known faces: • Prosopagnosia, also known as face blindness, is a neurological disorder characterized by the inability or difficulty to recognize and identify familiar faces, including those of friends, family members, and even one's own face. • It is caused due to lesion in the inferior temporal lobe. • People with prosopagnosia may have normal vision and intellectual abilities but struggle specifically with facial recognition. Incorrect Options: • Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 950-950; Solution Pages 987-987","sourcePage":950,"sourcePageEnd":950},{"id":"physiology-33-52","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":52,"question":"What is the mediator of fast vibration sense?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Merkel’s disc"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meissner’s corpuscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pacinian corpuscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ruffini’s end organ"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Pacinian corpuscles: • Pacinian Corpuscles is the largest and sensitive tactile receptor. Responsible for vibration detection 200 Hz or more. It is responsible for the mediation of fast vibration sense. Incorrect Options: Option - Merkel’s disc: Merkel’s Disk detects low-frequency vibrations at 5 Hz. Option B - Meissner’s corpuscles: Meissner is responsible for vibration detection with a frequency lower than 50 Hz. Option D - Ruffini’s end organ: The best stimulus is a stretch on the skin by some large object for Ruffini’s End Organ.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 951-951; Solution Pages 987-987","sourcePage":951,"sourcePageEnd":951},{"id":"physiology-33-53","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":53,"question":"What are the factors that can lead to a person drinking water as a result of the stimulation of the thirst mechanism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dehydration"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hemorrhage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vomiting"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - All of the above: • All the given options stimulate the thirst mechanism. • Dehydration, hemorrhage, and vomiting will lead to loss of isotonic fluid from the blood leading to increased renin secretion. Renin leads to an increase in the concentration of angiotensin 2 (Ag ll), which acts as a stimulus for the subfornical nucleus (SFO) osmoreceptor center in the anterior hypothalamus. Incorrect Options: • Option A, B & C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 951-951; Solution Pages 988-988","sourcePage":951,"sourcePageEnd":951},{"id":"physiology-33-54","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-33","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":54,"question":"Center controlling vomiting?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Apneustic center"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pneumotaxic centre"},{"letter":"C","text":"Area Postrema"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pre Botzinger complex"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Area Postrema: • The area postrema is a specialized region located in the medulla oblongata at the base of the fourth ventricle in the brainstem. It is commonly referred to as the \"vomiting center\" or the \" chemoreceptor trigger zone\" (CTZ). • The area postrema is responsible for inducing vomiting activated by toxic substances like drugs or due to hormones as seen in pregnancy. Incorrect Options: Option A - Apneustic center: The apneustic center is responsible to control the depth of respiration. Option B - Pneumotaxic center: The pneumotaxic center, also located in the pons, is involved in controlling the rate of respiration. Option D - Pre Botzinger complex: Pre Botzinger complex is known as the pacemaker of respiration.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 951-951; Solution Pages 988-988","sourcePage":951,"sourcePageEnd":951},{"id":"physiology-34-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which statement correctly describes the role of the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei in pituitary hormone secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The supraoptic nucleus primarily produces oxytocin, while the paraventricular nucleus primarily produces ADH."},{"letter":"B","text":"The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei contain neurons that release hormones from axon terminals in the anterior pituitary gland."},{"letter":"C","text":"Magnocellular neurons in these nuclei synthesize ADH and oxytocin and release them from the posterior pituitary gland."},{"letter":"D","text":"The supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei are located in the anterior pituitary gland and control its hormone secretion directly."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei synthesize ADH and oxytocin, and these hormones are released from the posterior pituitary gland. • Supraoptic and Paraventricular Nuclei: Located in the hypothalamus. (Option D) Contain magnocellular neurons that synthesize and secrete posterior pituitary hormones: ADH and oxytocin. The supraoptic nucleus primarily produces ADH. (Option A) The paraventricular nucleus primarily produces oxytocin. (Option A) Each nucleus can also synthesize smaller amounts of the other hormone. These neurons release hormones from axon terminals in the posterior pituitary. (Option B) • Located in the hypothalamus. (Option D) • Contain magnocellular neurons that synthesize and secrete posterior pituitary hormones: ADH and oxytocin. • The supraoptic nucleus primarily produces ADH. (Option A) • The paraventricular nucleus primarily produces oxytocin. (Option A) • Each nucleus can also synthesize smaller amounts of the other hormone. • These neurons release hormones from axon terminals in the posterior pituitary. (Option B) • Located in the hypothalamus. (Option D) • Contain magnocellular neurons that synthesize and secrete posterior pituitary hormones: ADH and oxytocin. • The supraoptic nucleus primarily produces ADH. (Option A) • The paraventricular nucleus primarily produces oxytocin. (Option A) • Each nucleus can also synthesize smaller amounts of the other hormone. • These neurons release hormones from axon terminals in the posterior pituitary. (Option B) • Magnocellular neurons are also found in the suprachiasmatic nuclei and extend from paraventricular nuclei to the brainstem and spinal cord. Median Eminence • Not a nucleus but is crucial for hormone transport. • Links the hypothalamus to the anterior pituitary. • Facilitates communication between the hypothalamus and anterior pituitary. • Hypothalamic neurons secrete releasing and inhibitory hormones into the median eminence. • Hormones travel through hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal vessels to the anterior pituitary. Arcuate Nucleus • Houses neurons that secrete growth hormone-releasing hormone (GHRH). • GHRH stimulates the anterior pituitary to secrete growth hormone. • Indirectly contributes to pituitary hormone secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 989-989; Solution Pages 998-999","sourcePage":989,"sourcePageEnd":989},{"id":"physiology-34-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 52-year-old man has had chronic polyuria & polydipsia for the past 6 months. He has a history of long-term lithium use for bipolar disorder. O/E, he is slightly dehydrated. Laboratory tests show normal blood glucose and ADH levels, and urinalysis indicates dilute urine with low osmolality. Kidney function tests are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Neurogenic DI"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nephrogenic DI"},{"letter":"C","text":"SIADH"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diabetes Mellitus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"• Nephrogenic Diabetes Insipidus occurs when the kidneys are unable to respond to ADH despite its normal secretion. • The patient’s long-term use of lithium, a known cause of nephrogenic DI, aligns with the symptoms of polyuria and polydipsia and the laboratory findings of dilute urine. • The presence of normal kidney function tests suggests that the primary issue is with ADH action in the kidneys rather than an intrinsic kidney disease. A) Neurogenic DI results from a deficiency of ADH due to problems in the hypothalamus or posterior pituitary. This is not consistent with the patient’s history of lithium use, which is more commonly associated with nephrogenic DI. Additionally, neurogenic DI would typically present with a different clinical scenario, such as a recent head injury or central nervous system issues. Type Description Causes Key Findin Treatment gs Nephrogenic The kidney fails to - Acquired: - Due to -Polyuria Chlorpropamide Diabetes respond to ADH drugs such as -Dilute (increases renal Insipidus (DI) despite its normal Lithium, demeclocycl urine with response to ADH) secretion. ine, rifampicin, low osmol aminoglycosides, ality cisplatin, amphoteric -Normal in B blood gluc - Genetic: X-linked ose levels recessive (V2 receptor deficiency), autosomal (aquapori n gene deficiency) Neurogenic/ Deficiency of ADH -Head injury - Polyuria DDAVP (synthetic Central Diabe secretion due to -Tumors (craniophar -Dilute ADH analog), tes Insipidus central issues yngioma, pituitary urine with Vasopressin (hypothalamus or tumors) low osmol injection, Clofibrate posterior pituitary). -Infections (meningiti ality therapy s, encephalitis) -Normal -Vascular lesions blood gluc (Sheehan’s ose levels syndrome, carotid aneurysm) -Congenital defects Syndrome of Excessive ADH -Head injury - Hyponatr - Fluid restriction Inappropriate secretion leads to -Ectopic ADH emia (low - Sodium replaceme ADH Secretio water retention and production (e.g., sodium) nt n (SIADH) dilutional hyponatre tumors: lung, - Dilute - Address the mia. pancreas, ovary, serum underlying cause bladder) osmolality -Neurologic - Concentr diseases (multiple ated urine sclerosis, Guillain-B with high arré syndrome, osmolality brain abscess, (Option C) meningitis, encephalitis) -Drugs (desmopressi n, chlorpropamide, oxytocin, vincristine, phenothiazines, carbamazepine) D) Diabetes Mellitus causes polyuria and polydipsia but is associated with elevated blood glucose levels and hyperosmolar urine due to glucose. The patient’s normal blood glucose levels and dilute urine do not fit the profile of diabetes mellitus. Feature Diabetes Insipidus Diabetes Mellitus Urine Osmolality <300 mOsm/L (dilute urine) >1200 mOsm/L (concentrated urine) Urine Taste Tasteless Sweet due to glucose Cause ADH deficiency or resistance Glucose loss in urine","sourceRef":"Question Pages 989-989; Solution Pages 999-1000","sourcePage":989,"sourcePageEnd":989},{"id":"physiology-34-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements about ADH receptors and their functions is FALSE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"V2 receptors activate the Gs protein, which increases cyclic AMP levels to facilitate water reabsorption in the kidneys."},{"letter":"B","text":"V1A receptors are involved in increasing blood pressure by causing vasodilation in blood vessels."},{"letter":"C","text":"V1B receptors are located in the pituitary gland and enhance the release of ACTH through phosphatidylinositol hydrolysis."},{"letter":"D","text":"V2 receptors increase the permeability of kidney tubules to water by promoting the insertion of aquaporins into the cell membrane."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"V1A receptors are involved in increasing blood pressure by causing vasoconstriction, not vasodilation. Receptor Location Main Functions Mechanism by Type G-Protein Coupling V1A Smooth muscles of Causes vasoconstriction Gq - phosphatidylinositol blood vessels hydrolysis Increases calcium concentration by activating phospholipase C V1B Pituitary gland Increases CRH and Gq - phosphatidylinositol (corticotrophs) ACTH secretion hydrolysis Stimulates CRH and ACTH release via V1B receptors V2 Kidney tubules Increases water Gs - cyclic AMP increase reabsorption Leading to the insertion of aquaporins into the membrane Other functions of ADH: • Increases Water Reabsorption in kidneys. • Raises Blood Pressure d/t vasoconstriction • Supports Memory: Acts as a neurotransmitter in memory-related brain areas. • Aids Ejaculation by contracting smooth muscles of the spermatic cord • Boosts ACTH Production from the pituitary gland. • Reduces Cardiac Output by acting on a specific brain area. • Stimulates Glycogen Breakdown in the liver. • Neurotransmitter Function in some parts of the brain and spinal cord.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 989-990; Solution Pages 1000-1001","sourcePage":989,"sourcePageEnd":990},{"id":"physiology-34-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements is false regarding prepro-pressophysin and prepro-oxyphysin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prepro-pressophysin is the precursor for vasopressin, while prepro-oxyphysin is the precursor for oxytocin."},{"letter":"B","text":"Both prepro-pressophysin and prepro-oxyphysin are synthesized in the hypothalamus."},{"letter":"C","text":"Prepro-oxyphysin contains neurophysin II, which is involved in the transport of oxytocin."},{"letter":"D","text":"Prepro-pressophysin and prepro-oxyphysin are transported to the posterior pituitary via Herring bodies."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Prepro-oxyphysin contains neurophysin I, not neurophysin II. Neurophysin I is responsible for the transport of oxytocin, while neurophysin II is associated with vasopressin transport. Feature Prepro-pressophysin Prepro-oxyphysin Precursor for Vasopressin Oxytocin (Option A) Components Signal Peptide Signal Peptide Vasopressin Oxytocin Neurophysin II Neurophysin I Function of Active Regulates water retention in Plays a key role in childbirth and Hormone kidneys, affects blood pressure lactation, influences social bonding Processing Location Golgi apparatus Transport to Via secretory vesicles called Herring Posterior Pituitary Bodies (Option D) Synthesis Location Magnocellular neurons in the hypothalamus (Option B) Gene Location Chromosome 20","sourceRef":"Question Pages 990-990; Solution Pages 1001-1002","sourcePage":990,"sourcePageEnd":990},{"id":"physiology-34-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 35-year-old woman presents with irregular menstrual periods, spontaneous lactation, and elevated prolactin levels. She is not pregnant or breastfeeding. She is under work-related stress and uses opioids for chronic pain. Which of the following is NOT likely contributing to her elevated prolactin levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Angiotensin-II"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dopamine agonists"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stress"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Dopamine inhibits prolactin secretion. Increased dopamine activity due to dopamine agonists would decrease prolactin levels, making it unlikely to contribute to elevated prolactin levels. Table rendering failed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 990-990; Solution Pages 1002-1002","sourcePage":990,"sourcePageEnd":990},{"id":"physiology-34-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What is the likely diagnosis for a 45 y/o man presenting with increasing shoe size, prominent facial features, frequent headaches, enlarged hands and feet, joint pain, coarse facial features, protruding jaw, MRI findings of an enlarged sella turcica compressing the optic chiasm, and elevated levels of growth hormone (GH) and prolactin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acromegaly"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gigantism"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pituitary Dwarfism"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypothyroidism"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"The clinical scenario describes symptoms typical of acromegaly, including enlarged hands and feet, coarse facial features, joint pain, headaches, and an enlarged sella turcica with a mass effect on the optic chiasma. The elevated GH and prolactin levels further support this diagnosis. Table rendering failed The image below shows gigantism. The image below shows pituitary dwarfism. D) Hypothyroidism: Hypothyroidism may present with symptoms such as fatigue, weight gain, and cold intolerance, but it does not cause the physical changes described (enlargement of hands, feet, and facial features) or elevated GH levels. Thus, hypothyroidism is an unlikely diagnosis in this scenario.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 991-991; Solution Pages 1002-1003","sourcePage":991,"sourcePageEnd":991},{"id":"physiology-34-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which Insulin-like Growth Factor (IGF) is known to be more potent in promoting cell growth?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"IGF-I"},{"letter":"B","text":"IGF-II"},{"letter":"C","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Growth Hormone (GH)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"IGF-I (Somatomedin-C) is the most potent in promoting cell growth and mediating the effects of growth hormone (GH). It plays a crucial role in skeletal and cartilage growth especially during postnatal development. B) IGF-II: IGF-II is important for fetal development but is generally less potent than IGF-I in promoting postnatal cell growth. Its actions are not as closely linked to GH regulation as IGF-I. C) Insulin: While insulin shares structural similarities with IGFs and can promote cellular growth and metabolism, it primarily regulates glucose uptake and metabolism rather than directly mediating growth hormone effects. D) Growth Hormone (GH): Growth Hormone (GH) stimulates the production of IGFs but is not an IGF itself. IGFs, particularly IGF-I, are responsible for directly promoting cell growth. • Somatomedins, also known as Insulin-like Growth Factors (IGFs), are polypeptide growth factors primarily produced in the liver, although they are also formed in cartilage and other tissues. • These factors play a critical role in mediating the effects of growth hormone (GH) on growth, cartilage, and protein metabolism. • The two most important circulating somatomedins in humans are IGF-I (somatomedin C) and IGF-II. Features IGF - I ( Somatomedin C) IGF - II Primary Function Skeletal and cartilage growth Crucial for fetal growth Regulation by GH Strongly regulated after birth Not significantly regulated by GH postnatally Potency More potent in growth promotion Less potent compared to IGF-I Plasma Concentration Mirrors GH secretion Higher concentration than IGF-I Role in Development Important for both fetal and Mainly involved in fetal development postnatal growth","sourceRef":"Question Pages 991-991; Solution Pages 1003-1004","sourcePage":991,"sourcePageEnd":991},{"id":"physiology-34-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45 y/o woman is found to have elevated growth hormone (GH) levels during routine screening. Despite these high levels, her Insulin-like Growth Factor-I (IGF-1) levels are also elevated, suggesting a failure in the feedback mechanism. Which condition could most likely explain the persistent high GH levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pituitary adenoma"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypothalamic lesion impairing somatostatin release"},{"letter":"C","text":"Reduced IGF-1 receptor sensitivity in the pituitary"},{"letter":"D","text":"Excessive GHRH secretion from an ectopic source"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Reduced IGF-1 receptor sensitivity in the pituitary: If the pituitary gland's response to IGF-1 (also called somatomedins) is diminished, it would fail to inhibit GH secretion effectively, resulting in persistently high GH levels despite high IGF-1. A) Pituitary adenoma: This could cause elevated GH levels due to tumor overproduction, but the scenario focuses on feedback failure despite high IGF-1. B) Hypothalamic lesion impairing somatostatin release: This would decrease inhibition of GH secretion, potentially leading to elevated GH, but does not explain the elevated IGF-1 levels. D) Excessive GHRH secretion from an ectopic source: While this could increase GH levels, it does not directly address the feedback failure involving IGF-1 mentioned in the scenario. Factors Controlling GH Secretion: • Direct Feedback from GH to Hypothalamus: GH can inhibit its production by reducing GHRH secretion from the hypothalamus. • Indirect Feedback via IGF-1: GH stimulates the liver to produce IGF-1, which then inhibits GH secretion by acting directly on the pituitary and indirectly by stimulating somatostatin release from the hypothalamus. • Negative Feedback through Somatostatin: Somatostatin, a potent inhibitor of GH secretion, is released in response to elevated GH or IGF-1 levels, contributing to the negative feedback loop.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 991-991; Solution Pages 1004-1005","sourcePage":991,"sourcePageEnd":991},{"id":"physiology-34-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Researchers are studying the effects of various factors on growth hormone (GH) secretion. They observed a significant increase in GH levels in participants who fasted overnight before the study. Which of the following best explains this observation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased somatostatin secretion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elevated blood glucose levels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Starvation or protein deficiency"},{"letter":"D","text":"Reduced ghrelin levels"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Prolonged fasting or protein deficiency can lead to elevated GH levels as the body attempts to conserve proteins and mobilize other energy stores, such as fats. A) Increased somatostatin secretion: This would typically decrease GH levels, not increase them, as somatostatin inhibits GH release. B) Elevated blood glucose levels: High blood glucose levels are associated with reduced GH secretion, not an increase. D) Reduced ghrelin levels: Ghrelin stimulates GH release, so reduced ghrelin levels would likely lead to a decrease in GH, not an increase. Factors controlling GH secretion and their effects Hypothalamic Control Growth Hormone-Releasing Hormone (GHRH): Increases GH synthesis and secretion. Somatostatin (GHIH): Decreases GH secretion. Nutritional Factors Hypoglycemia: Promotes GH secretion to increase blood glucose levels and mobilize energy. Amino Acids: Stimulate GH release, supporting protein synthesis and growth. Starvation or Protein Deficiency: This leads to elevated GH levels to conserve body proteins and utilize fats as an energy source. Physiological Factors Exercise: Enhances GH secretion Sleep: GH surges during deep sleep are vital for growth and tissue repair processes. Stress: Physical and emotional stressors can increase GH secretion Hormonal Factors Ghrelin: Increases GH secretion and stimulates appetite. Sex Hormones (Estrogen and Testosterone): Contribute to increased GH secretion during puberty, supporting growth and development. Negative Feedback Control: GH and IGF-1 levels feedback to inhibit further GH release, maintaining homeostasis and preventing excess GH production.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 992-992; Solution Pages 1005-1006","sourcePage":992,"sourcePageEnd":992},{"id":"physiology-34-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 30-year-old woman presents with galactorrhea (inappropriate milk secretion) and menstrual irregularities. After excluding other causes, the physician considers a potential pituitary dysfunction. Which hypothalamic hormone contributes to increased prolactin levels in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dopamine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH), in addition to its primary role in stimulating TSH release from the anterior pituitary, can also increase prolactin secretion. This dual effect may contribute to conditions like galactorrhea when prolactin levels are elevated. • The pituitary gland plays a pivotal role in regulating various endocrine functions, including growth, metabolism, and reproduction. • The activity of the pituitary gland is primarily governed by the hypothalamus, which releases specific hormones, either stimulating or inhibiting, that reach the pituitary. • The regulation of pituitary hormone secretion is a finely tuned process, primarily controlled by feedback mechanisms. Negative feedback loops are the most common, where the end products of a hormone's action inhibit further hormone release to maintain homeostasis. An example is cortisol, which inhibits its own secretion through feedback on both the hypothalamus and pituitary. Positive feedback, although less common, occurs in processes like childbirth, where oxytocin release enhances uterine contractions, leading to further oxytocin release. • Negative feedback loops are the most common, where the end products of a hormone's action inhibit further hormone release to maintain homeostasis. An example is cortisol, which inhibits its own secretion through feedback on both the hypothalamus and pituitary. • Positive feedback, although less common, occurs in processes like childbirth, where oxytocin release enhances uterine contractions, leading to further oxytocin release. • Negative feedback loops are the most common, where the end products of a hormone's action inhibit further hormone release to maintain homeostasis. An example is cortisol, which inhibits its own secretion through feedback on both the hypothalamus and pituitary. • Positive feedback, although less common, occurs in processes like childbirth, where oxytocin release enhances uterine contractions, leading to further oxytocin release. A) Corticotropin-releasing hormone (CRH): This hormone primarily stimulates the release of adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) from the anterior pituitary, which in turn influences cortisol production by the adrenal glands. CRH does not directly affect prolactin levels. B) Dopamine: This neurotransmitter serves as the primary inhibitor of prolactin secretion. Increased dopamine levels typically decrease prolactin release, the opposite of what is described in the scenario. D) Gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH): This hormone primarily regulates the release of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH), which are involved in reproductive function. GnRH does not significantly influence prolactin secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 992-992; Solution Pages 1006-1007","sourcePage":992,"sourcePageEnd":992},{"id":"physiology-34-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which cell type constitutes the majority in the anterior pituitary cell population?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corticotropes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Somatotropes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lactotropes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyrotropes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Somatotropes are the most abundant cells in the anterior pituitary, making up 50% of the total. They secrete growth hormone (GH), crucial for growth and metabolism. Somatotropes are also known as acidophils due to their staining properties with acidic dyes. Cell Type - Staining Cell Type Hormone Percentage of Total Secretory Cells (%) Acidophilic Somatrope GH 50 Lactotrope Prolactin 10-30 Basophilic Gonadotrope FSH, LH 20 Corticotrope ACTH 10 Thyrotrope TSH 5 A) Corticotropes: These cells produce adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), representing about 10% of the anterior pituitary cell population. They are involved in stimulating the adrenal cortex to secrete cortisol. C) Lactotropes: Also known as mammotropes, these cells secrete prolactin, which is important for milk production in breastfeeding mothers. They do not constitute the majority of the anterior pituitary cells. D) Thyrotropes: These cells secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH), which stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroid hormones and are not the predominant cell type in the anterior pituitary.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 992-992; Solution Pages 1007-1008","sourcePage":992,"sourcePageEnd":992},{"id":"physiology-34-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A researcher observes that certain cancer cells in a tissue sample are proliferating rapidly due to communication involving transforming growth factor α (TGFα) present on their surfaces. Which of the following cellular communication methods is being used?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Endocrine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Paracrine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Autocrine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Juxtacrine"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Juxtacrine communication is the communication through cell adhesion to growth factors expressed on the cell surface. Cells having receptors for TGFα interact with each other by attaching themselves to the TGFα present on their cell surface. The chemical signaling of hormones takes place through the following pathways Ty Endocrine Paracrine Autocrine Juxtacrine pe of c ell sig na lin g M Hormones are Hormones diffuse Hormones bind to Communication ec secreted from into the extracellular receptors on the occurs through cell ha endocrine glands space and signal same cell that adhesion to growth nis and travel through neighboring tissues. secretes them, factors expressed m the bloodstream to modifying the on cell surfaces. reach distant target function of the tissues. parent cell. Ex Most hormones Somatostatin from The platelet-activatin Cells with receptors a signal target tissues D cells of pancreatic g factor secreted by for transforming m far from their gland islets influences platelets activates growth factor α pl of origin, facilitating insulin and the same platelets. (TGFα) interact by e intercellular glucagon secretion attaching to TGFα communication from neighboring present on their cell across the body. islet cells. surfaces","sourceRef":"Question Pages 993-993; Solution Pages 1008-1009","sourcePage":993,"sourcePageEnd":993},{"id":"physiology-34-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) and the adenohypophysis (anterior pituitary)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The adenohypophysis is derived from neural tissue."},{"letter":"B","text":"The adenohypophysis is linked to the hypothalamus through the hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system."},{"letter":"C","text":"The neurohypophysis contains five types of hormone-secreting cells, whereas the adenohypophysis does not produce hormones."},{"letter":"D","text":"The neurohypophysis secretes growth hormone and thyroid-stimulating hormone"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"The adenohypophysis is connected to the hypothalamus through a specialized vascular network called the hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal system, which allows for hormonal regulation of hormone secretion. In contrast, the neurohypophysis is connected via nerve fibers, resulting in direct neural control of hormone release. Structure of the Pituitary Gland The pituitary gland has two distinct parts: the neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) and the adenohypophysis (anterior pituitary). Feature Adenohypophysis (Anterior Pituitary) Neurohypophysis (Posterior Pituitary) Embryological Epithelial tissue (Rathke's pouch). Neural tissue Origin (Option A) Cellular Composed of Epithelioid cells Made up of pituicytes (glial-like Composition Contains five types of cells). hormone-secreting cells: (Option Contains nerve fibers from the C&D) supraoptic and paraventricular Somatotropes (secrete growth nuclei of the hypothalamus. hormone) Nerve fibers form bulbous structures Corticotropes (secrete near capillaries for hormone adrenocorticotropic hormone) secretion. Thyrotropes (secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone) Gonadotropes (secrete follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone) Lactotropes (secrete prolactin) Hormones GH, ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, Prolactin ADH, Oxytocin (Option D) Produced Produced in the hypothalamus, transported via neurophysins, stored in secretory granules, and released into capillaries upon stimulation. Connection to Hypothalamic-hypophyseal portal Nerve fibres Hypothalamus system Mechanism of Hormonal regulation via the Direct neural control via nerve fibers Hormone Secre hypothalamus hypophyseal portal tion system","sourceRef":"Question Pages 993-993; Solution Pages 1009-1010","sourcePage":993,"sourcePageEnd":993},{"id":"physiology-34-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes a key difference between Type 1 and Type 2 steroidal or intracellular hormone receptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type 1 receptors are located in the nucleus, while Type 2 receptors are found in the cytoplasm."},{"letter":"B","text":"Type 1 receptors are bound to heat shock proteins in their inactive state, whereas Type 2 receptors can bind DNA even without a ligand."},{"letter":"C","text":"Type 1 receptors function as heterodimers, while Type 2 receptors function as homodimers."},{"letter":"D","text":"Type 1 receptors do not have intrinsic enzymatic activity, whereas Type 2 receptors do."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Type 1 receptors are bound to heat shock proteins (HSPs) in their inactive state, while Type 2 receptors can bind DNA even without a ligand, often repressing transcription until activated by a ligand. Steroidal or intracellular hormone receptors are classified into two main types: Type 1 and Type 2. These receptors mediate the effects of steroid hormones and other lipophilic molecules. Aspect Type 1 Receptors Type 2 Receptors Location: Cytoplasm (Option A) Nucleus (Option A) Exampl Glucocorticoid, mineralocorticoid, Thyroid hormone and retinoic acid es: androgen, and progesterone receptors receptors Activatio Inactive State: Bound to chaperone DNA Binding: Can bind DNA even n Mecha proteins- heat shock proteins without a ligand, often repressing nism: (HSPs) like HSP90 transcription. The hormone receptor Hormone Binding: Causes is attached to a molecule RXR dissociation from HSPs which forms a heterodimer. (Option Dimerization: Forms homodimers C) (Option C) Ligand Binding: Enhances or Nuclear Translocation: Homodimers represses transcription depending move to the nucleus and bind to on coactivators or corepressors hormone response elements Transcriptional Regulation: (HREs) on DNA Modulates gene expression via Transcription: Initiates activation of interaction with HREs target genes (Option D) Both Type 1 and Type 2 receptors do not have intrinsic enzymatic activity. Instead, they function as transcription factors that regulate gene expression in response to hormonal signaling.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 993-994; Solution Pages 1010-1011","sourcePage":993,"sourcePageEnd":994},{"id":"physiology-34-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":15,"question":"What is the primary mechanism of action for receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Activation through intracellular ligand binding"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dimerization upon extracellular hormone-binding followed by phosphorylation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Formation of ion channels in the membrane"},{"letter":"D","text":"Direct activation of G proteins inside the cell"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"RTKs are activated when a hormone binds to the extracellular part of the receptor, inducing dimerization. This leads to the activation of the cytoplasmic kinase domains through trans-phosphorylation, initiating downstream signaling. A) RTKs are activated by hormone binding to the extracellular domain, not by intracellular ligands. C) RTKs do not form ion channels. This mechanism is characteristic of other types of receptors, such as ionotropic receptors. D) RTKs do not directly activate G proteins. This mechanism is associated with G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), not RTKs. Receptor tyrosine kinases (RTKs) • Enzyme-linked receptors located on cell membranes. • Pass through the membrane once; hormone-binding site is extracellular, catalytic site is intracellular. Mechanism of Action: • Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to the extracellular part of the RTK. • Enzyme Activation: Binding activates or inactivates an intracellular enzyme. Some RTKs have intrinsic enzymatic activity, while others depend on associated enzymes. Example: Leptin Receptor • Family: Cytokine receptors. • Hormone: Leptin (regulates appetite and energy balance). • Associated Enzyme: JAK2 (Janus kinase family). Signaling Process: • Dimerization: Leptin binding causes receptor dimerization. • Phosphorylation & Conformation Change: Leptin binding alters receptor conformation, phosphorylating the intracellular part of the JAK receptor. Intracellular Signaling: • Activated JAK2 phosphorylates tyrosine residues within the receptor-JAK2 complex, activating STAT proteins, which lead to target gene activation and protein synthesis. • JAK2 also activates additional pathways, including MAPK and PI3K. • Rapid: Due to activation of the above-said intracellular enzymes. • Slower: Requires new protein synthesis. • Fibroblast Growth Factor • Growth Hormone • Hepatocyte Growth Factor • Insulin • Insulin-like Growth Factor-1 • Leptin • Prolactin • Vascular Endothelial Growth Factor","sourceRef":"Question Pages 994-994; Solution Pages 1011-1012","sourcePage":994,"sourcePageEnd":994},{"id":"physiology-34-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following best describes the activation mechanism of transmembrane guanylyl cyclases (pGCs)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ligand binding induces a conformational change, activating cGMP synthesis."},{"letter":"B","text":"Phosphorylation directly activates cGMP synthesis."},{"letter":"C","text":"The intracellular domain initiates gene transcription."},{"letter":"D","text":"Intracellular proteins bind to activate cGMP synthesis."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Ligand binding to the extracellular domain causes a conformational change that activates the cyclase domains, leading to cGMP synthesis. B) Phosphorylation within the kinase homology domain (KHD) is important but does not directly activate cGMP synthesis; ligand-induced conformational change is the primary mechanism. C) The intracellular domains of pGCs do not bind to DNA or initiate gene transcription; they are involved in cGMP synthesis. D) While some pGCs are regulated by intracellular proteins, the primary activation mechanism for most pGCs involves ligand binding to the extracellular domain. Guanylyl Cyclase Receptor: • Transmembrane guanylyl cyclases (pGCs) are homodimers (~1,050 amino acids each). • Composed of a short transmembrane domain (21-25 hydrophobic amino acids) separating extracellular and intracellular regions. • Extracellular domain acts as a receptor for specific peptide hormones. Domains: • Extracellular Domain: Binds specific ligands. • Juxtamembranous Protein Kinase-Homology Domain (KHD): ~250 residues, lacks kinase activity. • Cyclase Domain: Catalytic region for cGMP formation at the COOH-terminal end. Activation Mechanism: • Ligand Binding: Specific ligands (e.g., ANP) bind, causing a conformational change. • Conformational Change: Rotation within the dimer activates cGMP synthesis by approximating cyclase domains. • Phosphorylation: Activation is modulated by phosphorylation in the KHD; ligand binding may expose phosphorylated sites to phosphatases for dephosphorylation and inactivation. • Desensitization: Prolonged ligand exposure can lead to dephosphorylation of the KHD, reducing pGC activity. Intracellular Signalling: • cGMP Production: Activated cyclase domain produces cGMP from GTP, serving as a second messenger. • cGMP Targets: cGMP-Dependent Protein Kinases (cGKs): Phosphorylate proteins to elicit cellular responses. Phosphodiesterases (PDEs): Regulate cGMP levels by hydrolyzing it. cGMP-Gated Channels: Involved in phototransduction and olfactory signaling • cGMP-Dependent Protein Kinases (cGKs): Phosphorylate proteins to elicit cellular responses. • Phosphodiesterases (PDEs): Regulate cGMP levels by hydrolyzing it. • cGMP-Gated Channels: Involved in phototransduction and olfactory signaling • cGMP-Dependent Protein Kinases (cGKs): Phosphorylate proteins to elicit cellular responses. • Phosphodiesterases (PDEs): Regulate cGMP levels by hydrolyzing it. • cGMP-Gated Channels: Involved in phototransduction and olfactory signaling","sourceRef":"Question Pages 994-994; Solution Pages 1012-1014","sourcePage":994,"sourcePageEnd":994},{"id":"physiology-34-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following statements about the adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway and the phospholipase C pathway is INCORRECT?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In the adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway, hormone binding to a Gs-coupled GPCR leads to activation of adenylyl cyclase, which converts ATP to cAMP."},{"letter":"B","text":"In the phospholipase C pathway, phospholipase C converts PIP2 into inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adenylyl cyclase activation in the adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway leads to the inhibition of cAMP-dependent protein kinase."},{"letter":"D","text":"In the phospholipase C pathway, IP3 mobilizes calcium ions from internal stores, and DAG activates protein kinase C to mediate cellular responses."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Adenylyl cyclase activation in the adenylyl cyclase-cAMP pathway leads to the activation of cAMP-dependent protein kinase. Adenylyl Cyclase-cAMP Pathway • Activation: (Option A) Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR coupled to a Gs protein on the cell membrane. G Protein Activation: Gs protein stimulates adenylyl cyclase. cAMP Formation: Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cyclic AMP (cAMP). • Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR coupled to a Gs protein on the cell membrane. • G Protein Activation: Gs protein stimulates adenylyl cyclase. • cAMP Formation: Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cyclic AMP (cAMP). • The action of cAMP: Activation: cAMP activates cAMP-dependent protein kinase. Phosphorylation: This kinase phosphorylates specific proteins in the cell. Effects: Varies by cell type: Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. • Activation: cAMP activates cAMP-dependent protein kinase. • Phosphorylation: This kinase phosphorylates specific proteins in the cell. • Effects: Varies by cell type: Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. • Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. • Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. • Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR coupled to a Gs protein on the cell membrane. • G Protein Activation: Gs protein stimulates adenylyl cyclase. • cAMP Formation: Adenylyl cyclase converts ATP to cyclic AMP (cAMP). • Activation: cAMP activates cAMP-dependent protein kinase. • Phosphorylation: This kinase phosphorylates specific proteins in the cell. • Effects: Varies by cell type: Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. • Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. • Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. • Thyroid Cells: Stimulates production of thyroxine and triiodothyronine. • Adrenocortical Cells: Stimulates secretion of adrenocortical steroid hormones. Phospholipase C Pathway • Activation: Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR. Enzyme Activation: Activates phospholipase C attached to the receptor. Breakdown of PIP2: Phospholipase C converts phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate (PIP2) into inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG). (Option B) • Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR. • Enzyme Activation: Activates phospholipase C attached to the receptor. • Breakdown of PIP2: Phospholipase C converts phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate (PIP2) into inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG). (Option B) • The action of IP3 and DAG: IP3: Mobilises calcium ions from mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum. (Option D) Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. DAG: Activates protein kinase C. Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses. • IP3: Mobilises calcium ions from mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum. (Option D) Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. • Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. • DAG: Activates protein kinase C. Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses. • Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses. • Hormone Binding: Hormone binds to a GPCR. • Enzyme Activation: Activates phospholipase C attached to the receptor. • Breakdown of PIP2: Phospholipase C converts phosphatidylinositol bisphosphate (PIP2) into inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG). (Option B) • IP3: Mobilises calcium ions from mitochondria and the endoplasmic reticulum. (Option D) Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. • Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. • DAG: Activates protein kinase C. Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses. • Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses. • Effects: Influences smooth muscle contraction and cell secretion. • Phosphorylation: Protein kinase C phosphorylates various proteins, affecting cellular responses.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 994-995; Solution Pages 1014-1016","sourcePage":994,"sourcePageEnd":995},{"id":"physiology-34-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes a mechanism involving GPCRs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GPCRs have eight transmembrane segments."},{"letter":"B","text":"The α subunit of G proteins binds GDP when activated."},{"letter":"C","text":"Prolonged hormone exposure can decrease active GPCR numbers."},{"letter":"D","text":"The α subunit permanently inactivates the GPCR."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Prolonged hormone exposure can lead to down-regulation of GPCRs. • Membrane-Bound Receptors: GPCRs are integral membrane proteins vital for signal transduction, especially for protein, peptide, and catecholamine hormones. • Structure: Characterised by seven transmembrane segments that span the cell membrane. (Option A) • Binding Sites: Extracellular: Hormone-binding site. Intracellular: Interacts with G proteins. • Extracellular: Hormone-binding site. • Intracellular: Interacts with G proteins. • Extracellular: Hormone-binding site. • Intracellular: Interacts with G proteins. • Heterotrimeric: Consist of α, β, and γ subunits. • Inactive State: G protein complex binds GDP on the α subunit. • Interaction with Effectors: α subunit interacts with enzymes or ion channels. • Example: Activation of adenylyl cyclase, converting ATP to cAMP. • Intrinsic GTPase Activity: α subunit hydrolyzes GTP to GDP, inactivating the α subunit. • Dynamic Receptor Numbers: Receptor numbers on target cells can change. • Down-Regulation: Prolonged hormone exposure reduces receptor numbers, decreasing sensitivity. Mechanisms include: Receptor inactivation. Receptor internalization and sequestration. Receptor degradation by lysosomes. Reduced receptor production. • Receptor inactivation. • Receptor internalization and sequestration. • Receptor degradation by lysosomes. • Reduced receptor production. • Up-Regulation: Increases receptor numbers or sensitivity, enhancing responsiveness. • Receptor inactivation. • Receptor internalization and sequestration. • Receptor degradation by lysosomes. • Reduced receptor production. (Option D) The α subunit does not permanently inactivate the GPCR. Instead, it initiates downstream signaling cascades when activated by exchanging GDP for GTP. After activation, the GTP-bound α subunit dissociates from the βγ subunits and interacts with effector proteins to transmit signals within the cell. The GPCR itself can undergo desensitization and internalization after activation, but this is not caused by the α subunit permanently inactivating it.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 995-995; Solution Pages 1016-1017","sourcePage":995,"sourcePageEnd":995},{"id":"physiology-34-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which type of hormone receptor binds to hydrophobic hormones and acts primarily as a transcription factor within the nucleus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"G protein-coupled receptor (GPCR)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Insulin receptor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Estrogen receptor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ion channel receptor"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The estrogen receptor is a nuclear receptor that binds to hydrophobic hormones, translocates to the nucleus, and acts as a transcription factor to regulate gene expression. B) The insulin receptor is a tyrosine kinase receptor located on the cell surface. D) Ion channel receptors are cell surface receptors that allow ions to pass through the membrane in response to ligand binding, not involved in transcriptional regulation. Hormone receptors are categorized based on their cellular localization and signaling mechanisms, influenced by the chemical properties of the hormones they bind. Membrane-bound Receptors: • Associated Hormones: Hydrophilic hormones (peptide hormones, catecholamines) • Properties: Located on the cell surface Bind extracellular, water-soluble hormones Cannot pass through the lipid bilayer • Located on the cell surface • Bind extracellular, water-soluble hormones • Cannot pass through the lipid bilayer • Signaling Mechanism: Initiate intracellular signaling cascades Common example: G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) GPCRs interact with intracellular heterotrimeric G proteins Trigger downstream events altering cellular functions, such as enzyme activity or ion channel gating • Initiate intracellular signaling cascades • Common example: G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) • GPCRs interact with intracellular heterotrimeric G proteins • Trigger downstream events altering cellular functions, such as enzyme activity or ion channel gating • Types: G-protein coupled receptors Guanylyl cyclase receptors Tyrosine kinase receptors Cytokine receptors Serine kinase receptors • G-protein coupled receptors • Guanylyl cyclase receptors • Tyrosine kinase receptors • Cytokine receptors • Serine kinase receptors • Located on the cell surface • Bind extracellular, water-soluble hormones • Cannot pass through the lipid bilayer • Initiate intracellular signaling cascades • Common example: G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) • GPCRs interact with intracellular heterotrimeric G proteins • Trigger downstream events altering cellular functions, such as enzyme activity or ion channel gating • G-protein coupled receptors • Guanylyl cyclase receptors • Tyrosine kinase receptors • Cytokine receptors • Serine kinase receptors Nuclear Receptors • Associated Hormones: Hydrophobic hormones (steroid hormones, thyroid hormones) • Properties: Located intracellularly, typically in the cytoplasm Can passively diffuse across the cell membrane • Located intracellularly, typically in the cytoplasm • Can passively diffuse across the cell membrane • Signaling Mechanism: Translocate to the nucleus upon ligand binding Function as transcription factors Form homodimers or heterodimers Bind to hormone response elements on DNA Modulate gene expression by influencing transcriptional coactivators or corepressors • Translocate to the nucleus upon ligand binding • Function as transcription factors • Form homodimers or heterodimers • Bind to hormone response elements on DNA • Modulate gene expression by influencing transcriptional coactivators or corepressors • Located intracellularly, typically in the cytoplasm • Can passively diffuse across the cell membrane • Translocate to the nucleus upon ligand binding • Function as transcription factors • Form homodimers or heterodimers • Bind to hormone response elements on DNA • Modulate gene expression by influencing transcriptional coactivators or corepressors","sourceRef":"Question Pages 995-995; Solution Pages 1017-1019","sourcePage":995,"sourcePageEnd":995},{"id":"physiology-34-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes the activity of these hormones?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Protein hormones diffuse through the cell membrane and act on nuclear receptors."},{"letter":"B","text":"Steroidal hormones bind to cell surface receptors and initiate second messenger cascades."},{"letter":"C","text":"Protein hormones bind to cell surface receptors and initiate intracellular signaling cascades."},{"letter":"D","text":"Steroidal hormones are hydrophilic and cannot penetrate the cell membrane."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Protein hormones are hydrophilic and bind to receptors located on the cell surface, initiating intracellular signaling cascades involving second messengers like cAMP and calcium ions. Aspect Protein Hormones Steroid Hormones Nature Hydrophilic Hydrophobic (Option D) Cell Entry Cannot penetrate the cell Freely diffuse through the cell membrane (Option A) membrane (Option D) Receptor Locatio Cell surface receptors (Option A) Nuclear receptors (primarily in the n cytoplasm) Receptor Family GPCRs Nuclear receptor family Signaling Initiate intracellular signaling Receptor-hormone complex moves Mechanism cascades to the nucleus Intracellular Second messengers like cAMP and Form dimers, bind to DNA, regulate Activity calcium ions gene transcription (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 995-995; Solution Pages 1019-1019","sourcePage":995,"sourcePageEnd":995},{"id":"physiology-34-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following hormones can diffuse through the cell membrane and primarily act inside the cell by binding to nuclear receptors?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Catecholamines"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cortisol"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vasopressin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Cortisol is a steroid hormone, which is hydrophobic and can diffuse through the cell membrane. Once inside the cell, it binds to nuclear receptors to regulate gene transcription. Category Type Action Mechanism Examples Peptide/Protein Hormones Water-Soluble Bind to cell surface Insulin, Vasopressin receptors (Options A&D) Amine Hormones Water-Soluble Bind to cell surface Catecholamines receptors (Option B) Steroid Hormones Hydrophobic Diffuse into cells, bind Cortisol, Mineralocorticoid nuclear receptors s Thyroid Hormones Hydrophobic Diffuse into cells, bind Thyroid Hormone nuclear receptors Detailed Mechanism of Hydrophobic Hormones: • They bind to a family of large cytoplasmic proteins known as nuclear receptors. • Nuclear receptors: Found in the cytoplasm. Bind to hormones, forming receptor-ligand complexes. Complex moves to the nucleus. Forms homodimers or heterodimers. Binds to DNA to regulate gene transcription. • Found in the cytoplasm. • Bind to hormones, forming receptor-ligand complexes. • Complex moves to the nucleus. • Forms homodimers or heterodimers. • Binds to DNA to regulate gene transcription. • Found in the cytoplasm. • Bind to hormones, forming receptor-ligand complexes. • Complex moves to the nucleus. • Forms homodimers or heterodimers. • Binds to DNA to regulate gene transcription.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 996-996; Solution Pages 1020-1020","sourcePage":996,"sourcePageEnd":996},{"id":"physiology-34-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-34","chapter":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topic":"Hormone Mechanism & Pituitary hormone","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 35 y/o woman reports feeling tired during the day and struggles to stay awake at work. Her sleep study reveals that her sleep-wake cycle is disrupted, pointing to an irregular circadian rhythm. Which of the following is not an example of a circadian rhythm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sleep-wake cycle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Body temperature fluctuations."},{"letter":"C","text":"Menstrual cycle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Peaks of cortisol levels in serum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The menstrual cycle is an example of an infradian rhythm, which lasts longer than 24 hours, typically around 28 days Types of Biological Rhythm Bio Ultradian Circadian Infradian logi cal Rh yth m Me All types of ‘short-term Circadian rhythm The period of infradian ch rhythms’ with a frequency describes the 24-hour rhythms is longer than the ani of fewer than 24h but oscillations of biological circadian rhythms with sm commonly with periods in processes associated with ranges from days to years the range of 20 min to 6h the dark/light cycle and the earth’s daily rotation Ex The episodic secretion of Melatonin is secreted by Menstrual cycle- Pituitary am insulin from the beta-cell the pineal gland and the gonadotropins (follicle-stim ple of the pancreas regulates liver and it regulates the ulating and luteinizing blood glucose levels. circadian rhythms of hormones) and Ovarian GnRH from the various physiological hormones (estrogen and hypothalamus regulates activities. Its biosynthesis progesterone) show reproductive function. increases at night and is infradian rhythms. inhibited during daytime by the light detected by the retina. Serum concentrations of cortisol show marked variation within the day with the highest level detected in the early morning(Option D) Sleep-wake cycle(Option A) Body temperature fluctuations over 24 hrs (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 996-996; Solution Pages 1021-1021","sourcePage":996,"sourcePageEnd":996},{"id":"physiology-35-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35-year-old woman presents with hypertension and hypokalemia. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in the genes coding for epithelial sodium channels (ENaCs). Which of the following conditions is most likely associated with her symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Conn syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Liddle syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucocorticoid-remediable aldosteronism (GRA)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary hyperaldosteronism"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Liddle syndrome is characterized by hypertension and hypokalemia due to a mutation in the genes coding for epithelial sodium channels (ENaCs), leading to increased sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion. • Rare genetic disorder causing high blood pressure and low potassium levels. • Caused by a mutation in genes coding for epithelial sodium channels (ENaCs) in kidneys. • Leads to increased sodium reabsorption and potassium excretion. • Characterized by excessive aldosterone secretion. • Also a cause of high blood pressure and low potassium levels. • Classified into primary and secondary types. Feature Primary Hyperaldosteronism Secondary Hyperaldosteronism (Option A) (Option D) Cause Adrenal gland disorders High plasma renin activity conditions Example Adenomas, adrenal hyperplasia, Cirrhosis, heart failure, nephrosis, s carcinoma, Glucocorticoid-Remediab salt-losing adrenogenital syndrome le Aldosteronism (Option C) Renin Suppressed Elevated Activity GRA Me Hybrid gene sensitive to ACTH Not applicable chanism ECF Vol Normal or increased Decreased ume","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1022-1022; Solution Pages 1028-1029","sourcePage":1022,"sourcePageEnd":1022},{"id":"physiology-35-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which layer of the adrenal cortex is correctly paired with its primary hormone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Zona fasciculata - Androgens"},{"letter":"B","text":"Zona glomerulosa - Aldosterone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Zona reticularis - Cortisol"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Zona Glomerulosa produces mineralocorticoids mainly aldosterone and small amounts of deoxycorticosterone. Layers of Adrenal gland Adrenal Cortex: Zones of the cortex Hormones produced Main function Morphology of Zone Zona Glomerulosa Mineralocorticoid Maintenance of sodium Small clumps of cells (Option B) Aldosterone(main) balance Deoxycorticosterone Zona fasciculata Glucocorticoids Maintenance of sugars Columnar cells that form (Option A) Cortisol long cords Corticosterone Zona reticularis (Option Sex steroids Maintenance of sexual Network of intercalated C) Dehydroepiandrostero functioning cells ne Androstenedione Adrenal medulla: Medulla Catecholamines: Carbohydrate and fat Specialized sympathetic Norepinephrine metabolisms ganglion Epinephrine Fight or flight responses Clumps and strands of Small amounts of chromaffin cells dopamine","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1022-1022; Solution Pages 1029-1030","sourcePage":1022,"sourcePageEnd":1022},{"id":"physiology-35-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 50-year-old patient has been on long-term corticosteroid therapy for rheumatoid arthritis. The physician advises tapering the dose gradually rather than stopping it abruptly. What is the reason for this advice?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"To avoid the risk of adrenal insufficiency"},{"letter":"B","text":"To avoid the risk of Cushing's syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"To prevent a sudden increase in blood glucose levels"},{"letter":"D","text":"To prevent sudden increase in blood pressure"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Pituitary-adrenocortical axis takes about 10 months to recover from the suppressive effect of prolonged corticosteroid therapy, which can lead to adrenal insufficiency if steroids are stopped abruptly. Corticosteroid feedback mechanism: • Circadian rhythm: Diurnal pattern with peak in morning and low in evening, independent of external factors • The adrenal cortex atrophies and becomes unresponsive to the ACTH for about six months after a prolonged treatment with steroids. • Adrenal insufficiency is precipitated as a result. (Option A). Regulation of aldosterone: (Option B) Cushing's syndrome, (Option C) high blood sugar and (Option D) high blood pressure occur in higher levels of steroid levels in plasma and not in insufficiency.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1022-1022; Solution Pages 1030-1031","sourcePage":1022,"sourcePageEnd":1022},{"id":"physiology-35-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which among the following statements are true regarding the action of aldosterone to enhance sodium reabsorption at the molecular level in renal cells? (a) Inhibits the excretion of K+ to preserve Na+ (b) Activation of sgk gene (serum and glucocorticoid- regulated kinase) (c) Increases ENaC (Epithelial Na+ Channels) activity (d) Increases activity of Na+-K+ exchanger","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a, b, and c"},{"letter":"B","text":"a and c"},{"letter":"C","text":"b, c and d"},{"letter":"D","text":"c and d"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"The gene activated by aldosterone is the sgk gene (serum and glucocorticoid- regulated kinase), which increases ENaC (Epithelial Na+ Channels) activity. Aldosterone also stimulates the activity of Na+-K+ exchanger, which increases intracellular accumulation of Na+. Aldosterone Mechanism of action: • It has both genomic and nongenomic actions. • Aldosterone binds with the cytoplasmic receptor, and the complex moves to the nucleus where it induces transcription of mRNA leading to new protein synthesis that alters cell function. Genomic Action: • The gene activated by aldosterone is the sgk gene (serum and glucocorticoid- regulated kinase). (Statement b) • Increases ENaC (Epithelial Na+ Channels) activity (Statement c) • Increases insertion of these channels on cell membrane and also increases their activity Nongenomic Action: • It is due to stimulation of the activity of Na+-K+ exchanger (Statement c), which increases intracellular accumulation of Na+. (Statement a) aldosterone increases amounts of Na+ concentration in exchange for K+ and H+ in the renal tubules, producing a K+ diuresis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1023-1023; Solution Pages 1031-1032","sourcePage":1023,"sourcePageEnd":1023},{"id":"physiology-35-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following is the primary physiological trigger for the suppression of aldosterone secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased serum potassium levels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Expansion of the extracellular fluid (ECF) volume"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased renin release"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased blood pressure"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Expansion of the extracellular fluid (ECF) volume increases the mean arterial pressure in renal arteries, which inhibits renin thus decreasing the aldosterone secretion. Regulation of aldosterone: • Renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAS) plays a crucial role in the regulation of aldosterone levels in the plasma. • ACTH stimulates aldosterone synthesis and secretion, but the quantity of ACTH required to stimulate aldosterone secretion is more than the concentration required to stimulate cortisol secretion. Physiological Actions of Aldosterone: • The main function of aldosterone is to increase Na+ and water reabsorption and to promote K+ and H+ excretion from the kidney (Option A). • It acts primarily on P cells (principal cells) of collecting the duct and distal tubule of the kidney to increase the sodium reabsorption. • Chlorine is transported in the same direction to maintain electroneutrality due to Na+ reabsorption, causing osmotic reabsorption of water which leads to ECF expansion (Option B) and increases blood pressure (Option D). • (Option D) Increased renin release stimulates aldosterone secretion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1023-1023; Solution Pages 1032-1033","sourcePage":1023,"sourcePageEnd":1023},{"id":"physiology-35-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following describes the mechanism by which glucocorticoids regulate metabolic reactions and ACTH secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Directly catalyzing metabolic reactions and stimulating ACTH secretion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Binding to glucocorticoid receptors and acting as transcription factors"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inhibiting catecholamine action and decreasing mRNA synthesis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Influence cellular processes by directly altering gene expression."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Glucocorticoids bind to their receptors, forming complexes that act as transcription factors, promoting the transcription of specific DNA segments, and leading to the synthesis of enzymes that alter cell function. • Production: Steroid hormones produced by the adrenal cortex. • Mechanism of Action: Bind to glucocorticoid receptors. Form steroid-receptor complexes acting as transcription factors. Promote transcription of specific DNA segments. Lead to synthesis of enzymes altering cell function via mRNA generation. (Option C) • Bind to glucocorticoid receptors. • Form steroid-receptor complexes acting as transcription factors. • Promote transcription of specific DNA segments. • Lead to synthesis of enzymes altering cell function via mRNA generation. (Option C) • Nongenomic Actions: Influence cellular processes without directly affecting gene expression. (Option D) • Bind to glucocorticoid receptors. • Form steroid-receptor complexes acting as transcription factors. • Promote transcription of specific DNA segments. • Lead to synthesis of enzymes altering cell function via mRNA generation. (Option C) • Role in Metabolism: Presence necessary for certain metabolic reactions, though not directly causing them. Calorigenic Effects: Enable glucagon and catecholamines. Lipolytic Effects: Allow catecholamines. Pressor Responses & Bronchodilation: Facilitate actions of catecholamines. • Calorigenic Effects: Enable glucagon and catecholamines. • Lipolytic Effects: Allow catecholamines. • Pressor Responses & Bronchodilation: Facilitate actions of catecholamines. • Calorigenic Effects: Enable glucagon and catecholamines. • Lipolytic Effects: Allow catecholamines. • Pressor Responses & Bronchodilation: Facilitate actions of catecholamines.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1023-1023; Solution Pages 1033-1034","sourcePage":1023,"sourcePageEnd":1023},{"id":"physiology-35-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 45-year-old male with a history of type 2 diabetes presents with difficulty managing his blood glucose levels. Which of the following effects of glucocorticoids best explains the patient's difficulty in managing his diabetes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glucocorticoids enhance the action of insulin in peripheral tissues"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucocorticoids decrease gluconeogenesis in the liver"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucocorticoids promote protein anabolism"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glucocorticoids counteract the effects of insulin in peripheral tissues"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Glucocorticoids elevate plasma glucose levels by promoting gluconeogenesis and glycogenesis in the liver and counteracting insulin effects in peripheral tissues, making diabetes management more difficult. Effects of Glucocorticoids: Intermediary Metabolism: Promote protein catabolism. (Option C) Stimulate liver glycogenesis and gluconeogenesis. (Option B) Enhance glucose-6-phosphatase activity. Elevates plasma glucose level. Counteract insulin effects in peripheral tissues, worsening diabetes. (Option A) (Option D) In diabetes, elevate plasma lipid levels and promote ketone body formation. In healthy individuals, insulin secretion counters this effect. Permissive Action: Essential for actions of other hormones (e.g., glucagon and catecholamines). Necessary for catecholamine-induced lipolysis, pressor responses, and bronchodilation. Effects on ACTH Secretion: Regulate adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) secretion via negative feedback. Vascular Reactivity: Maintain blood vessel responsiveness to norepinephrine and epinephrine. Deficiency leads to decreased vascular responsiveness, capillary dilation, increased permeability, and potential vascular collapse. Water Metabolism: Regulate water balance. Adrenal insufficiency impairs water excretion, increasing water intoxication risk. Blood Cells and Lymphatic Organs: Decrease eosinophils and basophils. Increase neutrophils, platelets, and red blood cells. Decrease lymphocytes and reduce lymph node and thymus size. Stress Resistance: Crucial for stress response. Stress induces ACTH secretion, increasing glucocorticoid levels essential for survival. Regulation of Glucocorticoid Secretion:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1024-1024; Solution Pages 1034-1035","sourcePage":1024,"sourcePageEnd":1024},{"id":"physiology-35-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with symptoms of hypertension and hypokalemia. Laboratory tests reveal elevated levels of aldosterone.Which of the following statements is true regarding the enzyme and the zone responsible for producing aldosterone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aldosterone is produced in the zona fasciculata, and the key enzyme involved is 17α-Hydroxylase"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aldosterone is synthesized from pregnenolone via progesterone in the zona glomerulosa"},{"letter":"C","text":"In the zona reticularis, pregnenolone is converted to aldosterone by Aldosterone Synthase"},{"letter":"D","text":"The zona glomerulosa produces aldosterone directly from cholesterol, bypassing pregnenolone and progesterone"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"In the zona glomerulosa, pregnenolone is first transformed into progesterone, which is then further converted into aldosterone through several enzymatic steps. The final conversion to aldosterone is catalyzed by aldosterone synthase (P450c11AS or CYP11B2), an enzyme specific to this zona. Steroid Biosynthesis in the Adrenal Cortex: Overview: • Location: Adrenal Cortex • Starting Material: Cholesterol End Products: Mineralocorticoids, Glucocorticoids, Androgens Zona Conversion Pathway Key Enzymes Steroid Hormones Produced Zona Glomer Pregnenolone → Aldosterone Synthase Aldosterone ulosa Progesterone → (P450c11AS) Aldosterone Zona Fascicu Pregnenolone → 17α-Hydroxylase Cortisol, Corticostero lata Progesterone or ne 17α-Hydroxypregnenolone Zona Reticul Pregnenolone → 17α-Hydroxylase DHEA, Androstenedi aris 17α-Hydroxypregnenolone one Enzyme Specificity: Adrenal Cortex Zone Enzymes Present Steroid Hormones Produced Zona Glomerulosa Aldosterone Synthase (P450c11AS) Aldosterone (Option A) (Option B) Zona Fasciculata 17α-Hydroxylase, others (Option A) Cortisol, Corticosterone Zona Reticularis 17α-Hydroxylase, others DHEA, Androstenedione (Option C) Enzyme Absence: • Zona Glomerulosa: Lacks 17α-Hydroxylase (No cortisol or sex hormones) • Zona Fasciculata and Zona Reticularis: Lacks Aldosterone Synthase (No aldosterone)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1024-1024; Solution Pages 1035-1036","sourcePage":1024,"sourcePageEnd":1024},{"id":"physiology-35-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 3-year-old child presents with signs of early virilization and ambiguous genitalia. Laboratory tests show elevated levels of ACTH and 17-hydroxyprogesterone. Which enzyme deficiency is most likely responsible for this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"11β-hydroxylase deficiency"},{"letter":"B","text":"21β-hydroxylase deficiency"},{"letter":"C","text":"3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency"},{"letter":"D","text":"17α-hydroxylase deficiency"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"21β-hydroxylase deficiency leads to reduced cortisol and aldosterone production, causing elevated ACTH and increased production of androgens, resulting in virilization and ambiguous genitalia. Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia (CAH) • Definition: Group of genetic disorders affecting adrenal glands, causing enzyme deficiencies in steroid biosynthesis. • Effects: Disruption in cortisol and aldosterone production, leading to various symptoms. • Prevalence: Most common deficiency is 21β-hydroxylase deficiency (90%+ cases). • Impact: Reduced cortisol and aldosterone production. • Consequence: Increased ACTH secretion. (Option B) • Result: Increased precursor steroids converted to androgens → Virilization. Enzyme Deficiency Description Symptoms/Consequences Cholesterol Fatal in utero, prevents placental Death before birth. desmolase deficiency progesterone synthesis. Steroidogenic acute Known as congenital lipoid adrenal Low steroid production, female regulatory (StAR) hyperplasia. Prevents cholesterol genitalia regardless of genetic sex. protein deficiency movement into mitochondria. 3β-Hydroxysteroid Increases DHEA secretion (weak Masculinization in females, dehydrogenase androgen). hypospadias in males. (Option C) deficiency 17α-Hydroxylase Caused by mutated CYP17 gene. Female external genitalia, deficiency Prevents sex hormone production. hypertension, hypokalemia due to high 11-deoxycorticosterone. (Option D- not early virilization) 11β-Hydroxylase Increased 11-deoxycortisol and Virilization, hypertension due to salt deficiency 11-deoxycorticosterone secretion. and water retention. (Option A) • Glucocorticoid treatment: Recommended for all virilizing forms of CAH. Purpose: Addresses glucocorticoid deficit. Effect: Inhibits ACTH secretion, reducing androgen and steroid production. • Purpose: Addresses glucocorticoid deficit. • Effect: Inhibits ACTH secretion, reducing androgen and steroid production. • Purpose: Addresses glucocorticoid deficit. • Effect: Inhibits ACTH secretion, reducing androgen and steroid production.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1024-1025; Solution Pages 1036-1037","sourcePage":1024,"sourcePageEnd":1025},{"id":"physiology-35-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45-year-old woman she exhibits positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs. Which of the following mechanisms best explains the patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased ionized calcium levels due to hyperventilation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased ionized calcium levels due to albumin binding in alkalosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased total calcium levels due to chronic kidney diseas"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decreased total calcium levels due to vitamin D overdose"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Alkalosis, which can occur due to hyperventilation or other factors, leads to more calcium binding to albumin, thereby decreasing the levels of free-ionized calcium. This reduction in ionized calcium increases neuromuscular excitability, resulting in symptoms like muscle cramps andtingling sensations and positive Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs. Total Body Calcium Regulation: • Primary Reservoir: Bone acts as the main calcium reservoir. Serum calcium normal range = 8.5-10.5 mg/dL Forms of ECF Calcium: • Ionized Calcium (50%): Most physiologically important form, influencing heart, nervous system, and bone formation. • Protein-bound Calcium (41%): Bound to plasma proteins like albumin. • Anion-bound Calcium (9%): Bound to anions in the ECF. Binding Dynamics of Calcium and Hydrogen Ions: Acidosis: Competition with Hydrogen Ions: Hydrogen ions compete with calcium for binding sites on albumin, increasing levels of free calcium. Alkalosis: (option B) Increased Binding of Calcium: More calcium binds to albumin, reducing free calcium levels and increasing excitability. Other options: Option A and C) Increased calcium would not lead to symptoms of neuromuscular excitability Option D) Vitamin D overdose would cause increased calcium levels","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1025-1025; Solution Pages 1037-1038","sourcePage":1025,"sourcePageEnd":1025},{"id":"physiology-35-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 25-year-old woman is admitted to the emergency department with symptoms of muscle spasms and difficulty breathing. She has recently been hyperventilating due to anxiety and reports a tingling sensation in her fingers. On examination, she exhibits a positive Chvostek sign and Trousseau sign. Which of the following best explains the underlying mechanism of tetany in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased calcium absorption due to high levels of parathyroid hormone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased ionized calcium leading to increased excitability of nerve and muscle cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased protein ionization due to low pH causing more free Ca2+ availability"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhanced bone calcium mobilization from decreased parathyroid hormone levels"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Tetany is primarily caused by a decrease in extracellular ionized calcium (Ca2+). This reduction increases the excitability of nerve and muscle cells, leading to symptoms such as muscle spasms and laryngospasm. Tetany: • It is characterized by extensive skeletal muscle spasms • Cause: Decrease in extracellular ionized calcium (Ca2+), leading to increased excitability of nerve and muscle cells. • Mechanism: Increased Excitability: Reduced extracellular Ca2+ lowers its binding to voltage-gated sodium channels in nerve and muscle cells. Action Potential Generation: Lower Ca2+ binding decreases the activation threshold of these channels, making them more likely to open and trigger action potentials. • Complication: Laryngospasm can obstruct the airway and potentially cause fatal asphyxia. Calcium and Tetany: • Normal Total Calcium Levels: Total calcium might appear normal, but a decrease in ionized calcium can still cause tetany. • Influence of pH: Increased pH (alkalosis) from hyperventilation increases plasma protein ionization, binding more Ca2+ and reducing free Ca2+ availability. Signs of Tetany: • Chvostek Sign: Contraction of facial muscles upon tapping the facial nerve. • Trousseau Sign: Spasm of upper extremity muscles, causing wrist and thumb flexion with finger extension; elicited by occluding circulation with a blood pressure cuff.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1025-1025; Solution Pages 1038-1039","sourcePage":1025,"sourcePageEnd":1025},{"id":"physiology-35-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding parathyroid hormone (PTH) and calcium regulation is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"PTH promotes calcium reabsorption and phosphate absorption in the renal tubules"},{"letter":"B","text":"PTH directly enhances calcium absorption in the intestines by increasing the expression of calcium-binding proteins"},{"letter":"C","text":"PTH directly activates osteoclasts, which break down bone tissue and release calcium into the extracellular fluid"},{"letter":"D","text":"PTH is essential for the conversion of inactive vitamin D to its active form"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"PTH acts as a key regulator, preventing calcium levels from falling too low and maintaining a stable calcium concentration in the extracellular fluid, even in the face of fluctuations in calcium intake or loss. PTH and Calcium regulation: Factor Description Effect on Calcium Levels PTH A hormone secreted by the Increases parathyroid glands in response to low extracellular calcium levels. Bone Resorption PTH stimulates osteoblasts, which Increases (Option C) signal osteoclasts to break down bone tissue, releasing calcium and phosphate into the bloodstream. Renal Excretion PTH increases calcium reabsorption Increases (Option A) in the renal tubules, reducing calcium loss through urine. PTH decreases phosphate reabsorption in the kidneys. Intestinal PTH indirectly enhances calcium Increases Absorption and phosphate absorption in the (Option B) intestines. This active form of vitamin D promotes the formation of calbindin, a calcium-binding protein, in intestinal cells. Vitamin D While not directly regulated by PTH, Increases vitamin D plays a crucial role in calcium regulation. Vitamin D must be converted to its active form, 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol, in the kidneys. PTH is essential for this conversion. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1025-1026; Solution Pages 1039-1040","sourcePage":1025,"sourcePageEnd":1026},{"id":"physiology-35-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following best describes the primary role of vitamin D in calcium regulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vitamin D directly stimulates osteoclast activity to release calcium from bones"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitamin D enhances calcium reabsorption in the kidneys, reducing its excretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitamin D increases the production of parathyroid hormone (PTH), which mobilizes calcium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitamin D facilitates the intestinal absorption of calcium, increasing its uptake from food"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"The main role of vitamin D in calcium regulation is to enhance calcium absorption in the intestines along with potassium reabsorption. This increases the serum calcium and potassium concentration. Active Vitamin D production: Actions of 1,25-Dihydroxycholecalciferol Action Key concepts site Intestine Increases the production of calbindin (calcium-binding s protein) in intestinal epithelial cells which increases calcium absorption. Kidneys Increases calcium and phosphate reabsorption in the renal tubules, reducing their excretion in urine. This effect is relatively weak Bones Excessive vitamin D can cause bone resorption. Smaller quantities actually promote bone calcification.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1026-1026; Solution Pages 1040-1041","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1026},{"id":"physiology-35-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements best describes the primary mechanism by which calcitonin lowers blood calcium levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulates osteoblast activity to build new bone tissue"},{"letter":"B","text":"Increases the absorption of calcium in the intestines"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inhibits osteoclast activity to reduce bone resorption"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhances the reabsorption of calcium in the kidneys"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the parafollicular cells (C cells) of the thyroid gland that plays a crucial role in regulating calcium homeostasis. Its primary mechanism for lowering blood calcium levels involves inhibiting the activity of osteoclasts, which are the cells responsible for breaking down bone tissue (bone resorption) Calcitonin: • A hormone produced by the parafollicular cells (C cells) of the thyroid gland. • Its primary function is to lower blood calcium levels (hypocalcemic effect). Calcium Regulation: • Calcitonin helps to regulate calcium levels in the blood, working in opposition to parathyroid hormone (PTH), which increases blood calcium levels. Mechanism of Action Action site Key concepts Bone Inhibition of Osteoclast Activity by binding to calcitonin receptors on osteoclast (Option A) and (Option C) Decreased release of calcium and phosphate from bone Kidneys Increased Calcium Excretion (Option D) Reduces the reabsorption of calcium and phosphate in the renal tubules. Intestine Decreased Calcium Absorption (Minor Role) (Option B) Effect is less compared to kidney Secretion Triggers: • High blood calcium levels • Gastrin and pentagastrin (hormones involved in digestion)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1026-1026; Solution Pages 1041-1042","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1026},{"id":"physiology-35-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-35","chapter":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topic":"Adrenal Physiology and Calcium Regulation","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following markers is primarily used to assess bone resorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Osteocalcin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bone-Specific Alkaline Phosphatase (BSAP)"},{"letter":"C","text":"C-terminal propeptide of type I procollagen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cathepsins K"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Cathepsin K primarily in osteoclast plays an essential role in osteoclast-mediated bone matrix degradation. Markers Bone resorption Bone formation Tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase Osteocalcin (Option A) Cathepsins K (Option D) Bone-specific alkaline phosphatase (Option B) Collagen-related markers C-terminal propeptide of type I procollagen (Option C) Aminoterminal cross-linked telopeptide of type I collagen","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1026-1027; Solution Pages 1042-1042","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1027},{"id":"physiology-36-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements about the pancreatic islets is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-cells are the most prevalent and primarily secrete insulin."},{"letter":"B","text":"B cells constituting 60–75% of islet cells primarily secrete glucagon"},{"letter":"C","text":"F cells, which are the most abundant, primarily secrete pancreatic polypeptide."},{"letter":"D","text":"B cells are the most prevalent and secrete insulin."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) B cells are the most prevalent and secrete insulin.","explanation":"B cells are the most prevalent in the islets of Langerhans and they secrete insulin to lower blood glucose levels. Pancreas Overview: • Location: Abdominal cavity • Functions: Endocrine: Hormone secretion (Islets of Langerhans) Exocrine: Digestive enzyme secretion • Endocrine: Hormone secretion (Islets of Langerhans) • Exocrine: Digestive enzyme secretion • Islets of Langerhans: Size: 76–175 µm Distribution: Throughout the pancreas, concentrated in the tail Volume: ~2% of pancreas volume Blood Supply: Rich, drains into hepatic portal vein • Size: 76–175 µm • Distribution: Throughout the pancreas, concentrated in the tail • Volume: ~2% of pancreas volume • Blood Supply: Rich, drains into hepatic portal vein • Endocrine: Hormone secretion (Islets of Langerhans) • Exocrine: Digestive enzyme secretion • Size: 76–175 µm • Distribution: Throughout the pancreas, concentrated in the tail • Volume: ~2% of pancreas volume • Blood Supply: Rich, drains into hepatic portal vein Islet Cell Types: Cell Type Percentage of Islet Location Hormone Secreted Hormone Function Cells B cells 60-75% Center Insulin (Option B) Lowers blood glucose A cell (Option 20% Surround B Glucagon Increases blood A) cells glucose D cells Less common Encircle B Somatostatin Regulates islet cell cells secretion F cells Less common Encircle B Pancreatic polypept Regulates ion (Option C) cells ide transport in the intestine","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1043-1043; Solution Pages 1052-1053","sourcePage":1043,"sourcePageEnd":1043},{"id":"physiology-36-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which conditions would most likely result in a high insulin-glucagon molar (I/G ratio)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prolonged fasting"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adopting a low-carbohydrate diet"},{"letter":"C","text":"Starvation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Consumption of a large carbohydrate meal"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Consumption of a large carbohydrate meal","explanation":"Eating a meal rich in carbohydrates significantly increases insulin secretion to promote the storage of glucose as glycogen, thus resulting in a high I/G ratio. Prolonged fasting (Option A) decreases insulin levels while increasing glucagon secretion to mobilize stored energy, resulting in a low I/G ratio. A low-carbohydrate diet (Option B)reduces insulin levels and increases glucagon secretion to maintain blood glucose levels, leading to a lower I/G ratio. During starvation (Option C), the body relies heavily on glucagon to mobilize energy stores, resulting in a very low I/G ratio. Insulin Glucagon Ratio Condition Hepatic Glucose Storage/ I/G Ra Features Production tio Large Carbohydrate Storage 70 Indicates energy Meal abundance Promotes glycogenesis, lipogenesis, and protein synthesis IV Glucose Storage 25 Small Meal Storage 7 Overnight Fasting Production 2.3 Indicates the need to mobilise energy stores Promotes glycogenolysis, gluconeogenesis, and ketogenesis Low Carbohydrate Production 1.8 Diet Starvation Production 0.4","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1043-1043; Solution Pages 1053-1054","sourcePage":1043,"sourcePageEnd":1043},{"id":"physiology-36-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following actions is NOT a physiological effect of glucagon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stimulating glycogen synthesis in the liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Promoting gluconeogenesis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increasing glycogen breakdown in the liver"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enhancing lipolysis in the liver"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Stimulating glycogen synthesis in the liver","explanation":"Glucagon inhibits glycogen synthesis in the liver by inactivating glycogen synthase. It prevents the storage of glucose as glycogen, instead promoting glucose release into the bloodstream. Promoting gluconeogenesis (Option B): Glucagon activates enzymes involved in gluconeogenesis, increasing glucose production from non-carbohydrate sources, such as amino acids. Increasing glycogen breakdown in the liver (Option C): Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis, breaking down glycogen stores in the liver to increase blood glucose levels. Enhancing lipolysis in the liver (Option D): Glucagon promotes lipolysis, breaking down triglycerides in the liver to free fatty acids and glycerol, which can be used for energy production. Glucagon Stimulators Inhibitors Amino acids (alanine, arginine) High blood glucose levels Cortisol Somatostatin Exercise Insulin Infections Free fatty acids and ketones","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1043-1043; Solution Pages 1054-1054","sourcePage":1043,"sourcePageEnd":1043},{"id":"physiology-36-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following does not stimulate insulin secretion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"α-Adrenergic receptor activation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Activation of parasympathetic nerves"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stimulation of β-Adrenergic receptors"},{"letter":"D","text":"Release of GLP-1"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) α-Adrenergic receptor activation","explanation":"Activation of α-adrenergic receptors, typically by norepinephrine during sympathetic stimulation, inhibits insulin secretion. Parasympathetic activation enhances insulin secretion, through the release of acetylcholine, which acts on M3 receptors in pancreatic beta cells. (Option B) Stimulation of β2-adrenergic receptors in the sympathetic system can promote insulin secretion, especially when the inhibitory effects of α-adrenergic receptors are blocked. (Option C) GLP-1 is an incretin hormone that stimulates insulin secretion in response to meals, aiding in the regulation of blood glucose levels. (Option D) INSULIN Stimulators of Insulin Release Inhibitors of Insulin Release Glucose Low Blood Glucose Amino acids (e.g., arginine, lysine) Somatostatin Gastrointestinal hormones (e.g., GLP-1, GIP) α-Adrenergic receptor activation Parasympathetic stimulation (via acetylcholine) Leptin β-Adrenergic receptor activation Galanin Sulfonylurea drugs","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1043-1044; Solution Pages 1055-1055","sourcePage":1043,"sourcePageEnd":1044},{"id":"physiology-36-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the mechanisms of action of oral hypoglycemic drugs is FALSE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GLP-1 analogues enhance insulin release only at high glucose concentrations and reduce glucagon release"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sulfonylureas bind to the sulfonylurea receptor (SUR1) on pancreatic β-cells, stimulating insulin secretion even at low glucose concentrations."},{"letter":"C","text":"GLP-1 analogues increase gastric emptying by stimulating specific GLP-1 receptors."},{"letter":"D","text":"DPP-4 inhibitors increase endogenous GLP-1 levels by preventing its degradation."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) GLP-1 analogues increase gastric emptying by stimulating specific GLP-1 receptors.","explanation":"GLP-1 analogues slow gastric emptying and suppress appetite by activating specific GLP-1 receptors. ORAL HYPOGLYCEMIC DRUGS Sulphonylureas Mechanism of action: Sulfonylureas bind to the (Option B) sulfonylurea receptor (SUR1) on the membrane of pancreatic β-cells, which stimulates insulin secretion regardless of glucose concentration. This results in a significant and often unregulated release of insulin, potentially leading to severe hypoglycemia, especially at low glucose levels. Examples: Tolbutamide, Glipizide, Glibenclamide. GLP-1 Analogues Mechanism of Action: GLP-1 analogues activate GLP-1 (Option A) receptors, which are GPCRs located on pancreatic β-cells, α-cells, central and peripheral neurons, and gastrointestinal mucosa. This activation promotes insulin secretion specifically in response to high glucose levels, inhibits glucagon release, slows gastric emptying, and suppresses appetite. GLP-1 analogues also contribute to pancreatic β-cell health by reducing islet cell apoptosis. Examples: Exenatide, Liraglutide. DPP-4 Inhibitors Mechanism of Action: DPP-4 inhibitors block the (Option D) enzyme DPP-4, which normally breaks down endogenous GLP-1. By inhibiting this enzyme, these drugs increase the levels of GLP-1, thereby enhancing insulin secretion indirectly and improving glycemic control. Examples: Sitagliptin, Saxagliptin, Vildagliptin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1044-1044; Solution Pages 1055-1056","sourcePage":1044,"sourcePageEnd":1044},{"id":"physiology-36-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What is the primary mechanism by which glucose stimulates insulin secretion in pancreatic β-cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glucose stimulates insulin release directly by binding to its receptor on β-cells, triggering immediate secretion."},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucose is metabolized to pyruvate, increasing ATP, which inhibits ATP-sensitive K+ channels, causing Ca2+ influx and insulin release."},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucose lowers intracellular glutamate, promoting insulin secretion by altering pH in secretory granules and affecting their release."},{"letter":"D","text":"Glucose enhances the uptake of calcium into the β-cells without affecting ATP levels, thereby directly increasing the secretion of insulin."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Glucose is metabolized to pyruvate, increasing ATP, which inhibits ATP-sensitive K+ channels, causing Ca2+ influx and insulin release.","explanation":"• Glucose metabolism produces ATP, which inhibits ATP-sensitive K+ channels. This inhibition leads to depolarization of the β-cell membrane, causing Ca2+ influx through voltage-gated channels, and triggering insulin release. • Insulin secretion is regulated through a biphasic response to plasma glucose levels. Biphasic response of Insulin secretion First Rapid Phase Second Prolonged Phase Glucose enters pancreatic β-cells via GLUT-2 Pyruvate metabolism increases intracellular glutamate transporters. levels. (Option C) Inside the cell, glucose is phosphorylated by Glutamate acts as an intracellular second messenger, glucokinase and converted to pyruvate. priming a second pool of secretory granules for release. Pyruvate is metabolized in the mitochondria, It may also lower the pH in granules, aiding in their producing ATP. (Option D) maturation. Increased ATP inhibits ATP-sensitive K+ This leads to a prolonged phase of insulin secretion. channels, leading to reduced K+ efflux. Peak in about 60 minutes and then remain elevated for This depolarizes the β-cell membrane, causing 3–5 hours. voltage-gated Ca2+ channels to open. Ca2+ influx triggers the exocytosis of a pool of insulin-containing granules, Results in a rapid, short-lived increase in insulin secretion. Peak within 1–2 minutes and then decreases to basal level in another 2 to 3 minutes","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1044-1044; Solution Pages 1056-1057","sourcePage":1044,"sourcePageEnd":1044},{"id":"physiology-36-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Match the following actions of insulin with the correct category: Categories: a) Increased by insulin b) Decreased by insulin Insulin Action: Cell growth Reabsorption of K+, Na+, and phosphate from the kidney Food intake Entry of phosphate and magnesium into the cell Body weight Lipolysis Potassium uptake into cells","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a)-2,3,5,6,7 & b)-1,4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a)-4,5,6 & b)-1,2,3,7"},{"letter":"C","text":"a)-1,3,4,6 & b)-2,5,7"},{"letter":"D","text":"a)-1,2,4,5,7 & b)-3,6"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) a)-1,2,4,5,7 & b)-3,6","explanation":"a) Increased by insulin • Cell growth: Insulin promotes cell growth and proliferation. • Reabsorption of K+, Na+, and phosphate from the kidney: Insulin enhances the reabsorption of these electrolytes in the kidneys. • Entry of phosphate and magnesium into the cell: Insulin facilitates the uptake of phosphate and magnesium into cells. • Body weight: Insulin can increase body weight due to its effects on fat storage increasing adipose tissue mass. • Potassium uptake into cells: Insulin promotes the movement of potassium into cells, which can lower blood potassium levels. Therefore is very useful in the management of acute renal failure. Insulin is considered a physiological regulator of plasma K+ concentration. b) Decreased by insulin • Food intake: Insulin can promote satiety by increasing leptin levels in plasma, leading to decreased food intake. Also partly mediated by its inhibitory effect on neuropeptide Y release from the hypothalamus. • Lipolysis: Insulin inhibits lipolysis, which is the breakdown of fats into fatty acids and glycerol.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1045-1045; Solution Pages 1057-1058","sourcePage":1045,"sourcePageEnd":1045},{"id":"physiology-36-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following are the actions of insulin? 1) Increased lipogenesis 2) Increased ketogenesis 3) Increased glycogen synthesis 4) Increased glucose uptake 5) Increased gluconeogenesis 6) Increased protein synthesis","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3,4,5"},{"letter":"B","text":"2,5,6"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,3,4,6"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,3,6"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) 1,3,4,6","explanation":"• Lipogenesis: Insulin promotes the synthesis of fatty acids and triglycerides, especially in adipose tissue. • Glycogen synthesis: Insulin stimulates glycogen synthesis in both muscle and liver tissues. • Glucose uptake: Insulin increases glucose uptake into insulin-sensitive cells such as muscle and adipose tissue. • Protein synthesis: Insulin promotes the uptake of amino acids into cells, enhancing protein synthesis. • Ketogenesis: Insulin decreases ketogenesis. It reduces the breakdown of fatty acids and the formation of ketone bodies in the liver. (Option 2) • Gluconeogenesis: Insulin inhibits gluconeogenesis in the liver, reducing the production of glucose(Option 5)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1045-1045; Solution Pages 1058-1058","sourcePage":1045,"sourcePageEnd":1045},{"id":"physiology-36-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A medical student curious about the physiological effects of insulin learns that insulin has rapid, intermediate, and delayed actions on various metabolic processes. Which of the following is an intermediate action of insulin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased transport of K+ into insulin-sensitive cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stimulation of protein synthesis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase in mRNAs for lipogenic enzymes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased transport of amino acids into insulin-sensitive cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Stimulation of protein synthesis","explanation":"Stimulation of protein synthesis is an intermediate action of insulin that occurs within minutes. PRINCIPAL ACTIONS OF INSULIN Rapid (seconds) Increased transport of Glucose, Amino acids (Option D) K+ (Option A) into insulin-sensitive cells Intermediate (minutes) Stimulation of protein synthesis Inhibition of protein degradation Activation of glycolytic enzymes and glycogen synthase Inhibition of phosphorylase and gluconeogenic enzymes Delayed (hours) Increase in mRNAs for lipogenic and other enzymes (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1045-1045; Solution Pages 1059-1059","sourcePage":1045,"sourcePageEnd":1045},{"id":"physiology-36-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements about insulin receptors, GLUT, and SGLT is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The insulin receptor's β subunits are responsible for insulin binding."},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT-4 transporters increase glucose uptake in response to insulin."},{"letter":"C","text":"SGLT transporters primarily function in muscle and adipose tissue."},{"letter":"D","text":"SGLT-2 inhibitors work by increasing glucose absorption in the kidneys."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) GLUT-4 transporters increase glucose uptake in response to insulin. Explantion: Insulin triggers the translocation of GLUT-4 transporters to the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake. Insulin Receptor, GLUT, and SGLT Overview: Insulin Receptor: • Structure: Tetramer of two α and two β glycoprotein subunits • Synthesis: Single mRNA, separated via proteolysis, linked by disulfide bonds • Gene Location: Chromosome 19, with 22 exons • Subunit Functions: α Subunits: External, insulin-binding (Option A) β Subunits: Span the membrane, intracellular segments have tyrosine kinase activity Both subunits are glycosylated • α Subunits: External, insulin-binding (Option A) • β Subunits: Span the membrane, intracellular segments have tyrosine kinase activity • Both subunits are glycosylated • α Subunits: External, insulin-binding (Option A) • β Subunits: Span the membrane, intracellular segments have tyrosine kinase activity • Both subunits are glycosylated • Mechanism: Insulin Binding: Triggers tyrosine kinase activity in β subunits Autophosphorylation: Of β subunits at tyrosine residues Signal Cascade: Phosphorylation/dephosphorylation of cytoplasmic proteins, mainly serine and threonine residues, mediated by insulin receptor substrate (IRS-1) and other effectors • Insulin Binding: Triggers tyrosine kinase activity in β subunits • Autophosphorylation: Of β subunits at tyrosine residues • Signal Cascade: Phosphorylation/dephosphorylation of cytoplasmic proteins, mainly serine and threonine residues, mediated by insulin receptor substrate (IRS-1) and other effectors • Insulin Binding: Triggers tyrosine kinase activity in β subunits • Autophosphorylation: Of β subunits at tyrosine residues • Signal Cascade: Phosphorylation/dephosphorylation of cytoplasmic proteins, mainly serine and threonine residues, mediated by insulin receptor substrate (IRS-1) and other effectors GLUT (Glucose Transporters): • Function: Facilitate glucose movement into cells • Types: Seven known types have tissue-specific functions. • GLUT-4: Primary Location: Muscle and adipose tissue (Option C) Regulation: Directly regulated by insulin Mechanism: Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake • Primary Location: Muscle and adipose tissue (Option C) • Regulation: Directly regulated by insulin • Mechanism: Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake • Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles • Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake • Exercise: Induces insulin-independent GLUT-4 translocation, aiding in blood glucose regulation • Primary Location: Muscle and adipose tissue (Option C) • Regulation: Directly regulated by insulin • Mechanism: Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake • Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles • Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake • Absence of Insulin: GLUT-4 in intracellular vesicles • Presence of Insulin: Vesicles translocate and fuse with the cell membrane, increasing glucose uptake SGLT (Sodium Glucose Linked Transporters): • Function: Reabsorption of glucose in the kidneys (Option C) • Mechanism: • SGLT2 Location: Primarily in the proximal tubule of the nephron • Co-transport Process: Utilises the sodium gradient created by the Na+/K+ ATPase pump to transport sodium and glucose from the renal tubular lumen into the cells • Clinical Relevance: SGLT2 Inhibitors: Drugs like canagliflozin and empagliflozin Mechanism of Action: Prevent glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, leading to increased glucose excretion in urine (Option D) Therapeutic Use: Lower blood glucose levels in type 2 diabetes without causing hypoglycemia • SGLT2 Inhibitors: Drugs like canagliflozin and empagliflozin • Mechanism of Action: Prevent glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, leading to increased glucose excretion in urine (Option D) • Therapeutic Use: Lower blood glucose levels in type 2 diabetes without causing hypoglycemia • SGLT2 Inhibitors: Drugs like canagliflozin and empagliflozin • Mechanism of Action: Prevent glucose reabsorption in the kidneys, leading to increased glucose excretion in urine (Option D) • Therapeutic Use: Lower blood glucose levels in type 2 diabetes without causing hypoglycemia Note: Unlike other tissues, brain cells can take up and use glucose without the need for insulin.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1046-1046; Solution Pages 1059-1061","sourcePage":1046,"sourcePageEnd":1046},{"id":"physiology-36-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following statements about insulin synthesis is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Insulin synthesis begins in the Golgi apparatus with the formation of proinsulin."},{"letter":"B","text":"The C peptide is removed during the conversion of preproinsulin to proinsulin."},{"letter":"C","text":"The insulin gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 11."},{"letter":"D","text":"Insulin and C peptides are released from B cells in equimolar amounts."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Insulin and C peptides are released from B cells in equimolar amounts.","explanation":"Insulin and C peptides are released in equimolar amounts from B cells. Insulin Synthesis in B Cells: • Location: B cells of pancreatic islets • Gene: Chromosome: Short arm of chromosome 11 (Option C) Structure: Three exons, two introns • Chromosome: Short arm of chromosome 11 (Option C) • Structure: Three exons, two introns • Chromosome: Short arm of chromosome 11 (Option C) • Structure: Three exons, two introns Key Steps in Insulin Synthesis: • Synthesis: Preproinsulin is synthesized in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER). (Option A) • Folding: Preproinsulin folds and forms disulfide bonds to become proinsulin. • C Peptide Role: C peptide links the A and B chains and is crucial for folding. • Transport: Proinsulin moves to the Golgi apparatus. • Packaging: It is packaged into membrane-bound granules. • Proteolysis: Two proteases in the granules cleave the C peptide, producing insulin. (Option B) • Storage: Insulin is stored within the granules. • Granule Movement: Granules move to the plasma membrane via microtubules. • Exocytosis: Granules release insulin and C peptide into the bloodstream. • Release Ratio: 90–97% of the released product is insulin, with an equimolar amount of C peptide (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1046-1046; Solution Pages 1062-1062","sourcePage":1046,"sourcePageEnd":1046},{"id":"physiology-36-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 45-year-old female presents with a painless, enlarging neck mass. Ultrasound reveals multiple thyroid nodules. Which of the following histological findings is most consistent with an inactive thyroid gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Small follicles with cuboidal cells and minimal colloid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Large follicles with abundant colloid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Numerous granular endoplasmic reticulum, lysosomes, and Golgi complexes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parafollicular cells secreting calcitonin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Large follicles with abundant colloid","explanation":"Large follicles with abundant colloids are characteristic of an inactive thyroid gland. Histology of Thyroid gland • Formed by acini or thyroid follicles Thyroid Follicles: • Shape: Spherical • Consists of a single layer of epithelial cells surrounding a central colloid. • Colloid is a viscous gel-like substance containing thyroglobulin. • Inactive Gland: Follicles are large with abundant colloids. • Active Gland: Follicles are small, cells are cuboidal, and colloid is present in small quantities. (Option A) Follicular cells Parafollicular cells The Apical Membrane of follicular cells faces the colloid Also known as C cells and is covered with microvilli. Located close to the The cytoplasm contains numerous granular follicles. endoplasmic reticulum, lysosomes, and Golgi complexes. Basal Membrane is in close contact with numerous Secrete calcitonin. capillaries in the interfollicular space. Numerous granular endoplasmic reticulum, lysosomes, and Golgi complexes (Option C) are present in follicular cells but do not differentiate between active and inactive states. Parafollicular cells secrete calcitonin (Option D) but are not indicative of the activity state of the thyroid gland.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1046-1046; Solution Pages 1063-1064","sourcePage":1046,"sourcePageEnd":1046},{"id":"physiology-36-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the treatment of thyroid disorders is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Propylthiouracil is used to treat hypothyroidism."},{"letter":"B","text":"Radioactive iodine therapy is used for the treatment of Graves disease."},{"letter":"C","text":"Levothyroxine helps treat hyperthyroidism."},{"letter":"D","text":"Hashimoto thyroiditis causes a long-term increase in thyroid hormone production."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) Radioactive iodine therapy is used for the treatment of Graves disease.","explanation":"Radioactive iodine therapy destroys thyroid tissue, reducing hormone production, and is commonly used in Graves disease. Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism represent the two extremes of thyroid dysfunction, resulting from under-activity and overactivity of the thyroid gland, respectively. These conditions have distinct causes, symptoms, and treatments. • Causes: Iodine deficiency: Inadequate iodine intake inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis, leading to decreased secretion and compensatory thyroid hypertrophy eg. goiter. Autoimmune destruction: eg. Hashimoto thyroiditis (Option D) involves antibodies and immune cells attacking the thyroid gland, causing hypothyroidism. Pituitary or hypothalamic failure: Disruptions in the pituitary gland or hypothalamus → ↓TSH or TRH→ ↓ thyroid stimulation. Drug-induced hypothyroidism: eg. Amiodarone, Lithium Wolff-Chaikoff Effect: A mild and transient inhibition of iodide organic binding and hormone synthesis caused by large doses of iodide acting directly on the thyroid gland in healthy individuals. • Iodine deficiency: Inadequate iodine intake inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis, leading to decreased secretion and compensatory thyroid hypertrophy eg. goiter. • Autoimmune destruction: eg. Hashimoto thyroiditis (Option D) involves antibodies and immune cells attacking the thyroid gland, causing hypothyroidism. • Pituitary or hypothalamic failure: Disruptions in the pituitary gland or hypothalamus → ↓TSH or TRH→ ↓ thyroid stimulation. • Drug-induced hypothyroidism: eg. Amiodarone, Lithium • Wolff-Chaikoff Effect: A mild and transient inhibition of iodide organic binding and hormone synthesis caused by large doses of iodide acting directly on the thyroid gland in healthy individuals. • Clinical features: Reduced cognitive function, fatigue, lethargy. Cold intolerance Weight gain despite poor appetite Dry, yellowish skin (carotenemia) and puffiness (myxedema) due to protein complex accumulation. Constipation: Slowed digestive processes. Thinning hair on the scalp and/or other areas. • Reduced cognitive function, fatigue, lethargy. • Cold intolerance • Weight gain despite poor appetite • Dry, yellowish skin (carotenemia) and puffiness (myxedema) due to protein complex accumulation. • Constipation: Slowed digestive processes. • Thinning hair on the scalp and/or other areas. • Treatment: Addressing underlying causes: Iodine supplementation, hormone replacement therapy, and medication adjustments. Levothyroxine: Synthetic T4 administered to replace deficient thyroid hormone. (Option C) • Addressing underlying causes: Iodine supplementation, hormone replacement therapy, and medication adjustments. • Levothyroxine: Synthetic T4 administered to replace deficient thyroid hormone. (Option C) • Iodine deficiency: Inadequate iodine intake inhibits thyroid hormone synthesis, leading to decreased secretion and compensatory thyroid hypertrophy eg. goiter. • Autoimmune destruction: eg. Hashimoto thyroiditis (Option D) involves antibodies and immune cells attacking the thyroid gland, causing hypothyroidism. • Pituitary or hypothalamic failure: Disruptions in the pituitary gland or hypothalamus → ↓TSH or TRH→ ↓ thyroid stimulation. • Drug-induced hypothyroidism: eg. Amiodarone, Lithium • Wolff-Chaikoff Effect: A mild and transient inhibition of iodide organic binding and hormone synthesis caused by large doses of iodide acting directly on the thyroid gland in healthy individuals. • Reduced cognitive function, fatigue, lethargy. • Cold intolerance • Weight gain despite poor appetite • Dry, yellowish skin (carotenemia) and puffiness (myxedema) due to protein complex accumulation. • Constipation: Slowed digestive processes. • Thinning hair on the scalp and/or other areas. • Addressing underlying causes: Iodine supplementation, hormone replacement therapy, and medication adjustments. • Levothyroxine: Synthetic T4 administered to replace deficient thyroid hormone. (Option C) • Causes: Graves disease: Autoimmune condition where antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, leading to excessive T4 and T3 secretion and goiter. Toxic adenoma or multinodular goiter: Benign thyroid nodules autonomously produce thyroid hormones. Early stages of Hashimoto thyroiditis: Initial inflammation can cause a transient phase of hyperthyroidism. TSH-secreting pituitary tumor: Rare tumors secrete excessive TSH, overstimulating the thyroid gland. • Graves disease: Autoimmune condition where antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, leading to excessive T4 and T3 secretion and goiter. • Toxic adenoma or multinodular goiter: Benign thyroid nodules autonomously produce thyroid hormones. • Early stages of Hashimoto thyroiditis: Initial inflammation can cause a transient phase of hyperthyroidism. • TSH-secreting pituitary tumor: Rare tumors secrete excessive TSH, overstimulating the thyroid gland. • Symptoms: Nervousness and irritability: Increased CNS stimulation. Weight loss: Despite increased appetite (hyperphagia), elevated metabolism causes weight loss. Rapid heartbeat (tachycardia) and palpitations: Thyroid hormones stimulate cardiac function. Tremor: Fine tremor of the outstretched fingers. Eye changes: Protruding eyeballs (exophthalmos) in Graves disease. • Nervousness and irritability: Increased CNS stimulation. • Weight loss: Despite increased appetite (hyperphagia), elevated metabolism causes weight loss. • Rapid heartbeat (tachycardia) and palpitations: Thyroid hormones stimulate cardiac function. • Tremor: Fine tremor of the outstretched fingers. • Eye changes: Protruding eyeballs (exophthalmos) in Graves disease. • Treatment: Antithyroid medications: Thioureylenes like propylthiouracil (Option A) and methimazole inhibit thyroid hormone synthesis. Radioactive iodine therapy: Destroys thyroid tissue, reducing hormone production. • Antithyroid medications: Thioureylenes like propylthiouracil (Option A) and methimazole inhibit thyroid hormone synthesis. • Radioactive iodine therapy: Destroys thyroid tissue, reducing hormone production. • Graves disease: Autoimmune condition where antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor, leading to excessive T4 and T3 secretion and goiter. • Toxic adenoma or multinodular goiter: Benign thyroid nodules autonomously produce thyroid hormones. • Early stages of Hashimoto thyroiditis: Initial inflammation can cause a transient phase of hyperthyroidism. • TSH-secreting pituitary tumor: Rare tumors secrete excessive TSH, overstimulating the thyroid gland. • Nervousness and irritability: Increased CNS stimulation. • Weight loss: Despite increased appetite (hyperphagia), elevated metabolism causes weight loss. • Rapid heartbeat (tachycardia) and palpitations: Thyroid hormones stimulate cardiac function. • Tremor: Fine tremor of the outstretched fingers. • Eye changes: Protruding eyeballs (exophthalmos) in Graves disease. • Antithyroid medications: Thioureylenes like propylthiouracil (Option A) and methimazole inhibit thyroid hormone synthesis. • Radioactive iodine therapy: Destroys thyroid tissue, reducing hormone production. Surgery: Removal of the thyroid gland, especially if the goiter is large and causing compressive symptoms.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1047-1047; Solution Pages 1064-1067","sourcePage":1047,"sourcePageEnd":1047},{"id":"physiology-36-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":14,"question":"What is the primary metabolic consequence of fasting on thyroid hormone levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"↑T4 to T3 conversion, hence ↑ T3"},{"letter":"B","text":"↓T4 to T3 conversion, ↓plasma T3 levels, and ↑ RT3 levels."},{"letter":"C","text":"↓T3 and normal RT3."},{"letter":"D","text":"↑ BMR"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B) ↓T4 to T3 conversion, ↓plasma T3 levels, and ↑ RT3 levels.","explanation":"Prolonged fasting leads to decreased conversion of T4 to T3, leading to reduced plasma T3 levels and increased RT3 levels. Effect of diet on conversion of T4 to T3: Fasting: • Decreases conversion of T4 to T3, reducing plasma T3 concentration. (Option A) • Plasma T3 reduces by 10-20% within 24 hours and about 50% in 3-7 days. • Facilitates conversion of T4 to RT3, increasing RT3 levels. • Free and bound T4 levels remain normal. • During prolonged starvation (severe,chronic fasting), RT3 returns to normal but T3 remains depressed.(Option C) • Basal metabolic rate (BMR) falls. (Option D) • Urinary nitrogen excretion, an index of protein breakdown, decreases. • The decline in T3 conserves calories and protein Overfeeding: • Increases T3. • Decreased RT3.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1047-1047; Solution Pages 1067-1068","sourcePage":1047,"sourcePageEnd":1047},{"id":"physiology-36-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 28 y/o woman visits her physician with complaints of weight loss, increased appetite, and anxiety. She feels warmer than usual and has noticed an increased frequency of bowel movements.O/E, her heart rate is elevated, and lab tests reveal elevated levels of thyroid hormones. Which of the following best describes the effects of thyroid hormones on metabolism in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decrease oxygen consumption and promote energy storage in tissues."},{"letter":"B","text":"Stimulate lipogenesis and reduce the turnover of glucose in the body."},{"letter":"C","text":"Increase the rate of glucose absorption & lipid oxidation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inhibit proteolysis, thereby conserving muscle mass and reducing metabolic activity."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increase the rate of glucose absorption & lipid oxidation","explanation":"Thyroid hormones promote glucose absorption, stimulate lipid oxidation, and hence increase overall energy expenditure. • They increase BMR in every tissue except the anterior pituitary, brain, gonads, spleen, and lymph nodes. Functions of thyroid hormones: Effect of Thyroid hormone on metabolism Carbohydrate Metabolism Increase absorption of glucose from the intestine. Induce hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, raising plasma glucose. Increase glucose turnover by enhancing its uptake and oxidation. (Option B) Fat Metabolism Stimulate lipolysis, releasing free fatty acids and glycerol. Increase lipid turnover and facilitate oxidation. Stimulate cholesterol synthesis, oxidation, and biliary excretion. Enhance hepatic uptake of cholesterol by increasing LDL receptor expression. Net effect: a significant decrease in plasma cholesterol and total lipid levels. Protein metabolism Promote proteolysis in skeletal muscle, releasing amino (Option D) acids. Increase protein turnover by enhancing protein degradation. Effects of Thyroid Hormone on CVS Heart Rate and Increase the heart rate by upregulating and sensitizing Cardiac Output β-receptors in the SA and AV nodes, leading to tachycardia in hyperthyroidism. Enhance myocardial contractility by increasing stroke volume hence increasing cardiac output (CO). Blood Pressure Systolic blood pressure: ■heart rate and stroke volume results in increased CO Diastolic pressure: decreases due to calorigenic-induced thermogenic cutaneous vasodilation, resulting in decreased peripheral resistance. The overall effect is an increased pulse pressure, which is prominent in hyperthyroidism. Circulation Dynamics Enhanced cardiac output and vasodilation create hyperdynamic circulation, leading to shortened circulation time. Effects of Thyroid Hormone on other systems: Target syste Functions m CNS Important for the maturation of CNS especially during infancy and childhood Essential for overall brain functions including memory, learning, alertness, and reflexes. Lack of thyroid hormones during infancy can cause irreversible CNS development retardation. Skeletal Thyroid hormones increase MHC gene expression in muscle skeletal muscle, leading to muscle cramps and weakness in hypothyroidism. Hyperthyroidism also causes muscle weakness due to increased protein catabolism, with chronic cases resulting in thyrotoxic myopathy. Bones It is essential for normal growth, and musculoskeletal maturation, and promotes the expression of growth hormone (GH) genes. Increase osteoid activity, bone growth, and bone remodeling, and promote the growth of teeth, epidermis, nails, and hair. In hypothyroid children, bone growth slows, and epiphyseal closure is delayed. GIT Stimulates GI motility, ↑ appetite, ↑ Carbohydrate absorption Reproductio Follicular maturation and ovulation n Respiration During hypercapnia & hypoxia, they increase oxygen consumption in tissues by : (Option A) ↑ RR ↑ arterial PO2 ↑ erythropoietin synthesis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1047-1047; Solution Pages 1068-1070","sourcePage":1047,"sourcePageEnd":1047},{"id":"physiology-36-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 35 y/o male presents with symptoms of lethargy, hair loss, and cold intolerance. His lab results show elevated T4 levels but low T3 levels. Which of the following best explains the relationship between T4 and T3 in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T4 is the primary thyroid hormone and is converted to the more active T3 in peripheral tissues."},{"letter":"B","text":"T3 has a longer half-life than T4, allowing it to maintain steady levels in the bloodstream."},{"letter":"C","text":"Elevated T4 levels automatically indicate that T3 levels should also be elevated, due to their synchronous production."},{"letter":"D","text":"T4 is biologically active, while T3 is inactive and serves primarily as a storage form of thyroid hormone."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) T4 is the primary thyroid hormone and is converted to the more active T3 in peripheral tissues.","explanation":"T4 is the primary thyroid hormone secreted by the thyroid gland, and it is converted to the more active T3 in peripheral tissues. Difference between T4 & T3 Aspect T4 T3 Secretion More (35% of total) Less (7% of total) Plasma conc. 8 mg/dL 0.15 mg/dL Protein-bound 99.8% 99.8% Free concentration in plasma 2 ng/dL 0.3 ng/dL Half-life (t1/2) 7 days 1 day (Option B) Action Slower Faster Potency Less More (3-5 times) Binding to thyroid hormone receptors Low affinity High affinity T4 and T3 (Option C) can be produced independently, and high T4 does not guarantee high T3 levels and depends on its peripheral conversion. T3 is the biologically active form of thyroid hormone, not T4 (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1048-1048; Solution Pages 1070-1071","sourcePage":1048,"sourcePageEnd":1048},{"id":"physiology-36-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 50 y/o male presents with fatigue, weight gain, and feeling cold. Blood tests show normal T4, low T3, and elevated RT3 levels. Which of the following best explains the patient's symptoms and lab findings?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased activity of 5´-deiodinase in the liver and kidney"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decreased activity of 5-deiodinase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased activity of 5-deiodinase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Reduced secretion of thyroxine (T4) from the thyroid gland"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Increased activity of 5-deiodinase","explanation":"Increased activity of 5-deiodinase leads to increased conversion of T4 to RT3 by 5-deiodinase leading to higher levels of the physiologically inert RT3 and lower levels of the active T3 Peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 • The peripheral conversion of T4 to T3 is a crucial process that regulates the active thyroid hormone T3 levels, ensuring its availability to target cells. • The major product secreted by the thyroid gland is thyroxine (T4) Conversion to 35% of secreted T4 is converted to T3 in the circulation T3 by the enzyme 5'-deiodinase, mainly found in the liver and kidneys. This process produces about 85% of circulating T3, the most active hormone that primarily affects target cells. Conversion to 45% of T4 is also converted to reverse T3 (RT3) by the RT3 enzyme 5-deiodinase. RT3 is physiologically inert and accounts for 95% of circulating RT3. • The remaining 20% of T4 is conjugated with sulfates and glucuronides. • Deiodinases are enzymes responsible for the metabolism of thyroid hormones. • They play a crucial role in regulating the levels of active thyroid hormones in various tissues. • All deiodinases contain selenocysteine, an amino acid essential for their enzyme activity, making them potent catalysts for oxidoreductive reactions. There are three types of deiodinases: Type of deiodinas Location Function e D1 Liver Function as 5´-deiodinases, Kidney converting thyroxine (T4) to Thyroid triiodothyronine (T3) Pituitary Maintaining intracellular T3 levels in target tissues D2 Skeletal muscle Its function is the same as D1 Brain It maintains a steady supply of T3 to Pituitary neurons in the brain. Placenta Brown fat D3 Brain Converts T4 to reverse T3 (RT3), a Reproductive biologically inactive form. tissue Metabolism of T3 and T4: • T3 is degraded to diiodothyronines in the liver and kidney. • In the liver, T4 and T3 undergo conjugation with sulfates and glucuronic acid, which are then secreted in bile and re-enter the enterohepatic circulation, minimizing iodine loss. • T4 is also metabolized to tetraiodoacetic acid (tetrac) in various tissues. Increased activity of 5´-deiodinase (Option A) would lead to higher levels of T3, not lower, which contradicts the patient's lab findings. Decreased activity of 5-deiodinase (Option B) would result in reduced levels of RT3, not elevated levels as seen in the patient. The patient has normal T4 levels, indicating that thyroxine secretion is not the issue. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1048-1048; Solution Pages 1071-1072","sourcePage":1048,"sourcePageEnd":1048},{"id":"physiology-36-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 30y/o pregnant woman has elevated thyroid-binding globulin (TBG) but shows no symptoms of thyroid dysfunction. Her endocrinologist explains this is common during pregnancy and that her thyroid function remains normal. Which of the following best explains why the pregnant woman is experiencing this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"TSH secretion is decreased due to elevated TBG levels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elevated TBG levels reduce the production of thyroid hormones"},{"letter":"C","text":"A temporary fall in free thyroid hormones stimulates TSH secretion, restoring equilibrium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased TBG levels compensate for the reduced free hormone levels by decreasing the metabolic clearance rate of thyroid hormones"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) A temporary fall in free thyroid hormones stimulates TSH secretion, restoring equilibrium","explanation":"TBG levels are increased in pregnancy which binds to the thyroid hormone and reduces the levels of free thyroid hormone in the blood, this temporary fall in thyroid hormone stimulates TSH secretion, thereby restoring equilibrium. Consequences of Increased TBG Due to Pregnancy • TBG Elevation During Pregnancy: TBG levels are elevated in estrogen-treated patients and during pregnancy, necessitating the above compensatory mechanisms to maintain thyroid hormone balance. • Initial Impact: Decrease in Free Thyroid Hormones: A sudden, sustained increase in thyroid-binding proteins like TBG in the plasma causes a temporary fall in the concentration of free thyroid hormones • Increased TBG levels do bind more thyroid hormones, reducing the levels of free hormones temporarily. Overall binding capacity increases, but it does not necessarily compensate by decreasing the metabolic clearance rate significantly. The primary compensatory mechanism is the increased TSH secretion, which restores free hormone levels. (Option D) • Feedback Mechanism: TSH Secretion: The drop in free thyroid hormones stimulates the secretion of Thyroid Stimulating Hormone (TSH). (Option A) Increased Production: TSH increases the production of free thyroid hormones. New Equilibrium: A new equilibrium is reached where the total quantity of thyroid hormones in the blood is elevated (Option B), but the concentration of free hormones, their metabolism rate, and the TSH secretion rate return to normal. Euthyroid State: Despite the increased total thyroid hormone levels, patients remain euthyroid, meaning they are neither hyperthyroid nor hypothyroid.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1048-1048; Solution Pages 1073-1073","sourcePage":1048,"sourcePageEnd":1048},{"id":"physiology-36-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 38y/o woman presents with symptoms of fatigue, weight gain, and dry skin. Laboratory tests reveal low free T4 and elevated TSH levels. Further investigation shows increased levels of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG). Which of the following is most likely to be associated with increased levels of thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG)","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Phenytoin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Androgens"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucocorticoids"},{"letter":"D","text":"Estrogen"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D) Estrogen","explanation":"Estrogen is seen to cause an increase in levels of thyroxine-binding globulin Thyroid Binding Proteins Thyroid hormones bind with 3 types of plasma proteins Thyroxine Binding Globulin (TBG) Nature: Glycoprotein synthesized by the liver with a molecular weight of 54,000. Binding Capacity: Each TBG molecule has a single binding site for thyroid hormones. Binding Proportion: About 70% of T4 and 45% of T3 bind to TBG. Plasma Level Influences: Increase: During pregnancy and in patients receiving estrogen, major tranquilizers, and clofibrate. Decrease: In patients receiving glucocorticoids, androgens, danazol, L-asparaginase, salicylates, phenytoin, mitotane, and 5-fluorouracil (Option A, B, C) Thyroxine Binding Prealbumin Nature: A globulin, not an albumin, recently designated (TBPA) /Transthyretin as transthyretin. Binding Proportion: T3: Less than 1% binds to TBPA. T4: About 20% binds to TBPA. Albumin Binding Proportion: T3: About 55% binds to albumin. T4: About 10% binds to albumin. • Protein-bound forms of T3 and T4 serve as a reservoir, replenishing circulating hormones when secretion decreases or metabolism increases temporarily. • Protein binding protects the hormones against metabolic degradation, extending their half-lives (1 day for T3 and 7 days for T4). • Free-form concentrations provide feedback effects on hormone regulation. Changes in plasma protein levels temporarily affect thyroid hormone levels, maintaining a euthyroid state despite alterations in binding protein levels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1049-1049; Solution Pages 1073-1074","sourcePage":1049,"sourcePageEnd":1049},{"id":"physiology-36-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A patient with a history of Graves' disease is treated with a high dose of iodide. Initially, there is a reduction in thyroid hormone synthesis due to the Wolff-Chaikoff effect. However, after a few days, the patient's thyroid function returns to normal. Which of the following mechanisms explains this \"escape\" phenomenon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased degradation of thyroglobulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Downregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased conversion of T4 to T3 in peripheral tissues"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Downregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS)","explanation":"Downregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) reduces the intrathyroidal concentration of iodide, allowing normal thyroid hormone synthesis to resume despite high serum iodide levels. Wolff-Chaikoff Effect The Wolff-Chaikoff effect describes the thyroid's response to high doses of iodide, resulting in temporary inhibition of thyroid hormone synthesis. Mechanism: Inhibition of Organic Binding: Large doses of iodide inhibit the organic binding of iodide within the thyroid gland, reducing hormone synthesis. TSH Effect High iodide levels decrease cAMP production in thyroid Inhibition: cells, inhibiting the effect of thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). (Wolff-Chaikoff effect) This reduces the proteolysis of thyroglobulin, further decreasing hormone synthesis. Purpose The Wolff-Chaikoff effect serves to prevent the thyroid from synthesizing excessive amounts of thyroid hormones in the presence of large quantities of iodide. Thyroid Escape Phenomenon: The Wolff-Chaikoff effect is transient and lasts for only a few days. After the acute phase, the thyroid gland resumes normal hormone synthesis through the escape phenomenon. This is achieved by: • Down-regulation of NIS: The sodium iodide symporter (NIS) is down-regulated, reducing intrathyroidal inorganic iodine concentration. • Resumption of TPO-H2O2 System: Normal activity of the thyroid peroxidase (TPO) and hydrogen peroxide system resumes, allowing the normal synthesis of thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3). Increased degradation of thyroglobulin (Option A) does not explain the escape phenomenon Upregulation of the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) (Option B) would increase iodide uptake, which is contrary to the escape mechanism. Increased conversion of T4 to T3 (Option C) in peripheral tissues is unrelated to the escape phenomenon in the context of thyroid hormone synthesis regulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1049-1049; Solution Pages 1074-1075","sourcePage":1049,"sourcePageEnd":1049},{"id":"physiology-36-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-36","chapter":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topic":"Thyroid Hormones & Endocrine Pancreas","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A patient presents with symptoms of hypothyroidism despite adequate iodine intake. Upon investigation, it is found that there is a defect in the enzyme responsible for a key step in thyroid hormone synthesis. Which of the following steps in thyroid hormone synthesis is not facilitated by thyroid peroxidase (TPO)?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oxidation of iodide to iodine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Coupling of iodotyrosines"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iodide trapping"},{"letter":"D","text":"Binding of iodine to thyroglobulin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C) Iodide trapping","explanation":"Iodide trapping involves the active transport of iodide into the thyroid follicular cells via the sodium-iodide symporter (NIS) and is not facilitated by thyroid peroxidase. Thyroid Hormone Synthesis 1. Iodide Trapping Mechanism: Iodide is actively transported into thyroid follicles (iodide pump). Process: Na+ and I■ co-transported into the cell by Na+-I■ symporter (NIS).TSH induces expression and retention of NIS. Na+ is pumped out by the Na+-K+ pump. Iodide is pumped against electrical and chemical gradients using ATP. Other Tissues: Salivary glands, placenta, choroid plexus, mammary gland, gastric mucosa, and ciliary body also accumulate iodide but do not form thyroid hormones. Iodide trapping is stimulated by TSH. 2. Conversion of Iodide Process: Iodide is converted to iodine by oxidation. into Iodine Oxidation (organification) facilitated by thyroid peroxidase. (Option A) 3. Thyroglobulin Thyroglobulin: Glycoprotein with two subunits and 123 Synthesis tyrosine residues. Synthesis: Synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum of thyroid cells. Packaged in Golgi apparatus and secreted into colloid by exocytosis. Facilitated by thyroid peroxidase. (Option D) Binding: Reactive iodine binds to tyrosine on thyroglobulin, forming monoiodotyrosine (MIT) and diiodotyrosine (DIT). 4. The Coupling Formation: Two DIT molecules condense to form Reaction thyroxine (T4). MIT and DIT condense to form triiodothyronine (T3). Reverse-T3 (RT3) formed by condensation of DIT with MIT. Mechanism: Intramolecular coupling (both DIT attached to thyroglobulin). Intermolecular coupling (DIT detached from thyroglobulin). Facilitation: Coupling reaction facilitated by thyroid peroxidase (Option B). 5. Proteolysis of Process: Colloid ingested by thyroid cells via Thyroglobulin endocytosis. Lysosomal enzymes digest peptide bonds in thyroglobulin. Free T4, T3, DIT, and MIT formed in the cytoplasm. Iodotyrosine deiodinase deiodinates MIT and DIT. T3 and T4 are released into circulation. Iodine from deiodination is reutilized by the gland. 6. Secretion of Thyroid Release: T3 and T4 are secreted into general circulation. Hormones MIT and DIT are not secreted by the thyroid gland. Functions of Thyroid Cells: • Accumulate iodide from blood. • Synthesize and secrete thyroglobulin. • Digest colloid and release thyroid hormones.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1049-1050; Solution Pages 1075-1077","sourcePage":1049,"sourcePageEnd":1050},{"id":"physiology-37-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which hormone is specifically produced by the adrenal gland but not by the ovaries in females?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Progesterone"},{"letter":"B","text":"DHEA"},{"letter":"C","text":"Estrogen"},{"letter":"D","text":"DHEA-S"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option: Option D. DHEA-S (Dehydroepiandrosterone sulfate): DHEA-S is produced predominantly by the adrenal glands. The sulfation of DHEA to DHEA-S occurs mainly in the adrenal glands, and it is not produced by the ovaries. Incorrect Option: Option A. Progesterone: Progesterone is produced by both the adrenal glands and the ovaries (primarily by the corpus luteum during the menstrual cycle and by the placenta during pregnancy). Option B. DHEA (Dehydroepiandrosterone): DHEA is produced by both the adrenal glands and the ovaries. Option C. Estrogen: Estrogen is produced by both the adrenal glands and the ovaries (primarily by the ovarian follicles, corpus luteum, and placenta).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1078-1078; Solution Pages 1100-1100","sourcePage":1078,"sourcePageEnd":1078},{"id":"physiology-37-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which hormones have a permissive function in the achievement of puberty?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GnRH"},{"letter":"B","text":"Leptin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"D","text":"GH"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B. • Leptin is a hormone produced by adipose (fat) tissue. It plays a role in regulating energy balance and body weight. Leptin levels are influenced by the amount of body fat, and it communicates with the hypothalamus to regulate appetite and metabolism. Leptin also has an impact on the onset of puberty. Adequate leptin levels are required for the normal initiation and progression of puberty, particularly in females. It acts as a permissive hormone, providing signals to the hypothalamus that the body has sufficient energy reserves for puberty to proceed. Incorrect options: Option A. GnRH (Gonadotropin-Releasing Hormone): GnRH is a hormone produced and released by the hypothalamus. It plays a crucial role in the initiation and regulation of the reproductive system. It stimulates the release of luteinizing hormone (LH) and follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary gland, which in turn stimulates the production of sex hormones (estrogen and testosterone) by the ovaries and testes. While GnRH is necessary for the activation of the reproductive system, it does not directly play a permissive role in the attainment of puberty. Option C. Insulin: Insulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas, and its primary role is to regulate blood glucose levels. While insulin is not directly involved in the attainment of puberty, it plays a role in overall growth and development. Insulin deficiency or resistance can disrupt normal growth and delay the onset of puberty. Option D.GH (Growth Hormone): Growth hormone, also known as somatotropin, is produced by the anterior pituitary gland. It plays a significant role in growth, particularly during childhood and adolescence. While GH is not directly involved in the initiation of puberty, it is necessary for the proper development of the skeletal system and overall growth. Growth hormone deficiency can lead to growth retardation and delayed onset of puberty.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1078-1078; Solution Pages 1101-1101","sourcePage":1078,"sourcePageEnd":1078},{"id":"physiology-37-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the subsequent options is not a contributing factor to the development of secondary hyperparathyroidism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parathyroid adenoma"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lithium intake"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitamin D deficiency"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chronic kidney disease"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: • Secondary hyperparathyroidism refers to the excessive secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH) by the parathyroid glands in response to conditions outside the parathyroid glands themselves. It is important to note that primary hyperparathyroidism, which involves the overproduction of PTH due to a parathyroid adenoma, is not considered secondary hyperparathyroidism. Therefore, parathyroid adenoma is not a cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism. Incorrect Options: Option B: Lithium intake: Chronic use of lithium, a medication commonly prescribed for certain psychiatric disorders, can lead to the development of secondary hyperparathyroidism. Lithium interferes with the regulation of PTH secretion and can result in elevated PTH levels. Option C: Vitamin D deficiency: Vitamin D plays a crucial role in calcium homeostasis, and its deficiency can lead to secondary hyperparathyroidism. Inadequate vitamin D levels impair the absorption of calcium in the intestine, causing a decrease in serum calcium levels. This triggers the release of PTH, which acts to increase calcium levels through various mechanisms. Option D: Chronic kidney disease: Chronic kidney disease (CKD) is a common cause of secondary hyperparathyroidism. In CKD, there is a decline in kidney function, leading to impaired phosphate excretion and decreased production of active vitamin D. These abnormalities disrupt calcium and phosphate homeostasis, resulting in elevated PTH levels and secondary hyperparathyroidism.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1078-1078; Solution Pages 1102-1102","sourcePage":1078,"sourcePageEnd":1078},{"id":"physiology-37-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Insulin acts on glucose metabolism by (or) Insulin acts on glucose metabolism by","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"↑ Facilitated diffusion of glucose"},{"letter":"B","text":"↓ Permeability of glucose across cell membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"↑ Reabsorption of glucose from renal tubules"},{"letter":"D","text":"↑ Glucose reabsorption from intestine"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Answer Option A • Insulin is a hormone produced by the pancreas that plays a critical role in regulating glucose metabolism. Insulin acts on the cell membrane of target cells, such as muscle, liver, and adipose tissue, to facilitate glucose uptake and utilization. • Insulin acts on glucose metabolism by increasing the number of glucose transporters, such as GLUT4, on the cell surface of target cells. This leads to an increased uptake of glucose into the cells by facilitated diffusion. Therefore, option a is the correct choice. Incorrect Choices • Option b. ↓permeability of glucose across the cell membrane: Insulin does not decrease the permeability of glucose across the cell membrane. Instead, it increases the number of glucose transporters on the cell surface, which leads to an increased uptake of glucose into the cells. • Option c. ↑reabsorption of glucose from renal tubules: Insulin does not increase the reabsorption of glucose from renal tubules. Instead, it acts on target cells, such as muscle, liver, and adipose tissue, to facilitate glucose uptake and utilization. • Option d. ↑glucose reabsorption from intestine: Insulin does not increase glucose reabsorption from the intestine. Instead, it acts on target cells to facilitate glucose uptake and utilization.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1079-1079; Solution Pages 1102-1103","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1079},{"id":"physiology-37-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Inhibin is secreted by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Leydig cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sertoli cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Interstitial cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Peg cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option. B • Sertoli cells: Sertoli cells are located in the seminiferous tubules of the testes and play a crucial role in spermatogenesis, the process of sperm cell development. Sertoli cells not only support and nourish developing sperm cells but also secrete inhibin. Inhibin is a hormone that helps regulate the production of follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) from the anterior pituitary gland. In males, inhibin acts as a negative feedback mechanism, inhibiting the secretion of FSH and thereby regulating spermatogenesis. Incorrect options Option A) Leydig cells: Leydig cells, also known as interstitial cells, are found in the testes. They are responsible for producing and secreting testosterone, which is important for the development and maintenance of male reproductive organs and secondary sexual characteristics. Leydig cells do not secrete inhibin. Option C) Interstitial cells: Interstitial cells, or Leydig cells, as mentioned earlier, are found in the testes and are responsible for producing testosterone. They do not secrete inhibin. Option D) Peg cells: Peg cells, also known as secretory cells or nonciliated cells, are found in the female reproductive tract, particularly in the fallopian tubes (oviducts). They secrete various substances, including nutrients and protective factors, to support the fertilization and early development of the embryo. Peg cells do not secrete inhibin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1079-1079; Solution Pages 1103-1104","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1079},{"id":"physiology-37-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":6,"question":"The adrenal medulla is derived from neural crest cells. These neural crest cells originate from the dorsal aorta. Clusters of chromaffin cells become the distinct medulla after birth. The adrenal gland first appears 28-30 days post-conception. Adrenal medulla secrete which hormones?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sex hormones"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyroid stimulating hormones"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epinephrine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glucocorticoids"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option. C • Epinephrine: Yes, the adrenal medulla is responsible for the secretion of epinephrine, also known as adrenaline. Epinephrine is a hormone and neurotransmitter that plays a crucial role in the body's response to stress. It is released in response to various stimuli, such as physical or emotional stress, and it acts on various target tissues to increase heart rate, blood pressure, and metabolic rate. Incorrect options Option A) Sex hormones: The adrenal medulla does not secrete sex hormones. Sex hormones, such as estrogen and testosterone, are primarily produced by the gonads (ovaries in females and testes in males) under the influence of pituitary hormones. Option B) Thyroid stimulating hormone: The adrenal medulla does not secrete thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH). TSH is produced by the anterior pituitary gland and regulates the function of the thyroid gland. Option D) Glucocorticoids: Glucocorticoids, such as cortisol, are not secreted by the adrenal medulla. They are primarily produced by the outer layer of the adrenal gland called the adrenal cortex. Glucocorticoids are involved in regulating metabolism, immune response, and stress response.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1079-1079; Solution Pages 1104-1104","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1079},{"id":"physiology-37-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":7,"question":"For its action, which hormone is able to cross the plasma membrane?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epinephrine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyroxine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucagon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Insulin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct option B - Thyroxine: Thyroxine (T4) is a thyroid hormone that crosses the plasma membrane to exert its action. Once inside the target cells, thyroxine is converted to its active form, triiodothyronine (T3), which then binds to nuclear receptors and affects gene transcription, thus influencing various metabolic processes. Incorrect options: Option A - Epinephrine: Epinephrine (adrenaline) is a hormone that acts on cell surface receptors. It binds to specific receptors on the cell surface, triggering a cascade of intracellular signaling events to initiate its physiological effects. It does not cross the plasma membrane. Option C - Glucagon: Glucagon is a hormone that acts on cell surface receptors. It binds to specific receptors on target cells, primarily in the liver, promoting glycogen breakdown and the release of glucose into the bloodstream. Glucagon does not cross the plasma membrane. Option D - Insulin: Insulin is a hormone that acts on cell surface receptors. It binds to specific receptors on target cells, such as muscle and adipose tissue, promoting glucose uptake and regulating glucose metabolism. Insulin does not cross the plasma membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1080-1080; Solution Pages 1104-1105","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1080},{"id":"physiology-37-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which hormones possess receptors within the cell?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Epinephrine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glucagon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parathormone"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: Intracellular receptors, also known as nuclear receptors, are located inside the cell and primarily reside within the cytoplasm or nucleus. These receptors can directly bind to specific hormones that are able to pass through the cell membrane and enter the cell. Once inside, these hormones bind to their corresponding intracellular receptors, forming a hormone-receptor complex. This complex then enters the nucleus and influences gene expression by binding to specific DNA sequences. Thyroxine (T4) is a hormone produced by the thyroid gland. It is lipid-soluble, allowing it to diffuse across the cell membrane and bind to intracellular receptors. Inside the cell, thyroxine binds to specific nuclear receptors known as thyroid hormone receptors (TRs). The hormone-receptor complex then interacts with specific DNA sequences called thyroid hormone response elements (TREs) to regulate gene expression. This process influences various physiological processes, including metabolism, growth, and development. Incorrect Options: Option B (Epinephrine): Epinephrine, also known as adrenaline, is a hormone and neurotransmitter involved in the fight-or-flight response. However, it acts primarily through cell surface receptors known as adrenergic receptors, which are located on the cell membrane. Epinephrine does not have intracellular receptors. Option C (Glucagon): Glucagon is a hormone produced by the pancreas that regulates glucose metabolism. It acts primarily through cell surface receptors known as glucagon receptors, which are located on the cell membrane. Glucagon does not have intracellular receptors. Option D (Parathormone): Parathormone, also known as parathyroid hormone (PTH), is a hormone produced by the parathyroid glands. It acts primarily through cell surface receptors known as parathyroid hormone receptors, which are located on the cell membrane. Parathormone does not have intracellular receptors.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1080-1080; Solution Pages 1105-1106","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1080},{"id":"physiology-37-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":9,"question":"In regards to regulating local concentrations, the degradation of hormones is observed in __________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"11 β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase inactivation of glucocorticoids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyroid hormone deiodinases inactivating T3"},{"letter":"C","text":"Degradation of retinoic acid by Cyp26b1 in primordial germ cell of males"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D. All of the above Explanation: • Option A. 11 β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase inactivation of glucocorticoids: This enzyme, found in certain tissues such as the liver and adipose tissue, converts active glucocorticoids (such as cortisol) into inactive forms (such as cortisone), regulating local concentrations of these hormones. • Option B. Thyroid hormone deiodinases inactivating T3: Deiodinase enzymes, specifically type 3 deiodinase (D3), play a crucial role in inactivating thyroid hormones, particularly triiodothyronine (T3), by converting it into inactive forms like 3,3'-diiodothyronine (reverse T3). This process helps regulate local thyroid hormone concentrations and is essential for thyroid hormone homeostasis. • Option C. Degradation of retinoic acid by Cyp26b1 in primordial germ cell of males: Cyp26b1 is a cytochrome P450 enzyme that degrades retinoic acid, a derivative of vitamin A and an important signaling molecule. Its degradation in specific cells, such as primordial germ cells, helps regulate the local concentration of retinoic acid, which is essential for various developmental processes. Option A. 11 β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase inactivation of glucocorticoids: This enzyme, found in certain tissues such as the liver and adipose tissue, converts active glucocorticoids (such as cortisol) into inactive forms (such as cortisone), regulating local concentrations of these hormones. Option B. Thyroid hormone deiodinases inactivating T3: Deiodinase enzymes, specifically type 3 deiodinase (D3), play a crucial role in inactivating thyroid hormones, particularly triiodothyronine (T3), by converting it into inactive forms like 3,3'-diiodothyronine (reverse T3). This process helps regulate local thyroid hormone concentrations and is essential for thyroid hormonehomeostasis. Option C. Degradation of retinoic acid by Cyp26b1 in primordial germ cell of males: Cyp26b1 is a cytochrome P450 enzyme that degrades retinoic acid, a derivative of vitamin A and an important signaling molecule. Its degradation in specific cells, such as primordial germ cells, helps regulate the local concentration of retinoic acid, which is essential for various developmental processes. Therefore, the degradation of hormones by these mechanisms helps regulate local concentrations, making option D. All of the above the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1080-1080; Solution Pages 1106-1107","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1080},{"id":"physiology-37-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Enterokinase is an activator of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trypsinogen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trypsin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chymotrypsin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Antitrypsin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option: A • Enterokinase, also known as enteropeptidase, is an enzyme produced in the duodenum of the small intestine. Its primary role is to activate trypsinogen, which is an inactive precursor of trypsin. Trypsinogen is synthesized and released by the pancreas. • Enterokinase acts on trypsinogen, cleaving it at a specific site to generate active trypsin. Once trypsin is formed, it can then further activate other pancreatic zymogens, such as chymotrypsinogen and procarboxypeptidase, initiating the digestive process. Incorrect options: Option B. Trypsin: Trypsin is not activated by enterokinase. Instead, enterokinase is responsible for the activation of trypsinogen to form trypsin. Once trypsin is formed, it can activate other pancreatic zymogens and participate in the digestion of proteins. Option C. Chymotrypsin: Enterokinase does not directly activate chymotrypsin. Chymotrypsinogen, the inactive precursor of chymotrypsin, is activated by trypsin. Trypsin cleaves chymotrypsinogen to generate active chymotrypsin. Therefore, the activation of chymotrypsinogen is dependent on the presence of trypsin, not enterokinase. Option D. Antitrypsin: Antitrypsin, also known as alpha-1 antitrypsin, is a protein that functions as an inhibitor of proteases, including trypsin. It is not activated by enterokinase. Instead, antitrypsin acts to regulate and inhibit the activity of trypsin and other proteases to prevent excessive tissue damage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1080-1081; Solution Pages 1107-1107","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"physiology-37-11","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":11,"question":"What is the duration for which sperm stays in the epididymis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"24hours"},{"letter":"B","text":"48 hours"},{"letter":"C","text":"20 days"},{"letter":"D","text":"96hours"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct option. c • During puberty, cells are transformed into sperms with a head, midsection, and tail. The sperms use their long tails to push themselves out of the tubes into the epididymis. For the next 2 to 4 weeks the sperm swims through the epididymis as it develops further. Other options are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1081-1081; Solution Pages 1108-1108","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"physiology-37-12","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":12,"question":"All are Glucogenic hormones except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ADH"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucagon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyroxine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glucocorticoids"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option. A • Glucogenic hormones are hormones that promote the production or release of glucose in the body. They stimulate processes such as glycogenolysis (breakdown of glycogen), gluconeogenesis (synthesis of glucose from non-carbohydrate sources), and the release of glucose into the bloodstream. Incorrect options: Option B. Glucagon: Glucagon is a hormone produced by the alpha cells of the pancreas. It acts in opposition to insulin and promotes the breakdown of glycogen into glucose, increases gluconeogenesis, and enhances the release of glucose into the bloodstream. Option C. Thyroxine (thyroid hormone): Thyroxine is a hormone produced by the thyroid gland. While it primarily affects metabolism and energy utilization, it can indirectly influence gluconeogenesis and the production of glucose. Option D. Glucocorticoids: Glucocorticoids are a class of hormones, including cortisol, produced by the adrenal glands. They are involved in various metabolic processes, including the regulation of glucose metabolism. Glucocorticoids can stimulate gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, leading to an increase in blood glucose levels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1081-1081; Solution Pages 1108-1108","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"physiology-37-13","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Active form of Vitamin D3 is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcitriol"},{"letter":"B","text":"Calciferol"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcidiol"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ergocalciferol"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option. A • Calcitriol, also known as 1,25-dihydroxyvitamin D, is the active form of Vitamin D3. It is formed in the kidneys from Calcidiol (the intermediate form) through hydroxylation reactions. Calcitriol plays a crucial role in regulating calcium and phosphate levels in the body, promoting their absorption from the intestine and reabsorption from the kidneys. Incorrect options: Option B. Calciferol: Calciferol is a term that can refer to various forms of Vitamin D, including both Vitamin D2 (ergocalciferol) and Vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol). However, specifically referring to Calciferol as the active form of Vitamin D3 is not accurate. Option C. Calcidiol: Calcidiol, also known as 25-hydroxyvitamin D, is an intermediate form of Vitamin D that is produced in the liver from Vitamin D2 or Vitamin D3. It is not the active form of Vitamin D3 itself. Option D. Ergocalciferol: Ergocalciferol is a form of Vitamin D2 that is derived from plant sources. It undergoes conversion in the body to form the active form of Vitamin D, but specifically referring to Ergocalciferol as the active form of Vitamin D3 is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1081-1081; Solution Pages 1109-1109","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"physiology-37-14","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which hormone binds to tyrosine kinase receptors and has 4 subunits and 2 units for receptor binding?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucagon"},{"letter":"C","text":"T3"},{"letter":"D","text":"ADH"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option. A • The hormone that has 4 subunits and 2 units for tyrosine kinase receptor binding is Insulin. • Insulin is a peptide hormone that regulates glucose metabolism in the body. It is composed of two peptide chains, namely the A chain and B chain, which are held together by disulfide bonds. Insulin receptors are tyrosine kinase receptors located on the surface of target cells. • The insulin receptor is a tetramer, consisting of two α-subunits and two β-subunits. The α-subunits are located extracellularly and contain the insulin-binding sites, while the β-subunits span the cell membrane and possess the tyrosine kinase catalytic activity. When insulin binds to the α-subunits of the receptor, it triggers a series of signaling events leading to the activation of the tyrosine kinase domain in the β-subunits. This activation initiatesdownstream signaling pathways involved in glucose uptake, glycogen synthesis, and othercellular responses to insulin. Incorrect option: Option B. Glucagon: Glucagon is a peptide hormone that has its own receptor, but it does not bind to tyrosine kinase receptors. Glucagon receptors belong to the G protein-coupled receptor family and activate different signaling pathways compared to tyrosine kinase receptors. Option C. T3: T3 (triiodothyronine) is a thyroid hormone that acts primarily through nuclear receptors rather than tyrosine kinase receptors. The binding of T3 to its receptors in the nucleus regulates gene expression and metabolic processes. Option D. ADH (Antidiuretic Hormone, also known as vasopressin): ADH acts through G protein-coupled receptors, specifically V1 and V2 receptors, and does not involve tyrosine kinase receptor signaling.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1081-1082; Solution Pages 1109-1110","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"physiology-37-15","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which hormone positively controls the blood levels of 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol among the options provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Parathormone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcitonin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option. B • Parathormone (Parathyroid Hormone or PTH): Parathormone is a hormone secreted by the parathyroid glands. It plays a crucial role in the regulation of calcium and phosphate levels in the blood. Parathormone acts on the kidneys and stimulates the production of the active form of vitamin D3, 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol. Therefore, parathormone positively regulates blood levels of 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol. Incorrect options Option A) Thyroxine: Thyroxine, also known as T4, is a hormone secreted by the thyroid gland. While thyroid hormones play a role in calcium metabolism, they do not directly regulate the blood levels of 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol (active form of vitamin D3). Option C) Insulin: Insulin is a hormone secreted by the pancreas and plays a key role in glucose metabolism and regulation of blood sugar levels. While insulin can indirectly influence calcium metabolism, it does not directly regulate blood levels of 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol. Option D) Calcitonin: Calcitonin is a hormone secreted by the thyroid gland and plays a role in calcium homeostasis. Calcitonin helps to decrease blood calcium levels by inhibiting bone resorption and enhancing calcium excretion by the kidneys. However, it does not directlyregulate the blood levels of 1,25-OH-cholecalciferol.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1082-1082; Solution Pages 1110-1111","sourcePage":1082,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"physiology-37-16","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Determine the diagnosis indicated by the provided image.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gigantism"},{"letter":"B","text":"Acromegaly"},{"letter":"C","text":"Down’s syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dwarfism"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Acromegaly: Enlargement of lips, nose, and tongue, prognathism( growth of lower jaw), skull growth, and prominent supraorbital ridges with large frontal sinuses are features suggestive of acromegaly. It occurs due to excess levels of growth hormone after puberty. Incorrect Options: Option A- Gigantism: It occurs due to an increase in GH before puberty. Since the epiphyseal plates have not fused, children with this disorder grow significantly taller than their peers. Option C - Down’s syndrome: It is a genetic disorder characterized by Chromosome 21 trisomy. Patients with this disorder present with developmental and intellectual delays. Option D- Dwarfism: Height for age below the third percentile on standard pediatric growth charts is defined as dwarfism. It occurs due to a deficiency of growth hormone.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1082-1082; Solution Pages 1111-1111","sourcePage":1082,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"physiology-37-17","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":17,"question":"All of the body organs or tissues demonstrate insulin-independent glucose uptake, except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"RBC"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreas"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adipose tissue"},{"letter":"D","text":"Brain"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C- Adipose tissue: The question is asking about which body organ or tissue demonstrates insulin dependent uptake. • In adipose tissue, it is GLUT 4, and GLUT 4 is insulin dependent. Incorrect Options: Option A- RBC: In RBC there is GLUT 1 mechanism, so it is incorrect. Option B- Pancreas: In Pancreas there is GLUT 2 mechanism, so it is incorrect. Option D- Brain: In RBC there is GLUT 1 and 2 mechanism, so it is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1083-1083; Solution Pages 1111-1111","sourcePage":1083,"sourcePageEnd":1083},{"id":"physiology-37-18","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":18,"question":"What is the primary mechanism by which parathyroid hormone (PTH) elevates blood calcium levels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Increased renal reabsorption of Calcium."},{"letter":"B","text":"Stimulation of osteoclast activity."},{"letter":"C","text":"Stimulates Vitamin D synthesis."},{"letter":"D","text":"All are True."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D- All are True: • Increased renal reabsorption of Calcium - PTH increases renal calcium reabsorption (distal tubule) and decreases phosphate reabsorption (proximal tubule) • Stimulation of osteoclast activity - PTH indirectly increases the activity of osteoclasts. This releases calcium and phosphate from the bone turnover. • Stimulates Vitamin D synthesis -Increases absorption of calcium and phosphate in the small bowel by activating Vitamin D. Other Options: Option A- Increased renal reabsorption of Calcium: This statement is true. Option B- Stimulation of osteoclast activity: This statement is true. Option C- Stimulates Vitamin D synthesis: This statement is true.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1083-1083; Solution Pages 1112-1112","sourcePage":1083,"sourcePageEnd":1083},{"id":"physiology-37-19","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Thyroid hormone acts via which type of receptor?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cytoplasmic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tyrosine kinase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Nuclear"},{"letter":"D","text":"GPCR"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Nuclear: • This is the correct answer. Thyroid hormones, such as thyroxine (T4) and triiodothyronine (T3), act via nuclear receptors known as thyroid hormone receptors (TRs). These receptors are located within the cell's nucleus. When thyroid hormones bind to these receptors, they influence gene expression, metabolism, and various physiological processes. Incorrect Options: Option A - Cytoplasmic: This is not the correct answer. Thyroid hormones do not act via cytoplasmic receptors. Instead, they act primarily through nuclear receptors. Option B - Tyrosine kinase: This is not the correct answer either. Tyrosine kinase receptors are involved in cell signaling pathways, but they are not the receptors through which thyroid hormones exert their effects. Option D - GPCR (G-Protein Coupled Receptor): This is not the correct answer. GPCRs are a different class of receptors that are involved in transmitting signals from the extracellular environment to the inside of the cell through G proteins. Thyroid hormones do not primarily utilize GPCRs for their signaling mechanism.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1083-1083; Solution Pages 1112-1113","sourcePage":1083,"sourcePageEnd":1083},{"id":"physiology-37-20","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 38-year-old person arrives at the ER with complaints of tachycardia, hypertension, and hypokalemia. On further investigation, the person's serum aldosterone levels are elevated, while renin levels are suppressed. What would be the most probable diagnosis for this given condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Conn's syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cushing disease"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pheochromocytoma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diabetes Mellitus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Conn's syndrome (Primary Hyperaldosteronism): • This condition involves excessive production of aldosterone by the adrenal glands, usually due to a benign tumor in one of the adrenal glands (aldosterone-producing adenoma). Elevated aldosterone levels lead to increased sodium retention and potassium excretion, resulting in hypertension, hypokalemia (low potassium levels), and metabolic alkalosis. The renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system (RAAS) feedback loop is disrupted, leading to suppressed renin levels. Incorrect Options: Option B - Cushing's disease: Cushing's disease is caused by excessive production of cortisol, usually due to a pituitary adenoma producing excess adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). Symptoms include weight gain, central obesity, moon face, buffalo hump, muscle weakness, and hypertension. Hypokalemia and tachycardia are not characteristic features of Cushing's disease. Option C - Pheochromocytoma: Pheochromocytoma is a tumor of the adrenal medulla that produces excessive amounts of catecholamines (such as epinephrine and norepinephrine). This leads to episodic hypertension, palpitations, tachycardia, headaches, and sweating. Hypokalemia is not a typical feature of pheochromocytoma. Additionally, renin levels would not be suppressed in pheochromocytoma. Option D - Diabetes Mellitus: Diabetes mellitus is a metabolic disorder characterized by high blood sugar levels due to insufficient insulin production or ineffective use of insulin. While diabetes can lead to secondary hypertension, it does not typically cause hypokalemia or suppressed renin levels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1084-1084; Solution Pages 1113-1113","sourcePage":1084,"sourcePageEnd":1084},{"id":"physiology-37-21","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following factors were found to have an inhibitory effect on insulin secretion in the study conducted on a group of individuals investigating insulin regulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epinephrine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastric Inhibitory polypeptide"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Epinephrine: • Epinephrine can have both stimulatory and inhibitory effects on insulin secretion depending on the physiological state and the body's immediate needs. During stress, exercise, and hypoglycemia, epinephrine primarily inhibits insulin secretion to raise blood sugar levels and provide energy for the body's demands. In contrast, during periods of rest and digestion, epinephrine levels are low, allowing insulin to facilitate glucose uptake and storage. Incorrect Options: Option B - Gastric Inhibitory polypeptide: Gastric Inhibitory Polypeptide, also known as Glucose-Dependent Insulinotropic Peptide, is a hormone released from the gut in response to the ingestion of food, particularly carbohydrates. GIP plays a role in stimulating insulin secretion. It is an incretin hormone, meaning it promotes the release of insulin from pancreatic beta cells in response to elevated blood glucose levels. So, GIP has a stimulatory effect on insulin secretion by helping the body manage post-meal glucose levels. Option C - Acetylcholine: Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter with multiple functions, and its impact on insulin secretion is generally stimulatory. In the context of the original question, Acetylcholine would not be considered as having an inhibitory effect on insulin secretion. Instead, it can stimulate insulin release from pancreatic beta cells when activated. Option D - None of the above","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1084-1084; Solution Pages 1114-1114","sourcePage":1084,"sourcePageEnd":1084},{"id":"physiology-37-22","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following hormones are secreted from each layer of the structure marked in the image given below? a. Glomerulosa- Aldosterone b. Fasciculata- Glucocorticoids c. Reticulata- Androgens d. Medulla- Epinephrine","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a & b are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"a, b & c are correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"b & c are correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"a, b c & d are correct"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - a, b c & d are correct: Hormones of adrenal gland Layers (weight) Hormone production Cortex (72%) Z. Glomerulosa (15%) Mineralocorticoids: Aldosterone (main) Deoxycorticosterone Z. Fasciculata (50%) Glucocorticoids (main) Cortisol corticosterone Sex steroids Z. Reticularis (7%) Sex steroid (main) Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) Androstenedione Glucocorticoids Medulla (28%) Epinephrine (90%) Nor-epinephrine (10%)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1084-1085; Solution Pages 1114-1115","sourcePage":1084,"sourcePageEnd":1085},{"id":"physiology-37-23","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Label the given Image:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1. SST, 2. GHRH, 3. GH, 4. IGF-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"1. IGF 1, 2. GH, 3. GHRH, 4. SST"},{"letter":"C","text":"1. GHRH, 2. GH, 3. IGF-1, 4. SST"},{"letter":"D","text":"1. GH, 2. IGF-1, 3. SST, 4. GHRH"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1. SST, 2. GHRH, 3. GH, 4. IGF-1: • The image represents the feedback regulation of GH secretion. The hypothalamus releases GHRH to stimulate growth hormone (GH) secretion and somatostatin to inhibit it. GH prompts the production of insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-1), which then negatively influences GH secretion. IGF-1 acts at both pituitary and hypothalamus levels. Incorrect Options: Options B, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1085-1085; Solution Pages 1115-1115","sourcePage":1085,"sourcePageEnd":1085},{"id":"physiology-37-24","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which hormone increases during periods of stress, starvation, and deep sleep?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Growth Hormone (GH)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucocorticoids"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cortisol"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prolactin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Growth Hormone (GH): • GH is known to increase significantly during deep sleep (particularly in non-rapid eye movement or NREM sleep) and also plays a role in stress and starvation responses. It is crucial for post-natal growth and has its peak production during adolescence. It is stimulated by exercise and certain sleep stages. Incorrect Options: Option B - Glucocorticoids: These are hormones that are indeed elevated during stress and starvation but are not specifically associated with increased levels during deep sleep. They primarily include cortisol and are involved in the body's response to stress. Option C - Cortisol: This hormone increases during periods of stress and starvation but does not have a significant increase during deep sleep. It is a type of glucocorticoid and is more associated with the stress response rather than growth or sleep-related processes. Option D - Prolactin: This hormone primarily increases during pregnancy and breastfeeding. It is not notably increased by stress, starvation, or deep sleep. In fact, prolactin levels can be affected by REM sleep, rather than being elevated during deep sleep.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1086-1086; Solution Pages 1115-1116","sourcePage":1086,"sourcePageEnd":1086},{"id":"physiology-37-25","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":25,"question":"What are the characteristic laboratory findings in primary hypothyroidism?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Elevated TSH, low T4 levels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elevated TSH, low T3 levels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Elevated TSH, elevated T4 levels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Low TSH, low T4 levels"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Elevated TSH, low T4 levels: • In primary hypothyroidism, the thyroid gland fails to produce adequate amounts of thyroid hormones (T3 and T4). Consequently, the pituitary gland releases more TSH (Thyroid-Stimulating Hormone) in an attempt to stimulate the thyroid gland. As a result, TSH levels are elevated while T4 (and often T3) levels are low. Incorrect Options: Option B - Elevated TSH, low T3 levels: While T3 levels can also be low in primary hypothyroidism, the hallmark laboratory finding for primary hypothyroidism is elevated TSH with low T4 levels. T3 levels are not the primary focus in the diagnosis of primary hypothyroidism. Option C - Elevated TSH, elevated T4 levels: Elevated TSH with elevated T4 levels is not consistent with primary hypothyroidism. Elevated TSH typically accompanies low T4 levels in primary hypothyroidism. Elevated T4 levels would suggest hyperthyroidism rather than hypothyroidism. Option D - Low TSH, low T4 levels: In primary hypothyroidism, TSH levels are high due to the pituitary’s attempt to stimulate the underactive thyroid gland. Low TSH with low T4 levels would be more indicative of secondary or tertiary hypothyroidism, where the issue lies with the pituitary or hypothalamus, respectively.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1086-1086; Solution Pages 1116-1116","sourcePage":1086,"sourcePageEnd":1086},{"id":"physiology-37-26","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which receptor helps in the improvement of insulin resistance in Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus (DM2) with regular exercise and physical activities?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GLUT 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"GLUT 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"GLUT 4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - GLUT 4: • This glucose transporter is found predominantly in muscle and adipose tissue. GLUT 4 is regulated by insulin and is crucial for the insulin-mediated uptake of glucose into these tissues. Regular exercise increases the number of GLUT 4 receptors on the cell membrane, improving glucose uptake and insulin sensitivity in individuals with Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. Option A - GLUT 1: This glucose transporter is found in various tissues, including the brain and erythrocytes. It helps in the uptake of glucose across cell membranes but is not primarily responsible for the insulin-mediated glucose uptake in muscle cells. Option B - GLUT 2: This glucose transporter is located primarily in the liver, pancreas, and kidneys. It plays a role in glucose sensing and regulation of blood glucose levels but does not directly mediate the improvement of insulin resistance through physical activity. Option C - GLUT 3: This glucose transporter is found mainly in neurons and some other tissues. It is responsible for the uptake of glucose into the brain but does not significantly impact insulin resistance in skeletal muscles.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1086-1086; Solution Pages 1117-1117","sourcePage":1086,"sourcePageEnd":1086},{"id":"physiology-37-27","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Which of the following is responsible for the transport of iodine into the thyroid follicle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Na/I symporter"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pendrin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cl/I symporter"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Na/I symporter: • The Na/I symporter (NIS) is responsible for the active transport of iodine into the thyroid follicular cells. It uses the sodium gradient to co-transport iodine into the cell from the bloodstream. Incorrect Options: Option B - Pendrin: Pendrin is an iodide/chloride exchanger located on the apical membrane of thyroid follicular cells. It is involved in the movement of iodide into the follicle lumen but not in the transport of iodine into the thyroid follicular cells. Option C - Cl/I symporter: This option is incorrect as there is no known Cl/I symporter in the thyroid. Iodide transport into the thyroid follicle is not mediated by a chloride/iodide symporter.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1086-1087; Solution Pages 1117-1117","sourcePage":1086,"sourcePageEnd":1087},{"id":"physiology-37-28","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A hypertensive patient’s lab results reveal hypernatremia, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis. What is the most probable diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Conn's Syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Addison’s Disease"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diabetes Insipidus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cushing's Disease"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A) Conn's Syndrome","explanation":"Conn’s syndrome (Primary Hyperaldosteronism) is the most likely diagnosis in a hypertensive patient with: • Hypernatremia (↑ Na■) → due to excessive sodium retention. • Hypokalemia (↓ K■) → due to renal potassium wasting. • Metabolic alkalosis → due to increased H■ excretion in the kidneys. Pathophysiology: • Aldosterone overproduction (usually due to an adrenal adenoma or bilateral adrenal hyperplasia) leads to: ↑ Na■ reabsorption → Hypertension & Hypernatremia ↑ K■ excretion → Hypokalemia ↑ H■ excretion → Metabolic Alkalosis • ↑ Na■ reabsorption → Hypertension & Hypernatremia • ↑ K■ excretion → Hypokalemia • ↑ H■ excretion → Metabolic Alkalosis • ↑ Na■ reabsorption → Hypertension & Hypernatremia • ↑ K■ excretion → Hypokalemia • ↑ H■ excretion → Metabolic Alkalosis Addison’s Disease (Option B) is incorrect because Addison’s disease (primary adrenal insufficiency) presents with hypotension, hyponatremia, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis, opposite to this case. Diabetes Insipidus (Option C) is incorrect because DI causes hypernatremia, but it is due to free water loss (polyuria, polydipsia) rather than aldosterone excess. It is not associated with hypokalemia or metabolic alkalosis. Cushing’s Disease (Option D) is incorrect because while Cushing’s disease (excess ACTH and cortisol) can cause hypertension, it does not primarily cause hypokalemia and metabolic alkalosis. However, severe cases with ectopic ACTH production (e.g., small-cell lung cancer) can cause pseudo-Conn’s syndrome with a similar presentation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1087-1087; Solution Pages 1118-1118","sourcePage":1087,"sourcePageEnd":1087},{"id":"physiology-37-29","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Beta 2 receptor action leading to hyperglycemia/hypoglycemia is mediated by which of the following enzymes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Adenylyl cyclase"},{"letter":"B","text":"Guanylyl cyclase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Phospholipase C"},{"letter":"D","text":"Phospholipase A2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: • Beta-2 adrenergic receptors are G-protein coupled receptors found on the surface of various cells, including pancreatic beta cells. When these receptors are activated by agonists (e.g., epinephrine), they stimulate the production of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP) through the action of the enzyme adenylyl cyclase. • The increase in cAMP levels leads to a cascade of intracellular events, including the activation of protein kinase A (PKA). PKA then phosphorylates multiple target proteins, resulting in various physiological responses, including the regulation of glucose metabolism. • In the context of hyperglycemia, the activation of beta-2 adrenergic receptors and subsequent increase in cAMP levels can lead to the inhibition of insulin release from pancreatic beta cells. Insulin is responsible for promoting glucose uptake and utilization, so its inhibition can result in elevated blood glucose levels. • In contrast, hypoglycemia is not directly mediated by the beta-2 adrenergic receptor. Instead, hypoglycemia may occur as an indirect effect of beta-2 receptor activation due to increased glycogenolysis (breakdown of glycogen into glucose) and gluconeogenesis (production of glucose from non-carbohydrate sources) mediated by the activation of other signaling pathways, such as the alpha-adrenergic receptors. Incorrect Options: Option B- Guanylyl cyclase: Guanylyl cyclase is an enzyme responsible for the production of cyclic guanosine monophosphate (cGMP) from guanosine triphosphate (GTP). It is not directly involved in the signaling pathway associated with beta-2 adrenergic receptors and glucose metabolism. The activation of beta-2 receptors primarily leads to the stimulation of adenylyl cyclase, which increases the production of cyclic adenosine monophosphate (cAMP), not cGMP. Option C - Phospholipase C: Phospholipase C is an enzyme that cleaves phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2) into inositol triphosphate (IP3) and diacylglycerol (DAG). This leads to the release of calcium ions from intracellular stores and the activation of protein kinase C (PKC). Although phospholipase C plays a role in certain intracellular signaling pathways, it is not directly involved in the signaling pathway associated with beta-2 adrenergic receptors and glucose metabolism. Option D - Phospholipase A2: Phospholipase A2 is an enzyme involved in the hydrolysis of membrane phospholipids, specifically releasing arachidonic acid. Arachidonic acid serves as a precursor for the synthesis of various inflammatory mediators, such as prostaglandins andleukotrienes. While phospholipase A2 has important roles in inflammation and lipid metabolism,it is not directly involved in the signaling pathway associated with beta-2 adrenergic receptorsand glucose metabolism.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1087-1087; Solution Pages 1119-1120","sourcePage":1087,"sourcePageEnd":1087},{"id":"physiology-37-30","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":30,"question":"What type of pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor is responsible for recurrent episodes of secretory diarrhea?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"VIPoma"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glucagonoma"},{"letter":"C","text":"Insulinoma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Zollinger Ellison disease"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• VIPoma is a rare type of pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that produces vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP), a hormone that regulates various physiological processes in the body, including intestinal motility and secretion. VIPoma is also known as Verner-Morrison syndrome or WDHA syndrome (Watery Diarrhea, Hypokalemia, and Achlorhydria). • The excessive production of VIP by the tumor leads to recurrent episodes of secretory diarrhea, which can be severe and debilitating. Diarrhea is typically watery and voluminous, and it may be associated with abdominal cramping, nausea, and vomiting. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. Glucagonoma: Glucagonoma is a type of pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that produces glucagon, a hormone that regulates glucose metabolism. Glucagonoma is associated with a characteristic rash called necrolytic migratory erythema, as well as diabetes mellitus, weight loss, and anemia. Glucagonoma does not typically cause secretory diarrhea. • Option c. Insulinoma: Insulinoma is a type of pancreatic neuroendocrine tumor that produces insulin, a hormone that regulates glucose metabolism. Insulinoma is associated with hypoglycemia, which can cause symptoms such as confusion, weakness, and sweating. Insulinoma does not typically cause secretory diarrhea. • Optiond. Zollinger Ellison disease: Zollinger Ellison disease is a condition caused by a gastrin-secreting tumor, typically located in the pancreas or duodenum. The excessive production of gastrin leads to hypersecretion of gastric acid, which can cause peptic ulcers and other gastrointestinal symptoms. Zollinger Ellison disease does not typically cause secretory diarrhea.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1087-1087; Solution Pages 1120-1120","sourcePage":1087,"sourcePageEnd":1087},{"id":"physiology-37-31","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":31,"question":"In the peripheral tissue, which enzyme is accountable for converting T4 to T3?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Iodothyronine deiodinase I"},{"letter":"B","text":"Iodothyronine deiodinase III"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iodothyronine deiodinase II"},{"letter":"D","text":"Iodotyrosine deiodinase"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• T4 (thyroxine) is the main hormone secreted by the thyroid gland, but it is relatively inactive. T3 (triiodothyronine) is the active form of thyroid hormone that exerts most of the biological effects in the body. The conversion of T4 to T3 occurs primarily in the peripheral tissues, such as the liver, kidney, and skeletal muscle. • The enzyme responsible for the conversion of T4 to T3 is iodothyronine deiodinase I (D1). D1 is expressed in various tissues, including the liver, kidney, and thyroid gland. D1 catalyzes the removal of an iodine atom from the outer ring of T4, producing T3. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. Iodothyronine deiodinase III: Iodothyronine deiodinase III (D3) is an enzyme that can inactivate thyroid hormones by removing iodine atoms from the inner ring of T4 and T3. D3 is not involved in the conversion of T4 to T3. • Option c. Iodothyronine deiodinase II: Iodothyronine deiodinase II (D2) is another enzyme that can convert T4 to T3, but it is primarily expressed in the thyroid gland and pituitary gland. D2 plays a role in the regulation of thyroid hormone levels in these tissues, but it is not as important for the peripheral conversion of T4 to T3. • Optiond. Iodotyrosine deiodinase: Iodotyrosine deiodinase is not a known enzyme involved in the conversion of T4 to T3, it helps the thyroid iodide salvage by catalyzing the deiodination of mono- and diiodotyrosine, the halogenated waste products of the manufacture of thyroid hormone.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1088-1088; Solution Pages 1121-1121","sourcePage":1088,"sourcePageEnd":1088},{"id":"physiology-37-32","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which of the following effects of puberty are not influenced by estrogen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"facial hair growth"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cervical mucus secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vaginal cornification"},{"letter":"D","text":"Menstruation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• Puberty is a period of development during which the body undergoes significant changes, including the development of secondary sexual characteristics. Estrogen is a hormone that plays a key role in many of these changes, especially in females. • Hair growth is not mediated by estrogen during puberty. Androgen hormones, such as testosterone, are responsible for the development of pubic, facial and axillary hair in both males and females. • Therefore, option a is the correct choice. Incorrect Choices: • Option B) Cervical mucus secretion: Cervical mucus secretion is mediated by estrogen during puberty. Estrogen stimulates the production of cervical mucus, which helps to facilitate the transport of sperm through the female reproductive tract. • Option C) Vaginal cornification: Vaginal cornification is mediated by estrogen during puberty. Estrogen stimulates the growth and development of the vaginal epithelium, leading to the thickening and cornification of the cells. • OptionD) Menstruation: Menstruation is mediated by estrogen during puberty. Estrogen promotes the thickening of the endometrial lining of the uterus, which is shed during menstruation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1088-1088; Solution Pages 1121-1122","sourcePage":1088,"sourcePageEnd":1088},{"id":"physiology-37-33","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":33,"question":"What is true about oxytocin among the options provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is responsible for galactokinesis"},{"letter":"B","text":"It can be given by oral route"},{"letter":"C","text":"It is secreted by anterior pituitary"},{"letter":"D","text":"In higher doses, it exerts diuretic like action"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• Oxytocin is a hormone that is produced by the hypothalamus and released by another gland, the posterior pituitary gland. It plays a role in a variety of physiological processes, including labor and lactation, social bonding, and stress response. • One of the effects of oxytocin is its ability to stimulate milk ejection (galactokinesis) during lactation. Oxytocin acts on myoepithelial cells in the mammary glands, causing them to contract and expel milk from the alveoli into the ducts. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. It can be given by oral route: This statement is incorrect. Oxytocin is not effective when given by oral route because it is rapidly degraded by enzymes in the gastrointestinal tract. • Option c. It is secreted by anterior pituitary: This statement is incorrect. Oxytocin is secreted by the posterior pituitary gland, not the anterior pituitary gland. • Optiond. Oxytocin is known to have antidiuretic properties. Therefore, in higher dose it will result in extreme water intoxication. Hence, option d is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1088-1088; Solution Pages 1122-1122","sourcePage":1088,"sourcePageEnd":1088},{"id":"physiology-37-34","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":34,"question":"What hormone is responsible for the breast cramps experienced by a mother while breastfeeding her child?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Progesterone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Estrogen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Prolactin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oxytocin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"• Oxytocin is the hormone responsible for the let-down reflex during breastfeeding. It is released from the posterior pituitary gland in response to nipple stimulation, and it causes the contraction of the myoepithelial cells surrounding the alveoli in the breast. This contraction leads to the ejection of milk from the alveoli into the ducts, where it can be accessed by the infant. • Therefore, the correct answer is oxytocin. Incorrect Choices: • Option a. Progesterone: Progesterone is involved in the development of the mammary glands during pregnancy, but it is not responsible for the cramps that the mother is experiencing during breastfeeding. • Option b. Estrogen: Estrogen is involved in the development of the mammary glands during pregnancy, but it is not responsible for the cramps that the mother is experiencing during breastfeeding. • Option c. Prolactin: Prolactin is involved in milk production in the mammary glands, but it is not responsible for the cramps that the mother is experiencing during breastfeeding.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1088-1089; Solution Pages 1122-1123","sourcePage":1088,"sourcePageEnd":1089},{"id":"physiology-37-35","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Which of the following mechanisms is similar to cervical dilation and delivery occurring during uterine contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Milk ejection by oxytocin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Water retention by ADH"},{"letter":"C","text":"FSH secretion by anterior pituitary"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• Cervical dilation and delivery during labor are controlled by the contraction of the uterine muscles. These contractions are stimulated by the hormone oxytocin, which is released by the pituitary gland in response to the stretching of the cervix and uterus. Oxytocin causes the uterine muscles to contract, which in turn leads to cervical dilation and delivery. • The mechanism of cervical dilation and delivery on uterine contraction is similar to milk ejection by oxytocin. During lactation, oxytocin is also released in response to suckling by the infant, causing the contraction of the myoepithelial cells in the mammary glands and the ejection of milk. • Therefore, the correct choice is option a, milk ejection by oxytocin. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. Water retention by ADH is not related to cervical dilation and delivery during labor. ADH is involved in the regulation of water balance in the body by promoting water reabsorption in the kidneys. • Option c. FSH secretion by the anterior pituitary is involved in the regulation of the menstrual cycle and the growth and development of ovarian follicles, but it is not related to cervical dilation and delivery during labor. • Optiond. None of the above is an incorrect option as cervical dilation and delivery during labor is similar to milk ejection by oxytocin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1089-1089; Solution Pages 1123-1124","sourcePage":1089,"sourcePageEnd":1089},{"id":"physiology-37-36","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which of the subsequent effects of puberty are not influenced by estrogen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hair growth"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cervical mucus secretion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vaginal cornification"},{"letter":"D","text":"Menstruation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• Puberty is a stage of development when sexual maturity is reached, and it is driven by a complex interplay of hormones. Estrogen is one of the major hormones involved in female puberty, and it is responsible for several changes in the body. • During puberty, estrogen stimulates the growth of pubic and underarm hair, but it does not directly mediate hair growth on other parts of the body, such as the legs and arms. Therefore, the hair growth during puberty is not mediated by estrogen, which make option a, the correct choice. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. Cervical mucus secretion: Cervical mucus secretion, that is the production and release of fluid from the cervix is regulated by estrogen levels, which increase during puberty. Thus, option b is incorrect choice. • Option c. Vaginal cornification: Vaginal cornification is regulated by estrogen levels, which increase during puberty. • Option d. Menstruation: Menstruation is triggered by estrogen, which stimulates the growth of the endometrium in preparation for pregnancy. Thus, option d, is an incorrect choice.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1089-1089; Solution Pages 1124-1124","sourcePage":1089,"sourcePageEnd":1089},{"id":"physiology-37-37","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":37,"question":"A 12-year-old girl is undergoing pubertal changes. Her tanner stage is stage 4 for breast development and pubic hair scale. Which of the following pubertal changes is not solely due to estrogen? (or) Which of the following pubertal changes in a 12-year-old girl, specifically breast development and pubic hair scale at stage 4, is not exclusively caused by estrogen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vaginal cornification"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pubic and axillary hair growth"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cervical mucus production"},{"letter":"D","text":"Menstruation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option: Option B. Pubic and axillary hair growth: The growth of pubic and axillary (underarm) hair is influenced by androgens, particularly testosterone. Androgens play a crucial role in the development of secondary sexual characteristics, including body hair. While estrogen does have some influence on body hair growth, the primary driver for the development of pubic and axillary hair is androgens, particularly testosterone. Estrogen contributes to other pubertal changes, buthair growth is not exclusively caused by estrogen. Incorrect Options: Option A. Vaginal cornification: Vaginal cornification, also known as the appearance of cornified epithelial cells in vaginal smears, is influenced by estrogen. This is one of the effects of estrogen on the genital tract during puberty. Option C. Cervical mucus production: Estrogen is responsible for cervical mucus production during puberty. Increasing levels of estrogen lead to changes in the cervical mucus that are favorable for sperm penetration and fertility. Option D. Menstruation: Menstruation, or the onset of the menstrual cycle (menarche), is primarily triggered by increased levels of estrogen and its interactions with other hormones. It is considered a key milestone in female puberty and is indeed influenced by estrogen. So, the correct answer is Option (B) \"Pubic and axillary hair growth.\"","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1089-1090; Solution Pages 1124-1125","sourcePage":1089,"sourcePageEnd":1090},{"id":"physiology-37-38","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Which one of the following hormones acts as anorexigenic agent?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Melanin-concentrating hormone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endorphins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ghrelin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Leptin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D - Leptin: Decrease Feeding (Anorexigenic) Increase Feeding (Orexigenic) α-Melanocyte-stimulating hormone Neuropeptide Y Leptin Agouti-related protein Serotonin Melanin-concentrating hormone Norepinephrine Orexins A and B Corticotropin-releasing hormone Endorphins Insulin Galanin Cholecystokinin Amino acids (glutamate and γ-aminobutyric acid) Glucagon-like peptide Cortisol Cocaine and amphetamine-regulated transcript Ghrelin Peptide YY Endocannabinoids Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect. They are orexigenic in nature.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1090-1090; Solution Pages 1125-1126","sourcePage":1090,"sourcePageEnd":1090},{"id":"physiology-37-39","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":39,"question":"For glucose uptake, which tissues rely on insulin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Brain"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skeletal muscle and adipose tissue"},{"letter":"D","text":"Kidneys"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Correct Option C - Skeletal muscle and adipose tissue: • This is the correct answer. Skeletal muscle and adipose tissue rely on insulin for glucose uptake. Insulin promotes the translocation of glucose transporters (GLUT4) to the cell surface in both of these tissues. GLUT4 allows glucose to enter these cells, where it can be used for energy (in muscle cells) or stored as fat (in adipose tissue). Incorrect Options: Option A - Liver: The liver does not solely rely on insulin for glucose uptake. It plays a role in both insulin-dependent and insulin-independent glucose uptake. Insulin suppresses glucose production by the liver (gluconeogenesis) and promotes glycogen synthesis in the liver, which leads to glucose storage. Option B - Brain: The brain does not rely on insulin for glucose uptake. It is a unique tissue that can take up glucose independently of insulin. Brain cells have glucose transporters (GLUT1) that facilitate glucose transport across the blood-brain barrier. Option D - Kidneys: The kidneys do not significantly rely on insulin for glucose uptake. The kidneys have glucose transporters (GLUT2) that facilitate glucose reabsorption from the glomerular filtrate back into the bloodstream. This process is not primarily regulated by insulin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1090-1090; Solution Pages 1126-1126","sourcePage":1090,"sourcePageEnd":1090},{"id":"physiology-37-40","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":40,"question":"What is the primary protein that binds to thyroxine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Follistatin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transthyretin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyroxine-binding globulin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transferrin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C Incorrect choices: Option A. Follistatin is a protein that binds to and regulates the activity of certain growth factors, particularly members of the transforming growth factor-beta (TGF-beta) superfamily. It is not involved in binding thyroxine (T4) or functioning as a thyroxine-binding protein. Option B. Transthyretin, also known as prealbumin, is a transport protein primarily involved in the transport of thyroid hormones (including thyroxine) and retinol-binding protein (RBP) in the blood. While transthyretin does contribute to the transport of thyroxine, it is not the major thyroxine-binding protein.globulin (TBG): Thyroxine-binding globulin is the major protein responsible for binding and transporting the thyroid hormone thyroxine (T4) in the bloodstream. It is synthesized in the liver and plays a crucial role in maintaining appropriate levels of free thyroxine. TBG binds to thyroxine with high affinity and releases it as needed to meet the metabolic demands of the body. TBG levels can be influenced by various factors, such as pregnancy, estrogen levels, and certain medications. Option D. Transferrin is a protein involved in the transport of iron in the blood. It binds to iron ions and carries them to various tissues in the body. Transferrin is not directly involved in the binding or transport of thyroxine. In summary, the major thyroxine-binding protein in the blood is thyroxine-binding globulin (TBG), which specifically binds and transports thyroxine.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1090-1090; Solution Pages 1126-1127","sourcePage":1090,"sourcePageEnd":1090},{"id":"physiology-37-41","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":41,"question":"In your OPD, a child with loose stools for a day came to you. During examination, you observed signs of dehydration and provided the child with ORS. Through which receptors does ORS aid in glucose absorption?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"SGLT 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"SFLT 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"GLUT 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"Na-Ca transporter"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: • SGLT 1 is primarily found in the small intestine, particularly in the brush border membrane of the enterocytes, which are the absorptive cells lining the intestinal wall. It is responsible for the active transport of glucose and sodium from the intestinal lumen into the enterocytes. • When oral rehydration solution (ORS) is given to a child with loose stools and signs of dehydration, it contains glucose and electrolytes, including sodium. The glucose in the ORS is absorbed in the small intestine through SGLT 1. The active transport of glucose via SGLT 1 co-transporter is coupled with the absorption of sodium ions. This mechanism helps to enhance the absorption of both glucose and sodium, promoting rehydration. Incorrect Options Option B - SGLT 2 (Sodium-Glucose Transporter 2) is primarily found in the kidneys and plays a role in renal glucose reabsorption. It is not directly involved in glucose absorption in the intestines. Option C - GLUT 4 (Glucose Transporter 4) is an insulin-regulated glucose transporter primarily found in muscle and adipose tissue. It facilitates glucose uptake into these tissues in response to insulin stimulation. GLUT 4 is not directly involved in glucose absorption in the intestines. Option D - Na-Ca transporter refers to sodium-calcium exchangers, which are responsible for the exchange of sodium and calcium ions across the cell membrane. They are not directly involved in glucose absorption in the intestines.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1091-1091; Solution Pages 1127-1128","sourcePage":1091,"sourcePageEnd":1091},{"id":"physiology-37-42","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":42,"question":"What is found in skeletal muscle among the options provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GLUT-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"GLUT-5"},{"letter":"C","text":"GLUT-7"},{"letter":"D","text":"GLUT-2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option: Option A. • GLUT stands for glucose transporter, and GLUT proteins facilitate the transport of glucose across cell membranes. Among the options given, GLUT-4 is the glucose transporter that is primarily found in skeletal muscle and adipose tissue. • GLUT-4 is responsible for insulin-regulated glucose uptake into skeletal muscle cells. It plays a crucial role in maintaining blood glucose homeostasis. When insulin is released in response to high blood glucose levels, it triggers the translocation of GLUT-4 from intracellular vesicles to the cell membrane of skeletal muscle cells. This allows GLUT-4 to transport glucose from the bloodstream into the muscle cells, where it can be utilized for energy production or stored as glycogen. Incorrect options: Option B. GLUT-5: GLUT-5 is primarily found in the small intestine and is responsible for the uptake of fructose, not glucose. It is less relevant to skeletal muscle glucose uptake. Option C. GLUT-7: GLUT-7 is mainly found in the liver and is involved in glucose uptake and release in hepatocytes. It is not present in skeletal muscle. Option D. GLUT-2: GLUT-2 is found in various tissues, including the liver, pancreas, and small intestine. It facilitates glucose uptake in these tissues and is involved in glucose sensing and transport across the cell membrane. However, GLUT-2 is not primarily present in skeletal muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1091-1091; Solution Pages 1128-1128","sourcePage":1091,"sourcePageEnd":1091},{"id":"physiology-37-43","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":43,"question":"Prolactin level is highest during?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"24 hours after ovulation"},{"letter":"B","text":"24 hours after delivery"},{"letter":"C","text":"REM sleep"},{"letter":"D","text":"After running four 1 hours"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B: 24 hours after delivery • Prolactin levels are highest after delivery, specifically around 24 hours after giving birth. This surge in prolactin secretion stimulates milk production and lactation in breastfeeding mothers. The high levels of prolactin help initiate and maintain breastfeeding and support the production of breast milk. Incorrect Options: Option A: Prolactin levels are not heighest after 24 hours after ovulation. Option C: Prolactin levels are not heighest after REM sleep. Option D: Prolactin levels are not heighest after After running four 1 hours.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1091-1091; Solution Pages 1129-1129","sourcePage":1091,"sourcePageEnd":1091},{"id":"physiology-37-44","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":44,"question":"What is the accurate order of muscle utilization during ejaculation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ischiocavernosus contraction-external urethral sphincter relaxation-Bulbospongiosus contraction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ischiocavernosus relaxation-External urethral sphincter contraction-Bulbospongiosus relaxation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ischiocaveernosus contraction – external urethral sphincter relaxation – Bulbospongiosus relaxation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ischiocavernosus contraction – External urethral sphincter relaxation – Bulbospongiosus relaxation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"c • During ejaculation, the ischiocavernosus contracts, followed by the relaxation of the external urethral sphincter and then the bulbospongiosus relaxation. Incorrect Choices: Option A: This is the incorrect sequence. Option B: This is the incorrect sequence. Option D: This is the incorrect sequence.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1091-1092; Solution Pages 1129-1129","sourcePage":1091,"sourcePageEnd":1092},{"id":"physiology-37-45","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":45,"question":"Oxytocin plays a role in –","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Milk production"},{"letter":"B","text":"Milk ejection"},{"letter":"C","text":"Milk storage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Osmoregulation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B • Milk ejection or galactokinase is by oxytocin. Incorrect Choices: Option A: The hormone responsible for milk production or galactopoiesis prolactin. Option C: Oxytocin is not responsible for milk storage. Option D: ADH is responsible for osmoregulation.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1092-1092; Solution Pages 1129-1130","sourcePage":1092,"sourcePageEnd":1092},{"id":"physiology-37-46","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":46,"question":"The predominant organ producing insulin-like growth factor I is","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pituitary gland"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pancreas"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adrenal glands"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: Liver • The liver is the predominant organ producing insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I). • IGF-I is synthesized primarily in the liver in response to growth hormone (GH) stimulation. • It plays a crucial role in growth and development, as well as in the regulation of various metabolic processes. Incorrect Options: Option B: Pituitary gland- The pituitary gland does not produce significant amounts of insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I). Instead, the pituitary gland produces and releases growth hormone (GH), which stimulates the liver to produce IGF-I. Option C: Pancreas- While the pancreas is responsible for producing and secreting insulin, which plays a role in regulating blood sugar levels, it is not the primary organ producing insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I). IGF-I is mainly synthesized in the liver. Option D: Adrenal glands- The adrenal glands are involved in the production and secretion of various hormones, including cortisol and adrenaline, but they do not play a significant role in the production of insulin-like growth factor I (IGF-I).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1092-1092; Solution Pages 1130-1130","sourcePage":1092,"sourcePageEnd":1092},{"id":"physiology-37-47","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":47,"question":"Which hormone is responsible for the breast cramps experienced by a mother during breastfeeding her child?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Progesterone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Estrogen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Prolactin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oxytocin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D • Oxytocin is the hormone in charge of breast cramps when breastfeeding. The milk ducts constrict as a result, releasing milk. The milk ducts contracting can occasionally bring Cramping in the breasts. When a child suckles on the breast, the hormone oxytocin is produced. A naturally occurring hormone called oxytocin controls important parts of both the male and female reproductive systems, such as breastfeeding and labor, as well as some aspects of behavior. Oxytocin is created by the nerve center, which the posterior pituitary organ then stores and distributes into the course. Oxytocin has two primary effects on the body: it causes uterine contractions during work and labor, and childbirth causes breast tissue compressions to help in breastfeeding after labor. Additionally, oxytocin is a chemical messenger in the brain that plays a significant role in numerous human behaviors and social interactions, such as: Sexual arousal Recognition Trust Romantic attachment Parent-infant bonding • Sexual arousal • Recognition • Trust • Romantic attachment • Parent-infant bonding • Sexual arousal • Recognition • Trust • Romantic attachment • Parent-infant bonding Incorrect Choices: Option A. Progesterone: The reproductive system depends heavily on the hormone progesterone. Chemical messengers called hormones instruct the body on how to function. Progesterone promotes menstruation in women and keeps the first trimester of pregnancy stable. Option B. Estrogen: The development of female sexual traits is brought on by the steroid hormone estrogen, which is connected to the female reproductive system. Estriol, estradiol, and estrone are common names for estrogen. Option C. Prolactin: Particularly in females, prolactin plays a key role in the physiology of the breast. The ability to produce milk normally depends on the prolactin hormone level. Prolactin level imbalances may impair this capacity. A mother won't be able to make milk if the levels are too low, while non-breastfeeding females or men may experience galactorrhea if the levels are improperly high.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1092-1093; Solution Pages 1130-1131","sourcePage":1092,"sourcePageEnd":1093},{"id":"physiology-37-48","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":48,"question":"The primary source of testosterone production is primarily contributed by _________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Leydig cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sertoli cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Seminiferous tubules"},{"letter":"D","text":"Epididymis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option is A: Leydig cells. • Testosterone production is primarily contributed by Leydig cells, also known as interstitial cells, which are found in the testes. Leydig cells are located in the connective tissue surrounding the seminiferous tubules in the testes. • Leydig cells are responsible for the synthesis and secretion of testosterone, which is the primary male sex hormone. Testosterone plays a crucial role in the development and maintenance of male reproductive organs, secondary sexual characteristics, and overall reproductive function. Incorrect options: Option B: Sertoli cells: Sertoli cells are located within the seminiferous tubules of the testes. They have essential functions in supporting and nourishing developing sperm cells, including providing nutrients and regulating their growth. However, Sertoli cells are not primarily responsible for testosterone production. Option C: Seminiferous tubules: Seminiferous tubules are the structures within the testes where sperm production takes place. While the seminiferous tubules play a critical role in spermatogenesis, they are not directly involved in testosterone production. Option D: Epididymis: The epididymis is a long, coiled tube located on the back of each testicle. It serves as a storage and maturation site for sperm cells. The epididymis does not have a primary role in testosterone production.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1093-1093; Solution Pages 1132-1132","sourcePage":1093,"sourcePageEnd":1093},{"id":"physiology-37-49","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":49,"question":"Which of the following are responsible for regulating the levels of calcium in the bloodstream?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcitonin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Parathormone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitamin D"},{"letter":"D","text":"All the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D Option A. Calcitonin: The thyroid gland is responsible for the release of calcitonin. Its primary function is to decrease the levels of serum calcium by increasing the movement of calcium from the blood into the bones. It inhibits bone breakdown (resorption) by osteoclasts. Upon the binding of the calcitonin to the receptors, a signal is sent to the osteoclasts to slow down theiractivity. Thus more calcium is deposited in the bones lowering the amount of calcium in theblood. Option B. Parathormone: Parathyroid hormone (PTH) is a hormone produced by the parathyroid glands which are present in the neck. It works exactly opposite to calcitonin, i.e., it helps to raise serum calcium levels by increasing calcium absorption from the intestines and bones and the reabsorption of calcium by the kidneys. When PTH binds to these receptors, these cells increase their activity. It promotes bone resorption by activating the osteoclasts. PTH also decreases the excretion of calcium from the kidneys into the urine. It stimulates the activation of vitamin D in the kidneys, further enhancing calcium absorption from the intestines. Option C. Vitamin D: While not a hormone in the traditional sense, vitamin D plays a crucial role in calcium regulation.Vitamin D is a nutrient that is essential for calcium absorption. When the skin is exposed to sunlight, vitamin D is produced in the body. It is the third component involved in the calcium homeostasis. It works similarly to the PTH. • Vitamin D helps to increase the absorption of calcium from the intestines by binding to receptors on cells in the small intestine. Upon binding, it signals these cells to increase their activity. This allows more calcium to be absorbed from the intestines, which raises the amount of calcium in the blood. It also promotes calcium reabsorption in the kidneys, reducing its excretion in urine. By enhancing intestinal absorption and renal reabsorption of calcium, vitamin D helps to raise serum calcium levels.","explanation":"Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1093-1093; Solution Pages 1132-1133","sourcePage":1093,"sourcePageEnd":1093},{"id":"physiology-37-50","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":50,"question":"Which of the following does not have the presence of the sodium iodide symporter?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroid gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"Placenta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Salivary gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pituitary gland"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D. Pituitary gland: The pituitary gland does not have the presence of the sodium iodide symporter. Unlike the thyroid gland and certain other tissues, the pituitary gland does not actively concentrate iodine, and the expression of NIS is not found in this gland. In summary, while NIS is present in the thyroid gland, placenta, and salivary glands, it is not found in the pituitary gland. Incorrect Options: Option A. Thyroid gland: The sodium iodide symporter (NIS) is present in the thyroid gland. NIS is a membrane glycoprotein located in the basolateral membrane of thyroid follicular cells. It plays a crucial role in iodine uptake, which is essential for the synthesis of thyroid hormones (thyroxine/T4 and triiodothyronine/T3). Option B. Placenta: NIS is also present in the placenta. It is responsible for concentrating iodine in the placental tissue, which is necessary for the development of the fetal thyroid gland and the production of thyroid hormones during pregnancy. Option C. Salivary gland: NIS is expressed in salivary glands. It is involved in the uptake of iodine by these glands, which is then secreted into saliva.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1093-1093; Solution Pages 1133-1134","sourcePage":1093,"sourcePageEnd":1093},{"id":"physiology-37-51","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":51,"question":"To which biochemical moiety is beta hCG structurally similar?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"LH"},{"letter":"B","text":"FSH"},{"letter":"C","text":"ACTH"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oxytocin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"• Beta hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin) is structurally similar to LH (luteinizing hormone). Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) is a hormone produced during pregnancy. The placenta primarily secretes it and has various functions, including supporting the development of the corpus luteum and maintaining progesterone production. • Structurally, hCG comprises two subunits, an alpha subunit, and a beta subunit. The beta subunit of hCG shares a significant structural similarity with the beta subunit of luteinizing hormone (LH). In fact, LH and hCG have similar amino acid sequences in their beta subunits, which allows for cross-reactivity in laboratory assays. • While hCG and LH have similarities in their beta subunits, it's important to note that they have different physiological functions. LH primarily regulates the menstrual cycle and ovulation, while hCG plays a crucial role in supporting pregnancy. Incorrect Choices: • Option b. FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone): FSH is also produced by the anterior pituitary gland. It works in conjunction with LH to regulate the menstrual cycle in females and sperm production in males. FSH stimulates the growth and maturation of ovarian follicles in females and the production of sperm cells in males. Unlike beta hCG, FSH is not structurally similar to beta hCG. • Option c. ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone): ACTH is a hormone secreted by the anterior pituitary gland that stimulates the adrenal glands' production and release of cortisol. It plays a role in the body's response to stress and helps regulate the adrenal cortex. ACTH is not structurally similar to beta hCG. • Option d. Oxytocin: Oxytocin is a hormone produced by the hypothalamus and released by the posterior pituitary gland. It plays a role in various reproductive and social behaviors. In females, oxytocin is involved in uterine contractions during childbirth and milk letdown during breastfeeding. It also has a role in bonding and social interactions. Oxytocin is not structurally similar to beta hCG.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1094-1094; Solution Pages 1134-1135","sourcePage":1094,"sourcePageEnd":1094},{"id":"physiology-37-52","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":52,"question":"What hormone is accountable for the breast cramps experienced by a mother during breastfeeding her child, as reported?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Progesterone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Estrogen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Prolactin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Oxytocin"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D: Oxytocin is the hormone in charge of breast cramps when breastfeeding. The milk ducts constrict as a result, releasing milk. The milk ducts contracting can occasionally bring Cramping in the breasts. When a child suckles on the breast, the hormone oxytocin is produced. Additionally, oxytocin is a chemical messenger in the brain that plays a significant role in numerous human behaviors and social interactions, such as: • Sexual arousal • Recognition • Trust • Romantic attachment • Parent-infant bonding Incorrect Options: Option A. Progesterone: The reproductive system depends heavily on the hormone progesterone. Chemical messengers called hormones instruct the body on how to function. Progesterone promotes menstruation in women and keeps the first trimester of pregnancy stable. Progesterone's major job is to get the endometrium (uterine lining) ready for a fertilized egg to develop and implant. The endometrium loses during menstruation if there is no pregnancy. Progesterone rises if fertilization occurs to support the pregnancy. This is irrelevant to nursing. Option B. Estrogen: The development of female sexual traits is brought on by the steroid hormone estrogen, which is connected to the female reproductive system. Estriol, estradiol, and estrone are common names for estrogen. Estradiol is the most widely used estrogen hormone for hormone replacement therapy (HRT) in the management of menopausal symptoms among the aforementioned types of estrogen. During the follicular phase of the menstrual cycle, estrogen aids in the proliferation of endometrial cells in the uterus, thickening the uterine lining in preparation for pregnancy. This is irrelevant to nursing. Option C. Prolactin: Particularly in females, prolactin plays a key role in the physiology of the breast. Clinically severe pathologic disorders emerge from insufficient or excessive prolactin production. The ability to produce milk normally depends on the prolactin hormone level. Prolactin level imbalances may impair this capacity. A mother won't be able to make milk if the levels are too low, while non-breastfeeding females or men may experience galactorrhea if the levels are improperly high. Prolactin's primary involvement is in milk production; it plays no part in breast contractions during nursing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1094-1094; Solution Pages 1135-1136","sourcePage":1094,"sourcePageEnd":1094},{"id":"physiology-37-53","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":53,"question":"Which hormones employ signaling through receptor tyrosine kinase?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Insulin"},{"letter":"B","text":"TSH"},{"letter":"C","text":"TRH"},{"letter":"D","text":"MSH"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A: Insulin • Among the hormones listed, the one that uses receptor tyrosine kinase signaling is Insulin. • Insulin is a peptide hormone produced by the beta cells of the pancreas. It acts on target cells by binding to insulin receptors, which are receptor tyrosine kinases. When insulin binds to its receptor, it activates the intrinsic tyrosine kinase activity of the receptor, leading to a cascade of signaling events inside the cell. Incorrect options: Option B, C & D: TSH and TRH both belong to the family of G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs) and signal through G-protein-mediated pathways. TSH acts on the thyroid gland to stimulate the production and release of thyroid hormones, while TRH regulates TSH release from the pituitary gland. MSH is a peptide hormone that regulates pigmentation and is primarily involved in the activation of melanocortin receptors, which are also GPCRs.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1094-1094; Solution Pages 1136-1136","sourcePage":1094,"sourcePageEnd":1094},{"id":"physiology-37-54","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":54,"question":"Among the given options, which condition is expected to exhibit the greatest levels of ACTH and cortisol?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Early morning"},{"letter":"B","text":"Normal individuals with dexamethasone administration"},{"letter":"C","text":"Addison’s disease"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cushing’s disease"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Correct Option D: Cushing’s disease • The condition that would have the highest levels of ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) and cortisol is Cushing's disease. • Cushing's disease is a condition characterized by the overproduction of ACTH by a pituitary tumor (adenoma). This excessive ACTH production leads to increased stimulation of the adrenal glands, causing them to produce and release excessive amounts of cortisol, a stress hormone. Therefore, both ACTH and cortisol levels are elevated in Cushing's disease. Incorrect options: Opton A: Early morning: In normal individuals, ACTH and cortisol levels are typically highest in the early morning. This is known as the diurnal variation of cortisol secretion, where cortisol levels naturally peak shortly after waking up. However, ACTH levels do not remain consistently elevated throughout the day. Option B: Normal individuals with dexamethasone administration: Dexamethasone is a synthetic glucocorticoid that is commonly used to suppress cortisol production. In normal individuals, the administration of dexamethasone would lead to a decrease in both ACTH and cortisol levels due to the negative feedback on the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis. Option C: Addison's disease: Addison's disease is characterized by a deficiency of cortisol production due to adrenal insufficiency. In this condition, both ACTH and cortisol levels are decreased. ACTH levels are elevated as a compensatory mechanism to stimulate the adrenal glands, but the adrenal glands are unable to produce sufficient cortisol.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1094-1095; Solution Pages 1136-1137","sourcePage":1094,"sourcePageEnd":1095},{"id":"physiology-37-55","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":55,"question":"Which specific antibody is detected during a recent infection?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ig M"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ig G"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ig E"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ig A"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct option: Option A. Ig M (Immunoglobulin M) is an antibody class that is typically produced in response to a recent infection. It is the first antibody produced during the primary immune response and is often associated with an acute or recent infection. Ig M antibodies are generally larger in size and have multiple binding sites, allowing them to efficiently bind to and neutralize pathogens. Incorrect options: Option B. IgG (Immunoglobulin G): IgG antibodies are the most abundant and long-lasting antibody class in the bloodstream. They are typically produced during the secondary immune response and persist for an extended period, providing long-term protection against infections. While IgG antibodies can indicate a previous or ongoing infection, they are not specific to recentinfections. Option C. Ig E (Immunoglobulin E): Ig E antibodies are primarily involved in allergic and hypersensitivity reactions. They are responsible for triggering the release of histamine and other inflammatory substances in response to allergens. IgE antibodies are not directly associated with recent infections. Option D. Ig A (Immunoglobulin A): Ig A antibodies are predominantly found in mucosal areas such as the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts. They play a crucial role in protecting mucosal surfaces from pathogens. While IgA antibodies can be involved in fighting infections, they are not specifically indicative of recent infections.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1095-1095; Solution Pages 1137-1138","sourcePage":1095,"sourcePageEnd":1095},{"id":"physiology-37-56","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":56,"question":"Histamine is secreted by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enterochromaffin cell"},{"letter":"B","text":"Parietal cell"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oxyntic cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chief cell"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Enterochromaffin cell: • Histamine is primarily secreted by enterochromaffin cells, which are a type of neuroendocrine cells found in the gastrointestinal tract. These cells release histamine in response to gastrin and acetylcholine. • Histamine has diverse physiological functions and plays a significant role in various processes in the body. In the gastrointestinal tract, histamine acts as a paracrine and neurotransmitter molecule. It can stimulate gastric acid secretion, increase blood flow to the gastric mucosa, and regulate smooth muscle contraction in the gut. Incorrect Options: Option B - Parietal cell: Parietal cells are responsible for producing and secreting gastric acid (hydrochloric acid) in the stomach. Option C - Oxyntic cell: Oxyntic cells, or parietal cells, are another name for the cells that produce gastric acid. Option D - Chief cell: Chief cells are responsible for secreting pepsinogen, the inactive precursor of the enzyme pepsin, which is involved in protein digestion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1095-1095; Solution Pages 1138-1138","sourcePage":1095,"sourcePageEnd":1095},{"id":"physiology-37-57","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":57,"question":"Inhibitory control by the hypothalamus is exerted on:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prolactin only"},{"letter":"B","text":"Prolactin and growth hormone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Growth hormone only"},{"letter":"D","text":"Neither"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Prolactin and growth hormone: • Inhibitory control by the hypothalamus is exerted on prolactin and growth hormone: The hypothalamus releases inhibitory hormones (dopamine and somatostatin) that act on the anterior pituitary to suppress the secretion of both prolactin and growth hormone. • The hypothalamus releases inhibitory hormones (dopamine and somatostatin) that act on the anterior pituitary to suppress the secretion of both prolactin and growth hormone. • The hypothalamus releases inhibitory hormones (dopamine and somatostatin) that act on the anterior pituitary to suppress the secretion of both prolactin and growth hormone. Incorrect Options: • Options A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1095-1095; Solution Pages 1139-1139","sourcePage":1095,"sourcePageEnd":1095},{"id":"physiology-37-58","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":58,"question":"A 45-year-old person presents to the outpatient department with a 6-month history of increased urine output and increased water intake. On investigation, the following results are obtained: HbA1C - 5.5%, Blood glucose - 80 mg/dL, Postprandial glucose - 130 mg/dL, and decreased urine osmolarity. What would be the most probable diagnosis for this given condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"DM"},{"letter":"B","text":"Diabetes Insipidius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Conn's Disease"},{"letter":"D","text":"Addison disease"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - Diabetes Insipidus: • This is the correct answer. Diabetes insipidus is characterized by the inability of the kidneys to concentrate urine properly, leading to excessive dilute urine output (polyuria) and increased thirst (polydipsia). In this case, the decreased urine osmolarity is a key clue. • Diabetes insipidus can be central (due to a lack of antidiuretic hormone, ADH) or nephrogenic (due to kidney insensitivity to ADH). Incorrect Options: Option A - DM (Diabetes Mellitus): This option is unlikely because the HbA1C level of 5.5% and the blood glucose levels are within the normal range (HbA1C < 6.5% and blood glucose < 126 mg/dL for diagnosis of diabetes). Option C - Conn's Disease (Primary Hyperaldosteronism): This condition is characterized by excessive production of aldosterone by the adrenal glands, leading to high blood pressure and low blood potassium levels. Option D - Addison's Disease (Adrenal Insufficiency): This condition involves the adrenal glands not producing enough cortisol and, in some cases, aldosterone. Symptoms often include fatigue, weight loss, and low blood pressure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1096-1096; Solution Pages 1139-1140","sourcePage":1096,"sourcePageEnd":1096},{"id":"physiology-37-59","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":59,"question":"Which of the options below can be used to elucidate the mechanism by which ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) exerts its effects?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"cAMP"},{"letter":"B","text":"cGMP"},{"letter":"C","text":"IP3-DAG"},{"letter":"D","text":"JAK/STAT"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Correct Option B - cGMP: • ANP (atrial natriuretic peptide) is a hormone that is produced by the heart and plays a role in regulating blood pressure and fluid balance in the body. ANP acts on the kidneys to increase the excretion of sodium and water, which helps to reduce blood volume and lower blood pressure. • The mechanism of action of ANP involves the activation of cGMP (cyclic guanosine monophosphate). ANP binds to its receptor on the surface of target cells, which activates the enzyme guanylyl cyclase. Guanylyl cyclase then converts GTP (guanosine triphosphate) to cGMP, which acts as a second messenger to mediate the effects of ANP. Incorrect Options: Option A - cAMP: Hormones acting through this pathway are ACTH, TSH, FSH, LH, catecholamines, somatostatin, glucagon, and secretin. Option C - IP3-DAG: Hormones acting through this pathway are TRH, GnRH, GHRH, Oxytocin, and ADH (V1 receptor). Option D - JAK/STAT: Hormones acting through this pathway are growth hormone and prolactin hormone.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1096-1096; Solution Pages 1140-1140","sourcePage":1096,"sourcePageEnd":1096},{"id":"physiology-37-60","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":60,"question":"What is the cause of \"Vasopressin escape\" in SIADH?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upregulation of aquaporin 3 production"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decrease in ADH section"},{"letter":"D","text":"Upregulation of aquaporin 2 production"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Downregulation of aquaporin 2 production: • In SIADH, excessive ADH secretion causes water retention. Initially, ADH upregulates aquaporin 2 channels, increasing water reabsorption. Over time, aquaporin 2 production decreases, leading to \"vasopressin escape\" and allowing water excretion despite continued ADH release. Incorrect Options: • Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1096-1096; Solution Pages 1140-1141","sourcePage":1096,"sourcePageEnd":1096},{"id":"physiology-37-61","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-37","chapter":"Previous Year Questions","topic":"Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":61,"question":"In serous acinar cells, what is the typical location of the Golgi apparatus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supranuclear"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adjacent to nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bottom of the cell"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A (source answer-key index 1)","explanation":"Correct Option A - Supranuclear: • In serous acinar cells, the Golgi apparatus is typically located above the nucleus, towards the apical (top) region of the cell. This positioning allows for the packaging and modification of proteins produced by the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER) before they are transported to the apical surface for secretion. Incorrect Options: • Option B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1096-1097; Solution Pages 1141-1141","sourcePage":1096,"sourcePageEnd":1097},{"id":"physiology-38-1","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient is being evaluated for male infertility. The fertility specialist explains the changes sperm undergo in the epididymis. Which of the following statements correctly describes a change that sperm undergo in the epididymis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ability to bind to zona pellucida develops after ejaculation."},{"letter":"B","text":"Cytoplasm and cell volume decrease in the testes."},{"letter":"C","text":"Sperm gain motility and chemotaxis ability."},{"letter":"D","text":"Capacitation for fertilization happens in the epididymis."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Sperm gain motility and chemotaxis ability. Explanation: Sperm acquire motility and the ability to sense chemical signals during their passage through the epididymis. Change Description Motility Acquisition Sperm leaving the testes are not fully mobile. They gain progressive motility in the epididymis. Motility is suppressed by inhibitory proteins until ejaculation. Cation Channel Activation Activation of CatSper family cation channels occurs. Alkaline-sensitive Ca2+ channels become active as sperm move to more alkaline cervical mucus. Chemotaxis Development Develop the ability to sense chemical signals from the ovaries, helping guide them toward the ovary. Concentration Become concentrated due to fluid reabsorption. Epididymal Fluid Epididymis and Sertoli cells secrete nutrient-rich fluid Contribution containing hormones, enzymes, and nutrients essential for maturation. Sperm Storage Epididymis serves as the primary storage site. Sperm can maintain fertility for at least a month. Timeframe for Motility Sperm gain the capability for motility after 18 to 24 hours in the epididymis. Full maturation and hypermotility occur in the female genital tract. Acrosome Maturation The acrosome, located at the sperm head and containing enzymes for fertilization, matures while sperm are stored in the epididymis. Decreased Cytoplasm and Sperm lose excess cytoplasm and decrease in overall Cell Volume cell volume as they mature in the epididymis. Zona Pellucida Binding Sperm acquire receptors during epididymal transit that Ability enable them to bind to the zona pellucida, facilitating penetration and fertilization of the egg. Capacitation Post-Epididymi Full fertilization capacity (capacitation) occurs after s sperm leave the epididymis. This involves removal of inhibitory factors and changes to the sperm membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1142-1142; Solution Pages 1146-1147","sourcePage":1142,"sourcePageEnd":1142},{"id":"physiology-38-2","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 15-year-old male is undergoing puberty and starts experiencing physical and hormonal changes associated with this developmental stage. Which hormonal changes and effects are typical during puberty in males?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decreased testosterone reduces muscle mass."},{"letter":"B","text":"Increased LH boosts testosterone production."},{"letter":"C","text":"Elevated FSH enlarges penis and scrotum."},{"letter":"D","text":"Higher inhibin increases FSH secretion."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) Increased LH boosts testosterone production. Explanation: During puberty, increased LH stimulates Leydig cells in the testes to produce more testosterone, leading to the development of secondary sexual characteristics. Puberty and Male Reproductive System Changes Increased Testosterone Surge in testosterone by Leydig cells in testes. Production Stimulated by LH from anterior pituitary. (Option B) Primary Sexual Characteristics Growth of penis, scrotum, and testes. (Option C) Secondary Sexual Characteristic Body Hair: Increased growth on pubic area, face, chest, s etc. Voice Changes: Deeper voice due to larynx hypertrophy. Skin Changes: Thicker skin, increased sebaceous secretion, acne. Muscle Development: Increased muscle mass (50% greater than females). (Option A) Bone Growth: Enhanced thickness, calcium retention. Metabolic Effects: Increased basal metabolic rate (5-10%), potential increase in red blood cells. Spermatogenesis Sperm production begins and continues throughout life within seminiferous tubules of testes. Role of Hormones in FSH: Stimulates Sertoli cells for sperm support. Spermatogenesis Testosterone: Essential for germ cell growth. Inhibin: Produced by Sertoli cells to regulate FSH and spermatogenesis rate. (Option D) Sperm Maturation and Storage Immature sperm mature in the epididymis, gaining motility (suppressed until ejaculation), stored for at least a month. Hypothalamic-Pituitary-Testicular Axis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1142-1142; Solution Pages 1147-1148","sourcePage":1142,"sourcePageEnd":1142},{"id":"physiology-38-3","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sertoli cells and Leydig cells together form the blood-testis barrier"},{"letter":"B","text":"Leydig cells secrete testosterone and androgen-binding protein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Leydig cells can synthesize cholesterol de novo within them"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sertoli cells secrete Inhibin B to suppress the release of LH"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Leydig cells can synthesize cholesterol de novo within them Explanation: Leydig cells meet their cholesterol needs through both de novo synthesis and uptake of extracellular cholesterol from blood for the synthesis of testosterone. Function of Leydig cells: • Testosterone secretion is facilitated by Leydig cells. • Cholesterol serves as the primary precursor for testosterone synthesis. Leydig cells can synthesize cholesterol internally and also absorb it from the blood via LDL and HDL receptors. • Converts DHEA into testosterone. • Testosterone moves into the seminiferous tubules (adluminal compartment). Function of Sertoli cells: • Only Sertoli cells form the blood-testis barrier (Option A) • Nourishment and protection to developing sperm cells. • Sertoli cells contain receptors for FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) and androgens, which indirectly support spermatogenesis. • Sertoli cells produce androgen binding protein (ABP). (Option B) • Sertoli cells express the aromatase enzyme, responsible for converting testosterone to estrogen. • They produce extracellular matrix components and anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH/MIS), which induces regression of Müllerian ducts during male fetal development. • Sertoli cells secrete Inhibin B, a crucial factor in the testes that negatively regulates FSH secretion (Option D) through feedback mechanisms.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1142-1142; Solution Pages 1148-1149","sourcePage":1142,"sourcePageEnd":1142},{"id":"physiology-38-4","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 14-year-old male presents with delayed puberty. Physical examination reveals small testicular volume and a lack of secondary sexual characteristics. Hormonal tests indicate low testosterone levels but elevated FSH levels. Which of the following functions of Sertoli cells is most likely compromised in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Secretion of testosterone in response to LH stimulation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Production of inhibin, leading to increased FSH levels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone by 5α-reductase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Support of spermatogenesis through FSH-mediated stimulation"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: D) Support of spermatogenesis through FSH-mediated stimulation Explanation: The most plausible explanation for the observed hormonal imbalances and clinical features is a compromised ability of Sertoli cells to respond to FSH stimulation, leading to both impaired spermatogenesis and, consequently, low testosterone levels. The reduced negative feedback from inhibin due to impaired Sertoli cell function further contributes to the elevated FSH levels. Hormones of Spermatogenesis: Follicular stimulating hormone: • FSH is essential for the initial development of spermatogonia cells. • Main targets of FSH action include: Proliferation of Sertoli cell Proliferation of Spermatogonia up to primary spermatocyte stage Support the process of meiosis. • Proliferation of Sertoli cell • Proliferation of Spermatogonia up to primary spermatocyte stage • Support the process of meiosis. • Proliferation of Sertoli cell • Proliferation of Spermatogonia up to primary spermatocyte stage • Support the process of meiosis. Testosterone: • Testosterone is particularly required for the terminal stages of spermatogenesis • Main targets of testosterone action include: Spermiogenesis (spermatid to mature sperm) Spermiation (release of sperm) Meiosis (suppression of testosterone leads to block in meiosis) • Spermiogenesis (spermatid to mature sperm) • Spermiation (release of sperm) • Meiosis (suppression of testosterone leads to block in meiosis) • Spermiogenesis (spermatid to mature sperm) • Spermiation (release of sperm) • Meiosis (suppression of testosterone leads to block in meiosis) Estrogen: • Main targets of Estrogen include: Fluid resorption and concentration of sperm Acrosome biogenesis of sperm • Fluid resorption and concentration of sperm • Acrosome biogenesis of sperm • Fluid resorption and concentration of sperm • Acrosome biogenesis of sperm Growth hormone: • Growth hormone specifically promotes early division of the spermatogonia Option A) Secretion of testosterone in response to LH stimulation is a function of Leydig cell Option B) Production of inhibin leading to increased FSH levels simply does not explain the low testosterone levels seen in this patient Option C) While testosterone does play a role in spermatogenesis, its production is mainly stimulated by LH acting on Leydig cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1143-1143; Solution Pages 1149-1151","sourcePage":1143,"sourcePageEnd":1143},{"id":"physiology-38-5","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following components is most accurately matched with its contribution to the total volume of semen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Seminal vesicles contribute 20% of the total volume."},{"letter":"B","text":"Prostate contributes 60% of the total volume."},{"letter":"C","text":"Bulbourethral glands contribute the majority of the semen volume."},{"letter":"D","text":"Seminal vesicles contribute 60% of the total volume."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D (source answer-key index 4)","explanation":"Answer: D) Seminal vesicles contribute 60% of the total volume. Explanation: The seminal vesicles are responsible for producing approximately 60% of the total volume of semen. The fluid from the seminal vesicles is rich in fructose and other substances that provide energy and aid in the motility of sperm. Sperm Maturation: • Spermatogenesis: Begins during adolescence; takes ~74 days. • Initial Mobility: Sperm are not fully mobile upon leaving the testes. • Maturation: Occurs in the epididymis where sperm gain motility. • Motility Mechanism: Involves activation of cation channel proteins (CatSper family) in the sperm tail. • Alkaline Sensitivity: CatSpers form an alkaline-sensitive Ca2+ channel, activated as sperm transition from acidic vagina to alkaline cervical mucus. Sperm Capacitation • Location: Occurs in the isthmus of the uterine tubes. • Purpose: Prepares sperm for the acrosome reaction and increases motility. Contents of Semen • Composition: Sperm and fluids from seminal vesicles, prostate, Cowper glands, and potentially urethral glands. • Volume: 2.5–3.5 mL per ejaculation after several days of abstinence. • Sperm Count: About 100 million sperm per mL. • Fluid Source: Seminal vesicles: 60% (Option A) (Option D) Prostate: 20% (Option B) Bulbourethral glands: Remaining portion (Option C) • Seminal vesicles: 60% (Option A) (Option D) • Prostate: 20% (Option B) • Bulbourethral glands: Remaining portion (Option C) • Seminal vesicles: 60% (Option A) (Option D) • Prostate: 20% (Option B) • Bulbourethral glands: Remaining portion (Option C) Surface Proteins: Sperm contain proteins like fertilin for egg cell membrane fusion","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1143-1143; Solution Pages 1151-1152","sourcePage":1143,"sourcePageEnd":1143},{"id":"physiology-38-6","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":6,"question":"At which phase of the menstrual cycle does the primary oocyte complete meiosis I to become a secondary oocyte?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Follicular phase"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ovulation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Luteal phase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Menstrual phase"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) Ovulation Explanation: During ovulation, the surge in luteinizing hormone (LH) triggers the completion of meiosis I in the primary oocyte, resulting in the formation of a secondary oocyte and a first polar body. The secondary oocyte then arrests in metaphase II and is released from the follicle. Table rendering failed • Menstrual phase: The menstrual phase involves the shedding of the uterine lining if fertilization does not occur, and does not involve the completion of meiosis I in the oocyte. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1143-1143; Solution Pages 1152-1152","sourcePage":1143,"sourcePageEnd":1143},{"id":"physiology-38-7","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 27-year-old woman with a regular 30-day cycle is in the luteal phase. Which of the following statements accurately reflects the hormonal environment and physiological changes during this phase?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corpus luteum breaks down, leading to decreased estrogen and progesterone."},{"letter":"B","text":"Progesterone levels increase significantly due to the activity of the corpus luteum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Granulosa and theca interna cells secrete estrogen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Initiated by increased follicle-stimulating hormone"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) Progesterone levels increase significantly due to the activity of the corpus luteum Explanation: Phase Description Hormones Duration & Events Menstrual Occurs when the ovum is Decreased Duration: 4-7 days Phase not fertilized. (Option A) Estrogen Blood loss: ~40 mL Corpus luteum breaks Decreased Serous fluid loss: down, leading to Progesterone ~35 mL decreased estrogen and progesterone. Endometrium regresses and sheds. Follicular Initiated by increased Follicle-Stimulatin Duration: Variable Phase (Pre-ovu follicle-stimulating g Hormone (FSH) Follicle growth and latory) hormone (FSH) from the Estrogen maturation occur anterior pituitary. (Option D) Stimulates growth of 6-12 primordial follicles. Granulosa and theca interna cells secrete estrogen. (Option C) Estrogen promotes FSH receptor formation and accelerates follicular growth. One follicle becomes dominant (preovulatory follicle). Luteal Phase Begins after ovulation with Luteinizing Duration: About 12 (Post-ovulatory) corpus luteum formation. Hormone (LH) days Corpus luteum secretes Progesterone Decline in hormones progesterone and some Estrogen leads to menstruation estrogen. Prepares endometrium for implantation. If no implantation, corpus luteum involutes, decreasing hormone levels and triggering menstruation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1144-1144; Solution Pages 1152-1153","sourcePage":1144,"sourcePageEnd":1144},{"id":"physiology-38-8","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":8,"question":"According to the Two-Cell-Two-Gonadotropin Hypothesis, which of the following hormones primarily stimulates granulosa cells in the ovaries to promote the production of estrogen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"LH"},{"letter":"B","text":"FSH"},{"letter":"C","text":"Progesterone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prolactin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B (source answer-key index 2)","explanation":"Answer: B) FSH Explanation: Granulosa cells have many FSH receptors, and FSH stimulates the secretion of estradiol by increasing the aromatase activity. Two-Cell-Two-Gonadotropin Hypothesis: • Naturally occurring estrogens are 17β-estradiol, estrone, and estriol. • Many LH receptors are present on theca interna cells and LH increases conversion of cholesterol to androstenedione. • The theca interna cells supply androstenedione to the granulosa cells. • Granulosa cells have many FSH receptors, and FSH (Option B) increases secretion of estradiol by increasing aromatase activity. • Thus, both LH and FSH are required for the production of estrogen. LH (Option A) increases progesterone secretion. Progesterone (Option C) and prolactin (Option D) are not directly involved in production of estrogen in granulosa cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1144-1144; Solution Pages 1153-1154","sourcePage":1144,"sourcePageEnd":1144},{"id":"physiology-38-9","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with hot flashes, night sweats, and irregular menstrual cycles, suggesting a decrease in estrogen levels. Which of the following is a primary source of estrogen in premenopausal women, and which of the following is an effect of estrogen on the body?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Adrenal cortex; Maintenance of bone density"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hepatocytes; enlargement of breast"},{"letter":"C","text":"Granulosa cells, proliferation of endometrium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corpus luteum, decreases HDL cholesterol"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) Granulosa cells, proliferation of endometrium Explanation: Estrogen is primarily secreted by ovaries in granulosa cells and one of its effects is proliferation of endometrium. Estrogen Progesterone Source Granulosa cells (Option C), corpus Corpus luteum, placenta and luteum, and placenta ovarian follicles Effects on Growth of ovarian follicle Antiestrogenic effect on the female genitalia Proliferation of endometrium (Option myometrium, decreasing their C) excitability Increase the motility of the uterine Secretory effect on the endometrium tubes Increase blood flow in the uterus Increase the amount of uterine muscle and contractility. Female Seconda Enlargement of breasts Stimulates the development of ry Sex Character Female distribution of fat lobules and alveoli istics Wide carrying angle Effects on the Increase libido Thermogenic and responsible for Central Nervous Increase the proliferation of the rise in basal body temperature System dendrites on neurons and the during ovulation number of synaptic knobs Effects on Increased secretion of Large doses of progesterone inhibit Endocrine angiotensinogen and thyroid-binding LH secretion and potentiate the Organs globulin inhibitory effect of estrogens, preventing ovulation. Other effects Retention of salt and water and gain Stimulates respiration, and the weight just before menstruation alveolar PCO2 Plasma cholesterol-lowering action Increases plasma LDL and Vasodilation by increasing the local decreases HDL (Option D) production of NO. Maintains bone mineral density (Option A) The amount of progestin, androgen, and estrogen produced by the adrenal cortex is a minor fraction of the total amount produced in the body. Hence, not the primary source of estrogen. (Option B) Hepatocytes are not involved in estrogen production. Breast enlargement is an effect of estrogen. (Option D) Corpus luteum secretes more progesterone than estrogen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1144-1144; Solution Pages 1154-1155","sourcePage":1144,"sourcePageEnd":1144},{"id":"physiology-38-10","subject":"Physiology","chapterId":"physiology-38","chapter":"Reproductive Physiology","topic":"Reproductive Physiology","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following best describes the feedback regulation mechanism of ovarian function involving estrogen and the hypothalamus-pituitary axis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"High levels of estrogen stimulate the release of LH and FSH from the pituitary gland."},{"letter":"B","text":"Low levels of estrogen inhibit the release of GnRH from the hypothalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"High levels of estrogen inhibit the release of GnRH from the hypothalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Low levels of estrogen stimulate the release of progesterone from the ovaries"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C (source answer-key index 3)","explanation":"Answer: C) High levels of estrogen inhibit the release of GnRH from the hypothalamus Explanation: Higher estrogen levels inhibit the release of GnRH from the hypothalamus by negative feedback mechanism. • Thecal cells are regulated by LH. • Granulosa cells are regulated by both LH and FSH. • Circulating estrogens inhibit the secretion of GnRH, LH, and FSH. • Inhibin from the granulosa cells inhibits FSH secretion. • A moderate, constant level of circulating estrogen exerts a negative feedback effect on LH secretion. • At 36–48 h before ovulation, the estrogen feedback effect becomes positive, and this initiates the burst of LH secretion (LH surge) that produces ovulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1145-1145; Solution Pages 1155-1156","sourcePage":1145,"sourcePageEnd":1145}]},{"subject":"Anatomy","topics":[{"id":"anatomy-1","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","questionCount":12},{"id":"anatomy-2","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","questionCount":16},{"id":"anatomy-3","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","questionCount":17},{"id":"anatomy-4","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","questionCount":26},{"id":"anatomy-5","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","questionCount":25},{"id":"anatomy-6","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":65},{"id":"anatomy-7","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","questionCount":12},{"id":"anatomy-8","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","questionCount":11},{"id":"anatomy-9","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","questionCount":16},{"id":"anatomy-10","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","questionCount":27},{"id":"anatomy-11","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","questionCount":17},{"id":"anatomy-12","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","questionCount":15},{"id":"anatomy-13","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","questionCount":22},{"id":"anatomy-14","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","questionCount":11},{"id":"anatomy-15","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":68},{"id":"anatomy-16","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","questionCount":13},{"id":"anatomy-17","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","questionCount":18},{"id":"anatomy-18","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","questionCount":13},{"id":"anatomy-19","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","questionCount":17},{"id":"anatomy-20","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","questionCount":15},{"id":"anatomy-21","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":64},{"id":"anatomy-22","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","questionCount":24},{"id":"anatomy-23","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","questionCount":20},{"id":"anatomy-24","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","questionCount":15},{"id":"anatomy-25","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":40},{"id":"anatomy-26","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","questionCount":12},{"id":"anatomy-27","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","questionCount":30},{"id":"anatomy-28","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","questionCount":19},{"id":"anatomy-29","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":44},{"id":"anatomy-30","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","questionCount":24},{"id":"anatomy-31","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","questionCount":14},{"id":"anatomy-32","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","questionCount":17},{"id":"anatomy-33","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","questionCount":12},{"id":"anatomy-34","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","questionCount":10},{"id":"anatomy-35","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","questionCount":8},{"id":"anatomy-36","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":46},{"id":"anatomy-37","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","questionCount":10},{"id":"anatomy-38","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","questionCount":14},{"id":"anatomy-39","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","questionCount":8},{"id":"anatomy-40","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","questionCount":12},{"id":"anatomy-41","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":23},{"id":"anatomy-42","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","questionCount":15},{"id":"anatomy-43","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","questionCount":10},{"id":"anatomy-44","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","questionCount":25},{"id":"anatomy-45","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":31},{"id":"anatomy-46","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","questionCount":19},{"id":"anatomy-47","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","questionCount":20},{"id":"anatomy-48","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":21},{"id":"anatomy-49","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","questionCount":17},{"id":"anatomy-50","subject":"Anatomy","title":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","questionCount":8}],"questions":[{"id":"anatomy-1-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect about the organization of the nervous system?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gray matter in the CNS is called a nucleus."},{"letter":"B","text":"PNS ganglia are mostly derived from the neural tube."},{"letter":"C","text":"White matter in the CNS forms structures like tracts and fasciculi."},{"letter":"D","text":"Axons in the CNS are covered by oligodendrocytes, and in the PNS by Schwann cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. PNS ganglia are mostly derived from the neural tube.","explanation":"- PNS ganglia are derived from neural crest cells, not the neural tube. The neural tube gives rise to the CNS. - Grey matter in the CNS consists of nerve cell bodies, called nuclei. (Option A) - CNS white matter consists of myelinated axons and these axons form structures like tracts and fasciculi. (Option C) - Oligodendrocytes cover CNS axons, while PNS axons are covered by Schwann cells. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 5; Solution Pages 10-11","sourcePage":5,"sourcePageEnd":5},{"id":"anatomy-1-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 30 year old patient presents with sudden vision loss in one eye and pain during eye movement. The patient has a history of recurrent neurological symptoms and Imaging studies reveal inflammation and demyelination of the optic nerve. The patient is diagnosed with multiple sclerosis (MS). Why is the optic nerve affected in MS, despite being considered a peripheral nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The optic nerve is not affected by MS."},{"letter":"B","text":"It is an extension of the diencephalon and is myelinated by Schwann cells, which are affected in MS."},{"letter":"C","text":"It is an extension of the diencephalon and myelinated by oligodendrocytes, which are involved in MS."},{"letter":"D","text":"It does not regenerate, making it more vulnerable to damage."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It is an extension of the diencephalon and myelinated by oligodendrocytes, which are involved in MS.","explanation":"which are involved in MS. - The optic nerve is part of the CNS because it develops as an extension of the diencephalon during embryological development. - Unlike peripheral nerves, it is myelinated by oligodendrocytes, not Schwann cells. - Multiple sclerosis (MS) is a demyelinating disease that affects oligodendrocytes , which are responsible for the myelination of CNS axons. - Since the optic nerve is part of the CNS, it is susceptible to the demyelinating process in MS. - Optic neuritis , which involves inflammation and demyelination of the optic nerve, is a common presenting symptom in MS, leading to vision loss and pain in eye movement . (Option A) Optic Nerve: - Ganglion cell neurons in the retina collect visual information and transmit it towards the CNS via the optic nerve. - Thus although being a peripheral nerve, the optic nerve is myelinated by oligodendrocytes- since it is a part of the CNS. (Option B) The optic nerve does not regenerate, making it more vulnerable to damage (Option D): The issue in MS is CNS demyelination, not the regeneration capacity.","sourceRef":"Question Page 5; Solution Pages 11-12","sourcePage":5,"sourcePageEnd":5},{"id":"anatomy-1-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following options correctly identifies the true statement about the formation of the neural tube? Neural plate → Neural groove → Neural tube The anterior neuropore closes on day 28 The posterior neuropore closes around day 25","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 is correc"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 3 is correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 and 2 is correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"All are correct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1 is correc","explanation":"Neural plate → Neural groove → Neural tube describes the sequence of neural tube formation. - The neural plate forms from ectoderm cells in the midline of the embryo around the 3rd week of development marking the beginning of primary neurulation. - The neural plate bends to form the neural groove . - The neural groove closes to form the neural tube , starting from the middle and moving toward both ends. - Surface ectoderm forms above the neural tube. - The neural tube elongates as cells narrow and stretches from head to tail. - The first closure site forms in the rhombencephalic region and spreads in both directions. - The second closure site forms in the prosencephalic region and extends caudally. - The Rostral/ Anterior neuropore (top end) closes on day 25 when around 20 somites (segments) are present. (Statement 2) - The Caudal/ Posterior neuropore (bottom end) closes around the level of the future second sacral vertebra on day 28 . (Statement 3)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 5-6; Solution Pages 12-13","sourcePage":5,"sourcePageEnd":6},{"id":"anatomy-1-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following is not a characteristic of ectodermal placodes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Developing into the lens vesicle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Forming the olfactory epithelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Contributing to the development of the membranous labyrinth of the inner ear"},{"letter":"D","text":"Directly gives rise to neurons in the Vestibulocochlear ganglion"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Directly gives rise to neurons in the Vestibulocochlear ganglion","explanation":"- Otic Placodes are specific types of ectodermal placodes that form the inner ear structures. - They invaginate to become otic vesicles, which eventually develop into the membranous labyrinth of the inner ear, including the cochlea (for hearing) and the vestibular apparatus (for balance). Vestibulocochlear Ganglion: This is a cluster of nerve cell bodies located near the inner ear. It contains sensory neurons that are responsible for hearing and balance. - While the otic placodes contribute to the development of the inner ear structures, the neurons of the vestibulocochlear ganglion do not arise directly from the ectodermal placodes. - Instead, the otic placodes form the otic vesicles, and neurons for the vestibulocochlear ganglion develop from the cells that bud off from these vesicles. Ectodermal Placodes: - Neural crest cells migrate before neural tube closure, while neuroepithelial cells become part of the surface ectoderm, forming placodes. - Placodes thicken after receiving an inductive signal, generating migratory neuronal cells for cranial sensory ganglia or forming a vesicle. - Ectodermal placodes can either form nervous tissue (neurogenic) or non-neurogenic tissues like the lens vesicle. (Option A) Important Placodes Hypophysial Placode Forms Rathke’s pouch (future pituitary gland). Olfactory Placode Develops into nasal cavity epithelium. (Option B) Epibranchial Placodes Contribute to facial, glossopharyngeal, and vagus ganglia. Otic Placodes Develop into the inner ear (membranous labyrinth) structures. (Option C) Trigeminal Placodes Contribute to trigeminal ganglia.","sourceRef":"Question Page 6; Solution Pages 13-15","sourcePage":6,"sourcePageEnd":6},{"id":"anatomy-1-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following structural abnormalities is most commonly associated with the given image? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Malformation of the vertebral arches"},{"letter":"B","text":"Incomplete development of the basichondrocranium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Herniation of brain tissue through the skull"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fusion of the cranial sutures"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Incomplete development of the basichondrocranium","explanation":"Anencephaly is associated with severe abnormalities in the base of the skull (basichondrocranium), affecting its shape, position, and ossification. Anencephaly: - Neurulation failure : Anencephaly occurs when the neural tube fuses in the spinal cord (Caudal neuropore) but fails to fuse in the brain region (Cranial neuropore), leading to missing parts of the brain and skull. - The cranial and caudal neuropores close around day 25 and 28 of development respectively in a normally developing fetus. Types of Neural Tube Defects: Neural Tube Defect Description Anencephaly (Cranioschisis) Failure of neural tube fusion in the brain region, results in missing parts of the brain and skull. Craniorachischisis Totalis Complete failure of the neural tube to fuse, affecting both the brain and spinal cord. Spina Bifida Incomplete fusion of the spinal neural tube or failure of formation of vertebral arches leads to defects in the spinal cord. Malformation of the vertebral arches (Option A) : This is characteristic of spina bifida, not anencephaly. Herniation of brain tissue through the skull (Option C) : This describes encephalocele, a different neural tube defect, not anencephaly. Fusion of the cranial sutures (Option D) : This refers to craniosynostosis, which is not related to anencephaly.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 6-7; Solution Pages 15-16","sourcePage":6,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"anatomy-1-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is a common feature of craniorachischisis compared to rachischisis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Craniorachischisis results from failure of caudal neural tube closure, while rachischisis affects the entire tube."},{"letter":"B","text":"Craniorachischisis exposes both the brain and spinal cord; rachischisis exposes only the spinal cord."},{"letter":"C","text":"Craniorachischisis is less severe than rachischisis and affects only the vertebrae."},{"letter":"D","text":"Craniorachischisis involves complete neural tube closure, while rachischisis exposes both the brain and spinal cord."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Craniorachischisis exposes both the brain and spinal cord; rachischisis exposes only the spinal cord.","explanation":"exposes only the spinal cord. This option correctly identifies the extent of exposure associated with each condition, with craniorachischisis being more severe and involving both structures. Rachischisis and Craniorachischisis: Condition Rachischisis Craniorachischisis Description A type of spina bifida where a portion The most severe form of neural tube of the spinal cord remains open. defect- involving exposure of both the brain and spinal cord. Neural Tube Involvement Occurs due to the failure of fusion of Occurs due to the failure of fusion of the caudal portion of the neural tube. both the cranial as well as the caudal (Option A) portions of the neural tube. (Option D) Severity Severe, but localised to the spinal Most severe (Option C) cord Exposure Spinal cord neural tissue is exposed Both the brain and spinal cord are exposed. Diagram","sourceRef":"Question Page 7; Solution Pages 16-17","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"anatomy-1-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements about spina bifida is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spina bifida occulta presents with an abnormal U or V shape in the vertebrae."},{"letter":"B","text":"Meningocele involves protrusion of the meninges and spinal cord through the defect."},{"letter":"C","text":"Diastematomyelia is rarely associated with spina bifida and splits the spinal cord."},{"letter":"D","text":"The incidence of myelomeningocele has decreased due to prenatal diagnosis and termination options."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Meningocele involves protrusion of the meninges and spinal cord through the defect.","explanation":"defect. - Meningocele involves only the protrusion of the meninges , not the spinal cord. - When the spinal cord is also involved, the condition is called myelomeningocele . Spina bifida is a birth defect where the neural tube fails to close properly, affecting the caudal portion of the spinal neural tube. Types: Type Description Spina Bifida Cystica Involves a visible sac or cyst containing the spinal cord or nerves protruding from the back.Two types: Meningocele: A sac containing only the meninges and CSF, with the spinal cord intact. Myelomeningocele: Sac contains both the meninges and a portion of the spinal cord; the most severe form. Spina Bifida Occulta The defect is hidden under the skin, typically found in the lumbosacral or sacral regions; it affects ~5% of the population. Impact on Spinal Cord Development: Impact Description Ascent of the Conus Medullaris The conus medullaris normally ascends relative to the vertebral column. In spina bifida, this ascent is impaired, leading to a tethered cord. Tethered Cord The spinal cord is anchored at the defect site, restricting normal upward movement, which can cause neurological complications. Diastematomyelia An extremely rare condition where the spinal cord is split into two columns by a bony or cartilaginous spur, often associated with tethered cord. (Option C) Prenatal Diagnosis: - Detection : Spina bifida can be identified through ultrasound imaging during pregnancy. - Spina Bifida Occulta Indicator : The posterior ossification centres of the vertebrae may display an abnormal U or V shape. (Option A) - Incidence : The prevalence of severe types, such as myelomeningocele, has decreased due to improved prenatal diagnosis and options for termination. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 7; Solution Pages 17-19","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":7},{"id":"anatomy-1-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following correctly pairs a brain region with its corresponding structure or function?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Myelencephalon: Develops into the cerebellum, involved in motor control and balance."},{"letter":"B","text":"Tectum: Located in the hindbrain and responsible for auditory and visual reflexes."},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypothalamus: Part of the diencephalon, regulates hunger, thirst, body temperature, and sleep-wake cycles."},{"letter":"D","text":"Frontal Lobe: Primarily involved in visual processing and spatial awareness."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Hypothalamus: Part of the diencephalon, regulates hunger, thirst, body temperature, and sleep-wake cycles.","explanation":"temperature, and sleep-wake cycles. Parts of the Brain: Brain Region Components Functions/Derivatives Prosencephalon (Forebrain) Diencephalon Relay centre for sensory information, regulates endocrine system, autonomic functions Thalamus Relays sensory information to the cerebral cortex. Hypothalamus Regulates hunger, thirst, temperature, sleep-wake cycles, endocrine system Epithalamus The pineal gland (melatonin production) Subthalamus Involved in motor control Telencephalon Forms cerebral hemispheres, median part in front of the interventricular foramen and lateral ventricle Cerebral Cortex Higher cognitive functions (language, memory, reasoning, movement) Frontal Lobe (Option D) Planning, decision-making, problem-solving, voluntary movement Parietal Lobe Sensory information, spatial awareness, navigation Temporal Lobe Auditory processing, language comprehension Occipital Lobe Visual processing Lateral Ventricles Fluid-filled cavities for cerebrospinal fluid circulation Mesencephalon (Midbrain) Cerebral Peduncles Connects cerebrum to the brainstem Tectum (Option B) Made of superior and inferior colliculi. Tegmentum Contains nuclei of 5,6,7,8 nerves. Rhombencephalon (Hindbrain) Myelencephalon (Option A) Develops into the medulla oblongata (breathing, heart rate) Metencephalon Forms the pons (signal relay) and cerebellum (motor control, balance)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 7-8; Solution Pages 19-20","sourcePage":7,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"anatomy-1-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements about the treatment of aqueduct stenosis is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ventriculoperitoneal shunting permanently cures aqueduct stenosis."},{"letter":"B","text":"Endoscopic third ventriculostomy involves diverting CSF to the abdominal cavity."},{"letter":"C","text":"Aqueduct stenosis can be treated with medications alone in most cases."},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventriculoperitoneal shunting involves diverting CSF to reduce intracranial pressure."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Ventriculoperitoneal shunting involves diverting CSF to reduce intracranial pressure.","explanation":"pressure. Ventriculoperitoneal shunting diverts CSF to relieve pressure caused by hydrocephalus. Aqueduct Stenosis: Category Details Causes Congenital: Developmental anomalies Acquired: Tumours, infections, trauma Symptoms Headache, nausea, vomiting (Due to CSF accumulation) Cognitive impairments due to increased intracranial pressure. Diagnosis MRI or CT scans showing aqueduct narrowing and ventricular enlargement. Treatment Ventriculoperitoneal shunting : Diverts excess CSF (Option A) Endoscopic third ventriculostomy : Bypasses obstruction (high relapse rate) (Option B) Aqueduct stenosis can be treated with medications alone in most cases (Option C): It usually requires surgical intervention, as medications alone are not sufficient.","sourceRef":"Question Page 8; Solution Pages 20-21","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"anatomy-1-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements about the cerebellar peduncles and corpora quadrigemina is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior cerebellar peduncles carry afferent signals from the cerebellum to the medulla."},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle cerebellar peduncles transmit sensory information from the spinal cord to the cerebellum."},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the medulla and aid in balance and posture."},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior colliculi are involved in auditory processing and relay signals to the auditory cortex."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect the cerebellum to the medulla and aid in balance and posture.","explanation":"in balance and posture. The inferior cerebellar peduncles carry both afferent and efferent fibres, connecting the cerebellum to the medulla oblongata and playing a role in balance and posture control. Cerebellar Peduncles Peduncle Function Components Role Superior Cerebellar Carry efferent (outgoing) It contains axons from the Movement planning and Peduncles signals from the Dentate, Emboliform and coordination. cerebellum to the the Globose nuclei of the midbrain. (Option A) cerebellum. Connects the cerebellum to the midbrain. Middle Cerebellar The main route for afferent Connects cerebellum to Provides information about Peduncles (incoming) signals to the the pons. (Option B) planned movements. cerebellum. Inferior Cerebellar Carry both afferent and It contains axons from the Receives sensory Peduncles (Option C) efferent fibres. Connects fastigial nucleus of the information for balance cerebellum to the medulla cerebellum. and posture, and sends oblongata. signals to vestibular nuclei and reticular formation. Corpora Quadrigemina (Tectum) Colliculi Function Components Role Superior Colliculi Involved in visual Receives input from optic Coordinates head and eye processing and eye nerves and visual movements in response to movement control. (Option processing areas. visual stimuli. D) Inferior Colliculi Plays a role in auditory Receives input from Projects to the medial processing. [musIC- auditory pathways. geniculate nucleus of the Inferior Colliculus] thalamus for auditory signal relay.","sourceRef":"Question Page 8; Solution Pages 21-22","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":8},{"id":"anatomy-1-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify the labelled structures in the given diagram. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Fornix; B- Hippocampus; C- Corpus Callosum"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Hippocampus; B- Fornix; C- Corpus Callosum"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Fornix; B- Corpus Callosum; C- Hippocampus"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Hippocampus; B- Fornix; C- Corpus Callosum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. A- Fornix; B- Corpus Callosum; C- Hippocampus","explanation":"The diagram above shows the thalamus and the structures surrounding it. Structure Description Thalamus Grey matter mass; sensory and motor roles Fornix A c-shaped fibre bundle; connects the hippocampal formations of the two sides. The main efferent tract of the hippocampus. Corpus Callosum The largest commissure of the brain; links the cerebra hemispheres. Caudate Nucleus The comma-shaped nucleus of the basal ganglia Choroid Plexus Produces CSF; vascular network. Hippocampus Seahorse-shaped (Curved and elongated); stores long-term memory and is a part of the limbic system.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 8-9; Solution Pages 22-23","sourcePage":8,"sourcePageEnd":9},{"id":"anatomy-1-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-1","chapter":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topic":"Lesson 1 - Nervous System Development","topicIndex":1,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the pathway of the Papez circuit?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cingulate cortex → Hippocampus → Mammillary bodies → Anterior thalamic nuclei → Fornix"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cingulate cortex → Mammillary bodies → Hippocampus → Fornix → Anterior thalamic nuclei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hippocampus → Cingulate cortex → Anterior thalamic nuclei → Mammillary bodies → Fornix"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hippocampus → Fornix → Mammillary bodies → Anterior thalamic nuclei → Cingulate cortex"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Hippocampus → Fornix → Mammillary bodies → Anterior thalamic nuclei → Cingulate cortex","explanation":"Cingulate cortex The Papez circuit starts in the hippocampus, travels through the fornix to the mammillary bodies, then to the anterior thalamic nuclei, and finally projects back to the cingulate cortex. Papez circuit: - It is a part of the Limbic system. - Fornix is a collection of axons. - Fornix takes the information from the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies of the hypothalamus, to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus (mammilo–thalamic tract). - From the anterior nucleus of the thalamus, the pathway comes back to the hippocampus, thus completing the circuit. - Lesions in the Papez circuit occur in Alzheimer's disease and in alcoholics with vitamin B1 deficiency, leading to Korsakoff's psychosis characterized by anterograde amnesia, confabulation, confusion and disorientation","sourceRef":"Question Page 9; Solution Pages 23-24","sourcePage":9,"sourcePageEnd":9},{"id":"anatomy-2-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which structure forms the lateral wall of the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corpus callosum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Head of the caudate nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hippocampus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Head of the caudate nucleus","explanation":"Relations of Lateral Ventricles Corpus Callosum (Option A ruled out) Caudate Nucleus (Option B) Roof of the body of lateral ventricle Roof, floor and Head - Lateral wall and floor of Anterior Horn Body - Part Anterior Wall of the Frontal horn Roof, medial and lateral of the Floor of the body of the Lateral ventricle Tail - Roof wall of Occipital horn Lateral part of the Roof of the of inferior/temporal horn Temporal Horn Caudate nucleus and Lateral Ventricle Corpus callosum and Lateral Ventricle Thalamus (Option C): The thalamus forms the walls of the third ventricle and does not form part of the lateral wall of the anterior horn of the lateral ventricle. Hippocampus (Option D): The hippocampus is located in the inferior (temporal) horn of the lateral ventricle and does not form the lateral wall of the anterior horn.","sourceRef":"Question Page 25; Solution Pages 31-33","sourcePage":25,"sourcePageEnd":25},{"id":"anatomy-2-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which cranial nerve sensory fibres transmit sensory information from the skin and mucous membranes of the face, head, and neck?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres"},{"letter":"B","text":"General Visceral Afferent (GVA) fibres"},{"letter":"C","text":"Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres"},{"letter":"D","text":"General Somatic Afferent (GSA) fibres"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. General Somatic Afferent (GSA) fibres","explanation":"- GSA fibres carry sensory input like pain, temperature, touch, and proprioception from the skin, mucous membranes, and other somatic regions of the face, head, and neck. - The sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve processes this input. - Cranial Nerves involved in GSA - Trigeminal (V), Facial (VII), Glossopharyngeal (IX), Vagus (X). Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres (Option A): - SSA fibres transmit information related to special senses like vision and hearing, not the general sensations of touch and pain. General Visceral Afferent (GVA) fibres (Option B): - GVA fibres carry sensory information from internal organs, like the lungs and stomach, not the skin and mucous membranes. - Cranial Nerves involved in GVA - Glossopharyngeal (IX), Vagus (X). Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres (Option C): - SVA fibres are involved in special senses such as taste and smell, not general sensory functions like pain or temperature.","sourceRef":"Question Page 25; Solution Pages 33-34","sourcePage":25,"sourcePageEnd":25},{"id":"anatomy-2-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 7-year-old girl presents with severe headaches, vomiting, and papilloedema. Imaging reveals obstructive hydrocephalus due to a tumour blocking the exit of cerebrospinal fluid from the fourth ventricle. Which of the following structures is most likely blocked?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The Foramen of Monro"},{"letter":"B","text":"Foramina of Luschka"},{"letter":"C","text":"Foramen rotundum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Foramen ovale"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Foramina of Luschka","explanation":"The foramina of Luschka and the Foramen of Magendie allow CSF to exit from the fourth ventricle into the subarachnoid space, and their obstruction can lead to hydrocephalus. Foramen of Magendie and Luschka: Aspect Foramen of Magendie (Median Foramina of Luschka (Lateral aperture) aperture) Location Caudal to the cerebellar nodule At the ends of the lateral recesses of the 4th ventricle. Function Main communication between Allow communication between the ventricles and subarachnoid space fourth ventricle and the subarachnoid space. Associated Pathology Obstruction leads to hydrocephalus. Obstruction leads to hydrocephalus. A blockage here would prevent CSF A blockage here prevents CSF from from exiting medially into the exiting laterally from the fourth subarachnoid space. ventricle. Symptoms of Obstruction Headache, vomiting, papilloedema Headache, vomiting, papilloedema The Foramen of Monro (Option A): Also known as the interventricular foramen, connects the lateral ventricles to the third ventricle, not the fourth ventricle. Foramen rotundum (Option C): It is an opening in the sphenoid bone of the skull. It transmits the maxillary nerve (V2) and is unrelated to CSF flow or the ventricular system. Foramen ovale (Option D): This is also an opening in the sphenoid bone of the skull. It transmits the mandibular nerve (V3) and is not involved in CSF drainage from the fourth ventricle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 25-26; Solution Pages 34-35","sourcePage":25,"sourcePageEnd":26},{"id":"anatomy-2-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the given options are correctly matched based on the image given? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3- Lateral recess, 5- Frontal horn"},{"letter":"B","text":"1- Occipital horn, 7- Third Ventricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"2- Fourth Ventricle, 4- Aqueduct"},{"letter":"D","text":"3- Temporal horn, 6- Aqueduct"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 2- Fourth Ventricle, 4- Aqueduct","explanation":"- Each cerebral hemisphere contains one of the two large lateral ventricles. This ventricle is roughly C-shaped and can be divided into several sections. Body Anterior horn - extends into the frontal lobe Posterior horn -extends into the Occipital lobe Inferior horn - extends into the Temporal lobe - Body - Anterior horn - extends into the frontal lobe - Posterior horn -extends into the Occipital lobe - Inferior horn - extends into the Temporal lobe - The third ventricle is a narrow space located between the two thalami. It connects to the lateral ventricles in the front via the interventricular foramina (of Monro) and to the fourth ventricle in the back through the cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius). - The cerebral aqueduct, also known as the aqueduct of Sylvius, is a narrow passage approximately 1.8 cm long that links the third ventricle to the fourth ventricle. - The fourth ventricle is sandwiched between the brainstem and the cerebellum. - From the fourth ventricle, the CSF moves into the central canal of the spinal cord. - Body - Anterior horn - extends into the frontal lobe - Posterior horn -extends into the Occipital lobe - Inferior horn - extends into the Temporal lobe","sourceRef":"Question Page 26; Solution Page 35","sourcePage":26,"sourcePageEnd":26},{"id":"anatomy-2-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What is a primary characteristic of Benign External Hydrocephalus (BEH) in infants?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Severe neurological symptoms"},{"letter":"B","text":"Macrocephaly without significant symptoms"},{"letter":"C","text":"Necessitates immediate surgical intervention"},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased ventricular enlargement"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Macrocephaly without significant symptoms","explanation":"- BEH in infants is defined by an increase in head size (macrocephaly) without accompanying significant neurological symptoms. - It is often self-limiting and can be monitored. Benign External Hydrocephalus Definition Increase in subarachnoid space volume with minimal ventricular enlargement, leading to macrocephaly (large head). Causes Impaired CSF absorption because of arachnoid villi immaturity Venous defects Clinical features Increased head circumference- raised ICP Poor motor development Hypotonia, vomiting and seizures. Investigations Through CT or MRI imaging showing subarachnoid space widening; Clinical assessment. Treatment Conservative Observation: For cases without significant symptoms. Surgical Options: Considered if increased ICP or other complications are present (e.g., ventriculoperitoneal shunt). Severe neurological symptoms (Option A): BEH is characterized by macrocephaly without significant neurological symptoms. Infants with BEH typically do not exhibit major cognitive or physical impairments. Necessitates immediate surgical intervention (Option C): - BEH generally does not require immediate surgical intervention. - Most cases are mild and self-resolving; surgery is only considered if there are complications or increased intracranial pressure. Increased ventricular enlargement (Option D): - In BEH, there is an increase in the subarachnoid space volume with minimal or no enlargement of the ventricles. - This distinguishes it from other types of hydrocephalus where ventricular enlargement is more prominent.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 26-27; Solution Pages 35-37","sourcePage":26,"sourcePageEnd":27},{"id":"anatomy-2-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Match the following wall of the 3rd ventricle with its respective component. 1. Anterior wall a) Hypothalamus 2. Posterior Wall b) Mammillary bodies 3. Lateral wall c) Lamina terminalis 4. Floor d) Pineal gland 1. Anterior wall a) Hypothalamus 2. Posterior Wall b) Mammillary bodies 3. Lateral wall c) Lamina terminalis 4. Floor d) Pineal gland **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1- d, 2- a, 3- b, 4- c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1- b, 2- a, 3- b, 4- d"},{"letter":"C","text":"1- a, 2- b, 3- d, 4- c"},{"letter":"D","text":"1- c, 2- d, 3- a, 4- b"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1- c, 2- d, 3- a, 4- b","explanation":"- The Third ventricle is a cavity located in the diencephalon. It is a narrow, midline space positioned between the two thalami and part of the hypothalamus. - It connects to the lateral ventricle via the interventricular foramen (of Monro) anteriorly, and posteriorly, it connects to the fourth ventricle through the cerebral aqueduct (of Sylvius). - It has an anterior wall, posterior wall, roof, floor and two lateral walls. Wall of 3rd Ventricle Components Anterior Wall Anterior column of fornix Anterior commissure Lamina terminalis Posterior Wall Pineal gland Posterior commissure Commencement of cerebral aqueduct Roof Formed by the ependyma that stretches across the upper limits of two thalami Floor Optic chiasma Tuber cinereum and infundibulum Mammillary bodies Posterior perforated substance Tegmentum of the midbrain Lateral Walls Divided by Hypothalamic sulcus into: Large superior part- Medial surface of the thalamus. Small inferior part- Hypothalamus and it is continuous with the ventricular floor. - Corpora arenacea, or brain sand , are calcified deposits in the pineal gland composed of calcium salts. They are a common and typically benign finding in the brain, particularly in older individuals. - Corpora arenacea can be seen as an opacity in the pineal gland area on radiological imaging , especially on X-rays , CT scans , or MRI .","sourceRef":"Question Page 27; Solution Pages 37-38","sourcePage":27,"sourcePageEnd":27},{"id":"anatomy-2-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Match the following boundary of the 4th ventricle with its component. 1. Lateral wall a) Superior medullary velum 2. Posterior wall b) Superior medulla 3. Rhomboid fossa c) Superior cerebellar peduncle 1. Lateral wall a) Superior medullary velum 2. Posterior wall b) Superior medulla 3. Rhomboid fossa c) Superior cerebellar peduncle **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-a"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-c, 2-a, 3-b"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-b, 2-a, 3-c"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 1-c, 2-a, 3-b","explanation":"- The fourth ventricle is a tent-shaped cavity filled with cerebrospinal fluid (CSF). - It is located anterior to the cerebellum and posterior to the pons and the upper part of the medulla oblongata. - The ventricle is lined with ependyma. - It connects above to the cerebral aqueduct of the midbrain. - It connects below to the central canal of the medulla oblongata and the spinal cord. - The ventricle has lateral boundaries, a roof, and a rhomboid-shaped floor. Boundaries of 4th Ventricle Description Lateral Wall Inferolaterally- Inferior cerebellar peduncle, and gracile and cuneate tubercles Superolaterally- Superior cerebellar peduncle Roof (Posterior Wall) Superior Part : Medial borders of the two superior cerebellar peduncles. Superior medullary velum (a sheet of white matter). Inferior Part : Inferior medullary velum (ventricular ependyma and pia mater) Median aperture (foramen of Magendie) in the midline. Lateral Recesses : Extend laterally around the medulla. Open anteriorly as the foramina of Luschka (lateral openings of the fourth ventricle). Rhomboid Fossa (Floor) The posterior surface of the pons The upper part of the medulla","sourceRef":"Question Pages 27-28; Solution Pages 38-40","sourcePage":27,"sourcePageEnd":28},{"id":"anatomy-2-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which structure in the floor of the 4th ventricle consists of transverse white fibres derived from the arcuate nuclei?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sulcus limitans"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stria medullaris"},{"letter":"C","text":"Locus ceruleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Area postrema"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Stria medullaris","explanation":"The stria medullaris consists of transverse white fibres originating from the arcuate nuclei, which cross the floor of the 4th ventricle to enter the inferior cerebellar peduncle. Structures and relation with the 4th Ventricle - The floor of the 4th ventricle is divided into right and left halves by the median sulcus , extending from the aqueduct to the central canal. - Stria Medullaris : White fibres (derived from arcuate nuclei) cross the floor and enter the inferior cerebellar peduncle. - Medial Eminence : Longitudinal elevation on either side of the median sulcus, bounded laterally by the sulcus limitans . (Option A ruled out) - Vestibular Area : Lateral to sulcus limitans, it overlies the vestibular nuclei. - Superior Fovea & Locus Ceruleus : At the upper sulcus limitans, the superior fovea is a triangular depression; locus ceruleus is a bluish-grey area due to pigmented nerve cells. (Option C ruled out) - Facial Colliculus : Oval swelling in the pontine floor produced by facial nerve fibres looping over the abducent nucleus. - Hypoglossal and Vagal Triangles : Located in the medullary part of the floor; the hypoglossal triangle overlies the hypoglossal nucleus, and the vagal triangle overlies the vagus, glossopharyngeal, and accessory nerve nuclei. - Area Postrema : A small area between the vagal triangle and the gracile tubercle. (Option D ruled out) - Obex : A fold formed by the meeting of two teniae at the inferior angle, marking the lower part of the ventricle. - Central Canal : Opens into the 4th ventricle superiorly, extends through the medulla and spinal cord, lined with ependyma but lacks a choroid plexus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 28; Solution Pages 40-41","sourcePage":28,"sourcePageEnd":28},{"id":"anatomy-2-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following does not cross the midline?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corpus Callosum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior Commissure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fornix"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arcuate Fasciculus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Arcuate Fasciculus","explanation":"- The Arcuate Fasciculus is an association tract that connects different areas within the same hemisphere and does not cross the midline. - The white matter in the cerebral hemispheres consists of long, myelinated axons bundled into larger tracts called fasciculi. - These white matter tracts are classified as association, commissural, or projection pathways based on where they start, travel, and end . Commissural fibres Projection They connect one hemisphere to the opposite side, Projection fibres connect the cerebral cortex with linking corresponding (homotopic) or different subcortical structures, including the brainstem and spinal (heterotopic) areas. Corpus Callosum : The largest cord, and include both descending (corticofugal) and commissural structure connecting the two cerebral ascending (corticopetal) fibres. These fibres converge in hemispheres, located at the bottom of the longitudinal the corona radiata , pass through the internal capsule , fissure. (Option A ruled out) Anterior Commissure : A and are flanked by the caudate nucleus and thalamus smaller bundle of nerve fibres crossing the midline at the medially, and the lentiform nucleus laterally. lamina terminalis. (Option B ruled out) Fornix : Composed of myelinated fibres, it connects the hippocampus to the mammillary bodies in the hypothalamus. It connects the hippocampal formations of the 2 sides. (Option C ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 28; Solution Pages 41-42","sourcePage":28,"sourcePageEnd":28},{"id":"anatomy-2-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which association fibre connects Wernicke's area with Broca's area","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Uncinate Fasciculus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior Longitudinal Fasciculus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Arcuate Fasciculus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior Longitudinal Fasciculus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Arcuate Fasciculus","explanation":"The Arcuate Fasciculus connects Wernicke's area, located in the superior temporal gyrus and responsible for language comprehension, with Broca's area, found in the inferior frontal gyrus and involved in language production. Association Tracts - Association tracts connect different areas within the same hemisphere, with short U-fibers linking nearby gyri and long fibres connecting gyri across different lobes. - Short fibres are closer to the surface, near the cortex, while longer fibres run deeper within the white matter, allowing for fast communication and sensory processing. - Examples of association tracts are: Arcuate fasciculus Uncinate fasciculus (Option A ruled Inferior and Superior longitudinal out) fasciculus (Option B & D ruled out) It is seen on the left side of the Connecting the temporal lobe with Superior longitudinal fasciculus : cerebrum. In 95% of people, the the frontal lobe. It does not cross the Connects the anterior part of the language area is on the left dominant midline. frontal lobe to the occipital and hemisphere. Wernicke's sensory temporal lobes. Inferior longitudinal speech area in the temporal lobe is fasciculus : Connects the occipital connected with Broca's motor speech lobe to the temporal lobe. They do area in the inferior frontal gyrus by not cross the midline. arcuate fasciculus. It does not cross the midline.","sourceRef":"Question Page 28; Solution Pages 42-43","sourcePage":28,"sourcePageEnd":28},{"id":"anatomy-2-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which part of the corpus callosum contains fibres that connect the right and left occipital cortices?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rostrum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Genu"},{"letter":"C","text":"Body or Trunk"},{"letter":"D","text":"Splenium"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Splenium","explanation":"- The splenium is part of the corpus callosum where fibers connect the right occipital cortex with the left occipital cortex. - Corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain and connects the two cerebral hemispheres. Corpus callosum Parts - Rostrum (anterior) : Axons here connect the orbitofrontal cortex on the right and left side. (Option A ruled out) - Genu: Most anterior portion of the corpus callosum; forms the anterior wall of the frontal horn of the lateral ventricle and contains fibres that connect the prefrontal cortex. (Option B ruled out) - Body (central part): It connects motor and premotor areas in the frontal and parietal lobes (Option C ruled out) - Splenium (posterior): Axons connect the right occipital cortex with the left occipital cortex. Corpus Callosum Fibers: - Frontal Forceps (Forceps Minor): Connects the frontal lobes across hemispheres Fibers pass through the genu of the corpus callosum - Connects the frontal lobes across hemispheres - Fibers pass through the genu of the corpus callosum - Occipital Forceps (Forceps Major): Connects the occipital visual cortices across hemispheres Fibers pass through the splenium of the corpus callosum - Connects the occipital visual cortices across hemispheres - Fibers pass through the splenium of the corpus callosum - Connects the frontal lobes across hemispheres - Fibers pass through the genu of the corpus callosum - Connects the occipital visual cortices across hemispheres - Fibers pass through the splenium of the corpus callosum Tapetum Fibers: Fibres from splenium and isthmus that arc forward along the lateral wall of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 28-29; Solution Pages 43-45","sourcePage":28,"sourcePageEnd":29},{"id":"anatomy-2-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which cranial nerve motor fibres are responsible for innervating skeletal muscles that develop from somites?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"General Somatic Efferent fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Special Visceral Efferent fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"General Visceral Efferent fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Special Somatic Afferent fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. General Somatic Efferent fibers","explanation":"Motor Neural Columns GSE (General Somatic GVE (General Visceral SVE (Special Visceral Efferent) (Option A) Efferent) (Option C ruled Efferent ) (Option B ruled out) out) Innervation Motor innervation to Autonomic (involuntary) Innervate muscles that are skeletal muscles that innervation to smooth derived from pharyngeal develop from somites. muscles and glands. arches, not somites. Function Voluntary movements Functions such as pupil Functions like facial such as eye and tongue constriction, salivation, expression, mastication, movements. digestion, and heart rate and swallowing. regulation. Nerves Oculomotor (III) Trochlear Oculomotor (III) Facial Facial (VII) Trigeminal (V) (IV) Abducens (VI) (VII) Glossopharyngeal Accessory spinal nerve (XI) Hypoglossal (XII) (IX) Vagus (X) Neural Columns Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres (Option D): - SSA fibres are not motor but sensory, transmitting sensory information related to vision and hearing. - These fibres are involved in processing external stimuli rather than controlling motor functions.","sourceRef":"Question Page 29; Solution Pages 45-46","sourcePage":29,"sourcePageEnd":29},{"id":"anatomy-2-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which cranial nerve sensory fibres are primarily responsible for transmitting sensations from the tongue to the brainstem?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres"},{"letter":"B","text":"Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres"},{"letter":"C","text":"General Somatic Afferent (GSA) fibres"},{"letter":"D","text":"General Visceral Afferent (GVA) fibres"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres","explanation":"Special Sensory Column Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres (Option B) Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres (Option A ruled out) SVA fibres are sensory fibres responsible for transmitting SSA fibres are responsible for transmitting sensory taste sensations from the tongue to the brainstem. These information related to the special senses of smell, vision, fibres terminate in the nucleus solitarius in the medulla hearing, and balance. The optic nerve (II), olfactory nerve Cranial nerves - Facial (VII), Glossopharyngeal (IX). (III), vestibulocochlear nerve (VIII) are part of SSA. General Somatic Afferent (GSA) fibres (Option C): - GSA fibres carry general sensory information such as touch, pain, and temperature from the skin and mucous membranes of the head and neck. - They do not transmit special senses like taste. General Visceral Afferent (GVA) fibres (Option D): - GVA fibres convey sensory information from internal organs (viscera) such as distension, pressure, and pain. - They are not involved in transmitting taste or other special sensory modalities.","sourceRef":"Question Page 29; Solution Pages 46-47","sourcePage":29,"sourcePageEnd":29},{"id":"anatomy-2-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":14,"question":"What is the primary function of the alar plate in the developing neural tube?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Development of motor nuclei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Processing sensory information"},{"letter":"C","text":"Formation of autonomic motor fibres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Regulation of somatic motor output"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Processing sensory information","explanation":"The alar plate is involved in developing sensory nuclei, which process sensory information such as pain, touch, and proprioception, as well as special sensory modalities like taste, hearing, and vision. They give rise to - Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) fibres - Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) fibres - General Somatic Afferent (GSA) fibres - General Visceral Afferent (GVA) fibres Development of motor nuclei (Option A): The development of motor nuclei is linked to the basal plate, not the alar plate, which primarily focuses on sensory processing. Formation of autonomic motor fibres (Option C): Autonomic motor fibres originate from the basal plate and are part of the general visceral efferent (GVE) system, not the alar plate. Regulation of somatic motor output (Option D): Somatic motor output is regulated by motor neurons that arise from the basal plate, not the alar plate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 29-30; Solution Pages 47-48","sourcePage":29,"sourcePageEnd":30},{"id":"anatomy-2-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following cranial nerve nuclei originates from the General Visceral Efferent (GVE) column of the basal plate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Edinger-Westphal nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoglossal nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cochlear nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Edinger-Westphal nucleus","explanation":"- The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is part of the GVE column via the Oculomotor (III) nerve , responsible for functions like pupil constriction and accommodation. - Along with the Edinger-Westphal nucleus, The GVE column also involves the Salivatory nuclei (for saliva secretion) and the vagal nucleus (controls the heart, lungs, and digestive system) The basal plate of the neural tube has motor function and gives rise to - General Somatic Efferent (GSE) fibres - Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) fibres - General Visceral Efferent (GVE) fibres Hypoglossal nucleus (Option B): The hypoglossal nucleus belongs to the General Somatic Efferent (GSE) column and controls voluntary movements of the tongue. Facial nucleus (Option C): The facial nucleus is part of the Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) column and controls facial muscles, not autonomic functions. The cochlear nucleus (Option D): The cochlear nucleus is involved in sensory functions related to hearing and originates from the Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) column, not the GVE column.","sourceRef":"Question Page 30; Solution Pages 48-49","sourcePage":30,"sourcePageEnd":30},{"id":"anatomy-2-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-2","chapter":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topic":"Lesson 2 - Ventricular System, White Matter, and Neural Columns","topicIndex":2,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 5 year old boy came with complaints of sudden onset weakness in his lower limbs. His parents mention a history of viral illness with fever and sore throat. On examination, the boy has flaccid paralysis of his legs with muscle wasting, absent deep tendon reflexes, and no sensory deficits. Which of the following structures is primarily affected in this condition, leading to muscle paralysis and wasting?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior white columns of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior grey columns of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinothalamic tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cerebellar peduncles"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Anterior grey columns of the spinal cord","explanation":"- The clinical scenario of this child points to a diagnosis of poliomyelitis due to the history of acute flaccid paralysis following fever and lower motor neuron involvement. - Poliomyelitis primarily affects the anterior grey columns, leading to lower motor neuron damage, causing muscle paralysis and wasting. Poliomyelitis : - Viral infection of anterior grey columns of the spinal cord and cranial nerve motor nuclei. - Causes muscle paralysis and wasting, often affecting lower limbs more. Severe cases can impair respiration and affect facial, pharyngeal, laryngeal, and tongue muscles. - It is a lower motor neuron palsy. Posterior white columns of the spinal cord (Option A): These columns are responsible for sensory functions, not motor control. Spinothalamic tract (Option C): This tract transmits sensory information related to pain and temperature, not motor function. Cerebellar peduncles (Option D): These are involved in coordinating movement, not directly causing paralysis associated with poliomyelitis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 30; Solution Pages 49-50","sourcePage":30,"sourcePageEnd":30},{"id":"anatomy-3-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 5 year old child presents with developmental delays, including difficulties in motor coordination and speech. MRI reveals a complete absence of the corpus callosum. Which of the following is not typically associated with this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Intellectual disability"},{"letter":"B","text":"Seizures"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hemispatial neglect"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hyperactivity and impulsivity"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Hemispatial neglect","explanation":"Hemispatial neglect is not typically associated with agenesis of the corpus callosum. - Hemispatial neglect is often seen in patients with damage to one hemisphere , particularly the right hemisphere, leading to inattention to the opposite side of space (usually the left). - In agenesis, both hemispheres may function independently, so hemispatial neglect is not a hallmark of this condition. Agenesis of the corpus callosum is a rare congenital condition in which the corpus callosum , the structure connecting the two cerebral hemispheres, fails to develop properly or is absent. - This can lead to a range of neurological and developmental issues. Causes: - Genetic : Chromosomal abnormalities (e.g., trisomy 13, 18) or mutations in specific genes. - Environmental : Prenatal factors like infections, alcohol, or drug exposure. Clinical Features: - Developmental delays : Intellectual disability, motor delays, and speech impairments. - Seizures : Common due to lack of communication between brain hemispheres. - Motor problems : Coordination issues, ataxia. - Behavioral issues : Hyperactivity, impulsivity, and autistic-like behaviors. - Cognitive challenges : Attention deficits and difficulty with higher cognitive functions. Associated Conditions: - Aicardi syndrome, Andermann syndrome, holoprosencephaly, fetal alcohol syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Page 51; Solution Pages 59-61","sourcePage":51,"sourcePageEnd":51},{"id":"anatomy-3-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following is part of the complete syndrome triad associated with Anterior Choroidal Artery Syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bilateral hemiplegia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contralateral hemisensory loss"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bitemporal hemianopia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ipsilateral homonymous hemianopia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Contralateral hemisensory loss","explanation":"Anterior choroidal artery syndrome is associated with the clinical triad of contralateral hemiplegia, contralateral hemisensory loss and contralateral homonymous hemianopia Anterior Choroidal Artery Syndrome: - Cause : Infarction in the anterior choroidal artery territory. - Pathophysiology : AChA anastomoses with posterior choroidal artery and posterior cerebral artery. Infarction occurs in the posterior limb of the internal capsule due to a lack of collateral supply. - AChA anastomoses with posterior choroidal artery and posterior cerebral artery. - Infarction occurs in the posterior limb of the internal capsule due to a lack of collateral supply. - Complete syndrome triad : Contralateral hemiplegia - Weakness or paralysis on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. (Option A) Contralateral hemisensory loss - Loss of sensation on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. Contralateral homonymous hemianopia - Loss of the same visual field (either right or left) in both eyes, opposite to the side of brain damage. (Options C and D) Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Contralateral hemiplegia - Weakness or paralysis on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. (Option A) - Contralateral hemisensory loss - Loss of sensation on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. - Contralateral homonymous hemianopia - Loss of the same visual field (either right or left) in both eyes, opposite to the side of brain damage. (Options C and D) Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. - Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Neuropsychological associations : Left neglect syndrome in right-sided lesions. Language disorders in left-sided lesions. - Left neglect syndrome in right-sided lesions. - Language disorders in left-sided lesions. - AChA anastomoses with posterior choroidal artery and posterior cerebral artery. - Infarction occurs in the posterior limb of the internal capsule due to a lack of collateral supply. - Contralateral hemiplegia - Weakness or paralysis on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. (Option A) - Contralateral hemisensory loss - Loss of sensation on one side of the body opposite to brain injury. - Contralateral homonymous hemianopia - Loss of the same visual field (either right or left) in both eyes, opposite to the side of brain damage. (Options C and D) Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. - Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Homonymous quadruple sectoranopia if the lateral geniculate body is involved. - Incongruent homonymous hemianopia if the optic tract is involved. - Left neglect syndrome in right-sided lesions. - Language disorders in left-sided lesions.","sourceRef":"Question Page 51; Solution Pages 61-62","sourcePage":51,"sourcePageEnd":51},{"id":"anatomy-3-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The labyrinthine artery arises from which of the following arteries?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"External carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vertebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Basilar artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Basilar artery","explanation":"- Labyrinthine artery is a long, slender branch that typically originates from either the basilar artery or the anterior-inferior cerebellar artery . - It travels alongside the vestibulocochlear nerve and passes through the internal auditory meatus to supply blood to the inner ear. It is an end artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 51-52; Solution Pages 62-63","sourcePage":51,"sourcePageEnd":52},{"id":"anatomy-3-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":4,"question":"In the image below, which structure is labeled as the rostrum of the corpus callosum? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Structure A"},{"letter":"B","text":"Structure B"},{"letter":"C","text":"Structure C"},{"letter":"D","text":"Structure D"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Structure D","explanation":"The corpus callosum is the largest commissure of the brain. It is divided from before backwards into the following four parts: - Its fibers curve forward into the front parts of the frontal lobes, forming a fork-like shape known as the forceps minor . - Its fibers connect the parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes of both brain hemispheres. - The fibers connecting the occipital lobes form another fork-like shape known as the forceps major . Function: The corpus callosum is largely responsible for interhemispheric transfer of information which is essential for bilateral responses and learning processes.","sourceRef":"Question Page 52; Solution Pages 63-64","sourcePage":52,"sourcePageEnd":52},{"id":"anatomy-3-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 35 year old patient presents with rapid weight gain and persistent hunger despite adequate food intake. MRI reveals a lesion in the hypothalamus. Which of the following hypothalamic nuclei, when lesioned, is most likely to cause an uncontrolled appetite and extreme obesity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior Hypothalamic Area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventromedial nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral hypothalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Suprachiasmatic Nucleus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ventromedial nucleus","explanation":"Lesions in the ventromedial nucleus of the hypothalamus are known to lead to uncontrolled appetite and extreme obesity, as this area is critical for satiety and regulating food intake. Hypothalamic Nuclei and Effects of Lesions Area/Nuclei Function Lesion Anterior Hypothalamic Area & Influences parasympathetic Can disrupt thermoregulation and Preoptic Area (Option A) responses (e.g., lowers blood lead to hyperthermia. pressure, slows heart rate, heat regulation ) Posterior & Lateral Nuclei Mediates sympathetic responses May cause impaired sympathetic (e.g., increases blood pressure, responses and issues with stress accelerates heart rate, cold response. regulation ). Lateral Hypothalamus (Option C) Acts as the hunger & thirst centre; Leads to anorexia and significant stimulates food intake. weight loss. Medial Hypothalamus- Ventromedial Acts as the satiety centre ; inhibits Causes uncontrolled appetite and nucleus eating. extreme obesity. Supraoptic & Paraventricular Nuclei Secretes vasopressin and oxytocin; Can disrupt water balance and lead regulates water balance and to diabetes insipidus. reproductive functions. Suprachiasmatic Nucleus (Option D) Regulates circadian rhythms and Can disrupt sleep-wake cycles and biological clocks. lead to insomnia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 52-53; Solution Pages 64-65","sourcePage":52,"sourcePageEnd":53},{"id":"anatomy-3-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is the function of the Ventral Posteromedial Nucleus within the Ventral Group Nuclei?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Influences motor activities by connecting with the premotor cortex."},{"letter":"B","text":"Receives sensory information from the cerebellum and red nucleus."},{"letter":"C","text":"Receives trigeminal and gustatory pathways."},{"letter":"D","text":"Projects to the primary motor areas of the cerebral cortex."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Receives trigeminal and gustatory pathways.","explanation":"The Ventral Posteromedial Nucleus receives trigeminal and gustatory pathways which are responsible for processing sensory information from the face. Ventral Group Nuclei - Connects to the reticular formation, substantia nigra, corpus striatum, and premotor cortex - Influences motor cortex activities. (Option A) - Similar connections to the ventral anterior nucleus. - Receives input from the cerebellum and red nucleus (Option B) - Projects to motor and premotor areas. (Option D) - Ventral Posteromedial Nucleus: Receives trigeminal and gustatory pathways. - Ventral Posterolateral Nucleus: Receives sensory tracts; projects to primary somatic sensory areas in the postcentral gyrus (BA 1, 2, 3)","sourceRef":"Question Page 53; Solution Pages 65-66","sourcePage":53,"sourcePageEnd":53},{"id":"anatomy-3-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following thalamic nuclei is concerned with recent memory and emotional tone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial geniculate body"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior Thalamic Nuclei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsomedial Nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral geniculate body"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Anterior Thalamic Nuclei","explanation":"Anterior Thalamic Nuclei are directly connected to the limbic system and play a crucial role in emotional tone and mechanisms of recent memory. Thalamic Nuclei: Anterior part Medial part Lateral part Contains anterior thalamic nuclei. Contains the large dorsomedial Subdivided into dorsal and ventral Receives mammillothalamic tract nucleus and smaller nuclei. Two-way tiers. Dorsal Tier: Includes lateral from mammillary nuclei. Reciprocal connections with prefrontal cortex dorsal nucleus, lateral posterior connections with cingulate gyrus and and hypothalamic nuclei. nucleus, and pulvinar. hypothalamus. Functions related to Interconnected with all other thalamic Interconnections with other thalamic the limbic system. Concerned with nuclei. Responsible for integrating nuclei, parietal lobe, cingulate gyrus, emotional tone and recent memory. It sensory (somatic, visceral, olfactory) occipital, and temporal lobes. Ventral is a part of the Papez circuit information with emotional and Tier: Nuclei arranged in a subjective states. (Option C) craniocaudal sequence Further divided into ventral anterior, ventral posterior and ventral lateral nuclei Other Thalamic Nuclei - Intralaminar Nuclei: Influence consciousness and alertness; receive input from the reticular formation. - Midline Nuclei: Functions unclear; receive afferent fibres from the reticular formation. - Reticular Nucleus: Regulates thalamic activity; connects to other thalamic nuclei. - Medial Geniculate Body: Processes auditory information; projects to the auditory cortex. (Option A) - Lateral Geniculate Body: Processes visual input; projects to the visual cortex. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 53; Solution Pages 66-67","sourcePage":53,"sourcePageEnd":53},{"id":"anatomy-3-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 62 year old patient presents with sudden onset of right-sided weakness and facial droop. Neurological examination reveals upper motor neuron signs with weakness in both the arm and leg on the right side. MRI confirms an infarct in the middle cerebral artery (MCA) territory involving the genu of the internal capsule. Which of the following tracts passes through the genu?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corticobulbar tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Corticospinal tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinothalamic tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medial lemniscus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Corticobulbar tract","explanation":"- The genu of the internal capsule contains the corticobulbar tract (corticonuclear tract), which is responsible for motor control of the cranial nerves, especially facial movements. - Damage here can result in facial weakness, as seen in the patient. Blood Supply of the Internal Capsule: Artery Supplies Tracts Affected in Lesions Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA) The ventral part of the anterior limb Corticopontine tract affecting communication between cortex and cerebellum Heubner's Artery Ventral anterior limb Corticopontine and frontopontine tracts : Impaired voluntary movements. Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA) Parts of the anterior limb Genu Corticobulbar tract (genu) : Facial Posterior limb muscle weakness and motor deficits in cranial nerves Corticospinal tract (posterior limb) : Contralateral limb weakness or paralysis (Option B) Internal Carotid Artery (ICA) - Varies depending on perforator origin, affecting similar tracts as MCA and AChA supplies Anterior Choroidal Artery (AChA) Ventral posterior limb Retro-lenticular Corticospinal tract (posterior limb) : Sub-lenticular portions Contralateral motor deficits Optic radiations (retro-lenticular part) : Visual field defects Auditory radiations (sub lenticular part) : Auditory processing issues Spinothalamic tract (Option C) : - This tract is responsible for transmitting pain and temperature sensations from the body to the brain. - It does not pass through the genu of the internal capsule. Medial lemniscus (Option D) : - This tract carries proprioception and vibration sense from the body to the brain, passing through the brainstem, not the genu of the internal capsule.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 53-54; Solution Pages 67-70","sourcePage":53,"sourcePageEnd":54},{"id":"anatomy-3-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following tracts passes through the genu of the internal capsule?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corticospinal tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Corticonuclear tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Optic radiation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Auditory radiation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Corticonuclear tract","explanation":"The corticonuclear tract, which contains motor fibres to cranial nerve motor nuclei, specifically passes through the genu of the internal capsule. Tracts Passing Through Internal Capsule: - Genu : Corticonuclear tract (motor fibres to cranial nerve motor nuclei). - Corticonuclear tract (motor fibres to cranial nerve motor nuclei). - Posterior Limb : Corticospinal tract (motor fibres to the contralateral spinal cord) (Option A) . Corticopontine fibres from the parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes. Fibres for the upper limb (anteriorly) and trunk/lower limbs (posteriorly). Superior Thalamic Peduncle (through anterior and posterior limbs): From ventral posterior and lateral posterior thalamic nuclei to parietal cortex (sensory areas). - Corticospinal tract (motor fibres to the contralateral spinal cord) (Option A) . - Corticopontine fibres from the parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes. - Fibres for the upper limb (anteriorly) and trunk/lower limbs (posteriorly). - Superior Thalamic Peduncle (through anterior and posterior limbs): From ventral posterior and lateral posterior thalamic nuclei to parietal cortex (sensory areas). - Corticonuclear tract (motor fibres to cranial nerve motor nuclei). - Corticospinal tract (motor fibres to the contralateral spinal cord) (Option A) . - Corticopontine fibres from the parietal, temporal, and occipital lobes. - Fibres for the upper limb (anteriorly) and trunk/lower limbs (posteriorly). - Superior Thalamic Peduncle (through anterior and posterior limbs): From ventral posterior and lateral posterior thalamic nuclei to parietal cortex (sensory areas). Other Tracts Passing Through the Internal Capsule: Anterior limb Retrolentiform part Sublentiform part Frontopontine fibres (Arnold's Parietopontine,occipitopontine, and Temporopontine fibres Auditory bundle) Corticorubral fibres occipitotectal fibres Optic radiation radiation (fibres from the medial (Option C) geniculate body to the auditory cortex) (Option D) Thalamic fibres to the temporal cortex and amygdala.","sourceRef":"Question Page 54; Solution Pages 70-71","sourcePage":54,"sourcePageEnd":54},{"id":"anatomy-3-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which sulcus is involved in separating the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral Sulcus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Central Sulcus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcarine Sulcus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parieto-occipital Sulcus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Central Sulcus","explanation":"- The central sulcus separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe . - It runs roughly from the top of the brain down towards the lateral sulcus. Lobes of the Cerebrum - Frontal Lobe - Temporal Lobe - Parietal Lobe - Occipital Lobe Sulci of the Cerebrum - Lateral Sulcus : Seen laterally; separates the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes. (Option A) - Central Sulcus : Runs Antero-inferiorly; separates the frontal lobe from the parietal lobe; visible laterally and medially. - Calcarine Sulcus : Visible medially; located in the occipital lobe, associated with the visual cortex. (Option C) - Parieto-occipital Sulcus : Seen medially; separates the parietal and occipital lobes. (Option D) Lateral View Medial View Central Sulcus : Divides the frontal lobe (anterior) from Central Sulcus : Separates the frontal motor cortex the parietal lobe (posterior). Lateral Sulcus : Distinguishes (pre-central gyrus) from the parietal sensory cortex. the temporal lobe from the frontal and parietal lobes. Paracentral Lobule : Controls the contralateral lower limb, Pre-central Gyrus : Located in front of the central sulcus; pelvis, and perineum. Calcarine Sulcus : Associated with it contains the frontal motor cortex, which controls skeletal the occipital visual cortex. Limbic Lobe : Involved in muscles. Post-central Gyrus : Located behind the central emotions and memory. Cingulate Gyrus : An arch-shaped sulcus; houses the parietal sensory cortex for general convolution above the corpus callosum. sensations (touch, pain, temperature, proprioception). Insula : Found in the floor of the lateral sulcus. Cerebral Cortex Layers and Functions: Has six layers of neurons. Layer Function I- Molecular layer Receives nonspecific input signals from lower brain centers. Control the excitability of stimulated areas. II- External granular layer Receives nonspecific input and sends axons to the opposite cortex through the corpus callosum. III- External pyramidal layer Sends axons to related cortical areas on the opposite side through the corpus callosum. The outer band of Baillarger is a part of this layer of cerebral cortex , specifically located in the motor cortex and somatosensory cortex . IV- Internal granular layer The primary layer for receiving input sensory signals, which then spread to the surface and deeper layers. V- Internal pyramidal layer Sends large axons to distant nervous system parts like basal ganglia, brain stem, and spinal cord. Controls signal transmission. VI- Fusiform/ polymorphic layer Sends axons to the thalamus. Regulates excitability of incoming sensory signals.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 54-55; Solution Pages 71-73","sourcePage":54,"sourcePageEnd":55},{"id":"anatomy-3-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the anatomical relations of the internal capsule?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The anterior limb lies between the thalamus and the posterior part of the lentiform nucleus."},{"letter":"B","text":"The genu is located posterior to the head of the caudate nucleus."},{"letter":"C","text":"The medial boundary consists of the thalamus and the caudate nucleus."},{"letter":"D","text":"The retro lentiform part is situated above the lentiform nucleus."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The medial boundary consists of the thalamus and the caudate nucleus.","explanation":"The internal capsule is positioned medially next to both the thalamus and the caudate nucleus Internal Capsule - The internal capsule is a compact bundle of projection fibres that connects the cerebral cortex with subcortical centres such as the thalamus, brainstem, and spinal cord. - It contains both afferent (sensory) fibres from the thalamus to the cortex and efferent (motor) fibres from the cortex to the cerebral peduncle and midbrain. Relations of Internal Capsule - Medially : Thalamus and caudate nucleus - Laterally : Lentiform nucleus - Anterior limb : Between the head of the caudate nucleus and the anterior part of the lentiform nucleus (Option A) - Posterior limb : Between the thalamus and the posterior part of the lentiform nucleus. - Genu : Bend between the anterior and posterior limbs, concave laterally (Option B) - Retrolentiform part : Behind the lentiform nucleus (Option D) - Sublentiform part : Below the lentiform nucleus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 55; Solution Pages 73-74","sourcePage":55,"sourcePageEnd":55},{"id":"anatomy-3-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Match the following: **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-Putamen; B- Globus Pallidus; C- Caudate Nucleus; D- Subthalamic Nucleus; E- Substantia Nigra; F- Thalamus"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Caudate Nucleus; B- Putamen; C- Thalamus; D- Globus Pallidus; E- Subthalamic Nucleus; F- Substantia Nigra"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Caudate Nucleus; B- Globus Pallidus; C- Substantia Nigra; D- Globus Pallidus; E- Thalamus; F- Subthalamic Nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Caudate Nucleus; B- Putamen; C- Globus Pallidus; D- Thalamus; E- Subthalamic Nucleus; F- Substantia Nigra"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. A- Caudate Nucleus; B- Putamen; C- Thalamus; D- Globus Pallidus; E- Subthalamic Nucleus; F- Substantia Nigra","explanation":"Subthalamic Nucleus; F- Substantia Nigra - The basal ganglia work together to control movement by balancing excitatory and inhibitory signals. - They help to initiate, plan, and execute movements, as well as to suppress unwanted movements. - Dysfunctions in the basal ganglia can lead to various movement disorders, such as Parkinson's disease, Huntington's disease, and Tourette syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 55-56; Solution Pages 74-75","sourcePage":55,"sourcePageEnd":56},{"id":"anatomy-3-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 70 year old female presents with tremors, rigidity, and bradykinesia. Neurological examination reveals degeneration of dopaminergic neurons in a specific region of the brain. Which component of the basal ganglia is primarily affected in this patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caudate Nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Putamen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Substantia Nigra"},{"letter":"D","text":"Globus Pallidus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Substantia Nigra","explanation":"Substantia Nigra produces dopamine, and its degeneration is primarily associated with Parkinson's disease, leading to the classic symptoms of tremors, rigidity, and bradykinesia. Main Components of the Basal Ganglia: Component Location Role/Function Caudate Nucleus (Option A) Near the thalamus and internal C-shaped structure; contributes to capsule lateral ventricle floor; forms the striatum with the putamen; receives cortical input. Putamen (Option B) Basal forebrain, close to claustrum Part of the corpus striatum; receives input from various cortical areas and is involved in motor control. Globus Pallidus (Option D) Basal ganglia; divided into internal The primary output nucleus; is and external segments involved in movement control pathways (direct and indirect). Subthalamic Nucleus Diencephalon Receives input from external globus pallidus; projects to internal globus pallidus; uses glutamate (excitatory neurotransmitter) as part of the indirect pathway. Lesions in STN lead to hemiballismus. Substantia Nigra Midbrain; divided into pars compacta Produces dopamine (crucial for and pars reticulata smooth, coordinated movements); degeneration of neurons in pars compacta is implicated in Parkinson's disease . Nucleus Accumbens Ventral striatum Involved in reward, motivation, and addiction; linked to the brain's reward circuitry.","sourceRef":"Question Page 56; Solution Pages 75-76","sourcePage":56,"sourcePageEnd":56},{"id":"anatomy-3-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 65 year old male presents with fluent but nonsensical speech and difficulty understanding spoken language. He is unaware of his language difficulties, and his reading and writing abilities are also severely impaired. Which type of aphasia is most likely present in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Global aphasia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Broca's aphasia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Wernicke's aphasia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anomic aphasia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Wernicke's aphasia","explanation":"- Wernicke's aphasia: Fluent speech but impaired comprehension, often with nonsensical language. - Reading and writing are typically affected. - The patient may be unaware of their deficits, which fits this case. Aphasia : - Language impairment affecting communication, caused by brain damage. - It has different forms depending on the location and extent of the damage. Types of Aphasia: Type Description Characteristics Global Aphasia (Option A) Most severe form; few recognizable Common after stroke; may improve if words, little/no comprehension brain damage is limited Broca's Aphasia (Option B) Non-fluent aphasia Severely reduced Good comprehension, poor writing; speech output; can't speak fluently; speech is laborious short utterances (≤ 4 words) Wernicke's Aphasia (Option C) Fluent aphasia Impaired Speech filled with jargon; severe comprehension; fluent but reading/writing issues nonsensical speech Anomic Aphasia (Option D) Difficulty finding correct nouns/verbs. Fluent speech, speech filled with vague words; good comprehension/re ading. Conduction Aphasia Damage to the arcuate fasciculus, a Difficulty repeating sentences, white matter tract connecting the trouble using phonemes correctly, Broca’s and Wernicke’s areas. and impaired ability to name objects. Language production and auditory comprehension are largely intact. Primary Progressive Aphasia Gradual decline in language skills Slow progression in speech, due to neurodegenerative diseases. comprehension, reading, and writing. Mixed Non-fluent Aphasia Similar to severe Broca's but with Sparse, effortful speech; basic limited comprehension reading/writing skills","sourceRef":"Question Page 56; Solution Pages 76-77","sourcePage":56,"sourcePageEnd":56},{"id":"anatomy-3-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Match the Brodmann areas with their respective functions: Brodmann Area Function a) BA 1, 2, 3 1) Primary visual cortex b) BA 4 2) Primary motor cortex c) BA 17 3) Primary somatosensory cortex d) BA 44, 45 4) Language production (Broca’s area) Brodmann Area Function a) BA 1, 2, 3 1) Primary visual cortex b) BA 4 2) Primary motor cortex c) BA 17 3) Primary somatosensory cortex d) BA 44, 45 4) Language production (Broca’s area) **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-1; b-2; c-4; d-3"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-3; b-2; c-4; d-1"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-3; b-2; c-1; d-4"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-2; b-3; c-1; d-4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. a-3; b-2; c-1; d-4","explanation":"Brodmann’s numbers Function Location BA 1, 2, 3 Primary Somatosensory Cortex Postcentral gyrus. Processes tactile information from the body. BA 4 Primary Motor Cortex Controls Precentral gyrus. voluntary movements via corticospinal and cortico-nuclear tracts. BA 8 Prefrontal Cortex Involved in The anterior part of the frontal lobe. planning and decision-making. BA 17 Primary Visual Cortex (V1) Occipital lobe (calcarine sulcus, Processes visual information from cuneus, and lingual gyrus). the lateral geniculate nucleus. BA 22 Language comprehension ( Superior temporal gyrus Wernicke’s area ) BA 41 Primary auditory cortex Superior temporal gyrus BA 42 Secondary auditory cortex Adjacent to BA 41 BA 44 and 45 Broca’s area Speech production. BA Inferior frontal gyrus of the frontal 44 (Opercular part): Syntactic lobe. processing (grammar). BA 45 (Triangular part): Semantic processing (meaning). Left BA 44 is typically larger, reflecting language dominance.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 56-57; Solution Pages 77-78","sourcePage":56,"sourcePageEnd":57},{"id":"anatomy-3-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 68 year old male presents with sudden onset weakness in his right arm and face, along with difficulty speaking. MRI reveals an ischemic stroke. Which artery is most likely involved in this patient’s stroke based on his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle cerebral artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vertebral artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Middle cerebral artery","explanation":"The middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral surface of the brain, including areas responsible for motor control of the arm, face, and language centres (Broca's and Wernicke's areas), which align with the patient's symptoms. Blood Supply to the Cerebrum: Surface Type Major Artery Supplied By Lateral Surface Middle cerebral artery (primary), anterior cerebral artery (superomedial border), posterior cerebral artery (occipital lobe, inferior temporal gyrus) Medial & Inferior Surface Anterior cerebral artery (largest area), middle cerebral artery (temporal pole, orbital surface), posterior cerebral artery (remaining areas) Important considerations: - Watershed areas : Regions of the brain located at the outermost edges of the major cerebral arterial territories are known as watershed areas. These areas are the most vulnerable to insufficient blood supply during cerebral hypoperfusion. - These areas are the most vulnerable to insufficient blood supply during cerebral hypoperfusion. - Macular sparing : When the posterior cerebral artery is occluded, Central vision may be spared due to collateral circulation from the middle cerebral artery. - These areas are the most vulnerable to insufficient blood supply during cerebral hypoperfusion. The anterior cerebral artery (Option A) primarily supplies the medial portions of the frontal lobes, affecting leg motor control, not the arm or face. - It might also present with urinary incontinence, mutism, apathy, or euphoria. The posterior cerebral artery (Option C) supplies the occipital lobe and inferior temporal lobe, which are more associated with visual processing, not motor control or speech. The vertebral artery (Option D) supplies the brainstem and cerebellum, which would cause different symptoms like coordination issues, brainstem stroke syndromes, or vertigo.","sourceRef":"Question Page 57; Solution Pages 78-81","sourcePage":57,"sourcePageEnd":57},{"id":"anatomy-3-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-3","chapter":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topic":"Lesson 3 - Cerebrum and Associated Structures","topicIndex":3,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which statement correctly describes the representation of body parts in the motor and sensory homunculi?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In the motor homunculus, finer motor control areas like arms and legs have larger cortical regions."},{"letter":"B","text":"The sensory homunculus shows body parts with higher acuity, like fingertips, having smaller cortical areas."},{"letter":"C","text":"Both homunculi are in the precentral gyrus and represent the same body parts."},{"letter":"D","text":"The motor homunculus in the precentral gyrus has larger areas for fine motor parts like hands and face."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The motor homunculus in the precentral gyrus has larger areas for fine motor parts like hands and face.","explanation":"motor parts like hands and face. Homunculus: The homunculus maps body parts onto the cerebral cortex, illustrating how different areas of the brain correspond to specific body regions based on motor and sensory functions. - Areas with more intricate sensory or motor functions occupy more space in the brain. Motor Homunculus: - Location : Precentral gyrus, frontal lobe - It is a map (‘motor homunculus’) of the opposite body half, where, The face is represented most laterally and inferiorly, The hand is represented in the central region Leg and foot are represented on the medial surface of the hemisphere - The face is represented most laterally and inferiorly, - The hand is represented in the central region - Leg and foot are represented on the medial surface of the hemisphere - The face is represented most laterally and inferiorly, - The hand is represented in the central region - Leg and foot are represented on the medial surface of the hemisphere Sensory Homunculus: - Location : Postcentral gyrus, parietal lobe. (Option C) - Function : Represents sensory perception, mapping body parts based on sensitivity to touch, pain, and temperature. Body parts with higher sensory acuity, like lips and fingertips, occupy larger areas. (Option B) Visual Representation: - Distorted human figures where body parts are sized according to their brain representation. - For instance, hands and faces are shown disproportionately large to reflect their greater cortical representation. In the motor homunculus, finer motor control areas like arms and legs have larger cortical regions (Option A): Hands and faces occupy a larger area than arms and legs in the motor homunculus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 57-58; Solution Pages 81-82","sourcePage":57,"sourcePageEnd":58},{"id":"anatomy-4-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which cerebellar zone is mainly associated with processing proprioceptive information?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Archicerebellum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Paleocerebellum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Neocerebellum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibulocerebellum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Paleocerebellum","explanation":"- The paleocerebellum is responsible for processing proprioceptive information obtained from the spinal cord. - It plays a vital role in coordinating limb movements and maintaining body posture through feedback from muscle and joint receptors. - Comprises the anterior lobe (excluding the lingula) and parts of the posterior lobe, particularly the pyramid and uvula of the inferior vermis. Archicerebellum (Option A): - This zone is primarily linked to the vestibular system, which focuses on balance and posture rather than proprioceptive input from the body. - The nodules (lobule X) and lingula (lobule I) are part of the archicerebellum. Neocerebellum (Option C): - Its function is integrating sensory information from the cerebral cortex for higher-level motor planning. - Mainly consists of the posterior lobe, particularly the large cerebellar hemispheres, excluding the pyramid and uvula. Vestibulocerebellum (Option D): - This term refers to the archicerebellum, which is more focused on balance and eye movement rather than proprioception","sourceRef":"Question Page 83; Solution Pages 94-95","sourcePage":83,"sourcePageEnd":83},{"id":"anatomy-4-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which pathway involves inhibitory projections originating from the cerebellar nuclei, contributing to the regulation of motor activity and coordination?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rubrospinal tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nucleo-olivary pathway"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventral spinocerebellar tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibulospinal tract"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Nucleo-olivary pathway","explanation":"- The nucleo-olivary pathway sends inhibitory projections from the cerebellar nuclei , particularly regulating motor activity and coordination. - This pathway helps in adjusting cerebellar output to maintain smooth, coordinated movement. Cerebellar Output Excitatory Cerebellar nuclei to Brainstem nuclei (via Glutamate) Inhibitory Purkinjee cells to Cerebellum (via GABA) Cerebellar Nuclei to Inferior Olive: Nucleo-Olivary Pathway (via GABA) Rubrospinal tract (Option A): - The rubrospinal tract transmits signals from the red nucleus to the spinal cord, primarily assisting in controlling limb movements. - While influenced by cerebellar output, it does not carry inhibitory projections from the cerebellar nuclei. Ventral spinocerebellar tract (Option C): - This tract transmits proprioceptive signals to the cerebellum, mainly providing feedback from spinal interneurons. - However, it does not carry inhibitory projections from the cerebellar nuclei. Vestibulospinal tract (Option D): - This pathway sends excitatory signals from the vestibular nuclei to the spinal cord, helping in balance and posture control. - It is not related to the cerebellar inhibitory pathways.","sourceRef":"Question Page 83; Solution Pages 95-97","sourcePage":83,"sourcePageEnd":83},{"id":"anatomy-4-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements about cerebellar circuitry is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Climbing fibres directly excite Purkinje cells by forming extensive synaptic connections."},{"letter":"B","text":"Mossy fibres excite Purkinje cells through granule cells, creating a diffuse excitatory effect."},{"letter":"C","text":"Purkinje cells send excitatory signals to deep cerebellar nuclei."},{"letter":"D","text":"Stellate, basket, and Golgi cells serve as inhibitory interneurons in the cerebellar cortex."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Purkinje cells send excitatory signals to deep cerebellar nuclei.","explanation":"- Purkinje cells send inhibitory signals, not excitatory, to deep cerebellar nuclei. - The cerebellar cortex contains five types of intrinsic neurons: Purkinje cells, granule cells, stellate cells, basket cells, and Golgi cells. - All are inhibitory except for granule cells, making this unique arrangement of inhibitory neurons exclusive to the cerebellum in the central nervous system Cerebellum Circuit - The cerebellar circuitry operates with a balance between excitatory and inhibitory inputs. - Climbing fibres (from olivocerebellar tracts) excite Purkinje cells by forming extensive synapses with their dendrites. One climbing fibre connects with multiple Purkinje cells. (Option A ruled out) - One climbing fibre connects with multiple Purkinje cells. (Option A ruled out) - Mossy fibres (from other cerebellar tracts) excite thousands of Purkinje cells indirectly via granule cells , creating a more diffuse excitatory effect. (Option B ruled out) - The stellate, basket and Golgi cells serve as inhibitory interneurons within the cerebellar cortex, controlling the spread and intensity of excitation in Purkinje cells. (Option D ruled out) - This inhibition refines and modulates the excitatory input from both the climbing and mossy fibres, ensuring precise motor coordination. - Purkinje cells send inhibitory signals to deep cerebellar nuclei (Output centres), which integrate both excitatory inputs from climbing and mossy fibres and inhibitory input from Purkinje cells. - This combination of inputs allows the cerebellum to finely tune motor commands before transmitting them to other regions of the brain and spinal cord. - Glutamate is the primary excitatory neurotransmitter used by climbing and mossy fibres to activate Purkinje cells. - Norepinephrine and serotonin , modulate this excitatory action, influencing the overall effect on the cerebellar cortex. - Finally, the cerebellum communicates with the rest of the central nervous system through three peduncles: Superior cerebellar peduncles link to the midbrain. Middle cerebellar peduncles connect to the pons. Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect to the medulla. - Superior cerebellar peduncles link to the midbrain. - Middle cerebellar peduncles connect to the pons. - Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect to the medulla. - Through these pathways, the cerebellum integrates sensory information and helps coordinate motor output essential for smooth, coordinated movements. - One climbing fibre connects with multiple Purkinje cells. (Option A ruled out) - Superior cerebellar peduncles link to the midbrain. - Middle cerebellar peduncles connect to the pons. - Inferior cerebellar peduncles connect to the medulla.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 83-84; Solution Pages 97-98","sourcePage":83,"sourcePageEnd":84},{"id":"anatomy-4-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 62-year-old male presents to the emergency department with a sudden onset of severe vertigo, bilateral hearing loss, and tinnitus. He also exhibits facial weakness on the right side and demonstrates ataxia when attempting to walk. An MRI performed to evaluate the condition is given below. Which artery is most likely affected in this patient’s condition? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Middle cerebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vertebral artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","explanation":"- The symptoms—severe vertigo, bilateral hearing loss, tinnitus, and facial weakness—are indicative of an AICA stroke. - The MRI image shows a B/L infarct in the anterior cerebellum, which further confirms the involvement of AICA. - The AICA arises from the caudal third of the basilar artery and supplies the inner ear, lateral pons, and the anterior inferior cerebellum. Diffusion-weighted MRI shows B/L infarct in the anterior cerebellum - The anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) generally arises from the caudal third of the basilar artery and supplies the following areas: Inner ear Lateral pons Middle cerebellar peduncle Anterior inferior cerebellum (including the flocculus) - Inner ear - Lateral pons - Middle cerebellar peduncle - Anterior inferior cerebellum (including the flocculus) - Symptoms of AICA stroke: Vertigo (central or peripheral due to the arterial supply) - most common symptom Hearing loss Tinnitus Facial weakness Facial sensory loss Crossed sensory loss Horner syndrome Gait ataxia Limb ataxia - Vertigo (central or peripheral due to the arterial supply) - most common symptom - Hearing loss - Tinnitus - Facial weakness - Facial sensory loss - Crossed sensory loss - Horner syndrome - Gait ataxia - Limb ataxia - Diagnosed by MRI . - Inner ear - Lateral pons - Middle cerebellar peduncle - Anterior inferior cerebellum (including the flocculus) - Vertigo (central or peripheral due to the arterial supply) - most common symptom - Hearing loss - Tinnitus - Facial weakness - Facial sensory loss - Crossed sensory loss - Horner syndrome - Gait ataxia - Limb ataxia Middle cerebral artery (Option A): Supplies the lateral surface of the cerebral hemispheres, not typically associated with vertigo or hearing loss. Superior cerebellar artery (Option B): Supplies the superior part of the cerebellum and may cause ataxia, but not the specific vestibular symptoms seen in this case. Vertebral artery (Option D): Supplies the posterior circulation, including parts of the brainstem, but does not directly cause the combination of symptoms presented here.","sourceRef":"Question Page 84; Solution Pages 98-100","sourcePage":84,"sourcePageEnd":84},{"id":"anatomy-4-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which type of fibres enter the cerebellum via the middle cerebellar peduncle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Olivo-cerebellar fibres"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cerebro-ponto-cerebellar fibres"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinocerebellar fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibulo-cerebellar fibres"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Cerebro-ponto-cerebellar fibres","explanation":"Cerebro-ponto-cerebellar fibres, a part of mossy fibres, originate from the cerebral cortex, synapse in the pons, and then project to the cerebellum via the middle cerebellar peduncle , playing a key role in coordinating movement and processing sensory information. - They have excitatory terminals on granule cells , which excite Purkinje cells. - Climbing fibres, on the other hand, enter via the inferior cerebellar peduncle and target Purkinje cells directly. Afferent fibres Tract Enter Cerebellum Via Target and Function Climbing fibres Olivo-cerebellar (Option A Inferior cerebellar Excitatory terminals on ruled out) peduncle (decussates) Purkinje cells (glutamate) Mossy fibres Cerebro-ponto- cerebellar Middle cerebellar peduncle Excitatory terminals on (Option B) (decussates) granule cells (glutamate), which are excitatory to Purkinje cells Spinocerebellar (Option C Inferior cerebellar ruled out) peduncle Superior cerebellar peduncle Vestibulo-cerebellar Inferior cerebellar peduncle (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 84-85; Solution Pages 100-101","sourcePage":84,"sourcePageEnd":85},{"id":"anatomy-4-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following structures receives direct projections from the dentate nucleus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Premotor cortex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"The anterior nucleus of the thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parietal cortex"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus","explanation":"- The ventrolateral nucleus of the thalamus is the primary structure receiving direct projections from the dentate nucleus via the superior cerebellar peduncle. - This pathway is crucial for motor control and coordination. Premotor cortex (Option A): - The premotor cortex is involved in planning and organising movements but does not receive direct projections from the dentate nucleus. - Information reaches the premotor cortex indirectly through the thalamus. The anterior nucleus of the thalamus (Option C): - The anterior nucleus of the thalamus primarily relays information related to limbic functions and is not a target for direct projections from the dentate nucleus. Parietal cortex (Option D): - The parietal cortex is involved in sensory processing and higher cognitive functions. - While it receives input from the thalamus, it does not receive direct projections from the dentate nucleus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 85; Solution Pages 101-102","sourcePage":85,"sourcePageEnd":85},{"id":"anatomy-4-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":7,"question":"The fastigial nucleus primarily projects to which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Red nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vestibular nuclei"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cerebral cortex"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Vestibular nuclei","explanation":"The vestibular nuclei receive major projections from the fastigial nucleus , playing a key role in maintaining balance, regulating posture, and coordinating head and eye movements via the vestibulospinal tracts. Connections of Cerebellum Part of cerebellum Cerebellar Nuclei Centre Neocerebellum Dentate Red Nucleus and Thalamus (Option B ruled out) Paleocerebellum Globose and Emobliform Red Nucleus (Option A ruled out) Archicerebellum Fastigial Vestibular Nuclei (Option C) Cerebral cortex (Option D): - The cerebral cortex is influenced indirectly by cerebellar nuclei, especially via the thalamus. - However, the fastigial nucleus does not send direct projections to the cortex.","sourceRef":"Question Page 85; Solution Pages 102-104","sourcePage":85,"sourcePageEnd":85},{"id":"anatomy-4-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following intrinsic neurons are correctly matched with their respective layers in the cerebellar cortex? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1- Plexiform layers; Golgi cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"2- Molecular layer; Purkinje cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"2- Granular layer; Basket cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"3- Granular layer; Golgi cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 3- Granular layer; Golgi cells","explanation":"- Golgi cells are found in the granular layer of the cerebellar cortex. - They are inhibitory interneurons that play a role in modulating the activity of granule cells. The cerebellar cortex consists of the following three distinct layers: Layers of the cerebellar cortex Molecular (plexiform) layer (Option C Purkinje cell layer (Option B ruled out) Granular layer (Option A ruled out) ruled out) It primarily contains numerous It consists of a single row of large The inner granular layer is made up dendritic branches from Purkinje flask-shaped cells, the Purkinje cells . of many closely packed small cells, with relatively few widely granule cells , along with a few larger spaced nerve cells. Golgi cells. These nerve cells are of two types: The axons of Purkinje cells are the Golgi cells are inhibitory while the (a) Basket cells (b) Stellate cells. only output from the cerebellar cortex granular cells are excitatory. and have an inhibitory effect on the intracerebellar nuclei. Among the five types of intrinsic neurons (Basket cells, Stellate cells, Purkinje cells, Granule cells and Golgi cells)- All are inhibitory except granule cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 85-86; Solution Page 104","sourcePage":85,"sourcePageEnd":86},{"id":"anatomy-4-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following deep cerebellar nuclei is primarily involved in the smooth execution of skilled voluntary movements?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fastigial nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Emboliform nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Globose nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dentate nucleus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Dentate nucleus","explanation":"- The deep cerebellar nuclei are clusters of grey matter situated within the white matter of the cerebellum. - These nuclei are critical hubs for processing and relaying information related to motor control and coordination. Deep Cerebellar nuclei Dentate Nucleus (Option D) Emboliform Nucleus Globose Nucleus Fastigial Nucleus Largest and most lateral. Oval-shaped, medial to the Rounded, positioned Most medial, near the Receives input from the dentate nucleus. The between the emboliform midline. Receives input neocerebellum. Involved in emboliform nucleus and fastigial nuclei. Also from the archicerebellum , coordinating skilled, combined with the globose part of the paleocerebellum. specifically the voluntary movements, nucleus is called Nucleus Works together with the flocculonodular lobe. especially of the limbs. Interposus. Receives input emboliform nucleus for Important for balance, Efferent fibres exit through from the paleocerebellum. motor control. (Option C posture, and coordinating the superior cerebellar Plays a role in general ruled out) head and eye movements. peduncle . motor coordination. (Option A ruled out) (Option B ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 86; Solution Pages 104-106","sourcePage":86,"sourcePageEnd":86},{"id":"anatomy-4-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":10,"question":"What is the significance of the second decussation of the ventral spinocerebellar tract within the cerebellum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enables contralateral proprioceptive processing"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ensures ipsilateral proprioceptive termination"},{"letter":"C","text":"Blocks incorrect proprioceptive signals"},{"letter":"D","text":"Causes all fibres to terminate bilaterally"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ensures ipsilateral proprioceptive termination","explanation":"The second crossing within the cerebellum ensures that the proprioceptive signals from one side of the body ultimately terminate on the same side of the cerebellum , aiding in precise coordination of movement. Ventral Spinocerebellar Tract Pathway Begins in the lumbar and sacral spinal cord (intermediate grey matter and dorsal horn base). ↓ Fibers cross the midline in the spinal cord via the ventral white commissure. The tract ascends through the spinal cord and brainstem, running near the spinothalamic tract. Enters the cerebellum through the superior cerebellar peduncle at the level of the midbrain. Fibres recross within the cerebellum’s white matter. The majority of fibres end in the anterior cerebellar vermis, with a smaller portion terminating bilaterally. Enables contralateral proprioceptive processing (Option A): The second decussation does not lead to contralateral processing but instead causes the proprioceptive signals to be processed on the same side (ipsilateral) as their origin. Blocks incorrect proprioceptive signals (Option C): The second decussation does not prevent signal transmission but ensures that signals from the body are processed correctly by the cerebellum after crossing back to the original side. Causes all fibres to terminate bilaterally (Option D): Only about 15% of fibres may terminate bilaterally, the main role of the second decussation is not to cause bilateral termination but to direct most fibres to terminate ipsilaterally.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 86-87; Solution Pages 106-107","sourcePage":86,"sourcePageEnd":87},{"id":"anatomy-4-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":11,"question":"From which anatomical structure does the dorsal spinocerebellar tract originate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior horn of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cerebellar cortex"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medulla oblongata"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior thoracic nucleus (Clarke's column)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Posterior thoracic nucleus (Clarke's column)","explanation":"- The dorsal spinocerebellar tract originates from neurons located in the Posterior thoracic nucleus (Clarke's column) nucleus, situated in the thoracic and upper lumbar regions of the spinal cord. - It enters the cerebellum through the inferior cerebellar peduncle and terminates in the ipsilateral cerebellar cortex. - These neurons process input from proprioceptive receptors, such as muscle spindles and Golgi tendon organs. It carries unconscious proprioception. Tracts of the spinal cord Anterior horn of the spinal cord (Option A): - This region mainly contains motor neurons responsible for transmitting signals to skeletal muscles. - It does not give rise to the dorsal spinocerebellar tract, which is primarily involved in sensory processing. Cerebellar cortex (Option B): - This area is the termination for the dorsal spinocerebellar tract fibres, where proprioceptive information is integrated for coordination and balance, rather than being the site of origin for the tract itself. Medulla oblongata (Option C): - This structure is part of the brainstem and plays a role in autonomic functions and motor control. - However, it does not serve as the origin point for the dorsal spinocerebellar tract.","sourceRef":"Question Page 87; Solution Pages 107-108","sourcePage":87,"sourcePageEnd":87},{"id":"anatomy-4-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which cerebellar peduncle is primarily composed of only afferent fibres?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior cerebellar peduncle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle cerebellar peduncle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar peduncle"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Middle cerebellar peduncle","explanation":"- The middle cerebellar peduncle is composed exclusively of afferent fibres originating from the contralateral pontine nuclei. - It plays a crucial role in relaying information from various areas of the cerebral cortex to the cerebellum, facilitating the coordination of movement and motor planning. Cerebellar Peduncles Afferent Efferent Superior cerebellar peduncle Ventral Spinocerebellar Tract All efferent fibres from dentate, emboliform, fastigial and globose nuclei (Option C ruled out) Middle cerebellar peduncle Pontocerebellar Mossy Fibre Pathway No efferent fibres (Option A) Inferior cerebellar peduncle Dorsal Spinocerebellar Tract Few efferents from the cerebellum to Olivocerebellar Tract the medulla oblongata, mainly to Reticulocerebellar Tract vestibular nuclei and reticular formation. (Option A ruled out) All of the above (Option D): - Incorrect, as the only peduncle that consists solely of afferent fibres is the middle cerebellar peduncle. - The others include both afferent and efferent fibres.","sourceRef":"Question Page 87; Solution Pages 108-110","sourcePage":87,"sourcePageEnd":87},{"id":"anatomy-4-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which cerebellar lobe is associated with maintaining balance and controlling eye movements?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flocculonodular Lobe"},{"letter":"B","text":"The anterior lobe"},{"letter":"C","text":"The posterior lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Neocerebellum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Flocculonodular Lobe","explanation":"- The Flocculonodular Lobe, also known as the vestibulocerebellum, has direct and strong connections to the vestibular system, which governs balance, posture, and eye movements. - The flocculonodular lobe helps coordinate head, neck, and eye movements based on vestibular input, which is crucial for maintaining equilibrium. Cerebellum The anterior lobe (Option B): - Found on the superior surface of the cerebellum, this lobe plays a key role in motor coordination, through its connections to the spinocerebellar tracts that transmit sensory information from the spinal cord. The posterior lobe (Option C) is involved in motor planning and higher cognitive functions. - Its connections with association areas of the cerebral cortex, such as the prefrontal and parietal regions, allow it to contribute to the coordination of complex movements rather than balance. Neocerebellum (Option D): - Referring primarily to the posterior lobe, the neocerebellum is involved in regulating complex movements and processing higher cognitive functions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 87-88; Solution Pages 110-111","sourcePage":87,"sourcePageEnd":88},{"id":"anatomy-4-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Identify the correct option.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"6- Trigeminal nerve; 7- Trochlear nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"5- Abducens nerve; 3- Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"1- Hypoglossal nerve; 2- Facial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"4- Vestibulocochlear nerve; 8- Optic Nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 5- Abducens nerve; 3- Vagus nerve","explanation":"Attachment of the nerves to the brain: - 1, 2 - Forebrain - 3- Arises from ventral midbrain - 4- Dorsal midbrain (cannot see its origin in the Anterior view of the brainstem) - 5- Lateral pons - 6, 7, 8- Ponto medullary junction (from medial to lateral respectively) - 9, 10, 11- Lateral to olives (from superior to inferior respectively) - 12- Between pyramid and olives","sourceRef":"Question Page 88; Solution Pages 111-112","sourcePage":88,"sourcePageEnd":88},{"id":"anatomy-4-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Match the following neural columns with their corresponding cranial nerve nuclei. 1. General Somatic Efferent (GSE) A) Nucleus ambiguus 2. Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) B) Edinger-Westphal nucleus 3. General Visceral Efferent (GVE) C) Oculomotor nucleus 4. Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) D) Nucleus of the solitary tract 1. General Somatic Efferent (GSE) A) Nucleus ambiguus 2. Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) B) Edinger-Westphal nucleus 3. General Visceral Efferent (GVE) C) Oculomotor nucleus 4. Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) D) Nucleus of the solitary tract **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-A, 2-B, C-3, 4-D"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-D, 2-C, 3-B, 4-A"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-B, 2-A, 3-D, 4-C"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 1-C, 2-D, 3-B, 4-A","explanation":"1. General Somatic Efferent (GSE) The GSE column C) Oculomotor nucleus In the brainstem, it is represented controls muscles derived from somites, specifically the by nuclei like the oculomotor nucleus , which innervates striated muscles. the extraocular muscles, facilitating eye movement. 2. Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) The SVA column is D) Nucleus of the solitary tract In the brainstem, the responsible for processing special sensory information nucleus of the solitary tract receives taste signals from the related to taste. tongue, transmitted by cranial nerves like the facial and glossopharyngeal nerves. 3. General Visceral Efferent (GVE) The GVE column B) Edinger-Westphal nucleus The Edinger-Westphal regulates autonomic functions, particularly nucleus in the midbrain is part of this system, controlling parasympathetic innervation. pupil constriction and lens accommodation. 4. Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) The SVE column A) Nucleus ambiguus The nucleus ambiguus provides innervates muscles derived from the branchial arches, motor innervation to muscles involved in these actions, responsible for functions like swallowing and speech. via cranial nerves like the vagus and glossopharyngeal nerves.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 88-89; Solution Pages 112-113","sourcePage":88,"sourcePageEnd":89},{"id":"anatomy-4-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following correctly arranges the brainstem nuclei from medial to lateral in the brainstem?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oculomotor nucleus → Trochlear nucleus → Edinger Westphal nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoglossal nucleus → Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus → Nucleus tractus solitarius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Motor nuclei of Trigeminal → Motor nuclei of Facial → Abducent nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Nucleus ambiguus → Hypoglossal nucleus → Nucleus tractus solitarius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Hypoglossal nucleus → Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus → Nucleus tractus solitarius","explanation":"The structures passing from medial to lateral in the central grey matter is Hypoglossal nucleus → Dorsal motor nucleus of the vagus → Nucleus tractus solitarius Oculomotor nucleus → Trochlear nucleus → Edinger Westphal nucleus (Option A): Edinger Westphal lies dorsal to the oculomotor nucleus and not lateral. Motor nuclei of Trigeminal → Motor nuclei of Facial → Abducent nucleus (Option C): The motor nucleus of the facial nerve lies ventrolateral to the abducent nucleus. Nucleus ambiguus → Hypoglossal nucleus → Nucleus tractus solitarius (Option D): Hypoglossal nuclei are medial to Nucleus Ambiguus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 89; Solution Pages 113-114","sourcePage":89,"sourcePageEnd":89},{"id":"anatomy-4-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which nuclei belong to the Special Somatic Afferent (SSA) column?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cochlear and vestibular nuclei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sensory nucleus of the trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoglossal and abducens nuclei"},{"letter":"D","text":"Nucleus ambiguus and facial nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Cochlear and vestibular nuclei","explanation":"Cochlear and vestibular nuclei are part of the SSA column, carrying special senses of hearing and balance. Neural column Nuclei and Cranial Nerves GSE (General SVE (Special GVE (General GVA/SVA (Gene GSA (General SSA (Special Somatic Efferent) Visceral Efferent) Visceral Efferent) ral/Special Somatic Afferent) Somatic Afferent) (Option C ruled (Option D ruled Visceral Efferent) (Option B ruled (Option A) out) out) out) Oculomotor Motor nucleus of Edinger Westpha Nucleus Tractus Sensory nuclei Vestibular nuclei nucleus (III) Trigeminal l nucleus (III) Solitarius (GVA - of Trigeminal (VIII) Cochlear Trochlear Nerve (V) Motor Superior salivato IX, X) (SVA - Nerve (V, IX, X) nuclei (VIII) The nucleus (IV) nucleus of the ry nucleus (VII) VII, IX) lateral geniculate Abducent nucleu facial nerve (VII) Inferior salivatory nucleus of the s (VI) Hypogloss Nucleus ambiguu nucleus (IX) thalamus (II). al nucleus (XII) s (IX, X, XI) Dorsal nucleus Olfactory bulb (I) of Vagus (X) Mnemonic: Rule of 12: Any nucleus that is inclusive of 12 is medial. It includes nuclei III, IV, VI and XII. The rest are lateral. Rule of M: 3 primary medial tracts of the Brainstem. Includes Motor Tract (Corticospinal Tract), Medial Lemniscus and Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus. Rule of S: Lateral or “side” tracts. Includes Spinothalamic tract, Sympathetic tract and spinocerebellar tracts. B- Branchial Motor, CST-Corticospinal, STT- Spinothalamic, ML- Medial Lemniscus, Sm- Somatic motor, Sp-Special sensory, Ss- Somatic sensory Section through medulla showing arrangement of cranial nerve nuclei","sourceRef":"Question Page 89; Solution Pages 114-116","sourcePage":89,"sourcePageEnd":89},{"id":"anatomy-4-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which artery is most commonly occluded, leading to medial medullary syndrome and lateral pontine syndrome, respectively?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior cerebral artery, PICA"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior cerebral artery, Basilar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior spinal artery, AICA"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vertebral artery, superior cerebellar artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior spinal artery, AICA","explanation":"- Anterior Spinal Artery: Its occlusion leads to medial medullary syndrome, resulting in contralateral hemiplegia, loss of vibration and position sense, and tongue paralysis on the same side. - AICA (Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery): Occlusion leads to lateral pontine syndrome, causing symptoms like facial paralysis, vertigo, nystagmus, and ataxia. Medial Medullary Syndrome (Dejerine Syndrome) - Cause: Occlusion of the anterior spinal artery - Features: Contralateral Hemiplegia: Due to damage to the medullary pyramid. Loss of Vibration and Position Sense: Resulting from damage to the medial lemniscus. Tongue Paralysis (Same Side): Caused by injury to the hypoglossal (XII) cranial nerve. - Contralateral Hemiplegia: Due to damage to the medullary pyramid. - Loss of Vibration and Position Sense: Resulting from damage to the medial lemniscus. - Tongue Paralysis (Same Side): Caused by injury to the hypoglossal (XII) cranial nerve. - Contralateral Hemiplegia: Due to damage to the medullary pyramid. - Loss of Vibration and Position Sense: Resulting from damage to the medial lemniscus. - Tongue Paralysis (Same Side): Caused by injury to the hypoglossal (XII) cranial nerve. Lateral Inferior Pontine Syndrome - Cause: Occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA) - On Side of Lesion: Nystagmus, Vertigo, Nausea, Vomiting, Oscillopsia: Due to vestibular nerve/nucleus involvement. Facial Paralysis Impaired Conjugate Gaze: Involvement of the center for lateral gaze. Deafness and Tinnitus: Related to the auditory nerve or cochlear nucleus. Ataxia: Affecting the middle cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar hemisphere. Impaired Sensation Over Face: Due to involvement of the descending tract and nucleus of the fifth nerve. - Nystagmus, Vertigo, Nausea, Vomiting, Oscillopsia: Due to vestibular nerve/nucleus involvement. - Facial Paralysis - Impaired Conjugate Gaze: Involvement of the center for lateral gaze. - Deafness and Tinnitus: Related to the auditory nerve or cochlear nucleus. - Ataxia: Affecting the middle cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar hemisphere. - Impaired Sensation Over Face: Due to involvement of the descending tract and nucleus of the fifth nerve. - On Side Opposite Lesion: Impaired Pain and Thermal Sensation: Involving the spinothalamic tract over one-half of the body, which may include the face. - Impaired Pain and Thermal Sensation: Involving the spinothalamic tract over one-half of the body, which may include the face. - Nystagmus, Vertigo, Nausea, Vomiting, Oscillopsia: Due to vestibular nerve/nucleus involvement. - Facial Paralysis - Impaired Conjugate Gaze: Involvement of the center for lateral gaze. - Deafness and Tinnitus: Related to the auditory nerve or cochlear nucleus. - Ataxia: Affecting the middle cerebellar peduncle and cerebellar hemisphere. - Impaired Sensation Over Face: Due to involvement of the descending tract and nucleus of the fifth nerve. - Impaired Pain and Thermal Sensation: Involving the spinothalamic tract over one-half of the body, which may include the face. Anterior cerebral artery, PICA (Option A): - Anterior Cerebral Artery: Occlusion affects the medial frontal and parietal lobes, causing contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss, but does not cause the medial medullary syndrome. - PICA: Occlusion leads to lateral medullary syndrome (Wallenberg syndrome), not lateral pontine syndrome. Posterior cerebral artery, Basilar artery (Option B): - Posterior Cerebral Artery: Occlusion affects the occipital lobe, causing visual field deficits and sensory disturbances, but not medial medullary syndrome. - Basilar Artery: Occlusion leads to various brainstem syndromes, but is not specifically linked to lateral pontine syndrome. Vertebral artery, Superior cerebellar artery (Option D): - Vertebral Artery: Occlusion affects posterior circulation, causing various syndromes, but not medial medullary syndrome. - Superior Cerebellar Artery: Occlusion primarily impacts the cerebellum and brainstem, but does not cause lateral pontine syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 89-90; Solution Pages 116-118","sourcePage":89,"sourcePageEnd":90},{"id":"anatomy-4-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which arteries supply the posterior region of the superior medulla and pons respectively?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery and Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basilar Artery and Vertebral Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior Spinal Artery and Posterior Spinal Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior Cerebellar Artery and Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery and Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery","explanation":"Blood supply of Medulla Oblongata: - Caudally Anterior-Medial Supply: Anterior Spinal Artery (Option C ruled out) Posterior-Lateral Supply: Posterior Spinal Artery - Anterior-Medial Supply: Anterior Spinal Artery (Option C ruled out) - Posterior-Lateral Supply: Posterior Spinal Artery - Superiorly Lateral Supply: Vertebral Artery (Option B ruled out) Anterior Supply: Basilar Artery branches Posterior Supply: Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA) - Lateral Supply: Vertebral Artery (Option B ruled out) - Anterior Supply: Basilar Artery branches - Posterior Supply: Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA) - Anterior-Medial Supply: Anterior Spinal Artery (Option C ruled out) - Posterior-Lateral Supply: Posterior Spinal Artery - Lateral Supply: Vertebral Artery (Option B ruled out) - Anterior Supply: Basilar Artery branches - Posterior Supply: Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA) Blood supply of Pons: - Main Supply: Pontine branches of the Basilar Artery - Posterior-Lateral Supply: Branches of the Superior Cerebellar Artery (Option D ruled out) and the Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (AICA)","sourceRef":"Question Page 90; Solution Pages 118-120","sourcePage":90,"sourcePageEnd":90},{"id":"anatomy-4-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which artery supplies the posterior region of the midbrain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Basilar Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior Choroidal Arteries"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior Cerebral Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior Cerebellar Artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Superior Cerebellar Artery","explanation":"Superior Cerebellar Artery provides significant blood supply to the posterior region of the midbrain Midbrain Blood Supply: - Anterior-Medial Supply: branches of the Basilar artery (Option A ruled out) - Anterior-Lateral Supply: branches of the Posterior Choroidal arteries and Quadrigeminal artery from the Posterior Cerebral Artery (Option B & C ruled out) - Posterior Supply: Quadrigeminal Artery and Superior Cerebellar Artery","sourceRef":"Question Page 90; Solution Page 120","sourcePage":90,"sourcePageEnd":90},{"id":"anatomy-4-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":21,"question":"The baroreceptor reflex that helps regulate blood pressure involves general visceral afferent (GVA) fibres from which of the following receptors and pathways?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Carotid body via the glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aortic bodies via the vagus nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Carotid sinus via the vagus nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulmonary arteries via the glossopharyngeal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Aortic bodies via the vagus nerve","explanation":"The aortic bodies detect changes in blood pressure and relay sensory input through GVA fibres of the vagus nerve, contributing to the baroreceptor reflex. Baroreceptor Reflex: - Baroreceptors in the aortic arch and carotid sinus monitor blood pressure changes. - GVA fibres from the aortic arch transmit signals via the vagus nerve (X), and from the carotid sinus via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). - The brainstem's nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) receives signals from GVA fibres. - GVE fibres originating from the vagus nerve (X) from the dorsal motor nucleus mediate parasympathetic responses. - They adjust heart rate by influencing the sinoatrial (SA) node, AV node, myocardium and other cardiac structures, helping to lower blood pressure when necessary. Carotid body via the glossopharyngeal nerve (Option A): Carotid body detects gases, not pressure; the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) relates to baroreceptors in the carotid sinus. Carotid sinus via the vagus nerve (Option C): While the carotid sinus does detect blood pressure changes, it transmits signals via the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), not the vagus nerve. Pulmonary arteries via the glossopharyngeal nerve (Option D): Pulmonary arteries have baroreceptors, but they are transmitted via the vagus nerve (CN X), not the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), which mainly serves the carotid body and sinus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 90-91; Solution Pages 120-122","sourcePage":90,"sourcePageEnd":91},{"id":"anatomy-4-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":22,"question":"The nucleus of the solitary tract receives ______ sensations from the carotid body and carotid sinus primarily through _____ nerve.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"General Somatic Efferent; Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Special Visceral Afferent; Facial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"General Visceral Afferent; Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Special Somatic Efferent; Trigeminal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. General Visceral Afferent; Glossopharyngeal nerve","explanation":"- The nucleus of the solitary tract receives general visceral afferent sensations from the carotid body and carotid sinus primarily through the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). - The glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX) carries sensory information from the carotid body (chemoreceptor) and carotid sinus (baroreceptor) to the nucleus of the solitary tract, which is involved in regulating blood pressure and respiratory responses. Nucleus of Tractus Solitarius - It is a composite nucleus located in the medulla. Its functions are: - General Visceral Afferent (Lower Part): Receives visceral sensations. Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): From tonsil, pharynx, posterior tongue, carotid body, and carotid sinus. Vagus nerve (X): From the pharynx, larynx, trachea, oesophagus, and thoracic/abdominal viscera. (Option A ruled out) - Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): From tonsil, pharynx, posterior tongue, carotid body, and carotid sinus. - Vagus nerve (X): From the pharynx, larynx, trachea, oesophagus, and thoracic/abdominal viscera. (Option A ruled out) - Special Visceral Afferent (Upper Part): Receives taste sensations. Facial nerve (VII): Anterior two-thirds of tongue and palate ( except circumvallate papillae ). (Option B ruled out) The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): Posterior one-third of the tongue, including circumvallate papillae. Vagus nerve (X): Posterior tongue and epiglottis. - Facial nerve (VII): Anterior two-thirds of tongue and palate ( except circumvallate papillae ). (Option B ruled out) - The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): Posterior one-third of the tongue, including circumvallate papillae. - Vagus nerve (X): Posterior tongue and epiglottis. - Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): From tonsil, pharynx, posterior tongue, carotid body, and carotid sinus. - Vagus nerve (X): From the pharynx, larynx, trachea, oesophagus, and thoracic/abdominal viscera. (Option A ruled out) - Facial nerve (VII): Anterior two-thirds of tongue and palate ( except circumvallate papillae ). (Option B ruled out) - The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX): Posterior one-third of the tongue, including circumvallate papillae. - Vagus nerve (X): Posterior tongue and epiglottis. Special somatic efferent; Trigeminal nerve (Option D): Special somatic efferent fibers are related to the motor innervation of certain muscles, while the trigeminal nerve (CN V) primarily carries sensory information (touch, pain) from the face and does not innervate the carotid body or sinus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 91; Solution Pages 122-124","sourcePage":91,"sourcePageEnd":91},{"id":"anatomy-4-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following statements about the jaw jerk reflex is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a spinal reflex initiated by tapping the jaw."},{"letter":"B","text":"It involves the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus as an integration centre."},{"letter":"C","text":"The efferent pathway includes the mandibular nerve."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is a monosynaptic reflex involving a single synapse."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. It is a spinal reflex initiated by tapping the jaw.","explanation":"The jaw jerk reflex is a deep tendon reflex with supraspinal involvement which is initiated by tapping the jaw. Masseter reflex or Jaw jerk reflex It is a deep tendon reflex. - The reflex is initiated by tapping the midline of the mandible, which rapidly stretches the jaw-closing muscles (masseter, temporalis, medial pterygoid). - The stretch activates muscle spindle afferents, which are sensory receptors in the muscles. - Afferent pathway: Muscle spindle afferents → Mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve. - Integration centre (Relay): Mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus. (Option B ruled out) - Efferent pathway: Motor nucleus of the trigeminal nerve → Mandibular nerve. (Option C ruled out) - This results in the contraction of these jaw-closing muscles and finally closure of the jaw. - The jaw jerk reflex is the only significant supraspinal monosynaptic reflex due to its relay in the mesencephalic trigeminal nucleus. (Option D ruled out) - The jaw jerk reflex can be tested in neurological examinations to assess the integrity of the trigeminal nerve and the brainstem pathways.","sourceRef":"Question Page 91; Solution Pages 124-125","sourcePage":91,"sourcePageEnd":91},{"id":"anatomy-4-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which of the following nuclei of the trigeminal nerve is primarily responsible for proprioceptive sensations from the muscles of mastication?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Main Sensory Nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spinal Nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesencephalic Nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Motor Nucleus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Mesencephalic Nucleus","explanation":"Mesencephalic Nucleus is located around the cerebral aqueduct and carries proprioceptive impulses from the muscles of mastication, facial, and extraocular muscles. The trigeminal nerve has four nuclei as follows: - Located in the posterior part of the pons, lateral to the motor nucleus. - Continuous below with the spinal nucleus. - Extends from the pons to the second cervical segment of the spinal cord. - Receives sensations of pain and temperature. (Option B ruled out) - Located in the lateral part of the grey matter around the cerebral aqueduct. - Receives proprioceptive impulses from muscles of mastication, facial, and extraocular muscles. - Located in the pons, medial to the main sensory nucleus. - Controls muscles of mastication, tensor tympani, tensor veli palatini, mylohyoid, and anterior belly of digastric. (Option D ruled out) Sensory component of Trigeminal Nuclei The motor component of Trigeminal Nuclei Carries pain, temperature, touch, pressure, and The motor nucleus receives inputs from both cerebral proprioception from the face. Main sensory nucleus - hemispheres, the reticular formation, and the red nucleus touch and pressure. (Option A ruled out) Spinal nucleus - and forms a reflex arc with the mesencephalic nucleus. pain and temperature. It extends till C2 of the spinal cord. Supplies muscles of mastication- Temporalis Masseter Mesencephalic nucleus - proprioception. Lateral pterygoid Medial pterygoid Other muscles like Tensor tympani Tensor veli palatini Mylohyoid Anterior belly of digastric","sourceRef":"Question Pages 91-92; Solution Pages 125-126","sourcePage":91,"sourcePageEnd":92},{"id":"anatomy-4-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding damage to the midline brainstem nuclei is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Damage to the oculomotor nucleus can result in the eye looking laterally and ptosis."},{"letter":"B","text":"Injury to the trochlear nucleus leads to difficulty in upward gaze and may cause a compensatory head tilt to the same side."},{"letter":"C","text":"Damage to the abducens nucleus results in medial strabismus, causing the affected eye to turn inward."},{"letter":"D","text":"Lesion of the hypoglossal nucleus leads to weakness and atrophy of the tongue on the affected side."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Injury to the trochlear nucleus leads to difficulty in upward gaze and may cause a compensatory head tilt to the same side.","explanation":"cause a compensatory head tilt to the same side. Damage to the trochlear nucleus causes difficulty in downward and lateral gaze, often resulting in a compensatory head tilt to the opposite side. Lesions to Medial Brainstem Nuclei- Together form the somatic motor column (GSE) - Oculomotor Nucleus (CN III) : (Option A ruled out) The eye cannot move upward, downward, or inward. At rest, the eye is in a down-and-out position. The patient experiences double vision (diplopia). The upper eyelid droops (ptosis) from paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris. The pupil is widely dilated and nonreactive to light due to paralysis of the sphincter pupillae and unopposed sympathetic dilator activity. Accommodation is impaired. - The eye cannot move upward, downward, or inward. - At rest, the eye is in a down-and-out position. - The patient experiences double vision (diplopia). - The upper eyelid droops (ptosis) from paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris. - The pupil is widely dilated and nonreactive to light due to paralysis of the sphincter pupillae and unopposed sympathetic dilator activity. - Accommodation is impaired. - Trochlear Nucleus (CN IV) : Difficulty in downward and lateral gaze. Patients may exhibit a compensatory head tilt to the opposite side to manage diplopia (double vision). - Difficulty in downward and lateral gaze. - Patients may exhibit a compensatory head tilt to the opposite side to manage diplopia (double vision). - Abducens Nucleus (CN VI) : Medial strabismus (the affected eye cannot abduct), leading to horizontal diplopia. The affected eye is often turned inward. Diplopia is also seen. (Option C ruled out) - Medial strabismus (the affected eye cannot abduct), leading to horizontal diplopia. - The affected eye is often turned inward. Diplopia is also seen. (Option C ruled out) - Hypoglossal Nucleus (CN XII) : Weakness of the tongue on the side of the lesion, causing atrophy and fasciculations. On protrusion, the tongue may deviate toward the affected side. (Option D ruled out) - Weakness of the tongue on the side of the lesion, causing atrophy and fasciculations. - On protrusion, the tongue may deviate toward the affected side. (Option D ruled out) - The eye cannot move upward, downward, or inward. - At rest, the eye is in a down-and-out position. - The patient experiences double vision (diplopia). - The upper eyelid droops (ptosis) from paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris. - The pupil is widely dilated and nonreactive to light due to paralysis of the sphincter pupillae and unopposed sympathetic dilator activity. - Accommodation is impaired. - Difficulty in downward and lateral gaze. - Patients may exhibit a compensatory head tilt to the opposite side to manage diplopia (double vision). - Medial strabismus (the affected eye cannot abduct), leading to horizontal diplopia. - The affected eye is often turned inward. Diplopia is also seen. (Option C ruled out) - Weakness of the tongue on the side of the lesion, causing atrophy and fasciculations. - On protrusion, the tongue may deviate toward the affected side. (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 92; Solution Pages 126-128","sourcePage":92,"sourcePageEnd":92},{"id":"anatomy-4-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-4","chapter":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topic":"Lesson 4 - Brainstem, Cranial Nerves, and Cerebellum","topicIndex":4,"questionNumber":26,"question":"A 65 y/o patient presents with sudden dizziness, difficulty swallowing, ipsilateral loss of temperature sensation on the face, contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the trunk, ipsilateral ataxia, and giddiness. Which anatomical structure is responsible for the ipsilateral loss of temperature sensation on the face of this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinothalamic tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spinal nucleus and tract of the trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior cerebellar peduncle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibular nuclei"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Spinal nucleus and tract of the trigeminal nerve","explanation":"The patient is diagnosed with Wallenberg Syndrome due to the presence of ipsilateral loss of temperature sensation on the face, contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the trunk, difficulty swallowing, ipsilateral ataxia, and giddiness. The spinal nucleus and tract of the trigeminal nerve are responsible for the ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation over the face, which is a hallmark feature of Wallenberg Syndrome. Wallenberg Syndrome (Lateral Medullary Syndrome) - Due to stroke or infarction in the lateral part of the medulla oblongata. - Mainly due to occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA), a branch of the vertebral artery. Sometimes the vertebral artery directly supplies the lateral brainstem. - Affects the dorsolateral aspect of the medulla and the inferior surface of the cerebellum. Anatomical structure affected and features:","sourceRef":"Question Page 92; Solution Pages 128-129","sourcePage":92,"sourcePageEnd":92},{"id":"anatomy-5-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect regarding Weber syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lesion is in the oculomotor nerve nucleus and corticospinal fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hemiplegia is present on the opposite side of the lesion"},{"letter":"C","text":"There is a lateral squint on the same side of the lesion"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. None of the above","explanation":"All of the above options are correct about Weber syndrome. Weber syndrome: Lesion in the ventromedial portion of the midbrain involving the oculomotor nerve (Cranial nerve III) and corticospinal fibres. Clinical manifestation: - Hemiplegia on the opposite side due to involvement of corticospinal fibres - The pupil points downwards and laterally due to paralysis of the oculomotor nerve (Cranial nerve III)","sourceRef":"Question Page 130; Solution Page 141","sourcePage":130,"sourcePageEnd":130},{"id":"anatomy-5-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The PNS relays impulses in neurons away from the viscera."},{"letter":"B","text":"The sympathetic nervous system has thoracolumbar outflow."},{"letter":"C","text":"Erection is controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system."},{"letter":"D","text":"Tearing is regulated by the parasympathetic nervous system."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The PNS relays impulses in neurons away from the viscera.","explanation":"In the parasympathetic system, the relay of impulses happens in neurons that are close to or directly within the target organs (viscera), not away from them. Feature Parasympathetic Nervous System Sympathetic Nervous System Outflow Craniosacral outflow Thoracolumbar outflow (Option B ruled out) Preganglionic Neuron Located in the midbrain, pons, Located in the lateral horn of the medulla oblongata, and S2–S4 spinal cord from Th 1–12 and L 1–2 segments of the spinal cord segments Preganglionic Fibers Longer, myelinated Shorter, myelinated Relay of Impulses Occurs in neurons close to viscera Occurs in neurons a little away from the viscera they supply Postganglionic Fibers Short, nonmyelinated Long, nonmyelinated Neurotransmitter at Preganglionic Acetylcholine Acetylcholine Endings Neurotransmitter at Postganglionic Acetylcholine Adrenaline (except sweat glands, Endings where acetylcholine is used) Postganglionic Supply Only viscera are supplied, do not All viscera are supplied; the supply blood vessels of skeletal exception is the suprarenal gland, muscles which is supplied by preganglionic fibers Heart rate Decreased Increased Blood pressure Mildly decreased Increased Urinary bladder Voiding (decreased tone) Increased sphincter tone Bowel motility Increased Decreased motility Lungs Bronchoconstriction Bronchodilation Sweat glands - Sweating Pupils Constriction Dilation Sexual functions Erection (Option C ruled out) Ejaculation, orgasm Lacrimal glands Tearing (Option D ruled out) -","sourceRef":"Question Page 130; Solution Pages 141-143","sourcePage":130,"sourcePageEnd":130},{"id":"anatomy-5-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Where does the hypothalamospinal tract terminate before synapsing with the second-order neurons?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior cervical ganglion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior cervical ganglion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior horn of the spinal cord"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Intermediolateral cell column of the spinal cord","explanation":"The hypothalamospinal tract terminates in the intermediolateral cell column (IML) of the spinal cord, where it synapses with the second-order sympathetic neurons.","sourceRef":"Question Page 130; Solution Page 143","sourcePage":130,"sourcePageEnd":130},{"id":"anatomy-5-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":4,"question":"All of the following options are branches of the internal carotid artery except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ophthalmic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior choroidal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior communicating artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior inferior cerebellar artery","explanation":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery is a branch of the basilar artery. The internal carotid artery is divided into three parts based on its course. - Petrous part - Cavernous part - Intracranial part Branches of the intracranial part of internal carotid artery: Branches Areas supplied Ophthalmic artery (Option A ruled out) Contents of orbit Posterior communicating artery (Option C ruled out) Crus cerebri, optic tract, hypophysis and hypothalamus Anterior choroidal artery (Option B ruled out) Crus cerebri and choroid plexus of inferior horn of lateral ventricle Anterior cerebral artery Deep branches supply part of the internal capsule and the basal nuclei. Cortical branches supply the medial surface of the hemisphere. Middle cerebral artery Deep or perforating branches supply the anterior limb of the internal capsule and part of the basal nuclei. Cortical branches supply temporal, frontal and parietal lobe. - Branches in the petrous part are caroticotympanic artery and pterygoid artery - Branches in the cavernous part are meningohypophyseal trunk and the inferolateral trunk","sourceRef":"Question Pages 130-131; Solution Pages 143-145","sourcePage":130,"sourcePageEnd":131},{"id":"anatomy-5-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A transverse section of the medulla oblongata at the level of the motor (pyramidal) decussation reveals modifications to the typical spinal cord structure. Which of the following changes is observed at this level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Formation of the medial lemniscus from second-order axons"},{"letter":"B","text":"Crossing of corticospinal fibres from the anterior funiculi"},{"letter":"C","text":"Presence of the vestibular nuclei near the fourth ventricle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Appearance of the inferior olivary complex lateral to the pyramid"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Crossing of corticospinal fibres from the anterior funiculi","explanation":"At the level of the motor decussation, corticospinal fibres cross from the anterior funiculi to the contralateral side, a hallmark of this level. Internal Organization of the Medulla Oblongata 1. Transverse Section at Motor (Pyramidal) Decussation Level - The inferior medulla adapts to accommodate brainstem nuclei and fibres. - Corticospinal fibres cross at the motor decussation, decreasing the pyramid size. (Option B) - Ventral grey column divides into: Supraspinal nucleus (first cervical nerve origin, continuous with the hypoglossal nerve nucleus). Nucleus of the accessory nerve (aligned with the nucleus ambiguus). - Supraspinal nucleus (first cervical nerve origin, continuous with the hypoglossal nerve nucleus). - Nucleus of the accessory nerve (aligned with the nucleus ambiguus). - Surrounding tracts : Ventral corticospinal tract (medioventral). Ventral and dorsal spinocerebellar tracts (dorsolateral). - Ventral corticospinal tract (medioventral). - Ventral and dorsal spinocerebellar tracts (dorsolateral). - Dorsal grey column modified by the presence of the nucleus gracilis and cuneatus, continuous with spinal structures. - Supraspinal nucleus (first cervical nerve origin, continuous with the hypoglossal nerve nucleus). - Nucleus of the accessory nerve (aligned with the nucleus ambiguus). - Ventral corticospinal tract (medioventral). - Ventral and dorsal spinocerebellar tracts (dorsolateral). 2. Transverse Section at Sensory Decussation (Dorsal Column–Medial Lemniscus System) - Nuclei and tracts present : Nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus, receiving first-order afferent fibres. Second-order axons (internal arcuate fibres) cross the midline as sensory decussation to form the medial lemniscus. (Option A ruled out) - Nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus, receiving first-order afferent fibres. - Second-order axons (internal arcuate fibres) cross the midline as sensory decussation to form the medial lemniscus. (Option A ruled out) - Medial lemniscus : Located dorsal to the pyramid. Inferior olivary complex appears lateral to it. (Option D ruled out) - Located dorsal to the pyramid. - Inferior olivary complex appears lateral to it. (Option D ruled out) - Other key structures : Dorsal spinocerebellar and trigeminal nerve tracts, reticular formation, corticospinal tract, medial longitudinal fasciculus, and tectospinal tract. - Dorsal spinocerebellar and trigeminal nerve tracts, reticular formation, corticospinal tract, medial longitudinal fasciculus, and tectospinal tract. - Nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus, receiving first-order afferent fibres. - Second-order axons (internal arcuate fibres) cross the midline as sensory decussation to form the medial lemniscus. (Option A ruled out) - Located dorsal to the pyramid. - Inferior olivary complex appears lateral to it. (Option D ruled out) - Dorsal spinocerebellar and trigeminal nerve tracts, reticular formation, corticospinal tract, medial longitudinal fasciculus, and tectospinal tract. 3. Transverse Section at Caudal End of Fourth Ventricle - Grey matter increases due to nuclei associated with cranial nerves IX, X, XI, and VIII. - Key nuclei : Inferior olivary complex. Hypoglossal nucleus, vagus dorsal motor nucleus, salivatory nuclei, nucleus solitarius, and vestibular nuclei. (Option C ruled out) - Inferior olivary complex. - Hypoglossal nucleus, vagus dorsal motor nucleus, salivatory nuclei, nucleus solitarius, and vestibular nuclei. (Option C ruled out) - Restiform body forms the dorsal lateral border, carrying afferent fibers (spinocerebellar and olivocerebellar tracts). - Other structures : Spinal trigeminal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, reticular formation. Spinocerebellar, spinothalamic, and rubrospinal tracts ventral to the olivary complex and pyramid. - Spinal trigeminal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, reticular formation. - Spinocerebellar, spinothalamic, and rubrospinal tracts ventral to the olivary complex and pyramid. - Inferior olivary complex. - Hypoglossal nucleus, vagus dorsal motor nucleus, salivatory nuclei, nucleus solitarius, and vestibular nuclei. (Option C ruled out) - Spinal trigeminal nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, reticular formation. - Spinocerebellar, spinothalamic, and rubrospinal tracts ventral to the olivary complex and pyramid.","sourceRef":"Question Page 131; Solution Pages 145-148","sourcePage":131,"sourcePageEnd":131},{"id":"anatomy-5-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is the correct match of brainstem components with the respective cranial nerve nuclei that originate from them? Column A Column B a. Midbrain 1. Trigeminal nerve b. Pons 2. Oculomotor nerve c. Medulla oblongata 3. Glossopharyngeal nerve d. Pontomedullary junction 4. Facial nerve Column A Column B a. Midbrain 1. Trigeminal nerve b. Pons 2. Oculomotor nerve c. Medulla oblongata 3. Glossopharyngeal nerve d. Pontomedullary junction 4. Facial nerve","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-1, b-2, c-4, d-3"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-3, b-4, c-1, d-2"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4","explanation":"Components of brainstem Cranial nerves nuclei Midbrain Oculomotor (III) and trochlear (IV) Pons Trigeminal (V) Medulla oblongata Glossopharyngeal (IX), vagus (X), cranial accessory (XIcr) and hypoglossal (XII) Pontomedullary junction Abducens (VI), facial (VII) and vestibulocochlear (VIII)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 131-132; Solution Page 148","sourcePage":131,"sourcePageEnd":132},{"id":"anatomy-5-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following findings is not a clinical manifestation of Wallenberg syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Contralateral ataxia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Horner's syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss"},{"letter":"D","text":"Contralateral body pain and temperature loss"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Contralateral ataxia","explanation":"Ipsilateral ataxia is seen in Wallenburg sybdrome due to damage to inferior cerebellar peduncle, spino cerebellar tracts, and part of cerebellum. Wallenburg sybdrome: - Also known as Lateral medullary syndrome Pathology: - Blockage of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery. - It supplies areas behind the inferior olivary nucleus. - Damage to the lateral segment of the medulla posterior to the inferior olivary nucleus. Clinical manifestation: Anatomical location Presenting clinical feature Nucleus ambiguous gives fibers to IX, X, and XI cranial Ipsilateral paralysis of muscles of the soft palate, pharynx, nerves and larynx Spinal nucleus and spinal tract of the trigeminal nerve Ipsilateral facial pain and temperature loss (Option C ruled out) Lateral spinothalamic tract Contralateral body pain and temperature loss (Option D ruled out) Vestibular nuclei Vertigo Inferior cerebellar peduncle, spino cerebellar tracts, and Ipsilateral ataxia (Option A) part of cerebellum Sympathetic fibres descend from the hypothalamus to Horner’s syndrome (Option B ruled out) , comprising cells in the lateral horn of spinal cord ptosis, enophthalmos, miosis, and anhydrosis","sourceRef":"Question Page 132; Solution Pages 148-149","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":132},{"id":"anatomy-5-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following nerve palsy would suggest a lesion affecting the structures within the interpeduncular fossa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oculomotor nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Oculomotor nerve","explanation":"A lesion affecting the interpeduncular fossa could damage the oculomotor nerve (CN III), which emerges from this region, leading to diplopia and ptosis. Interpeduncular fossa - It is a deep depression of the ventral surface of the midbrain between the two crura cerebri. - The lateral wall of the interpeduncular fossa bears the oculomotor sulcus, from which rootlets of the oculomotor nerve emerge from the brainstem and aggregate into a single fascicle. (Option C) Contents - Oculomotor nerve (Option C) - Circle of Willis - Bifurcation of the basilar artery - Peduncular segments of the posterior cerebral artery (PCA) and superior cerebellar artery, (SCA) - Medial and lateral posterior choroidal arteries - Basal vein of Rosenthal Glossopharyngeal nerve (Option A): and vagus nerve (Option B): The rootlets of the cranial nerves IX (Glossopharyngeal) and X (vagus) emerge through the posterolateral fissure, behind the olive. Hypoglossal nerve (Option D): The rootlets of the hypoglossal nerve emerge from the anterolateral sulcus between the pyramid and the olive.","sourceRef":"Question Page 132; Solution Pages 149-151","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":132},{"id":"anatomy-5-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which among the following options is correctly matched for the alphabet labeled in the below image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Pyramid"},{"letter":"B","text":"B- Trochlear nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"C- Basilar sulcus"},{"letter":"D","text":"D- Olive"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. D- Olive","explanation":"- Image in the question represents the ventral surface of the brainstem. - Alphabet D labelled in the image is olive, a pair of prominent oval structures on either side of the medullary pyramids in the medulla. Ventral surface (Frontal view) of the brainstem: - Alphabet C is Pyramid - Alphabet A is the basilar sulcus of pons - Alphabet B is occulomotor nerve","sourceRef":"Question Pages 132-133; Solution Page 151","sourcePage":132,"sourcePageEnd":133},{"id":"anatomy-5-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with a severe infection of the face. Which of the following venous structures could allow the infection to spread to the cavernous sinus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior petrosal sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior ophthalmic vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior petrosal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Basilar plexus of veins"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Inferior ophthalmic vein","explanation":"The inferior ophthalmic vein connects the facial veins to the cavernous sinus, making it a route for the spread of infections from the face. Cavernous sinus: It is two large venous spaces situated in the middle cranial fossa, on either side of the body of the sphenoid bone. Important structures related to Cavernous Sinus: Tributaries or Incoming Channels - From the orbit: Superior ophthalmic vein Inferior ophthalmic vein- connects facial vein to cavernous sinus (Option B) Central vein of the retina- drains either into the superior ophthalmic vein or directly into the cavernous sinus. - Superior ophthalmic vein - Inferior ophthalmic vein- connects facial vein to cavernous sinus (Option B) - Central vein of the retina- drains either into the superior ophthalmic vein or directly into the cavernous sinus. - From the brain: Superficial middle cerebral vein Inferior cerebral veins from the temporal lobe - Superficial middle cerebral vein - Inferior cerebral veins from the temporal lobe - From the meninges Sphenoparietal sinus Frontal trunk of the middle meningeal- drains into the pterygoid plexus or the sphenoparietal or cavernous sinus - Sphenoparietal sinus - Frontal trunk of the middle meningeal- drains into the pterygoid plexus or the sphenoparietal or cavernous sinus - Superior ophthalmic vein - Inferior ophthalmic vein- connects facial vein to cavernous sinus (Option B) - Central vein of the retina- drains either into the superior ophthalmic vein or directly into the cavernous sinus. - Superficial middle cerebral vein - Inferior cerebral veins from the temporal lobe - Sphenoparietal sinus - Frontal trunk of the middle meningeal- drains into the pterygoid plexus or the sphenoparietal or cavernous sinus Draining Channels or Communications - Into the transverse sinus through the superior petrosal sinus. (Option A ruled out) - Into the internal jugular vein through the inferior petrosal sinus. (Option C ruled out) - Into the pterygoid plexus of veins through the emissary veins. - Into the facial vein through the superior ophthalmic vein. - The right and left cavernous sinuses communicate with each other through the anterior and posterior intercavernous sinuses and the basilar plexus of veins. (Option D ruled out) - Note: All these communications are valveless, and blood flows in both directions. Note : The superior ophthalmic vein has bidirectional flow, whereas the inferior ophthalmic vein has unidirectional blood flow. Dangerous areas of face: (Upper lip and lower part of the nose) - The facial vein communicates with the cavernous sinus through the pterygoid venous plexus, superior ophthalmic vein, and inferior ophthalmic vein. - Infections from the face spread in a retrograde direction and cause thrombosis of the cavernous sinus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 133; Solution Pages 151-154","sourcePage":133,"sourcePageEnd":133},{"id":"anatomy-5-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with symptoms of headache and visual disturbances. Imaging studies reveal a large clot in the superior sagittal sinus. Which of the following statements about the superior sagittal sinus is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It drains directly into the internal jugular vein."},{"letter":"B","text":"It runs along the midline, attached to the falx cerebri, and drains into the confluence of sinuses."},{"letter":"C","text":"It is located in the falx cerebelli and connects with the occipital sinus."},{"letter":"D","text":"It receives blood from the diploic veins within the cranial bones."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. It runs along the midline, attached to the falx cerebri, and drains into the confluence of sinuses.","explanation":"confluence of sinuses. The superior sagittal sinus runs along the midline, is attached to the falx cerebri, and typically drains into the confluence of sinuses. Dural Venous Sinuses: Anatomical Features and Location: - Network of venous channels: Drain blood from the brain and cranial bones. - Location: Between endosteal and meningeal layers of the dura mater. - Structure: Lined by endothelium, lacks valves and muscular tissue. - Development: Arise from venous plexuses, often retain plexiform structure. Sinuses and Connections: Sinus Location Drainage Superior sagittal sinus (Option B) Along midline, attached to falx Confluence of sinuses, usually right cerebri (Option C ruled out) side (Option A ruled out) Inferior sagittal sinus Lower edge of falx cerebri Continues as straight sinus Straight sinus Junction of falx cerebri and tentorium Drains into left transverse sinus cerebelli Transverse sinuses Along tentorium cerebelli Continue as sigmoid sinuses Sigmoid sinuses From transverse sinuses Becomes internal jugular vein Occipital sinus In falx cerebelli Drains into confluence of sinuses Cavernous sinuses Plexiform, on either side of sphenoid Receives blood from ophthalmic bone veins, drains via superior and inferior petrosal sinuses Intercavernous sinuses Connects the two cavernous sinuses - Superior petrosal sinuses Drains cavernous sinus into - transverse sinus Inferior petrosal sinuses Drains cavernous sinus into internal - jugular vein Sphenoparietal sinuses Under lesser wings of sphenoid bone Drains into cavernous sinus Basilar sinus and plexus Over clivus, connects cavernous and - petrosal sinuses Marginal sinus Encircles foramen magnum Connects with basilar plexus and occipital sinus Tributaries: - Cerebral veins: Drain brain parenchyma. - Diploic veins: Within cranial bones. (Option D ruled out- The superior sagittal sinus does not receive blood from diploic veins; these veins are tributaries to other sinuses. ) - Meningeal veins: Drain dura mater. - Emissary veins: Connect intracranial and extracranial veins. Clinical Significance: - Spread of infection: Emissary veins can facilitate infection spread from extracranial sites to sinuses. - Venous sinus thrombosis: Blood clots can obstruct drainage, leading to complications such as venous infarction and hemorrhage. - Surgical considerations: Knowledge of sinus anatomy is crucial for avoiding injury and hemorrhage during neurosurgery. Additional Insights: - Variability: Sinus size and connections can vary, especially in infancy. - Imaging techniques: Angiography, MR venography, and CT venography are vital for visualizing sinuses and variations. - Innervation: Cranial dura mater is mainly innervated by trigeminal nerve and upper cervical nerves, explaining headache symptoms from dura stimulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 133-134; Solution Pages 154-157","sourcePage":133,"sourcePageEnd":134},{"id":"anatomy-5-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 56-year-old patient presents with difficulty speaking, swallowing, and hoarseness of voice. On examination, there is noticeable wasting of one side of the tongue and weakness in the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles. The patient also exhibits contralateral hemiplegia and contralateral loss of sensation. Which of the following lesions is most likely responsible for these symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Multiple sclerosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meningioma of the cerebellum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medullary infarct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibular schwannoma"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Medullary infarct","explanation":"- Medullary infarct is the most likely cause as it affects the medulla oblongata, leading to symptoms like bulbar palsy (difficulty speaking and swallowing), tongue wasting, muscle weakness, contralateral hemiplegia , and loss of sensation. - These are consistent with the involvement of both ascending and descending tracts through the medulla. Lower Brainstem Lesions Lower brainstem lesions affect the medulla oblongata and can lead to various neurological symptoms. These are often seen as bulbar palsy . - Dysarthria: Difficulty speaking due to impaired control of tongue, lips, and vocal cords. - Dysphagia (CN IX): Difficulty swallowing caused by weakened throat muscles. - Dysphonia: Voice problems, such as hoarseness. - Tongue Wasting (CN XII): Shrinkage and weakness of one side of the tongue, leading to deviation. - Muscle Weakness/Wasting: Affects sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles (CN XI) , impacting head turning and shoulder elevation. - Contralateral Hemiplegia: Paralysis or weakness on the opposite side of the body due to corticospinal tract involvement. - Contralateral Hemianesthesia: Loss of sensation on the opposite side of the body from anterolateral system or dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway involvement. - Cerebellar Signs: Coordination and balance difficulties from cerebellar afferent or efferent pathway damage. - Nystagmus: Involuntary eye movements indicating vestibular pathway disruptions. - Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia: Impaired horizontal eye movements due to medial longitudinal fasciculus lesions. - Space-Occupying Tumours: Examples include meningiomas, vestibular schwannomas, or metastatic carcinomas. - Intrinsic Diseases: Gliomas: Tumours from glial cells. Demyelination: Such as in multiple sclerosis. Stroke: Resulting from blood flow interruption. - Gliomas: Tumours from glial cells. - Demyelination: Such as in multiple sclerosis. - Stroke: Resulting from blood flow interruption. - Gliomas: Tumours from glial cells. - Demyelination: Such as in multiple sclerosis. - Stroke: Resulting from blood flow interruption. Multiple sclerosis (Option A) primarily causes demyelination and could present with various neurological symptoms but is less likely to produce isolated medullary symptoms with the specific combination of deficits described. Meningioma of the cerebellum (Option B) would more likely cause cerebellar symptoms rather than a specific combination of bulbar palsy and contralateral deficits. Vestibular schwannoma (Option D) typically presents with hearing loss and balance issues rather than the described bulbar symptoms and contralateral deficits.","sourceRef":"Question Page 134; Solution Pages 157-158","sourcePage":134,"sourcePageEnd":134},{"id":"anatomy-5-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the corticonuclear tract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It terminates exclusively in the spinal cord with an ipsilateral projection."},{"letter":"B","text":"It descends through the internal capsule and pons, terminating on cranial nerve nuclei with a contralateral projection."},{"letter":"C","text":"It controls involuntary movements of the head and neck muscles."},{"letter":"D","text":"The term \"corticobulbar\" is more accurate than \"corticonuclear\" for describing its function."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. It descends through the internal capsule and pons, terminating on cranial nerve nuclei with a contralateral projection.","explanation":"nerve nuclei with a contralateral projection. - The corticonuclear tract fibres descend from the cerebral cortex through the internal capsule and pons. - They then terminate on cranial nerve nuclei in the brainstem. - The fibres exhibit a contralateral projection pattern, meaning they cross the midline of the brainstem to innervate the nuclei on the opposite side of the brainstem. Corticonuclear Tract: - Origin and Pathway: Arises from neurons in the cerebral cortex Descends through: (Option B) Subcortical white matter Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Arises from neurons in the cerebral cortex - Descends through: (Option B) Subcortical white matter Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Subcortical white matter - Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule - Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) - Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Termination: (Option A ruled out) Unlike corticospinal fibres, terminates on cranial nerve nuclei and brainstem nuclei: (Option B) Midbrain Pons Medulla oblongata In the medulla, projects to: Nucleus ambiguus Hypoglossal nucleus Exhibits a contralateral projection pattern (crosses midline to opposite brainstem side) - Unlike corticospinal fibres, terminates on cranial nerve nuclei and brainstem nuclei: (Option B) Midbrain Pons Medulla oblongata - Midbrain - Pons - Medulla oblongata - In the medulla, projects to: Nucleus ambiguus Hypoglossal nucleus - Nucleus ambiguus - Hypoglossal nucleus - Exhibits a contralateral projection pattern (crosses midline to opposite brainstem side) - Function: Controls voluntary movements (Option C ruled out) of head and neck muscles, including: Facial expression Chewing Swallowing Tongue movement Speech - Controls voluntary movements (Option C ruled out) of head and neck muscles, including: Facial expression Chewing Swallowing Tongue movement Speech - Facial expression - Chewing - Swallowing - Tongue movement - Speech - Terminology: (Option D ruled out) \"Bulbar\" (from \"bulb,\" an outdated term for medulla oblongata) is less accurate than \"corticonuclear\" \"Corticonuclear\" is preferred as it covers a broader range of brainstem nuclei beyond the medulla - \"Bulbar\" (from \"bulb,\" an outdated term for medulla oblongata) is less accurate than \" corticonuclear\" - \"Corticonuclear\" is preferred as it covers a broader range of brainstem nuclei beyond the medulla - Arises from neurons in the cerebral cortex - Descends through: (Option B) Subcortical white matter Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Subcortical white matter - Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule - Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) - Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Subcortical white matter - Genu and posterior limb of the internal capsule - Ventral midbrain (crus cerebri) - Pons (fragmented into fascicles by pontocerebellar fibers) - Unlike corticospinal fibres, terminates on cranial nerve nuclei and brainstem nuclei: (Option B) Midbrain Pons Medulla oblongata - Midbrain - Pons - Medulla oblongata - In the medulla, projects to: Nucleus ambiguus Hypoglossal nucleus - Nucleus ambiguus - Hypoglossal nucleus - Exhibits a contralateral projection pattern (crosses midline to opposite brainstem side) - Midbrain - Pons - Medulla oblongata - Nucleus ambiguus - Hypoglossal nucleus - Controls voluntary movements (Option C ruled out) of head and neck muscles, including: Facial expression Chewing Swallowing Tongue movement Speech - Facial expression - Chewing - Swallowing - Tongue movement - Speech - Facial expression - Chewing - Swallowing - Tongue movement - Speech - \"Bulbar\" (from \"bulb,\" an outdated term for medulla oblongata) is less accurate than \" corticonuclear\" - \"Corticonuclear\" is preferred as it covers a broader range of brainstem nuclei beyond the medulla","sourceRef":"Question Pages 134-135; Solution Pages 158-161","sourcePage":134,"sourcePageEnd":135},{"id":"anatomy-5-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":14,"question":"At the level of the facial colliculus, the fibres of which cranial nerves form a loop around the abducens nucleus, creating the colliculus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Facial nerve","explanation":"The fibres of the facial nerve curve dorsally around the abducens nucleus, forming the facial colliculus. Internal Organization of the Pons Section Dorsal Aspect Ventral Aspect Facial Colliculus Level Medial longitudinal fasciculus: Medial lemniscus: ovoid bundle in beneath fourth ventricle, lateral to ventral pontine tegmentum median sulcus Abducens nucleus: sends fibres Trapezoid body fibers and nuclei ventro-laterally and caudally cross midline along medial lemniscus Facial nerve nucleus: ventral, lateral, Central tegmental tract: longitudinally caudal to abducens nucleus through mid-pontine tegmentum Facial nerve fibres curve dorsally and Pontine nuclei: transverse laterally over abducens nucleus pontocerebellar fibres form (facial colliculus) (Option C) contralateral middle cerebellar peduncle Inferior cerebellar peduncle Descending fibres (frontopontine, (restiform and juxtarestiform bodies): corticospinal, parieto-temporo-occipit superolateral to vestibular nuclei o-pontine): longitudinally through basal pons Trigeminal Nerve Nuclei Level Superior cerebellar peduncle: lateral Medial and lateral lemnisci: convex wall of fourth ventricle lemniscal arch, separating tegmentum from basal pons Superior medullary velum: roof of Trapezoid body fibres and nuclei: fourth ventricle curve across midline in ventral tegmentum Superior fovea: lateral margin of fourth ventricle, overlies trigeminal nerve nuclei Mesencephalic tract of trigeminal nerve: dorsolateral tegmentum Glossopharyngeal nerve (Option A) : This nerve is not involved at the level of the facial colliculus. Trigeminal nerve (Option B) : The trigeminal nerve nuclei are located higher in the mid-pons, not at the level of the facial colliculus. Hypoglossal nerve (Option D) : This nerve is located in the medulla, not the pons, and does not contribute to the formation of the facial colliculus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 135; Solution Pages 161-163","sourcePage":135,"sourcePageEnd":135},{"id":"anatomy-5-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following conditions is associated with lesions at the level marked in the midbrain? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Weber’s syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Benedikt’s syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parinaud’s syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. All of the above","explanation":"The superior colliculus is marked in the above image and all the given syndromes are associated with lesions at this level. Transverse section at level of the superior colliculus in midbrain Grey Matter - Oculomotor nerve nucleus with Edinger Westphal nucleus in the ventromedial part. - The mesencephalic nucleus of the trigeminal nerve in the lateral part. - Superior colliculus: Afferents from the retina (visual), and various other centres. Efferents to the spinal cord (tectospinal tract). Reflex movements of the eyes, and the head and neck in response to visual stimuli. - Afferents from the retina (visual), and various other centres. - Efferents to the spinal cord (tectospinal tract). - Reflex movements of the eyes, and the head and neck in response to visual stimuli. - Pretectal nucleus: Location: Deep to the superolateral part of the superior colliculus. Afferents from the lateral roots of the optic tract. Efferents to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei of both sides. Part of the pathway for the light reflex and the consensual reflex. It causes an Argyll Robertson pupil in which the light reflex is lost but the accommodation reflex remains intact. - Location: Deep to the superolateral part of the superior colliculus. - Afferents from the lateral roots of the optic tract. - Efferents to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei of both sides. - Part of the pathway for the light reflex and the consensual reflex. - It causes an Argyll Robertson pupil in which the light reflex is lost but the accommodation reflex remains intact. - Red nucleus: Afferents from the superior cerebellar peduncle, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus and cerebral cortex. Efferents to the spinal cord (rubrospinal tract), reticular formation, thalamus, olivary nucleus, subthalamic nucleus. Plays a role in inhibitory influence on muscle tone. - Afferents from the superior cerebellar peduncle, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus and cerebral cortex. - Efferents to the spinal cord (rubrospinal tract), reticular formation, thalamus, olivary nucleus, subthalamic nucleus. - Plays a role in inhibitory influence on muscle tone. - Substantia nigra - Afferents from the retina (visual), and various other centres. - Efferents to the spinal cord (tectospinal tract). - Reflex movements of the eyes, and the head and neck in response to visual stimuli. - Location: Deep to the superolateral part of the superior colliculus. - Afferents from the lateral roots of the optic tract. - Efferents to the Edinger-Westphal nuclei of both sides. - Part of the pathway for the light reflex and the consensual reflex. - It causes an Argyll Robertson pupil in which the light reflex is lost but the accommodation reflex remains intact. - Afferents from the superior cerebellar peduncle, globus pallidus, subthalamic nucleus and cerebral cortex. - Efferents to the spinal cord (rubrospinal tract), reticular formation, thalamus, olivary nucleus, subthalamic nucleus. - Plays a role in inhibitory influence on muscle tone. White Matter - Crus cerebri - Tegmentum - Tectum shows the posterior commissure connecting the two superior colliculi. Syndromes associated with lesions at the level of superior colliculus: Weber’s syndrome: - Involves III nerve nucleus and corticospinal fibres. - Features: Hemiplegia on the opposite side due to the involvement of corticospinal fibres. Pupil points downwards and laterally due to paralysis of the III nerve. - Hemiplegia on the opposite side due to the involvement of corticospinal fibres. - Pupil points downwards and laterally due to paralysis of the III nerve. - Hemiplegia on the opposite side due to the involvement of corticospinal fibres. - Pupil points downwards and laterally due to paralysis of the III nerve. Benedikt’s syndrome: - Most of the tegmentum of the midbrain is damaged. - Involves medial lemniscus, red nucleus, superior cerebellar peduncle, and fibres of III nerve. - Features: Medial lemniscus- Loss of proprioception. III nerve- Pupil points downwards and laterally. Red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle- Tremors and twitching of the opposite side. - Medial lemniscus- Loss of proprioception. - III nerve- Pupil points downwards and laterally. - Red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle- Tremors and twitching of the opposite side. - Medial lemniscus- Loss of proprioception. - III nerve- Pupil points downwards and laterally. - Red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle- Tremors and twitching of the opposite side. Parinaud’s syndrome : - Lesion of superior colliculi. - Features: Weakness of upward gaze and vertical nystagmus due to damage to the superior colliculus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 135-136; Solution Pages 163-166","sourcePage":135,"sourcePageEnd":136},{"id":"anatomy-5-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements is not correct regarding lemniscus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a collection of axons running in the spinal cord"},{"letter":"B","text":"The lateral lemniscus is a part of the auditory pathway"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal leminiscus is affected in wallenberg syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spinal lemniscus carries pain and temperature sensation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. It is a collection of axons running in the spinal cord","explanation":"Lemniscus is a collection of axons running in the brainstem. Lemniscus - Collection of axons running in the brainstem (Option A) - Four types (MTSL) M edial lemniscus L ateral lemniscus T rigeminal lemniscus S pinal lemniscus Medial lemniscus Continuation of dorsal column (nucleus gracilis and cuneatus) Sensations it carries are: - Pressure - Vibration - Tactile discrimination - Stereognosis - Conscious proprioception Lateral lemniscus - Formed by fibres arising in nuclei lying in close relation to the trapezoid body (superior olivary nucleus and nucleus of trapezoid body). - It carries auditory information from the cochlear nuclei. (Option B ruled out) Trigeminal lemniscus - Contains fibres arising in the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve and traveling to the thalamus. - Extends into the midbrain, medulla, and the spinal cord Spinal lemniscus Carries pain and temperature. (Option D ruled out) Lateral medullary ischemia- Wallenberg syndrome (Option C ruled out) - Occurs due to blockage of posterior inferior cerebellar artery. - It supplies areas behind the inferior olivary nucleus. - It involves spinal leminiscus (c/l loss of pain and temp from body) and i/l loss of pain and temp from face (due to involvement of spinal nucleus of V nerve - Injury to leminiscus always leads to C/l loss of function","sourceRef":"Question Page 136; Solution Pages 166-167","sourcePage":136,"sourcePageEnd":136},{"id":"anatomy-5-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 55-year-old man diagnosed with B12 deficiency presents with difficulty maintaining balance. On examination, he shows a loss of vibration and fine touch sensation in his lower limbs, while pain and temperature sensations are intact. Which of the following pathways is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral Spinothalamic Tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral Spinothalamic Tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tract of Goll"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corticospinal Tract"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Tract of Goll","explanation":"Tract of Goll (Gracile fasciculus) is a part of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway, responsible for transmitting fine touch, vibration, and proprioception from the lower limbs. B12 deficiency can lead to subacute combined degeneration, affecting this pathway and causing the sensory deficits seen in the patient. Tracts of the spinal cord Column Descending tracts Ascending tracts Anterior white column Anterior corticospinal, Anterior spinothalamic vestibulospinal, tectospinal, medial reticulospinal Lateral white column Lateral corticospinal, rubrospinal, Lateral spinothalamic, anterior lateral reticulospinal, spinocerebellar, posterior hypothalamospinal spinocerebellar, spinotectal Posterior white column(Dorsal - Fasciculus gracilis (tract of Goll) - column) Fasciculus cuneatus (tract of Burdach) Dorsal Column (Posterior Column-Medial Lemniscus Pathway): Category Details Components Fasciculus Gracilis: Carries sensory information from the lower limbs (Option C) Fasciculus Cuneatus: Carries sensory information from the upper limbs Sensations Pressure Tactile discrimination Vibration Conscious Proprioception Stereognosis Neuronal Pathway 1st Order Neuron: Located in the dorsal root ganglion 2nd Order Neuron: Located in the medulla oblongata 3rd Order Neuron: Located in the thalamus Spinothalamic Tract (Spinal Lemniscus System): Category Details Types (Options A & B ruled out) Lateral Spinothalamic Tract: Carries pain and temperature sensations Ventral Spinothalamic Tract: Carries crude touch and pressure sensations Pathway 1st Order Neuron: Located in the dorsal root ganglion 2nd Order Neuron: Located in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord 3rd Order Neuron: Located in the thalamus Pyramidal Tract (Corticospinal Tract): Involved in voluntary motor control (Option D ruled out) . Category Details Types Lateral Corticospinal Tract: Contains about 90% of the corticospinal fibers Ventral Corticospinal Tract: Contains the remaining fibers Function Controls fine and skilled voluntary motor activities, such as threading a needle Works in conjunction with the corticobulbar (corticonuclear) tracts Neuronal Pathway Receives axons from the cerebrum (upper motor neuron, UMN type of fibers) Effects of Damage Damage to Pyramidal System: Causes spastic paralysis of skeletal muscles below the level of the lesion Damage to Lower Motor Neurons: Causes flaccid paralysis at the level of the lesion","sourceRef":"Question Page 136; Solution Pages 167-169","sourcePage":136,"sourcePageEnd":136},{"id":"anatomy-5-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following arteries is primarily responsible for supplying the sublenticular part of the internal capsule, which carries auditory fibres?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Recurrent branch of anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Direct branches from the internal carotid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Branch of anterior choroidal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Branch of posterior communicating"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Branch of anterior choroidal","explanation":"- Sublenticular part of the internal capsule is supplied by the branch of anterior choroidal artery and the branch of the posterior cerebral artery. - It carries ascending auditory fibres from temporal lobe to thalamus. Vascular supply of internal capsule Sublenticular part of the internal capsule is supplied by the branch of anterior choroidal artery and branch of posterior cerebral artery. Recurrent branch of anterior cerebral artery (Option A): Supplies anterior limb of the internal capsule. Direct branches from the internal carotid (Option B): Supplies genu of the internal capsule. Branch of posterior communicating (Option D): Supplies genu and part of the posterior limb of the internal capsule.","sourceRef":"Question Page 136; Solution Pages 169-170","sourcePage":136,"sourcePageEnd":136},{"id":"anatomy-5-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 62-year-old presents with sudden-onset left-sided weakness, primarily affecting the arm and face. On examination, he has difficulty with speech, displaying both expressive and receptive aphasia. He also shows left-sided facial drooping and an inability to move his left upper limb effectively. Sensation is relatively intact in his lower limb. Which artery is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior cerebral artery (ACA)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior cerebral artery (PCA)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle cerebral artery (MCA)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Basilar artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Middle cerebral artery (MCA)","explanation":"- The MCA supplies the lateral aspects of the cerebral hemispheres, including the motor and sensory cortices controlling the face, upper limb, and speech areas (in the dominant hemisphere). - The patient's symptoms of weakness in the face and arm, combined with aphasia, strongly suggest an MCA stroke , as this artery is responsible for these regions. An ACA stroke typically affects the lower limbs, and a PCA stroke would more likely present with visual field defects or sensory disturbances. Artery Affected Areas Involved Clinical Manifestations Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA) Medial surface of the frontal and Contralateral weakness and sensory (Option A) parietal lobes, corpus callosum loss (lower limb > upper limb) Urinary incontinence, Personality changes (frontal lobe involvement), Transcortical motor aphasia (dominant hemisphere) Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA) Lateral surface of the frontal, Contralateral weakness and sensory (Option C) temporal, and parietal lobes, basal loss (face and upper limb > lower ganglia, internal capsule limb) Aphasia (dominant hemisphere), Hemineglect (non-dominant hemisphere), Homonymous hemianopia Gaze preference towards the side of the lesion Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA) Occipital lobe, inferior part of Contralateral homonymous (Option B) temporal lobe, thalamus hemianopia , Visual agnosia , Thalamic pain syndrome, Cortical blindness (if bilateral), Memory deficits (if temporal lobe is involved)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 136-137; Solution Pages 170-171","sourcePage":136,"sourcePageEnd":137},{"id":"anatomy-5-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Identify the artery highlighted in blue in the following image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior communicating artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior communicating artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle cerebral artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior communicating artery","explanation":"- The anterior communicating artery connects the two anterior cerebral arteries and forms part of the Circle of Willis. - This artery plays a critical role in allowing collateral blood flow between the left and right sides of the cerebral circulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 137-138; Solution Page 171","sourcePage":137,"sourcePageEnd":138},{"id":"anatomy-5-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with Horner’s syndrome, but notably, the patient does not exhibit anhidrosis. Based on the absence of anhidrosis, which of the following is the most likely location of the lesion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First-order neuron (central)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Second-order neuron (peripheral)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Third-order neuron (postganglionic)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior cervical ganglion"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Third-order neuron (postganglionic)","explanation":"- The absence of anhidrosis in Horner's syndrome suggests that the lesion is likely located in the postganglionic (third-order neuron) segment of the oculosympathetic pathway. - The sweat glands are primarily innervated by sympathetic fibers that branch off earlier. - Hence, in third-order Horner's syndrome, if the disruption occurs after these fibers have already branched off, sweating pathways are unaffected, and anhidrosis does not occur. - In contrast, anhidrosis is always present in first-order or second-order Horner's syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Page 138; Solution Pages 171-172","sourcePage":138,"sourcePageEnd":138},{"id":"anatomy-5-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following statements about prevertebral/paravertebral ganglia is/are true? Prevertebral ganglia are also known as collateral ganglia. Prevertebral ganglia are a part of the parasympathetic nervous system. Paravertebral ganglia supply the abdominal viscera. The celiac ganglion is a paravertebral ganglion.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"I only"},{"letter":"B","text":"I and III only"},{"letter":"C","text":"II, III and IV only"},{"letter":"D","text":"All the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. I only","explanation":"- Prevertebral ganglia are also known as collateral ganglia. - Prevertebral ganglia and paravertebral ganglia both are part of the sympathetic nervous system , not the parasympathetic nervous system. - Paravertebral ganglia (sympathetic trunk ganglia) primarily supply the body wall and limbs, while prevertebral ganglia (e.g., celiac ganglion) supply the abdominal viscera . - The celiac ganglion is a prevertebral (collateral) ganglion , not a paravertebral ganglion. Characteristic Prevertebral Ganglia Paravertebral Ganglia Alternate Names Also known as collateral ganglia or Also known as sympathetic chain preaortic ganglia. (Statement I) ganglia. Sympathetic Pathway Both are part of the sympathetic nervous system. (Statement II ruled out) Location Anterior to the aorta, close to major Adjacent to the vertebral column, arteries like the celiac, superior forming sympathetic chains on either mesenteric, and inferior mesenteric side of the spine. arteries. Structure Associated with the abdominal aorta Forms a chain along the spine, with and related to major arteries. ganglia in the cervical, thoracic, lumbar, sacral, and coccygeal regions. Function Supplies abdominal and pelvic Supplies the thoracic viscera, blood organs, controls visceral functions vessels, skin, sweat glands, and like peristalsis and secretion. ( arrector pili muscles. Statement III ruled out) Preganglionic Fibers Receive preganglionic fibers from the Receive preganglionic fibers from the splanchnic nerves (e.g., greater, spinal cord segments (T1-L2) via lesser, least splanchnic). white rami communicantes. Postganglionic Fibers Postganglionic fibers form plexuses Postganglionic fibers distributed via like the celiac and hypogastric grey rami communicantes to spinal plexus, supplying abdominal and nerves and target skin, blood pelvic organs. vessels, and thoracic viscera. Notable Ganglia Includes the celiac, superior Includes cervical, thoracic, lumbar, mesenteric, inferior mesenteric, and sacral ganglia, and ganglion impar. aorticorenal ganglia. (Statement IV ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 138; Solution Pages 172-174","sourcePage":138,"sourcePageEnd":138},{"id":"anatomy-5-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Preganglionic fibers in both the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are myelinated."},{"letter":"B","text":"Postganglionic fibers in both the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems are unmyelinated"},{"letter":"C","text":"Most sweat glands are sympathetic and adrenergic."},{"letter":"D","text":"Acetylcholine is a neurotransmitter found in sympathetic ganglia."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Most sweat glands are sympathetic and adrenergic.","explanation":"- Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic, not adrenergic. - Only a few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic. ANS - Effectors: General Visceral Efferent (GVE) - Smooth muscles - Cardiac muscles - Glands Neurotransmitters and Efferent Innervation: - Autonomic Efferent Innervation - GVE Neuron Bodies: Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons Predominantly sympathetic neurons Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Neuron Bodies: Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons Predominantly sympathetic neurons Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord - Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons - Predominantly sympathetic neurons - Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Preganglionic Fibers: Myelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option A ruled out) White in color - Myelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option A ruled out) - White in color - Postganglionic Fibers: Unmyelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option B ruled out) Grey in color - Unmyelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option B ruled out) - Grey in color - Neurotransmitters: Acetylcholine: Found in parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia (Option D ruled out) Effector Organ Responses: Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Acetylcholine: Found in parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia (Option D ruled out) - Effector Organ Responses: Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) - Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic - Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Neuron Bodies: Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons Predominantly sympathetic neurons Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord - Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons - Predominantly sympathetic neurons - Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Lateral horn cells of the spinal cord - Can be sympathetic or parasympathetic neurons - Predominantly sympathetic neurons - Sacral spinal cord: Parasympathetic neurons are also present - Myelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option A ruled out) - White in color - Unmyelinated (both in sympathetic and parasympathetic systems) (Option B ruled out) - Grey in color - Acetylcholine: Found in parasympathetic and sympathetic ganglia (Option D ruled out) - Effector Organ Responses: Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) - Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic - Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Parasympathetic: Cholinergic (acetylcholine) - Sympathetic: Adrenergic (epinephrine and norepinephrine) (rare) Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic - Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Sweat Glands: Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic - Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic - Most sweat glands (eccrine) are sympathetic and cholinergic - Few sweat glands (apocrine) are sympathetic and adrenergic","sourceRef":"Question Pages 138-139; Solution Pages 174-178","sourcePage":138,"sourcePageEnd":139},{"id":"anatomy-5-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 64-year-old woman presents with progressive weakness in her left leg. On examination, you observe that her left leg has decreased muscle tone, and shows significant muscle atrophy. Given these findings, which of the following additional signs is likely to be present?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Exaggerated Deep Tendon Reflexes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Positive Babinski Sign"},{"letter":"C","text":"Muscle Spasticity"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fasciculations"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fasciculations","explanation":"- The patient’s findings of decreased muscle tone, muscle atrophy, and flaccid paralysis are indicative of Lower Motor Neuron (LMN) involvement. - In LMN lesions, you typically see muscle atrophy and decreased muscle tone along with absent deep tendon reflexes. - Fasciculations, which are involuntary muscle twitches, are a common additional sign in LMN lesions due to denervation of the muscle fibers. Feature LMN Paralysis UMN Paralysis Muscle Tone Decreased Increased Type of Paralysis Flaccid Spastic (Option C ruled out) Muscle Atrophy Occurs later No atrophy Reaction of Degeneration Seen Not seen Tendon Reflexes Absent Exaggerated (Option A ruled out) Side of Affected Body Ipsilateral Contralateral Babinski Sign Negative Positive (Option B ruled out) Fasciculation Present Absent","sourceRef":"Question Page 139; Solution Pages 178-179","sourcePage":139,"sourcePageEnd":139},{"id":"anatomy-5-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-5","chapter":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topic":"Lesson 5 - Neuroanatomy","topicIndex":5,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A 35-year-old patient presents following a fall from a ladder. The patient exhibits specific neurological symptoms: loss of pain and temperature sensation in the left leg, difficulty identifying the position of the right leg with eyes closed, and complete loss of sensation at the T12 level of the trunk on the right. Which of the following statements is false for this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of proprioception in the right leg occurs due right dorsal column."},{"letter":"B","text":"Loss of pain in the left leg occurs due to the left spinothalamic tract."},{"letter":"C","text":"Damage to the ventral nerve roots at the T12 level results in loss of sensation."},{"letter":"D","text":"Spastic paralysis of the right leg"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Loss of pain in the left leg occurs due to the left spinothalamic tract.","explanation":"- The patient's presentation is consistent with Brown-Séquard Syndrome, resulting from a hemisection of the spinal cord. - In Brown-Séquard syndrome, pain, and temperature sensations are lost on the opposite side of the body from the lesion due to the crossing of the spinothalamic tract fibers. - Therefore, loss of pain in the left leg would be due to damage to the right spinothalamic tract, not the left. Brown-Sequard syndrome Below the Level of Lesion: - Ipsilateral Upper Motor Neuron Paralysis: Due to damage to the pyramidal tract (corticospinal tract) (Option D ruled out) . - Ipsilateral Loss of Conscious Proprioceptive Sensations: Caused by damage to the posterior white column (Option A ruled out) . - Contralateral Loss of Pain and Temperature Sensations: Due to damage to the lateral spinothalamic tract and anterior spinothalamic tract. At the Level of Lesion: (Option C ruled out) - Ipsilateral Lower Motor Neuron Paralysis: Resulting from damage to the ventral nerve roots. - Ipsilateral Anesthesia Over the Skin of the Segment: Due to injury to the ventral nerve roots. Above the Level of Lesion: - Ipsilateral Hyperesthesia: Resulting from irritation of the dorsal nerve roots.","sourceRef":"Question Page 139; Solution Pages 179-180","sourcePage":139,"sourcePageEnd":139},{"id":"anatomy-6-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Identify the defect in the given image: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Open neural tube defect"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meningoencephalocele"},{"letter":"C","text":"Occult Spina bifida"},{"letter":"D","text":"Encephalocele"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Open neural tube defect","explanation":"The given image depicts a newborn with an open neural tube defect, specifically rachischisis or myeloschisis, which is a severe form of spina bifida where the neural tissue is exposed without skin or meningeal covering. Neural tube defects: Category Details Definition Failure of proper closure of neural tube & covering mesoderm/ectoderm; most common congenital CNS anomaly. Inheritance Primary NTDs have multifactorial inheritance. Maternal risk factors Alcohol, radiation exposure, IDDM, valproate, carbamazepine, zinc and folate deficiency. Types of NTDs Spina bifida occulta, meningocele, encephalocele, myelomeningocele, anencephaly, caudal regression syndrome, dermal sinus, tethered cord, syringomyelia, diastematomyelia, lipoma, iniencephaly. Spina bifida occulta Midline defect of vertebral bodies without protrusion of spinal cord or meninges ; usually asymptomatic and not of consequence. Craniorachischisis Rare malformation with skull and spinal bone defects ; brain and nerves exposed ; may have other defects like imperforate anus. Meningocele Meninges herniate through vertebral arches or sacrum; spinal cord norma l. Fluctuant midline mass that may transilluminate. Surgery may be delayed in asymptomatic cases with normal neuro findings and skin covering. Myelomeningocele Most common in lumbosacral region ; sac-like cystic structure with neural tissue; 80% develop hydrocephalus with type II Chiari malformation. Anencephaly Large defect of calvarium, meninges, and scalp; associated with rudimentary brain due to failure of closure of rostral neuropore. Iniencephaly Retroflexion of head with occipital and upper spine defects, nearly absent neck; defect can range from acrania to minimal defect. Prenatal diagnosis Detailed ultrasound, MSAFP (maternal serum α-fetoprotein), amniotic fluid acetylcholinesterase ( AChE ) . Prevention 0.4 mg folic acid daily for all women of childbearing age ; 4 mg daily for high-risk women (prior affected child). Risk of recurrence 3-4% after 1 affected child; 10% after 2 affected children. Treatment Surgery for defect; VP shunt if hydrocephalus present. Open lesions should be closed within 24 hours. Antibiotics if infection is present. Meningoencephalocele (Option B): Incorrect because a meningoencephalocele is a sac-like protrusion of meninges and brain tissue through a skull defect, typically in the occipital region. Occult Spina Bifida (Option C): Incorrect because occult spina bifida is a closed defect where the vertebral arch fails to fuse, but the skin remains intact, often presenting with subtle external markers like a tuft of hair or dimple. Encephalocele (Option D): is a rare birth defect that occurs when the neural tube doesn't close completely during fetal development. This results in a sac-like protrusion of brain tissue or membranes through a hole in the skull.","sourceRef":"Question Page 181; Solution Pages 223-224","sourcePage":181,"sourcePageEnd":181},{"id":"anatomy-6-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":2,"question":"The type of fibers present in the fornix are:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Association and commissural fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Association and projection fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Commissural and projection fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Association fibers"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Association and commissural fibers","explanation":"Correct Option A - Association and commissural fibers The fornix, vital for memory and limbic system functions, comprises association fibers and commissural fibers. Association fibers links nearby brain regions within the same hemisphere, like the hippocampus, mamillary bodies, and septal nuclei. Commissural fibers connect the hippocampus of one hemisphere to its counterpart in the other hemisphere through the anterior commissure. Incorrect Options: Option B - Association and projection fibers:Projection fibers link the cerebral cortex with other brain areas or the spinal cord.. However, projection fibers are not a major component of the fornix. Option C - Commissural and projection fibers: Commissural fibers connect corresponding regions between the two cerebral hemispheres, but projection fibers are not a significant part of the fornix. Option D - Association fibers: Association fibers are involved in connecting different regions within the same hemisphere. However, the fornix also contains commissural fibers.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 181-182; Solution Pages 224-225","sourcePage":181,"sourcePageEnd":182},{"id":"anatomy-6-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which structure is not present in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hippocampus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tail of the caudate nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fimbriae"},{"letter":"D","text":"Collateral eminence"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Tail of the caudate nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option B - Tail of the caudate nucleus: - The tail of the caudate nucleus is not present in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. The caudate nucleus is part of the basal ganglia and its tail arches around and terminates in the roof of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. Incorrect Options: Option A - Hippocampus: - The hippocampus is present in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. It is a key structure involved in memory and spatial navigation. Option C - Fimbriae: - The fimbriae of the hippocampus are present in the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle. They are bundles of axons that emerge from the hippocampus and converge to form the fornix. Option D - Collateral eminence: - The collateral eminence is a ridge on the floor of the inferior horn of the lateral ventricle caused by the underlying collateral sulcus. It is indeed present in the floor of the inferior horn.","sourceRef":"Question Page 182; Solution Pages 225-226","sourcePage":182,"sourcePageEnd":182},{"id":"anatomy-6-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Identify the marked structure: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal capsule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Body of fornix"},{"letter":"C","text":"Globus pallidus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral ventricle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Internal capsule","explanation":"Correct Option A -Internal capsule: - Projection fibers connecting the higher brain center with the lower brain center - Projection fibers bisect the corpus striatum, part of the basal ganglia, into two components: Lentiform nucleus is lateral Caudate nucleus is medial - Lentiform nucleus is lateral - Caudate nucleus is medial - Lentiform nucleus is lateral - Caudate nucleus is medial Incorrect Options: - Options B, C & D: These do not indicate the marked structure given in the question","sourceRef":"Question Pages 182-183; Solution Pages 226-227","sourcePage":182,"sourcePageEnd":183},{"id":"anatomy-6-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which ganglia is located in the marked area? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Otic ganglia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ciliary ganglia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pterygopalatine ganglia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Submandibular ganglia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pterygopalatine ganglia","explanation":"Correct Option C - Pterygopalatine ganglia: - Pterygopalatine ganglia is present at the location marked in the given image. Incorrect Options: - Options A, B, and D are not associated with the nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 183; Solution Page 227","sourcePage":183,"sourcePageEnd":183},{"id":"anatomy-6-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":6,"question":"The nerve passing through the red arrow marked region has been lesioned. Which of the following will be the clinical presentation? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of sensation in the mucosa of nasopharynx"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loss of sensation in maxillary area of face"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of sensation in jaw area"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of sensation in scalp and nose"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Loss of sensation in scalp and nose","explanation":"Correct Option D - Loss of sensation in scalp and nose: - The superior orbital fissure is a crucial anatomical opening in the skull through which several important nerves pass. These include the oculomotor nerve (CN III), trochlear nerve (CN IV), abducens nerve (CN VI), and the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve (CN V). - The ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve provides sensory innervation to the scalp, forehead, upper eyelid, nose, and the mucosa of the frontal sinuses. If the nerve passing through the superior orbital fissure is lesioned, the patient would experience loss of sensation in the scalp and nose due to the impairment of the ophthalmic nerve (V1). Incorrect Options: Option A - Loss of sensation in the mucosa of nasopharynx: - Incorrect: This sensation is typically provided by branches of the maxillary nerve (V2) and glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX), not by the ophthalmic nerve (V1), which passes through the superior orbital fissure. Option B - Loss of sensation in maxillary area of face: - Incorrect: The maxillary division (V2) of the trigeminal nerve provides sensation to the maxillary region, but it exits the skull through the foramen rotundum, not the superior orbital fissure. Option C - Loss of sensation in jaw area: - Incorrect: The mandibular division (V3) of the trigeminal nerve provides sensation to the jaw, but it exits through the foramen ovale, not the superior orbital fissure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 183-184; Solution Pages 227-228","sourcePage":183,"sourcePageEnd":184},{"id":"anatomy-6-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 35 year old female presented with lesion below shown. This would result in which kind of field abnormality?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Homonymous hemianopia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bitemporal hemianopia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Complete blindness"},{"letter":"D","text":"Single vision field loss"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Bitemporal hemianopia","explanation":"Correct Option B - Bitemporal hemianopia: - Craniopharyngiomas are often located near the pituitary gland and the optic chiasm. A tumor in this area can compress the optic chiasm, which affects the crossing fibers of the optic nerves from the nasal (medial) fields of vision. This results in loss of vision in thetemporal (lateral) fields of both eyes, known as bitemporal hemianopia. Craniopharyngiomas are often located near the pituitary gland and the optic chiasm. A tumor in this area can compress the optic chiasm, which affects the crossing fibers of the optic nerves from the nasal (medial) fields of vision. This results in loss of vision in the temporal (lateral) fields of both eyes, known as bitemporal hemianopia. Incorrect Options: Option A - Homonymous hemianopia : This type of vision loss occurs when there is damage to the optic tract or the visual cortex, leading to loss of vision in the same field of both eyes (either left or right). This is not typically associated with craniopharyngiomas. Option C - Complete blindness : Complete blindness would occur with damage to both the optic nerves or the visual pathways at a more extensive level, not just from a craniopharyngioma affecting the optic chiasm. Option D - Single vision field loss : This would imply a unilateral field loss, which is not characteristic of craniopharyngiomas. They usually affect the visual fields in a bitemporal manner due to their location affecting the optic chiasm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 184-185; Solution Pages 228-229","sourcePage":184,"sourcePageEnd":185},{"id":"anatomy-6-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Ptosis in horner’s syndrome is due to paralysis of which muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior tarsal muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Orbitalis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Orbicularis oculi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sphincter pupillae"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior tarsal muscle","explanation":"Correct Option A - Superior Tarsal Muscle (Müller's Muscle): - This muscle is a smooth muscle that assists the levator palpebrae superioris in raising the upper eyelid. In Horner's syndrome, there is a disruption of the sympathetic innervation, leading to paralysis of the superior tarsal muscle. This results in a mild ptosis (drooping of the upper eyelid). Incorrect Options: Option B - Orbitalis: - Incorrect: The orbitalis muscle (also known as Müller's muscle) is not directly involved in eyelid movement but is instead associated with the structure of the orbit. It is not the cause of ptosis in Horner's syndrome. Option C - Orbicularis Oculi: - Incorrect: The orbicularis oculi muscle is responsible for closing the eyelids and is innervated by the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). It is not affected in Horner's syndrome and is not responsible for ptosis. Option D - Sphincter Pupillae: - Incorrect: The sphincter pupillae muscle is responsible for constricting the pupil and is innervated by the parasympathetic nervous system. It is not involved in eyelid movement and is not the cause of ptosis in Horner's syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Page 185; Solution Pages 229-230","sourcePage":185,"sourcePageEnd":185},{"id":"anatomy-6-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":9,"question":"What are the effects of a lesion in Brodmann area 22?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Expressive aphasia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Receptive aphasia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Poor repetition of language"},{"letter":"D","text":"Poor naming"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Receptive aphasia","explanation":"Correct Option B - Receptive aphasia: - Brodmann area 22, also known as Wernicke's area, is primarily responsible for language comprehension. Lesions in this area lead to difficulties in understanding spoken and written language, resulting in receptive aphasia . Patients with this condition can speak fluently but often produce nonsensical sentences and have trouble understanding others. Incorrect Options: Option A - Expressive aphasia : This is typically associated with damage to Brodmann area 44 and 45 (Broca's area) rather than Brodmann area 22. Expressive aphasia affects speech production, making it difficult for the patient to form grammatically correct sentences. Option C - Poor repetition of language : Poor repetition of language is often linked to damage in the arcuate fasciculus, which connects Wernicke's area and Broca's area, rather than directly to Brodmann area 22. Option D - Poor naming : Poor naming is often associated with damage to areas involved in language processing and retrieval, such as the left temporal lobe, but not specifically with Brodmann area 22 alone.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 185-186; Solution Page 230","sourcePage":185,"sourcePageEnd":186},{"id":"anatomy-6-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A patient with loss of sensation on lateral 31/2 hand and thenar hypertrophy. Nerve involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Median"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Median","explanation":"Correct Option A - Median: - The median nerve supplies sensation to the lateral 3.5 fingers of the hand, including the thumb, index finger, middle finger, and part of the ring finger. Loss of sensation in these areas and thenar muscle hypertrophy (which indicates overuse or compensatory muscle growth) suggests involvement of the median nerve. This nerve is also responsible for the motor function of the thenar eminence (the group of muscles at the base of the thumb). Incorrect Options: Option B - Ulnar : The ulnar nerve supplies sensation to the medial 1.5 fingers (the little finger and half of the ring finger) and controls the intrinsic muscles of the hand. Loss of sensation in the lateral 3.5 fingers and thenar hypertrophy are not characteristic of ulnar nerve involvement. Option C - Radial : The radial nerve primarily affects the posterior aspect of the arm and forearm, and its dysfunction typically results in wrist drop and loss of sensation in the posterior hand. The symptoms described do not align with radial nerve issues. Option D - Anterior Interosseous Nerve : This is a branch of the median nerve that supplies deep muscles of the forearm, like the flexor pollicis longus and the lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus. It does not affect sensation on the hand's surface or the thenar muscles.","sourceRef":"Question Page 186; Solution Pages 230-231","sourcePage":186,"sourcePageEnd":186},{"id":"anatomy-6-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A patient with nerve injury was asked to make O with index finger and thumb- but was unable to do so. Which muscle is involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Opponens pollicis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palmar interossei"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Abductor pollicis brevis","explanation":"Correct Option B - Abductor Pollicis Brevis: - This muscle is responsible for the abduction of the thumb, which is essential for forming the \" O\" shape with the thumb and index finger. Difficulty in making this gesture indicates an issue with the abductor pollicis brevis, which is innervated by the median nerve. Incorrect Options: Option A - Opponens Pollicis : This muscle allows for the opposition of the thumb, which means it helps in bringing the thumb across the palm to touch the other fingers. While it's important for thumb movements, its function is not specifically related to the formation of an \"O\" shape. Option C - Flexor Pollicis Brevis : This muscle assists in flexing the thumb. While it contributes to thumb movements, it does not directly impact the ability to form an \"O\" shape with the thumb and index finger. Option D - Palmar Interossei : These muscles are responsible for adducting the fingers and contributing to the grip. They do not play a primary role in forming the \"O\" shape with the thumb and index finger.","sourceRef":"Question Page 186; Solution Pages 231-232","sourcePage":186,"sourcePageEnd":186},{"id":"anatomy-6-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":12,"question":"An absent gag reflex can result from injury to which cranial nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"9 and 10"},{"letter":"B","text":"10 and 7"},{"letter":"C","text":"7 and 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"5 and 9"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 9 and 10","explanation":"Correct Option A - 9 and 10 - Cranial Nerves IX (Glossopharyngeal) and X (Vagus): The gag reflex involves both sensory and motor components. The sensory component of the gag reflex is mediated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX), which detects the stimulus at the back of the throat. The motor component is controlled by the vagus nerve (cranial nerve X), which causes the muscles in the throat to contract. Injury to either of these nerves can result in an absent gag reflex. Incorrect Options: Option B - 10 and 7: - While cranial nerve X (vagus) is involved in the gag reflex, cranial nerve VII (facial nerve) is not. The facial nerve primarily controls muscles of facial expression, not the gag reflex. Option C - 7 and 5: - Incorrect: Cranial nerve V (trigeminal) provides sensation to the face and motor control for mastication, and cranial nerve VII controls facial expression. Neither of these nerves is involved in the gag reflex. Option D - 5 and 9: - While cranial nerve IX (glossopharyngeal) is involved in the gag reflex, cranial nerve V (trigeminal) is not. The trigeminal nerve is responsible for facial sensation and chewing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 186-187; Solution Page 232","sourcePage":186,"sourcePageEnd":187},{"id":"anatomy-6-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Identify the arrow marked nerve: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial pectoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral pectoral nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thoracodorsal nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Medial pectoral nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Medial pectoral nerve: - The medial pectoral nerve arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus and innervates the pectoralis minor and part of the pectoralis major muscles. The nerve typically pierces the pectoralis minor before reaching the pectoralis major. In an anatomical image or dissection, the medial pectoral nerve can be identified as the nerve that passes through or near the pectoralis minor and then continues to supply the pectoralis major. Incorrect Options: Option B - Lateral Pectoral Nerve: - The lateral pectoral nerve arises from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus and primarily innervates the pectoralis major muscle. Unlike the medial pectoral nerve, it does not pierce the pectoralis minor but rather passes above it. The lateral pectoral nerve is generally found more laterally and superiorly than the medial pectoral nerve. Option C - Long Thoracic Nerve: - The long thoracic nerve innervates the serratus anterior muscle. It runs down the side of the thorax and can be seen on the surface of the serratus anterior. It is not associated with the pectoral muscles and is positioned more posteriorly and laterally compared to the pectoral nerves. Option D - Thoracodorsal Nerve: - The thoracodorsal nerve arises from the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and innervates the latissimus dorsi muscle. It is found on the posterior side of the thorax and is not involved with the pectoral muscles, making it distinct from the medial pectoral nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 187; Solution Pages 232-233","sourcePage":187,"sourcePageEnd":187},{"id":"anatomy-6-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle occurs at which level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"B","text":"Midbrain"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diencephalon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medulla"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Midbrain","explanation":"Correct Option B - Midbrain - The superior cerebellar peduncle, also known as the brachium conjunctivum, is one of the three major fiber tracts that connect the cerebellum with the rest of the brain. It is located on the dorsal aspect of the brainstem. - The decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle refers to the crossing over of its fibers from one side of the brainstem to the other. This decussation occurs at the level of the midbrain, specifically in the region called the upper midbrain tegmentum. Incorrect Options: Option A - Pons: While the pons is involved in the transmission of various neural signals, the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle does not occur at this level. Option C - Diencephalon:The diencephalon, housing the thalamus and hypothalamus, lies above the midbrain. The superior cerebellar peduncle's decussation doesn't happen within the diencephalon.. Option D - Medulla:. While the medulla is involved in various vital functions, including cardiovascular and respiratory control, the decussation of the superior cerebellar peduncle does not occur at the level of the medulla.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 187-188; Solution Pages 233-234","sourcePage":187,"sourcePageEnd":188},{"id":"anatomy-6-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Identify the wrongly marked structure in the diagram. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-Corpus callosum"},{"letter":"B","text":"C-Hypothalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"B- Fornix"},{"letter":"D","text":"D-Cerebellum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. C-Hypothalamus","explanation":"Correct Option B - C-Hypothalamus: - The image shows the mid-saggital section of brain , the wrongly marked structure is C-hypothalamus as the structure marked as ‘C’ corresponds to the pituitary gland. Incorrect Options: - Choices A, C, D are incorrect as they are matched with the respective structures in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Page 188; Solution Page 234","sourcePage":188,"sourcePageEnd":188},{"id":"anatomy-6-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":16,"question":"What is the destination of the relayed signals from the Purkinje cells in the cerebellum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caudate nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lentiform nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dentate nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Red nucleus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Dentate nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option C - Dentate Nucleus - Purkinje cells of the cerebellum relay their signals into the dentate nucleus. The dentate nucleus is one of the deep cerebellar nuclei located within the cerebellum. It receives input from the Purkinje cells located in the cerebellar cortex. - The dentate nucleus is the largest of the deep cerebellar nuclei and plays a crucial role in motor coordination and planning. Incorrect Options: Option A - Caudate nucleus: The caudate nucleus is a part of the basal ganglia, which is involved in motor control. It does not directly receive input from the Purkinje cells of the cerebellum. Option B - Lentiform nucleus:The lentiform nucleus, part of the basal ganglia, includes the globus pallidus and putamen. Like the caudate nucleus, it doesn't directly relay signals from Purkinje cells of the cerebellum.. Option D - Red nucleus: The red nucleus in the midbrain aids in motor coordination, receiving input from the cerebellum, but not specifically from Purkinje cells. Its primary output targets the spinal cord and brainstem.","sourceRef":"Question Page 188; Solution Pages 234-235","sourcePage":188,"sourcePageEnd":188},{"id":"anatomy-6-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":17,"question":"The structure indicated in the provided image originates from the (or) The structure indicated in the provided image originates from the **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mamillary body"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hippocampus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Amygdala"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Hippocampus","explanation":"Correct Option B - Hippocampus - This image shows coronal section of brain and marked structure is fornix. - The hippocampus is a curved structure located in the medial temporal lobe and is closely associated with memory and spatial navigation. The fornix arises from the hippocampus and serves as the major output pathway from this structure. Incorrect Options: Option A - Mamillary body: The mamillary bodies, found in the hypothalamus, are involved in regulating physiological processes. Although the fornix is connected to them, it doesn't originate from the mamillary bodies. Option C - Thalamus: The thalamus acts as a central relay for sensory and motor signals to the cerebral cortex. While it connects with many brain structures, it isn't the source of the fornix. Option D - Amygdala: The amygdala, deep in the temporal lobe, processes emotions and memory. While linked to the hippocampus and part of the limbic system, it isn't where the fornix originates.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 188-189; Solution Page 235","sourcePage":188,"sourcePageEnd":189},{"id":"anatomy-6-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A patient presents to you with inability to form words but can make noises. He also knows what he wants to say. What area of the brain has been damaged in this patient? (or) A patient presents to you with inability to form words but can make noises. He also knows what he wants to say. What area of the brain has been damaged in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Wernicke’s area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Broca’s area"},{"letter":"C","text":"Heschl’s area"},{"letter":"D","text":"Striate area"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Broca’s area","explanation":"Correct Option B - Broca’s Area - Broca's area, located in the frontal lobe of the dominant hemisphere, controls speech production by coordinating motor movements. Damage to this area can cause Broca's aphasia or expressive aphasia, characterized by difficulty forming words and producing fluent speech. While speech is impaired, comprehension usually remains intact. Incorrect Options: Option A - Wernicke's area: Damage to Wernicke's area results in receptive aphasia or Wernicke's aphasia, characterized by impaired language comprehension and fluent but nonsensical speech. Option C - Heschl's area: Heschl's area, the primary auditory cortex in the superior temporal gyrus, processes auditory information. Damage here isn't likely to cause the described symptoms. Option D - Striate area: The striate area is involved in visual processing and is not directly associated with language production or comprehension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 189-190; Solution Pages 235-236","sourcePage":189,"sourcePageEnd":190},{"id":"anatomy-6-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following sensations is not perceived by the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Temperature"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pain"},{"letter":"C","text":"Touch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Proprioception"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Proprioception","explanation":"Correct Option D - Proprioception - Proprioception, which refers to the sense of body position and movement, is not primarily processed by the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve. Proprioceptive information from the face and head is primarily conveyed through other sensory pathways, such as the proprioceptive fibers within cranial nerves like the facial nerve (CN VII) and glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX). Incorrect Options: Option A - Temperature: Temperature sensations from the face and head are transmitted through the trigeminal nerve and processed in the spinal nucleus. Option B - Pain: The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve processes pain sensations from the face and head. Option C - Touch: The spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve also processes touch sensations from the face and head. It receives sensory input related to light touch, pressure, and tactile sensations.","sourceRef":"Question Page 190; Solution Pages 236-237","sourcePage":190,"sourcePageEnd":190},{"id":"anatomy-6-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Neostriatum consists of? (or) Neostriatum consists of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caudate nucleus and globus pallidus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Globus pallidus and putamen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Globus pallidus and lentiform nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Caudate nucleus and putamen"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Caudate nucleus and putamen","explanation":"Correct Option 4 - Caudate nucleus and putamen Neostriatum consists of the caudate nucleus and putamen Incorrect Options: Option A - Is incorrect Option B - Is incorrect Option C - Is incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Page 190; Solution Page 237","sourcePage":190,"sourcePageEnd":190},{"id":"anatomy-6-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":21,"question":"The following are non depolarizing muscle relaxants except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pancuronium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rocuronium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Suxamethonium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vecuronium"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Suxamethonium","explanation":"Correct Answer C - Suxamethonium: - The non-depolarizing muscle relaxants in the list you provided are Pancuronium, Rocuronium, and Vecuronium. Suxamethonium (also known as succinylcholine) is not a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant but rather a depolarizing muscle relaxant. Other Options: Option A - Pancuronium: Pancuronium is a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant that acts by competitively blocking the action of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction.. Option B - Rocuronium: Rocuronium is another non-depolarizing muscle relaxant that works by blocking the nicotinic acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction, leading to muscle relaxation. Option D - Vecuronium: Vecuronium is also a non-depolarizing muscle relaxant that acts by blocking acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 190-191; Solution Pages 237-238","sourcePage":190,"sourcePageEnd":191},{"id":"anatomy-6-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":22,"question":"The neurotransmitter secreted by the area marked in the given image is? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acetylcholine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dopamine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glutamine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Noradrenaline"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Dopamine","explanation":"Correct Option B - Dopamine: The marked area corresponds to the substantia nigra, located in the midbrain. It's pivotal for producing and releasing dopamine, a neurotransmitter crucial for motor control, reward and motivation, and emotional regulation. Dopaminergic neurons in the substantia nigra release dopamine into areas like the basal ganglia, vital for voluntary movements and motor coordination. Incorrect Options: Option A - Acetylcholine: Acetylcholine influences muscle contraction, memory, and learning, but it's not primarily secreted by the substantia nigra. Option C - Glutamine: Glutamine is an amino acid that serves as a building block for proteins in the body. It is not a neurotransmitter involved in the substantia nigra's function. Option D - Noradrenaline: Noradrenaline, crucial for the \"fight-or-flight\" response and attention, is primarily produced in the locus coeruleus, not the substantia nigra.","sourceRef":"Question Page 191; Solution Page 238","sourcePage":191,"sourcePageEnd":191},{"id":"anatomy-6-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Failure of closure of rostral neuropore at 25th day leads to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hydranencephaly"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rachischisis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anencephaly"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spina bifida"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anencephaly","explanation":"Correct Option C - Anencephaly: - Failure of closure of the rostral neuropore at around the 25th day of embryonic development leads to the condition known as anencephaly . - Anencephaly is a severe congenital malformation characterized by the absence of a major portion of the brain and skull. Incorrect Options: Option A - Hydranencephaly: Hydranencephaly, marked by near-total or complete absence of cerebral hemispheres, isn't specifically due to the failure of rostral neuropore closure. Option B - Rachischisis: Rachischisis is a severe type of spina bifida where the neural tube doesn't close along the spine's entire length, not involving rostral neuropore closure. Option D - Spina bifida: Spina bifida, involves incomplete neural tube closure, leading to spinal cord defects, but it's not directly linked to rostral neuropore closure failure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 191-192; Solution Pages 238-239","sourcePage":191,"sourcePageEnd":192},{"id":"anatomy-6-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 55-year-old man is accompanied by his wife to the Neurology OPD. His wife expressed concern about her husband's condition and they were directed to this facility by a local doctor. According to the doctor's note, the patient is unable to repeat spoken words, but can comprehend them and respond appropriately by nodding. The patient also experiences difficulty in naming people and writing. The doctor referred the patient to a specialized facility for a CT brain scan. As the intern on duty, you would inform the Chief Medical Officer (CMO) that this condition could be indicative of a brain lesion in which specific area?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Arcuate fasciculus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior commissure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corpus callosum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fornix"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Arcuate fasciculus","explanation":"Correct option A - Arcuate fasciculus Difficulty in repeating spoken words, trouble with naming and writing, yet intact comprehension of spoken language, suggests Conduction Aphasia. It's caused by a disconnection between language centers in the brain, possibly affecting the Arcuate Fasciculus. This white matter tract links Broca's area (speech production) with Wernicke's area (language comprehension). Damage here can lead to symptoms like those described in conduction aphasia, where patients struggle to repeat words or phrases accurately despite understanding them. Incorrect Options: Option B - Anterior commissure: The anterior commissure is involved in the connection between the two cerebral hemispheres but is not typically associated with the specific language symptoms described. Option C - Corpus callosum: Damage to the corpus callosum can result in a variety of symptoms but is not specifically associated with the language deficits described. Option D - Fornix: The fornix is a white matter structure involved in memory processes but is not typically associated with the language symptoms described.","sourceRef":"Question Page 192; Solution Page 239","sourcePage":192,"sourcePageEnd":192},{"id":"anatomy-6-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which pathway provides afferent fibers to the mammillary body, a structure located beneath the brain's surface?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corpus callosum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pituitary gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fornix"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fornix","explanation":"Correct Option D - Fornix: - The fornix is a bundle of nerve fibers that plays a crucial role in the limbic system, including its connection with the mammillary body. The mammillary bodies receive afferent fibers via the fornix, which connects the hippocampus (a critical component of the limbic system) to the mammillary bodies. Incorrect Options: Option A - Corpus callosum: The corpus callosum connects the brain's two hemispheres but isn't directly involved in the afferent pathways to the mammillary body. Option B - Thalamus: While the thalamus is interconnected with the mammillary body through neural circuits, it does not directly send afferent fibers to the mammillary body. Option C - Pituitary gland: Although pituitary gland has connections with the hypothalamus, it does not send afferent fibers to the mammillary body.","sourceRef":"Question Page 192; Solution Pages 239-240","sourcePage":192,"sourcePageEnd":192},{"id":"anatomy-6-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which muscle among the following is not innervated by the structure labeled as A?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior rectus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior oblique"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial rectus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Levator palpebrae superioris (LPS)"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Superior oblique","explanation":"Correct Option B - Superior oblique: Superior oblique muscle is not supplied by the structure marked A, which is the oculomotor nerve. Incorrect Option: Option A - The oculomotor nerve innervates the superior rectus muscle, which is responsible for elevating the eye and medially rotating it. Option C - The oculomotor nerve innervates the medial rectus muscle, which is responsible for adducting the eye (moving it inward towards the midline). Option D - The oculomotor nerve innervates the LPS muscle, which is responsible for elevating the upper eyelid.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 192-193; Solution Page 240","sourcePage":192,"sourcePageEnd":193},{"id":"anatomy-6-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A lesion of the structure marked in the image below causes? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Contralateral hemiplegia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ataxia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tinnitus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ipsilateral facial palsy"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Contralateral hemiplegia","explanation":"Correct Option A - Contralateral hemiplegia: - The highlighted area is the medullary pyramids, situated in the medulla oblongata of the brainstem. Lesions here result in contralateral hemiplegia, where damage affects motor fibers crossing over to the opposite side of the body. These fibers form the corticospinal tracts, transmitting motor signals from the brain to the spinal cord and muscles. Incorrect Options: Option B - Ataxia refers to a lack of coordination of voluntary muscle movements. Lesions in other areas of the brain, such as the cerebellum, are more commonly associated with ataxia. Option C - Tinnitus, the perception of ringing or noise in the ears, is usually linked to auditory system problems like inner ear or auditory nerve damage, not lesions in the medullary pyramids. Option D - Facial palsy is usually due to lesions in the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) from the pons, not the medullary pyramids.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 193-194; Solution Pages 240-241","sourcePage":193,"sourcePageEnd":194},{"id":"anatomy-6-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":28,"question":"The blood supply to the structure marked in the image is by: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vertebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle cerebral artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior cerebral artery","explanation":"Correct option C - Anterior cerebral artery: - The anterior cerebral artery is one of the main arteries that supply blood to the brain. It arises from the internal carotid artery and provides blood supply to the medial and superior parts of the cerebral hemispheres. It also supplies blood to the fornix, which is part of the limbic system. Incorrect Options: Option A - Vertebral artery: The vertebral arteries, from the subclavian arteries, ascend through vertebrae to nourish the back of the brain, but they don't directly supply the fornix. Option B - Internal carotid artery: The internal carotid arteries are major arteries that supply blood to the anterior part of the brain, but they do not directly supply the fornix. Option D - Middle cerebral artery: The middle cerebral artery, a major branch of the internal carotid artery, feeds blood to the lateral parts of the cerebral hemispheres but doesn't directly nourish the fornix.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 194-195; Solution Page 241","sourcePage":194,"sourcePageEnd":195},{"id":"anatomy-6-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Identify the structure marked as ‘C’ in the below image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caudate nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Insula"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lentiform nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Third ventricle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lentiform nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option C - Lentiform nucleus - The basal ganglia is a nucleus deeply embedded in the brain hemisphere. It is a part of the cerebral hemisphere situated at the base of the forebrain and the top of the midbrain. - Globus pallidus is one of the basal ganglia components - Globus pallidus, along with putamen, forms the lentiform nucleus. - Lentiform nucleus is found lateral to the head of the caudate nucleus and thalamus. Incorrect Option: Option A - The caudate nucleus is a subcortical paired “C “shaped structure which lies deep inside the brain near thalamus Option B - The insula is a small region of the cerebral cortex located deeply in the cerebral cortex. It is a large fissure that separates the frontal and parietal lobes from the temporal lobe. Option D - The Third ventricle is a funnel-shaped structure that lies in the center of the brain. It is sandwiched between the right and left thalamus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 195-196; Solution Pages 241-242","sourcePage":195,"sourcePageEnd":196},{"id":"anatomy-6-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":30,"question":"An exhausted man was brought to the casualty with minimal response. He had a progressive history of weakness in his body for 2 days with facial droop, and slurring speech . Which vessel is likely to be thrombosed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Middle cerebral artery","explanation":"Correct Option C - Middle cerebral artery - The middle cerebral artery (MCA) mainly supplies the oxygenated blood to the specific part of the brain parenchyma. The cortical branches of MCA supply the brain parenchyma of the primary motor and somatosensory cortical areas of the face, trunk, and upper limbs, apart from the insular and auditory cortex. Incorrect Options: Option A - Posterior cerebral artery (PCA) is vital for primary and secondary visual functions, including sensation and interpretation of visual input. Option B - The anterior cerebral artery (ACA) nourishes the medial and superior frontal and anterior parietal lobes. ACA infarcts are rare due to collateral circulation from the anterior communicating artery. Option D - The posterior inferior cerebellar artery branches from the vertebral artery, nourishing the medulla oblongata, choroid plexus, fourth ventricle, and lower part of the cerebellar hemisphere.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 196-197; Solution Pages 242-243","sourcePage":196,"sourcePageEnd":197},{"id":"anatomy-6-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":31,"question":"What is the blood supply of the marked area in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior cerebral artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Posterior inferior cerebellar artery: - The highlighted structure is the inferior cerebellar peduncle, a white matter bundle linking the medulla oblongata to the cerebellum. It is mainly supplied by the Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery (PICA), a branch of the vertebral artery. Incorrect Options: Option B - Anterior inferior cerebellar artery: - The anterior inferior cerebral artery (AICA) a branch of the basilar artery, supplies blood to the anteroinferior cerebellum, middle cerebellar peduncle, and inferolateral pons. Option C - Superior cerebellar artery: - Superior cerebellar artery (SCA) supplies the superior surface of the cerebellum, superior and middle cerebellar peduncles , and the lower midbrain. Option D - Posterior Cerebral Artery: - The Posterior Cerebral Artery (PCA) provides blood supply to the occipital cortex, inferior and medial temporal cortices, thalamus, and rostral midbrain.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 197-198; Solution Pages 243-244","sourcePage":197,"sourcePageEnd":198},{"id":"anatomy-6-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which of the following statements is incorrect related to the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Benign tumor of coccyx"},{"letter":"B","text":"Presence of teeth and hair"},{"letter":"C","text":"Result from the aberrant migration of primordial germ cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prechordal plate anomaly"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Prechordal plate anomaly","explanation":"Correct Option D - Prechordal plate anomaly: - Sacrococcygeal teratomas are tumors that originate from primordial germ cells and are typically found at the base of the spine. They are associated with the remnants of the primitive streak rather than the prechordal plate. Incorrect Options: Option A - Benign tumor of coccyx: Sacrococcygeal teratoma is a tumor that arises at the base of the spine, often from remnants of primitive tissue, and it can exhibit both benign(mostly) and malignant (few) characteristics. Option B - Presence of teeth and hair: Teratomas can have any of the 3 germ layers (Endoderm, ectoderm or mesoderm) because primordial germ cells themselves are pluripotent cells.. Option C - Result from the aberrant migration of primordial germ cell: Abrupt migration of primordial germ cells may cause oropharyngeal teratoma and sacrococcygeal teratoma.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 198-199; Solution Page 244","sourcePage":198,"sourcePageEnd":199},{"id":"anatomy-6-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":33,"question":"The marked structure relays efferents to which nucleus of the thalamus? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior nucleus of thalamus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral nucleus of thalamus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ventro -postero-lateral nucleus of thalamus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior nucleus of thalamus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior nucleus of thalamus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Anterior nucleus of thalamus: - The marked structure is the fornix. Fornix is the major efferent tract of the hippocampus. - The fornix sends efferents to the mammillary bodies, and the mammillothalamic tract connects the mammillary bodies to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus. This pathway is part of the Papez circuit, involved in memory and emotional processing. Incorrect Option: Option B - Lateral nucleus of thalamus: - The lateral nucleus functions as the hunger center, promoting eating when stimulated, while its destruction leads to starvation. Option C - Ventro -postero-lateral nucleus of thalamus: - The ventro-postero-lateral nucleus of the thalamus processes sensations like pain, vibration, proprioception, and light touch from the spinothalamic tract and dorsal column pathways. Option D - Posterior nucleus of the thalamus: - The posterior nucleus aids in thermoregulation by promoting heat conservation. It triggers the sympathetic nervous system, causing peripheral vasoconstriction to retain heat.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 199-200; Solution Pages 244-245","sourcePage":199,"sourcePageEnd":200},{"id":"anatomy-6-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":34,"question":"At which level does the nerve supply for the marked structure arise? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Red nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sub thalamic nuclei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Decussation of pyramidal tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Olivary nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Red nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Red nucleus: - The marked structure is the medial rectus muscle. - The nerve supply for the medial rectus muscle arises from the oculomotor nerv e (cranial nerve III). - The cell bodies of the motor fibers supplying the medial rectus muscle are located in the oculomotor nucleus, which is situated in the midbrain close to the red nucleus. Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are not associated with the nerve supply of the medial rectus muscle. Option B - Subthalamic nuclei: - The subthalamic nuclei are located in the diencephalon, specifically in the ventral part of the thalamus. - They are involved in motor control and form part of the basal ganglia circuitry. - The subthalamic nuclei play a role in the regulation of movement, particularly in coordinating and modulating voluntary motor activities. Option C - Decussation of pyramidal tract: - The decussation of the pyramidal tract refers to the crossing over of motor fibers to the opposite side of the body. - It occurs at the level of the medulla oblongata. - The pyramidal tract consists of the corticospinal fibers, which are responsible for voluntary motor control. Option D - Olivary nucleus: - The olivary nucleus is located in the medulla oblongata and is involved in motor coordination, specifically in the control of fine movements. - The olivary nucleus is primarily associated with the olivocerebellar pathway, which plays a role in motor learning and coordination.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 200-201; Solution Pages 245-246","sourcePage":200,"sourcePageEnd":201},{"id":"anatomy-6-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":35,"question":"A woman presented with sudden onset of dysphagia, dysarthria, and gait ataxia. MRI revealed an acute infarct in the right dorsolateral medulla. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dejerine’s anterior bulbar syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Wallenberg syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Babinski-Nageotte syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Weber syndrome"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Wallenberg syndrome","explanation":"Correct Option B - Wallenberg syndrome: - Results from acute infarct in dorsolateral medulla region of brainstem. - Symptoms: Dysphagia (difficulty swallowing), dysarthria (difficulty speaking), gait ataxia (unsteadiness while walking). - Dorsolateral medulla: Controls various functions; symptom severity varies based on infarct location and extent Incorrect Options: Option A - Dejerine's anterior bulbar syndrome: - Rare condition from damage to lower part of brainstem. - Symptoms: Paralysis of muscles for chewing, swallowing, and speaking. - Different from Wallenberg syndrome, which involves dorsolateral medulla specifically. Option C - Babinski-Nageotte syndrome: - Babinski-Nageotte syndrome is a term that is not commonly used in medical literature. It may refer to a combination of symptoms involving weakness or paralysis of the arm and leg on one side of the body, along with abnormal reflexes. However, this is not a well-defined syndrome Option D - Weber syndrome: - Results from midbrain damage, leading to weakness or paralysis on one side of the body. - Other symptoms: Abnormal eye movements.","sourceRef":"Question Page 201; Solution Pages 246-247","sourcePage":201,"sourcePageEnd":201},{"id":"anatomy-6-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Arrange in sequence the structures involved in the direct pathway of the basal ganglia:- Striatum Globus pallidus Substantia nigra Thalamus Cortex","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3,4,5"},{"letter":"B","text":"5,4,2,31"},{"letter":"C","text":"5,1,2,3,4"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,3,4,2,5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 5,1,2,3,4","explanation":"Correct Option C - 5,1,2,3,4: - The correct sequence of structures involved in the direct pathway of the basal ganglia is as follows: Cortex → Striatum → Globus pallidus → Substantia nigra → Thalamus Incorrect Options - A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 201-202; Solution Page 247","sourcePage":201,"sourcePageEnd":202},{"id":"anatomy-6-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":37,"question":"The bleeding shown in the image is usually due to which of the following vessels? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bridging veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle meningeal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lenticulostriate artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vertebral artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Middle meningeal artery","explanation":"Correct Option B - Middle meningeal artery: - The image likely shows an epidural hematoma, as suggested by the location and nature of the bleeding near the dura mater and skull - The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the maxillary artery that supplies the meninges. - It is the most common source of bleeding in an epidural hematoma, which is characterized by blood accumulation between the dura mater and the skull. - Epidural hematomas often present with a biconvex (lens-shaped) appearance on imaging studies. Incorrect Options: Option A - Bridging veins: - Bridging veins are veins that drain the cerebral cortex and traverse the subdural space to empty into the dural venous sinuses. - When these veins are torn, it typically results in a subdural hematoma, which is a collection of blood between the dura mater and the arachnoid mater. Option C - Lenticulostriate artery: - The lenticulostriate arteries are small branches of the middle cerebral artery that supply deep structures in the brain, including parts of the basal ganglia and internal capsule. - Rupture of these arteries typically causes intracerebral hemorrhage. Option D - Vertebral artery: - The vertebral arteries are major arteries of the neck that supply blood to the posterior part of the brain. - Injuries to the vertebral arteries can result in subarachnoid hemorrhage or posterior fossa bleeding.","sourceRef":"Question Page 202; Solution Pages 247-248","sourcePage":202,"sourcePageEnd":202},{"id":"anatomy-6-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Identify the cranial nerve that arises from the marked part. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oculomotor nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abducens nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facial nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Abducens nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Abducens nerve: - The image shows the brainstem and the marked region is that of the pontomedullary junction. - The Abducens nerve or cranial nerve VI arises from the pontomedullary junction. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 202-203; Solution Page 248","sourcePage":202,"sourcePageEnd":203},{"id":"anatomy-6-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":39,"question":"Which of the following options are included in the limbic system? Amygdala Hippocampus Thalamus","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,3"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,2,3"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1,2,3","explanation":"Correct Option D - 1,2,3: - The limbic system is a complex network of structures in the brain that plays a vital role in emotion, memory, and behavior. - The limbic system consists of several interconnected components, including the thalamus, hypothalamus, basal ganglia, cingulate gyrus, hippocampus, and amygdala. Incorrect Options - A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 203; Solution Pages 248-249","sourcePage":203,"sourcePageEnd":203},{"id":"anatomy-6-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":40,"question":"The correct sequence of the auditory pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cochlea → Spiral Ganglion → Cochlear Nerve → Superior Olivary N"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spiral Ganglion → Cochlea → Cochlear Nerve → Superior Olivary N"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spiral Ganglion → Cochlear Nerve → Cochlea → Superior Olivary N"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cochlea → Spiral Ganglion → Cochlear Nerve → Inferior Olivary N"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Cochlea → Spiral Ganglion → Cochlear Nerve → Superior Olivary N","explanation":"Correct Option A: Cochlea → Spiral Ganglion → Cochlear Nerve → Superior Olivary N: Auditory pathway: Cochlea ↓ Cochlear ganglion/Spiral ganglion ↓ Cochlear nerve ↓ Superior olivary nucleus ↓ Lateral lemniscus ↓ Inferior colliculus ↓ Medial geniculate body ↓ Sublentiform fibres of the internal capsule ↓ Heschl's gyrus - Brodmann's area 41,42 - temporal lobe Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 203-204; Solution Page 249","sourcePage":203,"sourcePageEnd":204},{"id":"anatomy-6-41","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Which artery is involved in Lateral medullary syndrome?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior cerebellar artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Posterior inferior cerebellar artery: - The occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery causes lateral medullary syndrome also called the Wallenberg syndrome. Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 204; Solution Page 249","sourcePage":204,"sourcePageEnd":204},{"id":"anatomy-6-42","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":42,"question":"The patient presented with ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the face, Horner's syndrome, and Contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the body. Which artery is involved in the syndrome based on clinical features?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior inferior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior cerebellar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Basilar artery."},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior inferior cerebellar artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Posterior inferior cerebellar artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Posterior inferior cerebellar artery: - The patient’s presentation with ipsilateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the face, Horner's syndrome, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation in the body is suggestive lateral medullary syndrome also called the Wallenberg syndrome. - The occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery causes lateral medullary syndrome also called the Wallenberg syndrome. Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 204; Solution Pages 249-250","sourcePage":204,"sourcePageEnd":204},{"id":"anatomy-6-43","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":43,"question":"Arrange the neural columns in medial-to-lateral order in the floor of fourth ventricle 1. Special somatic afferent 2. Special visceral afferent 3. General somatic afferent 4. General somatic afferent","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GVA, SVA, GSA, SSA"},{"letter":"B","text":"SVA, GSA, GVA, SSA"},{"letter":"C","text":"SSA, GSA, SVE, GVA"},{"letter":"D","text":"GSA, SSA, SVA, GSA"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. GVA, SVA, GSA, SSA","explanation":"Correct Option A - GVA, SVA, GSA, SSA: - The neural columns are arranged in the alar lamina of the developing spinal cord in the following order: Medial to lateral: GVE → SVE → SE → GVA →SVA → GSA → SSA Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 204-205; Solution Pages 250-251","sourcePage":204,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"anatomy-6-44","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":44,"question":"A patient presented with vision loss. On radiological investigation, an aneurysm causing damage to the optic chiasma was noted. Which of the following arteries is most likely to be the artery that is causing the damage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior communicating artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior choroidal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior communicating artery","explanation":"Correct option A - Anterior communicating artery: - The anterior communicating artery is a blood vessel that connects the right and left anterior cerebral arteries. - It runs just above the optic chiasm. - An aneurysm in the anterior communicating artery can press against or compress the optic chiasm, leading to vision loss. Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Page 205; Solution Page 251","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"anatomy-6-45","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":45,"question":"A 34-year-old man presented with a fever and massive splenomegaly. The spleen was 15 cm on palpation and directed obliquely when measured from the right iliac fossa. Which of the following structures prevent the vertical and downward descent of the spleen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lienorenal ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrosplenic ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Phrenicocolic ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lienophrenic ligament"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Phrenicocolic ligament","explanation":"Correct Option C - Phrenicocolic ligament: - The phrenicocolic ligament plays a crucial role in preventing the vertical and downward descent of the spleen. - The phrenicocolic ligament is a fold of peritoneum that connects the left colic flexure to the diaphragm. - It lies anterior to the spleen and helps to hold the spleen in an oblique position, preventing its vertical and downward displacement. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D do not prevent the vertical and downward descent of the spleen. Option A - Lienorenal ligament: The lienorenal ligament connects the spleen to the left kidney and helps to anchor the spleen in its normal position. Option B - Gastrosplenic ligament: - The gastrosplenic ligament connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen. - While it provides some support to the spleen, its main function is to maintain the relationship between the stomach and the spleen. Option D - Lienophrenic ligament: The lienophrenic ligament connects the diaphragm to the superior pole of the spleen.","sourceRef":"Question Page 205; Solution Pages 251-252","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":205},{"id":"anatomy-6-46","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":46,"question":"A patient presented with clinical features of ataxia and incoordination. This is due to thrombosis of which artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior cerebral"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle cerebral"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal carotid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior cerebellar"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Superior cerebellar","explanation":"Correct Option D - Superior cerebellar: - Ataxia and incoordination are neurological symptoms associated with the dysfunction of the cerebellum. - Thrombosis in the superior cerebellar artery can lead to impaired blood flow to the cerebellum, resulting in ataxia and incoordination. Incorrect Options - A, B, and C do not supply the cerebellum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 205-206; Solution Page 252","sourcePage":205,"sourcePageEnd":206},{"id":"anatomy-6-47","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":47,"question":"Where is the probable location of the lesion in a patient who has presented with weakness and loss of pain and temperature sensation on the right side of the face along with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left leg?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial medulla"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral pons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial pons"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral medulla"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lateral pons","explanation":"Correct Option B - Lateral pons: - The patient’s presentation with weakness and loss of pain and temperature sensation on the right side of the face along with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the left leg is suggestive of lateral pontine syndrome. - In lateral pontine syndrome, the lesion affects structures such as the descending corticospinal tract and the spinothalamic tract. - This results in ipsilateral facial nerve palsy and contralateral loss of pain and temperature sensation. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Page 206; Solution Page 252","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":206},{"id":"anatomy-6-48","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":48,"question":"Identify the cell marked in the histological image of the cerebellum shown below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Purkinje cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basket cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Golgi cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Granule cell"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Purkinje cells","explanation":"Correct Option A - Purkinje cells: - In the given histological image of the cerebellum, the marked cell resembling the characteristic morphology of a large, flask-shaped neuron with multiple dendritic branches, is a Purkinje cell. - They are characterized by a distinct morphology with a large soma, multiple branching dendrites, and a single axon that projects into the deep cerebellar nuclei. Incorrect Options: Option B - Basket cells: Basket cells have a characteristic basket-like arrangement of their axonal terminals around the soma of Purkinje cells. Option C - Golgi cells: Golgi cells have a more scattered distribution compared to basket cells and form synapses onto granule cells in the cerebellar cortex. Option D - Granule cells: Granule cells are small, densely packed cells that form the granule cell layer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 206-207; Solution Pages 252-253","sourcePage":206,"sourcePageEnd":207},{"id":"anatomy-6-49","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":49,"question":"At what level does the spinal cord end in infants?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L1"},{"letter":"B","text":"L2"},{"letter":"C","text":"L3"},{"letter":"D","text":"L4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. L3","explanation":"Correct Option C - L3: In neonates, the spinal cord ends at the upper border of L3 after which the cord moves up with age whereas in adults, the spinal cord terminates at the level of the lower border of L1. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Page 207; Solution Page 253","sourcePage":207,"sourcePageEnd":207},{"id":"anatomy-6-50","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":50,"question":"Please identify the structure indicated in the image below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Midbrain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medulla oblongata"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spinal cord"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pons","explanation":"Correct Option B - Pons: - The marked structure in the image is the pons. - It is visible on an MRI or CT scan as a larger bulge in the brainstem. - In a sagittal section, the pons may appear as a larger, oval-shaped structure located below the midbrain and above the medulla oblongata. Incorrect Options: Option A - Midbrain: - In a sagittal section, the midbrain may appear as a small round structure located above the pons and below the thalamus. - It is located anterior to the fourth ventricle and has a distinctive \"butterfly\" shape on axial views. Option C - Medulla oblongata: - The medulla oblongata is visible on an MRI or CT scan as a tapering structure that connects the brainstem to the spinal cord. - In a sagittal section, the medulla oblongata may appear as a smaller, pointed structure located below the pons. - The medulla oblongata appears as a tapering structure that connects the brainstem to the spinal cord and is located at the bottom of the brainstem. Option D - Spinal cord: - The spinal cord is located within the spinal canal, which is formed by the vertebrae of the spine. - In a sagittal section, the spinal cord may appear as a central canal surrounded by white matter and gray matter.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 207-208; Solution Pages 253-254","sourcePage":207,"sourcePageEnd":208},{"id":"anatomy-6-51","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":51,"question":"A 6-month-old boy was brought to the casualty with seizures. The pediatrician tries to do CSF sampling. What are the structures punctured by the pediatrician while piercing through the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scalp, dura mater, arachnoid mater"},{"letter":"B","text":"Scalp, epicranium, dura mater"},{"letter":"C","text":"Scalp synchondral membrane, dura mater, arachnoid mater"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pericranium, dura mater, endocranium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Scalp, dura mater, arachnoid mater","explanation":"Correct Option A - Scalp, dura mater, arachnoid mater: - The structures pierced while collecting CSF samples from anterior fontanelle are scalp, dura mater, and arachnoid mater. - The procedure involves inserting a needle into the anterior fontanelle, which is the soft spot on the baby's skull where the bones have not yet fully fused. . Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Page 208; Solution Pages 254-255","sourcePage":208,"sourcePageEnd":208},{"id":"anatomy-6-52","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":52,"question":"Fibers from the marked structure terminate at which of the following? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fastigial nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior olivary nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Red nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subthalamus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Red nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option C - Red nucleus - The marked structure is a superior cerebellar peduncle. - The fibers from the superior cerebellar peduncle terminate at various destinations, including the red nucleus, which is located in the midbrain. - The red nucleus is involved in motor control and plays a role in coordinating movement. - It receives input from the cerebellum via the superior cerebellar peduncle and sends output signals to other motor-related structures. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are not associated with the fibers of the superior cerebellar peduncle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 208-209; Solution Page 255","sourcePage":208,"sourcePageEnd":209},{"id":"anatomy-6-53","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":53,"question":"A woman with right-sided loss of sensations of both the upper and lower limb complains of shooting pain from her fingers to the right shoulder and a burning sensation when touching cold water. Motor functions are normal. Which of the following structures is likely to be involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. B","explanation":"Thalamic pain syndrome: - It is characterized by chronic, severe, and often debilitating pain. - The condition is characterized by persistent, intense pain that is often described as burning, shooting, or electric shock-like in nature . - The pain is typically localized to the contralateral side of the bod y (opposite to the side of thalamic damage) and may affect multiple regions, including the face, limbs, or trunk. Apart from the debilitating pain, individuals with thalamic pain syndrome may also experience other sensory abnormalities, such as altered temperature perception, increased sensitivity to touch (allodynia), or even the absence of normal sensations (hypoesthesia).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 209-210; Solution Page 255","sourcePage":209,"sourcePageEnd":210},{"id":"anatomy-6-54","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":54,"question":"Through which of the following marked sites does COVID 19 spread to the brain? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"D"},{"letter":"B","text":"C"},{"letter":"C","text":"B"},{"letter":"D","text":"A"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. A","explanation":"Correct Option D - A: - A in the above diagram is the cribriform plate. - It has been reported that COVID-19 can potentially enter the brain through the olfactory route. - The virus may gain access to the cribriform plate by infecting the cells of the nasal epithelium, which express angiotensin-converting enzyme 2 (ACE2) receptors. - From there, the virus can travel along the olfactory nerves, passing through the olfactory foramina of the cribriform plate and reaching the olfactory bulb. - From the olfactory bulb, the virus may further spread to other areas of the brain. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D are not the sites of the spread of COVID-19 to the brain. Option A: D: D in the above diagram is the foramen ovale. Option B: C: C in the above diagram is the clinoid process. Option C: B: B in the above diagram is the optic canal.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 210-211; Solution Pages 255-256","sourcePage":210,"sourcePageEnd":211},{"id":"anatomy-6-55","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":55,"question":"Identify the cranial nerve passing through the given structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"III"},{"letter":"B","text":"V"},{"letter":"C","text":"IV"},{"letter":"D","text":"VI"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. IV","explanation":"Correct Option C - IV - The structure marked in the image is the tectum of the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus. - The cranial nerve that passes through the tectum of the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus is the trochlear nerve. Incorrect Options: Option A - III: The oculomotor nerve is located more ventrally in the midbrain. Option B - V: It arises from the pons. Option D - VI: It originates from the pons and passes through the cavernous sinus before reaching the eye muscles.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 211-212; Solution Pages 256-257","sourcePage":211,"sourcePageEnd":212},{"id":"anatomy-6-56","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":56,"question":"A young child with absent thymus is brought to the OPD with features of hypoparathyroidism and tetany. Which of the following marked area in the picture is defective in this condition? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 3","explanation":"Correct Option C - 3 - In this condition, where a young child presents with absent thymus, hypoparathyroidism, and tetany, the defect is likely associated with the third pharyngeal pouch and the syndrome is named DiGeorge syndrome . - The third pharyngeal pouch gives rise to th e thymus and the inferior parathyroid glands. Incorrect Options: Option A - First Pharyngeal Pouch: The first pouch forms the auditory tube and middle ear cavity, leading to ear problems like hearing loss or recurrent ear infections if defective. Option B - Second Pharyngeal Pouch:The second pharyngeal pouch forms the epithelial lining of the palatine tonsils. Problems with this pouch typically lead to issues like recurrent tonsillitis or difficulty swallowing.. Option C - Fourth Pharyngeal Pouch: The fourth pouch forms the superior parathyroid glands and contributes to thyroid C-cell development. Issues with this pouch can affect parathyroid and thyroid function.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 212-213; Solution Page 257","sourcePage":212,"sourcePageEnd":213},{"id":"anatomy-6-57","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":57,"question":"Fibers from the marked structure end in which of the following areas of the thalamus? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reticular nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventral lateral nucleus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior nucleus","explanation":"Correct Option C - Anterior nucleus: The mamillary bodies, in the posterior hypothalamus, play a vital role in memory within the Papez circuit. They relay memory-related information to the anterior nucleus of the thalamus through the mammillothalamic tract, facilitating memory consolidation and retrieval. This anterior nucleus, integral to the limbic system, maintains connections with the cingulate gyrus andhippocampus. Incorrect Options: Option A - Posterior nucleus: The posterior nucleus of the thalamus processes somatosensory data from the spinothalamic tract, handling sensations like pain, temperature, and touch. However, it is not where mamillary body fibers terminate. Option B - Reticular nucleus: The reticular nucleus, encircling the thalamus, regulates thalamic activity and information flow but isn't directly targeted by mamillary body fibers. Option D - Ventral lateral nucleus: The ventral lateral nucleus of the thalamus is key for motor control, connecting with the basal ganglia and cerebellum before sending signals to the motor cortex. However, it isn't directly linked to mamillary body fibers.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 213-214; Solution Pages 257-258","sourcePage":213,"sourcePageEnd":214},{"id":"anatomy-6-58","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":58,"question":"The cell marked in the below slide is inhibitory to **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deep nuclei of cerebellum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vestibular nuclei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior olivary nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Basal ganglia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Deep nuclei of cerebellum","explanation":"Correct Option A - Deep nuclei of cerebellum: - Purkinje cells inhibit the deep nuclei of the cerebellum, including the fastigial nucleus, interposed nuclei, and dentate nucleus, via gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA). This inhibition fine-tunes cerebellar output, regulating timing, force, and coordination of movements. Incorrect Options - Option B - Vestibular nuclei: Purkinje cells don't directly inhibit the vestibular nuclei in the brainstem. These nuclei, vital for balance and spatial orientation, receive input from the vestibular system. Their interaction with Purkinje cells isn't inhibitory. Option C - Inferior olivary nucleus:. The interaction between the Purkinje cells and the inferior olivary nucleus is part of the cerebellar circuitry involved in motor coordination and learning, but it does not involve direct inhibition. Option D - Basal ganglia: The Purkinje cells and the basal ganglia are not directly connected in terms of inhibitory interactions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 214-215; Solution Page 258","sourcePage":214,"sourcePageEnd":215},{"id":"anatomy-6-59","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":59,"question":"Which fibre is marked by the arrow in the image given below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Short association"},{"letter":"B","text":"Long association"},{"letter":"C","text":"Projection"},{"letter":"D","text":"Commissural"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Projection","explanation":"Correct Option C - Projection: The region marked in the image is the genu of the internal capsule. The internal capsule is composed of projection fibers passing to and from the brainstem and the cerebral cortex. It consists of 3 parts: an anterior limb, a posterior limb, and a genu. Projection fibers are neural fibers that transmit signals between the cerebral cortex and lower brain regions or the spinal cord. They carry sensory information from the peripheral nervous system to the brain (ascending pathways) and motor commands from the brain to the peripheral nervous system (descending pathways). Incorrect Option: Option A - Short association: Short association fibers are neural connections that link different areas within the same cerebral hemisphere. In the internal capsule image, they wouldn't stand out prominently as it's primarily composed of projection fibers. Option B - Long association: Long association fibers connect different cortical areas within the same hemisphere and allow for communication and integration between distant regions. They would not be expected to be specifically marked in the image of the internal capsule. Option D - Commissural: Commissural fibers are neural fibers that connect corresponding areas in the two cerebral hemispheres, allowing for communication and integration between the left and right hemispheres.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 215-216; Solution Pages 258-259","sourcePage":215,"sourcePageEnd":216},{"id":"anatomy-6-60","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":60,"question":"Identify the structure marked B in the image given below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pineal gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"Falx cerebri"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fornix"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pituitary gland"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Fornix","explanation":"Correct Option C - Fornix The fornix is a bundle of nerve fibers that plays a crucial role in the limbic system and is involved in memory and spatial navigation. It connects various parts of the brain, including the hippocampus and mammillary bodies. This is the structure marked as B in the image. Incorrect Option: Option A - Pineal gland The pineal gland is a small endocrine gland located near the center of the brain, but it is not the structure marked in the image. Option B - Falx cerebri The falx cerebri is a fold of dura mater that separates the two cerebral hemispheres. It is not the structure marked in the image. Option D - Pituitary gland The pituitary gland is an endocrine gland located at the base of the brain, but it is not the structure marked in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 216-217; Solution Pages 259-260","sourcePage":216,"sourcePageEnd":217},{"id":"anatomy-6-61","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":61,"question":"In this image of basal ganglia, all of the following are correctly labeled except? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-caudate nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"2-globus pallidus"},{"letter":"C","text":"3-putamen"},{"letter":"D","text":"4-internal capsule"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 4-internal capsule","explanation":"Correct Option D - 4-internal capsule: - The Structure marked as 4 in the image is the external capsule . It is not the internal capsule. It is related medially to the caudate nucleus and Thalamus and laterally to the Lentiform nucleus. Incorrect Option: Option A - 1-Caudate nucleus: The structure marked No.1 in the image correctly labeled the caudate nucleus. Option B - 2-Globus pallidus : The structure marked No.2 in the image correctly labeled the globus pallidus. Option C - 3-putamen: The structure marked No.3 in the image correctly labeled the Putamen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 217-218; Solution Pages 260-261","sourcePage":217,"sourcePageEnd":218},{"id":"anatomy-6-62","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":62,"question":"Broca’s area is situated in the_________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior frontal gyrus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior temporal gyrus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Angular gyrus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Postcentral gyrus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Inferior frontal gyrus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Inferior frontal gyrus - Broca's area, also known as Brodmann area 44 and 45 , is a region of the brain associated with language production and speech articulation. - Broca's area is situated in the inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant hemisphere. - It is specifically involved in language production and speech articulation, playing a crucial role in the ability to express oneself verbally. Incorrect Options : Option B - Superior temporal gyrus: The superior temporal gyrus handles auditory processing and language comprehension but is not where Broca's area is located. Option C - Angular gyrus:The angular gyrus, in the parietal lobe, aids in reading, writing, and math, but it is not the precise location of Broca's area. . Option D - Postcentral gyrus: The postcentral gyrus handles somatosensory data like touch and pressure, not speech production like Broca's area.","sourceRef":"Question Page 218; Solution Pages 261-262","sourcePage":218,"sourcePageEnd":218},{"id":"anatomy-6-63","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":63,"question":"Which of the following cranial nerve nuclei is not a part of the special visceral afferent (SVA) column?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trochlear nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Trochlear nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B - Trochlear nerve - The trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV) is primarily involved in eye movement, specifically the downward and inward rotation of the eye. - It is part of the general somatic efferent column. - It does not have a role in special visceral afferent functions such as taste or smell. Incorrect Options: Option A - Facial nerve: The facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) carries taste sensations from the front two-thirds of the tongue to the nucleus solitarius, part of the special visceral afferent (SVA) column. Option C - Vagus nerve: The vagus nerve (cranial nerve X) carries taste sensations from the epiglottis and lower pharynx to the nucleus solitarius, part of the special visceral afferent (SVA) column. Option D - Glossopharyngeal nerve: The glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX) transmits taste sensations from the back one-third of the tongue to the nucleus solitarius via the special visceral afferent (SVA) column.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 218-219; Solution Page 262","sourcePage":218,"sourcePageEnd":219},{"id":"anatomy-6-64","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":64,"question":"Which is the last fontanelle to close?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Occipital"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mastoid"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior","explanation":"Correct Option A - Anterior - The anterior fontanelle closes between 18-24 months after birth . Incorrect Options: Option B - Posterior: The posterior fontanelle close, usually by the age of 2 to 3 months. Therefore, it is not the last fontanelle to close. Option C - Occipital: The occipital fontanelle is not a term used to describe a fontanelle. The occipital region of the skull refers to the back of the head, but it does not specifically indicate the presence of a fontanelle. Option D - Mastoid: The mastoid fontanelle close, usually by the age of 1 year Therefore, it is not the last fontanelle to close.","sourceRef":"Question Page 219; Solution Pages 262-263","sourcePage":219,"sourcePageEnd":219},{"id":"anatomy-6-65","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-6","chapter":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 6 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":6,"questionNumber":65,"question":"The nerve of the second branchial arch is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vagal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Facial nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Facial nerve - The nerve of the second branchial arch is the facial nerve . - During embryonic development, the branchial arches form the basic structure of the face and neck, and each branchial arch is associated with specific cranial nerves. The second branchial arch is innervated by the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). Incorrect Options: Option B - Glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX): The glossopharyngeal nerve is associated with the third branchial arch Option C - Trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V): The trigeminal nerve is associated with the first branchial arch Option D - Vagal nerve (cranial nerve X): The vagal nerve is associated with the fourth and sixth branchial arches , not the second arch.","sourceRef":"Question Page 219; Solution Page 263","sourcePage":219,"sourcePageEnd":219},{"id":"anatomy-7-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which among the following are Skeletal derivatives of 3rd pharyngeal arch? Greater cornu of hyoid Stylohyoid ligament Lesser cornu of hyoid Upper half of body of hyoid Lower half of body of hyoid","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"All the above"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"5 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 and 5"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1 and 5","explanation":"Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch Skeletal derivatives Muscles of arch (Option D) Third Glossopharyngeal Common carotid Greater cornu of Stylopharyngeus nerve artery Internal hyoid Lower half of carotid artery body of hyoid","sourceRef":"Question Page 264; Solution Pages 270-271","sourcePage":264,"sourcePageEnd":264},{"id":"anatomy-7-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Arrange the events in the development of the thyroid gland in the correct sequence: Fusion with caudal pharyngeal complex Formation of pharyngeal arches Thyroglossal duct formation Tuberculum impar is formed","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2,4,3,1"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,2,3,4"},{"letter":"C","text":"4,3,2,1"},{"letter":"D","text":"3,1,2,4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 2,4,3,1","explanation":"Development of thyroid gland:","sourceRef":"Question Page 264; Solution Page 271","sourcePage":264,"sourcePageEnd":264},{"id":"anatomy-7-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements on double aortic arch is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dyspnea and dysphagia are symptoms"},{"letter":"B","text":"Compression of esophagus and trachea"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right dorsal aorta is persisted"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right aortic arch passes in front of the trachea and esophagus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Right aortic arch passes in front of the trachea and esophagus","explanation":"In a double aortic arch, the right aortic arch passes behind the trachea and esophagus, not in front of it. Double aortic arch: - A developmental anomaly in which a vascular ring surrounds the trachea and esophagus. - The dorsal aorta is a midline artery dorsal to the gut - In normal development, the right dorsal aorta between the origin of the 7th cervical intersegmental artery and its fusion with the left dorsal aorta disappears. - In this case, the right dorsal aorta persisted. - The right aortic arch passes behind the trachea and esophagus. (Option D) - The left aortic arch passes in front of the trachea and esophagus, forming a vascular ring. - The vascular ring compresses the trachea and esophagus, leading to dyspnea and dysphagia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 264-265; Solution Pages 271-273","sourcePage":264,"sourcePageEnd":265},{"id":"anatomy-7-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which pharyngeal arch primarily contributes to the development of the anterior two-thirds of the tongue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Second pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Third pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fourth pharyngeal arch"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. First pharyngeal arch","explanation":"Development of tongue: Begins around the fourth week of intrauterine life. Anterior 2/3rd of tongue: - The development begins with the growth of medial swelling from the first pharyngeal arch known as tuberculum impar. - Gradually, two lateral lingual swellings start to grow in the 5th week from the same arch. - As the lateral swelling grows in size they eventually merge and overlap tuberculum impar leading to the formation of anterior 2/3rd of tongue. Posterior 1/3rd of tongue: - From the mesoderm of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th pharyngeal arches, a median swelling known as hypobranchial eminence begins to develop and forms the posterior 1/3rd of the tongue. Posterior most of tongue: - Develops from third median swelling arising from fourth pharyngeal arch. Taste buds development: - First sign of taste bud development on lingual epithelium occurs at 8th week of gestation. - Between 9-11th week of gestation, many taste bud primordia develop.","sourceRef":"Question Page 265; Solution Pages 273-274","sourcePage":265,"sourcePageEnd":265},{"id":"anatomy-7-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 4-year-old child presents with recurrent infections and signs of hypocalcemia. Investigation shows low levels of parathyroid hormone. The physician suspects an embryological defect. Which among the following could be a possible reason?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Defect in the development of 1st pharyngeal pouch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Defect in the development of 2nd pharyngeal pouch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Defect in the development of 3rd pharyngeal pouch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Defect in the development of 5th pharyngeal pouch"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Defect in the development of 3rd pharyngeal pouch","explanation":"- Here the scenario is suggestive of defect in thymus development (recurrent infections) and defect in development of parathyroid gland ( signs of hypocalcemia and low levels of parathyroid hormone). - The 3rd pharyngeal pouch is responsible for the development of thymus and inferior parathyroid gland while the 4th pharyngeal pouch is responsible for the development of superior parathyroid gland. Hence, there could be a defect in only the 3rd pharyngeal pouch or defect could be in both 3rd and 4th pharyngeal pouches. Pharyngeal arches, clefts and pouches Pharyngeal arches Pharyngeal arches are key structures in head and neck development, appearing in the fourth and fifth weeks as mesenchymal tissue bars. They resemble gills but do not function as such in humans. Pharyngeal arches are divided by pharyngeal clefts on the outside and pharyngeal pouches on the inside. Pharyngeal pouches develop as outpocketings from the lateral walls of the pharynx and do not connect to the exterior, playing crucial roles in organ development. Each arch incorporates neural crest cells essential for forming facial skeleton components, while mesoderm differentiates into facial and neck musculature. Each arch has its own cranial nerve and artery. Pharyngeal Pouches Four Pairs (fifth is rudimentary) First Pouch : Forms the middle ear cavity and Eustachian tube, contributes to the tympanic membrane. Second Pouch : Develops into palatine tonsils; remnants persist as the tonsillar fossa. Third Pouch: Dorsal wing becomes the inferior parathyroid gland; ventral wing forms the thymus, migrating to the thorax. Fourth Pouch : Dorsal region forms the superior parathyroid gland; ventral region becomes the ultimobranchial body, integrating into the thyroid. Pharyngeal Clefts Five-week-old Embryo: Four clefts are present. First Cleft: Does not form the external auditory meatus (EAM); instead, the EAM arises from surface ectoderm invagination. It eventually disappears due to overgrowth by the second arch. Second, Third, and Fourth Clefts: Overgrown by the second arch, they lose exterior contact and may temporarily form the cervical sinus, which ultimately disappears.","sourceRef":"Question Page 265; Solution Pages 274-275","sourcePage":265,"sourcePageEnd":265},{"id":"anatomy-7-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following muscles of the first pharyngeal arch are migratory? Mylohyoid Masseter Temporalis Tensor tympani Tensor palati","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"All the above"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"All except 5 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"All except 4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. All except 4","explanation":"Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch Skeletal derivatives Muscles of arch First Maxillary (Sensory) Maxillary artery Meckel's cartilage Masseter Temporalis and mandibular Malleus Incus Medial pterygoid (Motor and sensory) Anterior ligament of Lateral pterygoids branches of malleus Sphenoman Mylohyoid Anterior trigeminal nerve dibular ligament belly of digastric Mandible Maxilla Tensor tympani, and Zygomatic Palatine Tensor palati All are Temporal (part) migratory except T. tympani (Option D) Pharyngeal arch Muscles First Masseter Temporalis Medial pterygoid Lateral pterygoids Mylohyoid Anterior belly of digastric Tensor tympani, and Tensor palati Second Stapedius Stylohyoid Posterior belly of digastric Epicranius Orbicularis oculi Orbicularis oris Zygomaticus Buccinator Nasal muscles Platysma Levator labii superioris, levator labii inferioris Levator anguli oris Auricular muscles Third Stylopharyngeus Fourth Larynx–Cricothyroid Muscles of pharynx Intrinsic muscles of soft palate except tensor palati Sixth All intrinsic muscles of larynx except cricothyroid","sourceRef":"Question Pages 265-266; Solution Pages 275-277","sourcePage":265,"sourcePageEnd":266},{"id":"anatomy-7-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which among the following are formed from Reichert's cartilage? Stapes Styloid process Stylohyoid ligament Lesser cornu of hyoid Upper half of body of hyoid","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"All the above"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"All except 5 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"All except 4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. All the above","explanation":"Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch Skeletal derivatives Muscles of arch Second Facial nerve Hyoid artery The cartilage of the Stapedius Stylohyoid Stapedial artery second arch Posterior belly of (Reichert’s) forms digastric Epicranius the following: Orbicularis oculi (Option A) Stapes Orbicularis oris Styloid process Zygomaticus Stylohyoid ligament Buccinator Nasal Lesser cornu of muscles Platysma hyoid Upper half of Levator labii body of hyoid superioris, levator labii inferioris Levator anguli oris Auricular muscles All are migratory except stapedius, stylohyoid","sourceRef":"Question Page 266; Solution Page 277","sourcePage":266,"sourcePageEnd":266},{"id":"anatomy-7-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Match the following pharyngeal arch to its corresponding nerve and artery derivatives. Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch 1) Fourth a) Superior laryngeal nerve (Vagus) i) Arch of aorta ii) Subclavian artery 2) Sixth b) Recurrent laryngeal nerve (vagus) iii) Ductus arteriosus iv) Pulmonary artery Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch 1) Fourth a) Superior laryngeal nerve (Vagus) i) Arch of aorta ii) Subclavian artery 2) Sixth b) Recurrent laryngeal nerve (vagus) iii) Ductus arteriosus iv) Pulmonary artery **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 - a - i and iii"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 - b - iii and iv"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 - a - i and ii"},{"letter":"D","text":"Option B and C"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Option B and C","explanation":"Arch Nerve of arch Artery of arch Skeletal derivatives Muscles of arch Fourth Superior laryngeal Arch of aorta (left Thyroid Cricoid Larynx: Cricothyroid branch of vagus side) Subclavian Corniculate Muscles of pharynx nerve artery (right side) Cuneiform Arytenoid Intrinsic muscles of Thyrohyoid ligament soft palate except Epiglottis: From tensor palati mesodermal core of hypobranchial eminence Sixth Recurrent laryngeal Ductus arteriosus All intrinsic muscles branch of vagus (left side) Pulmonary of larynx except nerve artery (right side) cricothyroid Note: High-pitch phonation is controlled by the cricothyroid muscle, which develops from the 4th pharyngeal arch and is innervated by the external branch of the superior laryngeal nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 266; Solution Pages 277-278","sourcePage":266,"sourcePageEnd":266},{"id":"anatomy-7-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Match the pharyngeal arches to the nerves arising from it. Pharyngeal arch Nerve a. First 1. Facial nerve b. Second 2. Glossopharyngeal nerve c. Third 3. Chorda tympani d. Sixth 4. Recurrent laryngeal nerve Pharyngeal arch Nerve a. First 1. Facial nerve b. Second 2. Glossopharyngeal nerve c. Third 3. Chorda tympani d. Sixth 4. Recurrent laryngeal nerve","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4","explanation":"Pharyngeal Arch Pretrematic Nerve Post Trematic Nerve First Chorda tympani (branch of facial Maxillary (sensory) and mandibular nerve) (motor & sensory) branches of trigeminal nerve (CN V) Second Possible: Tympanic branch of Facial nerve (CN VII) glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) Third Possible: Auricular branch of vagus Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) nerve (CN X) Fourth - Superior laryngeal branch of vagus nerve (CN X) Sixth - Recurrent laryngeal branch of vagus nerve (CN X)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 266-267; Solution Pages 278-279","sourcePage":266,"sourcePageEnd":267},{"id":"anatomy-7-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is the correct pairing of the pharyngeal pouch with its derivative?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1st pharyngeal pouch-Thymus"},{"letter":"B","text":"2nd pharyngeal pouch-Tonsillar fossa"},{"letter":"C","text":"3rd pharyngeal pouch-Auditory tube"},{"letter":"D","text":"4th pharyngeal pouch-Palatine tonsil"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2nd pharyngeal pouch-Tonsillar fossa","explanation":"Pharyngeal Pouch Derivatives 1st Pharyngeal Pouch Tympanic cavity Auditory tube Inner surface of the eardrum 2nd Pharyngeal Pouch Palatine tonsil Tonsillar fossa 3rd Pharyngeal Pouch Thymus Inferior parathyroid glands 4th Pharyngeal Pouch Superior parathyroid glands Thyroid gland","sourceRef":"Question Page 267; Solution Pages 279-280","sourcePage":267,"sourcePageEnd":267},{"id":"anatomy-7-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Match the pharyngeal arch with the artery developing from it Pharyngeal arch Artery a. First 1. Hyoid artery b. Second 2. Maxillary artery c. Third 3. Arch of aorta d. Fourth 4. Internal carotid artery Pharyngeal arch Artery a. First 1. Hyoid artery b. Second 2. Maxillary artery c. Third 3. Arch of aorta d. Fourth 4. Internal carotid artery **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-3, b-2, c-1, d-4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. a-2, b-1, c-4, d-3","explanation":"Pharyngeal Arch Artery of Arch First Maxillary artery Second Hyoid artery , Stapedial artery Third Common carotid artery, Internal carotid artery Fourth Arch of aorta (left side), Subclavian artery (right side) Sixth Ductus arteriosus (left side), Pulmonary artery (right side)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 267-268; Solution Pages 280-281","sourcePage":267,"sourcePageEnd":268},{"id":"anatomy-7-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-7","chapter":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topic":"Lesson 7 - Pharyngeal Arches, Pouches and Clefts","topicIndex":7,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following nerves provides general sensation to the area of the tongue marked in blue? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lingual nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lingual nerve","explanation":"Nerve supply of tongue: Sensory innervation: Part of Tongue General Sensation Taste Sensation Anterior two-third Trigeminal nerve (V3) (specifically Chorda tympani branch of the facial the lingual branch) nerve (CN VII) Posterior one-third Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) Root of the tongue and the area Vagus nerve (CN X) Vagus nerve (CN X) around the epiglottis Motor innervation: - The hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) provides motor innervation to all of the intrinsic and extrinsic muscles of the tongue except for the palatoglossus muscle, which is innervated by the vagus nerve (CN X).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 268-269; Solution Pages 281-282","sourcePage":268,"sourcePageEnd":269},{"id":"anatomy-8-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":1,"question":"The base of the skull is primarily formed by which type of ossification?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Intramembranous ossification"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endochondral ossification"},{"letter":"C","text":"Partly intramembranous and partly endochondral"},{"letter":"D","text":"Perichondral ossification"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Endochondral ossification","explanation":"There are two major modes of osteogenesis which involve transformation of pre-existing mesenchymal tissue into bone tissue. Membranous Ossification Endochondral Ossification Bone forms directly from mesenchymal tissue without a Bone is formed by first creating a cartilage template, cartilage intermediate. Cranial vault forms through which is then replaced by bone. Cranial base forms membranous ossification Bones that undergo though endochondral ossification Bones that undergo membranous ossification: Flat bones of the skull which endochondral ossification: Base of the skull: Occipital include bones like the frontal bone parietal bones nasal bone ( except the squamous part ) Sphenoid bone bones lacrimal bones squamous part of the temporal and Ethmoid bone Petrous part of the temporal bone Condyle occipital bones Maxilla and Mandible (except the condylar of the mandible. Process : In endochondral ossification, a process of the mandible, which forms by endochondral cartilage model of the bone forms first. This cartilage is ossification). Process : Mesenchymal cells differentiate progressively replaced by bone as it is calcified and into osteoblasts, which start forming bone directly by invaded by blood vessels, bringing osteoblasts and secreting osteoid (bone matrix). This process is osteoclasts to reshape the bone. characteristic of the bones that contribute to the vault of the skull (calvaria). Note-Occipital bone is a membranocartilaginous bone since the part above nuchal line is formed by membranous ossification and rest is formed by cartilagenous ossification","sourceRef":"Question Page 283; Solution Pages 289-290","sourcePage":283,"sourcePageEnd":283},{"id":"anatomy-8-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which bones form the weakest point of the skull?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones"},{"letter":"B","text":"Frontal, occipital, and temporal bones"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sphenoid and ethmoid bones"},{"letter":"D","text":"Occipital, temporal, and parietal bones"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones","explanation":"Pterion, the junction where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet is deemed the skull’s weakest part. Skull sutures: Cranial sutures are syndesmosis between the cranial bones. Syndesmosis is fibrous joint between 2 bones. Sutures Meeting Point Coronal suture Junction between the frontal and parietal bones Sagittal suture Junction between the parietal bones Lambdoid suture Junction between the parietal and occipital bones Squamous suture Junction between the parietal and temporal bones - Bregma : The point where the sagittal suture and the coronal suture meet. In infants, the anterior fontanelle eventually ossifies and forms the bregma. - Lambda : The point where the sagittal suture and lambdoid suture meet. Anterior Fontanelle Posterior Fontanelle A soft spot in a newborn’s skull, located at the junction of Located at the junction of the sagittal suture and the the sagittal, coronal and frontal (metopic) sutures. lambdoid suture. It is diamond-shaped. It is triangular in shape. Typically closes by 18-24 months of age. Typically closes usually by 6 to 8 weeks after birth.","sourceRef":"Question Page 283; Solution Pages 290-292","sourcePage":283,"sourcePageEnd":283},{"id":"anatomy-8-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which among the following are pneumatic bones?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3,6,7"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,2,3,5,7"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,2,4,6"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,3,5,6,7"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1,2,3,6,7","explanation":"Labelings in the image are: 1 - Frontal 2 - Sphenoid 3 - Maxilla 4 - Mandible 5 - Parietal 6 - Temporal 7 - Mastoid Pneumatic bones: - Frontal (1): Contains sinuses - Sphenoid (2): Contains sinuses - Maxilla (3): Contains sinuses - Temporal (6): Contains ear cavity - Mastoid (7): Contains mastoid air cells","sourceRef":"Question Pages 283-284; Solution Pages 292-294","sourcePage":283,"sourcePageEnd":284},{"id":"anatomy-8-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":4,"question":"All among the following would be of adult size at birth except","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mastoid process"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mastoid antrum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tympanic membrane"},{"letter":"D","text":"Semicircular canal"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Mastoid process","explanation":"Mastoid process is absent at birth and is formed only by 2 years of age and mastoid air cells by 6 years of age, whereas mastoid or tympanic antrum are of adult size at birth . Structures at adult size (at birth) Structures NOT at adult size (at birth) Tympanic membrane (Option C) Tympanic cavity Ear Tegmen tympani External ear (auricle/pinna, external ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes) Mastoid (tympanic) auditory canal) Mastoid process (forms by 2 years age) antrum (Option B) Internal ear (vestibule, cochlea, (Option A) (a shorter mastoid process makes the semicircular canals) (Option D) underlying stylomastoid foramen susceptible to injuries during ear surgeries) Mastoid air cells (forms by 6 years age) Eustachian (auditory) tube To remember: Imagine middle ear cavity as a living room, its door (TM), furniture (ossicles), the bedroom (inner ear), and attached toilet (mastoid antrum) are fixed at birth while the garden outside (air cells) and fence (mastoid process) grows Eustachian tube in child: S horter, broader and straight, hence more risk of infection to middle ear following upper respiratory tract infection.","sourceRef":"Question Page 284; Solution Page 294","sourcePage":284,"sourcePageEnd":284},{"id":"anatomy-8-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A patient following a road traffic accident presented to the ED in a confused state. Ecchymosis around the eyes was noted, and CT showed the following. Which of the following does not contribute to the floor of the affected structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cribriform plate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser wing of sphenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Orbital plate of the frontal bone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Greater wing of sphenoid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Greater wing of sphenoid","explanation":"Given clinical scenario and radiological images are suggestive of Anterior cranial fossa fracture. The greater wing of the sphenoid bone does not contribute to the floor of Anterior cranial fossa. Anterior cranial fossa (ACF): Floor Cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone (anterior) (Option A) Superior surface of the anterior part of the body of the sphenoid or Jugum sphenoidale (posterior) Lesser wing of sphenoid (posterior) (Option B) Orbital plate of frontal (on either side) (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 284-285; Solution Pages 294-296","sourcePage":284,"sourcePageEnd":285},{"id":"anatomy-8-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Following an RTA, a structure passing through the marked foramen was injured. Which of the following defects is likely to be seen in this patient? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of corneal reflex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loss of jaw jerk"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inability to abduct eye"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of sensation over cheek"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Loss of sensation over cheek","explanation":"- Image shows Sphenoid bone with lesser wing and the opening marked is foramen rotundum . - Maxillary division of Trigeminal nerve (V2) and emissary vein passes through foramen rotundum and hence loss of sensations of cheek would be the defect seen in this patient. Superior view: Optic canal Superior orbital fissure Foramen rotundum Foramen spinosum Posterior view - Greater wing: Superior orbital fissure Scaphoid fossa Pterygoid fossa Posterior view - Lesser wing: Optic canal Opening of sphenoid sinus Superior orbital fissure Foramen rotundum Pterygoid canal - Loss of corneal reflex (Option A): Seen with injury to Ophthalmic division of trigeminal (V2) , which passes through superior orbital fissure . - Loss of jaw jerk (Option B): Seen with injury to Mandibular division of trigeminal (V3) , which passes through foramen spinosum . - Inability to abduct eye (Option C): Abduction of eye is done by lateral rectus, supplied by Abducens Nerve (VI) which passes through superior orbital fissure .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 285-286; Solution Pages 296-297","sourcePage":285,"sourcePageEnd":286},{"id":"anatomy-8-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A young female with history of recurrent attacks of pain over medial canthus of eye and cheek presents with chills, rigor, diplopia on lateral gaze and restriction of eye movements. She was diagnosed with PCOS and is on OCPs for the same since 6 months. On examination, edema over eyelids and exophthalmos were noted. Choose the correct combination regarding communication of the most likely structure affected in this patient.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Transverse sinus through Inferior petrosal sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal jugular vein through emissary veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pterygoid plexus through superior petrosal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facial vein through superior ophthalmic vein"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Facial vein through superior ophthalmic vein","explanation":"Given clinical scenario of restriction of eye movements (III, IV nerve involvement) pain over medial canthus (V1) , pain over cheek (V2 ), diplopia of lateral gaze (VI) with exophthalmos and eyelid edema (venous congestion symptoms) and no history of blindness is suggestive of Cavernous sinus syndrome. CT image showing cavernous sinus thrombosis: Tributaries and communications of cavernous sinus: Tributaries: From the Orbit Superior ophthalmic vein Inferior ophthalmic vein Central vein of the retina (drains into either the superior ophthalmic vein or directly into the cavernous sinus) From the Brain Superficial middle cerebral vein Inferior cerebral veins From the Meninges Sphenoparietal sinus Frontal trunk of the middle meningeal vein Communications Vein/sinus Via Transverse sinus Superior petrosal sinus (Option A) Internal jugular vein Inferior petrosal sinus (Option B) Pterygoid plexus Emissary veins (Option C) Facial vein Superior ophthalmic vein (Option D) Right and Left cavernous sinuses Anterior and posterior intercavernous sinuses","sourceRef":"Question Pages 286-287; Solution Pages 297-300","sourcePage":286,"sourcePageEnd":287},{"id":"anatomy-8-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following nerves pass through the internal acoustic meatus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoglossal and vagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal and accessory"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoglossal and facial"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facial and vestibulocochlear"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Facial and vestibulocochlear","explanation":"Internal acoustic meatus is seen on the floor of the posterior cranial fossa and it transmits facial and vestibulocochlear nerves Posterior cranial fossa: - Largest and deepest of the three cranial fossae - Houses the hindbrain (cerebellum, pons, and medulla) Structure on the floor of Posterior Cranial Fossa Structures Passing Through Foramen Magnum Medulla Spinal cord Vertebral arteries Transverse Sulcus Transverse sinus Deep Fossae Cerebellar hemispheres Hypoglossal Canal Hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) Internal Acoustic Meatus Facial (CN VII) nerve Vestibulocochlear (CN VIII) nerve Aqueduct of Vestibule Endolymphatic duct Jugular Foramen Glossopharyngeal (CN IX) nerve Vagus (CN X) nerve Accessory (CN XI) nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 287; Solution Pages 300-301","sourcePage":287,"sourcePageEnd":287},{"id":"anatomy-8-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following is not a structure passing through the superior orbital fissure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trochlear nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nasociliary nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abducens nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facial nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Facial nerve","explanation":"- Facial nerve does not pass through the superior orbital fissure. - Superior orbital fissure: triangular gap through which the middle cranial fossa connects with the orbit. Part Structures Passing Through Lateral Part Lacrimal nerve (branch of ophthalmic nerve) Frontal nerve (branch of ophthalmic nerve) Trochlear nerve (CN IV) Superior ophthalmic vein Meningeal branch of lacrimal artery Anastomotic branch of middle meningeal artery (anastomoses with recurrent branch of lacrimal artery) Middle Part Upper division of oculomotor nerve (CN III) Lower division of oculomotor nerve (CN III) Nasociliary nerve Abducens nerve (CN VI) Medial Part Inferior ophthalmic vein Sympathetic nerve from plexus around internal carotid artery","sourceRef":"Question Page 287; Solution Pages 301-302","sourcePage":287,"sourcePageEnd":287},{"id":"anatomy-8-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following nerves does not pass through the common tendinous ring of Zinn?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nasociliary nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abducens nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oculomotor nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. None of the above","explanation":"All of the given nerves pass through the common tendinous ring of Zinn - All the recti muscles arise from the common tendinous ring of Zinn - Structures passing through the common tendinous ring of Zinn Superior and inferior divisions of the oculomotor (3rd cranial) nerve Nasociliary nerve Abducent (6th cranial) nerve Sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion Contents of the optic canal (optic nerve and ophthalmic artery) - Superior and inferior divisions of the oculomotor (3rd cranial) nerve - Nasociliary nerve - Abducent (6th cranial) nerve - Sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion - Contents of the optic canal (optic nerve and ophthalmic artery) - Superior and inferior divisions of the oculomotor (3rd cranial) nerve - Nasociliary nerve - Abducent (6th cranial) nerve - Sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion - Contents of the optic canal (optic nerve and ophthalmic artery)","sourceRef":"Question Page 287; Solution Pages 302-304","sourcePage":287,"sourcePageEnd":287},{"id":"anatomy-8-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-8","chapter":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topic":"Lesson 8 - Skull Bones and Cranial Cavity- Part I","topicIndex":8,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Match the walls of the orbit with the bones forming it Wall Bone a. Roof 1. Greater wing of sphenoid b. Lateral wall 2. Orbital surface of maxilla c. Floor 3. Orbital plate of frontal d. Medial wall 4. Lacrimal Wall Bone a. Roof 1. Greater wing of sphenoid b. Lateral wall 2. Orbital surface of maxilla c. Floor 3. Orbital plate of frontal d. Medial wall 4. Lacrimal **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-1, b-2, c-3, d-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-4, b-3, c-2, d-1"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-2, b-1, c-3, d-4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. a-3, b-1, c-2, d-4","explanation":"Orbital Wall Structures Forming the Wall Roof Orbital plate of the frontal bone (main part) Lesser wing of the sphenoid (posterior part) Lateral Wall Greater wing of the sphenoid bone (posterior part) Orbital surface of the frontal process of the zygomatic bone (anterior part) Floor Orbital surface of the maxilla (main part) Lower part of the orbital surface of the zygomatic bone (anterolaterally) Orbital process of the palatine bone (posterior angle) Medial Wall From before backward: Frontal process of the maxilla Lacrimal bone Orbital plate of the ethmoid bone Body of the sphenoid bone","sourceRef":"Question Pages 287-288; Solution Pages 304-305","sourcePage":287,"sourcePageEnd":288},{"id":"anatomy-9-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Match each type of haemorrhage with its corresponding source of bleeding and Location. Which of the following combinations is correct? Type of Hemorrhage Source of Bleeding Location a. Epidural Hemorrhage 1. Middle meningeal artery i. b. Subdural Hemorrhage 2. Bridging veins ii. c. Subarachnoid Hemorrhage 3. Cerebral arteries iii. d. Intracerebral Hemorrhage 4. Ruptured cerebral aneurysm iv. emorrhage Source of Bleeding Location Hemorrhage 1. Middle meningeal artery i. al Hemorrhage 2. Bridging veins ii. hnoid Hemorrhage 3. Cerebral arteries iii. ebral Hemorrhage 4. Ruptured cerebral aneurysm iv. **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-2-i"},{"letter":"B","text":"b-1-ii"},{"letter":"C","text":"c-4-iii"},{"letter":"D","text":"d-3-iv"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. c-4-iii","explanation":"- Subarachnoid haemorrhage often results from a ruptured cerebral aneurysm (e.g., a berry aneurysm). It can also occur due to head trauma or arteriovenous malformations (AVMs). - Bleeding occurs in the subarachnoid space, which lies between the arachnoid and pia mater. Type of Hemorrhage Source of Bleeding Location of Blood Symptoms Collection Epidural Hemorrhage Middle meningeal artery Between the skull bone Acute symptoms, late and endosteal layer of the paralysis starting with the dura mater. face and spreading to the lower body A lucid interval refers to a temporary period of clarity and responsiveness after a head injury, is seen in epidural hematoma , followed by a rapid neurological decline as the hematoma expands Subdural Hemorrhage Bridging veins (venous Between dura mater and Slower progression of bleed) arachnoid mater. cerebral compression, haphazard progression of paralysis. Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Rupture of small arteries Subarachnoid space at the Severe headache (e.g., aneurysms) base of the brain (cisterns) (\"thunderclap headache\"), often caused by aneurysm rupture or hypertension Intracerebral Hemorrhage Rupture of arteries within Brain tissue (parenchyma) Sudden onset of focal brain parenchyma itself, often in basal neurological deficits, ganglia or cortex headache, vomiting, altered consciousness.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 306-307; Solution Pages 316-317","sourcePage":306,"sourcePageEnd":307},{"id":"anatomy-9-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents to the emergency department after a motor vehicle accident with severe head trauma. His GCS is 5. On examination, there is a loss of sensation below the level of the neck and flaccid paralysis of all four limbs. Which of the following structures pass through the same cranial foramen as the structure involved in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cranial root of accessory nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal root of accessory nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle meningeal artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Spinal root of accessory nerve","explanation":"- The structure involved in this patient is the spinal cord, which is the continuation of the medulla oblongata, which passes through the foramen magnum , the largest foramen in the skull, located in the occipital bone. It allows for the passage of several critical structures, including: Medulla Oblongata exits the skull through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord. Spinal roots of the spinal accessory nerve . Meninges, which surround the brain and spinal cord. Vertebral arteries and veins . - Medulla Oblongata exits the skull through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord. - Spinal roots of the spinal accessory nerve . - Meninges, which surround the brain and spinal cord. - Vertebral arteries and veins . - Medulla Oblongata exits the skull through the foramen magnum and continues as the spinal cord. - Spinal roots of the spinal accessory nerve . - Meninges, which surround the brain and spinal cord. - Vertebral arteries and veins . Labyrinthine artery (Option A) passes through the Internal acoustic meatus . Internal Carotid Artery(Option B) enters the skull through the carotid canal. Posterior meningeal artery (Option D) passes through the Jugular foramen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 307-308; Solution Pages 317-318","sourcePage":307,"sourcePageEnd":308},{"id":"anatomy-9-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 30-year-old female presents with severe headaches and neurological deficits. Imaging reveals thrombosis of the inferior sagittal sinus. Which sinus does the inferior sagittal sinus primarily drain into?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Confluence of Sinuses"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transverse sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Straight sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sigmoid sinus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Straight sinus","explanation":"The inferior sagittal sinus drains into the straight sinus , which then continues to drain into the left transverse sinus. Fate of Dural Sinuses: Sinus Features Drainage Superior Sagittal Sinus Located between the two layers of Right transverse sinus the falx cerebri. Inferior Sagittal Sinus Smaller channel between the lower Straight Sinus layers of the falx cerebri Straight Sinus Lies in the junction of the falx cerebri Left transverse sinus and tentorium cerebelli Transverse Sinus Runs laterally from the internal Continuous with the Sigmoid sinus occipital protuberance Sigmoid Sinus S-shaped, receiving tributaries from Internal Jugular Vein the mastoid & internal auditory veins. Occipital Sinus Runs downward from the internal Confluence of Sinuses. occipital protuberance Sphenoparietal Sinus Found along the lateral aspect of the Anterior Cavernous Sinus sphenoid bone. Superior Petrosal Sinus Located above the petrous part of Transverse sinus the temporal bone. Inferior Petrosal Sinuses Located near the base of the skull. Internal Jugular Vein Petrosquamous Sinus Lies in petrosquamous suture if Transverse sinus present. Confluence of Sinuses: (Torcular Herophili) A significant anatomical structure located at the posterior part of the skull, near the internal occipital protuberance & serves as a junction where several major dural venous sinuses meet and drain into one another. Components: - Superior Sagittal Sinus : Drains venous blood from the cerebral hemispheres. - Straight Sinus : Formed by the union of the inferior sagittal sinus and the great cerebral vein, draining deeper structures of the brain. - Occipital Sinus : A small sinus that runs along the posterior aspect of the falx cerebelli, draining into the confluence.","sourceRef":"Question Page 308; Solution Pages 318-320","sourcePage":308,"sourcePageEnd":308},{"id":"anatomy-9-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 34 y/o male presents to the emergency department following a road traffic accident. He complains of difficulty in chewing and numbness over the lower jaw. Upon examination, he has weakness in the muscles of mastication. Which of the following structures do not course along with the nerve implicated in the patient's symptoms through the same cranial foramen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lesser Petrosal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mandibular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Accessory meningeal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vidian’s nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Vidian’s nerve","explanation":"- The patient's symptoms indicate mandibular nerve (V3) involvement, as it innervates the muscles of mastication and provides sensory function to the lower face. This nerve exits through the foramen ovale . - The Vidian nerve , formed by the union of the greater petrosal nerve and the deep petrosal nerve , passes through the anterior wall of the foramen lacerum. Structures that traverse foramen ovale (Mnemonic: MALE) - M andibular nerve (Option B). - A ccessory meningeal artery (Option C). - L esser petrosal nerve (Option A). - E missary vein - Occasionally, the Anterior trunk of the middle meningeal vein At times, there is a canaliculus innominatus situated between the foramen ovale and the foramen spinosum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 308; Solution Pages 320-321","sourcePage":308,"sourcePageEnd":308},{"id":"anatomy-9-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 30-year-old man presents with numbness in his upper lip and cheek, along with difficulty sensing temperature changes, following a dental procedure. Through which of the following marked structures does the affected nerve pass? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. D","explanation":"The nerve affected in the given scenario is the Maxillary nerve , which passes through the foramen rotundum in the middle cranial fossa. Foramen of Middle Cranial Fossa Structures Passing through Foramen Rotundum Maxillary Nerve Foramen Ovale (Option C) M andibular nerve A ccessory meningeal artery L esser petrosal nerve E missary vein Occasionally, the Anterior trunk of the middle meningeal vein Foramen Spinosum (Option B) M iddle meningeal artery and vein, E missary vein N ervus spinosus(Meningeal branch of mandibular nerve) Foramen Lacerum (Option A) A triangular opening at the base of the skull, but in living individuals, it is filled with cartilage, and no major structures pass through it. Foramen lacerum contributed by 3 bones-temporal, occipital and sphenoid The internal carotid artery and nerve plexus traverse its superior end; The nerve to the pterygoid canal passes through its anterior wall. The meningeal branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery and an emissary vein pass through it .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 308-309; Solution Pages 321-323","sourcePage":308,"sourcePageEnd":309},{"id":"anatomy-9-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following contributes to the formation of the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lesser wing of the sphenoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Maxilla"},{"letter":"C","text":"Zygomatic bone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Greater wing of the sphenoid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Greater wing of the sphenoid","explanation":"- The marked area is Pterion , located at the anterolateral fontanelle and is formed by the union of Frontal bone Parietal bone Squamous temporal bones & Greater wing of the sphenoid. - Frontal bone - Parietal bone - Squamous temporal bones & - Greater wing of the sphenoid. - Frontal bone - Parietal bone - Squamous temporal bones & - Greater wing of the sphenoid. - At birth, this area is filled with a membrane, which typically closes by around 2 months of age , though it can sometimes be delayed until 6 months. - The anterior branch of the middle meningeal artery runs beneath the Pterion and Stem of lateral sulcus . - Injury to this area can result in extradural or epidural haemorrhage . - The pterion is a frequent location for trephination (creating an opening in the skull) during surgical procedures. Lesser wing of sphenoid (Option A) helps separate the anterior and middle cranial fossae and serves as an attachment point for various muscles and ligaments. Maxilla (Option B) is a paired facial bone that forms the upper jaw and holds the upper teeth. Zygomatic bone (Option C) also known as the cheekbone, provides support for the muscles involved in facial expressions and chewing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 309-310; Solution Pages 323-325","sourcePage":309,"sourcePageEnd":310},{"id":"anatomy-9-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 25 y/o male presents to the emergency department after a road traffic accident. He was initially unconscious but regained full consciousness shortly afterward, reporting only a mild headache. On examination, the patient starts to experience drowsiness, and eventually loses consciousness again. A CT scan was performed, what is the most likely source of her bleeding?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Emissary veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Rupture of berry aneurysm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diploic veins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle meningeal artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Middle meningeal artery","explanation":"Findings of CT with biconvex hyperdense shadow indicate Epidural Hematoma and the most likely source for this bleeding is the Middle Meningeal artery (MMA) (Commonly Anterior division at level of pterion) This is a surgical emergency. Middle Meningeal artery : - It is a branch of the first part of the maxillary artery, originating in the infratemporal fossa. - It enters the middle cranial fossa through the foramen spinosum and splits into frontal and parietal branches by grooving at the temporal bone. - It mainly supplies small branches to the dura mater and, within the cranial cavity, further divides into Trigeminal ganglion Greater petrosal nerve Tensor tympani Temporal branches An anastomotic branch It holds surgical importance as it can be torn in head injuries, as it can be torn in head injuries, often involving the frontal or anterior branch, leading to extradural haemorrhage. - Trigeminal ganglion - Greater petrosal nerve - Tensor tympani - Temporal branches - An anastomotic branch - It holds surgical importance as it can be torn in head injuries, as it can be torn in head injuries, often involving the frontal or anterior branch, leading to extradural haemorrhage. - A lucid interval refers to a temporary period of clarity and responsiveness after a head injury, typically seen in epidural hematoma , followed by a rapid neurological decline as the hematoma expands and compresses the brain. - Trigeminal ganglion - Greater petrosal nerve - Tensor tympani - Temporal branches - An anastomotic branch - It holds surgical importance as it can be torn in head injuries, as it can be torn in head injuries, often involving the frontal or anterior branch, leading to extradural haemorrhage. Emissary veins (Option A) serve as a venous link between intracranial and extracranial spaces, which can contribute to subdural haemorrhage. Rupture of a berry aneurysm (Option B) causes subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH), with the most likely cause being persistent hypertension . Diploic veins (Option C) begin within the two tables of the skull, with four veins on each side, and can contribute to subdural or extradural haemorrhage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 310-311; Solution Pages 325-328","sourcePage":310,"sourcePageEnd":311},{"id":"anatomy-9-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 50-year-old man with a history of poorly controlled hypertension presents with sudden severe headache, blurred vision, slurred speech, and right-sided weakness. A CT scan shows an acute haemorrhage in the basal ganglia. What is the most likely cause?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Berry aneurysm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mycotic aneurysm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ruptured arteriovenous malformation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Charcot–Bouchard aneurysm"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Charcot–Bouchard aneurysm","explanation":"- Charcot–Bouchard aneurysms are small microaneurysms that develop in the penetrating arteries, particularly in regions such as the basal ganglia, thalamus, pons, and cerebellum. - They are strongly associated with chronic hypertension and are a common cause of intracerebral haemorrhage, often resulting from the rupture of deep-seated arteries in the brain tissue. - The lenticulostriate branches (LSA) of the middle cerebral artery are commonly affected, leading to: Sudden onset headache Contralateral hemiplegia or hemianesthesia Aphasia Visual disturbances Vomiting - Sudden onset headache - Contralateral hemiplegia or hemianesthesia - Aphasia - Visual disturbances - Vomiting - Sudden onset headache - Contralateral hemiplegia or hemianesthesia - Aphasia - Visual disturbances - Vomiting Berry aneurysms (Option A) are larger aneurysms located at the bifurcations of major cerebral arteries. - Rupture of these aneurysms can lead to subarachnoid haemorrhage, often associated with hypertension, causing bleeding between the arachnoid and pia mater. - This condition typically presents as an extremely severe headache, often described as the worst headache of one's life. Mycotic aneurysm (Option B) is caused by infection, often associated with endocarditis or other systemic infections, leading to weakened vessel walls and aneurysm formation. It presents with fever, localised pain, inflammation, and headaches. Rupture arteriovenous malformation (Option C) may cause intracerebral hemorrhage but is less strongly associated with chronic hypertension and typically affects younger individuals.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 311-312; Solution Pages 328-330","sourcePage":311,"sourcePageEnd":312},{"id":"anatomy-9-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following combinations of structures passing through the posterior cranial fossa is incorrectly matched with their corresponding openings?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinal cord, vertebral arteries – Foramen Magnum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve, vagus nerve – Jugular Foramen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nerve, vestibulocochlear nerve – Internal acoustic meatus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglossal nerve, emissary vein – Hypoglossal Canal"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Spinal cord, vertebral arteries – Foramen Magnum","explanation":"The medulla passes through the Foramen Magnum and then continues as the spinal cord Structures passing through the posterior cranial fossa Opening Structures Foramen Magnum Medulla oblongata Meninges (dura, arachnoid, pia mater) Vertebral arteries and veins Spinal accessory nerve (Spinal root ascends) Spinal arteries Jugular Foramen Glossopharyngeal nerve Vagus nerve Spinal accessory nerve (Cranial and Spinal root) Internal jugular vein (exits skull here) Sigmoid sinus Internal Acoustic Meatus Facial nerve Vestibulocochlear nerve Nervus intermedius Labyrinthine arteries and veins Hypoglossal Canal Hypoglossal nerve The meningeal branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery Emissary vein from the basilar plexus","sourceRef":"Question Page 312; Solution Pages 330-332","sourcePage":312,"sourcePageEnd":312},{"id":"anatomy-9-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements about the course of the spinal accessory nerve is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Passes through the internal acoustic meatus before innervating the trapezius muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"Exits the skull via the jugular foramen alongside the vagus nerve."},{"letter":"C","text":"Passes through the foramen rotundum with the maxillary nerve."},{"letter":"D","text":"Originates from the dorsal horn of C1 to C5/6 spinal cord segments."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Exits the skull via the jugular foramen alongside the vagus nerve.","explanation":"The spinal accessory nerve exits the skull via the jugular foramen, travelling within the same dural sheath as the vagus nerve, separated by a fold of arachnoid mater. Course of the spinal accessory nerve Origin and Ascent within the Spinal Cord Arises from motor neurons in the lateral aspect of the ventral horn of the cervical spinal cord (C1/2 to C5/6). Rootlets combine to form the spinal accessory nerve trunk, ascend within the vertebral canal and enter the skull through the foramen magnum, Intracranial Course Inside the skull, the nerve turns upward and moves laterally toward the jugular foramen. Travels through the jugular foramen in the same dural sheath as the vagus nerve, separated by a fold of arachnoid mater. Extracranial Course and Branching Exits the jugular foramen. Crossed by the occipital artery as it descends obliquely, running medial to the styloid process, stylohyoid muscle, and posterior belly of the digastric. Travels with the superior sternocleidomastoid branch of the occipital artery to the upper part of the sternocleidomastoid muscle and forms an anastomosis with C2 and C3 nerve fibres. Path to the Trapezius Muscle Emerges from the sternocleidomastoid muscle. Crosses the posterior triangle of the neck, lying on top of the levator scapulae and may divide into a plexus on the deep surface of the trapezius muscle, receiving contributions from C2-C4. Innervation Sole motor supply to the sternocleidomastoid muscle Provides motor innervation to the upper and middle parts of the trapezius muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 312-313; Solution Pages 332-333","sourcePage":312,"sourcePageEnd":313},{"id":"anatomy-9-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45-year-old patient undergoes surgery for a suspected brain mass. During the operation, the surgeon identifies a dural fold extending between the cerebellum and the occipital lobes, creating a partition in the cranial cavity. Which dural fold is being described?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Falx cerebri"},{"letter":"B","text":"Falx cerebelli"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diaphragma sellae"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tentorium cerebelli"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Tentorium cerebelli","explanation":"- The tentorium cerebelli is a horizontal structure that separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes. - It extends across the posterior cranial fossa, attaching to the clinoid processes of the sphenoid bone and the inner surface of the occipital bone, thereby creating a partition between these regions. Falx cerebri (Option A) separates the left and right cerebral hemispheres and contains the superior sagittal sinus along its upper margin. Falx cerebelli (Option B) separates the cerebellar hemispheres and contains the occipital sinus within its posterior margin. Diaphragma sellae (Option C) forms a roof over the pituitary gland, containing an opening through which the infundibulum passes.","sourceRef":"Question Page 313; Solution Pages 333-334","sourcePage":313,"sourcePageEnd":313},{"id":"anatomy-9-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following sinuses is NOT located between the meningeal and endosteal layers of the dura mater?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transverse sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sigmoid sinus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior sagittal sinus","explanation":"The inferior sagittal sinus is found between two meningeal layers in the free edge of the falx cerebri and not between the meningeal and endosteal layer of the dura. Dural venous sinuses: - Large, blood-filled spaces located within the dura mater of the cranial cavity & drain venous blood from the brain and the skull. - Formation: Separation of Dura Layers : They arise from the separation of the two layers of the dura mater—endosteal and meningeal layers. Reduplication of Meningeal Layer : Some sinuses are formed by a duplication of the meningeal layer. - Separation of Dura Layers : They arise from the separation of the two layers of the dura mater—endosteal and meningeal layers. - Reduplication of Meningeal Layer : Some sinuses are formed by a duplication of the meningeal layer. - Separation of Dura Layers : They arise from the separation of the two layers of the dura mater—endosteal and meningeal layers. - Reduplication of Meningeal Layer : Some sinuses are formed by a duplication of the meningeal layer. - Dural folds are formed by the folding of the meningeal layer and project into the cranial cavity. Falx Cerebri : Extends vertically between the two cerebral hemispheres & contains the superior and inferior sagittal sinuses. Tentorium Cerebelli : A horizontal fold that separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes of the cerebrum. It contains the transverse sinuses along its margins. Falx Cerebelli : Lies between the two cerebellar hemispheres & contains Occipital sinus. - Falx Cerebri : Extends vertically between the two cerebral hemispheres & contains the superior and inferior sagittal sinuses. - Tentorium Cerebelli : A horizontal fold that separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes of the cerebrum. It contains the transverse sinuses along its margins. - Falx Cerebelli : Lies between the two cerebellar hemispheres & contains Occipital sinus. - Falx Cerebri : Extends vertically between the two cerebral hemispheres & contains the superior and inferior sagittal sinuses. - Tentorium Cerebelli : A horizontal fold that separates the cerebellum from the occipital lobes of the cerebrum. It contains the transverse sinuses along its margins. - Falx Cerebelli : Lies between the two cerebellar hemispheres & contains Occipital sinus. The Inferior sagittal sinus and the Straight sinus are not located between the meningeal and endosteal layers of the dura mater. The inferior sagittal sinus is found in the free edge of the falx cerebri & straight sinus is located at the junction of the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli.","sourceRef":"Question Page 313; Solution Pages 334-336","sourcePage":313,"sourcePageEnd":313},{"id":"anatomy-9-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 40-year-old female presents with double vision and drooping eyelids. An MRI reveals involvement of the cavernous sinus, prompting the neurologist to evaluate the cranial nerves associated with this structure. Which of the following nerves is not a content of the cavernous sinus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ophthalmic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Maxillary nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mandibular nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abducens nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Mandibular nerve","explanation":"The cavernous sinus contains the oculomotor nerve, trochlear nerve, ophthalmic nerve, maxillary nerve, and abducens nerve. The mandibular nerve does not pass through the cavernous sinus; it exits the skull via the foramen ovale. Cavernous Sinus: - Large venous space located on either side of the Sphenoid body & Sella turcica. - Interior of the sinus is divided into smaller compartments (caverns) by trabeculae. - The floor is formed by the endosteal layer of the dura mater & lateral wall, roof, and medial wall are formed by the meningeal layer. - Relations: Superior Optic chiasma, optic tract, internal carotid artery, anterior perforated substance. Inferior Foramen lacerum, junction of the body and greater wing of the sphenoid. Medial Pituitary gland (hypophysis cerebri), sphenoid air sinus. Lateral Temporal lobe (uncus), cavum trigeminale containing the trigeminal ganglion. Anterior Superior orbital fissure, apex of the orbit. Posterior Crus cerebri of the midbrain, apex of the petrous temporal bone. - Contents: Structures present in the Lateral Wall Structures passing through the sinus Superior to Inferior : Oculomotor nerve Trochlear nerve Internal Carotid Artery : Surrounded by a sympathetic Ophthalmic nerve Maxillary nerve nerve plexus. Abducent Nerve runs alongside the artery. It is first involved in cavernous sinus thrombosis. Thus abduction defect is the earliest clinical sign observed.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 313-314; Solution Pages 336-338","sourcePage":313,"sourcePageEnd":314},{"id":"anatomy-9-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 35-year-old patient presents with symptoms of cavernous sinus thrombosis, including severe headache, visual disturbances, and facial swelling. Which of the following is not a tributary of the cavernous sinus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior ophthalmic vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior ophthalmic vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Deep facial vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sphenoparietal sinus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Deep facial vein","explanation":"The deep facial vein typically drains the face and communicates with the internal jugular vein rather than entering the cavernous sinus. Tributaries of the Cavernous Sinus: The cavernous sinus receives blood from three primary sources: From the Orbit Superior ophthalmic vein (Option A) Inferior ophthalmic vein (Option B) Central vein of the retina (occasionally) From the Meninges Sphenoparietal sinus (Option C) Anterior trunk of the middle meningeal vein From the Brain Superficial middle cerebral vein A few inferior cerebral veins Communications of the Cavernous Sinus: - Transverse sinus (via superior petrosal sinus) - Internal jugular vein (via inferior petrosal sinus) - Pterygoid venous plexus (via emissary veins through foramen ovale, foramen lacerum, and emissary sphenoidal foramen) - Facial vein through Superior ophthalmic vein → angular vein → facial vein Emissary veins → pterygoid venous plexus → deep facial vein → facial vein - Superior ophthalmic vein → angular vein → facial vein - Emissary veins → pterygoid venous plexus → deep facial vein → facial vein - Superior ophthalmic vein → angular vein → facial vein - Emissary veins → pterygoid venous plexus → deep facial vein → facial vein - The dangerous area of the face refers to the triangular region that stretches from the corners of the mouth to the bridge of the nose. - This area is clinically significant because infections in this region can potentially spread to the brain due to communication with cavernous sinus. Boundaries: - Superior boundary : Bridge of the nose. - Lateral boundaries : Extends to the corners of the mouth. - Base : Upper lip. - Opposite cavernous sinuses (via anterior and posterior intercavernous sinuses) - Superior sagittal sinus (via superficial middle cerebral vein & superior anastomotic vein) - Internal vertebral venous plexus (via basilar venous plexus)","sourceRef":"Question Page 314; Solution Pages 338-340","sourcePage":314,"sourcePageEnd":314},{"id":"anatomy-9-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 60-year-old patient is evaluated for chronic headaches and altered mental status. A CT scan reveals potential thrombosis in an unpaired dural sinus. Which of the following sinus is unpaired?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sigmoid sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior petrosal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transverse sinus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior sagittal sinus","explanation":"The superior sagittal sinus is an unpaired sinus located along the midline of the brain while others are paired sinuses, which exist in two symmetrical halves. Dural Venous Sinuses: - These are channels that drain blood from the brain and are located between the layers of the dura mater. - They can be classified as 7 Paired & 7 Unpaired. Unpaired Sinuses: Unpaired Sinuses Superior Sagittal Sinus Located along the midline, running from the front of the skull to the back. It collects blood from the brain's superficial structures. Inferior Sagittal Sinus Runs parallel to the superior sagittal sinus but is smaller and located lower. It drains blood from the deep structures of the brain. Straight Sinus Formed by the junction of the inferior sagittal sinus and the great cerebral vein. It drains into the confluence of sinuses. Occipital Sinus Located in the posterior cranial fossa, it drains the posterior part of the brain. Anterior Intercavernous Sinus Connects the two cavernous sinuses at the front, allowing for communication between them. Posterior Intercavernous Sinus Connects the cavernous sinuses at the back, also facilitating inter-sinus communication. Basilar Venous Plexus Drains blood from the brainstem and parts of the cerebellum. Paired Sinuses: Paired Sinuses Transverse Sinus Located on either side of the brain, they drain blood from the superior sagittal sinus to the sigmoid sinuses. Sigmoid Sinus Continuations of the transverse sinuses, curve down to drain into the internal jugular veins. Superior Petrosal Sinus Located above the petrous part of the temporal bone, they drain blood from the cavernous sinus to the transverse sinuses. Inferior Petrosal Sinus Located near the base of the skull, they drain blood from the cavernous sinus directly into the internal jugular vein. Sphenoparietal Sinus Found along the lateral aspect of the sphenoid bone. Cavernous Sinus Paired structures located on either side of the sella turcica & contain cranial nerves and the internal carotid artery. Petrosquamous Sinus Lies in petrosquamous suture if present & drains into the transverse sinus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 314; Solution Pages 340-343","sourcePage":314,"sourcePageEnd":314},{"id":"anatomy-9-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-9","chapter":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topic":"Lesson 9 - Cranial Cavity- Part II","topicIndex":9,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 5-year-old child is brought to the pediatric clinic with symptoms of hydrocephalus and developmental delays. An MRI reveals a malformation of a vein associated with increased intracranial pressure. Which of the following veins primarily contributes to its formation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal cerebral veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basal veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Veins from the cerebellum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Internal cerebral veins","explanation":"- The scenario with the given image findings indicates a Vein of Galen malformation , which involves the abnormal dilation of the vein formed by the union of the internal cerebral veins. - In malformations, abnormal connections and dilations can occur, leading to significant clinical symptoms and complications such as hydrocephalus, developmental delays, seizures, congestive heart failure, headaches, neurological deficits. Great Cerebral Vein (Vein of Galen): - Single vessel - Formed by union of thalamostriate, choroidal and septal vein, located beneath and posterior to the splenium of the corpus callosum. - Shortly after its formation, it receives blood from the two basal veins. - Finally, it joins the inferior sagittal sinus to create the straight sinus . Tributaries of the Great Cerebral Vein: - Internal cerebral veins - Basal veins - Veins from the colliculi (tectum of the midbrain) - Veins draining the cerebellum and adjacent regions of the occipital lobes of the cerebrum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 314-315; Solution Pages 343-344","sourcePage":314,"sourcePageEnd":315},{"id":"anatomy-10-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 32-year-old male presents with difficulty chewing and numbness on the right side of his jaw following a recent sports injury. Examination reveals weakened jaw clenching on the right and decreased sensation over the anterior cheek, but intact sensation in the lower lip. Which of the following nerves might be affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior division of mandibular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior division of mandibular nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Maxillary nerve"},{"letter":"A","text":"Branches of the Main trunk:"},{"letter":"B","text":"Branches of the Anterior division:"},{"letter":"C","text":"Branches of the Posterior Division:"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Anterior division of mandibular nerve","explanation":"The presented scenario indicates a lesion of the anterior division of the Mandibular nerve , characterized by symptoms such as weakness of the jaw and reduced sensation in the anterior cheek, while sensation in the lower lip remains intact. Mandibular Nerve: - It is the largest, mixed branch of the trigeminal nerve (nerve of the first branchial arch ). - Course: A. Branches of the Main trunk: B. Branches of the Anterior division: C. Branches of the Posterior Division:","sourceRef":"Question Page 345; Solution Pages 358-360","sourcePage":345,"sourcePageEnd":345},{"id":"anatomy-10-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following cranial nerves does not receive motor fibers from the nucleus ambiguus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vagus nerve (X)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Accessory nerve (XI)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trigeminal nerve (V)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Trigeminal nerve (V)","explanation":"The trigeminal nerve does not receive fibers from the nucleus ambiguus; it is primarily sensory and motor for muscles of mastication, supplied by the trigeminal motor nucleus, not the nucleus ambiguus. Nucleus Ambiguus - Location and Function: The nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus. It provides fibres to cranial nerves IX (Glossopharyngeal) , X (Vagus) , and the cranial root of XI (Accessory) nerve. (Option A, B, and C ruled out) - The nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus. - It provides fibres to cranial nerves IX (Glossopharyngeal) , X (Vagus) , and the cranial root of XI (Accessory) nerve. (Option A, B, and C ruled out) - Cranial Part: Supplies muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx via cranial nerves IX, X, and XI. - Supplies muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx via cranial nerves IX, X, and XI. - Caudal Part: Sends fibres to the spinal root of the accessory nerve (XI) , supporting motor function. - Sends fibres to the spinal root of the accessory nerve (XI) , supporting motor function. - Innervation Source: Receives inputs from ipsilateral and contralateral motor cortices , ensuring coordinated motor control. - Receives inputs from ipsilateral and contralateral motor cortices , ensuring coordinated motor control. - The nucleus ambiguus is a motor nucleus. - It provides fibres to cranial nerves IX (Glossopharyngeal) , X (Vagus) , and the cranial root of XI (Accessory) nerve. (Option A, B, and C ruled out) - Supplies muscles of the palate, pharynx, and larynx via cranial nerves IX, X, and XI. - Sends fibres to the spinal root of the accessory nerve (XI) , supporting motor function. - Receives inputs from ipsilateral and contralateral motor cortices , ensuring coordinated motor control.","sourceRef":"Question Page 345; Solution Pages 360-362","sourcePage":345,"sourcePageEnd":345},{"id":"anatomy-10-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 60-year-old woman presents with unilateral facial pain and sensory loss on the left side of her face. Examination reveals that she cannot feel light touch, pain, or temperature on the left side, but her corneal reflex remains intact. Which of the following is the most likely explanation for her symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lesion of right mesencephalic nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesion of left mesencephalic nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lesion of right spinal trigeminal nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lesion of left spinal trigeminal nucleus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Lesion of left spinal trigeminal nucleus","explanation":"- The patient's symptoms of unilateral facial pain and sensory loss (light touch, pain, and temperature) on the left side of her face, along with an intact corneal reflex, suggest a lesion affecting the sensory pathways of the trigeminal nerve. - The spinal trigeminal nucleus transmits pain and temperature sensations from the face. - A lesion of the spinal trigeminal nucleus on one side affects the ipsilateral (same side) face. - The corneal reflex involves the ophthalmic branch of the trigeminal nerve (CN V1) and the facial nerve (CN VII). - Since this reflex is intact, the involvement is limited to the spinal trigeminal nucleus, sparing the pathways responsible for the corneal reflex. Nuclei of the trigeminal nerve: Nucleus Location Function Mesencephalic Nucleus Midbrain Proprioception (position sense) of muscles of mastication and jaw. A lesion here would affect the jaw-jerk reflex. Main Sensory Nucleus Pons Mediates tactile (light touch) and pressure sensations from the face. Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Extends from the pons to the spinal Handles pain and temperature cord. sensations from the face. Motor Nucleus Pons Controls muscles involved in chewing (mastication).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 345-346; Solution Pages 362-363","sourcePage":345,"sourcePageEnd":346},{"id":"anatomy-10-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly distinguishes the role of the hypoglossal nerve from the olfactory nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The hypoglossal nerve supplies the muscles of the tongue, while the olfactory nerve mediates smell via the thalamus."},{"letter":"B","text":"The hypoglossal nerve is involved in motor control of the tongue, while the olfactory nerve mediates smell without synapsing in the thalamus."},{"letter":"C","text":"The hypoglossal nerve supplies the tongue and is part of the olfactory pathway."},{"letter":"D","text":"Both the hypoglossal and olfactory nerves are involved in taste and smell."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The hypoglossal nerve is involved in motor control of the tongue, while the olfactory nerve mediates smell without synapsing in the thalamus.","explanation":"olfactory nerve mediates smell without synapsing in the thalamus. The hypoglossal nerve controls tongue movements, and the olfactory nerve processes smell without thalamic involvement. Hypoglossal Nerve and Olfactory Pathway - Hypoglossal Nerve (Cranial Nerve XII): Supplies the muscles of the tongue . Belongs to the general somatic efferent column . Not involved in the olfactory pathway. (Option C and D ruled out) - Supplies the muscles of the tongue . - Belongs to the general somatic efferent column . - Not involved in the olfactory pathway. (Option C and D ruled out) - Olfactory Pathway (Cranial Nerve I): Belongs to the special visceral afferent column. Receptors and First Neuron: Bipolar olfactory cells in the nasal mucosa serve as receptors and first neurons. Second Neuron: Mitral and tufted cells in the olfactory bulb form the olfactory tract and reach the primary olfactory areas (e.g., periamygdaloid and prepiriform areas). Third Neuron: Located in the primary olfactory cortex, including the anterior perforated substance. Fourth Neuron: Fibres project to the secondary olfactory cortex in the entorhinal area and tertiary cortex in the orbitofrontal region. Pathway bypasses the thalamus and is solely sensory, unrelated to the hypoglossal nerve. (Option A ruled out) - Belongs to the special visceral afferent column. - Receptors and First Neuron: Bipolar olfactory cells in the nasal mucosa serve as receptors and first neurons. - Second Neuron: Mitral and tufted cells in the olfactory bulb form the olfactory tract and reach the primary olfactory areas (e.g., periamygdaloid and prepiriform areas). - Third Neuron: Located in the primary olfactory cortex, including the anterior perforated substance. - Fourth Neuron: Fibres project to the secondary olfactory cortex in the entorhinal area and tertiary cortex in the orbitofrontal region. - Pathway bypasses the thalamus and is solely sensory, unrelated to the hypoglossal nerve. (Option A ruled out) - Supplies the muscles of the tongue . - Belongs to the general somatic efferent column . - Not involved in the olfactory pathway. (Option C and D ruled out) - Belongs to the special visceral afferent column. - Receptors and First Neuron: Bipolar olfactory cells in the nasal mucosa serve as receptors and first neurons. - Second Neuron: Mitral and tufted cells in the olfactory bulb form the olfactory tract and reach the primary olfactory areas (e.g., periamygdaloid and prepiriform areas). - Third Neuron: Located in the primary olfactory cortex, including the anterior perforated substance. - Fourth Neuron: Fibres project to the secondary olfactory cortex in the entorhinal area and tertiary cortex in the orbitofrontal region. - Pathway bypasses the thalamus and is solely sensory, unrelated to the hypoglossal nerve. (Option A ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 346; Solution Pages 363-365","sourcePage":346,"sourcePageEnd":346},{"id":"anatomy-10-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 62-year-old patient presents with hoarseness and difficulty swallowing. Physical examination reveals a diminished gag reflex and weakened pharyngeal elevation during swallowing. Which of the following structures is not innervated by the affected nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Carotid body"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stylopharyngeus muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parotid gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palatopharyngeus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Palatopharyngeus","explanation":"- The affected nerve in the given scenario is Glossopharyngeal nerve (Cranial nerve IX). - The Palatopharyngeus muscle is innervated by branches of the pharyngeal plexus from the vagus nerve CN (X). Glossopharyngeal Nerve: (Cranial Nerve IX) - Mixed nerve , primarily responsible for sensory functions. - Functional Components and Nuclei: Fibers Function Nucleus Special Visceral Efferent Supplies Stylopharyngeus muscle Nucleus Ambiguous General Visceral Efferent Secretomotor innervation to the Inferior Salivatory Nucleus. Parotid gland Special Visceral Afferent Taste sensations from the posterior Nucleus Tractus Solitarius. one-third of the tongue (including vallate papillae) General Visceral Afferent Carry pain, touch, and temperature Dorsal Nucleus of the Vagus. sensations from the pharynx, tonsils, soft palate, and posterior one-third of the tongue General Somatic Afferent Carry proprioceptive sensations from Spinal Nucleus of the Trigeminal the stylopharyngeus muscle and skin nerve. of the auricle. - Branches and their functions: Tympanic Branch (Jacobson’s Nerve): Enters the middle ear and forms the tympanic plexus , which gives: Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells . Carotid Nerve (Herring’s Nerve) : Supplies Carotid sinus and Carotid body (Option A) Pharyngeal Branch: Forms pharyngealplexus on the middle constrictor muscle. Branch to Stylopharyngeus : Only motor branch,innervating Stylopharyngeus muscle (Option B) Tonsillar Branches : Sensory innervation tothe mucous membrane of the tonsils, fauces, and palate. Lingual Branches : Provide tasteand general sensory innervation to the posterior one-third of the tongue and vallate papillae . - Tympanic Branch (Jacobson’s Nerve): Enters the middle ear and forms the tympanic plexus , which gives: Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells . - Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) - Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells . - Carotid Nerve (Herring’s Nerve) : Supplies Carotid sinus and Carotid body (Option A) - Pharyngeal Branch: Forms pharyngeal plexus on the middle constrictor muscle. - Branch to Stylopharyngeus : Only motor branch, innervating Stylopharyngeus muscle (Option B) - Tonsillar Branches : Sensory innervation to the mucous membrane of the tonsils, fauces, and palate. - Lingual Branches : Provide taste and general sensory innervation to the posterior one-third of the tongue and vallate papillae . - Tympanic Branch (Jacobson’s Nerve): Enters the middle ear and forms the tympanic plexus , which gives: Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells . - Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) - Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells . - Carotid Nerve (Herring’s Nerve) : Supplies Carotid sinus and Carotid body (Option A) - Pharyngeal Branch: Forms pharyngeal plexus on the middle constrictor muscle. - Branch to Stylopharyngeus : Only motor branch, innervating Stylopharyngeus muscle (Option B) - Tonsillar Branches : Sensory innervation to the mucous membrane of the tonsils, fauces, and palate. - Lingual Branches : Provide taste and general sensory innervation to the posterior one-third of the tongue and vallate papillae . - Lesser petrosal nerve : Carries preganglionic parasympathetic fibers to the otic ganglion , supplying the parotid gland (Option C) - Twigs to the tympanic cavity , auditory tube , and mastoid air cells .","sourceRef":"Question Page 346; Solution Pages 365-368","sourcePage":346,"sourcePageEnd":346},{"id":"anatomy-10-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 55-year-old patient presents with difficulty swallowing and a hoarse voice after recent neck surgery. What structure does the vagus nerve primarily run between as it descends in the neck?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal carotid artery and external jugular vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal jugular vein and common carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subclavian artery and internal thoracic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"External carotid artery and internal jugular vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Internal jugular vein and common carotid artery","explanation":"- The vagus nerve descends within the carotid sheath, positioned between the internal jugular vein (laterally) and the common carotid artery (medially). - Injury or irritation to the vagus nerve can lead to symptoms like difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) and hoarseness due to its innervation of the laryngeal muscles and pharynx. Course and Relations of the Vagus Nerve: - Origin : Originates from the lateral aspect of the medulla oblongata, lateral to the olivary nucleus. - Intracranial course : Fibers run forward and laterally through the medullary reticular formation between the olivary nucleus and inferior cerebellar peduncle. - Trunk formation : The rootlets converge to form a trunk that crosses the jugular tubercle alongside the glossopharyngeal and accessory nerves before reaching the jugular foramen. - Cranial exit : The nerve exits the cranial cavity through the middle part of the jugular foramen, situated between the sigmoid and inferior petrosal sinuses. - Neck descent : It descends within the carotid sheath, located between the internal jugular vein (laterally) and the internal/common carotid arteries (medially) . - Thoracic entry : The right vagus crosses in front of the subclavian artery to reach the right trachea; the left vagus passes between the left common carotid and left subclavian arteries. Branches and Distribution: - Meningeal Branch : Supplies the dura mater of the posterior cranial fossa. - Auricular Branch (Arnold's Nerve) : Supplies skin around the external auditory meatus; can cause reflex coughing or vomiting when stimulated. - Pharyngeal Branch : Contributes to the pharyngeal plexus and innervates most pharyngeal and soft palate muscles. - Branches to Carotid body : Involved in baroreception. - Superior Laryngeal Nerve : Divides into: External Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies the cricothyroid muscle. Internal Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies sensory innervation to the larynx above the vocal cords. - External Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies the cricothyroid muscle. - Internal Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies sensory innervation to the larynx above the vocal cords. - Cervical Cardiac Branches : Provide parasympathetic fibers to the heart. - Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve : innervates all intrinsic laryngeal muscles except cricothyroid. Right: Loops around the right subclavian artery. Left: Loops under the arch of the aorta. - Right: Loops around the right subclavian artery. - Left: Loops under the arch of the aorta. - External Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies the cricothyroid muscle. - Internal Laryngeal Nerve : Supplies sensory innervation to the larynx above the vocal cords. - Right: Loops around the right subclavian artery. - Left: Loops under the arch of the aorta.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 346-347; Solution Pages 368-369","sourcePage":346,"sourcePageEnd":347},{"id":"anatomy-10-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 38-year-old female with down-out and intorsion of the eye presents with worsening night vision, occasional headaches, and mild dizziness. Examination shows ptosis and a larger, non-reactive left pupil. Which of the following structures is the parasympathetic component of the affected nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Edinger-Westphal nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Oculomotor nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior colliculus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Red nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Edinger-Westphal nucleus","explanation":"The given scenario suggests the lesion of the parasympathetic component of the oculomotor nerve, which is the Edinger-Westphal nucleus. Oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) Type Motor Origin Midbrain (Oculomotor sulcus on the medial aspect of the cerebral peduncle). Course Components Oculomotor nucleus (somatic component) (Option B): General somatic efferent (GSE) fibres emerge from this site and supply - Superior division : Levator palpebrae superioris Superior rectus Inferior division : Medial rectus Inferior rectus Inferior oblique Edinger-Westphal nucleus (parasympathetic component) (Option A): General visceral efferent (GVE) fibres arise from this site and supply: Sphincter pupillae Ciliaris Note: - The nerve fibers innervating the pupillary muscles are located superficially in the nerve trunk and pial blood vessels supply these fibers. - The main trunk of the fibers is supplied by the vasa vasorum. - Diabetic and hypertensive microangiopathy will affect the vasa vasorum and thus spare the pupillary fibers. - Hence, pupil-sparing 3rd nerve lesions are seen with ischemic lesions such as diabetes, hypertension, or arteriosclerotic disease. Superior colliculus (Option C) of the midbrain serves as the origin for the tectospinal tract (oculomotor nerve originates at the same level). - Axons from its neurons decussate at the dorsal tegmental decussation and mediate reflex movements of the head and neck in response to visual stimuli. Red nucleus (Option D): The rubrospinal tract originates from the red nucleus in the midbrain. - Its fibres decussate at the ventral tegmental decussation and regulate the tone of limb flexor muscles by providing excitatory input to their motor neurons.","sourceRef":"Question Page 347; Solution Pages 369-371","sourcePage":347,"sourcePageEnd":347},{"id":"anatomy-10-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with facial asymmetry and difficulty smiling. Upon examination, the physician suspects a lesion affecting the facial nerve.Where does the facial nerve divide into its five terminal branches within the parotid gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the stylomastoid foramen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Behind the neck of the mandible"},{"letter":"C","text":"Near the spine of the sphenoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Within the middle ear"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Behind the neck of the mandible","explanation":"The facial nerve divides into its five terminal branches (temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical) behind the neck of the mandible, within the parotid gland. Anatomical Landmark for Identification of the Facial Nerve Trunk - Location of Exit: The facial nerve exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen . (Option A ruled out) - The facial nerve exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen . (Option A ruled out) - Proximity to Structures: Crosses the lateral side of the base of the styloid process . Enters the posteromedial surface of the parotid gland . - Crosses the lateral side of the base of the styloid process . - Enters the posteromedial surface of the parotid gland . - In Relation to Parotid Gland: Within the parotid gland, it runs forward and crosses: The retromandibular vein . The external carotid artery . - Within the parotid gland, it runs forward and crosses: The retromandibular vein . The external carotid artery . - The retromandibular vein . - The external carotid artery . - Division Point: Behind the neck of the mandible, the trunk divides into its five terminal branches : temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. - Behind the neck of the mandible, the trunk divides into its five terminal branches : temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. - Nearby Landmarks: Stylomastoid foramen serves as a key entry/exit point for surgical or clinical identification. The nerve lies near the spine of the sphenoid after exiting the middle ear through the petrotympanic fissure. (Option C ruled out) - Stylomastoid foramen serves as a key entry/exit point for surgical or clinical identification. - The nerve lies near the spine of the sphenoid after exiting the middle ear through the petrotympanic fissure. (Option C ruled out) - The facial nerve exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen . (Option A ruled out) - Crosses the lateral side of the base of the styloid process . - Enters the posteromedial surface of the parotid gland . - Within the parotid gland, it runs forward and crosses: The retromandibular vein . The external carotid artery . - The retromandibular vein . - The external carotid artery . - The retromandibular vein . - The external carotid artery . - Behind the neck of the mandible, the trunk divides into its five terminal branches : temporal, zygomatic, buccal, marginal mandibular, and cervical. - Stylomastoid foramen serves as a key entry/exit point for surgical or clinical identification. - The nerve lies near the spine of the sphenoid after exiting the middle ear through the petrotympanic fissure. (Option C ruled out) Within the middle ear (Option D): The facial nerve passes through the middle ear but does not divide there. - It exits the middle ear through the petrotympanic fissure before running toward the stylomastoid foramen and ultimately dividing behind the mandible.","sourceRef":"Question Page 347; Solution Pages 371-372","sourcePage":347,"sourcePageEnd":347},{"id":"anatomy-10-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 35-year-old female presents with sharp, shooting pain and numbness in her forehead, upper eyelid, upper face, upper lip, and part of her cheek after a recent car accident. She has a diminished corneal reflex on the right side. Which divisions of the trigeminal nerve are affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"V1, V2"},{"letter":"B","text":"V2, V3"},{"letter":"C","text":"V1, V3"},{"letter":"D","text":"V1, V2, V3"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. V1, V2","explanation":"The scenario with sharp, shooting pain in the forehead, upper lip, and cheek, along with a loss of the corneal reflex, indicates that the V1 and V2 divisions of the trigeminal nerve are likely affected. Trigeminal nerve (5th cranial nerve): Has three divisions Ophthalmic division (V1) Maxillary division (V2) Mandibular division (V3) Type Sensory Sensory Mixed Course Branches Frontal Supratrochlear Infraorbital Zygomaticofacia Mental Buccal Auriculo-tem Supraorbital Nasociliary l Zygomaticotemporal poral Branch to the ciliary ganglion 2–3 long ciliary nerves Posterior ethmoidal Infratrochlear Anterior ethmoidal Lacrimal: Branch to the upper eyelid and secretomotor fibres to the lacrimal gland. Supply Forehead Upper eyelids Lower eyelids Lateral Lower lip Chin Lower teeth Root of nose Tip of nose sides of the nose Upper lip Lower part of the cheek Frontal air sinus Scalp Cheek (upper part) Lateral side (Upper 2/3rd) Anterior part of the of auricle, temporal region temporal region","sourceRef":"Question Pages 347-348; Solution Pages 372-374","sourcePage":347,"sourcePageEnd":348},{"id":"anatomy-10-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 60-year-old female presents with drooping of her left eyelid, double vision, and a dilated, fixed left pupil. She also reports difficulty focusing on close objects and some mild forward projection of her left eye. Neurological examination reveals that the left eye is turned downward and outward. A CT scan shows no evidence of mass lesions or hemorrhage.Which of the following is most likely responsible for the patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Complete Oculomotor Nerve Palsy"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trochlear Nerve Palsy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abducens Nerve Palsy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supranuclear Lesion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Complete Oculomotor Nerve Palsy","explanation":"The patient's symptoms, including ptosis (drooping eyelid), diplopia (double vision), dilated pupil with absent light reflex, lateral squint (due to unopposed action of lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles), and difficulty focusing on near objects (loss of accommodation), are classic signs of oculomotor nerve palsy. - The absence of mass lesions or hemorrhage in the CT scan makes this diagnosis more likely than other options. Oculomotor Nerve Palsy and Associated Nerve Fibres - Symptoms of Complete Oculomotor Nerve Palsy: Ptosis: Drooping of the upper eyelid due to paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle. Lateral Squint: Eyeball turns laterally due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles. Pupillary Dilatation: Due to paralysis of parasympathetic fibres to the sphincter pupillae muscle. Loss of Accommodation: Caused by paralysis of the ciliary muscles . Slight Proptosis: Forward projection of the eye. Diplopia: Double vision due to misalignment of the eyes. Absence of Pupillary Light Reflex: Affected eye does not respond to light. - Ptosis: Drooping of the upper eyelid due to paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle. - Lateral Squint: Eyeball turns laterally due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles. - Pupillary Dilatation: Due to paralysis of parasympathetic fibres to the sphincter pupillae muscle. - Loss of Accommodation: Caused by paralysis of the ciliary muscles . - Slight Proptosis: Forward projection of the eye. - Diplopia: Double vision due to misalignment of the eyes. - Absence of Pupillary Light Reflex: Affected eye does not respond to light. - Causes and Associated Lesions: Weber’s Syndrome: Midbrain lesion causing contralateral hemiplegia and ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy . Supranuclear Paralysis: Loss of conjugate eye movement due to higher center damage. Compression of the Oculomotor Nerve: Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. - Weber’s Syndrome: Midbrain lesion causing contralateral hemiplegia and ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy . - Supranuclear Paralysis: Loss of conjugate eye movement due to higher center damage. - Compression of the Oculomotor Nerve: Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. - Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. - Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. - Ptosis: Drooping of the upper eyelid due to paralysis of the levator palpebrae superioris muscle. - Lateral Squint: Eyeball turns laterally due to unopposed action of the lateral rectus and superior oblique muscles. - Pupillary Dilatation: Due to paralysis of parasympathetic fibres to the sphincter pupillae muscle. - Loss of Accommodation: Caused by paralysis of the ciliary muscles . - Slight Proptosis: Forward projection of the eye. - Diplopia: Double vision due to misalignment of the eyes. - Absence of Pupillary Light Reflex: Affected eye does not respond to light. - Weber’s Syndrome: Midbrain lesion causing contralateral hemiplegia and ipsilateral oculomotor nerve palsy . - Supranuclear Paralysis: Loss of conjugate eye movement due to higher center damage. - Compression of the Oculomotor Nerve: Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. - Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. - Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. - Caused by extradural haematoma , affecting parasympathetic fibres first, resulting in pupil dilation with little response to light. - Aneurysm of the posterior cerebral or superior cerebellar arteries may compress the nerve as it passes between these arteries. Trochlear Nerve Palsy (Option B) : Trochlear nerve palsy typically causes vertical diplopia and difficulty looking downward. It would not lead to the dilated pupil or the outward deviation of the eye seen in this patient. It also wouldn't cause ptosis or loss of accommodation. Abducens Nerve Palsy (Option C) : Abducens nerve palsy causes the eye to deviate inward due to loss of lateral rectus muscle function, not outward as in this patient. It does not typically result in pupil dilation or ptosis. Supranuclear Lesion (Option D) : Supranuclear lesions affect conjugate eye movements and may cause abnormal eye movements. But they would not typically cause the specific symptoms of pupil dilation, ptosis, and lateral squint seen in oculomotor nerve palsy.","sourceRef":"Question Page 348; Solution Pages 374-378","sourcePage":348,"sourcePageEnd":348},{"id":"anatomy-10-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify the structure marked with the arrow and select which nerve root arises from it. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abducens nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vagus nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Hypoglossal nerve","explanation":"The olive, marked by the arrow, is located near the preolivary sulcus where the hypoglossal nerve rootlets emerge. Olive (Inferior Olivary Nucleus) - Location and Structure: The olive is an oval elevation in the medulla , formed by the inferior olivary nucleus . Appears like a crumpled purse due to its grey matter. Located in the upper lateral region of the medulla. The principal olivary nucleus is C-shaped with a medial hilum . The medial and dorsal accessory olivary nuclei are near the olive. - The olive is an oval elevation in the medulla , formed by the inferior olivary nucleus . - Appears like a crumpled purse due to its grey matter. - Located in the upper lateral region of the medulla. - The principal olivary nucleus is C-shaped with a medial hilum . - The medial and dorsal accessory olivary nuclei are near the olive. - Function: Receives afferent fibres from the cerebral cortex , red nucleus , periaqueductal grey , and spinal cord . Sends efferent olivocerebellar fibres to the cerebellum, forming climbing fibres . Climbing fibres are mostly contralateral , with some fibres crossing bilaterally within the cerebellum. These fibres split into 7–10 branches , sending collaterals to the deep cerebellar nuclei before reaching the molecular layer of the cerebellum. - Receives afferent fibres from the cerebral cortex , red nucleus , periaqueductal grey , and spinal cord . - Sends efferent olivocerebellar fibres to the cerebellum, forming climbing fibres . - Climbing fibres are mostly contralateral , with some fibres crossing bilaterally within the cerebellum. - These fibres split into 7–10 branches , sending collaterals to the deep cerebellar nuclei before reaching the molecular layer of the cerebellum. - Anatomy and Nerve Rootlets: The olive is bounded by the preolivary sulcus (ventrally) and retro-olivary sulcus (dorsally). Hypoglossal nerve rootlets emerge from the pre-olivary sulcus . Vestibular nerve rootlets emerge inferior to the inferior cerebellar peduncle . Glossopharyngeal , vagus , and accessory nerve rootlets emerge at the retro-olivary sulcus . Olivocerebellar fibres aggregate into the contralateral restiform body , a key part of the olivocerebellar tract. - The olive is bounded by the preolivary sulcus (ventrally) and retro-olivary sulcus (dorsally). - Hypoglossal nerve rootlets emerge from the pre-olivary sulcus . - Vestibular nerve rootlets emerge inferior to the inferior cerebellar peduncle . - Glossopharyngeal , vagus , and accessory nerve rootlets emerge at the retro-olivary sulcus . - Olivocerebellar fibres aggregate into the contralateral restiform body , a key part of the olivocerebellar tract. - The olive is an oval elevation in the medulla , formed by the inferior olivary nucleus . - Appears like a crumpled purse due to its grey matter. - Located in the upper lateral region of the medulla. - The principal olivary nucleus is C-shaped with a medial hilum . - The medial and dorsal accessory olivary nuclei are near the olive. - Receives afferent fibres from the cerebral cortex , red nucleus , periaqueductal grey , and spinal cord . - Sends efferent olivocerebellar fibres to the cerebellum, forming climbing fibres . - Climbing fibres are mostly contralateral , with some fibres crossing bilaterally within the cerebellum. - These fibres split into 7–10 branches , sending collaterals to the deep cerebellar nuclei before reaching the molecular layer of the cerebellum. - The olive is bounded by the preolivary sulcus (ventrally) and retro-olivary sulcus (dorsally). - Hypoglossal nerve rootlets emerge from the pre-olivary sulcus . - Vestibular nerve rootlets emerge inferior to the inferior cerebellar peduncle . - Glossopharyngeal , vagus , and accessory nerve rootlets emerge at the retro-olivary sulcus . - Olivocerebellar fibres aggregate into the contralateral restiform body , a key part of the olivocerebellar tract. Abducens nerve (Option A) : The abducens nerve arises from the pons, not the medulla. Glossopharyngeal nerve (Option A): The glossopharyngeal nerve rootlets emerge from the retro-olivary sulcus but not from the olive itself. Vagus nerve (Option D) :The vagus nerve rootlets also emerge from the retro-olivary sulcus, not from the olive directly.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 348-349; Solution Pages 378-380","sourcePage":348,"sourcePageEnd":349},{"id":"anatomy-10-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following cranial nerves is not involved in the Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) pathway?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facial nerve (VII)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve (IX)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vagus nerve (X)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trochlear nerve (IV)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Trochlear nerve (IV)","explanation":"The trochlear nerve is a General Somatic Efferent (GSE) nerve responsible for motor control of the superior oblique muscle and does not participate in the SVA pathway. Trochlear Nerve and Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) Column - Trochlear Nerve (Cranial Nerve IV): Located in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus . Belongs to the General Somatic Efferent (GSE) column. Responsible for the movement of the superior oblique muscle of the eye. Not involved in the Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) column, as it lacks any sensory function for taste or olfaction. - Located in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus . - Belongs to the General Somatic Efferent (GSE) column. - Responsible for the movement of the superior oblique muscle of the eye. - Not involved in the Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) column, as it lacks any sensory function for taste or olfaction. - Special Visceral Afferent (SVA): Mediates taste and olfaction via the nucleus of tractus solitarius . Involves cranial nerves VII (Facial), IX (Glossopharyngeal), and X (Vagus) . (Option A,B,C ruled out) The trochlear nerve does not contribute to these sensory pathways. - Mediates taste and olfaction via the nucleus of tractus solitarius . - Involves cranial nerves VII (Facial), IX (Glossopharyngeal), and X (Vagus) . (Option A,B,C ruled out) - The trochlear nerve does not contribute to these sensory pathways. - Located in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus . - Belongs to the General Somatic Efferent (GSE) column. - Responsible for the movement of the superior oblique muscle of the eye. - Not involved in the Special Visceral Afferent (SVA) column, as it lacks any sensory function for taste or olfaction. - Mediates taste and olfaction via the nucleus of tractus solitarius . - Involves cranial nerves VII (Facial), IX (Glossopharyngeal), and X (Vagus) . (Option A,B,C ruled out) - The trochlear nerve does not contribute to these sensory pathways.","sourceRef":"Question Page 349; Solution Pages 380-381","sourcePage":349,"sourcePageEnd":349},{"id":"anatomy-10-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the arrangement of cranial nerve nuclei from ventral to dorsal in the brainstem?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"General Visceral Efferent (GVE), General Visceral Afferent (GVA), Special Visceral Afferent (SVA), Special Somatic Afferent (SSA)."},{"letter":"B","text":"General Somatic Afferent (GSA), Special Somatic Afferent (SSA), General Visceral Efferent (GVE), General Somatic Efferent (GSE)."},{"letter":"C","text":"General Somatic Efferent (GSE), Special Visceral Efferent (SVE), General Visceral Efferent (GVE), General Somatic Afferent (GSA)."},{"letter":"D","text":"Special Visceral Afferent (SVA), Special Somatic Afferent (SSA), General Somatic Efferent (GSE), General Visceral Afferent (GVA)."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. General Visceral Efferent (GVE), General Visceral Afferent (GVA), Special Visceral Afferent (SVA), Special Somatic Afferent (SSA).","explanation":"Visceral Afferent (SVA), Special Somatic Afferent (SSA). Functional Columns of Cranial Nerve Nuclei (Ventral to Dorsal Arrangement) - Motor Columns (Basal Lamina, Medial Position): General Somatic Efferent (GSE): Supplies muscles derived from somites (e.g., eye and tongue muscles). Special Visceral Efferent (SVE): Supplies branchial apparatus-derived muscles (e.g., facial and pharyngeal muscles). General Visceral Efferent (GVE): Autonomic motor supply to viscera, vessels, and glands. - General Somatic Efferent (GSE): Supplies muscles derived from somites (e.g., eye and tongue muscles). - Special Visceral Efferent (SVE): Supplies branchial apparatus-derived muscles (e.g., facial and pharyngeal muscles). - General Visceral Efferent (GVE): Autonomic motor supply to viscera, vessels, and glands. - Sensory Columns (Alar Lamina, Lateral Position): General Visceral Afferent (GVA): Sensory input from viscera. Special Visceral Afferent (SVA): Sensory input from taste and olfaction (branchial apparatus-related). General Somatic Afferent (GSA): Sensory input from skin and somatic structures. Special Somatic Afferent (SSA): Sensory input for special senses (e.g., vision, hearing, balance). - General Visceral Afferent (GVA): Sensory input from viscera. - Special Visceral Afferent (SVA): Sensory input from taste and olfaction (branchial apparatus-related). - General Somatic Afferent (GSA): Sensory input from skin and somatic structures. - Special Somatic Afferent (SSA): Sensory input for special senses (e.g., vision, hearing, balance). - General Somatic Efferent (GSE): Supplies muscles derived from somites (e.g., eye and tongue muscles). - Special Visceral Efferent (SVE): Supplies branchial apparatus-derived muscles (e.g., facial and pharyngeal muscles). - General Visceral Efferent (GVE): Autonomic motor supply to viscera, vessels, and glands. - General Visceral Afferent (GVA): Sensory input from viscera. - Special Visceral Afferent (SVA): Sensory input from taste and olfaction (branchial apparatus-related). - General Somatic Afferent (GSA): Sensory input from skin and somatic structures. - Special Somatic Afferent (SSA): Sensory input for special senses (e.g., vision, hearing, balance). - The nuclei are organized into longitudinal columns in the brainstem. - Motor nuclei are positioned medially , derived from the basal lamina . - Sensory nuclei are positioned laterally , derived from the alar lamina . - Pontine flexure influences the horizontal stretching of the neural tube, affecting this arrangement.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 349-350; Solution Pages 381-383","sourcePage":349,"sourcePageEnd":350},{"id":"anatomy-10-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 54-year-old male presents with double vision and difficulty moving his left eye outward. Examination reveals the left eye is deviated medially with limited abduction. Which muscle is involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right medial rectus palsy"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left medial rectus palsy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right lateral rectus palsy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left lateral rectus palsy"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Left lateral rectus palsy","explanation":"The scenario suggests damage to the left abducens nerve, which leads to left lateral rectus palsy and an ipsilateral medial squint , while the right side remains unaffected. Abducens Nerve (cranial nerve VI) Type Motor Origin Pons (lower border, opposite the pyramid of the medulla). Course Functional component General somatic efferent fibres : Arise from the abducent nucleu s in the pons and supply the lateral rectus muscle of the eyeball. General somatic afferent fibres carry proprioceptive sensations from the lateral rectus and terminate in the mesencephalic nucleus of the trigeminal nerve. Supply Lateral rectus muscle of the eyeball Clinical aspect Lesion or injury (elevated intracranial pressure) of the abducens nerve results in paralysis of the lateral rectus muscle, leading to: Inability to abduct the eye Diplopia (double vision) Convergent squint (due to the unopposed action of medial rectus) (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 350-351; Solution Page 383","sourcePage":350,"sourcePageEnd":351},{"id":"anatomy-10-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Below is an image of a transverse section of the lower pons, specifically at the level of the facial colliculus. Please identify the structures marked as \"X\" and \"Y.\" **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abducens nucleus and Facial nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facial nucleus and Abducens nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trigeminal nucleus and Vestibular nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vestibular nucleus and Trigeminal nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Abducens nucleus and Facial nucleus","explanation":"- The abducens nucleus (X) is located medially and is responsible for innervating the lateral rectus muscle, which controls lateral eye movement. - The facial nucleus (Y) is positioned more laterally and ventrally. - It is responsible for the motor function of the muscles of facial expression. - The fibres of the facial nerve loop around the abducens nucleus before exiting the brainstem, which forms the facial colliculus. Note: Abducens nerve palsy can be a false localizing sign. This occurs when lesions cause increased intracranial pressure, which stretches the sixth nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 351; Solution Pages 383-384","sourcePage":351,"sourcePageEnd":351},{"id":"anatomy-10-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 29-year-old male presents with a head tilt and double vision that worsens when looking down, especially while reading or using his phone. Examination shows a significant limitation in the left eye's downward movement, extorsion of the eyeball, and a noticeable right head tilt. At what site does the decussation of the affected nerve occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Crus cerebri"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior colliculus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior medullary velum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Substantia nigra"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Superior medullary velum","explanation":"The presented scenario is indicative of a trochlear nerve lesion , characterized by diplopia and a head tilt to the opposite side. Trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV) Type Motor Origin Dorsal aspect of the midbrain at the level of inferior colliculus Special features The only cranial nerve which emerges on the dorsal aspect of the brain. The most slender nerve of all the cranial nerves. The smallest cranial nerve. Only cranial nerve whose nuclear fibres decussate before emerging on the surface of the brain. Nucleus receives only ipsilateral corticonuclear fibres. Phylogenetically, it is the nerve of the 3rd eye. Course Nuclei Trochlear nucleus is situated in the ventromedial part of the central grey matter in the midbrain at the level of the inferior colliculus. Supply Superior oblique muscle of the eyeball. Clinical aspect Trochlear nerve lesions/injury causes paralysis of the superior oblique muscle, presenting clinically as: Extorsion of the eyeball and weakness of downward gaze (difficulty while going downstairs or reading the newspaper) Diplopia (double vision) occurs when the patient looks laterally and glances downward (there is a compensatory head tilt to the opposite side). Crus cerebri (Option A) is the section of the cerebral peduncle located anterolaterally to the substantia nigra. - It contains vital descending tracts that link the cerebral cortex to the anterior horn cells of the spinal cord, cranial nerve nuclei, and pontine nuclei and is not related to the trochlear nerve. Superior colliculus (Option B) receives inputs from the retina and other centres, sending outputs to the spinal cord via the tectospinal tract. - It controls reflex movements of the eyes, head, and neck in response to visual stimuli. - The oculomotor nerve originates at this level. Substantia nigra (Option D) is a crescent-shaped band of pigmented grey matter located between the tegmentum and the crus cerebri. - It is a part of the basal ganglia, primarily involved in motor control through the dopaminergic system. - It contains deeply pigmented nerve cells rich in melanin and iron.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 351-352; Solution Pages 384-386","sourcePage":351,"sourcePageEnd":352},{"id":"anatomy-10-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 62-year-old male with diabetes arrives at the emergency department with sudden right-sided weakness and trouble speaking. The image below represents the examination findings. His examination shows left-sided proptosis, the left eye in a \"down and out\" position, and right-sided facial droop. What is the most likely diagnosis? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Horner’s syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Benedikt’s syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parinaud’s syndrome"},{"letter":"D","text":"Weber’s syndrome"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Weber’s syndrome","explanation":"- The presented case is diagnosed as Weber's Syndrome , which is characterized by right-sided hemiparesis, along with left-sided proptosis and a \"down and out\" position of the left eye. - This syndrome impacts the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III) nucleus and corticospinal fibers , leading to the l oss of the medial lemniscus, red nucleus, superior cerebellar peduncle, and oculomotor nerve fibers. - Characteristic features: Ipsilateral squint (pupil points downwards and laterally) due to injury to the oculomotor nerve. Contralateral hemiplegia due to involvement of corticospinal tract. Involvement of the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (riMLF) in the midbrain causes Weber syndrome with vertical gaze palsy. - Ipsilateral squint (pupil points downwards and laterally) due to injury to the oculomotor nerve. - Contralateral hemiplegia due to involvement of corticospinal tract. - Involvement of the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (riMLF) in the midbrain causes Weber syndrome with vertical gaze palsy. - Ipsilateral squint (pupil points downwards and laterally) due to injury to the oculomotor nerve. - Contralateral hemiplegia due to involvement of corticospinal tract. - Involvement of the rostral interstitial nucleus of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (riMLF) in the midbrain causes Weber syndrome with vertical gaze palsy. Horner’s syndrome (Option A) is caused by damage to the cervical sympathetic trunk, which is characterized by: - Ptosis (drooping of the upper eyelid) - Miosis (constriction of the pupil) - Anhydrosis (loss of sweating on that side of the face) - Enophthalmos (retraction of the eyeball) - Loss of the ciliospinal reflex (pinching the skin on the nape of the neck does not cause pupil dilation, which typically occurs). Benedikt’s syndrome (Option B) is due to damage to most of the tegmentum of the midbrain, which is characterized by: - Loss of proprioception due to the lesion of the medial lemniscus - The pupil points downwards and laterally due to injury to the third nerve - Tremors and twitching of opposite side due to damage to the red nucleus and superior cerebellar peduncle. Parinaud’s syndrome (Option C) is due to a lesion of the superior colliculi (as shown in the above image), characterised by weakness of upward gaze and vertical nystagmus due to involvement of the superior colliculus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 352; Solution Pages 386-388","sourcePage":352,"sourcePageEnd":352},{"id":"anatomy-10-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 50-year-old patient experiences numbness in the area around the neck following a cervical block performed for neck surgery. Which of the following is not a branch of the cervical plexus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Greater Auricular Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supraclavicular Nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Phrenic Nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Phrenic Nerve","explanation":"The Phrenic nerve arises primarily from the cervical spinal roots C3, C4, and C5 & its primary function is to provide motor innervation to the diaphragm, essential for breathing. Cervical Plexus: - Formed by the ventral rami of the upper four cervical nerves (C1-C4) - The Four roots interconnect to form three loops. - Position and Relations: Posteriorly : It is related to muscles arising from the posterior tubercles of the transverse processes, including the levator scapulae and scalenus medius. Anteriorly : It is associated with the prevertebral fascia, the internal jugular vein, and the sternocleidomastoid muscle. - Posteriorly : It is related to muscles arising from the posterior tubercles of the transverse processes, including the levator scapulae and scalenus medius. - Anteriorly : It is associated with the prevertebral fascia, the internal jugular vein, and the sternocleidomastoid muscle. - Posteriorly : It is related to muscles arising from the posterior tubercles of the transverse processes, including the levator scapulae and scalenus medius. - Anteriorly : It is associated with the prevertebral fascia, the internal jugular vein, and the sternocleidomastoid muscle. - Branches: Superficial (Cutaneous) Branches: Lesser Occipital (C2) Great Auricular (C2, C3) Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Superficial (Cutaneous) Branches: Lesser Occipital (C2) Great Auricular (C2, C3) Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Lesser Occipital (C2) - Great Auricular (C2, C3) - Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) - Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Superficial (Cutaneous) Branches: Lesser Occipital (C2) Great Auricular (C2, C3) Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Lesser Occipital (C2) - Great Auricular (C2, C3) - Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) - Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Lesser Occipital (C2) - Great Auricular (C2, C3) - Transverse Cutaneous Nerve of the Neck (C2, C3) - Supraclavicular Nerves (C3, C4) - Deep Branches : Communicating Branches : A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve. - Communicating Branches : A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve. - A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. - Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve. - Communicating Branches : A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve. - A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. - Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve. - A branch from C1 joins the hypoglossal nerve, supplying the thyrohyoid and geniohyoid muscles directly & superior belly of the omohyoid via ansa cervicalis. - Branches from C2 and C3 supply the sternocleidomastoid, while branches from C3 and C4 s upply the trapezius, communicating with the accessory nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 352-353; Solution Pages 388-391","sourcePage":352,"sourcePageEnd":353},{"id":"anatomy-10-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 60-year-old patient presents with difficulty swallowing and changes in voice quality. Upon examination, the physician suspects a neurological issue affecting the muscles of the larynx and pharynx. Which nucleus is responsible for the motor innervation of these muscles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal nucleus of the vagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nucleus ambiguus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Nucleus tractus solitarius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spinal nucleus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Nucleus ambiguus","explanation":"The nucleus ambiguus is the primary nucleus that provides motor innervation to the muscles of the larynx and pharynx via the vagus nerve. Vagus Nerve (Cranial Nerve X) - This mixed nerve is primarily motor in function and the longest of the cranial nerves. - Carry most of the parasympathetic output from the cranial region, including fibers from the cranial part of the accessory nerve. - Functional Components and Nuclei: Functional Column Function Nucleus Special Visceral Efferent Supply muscles of the palate, Nucleus Ambiguus pharynx, and larynx General Visceral Efferent Parasympathetic innervation to the Dorsal Nucleus of the Vagus (Option heart, bronchial tree, and GIT A) Special Visceral Afferent Taste sensations from the posterior Nucleus Tractus Solitarius (Option C) part of the tongue and epiglottis General Visceral Afferent Convey sensations from the mucous Nucleus Tractus Solitarius & Dorsal membranes of the pharynx, larynx, Nucleus of Vagus trachea, esophagus, and thoracic/abdominal viscera General Somatic Afferent Carry sensations from the skin of the Spinal Nucleus of Trigeminal Nerve Auricle (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 353; Solution Pages 391-393","sourcePage":353,"sourcePageEnd":353},{"id":"anatomy-10-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 65-year-old patient experiences redness and sweating in the right cheek while eating. Which of the following components is directly involved in the aberrant nerve regeneration causing these symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Jacobson’s nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Auriculotemporal preganglionic fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Auriculotemporal postganglionic fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sympathetic postganglionic fibers"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Auriculotemporal postganglionic fibers","explanation":"- The scenario indicates Frey's syndrome , which results from the aberrant regeneration of auriculotemporal postganglionic fibres following parotid surgery. - When these nerves are severed, the regenerating secretomotor fibres can aberrantly grow into the endoneural sheaths of fibres that supply cutaneous receptors for pain, touch, and temperature, as well as sympathetic fibres that innervate sweat glands and blood vessels. - As a result, a stimulus meant for salivation can trigger: Gustatory Sweating : When eating, the affected cheek (parotid region) may become red, hot, and painful, accompanied by beads of perspiration. Cutaneous Hyperesthesia : While shaving, the area in front of the ear may exhibit heightened sensitivity. - Gustatory Sweating : When eating, the affected cheek (parotid region) may become red, hot, and painful, accompanied by beads of perspiration. - Cutaneous Hyperesthesia : While shaving, the area in front of the ear may exhibit heightened sensitivity. - Gustatory Sweating : When eating, the affected cheek (parotid region) may become red, hot, and painful, accompanied by beads of perspiration. - Cutaneous Hyperesthesia : While shaving, the area in front of the ear may exhibit heightened sensitivity. Jacobson's nerve (Option A) also known as the tympanic nerve, is a branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve . - It carries preganglionic fibers that synapse in the otic ganglion and involved in the parasympathetic supply of the parotid.","sourceRef":"Question Page 353; Solution Pages 393-394","sourcePage":353,"sourcePageEnd":353},{"id":"anatomy-10-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of difficulty swallowing and loss of taste sensation from the posterior third of his tongue. On examination, there is a loss of the gag reflex on the right side. Further tests reveal decreased salivary secretion from the parotid gland. Given the clinical findings, which of the following structures is most likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Otic ganglion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Submandibular ganglion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ciliary ganglion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pterygopalatine ganglion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Otic ganglion","explanation":"The patient's symptoms suggest a lesion of the glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX) , which provides sensory innervation to the posterior third of the tongue and contributes to the gag reflex. Glossopharyngeal nerve Category Details Origin Arises from the brainstem. Nuclei involved: Nucleus ambiguus: Motor fibres. Inferior salivatory nucleus: Parasympathetic fibers. Pathway/Exit Travels between the internal carotid artery and the internal jugular vein. Exits the skull via the jugular foramen. Course Parasympathetic fibres travel via the lesser petrosal nerve. Synapse in the otic ganglion. Postganglionic fibres from the otic ganglion innervate the parotid gland. Motor Supply Innervates the stylopharyngeus muscle. Function: Elevates the pharynx during swallowing. Sensory Supply Sensory innervation to the posterior third of the tongue (taste and general sensation). Sensory innervation to the pharynx (necessary for the gag reflex). Parasympathetic Supply Parasympathetic secretomotor fibres to the parotid gland. Function: Salivation from the parotid gland. The submandibular ganglion (Option B) is part of the parasympathetic nervous system and is associated with the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). - Postganglionic fibres innervate the submandibular and sublingual glands, controlling their salivary secretion. Ciliary ganglion (Option C) is a parasympathetic ganglion associated with the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III). - It controls parasympathetic innervation to the eye, particularly the sphincter pupillae muscle (pupil constriction) and ciliary muscles (lens accommodation for near vision). Pterygopalatine ganglion (Option D) is also part of the parasympathetic nervous system but is associated with the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). - It controls parasympathetic innervation to the lacrimal gland and the mucous membranes of the nasal cavity, palate, and pharynx.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 353-354; Solution Pages 394-397","sourcePage":353,"sourcePageEnd":354},{"id":"anatomy-10-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with difficulty in smiling and closing eyelids on the right side. The patient does not have any history of recent trauma or infections. The image below shows the patient's face. Sensory function, including taste on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue and sensation to light touch, appears intact. Which nerve column is primarily responsible for this condition? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"General Somatic Efferent (GSE)"},{"letter":"B","text":"General Visceral Efferent (GVE)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Special Visceral Efferent (SVE)"},{"letter":"D","text":"General Somatic Afferent (GSA)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Special Visceral Efferent (SVE)","explanation":"- The patient’s image reveals weakness of the facial muscles on one side of the face. Based on the clinical presentation and the image Special Visceral Efferent (SVE) is most likely to be affected. - The muscles of facial expression, originating from the second branchial arch , are innervated by the facial nerve, with the SVE component arising from the facial nucleus in the brainstem. - The special visceral efferent (SVE) column innervates muscles derived from the branchial arches. Nerve Column Nucleus Origin Function Special Visceral Afferent Nucleus Solitarius Carries taste sensations from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue via the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve. General Visceral Efferent Superior salivatory nucleus Parasympathetic innervation to glands (lacrimal, submandibular, sublingual). Special Visceral Efferent Facial nucleus in the pons Innervates muscles derived from branchial (pharyngeal) arches. General Somatic Afferent Geniculate ganglion Carries sensory information from the skin (e.g., touch, pain). The General Somatic Efferent (GSE) (Option A) is responsible for innervating skeletal muscles derived from somites, including extraocular muscles and tongue muscles . - It is not relevant to the facial nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 354-355; Solution Pages 397-399","sourcePage":354,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"anatomy-10-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A 50-year-old woman presents with excessive tearing while eating, which started a few months ago. Upon further questioning, she recalls having facial nerve palsy about a year ago, which has since improved. Based on this presentation, at what level of the facial nerve pathway did the injury most likely occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the level of the geniculate ganglion"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the level of the chorda tympani"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the stylomastoid foramen"},{"letter":"D","text":"At the level of the greater petrosal nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. At the level of the geniculate ganglion","explanation":"- The clinical presentation suggests Bogorad syndrome , also known as Crocodile tears syndrome , occurring due to the misdirection of parasympathetic fibres that regenerate after facial nerve injury. - These fibres, which originally control salivation, are misdirected to the lacrimal gland, leading to involuntary tear production during eating. - The injury that leads to this phenomenon typically occurs around the geniculate ganglion , where the parasympathetic fibers for both salivation and lacrimation are present. - Damage here can lead to a mix of motor and sensory dysfunction, including taste disturbances and autonomic dysfunction. A- Suprageniculate lesions B- Suprastapedial lesions C- Infrastapedial lesions D- Infrachordal lesions Course of Facial Nerve Segment Description Clinical Significance (Topodiagnostic tests help in determining the location of facial nerve lesion) Intracranial (Cisternal) Emerges from the brainstem at the Lesions can cause facial weakness pontomedullary junction , medial to or paralysis. CN VIII (vestibulocochlear nerve). Intratemporal Meatal Segment It enters the internal acoustic meatus Vulnerable to compression in with CN VII I and the nervus acoustic neuromas. intermedius. Labyrinthine Segment ( Most Extends from the internal acoustic Damage can affect taste and hearing. common site for facial nerve palsy.) meatus to the geniculate ganglion; limited blood supply. Tympanic Segment Runs horizontally through the middle Injury in this segment may lead to ear cavity above the oval window altered middle ear function and and stapes. hearing. Mastoid Segment Longest segment; descends Injury can lead to facial nerve vertically through the mastoid bone, dysfunction, including Bell's palsy. giving off branches like the nerve to stapedius and chorda tympani (anterior 2/3 tongue ) Extratemporal Stylomastoid Foramen It exits the skull and enters the Damage here leads to facial muscle parotid gland. weakness but spares glands and taste. Parotid gland Divides into temporal, zygomatic, Facial nerve injury during parotid buccal, marginal mandibular, and surgery causes facial paralysis. cervical branches to innervate facial muscles. The nerve passes through but does not innervate the parotid. Topodiagnostic tests Key points Schirmer test Decreased lacrimation indicates a lesion proximal to the geniculate ganglion. Stapedial reflex It is lost in lesions above the nerve to stapedius. Taste test Impairment of taste indicates a lesion above the chorda tympani. Submandibular salivary flow test Decreased salivation shows injury above the chorda tympani.","sourceRef":"Question Page 355; Solution Pages 399-401","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"anatomy-10-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 35-year-old male presents with difficulty chewing and pain near his temporomandibular joint (TMJ) after sustaining blunt trauma to the side of his face. Upon examination, there is tenderness over the ramus of the mandible. Which muscle is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Temporalis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Masseter"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral Pterygoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medial Pterygoid"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Masseter","explanation":"Masseter covers the ramus of the mandible, making it most likely to be affected by trauma to the side of the face, as described in the scenario. - Its primary action is elevating the mandible , which is essential for biting and chewing. Anatomy of the mandible: - The largest and strongest bone in the face. - Parts of the Mandible: Body: Horizontal portion of the mandible. Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. Rami: Vertical portions on each side. Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. - Body: Horizontal portion of the mandible. Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. - Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. - Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). - Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. - Rami: Vertical portions on each side. Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. - Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . - Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. The largest and strongest bone in the face. Parts of the Mandible: - Body: Horizontal portion of the mandible. Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. - Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. - Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). - Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. - Rami: Vertical portions on each side. Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. - Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . - Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. - Alveolar process : Holds the lower teeth. - Mental protuberance (chin) and mental foramen (allows passage of nerves and vessels). - Mylohyoid line : For muscle attachment. - Coronoid process : Attachment for the temporalis muscle . - Condylar process : Forms the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) by articulating with the temporal bone. - Mandibular Canal: Runs inside the mandible from the mandibular foramen to the mental foramen . Houses the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels supplying the lower teeth. - Runs inside the mandible from the mandibular foramen to the mental foramen . - Houses the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels supplying the lower teeth. - Runs inside the mandible from the mandibular foramen to the mental foramen . - Houses the inferior alveolar nerve and vessels supplying the lower teeth. Muscles of mandible Muscle Location Action Nerve Supply Masseter Covers the ramus of the Elevates the mandible for Mandibular branch of the mandible biting and chewing trigeminal nerve (CN V3) Temporalis Attached to the coronoid Elevates and retracts the process mandible Lateral Pterygoid Located in the infratempora Protracts and depresses l fossa the mandible and moves it laterally. Medial Pterygoid It runs parallel to the Elevates and assists in masseter on the inner side protrusion of the mandible of the ramus of mandible.","sourceRef":"Question Page 355; Solution Pages 401-403","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":355},{"id":"anatomy-10-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A 34-year-old male presents with difficulty opening his mouth following a jaw injury. Which of the following muscles is primarily responsible for opening the mouth?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Masseter"},{"letter":"B","text":"Temporalis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial pterygoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral pterygoid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Lateral pterygoid","explanation":"The scenario describes trauma to the muscles responsible for depressing the jaw and opening the mouth, particularly the lateral pterygoid muscle. Muscles of mastication - They are responsible for moving the mandible at the temporomandibular joints during the process of chewing. - They are divided into: Principal muscles Accessory muscles Temporalis Masseter Lateral pterygoid Medial pterygoid Digastric Buccinator Mylohyoid Geniohyoid. - Characteristic features: Situated in or around the infratemporal fossa and originate from the mesoderm of the first pharyngeal arch. They attach to the ramus of the mandible and are innervated by the mandibular nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve. - Situated in or around the infratemporal fossa and originate from the mesoderm of the first pharyngeal arch. - They attach to the ramus of the mandible and are innervated by the mandibular nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve. - Situated in or around the infratemporal fossa and originate from the mesoderm of the first pharyngeal arch. - They attach to the ramus of the mandible and are innervated by the mandibular nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve. - Principal muscles of mastication Origin Insertion Nerve supply Action Masseter (Option A) Superficial layer Superficial layer: Masseteric nerve ( Elevates the (largest): Zygomatic Ramus of the Anterior division of mandible (close the arch (anterior 2/3rds mandible (lower part mandibular nerve) mouth). Protrusion of the lower border) of lateral surface). and adjoining Middle layer: Ramus zygomatic process. of mandible (central Middle layer: part). Deep layer: Zygomatic arch Ramus of the (lower border of mandible (rest of the posterior 1/3rd). part) Deep layer: Zygomatic arch (deep) surface. Temporalis (Option Temporal fossa Coronoid process Deep temporal Elevates mandible B) (excluding zygomatic Ramus of mandible nerves ( Anterior (close the mouth) bone) Temporal (anterior border). division of mandibula Side-to-side fascia r nerve) (grinding) movement Retraction of mandible Lateral pterygoid Upper head (smaller): Pterygoid fovea A branch from the Depresses mandible (Option D) Infratemporal (neck of mandible) anterior division of (open the mouth) surface and crest of Articular disc the mandibular nerve Protrusion Turns greater wing (anterior margin) chin to the opposite (sphenoid bone) and capsule of side (right lateral Lower head (larger): temporomandibular pterygoid turns chin lateral pterygoid joint to left) plate (lateral surface) Medial pterygoid Superficial head: Angle (rough area Nerve to medial Elevates the (Option C) tuberosity of maxilla on the medial pterygoid ( main mandible (close the and adjoining bone surface) and trunk of the mouth) Protrusion Deep head: lateral adjoining ramus of mandibular nerve) Grinding movement pterygoid plate the mandible. (medial surface) and adjoining surface of palatine bone","sourceRef":"Question Pages 355-356; Solution Pages 403-405","sourcePage":355,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"anatomy-10-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":26,"question":"A 48-year-old female with diabetes presents with severe, sharp right-sided facial pain triggered by light touch, along with numbness and tingling. Examination shows hyperesthesia and decreased sensation on the right side. Which of the following structures is most likely involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinal nucleus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Motor nucleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesencephalic nucleus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Principal sensory nucleus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Spinal nucleus","explanation":"- The given scenario suggests a lesion of the spinal nucleus of the trigeminal nerve. - The trigeminal nerve is associated with four nuclei (1 motor and three sensory). Nuclear columns of Trigeminal nerve ( Extends throughout the brainstem) - General somatic afferent column : Has three nuclei Spinal nucleus of Vth nerve (Option Principal sensory nucleus of Vth Mesencephalic nucleus (Option C) A) nerve (Option D) Fibers conveying pain and Fibers transmitting touch and This nucleus extends into the temperature sensations from the pressure sensations synapse in this midbrain and receives proprioceptive majority of the facial area synapse at nucleus impulses from the muscles of this location. mastication, the temporomandibular joint, and the teeth. - Special visceral efferent column: Motor nucleus of the Vth nerve (Option B) These fibres supply the muscles derived from the first pharyngeal arch, namely the muscles of mastication, the mylohyoid, the anterior belly of the digastric, the tensor palatini and the tensor tympani.","sourceRef":"Question Page 356; Solution Pages 405-407","sourcePage":356,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"anatomy-10-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-10","chapter":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topic":"Lesson 10 - Cranial Nerves and Cervical Plexus","topicIndex":10,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A 55-year-old woman presents with sudden numbness, tingling, heaviness, dull pain, and heightened sensitivity on the right side of her face. Examination shows reduced sensation in the right maxillary and mandibular areas, with intact motor function and no other deficits. From which location does the affected cranial nerve emerge?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Midbrain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cerebrum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pons"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medulla oblongata"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pons","explanation":"The presented scenario suggests a lesion at the sensory root of the trigeminal nerve, which originates from the pons. Trigeminal nerve (Cranial nerve V) - The largest and thickest mixed nerve, containing both sensory and motor components. - It is associated with four nuclei (1 motor and 3 sensory) and three primary divisions as: Ophthalmic division Maxillary division Mandibular division - Ophthalmic division - Maxillary division - Mandibular division - Ophthalmic division - Maxillary division - Mandibular division - Course: Midbrain (Option A) is the origin of cranial nerves III (oculomotor nerve) and IV (trochlear). Cerebrum (Option B) is the origin of cranial nerves I (Olfactory nerve) and II (optic nerve). Medulla oblongata (Option D) is the origin of cranial nerves IX (glossopharyngeal nerve), X (vagus nerve), XI (accessory nerve) , and XII (hypoglossal nerve).","sourceRef":"Question Page 356; Solution Pages 407-409","sourcePage":356,"sourcePageEnd":356},{"id":"anatomy-11-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents with a progressively enlarging mass in his neck. He has a history of heavy smoking and reports recent difficulty swallowing. A biopsy confirms squamous cell carcinoma of the base of the tongue. Which of the following lymph nodes is most likely to be involved in the initial spread of cancer from this region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Jugulodigastric node"},{"letter":"B","text":"Supraclavicular node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pretracheal node"},{"letter":"D","text":"Paratracheal node"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Jugulodigastric node","explanation":"The base of the tongue drains primarily into the jugulodigastric node , which is part of the deep cervical lymphatic chain. Deep Lymphatics of the Head and Neck - Deep Cervical Nodes: Final drainage points for lymph from the head and neck. Located along the carotid sheath. Divided into superior and inferior groups. - Final drainage points for lymph from the head and neck. - Located along the carotid sheath. - Divided into superior and inferior groups. - Deep Lymphatic Drainage Pathways: Lymphatics generally follow major vascular structures. Superficial cervical nodes : Along external jugular vein. Deep cervical nodes : Along internal jugular vein. Specific pathways: Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Lymphatics generally follow major vascular structures. - Superficial cervical nodes : Along external jugular vein. - Deep cervical nodes : Along internal jugular vein. - Specific pathways: Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) - Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) - Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Clinical Significance: Infection spread : Lymphatic networks allow infections to travel through spaces like the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal spaces, reaching the mediastinum. Cancer staging : Involvement of deep cervical nodes helps stage head and neck cancers, crucial for treatment decisions. Cancers from the lungs and upper GI tract may spread to supraclavicular nodes. - Infection spread : Lymphatic networks allow infections to travel through spaces like the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal spaces, reaching the mediastinum. - Cancer staging : Involvement of deep cervical nodes helps stage head and neck cancers, crucial for treatment decisions. Cancers from the lungs and upper GI tract may spread to supraclavicular nodes. - Final drainage points for lymph from the head and neck. - Located along the carotid sheath. - Divided into superior and inferior groups. - Lymphatics generally follow major vascular structures. - Superficial cervical nodes : Along external jugular vein. - Deep cervical nodes : Along internal jugular vein. - Specific pathways: Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) - Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) - Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Tongue : Drains into jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes (near lingual artery). (Option A) - Thyroid gland : Drains into pretracheal, paratracheal, and deep cervical nodes (along inferior thyroid artery). (Options C & D) - Infratemporal fossa : Drains into superior deep cervical nodes (near maxillary artery branches). - Infection spread : Lymphatic networks allow infections to travel through spaces like the retropharyngeal and parapharyngeal spaces, reaching the mediastinum. - Cancer staging : Involvement of deep cervical nodes helps stage head and neck cancers, crucial for treatment decisions. Cancers from the lungs and upper GI tract may spread to supraclavicular nodes. Supraclavicular node (Option B) : Typically involved in cancers of the lungs and upper GI tract.","sourceRef":"Question Page 410; Solution Pages 418-421","sourcePage":410,"sourcePageEnd":410},{"id":"anatomy-11-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 65-year-old patient presents with a neck mass and difficulty swallowing. On imaging, the mass is located in the area bordered by the internal jugular vein, scalenus anterior muscle, and longus colli muscle. Which lymph node group is most likely located in this area?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Level I (Submental and Submandibular nodes)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Level II (Upper Jugular nodes)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Level III (Middle Jugular nodes)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Level IV (Lower Jugular nodes)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Level IV (Lower Jugular nodes)","explanation":"Chassaignac's Triangle, defined by the internal jugular vein, scalenus anterior muscle, and longus colli muscle, contains the Level IV (Lower Jugular) nodes, which are important in the diagnosis of head and neck cancers. Cervical Lymph Nodes: Location, Drainage, and Clinical Significance - Lymph Node Structure: Arranged in two horizontal rings and two vertical chains on each side of the neck. Outer, superficial ring : Includes occipital, preauricular (parotid), submandibular, and submental nodes. Inner, deep ring (Waldeyer's ring) : Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) in the nasopharynx and oropharynx. Vertical chain : Along the carotid sheath, divided into superior and inferior groups. - Arranged in two horizontal rings and two vertical chains on each side of the neck. - Outer, superficial ring : Includes occipital, preauricular (parotid), submandibular, and submental nodes. - Inner, deep ring (Waldeyer's ring) : Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) in the nasopharynx and oropharynx. - Vertical chain : Along the carotid sheath, divided into superior and inferior groups. - Drainage Pathways: All lymph from head and neck drains into deep cervical nodes , either directly or via other nodes. Superficial cervical nodes along the external jugular vein drain into deep cervical nodes. - All lymph from head and neck drains into deep cervical nodes , either directly or via other nodes. - Superficial cervical nodes along the external jugular vein drain into deep cervical nodes. - Classification of Deep Cervical Nodes (Crucial for Cancer Spread): - Arranged in two horizontal rings and two vertical chains on each side of the neck. - Outer, superficial ring : Includes occipital, preauricular (parotid), submandibular, and submental nodes. - Inner, deep ring (Waldeyer's ring) : Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) in the nasopharynx and oropharynx. - Vertical chain : Along the carotid sheath, divided into superior and inferior groups. - All lymph from head and neck drains into deep cervical nodes , either directly or via other nodes. - Superficial cervical nodes along the external jugular vein drain into deep cervical nodes. Level Location Significance I (Option A) Submental, submandibular triangles Divided into IA (submental) and IB (submandibular) II (Option B) Upper jugular (skull base to hyoid Drains upper portion of internal bone) jugular vein and accessory nerve III (Option C) Middle jugular (hyoid to Surrounds middle third of internal omohyoid/cricothyroid) jugular vein IV Lower jugular (from Drains lower third of internal jugular omohyoid/cricothyroid to clavicle) vein V Posterior triangle Divided into Va (upper) and Vb (lower), near accessory nerve - Specific Drainage Points: Tongue lymph drains into submental, submandibular, jugulodigastric, and jugulo-omohyoid nodes. Jugulodigastric node : Key in tongue drainage, often enlarged in tonsillitis. - Tongue lymph drains into submental, submandibular, jugulodigastric, and jugulo-omohyoid nodes. - Jugulodigastric node : Key in tongue drainage, often enlarged in tonsillitis. - Chassaignac's Triangle: Formed by internal jugular vein, scalenus anterior, and longus colli muscles. Contains Level IV and scalene nodes, important in cancer diagnosis. (Option D) - Formed by internal jugular vein, scalenus anterior, and longus colli muscles. - Contains Level IV and scalene nodes, important in cancer diagnosis. (Option D) - Clinical Importance of Lymphatic Drainage Patterns: Guides surgical and radiation approaches in head and neck cancer. Lung cancers may spread to both supraclavicular nodes, while upper GI cancers spread to left supraclavicular nodes. - Guides surgical and radiation approaches in head and neck cancer. - Lung cancers may spread to both supraclavicular nodes, while upper GI cancers spread to left supraclavicular nodes. - Venous Circulation Connection: Lymph returns to systemic circulation via the right and left lymphovenous portals at the internal jugular and subclavian junctions. Right jugular trunk : Drains lymph from right arm, thorax, head, and neck into the right lymphatic duct or jugulo-subclavian junction. Left jugular trunk : Drains into the thoracic duct or directly into internal jugular or subclavian vein. - Lymph returns to systemic circulation via the right and left lymphovenous portals at the internal jugular and subclavian junctions. - Right jugular trunk : Drains lymph from right arm, thorax, head, and neck into the right lymphatic duct or jugulo-subclavian junction. - Left jugular trunk : Drains into the thoracic duct or directly into internal jugular or subclavian vein. - Tongue lymph drains into submental, submandibular, jugulodigastric, and jugulo-omohyoid nodes. - Jugulodigastric node : Key in tongue drainage, often enlarged in tonsillitis. - Formed by internal jugular vein, scalenus anterior, and longus colli muscles. - Contains Level IV and scalene nodes, important in cancer diagnosis. (Option D) - Guides surgical and radiation approaches in head and neck cancer. - Lung cancers may spread to both supraclavicular nodes, while upper GI cancers spread to left supraclavicular nodes. - Lymph returns to systemic circulation via the right and left lymphovenous portals at the internal jugular and subclavian junctions. - Right jugular trunk : Drains lymph from right arm, thorax, head, and neck into the right lymphatic duct or jugulo-subclavian junction. - Left jugular trunk : Drains into the thoracic duct or directly into internal jugular or subclavian vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 410; Solution Pages 421-423","sourcePage":410,"sourcePageEnd":410},{"id":"anatomy-11-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":3,"question":"During surgery, the surgeon notices an additional thyroid vein, known as the Kocher’s vein, which drains into the internal jugular vein. The surgeon carefully ligates this additional vein to avoid any postoperative complications. What is the most likely origin of this additional vein?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Between the middle and inferior thyroid veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Between the superior and inferior thyroid veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle of the thyroid lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyroid isthmus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Between the middle and inferior thyroid veins","explanation":"The Kocher’s vein originates between the middle and inferior thyroid veins. Venous drainage of the Thyroid Gland: Vein Origin Drainage Superior Thyroid Vein The upper pole of the thyroid Internal jugular vein Middle Thyroid Vein Middle of the thyroid lobe Internal jugular vein Inferior Thyroid Vein The lower border of the isthmus Plexus in front of trachea → Left brachiocephalic vein Kocher's Vein (Fourth Thyroid Vein) Between middle and inferior veins Internal jugular vein (Option A)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 410-411; Solution Pages 423-424","sourcePage":410,"sourcePageEnd":411},{"id":"anatomy-11-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":4,"question":"During a thyroidectomy, the surgeon identifies an artery supplying the lower part of the thyroid isthmus, originating from the arch of the aorta. What is the name of this artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Accessory thyroid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle thyroid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyroidea ima artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Thyroidea ima artery","explanation":"Rarely (in about 3% of cases), the thyroid gland receives additional blood supply from the thyroidea ima artery, which can originate from the brachiocephalic trunk or the arch of the aorta and enters the lower part of the isthmus. Accessory thyroid arteries (Option A) arising from tracheal and esophageal arteries also supply the thyroid. The Middle thyroid artery (Option B) does not exist. The inferior thyroid artery (Option C) does not arise from the arch of aorta.","sourceRef":"Question Page 411; Solution Pages 424-426","sourcePage":411,"sourcePageEnd":411},{"id":"anatomy-11-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A patient presents with the following symptoms: when sticking out the tongue, it deviates towards the left side, and there is noticeable atrophy on the left side of the tongue. Based on the image, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hypoglossal nerve injury (left side)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoglossal nerve injury (right side)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right vagus nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left vagus nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Hypoglossal nerve injury (left side)","explanation":"Injury to the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII) leads to tongue weakness or paralysis on the affected side. The tongue will deviate toward the side of the injury when protruded. In this case, the leftward deviation of the tongue and atrophy on the left side of the tongue suggest a left-sided hypoglossal nerve injury. Hypoglossal Nerve Lesions: - Types of Lesions : Nuclear Lesion : Damage at the hypoglossal nucleus. Supranuclear Lesion : Damage above the hypoglossal nucleus (e.g., cerebral cortex, corticobulbar tract, internal capsule, cerebral peduncles, pons). Infranuclear Lesion : Damage to the motor axons of the nerve. - Nuclear Lesion : Damage at the hypoglossal nucleus. - Supranuclear Lesion : Damage above the hypoglossal nucleus (e.g., cerebral cortex, corticobulbar tract, internal capsule, cerebral peduncles, pons). - Infranuclear Lesion : Damage to the motor axons of the nerve. - Symptoms of Lesions : Paralysis : Weakness of tongue muscles. Fasciculations : Scalloped tongue appearance. Atrophy : Muscle wasting of the tongue over time. - Paralysis : Weakness of tongue muscles. - Fasciculations : Scalloped tongue appearance. - Atrophy : Muscle wasting of the tongue over time. - Effects of Lesion Location : Unilateral Lesions : Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. Supranuclear Lesions : Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. Infranuclear and Nuclear Lesions : Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. May cause fasciculations. - Unilateral Lesions : Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. - Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). - Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. - Supranuclear Lesions : Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. - Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). - No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. - Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. - Infranuclear and Nuclear Lesions : Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. May cause fasciculations. - Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. - May cause fasciculations. - Impact of Lesions : Unilateral Lesions : Usually not serious; remaining nerve compensates. Bilateral Lesions : Can severely affect speech and swallowing due to inability to protrude the tongue. - Unilateral Lesions : Usually not serious; remaining nerve compensates. - Bilateral Lesions : Can severely affect speech and swallowing due to inability to protrude the tongue. - Causes of Hypoglossal Nerve Damage : Unilateral Damage : Tumors, infections, trauma (e.g., surgical trauma from carotid endarterectomy). Bilateral Damage : Rare, often from radiation therapy. - Unilateral Damage : Tumors, infections, trauma (e.g., surgical trauma from carotid endarterectomy). - Bilateral Damage : Rare, often from radiation therapy. - Nuclear Lesion : Damage at the hypoglossal nucleus. - Supranuclear Lesion : Damage above the hypoglossal nucleus (e.g., cerebral cortex, corticobulbar tract, internal capsule, cerebral peduncles, pons). - Infranuclear Lesion : Damage to the motor axons of the nerve. - Paralysis : Weakness of tongue muscles. - Fasciculations : Scalloped tongue appearance. - Atrophy : Muscle wasting of the tongue over time. - Unilateral Lesions : Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. - Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). - Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. - Supranuclear Lesions : Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. - Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). - No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. - Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. - Infranuclear and Nuclear Lesions : Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. May cause fasciculations. - Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. - May cause fasciculations. - Tongue deviates towards the damaged side when protruded (due to stronger genioglossus muscles on the opposite side). - Partial compensation by the intact hypoglossal nerve. - Tongue deviates away from the affected side when protruded (due to upper motor neuron crossing). - No atrophy, but causes slow, spastic tongue movements. - Often caused by strokes or pseudobulbar palsy. - Cause ipsilateral tongue atrophy and weakness. - May cause fasciculations. - Unilateral Lesions : Usually not serious; remaining nerve compensates. - Bilateral Lesions : Can severely affect speech and swallowing due to inability to protrude the tongue. - Unilateral Damage : Tumors, infections, trauma (e.g., surgical trauma from carotid endarterectomy). - Bilateral Damage : Rare, often from radiation therapy.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 411-412; Solution Pages 426-429","sourcePage":411,"sourcePageEnd":412},{"id":"anatomy-11-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements about the nerve supply of the four types of papillae on the tongue is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Filiform papillae are primarily innervated by the lingual nerve (branch of the mandibular nerve) for general sensation."},{"letter":"B","text":"Fungiform papillae are innervated by the chorda tympani (branch of the facial nerve) for taste sensation."},{"letter":"C","text":"Circumvallate papillae are innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve for both taste and general sensation."},{"letter":"D","text":"Foliate papillae are innervated by the vagus nerve for taste sensation."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Foliate papillae are innervated by the vagus nerve for taste sensation.","explanation":"The foliate papillae are actually innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (for taste sensation), not the vagus nerve. The vagus nerve is involved in other sensory functions, but it does not innervate the taste buds of the foliate papillae. Sensory Nerves: - The lingual nerve is the nerve of general sensation and the chorda tympani is the nerve of taste for the anterior two-thirds of the tongue except vallate papillae. - The glossopharyngeal nerve is the nerve for both general sensation and taste for the posterior one-third of the tongue including the circumvallate papillae. - The posteriormost part of the tongue is supplied by the vagus nerve through the internal laryngeal branch Papillae Types and Nerve Supply: - Filiform Papillae (FL) : Function : Most numerous, provide texture. Appearance : Whitish, keratinized. Nerve Supply : Lingual nerve (general sensation). - Function : Most numerous, provide texture. - Appearance : Whitish, keratinized. - Nerve Supply : Lingual nerve (general sensation). - Fungiform Papillae (FG) : Function : Contain taste buds. Appearance : Red, vascularized. Nerve Supply : Chorda tympani (facial nerve, CN VII) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation. - Function : Contain taste buds. - Appearance : Red, vascularized. - Nerve Supply : Chorda tympani (facial nerve, CN VII) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation. - Circumvallate Papillae (CV) : Function : Largest, contain taste buds. Structure : Surrounded by cleft, contain Von Ebner glands . Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste and sensation. - Function : Largest, contain taste buds. - Structure : Surrounded by cleft, contain Von Ebner glands . - Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste and sensation. - Foliate Papillae : Function : Contain taste buds. Appearance : Vertical folds, prominent in animals. Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation. - Function : Contain taste buds. - Appearance : Vertical folds, prominent in animals. - Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation. - Function : Most numerous, provide texture. - Appearance : Whitish, keratinized. - Nerve Supply : Lingual nerve (general sensation). - Function : Contain taste buds. - Appearance : Red, vascularized. - Nerve Supply : Chorda tympani (facial nerve, CN VII) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation. - Function : Largest, contain taste buds. - Structure : Surrounded by cleft, contain Von Ebner glands . - Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste and sensation. - Function : Contain taste buds. - Appearance : Vertical folds, prominent in animals. - Nerve Supply : Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) for taste; Lingual nerve for sensation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 412-413; Solution Pages 429-431","sourcePage":412,"sourcePageEnd":413},{"id":"anatomy-11-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which lymph nodes are primarily involved in the drainage of lymphatic vessels from the dorsal region of the tongue?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Submental nodes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Submandibular nodes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Jugulodigastric nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subclavicular nodes"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Jugulodigastric nodes","explanation":"Dorsal lymphatic vessels of the tongue typically drain into the jugulodigastric nodes. Lymphatic Drainage of the Tongue Region Drainage Pathways Nodes Involved Marginal Submental Route: Descends Submental (Option A) Jugulodigastric beneath mucosa, drains into Jugulo-omohyoid Submandibular submental nodes. Jugulodigastric (Option B) and Jugulo-omohyoid Route: Vessels pierce mylohyoid, follow hypoglossal nerve vein to jugulodigastric and jugulo-omohyoid nodes. Submandibular Route: Crosses sublingual gland, pierces mylohyoid, drains into submandibular nodes; may also target jugulodigastric or jugulo-omohyoid nodes. Posterior Marginal: Traverses pharyngeal wall to jugulodigastric nodes. Central Ascending Route: Vessels ascend Deep cervical nodes (Jugulodigastric, between genioglossus fibers, Jugulo-omohyoid) accompany lingual veins to deep cervical nodes. Submandibular Route : Some vessels pierce mylohyoid and drain into submandibular nodes. Dorsal Posteroinferior Course: Vessels run Jugulodigastric (Option C) posteroinferiorly, with bilateral Jugulo-omohyoid drainage near the midline. Convergence with Marginal: Joins marginal vessels, pierces pharyngeal wall. Direct Jugulo-omohyoid Drainage: Descends behind hyoid, penetrates thyrohyoid membrane, terminates in jugulo-omohyoid node. Clinical Implications - Bilateral Drainage: Comprehensive neck management needed for lingual malignancies. - Predicting Tumor Spread: Understanding patterns aids in accurate staging and therapy planning. Subclavicular nodes (Option D) : These nodes are not directly involved in the drainage from the tongue. They are more related to the drainage from the upper limb and part of the thorax.","sourceRef":"Question Page 413; Solution Pages 431-432","sourcePage":413,"sourcePageEnd":413},{"id":"anatomy-11-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which lymphoid tissue is not a part of Waldeyer’s ring?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Palatine tonsils"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nasopharyngeal tonsil (adenoid)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Submandibular lymph nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lingual tonsils"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Submandibular lymph nodes","explanation":"Submandibular lymph nodes are part of the superficial lymphatic ring, not Waldeyer’s ring. Waldeyer's Ring: - Definition : Inner, deep ring of lymphatic tissue in the head and neck Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) - Inner, deep ring of lymphatic tissue in the head and neck - Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) - Location : Situated in the nasopharynx and oropharynx Guards the entrance to respiratory and digestive tracts - Situated in the nasopharynx and oropharynx - Guards the entrance to respiratory and digestive tracts - Components : (Options A, B & D) Major Components: Palatine tonsils, Nasopharyngeal tonsil (adenoid), Lingual tonsil Less Prominent Components: Tubal tonsils, Lateral pharyngeal bands - Major Components: Palatine tonsils, Nasopharyngeal tonsil (adenoid), Lingual tonsil - Less Prominent Components: Tubal tonsils, Lateral pharyngeal bands - Functions : Traps pathogens inhaled or ingested Initiates immune responses by activating lymphocytes and generating antibodies Generation of B cells : Germinal centers in the tonsils play a critical role in B cell production - Traps pathogens inhaled or ingested - Initiates immune responses by activating lymphocytes and generating antibodies - Generation of B cells : Germinal centers in the tonsils play a critical role in B cell production - Clinical Significance : Susceptible to infections , e.g., tonsillitis involving palatine tonsils Potential site for tumors such as lymphoma - Susceptible to infections , e.g., tonsillitis involving palatine tonsils - Potential site for tumors such as lymphoma - Inner, deep ring of lymphatic tissue in the head and neck - Composed of mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) - Situated in the nasopharynx and oropharynx - Guards the entrance to respiratory and digestive tracts - Major Components: Palatine tonsils, Nasopharyngeal tonsil (adenoid), Lingual tonsil - Less Prominent Components: Tubal tonsils, Lateral pharyngeal bands - Traps pathogens inhaled or ingested - Initiates immune responses by activating lymphocytes and generating antibodies - Generation of B cells : Germinal centers in the tonsils play a critical role in B cell production - Susceptible to infections , e.g., tonsillitis involving palatine tonsils - Potential site for tumors such as lymphoma","sourceRef":"Question Page 413; Solution Pages 432-435","sourcePage":413,"sourcePageEnd":413},{"id":"anatomy-11-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A surgeon is performing a neck dissection and encounters the internal jugular vein. Which of the following structures does not directly drain into the internal jugular vein?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facial vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lingual vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Maxillary vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior thyroid vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Maxillary vein","explanation":"The maxillary vein drains into the retromandibular vein, which ultimately contributes to the external jugular vein , not the internal jugular vein. Head and Neck Venous Drainage: Vein Type Primary Drainage Area Main Tributaries Destination Superficial veins Scalp, face, and occipital Posterior retromandibular External, anterior, or scalp vein Posterior auricular posterior external jugular vein Submandibular veins veins External jugular vein Scalp and face Posterior retromandibular Subclavian vein vein Posterior auricular vein Anterior jugular vein Submandibular region Superficial submandibular External jugular or veins subclavian vein Posterior ext. jugular Occipital scalp - External jugular vein Deep veins Skull, brain, face, and neck Inferior petrosal sinus, Internal jugular or facial, lingual, and subclavian vein pharyngeal veins Superior & middle thyroid veins Internal jugular vein Skull, brain, face, and neck Inferior petrosal sinus, Subclavian vein facial, lingual, pharyngeal, and thyroid veins (Options A, B & D) Subclavian vein Arm and neck External jugular, dorsal Brachiocephalic vein scapular, and anterior jugular (sometimes) Retromandibular vein Face Maxillary vein, superficial External jugular or facial temporal vein (Option C) vein","sourceRef":"Question Page 413; Solution Pages 435-436","sourcePage":413,"sourcePageEnd":413},{"id":"anatomy-11-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 6-month-old infant is evaluated after a complicated delivery in which upward traction was applied to the arm. The parents report that the infant’s left hand has fingers curled and cannot extend them. On examination, the left eyelid droops, and the pupil is smaller. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Erb's Palsy"},{"letter":"B","text":"Klumpke's Palsy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial Nerve Palsy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar Nerve Injury"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Klumpke's Palsy","explanation":"The Infant displays signs of Klumpke's Palsy, such as claw hand deformity and Horner syndrome, which suggests that the C8-T1 nerve roots are involved. Lower Trunk (inferior): C8 & T1 roots join to form a lower trunk. Klumpke's Palsy - Injury : C8, T1 - Features : Claw Hand Deformity Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - Claw Hand Deformity Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei - Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension - MCP Flexion - IP Extension - Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - MCP Extension - IP Flexion - Claw Hand Deformity Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei - Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension - MCP Flexion - IP Extension - Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - MCP Extension - IP Flexion - Affected Muscles : Lumbricals & interossei - Action : MCP Flexion IP Extension - MCP Flexion - IP Extension - Injury Result : MCP Extension IP Flexion - MCP Extension - IP Flexion - MCP Flexion - IP Extension - MCP Extension - IP Flexion - Horner Syndrome:(T1) Ptosis : Drooping of the eyelid due to paralysis of the superior tarsal muscle (Muller muscle) Miosis : Constricted pupil due to paralysis of the dilator pupillae - Ptosis : Drooping of the eyelid due to paralysis of the superior tarsal muscle (Muller muscle) - Miosis : Constricted pupil due to paralysis of the dilator pupillae - Ptosis : Drooping of the eyelid due to paralysis of the superior tarsal muscle (Muller muscle) - Miosis : Constricted pupil due to paralysis of the dilator pupillae - Nerve Involvement : Lower trunk injury (C8, T1) Median nerve: Partial injury Ulnar nerve: Total compromise T1 sympathetic fibres affected - Lower trunk injury (C8, T1) - Median nerve: Partial injury - Ulnar nerve: Total compromise - T1 sympathetic fibres affected - Mechanism of Injury : Hyper-abduction of the arm Falls (e.g., holding a branch) Birth injuries - Hyper-abduction of the arm - Falls (e.g., holding a branch) - Birth injuries - Lower trunk injury (C8, T1) - Median nerve: Partial injury - Ulnar nerve: Total compromise - T1 sympathetic fibres affected - Hyper-abduction of the arm - Falls (e.g., holding a branch) - Birth injuries Erb's Palsy (Option A): It involves the upper trunk (C5-C6), leading to the classic \"waiter's tip\" posture, not the symptoms described. Radial Nerve Palsy (Option C): Would cause wrist drop, not the claw hand or Horner syndrome. Ulnar Nerve Injury (Option D): This does not typically result in Horner syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 413-414; Solution Pages 436-439","sourcePage":413,"sourcePageEnd":414},{"id":"anatomy-11-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 65-year-old male presents with complaints of dizziness, syncope, and numbness in his left arm during physical activity. On examination, his left radial pulse is weak, and the blood pressure in the left arm is significantly lower than in the right arm. What is the most likely pathophysiological mechanism behind this patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Retrograde blood flow in the left vertebral artery due to subclavian artery stenosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reduced blood flow in the left subclavian artery due to thoracic aortic dissection"},{"letter":"C","text":"Retrograde blood flow in the left internal carotid artery due to basilar artery stenosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Retrograde blood flow in the right vertebral artery due to subclavian artery stenosis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Retrograde blood flow in the left vertebral artery due to subclavian artery stenosis","explanation":"stenosis - The clinical scenario described above is suggestive of subclavian steal syndrome. - Obstruction to the subclavian artery proximal to the origin of the vertebral artery may lead to retrograde blood flow in the left vertebral artery. Subclavian steal Syndrome: Pathophysiology: Causes Atherosclerosis (most common) Takayasu disease (least common) Giant cell arteritis Blalock Taussig shunt Thoracic aortic dissection Thoracic outlet compression Interrupted aortic arch Congenital aortic coarctation Risk Factors Smoking, diabetes, obesity, lack of exercise, unhealthy diet, family history More common in individuals who are biologically male Complications Upper limb ischemia, neurological problems Asymptomatic Numbness of arm extends to fingertips (most frequent) Vertebrobasilar artery insufficiency Presyncope/syncope, neurologic deficits Upper extremity claudication Tingling sensation/numbness in the face Decreased blood pressure on the affected side Transient hemiparesis (weakness) of the affected side Diagnosis BP in left arm < BP in right arm Pulse in left arm < pulse in right arm CT angiography (Investigation of Choice) Doppler ultrasound scan Treatment Balloon stenting, angioplasty Endarterectomy","sourceRef":"Question Page 414; Solution Pages 439-440","sourcePage":414,"sourcePageEnd":414},{"id":"anatomy-11-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following arteries does not supply the upper part of the tonsil?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ascending pharyngeal artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending palatine artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Descending palatine artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Greater palatine artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ascending palatine artery","explanation":"- The upper part of the tonsil is supplied by the ascending pharyngeal, descending palatine, and the greater and lesser palatine arteries. - Ascending palatine artery does not contribute to the blood supply of the upper part of the tonsil, instead, it predominantly supplies the lower part. Blood Supply of the Tonsil: Artery Branch of Region Supplied Tonsillar artery Facial artery (sometimes Ascending Palatine tonsil and posterior lingual Palatine artery) musculature Dorsal lingual branches Lingual artery Anterior tonsillar region Ascending palatine artery (Option B) Facial artery Posterior tonsillar region (lower part) Ascending pharyngeal artery (Option External carotid artery Upper part of the tonsil (posterior) A) Descending palatine artery (Option C) Maxillary artery Upper part of the tonsil Greater and Lesser Palatine arteries Descending palatine artery Upper part of the tonsil (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 414; Solution Pages 440-442","sourcePage":414,"sourcePageEnd":414},{"id":"anatomy-11-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 12 y/o boy presents with nasal bleeding, after nose-picking. On examination, the bleeding site is located in the anterior nasal septum, and the diagnosis of epistaxis from Kiesselbach's plexus is made. Which of the following arteries is LEAST likely to contribute to the bleeding from this plexus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior ethmoid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior ethmoid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior labial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sphenopalatine artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior ethmoid artery","explanation":"Kiesselbach's plexus (Little's area) is the most common site of epistaxis due to its rich vascular supply. Arteries that contribute to this plexus include: - Anterior ethmoid artery - Septal branches of the superior labial artery and greater palatine artery - Sphenopalatine artery Arteries contributing to Kiesselbach’s plexus: Artery Branch of Region Supplied Anterior ethmoid artery (Option A) Ophthalmic artery Anterosuperior portion of the nasal septum Sphenopalatine artery (Option D) Maxillary artery Posteroinferior portion of the nasal septum Septal branch of superior labial Facial artery Caudal septum and columella artery (Option C) Greater palatine artery Maxillary artery Anteroinferior portion of the nasal septum Blood Supply of the Nasal septum: The posterior ethmoid artery (Option B) supplies the posterosuperior nasal septum and does not contribute to the vascular supply of Kiesselbach’s plexus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 414-415; Solution Pages 442-444","sourcePage":414,"sourcePageEnd":415},{"id":"anatomy-11-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which statement about the maxillary artery is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It arises behind the neck of the mandible."},{"letter":"B","text":"The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen ovale."},{"letter":"C","text":"The posterior superior alveolar artery supplies upper molar teeth."},{"letter":"D","text":"The sphenopalatine artery is the artery of epistaxis."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The middle meningeal artery passes through the foramen ovale.","explanation":"The middle meningeal artery transmits through the foramen spinosum, not the foramen ovale. Contents of the Foramen Ovale: (Mnemonic: MALE) - Mandibular nerve (V3): The third division of the trigeminal nerve, responsible for motor and sensory functions to the lower face. - Accessory meningeal artery: A small artery that supplies the meninges. - Lesser petrosal nerve: A branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) that carries parasympathetic fibers to the parotid gland. - Emissary veins: These veins connect the cavernous sinus with the pterygoid venous plexus. Maxillary Artery - Definition: A larger terminal branch of the external carotid artery, arises behind the neck of the mandible (Option A) . - Distribution: Supplies a wide territory. - It has 3 parts First Part - Deep Auricular - Anterior Tympanic - Middle Meningeal Transmits: Foramen spinosum Clinical significance: A ruptured middle meningeal artery (MMA) is the most common cause of an epidural hematoma (EDH). - Transmits: Foramen spinosum - Clinical significance: A ruptured middle meningeal artery (MMA) is the most common cause of an epidural hematoma (EDH). - Accessory Meningeal - Inferior Alveolar Transmits: Mandibular foramen Distribution: Lower 8 teeth and mylohyoid muscle - Transmits: Mandibular foramen - Distribution: Lower 8 teeth and mylohyoid muscle - Transmits: Foramen spinosum - Clinical significance: A ruptured middle meningeal artery (MMA) is the most common cause of an epidural hematoma (EDH). - Transmits: Mandibular foramen - Distribution: Lower 8 teeth and mylohyoid muscle Second Part - Masseteric Distribution: Masseter muscle - Distribution: Masseter muscle - Deep Temporal Distribution: Temporalis muscle - Distribution: Temporalis muscle - Pterygoid Distribution: Lateral and medial pterygoids - Distribution: Lateral and medial pterygoids - Buccal Distribution: Skin of the cheek - Distribution: Skin of the cheek - Distribution: Masseter muscle - Distribution: Temporalis muscle - Distribution: Lateral and medial pterygoids - Distribution: Skin of the cheek Third Part - Posterior Superior Alveolar (Option C) Distribution: Upper molar and premolar teeth and gums - Distribution: Upper molar and premolar teeth and gums - Infraorbital - Greater Palatine Distribution: Soft palate, tonsil, palatine glands, mucosa of upper gums - Distribution: Soft palate, tonsil, palatine glands, mucosa of upper gums - Pharyngeal - Artery of Pterygoid Canal - Sphenopalatine Distribution: Lateral and medial walls of nose, various sinuses. Clinical Significance: Known as the artery of epistaxis (Option D) . - Distribution: Lateral and medial walls of nose, various sinuses. - Clinical Significance: Known as the artery of epistaxis (Option D) . - Distribution: Upper molar and premolar teeth and gums - Distribution: Soft palate, tonsil, palatine glands, mucosa of upper gums - Distribution: Lateral and medial walls of nose, various sinuses. - Clinical Significance: Known as the artery of epistaxis (Option D) .","sourceRef":"Question Page 415; Solution Pages 444-446","sourcePage":415,"sourcePageEnd":415},{"id":"anatomy-11-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Identify the marked branch of the External carotid artery? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lingual Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facial Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Maxillary Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior Thyroid Artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Lingual Artery","explanation":"Lingual artery: - A major branch of the external carotid artery. - Supplies the tongue and the oral floor. - Arises anteromedially from the external carotid artery. - Located at the tip of the greater horn of the hyoid bone, between the superior thyroid artery and the facial artery. External Carotid Artery - Origin: Terminal branch of the common carotid artery - Position: Anterior to the internal carotid artery - Function: Chief artery supplying the front of the neck and face Branches: Anterior Branches (3) - Superior Thyroid Artery: Supplies the thyroid gland. - Lingual Artery: Supplies the tongue. - Facial Artery: Supplies the face. - Post Auricular Artery: Supplies the auricle (outer ear). - Occipital Artery: Supplies the occiput (back of the head). - Maxillary Artery: Supplies the maxilla (upper jaw) and deep facial structures. - Superficial Temporal Artery: Supplies the temporal region and scalp. - Ascending Pharyngeal Artery: Supplies the pharynx and tonsils.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 415-416; Solution Pages 446-447","sourcePage":415,"sourcePageEnd":416},{"id":"anatomy-11-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following is not the branch of the marked artery? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyrocervical Trunk"},{"letter":"B","text":"Costocervical trunk"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal scapular artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Axillary artery","explanation":"- The highlighted structure in the provided image indicates the subclavian artery. - The axillary artery is not a branch of the Subclavian Artery rather it is a continuation of the subclavian artery. Subclavian Artery - A major vessel supplying blood to the upper limb, neck, and brain. - It transitions into the axillary artery at the outer border of the first rib. - Right Side: Branch of the brachiocephalic artery. - Left Side: Branches from the arch of the aorta. - Arterial Path: Each artery arches laterally from the sternoclavicular joint to the outer border of the first rib, where it becomes the axillary artery. - Relation to Scalenus Anterior: Parts: The scalenus anterior muscle crosses the artery, dividing it into three parts: First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. - Parts: The scalenus anterior muscle crosses the artery, dividing it into three parts: First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. - First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. - Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. - Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. - Parts: The scalenus anterior muscle crosses the artery, dividing it into three parts: First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. - First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. - Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. - Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. - First Part: Medial to the scalenus anterior. - Second Part: Posterior to the scalenus anterior. - Third Part: Lateral to the scalenus anterior. Branches (Mnemonic: VIT C & D) - First Part: Vertebral Artery Internal Thoracic Artery Thyrocervical Trunk (Option A) - Vertebral Artery - Internal Thoracic Artery - Thyrocervical Trunk (Option A) - Second part: Costocervical trunk (Option B) - Costocervical trunk (Option B) - Third part: Dorsal scapular artery (Option C) - Dorsal scapular artery (Option C) - Vertebral Artery - Internal Thoracic Artery - Thyrocervical Trunk (Option A) - Costocervical trunk (Option B) - Dorsal scapular artery (Option C) Clinical point: The right subclavian artery may arise from the descending thoracic aorta. In such cases, it passes posterior to the esophagus, potentially leading to compression of the esophagus. This condition is known as dysphagia lusoria.","sourceRef":"Question Page 416; Solution Pages 447-450","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":416},{"id":"anatomy-11-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-11","chapter":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topic":"Lesson 11 - Scalenus Anterior, Head and Neck Vasculature and Lymphatic Drainage","topicIndex":11,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following statements about the scalenus anterior muscle is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The phrenic nerve lies anteriorly to the muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"The thoracic duct arches laterally on the right side."},{"letter":"C","text":"The brachial plexus is posterior to the muscle."},{"letter":"D","text":"The lateral part of the carotid sheath contains the internal jugular vein."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The thoracic duct arches laterally on the right side.","explanation":"The thoracic duct typically arches laterally on the left side at the level of the seventh cervical transverse process. Relations of Scalenus Anterior - Phrenic Nerve: Covered by prevertebral fascia (Option A) - Lateral Part of Carotid Sheath: Contains the internal jugular vein (Option D) - Sternocleidomastoid Muscle - Clavicle - Brachial Plexus (Option C) - Subclavian Artery - Scalenus Medius Muscle - Cervical Pleura: Covered by the suprapleural membrane - Lower Part: Inverted V-shaped interval formed with longus colli Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process Sympathetic Trunk First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses thelower part of the gap Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervicaltransverse process Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned aboveSternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Inverted V-shaped interval formed with longus colli Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process Sympathetic Trunk First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses the lower part of the gap Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervical transverse process Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned above Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval - Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process - Sympathetic Trunk - First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses the lower part of the gap - Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervical transverse process - Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned above - Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Upper Part: Separated from the longus capitis by the ascending cervical artery - Separated from the longus capitis by the ascending cervical artery - Inverted V-shaped interval formed with longus colli Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process Sympathetic Trunk First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses the lower part of the gap Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervical transverse process Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned above Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval - Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process - Sympathetic Trunk - First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses the lower part of the gap - Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervical transverse process - Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned above - Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Vertebral Vessels: Running vertically within the interval - Inferior Thyroid Artery: Arches medially at the level of the 6th cervical transverse process - Sympathetic Trunk - First Part of Subclavian Artery: Traverses the lower part of the gap - Thoracic Duct (Left Side): Arches laterally at the level of the 7th cervical transverse process - Carotid Sheath: Covers the structures mentioned above - Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Covers the carotid sheath - Separated from the longus capitis by the ascending cervical artery - Trunks of Brachial Plexus - Subclavian Artery: Emerges at this border and enters the posterior triangle","sourceRef":"Question Pages 416-417; Solution Pages 450-453","sourcePage":416,"sourcePageEnd":417},{"id":"anatomy-12-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following structures is enclosed by the prevertebral fascia in the neck?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trapezius muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cervical sympathetic trunk"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyroid gland"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Cervical sympathetic trunk","explanation":"Cervical sympathetic trunk is enclosed by the prevertebral fascia, which surrounds the vertebral column and associated structures. Prevertebral fascia/Lamina prevertebralis: Coronal extension from the internal aspect of the deep cervical fascia that runs anterior to the prevertebral muscles is called the Preethi layer of deep cervical fascia. Definition Thin layer of connective tissue that envelopes the prevertebral muscles and cervical sympathetic trunk. Location Anterior to the vertebral column, extending from the base of the skull to the superior mediastinum where it merges with the ligament of the vertebral column (T3/T4 level). Extension Axillary sheath: The cervical and brachial plexuses form from the nerves deep to the fascia, and as they extend, they pull the prevertebral fascia with them forming axillary sheath Functions Support to the vertebral column and associated muscles. Compartmentalization of various cervical structures which prevents infection spread Protective sheath to brachial plexus and subclavian vessels Clinical Significance Infection Spread: Particularly from the retropharyngeal space, which can lead to serious complications such as mediastinitis. Trapezius muscle (Option A): Enclosed by the investing layer of the deep cervical fascia, not the prevertebral fascia. Internal carotid artery (Option B): Contained within the carotid sheath, not the prevertebral fascia. Thyroid gland (Option D): Enclosed by the pretracheal fascia, not the prevertebral fascia.","sourceRef":"Question Page 454; Solution Pages 462-464","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":454},{"id":"anatomy-12-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of these form the scalp proper?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Skin of the scalp"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skin and superficial fascia of the scalp"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skin, superficial fascia, and deep fascia of the scalp"},{"letter":"D","text":"Skin, superficial fascia, deep fascia, and loose areolar tissue of the scalp"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Skin, superficial fascia, and deep fascia of the scalp","explanation":"The first three layers of the scalp - surgical layers of the scalp = scalp proper The scalp is made up of five layers: - Skin: Hairy and has numerous sweat glands and sebaceous glands - Superficial fascia (Connective tissue): Provides a medium for vessels and nerves to pass to the skin - Deep fascia in the form of the epicranial aponeurosis or galea aponeurotica with the occipitofrontalis muscle - Loose areolar tissue: Provides passage for emissary veins connecting extracranial veins to intracranial venous sinuses - Pericranium","sourceRef":"Question Page 454; Solution Pages 464-465","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":454},{"id":"anatomy-12-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":3,"question":"All of the following are the contents of the occipital triangle of the neck except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Transverse cervical artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Occipital artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal accessory nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"External jugular vein"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. External jugular vein","explanation":"The external jugular vein is a content of the subclavian triangle , not the occipital triangle, a posterior triangle-space on the side of the neck behind the sternocleidomastoid muscle. Boundaries Anterior: Posterior border of sternocleidomastoid Posterior: Anterior border of trapezius Base: Middle one-third of clavicle Division (by the inferior belly of omohyoid) Occipital part-larger upper part Supraclavicular part or Subclavian part-smaller lower part Division Nerves Vessels Lymph Nodes Occipital Triangle Spinal accessory nerve Transverse cervical artery Occipital nodes (CN XI) Cutaneous and vein Occipital artery Supraclavicular nodes branches of cervical plexus: Lesser occipital nerve Great auricular nerve Anterior cutaneous nerve of neck Supraclavicul ar nerves Muscular branches: To levator scapulae To trapezius To rhomboids Subclavian Triangle Roots and trunks of Subclavian artery and vein A few members of the brachial plexus Nerve to Suprascapular artery and supraclavicular chain serratus anterior (long vein Transverse cervical thoracic nerve) Nerve to artery and termination of subclavius Suprascapular the corresponding vein nerve External jugular vein","sourceRef":"Question Pages 454-455; Solution Pages 465-467","sourcePage":454,"sourcePageEnd":455},{"id":"anatomy-12-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Choose the appropriate set of correct statement(s) regarding the triangle shown in the image below (red-shaded region) 1) bounded posteroinferiorly by posterior belly of digastric 2) Floor of the triangle is formed by hyoglossus anteriorly 3) Submental artery runs through anterior part of triangle 4) Carotid artery runs through posterior part of triangle **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"only 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"only 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,3,4"},{"letter":"D","text":"2,3,4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 1,3,4","explanation":"The yellow shaded region is the submandibular/digastric triangle Floor of the triangle is formed by mylohyoid anteriorly and hyoglossus posteriorly; other statements are correct. Digastric triangle: Description: Also known as submandibular triangle Enclosed between two bellies of digastric muscle Attachments: Anteroinferiorly Anterior belly of the digastric muscle Posteroinferiorly Posterior belly of the digastric muscle (Statement 1) and stylohyoid muscle Base Base of the mandible, with an imaginary line from the mandible angle to the mastoid process Apex Intermediate tendon of the digastric muscle Floor: Anteriorly Mylohyoid (Statement 2) Posteriorly Hyoglossus Roof: Investing layer of deep cervical fascia: Encloses submandibular lymph nodes Superficial fascia: Platysma, cervical branch of facial nerve, ascending branch of transverse cervical nerve Contents: Anterior part Submandibular salivary gland Submandibular lymph nodes Hypoglossal nerve Facial vein (superficial to gland) Facial artery (deep to gland) Submental artery (Statement 3) Mylohyoid nerve and vessels Posterior part External carotid artery (Statement 4) Carotid sheath and contents: Internal carotid artery, internal jugular vein, vagus nerve Structures passing between the external and internal carotid arteries: Styloglossus, stylopharyngeus, glossopharyngeal nerve , styloid process","sourceRef":"Question Page 455; Solution Pages 467-470","sourcePage":455,"sourcePageEnd":455},{"id":"anatomy-12-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":5,"question":"You notice the following examination being done in ED, all of the following are boundaries of the space containing the palpated structure except","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior belly of digastric"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stylohyoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior belly of omohyoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior border of sternocleidomastoid"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior belly of digastric","explanation":"Carotid artery pulsation is being palpated here, which is present in the carotid triangle . Carotid triangle: Boundaries Anterosuperior: Posterior belly of digastric (vs Option A) ; Stylohyoid (Option B) Anteroinferior : Superior belly of omohyoid (Option C) Posterior: Anterior border of sternocleidomastoid (Option D) Floor: Thyrohyoid Hyoglossus Middle constrictor Inferior constrictor Contents Carotid arteries: Common carotid artery, Internal carotid artery, External carotid artery and its first five branches (superior thyroid, lingual, facial, ascending pharyngeal and occipital) Carotid sinus and carotid body Internal jugular vein Last three cranial nerves: Vagus nerve, Spinal accessory nerve, Hypoglossal nerve Carotid sheath Ansa cervicalis Cervical part of the sympathetic chain Deep cervical lymph nodes","sourceRef":"Question Pages 455-456; Solution Pages 470-471","sourcePage":455,"sourcePageEnd":456},{"id":"anatomy-12-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":6,"question":"During thyroid surgery, the surgeon retracts the muscles forming the floor of the muscular triangle of neck to access the thyroid gland. Which of the following muscles does he not retract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sternothyroid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sternohyoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Geniohyoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyrohyoid"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Geniohyoid","explanation":"Geniohyoid does not contribute to the floor of the muscular triangle of the neck. Muscular triangle: Location In the anterior region of the neck Boundaries Anterior: Midline of the neck (from hyoid bone to suprasternal notch) Anterosuperior: Superior belly of the omohyoid muscle Posteroinferior: Anterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle Floor Sternothyroid, sternohyoid, and thyrohyoid muscles Roof Investing layer: Deep cervical fascia Superficial fascia: Contains the anterior jugular vein and lymph nodes Contents Infrahyoid muscles: Sternohyoid & Omohyoid (superficial) and Sternothyroid & Thyrohyoid (deep) (Option C) Deeper structures: Thyroid gland, trachea, and esophagus","sourceRef":"Question Page 456; Solution Pages 471-472","sourcePage":456,"sourcePageEnd":456},{"id":"anatomy-12-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Image below shows a method of brachial plexus block. Identify the correct set of approaches and the corresponding space in the neck. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Interscalene approach - muscular triangle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Interscalene approach - posterior triangle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supraclavicular approach - muscular triangle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supraclavicular approach - posterior triangle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Interscalene approach - posterior triangle","explanation":"Procedure shown is the interscalene approach for brachial plexus block which is done via the space present between scalene muscles, known as Scalenus triangle or Subclavian triangle , which is a part of the posterior triangle of neck . Blocks in posterior triangle: Brachial plexus block: Approaches Interscalene approach: Difficult to perform Landmark: Transverse process of C6 vertebra Relatively less safe Indication Emergency and elective surgery of the upper extremity","sourceRef":"Question Pages 456-457; Solution Pages 472-473","sourcePage":456,"sourcePageEnd":457},{"id":"anatomy-12-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":8,"question":"You notice the following procedure being done in OT. Choose the appropriate set of incorrect statement(s) regarding the same. 1) Congenital long QT syndrome is an indication in children 2) Can be done to manage Hyperhidrosis and Raynaud’s phenomenon 3) Local anaesthetic is injected superior to longus colli muscle 4) Relatively unsafe procedure 5) Contralateral Horner syndrome is verification for correct placement of block","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,5"},{"letter":"B","text":"only 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,3"},{"letter":"D","text":"3,5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. only 5","explanation":"- Procedure shown in the image is stellate ganglion block . Ipsilateral horner syndrome is a verification for correct placement of block (not contralateral) (Statement 5) . Other statements are correct. - Stellate ganglion is considered an unsafe procedure due to its high risk of injuries to surrounding structures. (Statement 4) Stellate ganglion block: Indications Children: Congenital long QT syndrome , pain syndromes (eg. herpes zoster ophthalmicus) (Statement 1) Adults: Hyperhidrosis, Raynaud’s phenomenon (Statement 2) Procedure Local anesthetic is injected superior to longus colli muscle . (Statement 3) Cervicothoracic (stellate) sympathetic block is identified via USG-guided probe and block given. Verification Ipsilateral Horner syndrome is seen after correct placement of block (vs Statement 5) Complications Injury to vital vascular structures in proximity (carotid artery, internal jugular vein) Neural puncture (recurrent laryngeal nerve, vagus nerve) Injury to thyroid gland/esophagus/trachea Pneumothorax","sourceRef":"Question Pages 457-458; Solution Pages 473-474","sourcePage":457,"sourcePageEnd":458},{"id":"anatomy-12-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Patient presented with a painful swelling as shown below. Senior doctor says the pain is due to the underlying fascia. All of the following structures are enclosed by the layer forming this fascia except","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Levator scapulae"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Submandibular gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"External jugular vein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Levator scapulae","explanation":"The given clinical scenario is suggestive of parotid gland swelling. Pain is due to the underlying parotid fascia formed by the investing layer of deep cervical fascia. Levator scapulae is not enclosed by the investing layer. - Parotid fascia: Investing layer splits into two. Superficial layer: Thick and dense, attached to zygomatic arch Deep layer: Thin and attached to styloid process - Superficial layer: Thick and dense, attached to zygomatic arch - Deep layer: Thin and attached to styloid process - Superficial layer: Thick and dense, attached to zygomatic arch - Deep layer: Thin and attached to styloid process Investing layer of deep cervical fascia: Location Lies deep to the platysma, encircling the neck like a collar Forms the roof of the posterior triangle of the neck Superior Attachments External occipital protuberance Superior nuchal line Mastoid process, styloid process External acoustic meatus Base of the mandible Inferior Attachments Spine of scapula Posterior Attachments Ligamentum nuchae Spine of seventh cervical vertebra (C7) Anterior Attachments Symphysis menti Hyoid bone Enclosed Structures Muscles: Trapezius (Option B) , Sternocleidomastoid Salivary glands: Parotid , Submandibular gland (Option C) Spaces: Suprasternal space of Burns: Sternal heads of bilateral sternocleidomastoid; Jugular venous arch; Interclavicular ligament Supraclavicular space: External jugular vein (Option D) ; Supraclavicular nerves Forms pulleys for digastric and omohyoid tendons Forms roof of anterior and posterior triangles Forms stylomandibular ligament (separates parotid gland from the submandibular gland) and parotido-masseteric fascia","sourceRef":"Question Pages 458-459; Solution Pages 474-476","sourcePage":458,"sourcePageEnd":459},{"id":"anatomy-12-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a function of the pretracheal fascia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enclosing the thyroid gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"Enclosing the trachea and esophagus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Surrounding the carotid sheath"},{"letter":"D","text":"Limiting the spread of infection in the anterior neck"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Surrounding the carotid sheath","explanation":"- Pretracheal fascia does not surround the carotid sheath. - Pretracheal fascia/Middle cervical fascia/Porter’s fascia: Thin layer of deep fascia limited to anterior part of the neck and is a coronal extension from internal surface of the investing layer, that encloses the thyroid gland and stretches in front of trachea. It can be divided into two layers: - Muscular Layer: Encloses the infrahyoid muscles (strap muscles) such as the sternohyoid, sternothyroid, and omohyoid. - Visceral Layer: Encloses the thyroid gland, trachea and esophagus. Attachments: - Superiorly : Attached to the hyoid bone. - Inferiorly : Extends to the upper thorax and blends with the fibrous pericardium. - Laterally : Blends with the carotid sheath, though it does not enclose the structures within the sheath. Functions : - Support : Provides structural support for the thyroid gland, trachea, and esophagus. (Option A & B) - Infection Control : Helps limit the spread of infections within the neck, particularly in the anterior compartment. (Option D) - Facilitates Movement: Allows for movement of the thyroid gland and trachea during swallowing and breathing.","sourceRef":"Question Page 459; Solution Pages 476-477","sourcePage":459,"sourcePageEnd":459},{"id":"anatomy-12-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents to the emergency department with severe neck pain, difficulty swallowing, and fever. A CT scan reveals a deep neck infection that has spread into the mediastinum. Which anatomical space is most likely involved in this patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parapharyngeal space"},{"letter":"B","text":"Carotid space"},{"letter":"C","text":"Retropharyngeal space"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prevertebral space"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Retropharyngeal space","explanation":"Retropharyngeal space extends from the base of the skull down to the posterior mediastinum. Infections in this space can easily spread to the mediastinum, leading to mediastinitis . Classification of Spaces: - Suprahyoid spaces: Located above the hyoid bone, including the submandibular and prevertebral spaces. - Infrahyoid spaces: Located below the hyoid bone, typically related to deeper neck structures. Suprahyoid spaces: Submental space Deep to upper investing layer of deep cervical fascia, superficial to mylohyoid muscles Submandibular space Deep to the concerned half of the mandible, between the anterior and posterior bellies of the digastric muscle Sublingual space Deep to mylohyoid muscles in the floor of the mouth, continuous with the opposite side Peritonsillar space Part of intrapharyngeal space, around palatine tonsils between pillars of fauces Parapharyngeal space On either side of the posterolateral aspect of pharynx Medially - superior constrictor of pharynx Laterally - medial pterygoid Retropharyngeal space Anteriorly - buccopharyngeal fascia of pharynx Posteriorly - prevertebral layer Laterally - carotid sheath Superiorly - cranial base Infrahyoid Spaces: Pretracheal space Behind pretracheal fascia and infrahyoid muscles, in front of the anterior wall of esophagus. Retrovisceral space Between the posterior wall of pharynx/esophagus and prevertebral fascia. Prevertebral space Between prevertebral fascia and vertebral column. Carotid space Around the contents of carotid sheath, demarcated by parts of investing, pretracheal, and prevertebral layers. Parapharyngeal space (Option A): While this space is adjacent to the pharynx and can be a site of infection, it does not communicate directly with the mediastinum. Carotid space (Option B): This space contains the carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and vagus nerve but is not typically involved in spreading infections to the mediastinum. Prevertebral space (Option D): Infections here can affect the vertebrae or muscles but are less likely to spread to the mediastinum compared to the retropharyngeal space.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 459-460; Solution Pages 477-480","sourcePage":459,"sourcePageEnd":460},{"id":"anatomy-12-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following structures is not the content of carotid sheath?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Common carotid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal jugular vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"External jugular vein"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. External jugular vein","explanation":"Carotid Sheath: Condensation of the connective tissue around the great vessels of the neck, forming a tubular sheet of fascia. Contents Common carotid artery Internal carotid artery Internal jugular vein Vagus nerve. Anatomical Details The carotid sheath extends from the upper border of the thyroid cartilage down to the bifurcation of the carotid arteries. The internal jugular vein is lateral to the carotid artery; the vagus nerve is located between and posterior to the vessels. Anteriorly The carotid sheath is covered by the pretracheal fascia. Posteriorly The carotid sheath is bordered by the prevertebral fascia.","sourceRef":"Question Page 460; Solution Pages 480-481","sourcePage":460,"sourcePageEnd":460},{"id":"anatomy-12-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements about parotid gland is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The parotid duct passes through the masseter muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"The parotid gland is innervated by the facial nerve (CN VII)."},{"letter":"C","text":"The parotid gland is located anterior to the sternocleidomastoid muscle."},{"letter":"D","text":"The parotid gland primarily secretes mucous saliva."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The parotid duct passes through the masseter muscle.","explanation":"Parotid gland : - Largest salivary gland is located on each side of the face anterior to the ears and is made up of two lobes, a superficial lobe, and a deep lobe. - Located in the retromandibular fossa. - The superficial lobe lies lateral to the facial nerve and overlies the lateral surface of the masseter muscle. - The deep lobe lies medial to the facial nerve and is situated between the mastoid process of the temporal bone and the mandibular ramus. - A fascial capsule called the parotid sheath surrounds the parotid gland. - The duct of the parotid gland, also termed the Stensen duct , runs across the masseter muscle and exits into the oral cavity at the level of the upper second molar. - The parotid gland is supplied by the external carotid artery and its branches and drained by the external jugular vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 460; Solution Pages 481-482","sourcePage":460,"sourcePageEnd":460},{"id":"anatomy-12-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 60-year-old man is scheduled for a parotidectomy due to a tumor in the parotid gland. The surgeon reviews the anatomical relationships to avoid damage to surrounding structures. Which of the following structures is located superiorly to the parotid gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Styloglossus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Masseter muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Zygomatic arch"},{"letter":"D","text":"External carotid artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Zygomatic arch","explanation":"Zygomatic arch is located superiorly to the parotid gland. Relations to parotid gland Anteriorly Masseter muscle Buccal fat pad Posteriorly External ear Mastoid process Sternocleidomastoid muscle Medially Styloid process and associated muscles (styloglossus, stylohyoid, digastric) Carotid artery and internal jugular vein Cranial nerves IX (glossopharyngeal), X (vagus), and XI (accessory) Mandibles ramus Laterally Skin and superficial fascia Inferiorly Angle of the mandible Submandibular gland Superiorly Zygomatic arch","sourceRef":"Question Pages 460-461; Solution Pages 482-483","sourcePage":460,"sourcePageEnd":461},{"id":"anatomy-12-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-12","chapter":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topic":"Lesson 12 - Scalp, Neck Triangles and Parotid Gland","topicIndex":12,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following statements about the structures within the parotid gland are accurate? The external carotid artery enters the parotid gland through the posteromedial surface. The maxillary artery exits through the posterior surface of the gland. The superficial temporal artery branches into the transverse facial artery and emerges from the anterior part of the superior surface. The superficial temporal vein and maxillary vein unite to form the retromandibular vein, which divides into anterior and posterior divisions near the apex of the gland. The facial nerve enters the parotid gland and passes behind the retromandibular vein.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Only 1,3 and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only 1 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"All except 2"},{"letter":"D","text":"All the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Only 1,3 and 4","explanation":"Correct Answers: A) Only 1,3 and 4 - The maxillary artery exits through the anteromedial surface, not the posterior surface. - The facial nerve enters the parotid gland at the posteromedial surface, passing in front of the retromandibular vein, not behind it. Structures Within the Parotid Gland Arteries External Carotid Artery: Enters through the posteromedial surface. Maxillary Artery: Exits through the anteromedial surface. Superficial Temporal Artery : Branches into the transverse facial artery and emerges from the anterior part of the superior surface. Veins Superficial Temporal Vein and Maxillary Vein unite to form the Retromandibular Vein , which divides into anterior and posterior divisions near the apex of the gland. Facial Nerve Exits the skull through the Stylomastoid Foramen, enters the parotid gland at the posteromedial surface , passing in front of the retromandibular vein and external carotid artery. Divides into Temporofacial and Cervicofacial branches near the neck of the mandible, further branching into five terminal branches: Temporal, Zygomatic, Buccal, Marginal Mandibular, and Cervical, exiting along the anterior border and apex of the gland. Lymphatics Contains parotid lymph nodes located in the superficial fascia and deep to the deep fascia over the gland. Parotid Duct/ Stensen's Duct About 5 cm long. Origin: Emerges from the middle of the parotid gland's anterior border. Course: Runs forward over the masseter muscle, then makes a medial turn at the masseter's anterior border. Structures Pierced: Buccal Pad of Fat Buccopharyngeal Fascia Buccinator Muscle (penetrates obliquely) Termination: After passing through the buccinator, the duct travels briefly between the buccinator and oral mucosa, then opens into the vestibule of the mouth opposite the upper second molar's crown. This oblique passage prevents the duct from inflating with air when blowing.","sourceRef":"Question Page 461; Solution Pages 483-485","sourcePage":461,"sourcePageEnd":461},{"id":"anatomy-13-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the histology of the esophagus? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The upper third consists of smooth muscle, and the lower third is composed of striated muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"The adventitia is made up of dense connective tissue with blood vessels and nerves."},{"letter":"C","text":"The middle third contains a mix of striated and smooth muscle fibers."},{"letter":"D","text":"The entire esophagus is lined with keratinized stratified squamous epithelium."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The middle third contains a mix of striated and smooth muscle fibers.","explanation":"The middle portion of the esophagus has both striated and smooth muscle fibers. Histology of the Oesophagus - When empty, the mucous membrane forms longitudinal folds. - The lining is non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, providing protection. (Option D ruled out) - Lamina propria: Contains papillae extending into the epithelium. - Muscularis mucosae: More distinct as it progresses down the esophagus. - Submucosa: Contains mucus-secreting oesophageal glands with rounded or oval acini. - The muscularis externa consists of: (Option A ruled out) Striated muscle in the upper third A combination of striated and smooth muscle in the middle third (Option C) Smooth muscle in the lower third - Striated muscle in the upper third - A combination of striated and smooth muscle in the middle third (Option C) - Smooth muscle in the lower third - Adventitia : Composed of loose connective tissue containing capillaries and nerves. (Option B ruled out) - The esophagus lacks a serosa, unlike many other organs in the digestive tract. - Striated muscle in the upper third - A combination of striated and smooth muscle in the middle third (Option C) - Smooth muscle in the lower third","sourceRef":"Question Page 486; Solution Pages 498-499","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"anatomy-13-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which among the following is the correct pairing of the part of the larynx to the structure from which it is derived?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epiglottis-4th pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cricoid cartilage-5th pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intrinsic muscles-7th pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cricothyroid muscle-5th pharyngeal arch"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Epiglottis-4th pharyngeal arch","explanation":"Development of larynx: Component Derivation/Description Lining Epithelium Derived from endoderm Cartilages Epiglottis , thyroid, cuneiform cartilages: Derived from 4th pharyngeal arch Cricoid, arytenoid, corniculate cartilages: Derived from 6th pharyngeal arch Muscles Derived from branchial mesoderm All intrinsic muscles: Develops from the 6th pharyngeal arch Supplied by recurrent laryngeal nerve Cricothyroid muscle: Develops from the 4th pharyngeal arch Supplied by external laryngeal branch of superior laryngeal nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 486; Solution Page 499","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":486},{"id":"anatomy-13-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following structures of the larynx have seromucous glands? False vocal cord True vocal cord Ventricle Saccule","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"2 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 and 3"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1 and 4","explanation":"False vocal cord and saccule have seromucous glands Histology of larynx: Structure Histological Features False Vocal Cord Columnar ciliated respiratory-type epithelium with seromucous glands True Vocal Cord Stratified squamous epithelium Ventricle Respiratory-type columnar epithelium Saccule Respiratory-type columnar epithelium with seromucous glands","sourceRef":"Question Pages 486-487; Solution Pages 499-500","sourcePage":486,"sourcePageEnd":487},{"id":"anatomy-13-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 58-year-old male patient presents with difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) and nasal regurgitation of food. He also reports that his speech has become nasal and less clear. On examination, you notice that his soft palate does not elevate properly during swallowing, and there is an abnormal ridge in the nasopharynx. Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paralysis of the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Damage to the glossopharyngeal nerve (Cranial Nerve IX)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Weakness of the levator veli palatini muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dysfunction of the palatopharyngeus muscle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Paralysis of the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle","explanation":"- Pasavant's ridge is formed by the contraction of the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle during swallowing, which helps seal off the nasopharynx to prevent food from entering the nasal cavity. - If there is paralysis or dysfunction of this muscle, the ridge will not form properly, leading to nasal regurgitation and difficulty in swallowing, as seen in this patient. - Pasavant's ridge is a prominent anatomical structure in the nasopharynx that plays an important role in swallowing and speech. - It is a muscular ridge formed by the contraction of the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle during swallowing. Location: - Pasavant's ridge is located in the lateral wall of the nasopharynx behind the nasal cavity and above the soft palate. Formation : - The ridge is formed when the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle contracts during swallowing. - This muscle is part of the pharyngeal constrictor group and is responsible for the contraction of the pharynx during swallowing, which helps move food from the mouth to the esophagus. - The contraction of this muscle pulls the tissue in the nasopharynx into a ridge-like structure, creating the Pasavant's ridge . Function : - Closure of the nasopharynx: One of the primary functions of Pasavant's ridge is to help seal off the nasopharynx during swallowing. - This prevents food or liquid from regurgitating into the nasal cavity. - During swallowing, the ridge helps to create a physical barrier that assists the levator veli palatini (a muscle of the soft palate) in elevating and closing off the nasopharynx. - This coordinated closure helps direct food into the oropharynx (the middle part of the throat) and prevents it from entering the nasal passages. - Speech: Pasavant’s ridge can also contribute to proper speech by helping maintain the closure of the nasopharynx during speech sounds, especially those requiring the oral cavity to be isolated from the nasal cavity (e.g., in non-nasal speech sounds). Clinical Relevance: - Dysfunction of Pasavant's ridge can lead to nasal regurgitation of food, difficulty swallowing, and nasal speech. - This often occurs when there is weakness or paralysis of the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle, which leads to insufficient closure of the nasopharynx during swallowing. - Conditions like cranial nerve palsies (e.g., vagus nerve or glossopharyngeal nerve dysfunction), neurological disorders , or structural damage can impair the function of the superior pharyngeal constrictor and disrupt the formation of Pasavant's ridge. Damage to the glossopharyngeal nerve (Option B) would affect the stylopharyngeus muscle , but it would not typically cause a problem with Pasavant's ridge, as this ridge is related to the superior pharyngeal constrictor muscle. Weakness of the levator veli palatini muscle (Option C) would affect soft palate elevation, but it would not directly affect Pasavant’s ridge or result in nasal regurgitation. Dysfunction of the palatopharyngeus muscle (Option D) would impact pharyngeal function, but it does not directly relate to the formation of Pasavant’s ridge.","sourceRef":"Question Page 487; Solution Pages 500-502","sourcePage":487,"sourcePageEnd":487},{"id":"anatomy-13-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Mark the true statement about the structure being palpated here","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Blood supply is by superior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extends from C6-T6 in standing position"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve lies posterior to it"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Blood supply is by superior thyroid artery","explanation":"Structure being palpated here is the trachea. Trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. Trachea: Structure The trachea is a flexible fibrocartilaginous tube forming the start of the lower respiratory tract, supported by 16–20 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings . Function The trachea remains open due to these cartilage rings, while the gap between the posterior ends is bridged by smooth muscle (trachealis) and fibroelastic ligament, allowing esophageal expansion during swallowing. Location Extends from the lower border of the cricoid cartilage (C6 vertebra) to the lower border of the T4 vertebra. The upper half lies in the neck (cervical part) Lower half in the superior mediastinum (thoracic part). Variations Cadaver (supine): C6 to T4 Living (standing): C6 to T6 (Option B) Newborns: C6 to T3 Dimensions Length: 10–12 cm External diameter: 2 cm (males), 1.5 cm (females) Internal diameter: 12 mm (adults), 3 mm (newborns) Course Begins as a continuation of the larynx at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage (C6 vertebra), descends behind the manubrium, and bifurcates into two bronchi at the lower border of T4, slightly to the right. Relations Anterior: Arch of aorta Brachiocephalic trunk Left brachiocephalic vein Superior vena cava Posterior: Esophagus Left recurrent laryngeal nerve (Option D) Vertebral column Right: Azygous vein Left: Arch of aorta, Left subclavian vein, Left phrenic nerve On either sides: Lung and pleura, vagus nerve Histology Mucosa is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells (Option C) Blood supply Arterial supply: Inferior thyroid artery (Option A) Venous drainage: Left brachiocephalic vein Lymphatic drainage Pretracheal and paratracheal nodes","sourceRef":"Question Pages 487-488; Solution Pages 502-503","sourcePage":487,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"anatomy-13-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Patient who had suspected lung pathology was advised to undergo CT chest. While analysing the CT images, what vertebral level could this image likely correspond to? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T2"},{"letter":"B","text":"T3"},{"letter":"C","text":"T4"},{"letter":"D","text":"T5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. T4","explanation":"CT image given in question shows arch of aorta, which corresponds to T4 vertebral level. Vertebral levels and structures: Structure Vertebral level Cricoid cartilage End of larynx & pharynx Beginning of C6 esophagus & trachea Tracheal bifurcation Sternal angle (Angle of Louis) Arch of T4 (Option C) aorta Azygous vein drains into SVC Openings of diaphragm: IVC opening T8 Esophageal hiatus T10 Aortic hiatus T12 Constrictions of esophagus: Pharyngo-esophageal junction (cervical) C6 Arch of aorta crossing T4 Crossing of Left principal bronchus T6 Diaphragm opening T10","sourceRef":"Question Page 488; Solution Pages 503-506","sourcePage":488,"sourcePageEnd":488},{"id":"anatomy-13-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":7,"question":"All of the following are true about structures related to the extent of larynx except","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extent of larynx in adult female is C3-C6"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bifurcation of common carotid occurs at superior border of thyroid cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cricoid is the lowermost cartilage of larynx"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extent of larynx in children is C1-C4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Extent of larynx in adult female is C3-C6","explanation":"Extent of larynx in adult female is higher up , at the level of C1-C4 and that of adult male is C3-C6. Extent of larynx: Adult male C3-C6 Adult female C1-C4 (Option A) Children C1-C4 (Option D) Structures related to the level of the larynx: - Bifurcation of common carotid: At the superior border of thyroid cartilage (Option B) , carotid sinus present at the same level. - Cricoid cartilage: The lowermost cartilage of the larynx (Option C) , at the lower border of the C6 vertebra. - The larynx and pharynx end at the same level, at the lower border of the C6 vertebra. - Branches of vagi: Recurrent and superior laryngeal nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 488-489; Solution Page 506","sourcePage":488,"sourcePageEnd":489},{"id":"anatomy-13-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Choose the appropriate set of incorrect statement(s) about the course of the marked nerve in image 1) Left sided nerve loops around arch of aorta 2) Travels anterior to the lung hilum 3) Originates from the lateral aspect of medulla 4) Runs within the carotid sheath with IJV laterally and ICA medially 5) Right nerve has a longer course than the Left. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,5"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,4,5"},{"letter":"D","text":"2,5"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 2,5","explanation":"The nerve in the cadaveric image is the Vagus (X) . - Vagus travels posterior to lung hilum (not anterior), and the Right vagus has a shorter course than the Left . Vagus nerve course: Origin and common course Exits from the lateral aspect of the medulla (Statement 3) , posterior to the pontomedullary junction, and leaves the skull through the jugular foramen. Runs within the carotid sheath with IJV laterally and ICA medially (Statement 4) The right vagus has a shorter course than the Left (Statement 5) Branches Superior Laryngeal Nerve: Emerges in the neck and supplies the larynx, divides further into Internal laryngeal nerve: Responsible for cough reflex External laryngeal nerve: Supplies the Cricothyroid muscle Recurrent Laryngeal Nerves: Ascend from the brachiocephalic artery on the right and aortic arch on the left Supplies all muscles of the larynx except the cricothyroid","sourceRef":"Question Page 489; Solution Pages 506-508","sourcePage":489,"sourcePageEnd":489},{"id":"anatomy-13-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Arterial supply of larynx is by","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior laryngeal artery- branch of superior thyroid artery & inferior laryngeal artery- branch of inferior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Both superior and inferior laryngeal artery - branch of inferior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both superior and inferior laryngeal artery - branch of superior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior laryngeal artery- branch of subclavian artery & inferior laryngeal artery- branch of inferior thyroid artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior laryngeal artery- branch of superior thyroid artery & inferior laryngeal artery- branch of inferior thyroid artery","explanation":"artery- branch of inferior thyroid artery The arterial supply of the larynx is mainly provided by the Superior and inferior laryngeal arteries, which are branches of the superior and inferior thyroid arteries, respectively. Arterial supply: Artery Branch of Superior laryngeal artery Superior thyroid artery (branch of External carotid) Inferior laryngeal artery Inferior thyroid artery (branch of Thyrocervical trunk) Cricothyroid artery (occasionally) Superior thyroid artery (branch of External carotid) Rima glottidis: Has dual blood supply (from both superior and inferior laryngeal) Venous drainage: Vein Drains into Superior laryngeal vein Superior thyroid vein → Internal jugular vein Inferior laryngeal vein Inferior thyroid vein → Left Brachiocephalic vein","sourceRef":"Question Pages 489-490; Solution Pages 508-510","sourcePage":489,"sourcePageEnd":490},{"id":"anatomy-13-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":10,"question":"What is the structure marked as X in the laryngoscopy diagram given below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corniculate cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyroid cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cricoid cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arytenoid cartilage"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Corniculate cartilage","explanation":"Structures visible in laryngoscopy: - Epiglottis:Appears as a leaf-shaped structure at the upper part of the laryngeal inlet. - Vocal cords (vocal folds): True vocal cords: Visible as two pearly white, horizontally oriented bands. False vocal cords (vestibular folds): Located superior to the true vocal cords, these are less prominent and do not move during sound production. - True vocal cords: Visible as two pearly white, horizontally oriented bands. - False vocal cords (vestibular folds): Located superior to the true vocal cords, these are less prominent and do not move during sound production. - True vocal cords: Visible as two pearly white, horizontally oriented bands. - False vocal cords (vestibular folds): Located superior to the true vocal cords, these are less prominent and do not move during sound production. - Aryepiglottic folds:Folds of tissue that run from the sides of the epiglottis to the arytenoids. These are seen framing the laryngeal inlet. - These are seen framing the laryngeal inlet. - These are seen framing the laryngeal inlet.","sourceRef":"Question Page 490; Solution Pages 510-511","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":490},{"id":"anatomy-13-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Match the intrinsic muscles of the vocal cord with its function Function Muscle a. Abduct the vocal cords 1. Aryepiglottic b. Tense the vocal cords 2. Posterior cricoarytenoid c. Close the inlet of the larynx 3. Cricothyroid d. Open inlet of larynx 4. Thyroepiglotticus Function Muscle a. Abduct the vocal cords 1. Aryepiglottic b. Tense the vocal cords 2. Posterior cricoarytenoid c. Close the inlet of the larynx 3. Cricothyroid d. Open inlet of larynx 4. Thyroepiglotticus **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a2,b3,c1,d4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a1,b2,c3,d4"},{"letter":"C","text":"a4,b3,c2,d1"},{"letter":"D","text":"a3,b1,c2,d4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. a2,b3,c1,d4","explanation":"Intrinsic muscles of the larynx: Function Muscles Abduct the vocal cords Posterior cricoarytenoids (safety muscle of larynx) Adduct the vocal cords Lateral cricoarytenoids Transverse arytenoid Cricothyroids (tuning fork of larynx) Thyroarytenoids Tense the vocal cords Cricothyroids Relax the vocal cords Thyroarytenoids Vocalis Close the inlet of the larynx Oblique arytenoids Aryepiglottic Open the inlet of the larynx Thyroepiglotticus","sourceRef":"Question Pages 490-491; Solution Pages 511-512","sourcePage":490,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"anatomy-13-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following cartilages of the larynx is paired?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroid cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cricoid cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epiglottis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arytenoid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Arytenoid","explanation":"Cartilages of larynx-9 in total: - Unpaired cartilages Thyroid (Hyaline cartilage) Cricoid (Hyaline cartilage) Epiglottis (Elastic cartilage) - Thyroid (Hyaline cartilage) - Cricoid (Hyaline cartilage) - Epiglottis (Elastic cartilage) - Thyroid (Hyaline cartilage) - Cricoid (Hyaline cartilage) - Epiglottis (Elastic cartilage) - Paired cartilages: Arytenoid (Hyaline cartilage) Corniculate (Elastic cartilage) Cuneiform (Elastic cartilage) - Arytenoid (Hyaline cartilage) - Corniculate (Elastic cartilage) - Cuneiform (Elastic cartilage) - Arytenoid (Hyaline cartilage) - Corniculate (Elastic cartilage) - Cuneiform (Elastic cartilage) Thyroid cartilage: Feature Description Shape V-shaped in cross-section, with right and left laminae Laminae Roughly quadrilateral Anterior Borders Approach at an angle of about 90° in males, 120° in females. Laryngeal Prominence Formed by fusion of the lower parts of the anterior borders. Thyroid Notch Separates the upper parts of the anterior borders. Superior Cornua (Horns) Connected to the greater cornua of the hyoid bone by the lateral thyrohyoid ligament. Inferior Cornua (Horns) Articulates with the cricoid cartilage, forming the cricothyroid joint.","sourceRef":"Question Page 491; Solution Pages 512-514","sourcePage":491,"sourcePageEnd":491},{"id":"anatomy-13-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":13,"question":"From the sagittal section of the larynx given below, identify the part marked as X. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroid cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cricoid cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Arytenoid cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cuneiform cartilage"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Arytenoid cartilage","explanation":"- Epiglottis: Leaf-shaped cartilage located at the superior aspect of the larynx - Thyroid cartilage: The largest cartilage of the larynx, visible as a large shield-like structure with its anterior prominence forming the Adam's apple. - Cricoid cartilage: A ring-shaped cartilage below the thyroid cartilage, marking the lower limit of the larynx. - Arytenoid cartilages: Pair of cartilages located on top of the posterior part of the cricoid cartilage - Thyrohyoid membrane: Connects the thyroid cartilage to the hyoid bone. - Cricothyroid membrane: Connects the cricoid and thyroid cartilages, seen between them.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 491-492; Solution Page 514","sourcePage":491,"sourcePageEnd":492},{"id":"anatomy-13-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Arrange the structures of the pharynx in the correct sequential order from innermost to outermost.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mucosa, Submucosa, Buccopharyngeal fascia, Pharyngobasilar fascia, Muscular coat"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mucosa, Submucosa, Muscular coat, Buccopharyngeal fascia, Pharyngobasilar fascia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mucosa, Submucosa, Pharyngobasilar fascia, Muscular coat, Buccopharyngeal fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mucosa, Submucosa, Buccopharyngeal fascia, Muscular coat, Pharyngobasilar fascia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Mucosa, Submucosa, Pharyngobasilar fascia, Muscular coat, Buccopharyngeal fascia","explanation":"Buccopharyngeal fascia Structures of the pharynx in the correct sequential order from innermost to outermost is Mucosa, Submucosa, Pharyngobasilar fascia, Muscular coat and Buccopharyngeal fascia Pharynx- Anatomy and parts Muscular tube located behind the nose, mouth, and larynx, serving as a passage for both air and food. Approximately 12 cm long, widest at the top, and narrowest at the bottom. Boundaries of the Pharynx Superiorly : Base of the skull Inferiorly : Oesophagus (at the level of the sixth cervical vertebra) Posteriorly : Prevertebral fascia Anteriorly : Nasal cavity, oral cavity, and larynx Laterally : Styloid process, carotid arteries, cranial nerves Parts of the Pharynx Nasopharynx : Behind the nose, connects to the middle ear via the pharyngotympanic tube. Primarily located at the C1 vertebral levels. Oropharynx : Behind the oral cavity and corresponds to the C2 to upper part of C3 vertebral levels. Laryngopharynx: Behind the larynx, it extends from the upper border of epiglottis to the cricoid cartilage's lower border. It corresponds to the C3 to C6 vertebral levels. Structure of the Pharynx Mucosa : Innermost layer. Submucosa : Beneath the mucosa. Pharyngobasilar fascia : Fibrous sheet thickest in the upper part, fills gap between the superior constrictor and the skull. Muscular coat : Outer circular and inner longitudinal layers. Buccopharyngeal fascia : Covers the constrictor muscles and extends forward to cover the buccinator.","sourceRef":"Question Page 492; Solution Pages 514-516","sourcePage":492,"sourcePageEnd":492},{"id":"anatomy-13-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents with difficulty swallowing (dysphagia) and is scheduled for an oesophagoscopy. During the procedure, the gastroenterologist must navigate the natural constrictions of the esophagus. At approximately 27.5 cm from the patient's incisor teeth, which anatomical structure is causing the narrowing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cricopharyngeus muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aortic arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left main bronchus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diaphragmatic hiatus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Left main bronchus","explanation":"- Understanding the constrictions of esophagus is crucial for procedures like oesophagoscopy, which involves using a flexible camera to inspect the esophagus. - Awareness of these narrow points ensures the safe passage of the esophagoscope. - Constrictions occur at four main points: - Cricopharyngeus Muscle (6 inches from incisors): This first constriction is at the upper esophageal sphincter where the esophagus begins. It is caused by the cricopharyngeus muscle, which plays a role in swallowing. (Option A) - This first constriction is at the upper esophageal sphincter where the esophagus begins. - It is caused by the cricopharyngeus muscle, which plays a role in swallowing. (Option A) - Aortic Arch (9 inches from incisors) : The second narrowing occurs where the esophagus is crossed by the arch of the aorta. The pressure from the aorta causes this indentation. (Option B) - The second narrowing occurs where the esophagus is crossed by the arch of the aorta. - The pressure from the aorta causes this indentation. (Option B) - Left Main Bronchus (11 inches from incisors): Third constriction is where the left bronchus crosses over the esophagus, leading to a narrowing. (Option C) - Third constriction is where the left bronchus crosses over the esophagus, leading to a narrowing. (Option C) - Diaphragmatic Hiatus (15 inches from incisors): The final narrowing happens where the esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus. (Option D) - The final narrowing happens where the esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus. (Option D) - This first constriction is at the upper esophageal sphincter where the esophagus begins. - It is caused by the cricopharyngeus muscle, which plays a role in swallowing. (Option A) - The second narrowing occurs where the esophagus is crossed by the arch of the aorta. - The pressure from the aorta causes this indentation. (Option B) - Third constriction is where the left bronchus crosses over the esophagus, leading to a narrowing. (Option C) - The final narrowing happens where the esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the esophageal hiatus. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 492-493; Solution Pages 516-517","sourcePage":492,"sourcePageEnd":493},{"id":"anatomy-13-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 45 year old male presents with difficulty swallowing and occasional regurgitation of undigested food. A barium swallow study is shown below. Esophageal manometry reveals impaired relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter (LES). What is the most likely underlying cause of this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of parasympathetic innervation from the recurrent laryngeal nerves"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyperactivity of the sympathetic nerves from the upper thoracic ganglia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Degeneration of the esophageal plexus affecting peristalsis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Overactivity of the middle cervical ganglion fibres"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Degeneration of the esophageal plexus affecting peristalsis","explanation":"Achalasia cardia, with a \"bird-beak\" appearance in barium swallow study at the gastroesophageal junction is often associated with the degeneration of the esophageal plexus, leading to impaired peristalsis and failure of LES relaxation. Lymphatics and nerve supply of esophagus Lymphatic Drainage The lymphatic drainage of the esophagus varies by region Cervical esophagus: Drains into the deep cervical lymph nodes. Thoracic esophagus: Drains into the posterior mediastinal lymph nodes. Abdominal esophagus: Drains into the left gastric lymph nodes. Each section's lymphatic fluid is directed to nodes located in the neck, thorax, and upper abdomen. Nerve Supply The nerve supply of the esophagus involves both parasympathetic and sympathetic systems, enabling the control of swallowing and peristalsis. Parasympathetic nerves: Recurrent laryngeal nerves: Innervate the upper esophagus. Esophageal plexus: Derived from the vagus nerve, it supplies the lower esophagus. Sympathetic nerves: Middle cervical ganglion: Provides fibers that accompany the inferior thyroid arteries to the upper esophagus. Upper four thoracic ganglia: Supply the lower esophagus. Clinical Significance The esophageal plexus coordinates peristalsis and regulates the lower esophageal sphincter (LES), preventing reflux of stomach acid. Disorders such as achalasia cardia, where the LES fails to relax, can cause difficulty in swallowing and food buildup in the esophagus. It occurs due to the absence of nerve cells in the esophageal wall. Loss of parasympathetic innervation from the recurrent laryngeal nerves (Option A): The recurrent laryngeal nerves primarily supply the upper esophagus, and loss of their function would lead to issues with swallowing in the cervical region but not cause the typical \"bird-beak\" appearance of achalasia. Hyperactivity of the sympathetic nerves from the upper thoracic ganglia (Option B): Sympathetic nerves control vasoconstriction but are not responsible for the coordination of peristalsis. Overactivity of the middle cervical ganglion fibers (Option D): The middle cervical ganglion primarily supplies sympathetic fibers to the upper esophagus, which would not explain the patient's symptoms.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 493-494; Solution Pages 517-518","sourcePage":493,"sourcePageEnd":494},{"id":"anatomy-13-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 48 year old male with a long-standing history of alcohol-related liver cirrhosis presents to the ER department with hematemesis. On examination, icterus is seen and the abdomen is distended. An upper gastrointestinal endoscopy confirms the presence of bleeding oesophageal varices at the lower end of the oesophagus. Which of the following vessels is primarily responsible for creating the portosystemic anastomoses that lead to the development of these varices?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior phrenic veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left gastric vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Brachiocephalic vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal thoracic vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Left gastric vein","explanation":"The lower esophagus is drained by the left gastric vein, which connects to the portal vein. - In portal hypertension, increased pressure in the portal system can lead to dilation of the left gastric vein and the development of esophageal varices through portosystemic anastomoses. Blood supply and drainage of esophagus Arterial Supply The esophagus receives arterial blood primarily from branches of the left gastric artery. Additionally, different parts of the esophagus are supplied by: Cervical esophagus: Inferior thyroid arteries. Thoracic esophagus: Esophageal branches of the aorta. Abdominal esophagus: Esophageal branches of the left gastric artery. Venous Drainage The esophagus drains into both the portal and systemic venous systems, forming portosystemic anastomoses. Upper esophagus: Veins drain into the brachiocephalic veins. (Option C) Middle esophagus: Veins drain into the azygos veins. Lower esophagus: Veins drain into both the left gastric vein (portal system) and the azygos/hemiazygos veins (systemic system). (Option B) Clinical Significance: The portosystemic anastomoses at the lower end of the esophagus are crucial in conditions like portal hypertension. In portal hypertension, the pressure in the portal venous system increases (commonly due to liver disease), causing the anastomoses to dilate. This dilation leads to the formation of esophageal varices, which are fragile and can rupture, resulting in life-threatening bleeding (haematemesis). Inferior phrenic veins (Option A): These veins do not play a role in the venous drainage of the esophagus or the formation of varices related to portal hypertension. Internal thoracic vein (Option D) : Drains part of the chest wall and thoracic structures, but has no significant role in the development of oesophageal varices in portal hypertension.","sourceRef":"Question Page 494; Solution Pages 518-519","sourcePage":494,"sourcePageEnd":494},{"id":"anatomy-13-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the course of the esophagus in thorax and abdomen. The esophagus begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage. The esophagus enters the abdomen at the level of the T10 vertebra. The esophagus ends at the cardiac end of the stomach at T11 vertebra. The thoracic esophagus passes through the anterior mediastinum.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Only 1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"All except 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"All except 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"All the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. All except 4","explanation":"Esophagus passes through the superior and posterior mediastinum in the thorax. Course of esophagus Origin Begins at the lower border of the cricoid cartilage, connecting to the lower end of the pharynx. (Option C) Course in the Neck Travels downward in front of the vertebral column. Thoracic Course Passes through the superior and posterior mediastinum in the thorax. (Option C) Diaphragmatic Passage Passes through the esophageal hiatus at the level of T10 vertebra, slightly left of the midline. (Option C) Abdominal Course Becomes the abdominal esophagus, approximately 1.25 cm long, moves downward and left. Lies in front of the left crus of the diaphragm and inferior surface of the left lobe of the liver. Termination Ends at the cardiac end of the stomach at T11 vertebra, about 2.5 cm to the left of the midline. (Option C) Clinical Significance Oesophageal hiatus transmits the gastric nerves, esophageal branches of the left gastric artery, and veins. Veins draining the esophagus form portosystemic anastomoses, creating connections between portal and systemic venous systems. Portal hypertension can cause esophageal varices, which may rupture and lead to haematemesis (severe bleeding).","sourceRef":"Question Page 494; Solution Pages 519-520","sourcePage":494,"sourcePageEnd":494},{"id":"anatomy-13-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 8 year old child presents to the pediatric clinic with recurrent ear infections and complaints of ear pain, especially after upper respiratory infections. What is the most likely reason for this child's increased susceptibility to ear infections?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The Eustachian tube's shorter length allows for quicker drainage of secretions from the middle ear."},{"letter":"B","text":"The more horizontal orientation of the Eustachian tube in children impairs proper ventilation and drainage of the middle ear."},{"letter":"C","text":"The Eustachian tube opens more frequently during normal activities in children, causing increased exposure to environmental allergens."},{"letter":"D","text":"The Eustachian tube is typically narrow in children, which facilitates the entry of pathogens from the nasopharynx."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The more horizontal orientation of the Eustachian tube in children impairs proper ventilation and drainage of the middle ear.","explanation":"proper ventilation and drainage of the middle ear. Infections can spread from throat to middle ear; more common in children due to shorter, wider, straighter Eustachian tube. Eustachian Tube/ Pharyngotympanic tube/ Auditory tube Provides communication between middle ear and exterior; maintains equal air pressure; opens during swallowing, yawning, sneezing with help from tensor and levator veli palatini muscles Characteristics Shape: Trumpet-shaped Length: Approximately 4 cm Direction: Downward, forward, and medially Angles: 45° with the sagittal plane, 30° with the horizontal plane Parts: Bony part, cartilaginous part Bony Part Length: 12 mm Location: Lies in the petrous temporal bone near the tympanic plate. Cartilaginous Part Length: 25 mm Location: Lies in the sulcus tubae between greater wing of the sphenoid and apex of petrous temporal Vascular Supply Arterial Supply: Ascending pharyngeal artery, middle meningeal arteries, artery of the pterygoid canal Venous Drainage: Pharyngeal and pterygoid plexuses Lymphatic Drainage: Retropharyngeal nodes Nerve Supply Pharyngeal branch of the pterygopalatine ganglion at the ostium; Nervus spinosus branch of the mandibular nerve (cartilaginous part); Tympanic plexus from the glossopharyngeal nerve (bony part) Clinical Significance Infections: Can spread from throat to middle ear; more common in children due to shorter, wider, straighter Eustachian tube. (Option B) Eustachian Catarrh: Inflammation from colds or sore throats can block the tube, causing ear pain, especially during swallowing; enlargement of the tubal tonsil may obstruct the ostium in children The Eustachian tube's shorter length allows for quicker drainage of secretions from the middle ear (Option A): A shorter Eustachian tube does not lead to quicker drainage; it actually increases the risk of fluid accumulation. The Eustachian tube opens more frequently during normal activities in children, causing increased exposure to environmental allergens (Option C): The frequency of Eustachian tube opening does not correlate directly with increased exposure to allergens; the anatomical factors are more significant in this context. The Eustachian tube is typically narrow in children, which facilitates the entry of pathogens from the nasopharynx (Option D): The Eustachian tube is wider, shorter and more horizontal in children than adults, which contributes to infection risk.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 494-495; Solution Pages 520-522","sourcePage":494,"sourcePageEnd":495},{"id":"anatomy-13-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 32 year old male involved in smuggling accidentally swallowed a foreign object, which became lodged in his piriform fossa. After its removal, he reports numbness and loss of sensation in his throat. Which nerve is most likely damaged during the procedure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"External laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Internal laryngeal nerve","explanation":"The internal laryngeal nerve is responsible for sensory innervation to the supraglottic region of the larynx, and damage during foreign body removal from the piriform fossa can cause anesthesia in this area. - The piriform fossa is a small depression found on each side of the laryngopharynx. - It is positioned on both sides of the laryngeal inlet. - The boundaries of the piriform fossa include: Medial boundary: Aryepiglottic fold Lateral boundary: Thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane - Medial boundary: Aryepiglottic fold - Lateral boundary: Thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane - The internal laryngeal nerve runs just beneath the mucosa of the piriform fossa. Damage to the internal laryngeal nerve, such as during the removal of foreign bodies from the fossa, can result in loss of sensation (anesthesia) in the supraglottic region of the larynx. - Damage to the internal laryngeal nerve, such as during the removal of foreign bodies from the fossa, can result in loss of sensation (anesthesia) in the supraglottic region of the larynx. - Medial boundary: Aryepiglottic fold - Lateral boundary: Thyroid cartilage and thyrohyoid membrane - Damage to the internal laryngeal nerve, such as during the removal of foreign bodies from the fossa, can result in loss of sensation (anesthesia) in the supraglottic region of the larynx. Recurrent laryngeal nerve (Option A): This nerve innervates the muscles of the larynx and is not the specific nerve affected during this procedure. External laryngeal nerve (Option B): This nerve innervates the cricothyroid muscle but does not affect sensory innervation to the supraglottic part of the larynx. Glossopharyngeal nerve (Option D): While it provides sensory innervation to the pharynx, it does not innervate the supraglottic part of the larynx.","sourceRef":"Question Page 495; Solution Pages 522-523","sourcePage":495,"sourcePageEnd":495},{"id":"anatomy-13-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Match the spaces between the constrictor muscles with the structures that pass through them: Spaces Structures 1) Sinus of Morgagni A) Internal laryngeal nerve, superior laryngeal vessels 2) Space between superior and middle constrictors B) Recurrent laryngeal nerve, inferior laryngeal vessels 3) Space between middle and inferior constrictors C) Stylopharyngeus muscle, glossopharyngeal nerve 4) Space below the inferior constrictor D) Auditory tube, levator veli palatini muscle, ascending palatine artery, palatine branch of ascending pharyngeal artery Spaces Structures 1) Sinus of Morgagni A) Internal laryngeal nerve, superior laryngeal vessels 2) Space between superior and middle constrictors B) Recurrent laryngeal nerve, inferior laryngeal vessels 3) Space between middle and inferior constrictors C) Stylopharyngeus muscle, glossopharyngeal nerve 4) Space below the inferior constrictor D) Auditory tube, levator veli palatini muscle, ascending palatine arte **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-D; 2-C; 3-A; 4-B"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-B; 2-C; 3-A; 4-D"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-C; 2-D; 3-A; 4-B"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-D; 2-A; 3-C; 4-B"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1-D; 2-C; 3-A; 4-B","explanation":"Constrictors muscles and the spaces between them Constrictor Muscles of the Pharynx Form the outer circular layer and overlap each other. Superior Constrictor: Originates from the pterygoid hamulus, pterygomandibular raphe, mandible, and the side of the posterior tongue. Its upper border is attached to the base of the skull, leaving a space called the sinus of Morgagni, filled by the pharyngobasilar fascia. Middle Constrictor: Overlaps the lower part of the superior constrictor. Originates from the stylohyoid ligament, lesser cornua, and the upper border of the greater cornua of the hyoid bone. The stylopharyngeus muscle and glossopharyngeal nerve pass through the gap between the superior and middle constrictors. Inferior Constrictor: Overlapping the middle constrictor. Composed of two parts: Thyropharyngeus: Originates from the oblique line of the thyroid cartilage. Cricopharyngeus: Originates from the cricoid cartilage. The internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal vessels pass through the gap between the middle and inferior constrictors. All three constrictor muscles attach to the median raphe at the posterior pharyngeal wall. The top of this raphe connects to the pharyngeal tubercle at the skull base, while the lower edge of the inferior constrictor blends with the esophagus' circular muscle. Spaces Between the Constrictor Muscles (Option A) The overlapping nature of these muscles forms spaces through which nerves and blood vessels pass: Sinus of Morgagni (between the superior constrictor's upper border and the skull base): Allows the passage of the auditory tube, levator veli palatini muscle, ascending palatine artery, and the palatine branch of the ascending pharyngeal artery. Space between the superior and middle constrictors: Contains the stylopharyngeus muscle and glossopharyngeal nerve. Space between the middle and inferior constrictors: Contains the internal laryngeal nerve and superior laryngeal vessels. Space below the inferior constrictor: Contains the recurrent laryngeal nerve and inferior laryngeal vessels. Killian's Dehiscence Location: At the lower part of the posterior pharyngeal wall, below the vocal folds and above the cricopharyngeal sphincter. Clinically significant area as it is a weak point. (Thyropharyngeus muscle is not reinforced internally by other constrictors) The area is often associated with the formation of pharyngeal diverticula.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 495-496; Solution Pages 523-524","sourcePage":495,"sourcePageEnd":496},{"id":"anatomy-13-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-13","chapter":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topic":"Lesson 13 - Pharynx, Esophagus, and Larynx","topicIndex":13,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following nerves supplies motor fibres to all pharyngeal muscles except the stylopharyngeus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vagus nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior cervical sympathetic ganglion"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Vagus nerve","explanation":"The vagus nerve supplies motor fibres to all pharyngeal muscles except the stylopharyngeus, which is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve. Muscles of pharynx and nerve supply Muscles of the Pharynx Divided into two layers: Outer circular layer Inner longitudinal layer Constrictor muscles: Form the outer circular layer and overlap each other: Superior constrictor Middle constrictor Inferior constrictor: Has two parts: Thyropharyngeus Cricopharyngeus Longitudinal muscles: Form the inner longitudinal layer: Stylopharyngeus: From the styloid process to the inner surface of the middle and inferior constrictors. Palatopharyngeus: From the palate, running down the inner side of the constrictors. Salpingopharyngeus: From the auditory tube, merging with the palatopharyngeus. Nerve Supply The pharynx is innervated by the pharyngeal plexus (located on the middle constrictor), which includes: Vagus nerve: Carries fibers from the cranial accessory nerve. Glossopharyngeal nerve Superior cervical sympathetic ganglion Motor fibers: Supplied by the cranial accessory nerve via vagus nerve branches (except the stylopharyngeus, supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve). (Option C) Sensory fibers: Mostly from the glossopharyngeal nerve, partly from the vagus nerve. Parasympathetic secretomotor fibers: From the lesser palatine branches of the pterygopalatine ganglion.","sourceRef":"Question Page 496; Solution Pages 524-525","sourcePage":496,"sourcePageEnd":496},{"id":"anatomy-14-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 5-year-old child presents with conductive hearing loss. Imaging reveals a congenital anomaly involving the stapes footplate. Which embryological structure is primarily responsible for the development of the stapes footplate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Second pharyngeal arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Otic placode"},{"letter":"D","text":"Otic capsule"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Otic capsule","explanation":"- The stapes footplate develops from the otic capsule, a cartilaginous structure that surrounds the inner ear during development and later ossifies to form the bony labyrinth. - This capsule provides structural support and protection to the inner ear, which originates from the otic placode. (Option C) Embryology of Ear - Development of Auricle: Begins with proliferations from the first and second pharyngeal arches. By the 6th week, six tubercles form around the first branchial cleft, which fuse to create the auricle. Most of the auricle arises from the second pharyngeal arch, while the tragus and anterior meatus are derived from the first. The lobule is the last part to develop. By the 20th week of gestation, the pinna reaches its final adult shape. - Begins with proliferations from the first and second pharyngeal arches. - By the 6th week, six tubercles form around the first branchial cleft, which fuse to create the auricle. - Most of the auricle arises from the second pharyngeal arch, while the tragus and anterior meatus are derived from the first. - The lobule is the last part to develop. - By the 20th week of gestation, the pinna reaches its final adult shape. - Begins with proliferations from the first and second pharyngeal arches. - By the 6th week, six tubercles form around the first branchial cleft, which fuse to create the auricle. - Most of the auricle arises from the second pharyngeal arch, while the tragus and anterior meatus are derived from the first. - The lobule is the last part to develop. - By the 20th week of gestation, the pinna reaches its final adult shape. - External Auditory Meatus is developed by invagination of the first branchial cleft . - Middle Ear: Includes the Eustachian tube, Tympanic cavity, Attic, Antrum, and Mastoid air cells, which develop from the endoderm of the tubotympanic recess Ossicles: Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) Stapes from the second arch (Option B) The Footplate ofStapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Includes the Eustachian tube, Tympanic cavity, Attic, Antrum, and Mastoid air cells, which develop from the endoderm of the tubotympanic recess - Ossicles: Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) Stapes from the second arch (Option B) The Footplate of Stapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) - Stapes from the second arch (Option B) - The Footplate of Stapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Includes the Eustachian tube, Tympanic cavity, Attic, Antrum, and Mastoid air cells, which develop from the endoderm of the tubotympanic recess - Ossicles: Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) Stapes from the second arch (Option B) The Footplate of Stapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) - Stapes from the second arch (Option B) - The Footplate of Stapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Malleus and incus from the first arch (Option A) - Stapes from the second arch (Option B) - The Footplate of Stapes and annular ligament originates from the Otic capsule . - Membranous Inner Ear: The ectoderm ( neuroectoderm ) in the hindbrain region thickens to form an Auditory placode, which invaginates into the auditory vesicle (otocyst) that develops into: Endolymphatic duct and sac Utricle and semicircular ducts Saccule and cochlea - The ectoderm ( neuroectoderm ) in the hindbrain region thickens to form an Auditory placode, which invaginates into the auditory vesicle (otocyst) that develops into: Endolymphatic duct and sac Utricle and semicircular ducts Saccule and cochlea - Endolymphatic duct and sac - Utricle and semicircular ducts - Saccule and cochlea - The ectoderm ( neuroectoderm ) in the hindbrain region thickens to form an Auditory placode, which invaginates into the auditory vesicle (otocyst) that develops into: Endolymphatic duct and sac Utricle and semicircular ducts Saccule and cochlea - Endolymphatic duct and sac - Utricle and semicircular ducts - Saccule and cochlea - Endolymphatic duct and sac - Utricle and semicircular ducts - Saccule and cochlea - Tympanic Membrane: Outer Layer : Ectoderm Middle Layer : Mesoderm (fibrous) Inner Layer : Endoderm (mucosal) After a perforation , a healed tympanic membrane usually has only the ectoderm and endoderm , since the fibrous layer may not fully regenerate depending on the extent of damage. - Outer Layer : Ectoderm - Middle Layer : Mesoderm (fibrous) - Inner Layer : Endoderm (mucosal) - After a perforation , a healed tympanic membrane usually has only the ectoderm and endoderm , since the fibrous layer may not fully regenerate depending on the extent of damage. - Outer Layer : Ectoderm - Middle Layer : Mesoderm (fibrous) - Inner Layer : Endoderm (mucosal) - After a perforation , a healed tympanic membrane usually has only the ectoderm and endoderm , since the fibrous layer may not fully regenerate depending on the extent of damage.","sourceRef":"Question Page 526; Solution Pages 533-536","sourcePage":526,"sourcePageEnd":526},{"id":"anatomy-14-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following nerves is primarily responsible for the sensory innervation of the structure marked? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Great auricular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Auriculotemporal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lesser occipital nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Auricular branch of the vagus nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Auriculotemporal nerve","explanation":"The marked structure in the image is Tragus , which is supplied by the Auriculotemporal Nerve , a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (CN V). External Ear consists of two main parts: - Pinna (Auricle) - External Auditory Meatus Aspects Auricle (Pinna) External Auditory Meatus Composition Elastic cartilage, except lobule Cartilaginous part (Outer 1/3) with (fibrofatty tissue) hair and ceruminous glands Bony part (Inner 2/3), with thin skin continuous with the tympanic membrane, lacks hair and glands. Parts Concha (Depression leading to the external meatus) Helix (Prominent rim with anterior and posterior limbs) Antihelix ( Parallel ridge to the helix) Tragus & Antitragus (Separated by inter-tragic notch) Cymba Conchae (Above crus of helix) Lobule (Soft, fleshy part below antitragus) Nerve Supply Lateral Surface Lower third: Great Roof & Anterior Wall: auricular nerve (C2, C3) Upper Auriculotemporal nerve (CN V3) two-thirds: Auriculotemporal nerve Floor & Posterior Wall: Auricular (CN V3) Concha: Auricular branch of branch of vagus (CN X) vagus (CN X) (Alderman’s Nerve) Medial Surface Lower third: Great auricular nerve Upper two-thirds: Lesser occipital nerve (C2) Clinical Relevance McEwen’s triangle (Cymba Conchae) Furuncles (cartilaginous part) It is a surgical landmark on the susceptible to infections temporal bone to locate the mastoid antrum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 526-527; Solution Pages 536-538","sourcePage":526,"sourcePageEnd":527},{"id":"anatomy-14-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Match the following structures with their respective locations in the middle ear **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2","explanation":"Middle Ear (Tympanic cavity or Tympanum) - A narrow air-filled space located in the petrous part of the temporal bone, situated between the external ear and the internal ear. - Subdivisions: Epitympanum (Attic): Located above the tympanic membrane, it contains the head of the malleus and the body and short process of the incus. Mesotympanum: Opposite the tympanic membrane; contains the handle of the malleus, long process of the incus, and stapes. It is the narrowest part. Hypotympanum: Located below the tympanic membrane. - Epitympanum (Attic): Located above the tympanic membrane, it contains the head of the malleus and the body and short process of the incus. - Mesotympanum: Opposite the tympanic membrane; contains the handle of the malleus, long process of the incus, and stapes. It is the narrowest part. - Hypotympanum: Located below the tympanic membrane. - Epitympanum (Attic): Located above the tympanic membrane, it contains the head of the malleus and the body and short process of the incus. - Mesotympanum: Opposite the tympanic membrane; contains the handle of the malleus, long process of the incus, and stapes. It is the narrowest part. - Hypotympanum: Located below the tympanic membrane. - Boundaries: Boundaries of Middle ear cavity Roof (Tegmental Wall) Formed by the Tegmen tympani , separating the middle ear from the middle cranial fossa. Floor (Jugular Wall) A thin bony plate that separates the middle ear from the Internal Jugular Vein. Tympanic canaliculus for the tympanic branch of the glossopharyngeal nerve. Anterior Wall (Carotid Wall) Separates the Middle ear from the Internal Carotid Artery Upper part - Tensor Tympani canal . Middle part - Auditory tube opening. Inferior part - Perforated by Sympathetic nerves . Posterior Wall (Mastoid Wall) Aditus ad antrum : Opening to the mastoid antrum. Fossa incudis : Lodges the short process of the incus. Pyramid: Contains the stapedius muscle. Posterior canaliculus for the chorda tympani nerve. Lateral Wall (Membranous Wall) The tympanic membrane , with contributions from the squamous temporal bone. Medial Wall (Labyrinthine Wall) Separates the Tympanic cavity from the internal ear Promontory: Projection from the cochlea. Oval Window: Opening for the stapes. Round Window: Covered by the secondary tympanic membrane. Prominence of Lateral Semicircular Canal Tympanic Plexus formed by Glossopharyngeal nerve, is present on the promontory.","sourceRef":"Question Page 527; Solution Pages 538-540","sourcePage":527,"sourcePageEnd":527},{"id":"anatomy-14-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 60-year-old patient presents with dizziness and balance issues. The physician suspects a problem in the vestibular system. Which of the following structures is primarily responsible for detecting horizontal linear acceleration and head position relative to gravity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Saccule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Utricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Semicircular canals"},{"letter":"D","text":"Organ of Corti"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Utricle","explanation":"- The Utricle is a part of the vestibular system that detects horizontal linear acceleration and head position in relation to gravity, whereas the Saccule is responsible for vertical linear acceleration. - The semicircular canals detect rotational movements. - The Organ of Corti contains hair cells that convert sound vibrations into neural signals and are responsible for hearing. Anatomy of the Inner Ear The inner ear comprises two main components: - Membranous Labyrinth : A closed system of sacs and ducts filled with endolymph. - Bony Labyrinth : A rigid structure that encases the membranous labyrinth, with the space between them filled with perilymph. Membranous Labyrinth Components: - Cochlear Duct (Scala Media): It is located within the bony cochlea and contains the spiral organ of Corti, which is essential for hearing. Has a spiral structure with 2¾ turns , positioned between the scala vestibuli and scala tympani. Base: Formed by the osseous spiral lamina and the basilar membrane. Roof: Formed by the vestibular membrane (Reissner’s membrane). - It is located within the bony cochlea and contains the spiral organ of Corti, which is essential for hearing. - Has a spiral structure with 2¾ turns , positioned between the scala vestibuli and scala tympani. - Base: Formed by the osseous spiral lamina and the basilar membrane. - Roof: Formed by the vestibular membrane (Reissner’s membrane). - It is located within the bony cochlea and contains the spiral organ of Corti, which is essential for hearing. - Has a spiral structure with 2¾ turns , positioned between the scala vestibuli and scala tympani. - Base: Formed by the osseous spiral lamina and the basilar membrane. - Roof: Formed by the vestibular membrane (Reissner’s membrane). Sound Transmission: Sound Waves → Travel through Perilymph (Scala vestibuli → Helicotrema → Scala tympani) → Generate fluid waves → Cause Basilar membrane to vibrate → Stimulate hair cells(Organ of Corti) → Convert sound vibrations into electrical signals → Sent to brain via Cochlear nerve. - Saccule: A small sac within the vestibule, connected to both the cochlear duct and utricle. Detects vertical movements via sensory receptors known as maculae. - A small sac within the vestibule, connected to both the cochlear duct and utricle. - Detects vertical movements via sensory receptors known as maculae. - Utricle: A larger, oblong sac that connects to the semicircular ducts. Primarily detects horizontal movements and head tilting through maculae. - A larger, oblong sac that connects to the semicircular ducts. - Primarily detects horizontal movements and head tilting through maculae. - Semicircular Ducts: Located within the bony semicircular canals. Contain Cristae ampullaris for detecting angular acceleration and maintaining kinetic balance. - Located within the bony semicircular canals. - Contain Cristae ampullaris for detecting angular acceleration and maintaining kinetic balance. - A small sac within the vestibule, connected to both the cochlear duct and utricle. - Detects vertical movements via sensory receptors known as maculae. - A larger, oblong sac that connects to the semicircular ducts. - Primarily detects horizontal movements and head tilting through maculae. - Located within the bony semicircular canals. - Contain Cristae ampullaris for detecting angular acceleration and maintaining kinetic balance. Bony Labyrinth Components - Cochlea: Converts sound waves into mechanical vibrations. Structurally allows separation of sound frequencies, with higher frequencies detected at the basal coil and lower frequencies heard at the apical coil. - Converts sound waves into mechanical vibrations. - Structurally allows separation of sound frequencies, with higher frequencies detected at the basal coil and lower frequencies heard at the apical coil. - Vestibule: Central cavity situated between the cochlea and semicircular canals. - Semicircular Canals: Three canals are oriented at right angles to each other, opening into the vestibule. Vital for balance and spatial orientation, containing sensory structures (maculae in the vestibule and cristae in the semicircular canals) that detect head movements and positional changes. - Three canals are oriented at right angles to each other, opening into the vestibule. - Vital for balance and spatial orientation, containing sensory structures (maculae in the vestibule and cristae in the semicircular canals) that detect head movements and positional changes. - Converts sound waves into mechanical vibrations. - Structurally allows separation of sound frequencies, with higher frequencies detected at the basal coil and lower frequencies heard at the apical coil. - Three canals are oriented at right angles to each other, opening into the vestibule. - Vital for balance and spatial orientation, containing sensory structures (maculae in the vestibule and cristae in the semicircular canals) that detect head movements and positional changes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 527-528; Solution Pages 540-543","sourcePage":527,"sourcePageEnd":528},{"id":"anatomy-14-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Match the following labelled parts of the nasal septum: 1 a. Septal Cartilage 2 b. Vomer 3 c. Palatine crest 4 d. Perpendicular Plate of Ethmoid 1 a. Septal Cartilage 2 b. Vomer 3 c. Palatine crest 4 d. Perpendicular Plate of Ethmoid **Type:** Match the Following **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-c, 3-d, 4-b"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-d, 4-a"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c","explanation":"- The nasal septum is a median osseocartilaginous partition that divides the two nasal cavities. - It typically deviates slightly to one side, most often to the right. Structure of the Nasal Septum: Bony Components Cartilaginous Components Perpendicular Plate of Ethmoid : Forms the Septal Cartilage : Constitutes the major anterior part of posterosuperior part of the septum. Vomer : Makes up the the septum , fitting into the angle between the vomer and posteroinferior part. Nasal spine of the frontal bone. Crest the perpendicular plate of the ethmoid. Septal Processes formed by nasal bones. Sphenoidal crest. Nasal crest of Alar Cartilages : The medial crura of the two major alar from the fusion of the palatine processes of the maxillae cartilages unite at the midline, forming the columella (or and horizontal plates of the palatine bones. columellar septum). Below the columella, a small portion of the septum comprises a double layer of skin without bony or cartilaginous support, known as the membranous septum .","sourceRef":"Question Page 528; Solution Pages 543-545","sourcePage":528,"sourcePageEnd":528},{"id":"anatomy-14-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 32-year-old female presents to the ENT clinic with persistent nasal congestion, facial pain, and reduced sense of smell. After minimal relief from medications, a nasal endoscopy is planned. Which of the following openings is not present in the structure labelled “X” in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Frontal air sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Maxillary air sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior ethmoidal air sinuses"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior ethmoidal air sinuses"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Posterior ethmoidal air sinuses","explanation":"The provided image depicts the lateral wall of the nose , with the structure labelled as the hiatus semilunaris, which contains three openings: The maxillary sinus, the frontal sinus, and the anterior ethmoidal air cells. Lateral wall of the nose: - The conchae, or turbinates, are curved bony structures extending downward and medially. - They are classified into superior, middle, and inferior nasal conchae. Below these conchae lies over the recesses referred to as meatuses. - The atrium is a shallow depression located just in front of the middle meatus and above the nasal vestibule. - It is bordered superiorly by a slight mucous membrane ridge known as the agger nasi , which extends from the upper end of the middle concha. Site Location Openings Sphenoethmoidal recess Triangular depression located above Sphenoidal air sinus and behind the superior concha Superior meatus Lies below the superior concha Posterior ethmoidal air sinuses (Smallest) . (Option D) Middle meatus Lies underneath the middle concha. Bulla ethmoidalis: Middle ethmoidal Has the following parts: Ethmoidal air sinuses Hiatus semilunaris: bulla /bulla ethmoidalis (round Frontal air sinus (Option A) Anterior elevation produced by the underlying ethmoidal air sinuses (Option C) middle ethmoidal sinuses). Hiatus Maxillary air sinus (Option B) semilunaris (semicircular sulcus below the bulla ethmoidalis). Infundibulum (passage at the anterior end of middle meatus). Inferior meatus Lies underneath the inferior nasal Nasolacrimal duct (in the anterior concha (Largest ). part of inferior meatus) Hasner’s valve guards the opening of the nasolacrimal duct.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 528-529; Solution Pages 545-547","sourcePage":528,"sourcePageEnd":529},{"id":"anatomy-14-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 5-month-old child is experiencing excessive tearing in both eyes. The doctor recommends that the parents perform a massage along the nasolacrimal duct (NLD). How should the parents be instructed to carry out this procedure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Downward, backward and lateral"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upward, backward and medial"},{"letter":"C","text":"Downward, backward and medial"},{"letter":"D","text":"Upward, backward and lateral"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Downward, backward and lateral","explanation":"- The scenario describes a condition known as Epiphora in the bilateral eye, characterized by excessive tearing. - The canal involved in this condition is the Nasolacrimal Duct , which runs downward, backwards, and laterally. - Lacrimal glands produce tears and are located in the eye, draining into the nose through a membranous passage known as the nasolacrimal duct. - Massage in the direction of NLD encourages drainage and can assist in relieving blockages. Nasolacrimal duct Length 18 mm Extent It extends from the lacrimal sac and opens anteriorly into the inferior meatus of the nose. Orientation Runs downwards, backwards and laterally (Option A) Valve Hasner’s valve: An incomplete mucous fold at the lower end of the duct, preventing air from entering the duct and blowing into the eye when one blows their nose to clear nasal secretions. Clinical aspect Epiphora: Excessive secretion of the lacrimal fluid overflowing on the cheeks. It may occur due to: Hyperlacrimation: Excessive secretion of tears (intake of spicy food or emotional outbreak) Obstruction in lacrimal passages (lacrimal punctum, lacrimal canaliculi, lacrimal sac and nasolacrimal duct) Ectropion (eversion of lower eyelid). Patency evaluation Jones dye test 1 & 2 are procedures to assess the patency of the nasolacrimal duct and identify problems related to tear drainage.","sourceRef":"Question Page 529; Solution Pages 547-548","sourcePage":529,"sourcePageEnd":529},{"id":"anatomy-14-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":8,"question":"During retinoscopy, the ophthalmologist inquired of the intern about the development of the eyeball. Could you identify what will develop from the structure labeled \"X\" in the diagram below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Optic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lens"},{"letter":"C","text":"Light-sensitive neural layer"},{"letter":"D","text":"Retinal pigment epithelium"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Light-sensitive neural layer","explanation":"The marked structure in the above image represents the inner layer of the optic cup , which develops into the light-sensitive neural layer of the retina. Development of Retina - The retina develops from the optic vesicle. - Retinal detachment: In retinal detachment there is separation of two layers of the retina (i.e., pigment and neural layers). Optic nerve (Option A) is derived from optic stalk. - The center of the optic cup form the optic disc where the neural retina connects with the optic stalk, transforming it into the optic nerve. Lens (Option B) develops from the lens vesicle. - The posterior cells of the vesicle form transparent primary lens fibers whereas the cells in the equatorial region produce secondary lens fibers. Retinal pigment epithelium (Option D) is developed from the outer layer of the optic cup.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 529-530; Solution Pages 548-550","sourcePage":529,"sourcePageEnd":530},{"id":"anatomy-14-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":9,"question":"All of the following structures pass through the area labeled \"A\" in the image except? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Zygomatic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Infraorbital nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pterygopalatine ganglion branches"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lacrimal nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Lacrimal nerve","explanation":"The image provided depicts the orbit. The ‘A’ marked structure is the inferior orbital fissure , which transmits the zygomatic nerve, infraorbital nerve, and pterygopalatine ganglion branches . - However, the lacrimal nerve is transmitted from the superior oblique fissure. Anatomy of the Orbit: Structures passing through superior and inferior orbital fissures Superior orbital fissure Inferior orbital fissure Divided into three parts by the common tendinous ring of Zygomatic nerve (Option A) Maxillary nerve Branches of Zinn. Within the common tendinous ring: Superior and the pterygopalatine ganglion (Option C) , Infraorbital inferior divisions of the oculomotor (3rd cranial) nerve. nerve and vessels (Option B) Vein connecting inferior Nasociliary nerve Abducent (6th cranial) nerve ophthalmic vein with pterygoid venous plexus. Sympathetic root of the ciliary ganglion Above and lateral to the common tendinous ring: Trochlear (4th cranial) nerve Frontal nerve Lacrimal nerve Lacrimal artery Superior ophthalmic vein Recurrent meningeal branch of the lacrimal artery. Below and medial to the common tendinous ring: Inferior ophthalmic vein","sourceRef":"Question Pages 530-531; Solution Pages 550-552","sourcePage":530,"sourcePageEnd":531},{"id":"anatomy-14-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 50-year-old male presents with difficulty lifting his right eye, describing it as \" stuck\" when looking upward, along with double vision. Examination reveals a significant limitation in the upward gaze of the right eye. What is the action of the structure labelled \"A\" in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Elevation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adduction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intorsion"},{"letter":"D","text":"All of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. All of the above","explanation":"The structure marked in the given image is the Superior rectus muscle , which, in the primary position, performs elevation, adduction, and intorsion. Extraocular muscles Voluntary muscles Involuntary muscles Four recti muscles Superior rectus Inferior rectus Medial Superior tarsal or Muller's muscle Inferior tarsal Orbitalis. rectus Lateral rectus Two oblique muscles Superior oblique Inferior oblique Levator palpebrae superioris (1) Origin, insertion, and nerve supply of extraocular muscles: Origin Insertion Nerve supply Recti Common annular tendon Sclera little posterior to the Lateral rectus: Abducens or tendinous ring of Zinn. limbus (corneoscleral nerve Rest all other recti: (all recti have the same junction) The average Oculomotor nerve origin) distance from Limbus is: Superior: 7.7 mm Inferior: 6.5 mm Medial: 5.5 mm Lateral: 6.9 mm Superior oblique The undersurface of the Into the sclera behind the Trochlear nerve lesser wing of the sphenoid equator in the posterior superior quadrant of the eyeball. Inferior oblique The orbital surface of the Into the sclera behind the maxilla equator in the posterior superior quadrant of the eyeball, a little below the superior oblique. Oculomotor nerve Levator palpebrae Orbital surface of the Superior lamella: Anterior superioris lesser wing of the surface of the superior sphenoid bone. tarsus and skin of the upper eyelid. Inferior lamella: Upper margin of the superior tarsus and the superior conjunctival fornix. Actions of muscles on the eyeball (Mnemonic): SIN- S uperior rectus/ S uperior oblique assist i IN torsion RAD- R ectus muscles help in AD duction Superior rectus Medial rectus Inferior rectus Lateral rectus Superior oblique Inferior oblique (SR) (Option D) (MR) (IR) (LR) (SO) (IO) Elevation Adducti Adduction Depression Abduction Depression Elevation Abducti on Intorsion Adduction Abduction Intorsi on Extorsion Extorsion on The 3-step (Parks-Bielschowsky) test is utilized to diagnose hypertropia resulting from superior oblique palsy. - The first step involves conducting the cover-uncover and alternate-cover tests to identify which eye is hypertrophic. - Next, the alternative-cover test is performed while the patient looks to the left and right, followed by tests during left and right head tilts.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 531-532; Solution Pages 552-554","sourcePage":531,"sourcePageEnd":532},{"id":"anatomy-14-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-14","chapter":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topic":"Lesson 14 - Anatomy of Ear, Nose, and Eyeball","topicIndex":14,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following arteries is not typically injured during mandibular fracture repair?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior alveolar artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lingual artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Maxillary artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Facial artery","explanation":"Facial artery: - The facial artery supplies blood to the face, but it is typically not directly involved in mandibular fractures. - It is less likely to be injured in mandibular fracture repair, as it is located more superficially and does not directly supply the mandible. Mandibular fractures often involve injury to the arteries that supply the mandible and its surrounding structures. Inferior alveolar artery (Option A): This artery, which is a branch of the maxillary artery, is commonly injured during mandibular fractures, especially in fractures of the body or angle of the mandible. Lingual artery (Option B): The lingual artery, which supplies the tongue and floor of the mouth, can also be injured in fractures of the mandibular body or ramus. It is often at risk during fractures near the midline or in the region of the tongue. Maxillary artery (Option D): This major artery supplies blood to the mandible through its branches, such as the inferior alveolar artery. It is closely related to the mandible, as it originates behind the neck and passes between the ramus and the sphenomandibular ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Page 532; Solution Pages 554-555","sourcePage":532,"sourcePageEnd":532},{"id":"anatomy-15-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient presents with the palsy of the face as shown. Which of the following is most likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"7th nerve palsy"},{"letter":"B","text":"3rd nerve palsy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Erb’s palsy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Orbicularis oculi palsy"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 7th nerve palsy","explanation":"The image presents a clinical case of facial palsy. The child's facial asymmetry, particularly the drooping of one side of the face, suggests a lower motor neuron (LMN) facial nerve (CN VII) palsy, commonly known as Bell’s palsy. Bell’s palsy: Description Most common cause of facial palsy Characterized by idiopathic, LMN facial paralysis of acute onset Most commonly the Labyrinthine segment of facial nerve is involved. Males and females are equally affected. Aetiology Idiopathic: Most common cause Viral Infection: HSV >> Herpes zoster Vascular Ischemia Hereditary: Positive family history in 6-8% Autoimmune Risk Factors More common in diabetics (due to angiopathy) and pregnant women (due to fluid retention). Clinical Features Sudden onset of facial paralysis with inability to close the eye (Bell's phenomenon), asymmetry of the face, epiphora, drooping mouth, and dribbling of saliva. Associated symptoms may include ear pain, noise intolerance (stapedial paralysis), or loss of taste (chorda tympani involvement). Diagnosis Diagnosis is made by excluding other causes of peripheral facial paralysis. Treatment General: Reassurance, pain relief, eye protection and physiotherapy Medical: Prednisolone 60 mg per day for 5 days then reduced by 10 mg per day (for a total treatment time of 10 days) and 50 mg per day (in two divided doses) for 10 days, Acyclovir (Adult doses) Surgical: Nerve decompression Prognosis 85%-90% of patients fully recover Incomplete resolution are mainly seen in recurrent/late recovery cases 3rd N. Palsy (Option B): Affects the oculomotor nerve (CN III), causing ptosis, ophthalmoplegia, and pupil involvement, not facial asymmetry. Erb’s Palsy (Option C): A brachial plexus injury affecting arm movement, not facial muscles. Orbicularis Oculi Palsy (Option D): Would only affect eyelid closure, not the entire face.","sourceRef":"Question Page 556; Solution Pages 592-593","sourcePage":556,"sourcePageEnd":556},{"id":"anatomy-15-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A young male presented with weakness in the lower half of the right side of his face, with drooping of the angle of his mouth. He is unable to raise his eyebrows and close his eyes on both sides. Which of the following is the most appropriate diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Infranuclear facial palsy on right side"},{"letter":"B","text":"Supranuclear facial palsy on left side"},{"letter":"C","text":"Infranuclear facial on left side"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supranuclear facial on right side"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Infranuclear facial palsy on right side","explanation":"- Infranuclear facial palsy occurs when there is damage to the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) after it exits the brainstem. - This leads to weakness or paralysis of the muscles of facial expression on the same side (right side in this case) of the face. - The patient will have difficulty raising the eyebrows, closing the eyes, and drooping of the mouth on the affected side. Both the upper and lower facial muscles are affected. Facial nerve palsy: Feature Upper Motor Neuron Lesion Lower Motor Neuron Lesion (Supranuclear) (Infranuclear) Lesion Location Motor cortex, connection from motor Facial nucleus, anywhere along CN cortex to facial nucleus in pons VII Affected Side Contralateral Ipsilateral Muscles Involved Lower muscles of facial expression Upper and lower muscles of facial expression Forehead Involved? Spared, due to bilateral UMN Affected innervation Other Symptoms Variable; depends on size of lesion Incomplete eye closure (dry eyes, corneal ulceration), hyperacusis, loss of taste sensation to anterior tongue","sourceRef":"Question Pages 556-557; Solution Pages 593-594","sourcePage":556,"sourcePageEnd":557},{"id":"anatomy-15-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Identify the structure marked in the image given below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Buccopharyngeal fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Capsule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior constrictor muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pharyngobasilar fascia"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pharyngobasilar fascia","explanation":"The arrow in the image points to the pharyngobasilar fascia. Structures forming the Tonsillar bed Muscles forming the tonsillar bed Superior Constrictor Muscle Styloglossus Muscle Structures close to the tonsillar bed (outside the superior Facial Artery (Tonsillar Branch): Major blood supply to the constrictor muscle) tonsils, at risk for haemorrhage during surgery. Paratonsillar Vein: Venous drainage of the tonsil, risk of bleeding during tonsillectomy. Submandibular Salivary Gland Posterior Belly of Digastric Muscle Medial Pterygoid Muscle Mandible Glossopharyngeal Nerve: Provides sensory innervation to the tonsils.","sourceRef":"Question Page 557; Solution Pages 594-595","sourcePage":557,"sourcePageEnd":557},{"id":"anatomy-15-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following is the afferent limb of the corneal reflex?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Optic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trochlear nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Trigeminal nerve","explanation":"The corneal reflex (blink reflex) involves two components: - The ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve (CN V) detects touch or irritation on the cornea and sends sensory signals to the brainstem. - The facial nerve (CN VII) activates the orbicularis oculi muscle, causing eyelid closure. Reflex Afferent Nerve Efferent Nerve Accommodation II (Optic nerve) III (Oculomotor nerve) Corneal V■ (Ophthalmic, nasociliary branch) Bilateral VII (Facial nerve, temporal branch - orbicularis oculi) Cough X (Vagus nerve) X (Vagus nerve, also phrenic and spinal nerves) Gag IX (Glossopharyngeal nerve) X (Vagus nerve) Jaw Jerk V■ (Mandibular branch, sensory V■ (Mandibular branch, motor to from masseter) masseter) Lacrimation V■ (Ophthalmic branch) (loss of VII (Facial nerve) reflex does not preclude emotional tears) Pupillary II (Optic nerve) III (Oculomotor nerve) Optic nerve (Option A): Involved in the pupillary light reflex, not the corneal reflex. Facial nerve (Option B): Acts as the efferent limb, responsible for blinking, not the afferent limb. Trochlear nerve (Option D): Innervates the superior oblique muscle, which controls eye movement but has no role in the corneal reflex.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 557-558; Solution Pages 595-596","sourcePage":557,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"anatomy-15-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A singer presents with singing at high pitch. On examination, bowing of the vocal cord is seen on the right side. Which of the following muscles has been compromised?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior cricoarytenoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral cricoarytenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cricothyroid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyroarytenoid"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Cricothyroid","explanation":"Correct Option C - Cricothyroid: - The cricothyroid muscle is responsible for tensing and elongating the vocal cords, which is crucial for producing high-pitched sounds. When the cricothyroid muscle is compromised, it can lead to an inability to tense the vocal cords properly, resulting in bowing or a lack of tension, particularly during high-pitched singing. This bowing may be observed on laryngoscopy as a gap between the vocal cords, contributing to a weak or breathy voice. Incorrect Options: Option A - Posterior cricoarytenoid: - The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle is responsible for abducting (opening) the vocal cords. If this muscle were compromised, the vocal cords would remain in a partially closed position, leading to difficulty breathing rather than issues with pitch. Option B - Lateral cricoarytenoid: - The lateral cricoarytenoid muscle adducts (closes) the vocal cords. Its impairment would affect the closure of the vocal cords, leading to issues with sound production, but not specifically related to pitch. Option D - Thyroarytenoid: - The thyroarytenoid muscle helps to shorten and relax the vocal cords, lowering the pitch. While it plays a role in vocal cord tension, it is more involved in lower pitches and overall voice modulation rather than the high-pitch tension that the cricothyroid muscle manages.","sourceRef":"Question Page 558; Solution Pages 596-597","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"anatomy-15-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":6,"question":"During the surgical procedure for Patent Ductus Arteriosus, the structure that is in close relation to it can be damaged. Which of the following can be the structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thoracic duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hemiazygos vein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Left recurrent laryngeal nerve: - The left recurrent laryngeal nerve's pathway and anatomical proximity to the ligamentum arteriosum make it the structure most at risk during surgical procedures for patent ductus arteriosus. Incorrect Options: - Option B, C & D: These structures are not closely related to the patent ductus arteriosus","sourceRef":"Question Page 558; Solution Page 597","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":558},{"id":"anatomy-15-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which muscle movements are related to the Abducens nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral rectus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Medial rectus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior oblique"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior oblique"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Lateral rectus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Lateral rectus muscle: - The lateral rectus muscle is supplied by the Abducens nerve. Incorrect Options: Option B - Medial rectus: The medial rectus is supplied by the third nerve. Option C - Superior oblique: The superior oblique is supplied by the fourth nerve. Option D - Inferior oblique: The inferior oblique is supplied by the third nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 558-559; Solution Pages 597-598","sourcePage":558,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"anatomy-15-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following steps in thyroid surgery is least likely to result in hoarseness of voice?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ligation of superior thyroid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Division of strap muscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Removal of tubercle of Zuckerkandi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dissection of Beahr's triangle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ligation of superior thyroid artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ligation of superior thyroid artery: - Ligation of the superior thyroid artery is a step in thyroid surgery that generally poses a lower risk of causing hoarseness of voice. - This is because the superior laryngeal nerve, which can be affected during this procedure, primarily affects the cricothyroid muscle responsible for pitch modulation rather than causing hoarseness directly. - Therefore, this step is least likely to cause hoarseness compared to other steps that have a direct impact on the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Incorrect Options: Option B - Division of strap muscles: - Dividing the strap muscles (sternothyroid and sternohyoid) is a common procedure, but while it is generally safe, it is not related to the risk of voice changes directly associated with nerve damage. However, compared to ligating the superior thyroid artery, it is more likely to be involved in complex dissection areas where nerves might be at risk. Option C - Removal of tubercle of Zuckerkandl: - This procedure is closely associated with the recurrent laryngeal nerve. The tubercle of Zuckerkandl is an anatomical landmark near the recurrent laryngeal nerve, and its removal carries a significant risk of nerve injury, which can result in hoarseness of voice. Option D - Dissection of Beahrs triangle: - Beahrs triangle is an area near the recurrent laryngeal nerve. Dissecting this region can also pose a risk to the nerve, leading to a higher likelihood of hoarseness of voice if the nerve is damaged.","sourceRef":"Question Page 559; Solution Pages 598-599","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"anatomy-15-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which X-ray view is best for visualizing the frontal sinuses?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral view"},{"letter":"B","text":"Waters view"},{"letter":"C","text":"Caldwell view"},{"letter":"D","text":"Towne view"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Caldwell view","explanation":"Correct Option C - Caldwell view: - The Caldwell view (also known as the occipitofrontal view) is specifically designed to provide a clear image of the frontal sinuses. This view angles the X-ray beam to project the frontal sinuses without superimposition from other facial structures, making it ideal for assessing the frontal sinuses. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lateral view: - The lateral view is not ideal for visualizing the frontal sinuses. It is typically used to view the sphenoid sinus, the nasopharynx, and to assess any anteroposterior displacement of structures in the skull. Option B - Waters view: - The Waters view (also known as the occipitomental view) is best for visualizing the maxillary sinuses. It is used to see the orbits and the maxillary sinuses with minimal superimposition of the petrous ridges. Option D - Towne view: - The Towne view is primarily used for visualizing the occipital bone, the posterior cranial fossa, and for assessing the foramen magnum. It is not optimal for visualizing the frontal sinuses.","sourceRef":"Question Page 559; Solution Page 599","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"anatomy-15-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which muscle is responsible for causing trismus due to spasm? (or) Trismus is due to spasm of which of the following muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Buccinator"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral pterygoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial pterygoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mentalis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Medial pterygoid","explanation":"Correct Option C - Medial pterygoid: - The medial pterygoid muscle aids in jaw closure and raising the mandible during chewing. If it spasms or becomes hypertonic, it can lead to trismus, which is difficulty fully opening the mouth. The buccinator muscle, responsible for cheek movement and actions like blowing and sucking, doesn't directly cause trismus. Incorrect Options: Option A - Buccinator: The buccinator muscle compresses the cheek during chewing and paralysis causes food buildup in the mouth's vestibule and hinders cheek puffing. It doesn't cause trismus. Option B - Lateral pterygoid: The lateral pterygoid muscle helps in opening the mouth and moving the jaw sideways. Dysfunction or spasm of this muscle can contribute to TMJ disorders but isn't the main cause of trismus. Option D - Mentalis: The mentalis muscle, located in the chin, helps with pouting and chin wrinkling but isn't involved in jaw movement. Therefore, issues with this muscle don't cause trismus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 559; Solution Pages 599-600","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":559},{"id":"anatomy-15-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":11,"question":"The correct pair of lymph node level in the neck and anatomical boundaries amongst the following are:-","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Level c – cricoid to clavicle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Level 4 – hyoid to cricoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Level 5 – base of skull to cricoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Level 6 – hyoid to clavicle"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Level 6 – hyoid to clavicle","explanation":"Correct Option D - Level 6 – hyoid to clavicle: - Level 6 lymph nodes, also known as the anterior compartment lymph nodes, are located in the central compartment of the neck. They extend from the hyoid bone (above) to the clavicle (below). Level 6 lymph nodes are commonly associated with the thyroid gland and are important in the staging and management of thyroid cancer. Incorrect Options: Option A - Level c – cricoid to clavicle:. There is no specific lymph node level denoted by \"c\" in neck dissection classifications. Option B - Level 4 – hyoid to cricoid: Level 4 lymph nodes are located along the midline of the neck and extend from the hyoid bone (above) to the clavicle (below).. Option C - Level 5 – base of skull to cricoid: Level 5 lymph nodes are located in the posterior triangle of the neck, extending from the base of the skull (above) to the clavicle (below).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 559-560; Solution Page 600","sourcePage":559,"sourcePageEnd":560},{"id":"anatomy-15-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":12,"question":"At what level does the duct of the parotid gland, a paired salivary gland producing serous secretions, open?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First mandibular molar"},{"letter":"B","text":"Second mandibular molar"},{"letter":"C","text":"First maxillary pre molar"},{"letter":"D","text":"Second maxillary molar"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Second maxillary molar","explanation":"Correct Option D - Second maxillary molar: The duct of the parotid gland, Stensen's duct, opens into the mouth at the level of the second maxillary molar. This is the appropriate location where saliva from the parotid gland is released into the oral cavity. Incorrect Options: Option A - First mandibular molar: Stensen's duct, from the parotid gland, opens opposite the second upper molar, not the first mandibular molar. Option B - Second mandibular molar: Stensen's duct, from the parotid gland, opens opposite the second upper molar, not the second mandibular molar. Option C - First maxillary premolar: The first maxillary premolar is located more towards the front of the mouth, while the duct of the parotid gland opens at a different location.","sourceRef":"Question Page 560; Solution Pages 600-601","sourcePage":560,"sourcePageEnd":560},{"id":"anatomy-15-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":13,"question":"All of the following nerves are involved in the movement of the eyeball except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A","explanation":"Correct Option A - A The optic nerve, also known as cranial nerve II, is primarily responsible for transmitting visual information from the retina to the brain. It is involved in vision and does not directly control the movement of the eyeball. Incorrect Options Option B - Oculomotor nerve: Cranial nerve III, or the oculomotor nerve, innervates key extraocular muscles, dictating eye movements. It governs the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles.. Option C - Abducens nerve: The abducens nerve, or cranial nerve VI, controls the lateral rectus muscle of the eye. It is responsible for the abduction (outward movement) of the eye. Option D - Trochlear nerve: The trochlear nerve, or cranial nerve IV, innervates the superior oblique muscle of the eye. It plays a role in the downward and inward movement of the eye.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 560-561; Solution Page 601","sourcePage":560,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"anatomy-15-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Identify the foramen marked in the image below:- **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Foramen rotundum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Foramen ovale"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vidian canal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Foramen spoinosum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Foramen rotundum","explanation":"Correct Options A - Foramen rotundum Foramen Rotundum. The foramen rotundum is indeed located in the sphenoid bone of the skull. It is a rounded opening that lies in the greater wing of the sphenoid bone, on the medial part of the infratemporal surface. The foramen rotundum serves as a passage for the maxillary nerve (V2), which is a branch of the trigeminal nerve (cranial nerve V). Incorrect Options: Option B - Foramen Ovale: The foramen ovale is a separate opening in the sphenoid bone, located posterior and slightly lateral to the foramen rotundum. It is larger and oval-shaped. Option C - Vidian Canal:The vidian canal, or pterygoid canal, is a narrow bony passage in the sphenoid bone . It runs obliquely between the pterygopalatine fossa and the middle cranial fossa. Option D - Foramen Spinosum: The foramen spinosum, a small opening, is situated in the greater wing of the sphenoid bone, positioned posteriorly and laterally to the foramen ovale.","sourceRef":"Question Page 561; Solution Pages 601-602","sourcePage":561,"sourcePageEnd":561},{"id":"anatomy-15-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following bony structures is present behind the marked area? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Post clivus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser wing of sphenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sella turcica"},{"letter":"D","text":"Carotid canal"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lesser wing of sphenoid","explanation":"Correct Option B - Lesser wing of sphenoid: The lesser wing of the sphenoid bone is one of the two pairs of wings that extend from the body of the sphenoid bone. It is located at the anterior portion of the sphenoid bone and contributes to the formation of the middle cranial fossa. Incorrect Options Option A - Post clivus: The term \"post clivus\" is not a specific anatomical structure. The clivus refers to a sloping surface on the occipital bone that forms a posterior part of the cranial base. Option C - Sella turcica: The sella turcica, a bony depression found on the superior surface of the sphenoid bone, houses the pituitary gland. Option D - Carotid canal: The carotid canal, a bony canal situated in the petrous portion of the temporal bone, permits the passage of the internal carotid artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 561-562; Solution Pages 602-603","sourcePage":561,"sourcePageEnd":562},{"id":"anatomy-15-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following marked structures does not form part of the laryngeal skeleton? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B."},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A","explanation":"Correct Option A - Hyoid Bone: The hyoid bone is not considered part of the laryngeal skeleton. Instead, it is a U-shaped bone located in the neck region above the larynx. The hyoid bone serves as a point of attachment for various muscles involved in swallowing and speech production. It does not directly contribute to the structure or support of the larynx itself. Incorrect Options: Options B - Thyroid Cartilage: The thyroid cartilage, a key component of the larynx, forms the prominent Adam's apple and helps protect the vocal cords. Options C - Epiglottis: The epiglottis, a leaf-shaped cartilage above the larynx, aids in swallowing by covering the glottis and preventing food from entering the airway. Options D - Cricoid Cartilage: The cricoid cartilage, situated beneath the thyroid cartilage, forms the larynx's base, offering support and stability.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 562-563; Solution Page 603","sourcePage":562,"sourcePageEnd":563},{"id":"anatomy-15-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A patient presented with paradoxical respiration. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right Vagus Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left Vagus Nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Phrenic Nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Phrenic Nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D - Phrenic Nerve Phrenic Nerve: The phrenic nerve is indeed the most likely nerve to be affected in a patient with paradoxical respiration. The phrenic nerve plays a critical role in controlling the contraction of the diaphragm, the primary muscle involved in breathing. Damage or dysfunction of the phrenic nerve can result in weakened or paralyzed diaphragm movement, leading to paradoxical respiration where the chest moves inward during inhalation and outward during exhalation. Incorrect Options: Option A- Right Vagus Nerve: While right vagus nerve injury can lead to respiratory issues, it doesn't directly control the diaphragm, reducing its likelihood as a cause of paradoxical respiration. Option B - Left Vagus Nerve: While the left vagus nerve regulates thoracic and abdominal functions, it's not the primary nerve for diaphragmatic control. . Option C - Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve: While damage to the recurrent laryngeal nerve can result in vocal cord paralysis or weakness, it is not directly related to the innervation of the diaphragm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 563; Solution Pages 603-604","sourcePage":563,"sourcePageEnd":563},{"id":"anatomy-15-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following arteries passes in close proximity to the area marked by the arrow in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle meningeal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deep temporal artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Middle meningeal artery","explanation":"Correct Option B - Middle meningeal artery - The area marked by the arrow corresponds to the pterion, where several skull bones meet. The middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery, runs nearby. It supplies blood to the meninges surrounding the brain and spinal cord, passing through the pterion and branching out to supply surrounding structures. Incorrect Options: Option A - Internal carotid artery: The internal carotid artery is crucial for brain blood supply but doesn't closely pass by the pterion. Option C - Anterior cerebral artery: The anterior cerebral artery, a branch of the internal carotid artery, provides blood to the frontal lobes and medial brain regions, but it doesn't closely approach the pterion.. Option D - Deep temporal artery: The deep temporal arteries, stemming from the maxillary artery, nourish the temporal head region. Although they are nearby, they don't directly approach the pterion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 563-564; Solution Page 604","sourcePage":563,"sourcePageEnd":564},{"id":"anatomy-15-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":19,"question":"What is the primary function of the muscle indicated in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Elevation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Depression"},{"letter":"C","text":"Protrusion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Retraction"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Elevation","explanation":"Correct Option A - Elevation - The muscle in the image is the temporalis muscle, situated on the side of the head over the temporal bone. - The main action of the temporalis muscle is the elevation of the mandible (lower jaw). When the temporalis muscle contracts, it pulls the mandible upwards, closing the mouth and bringing the teeth together for biting and chewing. This action is essential for various oral functions, such as eating, speaking, and swallowing. Incorrect Options: Option B - Depression: Depression of the mandible refers to the downward movement of the lower jaw. The temporalis muscle is not involved in this action. Option C - Protrusion:Protrusion of the mandible is the forward movement of the lower jaw. Although the temporalis muscle can contribute, it's not the primary muscle responsible for this action.. Option D - Retraction: Retraction of the mandible refers to the backward movement of the lower jaw. The temporalis muscle does not play a significant role in this action.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 564-565; Solution Pages 604-605","sourcePage":564,"sourcePageEnd":565},{"id":"anatomy-15-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Identify the cranial nerve that passes through the foramen marked in the image below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oculomotor nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Olfactory nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Optic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Facial nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Oculomotor nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Oculomotor nerve - The image shows the superior orbital fissure, which is a bony opening located in the sphenoid bone of the skull. The oculomotor nerve (Cranial Nerve III) passes through this fissure. The oculomotor nerve is responsible for controlling the movement of several eye muscles, including the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles. It also innervates the levator palpebrae superioris muscle, which elevates the upper eyelid. Incorrect Options: Option B - Olfactory nerve (Cranial Nerve I): The olfactory nerve enters the skull through the cribriform plate of the ethmoid bone and is not associated with the superior orbital fissure.. Option C - Optic nerve (Cranial Nerve II): The optic nerve carries visual information from the eyes to the brain and enters the skull through the optic canal, not the superior orbital fissure. Option D - Facial nerve (Cranial Nerve VII):The facial nerve controls facial muscles and exits the skull through the stylomastoid foramen, not the superior orbital fissure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 565-566; Solution Pages 605-606","sourcePage":565,"sourcePageEnd":566},{"id":"anatomy-15-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":21,"question":"From which of the following does the inferior thyroid artery arise?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External carotid artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyrocervical trunk"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lingual artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Thyrocervical trunk","explanation":"Correct Option C - Thyrocervical Trunk - The inferior thyroid artery is a branch of the thyrocervical trunk, which is a branch of the subclavian artery. The thyrocervical trunk arises from the first part of the subclavian artery and gives rise to several branches, including the inferior thyroid artery. Incorrect Options: Option A - External carotid artery: The external carotid artery gives rise to the superior thyroid artery , which supplies the superior portion of the thyroid gland. However, it does not give rise to the inferior thyroid artery . Option B - Internal carotid artery: The internal carotid artery primarily supplies the brain with oxygenated blood and does not directly give rise to branches supplying the thyroid gland. Option D - Lingual artery: The lingual artery, a branch of the external carotid artery, supplies blood to the tongue and oral cavity structures but not the thyroid gland.","sourceRef":"Question Page 566; Solution Page 606","sourcePage":566,"sourcePageEnd":566},{"id":"anatomy-15-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following nerve passes through the mandibular foramen ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lingual nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Zygomatic nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Chorda tympani nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior alveolar nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Inferior alveolar nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D - Inferior alveolar nerve - The inferior alveolar nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). It passes through the mandibular foramen and provides sensory innervation to the lower teeth and gums in the mandible. Incorrect Option : Option A - Lingual nerve: - The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular nerve (V3), runs anteriorly to the foramen and provides sensory innervation to the anterior two-thirds of the tongue. Option B - Zygomatic nerve - The zygomatic nerve, a branch of the maxillary nerve, innervates the cheek and temple skin but doesn't pass through the mandibular foramen. Option C - Chorda tympani nerve - The chorda tympani nerve is a branch of the facial nerve (VII) and is not associated with the mandibular foramen..","sourceRef":"Question Pages 566-567; Solution Pages 606-607","sourcePage":566,"sourcePageEnd":567},{"id":"anatomy-15-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which division of the facial nerve innervates the muscles located in the lower lip?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Temporal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cervical"},{"letter":"C","text":"Buccal"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mandibular"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Mandibular","explanation":"Correct Option D - Mandibular - Mandibular: The mandibular branch of the facial nerve, also known as the marginal mandibular branch, supplies the muscles of the lower lip. This includes the depressor labii inferioris, mentalis, and other muscles involved in lower lip movement and expression Incorrect Options: Option A - Temporal: The temporal branch of the facial nerve supplies the muscles of the forehead and scalp. It is not involved in supplying the muscles of the lower lip. Option B - Cervical: The cervical branch of the facial nerve supplies the platysma muscle. It is not responsible for innervating the muscles of the lower lip Option C - Buccal: The buccal branch of the facial nerve supplies the muscles of the cheek. It does not directly innervate the muscles of the lower lip.","sourceRef":"Question Page 567; Solution Page 607","sourcePage":567,"sourcePageEnd":567},{"id":"anatomy-15-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":24,"question":"What is the nerve supply of the structure marked in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oculomotor nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trochlear nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trigeminal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abducens nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Trochlear nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B - Trochlear nerve: - The structure marked in the image is the Superior oblique muscle and it is supplied by the trochlear nerve. - The trochlear nerve is the fourth cranial nerve and is responsible for innervating the superior oblique muscle of the eye. - It is unique in its course and innervation compared to the other ocular motor nerves. Incorrect Option: Option A - The oculomotor nerve doesn't innervate the structure in the image. It controls eye movement muscles such as the superior rectus, inferior rectus, medial rectus, and inferior oblique muscles. Option C - The trigeminal nerve does not supply the structure marked in the image. The trigeminal nerve provides sensory innervation to the face, including the skin, mucous membranes, and teeth, and controls the muscles of mastication. Option D - The abducens nerve does not supply the structure marked in the image. The abducens nerve, the sixth cranial nerve, innervates the lateral rectus muscle of the eye, controlling outward eye movement.the trochlear nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 567-568; Solution Pages 607-608","sourcePage":567,"sourcePageEnd":568},{"id":"anatomy-15-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":25,"question":"During surgery, the patient experienced aspiration without any alteration in their voice. Which nerve is most likely to have been damaged?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Glossopharyngeal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D - Glossopharyngeal nerve: - The glossopharyngeal nerve (cranial nerve IX) is responsible for innervating the muscles of the pharynx, including the stylopharyngeus muscle. Lesioning of the glossopharyngeal nerve can lead to impaired swallowing function, which can manifest as aspiration without voice change. Incorrect Options: Option A - Aspiration without voice change suggests a specific lesion unrelated to the vagus nerve, despite its involvement in innervating laryngeal and pharyngeal muscles. Option B - A lesion of the recurrent laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus nerve, can result in voice changes like hoarseness. However, it's less likely to cause aspiration without voice change. Option C - A lesion of the superior laryngeal nerve, part of the vagus nerve, may alter voice quality by impacting the cricothyroid muscle. However, it typically isn't associated with aspiration without voice changes.","sourceRef":"Question Page 568; Solution Page 608","sourcePage":568,"sourcePageEnd":568},{"id":"anatomy-15-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":26,"question":"The glossopharyngeal nerve supplies which of the following glands?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Parotid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Submandibular"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sublingual"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lacrimal"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Parotid","explanation":"Correct Option A - Parotid: - The parotid gland receives innervation from the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) indirectly via the auriculotemporal nerve, which carries postganglionic parasympathetic fibers from the otic ganglion to the parotid gland. These fibers regulate salivary secretion. Incorrect Options: Option B - Submandibular: Innervated by the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) for parasympathetic stimulation and the lingual nerve (branch of trigeminal nerve, CN V3) for sensory input. Option C - Sublingual: Receives parasympathetic innervation via the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (CN VII) and sensory innervation from the lingual nerve (branch of trigeminal nerve, CN V3). Option D - Lacrimal: Innervated by the lacrimal nerve, a branch of the ophthalmic division (V1) of the trigeminal nerve (CN V), which carries both sensory and parasympathetic fibers for tear production.","sourceRef":"Question Page 568; Solution Pages 608-609","sourcePage":568,"sourcePageEnd":568},{"id":"anatomy-15-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Killian Dehiscence is formed due to:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior constrictor muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior Constrictor muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle pharyngeal constrictor muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Esophageal fibers"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Inferior Constrictor muscle","explanation":"Correct Option B - Inferior constrictor muscle: - Kilian’s dehiscence is a potential gap between thyropharyngeus oblique fibers and transverse fibers of cricopharyngeus muscle. These two muscles thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus are collectively called inferior constrictor muscle. Incorrect Options: - The rest of the options are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 568-569; Solution Page 609","sourcePage":568,"sourcePageEnd":569},{"id":"anatomy-15-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A 27-year-old male was climbing the stair, suddenly he fell & an iron nail hit on his neck. After the injury, the patient is having a hard time shrugging his shoulders and he is having difficulty turning his head. This might be due to an injury to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinal accessory Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deltoid Muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sternocleidomastoid Muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Long thoracic Nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Spinal accessory Nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Spinal accessory Nerve : - The spinal accessory nerve (CN XI) is responsible for innervating the trapezius and sternocleidomastoid muscles. In this scenario, the patient's difficulty in shrugging his shoulders and turning his head suggests an issue with the spinal accessory nerve, which controls these muscles. The injury caused by the nail hitting his neck could have damaged or impinged the spinal accessory nerve, leading to these symptoms. Incorrect Options: Option B - Deltoid Muscle: The symptoms described are not directly related to the function of the deltoid muscle, which is responsible for shoulder abduction. Option C - Sternocleidomastoid Muscle: Sternocleidomastoid muscle is involved in head and neck movements such as rotation and tilting, it is not responsible for shoulder shrugging. Option D - Long Thoracic Nerve: Damage to the long thoracic nerve can lead to scapular \" winging,\" where the scapula protrudes outward from the back due to weakened serratus anterior muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 569; Solution Pages 609-610","sourcePage":569,"sourcePageEnd":569},{"id":"anatomy-15-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":29,"question":"A 42-year-old female who had the extraction of her 3rd molar tooth reported loss of sensation in the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue and floor of the mouth. Which nerve is most likely to be damaged?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lingual Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glossopharyngeal Nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal Accessory Nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chorda Tympani Nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Lingual Nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Lingual Nerve: - The lingual nerve is a branch of the mandibular division of the trigeminal nerve (V3). It provides sensory innervation to the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue and the floor of the mouth. Damage to the lingual nerve during the extraction of the 3rd molar tooth (also known as a wisdom tooth) can result in loss of sensation in these areas. Incorrect Options: Option B - Glossopharyngeal Nerve: The glossopharyngeal nerve (IX) conveys taste sensation from the posterior 1/3rd of the tongue, not the anterior 2/3rd or floor of the mouth. Option C - Spinal Accessory Nerve: The spinal accessory nerve (XI) mainly controls head and shoulder movements. It's unrelated to tongue or mouth floor sensation. Option D - Chorda Tympani Nerve: The chorda tympani nerve conveys taste sensations from the front two-thirds of the tongue and regulates salivary glands. Not connected to mouth floor sensation. Damage usually from middle ear surgery, not molar extraction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 569; Solution Pages 610-611","sourcePage":569,"sourcePageEnd":569},{"id":"anatomy-15-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":30,"question":"A 42-year-old man presented with swelling due to a collection of blood around the eyes after sustaining a head injury. Which structural attachment is the cause of this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aponeurosis attachment"},{"letter":"B","text":"Periosteum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cellulitis"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Aponeurosis attachment","explanation":"Correct Option A - Aponeurosis attachment: - When a person sustains a head injury, especially around the eyes, blood can collect in the surrounding tissues due to ruptured blood vessels. The blood can accumulate in the subcutaneous space, causing swelling. The structural attachment that can contribute to this condition is the aponeurosis attachment. Incorrect Options: Option B - Periosteum: The periosteum is a tough connective tissue layer on bone surfaces. It aids in bone health but isn't directly linked to swelling around the eyes from head injuries. Option C - Cellulitis: Cellulitis, a bacterial skin infection, doesn't typically lead to blood collection and swelling post-head injury. Option D - None of the above: The correct answer is A. Aponeurosis attachment, as explained above. Therefore, the option \"None of the above\" is not the correct answer in this context.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 569-570; Solution Page 611","sourcePage":569,"sourcePageEnd":570},{"id":"anatomy-15-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":31,"question":"A 32-year-old patient presents with a swelling below the ear and complains of pain in the lobule of the ear. The patient also reports difficulty in completely closing the left eye and experiences trouble while eating food. What nerve is most likely to be damaged in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Greater Auricular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Auriculotemporal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vagus nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Facial nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Facial nerve - The facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) controls facial expressions, including eye closure and smiling. Damage to it can cause weakness or paralysis in these muscles. Additionally, it regulates muscles for eating and swallowing, which aligns with the patient's eating difficulties.. Incorrect Options: Option A - Greater Auricular nerve: Damage to this nerve might result in altered sensation around the ear, but it wouldn't explain the symptoms mentioned in the case. Option B - Auriculotemporal nerve: The auriculotemporal nerve provides sensation to the external ear and part of the scalp. However, it doesn't control facial movement, eye closure, or eating functions. Option D - Vagus nerve: While damage to the vagus nerve might cause some difficulties in swallowing and speech, it would not explain the specific symptoms of facial muscle weakness, inability to close the eye, or earlobe pain described in the case.","sourceRef":"Question Page 570; Solution Pages 611-612","sourcePage":570,"sourcePageEnd":570},{"id":"anatomy-15-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which of the following sulcus will be injured in a patient with trauma to the pointed area?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Central sulcus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stem of lateral sulcus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior frontal sulcus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intraparietal sulcus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Stem of lateral sulcus","explanation":"Correct Option B - Stem of lateral sulcus: - The marked part in the above image is the Pterion.The Pterion is a crucial anatomical landmark where several skull bones converge, forming an H-shaped sutural junction. It's particularly vulnerable due to the thinness of the bones in this region. - The lateral sulcus, or Sylvian sulcus, divides the temporal lobe below from the parietal lobe above. The middle cerebral artery (MCA), a branch of the internal carotid artery (ICA), runs within it, supplying the lateral cerebrum. Incorrect Options: Option A - Central sulcus: The central sulcus runs anterior-inferiorly, dividing the frontal motor cortex from the parietal sensory cortex, but it's not near the pterion. Option C - Inferior frontal sulcus: - The inferior frontal sulcus is located on the frontal lobe anterior to the stem of lateral sulcus and located close to pterion but not in direct association with the pterion. Option D - Intraparietal sulcus: - The intraparietal sulcus (IPS) runs along the lateral surface of the parietal lobe, with oblique and horizontal segments. It's not close to the pterion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 570-571; Solution Pages 612-613","sourcePage":570,"sourcePageEnd":571},{"id":"anatomy-15-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Which functional area is most likely to be impacted by the vascular injury to the inferior frontal gyrus in this female patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Motor speech area"},{"letter":"B","text":"Wernicke’s area"},{"letter":"C","text":"Auditory area"},{"letter":"D","text":"Visual area"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Motor speech area","explanation":"Correct Option A - Motor speech area: - The functional area that is likely to be affected when there is a vascular injury to the inferior frontal gyrus is the Motor speech area, also known as Broca's area. - Broca's area is located in the dominant hemisphere of the brain, typically in the left hemisphere for right-handed individuals in the frontal lobe, located in the prefrontal cortex. - It is involved in the production of speech and is responsible for the motor aspects of language expression. Damage to Broca's area can result in a condition called Broca's aphasia or expressive aphasia. Incorrect Options: Option B - Wernicke’s area: Wernicke’s area is located in the posterior segment of the superior temporal gyrus in the dominant hemisphere. Option C - Auditory area: The primary auditory cortex (A1) is located on the superior temporal gyrus in the temporal lobe. Option D - Visual area: It is located in the occipital lobe of the primary cerebral cortex, which is in the most posterior region of the brain.","sourceRef":"Question Page 571; Solution Pages 613-614","sourcePage":571,"sourcePageEnd":571},{"id":"anatomy-15-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Which artery passes in close proximity to the area of the skull shown in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior cerebral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle meningeal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deep temporal artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Middle meningeal artery","explanation":"Correct Option C- Middle meningeal artery: - The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the maxillary artery, itself a branch of the external carotid artery. - It passes through the foramen spinosum, located near the pterion . - The pterion is the H-shaped formation of sutures on the side of the calvarium representing the junction of four skull bones - sphenoid, temporal, frontal, and parietal. - Traumatic fractures in this area can result in rupture of the middle meningeal artery, leading to epidural hematoma, a potentially life-threatening condition. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D: These arteries do not pass in close proximity to the pterion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 571-572; Solution Pages 614-615","sourcePage":571,"sourcePageEnd":572},{"id":"anatomy-15-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":35,"question":"A student had his jaw locked while yawning. Which of the following muscles attached to the articular disc of the temporomandibular joint is under spasmodic contraction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral pterygoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Temporalis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial pterygoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Masseter"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Lateral pterygoid","explanation":"Correct Option A - Lateral pterygoid: - Jaw locking occurs when the mandible's condyle gets stuck in front of the temporal bone's tubercle in the temporomandibular joint (TMJ). This condition hinders or prevents mouth closure. - The lateral pterygoid muscle is crucial for jaw movements like protrusion, lateral deviation, and mouth opening. Temporomandibular joint dysfunction can cause jaw locking, often involving the lateral pterygoid muscle. Incorrect Options: Option B - Temporalis: The temporalis muscle is responsible for closing the jaw and retracting the mandible. Option C - Medial pterygoid: The medial pterygoid muscle is responsible for closing the jaw and moving it from side to side. Option D - Masseter: The masseter muscle is responsible for closing the jaw and moving it from side to side.","sourceRef":"Question Page 572; Solution Pages 615-616","sourcePage":572,"sourcePageEnd":572},{"id":"anatomy-15-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":36,"question":"A patient presented with jaw swelling and weight loss. Suspecting a malignancy, the submandibular gland was resected. Which is the most likely nerve to be damaged during submandibular gland resection?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nerve to mylohyoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior alveolar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lingual nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypoglossal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lingual nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Lingual nerve - During submandibular gland resection, the lingual nerve is the most likely nerve to be damaged. - The lingual nerve innervates the anterior two-thirds of the tongue, floor of the mouth, and lingual gingiva. It runs near the submandibular gland, making it vulnerable during resection. Damage may cause altered sensation or taste perception in these regions. Incorrect Options Option A - Nerve to mylohyoid: While the nerve to mylohyoid is in the vicinity of the submandibular gland, it is less likely to be directly involved in the resection procedure. Option B - Inferior alveolar nerve: The inferior alveolar nerve courses through the mandibular canal and does not directly pass through the submandibular gland. Therefore, it is less likely to be affected during the resection procedure. Option D - Hypoglossal nerve: The hypoglossal nerve innervates the muscles of the tongue and does not directly pass through or closely associated with the submandibular gland.","sourceRef":"Question Page 572; Solution Page 616","sourcePage":572,"sourcePageEnd":572},{"id":"anatomy-15-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Identify the papillae from the given histological section?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fungiform"},{"letter":"B","text":"Circumvallate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Filiform"},{"letter":"D","text":"Foliate"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Circumvallate","explanation":"Correct Option B: Circumvallate: - Circumvallate papillae are large, circular-shaped papillae located on the posterior part of the tongue. - They are characterized by their distinct morphology, which includes a circular groove or trench surrounding the papilla. - Location: Arranged in a V shape on the posterior part of the tongue. Incorrect Options: Option A- Fungiform papillae: Fungiform papillae, found all over the tongue, are smaller and lack the circular groove seen in circumvallate papillae. Options C - Filiform papillae: Filiform papillae, abundant on the tongue's dorsal surface, are slender and rough, lacking the distinct circular shape and grooves of circumvallate papillae. Options D - Foliate papillae: Foliate papillae, found on the tongue's lateral posterior part, feature vertical folds or ridges and lack the circular groove of circumvallate papillae.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 572-573; Solution Pages 616-617","sourcePage":572,"sourcePageEnd":573},{"id":"anatomy-15-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":38,"question":"How might a fracture of the jugular canal manifest itself?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of taste sensation in anterior 2/3rd of tongue"},{"letter":"B","text":"Loss of taste sensation in posterior 1/3rd of tongue"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of general sensation in anterior 2/3rd of tongue"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of motor function in anterior 2/3rd of tongue"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Loss of taste sensation in posterior 1/3rd of tongue","explanation":"Correct Option B - Loss of taste sensation in posterior 1/3rd of tongue: - The jugular canal carries the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX), which is responsible for taste sensation in the posterior 1/3rd of the tongue. Therefore, a fracture of the jugular canal could impair the glossopharyngeal nerve, causing loss of taste sensation in the posterior 1/3rd of the tongue. Incorrect Options: Option A - Loss of taste sensation in anterior 2/3 rd of tongue: Taste sensation in the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue is carried by the chorda tympani branch of the facial nerve (VII), not the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). Option C - Loss of general sensation in anterior 2/3 rd of tongue: General sensation in the anterior 2/3rd of the tongue is conveyed by the lingual nerve, a branch of the trigeminal nerve (V), not the glossopharyngeal nerve (IX). Option D - Loss of motor function in anterior 2/3 rd of tongue: Motor innervation in the tongue is predominantly controlled by the hypoglossal nerve (XII), apart from the palatoglossus muscle which is supplied by the vagus nerve (X).","sourceRef":"Question Page 573; Solution Page 617","sourcePage":573,"sourcePageEnd":573},{"id":"anatomy-15-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":39,"question":"Which foramen in the skull is created by the union of three bones?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Foramen lacerum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Foramen magnum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Foramen rotundum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Foramen jugulare"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Foramen lacerum","explanation":"Correct Option A - Foramen lacerum: - Foramen lacerum is a jagged opening in the base of the skull, which is formed by parts of three bones: the petrous part of the temporal bone, the greater wing of the sphenoid bone, and basilar part of the occipital bone. Its borders aren't always clear, given the fact it is generally filled with cartilage and fibrous tissue in a living person. Incorrect Options: Option B - Foramen magnum: The Foramen magnum is incorrect as it is bordered by the occipital bone alone. Option C - Foramen rotundum: The Foramen rotundum is located in the sphenoid bone and is solely formed by it.. Option D - Foramen jugulare: The Foramen jugulare, or jugular foramen is formed by two bones, the temporal and occipital.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 573-574; Solution Pages 617-618","sourcePage":573,"sourcePageEnd":574},{"id":"anatomy-15-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":40,"question":"Which is not a branch of the facial artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral nasal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mental"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior labial"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior labial"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Mental","explanation":"Correct Option B - Mental: - The mental artery is not a branch of the facial artery in the face. Instead, it is a branch of the inferior alveolar artery which stems from the maxillary artery, a major branch of the external carotid artery. This artery typically provides blood supply to the chin and lower lip. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lateral nasal: The lateral nasal branch of the facial artery supplies blood to the alar and dorsum of the nose. Option C - Superior labial: The Superior labial artery is a branch of the facial artery that supplies blood to the upper lip and nose. Option D - Inferior labial: The Inferior labial artery is another branch of the facial artery which supplies blood to the lower lip.","sourceRef":"Question Page 574; Solution Pages 618-619","sourcePage":574,"sourcePageEnd":574},{"id":"anatomy-15-41","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Identify the incorrect statement regarding the marked structure. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is the meeting point of frontal, parietal, temporal and sphenoid bones"},{"letter":"B","text":"Blow to the lateral side of the skull injures this structure"},{"letter":"C","text":"Accessory middle meningeal artery lies under this structure"},{"letter":"D","text":"It corresponds to site of anterolateral fontanelle of the fetal skull"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Accessory middle meningeal artery lies under this structure","explanation":"Correct Option C - Accessory middle meningeal artery lies under this structure: The pterion is a H-shaped suture point where the frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones meet. The middle meningeal artery, a branch of the maxillary artery, runs beneath it, while the accessory middle meningeal artery, originating from the maxillary or middle meningeal artery, ascends independently to the foramen ovale or a separate small foramen, unrelated to the pterion. Incorrect Options: Option A - It is the meeting point of frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones : The pterion is indeed the meeting point of frontal, parietal, temporal, and sphenoid bones. Option B - Blow to the lateral side of the skull injures this structure: This statement is also true. Option D - It corresponds to the site of anterolateral fontanelle of the fetal skull: The pterion does correspond to the site of the anterolateral fontanelle of the fetal skull.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 574-575; Solution Pages 619-620","sourcePage":574,"sourcePageEnd":575},{"id":"anatomy-15-42","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":42,"question":"Which of the following muscles is involved in smiling and grimacing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral rectus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior oblique"},{"letter":"C","text":"Risorius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Levator palpebrae superioris"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Risorius","explanation":"Correct Option C - Risorius: - The risorius muscle is innervated by the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). - It is a slender muscle of facial expression found in the face's cheek area. - It is involved in the actions of smiling and grimacing by pulling the corners of the mouth to the sides. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 575; Solution Page 620","sourcePage":575,"sourcePageEnd":575},{"id":"anatomy-15-43","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":43,"question":"A patient presents with swelling under the left ear lobule and complains of pain. Which of the following structures is the likely cause of the pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Greater auricular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Auriculotemporal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Investing layer of deep cervical fascia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Greater auricular nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B: Greater auricular nerve The greater auricular nerve, originating from the cervical plexus, innervates the skin around the ear, angle of the mandible, and parotid region. It runs superficially along the neck and over the sternocleidomastoid muscle, making it prone to injury or irritation, leading to pain and swelling. Pain and swelling under the left ear lobule could indicate involvement of this nerve, possibly due to inflammation or compression. Incorrect Options: Option A - Facial nerve: The facial nerve is primarily responsible for the innervation of the muscles of facial expression. It does not typically cause pain or swelling under the ear lobule. Option C - Auriculotemporal nerve: The auriculotemporal nerve supplies sensation to the external ear and temporal region. Though it's close to the area under the ear lobule, it's less likely to induce pain and swelling there. Option D - Investing layer of deep cervical fascia:The investing layer of deep cervical fascia envelops and organizes the neck's structures, defining its boundaries and compartments. However, it's not usually linked to pain or swelling under the ear lobule.","sourceRef":"Question Page 575; Solution Pages 620-621","sourcePage":575,"sourcePageEnd":575},{"id":"anatomy-15-44","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":44,"question":"During the clinical examination of the nervous system, which of the following nerves can be tested by touching the highlighted structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"IX"},{"letter":"B","text":"X"},{"letter":"C","text":"XI"},{"letter":"D","text":"XII"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. IX","explanation":"Correct Option A - IX The marked structure is uvula and it is supplied by the glossopharyngeal nerve . The glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) is responsible for the sensory innervation of the - posterior one-third of the tongue, including taste sensation, - sensation of the soft palate, pharynx, and the posterior portion of the oral cavity. - The nerve also plays a role in the motor control of the stylopharyngeus muscle, which is involved in swallowing and speech. Incorrect Options: Option B - X (Vagus nerve): Vagus nerve primarily plays a role in the motor control of the pharyngeal and laryngeal muscles, as well as in the autonomic innervation of various organs in the thorax and abdomen. Option C - XI (Accessory nerve): The accessory nerve primarily controls the motor function of the sternocleidomastoid and trapezius muscles and is not directly involved in the innervation of the structures mentioned in the question. Option D - XII (Hypoglossal nerve): The hypoglossal nerve controls the motor function of the muscles of the tongue and is not directly involved in the sensation of the structures mentioned in the question.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 575-576; Solution Pages 621-622","sourcePage":575,"sourcePageEnd":576},{"id":"anatomy-15-45","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":45,"question":"The framework of the external nose:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Upper 1/3rd bony and lower 2/3rd cartilaginous"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upper ½ is bony, and lower 1/2 is cartilaginous"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper 2/3rd is bony and the lower 1/3rd is cartilaginous"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Upper 1/3rd bony and lower 2/3rd cartilaginous","explanation":"Correct Option A: - The framework of the external nose is composed of bones and cartilage. - The upper one-third is made up of nasal bones - The lower two-thirds is made up of cartilages Incorrect Options - B, C & D are incorrect .","sourceRef":"Question Page 576; Solution Page 622","sourcePage":576,"sourcePageEnd":576},{"id":"anatomy-15-46","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":46,"question":"A patient presents with a cyst on the neck anterior to the sternocleidomastoid at the junction of the upper and middle third of the muscle. This cyst is due to the persistence of which of the following embryological clefts?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1st cleft"},{"letter":"B","text":"2nd cleft"},{"letter":"C","text":"3rd cleft"},{"letter":"D","text":"4th cleft"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2nd cleft","explanation":"Correct Option B - 2nd cleft - The second pharyngeal cleft is located between the second and third pharyngeal arches - It normally gives rise to the cervical sinus, a transient structure that contributes to the development of the tonsillar fossa and palatine tonsils. However, the persistence of the second cleft can lead to the formation of a branchial cyst Incorrect Options: - Option A - 1st cleft: The 1st pharyngeal cleft, located between the first and second pharyngeal arches, typically forms the external auditory meatus. However, its persistence doesn't lead to cyst formation in this area. - Option C - 3rd cleft: The 3rd pharyngeal cleft, found between the third and fourth pharyngeal arches, doesn't lead to cyst formation in the described location if it persists.. - Option D - 4th cleft: The fourth and fifth pharyngeal clefts contribute to forming structures in the head and neck. If the fourth cleft persists, it doesn't cause cyst formation as described.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 576-577; Solution Pages 622-623","sourcePage":576,"sourcePageEnd":577},{"id":"anatomy-15-47","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":47,"question":"The nerve supply of the larynx above the level of the vocal cords is by __","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Recurrent laryngeal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Glossopharyngeal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"External laryngeal nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior laryngeal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Superior laryngeal nerve - The nerve supply of the larynx above the level of the vocal cords is by the superior laryngeal nerve. - The superior laryngeal nerve, a branch of the vagus nerve, has two branches: the internal branch and the external branch. - The internal branch provides sensory fibers to the larynx's mucosa above the vocal cords' level. - The external branch supplies motor fibers to the cricothyroid muscle. Incorrect Options: Option B - The recurrent laryngeal nerve innervates the larynx below the vocal cords, supplying motor fibers to its intrinsic muscles except the cricothyroid. Option C - The glossopharyngeal nerve provides sensation and taste to the posterior one-third of the tongue and sensation to the oropharynx but doesn't directly innervate the larynx. Option D - The external laryngeal nerve, a branch of the superior laryngeal nerve, provides motor fibers to the cricothyroid muscle but doesn't directly innervate the larynx above the vocal cords.","sourceRef":"Question Page 577; Solution Page 623","sourcePage":577,"sourcePageEnd":577},{"id":"anatomy-15-48","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":48,"question":"Name the dural venous arrow marked in the radiograph. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Straight sinus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior sagittal sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Confluence of sinuses"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Straight sinus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Straight sinus - The marked structure is a straight sinus. The straight sinus is a small midline structure that runs from the junction of the falx cerebri and tentorium cerebelli to the confluence of sinuses. It can be seen in imaging studies such as MRI or CT scans. It appears as a thin curvilinear opacity on the midline of the skull radiograph, just posterior to the frontal bone and anterior to the occipital bone. Incorrect Options: Option B - Superior sagittal sinus: The superior sagittal sinus is a prominent midline structure running from the frontal to the occipital regions atop the brain, often visible on MRI or CT scans. Option C - Inferior sagittal sinus: The inferior sagittal sinus, a small midline structure, extends along the underside of the brain from the frontal to the occipital regions, visible on MRI or CT scans. Option D - Confluence of sinuses: The confluence of sinuses is where the superior sagittal, straight, and occipital sinuses meet, a significant neuroanatomical landmark identifiable on MRI or CT scans.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 577-578; Solution Pages 623-624","sourcePage":577,"sourcePageEnd":578},{"id":"anatomy-15-49","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":49,"question":"Which of the following dural venous sinuses receives the great cerebral vein of Galen as its tributary?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. B","explanation":"Correct Option B - B - The straight sinus, positioned at the junction of the falx cerebri and the tentorium cerebelli, receives tributaries like the great cerebral vein of Galen, formed by the union of the two internal cerebral veins. Other tributaries include the inferior sagittal sinus and the occipital sinus. Blood from the straight sinus ultimately drains into the confluence of sinuses, where the superior sagittal sinus, straight sinus, and occipital sinus converge. Incorrect Options: Option A - A: The superior sagittal sinus drains the blood from the front and top of the brain and empties into the confluence of sinuses, where it meets the straight sinus and other sinuses. Option C - C:The transverse sinuses, running horizontally at the back of the skull, receive blood from various sinuses including the superior sagittal sinus and inferior sagittal sinus. They then descend to form the sigmoid sinuses. . Option D - D: The sigmoid sinuses receive blood from the transverse sinuses, petrosal sinuses, and nearby veins, draining into the jugular veins that return blood to the heart.","sourceRef":"Question Page 578; Solution Pages 624-625","sourcePage":578,"sourcePageEnd":578},{"id":"anatomy-15-50","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":50,"question":"Infer the extent of the thyroid gland from the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"B to D"},{"letter":"B","text":"A to C"},{"letter":"C","text":"A to D"},{"letter":"D","text":"C to D"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. A to D","explanation":"Correct Option C - A to D - A- C5 - B- C6 - C- C7 - D- T1 The thyroid gland is situated in the front of the neck, below the larynx, between the C5 and T1 vertebrae. Incorrect Options: Option A - B to D: The structures that extend from C6 to T1 vertebrae include the posterior cervical muscles, including the semispinalis cervicis, splenius cervicis, and longissimus cervicis muscles.. Option B - A to C: The structures that extend from C5 to C7 vertebrae include the anterior scalene, middle scalene, and posterior scalene muscles, which form the scalene triangle. Option D - C to D: The structures that extend from C7 to T1 vertebrae include the first rib, which is located between these two vertebrae.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 578-579; Solution Page 625","sourcePage":578,"sourcePageEnd":579},{"id":"anatomy-15-51","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":51,"question":"A patient underwent a left submandibular gland excision during which the hypoglossal nerve was injured. Which of the following deficits would be seen in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deviated tongue to the contralateral side"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deviated tongue to the same side"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inability to protrude tongue"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of taste in posterior 1/3rd of the tongue"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Deviated tongue to the same side","explanation":"Correct Option B - Deviated tongue to the same side - The hypoglossal nerve innervates tongue muscles, including intrinsic and extrinsic muscles. Injury during submandibular gland excision can lead to weakness or paralysis on the same side, causing the tongue to deviate towards that side when protruded. This happens because the hypoglossal nerve supplies most tongue muscles on the same side, leading to animbalance in muscle function. The deviation may be subtle or quite noticeable, depending onthe severity of the injury. Incorrect Options: Option A - Deviated tongue to the contralateral side: Injury to the hypoglossal nerve during submandibular gland excision leads to ipsilateral tongue deviation when protruded, not contralateral. Option C - Inability to protrude tongue: While injury to the hypoglossal nerve may cause weakness or paralysis of the tongue muscles, it would not necessarily result in an inability to protrude the tongue.. Option D - Loss of taste in posterior 1/3rd of the tongue: . The hypoglossal nerve is not involved in taste sensation. The posterior 1/3rd of the tongue is innervated by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX).","sourceRef":"Question Page 579; Solution Pages 625-626","sourcePage":579,"sourcePageEnd":579},{"id":"anatomy-15-52","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":52,"question":"All of the following are paired cartilages except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Arytenoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyroid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cuneiform"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corniculate"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Thyroid","explanation":"Correct Option B - Thyroid - The larynx is made up of three paired and three unpaired cartilages. - The arytenoid, cuneiform and corniculate cartilages are paired, presenting one on the right and another on the left side. - The unpaired cartilages are epiglottis, thyroid and cricoid cartilage. These are larger cartilages present along the midline. Incorrect Options : Option A - Arytenoid : These are a pair of small pyramidal-shaped cartilages present on the upper border of the lamina of the cricoid cartilage. Option C - Cuneiform : These are a pair of small rod-shaped cartilages present in the aryepiglottic fold of the larynx. Option D - Corniculate: These are small conical cartilages that articulate with the arytenoid cartilages.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 579-580; Solution Pages 626-627","sourcePage":579,"sourcePageEnd":580},{"id":"anatomy-15-53","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":53,"question":"Which of the following features differentiated the exocrine pancreas from the parotid gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Basal lamina lining the serous acini"},{"letter":"B","text":"Absence of striated duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Presence of serous acini with acidophilic tip"},{"letter":"D","text":"Presence of apical acinar villi"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Absence of striated duct","explanation":"Correct Option B - Absence of striated duct: One key difference between the exocrine pancreas and the parotid gland is the absence of striated ducts in the pancreas. Striated ducts, found in glands like the parotid, modify secretions with characteristic basal infoldings. In contrast, the pancreas relies on intercalated ducts, lacking these basal infoldings, to transport enzymes from acinar cells to the main duct. Incorrect Options: Option A - Basal lamina lining the serous acini:. Both the exocrine pancreas and the parotid gland have a basal lamina lining the serous acini. The basal lamina provides structural support to the acinar cells. Option C - Presence of serous acini with acidophilic tip: Both the exocrine pancreas and the parotid gland can have serous acini with acidophilic (eosinophilic) cytoplasmic granules at their apical (or luminal) ends. Option D - Presence of apical acinar villi: Neither the exocrine pancreas nor the parotid gland have apical acinar villi..","sourceRef":"Question Page 580; Solution Page 627","sourcePage":580,"sourcePageEnd":580},{"id":"anatomy-15-54","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":54,"question":"All of the following are the contents of the carotid sheath except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sympathetic chain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Carotid artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal jugular vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"10 th cranial nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Sympathetic chain","explanation":"Correct Option A - Sympathetic chain: - The carotid sheath is a fibrous connective tissue structure located in the neck. It contains several important structures, including the carotid artery, internal jugular vein, and 10th cranial nerve (vagus nerve). - Sympathetic chain: The sympathetic chain, also known as the sympathetic trunk or sympathetic ganglia, is not a content of the carotid sheath. The sympathetic chain runs alongside the vertebral column and is responsible for carrying sympathetic nerve fibers. I ncorrect Options: Option B - Carotid artery: The carotid artery, specifically the common carotid artery, is a major blood vessel that supplies blood to the head and neck. It is a content of the carotid sheath. Option C - Internal jugular vein: The internal jugular vein, running parallel to the carotid artery, drains blood from the brain, face, and neck, and is contained within the carotid sheath. Option D - 10th cranial nerve (vagus nerve): The vagus nerve, or the 10th cranial nerve, innervates numerous thoracic and abdominal organs. In the neck, it travels within the carotid sheath.","sourceRef":"Question Page 580; Solution Pages 627-628","sourcePage":580,"sourcePageEnd":580},{"id":"anatomy-15-55","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":55,"question":"Medial lemniscus is a continuation of which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinothalamic tract"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spinocerebellar tract"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinotectal tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fasciculus gracilis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fasciculus gracilis","explanation":"Correct Option D - Fasciculus gracilis - The medial lemniscus is a continuation of the fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus, which are part of the dorsal column-medial lemniscus pathway. This pathway carries sensory information related to fine touch, vibration, and proprioception from the lower body (fasciculus gracilis) and upper body (fasciculus cuneatus) to the brain. - The fasciculus gracilis and fasciculus cuneatus are composed of ascending sensory fibers that originate from the dorsal root ganglia in the spinal cord. Incorrect Options: Option A - Spinothalamic tract: The spinothalamic tract is a separate pathway that carries pain, temperature, and crude touch sensations from the body to the thalamus. Option B - Spinocerebellar tract: The spinocerebellar tracts are involved in carrying proprioceptive information from the spinal cord to the cerebellum, which helps in coordinating and fine-tuning motor movements. Option C - Spinotectal tract: The spinotectal tract is involved in conveying sensory information related to pain and temperature from the body to the superior colliculus in the midbrain, which plays a role in orienting visual and auditory responses to stimuli.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 580-581; Solution Pages 628-629","sourcePage":580,"sourcePageEnd":581},{"id":"anatomy-15-56","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":56,"question":"Which among the following is a tensor of vocal cords?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cricothyroid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral cricoarytenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyroarytenoids"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior cricoarytenoids"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Cricothyroid","explanation":"Correct Option A - Cricothyroid - The correct option is a. Cricothyroid , as it is the primary tensor muscle of the vocal cords. - It controls the tension and pitch of the vocal cords, enabling modulation of vocal Incorrect Options: Option B - Lateral cricoarytenoid: The lateral cricoarytenoid muscle is an adductor muscle of the vocal cords. It assists in closing the vocal cords during phonation but does not directly act as a tensor. Option C - Thyroarytenoids: The thyroarytenoid muscles are relaxor of vocal cords. Option D - Posterior cricoarytenoids: The posterior cricoarytenoid muscles are the only abductor muscles of the vocal cords. They play a crucial role in opening the vocal cords during inhalation, allowing for the passage of air.","sourceRef":"Question Page 581; Solution Pages 629-630","sourcePage":581,"sourcePageEnd":581},{"id":"anatomy-15-57","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":57,"question":"The hard palate contains_________.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Keratinized epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands"},{"letter":"B","text":"Keratinized epithelium, absent submucosa, no salivary glands"},{"letter":"C","text":"Non-keratinized epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands"},{"letter":"D","text":"Non-keratinized epithelium, absent submucosa, minor salivary glands"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Keratinized epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands","explanation":"Correct Option A - Keratinized epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands - The hard palate contains keratinized epithelium, submucosa, and minor salivary glands. - This combination of structures is essential for the functional and anatomical characteristics of the hard palate. Incorrect Options: Option B - Keratinized epithelium, absent submucosa, no salivary glands: The hard palate does have a submucosa layer beneath the epithelium, which provides support and contains blood vessels, nerves, and minor salivary glands. Option C - Non-keratinized epithelium, submucosa, minor salivary glands: . As mentioned earlier, the hard palate is lined with keratinized epithelium, which provides a protective and resilient covering. Option D - Non-keratinized epithelium, absent submucosa, minor salivary glands: . The hard palate does have a submucosa layer that contributes to its structure and function.","sourceRef":"Question Page 581; Solution Page 630","sourcePage":581,"sourcePageEnd":581},{"id":"anatomy-15-58","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":58,"question":"Identify the thickened nerve marked in the arrow in the image shown below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Great auricular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser occipital nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Facial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Auriculotemporal nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Great auricular nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Great auricular nerve - Great auricular nerve because it matches the thickened nerve marked in the image, which corresponds to the course of the great auricular nerve along the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle - The great auricular nerve is a sensory nerve that originates from the cervical plexus and provides sensation to the skin of the ear and the region over the parotid gland. Incorrect Options: Option B - Lesser occipital nerve: The lesser occipital nerve, originating from the cervical plexus, supplies sensation to the posterior scalp skin. Though it runs alongside the great auricular nerve, it's not highlighted in the image. Option C - Facial nerve: The facial nerve controls facial muscles and transmits taste sensations from the anterior two-thirds of the tongue but doesn't innervate the ear or parotid region. Option D - Auriculotemporal nerve:The auriculotemporal nerve, innervates the external ear, temporal region, and part of the scalp. Unlike the great auricular nerve, it doesn't run along the posterior border of the sternocleidomastoid muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 581-582; Solution Pages 630-631","sourcePage":581,"sourcePageEnd":582},{"id":"anatomy-15-59","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":59,"question":"Killian’s dehiscence is seen between?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Palatopharyngeus and cricopharyngeus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thyropharyngeus and palatopharyngeus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cricopharyngeus and thyropharyngeus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palatopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Cricopharyngeus and thyropharyngeus","explanation":"Correct Option C - Cricopharyngeus and thyropharyngeus - Killian’s dehiscence is a potential gap that can be seen between the upper thyropharyngeus and lower cricopharyngeus muscle. These two muscles are the two parts of the inferior constrictor muscle of the pharyn x. Incorrect Options : Option A - Palatopharyngeus and cricopharyngeus: Killian's dehiscence is present between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscle and is not present between Palatopharyngeus and cricopharyngeus. Option B - Thyropharyngeus and palatopharyngeus: Killian's dehiscence is present between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscle and is not present between Thyropharyngeus and palatopharyngeus. Option D - Palatopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus: Killian's dehiscence is present between the thyropharyngeus and cricopharyngeus muscle and is not present between Palatopharyngeus and salpingopharyngeus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 582; Solution Page 631","sourcePage":582,"sourcePageEnd":582},{"id":"anatomy-15-60","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":60,"question":"Which of the following muscles is an abductor of the vocal cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral cricoarytenoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior cricoarytenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyroarytenoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vocalis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior cricoarytenoid","explanation":"Correct Option B - Posterior Cricoarytenoid - The posterior cricoarytenoid muscle, an intrinsic laryngeal muscle, abducts the vocal cords, acting as \"safety muscle\" the larynx. It moves the arytenoids to open the vocal cords, allowing for breathing. When closed, it causes the vocal cords to close, resulting in breathing difficulties. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lateral cricoarytenoid : The lateral cricoarytenoid is an adductor of the larynx along with all other intrinsic muscles except the posterior cricoarytenoid. Option C - Thyroarytenoid : Relaxes the vocal cords. Does not help in the abduction of the vocal cords Option D - Vocalis: Anteriorly it acts as a relaxer of the vocal cords and Posteriorly it acts as a tensor. It does not help in the Abduction of the vocal cords","sourceRef":"Question Pages 582-583; Solution Pages 631-632","sourcePage":582,"sourcePageEnd":583},{"id":"anatomy-15-61","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":61,"question":"A patient presents with a unilateral throbbing headache, photophobia, and excessive lacrimation. He also complains of hemifacial pain on the clenching of teeth. On examination, pupillary reaction, light reflex, and accommodation reflex are normal. Which of the following marked nerves is involved in the above scenario? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 3","explanation":"Correct Option C - 3 - 3 is the right choice since the side effects depicted involve the Trigeminal nerve ophthalmic division (V1), which supplies sensation to the forehead, scalp, and eye. Cluster headaches are known for one-sided pain around the eye or temple, followed by autonomic symptoms like lacrimation, nasal blockage, and ptosis. Hemifacial pain triggered by teeth clenching is also common in trigeminal neuralgia, affecting any of its divisions but usually the maxillary or mandibular. Incorrect Options: Option A - Is an incorrect choice since the ophthalmic nerve (V1) is a branch of the trigeminal nerve (V), not the first cranial nerve (I), which is the olfactory nerve. Option B - Is an incorrect choice since the optic nerve (II) is capable of visual work, not the symptoms described within the scenario. Option D - Is an incorrect choice since the facial nerve (VII) supplies the muscles of facial expression and the lacrimal and salivary glands but isn't included in the sensory symptoms portrayed within the scenario.","sourceRef":"Question Page 583; Solution Pages 632-633","sourcePage":583,"sourcePageEnd":583},{"id":"anatomy-15-62","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":62,"question":"What is the structure marked in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Genioglossus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyoglossus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Styloglossus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palatoglossus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Genioglossus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Genioglossus - The genioglossus muscle is a fan-shaped extrinsic tongue muscle that positions the tongue anteriorly, inferiorly, and medially. It originates from the upper genial tubercles of the mandible. - It originates from the upper genial tubercles of the mandible. its upper fibres insert into the tip of the tongue, middle fibres insert into the dorsum of the tongue and lower fibres insert into the hyoid bone. Incorrect Options: Option B - Hyoglossus :The muscle in the image isn't the hyoglossus because it originates from the hyoid bone and inserts into the side of the tongue.. Option C - Styloglossus : The muscle in the image isn't the styloglossus because it originates from the styloid process and inserts into the side of the tongue. Option D - Palatoglossus : The muscle in the image isn't the palatoglossus because it originates from the oral part of the palatine aponeurosis and inserts at the base of the tongue.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 583-584; Solution Pages 633-634","sourcePage":583,"sourcePageEnd":584},{"id":"anatomy-15-63","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":63,"question":"Inferior thyroid artery supplies which of the following structures? Thyroid Parathyroid Esophagus Thymus","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3 and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,2 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"1 and 2 only"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,2 and 4 only"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1,2,3 and 4","explanation":"Organs directly supplied by the inferior thyroid artery: Incorrect Options: Options B, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Page 584; Solution Page 634","sourcePage":584,"sourcePageEnd":584},{"id":"anatomy-15-64","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":64,"question":"A patient after a road traffic accident presents to the emergency room with difficulty in swallowing and slurred speech. Investigations reveal fractures in the occipitotemporal region. Which of the following areas should be tested in order to find the nerve which is involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2","explanation":"Correct Option B - 2 - Fractures in the occipitotemporal region may affect cranial nerves related to swallowing and speech. Testing the soft palate (option b) is essential to identify the involved nerve. Controlled by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) and the vagus nerve (CN X), the soft palate regulates swallowing and speech. Damage to this area can cause dysphagia and slurred speech. Testing the soft palate directly assesses the nerves responsible for these symptoms. Incorrect Options: Option A - 1: Cribriform plate: Fractures of the ethmoid bone's cribriform plate can lead to loss of smell due to damage to the olfactory nerve (CN I), but they usually don't cause swallowing difficulties or slurred speech.. Option C - 3: Tongue: Tongue damage typically doesn't cause swallowing difficulties or slurred speech since its muscles are primarily controlled by the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII), which isn't directly linked to the described symptoms. Option D - 4: Larynx: Damage to the larynx or its innervation by the recurrent laryngeal nerve (a branch of the vagus nerve - CN X) can cause voice changes and hoarseness, but it's not directly linked to difficulty in swallowing.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 584-585; Solution Pages 634-635","sourcePage":584,"sourcePageEnd":585},{"id":"anatomy-15-65","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":65,"question":"A 12-year-old presented with fever and difficulty swallowing. He had swelling in the marked region and was advised to undergo surgery. Post-surgery the gauze continued to soak with blood. Which of the following vessels must have been injured? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tonsillar branch of facial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending pharyngeal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Paratonsillar vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Retromandibular vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Paratonsillar vein","explanation":"Correct Option C - Paratonsillar vein: - The Paratonsillar vein is a venous structure located in the vicinity of the tonsillar region. - The paratonsillar vein is responsible for draining blood from the tonsillar area. - During surgery, if the paratonsillar vein is injured, it can result in persistent bleeding from the surgical site. Incorrect Options: Option A - Tonsillar branch of the facial artery: The tonsillar branch of the facial artery arises from the external carotid artery and travels along the face and neck, supplying blood to the palatine tonsils. Option B - Ascending pharyngeal artery: The ascending pharyngeal artery originating from the external carotid artery, courses along the neck's lateral aspect, supplying blood to the pharyngeal wall, soft palate, and throat structures. Option D - Retromandibular vein: The retromandibular vein, which drains into the external jugular vein, isn't directly linked to the tonsillar region. Therefore, injuries to this vein typically don't result in bleeding from that specific area.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 585-586; Solution Page 635","sourcePage":585,"sourcePageEnd":586},{"id":"anatomy-15-66","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":66,"question":"Identify the structure C.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epiglottis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyoid bone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyroid cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cricoid cartilage"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Epiglottis","explanation":"Correct Option A - Epiglottis: Epiglottis is a leaf-shaped cartilage placed in the anterior wall of the upper part of the larynx. Its upper end is broad and free, and projects upwards behind the hyoid bone and the tongue. Incorrect Options: Option B - Hyoid bone: It is a U-shaped bone (A) situated at the root of the tongue in the front of the neck and between the lower jaw and the largest cartilage of the larynx, or voice box. Option C - Thyroid cartilage: The tough, flexible tissue that forms the front part of the larynx marked by B. Option D - Cricoid cartilage: The cricoid cartilage (D) is a hyaline cartilage ring that fully encircles the trachea and composes the inferior-most boundary of the laryngeal skeleton.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 586-587; Solution Pages 635-636","sourcePage":586,"sourcePageEnd":587},{"id":"anatomy-15-67","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":67,"question":"Which of the following structures prevents the upward extension of thyroid swelling through its attachment to the thyroid cartilage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pretracheal fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sternothyroid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thyrohyoid membrane"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ligament of berry"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Sternothyroid","explanation":"Correct Option B - Sternothyroid: - The sternothyroid muscle plays a role in preventing the upward extension of thyroid swelling. - It is a strap-like muscle that originates from the sternum (specifically the manubrium) and inserts onto the thyroid cartilage of the larynx. - Its position and attachment help limit the upward expansion of the thyroid gland. Incorrect Options: Option A - The pretracheal fascia surrounds the trachea and thyroid gland, offering support and anchoring for the thyroid. However, it doesn't specifically prevent the thyroid from swelling upward. Option C - Thyrohyoid membrane is a fibrous sheet that connects the hyoid bone to the thyroid cartilage. It does not directly prevent the upward extension of thyroid swelling. Option D - The Ligament of Berry, also known as the thyroepiglottic ligament, connects the thyroid cartilage to the epiglottis. However, it doesn't directly prevent upward extension of thyroid swelling.","sourceRef":"Question Page 587; Solution Page 636","sourcePage":587,"sourcePageEnd":587},{"id":"anatomy-15-68","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-15","chapter":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 15 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":15,"questionNumber":68,"question":"Which of the following is not used for the identification of the facial nerve trunk?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Retrograde dissection from distal branch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tragal pointer"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior belly of digastric"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle","explanation":"Correct Option: C - Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle - Inferior belly of omohyoid muscle: The inferior belly of the omohyoid muscle is not directly associated with the identification of the facial nerve trunk. - It is a muscle located in the neck region and is not typically used as a landmark or reference point for facial nerve identification. Incorrect options: Option A: Retrograde dissection from distal branch: Retrograde dissection maps the course of the facial nerve by tracing it from its distal branches back to its main trunk. Option B: Tragal pointer: The tragal pointer serves as a reliable landmark in surgery for locating the main trunk of the facial nerve. Option D: Posterior belly of digastric: The posterior belly of the digastric muscle serves as a reference point for identifying the facial nerve trunk in surgery due to its proximity to the nerve","sourceRef":"Question Pages 587-588; Solution Pages 636-637","sourcePage":587,"sourcePageEnd":588},{"id":"anatomy-16-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35-year-old man presents with numbness and tingling in the lateral aspect of his forearm and thumb after a fall. Upon examination, the physician notes that the sensation is decreased in a specific area corresponding to a dermatome. Which of the following dermatomes is likely affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5"},{"letter":"B","text":"C6"},{"letter":"C","text":"C7"},{"letter":"D","text":"C8"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. C6","explanation":"C6 covers the lateral aspect of the forearm and the thumb(1st digit). The patient's symptoms of numbness and tingling in this area suggest involvement of the C6 nerve root. C5 (Option A) dermatome is primarily responsible for sensation over the shoulder and the upper lateral arm , not the forearm or thumb. C7 (Option C) dermatome covers the index finger (2nd digit) to ring finger (4th digit) and the posterior aspect of the forearm , which does not match the patient’s symptoms. C8 (Option D) dermatome includes the medial aspect of the forearm and the little finger (5th digit) , which is also not consistent with the presented symptoms.","sourceRef":"Question Page 638; Solution Pages 643-644","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":638},{"id":"anatomy-16-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A newborn is delivered with a shoulder dystocia complication. On examination, the infant has paralysis of the arm with the shoulder adducted, the elbow extended, and the forearm pronated. This injury most likely involves which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower trunk of the brachial plexus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper trunk of the brachial plexus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Upper trunk of the brachial plexus","explanation":"The described presentation is consistent with Erb’s palsy , a result of injury to the upper trunk (C5-C6) of the brachial plexus, which occurs at Erb’s point . This injury commonly happens in cases of shoulder dystocia during birth and leads to the \"waiter's tip\" posture. Erb's Point: Anatomy and Clinical Relevance - Location: Situated in the posterior triangle of the neck , where the upper trunk of the brachial plexus divides. - Situated in the posterior triangle of the neck , where the upper trunk of the brachial plexus divides. - Brachial Plexus Formation: The upper trunk is formed by the union of C5 and C6 ventral rami . - The upper trunk is formed by the union of C5 and C6 ventral rami . - Posterior Triangle of the Neck: Brachial plexus trunks emerge between the anterior and middle scalene muscles . Contains structures like the subclavian artery and scalene muscles . - Brachial plexus trunks emerge between the anterior and middle scalene muscles . - Contains structures like the subclavian artery and scalene muscles . - Situated in the posterior triangle of the neck , where the upper trunk of the brachial plexus divides. - The upper trunk is formed by the union of C5 and C6 ventral rami . - Brachial plexus trunks emerge between the anterior and middle scalene muscles . - Contains structures like the subclavian artery and scalene muscles . Clinical Significance of Erb's Point: Feature Description Anatomical Role Upper trunk divides into anterior and posterior divisions Clinical Importance Injuries to this area can cause Erb's palsy with specific motor/sensory deficits Erb's Palsy Results from damage to the upper trunk , leading to weakness in the upper limb, waiter-tip posture . Lower trunk of the brachial plexus (Option A) : Injury to the lower trunk would cause Klumpke’s palsy , affecting the hand muscles, not the arm posture described here. Radial nerve (Option B ) injury typically causes wrist drop , not the characteristic arm posture described in Erb’s palsy. Median nerve (Option D) injury leads to weakness in forearm flexion and the thenar muscles , not the upper limb weakness seen in this case.","sourceRef":"Question Page 638; Solution Pages 644-646","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":638},{"id":"anatomy-16-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A newborn presents with weakness in the right arm after a difficult vaginal delivery. The arm is limp and hangs by the side, with the forearm pronated and the hand facing inward in a \"waiter's tip\" position. The infant has difficulty with shoulder abduction and elbow flexion. Which of the following nerve roots are most likely involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5 and C6"},{"letter":"B","text":"C7 and C8"},{"letter":"C","text":"C8 and T1"},{"letter":"D","text":"C3 and C4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. C5 and C6","explanation":"C5 and C6 involvement is typical in Erb's palsy , causing weakness in muscles responsible for shoulder abduction , external rotation , elbow flexion , and forearm supination . Erb's Palsy : - Erb-Duchenne palsy results from damage to the upper trunk of the brachial plexus (C5 and C6 nerve roots). - Mechanism of Injury: Occurs due to traction or stretching of the neck during childbirth (shoulder dystocia) or traumatic separation of the head and shoulder. - Occurs due to traction or stretching of the neck during childbirth (shoulder dystocia) or traumatic separation of the head and shoulder. - Occurs due to traction or stretching of the neck during childbirth (shoulder dystocia) or traumatic separation of the head and shoulder. Anatomical Structures Involved: Structure Description Brachial Plexus Formed by ventral rami of C5-T1 spinal nerves Upper Trunk Union of C5 and C6 ventral rami Erb’s Point Located in the posterior triangle of the neck, where the upper trunk divides into anterior and posterior divisions Clinical Presentation: - Muscle Weakness/ Paralysis: Shoulder Abduction: Affected (supraspinatus, deltoid) External Rotation: Affected (infraspinatus, teres minor) Elbow Flexion: Affected (biceps brachii, brachialis) Forearm Supination: Affected (biceps brachii) - Shoulder Abduction: Affected (supraspinatus, deltoid) - External Rotation: Affected (infraspinatus, teres minor) - Elbow Flexion: Affected (biceps brachii, brachialis) - Forearm Supination: Affected (biceps brachii) - Arm Position: Limp arm by the side, forearm pronated, hand inward (\" waiter’s tip \" position). - Limp arm by the side, forearm pronated, hand inward (\" waiter’s tip \" position). - Sensory Deficits Numbness or tingling along the lateral arm , forearm , and hand , corresponding to C5 and C6 dermatomes. - Numbness or tingling along the lateral arm , forearm , and hand , corresponding to C5 and C6 dermatomes. - Shoulder Abduction: Affected (supraspinatus, deltoid) - External Rotation: Affected (infraspinatus, teres minor) - Elbow Flexion: Affected (biceps brachii, brachialis) - Forearm Supination: Affected (biceps brachii) - Limp arm by the side, forearm pronated, hand inward (\" waiter’s tip \" position). - Numbness or tingling along the lateral arm , forearm , and hand , corresponding to C5 and C6 dermatomes. Associated Findings: Condition Description Horner's Syndrome Involvement of T1 nerve root, indicating a more extensive injury Skeletal Abnormalities Bone deformities (e.g., glenoid dysplasia, clavicular shortening) due to muscle imbalance C7 and C8 (Option B) : Injuries here would affect the triceps and finger extension , not the typical presentation seen in Erb's palsy. C8 and T1 (Option C) : Damage here leads to Klumpke’s palsy , affecting hand muscles and causing a \"claw hand\" deformity. C3 and C4 (Option D) nerve roots contribute to the phrenic nerve and control the diaphragm, not the arm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 638-639; Solution Pages 646-649","sourcePage":638,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"anatomy-16-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A newborn presents with weakness in the hand following a difficult delivery. Examination reveals a claw hand deformity characterized by hyperextension at the metacarpophalangeal joints and flexion at the interphalangeal joints. Sensory loss is noted along the ulnar aspect of the forearm and hand. Which of the following is the most likely nerve root involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5"},{"letter":"B","text":"C7"},{"letter":"C","text":"C8"},{"letter":"D","text":"T1"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. T1","explanation":"The given findings are associated with Klumpke’s paralysis. T1 is the main nerve root involved in Klumpke's paralysis, leading to the characteristic claw hand deformity and sensory loss along the ulnar border of the forearm and hand. Klumpke’s Paralysis : Aspect Details Site of Injury Lower trunk of the brachial plexus Cause of Injury Undue arm abduction (e.g., clutching after a fall) Birth injury Nerve Roots Involved Primarily T1, partially C8 - Muscles Affected: Intrinsic muscles of the hand (T1) Ulnar flexors of the wrist and fingers (C8) - Intrinsic muscles of the hand (T1) - Ulnar flexors of the wrist and fingers (C8) - Hand Deformity Claw hand: Unopposed action of long flexors and extensors Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Claw hand: Unopposed action of long flexors and extensors Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints - Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Disabilities Associated: Complete claw hand Cutaneous anesthesia and analgesia along the ulnar border of forearm and hand Horner’s Syndrome (if T1 injured near sympathetic ganglion) Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflexVasomotor Changes Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation andlack of sweating Trophic Changes Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails,and atrophy of finger pulp - Complete claw hand - Cutaneous anesthesia and analgesia along the ulnar border of forearm and hand - Horner’s Syndrome (if T1 injured near sympathetic ganglion) Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflex - Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflex - Vasomotor Changes Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation and lack of sweating - Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation and lack of sweating - Trophic Changes Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails, and atrophy of finger pulp - Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails, and atrophy of finger pulp - Intrinsic muscles of the hand (T1) - Ulnar flexors of the wrist and fingers (C8) - Claw hand: Unopposed action of long flexors and extensors Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints - Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Hyperextension at metacarpophalangeal joints - Flexion at interphalangeal joints - Complete claw hand - Cutaneous anesthesia and analgesia along the ulnar border of forearm and hand - Horner’s Syndrome (if T1 injured near sympathetic ganglion) Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflex - Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflex - Vasomotor Changes Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation and lack of sweating - Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation and lack of sweating - Trophic Changes Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails, and atrophy of finger pulp - Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails, and atrophy of finger pulp - Symptoms: ptosis, miosis, anhidrosis, enophthalmos, loss of ciliospinal reflex - Warmer, drier skin in the sensory loss area due to arteriolar dilation and lack of sweating - Long-term paralysis results in dry, scaly skin, brittle nails, and atrophy of finger pulp C5 (Option A) is involved in upper trunk injuries, not Klumpke’s paralysis. C7 (Option B) does not play a primary role in the claw hand deformity associated with Klumpke’s paralysis. C8 (Option C) : While C8 is involved, the predominant nerve root affected in Klumpke’s paralysis is T1.","sourceRef":"Question Page 639; Solution Pages 649-651","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"anatomy-16-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following gives rise to the bones and connective tissues of the limb?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Intermediate mesoderm"},{"letter":"B","text":"The parietal (somatic) layer of lateral plate mesoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Paraxial mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Neural crest cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The parietal (somatic) layer of lateral plate mesoderm","explanation":"The parietal (somatic) layer of the lateral plate mesoderm forms the mesenchymal core of the limb bud, which will differentiate into the bones and connective tissues of the upper limb. Development of the Upper Limb: - The upper limb bud appears around the end of the 4th week of development. - These buds form as outpocketings from the lateral body wall. - The Mesenchymal Core of the limb buds is derived from the parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm, which later gives rise to bones and connective tissues. - The limb bud is covered by a layer of ectoderm, which thickens at the distal end to form the apical ectodermal ridge (AER). Period of Gestation Event 4th Week Appearance of the upper limb bud 6th Week Formation of hand plate and initiation of chondrification. 7th Week Limb rotation and further cartilage development. 12th Week Primary ossification centers in long bones. Intermediate mesoderm (Option A) forms structures like the urogenital system. Paraxial mesoderm (Option C) forms somites, which give rise to skeletal muscles and parts of the vertebrae. Neural crest cells (Option D) contribute to the peripheral nervous system, but not to the bones and connective tissues of the limbs.","sourceRef":"Question Page 639; Solution Pages 651-652","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":639},{"id":"anatomy-16-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 35-year-old man presents with numbness and tingling along the lateral aspect of his forearm and thumb following a fall on his outstretched arm. Which dermatome is most likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5"},{"letter":"B","text":"C6"},{"letter":"C","text":"C7"},{"letter":"D","text":"C8"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. C6","explanation":"The C6 dermatome covers the lateral aspect of the forearm, the thumb, and part of the index finger . Numbness or tingling in this area, suggests involvement of the C6 dermatome. Dermatomes of the Upper Limb: Spinal Nerve Root Dermatome (Sensory Area) C5 (Option A ruled out) Lateral upper arm, over the deltoid C6 (Option B) Lateral forearm and thumb(1st digit) C7 (Option C ruled out) Index finger (2nd digit) to Ring finger (4th digit) C8 (Option D ruled out) Medial forearm, little finger (5th digit) T1 Medial arm and elbow","sourceRef":"Question Pages 639-640; Solution Pages 652-653","sourcePage":639,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"anatomy-16-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 40-year-old male presents with difficulty extending his elbow after a recent injury. On examination, you notice weakness in elbow extension and diminished triceps reflex. Sensory examination is normal. Which myotome is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5"},{"letter":"B","text":"C6"},{"letter":"C","text":"C7"},{"letter":"D","text":"C8"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. C7","explanation":"The C7 myotome innervates the triceps muscle , which is responsible for elbow extension. Myotomes of the Upper Limb: Spinal Nerve Root Myotome (Motor Function) C5 (Option A ruled out) Shoulder abduction (deltoid), elbow flexion (biceps) C6 (Option B ruled out) Elbow flexion, wrist extension (biceps, wrist extensors) C7 (Option C) Elbow extension (triceps), wrist flexion (wrist flexors) C8 (Option D ruled out) Finger flexion, thumb extension (finger flexors, intrinsic hand muscles) T1 Finger abduction and adduction (intrinsic hand muscles, interossei)","sourceRef":"Question Page 640; Solution Page 653","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"anatomy-16-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 25-year-old male presents with weakness in the upper limb following a fall on his shoulder. Physical examination reveals impaired movement in the extensor muscles of the arm. Injury to which part of the brachial plexus is most likely responsible for this patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Upper trunk, anterior division"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle trunk, anterior division"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle trunk, posterior division"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lower trunk, anterior division"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Middle trunk, posterior division","explanation":"The posterior divisions of the brachial plexus innervate the extensor muscles of the upper limb . The middle trunk is a direct continuation of the C7 ventral ramus and its posterior division supplies the extensor muscles. Brachial Plexus Trunks: - Formation: Five ventral rami (C5–T1) form three trunks. Upper Trunk : Formed by C5 and C6. Middle Trunk : Continuation of C7. Lower Trunk : Formed by C8 and T1, crossing over the first rib. - Five ventral rami (C5–T1) form three trunks. - Upper Trunk : Formed by C5 and C6. - Middle Trunk : Continuation of C7. - Lower Trunk : Formed by C8 and T1, crossing over the first rib. - Location: Trunks emerge between anterior and middle scalene muscles. Positioned anteriorly to each other, with the subclavian artery running anteromedially. - Trunks emerge between anterior and middle scalene muscles. - Positioned anteriorly to each other, with the subclavian artery running anteromedially. - Divisions: Each trunk divides into: Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Each trunk divides into: Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. - Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Clinical Relevance: Trunks, especially the upper trunk, can be palpated in the supraclavicular fossa. Important for procedures like nerve blocks. - Trunks, especially the upper trunk, can be palpated in the supraclavicular fossa. - Important for procedures like nerve blocks. - Five ventral rami (C5–T1) form three trunks. - Upper Trunk : Formed by C5 and C6. - Middle Trunk : Continuation of C7. - Lower Trunk : Formed by C8 and T1, crossing over the first rib. - Trunks emerge between anterior and middle scalene muscles. - Positioned anteriorly to each other, with the subclavian artery running anteromedially. - Each trunk divides into: Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. - Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Posterior Divisions : Innervate extensor muscles. - Anterior Divisions : Innervate flexor muscles. - Trunks, especially the upper trunk, can be palpated in the supraclavicular fossa. - Important for procedures like nerve blocks. Brachial Plexus Trunk Formation and Divisions: Trunk Formation Divisions Upper Trunk Union of C5 and C6 rami Anterior: Flexor muscles (Option A ruled out) Posterior: Extensor muscles Middle Trunk Continuation of C7 Anterior: Flexor muscles (Option B ruled out) Posterior: Extensor muscles (Option C) Lower Trunk Union of C8 and T1 rami Anterior: Flexor muscles (Option A ruled out) Passing over first rib Posterior: Extensor muscles","sourceRef":"Question Page 640; Solution Pages 653-656","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":640},{"id":"anatomy-16-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 25-year-old male is brought to the emergency department after a high-speed motorcycle accident. He complains of severe pain and weakness in his right arm. On examination, there is loss of sensation in the right shoulder and upper arm with weakness in shoulder abduction and elbow flexion. Which of the following is the most likely type of brachial plexus lesion in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Intradural rupture"},{"letter":"B","text":"Compression injury"},{"letter":"C","text":"Closed traction injury"},{"letter":"D","text":"Iatrogenic injury"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Closed traction injury","explanation":"Closed traction injury commonly occurs due to violent forces, such as those experienced in high-speed accidents, which stretch or tear the nerves of the brachial plexus. The symptoms of pain, weakness, and sensory loss in this patient are consistent with this type of lesion. Brachial Plexus Lesions: - Damage or dysfunction in the brachial plexus, a nerve network from the spinal cord controlling shoulder, arm, and hand functions. - Causes of Brachial Plexus Lesions include: Trauma, birth trauma, tumors, radiation therapy Types of Lesions: Type Description Closed Traction Lesions (Option C) Result from forces that stretch or tear nerves, often in high-impact incidents like accidents. Intradural Ruptures/Avulsions (Option A ruled out) Nerve roots torn from the spinal cord; harder to treat than other injuries. Compression Injuries (Option B ruled out) Caused by pressure on the brachial plexus, including: Thoracic Outlet Syndrome (TOS): Compression between the clavicle and first rib. Hematoma: Internal bleeding compressing the plexus. Anomalous Muscles/Structures : Extra anatomical features causing compression. Iatrogenic Injury : Damage during medical procedures. (Option D ruled out) Symptoms: Symptom Description Sensory Changes Numbness, tingling, pain, or burning in the shoulder, arm, or hand. Motor Weakness Difficulty moving the shoulder, arm, or hand, with specific weakness patterns aiding localization. Autonomic Dysfunction Changes in sweating, skin temperature, or blood flow in the affected limb. Diagnosis: - Physical Exam: Assessing sensation, muscle strength, and reflexes. - Imaging Studies: X-rays, CT scans, or MRI to identify fractures, dislocations, or tumors. - Electrodiagnostic Tests: Nerve conduction studies (NCS) and electromyography (EMG) to assess nerve function and damage extent.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 640-641; Solution Pages 656-657","sourcePage":640,"sourcePageEnd":641},{"id":"anatomy-16-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 35-year-old male presents with weakness in elbow flexion and loss of sensation over the lateral aspect of his forearm. Which of the following nerves is most likely involved in this patient’s condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Musculocutaneous nerve","explanation":"The musculocutaneous nerve innervates the biceps brachii and brachialis muscles , responsible for elbow flexion. It also provides sensory innervation to the lateral aspect of the forearm. Brachial Plexus: - Brachial plexus : Nerve network for upper limb motor and sensory function. - Origins : From ventral rami of C5, C6, C7, C8, and T1 spinal nerves. - Structure : Roots → trunks → divisions → cords. - Function : Specific nerves innervate muscles and skin in the arm and hand. Brachial Plexus Cords: Cord Formation Lateral Cord Anterior divisions of upper and middle trunks Posterior Cord All three posterior divisions Medial Cord Anterior division of lower trunk (sometimes middle trunk) Innervations by Cord: Cord Nerve Motor Innervation Sensory Innervation Lateral Cord Musculocutaneous (C5, Biceps brachii, brachialis, Lateral forearm C6, C7) (Option B) coracobrachialis Lateral Root of Median Contributes to median Contributes to sensory (C5, C6, C7) nerve (primarily hand innervation of the hand muscles) Posterior Cord Axillary (C5, C6) (Option A Deltoid, teres minor Lateral shoulder ruled out) Radial (C5, C6, C7, C8, Triceps, brachioradialis, Posterior arm, forearm, T1) (Option C ruled out) wrist and finger extensors, dorsolateral hand supinator Upper Subscapular (C5, Subscapularis None C6) Thoracodorsal (C6, C7, C8) Latissimus dorsi None Lower Subscapular (C5, Subscapularis, teres major None C6) Medial Cord Ulnar (C8, T1) (Option D Flexor carpi ulnaris, flexor Medial hand, little finger, ruled out) digitorum profundus medial half of ring finger (medial half), most intrinsic hand muscles Median (C5, C6, C7, C8, Pronators, wrist/finger Palmar hand (thumb, T1) flexors (except those index, middle, lateral half supplied by ulnar), thenar of ring finger) muscles Medial Pectoral (C8, T1) Pectoralis major, pectoralis None minor Medial Cutaneous Nerve None Medial arm of Arm (C8, T1) Medial Cutaneous Nerve None Medial forearm of Forearm (C8, T1)","sourceRef":"Question Page 641; Solution Pages 657-659","sourcePage":641,"sourcePageEnd":641},{"id":"anatomy-16-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the variations in the prefixed and postfixed brachial plexus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prefixed brachial plexus is characterized by contributions from C5 to T2."},{"letter":"B","text":"T2 spinal nerve replaces the usual T1 contribution in postfixed brachial plexus."},{"letter":"C","text":"Prefixed brachial plexus has an increased contribution from C4 and decreased from T1."},{"letter":"D","text":"Postfixed brachial plexus is associated with an increased contribution from C3 and C4."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Prefixed brachial plexus has an increased contribution from C4 and decreased from T1.","explanation":"decreased from T1. - A prefixed brachial plexus has an increased contribution from C4 and decreased from T1 while a postfixed brachial plexus involves contributions from C6-T2, with diminished input from C5 and increased input from T2. - The variations are significant in clinical practice as they can affect the innervation patterns of the upper limb and may impact nerve injury presentations. Prefixed Postfixed Nerve root C4-C8 C6-T2 Shift Upward shift Downward shift C4 contribution Increased Nil or minimal C5 contribution Normal Reduced T1 contribution Reduced or nil Normal along with added T2 Risk No risk of compression High risk of compression (Thoracic outlet syndrome) Prefixed brachial plexus is characterized by contributions from C5 to T2 (Option A) is incorrect as in a prefixed brachial plexus, the contribution shifts upward , involving C4-C8 , not C5 to T2. There is minimal or no input from T1 in a prefixed plexus. T2 spinal nerve replaces the usual T1 contribution in postfixed brachial plexus (Option B) is incorrect as in a postfixed brachial plexus, the contribution extends downward and typically includes C6-T2 , but T2 does not replace T1. Instead, T1 remains part of the plexus, and T2 provides an additional contribution. Postfixed brachial plexus is associated with an increased contribution from C3 and C4 (Option D) is incorrect as a postfixed brachial plexus involves a downward shift , resulting in contributions from C6-T2 . There is no involvement of C3 and C4, which are part of the cervical plexus, not the brachial plexus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 641; Solution Pages 659-660","sourcePage":641,"sourcePageEnd":641},{"id":"anatomy-16-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which part of the brachial plexus does not give off any branches?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Roots"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trunks"},{"letter":"C","text":"Divisions"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cords"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Divisions","explanation":"The divisions of the brachial plexus (anterior and posterior) act as a passage for nerve fibers to reorganize and form cords but they themselves do not give rise to any branches. Part of brachial plexus Branches Roots Dorsal scapular nerve - C5 Long thoracic nerve - C5 to C7 Trunks Suprascapular nerve - C5 , C6 Nerve to subclavius - C5 , C6 Division None Cords Lateral pectoral nerve (Lateral cord: C5 - C7 ) Upper subscapular nerve (Posterior cord: C5, C6 ) Thoracodorsal nerve (Posterior cord: C6 - C8 ) Lower subscapular nerve (Posterior cord: C5 , C6 ) Medial pectoral nerve (Medial cord: C8 , T1 ) Medial cutaneous nerve of arm (Medial cord: C8 , T1 ) Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm (Medial cord: C8 , T1 ) Terminal branches Musculocutaneous nerve (Lateral cord, C5-C7) Axillary nerve (Posterior cord, C5, C6) Radial nerve (Posterior cord, C5-T1) Median nerve (Lateral and Medial cords, C5-T1) Ulnar nerve (Medial cord, C8, T1)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 641-642; Solution Pages 660-661","sourcePage":641,"sourcePageEnd":642},{"id":"anatomy-16-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-16","chapter":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topic":"Lesson 16 - Upper Limb Embryology and Nerves","topicIndex":16,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the anterior and posterior axial lines is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Axial lines form as a result of limb rotational and elongation processes during embryogenesis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axial lines divide dermatomes that are adjacent in the spinal nerve sequence"},{"letter":"C","text":"Overlapping of nerve supply can overlap across axial lines"},{"letter":"D","text":"Overlapping of Ventral and dorsal axial lines can occur"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Axial lines form as a result of limb rotational and elongation processes during embryogenesis","explanation":"embryogenesis Axial lines are the dividing lines between dermatomes that are not adjacent in the spinal nerve sequence which are formed as a result of limb rotational and elongation processes during embryogenesis. Axial lines Axial lines are the dividing lines between dermatomes that are not adjacent in the spinal nerve sequence. Types Ventral Axial Line (on the anterior surface of the limb). Dorsal Axial Line (on the posterior surface of the limb). Formation During embryological development, the limbs undergo lateral rotation (upper limb) or medial rotation (lower limb). This rotation creates a spiral arrangement of dermatomes and shifts certain dermatomes distally. Axial lines form as a result of these rotational and elongation processes. Significance Axial lines are important for understanding nerve injuries and mapping sensory deficits. Across these lines, there is no overlap between dermatomes, so sensory loss in these areas can help pinpoint the exact nerve or spinal segment involved. Axial lines divide dermatomes that are adjacent in the spinal nerve sequence (Option B): This is incorrect as axial lines do not divide adjacent dermatomes. Instead, they divide dermatomes from non-adjacent spinal nerve roots . Adjacent dermatomes (e.g., C5 and C6) overlap significantly, and no axial line exists between them. For example, the ventral axial line separates C5, C6 from C8, T1, and T2, which are non-adjacent segments. Overlapping of nerve supply can overlap across axial lines (Option C): This is incorrect as one of the defining features of axial lines is that there is no overlap of dermatomes across them. While overlapping of adjacent dermatomes is common elsewhere (e.g., C5 overlaps with C6), this does not happen across axial lines. Overlapping of ventral and dorsal axial lines can occur (Option D): This is incorrect as the ventral and dorsal axial lines are distinct anatomical landmarks and do not overlap. They are located on opposite surfaces of the limb: - The ventral axial line is on the anterior ( palmar ) surface of the limb. - The dorsal axial line is on the posterior (dorsal) surface of the limb.","sourceRef":"Question Page 642; Solution Pages 661-663","sourcePage":642,"sourcePageEnd":642},{"id":"anatomy-17-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a content of the cubital fossa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Brachial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Radial nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Cubital Fossa: - Triangular space present in front of distal humerus and elbow joint. - Boundaries: Medially Lateral border of pronator teres Laterally Medial border of brachioradialis Apex Point of convergence of medial and lateral boundaries Base Imaginary line connecting the medial and lateral epicondyles of humerus. Roof Formed by deep fascia and reinforced by bicipital aponeurosis.The deep fascia is continuity of brachial and antebrachial fascia. Floor Lower portion of brachialis forms the floor of the upper part of the fossa; the supinator muscle forms the floor of the lower part of the fossa. - Contents of cubital fossa: Median nerve: Lies medial to the brachial artery in the fossa and is anterior to brachioradialis and posterior to bicipital aponeurosis. Radial nerve: Enters the cubital region by passing forwards in interval between the brachialis and brachioradialis and then divides into superficial branch and deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve). Brachial artery: Centrally placed in fossa and divides into radial and ulnar arteries with immediate anterior relation is bicipital aponeurosis. Tendon of the biceps brachii and bicipital aponeurosis - Median nerve: Lies medial to the brachial artery in the fossa and is anterior to brachioradialis and posterior to bicipital aponeurosis. - Radial nerve: Enters the cubital region by passing forwards in interval between the brachialis and brachioradialis and then divides into superficial branch and deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve). - Brachial artery: Centrally placed in fossa and divides into radial and ulnar arteries with immediate anterior relation is bicipital aponeurosis. - Tendon of the biceps brachii and bicipital aponeurosis - Contents of cubital fossa from medial to lateral: (Mnemonic: MBBS) Median nerve Brachial artery Biceps brachii tendon Superficial branch of radial nerve - Median nerve - Brachial artery - Biceps brachii tendon - Superficial branch of radial nerve - Median nerve: Lies medial to the brachial artery in the fossa and is anterior to brachioradialis and posterior to bicipital aponeurosis. - Radial nerve: Enters the cubital region by passing forwards in interval between the brachialis and brachioradialis and then divides into superficial branch and deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve). - Brachial artery: Centrally placed in fossa and divides into radial and ulnar arteries with immediate anterior relation is bicipital aponeurosis. - Tendon of the biceps brachii and bicipital aponeurosis - Median nerve - Brachial artery - Biceps brachii tendon - Superficial branch of radial nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 664; Solution Pages 673-675","sourcePage":664,"sourcePageEnd":664},{"id":"anatomy-17-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following is a unique feature of the clavicle compared to other bones in the body?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It ossifies from both membranous and cartilaginous origins."},{"letter":"B","text":"It articulates directly with the scapula without an intervening joint cavity."},{"letter":"C","text":"It is the only bone in the body that does not have a medullary cavity."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is entirely covered by the periosteum, unlike other bones."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. It ossifies from both membranous and cartilaginous origins.","explanation":"- The clavicle is unique among bones in the human body due to its dual ossification process: - The medial and lateral parts of the clavicle develop through intramembranous ossification. - This process is typical for flat bones like the skull. - The sternal end of the clavicle ossifies through endochondral ossification, which involves a cartilage template being replaced by bone. - This process is more common for long bones like the femur and humerus. Features of the Clavicle: - Horizontally placed long bone (unique among long bones). - Two primary centers of ossification develop during intrauterine life to form the shaft (most long bones have only one primary center). - One secondary ossification center - Subcutaneous throughout and easily palpable. - Lacks a medullary cavity , unlike most long bones. Parts of the Clavicle: - Medial 2/3 : Anterior convexity. - Lateral 1/3 : Anterior concavity. - Junction of these segments is prone to fractures. Muscle Attachments: - Medial 2/3 (Anterior) : Origin of Pectoralis Major . - Lateral 1/3 (Anterior) : Origin of Deltoid . - Lateral 1/3 (Posterior) : Insertion of Trapezius . The clavicle articulates with the scapula at the acromioclavicular joint , which does have a joint cavity. (Option B) While it is true that the clavicle lacks a medullary cavity, it is not the only bone with this feature (Flat bones like the sternum and skull bones). (Option C) All bones in the body are covered by periosteum except at articular surfaces. This characteristic is not unique to the clavicle. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 664; Solution Pages 675-676","sourcePage":664,"sourcePageEnd":664},{"id":"anatomy-17-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following is not a content in the 4th compartment of extensor retinaculum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor digitorum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extensor indicis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Extensor pollicis brevis","explanation":"Extensor pollicis brevis is in the 1st compartment of the extensor retinaculum. Extensor Retinaculum - Thickened deep fascia on the back of the wrist Compartment of Extensor Retinaculum Structure(s) I Abductor pollicis longus Extensor pollicis brevis II Extensor carpi radialis longus Extensor carpi radialis brevis III Extensor pollicis longus IV Extensor digitorum Extensor indicis Posterior interosseous nerve Anterior interosseous artery V Extensor digiti minimi VI Extensor carpi ulnaris","sourceRef":"Question Pages 664-665; Solution Pages 676-678","sourcePage":664,"sourcePageEnd":665},{"id":"anatomy-17-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":4,"question":"After a fall from a height with undue arm abduction, the patient presented as shown. Mark the wrong statement about this condition.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C7,C8 & T1 are affected"},{"letter":"B","text":"Complete clawing of hand is seen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Associated horner syndrome can be seen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Due to injury to lower trunk of brachial plexus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. C7,C8 & T1 are affected","explanation":"Given clinical scenario is suggestive of Klumpke’s paralysis secondary to fall with abduction of arm causing injury to C8 and T1 nerve roots resulting in complete clawing of hand . Erb’s Paralysis & Klumpke’s Paralysis: Feature Erb’s Paralysis Klumpke’s Paralysis Site of Injury Upper trunk of the brachial plexus Lower trunk of the brachial plexus (Erb’s point) (Option D) Causes of Injury Undue separation of head from the Undue abduction of the arm Birth shoulder Birth injury/difficult childbirth injury During anesthesia Nerve Roots Involved Mainly C5, partly C6 Mainly T1, partly C8 (Option A) Muscles Paralyzed Biceps brachii Deltoid Brachialis Intrinsic muscles of the hand (T1) Brachioradialis Partly supraspinatus, Ulnar flexors of the wrist and fingers infraspinatus, supinator (C8) Deformity and Position Arm: Hangs by the side, adducted Complete clawing of hand due to and medially rotated Forearm: hyperextension at the Extended and pronated (Waiter’s tip metacarpophalangeal joints and position) Also known as Policeman flexion at the interphalangeal joints. tip deformity (Option B) Additional Features Sensory loss over lower deltoid. Bird Horner’s syndrome (Option C) if T1 is winging effect of scapula. injured. Vasomotor and trophic changes Cutaneous anesthesia along the ulnar border of the forearm and hand.","sourceRef":"Question Page 665; Solution Pages 678-679","sourcePage":665,"sourcePageEnd":665},{"id":"anatomy-17-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following statements about the carrying angle of the elbow is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The carrying angle is formed by the angle between the humerus and the ulna when the elbow is flexed."},{"letter":"B","text":"The carrying angle disappears during full flexion and pronation of the forearm."},{"letter":"C","text":"The carrying angle is greater in males, usually more than 15°."},{"letter":"D","text":"The carrying angle increases as the arm moves from full flexion to extension."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The carrying angle disappears during full flexion and pronation of the forearm.","explanation":"forearm. - The carrying angle of the elbow is the angle formed between the humerus and the forearm when the arm is fully extended. - This angle, typically 13° , is due to the medially and downwardly directed transverse axis of the elbow joint. - The factors that contribute to this angle include: Medial flange of the trochlea being 6 mm deeper than the lateral flange. Oblique placement of the superior articular surface of the coronoid process of the ulna. - Medial flange of the trochlea being 6 mm deeper than the lateral flange. - Oblique placement of the superior articular surface of the coronoid process of the ulna. - The carrying angle disappears during full flexion and pronation of the forearm, with the forearm aligning with the arm in the mid-prone position. - This allows better precision in hand control. The angle is typically 10°–15° in males and greater than 15° in females - Cubitus valgus is characterized by an increased carrying angle, causing the forearm to deviate outward , while cubitus varus involves a decreased carrying angle, causing the forearm to angle inward. - Medial flange of the trochlea being 6 mm deeper than the lateral flange. - Oblique placement of the superior articular surface of the coronoid process of the ulna.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 665-666; Solution Page 679","sourcePage":665,"sourcePageEnd":666},{"id":"anatomy-17-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following structures can be palpated in the infraclavicular fossa ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Subclavian vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Biceps tendon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Coracoid process"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thoracoacromial artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Coracoid process","explanation":"- The infraclavicular fossa , also known as the deltopectoral triangle , is a triangular depression located below the junction of the lateral and middle thirds of the clavicle. - It is defined by the following boundaries: Medially : The pectoralis major muscle. Laterally : The anterior fibers of the deltoid muscle. Superiorly : The clavicle . - Medially : The pectoralis major muscle. - Laterally : The anterior fibers of the deltoid muscle. - Superiorly : The clavicle . - The tip of the coracoid process of the scapula lies approximately 2–3 cm below the clavicle and is overlapped by the anterior fibers of the deltoid. - It can be felt during deep palpation just lateral to the infraclavicular fossa , which helps in locating important structures like the brachial plexus and subclavian artery in clinical assessments. - This area is clinically significant for procedures such as brachial plexus block and the assessment of vascular structures like the subclavian artery . - Medially : The pectoralis major muscle. - Laterally : The anterior fibers of the deltoid muscle. - Superiorly : The clavicle .","sourceRef":"Question Page 666; Solution Pages 679-681","sourcePage":666,"sourcePageEnd":666},{"id":"anatomy-17-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Identify the correctly marked attachments of the Upper end of the Humerus. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Supraspinatus; B- Infraspinatus; D- Pectoralis major"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Teres minor; B- Infraspinatus; D- Subscapularis"},{"letter":"C","text":"C- Pectoralis major; D- Supraspinatus; E- Subscapularis"},{"letter":"D","text":"B- Infraspinatus; C- Pectoralis major; D- Supraspinatus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A- Supraspinatus; B- Infraspinatus; D- Pectoralis major","explanation":"Upper end of Humerus: - Head: The smooth, rounded head forms one-third of a sphere, covered with articular hyaline cartilage. - Anatomical Neck: This constriction at the margins of the rounded head provides attachment to the capsular ligament of the shoulder joint , except at the superior region where the capsule is deficient for the tendon of the long head of the biceps brachii. - Surgical Neck: A short constriction below the tubercles and epiphyseal line, where fractures are common. It is in close proximity to the axillary nerve and posterior and anterior circumflex humeral vessels. - It is in close proximity to the axillary nerve and posterior and anterior circumflex humeral vessels. - It is in close proximity to the axillary nerve and posterior and anterior circumflex humeral vessels. - Greater Tubercle: The most lateral part of the upper end, with three flattened facets: Upper facet: Attachment for supraspinatus. Middle facet: Attachment for infraspinatus. Lower facet: Attachment for teres minor. Mnemonic : \"SIT\" - Upper facet: Attachment for supraspinatus. - Middle facet: Attachment for infraspinatus. - Lower facet: Attachment for teres minor. - Mnemonic : \"SIT\" - Upper facet: Attachment for supraspinatus. - Middle facet: Attachment for infraspinatus. - Lower facet: Attachment for teres minor. - Mnemonic : \"SIT\" - Lesser Tubercle: A small elevation on the front of the upper end, just above the surgical neck, providing attachment for the subscapularis muscle . - Intertubercular Sulcus (Bicipital Groove): A groove between the tubercles that contains: Long head of biceps (enclosed in a synovial sheath). Ascending branch of anterior circumflex humeral artery. It provides attachment to three muscles: Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . Teres major (medial lip). Latissimus dorsi (floor). Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major). - Long head of biceps (enclosed in a synovial sheath). - Ascending branch of anterior circumflex humeral artery. - It provides attachment to three muscles: Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . Teres major (medial lip). Latissimus dorsi (floor). Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major). - Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . - Teres major (medial lip). - Latissimus dorsi (floor). - Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major). - Long head of biceps (enclosed in a synovial sheath). - Ascending branch of anterior circumflex humeral artery. - It provides attachment to three muscles: Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . Teres major (medial lip). Latissimus dorsi (floor). Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major). - Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . - Teres major (medial lip). - Latissimus dorsi (floor). - Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major). - Pectoralis major (lateral lip) . - Teres major (medial lip). - Latissimus dorsi (floor). - Mnemonic : \"Lady between 2 Majors\" (Latissimus dorsi between Pectoralis major and Teres major).","sourceRef":"Question Page 666; Solution Pages 681-682","sourcePage":666,"sourcePageEnd":666},{"id":"anatomy-17-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the attachments on the coracoid process?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The short head of biceps brachii and coracobrachialis insert onto its medial border."},{"letter":"B","text":"The conoid ligament attaches to the tip of the coracoid process."},{"letter":"C","text":"The pectoralis minor muscle inserts onto the medial border of the upper surface."},{"letter":"D","text":"The coracoacromial ligament attaches to the superior ridge of the coracoid process."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The pectoralis minor muscle inserts onto the medial border of the upper surface.","explanation":"surface. Coracoid Process Attachments: - Muscle Attachments : Short head of biceps brachii and coracobrachialis arise from the tip via a common tendon. (Option A) Pectoralis minor inserts on the medial border of the upper surface . - Short head of biceps brachii and coracobrachialis arise from the tip via a common tendon. (Option A) - Pectoralis minor inserts on the medial border of the upper surface . - Ligament Attachments : Coracoacromial ligament attaches to the lateral border . (Option D) Conoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the knuckle . (Option B) Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the ridge on the superior aspect. Coracohumeral ligament attaches at the root , near the glenoid cavity. - Coracoacromial ligament attaches to the lateral border . (Option D) - Conoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the knuckle . (Option B) - Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the ridge on the superior aspect. - Coracohumeral ligament attaches at the root , near the glenoid cavity. - Short head of biceps brachii and coracobrachialis arise from the tip via a common tendon. (Option A) - Pectoralis minor inserts on the medial border of the upper surface . - Coracoacromial ligament attaches to the lateral border . (Option D) - Conoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the knuckle . (Option B) - Trapezoid ligament (part of coracoclavicular) attaches to the ridge on the superior aspect. - Coracohumeral ligament attaches at the root , near the glenoid cavity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 666-667; Solution Pages 682-684","sourcePage":666,"sourcePageEnd":667},{"id":"anatomy-17-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following bones is not directly involved in the radiocarpal (wrist) joint?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lunate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pisiform"},{"letter":"D","text":"Triquetrum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pisiform","explanation":"Pisiform is a sesamoid bone located in the proximal row of the carpal bones but does not participate directly in the radiocarpal articulation. Wrist joint Synovial ellipsoidal (condyloid) joint allowing flexion, extension, abduction, adduction, and circumduction. Articulating Bones The wrist joint involves the distal end of the radius and the proximal row of carpal bones (scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum). The ulna is not directly involved in the wrist joint but is adjacent to it, separated by an articular disc (the triangular fibrocartilage). Carpal Bones Involved Scaphoid, lunate, and triquetrum are directly involved in the articulation with the radius. The pisiform is not involved in the wrist joint. Joint Capsule A fibrous joint capsule encloses the wrist joint, which is lined by a synovial membrane that produces synovial fluid for lubrication. Ligaments Palmar radiocarpal ligament Dorsal radiocarpal ligament Ulnar collateral ligament Radial collateral ligament Movements Flexion Extension Radial deviation (abduction) Ulnar deviation (adduction) Circumduction Blood Supply The wrist joint is supplied by branches of the radial and ulnar arteries. Nerve Supply Median nerve Ulnar nerve Radial nerve Clinical Relevance Common conditions affecting the wrist joint include wrist fractures (especially of the scaphoid and radius), carpal tunnel syndrome, and arthritis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 667; Solution Pages 684-685","sourcePage":667,"sourcePageEnd":667},{"id":"anatomy-17-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":10,"question":"The muscles in the posterior aspect of the forearm are given below. Which among these is involved in the extension of the elbow joint?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anconeus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Brachioradialis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supinator"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extensor digitorum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anconeus","explanation":"Posterior forearm Origin Insertion Nerve Supply Actions muscles Anconeus (Option A) Lateral epicondyle of Lateral surface of Radial nerve Extends elbow joint humerus olecranon process of ulna Brachioradialis Upper two-thirds of Base of styloid Radial nerve Flexes forearm at (Option B) lateral supracondylar process of radius elbow ; rotates ridge of humerus forearm to midprone position Extensor carpi Lower one-third of Posterior surface of Radial nerve Extends and radialis longus lateral supracondylar base of second abducts hand at wrist ridge of humerus metacarpal bone Extensor carpi Lateral epicondyle of Posterior surface of Deep branch of Extends and radialis brevis humerus base of third radial nerve abducts hand at wrist metacarpal Extensor digitorum Lateral epicondyle of Bases of middle Deep branch of Extends fingers of (Option D) humerus phalanges of the radial nerve hand 2nd–5th digits Extensor digiti minimi Lateral epicondyle of Extensor expansion Deep branch of Extends metacarpop humerus of little finger radial nerve halangeal joint of little finger Extensor carpi Lateral epicondyle of Base of fifth Deep branch of Extends and ulnaris humerus metacarpal bone radial nerve adducts hand at wrist Supinator (Option C) Lateral epicondyle of Neck and upper third Deep branch of Supinates forearm humerus, annular of radius radial nerve when elbow is ligament of superior extended radioulnar joint, supinator crest of ulna Abductor pollicis Posterior surface of Base of first Deep branch of Abducts and longus shafts of radius and metacarpal bone radial nerve extends thumb ulna Extensor pollicis Posterior surface of Base of proximal Deep branch of Extends metacarpop brevis shaft of radius phalanx of thumb radial nerve halangeal joint of thumb Extensor pollicis Posterior surface of Base of distal Deep branch of Extends distal longus shaft of ulna phalanx of thumb radial nerve phalanx of thumb Extensor indicis Posterior surface of Extensor expansion Deep branch of Extends metacarpop shaft of ulna of index finger radial nerve halangeal joint of index finger","sourceRef":"Question Page 667; Solution Pages 685-687","sourcePage":667,"sourcePageEnd":667},{"id":"anatomy-17-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 28-year-old woman falls off her bicycle and lands on her outstretched hand. She presents with pain and swelling over the lateral aspect of her wrist. On examination, there is tenderness in the anatomical snuff box. Which underlying bone is most likely fractured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trapezium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lunate"},{"letter":"D","text":"Capitate"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Scaphoid","explanation":"Tenderness in the anatomical snuff box following a fall on an outstretched hand strongly suggests a scaphoid fracture , as the scaphoid bone lies in the floor of the anatomical snuff box. Anatomical snuff box/Radial fossa: - Triangular hollow visible on the lateral aspect of the wrist when the thumb is fully extended. - Boundaries: Anterolateral: Extensor pollicis brevis, Abductor pollicis longus Posteromedial: Extensor pollicis longus Roof: Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Anterolateral: Extensor pollicis brevis, Abductor pollicis longus - Posteromedial: Extensor pollicis longus - Roof: Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. - Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Anterolateral: Extensor pollicis brevis, Abductor pollicis longus - Posteromedial: Extensor pollicis longus - Roof: Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. - Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Dorsal cutaneous branch of radial nerve: Found in the skin and subcutaneous tissue of anatomical snuff box. - Cephalic vein: Arises from dorsal venous network of hand and crosses anatomical snuff box to travel to the anterolateral aspect of forearm. - Contents: Radial Artery: Crosses the floor of the anatomical snuff box. - Radial Artery: Crosses the floor of the anatomical snuff box. - Radial Artery: Crosses the floor of the anatomical snuff box.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 667-668; Solution Pages 687-689","sourcePage":667,"sourcePageEnd":668},{"id":"anatomy-17-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Choose the correct statement regarding the muscle being tested in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Unipennate muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Muscles of 1st and 2nd digits are supplied by median nerve and 3rd & 4th digit muscles are supplied by ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Causes flexion of interphalangeal and extension of metacarpophalangeal joints"},{"letter":"D","text":"Absent in thumb"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Absent in thumb","explanation":"- Test shown in the image is done to test Dorsal interossei , where the patient is asked to abduct the fingers against resistance. - Dorsal interossei are bipennate muscles , supplied by deep branch of the ulnar nerve. Palmar Interossei Dorsal Interossei Arrangement Unipennate (Option A) Bipennate Action Adduction of fingers (PAd) Abduction of fingers (DAb) Nerve supply Deep branch of ulnar nerve Absent in Middle finger Thumb and little finger (Option D) Number of muscles 4 in no. 4 in no. (middle finger has 2) Clinical test Card test (adduction of digits) Egawa test (abduction of digits) Lumbricals: Action Flexion at metacarpophalangeal joint and extension at interphalangeal joint (Option C) Nerve supply 1st & 2nd: Median nerve (Option B) 3rd & 4th: Ulnar nerve Arrangement 1st & 2nd: Unipennate 3rd & 4th: Bipennate Other points Also known as Linker muscles as they connect the flexor compartment of muscles with the extensor muscles. Insertion: via extensor expansion into dorsum of base of distal phalanx","sourceRef":"Question Page 668; Solution Pages 689-690","sourcePage":668,"sourcePageEnd":668},{"id":"anatomy-17-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Given below is an image showing insertion of a muscle (marked in red) on the palmar aspect of the hand. Identify the correct pair of muscles and its corresponding nerve supply. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Opponens digiti minimi - radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Opponens digiti minimi - ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abductor digiti minimi - median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abductor digiti minimi - ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Opponens digiti minimi - ulnar nerve","explanation":"- Muscle shown in the image above is Opponens digiti minimi , present in hypothenar compartment. - All the hypothenar muscles are innervated by deep branch of the Ulnar nerve. Muscles of Hypothenar Eminence: Muscle Attachments Nerve supply Action Abductor digiti minimi Origin: Pisiform Insertion: Deep branch of Ulnar nerve Abduction of little finger Base of proximal phalanx of little finger Flexor digiti minimi Origin: Flexor retinaculum Flexion of little finger Insertion: Base of proximal phalanx of little finger Opponens digiti minimi Origin: Flexor retinaculum Pulls 5th metacarpal Insertion: Medial border of forward fifth metacarpal bone","sourceRef":"Question Pages 668-669; Solution Pages 690-691","sourcePage":668,"sourcePageEnd":669},{"id":"anatomy-17-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Patient following RTA was brought to ED, where he was told that his ulnar nerve is injured. Which of the following muscles would be affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4"},{"letter":"B","text":"3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2"},{"letter":"D","text":"1"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 4","explanation":"- Image shows the thenar muscles of the hand . Only thenar muscle of the hand supplied by the ulnar nerve is Adductor pollicis (Option A) , which would be affected in this scenario. - Rest of the muscles of thenar eminence are supplied by the median nerve. Muscles of Thenar Eminence: Muscle Attachments Nerve supply Action Abductor pollicis brevis Origin: Scaphoid tubercle, Median nerve Abduction of thumb (Option C) crest of Trapezium and flexor retinaculum Insertion: Base of proximal phalanx of thumb Flexor pollicis brevis Origin: Capitate, crest of Median nerve Flexion of metacarpo-phala (Option B) Trapezium and flexor ngeal joint of thumb retinaculum Insertion: Base of proximal phalanx of thumb Opponens pollicis (Option Origin: crest of Trapezium Median nerve Opposition of thumb D) and flexor retinaculum towards fingers Insertion: Lateral half of palmar surface of the shaft of thumb metacarpal Adductor pollicis (Option A) Origin: Oblique head- base Ulnar nerve Adduction of thumb of 2nd-3rd metacarpal ; transverse head- shaft of 3rd metacarpal Insertion: Base of proximal phalanx of thumb (medial aspect) Clinical Tests: - Abductor pollicis brevis: Pen test is done. Patient is asked to abduct the thumb. Patients will be unable to do so in case of median nerve injury - Adductor pollicis: Froment's sign , also known as the book test , evaluates the function of the adductor pollicis muscle (supplied by ulnar nerve). When a patient is asked to firmly grip a book between the thumbs and fingers of both hands, the thumb on the affected side flexes at the interphalangeal joint ( flexor pollicis longus , innervated by the median nerve ).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 669-670; Solution Pages 691-694","sourcePage":669,"sourcePageEnd":670},{"id":"anatomy-17-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":15,"question":"All are true about the shown muscle except.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supplied by musculocutaneous nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Causes flexion of arm at shoulder joint"},{"letter":"C","text":"Paralyzed in Erb’s palsy"},{"letter":"D","text":"Attached to medial coracoid process"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Paralyzed in Erb’s palsy","explanation":"Shown muscle is Coracobrachialis , which may not be paralyzed in Erb’s palsy as all other options are correct. Coracobrachialis: - Origin: Medial coracoid process - Insertion: Medial aspect of middle humerus - Nerve supply: Musculocutaneous nerve (C5-C7) - Action: Flexion (+adduction) of arm at shoulder joint - May not be paralyzed in Erb’s palsy, as in case of Erb’s palsy: C5,C6 are compromised but C7 remains unaffected and hence Coracobrachialis is not paralyzed . (Option C) Muscles of Arm: Muscle Attachments Nerve supply Action Biceps brachii Origin: Short head- Musculocutaneous nerve Flexion and supination of Coracoid process ; Long (C5-C6) forearm head- Supragelnoid tubercle Insertion: Radial tuberosity Brachialis Origin: anterior humerus Motor: Musculocutaneous Flexion of forearm Insertion: coronoid process nerve Proprioceptive: & ulnar tuberosity Radial nerve Coraco- brachialis Origin: medial coracoid Musculocutaneous nerve Flexion of arm (Option B) process (Option D) (C5-C7) (Option A) Insertion: medial aspect of middle humerus","sourceRef":"Question Pages 670-671; Solution Pages 694-696","sourcePage":670,"sourcePageEnd":671},{"id":"anatomy-17-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":16,"question":"In which sequence do the following carpal bones typically ossify: capitate, pisiform, scaphoid?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capitate → Scaphoid → Pisiform"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pisiform → Capitate → Scaphoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Scaphoid → Capitate → Pisiform"},{"letter":"D","text":"Capitate → Pisiform → Scaphoid"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Capitate → Scaphoid → Pisiform","explanation":"- Carpal bones are 8 short bones arranged in 2 rows, each with 4 bones in the region of the wrist. - From lateral to medial, bones of the proximal row are the scaphoid, lunate, triquetral, and pisiform, and those of the distal row are the trapezium, trapezoid, capitate, and hamate. Carpal Bones Ossification Age Capitate (First one to ossify) 1-3 Months Hamate 2-4 Months Triquetrum During 3rd year of life Lunate During 4th year of life Scaphoid During 5th year of life Trapezium During 6th year of life Trapezoid During 6-7th year of life Pisiform (Last one to ossify) During 9-12th year of life","sourceRef":"Question Page 671; Solution Pages 696-697","sourcePage":671,"sourcePageEnd":671},{"id":"anatomy-17-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following muscles is responsible for flexing the distal interphalangeal joints of the fingers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum superficialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pronator quadratus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor pollicis longus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Flexor digitorum profundus","explanation":"The flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) is responsible for flexing the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the 2nd to 5th fingers. - The muscles of the anterior compartment of forearm are the flexor-pronator muscles and arranged in 3 layers: Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis Deep layer: - Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis - Flexor digitorum superficialis - Deep layer: - Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis - Flexor digitorum superficialis - Deep layer: - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Flexor digitorum superficialis 1. Flexor digitorum profundus: Origin Proximal three-quarters of the anterior and medial surfaces of the ulna and the interosseous membrane. Insertion Bases of the distal phalanges of the 2nd to 5th digits. Action Flexes the distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints of the 2nd to 5th fingers and flexion of wrist. Nerve Supply Medial part by ulnar nerve and lateral part by median nerve (C8, T1) 2. Flexor Pollicis Longus: Origin Anterior surface of the radius and adjacent interosseous membrane. Insertion Base of the distal phalanx of the thumb. Action Flexion of phalanges of thumb Nerve Supply Median nerve (anterior interosseous branch) (C8, T1). 3. Pronator Quadratus: Origin Distal one-quarter of the anterior surface of the ulna. Insertion Distal one-quarter of the anterior surface of the radius. Action Pronation of the forearm (pulls the radius over the ulna). Nerve Supply Median nerve (anterior interosseous branch) (C8, T1).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 671-672; Solution Pages 697-699","sourcePage":671,"sourcePageEnd":672},{"id":"anatomy-17-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-17","chapter":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topic":"Lesson 17 - Muscles and Bones of Forearm and Hand","topicIndex":17,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 35-year-old carpenter presents with pain and weakness in his right forearm after repetitive hammering. On examination, you note tenderness over the medial epicondyle and weakness in wrist flexion. There is no sensory loss. Based on these findings, which muscle is not likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor carpi ulnaris"},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palmaris longus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Flexor pollicis longus","explanation":"Flexor pollicis longus is a deep muscle of the anterior compartment of forearm which helps in flexion of distal phalanges of the thumb and no action in flexion of wrist. - The muscles of the anterior compartment of forearm are the flexor-pronator muscles and arranged in 3 layers: Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis Deep layer: Flexor digitorum profundus Flexor pollicis longus Pronator quadratus - Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis - Flexor digitorum superficialis - Deep layer: Flexor digitorum profundus Flexor pollicis longus Pronator quadratus - Flexor digitorum profundus - Flexor pollicis longus - Pronator quadratus - Superficial layer: Pronator teres Flexor carpi radialis Flexor carpi ulnaris Palmaris longus - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Intermediate layer: Flexor digitorum superficialis - Flexor digitorum superficialis - Deep layer: Flexor digitorum profundus Flexor pollicis longus Pronator quadratus - Flexor digitorum profundus - Flexor pollicis longus - Pronator quadratus - Pronator teres - Flexor carpi radialis - Flexor carpi ulnaris - Palmaris longus - Flexor digitorum superficialis - Flexor digitorum profundus - Flexor pollicis longus - Pronator quadratus Superficial muscles of anterior compartment of forearm consists of 5 muscles: 1. Pronator teres: Origin Humeral head- Medial epicondyle of the humerus (via the common flexor tendon) Ulnar head - Coronoid process of the ulna Insertion Lateral surface of the midshaft of the radius. Action Pronates the forearm and weak flexor of elbow. Nerve Supply Median nerve (C6, C7). 2. Flexor Carpi Radialis (FCR): Origin Medial epicondyle of the humerus (via the common flexor tendon). Insertion Base of the second and third metacarpal bones. Action Flexes and abducts the wrist (radial deviation). Nerve Supply Median nerve (C6, C7). 3. Palmaris Longus: Origin Medial epicondyle of the humerus (via the common flexor tendon). Insertion Flexor retinaculum and palmar aponeurosis. Action Flexion of hand at wrist makes palmar aponeurosis tense. Nerve Supply Median nerve (C7, C8). 4. Flexor Carpi Ulnaris: Origin Humeral head- Medial epicondyle of the humerus (via the common flexor tendon) Ulnar head- Olecranon and posterior border of the ulna. Insertion Pisiform, hook of hamate, and base of the fifth metacarpal. Action Flexes and adducts the wrist Nerve Supply Ulnar nerve (C7, C8). 5. Flexor Digitorum Superficialis: Origin Humeroulnar head- Medial epicondyle of the humerus and coronoid process of the ulna Radial head- Oblique line of the radius Insertion Middle phalanges of the 2nd to 5th digits. Action Flexion of middle and proximal phalanges of digits. Nerve Supply Median nerve (C7, C8, T1)","sourceRef":"Question Page 672; Solution Pages 699-702","sourcePage":672,"sourcePageEnd":672},{"id":"anatomy-18-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A surgeon is performing an axillary dissection for breast cancer and must be cautious not to damage structures piercing the clavipectoral fascia. Which of the following statements about these structures is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The cephalic vein pierces the clavipectoral fascia and runs through the deltopectoral groove before draining into the axillary vein."},{"letter":"B","text":"The thoracoacromial artery pierces the clavipectoral fascia and branches into pectoral, deltoid, axillary, and clavicular branches."},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral pectoral nerve passes through the clavipectoral fascia and innervates the pectoralis major muscle without piercing the pectoralis minor."},{"letter":"D","text":"Lymphatic vessels draining the lateral breast region pierce the clavipectoral fascia and drain into the central axillary lymph nodes."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The cephalic vein pierces the clavipectoral fascia and runs through the deltopectoral groove before draining into the axillary vein.","explanation":"deltopectoral groove before draining into the axillary vein. Clavipectoral fascia: - Fibrous sheet located beneath the clavicular part of the pectoralis major muscle. - Key structures passing through it are: Lateral pectoral nerve: (Option C ruled out) Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. Cephalic vein: (Option A) Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery Thoracoacromial artery: Branches from the second part of the axillary artery into four branches: pectoral, deltoid, acromial, and clavicular. (Option B ruled out) Lymphatics: Drain lymph from the breast and pectoral region to the apical axillary lymph nodes. (Option D ruled out) - Lateral pectoral nerve: (Option C ruled out) Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. - Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. - It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. - Cephalic vein: (Option A) Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery - Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. - It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery - Thoracoacromial artery: Branches from the second part of the axillary artery into four branches: pectoral, deltoid, acromial, and clavicular. (Option B ruled out) - Lymphatics: Drain lymph from the breast and pectoral region to the apical axillary lymph nodes. (Option D ruled out) - Lateral pectoral nerve: (Option C ruled out) Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. - Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. - It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. - Cephalic vein: (Option A) Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery - Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. - It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery - Thoracoacromial artery: Branches from the second part of the axillary artery into four branches: pectoral, deltoid, acromial, and clavicular. (Option B ruled out) - Lymphatics: Drain lymph from the breast and pectoral region to the apical axillary lymph nodes. (Option D ruled out) - Innervates both the pectoralis major and pectoralis minor. - It pierces the pectoralis minor before supplying the pectoralis major. - Originates from the dorsal venous network of the hand. - It runs in the deltopectoral groove , accompanied by the deltoid branch of the thoracoacromial artery - Suspensory ligament of axilla : The clavipectoral fascia continues below the pectoralis minor as the suspensory ligament of the axilla. - The suspensory ligament attaches to the dome of the axillary fascia and helps to maintain it.","sourceRef":"Question Page 703; Solution Pages 709-711","sourcePage":703,"sourcePageEnd":703},{"id":"anatomy-18-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding the rotator cuff muscles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The rotator cuff includes the Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Subscapularis, and Teres major, which collectively aid in shoulder abduction and external rotation."},{"letter":"B","text":"The Subscapularis is the only rotator cuff muscle involved in internal rotation of the humerus."},{"letter":"C","text":"The Infraspinatus and Teres minor are responsible for medial rotation of the shoulder joint."},{"letter":"D","text":"The rotator cuff muscles are primarily located in the anterior aspect of the shoulder joint"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The Subscapularis is the only rotator cuff muscle involved in internal rotation of the humerus.","explanation":"Correct option: B) The Subscapularis is the only rotator cuff muscle involved in internal rotation of the humerus. Rotator Cuff Muscles: Muscle (Tendon) Action Nerve Supply Supraspinatus Initiates abduction of the shoulder Suprascapular nerve(C5,C6) Infraspinatus External( lateral)rotation of the Suprascapular nerve(C5,C6) shoulder Teres minor External(lateral) rotation of the Axillary nerve (C5,C6) shoulder Subscapularis Internal (medial) rotation of the Upper and lower subscapular nerves shoulder (C5,C6,C7) The rotator cuff is composed of the Supraspinatus, Infraspinatus, Subscapularis, and Teres minor, not Teres major (Option A). The Infraspinatus and Teres minor are involved in external (lateral) rotation, not medial rotation. (Option C) The rotator cuff muscles are located primarily around the shoulder joint, with some being posterior (e.g., Infraspinatus, Teres minor) and one anterior (Subscapularis) (Option D).","sourceRef":"Question Page 703; Solution Pages 711-712","sourcePage":703,"sourcePageEnd":703},{"id":"anatomy-18-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following is the correct pairing of shoulder movement and the muscle involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Adduction-pectoralis major"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adduction-serratus anterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abduction-teres major"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adduction-supraspinatus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Adduction-pectoralis major","explanation":"Movement of the shoulder joint Muscle(s) Involved Other movements Adduction Pectoralis major (Option A) Teres Medial rotation of scapula major (Option C) Latissimus dorsi Coracobrachialis Short head of biceps brachii Long head of triceps brachii Abduction Supraspinatus (0-15) (Option D) Humerus elevation Clavicle axial Deltoid (15-90) Serratus anterior rotation AC joint movement Lateral (90°–180°) (Option B) Upper and rotation of scapula lower fibers of trapezius (90°–180°)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 703-704; Solution Pages 712-713","sourcePage":703,"sourcePageEnd":704},{"id":"anatomy-18-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Match the wall of the axilla with the structure forming it: Wall Structure forming a. Anterior wall 1. Serratus anterior b. Posterior wall 2. Bicipital groove c. Medial wall 3. Clavipectoral fascia d. Lateral wall 4. Subscapularis Wall Structure forming a. Anterior wall 1. Serratus anterior b. Posterior wall 2. Bicipital groove c. Medial wall 3. Clavipectoral fascia d. Lateral wall 4. Subscapularis **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a1,b2,c3,d4"},{"letter":"B","text":"a4,b3,c2,d1"},{"letter":"C","text":"a3,b4,c1,d2"},{"letter":"D","text":"a2,b3,c4,d1"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. a3,b4,c1,d2","explanation":"Wall of axilla Contributing structure Anterior Wall Pectoralis major Clavipectoral fascia Pectoralis minor Posterior Wall Subscapularis Teres major Latissimus dorsi Medial Wall Upper four ribs with intercostal muscles Upper part of serratus anterior Lateral Wall Bicipital groove of humerus Coracobrachialis Short head of biceps brachii","sourceRef":"Question Page 704; Solution Pages 713-714","sourcePage":704,"sourcePageEnd":704},{"id":"anatomy-18-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following fasciae is the axillary sheath derived from?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deep cervical fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Clavipectoral fascia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial cervical fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary fascia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Deep cervical fascia","explanation":"The axillary sheath is derived from the prevertebral layer of deep cervical fascia. Apex/Cervicoaxillary Canal: - Directed upwards and medially towards the root of the neck - Axillary artery, axillary vein, and brachial plexus enter the axilla through this canal - Axillary vessels and cords of the brachial plexus are in the axillary sheath which is derived from the prevertebral layer of deep cervical fascia - Boundaries of Cervicoaxillary Canal: Anteriorly Posterior surface of the clavicle Posteriorly Superior border of the scapula and medial aspect of the coracoid process Medially Outer border of the first rib Contents of the Axilla:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 704-705; Solution Pages 714-715","sourcePage":704,"sourcePageEnd":705},{"id":"anatomy-18-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 35-year-old construction worker falls from a ladder and lands on his outstretched hand, resulting in a clavicular fracture as shown in the X- Ray given below. Which ligament plays a crucial role in stabilizing the medial end of the clavicle to the first rib, preventing excessive elevation of the clavicle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Coracoclavicular ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Costoclavicular ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sternoclavicular ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Interclavicular ligament"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Costoclavicular ligament","explanation":"Costoclavicular ligament anchors the medial end of the clavicle to the first rib and acts as a strong stabilizer that limits excessive elevation of the clavicle . It is critical in maintaining clavicular positioning , particularly during weight-bearing activities involving the upper limb. - The clavicle is often fractured by an indirect fall on an outstretched hand . - The most common fracture site is at the junction of its two curvatures, the weakest point . - The lateral fragment is displaced downward due to the weight of the limb, as the trapezius muscle cannot fully support it. Location on Clavicle Attachment Lateral End (Acromial end) Joint capsule of the acromioclavicular joint Medial End (Sternal end) Fibrous capsule of sternoclavicular joint (all around) Articular disc (posterosuperiorly) Interclavicular ligament (superiorly) Lateral One-Third of Shaft Anterior border: Origin of deltoid muscle Posterior border: Insertion of trapezius muscle Conoid tubercle and trapezoid ridge: Attachment to the conoid and trapezoid parts of coracoclavicular ligament Medial Two-Thirds of Shaft Anterior surface: Origin of pectoralis major Rough superior surface: Origin of clavicular head of sternocleidomastoid Oval impression (inferior surface, medial end) : Attachment of costoclavicular ligament Subclavian groove: Insertion of subclavius muscle , margins give attachment to clavipectoral fascia Posterior surface (near medial end) : Origin of sternohyoid muscle Coracoclavicular ligament (Option A) primarily stabilizes the lateral end of the clavicle by connecting it to the coracoid process of the scapula, providing support for the acromioclavicular joint . Weight transmission from upper limb to axial skeleton. Sternoclavicular ligament (Option C) : While this ligament supports the sternoclavicular joint and maintains joint integrity , it is not the primary structure responsible for limiting clavicular elevation. The costoclavicular ligament plays a more crucial role in this function. Interclavicular ligament (Option D) connects the medial ends of the clavicles and provides stability across the jugular notch of the sternum. Its primary function is to prevent excessive depression of the clavicle , not elevation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 705; Solution Pages 715-717","sourcePage":705,"sourcePageEnd":705},{"id":"anatomy-18-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 45-year-old painter presents with shoulder pain and weakness, particularly when raising his arm. He reports difficulty lifting objects overhead. On physical examination, there is tenderness over the shoulder and weakness in initiating arm abduction. Which rotator cuff muscle is most likely injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Infraspinatus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Teres Minor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supraspinatus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subscapularis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Supraspinatus","explanation":"Supraspinatus is responsible for initiating arm abduction , commonly injured in overhead activities like painting. Rotator Cuff Muscles Muscles and muscle action The rotator cuff consists of four muscles: Supraspinatus: Located above the scapular spine, it aids in arm abduction and is the most commonly injured rotator cuff muscle. (Option C) Infraspinatus: Below the scapular spine, it helps with lateral arm rotation and shoulder stabilization. (Option A ruled out) Teres Minor: Works with the infraspinatus in lateral rotation and stabilizing the shoulder. (Option B ruled out) Subscapularis: On the front of the scapula, it is responsible for medial arm rotation and is the only rotator cuff muscle that doesn’t attach to the greater tubercle of the humerus and forgotten tendon. (Option D ruled out) Musculotendinous Cuff: The tendons of these muscles form the rotator cuff , which supports and stabilizes the shoulder joint during movement. Functions Stabilize the shoulder. Rotate the arm (medially and laterally). Assist with arm abduction. Control arm movement, especially overhead. Clinical Relevance Rotator cuff tears: Resulting in pain, weakness, and limited movement. Subacromial bursitis: Inflammation causing shoulder pain and stiffness. Axillary nerve injury: Can weaken shoulder abduction by affecting the deltoid muscle. Muscle (Tendon) Action Nerve supply Supraspinatus Initiates abduction of the shoulder Suprascapular nerve(C5,C6) Infraspinatus External and lateral rotation of the Suprascapular nerve(C5,C6) shoulder Teres minor External and lateral rotation of the Axillary nerve (C5,C6) shoulder Subscapularis Internal and medial rotation of the Upper and lower subscapular nerves shoulder (C5,C6,C7)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 705-706; Solution Pages 717-718","sourcePage":705,"sourcePageEnd":706},{"id":"anatomy-18-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 55 year old man, a former construction worker, presents with shoulder pain and limited range of motion. His examination finding is shown below in the image and there is tenderness over the lateral aspect of his ribcage. Which muscle is most likely compromised in function, and what nerve injury could be contributing to his condition? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectoralis Major; Medial pectoral nerve injury"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pectoralis Minor; Lateral pectoral nerve injury"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subclavius; Nerve to subclavius injury"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serratus Anterior; Long thoracic nerve injury"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Serratus Anterior; Long thoracic nerve injury","explanation":"Serratus Anterior is essential for stabilizing the scapula and upward rotation. Winging indicates weakness due to long thoracic nerve injury , common in repetitive overhead activities. Muscles of the Anterior Axioappendi cular Region Muscle Origin Insertion Nerve Supply Actions Pectoralis Major Anterior surface of Lateral lip of the Medial and lateral Adducts and (Option A ruled out) the medial two-thirds bicipital groove of pectoral nerves medially rotates the of the clavicle Front the humerus (via arm Flexes the arm of the manubrium bilaminar tendon) (clavicular part) and sternum up to Extends the flexed the 6th costal arm against resistanc cartilages Second to e (sternocostal part) sixth costal cartilages and sternal end of the 6th rib Aponeuros is of the external oblique muscle Pectoralis Minor 3rd, 4th, and 5th ribs Medial border and Medial and lateral Protracts the (Option B ruled out) near the costochondr upper surface of the pectoral nerves scapula Lowers the al junction coracoid process point of the shoulder Subclavius (Option First rib at the Subclavian groove Nerve to subclavius Stabilizes the C ruled out) costochondral on the middle third (from upper trunk of clavicle during junction of the clavicle brachial plexus) shoulder movements Protects axillary vessels and brachial plexus Serratus Anterior Eight digitations Costal surface of the Long thoracic nerve Protracts scapula (Option D) from the upper 8 ribs medial border of the (C5, C6, C7) Rotates scapula and fascia over the scapula upward for arm intercostal muscles abduction beyond 90 degrees Stabilizes the scapula during weight-bearing activities Assists in forced inspiration","sourceRef":"Question Page 706; Solution Pages 718-719","sourcePage":706,"sourcePageEnd":706},{"id":"anatomy-18-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 35-year-old swimmer presents with weakness during arm extension and difficulty pulling objects toward himself. He also reports pain during forceful expiration, such as coughing or sneezing. Which muscle is most likely involved, and what is its nerve supply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trapezius; Spinal accessory nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Latissimus Dorsi; Thoracodorsal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Rhomboid Minor; Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Levator Scapulae; Dorsal scapular nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Latissimus Dorsi; Thoracodorsal nerve","explanation":"Latissimus Dorsi is involved in arm extension, adduction, medial rotation and forceful expiration (such as coughing or sneezing); innervated by the thoracodorsal nerve. Muscles of the posterior Axioappend icular Region Muscle Origin Insertion Nerve Supply Actions Trapezius (Option A Medial one-third of Upper fibers: Spinal accessory Upper fibers: ruled out) the superior nuchal Posterior border of nerve (CN XI) Elevate the scapula line of the skull the lateral one-third Proprioceptive fibers (shoulder shrugging), External occipital of the clavicle Middle from C3 and C4. extend the neck protuberance fibers: Medial margin (bilaterally) Middle Ligamentum nuchae of the acromion and fibers: Retract the Spinous processes upper lip of the spine scapula Lower of C7 to T12 of the scapula Lower fibers: Rotate the vertebrae Correspon fibers: Apex of a scapula upward, ding supraspinous triangular area at the aiding arm abduction ligaments medial end of the beyond 90 degrees spine of the scapula Trapezius with (intervening bursa) rhomboids: Retract scapula and tilt glenoid inferiorly. Trapezius with serratus anterior: Overhead abduction and tilt glenoid superiorly. All fibers together: Stabilize the scapula during arm movements Latissimus Dorsi Posterior one-third Fibers twist to insert Thoracodorsal nerve Extends, adducts, (Option B) of the outer lip of the into the floor of the (C6, C7, C8). and medially rotates iliac crest Posterior intertubercular the arm (e.g., layer of lumbar sulcus of the swimming, climbing) fascia (connecting to humerus, wrapping Assists in forceful lumbar and sacral around the lower expiration (e.g., spines) Spinous border of the teres coughing, sneezing) processes of T7 to major. T12 Lower four ribs Inferior angle of the scapula Levator Scapulae Transverse processe Superior angle and Dorsal scapular Elevates and (Option D ruled out) s of C1 to C4 upper part of the nerve (C5) and stabilizes the vertebrae medial border of the branches from C3 scapula during arm scapula and C4 movements. Rhomboid Minor Lower part of Medial border of the Dorsal scapular Retracts and (Option C ruled out) ligamentum nuchae scapula at the base nerve (C5) stabilizes the and spinous of the triangular area scapula. processes of C7 and at the root of the T1 spine. Rhomboid Major Spinous processes Medial border of the Dorsal scapular Retracts and of T2 to T5 and scapula, below the nerve (C5) stabilizes the supraspinous root of the spine scapula. ligaments","sourceRef":"Question Pages 706-707; Solution Pages 719-721","sourcePage":706,"sourcePageEnd":707},{"id":"anatomy-18-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 60-year-old female patient presents to the ED with a tension pneumothorax. The physician decides to perform a chest drain insertion to relieve the pressure. As the physician prepares for the procedure, they review the anatomical landmarks to ensure they insert the drain safely. Which muscle defines the posterior boundary of the Triangle of Safety, ensuring that the physician avoids vital structures during the chest drain insertion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectoralis major"},{"letter":"B","text":"Latissimus dorsi"},{"letter":"C","text":"Serratus anterior"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trapezius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Latissimus dorsi","explanation":"The lateral border of the latissimus dorsi muscle forms the posterior boundary of the Triangle of Safety for ICD insertion. Triangle of Safety for Intercostal Chest Drain (ICD) insertion: - Anatomical area on the lateral chest wall where it is considered safest to insert a chest drain. - Boundaries of the triangle: Anteriorly Lateral border of the pectoralis major muscle. Posteriorly Lateral border of the latissimus dorsi muscle. Inferiorly A horizontal line at the level of the 5th intercostal space (usually aligned with the nipple in males). Superiorly Base of the axilla(armpit). - This area minimizes the risk of injury to vital structures such as nerves, blood vessels, and the lung itself. - The drain is typically inserted in the 4th or 5th intercostal space along the mid-axillary line.","sourceRef":"Question Page 707; Solution Pages 721-723","sourcePage":707,"sourcePageEnd":707},{"id":"anatomy-18-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which muscle forms the medial border of the Triangle of Auscultation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Latissimus dorsi"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Rhomboid major"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deltoid"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Trapezius","explanation":"The medial border of the Triangle of Auscultation is formed by the lateral edge of the trapezius muscle. Triangle of Auscultation: - Anatomical Location: Located on the posterior thorax, specifically on the back, just below the scapula. - Boundaries : Medial Border Lateral border of the trapezius muscle. Lateral Border Medial border of the scapula. Inferior Border Superior border of the latissimus dorsi muscle. Anterior or floor Rhomboid major, 6th and 7th ribs. - Clinical Significance: This area is important for auscultating lung sounds because it is relatively free from muscle and bone interference. It allows for clearer sounds of breath , particularly in patients with respiratory issues. - This area is important for auscultating lung sounds because it is relatively free from muscle and bone interference. - It allows for clearer sounds of breath , particularly in patients with respiratory issues. - Positioning : To enhance the visibility and accessibility of the triangle, patients are often instructed to lean forward and cross their arms, which brings the scapulae apart and exposes this area. - To enhance the visibility and accessibility of the triangle, patients are often instructed to lean forward and cross their arms, which brings the scapulae apart and exposes this area. - This area is important for auscultating lung sounds because it is relatively free from muscle and bone interference. - It allows for clearer sounds of breath , particularly in patients with respiratory issues. - To enhance the visibility and accessibility of the triangle, patients are often instructed to lean forward and cross their arms, which brings the scapulae apart and exposes this area.","sourceRef":"Question Page 707; Solution Pages 723-724","sourcePage":707,"sourcePageEnd":707},{"id":"anatomy-18-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following nerves is responsible for winging of scapula?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spinal accesory nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Suprascapular nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Long thoracic nerve","explanation":"- Winging of the scapula occurs due to paralysis or weakness of the serratus anterior muscle , which is innervated by the long thoracic nerve. - The serratus anterior muscle normally holds the scapula against the thoracic wall. - Damage to the long thoracic nerve results in a characteristic “winged” appearance of the scapula, especially when the patient pushes against a wall. Dorsal scapular nerve (Option A): - Innervates the rhomboid major and minor and levator scapulae, which retract and elevate the scapula. - Injury to this nerve does not cause winging of the scapula. Spinal accessory nerve (Option B): - Innervates the trapezius muscle, which elevates and rotates the scapula. - Damage leads to shoulder drooping and difficulty elevating the arm but does not cause scapular winging. Suprascapular nerve (Option D): - Innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles, which assist in shoulder abduction and external rotation. - Injury affects shoulder movement, not scapular positioning.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 707-708; Solution Pages 724-725","sourcePage":707,"sourcePageEnd":708},{"id":"anatomy-18-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-18","chapter":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topic":"Lesson 18 - Bones and Muscles of Proximal Region, Axilla, and Scapula","topicIndex":18,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding the muscles involved in abduction of shoulder?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supraspinatous is involved in initiation of abduction."},{"letter":"B","text":"Deltoid is responsible for 0-15 degree abduction."},{"letter":"C","text":"Supraspinatous is responsible for 15-90 degree abduction."},{"letter":"D","text":"Infraspinatous is responsible for 0-15 degree abduction."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Supraspinatous is involved in initiation of abduction.","explanation":"Muscles Involved in Shoulder Abduction: Shoulder abduction Supraspinatus (0-15) Deltoid (15-90) Serratus anterior (90°-180°) Upper and lower fibers of trapezius (90°-180°) Infraspinatus is not involved in abduction but rather in external rotation of the arm (Option D) The supraspinatus only initiates abduction (0-15°). The deltoid is the primary muscle responsible for abduction from 15-90° (Option B and C ).","sourceRef":"Question Page 708; Solution Pages 725-726","sourcePage":708,"sourcePageEnd":708},{"id":"anatomy-19-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A patient with a humeral fracture presents with difficulty extending the elbow and wrist. Which of the following nerves is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Radial nerve","explanation":"- Radial nerve innervates the extensor muscles of the upper limb , including the triceps brachii (for elbow extension) and the extensor muscles of the forearm (for wrist extension). It passes through the spiral groove of the humerus and is therefore at risk in humeral shaft fractures. - Damage to the radial nerve leads to wrist drop (inability to extend the wrist) and loss of elbow extension. Nerves Related to Humerus Nerve Location Relative to Humerus Axillary Nerve (Option D) Around the surgical neck of the humerus. Musculocutaneous Nerve It originates from the lateral cord of the brachial plexus, pierces coracobrachialis, travels between biceps and coracobrachialis. Radial Nerve (Option A) Passes through the spiral groove of the humerus, posterior to the brachial artery, anterior to the long head of triceps. Median Nerve (Option B) Passes anterior to the humerus, but does not innervate upper arm muscles. Ulnar Nerve (Option C) Travels down the medial side of the brachium, behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 727; Solution Pages 736-737","sourcePage":727,"sourcePageEnd":727},{"id":"anatomy-19-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 35-year-old office worker presents with numbness and tingling in her thumb, index, and middle fingers. The physician taps over the ventral aspect of the wrist, reproducing her symptoms. Which test is the physician likely performing?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tinel's sign"},{"letter":"B","text":"Phalen's test"},{"letter":"C","text":"Finkelstein's test"},{"letter":"D","text":"Froment's test"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Tinel's sign","explanation":"The patient experiences numbness and tingling specifically in her thumb, index, and middle fingers which are classic indicators of median nerve compression , often associated with carpal tunnel syndrome (CTS). Tinel's sign involves tapping or pressing over the median nerve at the wrist, which can reproduce symptoms of carpal tunnel syndrome , such as numbness and tingling in the fingers. Carpal tunnel Syndrome: - Causes (Median nerve compression): Tenosynovitis of flexor tendons (idiopathic) Myxedema (thyroxine deficiency) Fluid retention during pregnancy Fracture dislocation of the lunate bone Osteoarthritis of the wrist - Tenosynovitis of flexor tendons (idiopathic) - Myxedema (thyroxine deficiency) - Fluid retention during pregnancy - Fracture dislocation of the lunate bone - Osteoarthritis of the wrist - Clinical Features: Pain and Sensation: Burning pain or \"pins and needles\" in the sensory distribution of the median nerve (lateral 3½ digits), often worse at night. Sensory Preservation: No sensory loss over the thenar eminence, as it is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve, which lies superficial to the flexor retinaculum. Muscle Weakness: Weakness in the thenar muscles. Deformity: \" Ape-thumb deformity \", due to paralysis of the thenar muscles. - Pain and Sensation: Burning pain or \"pins and needles\" in the sensory distribution of the median nerve (lateral 3½ digits), often worse at night. - Sensory Preservation: No sensory loss over the thenar eminence, as it is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve, which lies superficial to the flexor retinaculum. - Muscle Weakness: Weakness in the thenar muscles. - Deformity: \" Ape-thumb deformity \", due to paralysis of the thenar muscles. - Tenosynovitis of flexor tendons (idiopathic) - Myxedema (thyroxine deficiency) - Fluid retention during pregnancy - Fracture dislocation of the lunate bone - Osteoarthritis of the wrist - Pain and Sensation: Burning pain or \"pins and needles\" in the sensory distribution of the median nerve (lateral 3½ digits), often worse at night. - Sensory Preservation: No sensory loss over the thenar eminence, as it is supplied by the palmar cutaneous branch of the median nerve, which lies superficial to the flexor retinaculum. - Muscle Weakness: Weakness in the thenar muscles. - Deformity: \" Ape-thumb deformity \", due to paralysis of the thenar muscles. - Diagnostic Tests: 1. Tinel’s sign (tingling upon percussion over the flexor retinaculum) 2. Phalen’s test (symptoms reproduced by wrist flexion) (Option B) 3. Nerve Conduction: Reduced conduction velocity in the median nerve (<30 m/s) is indicative of the condition. 4. PEN Test: Patient places their hand in a supine position on a table and is asked to touch a pen with their thumb. Thumb abduction is not possible due to median nerve compression, which is positive for carpal tunnel syndrome. - Treatment: Nighttime symptoms due to involuntary wrist flexion may respond to splinting. For recent onset symptoms , particularly those related to pregnancy or synovial thickening, corticosteroid injections may provide relief. Persistent symptoms , especially with evidence of axonal damage, often require surgical intervention, typically involving the division of the flexor retinaculum. - Nighttime symptoms due to involuntary wrist flexion may respond to splinting. - For recent onset symptoms , particularly those related to pregnancy or synovial thickening, corticosteroid injections may provide relief. - Persistent symptoms , especially with evidence of axonal damage, often require surgical intervention, typically involving the division of the flexor retinaculum. - Nighttime symptoms due to involuntary wrist flexion may respond to splinting. - For recent onset symptoms , particularly those related to pregnancy or synovial thickening, corticosteroid injections may provide relief. - Persistent symptoms , especially with evidence of axonal damage, often require surgical intervention, typically involving the division of the flexor retinaculum. Finkelstein's test (Option C) is used to diagnose De Quervain's tenosynovitis. It requires the patient to make a fist with the thumb inside and then ulnar deviate the wrist. Froment's sign (Option D) assesses ulnar nerve function by checking for weakness in pinching paper between the thumb and index finger.","sourceRef":"Question Page 727; Solution Pages 737-742","sourcePage":727,"sourcePageEnd":727},{"id":"anatomy-19-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 30-year-old man presents with a laceration to his right wrist after a slash injury. On examination, there is weakness of thumb opposition and numbness over the thenar eminence. Which structure is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Median nerve","explanation":"The median nerve passes through the carpal tunnel and is most commonly affected in the slash injuries that cut across the palmar side. - Wrist slash injuries commonly occur due to suicide cuts at wrist and can lead to: Loss of sensation in the thenar eminence Loss of sensation in the lateral 3 and half digits on palmar side Motor loss of LOAF muscles Atrophy of thenar eminence Ape thumb deformity - Loss of sensation in the thenar eminence - Loss of sensation in the lateral 3 and half digits on palmar side - Motor loss of LOAF muscles - Atrophy of thenar eminence - Ape thumb deformity - Loss of sensation in the thenar eminence - Loss of sensation in the lateral 3 and half digits on palmar side - Motor loss of LOAF muscles - Atrophy of thenar eminence - Ape thumb deformity Wrist Slash Injuries Nerves affected Median nerve Ulnar nerve (less common) : Innervates the intrinsic muscles of the hand (like the hypothenar muscles and lumbricals for the ring and little fingers ), and it also provides sensory innervation to the little finger and half of the ring finger. (Option C) Arteries affected Radial artery Ulnar artery Flexor carpi radialis (Option A) is a muscle of the anterior forearm that primarily contributes to wrist flexion and abduction but it does not affect thumb opposition or sensation in the thenar eminence. Radial nerve (Option B) provides motor innervation to the extensor muscles of the forearm and sensory innervation to the dorsal side of the hand but it does not involve in the thumb opposition, and numbness in the thenar eminence.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 727-728; Solution Pages 742-743","sourcePage":727,"sourcePageEnd":728},{"id":"anatomy-19-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 30-year-old man sustains an elbow dislocation with a medial epicondyle fracture. He now presents with difficulty flexing the fourth and fifth fingers and an inability to adduct the thumb. Which nerve is most likely injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"- Ulnar nerve (C7, C8 & T1) originates from the medial cord of the brachial plexus, running along the medial side of the brachial artery in the arm. - It passes posterior to the medial epicondyle of the humerus and enters the forearm between the two heads of the flexor carpi ulnaris , travelling between it and the flexor digitorum profundus. - At the wrist, the nerve passes through Guyon’s canal into the hand, where it divides into superficial and deep branches , providing motor and sensory innervation to the hand. - Damage to the ulnar nerve leads to: Difficulty in flexing the fourth and fifth fingers due to paralysis of the flexor digitorum profundus (medial part) and the lumbricals (3rd and 4th). Inability to adduct the thumb, as the adductor pollicis is also innervated by the ulnar nerve. - Difficulty in flexing the fourth and fifth fingers due to paralysis of the flexor digitorum profundus (medial part) and the lumbricals (3rd and 4th). - Inability to adduct the thumb, as the adductor pollicis is also innervated by the ulnar nerve. - Motor branches of Ulnar nerve: - Difficulty in flexing the fourth and fifth fingers due to paralysis of the flexor digitorum profundus (medial part) and the lumbricals (3rd and 4th). - Inability to adduct the thumb, as the adductor pollicis is also innervated by the ulnar nerve. Ulnar nerve (C7, C8 & T1- Root Values) Muscle Action Anterior Forearm Flexor carpi ulnaris Medial half of flexor digitorum Flexion and adduction of hand at wrist Flexion of distal profundus phalanges. Hand Hypothenar muscles Abducts little finger Flexes little finger Pulls fifth metacarpal forward as in cupping the palm 8 Interossei muscles Palmar Interossei Dorsal Interossei Ad ducts digits 2-5 (Mnemonic PAD) Ab duct digits 2-5 (Mnemonic DAB) Assists Lumbricals in MCP flexion and IP extension of digits 2-5 Lumbricals of Digits 4 & 5 Flexion of MCP Extension of PIP & DIP joints of digits 4 and 5 Adductor pollicis Adduction the thumb Ulnar Nerve Supply: Axillary nerve (Option A) is a smaller terminal branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus (C5, C6) that passes through the lower axilla into the quadrangular space. Injured in shoulder dislocations or surgical neck fractures of the humerus. It causes weakness in arm abduction and sensory loss over the lateral shoulder but does not affect finger flexion or thumb adduction. Radial nerve (Option B): Injured in humeral shaft fractures, which leads to wrist drop and sensory loss over the posterior forearm but does not impair finger flexion or thumb adduction. Median nerve (Option C) : Commonly injured in carpal tunnel syndrome or supracondylar fractures . It causes difficulty flexing the index and middle fingers and loss of thumb opposition, but it does not affect the fourth and fifth fingers or thumb adduction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 728; Solution Pages 743-745","sourcePage":728,"sourcePageEnd":728},{"id":"anatomy-19-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A patient with a history of recurrent shoulder dislocations presents with a deformity as shown below. On physical examination, the patient is unable to fully protract the scapula when pushing against a wall. Which nerve is most likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Suprascapular nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Accessory nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Long thoracic nerve","explanation":"- Winging of the scapula and inability to protract the scapula are signs of long thoracic nerve injury , which innervates the serratus anterior muscle and its paralysis causes these symptoms. - It also causes difficulty to hit the opponent with that hand in boxing and inability to make stores in swimming, hence it's also called Boxer’s or Swimmer’s palsy. - Etiology : Long thoracic nerve injury is common in Trauma Surgeries (mastectomy) Repetitive overhead movements (like swimming) Recurrent shoulder dislocations. - Trauma - Surgeries (mastectomy) - Repetitive overhead movements (like swimming) - Recurrent shoulder dislocations. - Trauma - Surgeries (mastectomy) - Repetitive overhead movements (like swimming) - Recurrent shoulder dislocations. Long Thoracic Nerve Origin C5 - C7 Insertion Serratus anterior muscle , which is responsible for scapular protraction and upward rotation . Nerve actions (Motor function) Protracts the scapula (pulls it forward around the thorax). Assists in arm anteversion (moving the arm forward). Lifts the ribs , aiding in respiration. Responsible for the anterolateral motion of the scapula , which is necessary for arm elevation . Dorsal scapular nerve (Option B) innervates the rhomboids and levator scapulae muscles, which affect scapular retraction and elevation but not protraction. Suprascapular nerve (Option C) innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles, which are primarily responsible for shoulder abduction and external rotation . It wouldn't affect scapular protraction. Accessory nerve (Option D) innervates the trapezius muscle, which is responsible for shoulder shrugging and upper back movement . Damage here might lead to weakness in shoulder elevation and scapular motion but not in protracting the scapula.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 728-729; Solution Pages 745-746","sourcePage":728,"sourcePageEnd":729},{"id":"anatomy-19-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following is the root value of the nerve that provides sensory innervation in the area marked (X)? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T2"},{"letter":"B","text":"T1, T2"},{"letter":"C","text":"C5"},{"letter":"D","text":"C5, C6"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. T2","explanation":"Sensory Supply of Arm Region Nerve Derivative of Root values Upper medial part Intercostobrachial 2nd intercostal T2 Lower medial part Medial cutaneous nerve of Medial cord of brachial T1, T2 arm plexus Upper lateral part Upper lateral cutaneous Axillary nerve C5, C6 nerve of arm Lower lateral part Lower lateral cutaneous Radial nerve C5, C6 nerve of arm Posterior region Posterior cutaneous nerve Radial nerve C5 of arm","sourceRef":"Question Page 729; Solution Pages 746-747","sourcePage":729,"sourcePageEnd":729},{"id":"anatomy-19-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 25-year-old male sustains a traumatic shoulder dislocation after a motor vehicle accident. On examination, the patient is unable to abduct his arm beyond 15 degrees and experiences numbness over the lateral shoulder. Which of the following nerve injury is the most likely cause of his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Suprascapular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subscapular nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Long Thoracic nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Axillary nerve","explanation":"- The most likely cause of this patient's symptoms is an axillary nerve injury , given the nerve's close proximity to the shoulder joint and the typical presentation of shoulder abduction weakness along with numbness over the lateral shoulder after a traumatic dislocation. - The axillary nerve provides motor innervation to the deltoid and teres minor muscles , and sensory innervation to the area over the lower part of the deltoid muscle . Common Causes of Axillary Nerve Injury: - Intramuscular injections : It should be given in the middle of the deltoid muscle to avoid axillary nerve injury. - Fracture of surgical neck of humerus. - Inferior dislocation of humerus. Symptoms: - Inability to abduct shoulder joint ( Deltoid paralysis ). - Loss of sensation in lower half of the deltoid ( regimental-badge area ). - Loss of the rounded contour of the shoulder due to Deltoid muscle atrophy. - Prominence of the greater tubercle of the humerus (due to muscle wasting). Suprascapular nerve (Option A) innervates the supraspinatus (initiation and continuation of shoulder abduction ) and the infraspinatus ( externally rotates the shoulder ) but it does not supply sensation to the skin over the shoulder, which makes it an unlikely cause of the sensory loss in this patient. Subscapular nerve injury (Option C) innervates the subscapularis muscle , responsible for internal rotation of the shoulder and adduction of the arm. It does not typically affect abduction or cause numbness over the lateral shoulder. Long thoracic nerve injury (Option D) innervates the serratus anterior muscle , which is responsible for scapular protraction and upward rotation . This does not cause weakness in shoulder abduction or numbness over the lateral shoulder.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 729-730; Solution Pages 747-748","sourcePage":729,"sourcePageEnd":730},{"id":"anatomy-19-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 35-year-old man presents with inability to extend his wrist and fingers. O/E, he exhibits a wrist drop and demonstrates weakness in elbow extension & loss of sensation over the posterior arm and the lateral aspect of the dorsum of the hand. Based on these findings, at which level is the radial nerve likely injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the axilla"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the spiral groove"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral epicondyle of the humerus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve at the forearm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. At the axilla","explanation":"Injury to the Radial nerve at the axilla affects all of its branches, leading to wrist drop, triceps paralysis, and sensory loss in the posterior arm and the lateral aspect of the dorsum of the hand. . Radial Nerve: - Also known as the musculospiral nerve , is the largest branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus, with root values of C5-C8 & T1. - Course and termination: The nerve runs behind the third part of the axillary artery and lies posterior to the brachial artery, entering the lower triangular space to reach the oblique radial sulcus on the back of the humerus. It then pierces the lateral intermuscular septum about 5 cm below the deltoid tuberosity, entering the anterior compartment of the arm , before descending into the cubital fossa. Finally, it terminates by dividing into a superficial branch and a deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve) just below the lateral epicondyle. - The nerve runs behind the third part of the axillary artery and lies posterior to the brachial artery, entering the lower triangular space to reach the oblique radial sulcus on the back of the humerus. - It then pierces the lateral intermuscular septum about 5 cm below the deltoid tuberosity, entering the anterior compartment of the arm , before descending into the cubital fossa. - Finally, it terminates by dividing into a superficial branch and a deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve) just below the lateral epicondyle. - Branches: - The nerve runs behind the third part of the axillary artery and lies posterior to the brachial artery, entering the lower triangular space to reach the oblique radial sulcus on the back of the humerus. - It then pierces the lateral intermuscular septum about 5 cm below the deltoid tuberosity, entering the anterior compartment of the arm , before descending into the cubital fossa. - Finally, it terminates by dividing into a superficial branch and a deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve) just below the lateral epicondyle. Axilla Posterior cutaneous nerve of arm : Sensory innervation to the skin on the back of the arm up to the elbow. Nerve to the long head of triceps . Nerve to the medial head of triceps . Spiral groove Lower lateral cutaneous nerve of the arm : Sensory innervation to the skin on the lateral arm. Posterior cutaneous nerve of the forearm : Sensory innervation to the middle back of the forearm. Nerve to the lateral head of triceps . Nerve to the medial head of triceps . Nerve to anconeus . Anterior Compartment Nerve to brachialis (lateral part). Nerve to brachioradialis . Nerve to extensor carpi radialis longus (ECRL) . Lateral Epicondyle Divides into: Deep branch (posterior interosseous nerve) : Supplies the extensor muscles of the forearm. Superficial branch (superficial radial nerve) : Sensory supply to the lateral dorsum of the hand and dorsal surfaces of the lateral 3½ digits. - Injuries of the Radial Nerve: Location Causes Symptoms Axilla (Option A) Crutch palsy Motor Loss: Triceps paralysis (loss of elbow extension) Wrist drop d/t ECRL involvement Finger drop d/t involvement of extensor digitorum Loss of digit extension Sensory Loss: Posterior arm Lateral part of dorsum of the hand Narrow strip on the back of the forearm Spiral Groove (Option B) Midshaft humeral fractures Incorrect Motor Loss: Loss of wrist and finger intramuscular injections Direct extension ( wrist drop & Finger drop ) pressure (Saturday night palsy). . Impaired supination (when the arm is extended). Sensory Loss: Limited to a small area between the first and second metacarpals over dorsum of hand. Elbow (Option C) (ECRL Preserved) Supra condylar fracture Finger drop at MCP Joint d/t involvement of extensor digitorum. Loss of extensio n in the wrist and fingers, but no wrist drop d/t ECRL Preservation. Weakness of supination. Sensory loss at the dorsum 3 1/2 fingers of the hand Posterior Interosseous Nerve Radial Tunnel Syndrome : No Wrist Drop : The ECRL remains Entrapment (Option D) Entrapment of the deep branch of the unaffected. No Sensory Loss : radial nerve at the elbow. Sensation is intact despite motor deficits. Finger drop at MCP Joint Pain localized over the extensor aspect of the forearm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 730; Solution Pages 748-751","sourcePage":730,"sourcePageEnd":730},{"id":"anatomy-19-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A patient is asked to hold a piece of paper between their thumb and index finger. When the examiner attempts to pull the paper away, the patient tries to hold the paper as shown in the image. Based on this observation, which muscle is likely affected? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adductor pollicis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Opponens pollicis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Adductor pollicis","explanation":"In this scenario, the patient flexes the distal phalanx of the thumb to grip the paper when the examiner attempts to pull it away, indicating weakness in the adductor pollicis muscle, which is innervated by the ulnar nerve , thereby confirming a positive Froment's test. Froment’s test: - Tests Adductor pollicis supplied by Ulnar nerve. - The patient is asked to hold a piece of paper (or a card) between the thumb and index finger of the affected hand & card is pulled out. - Positive Result: If the patient flexes the distal phalanx of the thumb while attempting to hold the paper, it indicates weakness of the adductor pollicis muscle due to ulnar nerve dysfunction. This flexion occurs due to flexor pollicis longus innervated by the median nerve. Flexor pollicis brevis (Option A) primarily flexes the thumb without significantly contributing to grip strength. Abductor pollicis brevis (Option B) aids in thumb abduction. Opponents pollicis (Option D) facilitates thumb opposition.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 730-731; Solution Pages 751-752","sourcePage":730,"sourcePageEnd":731},{"id":"anatomy-19-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45-year-old musician presents with a noticeable deformity affecting his ring and little fingers as shown in the image. Which muscle is primarily responsible for this deformity? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum superficialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lumbricals"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dorsal interossei"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lumbricals","explanation":"- The given image depicts the Ulnar claw hand due to the paralysis of the ulnar lumbricals. - Lumbricals normally flex the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and extend at the interphalangeal (IP) joints. - Impairment of the function of lumbricals due to ulnar injury creates the characteristic appearance of claw hand in the ring and little finger. Ulnar Nerve: - Arises from the medial cord of the brachial plexus (C8, T1 ) with contributions from C7. - Course : Axilla : Lies medial to the axillary artery. Arm : Descends along the medial side of the brachial artery, piercing the medial intermuscular septum to enter the posterior compartment. It runs behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus in the cubital tunnel. Forearm : Passes between the heads of flexor carpi ulnaris & Branches are: Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. Hand : Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Axilla : Lies medial to the axillary artery. - Arm : Descends along the medial side of the brachial artery, piercing the medial intermuscular septum to enter the posterior compartment. It runs behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus in the cubital tunnel. - Forearm : Passes between the heads of flexor carpi ulnaris & Branches are: Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. - Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. - Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. - Hand : Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. - Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Axilla : Lies medial to the axillary artery. - Arm : Descends along the medial side of the brachial artery, piercing the medial intermuscular septum to enter the posterior compartment. It runs behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus in the cubital tunnel. - Forearm : Passes between the heads of flexor carpi ulnaris & Branches are: Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. - Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. - Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. - Hand : Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. - Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Muscular : Flexor carpi ulnaris, medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Palmar Cutaneous : Sensory innervation to hypothenar eminence. - Dorsal Cutaneous : Sensory to medial dorsum of the hand and medial 1½ fingers. - Superficial Branch : Supplies palmaris brevis and sensory to medial 1½ fingers. - Deep Branch : Enters Guyon’s canal, innervating intrinsic hand muscles (except thenar and first two lumbricals). - Injury to Ulnar Nerve: At the Elbow At the Wrist Causes : Fracture/dislocation, cubital tunnel syndrome, Causes : Vulnerable to cuts, and compression in Guyon’s compression by flexor carpi ulnaris. canal. Symptoms : Atrophy of hypothenar eminence Claw-hand Symptoms : Ulnar claw hand Atrophy of hypothenar deformity (ring and little fingers) Loss of finger eminence Loss of finger abduction/adduction Positive abduction/adduction Sensory loss in the medial hand Froment's sign Positive Froment's sign Ulnar Paradox: More pronounced claw hand on injury to the ulnar nerve at the wrist due to intact Flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) , resulting in exaggerated flexion of the distal joints.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 731-732; Solution Pages 752-754","sourcePage":731,"sourcePageEnd":732},{"id":"anatomy-19-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45-year-old carpenter reports tingling and weakness in his ring and little fingers. Examination reveals hypothenar atrophy and a positive Froment's sign. Which structure is likely compressed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cubital tunnel"},{"letter":"B","text":"Guyon’s canal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Carpal tunnel"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palmar aponeurosis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Guyon’s canal","explanation":"The given clinical scenario of tingling in the ring and little fingers, weakness of intrinsic hand muscles, atrophy of the hypothenar eminence, and a positive Froment's sign are characteristic of Ulnar nerve compression in Guyon’s canal. Ulnar nerve supply in the hand: - Ulnar nerve travels alongside the ulnar artery and gives off a palmar cutaneous branch before reaching the flexor retinaculum, supplying the ulnar side of the palm & enters Guyon’s canal & divides into: Superficial Branch: Supplies the skin of the medial 1½ fingers & Palmaris brevis. Deep Branch: Supplies all intrinsic muscles of the hand except muscles of thenar eminence & 1st two lumbricals and terminates supplying Adductor pollicis , aka Graveyard of Ulnar nerve. - Superficial Branch: Supplies the skin of the medial 1½ fingers & Palmaris brevis. - Deep Branch: Supplies all intrinsic muscles of the hand except muscles of thenar eminence & 1st two lumbricals and terminates supplying Adductor pollicis , aka Graveyard of Ulnar nerve. - Superficial Branch: Supplies the skin of the medial 1½ fingers & Palmaris brevis. - Deep Branch: Supplies all intrinsic muscles of the hand except muscles of thenar eminence & 1st two lumbricals and terminates supplying Adductor pollicis , aka Graveyard of Ulnar nerve. Guyon’s Canal (Ulnar Tunnel Syndrome): (Option B) - Compression of the ulnar nerve within Guyon’s canal at the wrist. - Clinical Features: Claw-Hand Deformity: Ring and little fingers exhibit pronounced ulnar claw hand when compared to injury of the ulnar nerve at the elbow (ulnar paradox) due to preserved function of the FDP, resulting in marked flexion of DIP joints. Atrophy of the Hypothenar Eminence Loss of Finger Abduction and Adduction Positive Froment’s Sign: Unable to hold the book between thumb and index indicates weakness in the adductor pollicis . DIP Of thumb compensates for adductor pollicis weakness and thumb flexion occurs. - Claw-Hand Deformity: Ring and little fingers exhibit pronounced ulnar claw hand when compared to injury of the ulnar nerve at the elbow (ulnar paradox) due to preserved function of the FDP, resulting in marked flexion of DIP joints. - Atrophy of the Hypothenar Eminence - Loss of Finger Abduction and Adduction - Positive Froment’s Sign: Unable to hold the book between thumb and index indicates weakness in the adductor pollicis . DIP Of thumb compensates for adductor pollicis weakness and thumb flexion occurs. - Claw-Hand Deformity: Ring and little fingers exhibit pronounced ulnar claw hand when compared to injury of the ulnar nerve at the elbow (ulnar paradox) due to preserved function of the FDP, resulting in marked flexion of DIP joints. - Atrophy of the Hypothenar Eminence - Loss of Finger Abduction and Adduction - Positive Froment’s Sign: Unable to hold the book between thumb and index indicates weakness in the adductor pollicis . DIP Of thumb compensates for adductor pollicis weakness and thumb flexion occurs. Flexor Retinaculum: (Option A) - A strong fibrous band that spans the anterior concavity or palmar surface of the carpus, forming the carpal tunnel for the passage of flexor tendons of the digits . - It is a modification of the deep fascia that thickens in front of the carpal bones. - Attachments: Medially: Attached to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate. Laterally: Attached to the scaphoid tubercle & crest of the trapezium. - Medially: Attached to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate. - Laterally: Attached to the scaphoid tubercle & crest of the trapezium. - Relations: - Medially: Attached to the pisiform and the hook of the hamate. - Laterally: Attached to the scaphoid tubercle & crest of the trapezium. Structures Passing Superficial to the Flexor Retinaculum Structures Passing Deep to the Flexor Retinaculum (medial to lateral) (through the carpal tunnel) Ulnar Nerve & Artery Palmar cutaneous branch of ulnar Tendons of flexor digitorum superficialis (FDS) Tendons nerve Tendon of palmaris longus Palmar cutaneous of flexor digitorum profundus (FDP) Tendon of flexor branch of median nerve Superficial palmar branch of pollicis longus (FPL) Median nerve radial artery Carpal tunnel (Option C) is formed by the carpal bones and flexor retinaculum, contains the median nerve and flexor tendons . Compression of median nerve occurs in carpal tunnel syndrome, causing symptoms like tingling and weakness in the thumb, index, middle, and part of the ring finger. Palmar aponeurosis (Option D) is a fibrous tissue structure that supports the skin of the palm and anchors it to underlying muscles and tendons . Conditions like Dupuytren’s contracture (due to thickening and shortening of the palmar fascia) may affect it, but they do not present with the specific ulnar nerve symptoms seen in this patient.","sourceRef":"Question Page 732; Solution Pages 754-758","sourcePage":732,"sourcePageEnd":732},{"id":"anatomy-19-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 35-year-old patient struggles to make a fist. When attempting to flex, the index and middle fingers remain straight, while the ring and little fingers flex normally. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar nerve injury"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve injury at the wrist"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve injury at the elbow"},{"letter":"D","text":"Radial nerve injury"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Median nerve injury at the elbow","explanation":"The given symptoms, including the inability to flex the index and middle fingers, suggest a median nerve injury at the elbow , resulting in the \"benediction deformity.\" Median Nerve (C5 - T1): - Median nerve is a major nerve of the upper limb. - It is formed by two roots: Lateral Root: Fibers from C5 , C6 , and C7 , originating from the lateral cord. Medial Root: Fibers from C8 and T1 , originating from the medial cord. - Lateral Root: Fibers from C5 , C6 , and C7 , originating from the lateral cord. - Medial Root: Fibers from C8 and T1 , originating from the medial cord. - Course: Origin: Arises from the lateral and medial roots of the brachial plexus (C5-T1) in the axilla. Axilla: Lies lateral to the third part of the axillary artery. Arm: Initially lateral to the brachial artery, it crosses in front at the mid-humerus. Cubital Fossa: Medial to the brachial artery, giving branches to the forearm flexors , except flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. Forearm: Passes between the two heads of the pronator teres, giving off the anterior interosseous nerve , which supplies: Flexor pollicis longus Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus Pronator quadratus Wrist: Gives the palmar cutaneous branch before entering the carpal tunnel. Hand: After entering the palm, it divides into recurrent and palmar digital branches, supplying thenar muscles and sensory innervation to the lateral 3½ digits. - Origin: Arises from the lateral and medial roots of the brachial plexus (C5-T1) in the axilla. - Axilla: Lies lateral to the third part of the axillary artery. - Arm: Initially lateral to the brachial artery, it crosses in front at the mid-humerus. - Cubital Fossa: Medial to the brachial artery, giving branches to the forearm flexors , except flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Forearm: Passes between the two heads of the pronator teres, giving off the anterior interosseous nerve , which supplies: Flexor pollicis longus Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus Pronator quadratus - Flexor pollicis longus - Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus - Pronator quadratus - Wrist: Gives the palmar cutaneous branch before entering the carpal tunnel. - Hand: After entering the palm, it divides into recurrent and palmar digital branches, supplying thenar muscles and sensory innervation to the lateral 3½ digits. - Lateral Root: Fibers from C5 , C6 , and C7 , originating from the lateral cord. - Medial Root: Fibers from C8 and T1 , originating from the medial cord. - Origin: Arises from the lateral and medial roots of the brachial plexus (C5-T1) in the axilla. - Axilla: Lies lateral to the third part of the axillary artery. - Arm: Initially lateral to the brachial artery, it crosses in front at the mid-humerus. - Cubital Fossa: Medial to the brachial artery, giving branches to the forearm flexors , except flexor carpi ulnaris and medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Forearm: Passes between the two heads of the pronator teres, giving off the anterior interosseous nerve , which supplies: Flexor pollicis longus Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus Pronator quadratus - Flexor pollicis longus - Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus - Pronator quadratus - Wrist: Gives the palmar cutaneous branch before entering the carpal tunnel. - Hand: After entering the palm, it divides into recurrent and palmar digital branches, supplying thenar muscles and sensory innervation to the lateral 3½ digits. - Flexor pollicis longus - Lateral half of the flexor digitorum profundus - Pronator quadratus - Median Nerve Injuries: At the Elbow Lower Injury (Carpal Tunnel Syndrome) Causes: Supracondylar fractures, tight Causes: Compression due to tenosynovitis and fractures. tourniquets/entrapment by the pronator teres Symptoms: Loss of forearm pronation & weak wrist Symptoms: Burning pain/tingling in Lateral 3½ Digits, flexion. Benediction deformity (index and middle fingers especially at night. No sensory loss over the thenar remain straight when attempting to make a fist) (Pointing eminence Ape thumb deformity Thenar muscle wasting index finger) D/t involvement of FDS & FDP Ape-thumb Can make a fist (Pointing index finger not present) deformity (flattened thenar eminence, thumb adducted). Sensory loss in the lateral palm and lateral 3½ digits. Weak \"OK sign\" d/t involvement of FDP & FPL Anterior Interosseus Nerve Injury: - Pure motor nerve supplying FPL , FDP & Pronatar quadratus - Causes: Fractures of the radius (e.g., Galeazzi fractures) or humeral shaft fractures, forearm compartment syndrome - Cannot form “O” with Index finger and Thumb. - No Ape thumb deformity & sensory loss.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 732-733; Solution Pages 758-760","sourcePage":732,"sourcePageEnd":733},{"id":"anatomy-19-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Identify the X correctly from the below image of cubital fossa. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Brachial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cephalic vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Branchial cutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Cephalic vein","explanation":"The structure marked above is Cephalic vein. - Cubital fossa is a triangular space anterior to the elbow. - Bordered laterally by the brachioradialis and medially by the pronator teres , with its base formed by the anterior surfaces of the humeral epicondyles. Structure Present in the Cubital Fossa Contents from Medial to Lateral: (Mnemonic: MBBS) M edian nerve B rachial artery division to ulnar and radial artery B iceps brachii tendon S uperficial branch of radial nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 733; Solution Pages 760-762","sourcePage":733,"sourcePageEnd":733},{"id":"anatomy-19-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following correctly pairs a cutaneous nerve with its corresponding area of supply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"X-Median nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Z-Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Y-Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"X-Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. X-Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Cutaneous Nerve Supply of hand: Nerve Cutaneous Supply Ulnar Nerve The palmar and dorsal aspects of the medial 1½ digits and adjacent palm. Medial side of the dorsum of the hand. Median Nerve Lateral 3 ½ digits on the anterior side and 3 ½ nail beds on the posterior side, along with the thenar eminence and the skin over the palm. Radial Nerve Lateral & dorsum of the hand, 3 1⁄2 fingers, excluding the nail beds on the back of the hand - Tip of index finger - Median nerve - Tip of 5th finger - Ulnar nerve - 1st web space - Radial nerve","sourceRef":"Question Pages 733-734; Solution Pages 762-763","sourcePage":733,"sourcePageEnd":734},{"id":"anatomy-19-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A patient has a forearm laceration that results in an inability to flex the distal phalanges of the index and middle fingers. The affected muscle has dual nerve supply. Identify the muscle injured.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum superficialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Palmaris longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Flexor digitorum profundus","explanation":"- Flexor digitorum profundus is responsible for flexing the distal phalanges of the fingers . - This muscle has dual nerve supply: Median nerve: Innervates the lateral half , corresponding to the index and middle fingers. Ulnar nerve: Innervates the medial half , corresponding to the ring and little fingers. - Median nerve: Innervates the lateral half , corresponding to the index and middle fingers. - Ulnar nerve: Innervates the medial half , corresponding to the ring and little fingers. - Flexor digitorum profundus is a deep muscle of the hand responsible for flexing the distal phalanges following the action of the flexor digitorum superficialis (Option A) , which flexes the middle phalanges and is the main muscle for gripping. - Median nerve: Innervates the lateral half , corresponding to the index and middle fingers. - Ulnar nerve: Innervates the medial half , corresponding to the ring and little fingers. Flexor pollicis longus (Option B) is a deep muscle that aids in the flexion of the distal phalanx of the thumb and is solely innervated by the anterior interosseous nerve. Palmaris longus (Option C) is a superficial muscle that assists in wrist flexion and is innervated by the median nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 734; Solution Pages 763-765","sourcePage":734,"sourcePageEnd":734},{"id":"anatomy-19-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 46-year-old woman presents with difficulty in thumb abduction and weak opposition. She gives a history of repetitive wrist movements. Which anterior rami nerve roots are likely involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C5 - C7"},{"letter":"B","text":"C5, C6"},{"letter":"C","text":"C8, T1"},{"letter":"D","text":"C5 - T1"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. C5 - T1","explanation":"- Median nerve , which innervates the muscles responsible for thumb abduction and opposition , is formed from the anterior rami of spinal nerves C5 to T1, derived from the brachial plexus. - It primarily supplies the anterior forearm muscles and the thenar eminence of the hand, which includes: Muscles of the Thenar eminence: Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. Lumbricals (Lateral 2): Responsible for flexion at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and extension at the interphalangeal (IP) joints of the 2nd and 3rd digits - Muscles of the Thenar eminence: Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. - Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. - Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. - Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. - Lumbricals (Lateral 2): Responsible for flexion at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and extension at the interphalangeal (IP) joints of the 2nd and 3rd digits - Muscles of the Thenar eminence: Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. - Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. - Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. - Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. - Lumbricals (Lateral 2): Responsible for flexion at the metacarpophalangeal (MCP) joints and extension at the interphalangeal (IP) joints of the 2nd and 3rd digits - Abductor Pollicis Brevis: Responsible for thumb abduction. - Flexor Pollicis Brevis: Assists in thumb flexion. - Opponens Pollicis: Enables opposition of the thumb. - Sensory supply: Lateral 3½ digits in the palmar aspect & tip of same digits on the dorsum. Brachial Plexus: - The brachial plexus is a network of nerves formed by the anterior rami of the lower four cervical nerves ( C5, C6, C7, C8 ) and the first thoracic nerve ( T1 ) - Components of the Brachial Plexus: Roots : Five roots (C5 to T1), deep to the scalenus anterior muscle. Trunks : Upper trunk : C5 and C6 Middle trunk : C7 Lower trunk: C8 and T1 Divisions : Located behind the clavicle. Cords : Three cords form from the divisions Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Roots : Five roots (C5 to T1), deep to the scalenus anterior muscle. - Trunks : Upper trunk : C5 and C6 Middle trunk : C7 Lower trunk: C8 and T1 - Upper trunk : C5 and C6 - Middle trunk : C7 - Lower trunk: C8 and T1 - Divisions : Located behind the clavicle. - Cords : Three cords form from the divisions Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. - Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. - Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Roots : Five roots (C5 to T1), deep to the scalenus anterior muscle. - Trunks : Upper trunk : C5 and C6 Middle trunk : C7 Lower trunk: C8 and T1 - Upper trunk : C5 and C6 - Middle trunk : C7 - Lower trunk: C8 and T1 - Divisions : Located behind the clavicle. - Cords : Three cords form from the divisions Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. - Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. - Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Upper trunk : C5 and C6 - Middle trunk : C7 - Lower trunk: C8 and T1 - Lateral cord: Anterior divisions of the upper and middle trunks. - Medial cord: Anterior division of the lower trunk. - Posterior cord: Posterior divisions of all three trunks - Branches: From Roots Long thoracic nerve (C5, C6, C7) Dorsal scapular nerve (C5) From Trunks Suprascapular nerve (C5, C6) Nerve to subclavius (C5, C6) Lateral Cord Lateral pectoral nerve (C5, C6, C7) Lateral root of median nerve (C5, C6, C7) Musculocutaneous nerve (C5, C6, C7) Medial Cord Medial pectoral nerve (C8, T1) Medial cutaneous nerve of arm (C8, T1) Medial cutaneous nerve of forearm (C8, T1) Medial root of median nerve (C8, T1) Ulnar nerve (C7, C8, T1) Posterior Cord Radial nerve (C5, C6, C7, C8, T1) Axillary nerve (C5, C6) Thoraco-dorsal nerve (C6, C7, C8) Upper subscapular nerve (C5, C6) Lower subscapular nerve (C5, C6)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 734-735; Solution Pages 765-769","sourcePage":734,"sourcePageEnd":735},{"id":"anatomy-19-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-19","chapter":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topic":"Lesson 19 - Upper Limb Nerve Supply and their Lesions","topicIndex":19,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following nerves innervates the muscle indicated below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial branch of radial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior interosseous nerve","explanation":"The muscle marked ‘X’ is the Pronator quadratus , one of the deep muscles of the forearm and is supplied by the Anterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the Median nerve . Median nerve: - Begins in the axilla, at the level of the lower border of the teres major muscle, from the medial and lateral cords of the brachial plexus. - It descends through the arm without branching, travelling alongside the brachial artery. - It passes through the cubital fossa, enters the forearm between the heads of the pronator teres , and runs deep to the flexor digitorum superficialis. - In the hand, it enters through the carpal tunnel , supplying the thenar muscles (except adductor pollicis) and 2nd and 3rd lumbricals . - Motor Branches of the Median Nerve: Median Nerve (C5 to■■ T1- root value) supply of forearm Muscle Action Superficial muscles (Supplied by Pronator teres Pronation of the forearm. Median Nerve itself) Flexor carpi radialis Palmaris longus Flexion of the wrist. Flexor digitorum superficialis Flexion of the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) joints of the fingers. Deep muscles (supplied by Anterior Flexor digitorum profundus (lateral Flexion of the distal interphalangeal interosseous nerve) half) (DIP) joints of the index and middle fingers. Flexor pollicis longus Flexion of the thumb. Pronator quadratus Pronation of the forearm. Ulnar nerve (Option A) primarily innervates the intrinsic muscles of the hand & provides sensory innervation to the skin over the Hypothenar eminence and the central area of the palm. Posterior interosseous nerve (Option B) is a deep branch of the radial nerve and it mostly supplies the posterior aspect of the forearm. Superficial branch of radial nerve (Option C) is cutaneous branch runs along the forearm, supplying the dorsal aspect of the hand , the lateral side of the forearm , and the skin over the thumb and adjacent fingers.","sourceRef":"Question Page 735; Solution Pages 769-770","sourcePage":735,"sourcePageEnd":735},{"id":"anatomy-20-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35-year-old woman presents after a motor vehicle accident with a clavicle fracture. During surgery, the surgeon carefully avoids damaging nearby blood vessels, including those from the thyrocervical trunk. Which artery is not a branch of the thyrocervical trunk and is less likely to be encountered during this procedure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior Thyroid Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Suprascapular Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial Cervical Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dorsal Scapular Artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Dorsal Scapular Artery","explanation":"Dorsal Scapular Artery arises from the third part of the subclavian artery or may originate from the transverse cervical artery , but it is not a branch of the thyrocervical trunk. Thyrocervical trunk , a branch of the subclavian artery, typically gives rise to three main branches: - Inferior thyroid artery - Suprascapular artery - Transverse cervical artery (also called superficial cervical artery) Subclavian artery : - Right Subclavian Artery: From the brachiocephalic trunk (posterior or superior to the right sternoclavicular joint). - Left Subclavian Artery: Directly from the arch of the aorta (after the brachiocephalic trunk and left common carotid artery). - Clinical Significance of Subclavian artery: Palpation and Compression: Can be palpated in the supraclavicular triangle; compressed to control upper limb bleeding. Subclavian Steal Syndrome: Proximal narrowing of the subclavian artery leads to retrograde flow, which can cause dizziness or arm fatigue. Thoracic Outlet Syndrome: Compression of the subclavian artery and the lower part of the brachial plexus (joined at the first rib by the subclavian vessels) in this condition can cause pain, numbness, or weakness in the arm or hand. - Palpation and Compression: Can be palpated in the supraclavicular triangle; compressed to control upper limb bleeding. - Subclavian Steal Syndrome: Proximal narrowing of the subclavian artery leads to retrograde flow, which can cause dizziness or arm fatigue. - Thoracic Outlet Syndrome: Compression of the subclavian artery and the lower part of the brachial plexus (joined at the first rib by the subclavian vessels) in this condition can cause pain, numbness, or weakness in the arm or hand. - Palpation and Compression: Can be palpated in the supraclavicular triangle; compressed to control upper limb bleeding. - Subclavian Steal Syndrome: Proximal narrowing of the subclavian artery leads to retrograde flow, which can cause dizziness or arm fatigue. - Thoracic Outlet Syndrome: Compression of the subclavian artery and the lower part of the brachial plexus (joined at the first rib by the subclavian vessels) in this condition can cause pain, numbness, or weakness in the arm or hand.","sourceRef":"Question Page 771; Solution Pages 778-780","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":771},{"id":"anatomy-20-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 40-year-old man undergoes surgery for a tumour in the axilla. During the procedure, the surgeon identifies the second part of the axillary artery and its branches. Which of the following arteries arises from this segment of the axillary artery and must be carefully preserved during the dissection?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior thoracic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thoraco-acromial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subscapular artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior circumflex humeral artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Thoraco-acromial artery","explanation":"- Axillary artery is divided into three parts based on its position relative to the pectoralis minor muscle : First is medial, the second is behind, and the third is lateral to the muscle. - Thoracoacromial artery arises from the second part of the axillary artery, which is located posterior to the pectoralis minor muscle. - Superior thoracic artery arises from the first part , while the subscapular and anterior circumflex humeral arteries arise from the third part.","sourceRef":"Question Page 771; Solution Pages 780-781","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":771},{"id":"anatomy-20-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The following image illustrates the blood supply to the mammary gland. Identify the artery labelled \"A.\" **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral Thoracic Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal Thoracic Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior Intercostal Arteries"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subscapular Artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Internal Thoracic Artery","explanation":"The artery labeled \"A\" in the image is the Internal Thoracic Artery . Internal thoracic artery supplies the anteromedial part of the breast through its medial mammary branches. Blood supply of the Mammary gland Source of Blood Supply Arteries Description Internal Thoracic Artery (Option B) Medial Mammary Branches Originates from the subclavian artery and supplies the anteromedial part of the breast. Axillary Artery Lateral Thoracic Artery (Option A) It gives off lateral mammary branches to supply the lateral aspect of the breast. Subscapular Artery (Option D) Contributes to the blood supply to lateral and posterior aspects of the breast Anterior Intercostal Arteries (Option 2nd to 4th Intercostal Arteries Enlarges during lactation; Supplies C) superior regions of the breast","sourceRef":"Question Pages 771-772; Solution Pages 781-783","sourcePage":771,"sourcePageEnd":772},{"id":"anatomy-20-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 35-year-old patient presents with a shoulder injury following a fall. During a physical examination, the physician palpates the brachial artery to assess the blood supply to the arm. At which anatomical landmark does this artery begin as a continuation of the axillary artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower border of the teres minor"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upper border of the teres major"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lower border of the teres major"},{"letter":"D","text":"Upper border of the teres minor"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lower border of the teres major","explanation":"Brachial artery is a vital artery in the upper limb originating at the lower border of the teres major muscle and is a direct continuation of the axillary artery. Brachial Artery Aspect Description Origin Continuation of the axillary artery at the inferior border of the teres major muscle. Course Courses distally, spiralling from medial to anterior, lying midway between the medial and lateral epicondyles of the humerus. Terminates about 1 cm distal to the elbow into radial and ulnar arteries. Major Branches Profunda Brachii Artery : Supplies posterior compartment muscles. Nutrient Artery: Supplies the humerus. Superior Ulnar Collateral Artery: Anastomoses with posterior ulnar recurrent artery. Inferior Ulnar Collateral Artery: Anastomoses with anterior ulnar recurrent artery. Deltoid Artery: Supplies the deltoid muscle. Muscular Arteries: Supply various arm muscles. Radial Artery: Supplies the lateral forearm and contributes to the palmar arch. Ulnar Artery: Supplies the medial forearm and contributes to the palmar arch.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 772-773; Solution Pages 783-784","sourcePage":772,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"anatomy-20-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents with intermittent pain and numbness in his right hand, especially after gripping. The physician suspects vascular insufficiency. Which test is the physician likely to perform to assess the blood supply to the hand?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Allen's test"},{"letter":"B","text":"Phalen's test"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tinel's sign"},{"letter":"D","text":"Capillary refill test"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Allen's test","explanation":"Allen's test evaluates the patency of the radial and ulnar arteries supplying the hand. Allens test: - The examiner compresses both arteries at the wrist while the patient clenches their fist to drain blood from the hand. - Subsequently, pressure on one artery is released, and the return of hand colour is observed. - A rapid colour return indicates an adequate blood supply from that artery. - It ensures an adequate collateral circulation and is especially useful before procedures such as arterial blood gas sampling or harvesting the radial artery for grafting. Phalen's test (Option B) assesses carpal tunnel syndrome . The patient flexes their wrists by pressing the backs of their hands together for 60 seconds . A positive test results in tingling, numbness, or pain in the median nerve distribution, suggesting carpal tunnel compression. Tinel's sign (OptionC) is another test for carpal tunnel syndrome. The examiner taps over the median nerve at the wrist. If this elicits a tingling or \"pins and needles\" sensation in the fingers supplied by the median nerve , it indicates nerve irritation or damage. Capillary refill test (Option D) assesses peripheral perfusion and circulatory status . The examiner presses on the nail bed or skin until it blanches (turns white), then releases. A normal capillary refill time is less than 2 seconds , which indicates adequate blood flow.","sourceRef":"Question Page 773; Solution Pages 784-787","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"anatomy-20-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 72-year-old male with a history of atherosclerosis and hypertension presents with sudden severe pain, numbness, and weakness in his left arm. Examination shows difficulty gripping and sensory loss in the hand. Which anastomosis is most critical for collateral blood flow to the left upper limb in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Suprascapular artery and dorsal scapular artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Subscapular artery and circumflex scapular artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior thoracic artery and acromiothoracic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Suprascapular artery and circumflex scapular artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Suprascapular artery and circumflex scapular artery","explanation":"The scenario describes a case of subclavian artery occlusion , in which the suprascapular artery (branch of subclavian artery) and the circumflex scapular artery (branch of axillary artery) form the most critical anastomosis for ensuring collateral blood flow to the upper limb. Anastomosis of the Scapula: - It is formed between the branches of the 1st part of the subclavian artery and the 3rd part of the axillary artery at the following two locations: Around the body of the scapula Over the acromion process Suprascapular artery (branch of the thyrocervical trunk) Acromial branch of the thoracoacromial artery (2nd part of Circumflex scapular artery (branch of the subscapular axillary). Acromial branch of the suprascapular artery (1st artery from the 3rd part of the axillary artery) Deep branch part of subclavian). Acromial branch of the posterior of the transverse cervical artery (branch of the circumflex humeral artery (3rd part of axillary). thyrocervical trunk). - Collateral circulation through scapular anastomosis: The arterial anastomoses (scapular) provide collateral circulation through which blood can flow to the limb when the subclavian or axillary artery is blocked (anywhere between 1st part of the subclavian artery and 3rd part of axillary artery). Suprascapular artery and dorsal scapular artery (Option A) are the branches of the thyrocervical trunk and transverse cervical artery , respectively; both are subclavian artery branches. Subscapular and circumflex scapular arteries (Option B) are branches of the 3rd part of the axillary artery. Superior thoracic artery and acromiothoracic artery (Option C) are axillary artery branches from the 1st and 2nd part , respectively.","sourceRef":"Question Page 773; Solution Pages 787-788","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":773},{"id":"anatomy-20-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A patient with stage 5 chronic kidney disease is preparing for hemodialysis. During the examination, tenderness is noted in the right arm, and a prominent vein is visible. The doctor asks the intern to identify the vein, hinting that it pierces the clavipectoral fascia. What vein is being referenced?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Basilic vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Brachial vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cephalic vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median cubital vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Cephalic vein","explanation":"Cephalic vein is the preferred choice for hemodialysis in cases of renal failure. It pierces the clavipectoral fascia before its termination. Superficial veins of the upper limb: Deep veins of the upper limb: - Begin as small venae comitantes alongside the digital veins, which then continue proximally to form the superficial and deep palmar arches . Venae comitantes , which accompany radial, ulnar, and brachial arteries Venae comitantes of the brachial artery Axillary vein (continuation of the basilic vein, drains into the subclavian vein) - Venae comitantes , which accompany radial, ulnar, and brachial arteries - Venae comitantes of the brachial artery - Axillary vein (continuation of the basilic vein, drains into the subclavian vein) - Venae comitantes , which accompany radial, ulnar, and brachial arteries - Venae comitantes of the brachial artery - Axillary vein (continuation of the basilic vein, drains into the subclavian vein)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 773-774; Solution Pages 788-790","sourcePage":773,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"anatomy-20-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old female presents to the clinic following her mammogram results, revealing a palpable mass in her left breast and mild discomfort. Examination shows enlarged lymph nodes in the left axilla. Which of the following is the terminal group of axillary lymph nodes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Central"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pectoral"},{"letter":"C","text":"Apical"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supraclavicular"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Apical","explanation":"The apical group of lymph nodes is the terminal group of axillary lymph nodes. Lymphatic Drainage of the Mammary Gland: - It includes: Lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels Subdiaphragmatic and subperitoneal lymph plexuses - Lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels - Subdiaphragmatic and subperitoneal lymph plexuses - Lymph nodes and lymphatic vessels - Subdiaphragmatic and subperitoneal lymph plexuses - Drainage: Lymph Nodes/Plexus Part of breast Anterior axillary or pectoral group of lymph nodes (Option Lateral quadrants B) Internal mammary lymph nodes Medial quadrants Posterior intercostal nodes Lower lateral quadrant Subdiaphragmatic and subperitoneal lymph plexuses Lower medial quadrant Apical group of axillary lymph nodes (Option C) Deep surface - Subareolar plexus of Sappey is a network of lymphatic vessels located deep in the areola, which primarily drains lymphatic fluid from the breast into the anterior (pectoral) group of lymph nodes. - Axillary lymph nodes drain into supraclavicular lymph nodes. (Option D) - Clinical Significance: “Peau d’orange” appearance of the skin in the breast carcinoma due to obstruction of superficial lymphatics. Krukenberg’s tumour occurs when lymphatic vessels from the breast connect with the subperitoneal lymph plexus, allowing cancer cells to travel to the ovaries and form a secondary tumour. - “Peau d’orange” appearance of the skin in the breast carcinoma due to obstruction of superficial lymphatics. - Krukenberg’s tumour occurs when lymphatic vessels from the breast connect with the subperitoneal lymph plexus, allowing cancer cells to travel to the ovaries and form a secondary tumour. - “Peau d’orange” appearance of the skin in the breast carcinoma due to obstruction of superficial lymphatics. - Krukenberg’s tumour occurs when lymphatic vessels from the breast connect with the subperitoneal lymph plexus, allowing cancer cells to travel to the ovaries and form a secondary tumour.","sourceRef":"Question Page 774; Solution Pages 790-793","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"anatomy-20-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 32-year-old male presents to the emergency department with swelling and pain in his right hand, particularly around the base of the thumb, after puncturing it with a nail. Examination shows tenderness, swelling, and limited thumb movement. An infection is diagnosed. Which statement about this space is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It has a triangular shape"},{"letter":"B","text":"Infection can spread through the radial bursa"},{"letter":"C","text":"Associated short muscles of thumb anteriorly"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior wall is formed by the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th lumbricals"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior wall is formed by the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th lumbricals","explanation":"The case is diagnosed as an infection of the thenar space , where the 1st lumbrical forms the anterior wall , while the 2nd, 3rd, and 4th lumbricals constitute the anterior wall of the mid-palmar space. Midpalmar Space vs Thenar Space: Aspect Midpalmar Space Thenar Space Shape Triangular Triangular (Option A) Site Under the inner half of the hollow of Under the outer half of the hollow of the palm the palm Extent Distal margin of the flexor Distal margin of the flexor retinaculum (proximal) Distal palmar retinaculum (proximal) Proximal crease (distal) transverse palmar crease (distal) Communications Forearm space of Parona (proximal) Forearm space of Parona (proximal) Fascial sheaths of the 3rd and 4th The fascial sheath of the first lumbricals (distal) lumbrical (distal) Anterior wall Palmar aponeurosis Flexor tendons Short muscles of the thumb (Option of 3rd, 4th and 5th digits Medial three C) Flexor tendons of the index finger ( 2nd, 3rd, and 4th ) lumbricals 1st lumbrical Palmar aponeurosis (Option D) Posterior wall Fascia covering interossei and Transverse head of adductor pollicis metacarpals Lateral wall Intermediate palmar septum Tendon of flexor pollicis longus with radial bursa Lateral palmar septum Medial wall Medial palmar septum Intermediate palmar septum Infection inlet Ulnar bursa Radial bursa (Option B) Synovial sheath of the index finger Incision for drainage 3rd or 4th web space 1st web (posteriorly)","sourceRef":"Question Page 774; Solution Pages 793-795","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":774},{"id":"anatomy-20-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A teenager presents with severe pain and swelling in the tip of her right index finger after slamming it into a door. Examination reveals significant swelling, erythema, tenderness, increased sensitivity, and difficulty moving the finger. An infection of the structure marked ‘X’ is diagnosed. What is most likely? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Herpetic Whitlow"},{"letter":"B","text":"Felon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Paronychia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tendon Sheath Infection"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Felon","explanation":"- The given scenario is a case of Felon or infection in the Digital Pulp Space , labelled as “X” in the image. - The tips of the fingers and thumb contain subcutaneous fat organised into compartments, separated by fibrous septa extending to the terminal phalanx's periosteum. - Drainage procedure: A lateral incision should be made to access all loculi while carefully preserving tactile sensation on the dorsal aspect of the finger. - Complication: AVN ( avascular necrosis ) of distal 4-5th of the terminal phalanx due to occlusion of the digital artery due to pressure. Herpetic Whitlow (Option A) is a viral infection characterised by painful blisters on the fingertip. It typically follows exposure to the herpes virus rather than trauma. It usually presents with vesicular lesions rather than localised swelling and tenderness. Paronychia (Option C) affects the nail fold and presents with redness, swelling, and pain around the nail margin. It does not involve the digital pulp space and is not typically associated with severe fingertip swelling. Tendon Sheath Infection (Option D) involves the flexor tendon sheath and presents with diffuse swelling, pain, and difficulty moving the entire finger. It generally does not cause localised tenderness at the fingertip, distinguishing it from a felon.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 774-775; Solution Pages 795-796","sourcePage":774,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"anatomy-20-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 45-year-old patient is undergoing surgery on the forearm. During the procedure, the surgeon ligates the brachial artery near the elbow. Which anastomotic site helps maintain blood flow to the forearm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In front of the lateral epicondyle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Behind the lateral epicondyle"},{"letter":"C","text":"In front of the medial epicondyle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Behind the medial epicondyle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. In front of the medial epicondyle","explanation":"In front of the medial epicondyle : This anastomosis (inferior ulnar collateral ↔ anterior ulnar recurrent) ensures adequate blood flow to the forearm when the brachial artery is ligated near the elbow. Anastomoses Around the Elbow Joint: - It Links brachial artery with upper ends of radial and ulnar arteries. - Supply ligaments and bones of the elbow joint. - Anastomotic Locations : In Front of the Lateral Epicondyle: Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). Behind the Lateral Epicondyle : Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). In Front of the Medial Epicondyle : Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). Behind the Medial Epicondyle : Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - In Front of the Lateral Epicondyle: Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). - Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). - Behind the Lateral Epicondyle : Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). - Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). - In Front of the Medial Epicondyle : Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Behind the Medial Epicondyle : Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Clinical Relevance : Brachial Pulsation : Palpated medial to the biceps tendon in front of the elbow for blood pressure measurement. Brachial Compression : Best applied mid-arm (over coracobrachialis tendon) for hemostasis. Blood Collection : Brachial artery is a site for bloodgas analysis. - Brachial Pulsation : Palpated medial to the biceps tendon in front of the elbow for blood pressure measurement. - Brachial Compression : Best applied mid-arm (over coracobrachialis tendon) for hemostasis. - Blood Collection : Brachial artery is a site for blood gas analysis. - In Front of the Lateral Epicondyle: Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). - Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). - Behind the Lateral Epicondyle : Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). - Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). - In Front of the Medial Epicondyle : Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Behind the Medial Epicondyle : Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Radial Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Radial Recurrent (Radial Artery). - Middle Collateral (Profunda Brachii) ↔ Interosseous Recurrent (Posterior Interosseous Artery). - Inferior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Anterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Superior Ulnar Collateral (Brachial Artery) ↔ Posterior Ulnar Recurrent (Ulnar Artery). - Brachial Pulsation : Palpated medial to the biceps tendon in front of the elbow for blood pressure measurement. - Brachial Compression : Best applied mid-arm (over coracobrachialis tendon) for hemostasis. - Blood Collection : Brachial artery is a site for blood gas analysis. In front of the lateral epicondyle (Option A) : Involves radial collateral and radial recurrent arteries, which supply lateral elbow structures but not sufficient for forearm. Behind the lateral epicondyle (Option B) : Middle collateral and interosseous recurrent arteries provide supply behind the elbow but do not compensate fully for brachial artery ligation. Behind the medial epicondyle (Option D) : Superior ulnar collateral and posterior ulnar recurrent arteries supply posterior elbow , not anterior forearm structures.","sourceRef":"Question Page 775; Solution Pages 796-799","sourcePage":775,"sourcePageEnd":775},{"id":"anatomy-20-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A patient sustains a deep laceration to the palm, resulting in significant bleeding. The surgeon identifies damage to an arterial structure located superficial to the flexor tendons and supplying the medial 3½ digits. Which arterial structure is most likely injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superficial palmar arch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deep palmar arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superficial palmar arch","explanation":"Superficial palmar arch is located deep to the palmar aponeurosis but superficial to the flexor tendons , and it supplies the medial 3½ digits via its common digital branches. Superficial Palmar Arch Deep Palmar Arch Anastomosis Between ulnar and radial arteries. Between radial and ulnar arteries. Location Deep to palmaris brevis and Deep to flexor tendons, on proximal aponeurosis. Crosses palm over metacarpals and interossei. flexor tendons and median nerve Formed by Ulnar artery (superficial branch) and Radial artery (terminal part) and radial artery (superficial branch). ulnar artery (deep branch). Branches Three common digital arteries Three palmar metacarpal arteries (medial 3½ digits). One proper digital (supply medial metacarpals). Three branch. Lateral digital branches join perforating digital arteries palmar metacarpal arteries from (anastomose with dorsal metacarpal deep arch. arteries). Recurrent branch (supplies carpal bones and joints, ends in palmar carpal arch). Deep palmar arch (Option B) : Lies deep to the flexor tendons and primarily supplies the proximal metacarpals, not the medial 3½ digits. Radial artery (Option C) : Supplies the lateral side of the forearm and hand but does not provide primary branches to the medial digits. Ulnar artery (Option D) : Contributes to the superficial palmar arch but does not directly supply the medial digits; damage here would involve the arch rather than the artery itself.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 775-776; Solution Pages 799-800","sourcePage":775,"sourcePageEnd":776},{"id":"anatomy-20-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 28-year-old patient presents with weakness in thumb flexion and loss of forearm pronation after a deep laceration to the anterior forearm. Imaging reveals disruption of an artery accompanying the anterior interosseous nerve. Which artery is most likely damaged?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Common interosseous artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior interosseous artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior interosseous artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior interosseous artery","explanation":"Anterior interosseous artery runs with the anterior interosseous nerve along the interosseous membrane, supplying deep forearm muscles like the flexor pollicis longus and pronator quadratus. Injury here matches the clinical findings of impaired thumb flexion and pronation. Interosseous Membrane and Arteries: - Common Interosseous Artery : Arises below the radial tuberosity, divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. - Arises below the radial tuberosity, divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. - Anterior Interosseous Artery : Deep artery on the forearm, runs along the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous nerve. Pierces the membrane at the pronator quadratus to enter the extensor compartment. Branches: Muscular branches, nutrient branches to radius and ulna, median artery with the median nerve. - Deep artery on the forearm, runs along the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous nerve. - Pierces the membrane at the pronator quadratus to enter the extensor compartment. - Branches: Muscular branches, nutrient branches to radius and ulna, median artery with the median nerve. - Posterior Interosseous Artery : Gives off interosseous recurrent artery (anastomoses with middle collateral artery). Passes through a gap in the interosseous membrane to the back of the forearm. - Gives off interosseous recurrent artery (anastomoses with middle collateral artery). - Passes through a gap in the interosseous membrane to the back of the forearm. - Arises below the radial tuberosity, divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries. - Deep artery on the forearm, runs along the interosseous membrane with the anterior interosseous nerve. - Pierces the membrane at the pronator quadratus to enter the extensor compartment. - Branches: Muscular branches, nutrient branches to radius and ulna, median artery with the median nerve. - Gives off interosseous recurrent artery (anastomoses with middle collateral artery). - Passes through a gap in the interosseous membrane to the back of the forearm. Radial artery (Option A) : Supplies the lateral forearm and hand but does not accompany the anterior interosseous nerve or supply the affected muscles. Common interosseous artery (Option B) : Divides into anterior and posterior interosseous arteries early and does not accompany the nerve to the forearm. Posterior interosseous artery (Option D) : Supplies extensor muscles of the forearm and is unrelated to the anterior compartment or anterior interosseous nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 776; Solution Pages 800-801","sourcePage":776,"sourcePageEnd":776},{"id":"anatomy-20-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the median cubital vein is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It connects the cephalic vein to the basilic vein."},{"letter":"B","text":"It runs obliquely downward and laterally across the forearm."},{"letter":"C","text":"It is superficial to the bicipital aponeurosis, which separates it from the brachial artery."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is the primary choice for venipuncture and catheterization due to its stability."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. It runs obliquely downward and laterally across the forearm.","explanation":"Median cubital vein runs obliquely upward and medially , not downward and laterally Median Cubital Vein: - Anatomy : Connects cephalic to basilic vein. (Option A ruled out) Origin: Cephalic vein (2.5 cm below elbow). Ends: Basilic vein (2.5 cm above medial epicondyle). Runs obliquely upward and medially. - Connects cephalic to basilic vein. (Option A ruled out) - Origin: Cephalic vein (2.5 cm below elbow). - Ends: Basilic vein (2.5 cm above medial epicondyle). - Runs obliquely upward and medially. - Relations : Superficial to bicipital aponeurosis (separating it from brachial artery). (Option C ruled out) Receives tributaries from the median forearm vein. Stabilized by a perforator vein connecting to deep veins. - Superficial to bicipital aponeurosis (separating it from brachial artery). (Option C ruled out) - Receives tributaries from the median forearm vein. - Stabilized by a perforator vein connecting to deep veins. - Clinical Significance : Primary Choice : Ideal for venipuncture and catheterization due to stability. (Option D ruled out) - Primary Choice : Ideal for venipuncture and catheterization due to stability. (Option D ruled out) - Connects cephalic to basilic vein. (Option A ruled out) - Origin: Cephalic vein (2.5 cm below elbow). - Ends: Basilic vein (2.5 cm above medial epicondyle). - Runs obliquely upward and medially. - Superficial to bicipital aponeurosis (separating it from brachial artery). (Option C ruled out) - Receives tributaries from the median forearm vein. - Stabilized by a perforator vein connecting to deep veins. - Primary Choice : Ideal for venipuncture and catheterization due to stability. (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 776; Solution Pages 801-803","sourcePage":776,"sourcePageEnd":776},{"id":"anatomy-20-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-20","chapter":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topic":"Lesson 20 - Upper Limb Arterial supply, Venous Drainage, Lymphatic Drainage, & Hand Spaces","topicIndex":20,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 35-year-old woman with breast cancer presents with enlarged lymph nodes in her axilla. Biopsy reveals metastatic spread. Which group of axillary lymph nodes is most likely the initial site of cancer drainage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior (Pectoral) nodes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior (Scapular) nodes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Apical (Infraclavicular) nodes"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior (Pectoral) nodes","explanation":"Anterior (pectoral) nodes are located along the lateral thoracic vessels and primarily drain the anterior trunk wall and breast , making them the first site for lymphatic drainage from breast tissue. Axillary Lymph Nodes: - 20-30 nodes in axillary fibrofatty tissue. - Divided into five groups based on location and drainage. Groups : Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. - Groups : Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. - Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. - Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. - Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. - Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. - Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. - Clinical Importance : Drains upper limb, breast, and body walls above the umbilicus. Common site for infections and malignancies (e.g., breast cancer). Axillary Abscess : Incision through axillary floor near medial wall to avoid vessels. - Drains upper limb, breast, and body walls above the umbilicus. - Common site for infections and malignancies (e.g., breast cancer). - Axillary Abscess : Incision through axillary floor near medial wall to avoid vessels. 20-30 nodes in axillary fibrofatty tissue. Divided into five groups based on location and drainage. - Groups : Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. - Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. - Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. - Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. - Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. - Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. Groups : - Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. - Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. - Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. - Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. - Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. Anterior (Pectoral) : Along lateral thoracic vessels; drains anterior trunk wall and breast. Posterior (Scapular) : Along subscapular vessels; drains posterior trunk wall and axillary breast tail. Lateral : Medial to axillary vein; drains upper limb. Central : In axillary fat; drains anterior, posterior, and lateral groups. Apical (Infraclavicular) : Behind pectoralis minor; drains central nodes, upper breast, and thumb. - Drains upper limb, breast, and body walls above the umbilicus. - Common site for infections and malignancies (e.g., breast cancer). - Axillary Abscess : Incision through axillary floor near medial wall to avoid vessels. Posterior (Scapular) nodes (Option B) : Drains the posterior trunk wall and the axillary tail of the breast; unlikely to be the first site of drainage for the main breast tissue. Lateral nodes (Option C) : Drains the upper limb and does not directly receive lymph from the breast. Apical (Infraclavicular) nodes (Option D) : Drains central nodes and other axillary groups but is not the initial site for primary breast lymphatic drainage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 776-777; Solution Pages 803-805","sourcePage":776,"sourcePageEnd":777},{"id":"anatomy-21-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presented with tingling sensation of lateral 3 digits with loss of sensation of base of thumb on the dorsal aspect of the hand. Which is the nerve involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"AIN"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Radial nerve","explanation":"The radial nerve innervates the dorsal aspect of the hand, including the base of the thumb and the lateral three digits. The symptoms described (tingling and loss of sensation in this specific distribution) are highly suggestive of radial nerve involvement. Radial nerve lesions: Type Injury Clinical Features Low Lesions Fractures or dislocations at the elbow Cannot extend the metacarpophalang eal joints Thumb weakness Radial deviation of the wrist High Lesions Humerus fractures or prolonged Wrist drop Inability to extend MCP tourniquet pressure joints or elevate thumb Sensory loss around the anatomical snuffbox Very High Lesions Around the shoulder Weakness of the wrist and hand Paralysis of triceps and absence of triceps reflex Ulnar nerve (Option B): Affects the medial 1½ fingers, not the lateral 3 digits. Median nerve (Option C): Supplies palmar sensation to the lateral 3½ digits, but NOT the dorsal base of the thumb. AIN (Option D): The Anterior Interosseous Nerve is a branch of the median nerve, but it's a motor nerve. It supplies muscles in the forearm. It doesn't have a sensory component related to the described symptoms.","sourceRef":"Question Page 806; Solution Pages 847-848","sourcePage":806,"sourcePageEnd":806},{"id":"anatomy-21-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following nerves supplies the muscle that is involved in the moment given below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinal accessory nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Long thoracic nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spinal branch of axillary nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Radial nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Spinal accessory nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Spinal accessory nerve: - The movement shown in the image is shrugging, which is carried out by the trapezius muscles and supplied by the spinal accessory nerve. - Lesion of the accessory nerve or paralysis of the trapezius leads to drooping of shoulders and difficulty in shrugging and weakness of overhead abduction. Incorrect Options: - Option B, C & D: These nerves are not associated with the movement given in the question above.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 806-807; Solution Page 848","sourcePage":806,"sourcePageEnd":807},{"id":"anatomy-21-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A road traffic accident patient has presented to you with features of a nerve injury involving right upper limb.On examination,the patient is able to carry out flexion at metacarpo-phalangeal joint and extension at interphalangeal joint.The most likely nerve involved is ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Radial nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Radial nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D - Radial nerve: - The most likely nerve involved is the posterior interosseous nerve, which is a branch of the radial nerve. - The lumbricals and interossei can carry out flexion at MCP and extension at IP joints. Incorrect Options: Option A & C - Median nerve & Ulnar nerve: - The median nerve and ulnar nerve supply the lumbricals and interossei, which are involved in flexion at metacarpophalangeal joint and extension at the interphalangeal joint, and since these actions are present, these nerves are intact Option B - Anterior interosseous nerve: - The anterior interosseous nerve supplies the Flexor Digitorum Profundus muscle and involved in the flexion and extension of the index and middle finger. - Since the flexion of all the fingers is intact, this nerve is intact.","sourceRef":"Question Page 807; Solution Pages 848-849","sourcePage":807,"sourcePageEnd":807},{"id":"anatomy-21-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of arm is done by which muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectoralis minor"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pectoralis Major"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subclavius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serratus anterior"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pectoralis Major","explanation":"Correct Option B - Pectoralis Major: - his muscle is responsible for flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm at the shoulder joint. It's a key player in these movements. Incorrect Options: Option A - Pectoralis minor: This muscle is involved in stabilizing the scapula but doesn't play a significant role in the described movements. Option C - Subclavius: The subclavius muscle helps stabilize and depress the clavicle but is not a prime mover for the described actions. Option D - Serratus anterior: This muscle is more involved in protraction and upward rotation of the scapula, not the flexion, adduction, and medial rotation of the arm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 807; Solution Page 849","sourcePage":807,"sourcePageEnd":807},{"id":"anatomy-21-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A patient presents with hypothenar muscle wasting and loss of sensation of medial one and a half digits. Which of the following nerve is involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median Nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ulnar Nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ulnar Nerve: - The hypothenar muscles and the sensation of the medial one and a half digits are innervated by the ulnar nerve. Damage to the ulnar nerve can result in these symptoms. Incorrect Options: Option B - Median Nerve: The median nerve is responsible for innervating muscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm and the thenar muscles, not the hypothenar muscles. Option C - Radial Nerve: The radial nerve primarily innervates the muscles of the posterior compartment of the arm and forearm. It's not associated with the symptoms described. Option D - Musculocutaneous Nerve: This nerve innervates the muscles in the anterior compartment of the arm but is not involved in the symptoms described.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 807-808; Solution Pages 849-850","sourcePage":807,"sourcePageEnd":808},{"id":"anatomy-21-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which muscle originates from medial epicondyle of humerus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supinator"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pronator Teres"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pronator Quadratus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Brachioradialis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pronator Teres","explanation":"Correct Option B - Pronator Teres: - This muscle originates from the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Incorrect Options: Option A - Supinator: It originates from the lateral epicondyle of the humerus. Option C - Pronator Quadratus: This muscle originates from the distal part of the anterior ulna, not from the humerus. Option D - Brachioradialis: It originates from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus, not the medial epicondyle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 808; Solution Page 850","sourcePage":808,"sourcePageEnd":808},{"id":"anatomy-21-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":7,"question":"An injury to the shown area can lead to fracture of which bone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lunate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trapezium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hamate"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Scaphoid","explanation":"Correct Option A - Scaphoid: - The anatomical snuff box is a triangular-shaped depression on the dorsoradial aspect of the hand, and it's formed by the tendons of certain muscles. The scaphoid bone is an important player in this region. - The scaphoid bone is the most commonly fractured carpal bone, and a fall on the outstretched hand can lead to a fracture in the anatomical snuff box region. Incorrect Options: Option B - Lunate: The lunate is another carpal bone, but it's not typically associated with fractures in the anatomical snuff box. Option C - Trapezium: The trapezium is also a carpal bone, but it's not commonly associated with fractures in the anatomical snuff box region. Option D - Hamate: The hamate is located more towards the ulnar side of the hand and is not typically associated with fractures in the anatomical snuff box.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 808-809; Solution Pages 850-851","sourcePage":808,"sourcePageEnd":809},{"id":"anatomy-21-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":8,"question":"The image showed a normal elbow. What is the structure marked in the image called? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capitulum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trochles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Olecranon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Head of radius"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Capitulum","explanation":"Explanation: Not provided in source.","sourceRef":"Question Page 809; Solution Page 851","sourcePage":809,"sourcePageEnd":809},{"id":"anatomy-21-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":9,"question":"At what level is the inferior scapular angle located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T2"},{"letter":"B","text":"T4"},{"letter":"C","text":"T6"},{"letter":"D","text":"T7"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. T7","explanation":"Correct option: D - Explanation: The inferior scapular angle is at the level of the seventh thoracic vertebra (T7). - The scapula, also known as the shoulder blade, is a flat triangular bone that is located on the posterior aspect of the thorax. - The inferior angle of the scapula is the lowermost point of the bone, and it lies at approximately the level of the eighth thoracic vertebra. Incorrect option: Option A. T2: The inferior scapular angle is not at the level of the second thoracic vertebra (T2). It is located at a lower level in the thoracic region. Option B. T4: The inferior scapular angle is not at the level of the fourth thoracic vertebra (T4). It is located lower in the thoracic region. Option C. T6: The inferior scapular angle is not at the level of the sixth thoracic vertebra (T6). It is located lower in the thoracic region.","sourceRef":"Question Page 809; Solution Pages 851-852","sourcePage":809,"sourcePageEnd":809},{"id":"anatomy-21-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":10,"question":"An injury to the shown area can lead to fracture of which bone","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lunate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hamate"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trapezium"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Scaphoid","explanation":"Correct Option B: - Injuries to the anatomical snuffbox can lead to fractures of the scaphoid bone , which is one of the carpal bones located in the wrist. - The scaphoid bone is vulnerable to fractures due to its location and shape. - Fractures of the scaphoid bone can result from a fall onto an outstretched hand, particularly when the force is transmitted through the thumb. Incorrect Options : Option A . Lunate: The lunate is another carpal bone, but it is not directly associated with the anatomical snuffbox. Option C . Hamate: The hamate is a carpal bone located on the ulnar side of the wrist and is not directly associated with the anatomical snuffbox. Option D . Trapezium: The trapezium is a carpal bone located at the base of the thumb, but it is not part of the anatomical snuffbox","sourceRef":"Question Pages 809-810; Solution Pages 852-853","sourcePage":809,"sourcePageEnd":810},{"id":"anatomy-21-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Loss of flexion at the interphalangeal joint of thumb after supracondylar fracture is due to the involvement of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deep branch of ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior interosseous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D. Loss of flexion at the interphalangeal joint of the thumb after a supracondylar fracture is due to anterior interosseous nerve (AIN) involvement. AIN, a branch of the median nerve in the forearm, innervates deep flexor muscles, including the flexor pollicis longus responsible for thumb flexion. With a supracondylar humerus fracture, AIN compression or injury can weaken or paralyze the flexor pollicis longus, causing thumb joint flexion inability. Incorrect Options: Option A. Deep branch of ulnar nerve: The deep branch of the ulnar nerve innervates the hypothenar eminence and intrinsic hand muscles, not thumb interphalangeal joint flexion. Option B. Median nerve: Median nerve injury typically leads to a broader pattern of motor and sensory deficits in the hand. Option C. Posterior interosseous nerve: The posterior interosseous nerve, a branch of the radial nerve, mainly innervates extensor muscles in the forearm and doesn't play a direct role in thumb flexion.","sourceRef":"Question Page 810; Solution Page 853","sourcePage":810,"sourcePageEnd":810},{"id":"anatomy-21-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which muscle originates from the medial epicondyle of the humerus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supinator"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pronator quadratus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pronator teres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Brachioradialis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pronator teres","explanation":"Correct Option : C The muscle that originates from the medial epicondyle of the humerus is the pronator teres muscle. The pronator teres is located in the forearm and is responsible for pronating the forearm, which means rotating the forearm from a palm-up (supine) position to a palm-down (pronated) position. Incorrect Options: Option A. Supinator: The supinator muscle, situated in the forearm's posterior compartment, isn't rooted in the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Option B. Pronator quadratus: The pronator quadratus muscle, deep in the forearm, isn't linked to the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Option D. Brachioradialis: The brachioradialis muscle originates from the lateral supracondylar ridge of the humerus, not the medial epicondyle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 810-811; Solution Pages 853-854","sourcePage":810,"sourcePageEnd":811},{"id":"anatomy-21-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":13,"question":"One of the nerves supplying the upper limb, the upper lateral cutaneous nerve of arm, supplying the skin overlying the posterior border of the deltoid muscle as well as covering the long head of the triceps, is a branch of:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Axillary nerve","explanation":"Correct option: D - The axillary nerve provides sensory innervation to the skin overlying the posterior border of the deltoid muscle and extends down to cover the long head of the triceps muscle. Incorrect options: Option A . Musculocutaneous nerve: This nerve supplies motor innervation to the anterior compartment muscles of the arm and does not supply the mentioned sensory area. Option B . Radial nerve: The radial nerve supplies sensory innervation to the posterior aspect of the arm but does not specifically provide innervation to the mentioned area. Option C . Ulnar nerve: The ulnar nerve primarily supplies sensory innervation to the medial aspect of the hand and does not innervate the mentioned area.","sourceRef":"Question Page 811; Solution Page 854","sourcePage":811,"sourcePageEnd":811},{"id":"anatomy-21-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which single muscle is involved in the actions of arm's flexion, adduction and medial rotation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Serratus Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pectoralis major"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pectoralis minor"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subclavius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pectoralis major","explanation":"Correct option C : - Pectoralis major muscle is involved in the actions of arm's flexion, adduction, and medial rotation. - It is a large muscle located in the chest region and plays a significant role in various movements of the shoulder joint. Incorrect options: Option A . Serratus Anterior: This muscle is primarily involved in protraction and upward rotation of the scapula. Option C . Pectoralis minor: This muscle assists in stabilizing the scapula and has a role in protraction and downward rotation of the scapula. Option D . Subclavius: This muscle helps to stabilize and depress the clavicle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 811; Solution Pages 854-855","sourcePage":811,"sourcePageEnd":811},{"id":"anatomy-21-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":15,"question":"The rotator cuff includes the following muscles all except;","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supraspinatus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Infraspinatus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Teres major"},{"letter":"D","text":"Teres minor"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Teres major","explanation":"Correct Option: C. - Teres major: The teres major muscle is not considered part of the rotator cuff. It is a larger muscle located on the posterior side of the upper arm and is responsible for adduction and medial rotation of the arm. Incorrect Options : Options A . Supraspinatus: The supraspinatus muscle is one of the four muscles that make up the rotator cuff. It is located on the posterior side of the shoulder joint and assists in shoulder abduction. Options B . Infraspinatus: The infraspinatus muscle is another muscle that is part of the rotator cuff. It is situated below the supraspinatus muscle and is responsible for lateral rotation of the arm. Options D . Teres minor: The teres minor muscle is also included in the rotator cuff. It is located below the infraspinatus muscle and aids in lateral rotation of the arm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 811-812; Solution Page 855","sourcePage":811,"sourcePageEnd":812},{"id":"anatomy-21-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":16,"question":"The middle radioulnar joint is an example of a:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Plane synovial"},{"letter":"B","text":"Primary cartilaginous"},{"letter":"C","text":"Secondary cartilaginous"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibrous joint"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fibrous joint","explanation":"Correct Option D: - The middle radioulnar joint, located between the radius and ulna bones in the forearm, facilitates forearm rotation for pronation and supination. It's a fibrous joint known as a syndesmosis, characterized by dense connective tissue like ligaments or interosseous membranes holding the bones together. Incorrect Options: Option A: Plane synovial: Plane synovial joints, also known as gliding joints, are characterized by flat articular surfaces that allow sliding or gliding movements between bones. Option B: Primary cartilaginous: Primary cartilaginous joints, also known as synchondroses, are joints where the bones are connected by hyaline cartilage.. Option C: Secondary cartilaginous: Secondary cartilaginous joints, also known as symphyses, are joints where the bones are connected by fibrocartilage.","sourceRef":"Question Page 812; Solution Pages 855-856","sourcePage":812,"sourcePageEnd":812},{"id":"anatomy-21-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following muscles is not involved in the overhead abduction of the arm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Serratus anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deltoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pectoralis major"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pectoralis major","explanation":"Correct Option D: - Pectoralis major: The pectoralis major muscle is not directly involved in the overhead abduction of the arm. Its primary action is to adduct and internally rotate the arm, as in movements such as hugging or pressing the arms against the body. It does not play a significant role in raising the arm in an overhead position. Incorrect Options: Option A: Serratus anterior: The serratus anterior muscle plays an important role in stabilizing and upwardly rotating the scapula during arm abduction. Option B: Deltoid: The deltoid muscle, which is located on the shoulder, is a key muscle involved in the abduction of the arm. Option C: Trapezius: The trapezius muscle, a large muscle in the upper back and neck region, also contributes to the overhead abduction of the arm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 812; Solution Page 856","sourcePage":812,"sourcePageEnd":812},{"id":"anatomy-21-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following muscles is attached to the area marked in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abductor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Palmar interossei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Opponens pollicis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor pollicis brevis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Opponens pollicis","explanation":"Correct Option C: - The opponens pollicis muscle is indeed attached to the palmar aspect of the first metacarpal. This muscle originates from the flexor retinaculum and the tubercles of the trapezium and scaphoid bones in the wrist. Its tendon inserts onto the palmar surface of the first metacarpal bone. The opponens pollicis muscle functions to oppose the thumb, allowing for its opposition to the other fingers, a key movement for precision grip. Incorrect Options: Option A: Abductor Pollicis Longus: The abductor pollicis longus muscle abducts the thumb but doesn't directly attach to the palm side of the first metacarpal. Option B: Palmar Interossei: While palmar interossei muscles are in the vicinity of the first metacarpal bone, they are not specifically attached to the palmar aspect of the first metacarpal. Option D: Flexor Pollicis Brevis: The flexor pollicis brevis muscle helps with thumb flexion and opposition but doesn't directly attach to the palm side of the first metacarpal.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 812-813; Solution Pages 856-857","sourcePage":812,"sourcePageEnd":813},{"id":"anatomy-21-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A patient presented with a history of chop injury to the right hand is undergoing surgical repairs . It is observed that the patient is unable to completely fold the right ring finger, while making the fist , which structure injury results in this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor retinaculum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexor digitorum profundus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor pollicis longus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Flexor digitorum profundus","explanation":"Correct Answer -C: - The structure injury that would likely result in the patient's inability to completely fold the right ring finger while making a fist is the Flexor Digitorum Profundus (FDP). Incorrect options: Option A. Median nerve: Median nerve injury leads to sensory and motor deficits in the hand, affecting forearm anterior muscles like wrist and finger flexors, but not the ring finger specifically. Option B. Flexor retinaculum: Injury to the flexor retinaculum, like in carpal tunnel syndrome, can lead to hand numbness, tingling, or weakness but typically doesn't specifically impact the right ring finger's ability to fold.. Option D . Flexor pollicis longus: Injury to the flexor pollicis longus muscle affects thumb movement but doesn't directly impact the ability to fold the ring finger.","sourceRef":"Question Page 813; Solution Page 857","sourcePage":813,"sourcePageEnd":813},{"id":"anatomy-21-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":20,"question":"All of the following take part in the formation of the wrist joint, except:-","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Triquetrum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Distal end of radius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Distal end of ulna"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Distal end of ulna","explanation":"Correct Option D: - Distal end of ulna: The distal end of the ulna does not directly participate in the formation of the wrist joint. Instead, it forms the ulnar styloid process, which provides attachment for ligaments and provides stability to the wrist joint. The ulna primarily articulates with the radius at the distal radioulnar joint, which is separate from the wrist joint itself. Incorrect Options: Option A: Scaphoid: The scaphoid bone, a carpal bone of the wrist, articulates with the distal radius, crucial for wrist joint formation. Option B: Triquetrum: The triquetrum is another carpal bone that participates in the formation of the wrist joint. It articulates with the distal end of the ulna and the lunate bone. Option C: Distal end of radius: The radial articular surface at the end of the radius is vital for the wrist joint, connecting with carpal bones like the scaphoid and lunate.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 813-814; Solution Pages 857-858","sourcePage":813,"sourcePageEnd":814},{"id":"anatomy-21-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A patient presents with hypothenar muscle wasting and loss of sensation of medial one and a half digits. Which nerve is involved","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Musculocutaneous"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ulnar","explanation":"Correct option C. Ulnar. - The symptoms described, hypothenar muscle wasting and loss of sensation of the medial one and a half digits, indicate the involvement of the ulnar nerve. - The ulnar nerve is responsible for the innervation of the hypothenar muscles (located in the palm of the hand) and provides sensory innervation to the medial one and a half digits (the little finger and half of the ring finger). Incorrect Options : Option A . Musculocutaneous: The musculocutaneous nerve supplies the anterior arm muscles but not the hypothenar muscles or sensation to the described digits. Option B. Radial: The radial nerve innervates the extensor muscles of the arm and forearm and does not supply the hypothenar muscles or provide sensory innervation to the described digits. Option D . Median: The median nerve supplies the muscles of the anterior compartment of the forearm and does not innervate the hypothenar muscles or provide sensory innervation to the described digits.","sourceRef":"Question Page 814; Solution Pages 858-859","sourcePage":814,"sourcePageEnd":814},{"id":"anatomy-21-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which finger possesses two dorsal interossei muscles among the following options?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Index finger"},{"letter":"B","text":"Little finger"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle finger"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ring finger"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Middle finger","explanation":"Correct Option: Option C: The middle finger has two dorsal interossei muscles. The dorsal interossei muscles are a group of muscles located in the hand between the metacarpal bones. They play a role in finger abduction, which is the movement of the fingers away from the midline of the hand. The middle finger is the only finger that has two dorsal interossei muscles. Incorrect Option: Option A: The index finger has only one dorsal interosseous muscle. Option B: The little finger, also known as the Pinky finger, has only one dorsal interosseous muscle. Option D: The ring finger has only one dorsal interosseous muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 814; Solution Page 859","sourcePage":814,"sourcePageEnd":814},{"id":"anatomy-21-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":23,"question":"What are the effects of a Supracondylar fracture ? (or) What are the effects of a Supracondylar fracture ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Loss of sensation of the nail of index finger"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hypothenar muscles wasting"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loss of sensation at the tip of radial tubercle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Loss of sensation of the 4 th interdigital cleft"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Loss of sensation of the nail of index finger","explanation":"Correct Option: Option A. Loss of sensation of the nail of the index finger : Ape thumb deformity; Benediction hand; Weakness in wrist flexion; hand deviates to ulnar side on flexion; flexion of index and middle finger is lost; anterior abduction, opposition and flexion of thumb is compromised; sensory loss on the lateral 3 & ½ fingers; difficulty in making an 'O' with thumb and index finger. Incorrect Option: Option B. Hypothenar muscles wasting: Lesion of the median nerve wouldn't directly cause wasting or weakness of the hypothenar muscles, as they're innervated by the ulnar nerve, not the median nerve. Option C. Loss of sensation at the tip of the radial tubercle: A median nerve lesion wouldn't cause loss of sensation at the radial tubercle tip, as its innervation comes from radial nerve branches, not the median nerve. Option D. Loss of sensation of the 4th interdigital cleft: A median nerve lesion wouldn't lead to sensory loss in the 4th interdigital cleft since its innervation comes from the ulnar nerve, not the median nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 814-815; Solution Pages 859-860","sourcePage":814,"sourcePageEnd":815},{"id":"anatomy-21-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Identify the structure marked in the image given below:- **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial radial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ulnar artery","explanation":"Option B-Ulnar Artery: - The structure marked in the image is the ulnar artery. The ulnar artery is one of the main arteries in the forearm and hand. It runs along the ulnar side (medial side) of the forearm and supplies blood to the muscles and tissues of the forearm and hand. Incorrect Options: Option A. The structure marked in the image isn't the ulnar nerve, which provides motor and sensory innervation to the hand and forearm as a separate anatomical entity. Option C . The structure in the image isn't the superficial radial artery, which is situated on the radial (lateral) side of the forearm, nearer to the skin surface. Option D. The structure in the image isn't the median nerve, a major nerve in the arm that provides motor and sensory innervation to the hand and forearm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 815; Solution Page 860","sourcePage":815,"sourcePageEnd":815},{"id":"anatomy-21-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about the lesion of the structure indicated by the arrow?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ape thumb deformity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Claw hand"},{"letter":"C","text":"Claudication"},{"letter":"D","text":"Wrist drop"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Wrist drop","explanation":"Correct Option: Option D. - The lesion marked by the arrow leads to wrist drop. Wrist drop refers to a condition where the wrist is unable to maintain an extended position and hangs in a flexed position. It occurs due to weakness or paralysis of the muscles that extend the wrist, particularly the extensor muscles in the posterior compartment of the forearm. The most common cause of wrist drop is damage or compression of the radial nerve, which innervates these muscles. Incorrect Options: Option A. Ape thumb deformity is when the thumb aligns more with the fingers, resembling an ape's thumb. It often occurs due to median nerve injuries.. Option B. Claw hand is a deformity marked by hyperextended metacarpophalangeal joints and flexed interphalangeal joints, giving the hand a claw-like look. It's commonly linked to ulnar nerve injuries. Option C. Claudication is muscle pain due to poor blood flow during exercise, often in lower limbs, linked to peripheral artery disease.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 815-816; Solution Pages 860-861","sourcePage":815,"sourcePageEnd":816},{"id":"anatomy-21-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which of the subsequent structures of the brachial plexus does not provide any branches?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Roots"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cords"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trunks"},{"letter":"D","text":"Divisions"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Divisions","explanation":"Correct Option: Option D. - Divisions: Division does not give branches in the Brachial Plexus. - However, the Root, cords, trunks in the brachial plexus do give branches. Incorrect Options: Options A, B & C: Root, Cord and Tunks give branches","sourceRef":"Question Page 816; Solution Page 861","sourcePage":816,"sourcePageEnd":816},{"id":"anatomy-21-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Identify the tendon marked in the image **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor carpi ulnaris"},{"letter":"C","text":"Palmaris longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Median nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Flexor carpi radialis","explanation":"Correct Option A: Flexor carpi radialis - The tendon marked in the image is the tendon of the flexor carpi radialis muscle. Flexor carpi radialis muscle is a long flexor muscle lying in the anterior compartment of the forearm. It arises in the humeral epicondyle, close to the wrist area. It is a superficial muscle that becomes visible as the wrist flexes. Incorrect Options: Option B: Flexor carpi ulnaris is a fusiform muscle located in the anterior compartment of the forearm. It belongs to the group of superficial flexors of the forearm. Option C: The Palmaris longus (PL) muscle, typically found between the Flexor Carpi Ulnaris and Flexor Carpi Radialis muscles, is a long, slender muscle in the forearm's superficial volar compartment. Option D: The median nerve starts at the armpit and connects to nerve roots in the brachial plexus that run from the C5 to C8 cervical vertebrae and the T1 thoracic vertebra.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 816-817; Solution Pages 861-862","sourcePage":816,"sourcePageEnd":817},{"id":"anatomy-21-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A 34-year-old male patient presents to the OPD complaining of pain in the medial part of the elbow for the past 4-6 weeks. He states that the pain worsens while lifting weights in the gym. On examination, there is tenderness over the medial epicondyle, and reverse Cozen’s test is positive. Which of the following nerves is most likely to be injured in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B: Ulnar nerve - The patient suffers from medial epicondylitis or golfer’s elbow. The Ulnar nerve is posterior to the medial epicondyle within the cubital tunnel. The main symptom of medial epicondylitis is the 5 to 10 mm distal tenderness to the medial epicondyle. The pain is generally aggravated with resisted wrist flexion and pronation, known as reverse Cozen’s test. Incorrect Options: Option A: The Median Nerve supplies superficial and deep flexors in the forearm, thenar, and lumbricals. Injury at the elbow can cause \"hand of benediction\" due to its involvement. Option C: Radial nerve injury at the elbow leads to radial tunnel syndrome, causing nagging pain due to nerve compression. Wrist drop may result from forearm nerve compression. Option D: Injuries of axillary nerve, originating from C5-C6, often happen during orthopedic surgeries, shoulder dislocation or rotator cuff tears.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 817-818; Solution Pages 862-863","sourcePage":817,"sourcePageEnd":818},{"id":"anatomy-21-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":29,"question":"A man who was on crutches now presents with tingling and paraesthesia on the medial side of the arm, forearm, and hand. Which structure is most likely to be affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A","explanation":"Correct Option A: A - The structure A in the figure is the brachial plexus . The brachial plexus, located in the axilla, comprises nerve roots from C5 to T1, providing motor and sensory innervation to the shoulder, arm, and hand. - Crutch use results in abnormal and repetitive weight-bearing through the upper extremities that may result in secondary injury . If the nerves that originate from the lower trunk in the brachial plexus are injured, the arm, wrist and hand are affected causing tingling and paraesthesias on the medial side of the arm. Incorrect Options: Option B: The structure in the figure is a subclavian artery. Subclavian arteries below clavicles supply blood to arms and also contribute to head and neck blood supply. Option C: The structure C in the figure is a subclavian vein. The subclavian vein is a paired vein running under the clavicle and anterior to the subclavian artery. Option D: The structure D in the figure is the scalenus anterior muscle. It is found in the lateral part of the neck anterior to the subclavian artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 818; Solution Page 863","sourcePage":818,"sourcePageEnd":818},{"id":"anatomy-21-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":30,"question":"In the given image, the tendon marked by the arrow is? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor carpi radialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor carpi ulnaris"},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexor digitorum superficialis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Palmaris longus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Flexor carpi radialis","explanation":"Correct Option A - Flexor carpi radialis: - The above-marked structure in the diagram is Flexor carpi radialis as it is present on the radial side of the forearm. - Flexor carpi radialis originates from the medial epicondyle of the humerus and inserts into the bases of the second and third metacarpal bones. Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 818-819; Solution Pages 863-864","sourcePage":818,"sourcePageEnd":819},{"id":"anatomy-21-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":31,"question":"A 22-year-old male patient after an injury presented with a deformity as shown in the image. Which nerve will be damaged in the given condition? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial Nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median Nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous Nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Radial Nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B - Radial Nerve - In the given scenario, the image displaying wrist drop and a fracture of the shaft of the humerus is indicative of damage to the radial nerve Incorrect Options: Option A - Ulnar Nerve: The ulnar nerve mainly innervates forearm and hand muscles for wrist flexion and finger abduction. It's not typically involved in wrist drop. Option C - Median Nerve: The median nerve innervates specific forearm muscles and the hand's anterior compartment but isn't heavily involved in wrist extension, the main problem in the image. Option D - Musculocutaneous Nerve: The musculocutaneous nerve mainly innervates the anterior arm muscles, like the biceps brachii, aiding in elbow flexion, not wrist extension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 819-820; Solution Pages 864-865","sourcePage":819,"sourcePageEnd":820},{"id":"anatomy-21-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which bone is most likely to be fractured in an injury to the anatomical snuff box?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scaphoid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lunar"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cuboid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trapezius"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Scaphoid","explanation":"Correct Option A - Scaphoid - The anatomical snuff box is a triangular depression located on the radial aspect of the dorsum (back) of the hand. It is formed by the tendons of the extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis muscles. Fractures within the anatomical snuff box are commonly associated with falls onto an outstretched hand, particularly with the wrist extended. The most fractured bone in this region is the scaphoid bone, also known as the navicular bone. Incorrect Options: Option B - Lunar (lunate) bone: The lunate bone, found in the wrist joint, rarely fractures in injuries to the anatomical snuff box and isn't usually part of its formation. Option C - Cuboid bone: The cuboid bone is one of the tarsal bones located in the foot. It is not related to the anatomical snuff box or the wrist region. Option D - Trapezius: The trapezius is a large muscle located in the upper back and neck area. It is not a bone and is not related to the anatomical snuff box or wrist injuries.","sourceRef":"Question Page 820; Solution Page 865","sourcePage":820,"sourcePageEnd":820},{"id":"anatomy-21-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":33,"question":"The given finding is associated with ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trisomy 13"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trisomy 21"},{"letter":"C","text":"Klinefelter’s"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trisomy 18"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Trisomy 21","explanation":"Correct Option B - Trisomy 21 - Trisomy 21, commonly known as Down syndrome, results from an extra copy of chromosome 21. People with Down syndrome often have distinct facial features, intellectual disabilities, and certain medical problems. Approximately half of individuals with Down syndrome have a single transverse palmar crease, making this the correct answer. The simian crease is relatively common in Down syndrome. Incorrect Options: Option A - Trisomy 13: Trisomy 13, or Patau syndrome, results from an extra chromosome 13, leading to severe developmental issues and congenital abnormalities. While hand anomalies are common, a simian crease isn't typically present. Option C - Klinefelter's syndrome: Klinefelter's syndrome in males with an extra X chromosome (XXY) leads to male hypogonadism and physical differences, but it's not usually linked to a simian crease. Option D - Trisomy 18: Trisomy 18, or Edwards syndrome, stems from an extra chromosome 18, leading to severe developmental delays, intellectual disabilities, and congenital anomalies. While hand abnormalities may occur, a simian crease isn't a defining trait.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 820-821; Solution Pages 865-866","sourcePage":820,"sourcePageEnd":821},{"id":"anatomy-21-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":34,"question":"The marked muscle is supplied by which nerve? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Musculocutaneous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D - Musculocutaneous nerve: - The muscle that is marked in the diagram is the biceps brachii. - The three muscles innervated by the musculocutaneous nerve in the anterior part of the arm: biceps brachii, coracobrachialis, and brachialis . - The terminal branch of the lateral cord of the brachial plexus is the musculocutaneous nerve. Incorrect Options: Option A - Radial nerve supplies the triceps brachii, brachioradialis, etc. it does not supply the biceps brachii. Option B - Ulnar nerve does not supply biceps brachii. It innervates the forearm and hand muscles Option C - Median nerve does not supply biceps brachii. Some of the muscles innervated by the median nerve are flexor carpi radialis, palmaris longus, etc.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 821-822; Solution Page 866","sourcePage":821,"sourcePageEnd":822},{"id":"anatomy-21-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Which among the following aids in supination and pronation of the forearm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pivot joint"},{"letter":"B","text":"Saddle joint"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ellipsoid joint"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ball and socket joint"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Pivot joint","explanation":"Correct Option A - Pivot joint: - The forearm's radius and ulna bones are connected at the elbow joint to the upper arm's humerus bone. This joint allows for rotational movements like supination (palm up) and pronation (palm down) as the radius rotates around the stationary ulna. It's a pivot joint, permitting movement around a single axis, supported by ligaments and muscles for stability and control of motion range. Incorrect Options: Option B - Saddle joint: A saddle joint in the thumb enables a wide range of motion in multiple directions but isn't involved in the forearm's rotational movement. Option C - Ellipsoid joint: An ellipsoid joint is a type of joint found in the wrist. It allows for movement in two planes but not rotation around a single axis. Option D - Ball and socket joint: A ball and socket joint is found in the hip and shoulder joints and does not play a significant role in the rotational movement of the forearm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 822; Solution Pages 866-867","sourcePage":822,"sourcePageEnd":822},{"id":"anatomy-21-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Identify the anatomical structure marked in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abductor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extensor pollicis longus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Extensor pollicis longus","explanation":"Correct Option D - Extensor pollicis longus: - The pointed structure in the shown image is the medial boundary of the anatomical snuffbox which is away from the thumb formed by the extensor pollicis longus muscle. Incorrect Options: Option A - Abductor pollicis brevis: The abductor pollicis brevis muscle in the forearm assists in thumb abduction and borders the anatomical snuffbox. Option B - Extensor pollicis brevis: The extensor pollicis brevis muscle, situated in the forearm, extends the thumb and lies alongside the anatomical snuffbox. Option C - Abductor pollicis longus: The abductor pollicis longus muscle, in the forearm, aids in wrist abduction and lies near the proximal border of the anatomical snuffbox.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 822-823; Solution Pages 867-868","sourcePage":822,"sourcePageEnd":823},{"id":"anatomy-21-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Identify the type of joint shown in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Symphysis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Syndesmosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synchondrosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Synostosis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Syndesmosis","explanation":"Correct Option B - Syndesmosis: - The image is of the middle radio ulnar joint which is formed by the interosseous ligament. This is a type of fibrous joint which is syndesmosis. The syndesmosis is a type of joint that is formed by the union of two bones by a strong, fibrous tissue called a ligament. This type of joint is found in areas where stability is more important than mobility, such as the distal tibiofibular joint in the ankle and the distal radioulnar joint in the wrist. Incorrect Options: Option A - Symphysis: Symphysis joints fuse two bones with fibrocartilage, allowing limited movement crucial for shock absorption and flexibility, like in the pelvis and spine. Option C - Synchondrosis: Synchondrosis joints are formed by hyaline cartilage connecting two bones, commonly found in areas of growth and development like children's epiphyseal plates. Option D - Synostosis: Synostosis occurs when bones fuse due to various reasons like trauma or congenital conditions, leading to no mobility. Examples include cranial suture and radius-ulna fusion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 823-824; Solution Pages 868-869","sourcePage":823,"sourcePageEnd":824},{"id":"anatomy-21-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Which nerve can be injured when a fracture is sustained in the area marked with the red arrow in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Ulnar nerve: - Ulnar nerve crossed behind the medial epicondyle of the humerus. Fracture at medial epicondyle will injure Ulnar nerve - Injury to the ulnar nerve at the Flexor Carpi Ulnaris can result from repetitive strain, trauma, or conditions like cubital tunnel syndrome. Symptoms include pain or tingling in the little and ring fingers, hand or wrist weakness, and difficulty with fine motor tasks like gripping objects. Incorrect Options: - Option A - Radial nerve injury: The radial nerve in the humerus's spiral groove provides sensation to the back of the hand, thumb, and first two fingers. It also controls the muscles for extending the wrist, fingers, and thumb. - Option B - Median nerve injury- The median nerve runs from the forearm into the hand, controlling movement and sensation in the thumb, first three fingers, and forearm muscles. - Option D - Musculocutaneous nerve injury: The musculocutaneous nerve provides sensation to the lateral forearm and controls elbow flexor muscles. Injuries may cause weakness in elbow flexion, affecting lifting.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 824-825; Solution Pages 869-870","sourcePage":824,"sourcePageEnd":825},{"id":"anatomy-21-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":39,"question":"A patient is referred to a higher center with the diagnosis of fracture. On examination, the forearm pulses were not palpable. An upper arm X-ray was done, which is given below. Which artery is most likely to be injured in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ulnar artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Brachial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior interosseous artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Brachial artery","explanation":"Correct Option C - Brachial artery: - The brachial artery is a major artery in the upper arm and runs along the anterior aspect of the upper arm. It is the main blood vessel that supplies blood to the arm. The brachial artery divides into the radial and ulnar arteries in the elbow region. - An injury to the brachial artery can result in reduced blood flow to the entire arm, including the forearm and hand. This can cause a reduction or absence of pulses in the upper arm. Incorrect Options: - Option A - Radial artery : While an injury to the radial artery can result in reduced blood flow to the lateral aspect of the forearm and hand, it would not affect the pulses in the upper arm. - Option B - Ulnar artery : Like the radial artery, an injury to the ulnar artery can result in reduced blood flow to the medial aspect of the forearm and hand but would not affect the pulses in the upper arm. - Option D - Anterior interosseous artery : An injury to the anterior interosseous artery can result in reduced blood flow to the muscles in the anterior compartment of the forearm but would not affect the pulses in the upper arm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 825-826; Solution Pages 870-871","sourcePage":825,"sourcePageEnd":826},{"id":"anatomy-21-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":40,"question":"What is the nerve supply of the structure marked in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Median nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B - Median nerve: - The structure marked in the image, presumably depicts 1st lubrical which is innervated by the median nerve. This nerve is one of the three major nerves in the arm that originate from the brachial plexus. The median nerve supplies most of the flexor muscles in the forearm, the thenar muscles, and the lateral two lumbricals in the hand. Incorrect Options: - Option A - Radial nerve: The radial nerve innervates the muscles located on the posterior aspect of the arm and forearm.. - Option C - Ulnar nerve: The ulnar nerve innervates hand muscles except for the thenar muscles and two lateral lumbricals, along with flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of flexor digitorum profundus. - Option D - Musculocutaneous nerve: The Musculocutaneous nerve innervates the muscles in the anterior compartment of the arm including the biceps brachii, brachialis, and coracobrachialis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 826-827; Solution Page 871","sourcePage":826,"sourcePageEnd":827},{"id":"anatomy-21-41","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":41,"question":"Injury of the lower brachial plexus leads to Klumpke’s paralysis. What is the characteristic clinical finding seen in this lesion?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Policeman tip hand"},{"letter":"B","text":"Wrist drop"},{"letter":"C","text":"Claw hand"},{"letter":"D","text":"Horner’s syndrome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Claw hand","explanation":"Correct Option C - Claw hand: - Klumpke's paralysis stems from a lower brachial plexus injury involving C8 and T1 nerve roots, impacting intrinsic muscles of the hand responsible for flexion at the MCP joint and extension at the DIP and PIP joints. This imbalance leads to a characteristic \"claw hand\" presentation due to the loss of muscle innervation. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 827; Solution Pages 871-872","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":827},{"id":"anatomy-21-42","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":42,"question":"What is the action of the muscle shown in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Retracts the scapula"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adduction of the shoulder"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extension of the shoulder"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medial rotation of the shoulder"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Retracts the scapula","explanation":"Correct Option A - Retracts the scapula: The muscle identified in the image is the rhomboid major. Originating from the C7 and T1 vertebrae, it inserts along the medial border of the scapula. Primarily functioning as a retractor, the rhomboid major also plays roles in elevating the scapula and rotating it to depress the glenoid cavity. Incorrect Options: Option B - Adduction of the shoulder: The latissimus dorsi muscle is responsible for this action Option C - Extension of the shoulder: The latissimus dorsi muscle is responsible for this action Option D - Medial rotation of the shoulder: The Latissimus dorsi is the muscle responsible for the medial rotation of the shoulder along with other muscles of the shoulder and back","sourceRef":"Question Pages 827-828; Solution Page 872","sourcePage":827,"sourcePageEnd":828},{"id":"anatomy-21-43","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":43,"question":"Match the following: A) Glossopharyngeal nerve 1) Shrugging of shoulder B) Spinal accessory nerve 2) Touch sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue C) Facial nerve 3) Chewing D) Mandibular nerve 4) Taste from the anterior two third of the tongue A) Glossopharyngeal nerve 1) Shrugging of shoulder B) Spinal accessory nerve 2) Touch sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue C) Facial nerve 3) Chewing D) Mandibular nerve 4) Taste from the anterior two third of the tongue **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-2 , B-1 , C-4 , D-3"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-2 , B-3 , C-4 , D-1"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-4 , B-1 , C-2 , D-3"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-3 , B-1 , C-4 , D-2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A-2 , B-1 , C-4 , D-3","explanation":"Correct Option A - A-2 , B-1 , C-4 , D-3 A) Glossopharyngeal nerve 2) Touch sensation from the posterior one-third of the tongue B) Spinal accessory nerve 1) Shrugging of shoulder C) Facial nerve 4) Taste from the anterior two third of the tongue D) Mandibular nerve 3) Chewing Incorrect Options - B, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 828-829; Solution Pages 872-873","sourcePage":828,"sourcePageEnd":829},{"id":"anatomy-21-44","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":44,"question":"A patient presents with abnormal sensations in the little finger and part of the ring finger with weakness of the hand. Also, there is a loss of coordination in the fingers. What Is the nerve-injured in the given scenario?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ulnar nerve: - The ulnar nerve supplies all the intrinsic muscles of the hand except the thenar muscles and lateral two lumbricals and is responsible for the sensations of the medial 1 ½ fingers of the hand. - Hence abnormal sensations in the little finger and part of the ring finger with weakness on the palm side would be due to the ulnar nerve injury. Incorrect Options: Option B - Radial nerve: The radial nerve injury is characterised by wrist drop and does not cause the symptoms mentioned in this case. Option C - Median nerve : the median nerve supplies the thenar muscles and is responsible for sensations on the medial side of the index finger, middle finger, and lateral side of the ring finger . Option D - Axillary nerve: Responsible for the nerve supply of the deltoid muscle i n the shoulder region.","sourceRef":"Question Page 829; Solution Page 873","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":829},{"id":"anatomy-21-45","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":45,"question":"A 7-year-old boy was brought to the hospital with multiple fractures of the humerus secondary to a fall from height. On examination, there is difficulty in flexion of the elbow and supination of the forearm and associated loss of sensation over the lateral aspect of the forearm. Which is the nerve most likely to be injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Musculocutaneous nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Musculocutaneous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Musculocutaneous nerve: - The musculocutaneous nerve innervates elbow flexion muscles like the biceps brachii and brachialis. It also aids in forearm supination via its branch, the lateral cutaneous nerve of the forearm, which supplies sensation to the forearm's lateral aspect. - Consequently, difficulty in elbow flexion, supination, and loss of sensation in the forearm's lateral area suggest a musculocutaneous nerve injury, making it the most probable nerve involved. Incorrect Options Option A- Median nerve: The median nerve doesn't directly innervate the muscles for elbow flexion or forearm supination. Thus, it's unlikely to be the nerve injured in this case. Option B - Radial nerve: The radial nerve doesn't control elbow flexion or forearm supination directly, and loss of sensation in the forearm's lateral aspect isn't typical with radial nerve injuries. Options D - Ulnar nerve: The ulnar nerve primarily controls hand muscles, not elbow flexion or forearm supination, and doesn't provide sensation to the forearm's lateral aspect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 829; Solution Pages 873-874","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":829},{"id":"anatomy-21-46","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":46,"question":"Which of the following forms the lateral boundary of the anatomical snuff box?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extensor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abductor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus: - The extensor pollicis brevis and abductor pollicis longus muscles form the lateral boundary of the anatomical snuff box - These two muscles create a prominent ridge that can be palpated on the lateral aspect of the wrist. Incorrect Options: Option B - The extensor pollicis longus and abductor pollicis brevis muscles are situated posteriorly in the wrist and aren't part of the lateral boundary of the anatomical snuff box. Option C - The extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis muscles are in the anatomical snuff box, but the extensor pollicis longus marks the medial boundary, not the lateral. Option D - Although the abductor pollicis longus forms the lateral boundary, the abductor pollicis brevis muscle is not involved in forming the boundary of the anatomical snuff box.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 829-830; Solution Page 874","sourcePage":829,"sourcePageEnd":830},{"id":"anatomy-21-47","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":47,"question":"A patient presented with the extension of MCP joint and flexion of IP joints. Which muscle is affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor digitorum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Interossei and lumbricals"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abductor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Interossei and lumbricals","explanation":"Correct Option B: Interossei and lumbricals - The presentation indicates involvement of interossei and lumbrical muscles. Interossei muscles assist in finger abduction/adduction between metacarpal bones, while lumbrical muscles, arising from flexor digitorum profundus tendons, aid in MCP joint flexion and IP joint extension by inserting into the extensor hood. Incorrect Options: Option A: The extensor digitorum muscle primarily extends the fingers at the MCP joints. It does not cause flexion of the IP joints. Option C - The abductor pollicis longus primarily moves the thumb away from the hand laterally, facilitating thumb extension and opposition for tasks like grasping objects. Option D - The abductor pollicis brevis muscle is involved in thumb abduction and opposition. It does not play a role in finger joint movements.","sourceRef":"Question Page 830; Solution Pages 874-875","sourcePage":830,"sourcePageEnd":830},{"id":"anatomy-21-48","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":48,"question":"Identify the sign shown in the image below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Popeye sign"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hill-Sachs sign"},{"letter":"C","text":"Griesinger sign"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rising sun sign"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Popeye sign","explanation":"Correct Option A - Popeye sign - Popeye sign: The image shows a bulging appearance of the upper arm resembling the arm of the cartoon character Popeye. - This sign is seen in cases of biceps tendon injury or rupture, where the long head of the biceps tendon retracts and forms a bulge in the upper arm. Incorrect Options: Option B - Hill-Sachs sign:This sign indicates an impression fracture or defect on the humeral head, seen in anterior shoulder dislocation. It occurs when the humeral head impacts the glenoid rim during dislocation. . Option C - Griesinger sign: Griesinger sign is associated with mastoiditis, which involves swelling and tenderness behind the ear. It is not relevant to the image shown. Option D - Rising sun sign: otoscopic sign of active otospongiosis characterized by reddish discolouration of the cochlear promontory.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 830-831; Solution Page 875","sourcePage":830,"sourcePageEnd":831},{"id":"anatomy-21-49","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":49,"question":"A 30 years female patient is presenting with wrist pain on lateral side. On examination the following test was positive. Tendon sheaths of which two muscle have been involved","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis brevis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis","explanation":"Correct Option A - Abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis - The image shows a Finkelstein's test , which is used to diagnose De Quervain's tenosynovitis . - Procedure : The patient makes a fist with the thumb tucked inside the fingers, and the examiner then ulnarly deviates the wrist (toward the pinky side). - Positive Result : If the patient experiences pain on the lateral side of the wrist (along the thumb side), the test is considered positive. This indicates inflammation of the tendons. The image shows a Finkelstein's test , which is used to diagnose De Quervain's tenosynovitis . Muscles involved: - The tendon sheaths of the following two muscles are involved in De Quervain's tenosynovitis: Abductor pollicis longus (APL) . Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) . - Abductor pollicis longus (APL) . - Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) . - These muscles are part of the first dorsal compartment of the wrist, and their tendons can become inflamed due to overuse, leading to pain and difficulty with thumb movements. The tendon sheaths of the following two muscles are involved in De Quervain's tenosynovitis: - Abductor pollicis longus (APL) . - Extensor pollicis brevis (EPB) . Clinical Presentation: - The patient typically presents with pain on the lateral (radial) side of the wrist , particularly when gripping or lifting objects. - The condition is common in individuals who engage in repetitive thumb and wrist movements. Incorrect Option: Option B - The abductor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis longus muscles in the forearm have separate tendon sheaths and functions, typically not causing lateral wrist pain. Option C - The abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis brevis muscles, situated on the radial side of the wrist, mainly facilitate thumb movements and aren't commonly linked to lateral wrist pain. Option D - The abductor pollicis brevis and extensor pollicis longus muscles, involved in thumb movements, aren't typically linked to lateral wrist pain.","sourceRef":"Question Page 831; Solution Pages 875-877","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":831},{"id":"anatomy-21-50","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":50,"question":"What is the function of the muscle indicated in the provided image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Retraction of scapula"},{"letter":"B","text":"Protraction of scapula"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abduction of shoulder joint"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adduction of shoulder joint"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Abduction of shoulder joint","explanation":"- Correct Option C - Abduction of shoulder joint - The muscle marked in the question is the supraspinatus, which is present above the spine of the scapula and helps abduct the shoulder joint. The muscles involved in abducting the shoulder joint are the deltoid muscle, supraspinatus muscle, and the trapezius muscle Correct Option C - Abduction of shoulder joint Incorrect Options: Option A - Retraction of scapula: The muscles involved in retracting the scapula are the middle and lower trapezius muscles and the rhomboid major and minor muscles. Option B - Protraction of scapula: The muscles involved in protracting the scapula are the serratus anterior muscle. Option D - Adduction of shoulder joint: The muscles involved in adducting the shoulder joint are the latissimus dorsi muscle, pectoralis major muscle, and the teres major muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 831-832; Solution Pages 877-878","sourcePage":831,"sourcePageEnd":832},{"id":"anatomy-21-51","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":51,"question":"A patient with leprosy presented with the deformity as shown below. Which nerve injury causes the given condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ulnar nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ulnar nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ulnar nerve: - Ulnar nerve injury at the elbow: Flexor carpi ulnaris and the medial half of the flexor digitorum profundus are paralysed. Due to this paralysis, the medial border of the forearm becomes flattened. An attempt to produce flexion at the wrist results in abduction of the hand. The tendon of the flexor carpi ulnaris does not tighten on making a fist. Flexion of the terminal phalanges of the ring and little fingers is lost. Incorrect Options: Option B - The radial nerve runs down the underside of your arm and controls the movement of the triceps muscle, which is located at the back of the upper arm. Option C - The median nerve controls forearm, wrist, and hand movements, particularly coarse movements, by supplying most long muscles in the forearm's front. . Option D - It is the area where the nerve enters the supinator muscle and is the most common place for a compression of the nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 832-833; Solution Page 878","sourcePage":832,"sourcePageEnd":833},{"id":"anatomy-21-52","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":52,"question":"Biceps brachi functions as which type of muscle for lifting the forearm ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Synergistic"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fixator"},{"letter":"C","text":"Antagonist"},{"letter":"D","text":"Primary mover"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Primary mover","explanation":"Correct Option D - Primary mover: - The biceps brachii muscle is located in the upper arm and plays a crucial role in the movement of the forearm, specifically in flexion of the elbow joint. - The primary mover, also known as the agonist, is the muscle primarily responsible for generating a specific movement. In the case of lifting the forearm, the biceps brachii contracts concentrically (shortens) to flex the elbow joint, causing the forearm to move upward. Incorrect Options: Options A - Synergistic: Synergistic muscles work together to produce movement. While the brachialis and brachioradialis contribute to elbow flexion, the biceps brachii is primarily responsible for this action. Options B - Fixator: Fixator muscles are muscles that stabilize or fixate a joint to allow movement at another joint. The biceps brachii is not considered a fixator muscle for lifting the forearm. Options C - Antagonist: In elbow flexion, the triceps brachii serves as the antagonist muscle, extending the elbow to counteract the biceps brachii's flexion.","sourceRef":"Question Page 833; Solution Pages 878-879","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":833},{"id":"anatomy-21-53","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":53,"question":"A patient who has taken the first COVID vaccine comes for the second dose. An astute nurse noticed that the shoulder was flabby, flat, and was asymmetrical. There was an associated loss of contour of the shoulder joint. Injury to which of the structures might have resulted and was avoidable?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rotator cuff"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior circumflex artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral cutaneous nerve of arm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Axillary nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Axillary nerve","explanation":"Correct Option: D - Axillary nerve: - Based on the provided symptoms and observations, the injury to the structure that might have resulted and was avoidable is the axillary nerve . - The axillary nerve is a major nerve that supplies motor innervation to the deltoid muscle and sensory innervation to the skin over the lateral aspect of the shoulder. - It runs in close proximity to the shoulder joint and is susceptible to injury during vaccination if the injection is improperly administered. Incorrect Options: Option A - Rotator cuff : The symptoms described don't align with a rotator cuff injury. Typically, rotator cuff injuries cause shoulder pain, weakness, and limited range of motion.. Option B - Posterior circumflex artery : The symptoms described, like a flabby, flat shoulder and loss of shoulder joint contour, don't suggest vascular compromise, which might occur with injury to the posterior circumflex artery. Option C - Lateral cutaneous nerve of arm: The described symptoms don't indicate vascular compromise, as might occur with injury to the posterior circumflex artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 833-834; Solution Page 879","sourcePage":833,"sourcePageEnd":834},{"id":"anatomy-21-54","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":54,"question":"A 25 year old male presented with symptoms of inability to flex the distal interphalangeal joint of the 4th and 5th digits. He was also not able to hold a piece of paper between his fingers. What is the likely site of injury?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. C","explanation":"Correct Option C - C: - Based on the symptoms described, the likely site of injury in this case is the medial epicondyle of the humerus. - The flexor muscles responsible for flexing the distal interphalangeal joint of the 4th and 5th digits and providing grip strength are primarily innervated by the ulnar nerve. - The ulnar nerve courses through the medial side of the elbow, passing near or over the medial epicondyle. Incorrect Options: Option A - Shaft of Humerus: The symptoms described aren't typical of a humeral shaft injury, which usually involves pain, swelling, deformity, and limited range of motion. Option B - Lateral Epicondyle :The symptoms described aren't typically linked to a lateral epicondyle injury, which usually involves pain and tenderness on the outer side of the elbow.. Option D - Neck of Humerus : The symptoms described aren't typical of a humeral neck injury, which usually involves pain, swelling, deformity, and limited range of motion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 834-835; Solution Pages 879-880","sourcePage":834,"sourcePageEnd":835},{"id":"anatomy-21-55","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":55,"question":"Which part of this bone is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1: The coracoid process of the scapula is palpable in the infraclavicular fossa. The infraclavicular fossa is the depression located just below the clavicle and above the shoulder joint. By palpating this area, you can feel the bony prominence of the coracoid process, which projects anteriorly and laterally from the scapula. Incorrect Options: Option B - Acromion process:The acromion process, found at the top of the scapula, is the highest point of the shoulder and isn't palpable in the infraclavicular fossa, which lies beneath the clavicle.. Option C - Lateral angle of scapula: The lateral angle of the scapula is the outermost part of the scapula, away from the infraclavicular fossa. It is not palpable in this specific area. Option D - Inferior angle of scapula: The inferior angle of the scapula, situated near the midline of the back, is not palpable in the infraclavicular fossa, found below the clavicle on the front side of the body.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 835-836; Solution Pages 880-881","sourcePage":835,"sourcePageEnd":836},{"id":"anatomy-21-56","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":56,"question":"What is the most common complication of the central line insertion below the marked bone? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Arterial puncture"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thrombophlebitis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Empyema"},{"letter":"D","text":"Chylothorax"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Arterial puncture","explanation":"Correct Option A: Arterial puncture: During central line insertion below the clavicle, there is a risk of inadvertently puncturing an artery. The subclavian vein is located close to the subclavian artery in this region, and it is common for arterial puncture to occur during the procedure. Incorrect Options : Option B . Thrombophlebitis: Thrombophlebitis, inflammation, and blood clot formation in a vein, can occur after central line insertion, though it's not the most common complication below the clavicle. Option C . Empyema: Empyema can result from various factors like lung infections or pneumonia, not specific to the mentioned procedure site. Option D . Chylothorax: Chylothorax is more often linked to thoracic surgeries or trauma affecting the thoracic duct or nearby lymphatic structures.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 836-837; Solution Page 881","sourcePage":836,"sourcePageEnd":837},{"id":"anatomy-21-57","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":57,"question":"The arrow in the image denotes which of the following muscles? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor carpi radialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Brachioradialis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supinator"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor carpi radialis brevis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Extensor carpi radialis","explanation":"Correct Option: Option A: Extensor carpi radialis: The image shows the extensor carpi radialis muscle, which is indicated by the arrow. This muscle is located in the forearm and plays a role in extending and abducting the wrist. Incorrect options: Option B: Brachioradialis: The arrow in the image doesn't point to the brachioradialis muscle, which is located in the forearm but positioned more laterally compared to the extensor carpi radialis. Option C: Supinator: The arrow in the image doesn't point to the supinator muscle, situated in the forearm, assisting in forearm supination by rotating the palm upward. Option D: Flexor carpi radialis brevis: The arrow in the image doesn't indicate the flexor carpi radialis, a muscle responsible for wrist flexion.radialis brevis.\" The correct name for the muscle associated with flexing the wrist is the flexor carpi radialis, but it is not indicated by the arrow in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 837-838; Solution Pages 881-882","sourcePage":837,"sourcePageEnd":838},{"id":"anatomy-21-58","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":58,"question":"What is the nerve supply of the structure marked in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Median nerve","explanation":"Correct Option: Option B: Median nerve: The median nerve is the correct answer. It provides motor innervation to the lumbrical muscles of the hand, including the 2nd lumbrical. The lumbrical muscles are responsible for flexing the metacarpophalangeal joints and extending the interphalangeal joints of the fingers. Incorrect Options : Option A: Posterior interosseous nerve: The posterior interosseous nerve primarily supplies the extensor muscles of the forearm. It does not supply the structure marked in the image, which is the 2nd lumbrical. Option C: Anterior interosseous nerve: The anterior interosseous nerve supplies the deep flexor muscles of the forearm, but it does not directly innervate the lumbrical muscles. Option D: Ulnar nerve: The ulnar nerve innervates the muscles of the hypothenar eminence, interosseous muscles, and intrinsic muscles of the hand. It does not directly supply the 2nd lumbrical muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 838-839; Solution Pages 882-883","sourcePage":838,"sourcePageEnd":839},{"id":"anatomy-21-59","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":59,"question":"Nerve supply of the area marked by arrow in the image is: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior interosseous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Radial nerve","explanation":"Correct Option : Option A: Radial nerve: The radial nerve supplies the area marked by the arrow in the image, which corresponds to the anatomical snuff box. The anatomical snuff box is located on the dorsum (back) of the hand and is formed by tendons of the extensor pollicis longus and extensor pollicis brevis muscles. The radial nerve provides sensory innervation to this area. Incorrect Options: Option B: Posterior interosseous nerve: The posterior interosseous nerve is a branch of the radial nerve that supplies the deep extensor muscles of the forearm. It does not directly innervate the area marked in the image. Option C: Median nerve: The median nerve primarily supplies the palmar aspect of the hand, including the thenar muscles and lateral 3 and a half digits. It does not directly supply the area marked in the image. Option D: Ulnar nerve: The ulnar nerve primarily innervates the medial aspect of the hand, including the hypothenar muscles and the ulnar side of the palm. It does not directly supply the area marked in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 839-840; Solution Page 883","sourcePage":839,"sourcePageEnd":840},{"id":"anatomy-21-60","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":60,"question":"Which of the statements below accurately describes De Quervain's tenosynovitis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fingers are held in mild extension"},{"letter":"B","text":"It affects APL and EPB"},{"letter":"C","text":"Most common involvement is index finger"},{"letter":"D","text":"Treatment is surgery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. It affects APL and EPB","explanation":"Correct Option: Option B: It affects APL and EPB: This statement is correct. De Quervain's tenosynovitis specifically affects the tendons of the abductor pollicis longus (APL) and extensor pollicis brevis (EPB). These tendons run along the thumb side of the wrist and are responsible for thumb movement Incorrect Options: Option A: Fingers are held in mild extension: This is incorrect. In De Quervain's tenosynovitis, the affected fingers are typically flexed, not extended. Option C: Most common involvement is index finger: This is incorrect. De Quervain's tenosynovitis mainly affects the thumb side of the wrist, involving the tendons of APL and EPB, and doesn't usually involve the index finger. Option D: Treatment is surgery: This is incorrect. Surgery isn't usually the first treatment for De Quervain's tenosynovitis. Initially, conservative measures like rest, splinting, anti-inflammatory medication, and physical therapy are attempted.","sourceRef":"Question Page 840; Solution Pages 883-884","sourcePage":840,"sourcePageEnd":840},{"id":"anatomy-21-61","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":61,"question":"A 16-year-old boy came to Emergency with the following X-ray. On examination, he had a sensory loss in the lateral arm and sensory loss in the posterior aspect of the forearm. Which of the following is true about his condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fracture -dislocation of the elbow"},{"letter":"B","text":"Surgical neck humerus fracture with axillary nerve injury"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spine fracture with foot drop"},{"letter":"D","text":"Humerus shaft fracture with radial nerve palsy"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Humerus shaft fracture with radial nerve palsy","explanation":"Correct option: D The X-ray reveals a fracture in the shaft of the humerus, often accompanied by radial nerve damage, which affects wrist, finger, and thumb extension, along with sensation in the forearm and hand. Clinical signs like sensory loss in the lateral arm and posterior forearm support this diagnosis, possibly due to trauma or compression from the fracture. Other possibilities (elbow fracture-dislocation, surgical neck humerus fracture with axillary nerve injury, spine fracture causing foot drop) have differing clinical features. Incorrect options: Options A, B & C are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 840-841; Solution Page 884","sourcePage":840,"sourcePageEnd":841},{"id":"anatomy-21-62","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":62,"question":"A patient developed the following deformity, after overhead (weight-lifting) exercises. Which of the following muscles is involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Serratus anterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lattisimus dorsi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supraspinatus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Serratus anterior","explanation":"Correct Answer -B: Serratus Anterior : The Serratus Anterior muscle is located on the lateral aspect of the thorax and attaches to the medial border of the scapula. It is primarily responsible for protracting and stabilizing the scapula against the thoracic wall. Weakness or dysfunction of the Serratus Anterior can result in winged scapula, where the medial border of the scapula protrudes away from the thoracic wall. Incorrect options: Option A. Trapezius : The trapezius muscle supports upper back and scapular movement, but winged scapula isn't typically due to trapezius issues. Option C. LatissimusDorsi : The latissimus dorsi muscle spans from the lower back to the upper arm, aiding in shoulder movement and stability. However, it isn't directly linked to winged scapula. Option D. Supraspinatus : The supraspinatus, part of the rotator cuff, aids in shoulder abduction and stability. However, it's not usually connected to winged scapula.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 841-842; Solution Pages 884-885","sourcePage":841,"sourcePageEnd":842},{"id":"anatomy-21-63","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":63,"question":"What is the function of the muscle marked as A in the image below, at the metacarpophalangeal joint? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extension"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adduction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abduction"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Flexion","explanation":"Correct Option A: Flexion - The lumbrical muscles, marked as A in the image, play a role in flexion at the metacarpophalangeal joint. Incorrect Options: Option B. Extension: Extension straightens or widens the angle between bones. Lumbrical muscles don't aid in extending the metacarpophalangeal joint. Option C. Adduction: In the metacarpophalangeal joint, adduction brings fingers closer together. Lumbrical muscles aren't involved in adduction. Option D. Abduction: At the metacarpophalangeal joint, abduction spreads fingers apart. Lumbrical muscles aren't involved in abduction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 842-843; Solution Pages 885-886","sourcePage":842,"sourcePageEnd":843},{"id":"anatomy-21-64","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-21","chapter":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 21 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":21,"questionNumber":64,"question":"A patient is unable to abduct his arm. Which of the following root values is likely to be affected: -","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C4"},{"letter":"B","text":"C5"},{"letter":"C","text":"C6"},{"letter":"D","text":"C7"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. C5","explanation":"Correct Option B: - Arm abduction is mainly controlled by muscles innervated by the axillary nerve from the brachial plexus's posterior cord. It supplies the deltoid muscle, crucial for this action. - The axillary nerve is formed from contributions of the C5 and C6 nerve roots, which give rise to its posterior division in the upper trunk of the brachial plexus. Damage at the C5 level can weaken or paralyze the deltoid muscle, affecting arm abduction. Incorrect Options: Option A . C4: C4 nerve root mainly provides sensation to the upper shoulder, not directly controlling arm abduction. Damage to it wouldn't typically impair arm abduction. Option C . C6: C6 nerve root primarily controls elbow flexion and wrist extension. While it aids in initiating arm abduction, isolated damage to C6 wouldn't fully prevent arm abduction. Option D . C7: Damage to the C7 nerve root mainly affects elbow and wrist movements, not shoulder motion. It wouldn't usually cause an isolated inability to abduct the arm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 843; Solution Page 886","sourcePage":843,"sourcePageEnd":843},{"id":"anatomy-22-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following bones forms the posterior pillar of the medial longitudinal arch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Talus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Navicular"},{"letter":"C","text":"Calcaneum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Metatarsals"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Calcaneum","explanation":"Each longitudinal arch has: (a) two pillars, (b) a summit, and (c) joints. Medial Longitudinal Arch The medial longitudinal arch is made up of the calcaneum, talus, navicular, three cuneiform bones, and the medial three metatarsals. a) Pillars: - The medial half of the calcaneum forms the posterior pillar of the medial longitudinal arch. - The heads of the medial three metatarsals form the anterior pillar of the arch. b) Summit: The talus is located at the highest point (summit) of this arch and acts as its keystone. c) Joints: The main joints involved in the medial longitudinal arch are the talocalcaneonavicular joint and the subtalar joint. Lateral Longitudinal Arch - The lateral longitudinal arch is formed by the calcaneum, cuboid, and lateral two metatarsals. - It is low and nearly touches the ground, helping to support body weight during walking and running. - The posterior pillar is formed by the lateral tubercle of the calcaneum. - The anterior pillar is formed by the heads of the lateral two metatarsals. b) Summit: The summit is at the level of the articular facets on the superior surface of the calcaneum, near the subtalar joint. c) Joints: The main joint is the calcaneocuboid joint. The arch is lower and less flexible than the medial arch, designed to transmit weight and thrust. Reference:","sourceRef":"Question Page 887; Solution Pages 898-899","sourcePage":887,"sourcePageEnd":887},{"id":"anatomy-22-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Identify X.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Linea semilunaris"},{"letter":"B","text":"Linea alba"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spino-umbilical line"},{"letter":"D","text":"Addison’s plane"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Linea semilunaris","explanation":"The linea semilunaris is a curved groove that runs from the tip of the 9th costal cartilage to the pubic tubercle. - It is located a few centimeters lateral to the midline and corresponds to the outer border of the rectus abdominis muscle. Linea alba (Option B): The linea alba is a tendinous raphe that separates the two rectus muscles. - This midline groove divides the anterior abdominal wall into two halves, right and left. Spino-umbilical Line (Option C): Also known as the McBurney’s line, is an imaginary line that extends from the umbilicus to the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) . - The point at the junction of medial 2/3rd and lateral 1/3rd of this line corresponds to the McBurney’s point. - The base of the appendix lies deep to this point, and it is commonly used in diagnosing acute appendicitis , as tenderness in this area is a key clinical sign. Addison’s plane (Option D): Also known as the Transpyloric Plane, is an imaginary horizontal plane that passes through the midpoint of the line connecting the suprasternal notch to the pubic symphysis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 887-888; Solution Pages 899-901","sourcePage":887,"sourcePageEnd":888},{"id":"anatomy-22-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 35-year-old male presents with a foot drop and weakness in the dorsiflexion of the great toe. He mentions a recent history of a herniated lumbar disc. There is decreased sensation over the dorsum of the foot and the first web space between the great toe and the second toe. Which dermatome and root value are most likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L5"},{"letter":"B","text":"S1"},{"letter":"C","text":"L4"},{"letter":"D","text":"L1"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. L5","explanation":"- Foot Drop indicates weakness in dorsiflexion , primarily controlled by the L5 nerve root through the deep peroneal nerve . - The L5 dermatome supplies sensation to the dorsum of the foot and the 1st web space between the great toe and the 2nd toe. - The patient's decreased sensation in these areas indicates L5 nerve root involvement. - A herniated lumbar disc (most common type—paradiscal), particularly between L4-L5 > L5-S1, can compress the L5 nerve root , leading to the symptoms described. Dermatomes of the lower limb The area of skin supplied by a spinal nerve is termed a dermatome - Lower Limb Innervation: Originates from the ventrolateral body wall opposite spinal segments L1–S3 . Primarily innervated by the ventral rami of these segments. Except the superomedial gluteal region is innervated by the dorsal rami of L1–L3 and S1–S3. - Originates from the ventrolateral body wall opposite spinal segments L1–S3 . - Primarily innervated by the ventral rami of these segments. - Except the superomedial gluteal region is innervated by the dorsal rami of L1–L3 and S1–S3. - Limb Bud Orientation: Initially it has preaxial (great toe) and postaxial (little toe) borders. The limb bud rotates medially by 90°, repositioning the great toe medially and the little toe laterally. After rotation, dermatomes are arranged: Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). - Initially it has preaxial (great toe) and postaxial (little toe) borders. - The limb bud rotates medially by 90°, repositioning the great toe medially and the little toe laterally. - After rotation, dermatomes are arranged: Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). - Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). - Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). - Originates from the ventrolateral body wall opposite spinal segments L1–S3 . - Primarily innervated by the ventral rami of these segments. - Except the superomedial gluteal region is innervated by the dorsal rami of L1–L3 and S1–S3. - Initially it has preaxial (great toe) and postaxial (little toe) borders. - The limb bud rotates medially by 90°, repositioning the great toe medially and the little toe laterally. - After rotation, dermatomes are arranged: Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). - Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). - Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). - Preaxial border: L1–L4 (above downward). - Postaxial border: S1–S3 (below upward). Region Dermatome Nerve Supply Heel (Skin on the heel) S1 The posterior tibial nerve has two branches Lateral plantar nerve S1 towards the little toe . Medial plantar nerve L5 towards the great toe . The popliteal region, posterior thigh S2 Posterior cutaneous nerve (middle), calf, perineal region Lateral foot, dorsum of the foot (Little S1 (Option B) Sural nerve toe) First web space (Dorsum) L5 (Option A) Deep peroneal nerve Medial aspect of leg (Medial L4 (Option C) Saphenous nerve malleolus) Inguinal region (Proximal lower limb) L1 (Option D) Ilioinguinal nerve, Genitofemoral nerve Anterior thigh L2, L3, L4 (Lateral to medial) Femoral nerve Medial thigh L2, L3, L4 Obturator nerve Perineum S2, S3, S4 Pudendal nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 888; Solution Pages 901-904","sourcePage":888,"sourcePageEnd":888},{"id":"anatomy-22-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Identify the muscle/muscle head that is not included in the hamstring group of muscles.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":""},{"letter":"B","text":""},{"letter":"C","text":""},{"letter":"D","text":"**Correct Answer:** B."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B.","explanation":"The part marked as “b” in the image is the short head of the biceps femoris, which is not a part of the hamstring group of muscles. (a) Long head of biceps (b) Short head of biceps (c) Semitendinosus (d) Semimembranosus femoris femoris - Hamstrings include semitendinosus , semimembranosus, biceps femoris (long head) and ischial head (Hamstring portion) of adductor magnus - They are biarticular muscles that cross both the hip and knee joints, integrating hip extension with knee flexion. - It is important for movement , and stability , and is commonly tested for injury. The characteristic features of hamstring muscles are: - All arise from the ischial tuberosity. - All are inserted into one of the bones of the leg. - All are supplied by tibial part of the sciatic nerve. - All are flexors of the knee and extensors of the hip joint. Semitendinosus (Option C) Semimembranosus Biceps Femoris (Option D) Attachments Ischial tuberosity via Ischial tuberosity (long, flat Long head (Option A) : common tendon with tendon) Inserts on medial Ischial tuberosity (common biceps femoris Inserts on tibial condyle and forms tendon with semitendinosu the medial surface of the oblique popliteal ligament s) Short head (Option B): tibia (pes anserinus) From Linea aspera Inserts on head of fibula Relations Posterior to semimembrano Overlaps popliteal vessels Covered by gluteus sus Proximally overlaps Lies deep to semitendinosu maximus Lies superficial; popliteal vessels s deep to it are semimembranosus, sciatic nerve, adductor magnus Innervation Sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) Sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) Long head: Sciatic nerve through tibial division through tibial division (tibial division) Short head: common peroneal part of Sciatic nerve Actions Flexes knee Extends hip Flexes knee Extends hip Flexes knee Extends hip Medial rotation of lower leg Medial rotation of lower leg Lateral rotation of lower leg Testing Active knee flexion against Active knee flexion against Active knee flexion against resistance resistance resistance Two heads of the biceps femoris (Option D) join together to form a conjoint tendon in the lower third of the thigh that slopes down and laterally and inserts into the head of the fibula.","sourceRef":"Question Page 888; Solution Pages 904-906","sourcePage":888,"sourcePageEnd":888},{"id":"anatomy-22-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following muscles is/are supplied by the Nerve to the obturator internus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gamellus inferior and obturator internus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gamellus superior and obturator internus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Quadratus femoris and obturator internus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Obturator internus only"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Quadratus femoris and obturator internus","explanation":"- The nerve to obturator internus innervates both the obturator internus and superior gemellus muscles. It arises from the anterior divisions of the sacral plexus, originating from the L5, S1, and S2 nerve roots. - Quadratus femoris and Gamellus inferior are supplied by Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, 5, S1) Obturator Internus: Attachments Origin: Pelvic surface of the obturator membrane and surrounding bones Insertion: On the medial surface of the greater trochanter Vascular Supply Main supply: gemellar branches of the internal pudendal artery Intrapelvic and extrapelvic parts: branches of the obturator artery Innervation Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1, S2) Actions Laterally rotates the extended thigh. Abducts the flexed thigh. Gemellus Superior and Gamellus Inferior: Gemellus Superior (smaller than G. Gamellus Inferior inferior) Attachments Origin: Arises from the dorsal surface Origin: Arises from the upper part of of the ischial spine Blends with the the ischial tuberosity Blends with the upper border of obturator internus lower border of obturator internus tendon Insertion: Medial surface of tendon Insertion: Medial surface of the greater trochanter. the greater trochanter. Vascular Supply Supplied by internal pudendal artery , Mainly by the medial circumflex inferior gluteal artery, and sometimes femoral artery superior gluteal artery Innervation Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1, Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, L5, S2) S1) Actions Assists obturator internus in lateral Rotates the extended thigh laterally rotation of the thigh May assist in May contribute to abduction of the abduction of the flexed thigh flexed thigh Quadratus Femoris: Attachments Origin: Arises from the ischial tuberosity Insertion: Trochanteric crest of the femur Vascular Supply Inferior gluteal artery and medial circumflex femoral artery Innervation Nerve to quadratus femoris (L4, L5, S1) Actions Produces lateral rotation of the thigh","sourceRef":"Question Pages 888-889; Solution Pages 906-908","sourcePage":888,"sourcePageEnd":889},{"id":"anatomy-22-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which among the following structures do not pass through both the greater and lesser sciatic foramen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pudendal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal pudendal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Nerve to obturator internus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh","explanation":"The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh only passes through the greater sciatic foramen and not the lesser foramen. Structures passing through greater and lesser sciatic foramen: Greater sciatic notch/foramen/gateway of gluteal region Lesser sciatic notch The piriformis, emerging from the pelvis, fills the foramen Tendon of obturator internus Pudendal nerve (Option A) almost completely. It is the key muscle of the region. Internal pudendal vessels (Option B) Nerve to obturator Structures passing above the piriformis are: Superior internus (Option C) The upper and lower parts of the gluteal nerve Superior gluteal vessels Structures passing foramen are filled up by the origins of the two gemelli below the piriformis are: Inferior gluteal nerve Inferior muscles. gluteal vessels Sciatic nerve The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh Nerve to quadratus femoris Nerve to obturator internus (Option C) Internal pudendal vessels (Option B) Pudendal nerve (Option A)","sourceRef":"Question Page 889; Solution Pages 908-909","sourcePage":889,"sourcePageEnd":889},{"id":"anatomy-22-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 35-year-old athlete presents with difficulty climbing stairs and rising from a seated position. Upon examination, there is weakness in hip extension. Which muscle is most likely affected, and what is the nerve supply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Psoas major; Anterior rami of L1, L2, L3"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gluteus maximus; Inferior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gluteus maximus; Superior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sartorius; Femoral nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gluteus maximus; Inferior gluteal nerve","explanation":"Gluteus maximus is the chief extensor of the hip , and weakness would impair actions like rising from a seated position. - Its nerve supply is the inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2). Action of the Hip joint, corresponding muscles and their innervation: Action Muscles Nerve supply Flexion Psoas major (chief flexor) (Option A) Anterior rami of L1, L2, L3 Iliacus (chief flexor) Sartorius (Option Femoral nerve (L2, L3) D) Rectus femoris Pectineus Extension Gluteus maximus (chief extensor) Inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2) (Option B) Hamstring muscles Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) Abduction Gluteus medius and minimus (chief Superior gluteal nerve (L5, S1) abductors) Tensor fasciae latae (Option C) Adduction Adductor longus Adductor brevis Obturator nerve (L2, L3, L4) Adductor magnus (chief adductors) Gracilis Pectineus Double nerve supply (Femoral nerve and obturator nerve) Medial rotation Anterior fibres of gluteus minimus Superior gluteal nerve (L5, S1) and medius (chief medial rotators) Tensor fasciae latae Lateral rotation Piriformis Ventral rami of S1, S2 Obturator externus Posterior division of obturator nerve (L2, L3, L4) Obturator internus and Associated Nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1, gemelli, Quadratus femoris (These S2) muscles are generally termed short rotators)","sourceRef":"Question Page 889; Solution Pages 909-910","sourcePage":889,"sourcePageEnd":889},{"id":"anatomy-22-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":8,"question":"In a clinical setting, a physician suspects a weakness in the gluteus maximus muscle. Which of the following functions would not be affected by this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extension of flexed thigh"},{"letter":"B","text":"Raising trunk after stooping"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral rotation and abduction of the thigh"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stabilization of pelvis during quiet standing"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Stabilization of pelvis during quiet standing","explanation":"The gluteus maximus muscle stabilizes the hip during walking and not during quiet standing. - It is one of the major muscles of the gluteal region. Features Largest and most superficial in the gluteal region Broad, thick, and quadrilateral Has an overlying adipose fascia Fascicular architecture with large bundles of fibres separated by fibrous septa Origin Gluteal surface of the ilium behind the posterior gluteal line Outer slope of the dorsal segment of ilium Dorsal surfaces of the sacrum and ilium Sacrotuberous ligament Insertions 3/4th of the muscle into the iliotibial tract 1/4th of the muscle into the gluteal tuberosity Vascular Supply Primarily supplied by the inferior gluteal artery (≈ 2/3) Superior gluteal artery Additional supply from the first perforator of profunda femoris and medial circumflex femoral artery Minor branches from lateral circumflex femoral, lateral sacral, and internal pudendal arteries. Innervation Innervated by the inferior gluteal nerve (L5, S1, S2) Actions Extends flexed thigh (Option A) Stabilizes pelvis during gait (vs Option D) Active in raising trunk after stooping (Option B) Active in lateral rotation and abduction of the thigh (Option C) Stabilizes femur via iliotibial tract The gluteus maximus is generally inactive during Quiet standing When swaying forward at the ankle joints When bending forward at the hip joints to touch the toes Testing Tested by hip extension against resistance in prone position with knees flexed","sourceRef":"Question Pages 889-890; Solution Pages 910-911","sourcePage":889,"sourcePageEnd":890},{"id":"anatomy-22-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 30-year-old runner complains of difficulty flexing his knee after long-distance running. He reports that once his knee locks in an extended position, it takes effort to initiate flexion again. On examination, you notice that the patient struggles to begin flexion from full extension. Which muscle is likely impaired?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Quadriceps femoris"},{"letter":"B","text":"Popliteus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Biceps femoris"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vastus medialis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Popliteus","explanation":"The popliteus muscle is responsible for unlocking the knee by causing lateral rotation of the femur on the tibia during the initial phase of flexion. - Impairment of this muscle would make it difficult to initiate flexion from full extension. Action of the knee joint, corresponding muscles and their innervation Action Muscle Innervation Flexion Biceps femoris Semitendinosus Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, Semimembranosus S1, S2) Extension Quadriceps femoris (Option A) Femoral nerve Medial rotation of flexed leg Popliteus Tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1) Semimembranosus Semitendinosus Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) Lateral rotation of flexed leg Biceps femoris (Option C) Tibial part of the sciatic nerve (L5, S1, S2) Locking Vastus medialis (Option D) Femoral nerve Unlocking Popliteus (Option B) Tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1) Locking Unlocking Medial rotation of the femur on the tibia during the Lateral rotation of the femur on the tibia during the initial terminal phase of extension. phase of the flexion It is brought about by the quadriceps femoris It is brought about by the popliteus muscle Locked knee becomes rigid Unlocked knee can be further flexed All ligaments are taut All ligaments are relaxed Pathological locking - A meniscus tear can cause a knee to lock when a torn piece of cartilage falls off and gets stuck in the knee , or when a large tear folds over and gets stuck. This is called true locking . - Pseudo locking is when the knee can't fully extend due to pain or an effusion .","sourceRef":"Question Page 890; Solution Pages 911-913","sourcePage":890,"sourcePageEnd":890},{"id":"anatomy-22-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":10,"question":"The iliotibial tract provides attachment to which of the following muscles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gluteus medius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tensor fasciae latae"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gluteus maximus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both B and C"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Both B and C","explanation":"The deep fascia of the thigh presents two modifications – iliotibial tract and saphenous opening. Iliotibial tract: - The iliotibial tract splits into two layers to enclose the tensor fasciae latae and gluteus maximus muscles. - Between these muscles, it forms a thickened sheet called the gluteal aponeurosis, which also covers the gluteus medius. - The superficial lamina attaches to the tubercle of the iliac crest. - The deep lamina attaches to the capsule of the hip joint. - Inferiorly, the tract attaches to a smooth area on the anterior surface of the lateral condyle of the tibia. Function : - Stabilizes the knee both in extension and in partial flexion. - Used during walking and running. - When leaning forward with slightly flexed knees, the iliotibial tract supports the knee against gravity, preventing the individual from falling forward.","sourceRef":"Question Page 890; Solution Page 913","sourcePage":890,"sourcePageEnd":890},{"id":"anatomy-22-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following does not participate in the cruciate anastomosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior gluteal artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Medial circumflex femoral artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deep femoral artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Deep femoral artery","explanation":"Cruciate anastomosis is situated at the upper part of the back of the femur at the level of lesser trochanter. The cruciate anastomosis is formed by the following arteries:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 890-891; Solution Pages 913-914","sourcePage":890,"sourcePageEnd":891},{"id":"anatomy-22-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":12,"question":"The femoral nerve splits into anterior and posterior divisions, which are separated by which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Femoral vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral circumflex femoral artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inguinal ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Obturator artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lateral circumflex femoral artery","explanation":"- The chief nerve of the anterior compartment of the thigh is the femoral nerve. - It is the largest branch of the lumbar plexus and arises from the dorsal divisions of the anterior primary rami of the L2, L3, and L4 nerves. - The femoral nerve enters the thigh posterior to the inguinal ligament, just lateral to the femoral sheath. - About 2 cm below the inguinal ligament, the femoral nerve divides into anterior and posterior divisions, separated by the lateral circumflex femoral artery. Anterior Division: - Gives off two cutaneous branches and one muscular branch: Medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) Intermediate cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) Muscular branch to the sartorius muscle - Medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) - Intermediate cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) - Muscular branch to the sartorius muscle - Medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) - Intermediate cutaneous nerve of the thigh (cutaneous) - Muscular branch to the sartorius muscle Posterior Division: - Gives off one cutaneous branch and four muscular branches: Saphenous nerve (cutaneous) Muscular branches supplying the quadriceps femoris. - Saphenous nerve (cutaneous) - Muscular branches supplying the quadriceps femoris. - Saphenous nerve (cutaneous) - Muscular branches supplying the quadriceps femoris.","sourceRef":"Question Page 891; Solution Pages 914-916","sourcePage":891,"sourcePageEnd":891},{"id":"anatomy-22-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which two nerves provide the dual innervation of the pectineus muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Obturator Nerve and Femoral Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sciatic Nerve and Tibial Nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial Pectoral Nerve and Lateral Pectoral Nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Musculocutaneous Nerve and Radial Nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Obturator Nerve and Femoral Nerve","explanation":"The Pectenius muscle is a composite/hybrid muscle that is innervated by both the obturator and femoral nerve. Hybrid muscles Composite or hybrid muscles contain multiple fiber types that perform the same function and are innervated by different nerves for each fiber set. Muscle Innervation Pectoralis Major (Option C) Sternocostal Head Medial Pectoral Nerve (C8, T1) Clavicular Head Lateral Pectoral Nerve (C5, 6, 7) Subscapularis Upper & Lower Subscapular Nerves (C5, 6) Brachialis (Option D) Musculocutaneous Nerve (C5, 6, 7) (medial) Radial Nerve (C5, 6, 7, 8, T1) (lateral) Flexor Digitorum Profundus Lateral Part Anterior Interosseous Branch of Median Nerve (C8, T1) Medial Part Ulnar Nerve (C8, T1) Pectineus (Option A) Obturator Nerve (L2, 3, 4) Femoral Nerve (L2, 3, 4) Adductor Magnus Adductor Part Posterior Division of Obturator Nerve (L2, 3, 4) Hamstring Part Tibial Branch of Sciatic Nerve (L4, 5, S1) Biceps Femoris Long Head Tibial Branch of Sciatic Nerve (L4, 5, S1) Short Head Common Peroneal Division of Sciatic Nerve (L4, 5, S1) Digastric muscle Anterior belly Nerve to mylohyoid Posterior belly Facial nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 891; Solution Pages 916-917","sourcePage":891,"sourcePageEnd":891},{"id":"anatomy-22-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following regarding the marked structure is true? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nerve to obturator internus passes above it"},{"letter":"B","text":"Innervated by L5, S1 nerve roots"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pudendal nerve passes below it"},{"letter":"D","text":"It is a medial rotator of the hip joint"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pudendal nerve passes below it","explanation":"The structure marked here is the Piriformis muscle, and the Pudendal nerve passes inferior to it. Piriformis: Attachments Origin : Anterior surface of sacrum (3 digitations) Gluteal surface of ilium near posterior inferior iliac spine Capsule of sacroiliac joint Insertion: Medial side of the upper border of the greater trochanter of the femur The piriformis muscle emerges from the pelvis, almost filling the greater sciatic foramen completely and is the key muscle of the area. Structures running superior to piriformis Superior gluteal nerve and vessel (Option A) Structures running inferior to piriformis. Inferior gluteal nerve and vessels Greater trochanter Sciatic nerve Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh Nerve to quadratus femoris Nerve to obturator internus Internal pudendal vessels Pudendal nerve (Option C) Vascular Supply Main supply: Superior gluteal artery Contributions: Gemellar branches of internal pudendal, inferior gluteal artery, lateral sacral artery Innervation Innervated by superior gluteal nerve and ventral rami of S1 and S2 (Option B) Actions Laterally rotates extended thigh (Option D) Abducts flexed thigh Testing No isolated testing; assess for dysfunction by having the seated subject abduct thighs (buttock pain indicates potential involvement) Pudendal Nerve: - Origin : Arises from the ventral divisions of S2, S3, and S4 sacral ventral rami. - Formation Location : Just above the superior border of the sacrotuberous ligament and upper fibres of ischiococcygeus. - Leaves the pelvis via the greater sciatic foramen between piriformis and ischiococcygeus. - Pathway : Enters the gluteal region. Passes dorsal to the sacrospinous ligament, near its attachment to the ischial spine. Lies medially to the internal pudendal vessels. - Enters the gluteal region. - Passes dorsal to the sacrospinous ligament, near its attachment to the ischial spine. - Lies medially to the internal pudendal vessels. - Further Course : Accompanies the internal pudendal artery through the lesser sciatic foramen into the pudendal canal. - Branches : In the posterior part of the canal, gives rise to: Inferior anal nerve Perineal nerve Dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris (continues anteriorly) - Inferior anal nerve - Perineal nerve - Dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris (continues anteriorly) - Enters the gluteal region. - Passes dorsal to the sacrospinous ligament, near its attachment to the ischial spine. - Lies medially to the internal pudendal vessels. - Inferior anal nerve - Perineal nerve - Dorsal nerve of the penis or clitoris (continues anteriorly)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 891-892; Solution Pages 917-919","sourcePage":891,"sourcePageEnd":892},{"id":"anatomy-22-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 30-year-old male is being examined for abnormal gait. His Trendelenberg sign is positive. What is false about testing for the Trendelenburg sign?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The side of the hip that droops down is the unaffected side"},{"letter":"B","text":"The gluteus medius and minimus muscles are affected"},{"letter":"C","text":"The patients have a characteristic lurching gait"},{"letter":"D","text":"The uncompensated Trendelenburg sign is elicited with a walking patient"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The uncompensated Trendelenburg sign is elicited with a walking patient","explanation":"The uncompensated Trendelenburg sign is elicited in a patient in a standing position on one limb and the compensated is elicited in a walking patient. Tensor Fasciae Latae Gluteus Medius Gluteus Minimus Attachments Origin : Outer lip of the Origin: Gluteal surface of Origin: Gluteal surfaces of iliac crest extending from the ilium between anterior the ilium between anterior the anterior superior iliac and posterior gluteal lines & inferior gluteal lines. spine to the tubercle of the Insertion: Oblique ridge on Insertion: Anterior aspect crest. Insertion : Iliotibial the lateral surface of the of the Greater trochanter tract 3–5 cm below the greater trochanter. level of the greater trochanter. Relations Between gluteus medius Covered by gluteus Deep to gluteus medius and sartorius Covered by maximus (posterior third) Related to hip joint capsule fascia lata Deep to gluteus minimus Vascular Supply Mainly from lateral Deep branch of superior Main trunk and deep circumflex femoral artery gluteal artery Trochanteric branch of superior gluteal Supplied by superior anastomosis arter y Trochanteric gluteal artery anastomosis Innervation Superior gluteal nerve (L4, Superior gluteal nerve (L4, Superior gluteal nerve (L4, L5, S1) L5, S1) L5, S1) Actions Hip flexor and abductor Abducts thigh Medial & Abducts thigh Medial and Stabilizes pelvis Helps lateral rotation of thigh lateral rotation of thigh maintain extended knee Supports pelvis during single-leg stance Testing Depression below anterior Medial rotation against Medial rotation against superior iliac spine resistance Abduction resistance Abduction Abduction against against resistance against resistance resistance Trendelenberg sign: - When the body's weight is balanced on one leg, the gluteal muscles on the supported side help elevate the opposite, unsupported side of the pelvis. - However, if there is a malfunction in the abductor mechanism, the Sound side sinks (Option A) , which is referred to as a positive Trendelenburg sign (uncompensated). - This sign indicates issues with hip abductors, such as paralysis of the gluteus medius and minimus (Option B) ; problems with the fulcrum, including congenital or pathological hip dislocations; and concerns related to body weight, such as an unhealed fracture of the femoral neck. - When patients with the gluteus medius and minimus paralysis walk, they have a characteristic lurching gait (Option C) ie, during the single leg supporting phase of walking, there is leaning of the trunk to the affected side known as the compensated Trendelenburg sign . - Duck waddling gait is seen when both the sides are affected.","sourceRef":"Question Page 892; Solution Pages 919-921","sourcePage":892,"sourcePageEnd":892},{"id":"anatomy-22-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A patient reports pain and decreased sensation along the posterolateral aspect of the lower leg, extending onto the lateral side of the foot, following an ankle sprain. Which nerve is most likely injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sural nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Saphenous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Sural nerve","explanation":"The sural nerve innervates the posterolateral aspect of the leg . Injury can occur due to ankle sprains , fractures, or tight footwear, leading to pain and sensory loss in this region. Cutaneous Innervation of Lower Limb: - Primarily from the lumbar and sacral plexuses. - Except some proximal uni segmental nerves arise from T12 or L1 spinal nerves. - Thigh Innervation: Main supply: Lateral and Posterior Cutaneous Nerves of the Thigh and Cutaneous Branch of Femoral Nerve. - Main supply: Lateral and Posterior Cutaneous Nerves of the Thigh and Cutaneous Branch of Femoral Nerve. - Leg Innervation: Anteromedial Aspect: Saphenous nerve (Option B) Posterolateral Aspect: Sural nerve Anterolateral Aspect: Superficial peroneal nerve (Option C) - Anteromedial Aspect: Saphenous nerve (Option B) - Posterolateral Aspect: Sural nerve - Anterolateral Aspect: Superficial peroneal nerve (Option C) - Main supply: Lateral and Posterior Cutaneous Nerves of the Thigh and Cutaneous Branch of Femoral Nerve. - Anteromedial Aspect: Saphenous nerve (Option B) - Posterolateral Aspect: Sural nerve - Anterolateral Aspect: Superficial peroneal nerve (Option C) - Foot Innervation: Dorsum of the Foot: Mainly by superficial peroneal nerves. Sole of the Foot: Supplied by cutaneous branches of medial and lateral plantar nerves. - Dorsum of the Foot: Mainly by superficial peroneal nerves. - Sole of the Foot: Supplied by cutaneous branches of medial and lateral plantar nerves. - Nerve Injury Risks: Iliohypogastric, Ilioinguinal, and Genitofemoral Nerve Injury: Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Injury: (Option D) Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. Sural Nerve Injury: (Option A) Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot. - Iliohypogastric, Ilioinguinal, and Genitofemoral Nerve Injury: Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. - Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. - Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. - Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Injury: (Option D) Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. - Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. - Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. - Sural Nerve Injury: (Option A) Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot. - Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. - Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot. - Dorsum of the Foot: Mainly by superficial peroneal nerves. - Sole of the Foot: Supplied by cutaneous branches of medial and lateral plantar nerves. - Iliohypogastric, Ilioinguinal, and Genitofemoral Nerve Injury: Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. - Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. - Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. - Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Injury: (Option D) Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. - Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. - Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. - Sural Nerve Injury: (Option A) Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot. - Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. - Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot. - Common during lower abdomen or spine surgeries. - Can result in anesthesia, hyperesthesia, neuralgia or causalgia in their sensory distribution. - Vulnerable to entrapment beneath the inguinal ligament near the anterior superior iliac spine. - Causes paresthesia or dysaesthesia (meralgia paresthetica) on the anterolateral aspect of the thigh, intensified by walking or hip extension. - Occur at the level of the ankle due to fractures or sprain, tight shoes, or surgery. - Cause pain and diminished sensation along the postero-lateral lower leg extending onto the lateral surface of the ankle and foot.","sourceRef":"Question Page 892; Solution Pages 921-924","sourcePage":892,"sourcePageEnd":892},{"id":"anatomy-22-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 40-year-old man is involved in a car accident and sustains a fracture to the ischial tuberosity. He now presents with weakness in knee flexion and hip extension, with intact sensation. Which muscle group and nerve are likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Quadriceps; femoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hamstrings; sciatic nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adductors; obturator nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gluteal muscles; superior gluteal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Hamstrings; sciatic nerve","explanation":"- The hamstrings , innervated by the sciatic nerve , are responsible for knee flexion and hip extension. - A fracture of the ischial tuberosity can affect the attachment site of these muscles. Sciatic nerve Size and Origin: - Thickest nerve in the body (1.5 to 2 cm wide). - Arises from the sacral plexus in the pelvis. Components: - Tibial Part : Formed by ventral divisions of anterior primary rami (L4, L5, S1, S2, S3). - Common Peroneal Part : Formed by dorsal divisions of anterior primary rami (L4, L5, S1, S2). Course: - Begins in the pelvis, in front of the piriformis muscle. - Exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis. - Divides into tibial and common peroneal nerves at the superior angle of the popliteal fossa. Relations: - Deep Relations: Ischium, tendon of obturator internus, quadratus femoris, adductor magnus. - Ischium, tendon of obturator internus, quadratus femoris, adductor magnus. - Superficial Relations : Gluteus maximus (in the gluteal region) and long head of biceps femoris (in the thigh). - Gluteus maximus (in the gluteal region) and long head of biceps femoris (in the thigh). - Ischium, tendon of obturator internus, quadratus femoris, adductor magnus. - Gluteus maximus (in the gluteal region) and long head of biceps femoris (in the thigh). Branches: - Articular branches to the hip joint (gluteal region) . - Muscular branches (Option B) The tibial part supplies the semitendinosus, the semimembranosus, the long head of the biceps femoris, and the ischial head of the adductor magnus (medial side) The common peroneal part supplies only a short head of biceps femoris (lateral side). - The tibial part supplies the semitendinosus, the semimembranosus, the long head of the biceps femoris, and the ischial head of the adductor magnus (medial side) - The common peroneal part supplies only a short head of biceps femoris (lateral side). - The tibial part supplies the semitendinosus, the semimembranosus, the long head of the biceps femoris, and the ischial head of the adductor magnus (medial side) - The common peroneal part supplies only a short head of biceps femoris (lateral side). Clinical Correlation: - Compression against the femur can cause \"sleeping foot,\" resulting in lower limb paresthesia when sitting for long periods. Quadriceps; femoral nerve (Option A) : Quadriceps extend the knee, not flex it. Adductors; obturator nerve (Option C) : Adductors handle hip adduction, not knee flexion or hip extension. Gluteal muscles; superior gluteal nerve (Option D) : Gluteal muscles control hip abduction, not knee flexion or hip extension.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 892-893; Solution Pages 924-926","sourcePage":892,"sourcePageEnd":893},{"id":"anatomy-22-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Identify the marked structures in the given image: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a) Semitendinosus, b) Biceps femoris, c) Popliteal artery, d) Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"a) Semitendinosus, b) Biceps femoris, c) Tibial nerve, d) Popliteal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"a) Biceps femoris, b) Semitendinosus, c) Popliteal artery, d) Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"a) Biceps femoris, b) Semitendinosus, c) Tibial nerve, d) Popliteal artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. a) Biceps femoris, b) Semitendinosus, c) Tibial nerve, d) Popliteal artery","explanation":"The given image is the right popliteal fossa; therefore, - The Superolateral boundary is formed by a) Biceps femoris - The Superomedial boundary is formed by b) the Semitendinosus - M arked structure c) is Tibial nerve and d) is Popliteal artery because in the upper part of the fossa, nerve is lateral and the artery is medial. Popliteal fossa The popliteal fossa is a diamond-shaped hollow at the back of the knee, becoming prominent when flexed, and serves as a passage for major vessels and nerves between the thigh and leg. Boundaries Superomedial : Semitendinosus and Semimembranosus Superolateral: Biceps Femoris Inferomedial: Medial head of Gastrocnemius Inferolateral: Lateral head of Gastrocnemius and Plantaris Floor (anterior wall): Formed by the popliteal surface of the femur, knee joint capsule, oblique popliteal ligament, and popliteal fascia covering the popliteus muscle. Roof (posterior wall): Formed by the popliteal fascia, containing the short saphenous vein and three cutaneous nerves (posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh, medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh, sural communicating nerve). All structures pierce the roof except the medial cutaneous nerve of the thigh. Contents Main Contents: Popliteal artery and Additional Structures: Posterior branches Popliteal vein and cutaneous nerve of the thigh tributaries Tibial nerve and branches (terminal part) Descending genicular Common peroneal nerve and branch of obturator nerve Terminal branches Popliteal lymph nodes part of the short saphenous vein Popliteal fat pad Arrangement of Key Structures Upper part: Nerve, Vein, Artery (NVA/AVN pattern) (lateral to medial) Middle part: Nerve, Vein, Artery (superficial to deep) Lower part: Artery, Vein, Nerve (lateral to medial)","sourceRef":"Question Page 893; Solution Pages 926-929","sourcePage":893,"sourcePageEnd":893},{"id":"anatomy-22-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following sets of muscles is referred to as \"guy rope\" muscles that stabilise the bony pelvis on the femur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Semitendinosus, Semimembranosus, Gracilis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sartorius, Gracilis, Semitendinosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Semimembranosus, Sartorius, Adductor magnus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Semimembranosus, Gracilis, Sartorius"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Semitendinosus, Semimembranosus, Gracilis; B. Sartorius, Gracilis, Semitendinosus","explanation":"The term \"guy rope muscles\" refers to the Sartorius, Gracilis, and Semitendinosus muscles, which each originate from different compartments of the thigh—anterior, medial, and posterior, respectively. - These muscles play a crucial role in stabilizing the pelvis on the femur. Guy rope muscles 1. Sartorius 2. Gracilis 3. Semitendinosus Compartment Part of the anterior Part of the medial Part of the posterior compartment compartment compartment Innervation Femoral nerve Obturator nerve Sciatic nerve Action Hip flexion, abduction, and Hip adduction and knee Knee flexion and hip external rotation flexion extension Origin Anterior superior iliac Inferior pubic ramus Ischial tuberosity spine (ASIS) Insertion Upper part of the medial surface of the tibia . ( Pes anserinus or Goosefoot tendon is the broad, conjoined tendon formed by these 3 muscles, inserting on the medial surface of the proximal tibia) - Together, these muscles anchor below at the tibia, spreading out above to support the pelvic structure, functioning effectively like \"guy ropes\" to maintain stability. Bursa Anserinus , situated on the upper medial surface of the tibia, separates the tendons of the Sartorius, Gracilis, and Semitendinosus muscles at their insertion points and also isolates them from the tibial collateral ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 893-894; Solution Pages 929-930","sourcePage":893,"sourcePageEnd":894},{"id":"anatomy-22-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 25-year-old male presents with difficulty in adducting his thigh after sustaining an injury during a soccer match. Upon examination, weakness in adduction and medial rotation of the thigh is noted. Which of the following muscles is primarily responsible for these actions, and what is its nerve supply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectineus; Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gracilis; Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adductor longus; Obturator nerve (posterior division)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adductor part of adductor magnus; Obturator nerve (posterior division)"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Adductor part of adductor magnus; Obturator nerve (posterior division)","explanation":"- The adductor part of the adductor magnus muscle is primarily responsible for thigh adduction and medial rotation. - It is innervated by the obturator nerve, specifically the posterior division. - The weakness observed in these actions following the injury indicates the involvement of adductor magnus. Muscles of the medial (adductor) compartment of the thigh: Muscle Origin Insertion Nerve Supply Actions Pectineus (Option A) Pecten pubis Line extending from Femoral nerve and Adduction of the Superior ramus of the lesser trochanter obturator nerve thigh Flexion of thigh the pubis to the linea aspera Gracilis (Option B) Body of the pubis Upper part of the Obturator nerve Adduction of thigh and ischiopubic medial surface of Flexion and medial ramus tibia rotation of leg Adductor longus Body of the pubis Middle 1/3rd of linea Obturator nerve Adduction and (Option C) aspera medial rotation of thigh Adductor brevis Body and inferior Pectineal lines and Obturator nerve Adduction of thigh ramus of the pubis proximal part of linea aspera Adductor magnus Hamstring part: Adductor part: Adductor part: By Adduction and (largest muscle of inferolateral part of Medial margin of the obturator nerve medial rotation of this compartment) the ischial tuberosity gluteal tuberosity , Hamstring part : By thigh Weak extension (Option D) Adductor part: outer linea aspera, medial the tibial part of of hip joint part of the ischiopubi supracondylar line sciatic nerve c ramus Hamstring part: Adductor tubercle","sourceRef":"Question Page 894; Solution Pages 930-931","sourcePage":894,"sourcePageEnd":894},{"id":"anatomy-22-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following is not the action of the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexion of knee"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexion of thigh"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extension of knee"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexion of leg"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Extension of knee","explanation":"- The marked structure is Sartorius . - It is responsible for flexing the thigh at the hip, flexing the knee , and aiding in the lateral rotation of the thigh . - However, it does not perform knee extension; rather, it assists in knee flexion when the leg is raised. Muscles of the front of the thigh: Muscle Nerve Supply Actions 1. Quadriceps femoris (a) Rectus femoris Femoral nerve Flexion of the hip joint and extension of the knee joint (b) Vastus lateralis Femoral nerve Extension of the knee joint (c) Vastus medialis Femoral nerve Extension of the knee joint (d) Vastus intermedius Femoral nerve Extension of the knee joint 2. Articularis genu Femoral nerve Pulls up the synovial membrane of the knee joint during its extension 3. Sartorius Femoral nerve Hip: Flexion, abduction and lateral rotation Knee: Flexion, medial rotation Leg: Flexion (Options A, B, D) 4. Tensor fasciae latae Superior gluteal nerve (different from Abduction of the hip joint the rest)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 894-895; Solution Pages 931-933","sourcePage":894,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"anatomy-22-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 50-year-old male presents with leg pain and weakness after a heavy deadlift. He reports heaviness in his feet, difficulty standing on his toes, and performing calf raises. Neurological examination reveals weakness in plantar flexion, inability to flex his toes and diminished ankle reflexes. Which spinal cord segments are primarily involved in this patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L4, L5"},{"letter":"B","text":"S1, S2"},{"letter":"C","text":"L2, L3"},{"letter":"D","text":"L3, L4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. S1, S2","explanation":"The weakness in plantar flexion and difficulty in flexing the toes i ndicate involvement of the muscles innervated by the S1 and S2 spinal segments, primarily the gastrocnemius and soleus . Segmental innervation of muscles of the lower limb: Segments Muscles innervated L1 Psoas major L2, L3 (Option C) Gracilis, pectineus, sartorius L3, L4 (Option D) Quadriceps femoris, adductors of the thigh L4, L5 (Option A) Tensor fasciae latae, tibialis anterior, tibialis posterior L5, S1 Gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, extensor hallucis longus, extensor digitorum longus, peroneus longus, peroneus brevis, semitendinosus, semimembranosus S1, S2 (Option B) Gluteus maximus, biceps femoris, gastrocnemius , soleus . S2, S3 Flexor digitorum longus, flexor hallucis longus, interossei Segmental innervation of joints of lower limb Joints Movements Hip joint Flexion, adduction, and medial rotation (L2, L3) Extension, abduction, and lateral rotation (L4, L5) Knee joint Extension (L3, L4) Flexion (L5; S1) Ankle joint Dorsiflexion (L4, L5) Plantar flexion (S1, S2) Foot Invertors (L4, L5) Evertors (L5, S1)","sourceRef":"Question Page 895; Solution Pages 933-935","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"anatomy-22-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with a foot drop following a recent knee injury. Which of the following nerves, part of the sacral plexus, is most likely injured, leading to the inability to dorsiflex the foot?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior Femoral Cutaneous Nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Common peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pudendal nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Common peroneal nerve","explanation":"- Common peroneal nerve branches from the sciatic nerve and innervates the muscles responsible for dorsiflexion of the foot (e.g., tibialis anterior is supplied by deep peroneal nerve, which is a branch of common peroneal nerve). - Injury to this nerve often results in foot drop. Sacral Plexus: - The sacral plexus is a complex network of nerves formed by the anterior rami (ventral rami) of the L4, L5, S1, S2, and S3 spinal nerves, with some contributions from S4. - It is located on the posterior wall of the pelvis, resting on the anterior surface of the piriformis muscle. - The sacral plexus plays a critical role in providing motor and sensory innervation to parts of the pelvis, perineum, and lower limbs. Root Values and Divisions Root Values: L4 to S4 contribute to the sacral plexus. Divisions: - Anterior Division: Primarily supplies muscles involved in flexion and related functions. - Posterior Division: Supplies muscles associated with extension and related movements. Major Nerves of the Sacral Plexus Nerve Formation Pathway Functions Sciatic Nerve The largest nerve in the Exits the pelvis through the Supplies the posterior body, derived from both greater sciatic foramen, thigh muscles. It divides anterior and posterior passing beneath the into two key branches: divisions of L4, L5, S1, S2, piriformis muscle, and Tibial Nerve (Anterior and S3. travels down the posterior Division) (Option B): thigh. Innervates the posterior leg and foot muscles. Common Peroneal Nerve (Posterior Division): Innervates muscles of the lateral and anterior leg. Clinical Note : Injury to the common peroneal nerve can result in foot drop, a condition characterized by difficulty lifting the front part of the foot. Pudendal Nerve (Option D) Arises from the anterior Travels out of the pelvis Provides motor and divisions of S2, S3, and S4. through the greater sciatic sensory innervation to the foramen and into the perineum, including perineum via the lesser muscles of the pelvic floor sciatic foramen. and external genitalia. It is crucial for maintaining continence and sexual function. Superior Gluteal Nerve Originates from the Exits the pelvis via the Innervates the gluteus posterior divisions of L4, greater sciatic foramen, medius, gluteus minimus, L5, and S1. superior to the piriformis and tensor fasciae latae, muscle. which are responsible for hip abduction and pelvis stabilization during walking. Inferior Gluteal Nerve Originates from the Exits the pelvis through the Innervates the gluteus posterior divisions of L5, greater sciatic foramen, maximus, the primary S1, and S2. inferior to the piriformis. extensor of the hip joint. Posterior Femoral Arises from the anterior Travels alongside the Provides sensory Cutaneous Nerve (Option and posterior divisions of sciatic nerve, exiting the innervation to the skin on A) S1, S2, and S3. pelvis through the greater the posterior thigh and sciatic foramen. lower buttocks. Minor Nerves of the Sacral Plexus - Nerve to Piriformis (S1, S2) : Supplies the piriformis muscle, which assists in lateral rotation of the hip. - Nerve to Quadratus Femoris (L4, L5, S1) : Innervates the quadratus femoris and inferior gemellus muscles, responsible for lateral hip rotation and stabilization. - Nerve to Obturator Internus (L5, S1, S2): Innervates the obturator internus and superior gemellus muscles, involved in lateral rotation of the thigh. Lumbosacral Trunk The lumbosacral trunk, formed by the anterior rami of L4 and L5, contributes to the sacral plexus and joins the sacral nerves to form branches that innervate the lower extremities. Clinical Relevance - Sciatica: Compression or irritation of the sciatic nerve leads to pain that radiates down the leg (sciatica). - Pudendal Neuralgia: Compression of the pudendal nerve can cause pain or dysfunction in the perineal region, impacting bladder control, bowel function, and sexual health. - Piriformis Syndrome: In certain individuals, the sciatic nerve may pass through the piriformis muscle, leading to entrapment and pain. - Foot Drop: Injury to the common peroneal nerve can cause foot drop, where a person cannot dorsiflex the foot, leading to a characteristic \"slapping\" gait.","sourceRef":"Question Page 895; Solution Pages 935-938","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":895},{"id":"anatomy-22-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-22","chapter":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topic":"Lesson 22 - Muscles and Nerve Supply of the Hip, Thigh, and Gluteal Region","topicIndex":22,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents to the clinic with complaints of tingling and numbness in the outer aspect of his right thigh. He reports that the symptoms worsen when he sits for prolonged periods and improves when he stands or walks. Upon examination, there is a sensory deficit in the lateral thigh region. Which nerve root value is primarily involved in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L1 and L2"},{"letter":"B","text":"L2 and L3"},{"letter":"C","text":"L2, L3 and L4"},{"letter":"D","text":"L4 and L5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. L2 and L3","explanation":"- Based on the clinical scenario, the patient's symptoms align with meralgia paresthetica, marked by tingling and numbness in the outer thigh that worsens with prolonged sitting and improves with standing or walking. - This condition results from compression of the lateral femoral cutaneous nerve, which is primarily derived from the dorsal divisions of the L2 and L3 nerve roots. Lumbar Plexus: - The lumbar plexus is a network of nerves formed by the anterior rami (ventral rami) of the lumbar spinal nerves, specifically L1 to L4. - It is located within the psoas major muscle in the posterior abdominal wall. - The lumbar plexus is responsible for providing motor and sensory innervation to the pelvic region and the lower limb. Division of Roots The roots of the lumbar plexus can be categorised into two main divisions: - Anterior Division: Comprising roots that generally supply flexor muscles and associated sensory areas. - Posterior Division: Comprising roots that typically supply extensor muscles and their corresponding sensory territories. Major Nerves of the Lumbar Plexus Nerve Formation Pathway Functions Obturator Nerve Formed from the ventral It exits the pelvis through Innervates the muscles of divisions of L2, L3, and L4. the obturator foramen. the medial thigh (adductors) and provides sensory innervation to the skin of the medial thigh. Femoral Nerve Formed from the dorsal It passes under the Supplies the muscles of divisions of L2, L3, and L4. inguinal ligament into the the anterior thigh (such as (Option C) anterior compartment of quadriceps) and provides the thigh. sensory innervation to the anterior thigh and part of the medial leg. Lateral Femoral Formed from the dorsal It courses laterally across Provides purely sensory Cutaneous Nerve divisions of L2 and L3. the iliacus muscle and innervation to the skin over under the inguinal ligament. the lateral aspect of the thigh. This nerve is particularly important in conditions such as meralgia paresthetica, where it can become compressed, leading to tingling and pain in the outer thigh. Most commonly injured during Mc Roberts manure & Laproscopic hernia surgery. Proximal Nerves: - Iliohypogastric Nerve (L1) Function: Supplies the skin over the lower abdomen and contributes to abdominal muscle innervation. Most common nerve entrapped in open inguinal hernia surgery, leads to the common cause of pain syndrome. - Function: Supplies the skin over the lower abdomen and contributes to abdominal muscle innervation. - Most common nerve entrapped in open inguinal hernia surgery, leads to the common cause of pain syndrome. - Ilioinguinal Nerve (L1) Function: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the groin, part of the thigh, root of the penis, clitoris, and anterior ■ of the scrotum. - Function: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the groin, part of the thigh, root of the penis, clitoris, and anterior ■ of the scrotum. - Genitofemoral Nerve (L1, L2) (Option A) Branches: Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Branches: Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. - Efferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is injured in open hernia repair. - Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Afferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Lumbosacral Trunk (L4, L5) (Option D) Function: Contributes to the formation of the sacral plexus, which innervates the lower limb. - Function: Contributes to the formation of the sacral plexus, which innervates the lower limb. - Function: Supplies the skin over the lower abdomen and contributes to abdominal muscle innervation. - Most common nerve entrapped in open inguinal hernia surgery, leads to the common cause of pain syndrome. - Function: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the groin, part of the thigh, root of the penis, clitoris, and anterior ■ of the scrotum. - Branches: Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. - Efferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is injured in open hernia repair. - Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Afferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Genital Branch: Supplies the cremaster muscle and the skin of the scrotum in males, and the labia majora in females. Efferent for cremasteric reflex. It is injured in open hernia repair. - Efferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is injured in open hernia repair. - Femoral Branch: Supplies the skin of the upper anterior thigh. Afferent for cremasteric reflex. It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Afferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Efferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is injured in open hernia repair. - Afferent for cremasteric reflex. - It is anesthetized in femoral vein catheterization. - Function: Contributes to the formation of the sacral plexus, which innervates the lower limb.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 895-896; Solution Pages 938-942","sourcePage":895,"sourcePageEnd":896},{"id":"anatomy-23-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 28 y/o football player experiences sudden pain on the inside of his left knee after getting tackled while running with the ball. He reports feeling a pop and is unable to continue playing. O/E, there is swelling, tenderness along the medial joint line, and pain when attempting to flex the knee. Which of the following injuries is most likely?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral collateral ligament (LCL) sprain"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) tear"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial meniscus tear"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) tear"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Medial meniscus tear","explanation":"The mechanism of injury (sudden pain on the inside of the knee, feeling a pop, and tenderness along the medial joint line) is characteristic of a medial meniscus tear, which is often associated with twisting movements and trauma. Football Player Knee Injuries: Structures in the knee and injuries Cruciate Ligaments The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) is more vulnerable than the posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) due to rotational forces in football. (Options B&D; ruled out) Sudden direction changes can overstretch and tear the ACL. Collateral Ligaments (Lateral & medial) Less frequently injured but essential for side-to-side stability. (Option A ruled out) Side tackles (severe adduction or abduction) can strain these structures, leading to tears or sprains. Menisci Medial Meniscus is more prone to injury than the lateral meniscus. The medial meniscus attaches to the tibial collateral ligament- hence, it is more susceptible to tears from rotational forces. Bucket-handle tears are common in athletes and are characterised by longitudinal meniscus tears due to pivoting and twisting in a slightly flexed knee. Menisci","sourceRef":"Question Page 943; Solution Pages 951-952","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":943},{"id":"anatomy-23-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding sustentaculum tali is correct. It is a shelf like projection, projecting from posterior surface of calcaneus. Superior surface of sustentaculum tali bears a facet for talus. Inferior surface of sustentaculum tali bears a groove for the tendon of flexor hallucis longus The medial surface of sustentaculum tali provides attachment to spring ligament and superficial fibres of deltoid ligament","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Only 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"1, 2, 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2, 3, 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"Only 4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 2, 3, 4","explanation":"Calcaneus: The calcaneus is the largest and strongest bone of the foot. It is situated below the talus and extends behind it. - It is directed forward and laterally with an upward inclination. Anterior surface It is the smallest and bears a concavo-convex articular facet to articulate with cuboid. Posterior surface: It is divided into the following three parts: - The upper one-third is smooth and related to a synovial bursa. - The middle one-third provides attachment to tendocalcaneus and plantaris. - Lower one-third is subcutaneous and weight bearing during standing posture Superior (dorsal) surface : It is divided into the following three areas: - Posterior one-third (non-articular). - Middle one-third (articular). - Anterior one-third (partly articular and partly nonarticular). Plantar (inferior) surface: It is rough and marked by three tubercles. - A small tubercle at the anterior end is called anterior tubercle. - An elevation at its posterior end is called calcaneal tuberosity presenting a large medial and small lateral tubercles. - The triangular area between the three tubercles provides attachment to the long plantar ligament. - A groove in front of the anterior tubercle gives attachment to short plantar ligament Lateral surface - It is almost flat and subcutaneous. - It presents a small elevation in its anterior part called peroneal trochlea (tubercle) which lies 2 cm below the tip of lateral malleolus. Medial surface : - It is concave from above downward - A shelf-like projection called sustentaculum tali projects from its upper anterior part. (Statement 1) - The superior surface of sustentaculum tali bears a facet for talus (Statement 2) while its inferior surface bears a groove for the tendon of flexor hallucis longus. (Statement 3) - Tendon of flexor digitorum longus is related to the medial surface of sustentaculum tali. - Medial surface of sustentaculum tali provides attachments to the following structures from anterior to posterior (Statement 4) : (a) Spring ligament. (b) A slip from the tibialis posterior tendon. (c) Deltoid ligament (superficial fibres). (d) Medial talocalcaneal ligament. Reference:","sourceRef":"Question Page 943; Solution Pages 952-954","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":943},{"id":"anatomy-23-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which statement best describes the relationship of the tibial nerve in the popliteal fossa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is the most medial structure crossing to the lateral side of the fossa."},{"letter":"B","text":"It is deep to the popliteal artery and vein."},{"letter":"C","text":"It lies lateral to the popliteal vessels crossing to the medial side."},{"letter":"D","text":"It does not enter the popliteal fossa."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It lies lateral to the popliteal vessels crossing to the medial side.","explanation":"In the popliteal fossa, the tibial nerve is positioned laterally to the popliteal artery and vein. - As it continues its course, it crosses to the medial side of the popliteal artery, becoming more superficial as it descends. Tibial Nerve Origin and Course: - The tibial nerve, a major branch of the sciatic nerve, originates from the ventral branches of the fourth and fifth lumbar and first to third sacral rami. - It travels down the back of the thigh, enters the popliteal fossa, and passes anterior to the soleus arch, continuing into the leg. - In the popliteal fossa, it becomes superficial and is lateral to the popliteal vessels, eventually crossing to the medial side of the popliteal artery. (Option A, B & D ruled out) - In the leg, it descends with the posterior tibial vessels, lying on the tibialis posterior and bifurcating under the flexor retinaculum into the medial and lateral plantar nerves. Branches: - Articular Branches: Supply the knee joint. - Muscular Branches: Innervate gastrocnemius, soleus, plantaris, popliteus, tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, and flexor hallucis longus. - Sural Nerve: Descends between gastrocnemius heads, supplying the posterior and lateral skin of the distal leg and foot. - Medial and Lateral Plantar Nerves: Innervate the muscles and skin of the sole. - Medial Calcaneal Nerve: Supplies sensation to the heel area. Clinical Significance: The tibial nerve is prone to injury in the popliteal fossa and can be compressed at the tendinous arch of the soleus, leading to potential damage from compartment syndrome or tarsal tunnel syndrome.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 943-944; Solution Pages 954-955","sourcePage":943,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"anatomy-23-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 35 y/o man is hiking on uneven terrain. As he steps on a rock with his right foot, his foot tilts inward, his medial arch elevates, and his foot becomes more rigid. This movement is best described as:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pronation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Supination"},{"letter":"C","text":"Eversion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dorsiflexion"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Supination","explanation":"The described movement involves supination , a tri-planar motion that includes adduction, inversion, and plantar flexion , resulting in an elevated medial arch and a more rigid foot. - This is commonly seen when standing or walking on uneven surfaces, such as a rock, where stability is needed. Supination: - A tri-planar motion involving: Adduction Inversion Plantar flexion - Adduction - Inversion - Plantar flexion - Occurs mainly at the talocalcaneonavicular and subtalar joints . - Common in activities like standing with feet widely separated or standing on an inclined surface. - The medial arch is elevated in supination, making the foot more rigid and stable. - Adduction - Inversion - Plantar flexion Pronation: - The opposite tri-planar motion involves: Abduction Eversion (Option C ruled out) Dorsiflexion (Option D ruled out) - Abduction - Eversion (Option C ruled out) - Dorsiflexion (Option D ruled out) - It helps adapt the foot to uneven surfaces by making it more pliable. - It occurs more when standing with feet close together. (Option A ruled out) - Pronation flattens the medial longitudinal arch, spreading forces over a broader area of the foot. - Abduction - Eversion (Option C ruled out) - Dorsiflexion (Option D ruled out) Supination and Pronation Combination Movements: - Both supination and pronation involve multiple movements and rarely occur in isolation. - They allow the foot to adapt to different terrains and provide a balance between stability and flexibility during standing and movement. Reference:","sourceRef":"Question Page 944; Solution Pages 955-957","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"anatomy-23-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 30 y/o athlete reports sudden pain in the back of his left leg and an inability to push off the ground while sprinting. Examination reveals a significant weakness in plantar flexion. Which muscle is primarily responsible for the action of plantarflexion in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tibialis Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrocnemius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tibialis Posterior"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibularis Longus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gastrocnemius","explanation":"The gastrocnemius is one of the main muscles involved in plantarflexion and is often affected in injuries like Achilles tendon rupture. Movements Muscles Involved Nerve Supply Clinical Aspects Dorsiflexion (Extension) Tibialis Anterior (Option A Deep peroneal nerve Foot drop due to deep ruled out) , Extensor peroneal nerve injury. Digitorum Longus, Extensor Hallucis Longus, Fibularis Tertius Plantarflexion (Flexion) Tibialis Posterior, Flexor Tibial nerve Achilles tendon rupture, Digitorum Longus, Flexor weakness in plantarflexion Hallucis Longus (Deep); causes difficulty walking, Gastrocnemius, Soleus, especially uphill or on Plantaris (Superficial) stairs. Eversion Fibularis Longus, Fibularis Superficial peroneal nerve Injury to the superficial Brevis (Option D ruled out) peroneal nerve causes difficulty in foot eversion and ankle instability. Inversion Tibialis Anterior (Anterior Tibial nerve Weakness of inversion compartment), Tibialis may contribute to flatfoot, Posterior (Posterior pain along the medial foot, compartment) and instability. Tibialis Posterior (Option C): While it assists in plantarflexion, it is not the primary muscle responsible for this action during explosive activities. Note : The superficial posterior compartment of the leg does not have any neurovascular structures.","sourceRef":"Question Page 944; Solution Pages 957-958","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":944},{"id":"anatomy-23-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 40 y/o woman presents with ankle instability and pain on the lateral side after rolling her ankle while walking on uneven ground. Examination reveals weakness in foot eversion and tenderness over the base of the fifth metatarsal. Which muscle is primarily responsible for foot eversion in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tibialis Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fibularis Longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fibularis Brevis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gastrocnemius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Fibularis Longus","explanation":"The Fibularis Longus muscle is one of the primary movers for foot eversion and supports the arches of the foot. The lateral compartment of the leg Muscles Fibularis Longus Fibularis Brevis Attachment Origin: Head and proximal two-thirds Origin: Distal two-thirds of the fibula, of the fibula, deep fascia, intermuscular septa. Insertion: intermuscular septa, and sometimes Tuberosity on the base of the fifth the lateral condyle of the tibia. metatarsal. Insertion: Base of the first metatarsal and medial cuneiform. Action Eversion of foot Plantar flexion of the Eversion of foot Limits inversion, ankle Supports longitudinal and relieving strain on lateral ankle transverse arches of foot ligaments Innervation Superficial fibular nerve (L5, S1) Superficial fibular nerve (L5, S1) Clinical aspects Risk of tendon dislocation if fibular Limits inversion, relieving strain on retinaculum ruptures lateral ankle ligaments. Lateral Compartment of Leg Muscles Anterior and Lateral Compartment of Leg Tibialis Anterior (Option A): This muscle is primarily responsible for dorsiflexion and inversion, not eversion. Fibularis Brevis (Option C): While it causes eversion, it mainly inserts at the base of the fifth metatarsal and is less involved in the general eversion action compared to Fibularis Longus. Gastrocnemius (Option D): This muscle primarily acts in plantarflexion and does not contribute to foot eversion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 944-945; Solution Pages 958-960","sourcePage":944,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"anatomy-23-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 25-year-old runner presents with pain along the front part of his right leg and difficulty in lifting the foot upwards after increasing his training intensity. O/E, he shows weakness in extending the big toe and foot drop. Which muscle is most likely affected in this patient, leading to his symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tibialis Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extensor Hallucis Longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extensor Digitorum Longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibularis Tertius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Extensor Hallucis Longus","explanation":"The patient presents with difficulty in dorsiflexing the foot and weakness in extending the big toe, which is primarily the function of the extensor hallucis longus. Anterior compartmen t of the leg Muscles Attachments Innervation Action Clinical aspects Tibialis Anterior Lateral condyle, Deep fibular nerve Dorsiflexes foot Weakness: foot drop proximal 1/2-2/3 (L4, L5) Inverts foot Supports Can cause shin lateral tibial shaft, medial arch splints. interosseous membrane. Inserts onto medial cuneiform and base of 1st metatarsal. Extensor Hallucis The middle half of Deep fibular nerve Extends hallux Weakness: Difficulty Longus the medial fibula and (L5) Dorsiflexes foot extending the big toe. interosseous membrane. Inserts onto the dorsal base of the distal phalanx of the hallux. Extensor Digitorum Lateral tibial Deep fibular nerve Extends lateral 4 Weakness: Issues Longus condyle, proximal (L5, S1) toes Dorsiflexes foot extending toes Foot 3/4 medial fibula, drop risk interosseous membrane. Tendons insert onto middle and distal phalanges of lateral 4 toes. Fibularis Tertius Distal 1/3 medial Deep fibular nerve Dorsiflexes foot Absent in some fibula, interosseous (L5, S1) Everts the foot individuals Helps membrane. Inserts prevent foot drag in onto the base of the gait. 5th metatarsal. Tibialis Anterior (Option A): While it contributes to dorsiflexion, the primary symptom of difficulty extending the big toe suggests the involvement of extensor hallucis longus. Extensor Digitorum Longus (Option C): Although it extends the lateral toes, it does not directly affect the movements of the big toe. Fibularis Tertius (Option D): This muscle aids in dorsiflexion and eversion but is less likely to cause difficulty in extending the big toe specifically. Reference:","sourceRef":"Question Page 945; Solution Pages 960-961","sourcePage":945,"sourcePageEnd":945},{"id":"anatomy-23-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following is the correct statement regarding the ligaments of the hip joint. The fibrous capsule is made up of outer fibres which are longitudinal and inner circular fibres which are called zona orbicularis. Ligamentum teres femoris transmits the arteries to the head of femur. IIiofemoral ligament is inverted Y shaped ligament. The acetabular labrum helps in holding the femur in position.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3,4"},{"letter":"B","text":"1 and 4"},{"letter":"C","text":"Only 1"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1,2,3,4","explanation":"The ligaments of the hip joint are as follows: - Capsular ligament (joint capsule). - Iliofemoral ligament (strongest). - Pubofemoral ligament. - Ischiofemoral ligament. - Transverse acetabular ligament. - Acetabular labrum. - Ligamentum teres femoris (round ligament of the head of femur). 1. The capsular ligament is a strong and dense fibrous sac which encloses the joint. - The capsule is made up of two types of fibres—inner circular fibres and outer longitudinal fibres. - The inner circular fibres form a collar around the femoral neck ( zona orbicularis ). These fibres are not directly attached to the bones (Statement 1) - The outer longitudinal fibres are reflected along the neck toward the head to form the retinacula. 2. The iliofemoral ligament is an inverted Y-shaped ligament, which lies anteriorly and intimately blended with the capsule.(Statement 3) This ligament consists of three parts: - a lateral thick band of oblique fibres,a medial thick band of vertical fibres, and a large central thin portion - Iliofemoral ligament is the strongest ligament of body and prevents the trunk from falling backward in the standing posture 3. The pubofemoral ligament is a triangular ligament with base above and apex below 4. The ischiofemoral ligament is relatively weak and supports the capsule posteriorly 5. Round ligament of head of femur : This ligament is also called ligamentum teres of the head of the femur. - It is a flat triangular ligament with apex attached to the fovea of the head, and its base to the transverse acetabular ligament - It is ensheathed by a conical reflection of the synovial membrane. - It does not increase the stability of the joint. - It transmits arteries to the head of the femur derived from the acetabular branches of the obturator and medial circumflex femoral arteries.(Statement 2) - Morphologically, the ligament of the head of femur represents the part of capsule that has been included within the joint 6. The acetabular labrum is a fibrocartilaginous rim attached to the acetabular margin. - The labrum not only deepens the acetabulum (socket) but grasps the head of femur lightly to hold it in position.(Statement 4) 7. Transverse acetabular ligament : It is a part of acetabular labrum, which bridges the acetabular notch; however, it is devoid of cartilage cells. - The acetabular notch thus becomes converted into the foramen which transmits the acetabular vessels and nerves to the hip joint.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 945-946; Solution Pages 961-963","sourcePage":945,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"anatomy-23-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 35-year-old woman presents with pain and swelling in the prepatellar region of her left knee, which developed gradually over the past few weeks. She works as a housemaid and spends long hours kneeling on hard surfaces while cleaning floors. On physical examination, the knee is swollen, and there is tenderness over the anterior part of the knee. The swelling is soft and fluctuant, and there is no redness or heat over the area. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prepatellar bursitis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Baker’s cyst"},{"letter":"C","text":"Osteoarthritis of the knee"},{"letter":"D","text":"Clergyman’s knee"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Prepatellar bursitis","explanation":"- Prepatalar bursitis also known as Housemaid’s knee is the inflammation of the prepatellar bursa (prepatellar bursitis). - It occurs due to friction of bursa against the patella when it comes in contact with the ground during scrubbing of the floor by the housemaid. - Such a bursa may get very large and drop by its weight too much below to its original position. - Subcutaneous prepatellar bursa (bursa of housemaid’s knee). - It lies deep to the skin in front of the lower half of the patella and upper half of the ligamentum patellae and tibial tuberosity. Baker’s cyst (Option B) : The chronic inflammation of bursa deep to semimembranosus may present as cystic swelling in the medial part of the popliteal fossa called Baker’s cyst Osteoarthritis of the knee (Option C) : This is unlikely given the patient's relatively young age and the acute nature of the symptoms. - Osteoarthritis typically presents with chronic pain, stiffness, and decreased range of motion in older individuals, especially those over the age of 50. - The swelling in osteoarthritis is also typically associated with joint degeneration rather than soft, fluctuant swelling in the prepatellar region. Clergyman’s knee (Option D) : It is the inflammation of subcutaneous infrapatellar bursa (subcutaneous infrapatellar bursitis). - It occurs due to the friction of bursa against the tibial tuberosity due to kneeling (e.g., kneeling during prayer by Christian priests/clergymen, roofers, and floor tilers.","sourceRef":"Question Page 946; Solution Pages 963-964","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"anatomy-23-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which dermatome supplies the middle of the back of the thigh and leg?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S1"},{"letter":"B","text":"S2"},{"letter":"C","text":"L4"},{"letter":"D","text":"L5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. S2","explanation":"Segmental innervation of skin of Lower Limb Segments Area L1, L2, L3 The anterior aspect of the thigh from above downwards L4 (Option C ruled out) The medial aspect of the leg L5 (Option D ruled out) The lateral aspect of the leg and the medial side of the foot S1 (Option A ruled out) The lateral side of the foot and sole S2 (Option B) Middle of the back of the thigh and leg S3,S4 Buttocks Dermatomes of Lower Limb","sourceRef":"Question Page 946; Solution Pages 964-965","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":946},{"id":"anatomy-23-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 50 y/o man undergoes a total hip replacement. Postoperatively, he develops difficulty walking and reports numbness along the posterior thigh, lateral leg, and sole of the foot. O/E, he has weakness in extending the hip and flexing the knee. The injured nerve originates from which of the following spinal roots?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L2–L4"},{"letter":"B","text":"L4–S3"},{"letter":"C","text":"L5–S1"},{"letter":"D","text":"S1–S5"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. L4–S3","explanation":"The patient’s symptoms, including weakness in hip extension, knee flexion, and sensory loss along the posterior thigh and leg, are indicative of sciatic nerve injury. - The sciatic nerve originates from the ventral rami of the L4–S3 spinal roots. Sciatic Nerve Origin and Course The thickest nerve in the body. Arises from the ventral rami of L4–S3 spinal nerves. (Option A, C & D ruled out) Exits the pelvis through the greater sciatic foramen below the piriformis and descends between the greater trochanter and ischial tuberosity along the back of the thigh. Divides into the tibial and common peroneal nerves above the popliteal fossa. Distribution Motor: Undivided Trunk: Supplies all muscles of the back of the thigh (hip extension, knee flexion). Tibial Nerve: Innervates leg muscles (flexion) and foot muscles (plantar flexion). Common Peroneal Nerve: Supplies leg muscles (dorsiflexion). Sensory: Tibial Nerve: Supplies skin on the back of the calf, heel, lateral border of the foot, and sole. Common Peroneal Nerve: Supplies skin on the anterolateral and lateral surfaces of the leg and dorsum of the foot (except for the deep peroneal nerve area). Clinical correlation Injury of the sciatic nerve: Causes: Penetrating wounds, posterior hip dislocation, pelvic fractures, total hip replacement, misplaced injections in the gluteal region. Clinical Features: Inability to extend the thigh and flex the knee (hamstring paralysis). Loss of all movements below the knee (foot drop) leading to flail foot and high-stepping gait. Loss of sensation on the back of the thigh, entire leg, and foot (except for the saphenous nerve area). Sciatica: Shooting pain along the sciatic nerve distribution (buttock, posterior thigh, lateral leg, dorsum of the foot). Compression/irritation of L4–S3 spinal nerve roots due to herniated lumbar discs. Neuropathy: Entrapment in the piriformis muscle can lead to piriformis syndrome. Sciatic Nerve course Branches of Sciatic Nerve","sourceRef":"Question Pages 946-947; Solution Pages 965-967","sourcePage":946,"sourcePageEnd":947},{"id":"anatomy-23-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 55 y/o man presents with difficulty walking and reports pain along the medial aspect of his foot. Physical examination reveals weakness in inversion and signs of flatfoot. Which muscle is primarily responsible for the inversion of the foot in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gastrocnemius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Soleus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tibialis Posterior"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor Digitorum Longus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Tibialis Posterior","explanation":"Tibialis Posterior muscle is the principal inverter of the foot and supports the medial arch, which aligns with the patient's symptoms of flatfoot. Movements of Inversion and Eversion Inversion Eversion Tibialis anterior Tibialis posterior Flexor hallucis longus Peroneus brevis Peroneus longus Peroneus tertius Flexor digitorum longus Posterior compartment of Leg - The muscles in the posterior compartment of the lower leg form superficial and deep groups, separated by the transverse intermuscular septum. - The superficial posterior compartment of the leg is devoid of any neurovascular structures. Superficial group of muscles Muscles Attachments Action Nerve Clinical aspects Gastrocnemius Origin : Medial and Plantar flexion of the Tibial nerve (S1, S2) Testing: Plantar lateral condyles of foot Flexion of knee flexion against the femur Insertion : resistance (knee Calcaneal tendon extended). Soleus Origin : Posterior Primary plantar Tibial nerve (S1, S2) Testing: Plantar head and proximal flexor Stabilizes leg flexion against fibula, soleal line of during standing resistance (hip and tibia, tendinous arch knee flexed). of soleus Insertion : Calcaneal tendon Plantaris Origin : Lower part Assists gastrocnemiu Tibial nerve Minor role in foot of the lateral s in plantar flexion mechanics. supracondylar line (less significant) on the femur, oblique popliteal ligament Insertion : Calcaneus (medial to calcaneal tendon) Superficial group of muscles Dissection image of Superficial Structures Deep group of muscles Muscles Attachments Action Nerve Clinical aspects Popliteus Origin: Lateral Unlocks the knee by Tibial nerve (L4, L5, Injury may lead to femoral condyle laterally rotating the S1,) knee instability Insertion: Posterior femur on the tibia Important in knee surface of tibia biomechanics. above the soleal line Flexor Digitorum Origin: Posterior Plantar flexion of the Tibial nerve (L5, S1, The aberrant Longus surface of the tibia ankle Flexion of the S2) function may (medial to tibialis distal phalanges of contribute to toe posterior) Insertion: toes 2-5 deformities (e.g., Plantar surfaces of hammer toe). distal phalanges of toes 2-5 Flexor Hallucis Origin: Distal Plantar flexion of the Tibial nerve (L5, S1, Overuse in dancers Longus two-thirds of the ankle Flexion of the S2) may lead to posterior fibula, hallux tendinopathy (e.g., interosseus hallux saltans). membrane. Insertion: Important surgical Plantar surface of landmark at the the base of the distal ankle to avoid phalanx of the hallux neurovascular injury. Tibialis Posterior Origin: Interosseous The principal Tibial nerve (L4, L5) Dysfunction can lead membrane, posterior inverter of the foot to a flat foot (pes surface of tibia, Supports medial planus) due to fibula Insertion: longitudinal arch tendon elongation Navicular tuberosity, and attenuation of cuneiforms, bases of the spring ligament. metatarsals 2-4 Deep group of muscles Gastrocnemius (Option A): This muscle is primarily involved in plantarflexion and knee flexion, not inversion. Soleus (Option B): While it plays a significant role in plantarflexion, it does not contribute to foot inversion. Flexor Digitorum Longus (Option D): This muscle assists with toe flexion and plantarflexion but is not the primary muscle responsible for foot inversion","sourceRef":"Question Page 947; Solution Pages 967-970","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":947},{"id":"anatomy-23-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Where does the popliteus muscle originate, and what is its nerve supply?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial condyle of femur; Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral condyle of femur; Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tibial tuberosity; Sciatic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibular head; Peroneal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lateral condyle of femur; Tibial nerve","explanation":"The popliteus muscle originates from the anterior part of the popliteal groove on the lateral condyle of the femur and is supplied by a branch of the tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1). Popliteus Muscle Popliteus Muscle Origin Arises within the knee joint capsule from the anterior part of the popliteal groove located on the lateral condyle of the femur. Insertion The medial aspect of the head of the fibula via the popliteofibular ligament Medial two-thirds of the triangular area above the soleal line on the posterior surface of the tibia. Blood supply Inferior medial and lateral genicular arteries Posterior tibial artery and its branches. Nerve supply The branch of the tibial nerve (L4, L5, S1) . Actions Medially rotates the tibia on the femur; when the tibia is fixed, it laterally rotates the femur. It is the \"unlocking\" muscle of the knee by unlocking the locked knee by rotating the femur laterally during the initial stages of knee flexion. It pulls the lateral meniscus posteriorly, preventing it from becoming trapped and compressed between the femoral and tibial condyles. Options A, C, and D are incorrect because the popliteus muscle originates from the lateral condyle of the femur , not the medial condyle, the tibial tuberosity or the fibular head, respectively, and it is innervated by the tibial nerve , not the femoral nerve, sciatic nerve, or peroneal nerve respectively.","sourceRef":"Question Page 947; Solution Pages 970-972","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":947},{"id":"anatomy-23-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 30-year-old runner comes in with weakness when trying to lift his foot and numbness on the outside of his right leg after hurting his knee. Examination reveals weakness in dorsiflexion and tenderness over the lateral aspect of the popliteal fossa. Which of the following structures is most likely compressed in the popliteal fossa, leading to the patient's symptoms?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Popliteal artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Common peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Popliteal vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Common peroneal nerve","explanation":"The common peroneal nerve runs obliquely through the lateral aspect of the popliteal fossa, and injury or compression can lead to weakness in dorsiflexion (foot drop) and sensory loss over the lateral leg. Contents of the Popliteal Fossa: - Popliteal artery : Deepest structure, crossed posteriorly by the popliteal vein and tibial nerve. - Popliteal vein : Located anterior to the tibial nerve, crosses the artery. - Tibial nerve : Most superficial, runs vertically and crosses the fossa. - Common peroneal nerve : Runs obliquely from the superior to the lateral angle of the fossa. - The posterior cutaneous nerve of the thigh is located in the popliteal fossa. - Genicular branch of the obturator nerve : Follows the popliteal artery to the knee. - Popliteal lymph nodes : 6-7 nodes embedded in fat. - Fat : Surrounds and supports all the structures. - Relations: In the middle part of the fossa, Superficial to deep : Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) In the upper part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) In the lower part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Popliteal artery → Popliteal vein → Tibial Nerve (AVN) - In the middle part of the fossa, Superficial to deep : Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) - In the upper part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) - In the lower part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Popliteal artery → Popliteal vein → Tibial Nerve (AVN) - In the middle part of the fossa, Superficial to deep : Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) - In the upper part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Tibial nerve → Popliteal vein → Popliteal artery (NVA) - In the lower part of the fossa, Lateral to Medial: Popliteal artery → Popliteal vein → Tibial Nerve (AVN) Popliteal artery(Option A): Injury leads to vascular issues like ischemia, not motor or sensory deficits. Tibial nerve (Option B): Damage causes weakness in plantar flexion and sole numbness, not dorsiflexion. Popliteal vein (Option D): Damage causes venous issues like swelling, not neurological deficits.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 947-948; Solution Pages 972-973","sourcePage":947,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"anatomy-23-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following correctly matches a boundary of the popliteal fossa with its associated structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superolaterally - Semimembranosus and semitendinosus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferomedially - Medial head of the gastrocnemius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Roof of the popliteal fossa - Biceps femoris"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferolaterally - Popliteal surface of the femur"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Inferomedially - Medial head of the gastrocnemius","explanation":"The popliteal fossa is indeed bounded by the medial head of gastrocnemius inferomedially. Popliteal Fossa Shape : Diamond-shaped region located behind the knee. Boundaries of the Popliteal Fossa: Boundary Structure Superolaterally Biceps femoris muscle (Options A&C; ruled out) Superomedially Semimembranosus, semitendinosus Inferomedially The medial head of the gastrocnemius muscle. Inferolaterally The lateral head of the gastrocnemius muscle and the plantaris muscle. Additional Features: - Floor : (Option D ruled out) The popliteal surface of the femur Oblique popliteal ligament The posterior surface of the proximal tibia (covered by popliteus muscle and fascia) - The popliteal surface of the femur - Oblique popliteal ligament - The posterior surface of the proximal tibia (covered by popliteus muscle and fascia) - Roof : Deep fascia (continuous with fascia lata and deep fascia of the leg) Pierced by short saphenous vein and medial/lateral sural cutaneous nerves - Deep fascia (continuous with fascia lata and deep fascia of the leg) - Pierced by short saphenous vein and medial/lateral sural cutaneous nerves - The popliteal surface of the femur - Oblique popliteal ligament - The posterior surface of the proximal tibia (covered by popliteus muscle and fascia) - Deep fascia (continuous with fascia lata and deep fascia of the leg) - Pierced by short saphenous vein and medial/lateral sural cutaneous nerves","sourceRef":"Question Page 948; Solution Pages 973-975","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"anatomy-23-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the boundaries of the adductor canal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anteriorly bounded by the sartorius muscle, laterally by the rectus femoris."},{"letter":"B","text":"Posteromedially bounded by adductor longus and adductor magnus."},{"letter":"C","text":"Roofed by the iliotibial band and bounded posteriorly by the adductor brevis."},{"letter":"D","text":"Medially bounded by the gluteus maximus and anteriorly by the vastus lateralis."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posteromedially bounded by adductor longus and adductor magnus.","explanation":"The canal is anterolaterally bounded by the vastus medialis and posteromedially by the adductor longus and adductor magnus. Adductor Canal (Subsartorial canal or Hunter's canal): - Location : Distal two-thirds of the medial thigh - Extent : Begins at apex of the femoral triangle; ends at adductor hiatus in the tendon of adductor magnus. Boundaries of the Adductor Canal: Boundary Structure Anterolaterally Vastus medialis muscle Posteromedially Adductor longus and adductor magnus muscles Anteromedially (Roof) Subsartorial fascia (dense fascia between vastus medialis and adductor muscles) Contents of the Adductor Canal: - Femoral artery and vein - Descending genicular artery (a branch of the femoral artery). - Muscular branches of femoral artery and veins - Saphenous nerve - Nerve to vastus medialis - Length Anteriorly bounded by the sartorius muscle, laterally by the rectus femoris (Option A) : The anterior boundary is formed by the sub-sartorial fascia, not directly by the sartorius muscle. Roofed by the iliotibial band and bounded posteriorly by the adductor brevis (Option C): The roof is formed by the subsartorial fascia, not the iliotibial band, and the adductor brevis is not a boundary of the canal. Medially bounded by the gluteus maximus and anteriorly by the vastus lateralis (Option D) : The gluteus maximus does not form a boundary of the adductor canal, and the anterior boundary is not the vastus lateralis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 948; Solution Pages 975-976","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":948},{"id":"anatomy-23-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following muscles forms the lateral part of the floor of the femoral triangle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectineus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Iliacus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adductor longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sartorius"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Iliacus","explanation":"The iliacus, along with the psoas major, forms the lateral part of the floor of the femoral triangle. Femoral Triangle: - Location : Anteromedial aspect of the proximal thigh - Base : Inguinal ligament Borders: - Lateral : Medial border of sartorius muscle (Option D ruled out) - Medial : Medial border of adductor longus muscle - Apex : Intersection of sartorius and adductor longus Floor Muscles: - Lateral : Iliacus, psoas major - Medial : Pectineus, adductor longus (Options A&C; ruled out) Contents: - Femoral artery & vein : Artery palpable at the entry Vein lies medial to artery (proximal), and becomes posterior distally - Artery palpable at the entry - Vein lies medial to artery (proximal), and becomes posterior distally - Femoral nerve : Lateral to artery, outside the sheath, divides into branches - Lateral femoral cutaneous nerve : Enters triangle 2.1-3.5 cm below the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) - Lymph nodes & fat . - Artery palpable at the entry - Vein lies medial to artery (proximal), and becomes posterior distally Muscle Details: - Sartorius : Origin : ASIS Insertion : Tendinous insertion to the medial side of the tibia. Blood supply : Femoral artery Nerve supply : Femoral nerve (L2 and L3 spinal nerve roots). - Origin : ASIS - Insertion : Tendinous insertion to the medial side of the tibia. - Blood supply : Femoral artery - Nerve supply : Femoral nerve (L2 and L3 spinal nerve roots). - Adductor longus : Origin : Near pubic symphysis Insertion: tendinous insertion to the femur Blood supply : Profuncsa femoris artery Neve supply : Obturator nerve (spinal roots L2, L3, and L4) - Origin : Near pubic symphysis - Insertion: tendinous insertion to the femur - Blood supply : Profuncsa femoris artery - Neve supply : Obturator nerve (spinal roots L2, L3, and L4) - Origin : ASIS - Insertion : Tendinous insertion to the medial side of the tibia. - Blood supply : Femoral artery - Nerve supply : Femoral nerve (L2 and L3 spinal nerve roots). - Origin : Near pubic symphysis - Insertion: tendinous insertion to the femur - Blood supply : Profuncsa femoris artery - Neve supply : Obturator nerve (spinal roots L2, L3, and L4) Femoral Sheath - Funnel-shaped extension of extraperitoneal fascia, formed from transversalis fascia (anteriorly) and iliac fascia (posteriorly). - Divided into three compartments by two septa: Lateral compartment: Contains the femoral artery and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve. Intermediate compartment: Contains the femoral vein. Medial compartment (Femoral canal) : Contains lymph vessels and Cloquet's node. - Lateral compartment: Contains the femoral artery and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve. - Intermediate compartment: Contains the femoral vein. - Medial compartment (Femoral canal) : Contains lymph vessels and Cloquet's node. - Lateral compartment: Contains the femoral artery and femoral branch of the genitofemoral nerve. - Intermediate compartment: Contains the femoral vein. - Medial compartment (Femoral canal) : Contains lymph vessels and Cloquet's node. Femoral Canal: - The medial compartment of the femoral sheath forms a short fascial tube that narrows downward. - Allows expansion of the femoral vein during increased venous return. - Closed inferiorly by wall fusion, and its upper end is called the femoral ring, opening into the abdominal cavity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 948-949; Solution Pages 976-978","sourcePage":948,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"anatomy-23-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 28 y/o male presents with knee pain and instability after a football injury. The Lachmann Test is performed to assess for an ACL tear. At what degree of knee flexion is the Lachmann Test typically performed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"0 degrees"},{"letter":"B","text":"15-20 degrees"},{"letter":"C","text":"60-70 degrees"},{"letter":"D","text":"80-90 degrees"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 15-20 degrees","explanation":"The Lachmann Test is typically performed with the knee flexed at 15-20 degrees to assess for ACL injuries. Anterior Cruciate Ligament (ACL) Injury Assessment Tests: Anterior Drawer Test Lachmann Test This is a test to detect injury to the anterior cruciate This is another test to detect injury to the anterior cruciate ligament. ligament. Procedure : The patient is positioned supine, with the Procedure: This test is considered better than the anterior knee being tested flexed to 80–90°, and passive forward drawer test. In this test, the knee is maintained in 15° to traction is then applied to the tibia. (Option D ruled out) 20° of flexion. One hand supports the thigh just above the knee, while the other holds the upper tibia. The degree of anterior glide assesses the integrity of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). 0 degrees (Option A): The knee is too extended, making it difficult to accurately assess ACL integrity due to limited anterior tibial translation. 60-70 degrees (Option C): This degree of flexion is typically used for posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) testing, not ACL evaluation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 949; Solution Pages 978-980","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"anatomy-23-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 25 y/o male presents to the emergency after a sports injury involving a sudden pivot on his left knee. An MRI reveals a tear in the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). Which statement regarding the knee ligaments is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"ACL is an extracapsular ligament."},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral collateral ligament attaches to the patella."},{"letter":"C","text":"The medial meniscus is wider anteriorly."},{"letter":"D","text":"PCL prevents anterior displacement of the femur."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. PCL prevents anterior displacement of the femur.","explanation":"The PCL prevents anterior displacement of the femur and posterior displacement of the tibia when the knee is flexed, making it essential for knee stability. Extracapsular Ligaments Intracapsular Ligaments Ligamentum Patellae: Attaches from the lower border of Medial Meniscus Semicircular, wider posteriorly than the patella to the tibial tuberosity. Continuation of the anteriorly. (Option C ruled out) The peripheral margin quadriceps femoris tendon. Lateral (Fibular) Collateral adheres to the deep part of the tibial collateral ligament; Ligament: (Option B ruled out) Cord-like structure posterior fibres are continuous with the transverse attaching from the femur's lateral condyle to the fibula's ligament. Lateral Meniscus Circular. The posterior end is head. The Popliteus tendon is located between this attached to the medial condyle of the femur via two ligament and the lateral meniscus. Medial (Tibial) meniscofemoral ligaments; separated from the fibular Collateral Ligament: Flat band connecting the medial collateral ligament by the popliteus tendon. Anterior condyle of the femur to the medial surface of the tibia. Cruciate Ligament (ACL): (Option A ruled out) It attaches Firmly attached to the medial meniscus. Oblique Popliteal to the anterior intercondylar area of the tibia and ascends Ligament: Tendinous expansion from the to the posterior part of the lateral femoral condyle. semimembranosus muscle, strengthening the posterior Prevents posterior displacement of the femur and anterior capsule. displacement of the tibia when the knee is flexed. It is extrasynovial. Posterior Cruciate Ligament (PCL): It attaches to the posterior intercondylar area of the tibia and extends to the anterior part of the medial femoral condyle. Prevents anterior displacement of the femur and posterior displacement of the tibia when the knee is flexed. It is extrasynovial. Intercondylar area attachments Classification of Ligaments and Menisci by Location and Synovial Relation - Intracapsular and Intrasynovial: Menisci (lie within the joint capsule and are bathed by synovial fluid). - Intracapsular but Extrasynovial: Cruciate ligaments (located within the joint capsule but outside the synovial cavity). - Extracapsular and Extrasynovial: Collateral ligaments (outside both the joint capsule and synovial cavity).","sourceRef":"Question Page 949; Solution Pages 980-981","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"anatomy-23-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-23","chapter":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topic":"Lesson 23 - Lower Limb Anatomy Knee and Leg","topicIndex":23,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which muscles are involved in the locking and unlocking of the knee joint?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hamstring muscles for both locking and unlocking"},{"letter":"B","text":"Quadriceps femoris for locking and popliteus for unlocking"},{"letter":"C","text":"Popliteus for both locking and unlocking"},{"letter":"D","text":"Soleus for unlocking and hamstring for locking"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Quadriceps femoris for locking and popliteus for unlocking","explanation":"- The quadriceps femoris muscle group extends the knee and helps in the locking mechanism when the knee is fully extended. - The popliteus muscle is responsible for unlocking the knee by initiating flexion, particularly from a fully extended position. Aspect Locking of the Knee Joint Unlocking of the Knee Joint Movement When the foot is on the ground - the When the foot is on the ground medial rotation of the femur on the lateral rotation of the femur on the tibia is locking. tibia is unlocking. Phase of Motion Terminal phase of extension. Initial phase of flexion. Muscle Involved (Option A, C & D) Quadriceps femoris Popliteus muscle Knee Rigidity The locked knee becomes rigid. An unlocked knee can be further flexed. Ligament Tension All ligaments are taut. All ligaments are relaxed. Function Essential for bearing load during Facilitates the progress of locomotion. erect posture. Locking and Unlocking Mechanism of the Knee (Non-Weight-Bearing) - Locking (Screw-Home Mechanism): During knee extension, the tibia medially rotates relative to the femur, tightening ligaments (e.g., ACL) and stabilising the joint. - Unlocking: The popliteus muscle initiates knee flexion by laterally rotating the tibia, loosening the ligaments and allowing movement. This mechanism ensures stability during transitions and controlled movement when the foot is not planted.","sourceRef":"Question Page 949; Solution Pages 981-982","sourcePage":949,"sourcePageEnd":949},{"id":"anatomy-24-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following is incorrect about the highlighted area?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It consists of a superficial part and a deep part."},{"letter":"B","text":"Prone to injuries in eversion."},{"letter":"C","text":"Excessive tensile forces cause avulsion fracture."},{"letter":"D","text":"It's crossed by tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Prone to injuries in eversion.","explanation":"- The ligament shown in the image is known as the deltoid ligament. - It is prone to injuries in inversion, not eversion. Deltoid Ligament (Medial Ligament) - Location: Medial side of the ankle ; triangular shape. - Structure: Divided into superficial and deep parts, both attached to the medial malleolus. (Option A) - Attachments: Tuberosity of navicular bone; medial margin of spring ligament. - Attachments: Entire length of sustentaculum tali. - Attachments: Medial tubercle of posterior process of talus. - Anterior Tibiotalar Fibres: Attachments: Anterior part of the medial surface of the talus. - Attachments: Anterior part of the medial surface of the talus. - Characteristics: Crossed by tendons of tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus (Option D) . Very strong; typically results in avulsion fracture rather than tear under excessive tensile forces (Option C) . Prone to injuries during inversion. - Crossed by tendons of tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus (Option D) . - Very strong; typically results in avulsion fracture rather than tear under excessive tensile forces (Option C) . - Prone to injuries during inversion. - Attachments: Anterior part of the medial surface of the talus. - Crossed by tendons of tibialis posterior and flexor digitorum longus (Option D) . - Very strong; typically results in avulsion fracture rather than tear under excessive tensile forces (Option C) . - Prone to injuries during inversion. Lateral Ligament - Structure: Composed of three bands. - A flat band from anterior margin of lateral malleolus to neck of talus (in front of fibular facet). - Connects lower part of malleolar fossa of fibula to lateral tubercle of talus. - Long rounded cord from notch on lower border of lateral malleolus to tubercle on lateral surface of calcaneus. - Characteristics: Crossed by tendons of peroneus longus and brevis. - Crossed by tendons of peroneus longus and brevis. - Crossed by tendons of peroneus longus and brevis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 983; Solution Pages 989-991","sourcePage":983,"sourcePageEnd":983},{"id":"anatomy-24-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 42-year-old woman complains of difficulty adducting her toes. Which of the following muscles is primarily responsible for adducting the lateral three toes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Adductor halucis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Dorsal interossei"},{"letter":"C","text":"Plantar interossei"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adductor digitorum brevis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Plantar interossei","explanation":"The plantar interossei (fourth layer) are responsible for adducting the 3rd–5th toes (PAD – Plantar Adduct) . Third and fourth layers of the sole: Adductor hallucis (Option A) adducts the great toe at the metatarsophalangeal joint. The dorsal interossei (Option B) (fourth layer) performs abduction of the toes (DAB – Dorsal Abduct), not adduction. Adductor digitorum brevis (Option D) does not exist.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 983-984; Solution Pages 991-994","sourcePage":983,"sourcePageEnd":984},{"id":"anatomy-24-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 30-year-old athlete complains of difficulty in flexion at the metatarsophalangeal joint and extension at the interphalangeal joint of the second toe. Which of the following statements is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First lumbrical is affected and is supplied by the lateral plantar nerve."},{"letter":"B","text":"First lumbrical is affected and is supplied by the medial plantar nerve."},{"letter":"C","text":"Second lumbrical is affected and is supplied by the lateral plantar nerve."},{"letter":"D","text":"Second lumbrical is affected and is supplied by the medial plantar nerve."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. First lumbrical is affected and is supplied by the medial plantar nerve.","explanation":"The first lumbrical, which acts on the second toe, is supplied by the medial plantar nerve . - It facilitates flexion at the metatarsophalangeal joint and extension at the interphalangeal joints. - The lumbricals of the foot show a pattern of dual innervation: - The medial plantar nerve innervates the first lumbrical. - The second, third, and fourth lumbricals are innervated by the lateral plantar nerve. - This distribution mirrors the innervation seen in the hand , where a division between median and ulnar nerve innervation exists.","sourceRef":"Question Page 984; Solution Pages 994-995","sourcePage":984,"sourcePageEnd":984},{"id":"anatomy-24-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with complaints of claudication and cramping pain in his left lower limb while walking short distances, which is relieved by rest. On further examination, his left dorsalis pedis pulse is diminished, and he has cool skin on the affected leg. Doppler ultrasound reveals significant atherosclerosis at the bifurcation of the common iliac artery. Which of the following arteries, if most affected by this condition, is likely responsible for the reduced blood flow to his lower limb?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal iliac artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior epigastric artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"External iliac artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal pudendal artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. External iliac artery","explanation":"External iliac artery is the primary artery that continues as the femoral artery to supply the lower limb. - Atherosclerosis at the bifurcation of the common iliac artery directly affects the external iliac artery, reducing blood flow to the entire lower limb, causing claudication. - The internal iliac artery primarily supplies the pelvis and perineum. - The internal pudendal artery a branch of the internal iliac artery, supplies the genitalia and perineum, not the lower limb. - The inferior epigastric artery, a branch of the external iliac artery, supplies the anterior abdominal wall, not the lower limb. Reference:","sourceRef":"Question Page 984; Solution Pages 995-996","sourcePage":984,"sourcePageEnd":984},{"id":"anatomy-24-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":5,"question":"During surgery for a femoral shaft fracture, the surgeon notes damage to the vastus lateralis muscle. Which artery is primarily responsible for supplying the vastus lateralis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ascending branch of femoral circumflex artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending branch of medial femoral circumflex artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Descending branch of lateral femoral circumflex artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transverse branch if medial femoral circumflex artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Descending branch of lateral femoral circumflex artery","explanation":"The descending branch of the lateral femoral circumflex artery supplies the vastus lateralis muscle as it descends along its anterior border. Medial and Lateral Femoral Circumflex Arteries - Branches of the profunda femoris artery (from the femoral artery) - Main blood supply to adductor, extensor, and flexor thigh muscles - Anastomose with internal/external iliac arteries (superior) and popliteal artery (inferior) Lateral Femoral Circumflex Artery - Branches near the root of profunda femoris, run laterally - Divides into ascending, descending, and transverse branches Branches: Branch Course Function/Anastomosis Ascending (Option A) Ascends along intertrochanteric line, Supplies greater trochanter, forms under tensor fasciae latae anastomotic ring around femoral neck (with medial femoral circumflex artery) Descending (Option C) Descends posterior to rectus femoris, Supplies vastus lateralis, along anterior vastus lateralis anastomoses with superior lateral genicular artery (at knee) Transverse Passes laterally, anterior to vastus Forms cruciate anastomosis (with intermedius, pierces vastus lateralis medial femoral circumflex, inferior near greater trochanter gluteal, and first perforating arteries) Medial Femoral Circumflex Artery - Typically branches from profunda femoris, sometimes from the femoral artery - Supplies adductor muscles, runs medially around femur Branch Course Function/Anastomosis Transverse (Option D) Runs near quadratus femoris Contributes to cruciate anastomosis Ascending (Option B) Ascends on obturator externus Anastomoses with gluteal and lateral tendon, reaches trochanteric fossa femoral circumflex arteries, supplies hip joint via the acetabular branch. Anastomoses: Anastomosis Location Involved Arteries Trochanteric anastomosis Trochanteric fossa Ascending branch of medial femoral circumflex, superior/inferior gluteal arteries, lateral femoral circumflex, first perforating artery Cruciate anastomosis Lesser trochanter (inferior) Transverse branches of medial and lateral femoral circumflex, inferior gluteal artery, first perforating artery","sourceRef":"Question Pages 984-985; Solution Pages 996-998","sourcePage":984,"sourcePageEnd":985},{"id":"anatomy-24-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A patient is experiencing discomfort in the lower limb due to venous insufficiency. Which of the following mechanisms aid in the venous return of blood from the lower limb to the heart?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deep fascia limits muscle expansion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Contraction of calf muscles leads to compression of superficial veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Increased intrathoracic pressure"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vasodilation of superficial veins"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Deep fascia limits muscle expansion","explanation":"The deep fascia enhances the muscle pump by limiting outward muscle expansion, helping direct blood flow upwards through deep veins. Venous Drainage of the Lower Limb - The venous system of the lower limb returns deoxygenated blood to the heart. - Key aspects include vein groups, connections, and clinical significance. Groups of Veins - Superficial Veins: Located in subcutaneous tissue, above the deep fascia. - Deep Veins: Positioned deep to the fascia, accompany major arteries, and are more numerous than superficial veins. Important Superficial Veins Vein Type Description Great Saphenous Longest vein in the body; originates on the medial foot, ascends along the medial leg and thigh to the femoral vein; significant for venous return and surgical grafts. Small Saphenous Originates on the lateral foot, ascends posteriorly along the leg, drains into the popliteal vein in the fossa. Perforating Veins - Connect superficial and deep venous systems. - Contain valves for unidirectional blood flow, preventing backflow. Factors Aiding Venous Return - Valves: Ensure unidirectional flow towards the heart. - Muscle Pump: Calf muscle contraction compresses deep veins , aiding blood propulsion. (Option B) - Deep Fascia: Limits muscle expansion, enhancing pressure on deep veins during contraction. (Option A) - Negative Intrathoracic Pressure: Assists in drawing blood upwards during inspiration. (Option C) Clinical Significance: Varicose Veins - Definition: Enlarged, twisted veins caused by incompetent valves allowing backward blood flow, leading to dilation. - Contributing Factors: Malfunctioning valves, causing blood to backflow, pool, and lead to dilation. Incompetent perforating veins allow high-pressure deep blood to flow into the superficial system, exacerbating varicosity. - Malfunctioning valves, causing blood to backflow, pool, and lead to dilation. - Incompetent perforating veins allow high-pressure deep blood to flow into the superficial system, exacerbating varicosity. - Malfunctioning valves, causing blood to backflow, pool, and lead to dilation. - Incompetent perforating veins allow high-pressure deep blood to flow into the superficial system, exacerbating varicosity. Vasodilation of superficial veins (Option D) : Vasodilation would increase venous pressure and contribute to venous insufficiency, not aid in venous return.","sourceRef":"Question Page 985; Solution Pages 998-1000","sourcePage":985,"sourcePageEnd":985},{"id":"anatomy-24-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 30-year-old female presents to the emergency department with a swollen right leg and pain following a long-haul flight. Doppler ultrasound confirms a deep vein thrombosis (DVT) in the right calf. Which statement about the anatomy of the deep veins of the lower limb is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The anterior tibial vein runs alongside the fibular artery."},{"letter":"B","text":"The medial and lateral plantar veins contribute to the formation of the popliteal vein."},{"letter":"C","text":"Perforating veins carry blood from deep veins to superficial veins."},{"letter":"D","text":"The fibular veins drain directly into the femoral vein."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The medial and lateral plantar veins contribute to the formation of the popliteal vein.","explanation":"popliteal vein. The medial and lateral plantar veins unite to form the posterior tibial veins, which then contribute to the formation of the popliteal vein. Deep Veins of the Lower Limb - Deep veins are located beneath the deep fascia of the lower limb, running alongside major arteries and are more numerous than superficial veins. - They generally share names with their accompanying arteries and follow similar paths. Characteristics: Feature Description Connections Receive blood from superficial veins via perforating veins equipped with valves for unidirectional flow. Venous Return Role Critical for returning blood to the heart; compression during muscle contraction (especially calf muscles) propels blood upward (muscle pump). Clinical Significance Important in conditions like Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) and potential complications such as pulmonary embolism. Specific Deep Veins: Vein Description Plantar Digital Veins Originate in toes, contributing to plantar metatarsal veins. Plantar Metatarsal Veins Run in intermetatarsal spaces, connecting with dorsal veins via perforating veins. Deep Plantar Venous Arch Formed by plantar metatarsal veins; gives rise to medial and lateral plantar veins. Medial & Lateral Plantar Veins Course alongside corresponding arteries; contribute to posterior tibial veins. They contribute to the formation of the popliteal vein. (Option B) Posterior Tibial Veins Formed behind the medial malleolus by the union of medial and lateral plantar veins. Anterior Tibial Veins Accompany the anterior tibial artery. Fibular Veins Run alongside the fibular artery. Popliteal Vein Formed by the union of anterior and posterior tibial veins and the fibular vein; ascends to become femoral vein. Femoral Vein Main deep vein of the thigh; receives blood from popliteal vein, profunda femoris vein, and long saphenous vein. Profunda Femoris Vein Drains deep structures of the thigh; merges with the femoral vein. Conditions: - Deep Vein Thrombosis (DVT) : Formation of a thrombus in deep veins, often in the calf due to sluggish blood flow. - Pulmonary Embolism : Thrombus fragments can dislodge, travel to the lungs, and cause a life-threatening blockage. The anterior tibial vein runs alongside the fibular artery (Option A): The anterior tibial vein runs alongside the anterior tibial artery. Perforating veins carry blood from deep veins to superficial veins (Option C): Perforating veins allow blood to flow from superficial veins to deep veins. The fibular veins drain directly into the femoral vein. (Option D) : The fibular veins drain into the popliteal vein, not directly into the femoral vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 985; Solution Pages 1000-1002","sourcePage":985,"sourcePageEnd":985},{"id":"anatomy-24-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 35-year-old female presents with symptoms of heaviness and visible varicose veins primarily affecting her left leg. Duplex ultrasonography reveals dilation of the great saphenous vein (GSV) with incompetent valves. She is scheduled for vein stripping surgery. Which of the following statements regarding the anatomical features of the GSV compared to the small saphenous vein (SSV) is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The GSV has fewer valves than the SSV, making it less prone to varicosities"},{"letter":"B","text":"The GSV originates from the lateral end of the dorsal venous arch of the foot"},{"letter":"C","text":"The GSV is a continuation of the medial marginal vein."},{"letter":"D","text":"The GSV pierces the deep fascia to drain into the popliteal vein."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The GSV is a continuation of the medial marginal vein.","explanation":"The GSV arises from the medial marginal vein on the dorsum of the foot. Superficial Veins of the Lower Limb - Superficial veins are located in the subcutaneous tissue, above the deep fascia - They drain blood from superficial lower limb tissues - Important superficial veins: Great Saphenous Vein (GSV) and Small Saphenous Vein (SSV) Great Saphenous Vein (GSV) Feature Description Origin Arises on the dorsum of the foot, as a continuation of the medial marginal vein (Option C) Course Ascends in front of the medial malleolus Runs along the medial leg Courses behind the knee Inclines forward in the thigh Passes through the saphenous opening into the femoral vein Tributaries Dorsal venous arch of the foot Medial marginal vein Posterior arch vein Calf vein Accessory saphenous vein Anterior cutaneous vein of the thigh Superficial epigastric, circumflex iliac, and external pudendal veins Valves Contains 10-20 valves, more concentrated in the leg (Option A) Clinical Significance Commonly used in surgical grafting, especially for coronary artery bypass Small Saphenous Vein (SSV) Feature Description Origin Forms on the dorsum of the foot from the lateral dorsal venous arch (Option B) Course Ascends behind the lateral malleolus Runs along the calf Drains into the popliteal vein after piercing the deep fascia (Option D) Tributaries Receives cutaneous tributaries from the lateral foot, heel, and back of the leg Connections Communicates with deep veins and connects with GSV through branches Valves Contains 7-13 valves, with one near its termination Termination Variability May join GSV, bifurcate, or drain into other veins Clinical Relevance - Varicose Veins : Dilated, tortuous veins due to valve incompetence, commonly in GSV and tributaries Venous Return: Superficial veins aid blood return to the heart via muscle pump mechanism, facilitated by valves in perforating veins","sourceRef":"Question Pages 985-986; Solution Pages 1002-1004","sourcePage":985,"sourcePageEnd":986},{"id":"anatomy-24-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 45-year-old female presents with burning pain and tingling in the plantar aspect of her foot. She reports that the symptoms worsen with prolonged standing and improve with rest. You tap over the region just behind the medial malleolus, and the patient experiences tingling and numbness radiating into the sole of her foot. The underlying cause of her symptoms is likely related to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inadequate Blood Supply"},{"letter":"B","text":"Compressive Neuropathy"},{"letter":"C","text":"Morton’s neuroma"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arthritis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Compressive Neuropathy","explanation":"- The presentation is consistent with tarsal tunnel syndrome - The underlying cause is compressive neuropathy , compression of the tibial nerve as it passes through the tarsal tunnel. Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome Definition: Compressive neuropathy of the tibial nerve at the tarsal tunnel. - Symptoms Pain with prolonged standing or walking. Vague, misleading medial foot pain. Sharp, burning pain in the foot. Numbness and intermittent paresthesias in the plantar foot. - Pain with prolonged standing or walking. - Vague, misleading medial foot pain. - Sharp, burning pain in the foot. - Numbness and intermittent paresthesias in the plantar foot. - P/E Tinel’s test of the ankle is performed by tapping the tibial nerve directly over the flexor retinaculum posterior to the medial malleolus. The test is considered positive (Tinel’s sign) if the patient reports paresthesia or numbness in the distribution of the tibial nerve or its branches. (Option A, C, and D) Pain with ankle dorsiflexion and eversion. Compression test: pain with plantar flexion and inversion, digital pressure over the tarsal tunnel (highly sensitive and specific). - Tinel’s test of the ankle is performed by tapping the tibial nerve directly over the flexor retinaculum posterior to the medial malleolus. - The test is considered positive (Tinel’s sign) if the patient reports paresthesia or numbness in the distribution of the tibial nerve or its branches. (Option A, C, and D) - Pain with ankle dorsiflexion and eversion. - Compression test: pain with plantar flexion and inversion, digital pressure over the tarsal tunnel (highly sensitive and specific). - Pain with prolonged standing or walking. - Vague, misleading medial foot pain. - Sharp, burning pain in the foot. - Numbness and intermittent paresthesias in the plantar foot. - Tinel’s test of the ankle is performed by tapping the tibial nerve directly over the flexor retinaculum posterior to the medial malleolus. - The test is considered positive (Tinel’s sign) if the patient reports paresthesia or numbness in the distribution of the tibial nerve or its branches. (Option A, C, and D) - Pain with ankle dorsiflexion and eversion. - Compression test: pain with plantar flexion and inversion, digital pressure over the tarsal tunnel (highly sensitive and specific). Investigation: - Radiographs Weight-bearing radiographs to assess osseous structures. - Weight-bearing radiographs to assess osseous structures. - MRI Helpful to rule out accessory muscle or soft-tissue tumours. - Helpful to rule out accessory muscle or soft-tissue tumours. Radiographs - Weight-bearing radiographs to assess osseous structures. MRI - Helpful to rule out accessory muscle or soft-tissue tumours. Treatment - Nonoperative Lifestyle Modifications: Changes in activity to reduce symptoms. Medications: Anti-inflammatory medications. SSRIs have been used in some cases. Bracing/Orthosis: F ootwear changes to address hindfoot alignment. Casting: Short-leg cast is an option. - Lifestyle Modifications: Changes in activity to reduce symptoms. - Medications: Anti-inflammatory medications. SSRIs have been used in some cases. - Anti-inflammatory medications. - SSRIs have been used in some cases. - Bracing/Orthosis: F ootwear changes to address hindfoot alignment. - Casting: Short-leg cast is an option. - Operative Indications for Surgery: Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). Positive EMG findings. Reproducible physical findings. - Indications for Surgery: Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). Positive EMG findings. Reproducible physical findings. - Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. - Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). - Positive EMG findings. - Reproducible physical findings. - Lifestyle Modifications: Changes in activity to reduce symptoms. - Medications: Anti-inflammatory medications. SSRIs have been used in some cases. - Anti-inflammatory medications. - SSRIs have been used in some cases. - Bracing/Orthosis: F ootwear changes to address hindfoot alignment. - Casting: Short-leg cast is an option. - Anti-inflammatory medications. - SSRIs have been used in some cases. - Indications for Surgery: Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). Positive EMG findings. Reproducible physical findings. - Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. - Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). - Positive EMG findings. - Reproducible physical findings. - Failed conservative management for 3-6 months. - Identified compressive mass (e.g., ganglion cyst). - Positive EMG findings. - Reproducible physical findings.","sourceRef":"Question Page 986; Solution Pages 1004-1007","sourcePage":986,"sourcePageEnd":986},{"id":"anatomy-24-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with difficulty wearing shoes and discomfort in her toes. She reports pain in the forefoot and has noticed that the tips of her toes frequently press into the ground while walking. On physical examination, the toes are bent at the middle and distal joints, with hyperextension at the base. The deformity worsens when wearing tight shoes. What is the most likely diagnosis based on the description of the deformity?.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Claw toe"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hammer toe"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mallet toe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bunion toe"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Claw toe","explanation":"- The given image is of a claw toe, a lesser toe deformity - Characterised by hyperextension of the MTP joint, along with flexion at the proximal interphalangeal (PIP) and distal interphalangeal (DIP) joints. Claw Toe - Definition: Lesser toe deformity with MTP hyperextension and PIP/DIP flexion. - Diagnosis: Clinical evaluation showing: MTP hyperextension PIP flexion DIP flexion - Clinical evaluation showing: MTP hyperextension PIP flexion DIP flexion - MTP hyperextension - PIP flexion - DIP flexion - Treatment: Nonoperative Management: Shoe modifications Taping Surgical Management: Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Nonoperative Management: Shoe modifications Taping - Shoe modifications - Taping - Surgical Management: Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Progressive deformity - Fixed contractures - Dorsal toe ulcerations - Clinical evaluation showing: MTP hyperextension PIP flexion DIP flexion - MTP hyperextension - PIP flexion - DIP flexion - MTP hyperextension - PIP flexion - DIP flexion - Nonoperative Management: Shoe modifications Taping - Shoe modifications - Taping - Surgical Management: Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Progressive deformity - Fixed contractures - Dorsal toe ulcerations - Shoe modifications - Taping - Indicated for: Progressive deformity Fixed contractures Dorsal toe ulcerations - Progressive deformity - Fixed contractures - Dorsal toe ulcerations - Progressive deformity - Fixed contractures - Dorsal toe ulcerations Other toe deformities: (Option B, C and D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 986-987; Solution Pages 1007-1009","sourcePage":986,"sourcePageEnd":987},{"id":"anatomy-24-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 32-year-old man presents to the emergency department after a fall from a height. He complains of severe pain in the heel and inability to bear weight on his foot. On physical examination, there is swelling and tenderness at the heel. Which bone is most likely affected?.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcaneus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Talus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Navicular"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cuboid"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Calcaneus","explanation":"- The calcaneus is the largest tarsal bone located in the heel and is the most commonly fractured tarsal bone following a fall from height. Bone Group Bones Description & Function Tarsals Provide mechanical support and structure to the foot. Proximal Group Talus Transmits body weight to the foot. Articulates at three joints: Superiorly: Ankle joint (tibia/fibula) Inferiorly: Subtalar joint (calcaneus) Anteriorly: Talonavicular joint (navicular) Calcaneus Largest tarsal bone, forms the heel. Articulates at two joints: Superiorly: Subtalar joint (talus) Anteriorly: Calcaneocuboid joint (cuboid) Intermediate Group Navicular Boat-shaped; articulates with talus, cuneiforms, and cuboid. Has a tuberosity for tibialis posterior tendon attachment. Distal Group Cuboid Lateral; articulates with calcaneus and fourth/fifth metatarsals. Features a groove for the fibularis longus tendon. Cuneiforms (3) Medial, intermediate, lateral; wedge-shaped. Help form the transverse arch; attach to various muscles. Metatarsals I-V (1-5) Connect tarsals to phalanges; numbered medial to lateral. Have a head, neck, shaft, and base; facilitate movement and support. Phalanges Great Toe: 2 (proximal, distal) Toes 2-5: 3 each (proximal, intermediate, distal). Each phalanx has a base, shaft, and head, providing structure to toes.","sourceRef":"Question Page 987; Solution Pages 1009-1013","sourcePage":987,"sourcePageEnd":987},{"id":"anatomy-24-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 35-year-old male marathon runner presents with complaints of foot pain after long-distance runs. On examination, the foot shows a well-formed arch, and palpation reveals tenderness along the medial aspect of the foot. An X-ray confirms a normal medial longitudinal arch structure. Which of the following statements incorrectly describes the function or anatomy of the medial longitudinal arch in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Provides better shock absorption."},{"letter":"B","text":"The bony keystone is Talus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Has limited mobility"},{"letter":"D","text":"Main joint is Talocalcaneonavicular joint"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Has limited mobility","explanation":"- The given image is of the medial arch - The medial arch is more mobile and resilient, allowing it to function effectively as a shock absorber. Feature Medial Longitudinal Arch Lateral Longitudinal Arch Height Higher Lower Mobility More mobile, resilient Limited mobility, more rigid Shock Absorption Functions as a shock Transmits weight absorber(Option A rule out) Anterior End Heads of 1st, 2nd, 3rd metatarsals Heads of 4th, 5th metatarsals Posterior End Medial tubercle of calcaneum Lateral tubercle of calcaneum Summit Superior articular surface of the talus Articular facet on the superior surface of calcaneum (at the subtalar joint) Anterior Pillar Talus, navicular, 3 cuneiforms, 1–3 Cuboid and 4th, 5th metatarsals metatarsals Posterior Pillar Medial half of calcaneum Lateral half of calcaneum Main Joint Talocalcaneonavicular joint(Option D Calcaneocuboid joint rule out) Bony Key Stone Head of talus(Option B rule out) Cuboid Intersegmental Ties Spring ligament (Plantar calcaneo Long plantar ligament, Short plantar navicular ligament) ligament Tie Beams Medial part of plantar aponeurosis, Lateral part of plantar aponeurosis, Abductor hallucis, Medial part of Abductor digiti minimi, Lateral part of flexor digitorum brevis flexor digitorum brevis Slings Tibialis posterior, Flexor hallucis Peroneus longus, Peroneus brevis, longus Flexor digitorum longus Suspension Sling Tibialis anterior and peroneus longus Tibialis anterior and peroneus longus","sourceRef":"Question Page 987; Solution Pages 1013-1015","sourcePage":987,"sourcePageEnd":987},{"id":"anatomy-24-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 35-year-old patient presents with difficulty in flexing the lateral four toes. On examination, it is noted that the action of the flexor digitorum longus tendon is intact. Which of the following muscles, in the second layer of the sole, assists the flexor digitorum longus during this action?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abductor hallucis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum brevis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Quadratus plantae"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lumbricals"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Quadratus plantae","explanation":"The quadratus plantae in the second layer of the sole, assists the flexor digitorum longus by helping align and pull its tendon straight, thereby aiding in the flexion of the lateral four toes. First and Second layers of the sole: The abductor hallucis (Option A) (first layer) is involved in abducting the big toe. The flexor digitorum brevis (Option B) (first layer) also flexes the lateral four toes but acts more proximally at the middle phalanges and does not assist the action of flexor digitorum longus. The lumbricals (Option D) (second layer) are primarily involved in flexing the metatarsophalangeal joints and extending the interphalangeal joints, not in pure flexion.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 987-988; Solution Pages 1015-1018","sourcePage":987,"sourcePageEnd":988},{"id":"anatomy-24-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 35-year-old male presents with a deep laceration on the posterior aspect of his knee after a road traffic accident. On surgical exploration, the popliteal artery is injured. Which of the following is not a direct branch of the popliteal artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior medial genicular artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibular artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fibular artery","explanation":"The fibular (peroneal) artery is a branch of the posterior tibial artery , not a direct branch of the popliteal artery. Branches of the Popliteal Artery Branch Supply Muscular branches Supply the adductor magnus and hamstrings, gastrocnemius, soleus, and plantaris. Cutaneous branches Arise from the popliteal artery or indirectly from its muscular branches. Genicular branches: Five branches in total: two superior, two inferior, one middle. Middle genicular artery Pierces the oblique popliteal ligament, supplies cruciate ligaments and synovial membrane of the knee. Medial and lateral superior genicular arteries (Option A) Wind around the femur above the condyles, pass deep to the hamstrings. Medial and lateral inferior genicular arteries Wind around the tibial condyles, pass deep to the collateral ligaments of the knee, form anastomoses around the knee. Terminal Branches: It terminates at the lower border of popliteus by dividing into the anterior (Option C) and posterior tibial arteries (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Page 988; Solution Pages 1018-1019","sourcePage":988,"sourcePageEnd":988},{"id":"anatomy-24-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-24","chapter":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topic":"Lesson 24 - Vascular Supply of Lower Limb","topicIndex":24,"questionNumber":15,"question":"During a surgical procedure involving the reconstruction of the lower limb, the surgeon plans to use a perforator flap. Which of the following statements about perforating arteries is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Perforating arteries are exclusively found in the thigh region."},{"letter":"B","text":"Musculocutaneous perforators run along intermuscular septa before supplying the skin."},{"letter":"C","text":"Perforating arteries can originate from major source arteries or adjacent vessels and are essential for flap surgery."},{"letter":"D","text":"Septocutaneous perforators only supply the periosteum of bones."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Perforating arteries can originate from major source arteries or adjacent vessels and are essential for flap surgery.","explanation":"vessels and are essential for flap surgery. - Perforating arteries play a significant role in reconstructive flap surgeries as they can originate from major source arteries (e.g., femoral artery) or from vessels in adjacent tissues. - This versatility is critical for ensuring adequate blood supply to the flap, which is necessary for successful healing and function. Perforating Veins and Arteries in the Lower Limb Perforating Veins: Connecting Venous Systems - Function : Essential for venous return; connect superficial veins (e.g., great and small saphenous veins) to deep veins (e.g., femoral, popliteal, tibial veins). - Valve Role : Valves ensure one-way blood flow from superficial to deep veins, preventing backflow and enhancing venous return. - Classification : Direct Perforating Veins : Direct connections between superficial and deep veins. Examples : Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoralvein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg :Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators linkposterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. Indirect Perforating Veins : Connect throughmuscular veins before reaching deep veins. - Direct Perforating Veins : Direct connections between superficial and deep veins. - Examples : Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. - Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. - Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. - Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Indirect Perforating Veins : Connect through muscular veins before reaching deep veins. - Clinical Significance : Valve Incompetence : Faulty valves can cause varicose veins by allowing backflow from deep to superficial veins. Post-Thrombotic Incompetence : Following DVT, perforating veins may reopen without functioning valves, leading to chronic venous insufficiency. - Valve Incompetence : Faulty valves can cause varicose veins by allowing backflow from deep to superficial veins. - Post-Thrombotic Incompetence : Following DVT, perforating veins may reopen without functioning valves, leading to chronic venous insufficiency. - Direct Perforating Veins : Direct connections between superficial and deep veins. - Examples : Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. - Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. - Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. - Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Indirect Perforating Veins : Connect through muscular veins before reaching deep veins. - Great and Small Saphenous Veins: Major direct perforators. - Small Direct Perforators : Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. - Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. - Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Thigh : Adductor canal perforator connects great saphenous vein to femoral vein. - Below the Knee : Perforator links great saphenous vein to posterior tibial vein. - Leg : Lateral perforator joins small saphenous vein to peroneal vein; medial perforators link posterior arch vein to posterior tibial vein. - Valve Incompetence : Faulty valves can cause varicose veins by allowing backflow from deep to superficial veins. - Post-Thrombotic Incompetence : Following DVT, perforating veins may reopen without functioning valves, leading to chronic venous insufficiency. Perforating Arteries: Blood Supply to Lower Limb Tissues - Definition : Arteries that penetrate deep fascia to supply the suprafascial plexus (blood vessel network in subcutaneous tissue). - Types : Direct Cutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin directly from source artery. Periosteocutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin after traveling along a bone. Musculocutaneous Perforator : Passes through muscle before reaching the skin. Septocutaneous Perforator : Runs along an intermuscular septum to supply skin. Other Types : Include neurocutaneous, tendocutaneous, venocutaneous, and intercompartmental perforators. - Direct Cutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin directly from source artery. - Periosteocutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin after traveling along a bone. - Musculocutaneous Perforator : Passes through muscle before reaching the skin. - Septocutaneous Perforator : Runs along an intermuscular septum to supply skin. - Other Types : Include neurocutaneous, tendocutaneous, venocutaneous, and intercompartmental perforators. - Clinical Importance : Flap Surgery : Perforator flaps are used for wound coverage and reconstruction, supplied by perforating arteries. Donor Site : Lower limbs have an average of 90 perforating arteries, making them key for flap procedures. - Flap Surgery : Perforator flaps are used for wound coverage and reconstruction, supplied by perforating arteries. - Donor Site : Lower limbs have an average of 90 perforating arteries, making them key for flap procedures. - Direct Cutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin directly from source artery. - Periosteocutaneous Perforator : Supplies skin after traveling along a bone. - Musculocutaneous Perforator : Passes through muscle before reaching the skin. - Septocutaneous Perforator : Runs along an intermuscular septum to supply skin. - Other Types : Include neurocutaneous, tendocutaneous, venocutaneous, and intercompartmental perforators. - Flap Surgery : Perforator flaps are used for wound coverage and reconstruction, supplied by perforating arteries. - Donor Site : Lower limbs have an average of 90 perforating arteries, making them key for flap procedures. Applications of Perforator Flaps Region Application Gluteal Region Breast reconstruction; covering sacral/perineal defects Hip and Thigh Versatile flaps from anterolateral, anteromedial thighs, TFL for various reconstructions Knee and Leg Useful for knee and lower leg defects; posterior tibial artery flaps critical for reconstructions Ankle and Foot Local skin flaps preferred due to specialized skin structure Perforating arteries are exclusively found in the thigh region (Option A): Perforating arteries are found throughout the lower limb, not exclusively in the thigh. Musculocutaneous perforators run along intermuscular septa before supplying the skin (Option B): Musculocutaneous perforators pass through muscles before reaching the skin, not along intermuscular septa. Septocutaneous perforators only supply the periosteum of bones (Option D): Septocutaneous perforators supply the skin after traveling along intermuscular septa.","sourceRef":"Question Page 988; Solution Pages 1019-1025","sourcePage":988,"sourcePageEnd":988},{"id":"anatomy-25-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which nerve injury is most likely causing the loss of sensation over the dorsum of the foot and medial four toes, as well as the difficulty in eversion of the foot?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Deep peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Common peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior tibial nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Superficial peroneal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B: Superficial peroneal nerve - The superficial peroneal nerve is a mixed nerve that carries sensory information from the anterolateral part of the leg and a significant part of the dorsum of the foot except for the first web space. - The entrapment of the peroneal nerve is common and can present with loss of sensation over the dorsum of the foot. - The superficial peroneal nerve provides motor innervation to the peroneus longus and peroneus brevis muscles that lie on the lateral compartment of the leg. - The function of peroneus brevis is to sever the foot and plantarflex the ankle. Hence this patient is finding difficulty in everting the foot. Incorrect Options: Option A: The deep peroneal nerve innervates the extensor digitorum brevis and extensor hallucis brevis muscles of the leg and sensory supply to the tarsal joint and three metatarsophalangeal joints. If the nerve is damaged, it results in numbness or tingling at the top of the foot or the outer part of the upper or lower leg. It will result in an inability to hold the leg up. Option C : Common peroneal nerve is the main nerve that innervates the leg. It is one of the major branches of the sciatic nerve. It supplies to the anterior tibialis muscle, the extensor hallucis longus muscle, and the extensor digitorum longus muscle present in the anterior muscular compartment of the lower leg. These muscles are responsible for the dorsiflexion of the foot. The lateral muscular compartment of the lower extremity consists of the fibularis longus muscle and fibularis brevis muscle. The compartment is responsible for the eversion of the foot. Option D: Common peroneal nerve receives fibers from post division of L4 through S2. Injury of the nerve results in foot drop and pins and needles sensation in the leg.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1026; Solution Page 1051","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1026},{"id":"anatomy-25-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which nerve is most likely to be damaged if a patient who had surgery for varicose veins now presents with sensory loss in the medial aspect of the leg and foot?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sural nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Deep peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Saphenous nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Saphenous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D: - The sensory loss over the medial perspective of the leg and foot suggests damage to the saphenous nerve. - The saphenous nerve rises from the femoral nerve, providing the skin on the medial side of the leg and foot. - The root value of the saphenous nerve is L4. - Saphenous nerve supplies medial malleolus region and medial aspect of the dorsum of the foot but it does not reach the great toe. Incorrect Options : Option A . Sural nerve: - The sural nerve supplies the skin on the lateral side of the leg and foot and isn't likely to be harmed during varicose vein surgery. - Sural nerve is the branch of the tibial nerve bearing the dermatome S1 for the little toe. Option B . Superficial peroneal nerve: - The superficial peroneal nerve supplies the skin on the lateral perspective of the leg and dorsum of the foot. - Lateral leg compartment - Superficial peroneal nerve. - Superficial peroneal nerve works for foot eversion. Option C . Deep peroneal nerve: - The deep peroneal nerve supplies the muscles of the Anterior compartment of the leg and the skin on the dorsal perspective of the foot. - Deep peroneal nerve works as a nerve of extension.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1026; Solution Pages 1051-1053","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1026},{"id":"anatomy-25-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What is the action of the muscle at the hip, which is attached to the marked area in the image below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abduction and external rotation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Extension, adduction, and lateral rotation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexion and abduction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexion and external rotation"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Flexion and external rotation","explanation":"Correct Option D: Flexion and external rotation. - The muscle attached to the area marked in the given image is the iliopsias - It is the largest and strongest inner hip thigh muscle is primarily responsible for flexion and external rotation of the thigh at hip joint. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1026-1027; Solution Page 1053","sourcePage":1026,"sourcePageEnd":1027},{"id":"anatomy-25-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Abduction at the hip joint is done by","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gluteus maximus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gluteus medius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator internus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Quadratus femoris"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gluteus medius","explanation":"Correct option : B. - Explanation: Abduction at the hip joint, which refers to moving the leg away from the midline of the body, is primarily performed by the gluteus medius muscle . - The gluteus medius is a broad, thick muscle located on the lateral aspect of the hip. It acts as a powerful abductor of the hip joint and plays a crucial role in maintaining stability during activities such as walking and running. Incorrect options: Option A . Gluteus maximus: The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle of the buttocks and primarily functions in extending and externally rotating the hip joint. While it can assist in abduction, it is not the primary muscle responsible for this movement. Option C . Obturator internus: The obturator internus is a deep muscle of the hip that acts as an external rotator of the hip joint. It does not contribute significantly to hip abduction. Option D . Quadratus femoris: The quadratus femoris is a deep muscle of the hip that assists in hip joint stabilization and contributes to hip rotation. It is not a primary muscle involved in hip abduction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1027; Solution Pages 1053-1054","sourcePage":1027,"sourcePageEnd":1027},{"id":"anatomy-25-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Identify the marked bony process? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Capitulum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Olecranon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Trochlea"},{"letter":"D","text":"Radial head"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Radial head","explanation":"Correct Option D - Radial head: - Radial head: The radial head is a rounded bony structure located at the proximal end of the radius bone. It articulates with the capitulum of the humerus to form the radiocapitular joint, allowing rotational movements of the forearm. Incorrect options: Option A - Capitulum: The capitulum is a bony prominence located on the distal end of the humerus. It articulates with the head of the radius to form the radio capitular joint. Option B - Olecranon: The olecranon is a bony process located at the proximal end of the ulna, forming the prominent bony tip of the elbow. It serves as the insertion point for the triceps brachii muscle. Option C - Trochlea: The trochlea is a structure located on the distal end of the humerus and plays a crucial role in the formation of the elbow joint. It articulates with the trochlear notch of the ulna, allowing flexion and extension movements of the forearm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1027-1028; Solution Pages 1054-1055","sourcePage":1027,"sourcePageEnd":1028},{"id":"anatomy-25-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Foot eversion is caused by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor digitorum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peroneus longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tibialis anterior"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tibialis posterior"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Peroneus longus","explanation":"Correct option: B - Peroneus longus: The peroneus longus muscle, also known as the fibularis longus muscle, is located on the lateral side of the leg. It plays a significant role in foot eversion. When the peroneus longus contracts, it pulls the foot outward, causing eversion. Incorrect options: Option A: Extensor digitorum: The extensor digitorum muscle is located on the anterior aspect of the leg and primarily functions to extend the digits (toes) and dorsiflex the foot. It is not directly involved in foot eversion. Option C: Tibialis anterior: The tibialis anterior muscle is located on the anterior aspect of the leg. Its primary action is dorsiflexion and inversion of the foot. It is not directly involved in foot eversion. Option D: Tibialis posterior: The tibialis posterior muscle is located on the posterior aspect of the leg. Its primary action is plantarflexion and inversion of the foot. It works in the opposite direction of foot eversion.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1028; Solution Page 1055","sourcePage":1028,"sourcePageEnd":1028},{"id":"anatomy-25-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which muscle is supplied by the both tibial and common peroneal nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gracilis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Biceps femoris"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adductor longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adductor magnus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Biceps femoris","explanation":"Correct option B - Biceps femoris: The biceps femoris muscle is innervated by the tibial and common peroneal nerves. The tibial nerve supplies the long head of the biceps femoris, while the common peroneal nerve supplies the short head. Therefore, the tibial and common peroneal nerves do supply the biceps femoris muscle Incorrect options: Option A: Gracilis: The gracilis muscle is primarily innervated by the obturator nerve, not the tibial or common peroneal nerve. Therefore, the tibial and common peroneal nerves do not supply the gracilis muscle Option C: Adductor longus: The adductor longus muscle is primarily innervated by the obturator nerve, not the tibial or common peroneal nerve. Thus, the tibial and common peroneal nerves do not supply the adductor longus muscle. Option D: Adductor magnus: The adductor magnus muscle is primarily innervated by the obturator and sciatic nerves. The sciatic nerve, which is composed of the tibial and common peroneal nerves, supplies the posterior part of the adductor magnus. However, the tibial and common peroneal nerves alone do not supply the adductor magnus muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1028-1029; Solution Pages 1055-1056","sourcePage":1028,"sourcePageEnd":1029},{"id":"anatomy-25-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":8,"question":"The action of the muscle attached to the marked area? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adduction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abduction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extension"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Abduction","explanation":"Correct Option: Option C : The action of the muscle attached to the marked area is abduction. The muscle attached to the lateral surface of the greater trochanter is the gluteus Medius muscle. It plays a crucial role in the abduction of the hip joint. Abduction refers to the movement of a body part away from the midline of the body. In the case of the hip joint, abduction involves moving the leg away from the body's midline, out to the side. Incorrect Options: Option A: Flexion refers to bending or decreasing the angle between two body parts. The action of the gluteus Medius muscle is not primarily related to flexion. Option B: Adduction refers to the movement of a body part towards the midline of the body. The gluteus Medius muscle is not directly involved in adduction but rather acts as an antagonist to the adductor muscles. Option D: Extension refers to straightening or increasing the angle between two body parts. The gluteus Medius muscle does not primarily contribute to extension but rather to abduction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1029; Solution Pages 1056-1057","sourcePage":1029,"sourcePageEnd":1029},{"id":"anatomy-25-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Identify the marked tendon in the diagram: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Extensor hallucis longus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor hallucis longus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extensor digitorum longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tibialis anterior"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Extensor hallucis longus","explanation":"Correct option :A - Explanation: In the provided diagram, the marked tendon corresponds to the Extensor hallucis longus. - The Extensor hallucis longus is a muscle located in the anterior compartment of the leg. It originates from the middle third of the fibula and inserts onto the base of the distal phalanx of the great toe. - Its main action is to extend (dorsiflex) the great toe at the metatarsophalangeal joint and assist in dorsiflexion of the ankle joint. Incorrect Options: Option B . Flexor hallucis longus: This muscle is located in the posterior compartment of the leg and is responsible for flexing the great toe. Option C . Extensor digitorum longus: This muscle is also located in the anterior compartment of the leg and is responsible for extending (dorsiflexing) the lateral four toes. Option D . Tibialis anterior: This muscle is located in the anterior compartment of the leg and is responsible for dorsiflexion and inversion of the foot","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1029-1030; Solution Page 1057","sourcePage":1029,"sourcePageEnd":1030},{"id":"anatomy-25-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Identify the labeled tarsal bone in the image below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcaneus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cuboid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Navicular"},{"letter":"D","text":"Talus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Navicular","explanation":"Correct Option C: Navicular - There are 7 tarsal bones in the foot. - The Navicular is located medially in the midfoot between the talus posteriorly and the 3 cuneiform bones anteriorly. Incorrect Options: Option A. Calcaneus is the largest of the tarsal bones in the foot's heel. It bears the weight of the body as the heel touches the ground. Option B. The cuboid is the lateral bone of the foot lying in front of the calcaneus and behind the fourth and fifth metatarsal bones. Option D . The talus is the second largest tarsal bone, which lies above the calcaneus in the hindfoot.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1030-1031; Solution Pages 1057-1058","sourcePage":1030,"sourcePageEnd":1031},{"id":"anatomy-25-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A muscle is attached to the area marked in the given Image. What is the action of this muscle on the hip joint? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexion and medial rotation"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adduction and lateral rotation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Abduction and medial rotation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extension and lateral rotation"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Extension and lateral rotation","explanation":"Correct Option D: - The muscle attached to the area marked in the given image is the gluteus maximus. The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle in the gluteal region and is primarily responsible for extending the hip joint, which means it helps to move the thigh backward. This action is commonly referred to as hip extension. - Additionally, the gluteus maximus also assists in lateral rotation of the hip joint. Lateral rotation refers to the outward rotation of the thigh away from the midline of the body. Incorrect Options: Option A: Flexion and medial rotation: Flexion of the hip joint involves bringing the thigh forward, towards the abdomen. The gluteus maximus acts opposite to this motion by extending the hip, so it is not involved in hip flexion. Medial rotation refers to the inward rotation of the thigh towards the midline of the body, which is also not a primary action of the gluteus maximus. Option B: Adduction and lateral rotation: Adduction involves bringing the thigh towards the midline of the body. The gluteus maximus is not directly involved in hip adduction. Lateral rotation is the correct action associated with the gluteus maximus, as explained earlier. Option C: Abduction and medial rotation: Abduction refers to moving the thigh away from the midline of the body, which is not a primary action of the gluteus maximus. Medial rotation, as mentioned earlier, is not a primary action of the gluteus maximus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1031-1032; Solution Page 1058","sourcePage":1031,"sourcePageEnd":1032},{"id":"anatomy-25-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":12,"question":"The below image is of a femur bone. Which muscles are attached to “A”? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gluteus maximus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Iliopsoas"},{"letter":"C","text":"Quadriceps femoris"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adductor magnus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Iliopsoas","explanation":"Correct Option B: Iliopsoas - The A point marked on the image of the femur is the lesser trochanter of the femur. The Iliopsoas muscle arises from the Iliac fossa and lumbar spine and attaches to the lesser trochanter of the femur. - Iliopsoas is a large compound muscle of the inner hip composed of the iliacus and psoas major muscles. It is situated on the posterior abdominal wall. - The iliopsoas muscle is the primary flexor of the hip joint. It is also responsible for external hip rotation at the hip joint. It causes flexion of the trunk at the hip, lateral flexion of the trunk (psoas major only) Incorrect Options: Option A: Gluteus maximus is the largest and heaviest muscle in the body. It takes its origin from the dorsum of the hip bone as well as from the dorsum of the sacrum and also has ligaments. Insertion is on the gluteal tuberosity on the posterior aspect of the upper femur bone. Option C : Quadriceps femoris are the group of muscles in front of the thigh. Quadriceps perform a variety of movements, including running, jumping, kicking, and walking. There are 4 Quadriceps: 1. Rectus femoris. 2. Vastus lateralis 3. Vastus medialis 4. Vastus intermedius. When Quadriceps muscles contract, they extend the knee joint, straightening the leg. Option D: Adductor Magnus is a prime mover of the femur into adduction. One portion of the adductor muscle flexes the thigh and works as a medial rotator; the other extends the thigh and is a lateral rotator.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1032-1033; Solution Pages 1058-1059","sourcePage":1032,"sourcePageEnd":1033},{"id":"anatomy-25-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Lateral plantar nerve supply which of the following muscles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Flexor digitorum brevis, Abductor hallucis, Flexor hallucis brevis."},{"letter":"B","text":"Flexor digitorum brevis, Abductor hallucis, Extensor digitorum brevis ."},{"letter":"C","text":"Flexor digiti minimi brevis, Abductor hallucis, Abductor digiti minimi."},{"letter":"D","text":"Flexor digiti minimi brevis, Abductor digiti minimi, Adductor hallucis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Flexor digiti minimi brevis, Abductor digiti minimi, Adductor hallucis","explanation":"Correct Option D - Flexor digiti minimi brevis, Abductor digiti minimi, Adductor hallucis: Muscles supplied by the lateral nerve: - Abductor digiti minim i: Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)- Abducts and flexes the little toe - Quadratus Plantae: Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)- Flexes lateral four toes - Adductor hallucis: Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3) -Adducts the big toe - Flexor digiti minimi brevis: Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)- Flexes proximal phalanx of the little toe - Plantar interossei (3) : Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)- Adducts the toes 2-4, Flex metatarsophalangeal joints - Dorsal interossei (4) : Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)- Abducts the toes 2-4, Flex metatarsophalangeal joints - Lumbricals: Lateral 3: Lateral plantar nerve (S2, S3)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1033; Solution Pages 1059-1060","sourcePage":1033,"sourcePageEnd":1033},{"id":"anatomy-25-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 34-year-old female, who works as a maid complained of knee pain and swelling. Which of the following area gets damaged in Housemaid’s knee?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. B","explanation":"Correct Option B - B (Prepatellar bursa) - The correct answer is option b (Prepatellar bursa), as housemaid's knee involves inflammation of the prepatellar bursa due to repeated kneeling or trauma to the front of the knee.Housemaid's knee, also known as prepatellar bursitis, involves inflammation of the prepatellar bursa. The prepatellar bursa is located just in front of the patella (kneecap) and can become inflamed due to repetitive kneeling or trauma to the area. This condition is often seen in individuals who perform activities that involve prolonged kneeling, such as housemaids or carpet layers. Incorrect Options: Option A - A (Suprapatellar bursa): The suprapatellar bursa is located above the patella (kneecap) and helps reduce friction between the femur (thigh bone) and the quadriceps tendon. This bursa is not commonly associated with housemaid's knee. Option C - C (Infrapatellar bursa): The infrapatellar bursa is located below the patella, between the patellar ligament and the tibia (shin bone). It helps reduce friction during knee movements. Inflammation of this bursa is known as \"clergyman's knee\" or \"Clergyman's bursitis,\" which is caused by excessive kneeling and friction. Option D - D (Pes anserine bursa): The pes anserine bursa is located on the inner side of the knee, just below the joint. It is situated between the tendons of the sartorius, gracilis, and semitendinosus muscles and the underlying tibia. Inflammation of this bursa can lead to pes anserine bursitis, which typically presents as pain and tenderness on the inner side of the knee. This condition is not directly related to housemaid's knee.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1033-1034; Solution Pages 1060-1061","sourcePage":1033,"sourcePageEnd":1034},{"id":"anatomy-25-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":15,"question":"In the given image, the physician is trying to palpate which of the following arteries? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsalis pedis artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral plantar artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior tibial artery","explanation":"Correct Option B - Posterior tibial artery: - The posterior tibial artery is present in the posterior aspect of the leg and, runs posterior to the tibia, and supplies the calf region. Incorrect Options: Option A - Anterior tibial artery: Anterior tibial artery is present in the anterior aspect of the leg. Option C - Dorsalis pedis artery: The anterior tibial artery continuous as the dorsalis pedis artery Option D - Lateral plantar artery: The posterior tibial artery passes under the flexor retinaculum on the medial side of the ankle and divides into the medial and lateral plantar arteries to supply the sole.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1034-1035; Solution Page 1061","sourcePage":1034,"sourcePageEnd":1035},{"id":"anatomy-25-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A surgical procedure is performed on the great saphenous vein, around 2.5 cm anterior to the medial malleolus. Which of the following structures is most likely to be injured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Saphenous nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sural nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Deep peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tibial nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Saphenous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Saphenous nerve: - The great saphenous vein passes anterior to the medial malleolus and is closely associated with the saphenous nerve; therefore, it is more prone to injury during this procedure. Incorrect Options: - Option B - Sural nerve: The short saphenous vein runs posterior to the medial malleolus and is closely related to the sural nerve. - Option C and D are not closely associated with the great saphenous vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1035; Solution Page 1061","sourcePage":1035,"sourcePageEnd":1035},{"id":"anatomy-25-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Identify the muscle that is defective in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gluteus medius and Gluteus minimus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gluteus maximus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Piriformis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Obturator internus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Gluteus medius and Gluteus minimus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Gluteus medius and Gluteus minimus: - The Gluteus medius and Gluteus minimus are responsible for the abduction and medial rotation of the thigh and are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve. - A positive Trendelenburg test, which is indicated by a drop in the pelvic alignment when standing on one leg, is characteristic of a defect in these muscles. (as shown in the image) - This condition suggests a superior gluteal nerve injury, which results in a lurching motion towards the side of the lesion. Incorrect Options: - Option B, C & D: These are not the muscles affected in the given image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1035-1036; Solution Pages 1061-1062","sourcePage":1035,"sourcePageEnd":1036},{"id":"anatomy-25-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Behind the medial malleolus and in front of Achilles which artery is present?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peroneal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dorsalis pedis artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Posterior tibial artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Posterior tibial artery: - Posterior tibial artery : The posterior tibial artery is located behind the medial malleolus and in front of the Achilles tendon. It runs posterior to the medial malleolus and can be palpated in this region. Incorrect Options: Option B - Peroneal artery : The peroneal artery (also known as the fibular artery) is located along the lateral side of the leg and is not found behind the medial malleolus. It supplies the lateral compartment of the leg. Option C - Anterior tibial artery : The anterior tibial artery runs down the anterior part of the leg, in front of the interosseous membrane, and becomes the dorsalis pedis artery at the ankle. It is not located behind the medial malleolus. Option D - Dorsalis pedis artery : The dorsalis pedis artery is located on the dorsum (top) of the foot, not behind the medial malleolus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1036; Solution Pages 1062-1063","sourcePage":1036,"sourcePageEnd":1036},{"id":"anatomy-25-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Patient presenting with pain in back of thigh and leg after lifting heavy weights. Which segment is involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L4"},{"letter":"B","text":"L5"},{"letter":"C","text":"S1"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. S2","explanation":"Correct Option D - S2: - Pain in the back of the thigh and leg after lifting heavy weights is most commonly associated with the S2 nerve root. The S2 nerve root contributes to the sensation in the posterior aspect of the thigh and leg and can be affected by conditions such as sciatica or disc herniation. Incorrect Options: Option A - L4 : The L4 nerve root typically affects the anterior and medial aspect of the thigh, as well as the medial side of the leg and foot. It is less likely to be associated with pain specifically in the back of the thigh and leg. Option B - L5 : The L5 nerve root affects the lateral aspect of the leg and the dorsum of the foot. While it can cause pain radiating down the leg, it is not typically associated with pain specifically in the back of the thigh. Option C - S1 : The S1 nerve root affects the posterior and lateral aspects of the leg and the heel. Pain in the back of the thigh and leg can be related to S1, but S2 is more directly associated with the described pain after heavy lifting.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1036-1037; Solution Page 1063","sourcePage":1036,"sourcePageEnd":1037},{"id":"anatomy-25-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A patient diagnosed with sciatica had tender hamstrings. Which of the following supplies a hybrid muscle, which is spared in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Common peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tibial nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Obturator nerve","explanation":"Correct Option C - Obturator Nerve: - This nerve supplies the adductor muscles of the thigh, which are part of the hybrid muscle group that is spared in sciatica. The obturator nerve is primarily responsible for innervating the adductor muscles (adductor longus, adductor brevis, adductor magnus, and gracilis) which are not affected in sciatica, as sciatica mainly involves the sciatic nerve and its branches. Incorrect Options: Option A - Femoral Nerve : This nerve innervates the quadriceps and other muscles of the anterior thigh. In sciatica, the femoral nerve is generally not directly involved, but the tender hamstrings are more related to the sciatic nerve and its branches. Option B - Common Peroneal Nerve : This is a branch of the sciatic nerve, and sciatica can affect it, leading to symptoms like foot drop. It does not provide significant innervation to the hybrid muscle group that is mentioned in this context. Option D - Tibial Nerve : This is another branch of the sciatic nerve, and it innervates the posterior compartment of the leg and the plantar aspect of the foot. It is involved in sciatica and is not spared in this condition.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1037; Solution Pages 1063-1064","sourcePage":1037,"sourcePageEnd":1037},{"id":"anatomy-25-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Identify the marked structure in the given image **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ischial spine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior superior iliac spine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior inferior iliac spine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ischial tuberosity"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Anterior superior iliac spine","explanation":"Correct option: Option B. - The marked structure shown in the above image is the anterior superior iliac spine . - A bony projection is present at the anterior end of the iliac crest. This is called the anterior superior iliac spine. - This projection provides attachment to the lateral end of the inguinal ligament. It can be easily felt at the lateral end of the fold of the groin. Incorrect options: Option A . Ischial spine is superior to the ischial tuberosity. Option C . Anterior inferior iliac spine is the lower half of the anterior border of the ilium that projects forward. Option D . Ischial tuberosity is the lower end of the body of ischium (part of the hip bone).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1037-1038; Solution Pages 1064-1065","sourcePage":1037,"sourcePageEnd":1038},{"id":"anatomy-25-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following arteries is palpated in the image shown below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsalis pedis artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Popliteal artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior tibial artery","explanation":"Correct Option B: - The artery palpated in the image shown is the posterior tibial artery; it is palpated between the medial malleolus and Achilles tendon/tendocalcaneous. Locate the medial malleolus (the bony bump on the inside of the ankle). Place your index and middle fingers on the inside of the ankle and move your fingers behind the medial malleolus towards the back of the leg. You should be able to feel the posterior tibial artery pulsations in this area. Incorrect Options: Option A - Anterior tibial artery: To palpate the anterior tibial artery, ask the patient to lie down or sit with their legs slightly bent. Locate the ankle bones (medial malleolus and lateral malleolus) and identify the space between them. Place your index and middle fingers on the space between the ankle bones and move your fingers upwards towards the top of the foot. You should be able to feel the anterior tibial artery pulsations in this area. Option C - Dorsalis pedis artery: To palpate the dorsalis pedis artery, ask the patient to lie down or sit with their legs slightly bent. Locate the first and second metatarsal bones (the bones that connect to the big toe). Place your index and middle fingers on the top of the foot, just below the space between the first and second metatarsal bones, you can feel the pulsations there. Option D - Popliteal artery: To palpate the popliteal artery, ask the patient to lie down on their stomach or to sit with their legs hanging off the edge of the exam table. Flex the knee to about 90 degrees. Use your fingers to locate the popliteal fossa, which is the small depression behind the knee joint. Feel for the pulsation of the artery in the middle of the fossa. Press your fingersgently into the popliteal fossa and feel for the pulsation of the artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1038; Solution Pages 1065-1066","sourcePage":1038,"sourcePageEnd":1038},{"id":"anatomy-25-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":23,"question":"The patient who visited the outpatient department (OPD) displayed an irregular way of walking. During the examination, it was noticed that when the patient was instructed to balance on their left foot, there was a drop in their right hip. Which muscles are affected by paralysis in this particular patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right gluteus maximus and gluteus medius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right gluteus medius and gluteus minimus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left gluteus maximus and gluteus medius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left gluteus medius and gluteus minimus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Left gluteus medius and gluteus minimus","explanation":"Correct Option D - Left gluteus medius and gluteus minimus : - The left gluteus medius and gluteus minimus stabilize the pelvis during a single-leg stance on the left side. - If these muscles were paralyzed, it would result in difficulty stabilizing the pelvis, causing a right-sided hip drop when standing on the left foot. - A right-sided hip drop when standing on the left foot suggests that there is a weakness or paralysis of the left-sided muscles that stabilize the pelvis during a single-leg stance. Incorrect Options: Option A - Right gluteus maximus and gluteus medius : Right gluteus maximus and gluteus medius: if the right gluteus maximus and gluteus medius were paralyzed, it would result in difficulty extending the hip and abducting the hip, respectively, but would not cause a right-sided hip drop when standing on the left foot. Option B - Right gluteus medius and gluteus minimus: Right gluteus medius and gluteus minimus: If the right gluteus medius and gluteus minimus were paralyzed, they would have difficulty abducting the hip and stabilizing the pelvis during a single-leg stance on the right side. However, this would not cause a right-sided hip drop when standing on the left foot, as the left-sided muscles would stabilize the pelvis during a single-leg stance on the left side. Option C - Left gluteus maximus and gluteus medius: Left gluteus maximus and gluteus medius: If the left gluteus maximus and gluteus medius were paralyzed, they would have difficulty extending the hip and abducting the hip, respectively, but would not cause a right-sided hip drop when standing on the left foot.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1038-1039; Solution Page 1066","sourcePage":1038,"sourcePageEnd":1039},{"id":"anatomy-25-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which of the following marked sites is responsible for the impairment of dorsiflexion due to injury? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"1"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 3","explanation":"Correct Option A: The injury to common fibular nerve causes failure of dorsiflexion ,thus the marked structure 3 i.e. neck of fibula is the right answer Incorrect Options: Option B. Head of fibula - as route of common fibular nerve does not follow this pathway , so it, is the wrong answer Option C. Lateral condyle of femur -as route of common fibular nerve does not follow this pathway , so it, is the wrong answer Option D. Tibial tuberosity - as route of common fibular nerve does not follow this pathway , so it, is the wrong answer","sourceRef":"Question Page 1039; Solution Pages 1066-1067","sourcePage":1039,"sourcePageEnd":1039},{"id":"anatomy-25-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":25,"question":"What is the ligament that links the medial cuneiform bone to the base of the second metatarsal bone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chopart"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spring"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lisfranc"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deltoid"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lisfranc","explanation":"Correct Option C - Lisfranc: - The Lisfranc ligament is the specific ligament that connects the medial cuneiform to the base of the 2nd metatarsal. It is located in the midfoot region and provides significant stability to the foot's arch. - It passes from the lateral surface of the medial cuneiform to the adjacent angle of the second metatarsal bone. - This ligament bears the name of Jacques Lisfranc de St. Martin, a French surgeon who first described this anatomical structure. - Injuries to the Lisfranc ligament can lead to instability and often require surgical repair. Incorrect Options: Option A - Chopart: It is involved in the articulation between the talus and calcaneus bones in the foot, a joint complex known as the Chopart joint. Option B - Spring: The Spring ligament, also known as the plantar calcaneonavicular ligament, is located on the underside of the foot and supports the head of the talus, helping to maintain the foot's arch. It does not directly connect the medial cuneiform to the base of the 2nd metatarsal. Option D - Deltoid: The Deltoid ligament, a strong triangular ligament located on the inner side of the ankle, links the tibia to various bones of the foot but does not connect the medial cuneiform to the base of the 2nd metatarsal. This ligament provides significant support to the medial side of the ankle joint.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1039-1040; Solution Pages 1067-1068","sourcePage":1039,"sourcePageEnd":1040},{"id":"anatomy-25-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which of the following structures is not supplied by the superior gluteal nerve?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gluteus medius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gluteus minimus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Piriformis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tensor fasciae latae"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Piriformis","explanation":"Correct Option C - Piriformis: - The superior gluteal nerve, which arises from the posterior division of the sacral plexus (L4-S1), is known to innervate the gluteus medius, gluteus minimus, and tensor fasciae latae muscles. These muscles are responsible for hip abduction and medial rotation. - The Piriformis muscle, although located in the same region as the other muscles mentioned, is not innervated by the superior gluteal nerve. Instead, the piriformis muscle is supplied by a specific nerve known as the nerve to piriformis, which arises directly from the sacral plexus (S1 and S2). Incorrect Options: Option A, B, and D are supplied by the superior gluteal nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1040; Solution Page 1068","sourcePage":1040,"sourcePageEnd":1040},{"id":"anatomy-25-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Which nerve is affected in a 50-year-old male who has a positive Trendelenburg test on the right side?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right inferior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right superior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left superior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left inferior gluteal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Right superior gluteal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B - Right superior gluteal nerve: - In the given scenario, a 50-year-old male presented with a positive Trendelenburg test on the right side. The Trendelenburg test is used to assess the strength of the hip abductor muscles, primarily the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles, which are innervated by the superior gluteal nerve. - The Trendelenburg test involves asking the patient to stand on one leg while the examiner observes the position of the pelvis. In a positive Trendelenburg test, when the patient stands on the affected leg, the pelvis drops on the contralateral side instead of remaining level. This indicates weakness or dysfunction of the hip abductor muscles on the affected side. - Therefore, in this case, the positive Trendelenburg test on the right side suggests an injury or dysfunction of the muscles innervated by the right superior gluteal nerve. The correct answer is option b: Right superior gluteal nerve. Incorrect Options: Options A, C and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1040; Solution Pages 1068-1069","sourcePage":1040,"sourcePageEnd":1040},{"id":"anatomy-25-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":28,"question":"Which nerve supplies the Gluteus maximus? (or) Which nerve supplies the Gluteus maximus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior gluteal nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior gluteal nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Inferior gluteal nerve","explanation":"Correct option: B Inferior Gluteal Nerve - The inferior gluteal nerve (from the sacral plexus, specifically from L5, S1, and S2 nerve roots) primarily innervates the gluteus maximus muscle. - It arises from the posterior division of the sacral plexus and passes through the greater sciatic foramen to reach the gluteal region. - This nerve provides motor innervation to the gluteus maximus, which is the largest muscle in the buttocks and plays a significant role in movements such as hip extension and external rotation. Incorrect options: Option A: Femoral nerve - The femoral nerve primarily innervates muscles of the anterior thigh, such as the quadriceps femoris muscle group. It does not supply the gluteus maximus. Option C: Obturator nerve - The obturator nerve primarily innervates muscles of the medial thigh, such as the adductor muscles. It does not supply the gluteus maximus. Option D: Superior gluteal nerve - The superior gluteal nerve also arises from the sacral plexus, but it primarily innervates the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus muscles. It does not supply the gluteus maximus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1040-1041; Solution Pages 1069-1070","sourcePage":1040,"sourcePageEnd":1041},{"id":"anatomy-25-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Which nerve is most likely to be affected in a patient who underwent a coronary bypass graft with the great saphenous vein and is now experiencing paresthesia and sensory loss over the inner side of the leg and foot?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superficial peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Saphenous nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sural nerve"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Saphenous nerve","explanation":"Correct Option B: - The saphenous nerve is a branch of the femoral nerve and provides sensory innervation to the medial aspect of the leg and foot. - During coronary bypass graft surgery using the great saphenous vein, the saphenous nerve can be injured or compressed, leading to paresthesia and sensory loss over the medial leg and foot. Hence, the saphenous nerve is the most likely nerve to be involved in this case. Incorrect Options Option A - Superficial peroneal nerve: The superficial peroneal nerve innervates the anterior compartment of the leg and provides sensation to the dorsal foot and toes. It is not directly involved in the harvesting of the great saphenous vein or coronary bypass graft surgery. Therefore, it is not the most likely nerve to be involved in this scenario. Option C - Femoral nerve: The femoral nerve is a large nerve that innervates the anterior thigh muscles and provides sensory innervation to the anterior thigh and medial leg.It is not the most likely nerve to be involved in this situation. Option D. Sural nerve: The sural nerve is a sensory nerve that runs along the back of the leg, supplying the lateral aspect of the foot and ankle. It is not directly related to the great saphenous vein or the surgical procedure described. Therefore, it is not the most likely nerve to be involved in this case.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1041; Solution Page 1070","sourcePage":1041,"sourcePageEnd":1041},{"id":"anatomy-25-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which muscle is not involved in the inversion of foot?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tibialis anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tibialis posterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Peroneus longus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extensor hallucis longus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Peroneus longus","explanation":"Correct Option C. - Peroneus longus: The peroneus longus muscle, also known as fibularis longus, is located on the lateral side of the leg. It is primarily responsible for eversion (turning outward) of the foot, not inversion. Eversion is the opposite movement of inversion and involves turning the sole of the foot outward. Incorrect Options: Option A. Tibialis anterior: The tibialis anterior muscle is located on the front (anterior) side of the leg. It is responsible for dorsiflexion (lifting) of the foot and inversion of the foot. It plays an important role in controlling the foot position during walking and running. Option B. Tibialis posterior: The tibialis posterior muscle is located on the back (posterior) side of the leg. It is a powerful muscle that contributes to both inversion and plantar flexion (pointing) of the foot. It helps to maintain the arch of the foot and provides stability during walking and running. Option D. Extensor hallucis longus: The extensor hallucis longus muscle is located on the front (anterior) side of the leg. It is responsible for dorsiflexion of the foot and extension of the big toe. While it does not directly contribute to inversion of the foot, it plays a role in maintaining the foot's position during various movements.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1041; Solution Pages 1070-1071","sourcePage":1041,"sourcePageEnd":1041},{"id":"anatomy-25-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Identify the tarsal bone labeled in the image below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Calcaneus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cuboid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Navicular"},{"letter":"D","text":"Talus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Navicular","explanation":"Correct option C: Navicular - This is the dorsoplantar view of the ankle and foot which shows navicular bone. - The navicular bone is boat-shaped. It is situated on the medial side of the foot, in front of the head of the talus, and behind the three cuneiform bones. - Anatomical Position : It is held mediolaterally according to its side. Incorrect options: Option A: The calcaneus is the largest tarsal bone. It forms the prominence of the heel Option B: The cuboid is the lateral bone of the distal row of the tarsus, situated in front of the calcaneum and behind the fourth and fifth metatarsal bones Option D: Talus has no muscular attachment. Tendon of flexor hallucis longus courses between the two tubercles of its posterior process. Dorsoplantar view of the ankle and foot","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1041-1042; Solution Pages 1071-1072","sourcePage":1041,"sourcePageEnd":1042},{"id":"anatomy-25-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":32,"question":"Which artery is being palpated in the image shown below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsalis pedis artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Popliteal artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior tibial artery","explanation":"Correct Option B: Posterior tibial artery - The posterior tibial pulse can be felt against the calcaneum about 2 cm below and behind the medial malleolus. - The long tendon of plantaris is used for tendon transplantation in the body. - Tendo calcaneus can rupture in tennis players 5 cm above its insertion. Plantar flexion is not possible. The two ends must be stitched Incorrect options: Option A: The posterior tibial artery (Figure 30.6) lies just posterior to the medial malleolus. It can be felt most readily by curling the fingers of the examining hand anteriorly around the ankle. Option C: The dorsalis pedis artery pulse can be palpated on the dorsal surface of the foot. Option D: To palpate poplitieal artery wrap your hand around your knee so your fingers are against the soft spot in the back of your knee.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1042-1043; Solution Pages 1072-1073","sourcePage":1042,"sourcePageEnd":1043},{"id":"anatomy-25-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":33,"question":"The dermatome of the area marked in the given below image is. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L4"},{"letter":"B","text":"L5"},{"letter":"C","text":"S1"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. L4","explanation":"Correct option A: L4 - The cutaneous innervation of the lower limb is derived: a. Mainly from segments L1 to L5 and S1 to S3 of the spinal cord; and b. Partly from segments T12 and S4. - Medial malleolus and the medial aspect of the leg region - It has a proximal nerve with proximal root value. - The nerve is called saphenous nerve. - It is supplied by the L4 dermatome Incorrect options Option B: L5 is adjacent to L4 and supplies the lateral part of the posterior leg Option C: S1 is anteriorly dorsal surface of foot and supplies the little figer anteriorly Option D: S2 is posterior view and supplies the posterior middle portion of the keg and some part of thigh as well","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1043-1044; Solution Pages 1073-1074","sourcePage":1043,"sourcePageEnd":1044},{"id":"anatomy-25-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":34,"question":"the prevention of lateral dislocation of the patella during knee flexion.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vastus medialis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Patellar ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gracilis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rectus femoris"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Vastus medialis","explanation":"Correct option A - In knee flexion, the lateral dislocation of the patella (kneecap) is prevented by the action of the vastus medialis muscle. The vastus medialis is one of the four quadriceps muscles located in the anterior thigh. - The vastus medialis has a teardrop-shaped portion called the vastus medialis obliquus (VMO), which plays a crucial role in stabilizing the patella during knee movements. When the knee flexes, the VMO contracts and pulls the patella medially (towards the midline), preventing it from dislocating laterally. Incorrect options: Option B. Patellar ligament: The patellar ligament connects the patella to the tibia. It provides support and helps transmit forces during movements of the knee, but it does not have a specific role in preventing lateral dislocation of the patella during knee flexion. Option C. Gracilis: The gracilis muscle is a long, thin muscle located in the inner thigh. While it assists in flexing the knee, it does not have a primary function in preventing lateral dislocation of the patella. Its primary role is in hip adduction and knee flexion. Option D. Rectus femoris: The rectus femoris is one of the quadriceps muscles and is responsible for extending the knee joint. Although it is involved in knee flexion, it is not specifically responsible for preventing lateral dislocation of the patella. Its primary role is in knee extension and hip flexion.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1044; Solution Page 1074","sourcePage":1044,"sourcePageEnd":1044},{"id":"anatomy-25-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":35,"question":"Identify the pelvic diaphragm in the picture given below: -","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1","explanation":"Correct Choice: A - The pelvic diaphragm is identified as: Option a. Levator ani: The levator ani is a broad, thin muscle that forms the major component of the pelvic diaphragm. It consists of two main parts: the puborectalis and the pubococcygeus. The levator ani muscle provides support to the pelvic organs and helps maintain continence. Incorrect Choices: Option B. External anal sphincter: The external anal sphincter is a skeletal muscle that surrounds the anal canal but is not considered a part of the pelvic diaphragm. While it plays a role in maintaining fecal continence, it is not directly associated with the pelvic diaphragm. Option C. Internal anal sphincter: The internal anal sphincter is a smooth muscle located within the wall of the anal canal. Although it contributes to the control of defecation, it is not considered a part of the pelvic diaphragm. The pelvic diaphragm primarily consists of the levator ani muscle, which is distinct from the internal anal sphincter. Option D . Pudendal canal: The pudendal canal is a passageway in the pelvic floor that houses the pudendal nerve, artery, and vein. While it is located in the pelvic region, it is not a component of the pelvic diaphragm itself. The pelvic diaphragm refers specifically to the levator ani muscle and its associated structures.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1044-1045; Solution Pages 1074-1075","sourcePage":1044,"sourcePageEnd":1045},{"id":"anatomy-25-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":36,"question":"Which of the following structures are affected in a boy who has a lurching gait and dragging of his feet as a result of falling from a tree?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2","explanation":"Correct Option B. - Based on the symptoms described (lurching gait and dragging of the feet) after a fall from a tree, the structure that is most likely involved is the gluteus medius muscle (option b).The gluteus medius is one of the three gluteal muscles located in the gluteal region. - It plays an important role in stabilizing the pelvis during walking and is responsible for abduction and medial rotation of the hip joint. - When the gluteus medius muscle is weak or dysfunctional, it can result in a characteristic gait pattern known as a Trendelenburg gait. - In a Trendelenburg gait, the person leans towards the affected side during the stance phase of walking. This compensatory mechanism helps shift the body's center of gravity over the stance leg to maintain balance. - However, as a consequence, the unaffected leg cannot fully support the body's weight, leading to a lurching gait and dragging of the foot. While the other structures mentioned (gluteus maximus, piriformis, and sciatic nerve) are also located in the gluteal region and can contribute to gait abnormalities, the specific symptoms described in this case are more consistent with a dysfunction of the gluteus medius muscle. Incorrect Options: Option A . Gluteus maximus: The gluteus maximus is the largest muscle in the gluteal region and is primarily responsible for hip extension. While it can contribute to the overall function of walking, weakness or dysfunction of the gluteus maximus would not typically result in a lurching gait and dragging of the feet as described in the case. Therefore, option a is unlikely to be the structure involved. Option C . Piriformis: The piriformis is a small muscle located in the gluteal region that helps with hip rotation and stabilization. It is situated deep to the gluteus maximus. Although the piriformis muscle can cause symptoms such as sciatic nerve compression (piriformis syndrome), it would not directly result in a lurching gait and dragging of the feet. Therefore, option c is unlikely to be the structure involved. Option D . Sciatic nerve: While the sciatic nerve passes through the gluteal region and can be involved in various lower limb symptoms, it is not the most likely structure to be affected in this case. The symptoms of a lurching gait and dragging of the feet are more indicative of a muscular dysfunction rather than a specific nerve injury. Therefore, option d is not the most likely structure involved in this scenario.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1045-1046; Solution Pages 1075-1076","sourcePage":1045,"sourcePageEnd":1046},{"id":"anatomy-25-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Foot drop is caused by damage to:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Common peroneal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Femoral nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tibial nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sciatic nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Common peroneal nerve","explanation":"Correct Option: Option A: Common peroneal nerve: Foot drop is commonly caused by damage to the common peroneal nerve, also known as the common fibular nerve. The common peroneal nerve innervates the muscles responsible for ankle dorsiflexion and foot eversion. Damage to this nerve can occur due to various factors such as trauma, compression, nerve entrapment, or underlying medical conditions. Incorrect Option: Option B: Femoral nerve: The femoral nerve primarily innervates the muscles of the anterior thigh, including the quadriceps femoris muscle group. It is not directly responsible for ankle dorsiflexion or foot drop. Option C: Tibial nerve: The tibial nerve innervates the muscles of the posterior compartment of the leg, including the calf muscles that are involved in ankle plantar flexion. Damage to the tibial nerve may result in difficulty with plantar flexion but is not a common cause of foot drop. Option D: Sciatic nerve: The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body and consists of two components: the tibial nerve and the common peroneal nerve. While damage to the sciatic nerve can potentially cause foot drop, it is more specifically attributed to damage to the common peroneal nerve, which is a branch of the sciatic nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1046; Solution Pages 1076-1077","sourcePage":1046,"sourcePageEnd":1046},{"id":"anatomy-25-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":38,"question":"What is the name of the artery that can be felt between the medial malleolus and the calcaneal tendon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior tibial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsalis pedis artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Popliteal artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior tibial artery","explanation":"The correct answer for the artery that is palpated between the medial malleolus and the calcaneal tendon is the Posterior tibial artery. Let's go through the provided options and their explanations: In summary, the correct answer is the Posterior tibial artery. It is the artery that can be palpated between the medial malleolus and the calcaneal tendon, providing information about the circulation to the foot and ankle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1046-1047; Solution Page 1077","sourcePage":1046,"sourcePageEnd":1047},{"id":"anatomy-25-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":39,"question":"In which compartment of the leg is the neurovascular bundle NOT present?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral compartment"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior superficial compartment"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior deep compartment"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior compartment"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior superficial compartment","explanation":"Correct Option B: - The neurovascular bundle refers to a grouping of blood vessels, nerves, and lymphatic vessels that travel together in a common sheath. In the leg, the neurovascular bundle is absent in the posterior superficial compartment. Incorrect options: Option A Lateral compartment: The lateral compartment of the leg contains the fibularis (peroneal) muscles and their associated blood vessels and nerves. While the specific arrangement of the neurovascular bundle may vary, it is generally present in the lateral compartment. Option C: Posterior deep compartment: The posterior deep compartment of the leg contains the deep muscles, such as the tibialis posterior, flexor digitorum longus, and flexor hallucis longus, along with their associated blood vessels and nerves. The neurovascular bundle is typically present in this compartment. Option D: Anterior compartment: The anterior compartment of the leg contains the muscles responsible for dorsiflexion of the foot and toe extension, such as the tibialis anterior and extensor digitorum longus. It also houses the anterior tibial artery and its branches, as well as the deep peroneal nerve. The neurovascular bundle is present in this compartment.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1047; Solution Pages 1077-1078","sourcePage":1047,"sourcePageEnd":1047},{"id":"anatomy-25-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-25","chapter":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 25 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":25,"questionNumber":40,"question":"The muscle attached to the marked bony structure is? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ilio-psoas"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gluteus maximus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gluteus medius"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sartorius"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ilio-psoas","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ilio-psoas: - The iliopsoas muscle consists of the iliacus and psoas major muscles. - The iliopsoas muscle consists of the iliacus and psoas major muscles. It attaches to the lesser trochanter of the femur and primarily originates from the iliac fossa and lumbar vertebrae. The iliopsoas muscle is a major hip flexor and assists in anterior pelvic tilt. Incorrect Options: Option B - Gluteus maximus: Gluteus maximus is inserted into the iliotibial tract and gluteal tuberosity of the femur. Option C - Gluteus medius: Gluteus medius inserts into the lateral surface of the greater trochanter. Option D - Sartorius: Sartorius inserts into the medial side of the proximal tibia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1047-1048; Solution Page 1078","sourcePage":1047,"sourcePageEnd":1048},{"id":"anatomy-26-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements about the embryonic development of the heart is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Heart tube is derived from the splanchnopleuric mesoderm."},{"letter":"B","text":"Truncus arteriosus forms the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk."},{"letter":"C","text":"Bulbus cordis is located at the caudal end of the developing heart tube."},{"letter":"D","text":"Disappearance of the dorsal mesocardium creates the transverse sinus."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Bulbus cordis is located at the caudal end of the developing heart tube.","explanation":"Bulbus cordis is located at the cranial end of the developing heart tube , not the caudal end. Formation of Heart Tubes: - The endothelial heart tube is derived from the splanchnopleuric mesoderm, located near the pericardial cavity. (Option A) - Initially, the heart appears as right and left endothelial heart tubes. - These tubes fuse to form a single heart tube. - The heart tube has 5 dilatations, which are: Truncus arteriosus Bulbus cordis Primitive ventricle Primitive atrium Sinus venosus - Truncus arteriosus - Bulbus cordis - Primitive ventricle - Primitive atrium - Sinus venosus - Sinus venosus is located at the caudal (venous) end of the primitive heart tube. It consists of a body with two prolongations known as the right and left horns. Right horn: Forms smooth part of the right atrium (sinus venarum). Left horn: Forms coronary sinus. Each horn receives blood from three veins: Vitelline vein Umbilical vein Common cardinal vein - It consists of a body with two prolongations known as the right and left horns. - Right horn: Forms smooth part of the right atrium (sinus venarum). - Left horn: Forms coronary sinus. - Each horn receives blood from three veins: Vitelline vein Umbilical vein Common cardinal vein - Vitelline vein - Umbilical vein - Common cardinal vein - Bulbus cordis is the cranial most part of the developing heart tube. (Option C) Forms outflow (smooth) parts of the right ventricle (conus arteriosus) and left ventricle (aortic vestibule). - Forms outflow (smooth) parts of the right ventricle (conus arteriosus) and left ventricle (aortic vestibule). - Primitive ventricle: Forms inflow (rough) parts of both ventricles. - Primitive atrium: Forms trabeculated part of the right and left atrium. - Truncus arteriosus: Forms ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk. (Option B) - Truncus arteriosus - Bulbus cordis - Primitive ventricle - Primitive atrium - Sinus venosus - It consists of a body with two prolongations known as the right and left horns. - Right horn: Forms smooth part of the right atrium (sinus venarum). - Left horn: Forms coronary sinus. - Each horn receives blood from three veins: Vitelline vein Umbilical vein Common cardinal vein - Vitelline vein - Umbilical vein - Common cardinal vein - Vitelline vein - Umbilical vein - Common cardinal vein - Forms outflow (smooth) parts of the right ventricle (conus arteriosus) and left ventricle (aortic vestibule). Formation of Transverse Pericardial Sinus: - Heart Tube Folding: Brings the arterial and venous ends closer together. - Disappearance of the dorsal mesocardium creates the transverse sinus of the pericardium. (Option D) - Parietal and visceral layers of the serous pericardium become continuous at the arterial and venous ends of the heart. - Pericardium forms two tubes: One enclosing the aorta and pulmonary trunk. Other enclosing the SVC, IVC, and four pulmonary veins. - One enclosing the aorta and pulmonary trunk. - Other enclosing the SVC, IVC, and four pulmonary veins. - Two tubes are separated by the transverse sinus of the pericardium. - One enclosing the aorta and pulmonary trunk. - Other enclosing the SVC, IVC, and four pulmonary veins.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1079; Solution Pages 1084-1087","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1079},{"id":"anatomy-26-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A newborn baby is found to have bluish discoloration of lips, tongue, and nails with poor feeding and signs of congestive heart failure. She is diagnosed with patent truncus arteriosus. What developmental error leads to this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Overriding aorta and pulmonary stenosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Switching of the aorta and pulmonary artery position."},{"letter":"C","text":"Incomplete closure of interventricular septum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Complete absence of the spiral septum"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Complete absence of the spiral septum","explanation":"The absence of spiral aorticopulmonary septum (AP septum) is a pathologic finding in truncus arteriosus (TA). Formation of the Bulbus Cordis: - The bulbus cordis is the cranial-most region of the primitive heart tube, - Originates from the cardiogenic area, a specialized mesoderm region cranial to the prochordal plate. - Divides into 3 parts: Proximal one-third: - Merges with the primitive ventricle to form the bulboventricular chamber. - Contributes to the trabeculated part of the right ventricle. Middle part (Conus Cordis): - Forms outflow tracts of both ventricles. - Two septae are formed in this: proximal and distal bulbar septa. - The proximal septum contributes to the interventricular septum . - The distal septum divides the conus into the aortic vestibule and conus arteriosus. Distal part (Truncus Arteriosus): - Divides into ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk via the spiral aortopulmonary septum - Spiral septum is formed by the fusion of the right superior and left inferior truncus swellings or cushions. - Responsible for distributing blood to the body and lungs. Clinical Significance: - Persistent truncus arteriosus: Complete absence of spiral septum. (Option D) - Ventricular Septal Defects (VSDs): Result from incomplete closure of the interventricular septum, leading to holes in the ventricular wall. (Option C) Symptoms may include shortness of breath and an increased risk of infections. - Result from incomplete closure of the interventricular septum, leading to holes in the ventricular wall. (Option C) - Symptoms may include shortness of breath and an increased risk of infections. - Result from incomplete closure of the interventricular septum, leading to holes in the ventricular wall. (Option C) - Symptoms may include shortness of breath and an increased risk of infections. - Tetralogy of Fallot: Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities in the conus and truncus arteriosus: VSD Overriding aorta Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) Right ventricular hypertrophy Can cause cyanosis and impede blood flow to the lungs. - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities in the conus and truncus arteriosus: VSD Overriding aorta Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) Right ventricular hypertrophy - VSD - Overriding aorta - Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) - Right ventricular hypertrophy - Can cause cyanosis and impede blood flow to the lungs. - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities in the conus and truncus arteriosus: VSD Overriding aorta Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) Right ventricular hypertrophy - VSD - Overriding aorta - Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) - Right ventricular hypertrophy - Can cause cyanosis and impede blood flow to the lungs. - VSD - Overriding aorta - Pulmonary stenosis (Option A) - Right ventricular hypertrophy - Transposition of the Great Arteries (TGA): Occurs when the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. (Option B) Requires surgical intervention to correct blood flow issues. - Occurs when the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. (Option B) - Requires surgical intervention to correct blood flow issues. - Occurs when the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. (Option B) - Requires surgical intervention to correct blood flow issues.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1079; Solution Pages 1087-1093","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1079},{"id":"anatomy-26-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":3,"question":"An infant's echocardiogram reveals an enlarged coronary sinus. This finding most likely results from an abnormality in which embryological structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right horn of the sinus venosus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left horn of the sinus venosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right common cardinal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right vitelline vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Left horn of the sinus venosus","explanation":"Left horn of the sinus venosus regresses during development to form the coronary sinus , so a defect in this structure can result in an abnormal coronary sinus. Sinus Venosus: Structure of Sinus Venosus Sinus venosus is the caudal (venous) end of the primitive heart tube, consisting of a body with right and left horns. Each horn is connected to three major veins: Vitelline vein (from the yolk sac) Umbilical vein (from the placenta) Common cardinal vein (from the body wall) Communication with Atrial Chamber Sinus venosus initially communicates with the primitive atrium through a wide sinoatrial orifice. Grooves appear on the lateral walls of the heart, partially separating the sinus venosus from the atrium. Regression of the Left Horn Left horn and its tributaries become reduced in size and become part of the coronary sinus. Fate of Right Horn (Option A) Incorporated into the right atrium, forming the smooth-walled part of the right atrium (sinus venarum). Fate of Sinus Venosus Tributaries Right common cardinal vein becomes part of the superior vena cava. (Option C) Right vitelline vein forms the terminal part of the inferior vena cava. (Option D) Left horn regresses and becomes part of the coronary sinus. (Option B) Left common cardinal vein forms the oblique vein of the left atrium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1079-1080; Solution Pages 1093-1094","sourcePage":1079,"sourcePageEnd":1080},{"id":"anatomy-26-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements about cardiac jelly is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Provide structural support during early heart development"},{"letter":"B","text":"Facilitates end-systolic occlusion, improving pumping efficiency"},{"letter":"C","text":"Store elastic energy during systole for the refilling phase of the heart."},{"letter":"D","text":"Cellular matrix secreted by the endocardium in the developing heart."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Cellular matrix secreted by the endocardium in the developing heart.","explanation":"The cardiac jelly is an acellular matrix that is secreted by the myocardium in the developing heart. Cardiac Jelly (CJ): - A gelatinous, cell-free extracellular matrix filling the myoendocardial space of the embryonic heart tube. - Function : Acts as a hydraulic skeleton, providing structural support during early heart development. (Option A) Facilitates end-systolic occlusion of the embryonic heart lumen, improving pumping efficiency. (Option B) Stores elastic energy during systole, aiding the refilling phase of the heart. (Option C) - Acts as a hydraulic skeleton, providing structural support during early heart development. (Option A) - Facilitates end-systolic occlusion of the embryonic heart lumen, improving pumping efficiency. (Option B) - Stores elastic energy during systole, aiding the refilling phase of the heart. (Option C) - Acts as a hydraulic skeleton, providing structural support during early heart development. (Option A) - Facilitates end-systolic occlusion of the embryonic heart lumen, improving pumping efficiency. (Option B) - Stores elastic energy during systole, aiding the refilling phase of the heart. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1080; Solution Pages 1094-1095","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1080},{"id":"anatomy-26-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In the image depicting fetal heart development, which structures are labelled as A and B, respectively? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A: Ostium primum, B: Ostium secundum"},{"letter":"B","text":"A: Foramen ovale, B: Ostium primum"},{"letter":"C","text":"A: Septum primum, B: Septum secundum"},{"letter":"D","text":"A: Septum secundum, B: Septum primum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. A: Septum primum, B: Septum secundum","explanation":"- Septum primum (Label A) grows first and contributes to the division of the atria. - The septum secundum (Label B) grows later and overlaps the septum primum, forming the foramen ovale, which allows right-to-left blood flow in fetal life. Septum Primum Formed as a sickle-shaped fold from the roof and dorsal wall of the primitive atrium due to pressure from the bulbus cordis. Grows downward toward the atrioventricular (AV) canal and septum intermedium, fusing with the latter. Ostium Primum Before the septum primum reaches and fuses with the septum intermedium, a gap (foramen primum) allows blood flow between the right and left atria. Ostium Secundum Upper part of the septum primum breaks down, creating a new gap (foramen secundum) before the foramen primum closes. The septum primum now has a free upper edge. Septum Secundum Grows down from the roof of the atrial chamber, to the right of the septum primum, overlapping the free upper edge of the septum primum as it grows. Foramen Ovale Formation Overlapping septa create the foramen ovale, allowing blood to flow from right to left atrium during fetal life, preventing left-to-right flow. Patent throughout fetal life. Obliteration of Foramen Ovale Post-birth, the left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs, increasing pressure in this chamber greater than the right atrium, causing fusion of the septum primum and septum secundum, closing the foramen ovale. Annulus ovalis (adults) corresponds to the lower free edge of the septum secundum, while the fossa ovalis represents the septum primum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1080-1081; Solution Pages 1095-1097","sourcePage":1080,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"anatomy-26-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 5-year-old patient is diagnosed with an atrial septal defect (ASD) characterized by a large opening between the left and right atria. Which embryological defect is most commonly associated with this significant type of ASD?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ostium primum defect"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ostium secundum defect"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sinus venosus defect"},{"letter":"D","text":"Coronary Sinus Defect"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ostium secundum defect","explanation":"Ostium secundum defect is the most common significant ASD characterized by a large atrial opening. Defects in Interatrial Septal Development - Atrial Septal Defect (ASD): Varies from total absence of the atrial septum to small openings, such as probe patency of the oval foramen. Most significant defect: Ostium secundum defect , characterized by a large opening between the left and right atria. Causes: Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Varies from total absence of the atrial septum to small openings, such as probe patency of the oval foramen. - Most significant defect: Ostium secundum defect , characterized by a large opening between the left and right atria. - Causes: Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Varies from total absence of the atrial septum to small openings, such as probe patency of the oval foramen. - Most significant defect: Ostium secundum defect , characterized by a large opening between the left and right atria. - Causes: Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Excessive cell death and resorption of the septum primum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Inadequate development of the septum secundum. - Complete Absence of Atrial Septum: Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Complete Absence of Atrial Septum: Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. - Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Complete Absence of Atrial Septum: Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. - Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Known as common atrium or cor triloculare biventriculare. - Result from complete failure of the septum primum and septum secundum to form. - Premature Closure of Foramen Ovale: It can occur during prenatal life. Results in: Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. Often leads to death shortly after birth. - It can occur during prenatal life. - Results in: Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. - Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. - Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. - Often leads to death shortly after birth. - It can occur during prenatal life. - Results in: Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. - Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. - Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. - Often leads to death shortly after birth. - Massive hypertrophy of the right atrium and ventricle. - Underdevelopment of the left side of the heart. Ostium primum defect (Option A): Accounts for about 10% of septal defects and develops due to a deficiency in tissue near the atrioventricular valves, making it less common than ostium secundum. Sinus venosus defect (Option B) is located outside the fossa ovalis on the right septal surface. It is associated with anomalous right upper pulmonary venous return rather than a large atrial opening between the atria. Coronary Sinus Defect (Option C) is located in the wall separating the coronary sinus from the left atrium; it may be fenestrated or absent and does not create a significant opening between the left and right atria.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1081; Solution Pages 1097-1101","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"anatomy-26-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":7,"question":"During an echocardiogram, a raised ridge is observed around a depression on the interatrial septum. Which structure is most likely involved?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Septum primum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Septum secundum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fossa ovalis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Limbus fossa ovalis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Limbus fossa ovalis","explanation":"Limbus fossa ovalis is the raised ridge surrounding the fossa ovalis. Fossa Ovalis: (Option C) - Depressed area located in the lower portion of the right atrial side of the interatrial septum. - Remnant of the foramen ovale. - Septum primum which is on the left atrial side, forms the floor of the fossa ovalis. Limbus Fossa Ovalis: (Option D) - A raised ridge (annulus) that surrounds the perimeter of the fossa ovalis. - It is formed from the septum secundum, an infolding of the atrial roof. Septum primum (Option A) forms the floor of the fossa ovalis, not the raised ridge surrounding it. Septum secundum (Option B) contributes to the formation of the limbus fossa ovalis but does not directly appear as a raised ridge itself.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1081; Solution Pages 1101-1102","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1081},{"id":"anatomy-26-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":8,"question":"During the spiraling process of the septum, what anatomical change occurs in relation to the pulmonary trunk and aorta?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The pulmonary trunk moves posteriorly."},{"letter":"B","text":"The aorta moves to the right side of the body."},{"letter":"C","text":"The pulmonary trunk crosses anteriorly and to the left of the aorta."},{"letter":"D","text":"There is no change in the position of either vessel."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The pulmonary trunk crosses anteriorly and to the left of the aorta.","explanation":"- The spiraling of the aorticopulmonary septum positions the pulmonary trunk anteriorly and to the left of the aorta . - This arrangement is crucial for separating the pulmonary and systemic circulations. - The bulbus cordis elongates into three segments: Proximal part forms bulboventricular chamber Middle part: Conus cordis (outflow tract) Distal part (Truncus arteriosus) develops into the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk - Proximal part forms bulboventricular chamber - Middle part: Conus cordis (outflow tract) - Distal part (Truncus arteriosus) develops into the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk - Proximal part forms bulboventricular chamber - Middle part: Conus cordis (outflow tract) - Distal part (Truncus arteriosus) develops into the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk - Truncus arteriosus undergoes septation and spiraling. - There are two swellings along the truncus arteriosus- truncal swellings distally and conal swellings proximally. - The truncal swellings, resembling endocardial cushions, divide the lumen into the proximal ascending aorta and the pulmonary trunk. - The fusion of truncal and conal swellings establishes the pulmonary trunk’s origin from the right ventricle and the aorta’s origin from the left ventricle, with aortopulmonary valves forming at these fusion lines. - Spiraling of the septum allows the pulmonary trunk to cross anterior and to the left of the aorta . (Option C) - Failure of neural crest cells to migrate or disruptions to the aorticopulmonary septum leads to congenital heart disease Septation defects: Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Septation defects: Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form - Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Septation defects: Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form - Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Persistent truncus arteriosus: Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to form - Tetralogy of Fallot: Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Anterior and cephalad deviation of the aorticopulmonary septum - Comprises four defects linked to abnormalities: - Double outlet right ventricle - Spiraling defects: Transposition of the great vessels: Positions of the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. - Transposition of the great vessels: Positions of the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. - Transposition of the great vessels: Positions of the aorta and pulmonary artery are switched due to errors in truncus arteriosus division. The pulmonary trunk moves posteriorly (Option A): The pulmonary trunk moves anteriorly. The aorta moves to the right side of the body (Option B): The aorta does end up in a more rightward position relative to the pulmonary trunk, but it does not specifically move to the right side of the body. There is no change in the position of either vessel (Option D) : The spiraling process specifically reorganizes the positions of the pulmonary trunk and aorta.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1081-1082; Solution Pages 1102-1105","sourcePage":1081,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"anatomy-26-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A newborn presents with significant cyanosis and a single large vessel exiting the heart on imaging studies. The echocardiogram shows a defect in the interventricular septum.What embryological failure led to this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Failure of the atrioventricular septum to form."},{"letter":"B","text":"Failure of the conotruncal ridges to form."},{"letter":"C","text":"Failure of the aorticopulmonary septum to spiral."},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior displacement of the septum."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Failure of the conotruncal ridges to form.","explanation":"- In this scenario, the newborn exhibits significant cyanosis and a single large vessel which is suggestive of persistent truncus arteriosus . - It resulted from the failure of the conotruncal ridges to develop , which prevents the proper division of the outflow tracts into the aorta and pulmonary artery, leading to the mixing of oxygenated and deoxygenated blood. Aorticopulmonary septum anomalies: 1) Tetralogy of Fallot: - Most common conotruncal heart defect. - Cause: Due to unequal division of the conus and anterior displacement of the septum. (Option D) Features four cardiovascular issues: - Pulmonary infundibular stenosis - Overriding aorta - Ventricular septal defect (VSD) - Right ventricular hypertrophy - Tetralogy along with ASD is known as pentalogy of fallot . - Common in individuals with Alagille syndrome (also features liver abnormalities and distinctive facial traits). - Often associated with a JAG1 gene mutation affecting neural crest cells. 2) Persistent Truncus Arteriosus: - Cause: Failure of conotruncal ridges to form. (Option B) - Failure of conotruncal ridges to form. (Option B) - Failure of conotruncal ridges to form. (Option B) - Features: Leads to a single outflow tract and a defective interventricular septum. The truncus overrides both ventricles. Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood combine, with lung-bound blood entering systemic circulation.Results in congenital cyanotic heart disease. - Leads to a single outflow tract and a defective interventricular septum. - The truncus overrides both ventricles. - Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood combine, with lung-bound blood entering systemic circulation.Results in congenital cyanotic heart disease. - Leads to a single outflow tract and a defective interventricular septum. - The truncus overrides both ventricles. - Oxygenated and deoxygenated blood combine, with lung-bound blood entering systemic circulation.Results in congenital cyanotic heart disease. 3) Transposition of the Great Vessels: - Cause: Occurs when the conotruncal septum fails to spiral correctly . (Option C) - Occurs when the conotruncal septum fails to spiral correctly . (Option C) - Features:s Aorta connects to the right ventricle , pulmonary artery connects to the left ventricle . Patients cannot survive because deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium through the superior and inferior vena cavae, then flows to the right ventricle and into the ascending aorta, bypassing the lungs completely. PDA is essential for survival, allowing blood flow to the lungs for oxygenation. - Aorta connects to the right ventricle , pulmonary artery connects to the left ventricle . - Patients cannot survive because deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium through the superior and inferior vena cavae, then flows to the right ventricle and into the ascending aorta, bypassing the lungs completely. - PDA is essential for survival, allowing blood flow to the lungs for oxygenation. - Occurs when the conotruncal septum fails to spiral correctly . (Option C) - Aorta connects to the right ventricle , pulmonary artery connects to the left ventricle . - Patients cannot survive because deoxygenated blood from the body enters the right atrium through the superior and inferior vena cavae, then flows to the right ventricle and into the ascending aorta, bypassing the lungs completely. - PDA is essential for survival, allowing blood flow to the lungs for oxygenation. Failure of the atrioventricular septum to form (Option A) refers to atrioventricular septal defects, which involve issues in the separation of the atria and ventricles, not the outflow tracts.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1082; Solution Pages 1105-1108","sourcePage":1082,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"anatomy-26-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements about the development of the interventricular septum is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Muscular part grows from the floor of the bulboventricular cavity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bulbar part forms from the fusion of two bulbar ridges."},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior part of the membranous septum separates the right ventricle from the left atrium."},{"letter":"D","text":"Interventricular foramen forms above the muscular septum."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Posterior part of the membranous septum separates the right ventricle from the left atrium.","explanation":"the left atrium. The posterior part of the membranous septum separates the left ventricle from the right atrium , not the right ventricle from the left atrium. Formation of Interventricular Septum: The interventricular septum consists of three parts: - Muscular part - Bulbar part - Membranous part 1. Muscular Part: - Septum grows upward from the floor of the bulboventricular cavity, dividing it into right and left halves. (Option A) - This growth is driven by passive dilation and hemodynamic forces. - Septum fuses with the atrioventricular (AV) cushions (septum intermedium). - Interventricular foramen forms between the two ventricles at the upper margin of the septum and is eventually closed by the septum intermedium and proximal bulbar septum. (Option D) 2. Bulbar Part: - Two bulbar ridges (right and left) arise from the wall of the bulboventricular cavity. - These ridges fuse to form the proximal bulbar septum, which grows downward toward the muscular septum but leaves a gap. (Option B) 3. Membranous Part: - The gap between the muscular septum and the bulbar septum is filled by tissue from the AV cushions and bulbar ridges. - Membranous septum consists of: - Anterior part: Separates the right and left ventricles. - Posterior part: Separates the left ventricle from the right atrium. (Option C) Mechanism of Septal Formation: Interventricular septum is likely formed by the downward enlargement of the right and left ventricular cavities on either side of the septum, rather than by active septal growth itself.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1082; Solution Pages 1108-1109","sourcePage":1082,"sourcePageEnd":1082},{"id":"anatomy-26-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":11,"question":"In fetal circulation, which structure allows oxygenated blood to bypass the lungs and directly enter the systemic circulation, ensuring oxygen delivery to the fetal brain and upper body?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ductus venosus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ductus arteriosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Foramen ovale"},{"letter":"D","text":"Umbilical vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ductus arteriosus","explanation":"Ductus arteriosus allows most blood from the right ventricle to bypass the lungs by shunting it directly into the aorta, ensuring efficient delivery of oxygenated blood to the fetal brain and upper body. Fetal Circulation: Oxygenated Blood Flow: Placenta → Umbilical Vein → Ductus Venosus (bypasses liver) → Inferior Vena Cava → Right Atrium → Foramen Ovale → Left Atrium → Left Ventricle → Ascending Aorta → Oxygen to head, neck, and upper limbs Deoxygenated Blood Flow: Superior Vena Cava (SVC) → Right Atrium → Right Ventricle → Pulmonary Trunk → Ductus Arteriosus (bypass pulmonary circulation) → Descending Aorta → Lower body → Umbilical Arteries → Placenta (for oxygenation) Peculiarities of Fetal Circulation: - Ductus Venosus: Directs blood to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the liver and preserving oxygen content. (Option A) - Foramen Ovale: Allows equal distribution of blood to both sides of the heart, ensuring more oxygenated blood reaches vital organs in the upper body. (Option C) - Ductus Arteriosus: Directs blood from the pulmonary trunk to the aorta, bypassing the lungs and allowing blood to flow to the placenta for oxygenation. (Option B) - More oxygenated blood flows to the upper limbs, resulting in longer upper limbs compared to lower limbs in the fetus. Umbilical vein (Option D) carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus, but it doesn’t play a role in shunting blood past the lungs. - Its function is to deliver oxygenated blood to the fetal circulation through the ductus venosus.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1082-1083; Solution Pages 1109-1111","sourcePage":1082,"sourcePageEnd":1083},{"id":"anatomy-26-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-26","chapter":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topic":"Lesson 26 - Development of the Cardiovascular System and Fetal Circulation","topicIndex":26,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 3-day-old neonate presents with cyanosis, rapid breathing, and a machinery-like murmur on auscultation. Echocardiography reveals a left-to-right shunt at the level of the great vessels. Which of the following fetal circulatory changes is most likely responsible for this presentation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Persistent foramen ovale"},{"letter":"B","text":"Patent ductus arteriosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Closure of ductus venosus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Malrotation of the great arteries"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Closure of ductus venosus","explanation":". - Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) results from the ductus arteriosus failing to close after birth, creating a left-to-right shunt that causes oxygenated blood from the aorta to flow into the pulmonary artery. - This leads to excessive pulmonary blood flow, resulting in respiratory distress, cyanosis, and a machinery-like murmur, often worsening despite oxygen administration. Changes in the Circulation at Birth: Umbilical Arteries Thick muscle walls of the umbilical arteries contract immediately after birth, occluding their lumen. This prevents the loss of fetal blood into the placenta. Umbilical Vein and Ductus Venosus Lumen of the umbilical vein and ductus venosus occludes a few minutes after birth, allowing time for fetal blood from the placenta to return to the fetus. Ductus Arteriosus Closes initially due to muscle contraction in the vessel wall, and later, intimal proliferation permanently obliterates the lumen, redirecting blood from the right ventricle to the lungs for oxygenation. Pulmonary Vessels Increase in size, allowing more blood to reach the left atrium from the lungs, increasing left atrial pressure. Foramen Ovale Increased pressure in the left atrium (due to lung circulation) and reduced pressure in the right atrium (due to cessation of placental blood flow) lead to the closure of the valve of the foramen ovale. Fetal Circulatory Shunts and Their Changes After Birth Fetal Structure Function During Fetal Life Remnant Ductus arteriosus Shunts blood from the pulmonary Ligamentum arteriosum artery to the aorta, bypassing pulmonary circulation. Foramen ovale Shunts blood from the right atrium to Fossa ovalis the left atrium Ductus venosus Shunts blood from the umbilical vein Ligamentum venosum to the inferior vena cava, bypassing the liver Umbilical vein Carries oxygenated blood from the Ligamentum teres hepatis placenta to the fetus Umbilical arteries Carries deoxygenated blood from the Proximal part: Superior vesical artery fetus to the placenta Distal part: Medial umbilical ligaments Persistent foramen ovale (Option A) allows the shunting of blood from the right atrium to the left atrium, bypassing the lungs. - While it can cause cyanosis, the clinical presentation usually doesn't include a machinery murmur, and it is typically asymptomatic or mild compared to PDA. Closure of ductus venosus (Option C) occurs normally after birth and redirects blood flow, but it does not cause shunting or significant symptoms like cyanosis or a murmur. Malrotation of the great arteries (Option D) is a form of congenital heart defect that can cause cyanosis, but it would typically be detected earlier, often with profound cyanosis from birth. - Unlike PDA, it does not present with a machinery-like murmur and does not involve a left-to-right shunt.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1083; Solution Pages 1111-1113","sourcePage":1083,"sourcePageEnd":1083},{"id":"anatomy-27-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":1,"question":"In the given CT slice, A and B respectively represent? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Main bronchus, Ascending Aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Main bronchus, Descending Aorta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ascending Aorta, Descending Aorta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Descending Aorta, Ascending Aorta"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ascending Aorta, Descending Aorta","explanation":"The CT slice is at the level of T5, in which A represents the ascending aorta & B represents descending aorta .","sourceRef":"Question Page 1114; Solution Page 1129","sourcePage":1114,"sourcePageEnd":1114},{"id":"anatomy-27-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":2,"question":"The pleural cupola is located:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Above the diaphragm, covering the lower lung"},{"letter":"B","text":"Over the apex of the lung, reaching 2 to 3 cm above the clavicle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Near the midline of the chest at the level of the heart"},{"letter":"D","text":"At the base of the lungs, covered by the suprapleural membrane"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Over the apex of the lung, reaching 2 to 3 cm above the clavicle","explanation":"Cervical Pleura - Extends from the inner border of the 1st rib. - Covers the apex of the lung. - Passes downwards and medially to continue with the mediastinal pleura. - Forms a cup-like dome over the apex of the lung. - The summit of the dome reaches 2 to 3 cm higher than the medial third of the clavicle, at the level of the neck of the first rib. - Covers the cervical pleura externally. - A musculo-fascial expansion derived from the scalenus minimus muscle and endothoracic fascia. - Extends from the tip of the transverse process of the 7th cervical vertebra to the inner border of the 1st rib. - Medially continuous with the pretracheal fascia by the side of the trachea. - All structures related to the apex of the lung are superficial to the suprapleural membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1114-1115; Solution Pages 1129-1131","sourcePage":1114,"sourcePageEnd":1115},{"id":"anatomy-27-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Where is pain from the mediastinal pleura typically referred?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In the lower abdomen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Over the ipsilateral shoulder"},{"letter":"C","text":"In the lumbar region"},{"letter":"D","text":"In the lower back near the kidneys"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Over the ipsilateral shoulder","explanation":"Pleura Structure and Function - Visceral Pleura: Surrounds lung tissue. Composed of a single mesothelial cell layer. Blood supply from bronchial arteries. - Surrounds lung tissue. - Composed of a single mesothelial cell layer. - Blood supply from bronchial arteries. - Parietal Pleura: Lines the inner chest wall. Contains stomata that drain pleural fluid into lymphatic capillaries. Connective tissue present; innervated by sensory nerve fibers. - Lines the inner chest wall. - Contains stomata that drain pleural fluid into lymphatic capillaries. - Connective tissue present; innervated by sensory nerve fibers. - Surrounds lung tissue. - Composed of a single mesothelial cell layer. - Blood supply from bronchial arteries. - Lines the inner chest wall. - Contains stomata that drain pleural fluid into lymphatic capillaries. - Connective tissue present; innervated by sensory nerve fibers. Pleural Fluid - Normal Volume: 0.1 to 0.2 mL/kg. - Inflow Rate: 0.5 mL/hr in adults. - Maximal Removal Rate: 0.28 mL/kg/hr. - Accumulates when inflow exceeds removal (due to increased plasma filtration or impaired lymphatic drainage). Lymphatic Drainage - Lymph drains into regional lymph nodes along the sternum or vertebra, then into the thoracic and right lymphatic ducts. - Lymphatic vessels are 10-12 µm in diameter, large enough for intact erythrocytes. Innervation - Visceral Pleura: No connective tissue; innervated by the vagus nerve, insensitive to pain. - Parietal Pleura: Connective tissue; innervated by intercostal nerves and phrenic nerve. Phrenic Nerve: Predominantly innervates the central diaphragmatic region, referred pain to the ipsilateral shoulder. - Phrenic Nerve: Predominantly innervates the central diaphragmatic region, referred pain to the ipsilateral shoulder. - Phrenic Nerve: Predominantly innervates the central diaphragmatic region, referred pain to the ipsilateral shoulder. Pleural Membrane Function - Allows frictionless movement of the lungs relative to the chest wall during respiration. - Inspiration: Negative pressure in the pleural space leads to lung expansion, allowing air to enter the lungs","sourceRef":"Question Page 1115; Solution Pages 1131-1132","sourcePage":1115,"sourcePageEnd":1115},{"id":"anatomy-27-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the cardiac plexuses?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The superficial cardiac plexus is located behind the arch of the aorta, in front of the bifurcation of the trachea."},{"letter":"B","text":"The deep cardiac plexus receives cardiac branches from the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves, except the inferior cervical cardiac branch of the left vagus nerve."},{"letter":"C","text":"The superficial cardiac plexus distributes to the left coronary artery and right anterior pulmonary plexus."},{"letter":"D","text":"The deep cardiac plexus is formed only by the superior cervical cardiac branch of the left sympathetic chain."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The deep cardiac plexus receives cardiac branches from the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves, except the inferior cervical cardiac branch of the left vagus nerve.","explanation":"recurrent laryngeal nerves, except the inferior cervical cardiac branch of the left vagus nerve. Cardiac ganglion of Wrisberg, also called the juxta ductal body or the aortico-pulmonary ganglionic mass. Cardiac Plexuses Superficial Cardiac Plexus: - Location: Below the arch of the aorta, in front of the bifurcation of the pulmonary trunk, to the right of the ligamentum arteriosum (Option A) - Cardiac Ganglion of Wrisberg : Located close to the ligamentum arteriosum. - Formation: (a) Superior cervical cardiac branch of the left cervical sympathetic trunk. (b) Inferior cervical cardiac branch of the left vagus nerve. - Distribution: (Option C) (a) Deep cardiac plexus. (b) Right coronary artery. (c) Left anterior pulmonary plexus. Deep Cardiac Plexus: - Location: In front of the bifurcation of the trachea, behind the arch of the aorta. - Formation: (a) Cardiac branches from three cervical and upper 4-5 thoracic ganglia of the sympathetic chains (except the superior cervical cardiac branch of the left sympathetic chain) (Option D) (b) Cardiac branches from the vagus and recurrent laryngeal nerves (except the inferior cervical cardiac branch of the left vagus nerve) (Option B) - Distribution: Right and left halves of the plexus: (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. (b) Pulmonary plexus. (c) Separate branches to the atria. - Right and left halves of the plexus: (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. (b) Pulmonary plexus. (c) Separate branches to the atria. - (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. - (b) Pulmonary plexus. - (c) Separate branches to the atria. - Right and left halves of the plexus: (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. (b) Pulmonary plexus. (c) Separate branches to the atria. - (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. - (b) Pulmonary plexus. - (c) Separate branches to the atria. - (a) Corresponding coronary arteries. - (b) Pulmonary plexus. - (c) Separate branches to the atria.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1115; Solution Pages 1132-1134","sourcePage":1115,"sourcePageEnd":1115},{"id":"anatomy-27-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":5,"question":"An axial CT scan of the thorax shows a slice where the aortic arch is visible, along with the trachea bifurcating into the main bronchi. At which anatomical level is this axial section most likely taken? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T1-T2"},{"letter":"B","text":"T3-T4"},{"letter":"C","text":"T5-T6"},{"letter":"D","text":"T7-T8"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. T3-T4","explanation":"At this level, the aortic arch is usually seen, and the trachea begins to bifurcate into the right and left main bronchi. - Level of the Thoracic Inlet : This is at the top of the thoracic cavity, where the trachea and esophagus enter. - Level of the Aortic Arch : Located around the T4-T5 vertebral level, where the aorta arches over the left main bronchus. - Level of the Carina : At the T5-T6 level, this is where the trachea bifurcates into the right and left main bronchi. - Level of the Heart : The heart is typically seen from T6 to T9 . You can identify the chambers (right atrium, left atrium, ventricles) and major vessels (aorta, pulmonary arteries). - Level of the Diaphragm : The diaphragm typically corresponds to the T10 vertebral level, which can help in distinguishing between thoracic and abdominal structures. - Level of the Esophagus : The esophagus runs along the midline and can be seen at various levels, usually starting from the cervical region down to T10. - Level of the Thoracic Spine : The thoracic vertebrae can be used as references; typically, there are 12 thoracic vertebrae. T1-T2 (Option A): This level generally shows the upper mediastinum, including parts of the great vessels, but the aortic arch is not typically prominent. T5-T6 (Option C): Here, the main bronchi are visible, but the aortic arch may not be well-defined. T7-T8 (Option D): This level typically shows the lower mediastinum and may not include the aortic arch or the bifurcation of the trachea.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1115-1116; Solution Pages 1134-1135","sourcePage":1115,"sourcePageEnd":1116},{"id":"anatomy-27-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following structures is commonly associated with a notable impression on the left lung?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oesophagus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Azygos vein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Aorta","explanation":"- The left lung shows an impression from the aortic arch, which arches over the left main bronchus. - Lung impressions refer to the indentations or impressions made on the surface of the lungs by adjacent structures in the thoracic cavity. Right lung impressions Left lung impressions Cardiac Impression (Right Atrium & Auricle) Superior Cardiac Impression (Left Ventricle, Auricle, part of Right Vena Cava (SVC) Impression (Option B) Right Ventricle Ventricle) Pulmonary Trunk Impression Arch of Aorta (Small part) Right Brachiocephalic Vein (Lower part) Impression Descending Thoracic Aorta Impression Left Azygos Vein Impression (Option D) Esophageal Subclavian Artery Impression Thoracic Duct Impression Impression (Option C) Inferior Vena Cava (IVC) Esophageal Impression Left Brachiocephalic Vein Impression Tracheal Impression Right Vagus Nerve Impression Left Vagus Nerve Impression Left Phrenic Impression Right Phrenic Nerve Impression Nerve Impression Left Recurrent Laryngeal Nerve Impression","sourceRef":"Question Page 1116; Solution Pages 1135-1136","sourcePage":1116,"sourcePageEnd":1116},{"id":"anatomy-27-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 60-year-old patient presents with a history of progressive shortness of breath and swelling in the neck. Imaging reveals distended chest wall veins and a mediastinal mass with known metastasis. Which of the following is the most likely cause of these findings? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right heart failure"},{"letter":"B","text":") Superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary embolism"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thoracic aortic aneurysm"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. ) Superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction","explanation":"The presence of distended chest wall veins (collaterals) is highly indicative of increased venous pressure, commonly associated with superior vena cava (SVC) obstruction. - In this case, the history of a mediastinal mass and metastasis suggests that the mass is compressing the SVC, leading to venous obstruction. - This obstruction can cause the characteristic collateral circulation (distended chest wall veins) to develop, as the body attempts to reroute blood flow to bypass the obstruction. Right heart failure (Option A) : While this can cause jugular distension, it typically does not lead to the development of prominent chest wall collaterals without other signs of systemic congestion. Pulmonary embolism (Option C) : This may cause acute respiratory distress but does not typically lead to distended chest wall veins or collaterals. Thoracic aortic aneurysm (Option D) : This may cause symptoms related to compression of surrounding structures but does not specifically account for the venous distension seen with SVC obstruction.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1116-1117; Solution Pages 1136-1137","sourcePage":1116,"sourcePageEnd":1117},{"id":"anatomy-27-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 56-year-old male patient with a history of gastric carcinoma now presents with sharp or pleuritic pain that worsens with deep breaths or coughing. Pleural effusion is suspected and a pleural tap was done which showed the fluid shown in the image. Based on the knowledge of the course of thoracic duct, where does the thoracic duct drain lymph? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Into the right brachiocephalic vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Into the junction of the left subclavian vein and left internal jugular vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Directly into the inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"D","text":"Into the right lymphatic duct"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Into the junction of the left subclavian vein and left internal jugular vein","explanation":"If the pleural effusion fluid is chylous (chyle), it indicates leakage of lymphatic fluid, often due to disruption or injury to the thoracic duct. - Chylothorax is a condition where chyle accumulates in the pleural space, leading to pleural effusion. The thoracic duct drains lymph into the junction of the left subclavian vein and left internal jugular vein. Thoracic duct - The thoracic duct starts as the cisterna chyli in the abdomen at the lower border of the T12 vertebra. - It ascends through the aortic opening of the diaphragm. - It initially runs on the right side of the descending aorta in the posterior mediastinum. - At the T4 vertebra, it crosses from the right to the left side behind the esophagus. - It continues upward along the left border of the esophagus to the root of the neck. - At the root of the neck, it arches laterally at the C7 vertebra, in front of the vertebral arteries and behind the carotid system. - The duct collects lymph from the left jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks . - It descends in front of the first part of the left subclavian artery. - It ends by draining into the junction of the left subclavian vein and the left internal jugular vein. (Option A, C, & D ruled out) - The thoracic duct carries lymph from the lower limbs, pelvic cavity, abdominal cavity, and the left side of the thorax, head, neck, and arm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1117-1118; Solution Pages 1137-1138","sourcePage":1117,"sourcePageEnd":1118},{"id":"anatomy-27-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which part of the body is not drained by the right lymphatic duct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right side of the head and neck"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right upper limb"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right side of the thorax"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right abdomen"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Right abdomen","explanation":"The right lymphatic duct does not drain the right abdomen; this region is typically drained by the thoracic duct, which handles lymph from the lower half of the body, including the abdomen. Right Lymphatic Duct - The right lymphatic duct may form from the convergence of the right jugular, subclavian, and bronchomediastinal trunks. - These trunks drain lymph from the right side of the head and neck, the right upper limb, and the right side of the thorax. - When present, the right lymphatic duct is approximately 0.5 inches (1.3 cm) long. - It opens into the beginning of the right brachiocephalic vein. - Alternatively, the lymphatic trunks may drain directly into the major veins at the root of the neck.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1118; Solution Pages 1138-1139","sourcePage":1118,"sourcePageEnd":1118},{"id":"anatomy-27-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the azygos and hemiazygos veins?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The azygos vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava."},{"letter":"B","text":"The inferior hemiazygos vein drains into the superior vena cava."},{"letter":"C","text":"The superior hemiazygos vein drains into the azygos vein."},{"letter":"D","text":"In an obstruction of the SVC, the superior hemiazygos vein acts as the primary collateral pathway."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The superior hemiazygos vein drains into the azygos vein.","explanation":"The superior hemiazygos vein drains into the azygos vein at the level of the seventh thoracic vertebra. Azygos Vein - The origin of the azygos vein can vary, often starting from the union of the right ascending lumbar vein and the right subcostal vein in the abdomen. - It ascends through the aortic opening in the diaphragm, on the right side of the aorta. - The vein continues to rise until it reaches the level of the fifth thoracic vertebra. - At this level, the azygos vein arches forward above the root of the right lung. - It drains into the posterior surface of the superior vena cava. (Option A ruled out) - Tributaries: The eight lower right intercostal veins The right superior intercostal vein The superior and inferior hemiazygos veins Mediastinal veins. Pericardial veins Right subcostal vein. Right bronchial vein. Right ascending lumbar vein. - The eight lower right intercostal veins - The right superior intercostal vein - The superior and inferior hemiazygos veins - Mediastinal veins. - Pericardial veins - Right subcostal vein. - Right bronchial vein. - Right ascending lumbar vein. - If there is an obstruction of the SVC, the azygos vein acts as the primary collateral pathway to redirect blood from the upper half of the body to the IVC. (Option D ruled out) - The eight lower right intercostal veins - The right superior intercostal vein - The superior and inferior hemiazygos veins - Mediastinal veins. - Pericardial veins - Right subcostal vein. - Right bronchial vein. - Right ascending lumbar vein. Inferior Hemiazygos Vein - The inferior hemiazygos vein is formed by the union of the left ascending lumbar vein and the left subcostal vein in the abdomen. - It ascends through the left crus of the diaphragm and, around the level of the eighth thoracic vertebra, it turns to the right to join the azygos vein, receiving tributaries from the lower left intercostal and mediastinal veins. (Option B ruled out) Superior Hemiazygos Vein - The superior hemiazygos vein is created by the union of the left superior intercostal vein and the fourth to eighth intercostal veins. - It drains into the azygos vein at the level of the seventh thoracic vertebra.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1118-1119; Solution Pages 1139-1141","sourcePage":1118,"sourcePageEnd":1119},{"id":"anatomy-27-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following statements about the phrenic nerve is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The phrenic nerve contains both motor and sensory fibers."},{"letter":"B","text":"Efferent fibers of the phrenic nerve supply the diaphragm muscle."},{"letter":"C","text":"Afferent fibers transmit sensory information from the liver capsule."},{"letter":"D","text":"Paralysis of the phrenic nerve can result from pressure from malignant tumors in the mediastinum."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Afferent fibers transmit sensory information from the liver capsule.","explanation":"Afferent fibers transmit sensory information from both the upper and lower surfaces of the diaphragm, as well as from the pericardium and mediastinal pleura and not from the liver capsule. Phrenic Nerve - The phrenic nerves contain both efferent (motor) and afferent (sensory) fibers . (Option A ruled out) - The efferent fibers provide the sole motor supply to the diaphragm muscle. (Option B ruled out) - The afferent fibers transmit sensory information to the central nervous system from the peritoneum covering the diaphragm's undersurface, the pleura on the diaphragm's upper surface, and the pericardium and mediastinal parietal pleura. - Paralysis of the phrenic nerve may result from pressure from malignant tumors in the mediastinum. (Option D ruled out) - Historically, surgical methods like crushing or sectioning the nerve in the neck were used to treat lung tuberculosis affecting the lower lobes, leading to diaphragm paralysis on that side. This causes the elevated, immobile dome of the diaphragm to provide rest to the affected lung.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1119; Solution Pages 1141-1142","sourcePage":1119,"sourcePageEnd":1119},{"id":"anatomy-27-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes a difference between the right and left phrenic nerves?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The left phrenic nerve is shorter than the right"},{"letter":"B","text":"The right phrenic nerve passes anterior to the aortic arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"The left phrenic nerve passes through the caval foramen of the diaphragm"},{"letter":"D","text":"The right phrenic nerve has a more vertical course in the thorax"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The right phrenic nerve has a more vertical course in the thorax","explanation":"The right phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5) has a more vertical and shorter course through the thorax compared to the left phrenic nerve. Feature Right Phrenic Nerve Left Phrenic Nerve Length and Course Shorter and more vertical Longer and less vertical Relation to Subclavian Artery Separated from second part by Passes anterior to first part scalenus anterior Relation to Major Vessels Passes lateral to superior vena cava Passes anterior to aortic arch Relation to Heart Passes lateral to right atrium Passes over left atrium and ventricle Diaphragm Entry Through central tendon (caval Through muscular part, anterior to foramen or just lateral) central tendon Associated Ganglion Right phrenic ganglion, sometimes Left phrenic ganglion (less common) with accessory ganglion","sourceRef":"Question Page 1119; Solution Pages 1142-1143","sourcePage":1119,"sourcePageEnd":1119},{"id":"anatomy-27-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Regarding intercostal nerve blocks, which of the following statements is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The intercostal neurovascular bundle is arranged inferior to superior as vein, artery, nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"The lateral cutaneous branch of the intercostal nerve arises anterior to the midaxillary line"},{"letter":"C","text":"The innermost intercostal muscle separates the intercostal nerve from the pleura"},{"letter":"D","text":"The angle of the rib, approximately 6 to 8 cm from the midline, is used as a landmark for needle insertion"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The intercostal neurovascular bundle is arranged inferior to superior as vein, artery, nerve","explanation":"artery, nerve The intercostal neurovascular bundle follows a \"VAN\" arrangement from superior to inferior: Vein, Artery, Nerve. Intercostal nerve blocks Anatomical Feature Description Neurovascular Bundle Arrangement Superior to inferior: Vein, Artery, Nerve (VAN) Intercostal Nerve Location Inferior border of the rib, in the costal groove Separated from pleura by Innermost intercostal muscle between nerve and pleura (Option C) Landmark for Needle Insertion Angle of the rib, 6-8 cm from midline (Option D) Lateral Cutaneous Branch Arises anterior to the midaxillary line (Option B) Complications Pneumothorax (rare, 0.07% incidence), temporary intercostal muscle paralysis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1119-1120; Solution Page 1143","sourcePage":1119,"sourcePageEnd":1120},{"id":"anatomy-27-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":14,"question":"During a pleural tap procedure, which of the following structures is encountered immediately before reaching the parietal pleura?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal intercostal muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endothoracic fascia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Serratus anterior muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Innermost intercostal muscle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Endothoracic fascia","explanation":"- The endothoracic fascia is the layer encountered immediately before the parietal pleura during a pleural tap procedure. - The endothoracic fascia lines the innermost intercostal muscle from inside and is fused with the parietal pleura , making it the last layer encountered before reaching the pleural space where fluid can be aspirated - The order of structures punctured during a pleural tap, from superficial to deep: Skin Superficial fascia Serratus anterior (1st muscle to get punctured) External intercostal muscle Internal intercostal muscle Innermost intercostal muscle Endothoracic fascia (Fused with parietal pleura) Parietal pleura Pleural fluid (to be aspirated) - Skin - Superficial fascia - Serratus anterior (1st muscle to get punctured) - External intercostal muscle - Internal intercostal muscle - Innermost intercostal muscle - Endothoracic fascia (Fused with parietal pleura) - Parietal pleura - Pleural fluid (to be aspirated)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1120; Solution Pages 1143-1145","sourcePage":1120,"sourcePageEnd":1120},{"id":"anatomy-27-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 65-year-old man with a history of congestive heart failure presents with increasing dyspnea. Chest X-ray is given below. The physician decides to perform a thoracentesis. Which of the following anatomical landmarks is most important to be identified, to prevent damage to important structures at its level? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Midclavicular line"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior axillary line"},{"letter":"C","text":"Midscapular line"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior border of the rib"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Inferior border of the rib","explanation":"The Chest X-ray shows a right sided pleural effusion for which the physician decides to perform thoracocentesis. - The inferior border of the rib is the most important anatomical landmark to identify when performing a thoracentesis. - This is because the intercostal neurovascular bundle (including the intercostal artery, vein, and nerve) runs along the inferior border of each rib. - By inserting the needle just above the superior border of the rib below the intended intercostal space, the risk of damaging these structures is minimized. Pleural Tap : Indications Used for both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes in cases of pleural effusion. The fluid can be analyzed for various factors including culture, cytology, and biochemical analysis. Empyema thoracis for diagnostic purpose Position: The patient should be in a sitting position, leaning forward over a support. Site The procedure is performed at the 7th or 8th intercostal space, posteriorly, at the tip of the scapula. Needle insertion should target the upper edge/superior border of the rib in order to avoid injury to the neurovascular bundle which runs at the inferior margin of each rib. Needle A wide-bore needle (Abraham needle) is used under local anesthesia. Complications Infection Dry tap or bloody tap Sudden vagal shock Pain and respiratory distress","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1120-1121; Solution Pages 1145-1146","sourcePage":1120,"sourcePageEnd":1121},{"id":"anatomy-27-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following structures does not make up the anterior surface (sternocostal) of the heart?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left atrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right ventricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left ventricle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left auricle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Left atrium","explanation":"The left atrium is obscured by the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk, and primarily forms part of the posterior surface, not the anterior surface. Anterior surface of heart (Sternocostal) Posterior View of Heart (Base) {{caption_text}} {{caption_text}} The anterior surface consists mainly of the right atrium The base of the heart is mainly formed by the left atrium and right ventricle separated by the anterior (two-thirds) and right atrium (one-third). Major blood atrioventricular groove ( coronary sulcus ). (Option B) The vessels like superior vena cava, ascending aorta, and left auricle and left ventricle also contribute. (Option C & pulmonary trunk attach to the base. It is directed D) The right ventricle is separated from the left ventricle backwards and to the right, opposite the apex. The base by the anterior interventricular groove. The left atrium is lies opposite the apex and in front of thoracic vertebrae obscured by the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk. T5–T8 when lying down, descending to T6–T9 when The heart creates a cardiac notch on the mediastinal standing. It is separated from the vertebral column by the surface and anterior border of the left lung causing oblique pericardial sinus, esophagus, and aorta. superficial cardiac dullness","sourceRef":"Question Page 1121; Solution Pages 1146-1147","sourcePage":1121,"sourcePageEnd":1121},{"id":"anatomy-27-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 65-year-old male presents with shortness of breath and chest pain. On examination, he has a prominent sternal angle. A CT scan reveals an enlarged mediastinal lymph node at the level of the tracheal bifurcation. Which of the following anatomical structures is most likely to be found at the same level as the bifurcation of the trachea?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left atrium of the heart"},{"letter":"B","text":"Junction of the azygos vein and superior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Origin of the thoracic duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior border of the aortic arch"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Junction of the azygos vein and superior vena cava","explanation":"- The sternal angle ( angle of Louis) is a palpable, transverse ridge on the sternum located approximately 5 cm below the suprasternal notch. - It serves as a critical anatomical landmark for various significant structures and anatomical events. These include: The second costal cartilage articulates with the sternum at this level on both sides, making it a key reference point for counting ribs. It corresponds to the level of the intervertebral disc between the T4 and T5 vertebrae . A horizontal plane passing through this level divides the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum . The arch of the aorta both begins and ends at this level. The trachea bifurcates into the right and left principal bronchus at this level. The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries here. The upper border of the heart is located at this level. The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung and empties into the superior vena cava at this level. (Option B) - The second costal cartilage articulates with the sternum at this level on both sides, making it a key reference point for counting ribs. - It corresponds to the level of the intervertebral disc between the T4 and T5 vertebrae . A horizontal plane passing through this level divides the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum . - The arch of the aorta both begins and ends at this level. - The trachea bifurcates into the right and left principal bronchus at this level. - The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries here. - The upper border of the heart is located at this level. - The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung and empties into the superior vena cava at this level. (Option B) - The second costal cartilage articulates with the sternum at this level on both sides, making it a key reference point for counting ribs. - It corresponds to the level of the intervertebral disc between the T4 and T5 vertebrae . A horizontal plane passing through this level divides the superior mediastinum from the inferior mediastinum . - The arch of the aorta both begins and ends at this level. - The trachea bifurcates into the right and left principal bronchus at this level. - The pulmonary trunk divides into the right and left pulmonary arteries here. - The upper border of the heart is located at this level. - The azygos vein arches over the root of the right lung and empties into the superior vena cava at this level. (Option B) Left atrium of the heart (Option A) lies posteriorly and is located lower than the sternal angle, around the T6-T7 vertebral level. Origin of the thoracic duct (Option C) : is in the abdomen at the cisterna chyli , at the level of the L1-L2 vertebrae, far below the level of the sternal angle. Superior border of the aortic arch (Option D) : is located slightly above the level of the sternal angle, but its lower border (or the beginning and end of the arch) aligns with the T4-T5 level.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1121; Solution Pages 1147-1149","sourcePage":1121,"sourcePageEnd":1121},{"id":"anatomy-27-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":18,"question":"All the following are contents of the posterior mediastinum except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Descending thoracic aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Esophagus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Azygos and hemiazygos veins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulmonary trunk"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pulmonary trunk","explanation":"Pulmonary trunk is the content of middle mediastinum. Mediastinum: The mediastinum is an anatomical region between the two lungs in the thoracic cavity. Boundaries: - Extends vertically from the thoracic inlet (superior thoracic aperture) to the diaphragm. - Anterior - Sternum - Posterior - Thoracic vertebral column The posterior boundary of the mediastinum is longer than the anterior because of the oblique alignment of the superior thoracic aperture and the posteroinferior curvature of the diaphragm. - The posterior boundary of the mediastinum is longer than the anterior because of the oblique alignment of the superior thoracic aperture and the posteroinferior curvature of the diaphragm. - Laterally - mediastinal pleura, which are reflections of the parietal pleura. - It is divided into superior and inferior mediastinum by the sternal plane which passes horizontally from the manubriosternal joint to T4 to T5 vertebra. - The posterior boundary of the mediastinum is longer than the anterior because of the oblique alignment of the superior thoracic aperture and the posteroinferior curvature of the diaphragm. Anatomical Divisions: 1. Superior Mediastinum: - Located above the sternal angle (manubriosternal joint) - Contains critical structures such as: Trachea and initial part of the bronchi Esophagus Great vessels: aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and subclavian arteries Thymus gland (or its remnants in adults) Various nerves: vagus, phrenic, and cardiac nerves, left RLN Lymph nodes Thoracic duct - Trachea and initial part of the bronchi - Esophagus - Great vessels: aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and subclavian arteries - Thymus gland (or its remnants in adults) - Various nerves: vagus, phrenic, and cardiac nerves, left RLN - Lymph nodes - Thoracic duct - Trachea and initial part of the bronchi - Esophagus - Great vessels: aortic arch, brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid and subclavian arteries - Thymus gland (or its remnants in adults) - Various nerves: vagus, phrenic, and cardiac nerves, left RLN - Lymph nodes - Thoracic duct 2. Inferior Mediastinum: Located below the sternal angle and is further divided into anterior, middle, and posterior mediastinum. Anterior Mediastinum Middle Mediastinum Posterior Mediastinum Lies between the sternum and the The broadest part of the inferior Extends from the posterior pericardium Contains loose mediastinum Contains the heart pericardium to the vertebral column connective tissue, lymph nodes, and enclosed in the pericardial sac Contains important structures such remnants of the thymus, sterno Contains the roots of great vessels: as: (Option A, B, C) Descending -pericardial ligaments. Provides ascending aorta, pulmonary trunk, thoracic aorta Esophagus Azygos surgical access to the heart and and pulmonary veins, tracheal and hemiazygos veins Thoracic duct great vessels bifurcation and bronchi Includes the Sympathetic trunks and splanchnic phrenic nerves and cardiac plexus nerves Lymph nodes","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1121-1122; Solution Pages 1149-1151","sourcePage":1121,"sourcePageEnd":1122},{"id":"anatomy-27-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 55-year-old man presents with exertional chest pain radiating to his left arm. Coronary angiography reveals a significant blockage in the right coronary artery (RCA). Which of the following regions of the heart is most likely at risk of ischemia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral wall of the left ventricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior one-third of the interventricular septum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Apex of the heart"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Posterior one-third of the interventricular septum","explanation":"- The coronary arteries supply blood to specific regions of the heart. - The right coronary artery (RCA) typically supplies the right atrium , right ventricle , part of the left ventricle , and the posterior one-third of the interventricular septum in individuals with right-dominant coronary circulation. - The posterior descending artery (PDA) branches from the RCA in the right-dominant hearts and supplies the posterior part of the heart. - Coronary artery disease is a major cause of death worldwide. - It is characterized by a reduced blood flow to the heart muscle (myocardium), depriving it of necessary oxygen. - When a major coronary artery is suddenly blocked by an embolus (a plug or clot), the area of the heart muscle that relies on that artery for blood supply becomes ischemic. - This lack of blood flow results in the death of the affected myocardial tissue due to necrosis. The 3 most common sites of coronary artery occlusion are: 1. Anterior IV (LAD) branch of the LCA (40–50%). 2. RCA (30–40%). 3. Circumflex branch of the LCA (15–20%). Coronary Artery Lesions Infarct Location Proximal LAD Large anterior wall More Distal LAD LAD (Wraparound) Anteroapical Inferior wall Distal LAD Anteroseptal Early Obtuse, Marginal High lateral wall More Distal Marginal Branch, Circumflex Small lateral wall Circumflex Posterolateral Distal RCA Small inferior wall Proximal RCA Large inferior and posterior wall RCA Right ventricular, usually inferior The anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum and the apex of the heart (Options A and D) are supplied by the left anterior descending (LAD) artery, which is a branch of the left coronary artery (LCA). - These regions would be affected by a blockage in the LAD, not the RCA. The lateral wall of the left ventricle (Option B) is primarily supplied by the left circumflex (LCX) artery, a branch of the left coronary artery (LCA), and not RCA.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1122; Solution Pages 1151-1152","sourcePage":1122,"sourcePageEnd":1122},{"id":"anatomy-27-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":20,"question":"A 50-year-old male presents with inferior wall MI. Angiography shows occlusion in the artery supplying the SA node. Which of the following arteries is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right coronary artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left coronary artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Circumflex artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior interventricular artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Right coronary artery","explanation":"- The right coronary artery (RCA) supplies the SA node in approximately 60-70% of individuals. - Occlusion of the RCA can lead to ischemia in the SA node, resulting in arrhythmias and conduction disturbances. Blood Supply to the Conducting system of the heart: Right Coronary Artery (RCA) supplies - SA Node (60%) - AV Node (80%) Left Coronary Artery (LCA) supplies - SA Node (40%) - AV Bundle of His - Right bundle and left bundle branches - The moderator band was supplied by the interventricular septal branches of the anterior interventricular branch from the left coronary artery. Left coronary artery and Circumflex artery (Options B and C) : In the majority of the population, it is the RCA that supplies the SA node and not the LCA. Posterior interventricular artery (Option D) : Typically supplies the posterior ventricular walls but not the SA node.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1122; Solution Pages 1152-1154","sourcePage":1122,"sourcePageEnd":1122},{"id":"anatomy-27-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A transverse section through the heart at the level of the ventricles reveals several anatomical structures. Which of the following structures is most likely to be observed in this section?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left atrial appendage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Papillary muscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aortic arch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior vena cava"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Papillary muscles","explanation":"A transverse section at the level of the heart's ventricles cuts through the middle of the ventricles, allowing visualization of internal structures within the ventricles themselves and parts of the surrounding structures. Structures visible in the transverse section of the ventricles of the heart are: - Right and Left Ventricles - Papillary muscles (Superoposterior, Inferoseptal) - Arteries and Veins - Anterior and Posterior Interventricular, Left and Right marginal - Sulcus - Anterior and Inferior interventricular - Septomarginal trabecula (moderator band) - Interventricular septum, muscular part - Tricuspid valve, septal leaflet Papillary muscles (Option B) are located within the ventricles and are crucial for the function of the atrioventricular valves. - They are clearly visible in a transverse section at the level of the ventricles, as they project into the ventricular chambers and anchor the chordae tendineae. The left atrial appendage (Option A) is located in the left atrium, which is situated above the ventricles. - It would not be prominently visible in a transverse section through the ventricles. The aortic arch (Option C) is located above the level of the ventricles and would not be observed in a transverse section through the ventricles. The superior vena cava (Option D) is positioned above the heart and drains into the right atrium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1122-1123; Solution Pages 1154-1156","sourcePage":1122,"sourcePageEnd":1123},{"id":"anatomy-27-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A 45-year-old male patient is undergoing coronary angiography for chest pain evaluation. The angiogram reveals a dominant left coronary system, with the left coronary artery supplying the majority of the heart's posterior structures. Based on this finding, which of the following statements about cardiac dominance is most accurate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"In a left-dominant coronary system, RCA gives off the posterior descending artery (PDA) and the PDA supplies the right atrium."},{"letter":"B","text":"In a right-dominant coronary system, the LCA gives off the PDA and the PDA supplies the left atrium."},{"letter":"C","text":"In a left-dominant coronary system, the PDA is supplied by the left anterior descending artery, which arises from the left circumflex artery."},{"letter":"D","text":"In a right-dominant coronary system, the RCA supplies the PDA, which in turn supplies the majority of the interventricular septum."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. In a right-dominant coronary system, the RCA supplies the PDA, which in turn supplies the majority of the interventricular septum.","explanation":"turn supplies the majority of the interventricular septum. Cardiac dominance refers to the variable blood supply pattern to the heart's posterior structures, especially the diaphragmatic surfaces of both ventricles, through the coronary artery that supplies the posterior descending artery (PDA) or posterior interventricular artery. Right Dominance (Option B) Left Dominance (Option A, C) Codominance The posterior interventricular artery The PDA arises from the left Both the RCA and LCX contribute to (PDA) originates from the right circumflex artery (LCX), a branch of the formation of the PDA or provide coronary artery (RCA). Occurs in the left coronary artery (LCA). Found multiple branches serving similar approximately 67% of individuals. in about 15% of individuals. functions. Occurs in approximately 18% of individuals.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1123; Solution Pages 1156-1157","sourcePage":1123,"sourcePageEnd":1123},{"id":"anatomy-27-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Match the following coronary arteries with the areas of the heart they predominantly supply: Coronary Arteries Supplied Areas 1) Right conus artery a) Anterior 2/3 of interventricular septum 2) Left anterior descending artery (LAD) b) Infundibulum of right ventricle 3) Left circumflex artery (LCX) c) Posterior interventricular septum 4) Posterior descending artery (PDA) d) Lateral wall of the left ventricle e) Apex of the heart Coronary Arteries Supplied Areas 1) Right conus artery a) Anterior 2/3 of interventricular septum 2) Left anterior descending artery (LAD) b) Infundibulum of right ventricle 3) Left circumflex artery (LCX) c) Posterior interventricular septum 4) Posterior descending artery (PDA) d) Lateral wall of the left ventricle e) Apex of the heart **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1- c, 2- a, 3- d, 4- b"},{"letter":"B","text":"1- b, 2- e, 3 - a, 4- d"},{"letter":"C","text":"1- b, 2- a, 3- d, 4- c"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-d, 2- e, 3- b, 4- a"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 1- b, 2- a, 3- d, 4- c","explanation":"Artery Supplied Region Right Conus Artery (RCA) Infundibulum of the right ventricle Left Anterior Descending (LAD) Anterior 2/3 of the interventricular septum Left Circumflex Artery (LCX) Lateral wall of the left ventricle Posterior Descending Artery (PDA) Posterior interventricular septum Arterial Supply of the Heart: - The arterial supply to the heart is primarily provided by two main coronary arteries: the right coronary artery (RCA) and the left coronary artery (LCA) . - These arteries originate from the aortic sinus. Artery Right Coronary Artery (RCA) Left Coronary Artery (LCA) Origin Arises from the right aortic sinus. Arises from the left aortic sinus. Course Moves forward between the Passes forward between the pulmonary trunk and right auricle, pulmonary trunk and the left auricle, descending in the right and then splits into two main atrioventricular groove. branches. Branches Right Conus Artery: Supplies the Left Anterior Descending Artery pulmonary conus (infundibulum) and (LAD): Travels down the anterior the upper right ventricle. Anterior interventricular groove to the heart’s Ventricular Branches: 2 or 3 in apex. It supplies both ventricles and number - supply the anterior surface the anterior two-thirds of the of the right ventricle. Right Marginal interventricular septum. Left Artery: Largest of anterior ventricular Circumflex Artery (LCX): Circles branches - runs along the right around the left margin of the heart in margin of the heart towards the apex. the atrioventricular groove, giving off: Posterior Descending Artery (PDA) Left Marginal Artery: Supplies the left or the posterior interventricular margin of the left ventricle. Anterior artery: Travels in the posterior and Posterior Ventricular Branches: interventricular groove, supplying the Supply the left ventricle. Atrial inferior wall of both ventricles and the Branches: Supply the left atrium. posterior part of the interventricular Lateral or diagonal artery - larger septum. This artery often arises from ventricular branch - may arise the RCA but can also originate from directly from the trunk of the left the LCA in some individuals. Atrial coronary artery. A small left conus Branches: Supply the right atrium artery supplies the pulmonary conus. and contribute to the SA node in some individuals.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1123-1124; Solution Pages 1157-1159","sourcePage":1123,"sourcePageEnd":1124},{"id":"anatomy-27-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A 45-year-old man is undergoing evaluation for palpitations. ECG reveals a supraventricular tachycardia with HR of 190/min. An echocardiogram reveals hypertrophy of the right ventricle and enlargement of the right ventricular outflow tract. Which structure separates the inflow and outflow tracts in the right ventricle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Crista terminalis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Supraventricular crest"},{"letter":"C","text":"Moderator band"},{"letter":"D","text":"Interventricular septum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Supraventricular crest","explanation":"The supraventricular crest is located within the right ventricle and separates the inflow tract , which carries blood from the right atrium through the tricuspid valve, from the outflow tract, which directs blood into the pulmonary trunk. - It is a thick, muscular “U” shaped structure extending from the interventricular septum to the anterolateral wall of the right ventricle. - The crest provides the main attachment for the anterosuperior leaflet of the tricuspid valve. - The septal limb of the crest is connected to the septal limbs of the septomarginal trabeculation (moderator band). Crista terminalis (Option A) - Located in the right atrium - separates the smooth and rough parts of the atrium, not the right ventricle's inflow and outflow tracts. The moderator band (Option C) facilitates conduction in the right ventricle and contains the right bundle branch (RBB) . - Helps prevent overdistension of the right ventricle during systole by acting as a stabilizing structure. It does not separate the inflow and outflow regions. Interventricular septum (Option D) separates the left and right ventricles but does not partition the right ventricle's inflow and outflow tracts.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1124; Solution Pages 1159-1161","sourcePage":1124,"sourcePageEnd":1124},{"id":"anatomy-27-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":25,"question":"A 36-year-old woman presents with sudden-onset palpitations and lightheadedness. Her ECG shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 170 bpm. Vagal maneuvers temporarily relieve her symptoms. Atrioventricular Nodal Reentrant Tachycardia (AVNRT) is suspected. The reentrant circuit in AVNRT is most likely located in which anatomical region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Crista terminalis"},{"letter":"B","text":"His bundle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Koch’s triangle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right atrial appendage"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Koch’s triangle","explanation":"- AVNRT is a common type of regular supraventricular tachycardia. - It is characterized by a reentrant circuit involving fast and slow pathways, at the level of the AV node. Koch’s triangle is the anatomical region containing the AV node, which is critical in AVNRT. Pathways Involved: - Slow pathway: posterior to the AV node. - Fast pathway: anterior nodal extension. Anatomical Landmark: - Triangle of Koch: Used for catheter ablation (CA), bordered by: Coronary sinus ostium (inferiorly). Septal leaflet of tricuspid valve (anteriorly). Tendon of Todaro (posterosuperiorly). - Coronary sinus ostium (inferiorly). - Septal leaflet of tricuspid valve (anteriorly). - Tendon of Todaro (posterosuperiorly). - Coronary sinus ostium (inferiorly). - Septal leaflet of tricuspid valve (anteriorly). - Tendon of Todaro (posterosuperiorly). Circuit Involvement: - Limited to the AV node and surrounding myocardium. - May extend beyond the AV node, including parts of the tricuspid and mitral vestibules. Treatment: - Catheter ablation targets the slow (Koch’s Triangle) and fast pathways to interrupt the reentrant circuit. Crista terminalis, His bundle , and Right atrial appendage are not involved in AVNRT. (Options A, B, and D).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1124-1125; Solution Pages 1161-1162","sourcePage":1124,"sourcePageEnd":1125},{"id":"anatomy-27-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":26,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with recurrent episodes of supraventricular tachycardia. The electrophysiologist plans to perform catheter ablation. Which of the following structures is most likely to be found at the apex of Koch's triangle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sinoatrial node"},{"letter":"B","text":"Atrioventricular node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bundle of His"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right bundle branch"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Atrioventricular node","explanation":"The Triangle of Koch is situated on the right atrial aspect of the interatrial septum. Boundaries: Importance: It helps to localize the AV node - present at the apex of Koch’s triangle . Localising the AV node is important to safely perform radiofrequency catheter ablation within the right atrium in cases of AVNRT. Sinoatrial node (Option A) is located in the upper part of the right atrium, near the opening of the superior vena cava, not in Koch's triangle. While the bundle of His (Option C) is anatomically close to the AV node, it is not typically found at the apex of Koch's triangle. - It emerges from the AV node and penetrates the cardiac skeleton. Right bundle branch (Option D) is a continuation of the bundle of His and is found in the interventricular septum, not in Koch's triangle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1125; Solution Pages 1162-1164","sourcePage":1125,"sourcePageEnd":1125},{"id":"anatomy-27-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":27,"question":"A 32-year-old man undergoes a coronary angiography after presenting with chest pain. The cardiologist is explaining the venous drainage of the heart as observed in the angiographic images. Which of the following structures is primarily visible on the posterior view of the heart?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Great cardiac vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Small cardiac vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Coronary sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior cardiac veins"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Coronary sinus","explanation":"Venous drainage of the heart - anterior and posterior view: Anterior View of the Heart Posterior View of the Heart Anterior interventricular sulcus contains LAD and Great Posterior interventricular sulcus contains the posterior cardiac vein Right coronary sulcus contains the right descending artery (PDA) and middle cardiac vein. coronary artery and small cardiac vein. Posterior coronary sulcus contains the coronary sinus. Great cardiac vein , Small cardiac vein - drain into the Coronary sinus : Main vein of the heart’s drainage coronary sinus (Options A and B) Anterior cardiac veins - system, collecting blood from most of the cardiac veins, Directly drain into the right atrium (Option D) Right and draining into the right atrium. (Option B) Middle marginal vein - either joins small cardiac vein or directly cardiac vein -drains into the coronary sinus. Posterior vein drains into the right atrium. of Left ventricle - runs in the diaphragmatic surface of LV and drains into coronary sinus. Oblique vein of the left atrium (Vein of Marshall) : Small vein on the posterior surface of the left atrium, draining into the coronary sinus. Left marginal vein - accompanies marginal branch of LCA and drains into Coronary sinus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1125; Solution Page 1164","sourcePage":1125,"sourcePageEnd":1125},{"id":"anatomy-27-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":28,"question":"Which of the following veins does not drain into the coronary sinus but rather drains directly into the right atrium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Great Cardiac Vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle Cardiac Vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior Cardiac Veins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Small Cardiac Vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior Cardiac Veins","explanation":"Anterior cardiac veins are small veins that run parallel across the surface of the right ventricle and open directly into the right atrium, not into the coronary sinus. Venous Drainage of the Heart Venous blood from the heart is drained into the right atrium through: 1) Coronary Sinus - The principal vein of the heart, draining most venous blood into the right atrium. - The largest vein of the heart, located in the posterior part of the atrioventricular groove (left posterior coronary sulcus). - Develops from the left horn of the sinus venosus and part of the left common cardinal vein. - Tributaries of the Coronary Sinus Great Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries, joining the left end of the coronary sinus. (Option A) Middle Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the posterior interventricular artery, joining the coronary sinus near its termination. (Option B) Small Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the right ventricular artery in the right posterior coronary sulcus, draining into the right end of the coronary sinus. (Option D) Posterior Vein of the Left Ventricle Oblique Vein of the Left Atrium (Vein of Marshall) Right Marginal Vein Left Marginal Vein - Great Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries, joining the left end of the coronary sinus. (Option A) - Middle Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the posterior interventricular artery, joining the coronary sinus near its termination. (Option B) - Small Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the right ventricular artery in the right posterior coronary sulcus, draining into the right end of the coronary sinus. (Option D) - Posterior Vein of the Left Ventricle - Oblique Vein of the Left Atrium (Vein of Marshall) - Right Marginal Vein - Left Marginal Vein - Great Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the anterior interventricular and circumflex arteries, joining the left end of the coronary sinus. (Option A) - Middle Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the posterior interventricular artery, joining the coronary sinus near its termination. (Option B) - Small Cardiac Vein : Accompanies the right ventricular artery in the right posterior coronary sulcus, draining into the right end of the coronary sinus. (Option D) - Posterior Vein of the Left Ventricle - Oblique Vein of the Left Atrium (Vein of Marshall) - Right Marginal Vein - Left Marginal Vein 2) Anterior Cardiac Veins - A series of small veins (3 or 4) that run parallel across the surface of the right ventricle, opening into the right atrium. 3) Venae Cordis Minimae (Thebesian Veins) - Extremely small veins located in the walls of all four heart chambers, opening directly into their respective chambers. - Most numerous in the right atrium.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1125-1126; Solution Pages 1164-1166","sourcePage":1125,"sourcePageEnd":1126},{"id":"anatomy-27-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":29,"question":"In a chest X-ray given below, the inferior border of the heart is primarily contributed by which structure? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right Atrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior Vena Cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left Ventricle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right Ventricle"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Right Ventricle","explanation":"The right ventricle forms the majority of the inferior border of the heart in a normal CXR, extending from the inferior vena cava (IVC) to the apex of the heart. Borders of the Heart - Right Border : Formed by the right atrium. Extends from the opening of the superior vena cava (SVC) to the inferior vena cava (IVC). - Formed by the right atrium. - Extends from the opening of the superior vena cava (SVC) to the inferior vena cava (IVC). - Left Border : Formed mainly by the left ventricle. Partly by the left auricle. Extends from the left auricle to the apex of the heart. - Formed mainly by the left ventricle. - Partly by the left auricle. - Extends from the left auricle to the apex of the heart. - Inferior Border : Formed by the right ventricle. Partly by the right atrium and left ventricle(near apex). Extends from the opening of the IVC to the apex of the heart. - Formed by the right ventricle. - Partly by the right atrium and left ventricle(near apex). - Extends from the opening of the IVC to the apex of the heart. - Upper Border : Formed mainly by the left atrium. Partly by the right atrium. Also by the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk. - Formed mainly by the left atrium. - Partly by the right atrium. - Also by the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk. - Formed by the right atrium. - Extends from the opening of the superior vena cava (SVC) to the inferior vena cava (IVC). - Formed mainly by the left ventricle. - Partly by the left auricle. - Extends from the left auricle to the apex of the heart. - Formed by the right ventricle. - Partly by the right atrium and left ventricle(near apex). - Extends from the opening of the IVC to the apex of the heart. - Formed mainly by the left atrium. - Partly by the right atrium. - Also by the ascending aorta and pulmonary trunk.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1126; Solution Pages 1166-1168","sourcePage":1126,"sourcePageEnd":1126},{"id":"anatomy-27-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-27","chapter":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topic":"Lesson 27 - Heart and Thorax Anatomy","topicIndex":27,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which sulcus contains major coronary arteries?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sulcus of Waterston"},{"letter":"B","text":"Atrioventricular Sulcus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior Interventricular Sulcus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior Interventricular Sulcus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Atrioventricular Sulcus","explanation":"Atrioventricular (Coronary) Sulcus separates the atria from the ventricles and contains the major coronary arteries. Grooves of the Heart - Interatrial Sulcus (of Waterston) : Separates the two atria. (Option A) - Atrioventricular (Coronary) Sulcus : Separates the atria from the ventricles. Contains major coronary arteries. Descends obliquely on the anterior surface, separating the right atrium and its auricle from the right ventricle. Curves around the left border and connects with the inferior part. The left anterior part of the AV groove between the left auricle and left ventricle, houses the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery. - Separates the atria from the ventricles. - Contains major coronary arteries. - Descends obliquely on the anterior surface, separating the right atrium and its auricle from the right ventricle. - Curves around the left border and connects with the inferior part. - The left anterior part of the AV groove between the left auricle and left ventricle, houses the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery. - Separates the atria from the ventricles. - Contains major coronary arteries. - Descends obliquely on the anterior surface, separating the right atrium and its auricle from the right ventricle. - Curves around the left border and connects with the inferior part. - The left anterior part of the AV groove between the left auricle and left ventricle, houses the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery. - The posterior part of the AV groove lies between the base and the diaphragmatic surface of the heart, containing the coronary sinus. - Anterior Interventricular Sulcus : (Option C) Located near the left border of the heart on the sternocostal surface. Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. Contains the anterior interventricular artery and the great cardiac vein. - Located near the left border of the heart on the sternocostal surface. - Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. - Contains the anterior interventricular artery and the great cardiac vein. - Inferior Interventricular Sulcus : (Option D) Closer to the midpoint of the ventricular mass on the diaphragmatic surface. Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. Contains the posterior interventricular artery and the middle cardiac vein. - Closer to the midpoint of the ventricular mass on the diaphragmatic surface. - Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. - Contains the posterior interventricular artery and the middle cardiac vein. - Located near the left border of the heart on the sternocostal surface. - Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. - Contains the anterior interventricular artery and the great cardiac vein. - Closer to the midpoint of the ventricular mass on the diaphragmatic surface. - Extends from the atrioventricular sulcus to the notch at the cardiac apex. - Contains the posterior interventricular artery and the middle cardiac vein.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1126-1127; Solution Pages 1168-1170","sourcePage":1126,"sourcePageEnd":1127},{"id":"anatomy-28-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":1,"question":"At the midaxillary line, the inferior border of the right lung typically corresponds to which rib level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4th"},{"letter":"B","text":"6th"},{"letter":"C","text":"8th"},{"letter":"D","text":"10th"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 8th","explanation":"- The lower border of each lung lies two ribs higher than the parietal pleural reflection. - It crosses the eighth rib in the midaxillary line. Lung markings Anterior border: Right lung Left lung Corresponds to the anterior margin of the pleura up to the Corresponds to the anterior margin of the pleura up to the level of the 6th costal cartilage. level of the 4th costal cartilage. (Option A ) Curves downwards and medially to reach the sixth costal cartilage to form the cardiac notch. Lower border: - Lies two ribs higher than the parietal pleural reflection. - Same on both the lungs. - At 6th rib - Midclavicular line (Option B ) - At 8th rib - Midaxillary line - At 10 rib - Paravertebral line (Option D ) The posterior border: - Coincides with the posterior margin of the pleural reflection - Lower end lies at the level of 10th thoracic spine (2 space higher than plerua)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1171; Solution Page 1180","sourcePage":1171,"sourcePageEnd":1171},{"id":"anatomy-28-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 36-year-old male presents with sharp, stabbing pain in the right upper abdomen for 2 weeks. The pain is aggravated by deep breathing and movement, especially when bending or twisting. There is tenderness over the right costal margin, but no signs of gastrointestinal distress, fever, or abdominal distension. His medical history is unremarkable. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acute cholecystitis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Subcostal neuralgia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Appendicitis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Peptic ulcer disease"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Subcostal neuralgia","explanation":"- Subcostal neuralgia is caused by irritation or compression of the T12 (subcostal) nerve , which provides sensory innervation to the skin of the upper abdomen below the ribcage. - It typically presents with sharp, localized pain along the costal margin , especially aggravated by deep breathing or movement (such as twisting or bending). - The pain can be unilateral, as seen in this patient. - The absence of gastrointestinal symptoms (like nausea, vomiting, or changes in bowel habits), along with localized tenderness over the right costal margin , supports the diagnosis of subcostal neuralgia . - Subcostal neuralgia can be caused by various factors, including nerve compression, inflammation, or irritation of the intercostal nerves. - It may also follow trauma, surgical procedures (especially abdominal surgeries), or infections. - The condition can sometimes occur without any obvious underlying cause. - It is typically managed with pain relief, such as non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs (NSAIDs), and physical therapy to help relieve nerve compression. - The intercostal nerves are part of the somatic nervous system and arise from the ventral rami of the T1-T11 spinal nerves. - The 12th spinal nerve does not contribute to the intercostal nerves, but rather forms the subcostal nerve . - Origin: The intercostal nerves are derived from the ventral rami of the thoracic spinal nerves (T1-T11). - The intercostal nerves are derived from the ventral rami of the thoracic spinal nerves (T1-T11). - Course: These nerves travel along the intercostal spaces between the ribs, passing laterally and then anteriorly toward the sternum. - These nerves travel along the intercostal spaces between the ribs, passing laterally and then anteriorly toward the sternum. - They travel in the costal groove on the inferior aspect of each rib, along with the intercostal artery and vein . - The nerves are positioned in the neurovascular bundle along the inferior border of each rib, with the vein superior, the artery in the middle, and the nerve inferior. - Branches: Each intercostal nerve gives off several branches: Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Each intercostal nerve gives off several branches: Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. - Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. - Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. - Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Function: Motor: To the intercostal muscles (which aid in respiration). Sensory: To the skin of the chest, abdomen, and parts of the back. Sympathetic: To the sweat glands and blood vessels in the region. - Motor: To the intercostal muscles (which aid in respiration). - Sensory: To the skin of the chest, abdomen, and parts of the back. - Sympathetic: To the sweat glands and blood vessels in the region. - The intercostal nerves are derived from the ventral rami of the thoracic spinal nerves (T1-T11). - These nerves travel along the intercostal spaces between the ribs, passing laterally and then anteriorly toward the sternum. - Each intercostal nerve gives off several branches: Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. - Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. - Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. - Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Lateral cutaneous branch: Provides sensory innervation to the skin of the lateral chest and upper abdomen. - Anterior cutaneous branch: Supplies the skin over the anterior chest wall. - Muscular branches: Innervate the intercostal muscles, transversus thoracis, and sometimes the serratus anterior muscle. - Rami communicantes: Connect the intercostal nerve to the sympathetic trunk. - Motor: To the intercostal muscles (which aid in respiration). - Sensory: To the skin of the chest, abdomen, and parts of the back. - Sympathetic: To the sweat glands and blood vessels in the region. T12 (Subcostal Nerve): The T12 nerve (subcostal nerve) runs below the 12th rib and supplies the muscles of the anterior abdominal wall and skin just below the costal margin. Acute cholecystitis (Option A) typically presents with right upper quadrant pain that is often associated with nausea, vomiting, and fever. - The pain tends to be more constant and is aggravated by eating, particularly fatty foods. This patient lacks gastrointestinal symptoms and fever, making cholecystitis unlikely. Appendicitis (Option C) typically starts with periumbilical pain that eventually migrates to the right lower quadrant. - It is also accompanied by nausea, vomiting, and fever, which this patient does not have. The pain in appendicitis is generally deep and constant, not related to movement. Peptic ulcer disease (Option D) may present with epigastric pain and is often aggravated by food or empty stomach. - However, the pain tends to be dull and burning, rather than sharp and localized along the rib margin. - The absence of nausea, vomiting, and changes in appetite makes this diagnosis less likely.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1171; Solution Pages 1180-1184","sourcePage":1171,"sourcePageEnd":1171},{"id":"anatomy-28-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 48-year-old woman presents to the clinic complaining of a burning, aching sensation on the inner side of her left upper arm. She mentions that about two weeks ago she underwent mastectomy with axillary clearance. She describes the pain as localized along the inner part of the upper arm, extending from the axilla (armpit) to the medial elbow. The pain seems to worsen when she raises her arm. On physical examination, there is tenderness over the left axillary region and the medial upper arm, but no signs of swelling or lymphadenopathy. Based on the patient's history of breast surgery and the clinical presentation, which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Radial nerve injury"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cervical radiculopathy (C7)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intercostobrachial nerve injury"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thoracic outlet syndrome"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Intercostobrachial nerve injury","explanation":"- Intercostobrachial nerve injury is a well-known complication after breast surgery, particularly those involving the axillary region (such as axillary lymph node dissection or mastectomy). - This condition is referred to as post-mastectomy pain syndrome when associated with breast cancer surgery. - The pain may worsen with arm movements, and in some cases, hyperalgesia (heightened pain sensitivity) in the affected area is noted. - The intercostobrachial nerve arises from the second intercostal nerve (T2) and provides sensory innervation to the inner upper arm, the medial side of the axilla, and part of the chest. It is a purely sensory nerve and does not affect motor function. - Intercostobrachial nerve injury is a common complication following breast surgery—especially lumpectomy, mastectomy, or axillary lymph node dissection—due to the proximity of the nerve to the axillary region. During these procedures, the nerve can be cut, stretched, or irritated, leading to pain and sensory disturbances in its area of distribution. - The patient’s burning and aching pain localized to the inner upper arm after breast surgery, along with hyperalgesia (increased pain sensitivity) over the same area, strongly points to an injury to the intercostobrachial nerve. Radial nerve injury (Option A) typically presents with symptoms like weakness and numbness in the posterior arm, forearm, and dorsal hand, not localized pain along the inner upper arm. - Additionally, motor weakness would be present in the arm, which is not the case here. Cervical radiculopathy (Option B)(C7) usually causes pain, numbness, and weakness that radiates from the neck down to the hand, particularly affecting the middle finger and triceps muscle. Thoracic outlet syndrome (Option D) can cause pain and numbness in the neck, shoulder, and upper arm due to compression of the brachial plexus, subclavian artery, or subclavian vein. - However, this syndrome usually presents with neurovascular symptoms (e.g., weakness, coldness, or swelling in the arm) and is less likely to cause isolated sensory symptoms .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1171-1172; Solution Pages 1184-1185","sourcePage":1171,"sourcePageEnd":1172},{"id":"anatomy-28-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Identify the structure marked as X in the image. {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Subclavian vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Subclavian artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ulnar nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Subclavian vein","explanation":"The subclavian vein is a key vessel in the venous system that drains blood from the upper limb, the neck, and part of the chest. It is located in the thoracic region and plays a crucial role in returning deoxygenated blood to the heart. 1. Location and Course: - The subclavian vein is positioned deep to the clavicle (collarbone) and lies within the thoracic inlet, a narrow space between the neck and the chest. - It runs laterally and horizontally beneath the clavicle, crossing the first rib and passing anterior to the anterior scalene muscle. - It continues to run towards the right or left sternoclavicular joint, where it drains into the brachiocephalic vein on both sides (right and left). 2. Relations: - Posterior Relations: The subclavian vein is located anterior to the subclavian artery. The clavicle (collarbone) lies above the vein, and it is closely associated with the sternoclavicular joint and muscles like the pectoralis minor. - The subclavian vein is located anterior to the subclavian artery. - The clavicle (collarbone) lies above the vein, and it is closely associated with the sternoclavicular joint and muscles like the pectoralis minor. - Anterior Relations: The subclavian vein runs posterior to the anterior scalene muscle, which separates it from the subclavian artery. The vein is also related to the first rib, which lies beneath it. - The subclavian vein runs posterior to the anterior scalene muscle, which separates it from the subclavian artery. - The vein is also related to the first rib, which lies beneath it. - Medial Relations: On the medial side, it is adjacent to the brachiocephalic vein. - On the medial side, it is adjacent to the brachiocephalic vein. - Lateral Relations: The vein continues laterally and joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. The thoracic duct (on the left side) drains into the subclavian vein near the junction of the internal jugular vein. - The vein continues laterally and joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. - The thoracic duct (on the left side) drains into the subclavian vein near the junction of the internal jugular vein. - The subclavian vein is located anterior to the subclavian artery. - The clavicle (collarbone) lies above the vein, and it is closely associated with the sternoclavicular joint and muscles like the pectoralis minor. - The subclavian vein runs posterior to the anterior scalene muscle, which separates it from the subclavian artery. - The vein is also related to the first rib, which lies beneath it. - On the medial side, it is adjacent to the brachiocephalic vein. - The vein continues laterally and joins the internal jugular vein to form the brachiocephalic vein. - The thoracic duct (on the left side) drains into the subclavian vein near the junction of the internal jugular vein. - The subclavian vein is larger and more anterior and superficial than the subclavian artery. 3. Anatomy: - Right subclavian vein: It drains into the right brachiocephalic vein. - Left subclavian vein: It drains into the left brachiocephalic vein. - Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct drains lymph from the left side of the body into the left subclavian vein near its junction with the internal jugular vein. 4. Clinical Relevance: - The subclavian vein is often used for central venous catheter insertion, central venous pressure monitoring. - Subclavian vein thrombosis (also known as Paget-Schroetter syndrome) can occur due to repetitive trauma, leading to clots, especially in athletes or individuals with certain anatomical predispositions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1172-1173; Solution Pages 1185-1187","sourcePage":1172,"sourcePageEnd":1173},{"id":"anatomy-28-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following structures is the mediastinal surface of the right lung not related to?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right atrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Azygos vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thoracic aorta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Esophagus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Thoracic aorta","explanation":"Thoracic aorta is related to mediastinal surface of the left lung Mediastinal impression of right lung: Mediastinal impression of left lung: Mediastinal structures related to lungs: Right lung Left lung 1. Right atrium (Option A) 1. Left ventricle, left auricle, infundibulum and adjoining part of the right ventricle 2. A small part of the right ventricle 2. Pulmonary trunk 3. Superior vena cava 3. Arch of aorta 4. Lower part of the right brachiocephalic vein 4. Descending thoracic aorta (Option C) 5. Azygos vein (Option B) 5. Left subclavian artery 6. Esophagus (Option D) 6. Thoracic duct 7. Inferior vena cava 7. Oesophagus 8. Trachea 8. Left brachiocephalic vein 9. Right vagus nerve 9. Left vagus nerve 10. Right phrenic nerve 10. Left phrenic nerve 11. Left recurrent laryngeal nerve","sourceRef":"Question Page 1173; Solution Pages 1187-1189","sourcePage":1173,"sourcePageEnd":1173},{"id":"anatomy-28-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":6,"question":"In a healthy individual at rest, which of the following is a primary muscle involved in expiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Diaphragm"},{"letter":"B","text":"External intercostal muscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Scalene muscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. None of the above","explanation":"Expiration is a passive process at rest due to elastic recoil of the lung and chest wall. Type of Breathing Muscles Involved Process Remarks Quiet Expiration Elastic recoil of chest wall Passive Mainly elastic recoil, aided and pulmonary alveoli by abdominal muscle tone Forced Expiration Abdominal muscles, Active Strong contraction of Latissimus dorsi abdominal muscles The diaphragm (Option A) is primarily involved in inspiration. External intercostal muscles (Option B) are used for inspiration. Scalene muscles (Option C) are accessory muscles of inspiration and do not play a role in expiration.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1173; Solution Pages 1189-1190","sourcePage":1173,"sourcePageEnd":1173},{"id":"anatomy-28-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following muscles plays the primary role in quiet inspiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior abdominal wall muscles"},{"letter":"B","text":"External intercostal muscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Scalene muscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sternocleidomastoid muscle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. External intercostal muscles","explanation":"The external intercostal muscles are the primary muscles responsible for quiet inspiration, aiding in the elevation of the ribs and expansion of the thoracic cavity. Type of Breathing Muscles Involved Process Remarks Quiet Inspiration Diaphragm External Active Increase in A-P, Intercostal (Option B) transverse, and vertical diameters Forced Inspiration Scalene (Option C ) Active Exaggerated movements, Sternocleidomastoid Scapulae elevated and (Option D ) Erector fixed Spinae, Pectoral muscles, Trapezius, Levator Scapulae, Rhomboids Anterior abdominal wall muscles (Option A) and internal intercostal muscles are primarily involved in forced expiration. Scalene muscles (Option C) and the sternocleidomastoid muscle (Option D) are accessory muscles that come into play during deep or labored inspiration but not during quiet inspiration.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1173; Solution Pages 1190-1191","sourcePage":1173,"sourcePageEnd":1173},{"id":"anatomy-28-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which rib movements are responsible for increasing the anteroposterior diameter of the thoracic cavity during inspiration?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pump-handle movement of ribs 1-6"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bucket-handle movement of ribs 7-10"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bucket-handle movement of ribs 1-6"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pump-handle movement of ribs 7-10"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Pump-handle movement of ribs 1-6","explanation":"The pump-handle movement of the upper ribs (ribs 1-6) increases the anteroposterior (AP) diameter of the thoracic cavity during inspiration, elevating the sternum and ribs forward. Movements of the ribs during respiration: Mechanism Action Diameter Increased Ribs Involved Pump Handle (Option A) Elevation of ribs causing Anteroposterior Diameter 2nd to 6th Ribs forward movement of the (Vertebrosternal) (Option sternum D) Bucket Handle (Options B Elevation of ribs causing Transverse Diameter 7th to 10th Ribs and C ) outward movement of the (Vertebrochondral) rib shafts","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1173-1174; Solution Page 1191","sourcePage":1173,"sourcePageEnd":1174},{"id":"anatomy-28-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":9,"question":"The 2nd to 7th sternocostal joints are categorized as which type of joint?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Synovial"},{"letter":"B","text":"Symphysis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synarthrosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Synchondrosis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Synovial","explanation":"- The second to seventh sternocostal joints are synovial joints. - These are the joints between the costal cartilages of the ribs and the sternum. Thorax joints Manubriosternal Joint: - It is between the manubrium and the body of the sternum. - It lies in the midline. - It is usually a symphysis Xiphisternal joint: - It is between the xiphoid process and body of the sternum. - It is a symphysis type of joint. (Option B ) First sternocostal joint: - It is a type of synarthrosis (fibrous joint) (Option C ) - It is inaccurately called synchondrosis. (Option D ) The second to seventh sternocostal joints: - It is a synovial type of joint. (Option A) - Mobile joints for free movements. - Helps in respiratory movements. Interchondral joints: - Adjoining borders of the sixth to ninth costal cartilages articulate by apposition. - Each articulation is lined by a synovial membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1174; Solution Pages 1191-1193","sourcePage":1174,"sourcePageEnd":1174},{"id":"anatomy-28-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":10,"question":"The costal cartilages of which ribs attach directly to the sternum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1–7"},{"letter":"B","text":"8-12"},{"letter":"C","text":"11 and 12"},{"letter":"D","text":"8-10"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1–7","explanation":"The first seven pairs of ribs (ribs 1–7) are true ribs as they are directly attached to the sternum via their costal cartilages. Ribs True ribs False ribs Attach to the sternum directly. AKA vertebrosternal ribs Do not attach to the sternum directly. They are indirectly Arise from the vertebra. 1-7 are true ribs. (Option A) attached to the sternum through the costal margin. Arise from the vertebra. 8-12 are false ribs. (Option B ). 8,9,10 ribs form the costal margin. (Option D ). AKA vertebrochondral ribs 11,12 are floating ribs (They are not attached to the sternum at all). (Option C ) Costal groove: - It is situated along the lower part of the inner surface of a rib. - It contains the posterior intercostal vessels and intercostal nerve. - Pleural tap is done along upper border of lower rib to prevent injury to intercostal vessels and nerves.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1174; Solution Pages 1193-1195","sourcePage":1174,"sourcePageEnd":1174},{"id":"anatomy-28-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify the correct labels. {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A.Left main bronchus, B. Descending aorta, C. T5 vertebrae, D.Esophagus, H.Ascending aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"A.Left main bronchus, B. Ascending aorta, C. T5 vertebrae, D. Esophagus, H. Descending aorta"},{"letter":"C","text":"A.Right main bronchus, B. Descending aorta, C. T5 vertebrae, D. Ascending aorta, H. Pulmonary artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"A. Right main bronchus, B. Descending aorta, C. T5 vertebrae, D. Esophagus, H. Ascending aorta"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A.Left main bronchus, B. Descending aorta, C. T5 vertebrae, D.Esophagus, H.Ascending aorta","explanation":"H. Ascending aorta The anatomical positions of the structures mentioned and how they typically appear in a CT image of the thorax is given below.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1174-1175; Solution Page 1195","sourcePage":1174,"sourcePageEnd":1175},{"id":"anatomy-28-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":12,"question":"The pleura extends inferiorly to which rib at the midclavicular line in a normal adult?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"6th rib"},{"letter":"B","text":"8th rib"},{"letter":"C","text":"10th rib"},{"letter":"D","text":"12th rib"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 8th rib","explanation":"The costodiaphragmatic line of pleural reflection passes laterally from the lower limit of its anterior margin, crossing the eighth rib in the midclavicular line. Pleural markings Anterior margin: Right side Left side From the sternoclavicular joint vertically downwards to the Same as right side except at the level of 4 th costal midpoint of the xiphisternal joint (level of 6th costal cartilage it arches outwards and descends along the cartilage). (Option A ) sternal margin up to the 6th costal cartilage. For cardiac notch. Inferior margin (costodiaphragmatic line of pleural reflection): From the lower limit of its anterior margin, it crosses - 8th rib in the midclavicular line (Option B) - 10th rib in the midaxillary line (Option C ) - 12th rib at the lateral border of the sacrospinalis muscle (Option D ) Posterior margin: Extends from 2 cm lateral to the 12th thoracic spine to 2 cm lateral to the 7th cervical spine.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1175-1176; Solution Pages 1195-1197","sourcePage":1175,"sourcePageEnd":1176},{"id":"anatomy-28-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 40-year-old patient with known dextrocardia comes for a routine checkup. Where would you expect to hear the loudest apex beat on physical examination?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left 4th intercostal space"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right 5th intercostal space"},{"letter":"C","text":"Midline of the sternum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right 3rd intercostal space"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Right 5th intercostal space","explanation":"- Dextrocardia is a congenital anomaly where the heart is placed on the right side of the thorax. - The apex beat is heard in the right fifth intercostal space to the right of the inferior end of the sternum. Dextrocardia - It is a rare congenital anomaly in which the heart is located on the right side of the thoracic cavity. - Sometimes, the viscera of the abdomen and thorax including the heart are a mirror image of normal. This condition is called ‘situs inversus’. - The apex beat is heard in the right fifth intercostal space to the right of the inferior end of the sternum (Option B) - Kartagener syndrome (ciliary dyskinesia): Chronic sinusitis Bronchiectasis Dextrocardia - Chronic sinusitis - Bronchiectasis - Dextrocardia - Chronic sinusitis - Bronchiectasis - Dextrocardia","sourceRef":"Question Page 1176; Solution Pages 1197-1198","sourcePage":1176,"sourcePageEnd":1176},{"id":"anatomy-28-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":14,"question":"During bronchoscopy, the carina is an important landmark that separates the openings of the right and left main bronchi. Which of the following statements about the tracheal bifurcation is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The left main bronchus is shorter and more vertical than the right"},{"letter":"B","text":"The right main bronchus is wider and more horizontal than the left"},{"letter":"C","text":"The left main bronchus is longer and more horizontal than the right"},{"letter":"D","text":"The right main bronchus is narrower and more vertical than the left"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The left main bronchus is longer and more horizontal than the right","explanation":"The right main bronchus is approximately 2.5 cm long, and is wider, shorter, and more vertical than the left. Bifurcation of trachea - Level of bifurcation: T5–T6 intervertebral disc - The angle between left and right principal bronchus - 70■ - Bifurcates into left and right main (principal) bronchus - Right main bronchus Shorter in length (Option C) Wider lumen More vertical (Option C) Inhaled foreign bodies and aspiration occurs at the right main bronchus more often than the left - Shorter in length (Option C) - Wider lumen - More vertical (Option C) - Inhaled foreign bodies and aspiration occurs at the right main bronchus more often than the left - Shorter in length (Option C) - Wider lumen - More vertical (Option C) - Inhaled foreign bodies and aspiration occurs at the right main bronchus more often than the left","sourceRef":"Question Page 1176; Solution Pages 1198-1199","sourcePage":1176,"sourcePageEnd":1176},{"id":"anatomy-28-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which segment of the left lung is located in the inferior lobe?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S5: Inferior Lingular"},{"letter":"B","text":"S3: Anterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"S10: Posterior Basal"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2: Posterior"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. S10: Posterior Basal","explanation":"- S10: Posterior Basal is located in the inferior lobe of the left lung - S5, S3, and S2 are also in the superior lobe. Left Lung Superior Lobe: - S1: Apical - S2: Posterior - S3: Anterior - S4: Superior Lingular - S5: Inferior Lingular (Note: S1 and S2 may be fused) Inferior Lobe: - S6: Superior (Apical) - S7: Medial Basal - S8: Anterior Basal - S9: Lateral Basal - S10: Posterior Basal (Note: S7 and S8 may be fused)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1176; Solution Pages 1199-1200","sourcePage":1176,"sourcePageEnd":1176},{"id":"anatomy-28-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A 65-year-old male patient is admitted to the hospital after undergoing elective surgery. Post-anesthesia, he is found to be unconscious and placed in a supine position for monitoring. The healthcare team is concerned about the risk of aspiration due to his current state. Which location is most likely to collect aspirated material in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior Basal BPS of the right lower lobe"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior BPS of the right upper lobe"},{"letter":"C","text":"Apical Basal BPS of the right lower lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral BPS of the right middle lobe"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Apical Basal BPS of the right lower lobe","explanation":"- The Apical Basal BPS of the right lower lobe is the most likely site for aspirated material in a supine, unconscious patient - Due to its anatomical position and tendency to collect fluids. - Other segments, while dependent, are less favorable for aspiration accumulation. (Option A, B, and D ) Right Lung: - Superior Lobe: S1: Apical S2: Posterior S3: Anterior - S1: Apical - S2: Posterior - S3: Anterior - Middle Lobe: S4: Lateral S5: Medial - S4: Lateral - S5: Medial - Inferior Lobe: S6: Superior (Apical) S7: Medial Basal S8: Anterior Basal S9: Lateral Basal S10: Posterior Basal - S6: Superior (Apical) - S7: Medial Basal - S8: Anterior Basal - S9: Lateral Basal - S10: Posterior Basal - S1: Apical - S2: Posterior - S3: Anterior - S4: Lateral - S5: Medial - S6: Superior (Apical) - S7: Medial Basal - S8: Anterior Basal - S9: Lateral Basal - S10: Posterior Basal Most Dependent Location: - Superior segment of right lower lobe Aspiration Risk: - Supine posture. - Coma and unconscious patients (e.g., post-anesthesia). Collection Sites: - Posterior BPS of the right upper lobe. - Apical Basal BPS of the right lower lobe. Preferred Collection Site: - Apical Basal BPS of the right lower lobe > Posterior BPS of the right upper lobe.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1176-1177; Solution Pages 1200-1202","sourcePage":1176,"sourcePageEnd":1177},{"id":"anatomy-28-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 60-year-old female undergoes right lower lobe resection for a tumor. Which statement about bronchopulmonary segments is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Each segment shares a bronchus with adjacent segments."},{"letter":"B","text":"Segments are supplied by segmental arteries and share venous drainage."},{"letter":"C","text":"Each segment has independent arterial and venous supplies."},{"letter":"D","text":"The shape is pyramidal with the base at the hilum."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Segments are supplied by segmental arteries and share venous drainage.","explanation":"Bronchopulmonary Segments Features: - Well-defined anatomical segments of the lung, each aerated by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus. - Each segment has its own arterial supply, but shares venous drainage with adjacent segments. (Option B) (Option C ) - Anatomical, Functional, and Surgical Units: Serve as distinct sectors of the lung. - Aerosol Supply: Each segment is supplied by a tertiary (segmental) bronchus. (Option A ) - Shape: Pyramidal, with the apex oriented towards the root of the lung. (Option D ) - Vascular and Nerve Supply: Contains a segmental bronchus, segmental artery, autonomic nerves, and lymph vessels. - Venous Drainage: Segmental venules are located in the connective tissue between adjacent segments. - Surgical Resection: Surgeons isolate segments by working along the segmental veins during resection procedures.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1177; Solution Pages 1202-1204","sourcePage":1177,"sourcePageEnd":1177},{"id":"anatomy-28-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Which of the following statements correctly describes a difference between the right and left lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The left lung is longer and narrower than the right lung."},{"letter":"B","text":"The right lung has a cardiac notch."},{"letter":"C","text":"The right lung has only one fissure and two lobes."},{"letter":"D","text":"The left lung is larger and heavier than the right lung."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The left lung is longer and narrower than the right lung.","explanation":"- The left lung is longer and narrower to accommodate the heart's position, with a cardiac notch. - In contrast, the right lung is shorter and broader, allowing for a larger volume and more lobes. Feature Right Lung Left Lung Size Shorter and broader Longer and narrower (Option D ) Weight Larger, about 700 g Smaller, about 600 g Anterior Border Straight Interrupted by a cardiac notch (Option B ) Cardiac Impression Shallow/absent Deep Lingula Absent Present Lobes and Fissures 3 lobes, 2 fissures (Option C ) 2 lobes, 1 fissure Bronchopulmonary segments 10 8 -10","sourceRef":"Question Page 1177; Solution Pages 1204-1205","sourcePage":1177,"sourcePageEnd":1177},{"id":"anatomy-28-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-28","chapter":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topic":"Lesson 28 - Thorax Lungs, Joints, Respiratory Movements","topicIndex":28,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 55-year-old male patient presents with a persistent cough and shortness of breath. A chest X-ray shows a mass in the right lung. The attending physician is reviewing the anatomical structures relevant to potential surgical intervention. Which of the following statements about the anatomy of the right and left lungs is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The right superior pulmonary vein is located posteriorly to the bronchus."},{"letter":"B","text":"The eparterial bronchus in the right lung is found above the pulmonary artery."},{"letter":"C","text":"The most superior structure of the left lung hilum is the Pulmonary vein."},{"letter":"D","text":"The bronchus on the left side is shorter in length compared to the right bronchus."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The eparterial bronchus in the right lung is found above the pulmonary artery.","explanation":"- In the right lung, the eparterial bronchus is anatomically located superior to the right pulmonary artery. - This unique positioning is important for surgical considerations. Medial surface of the Lung - Right Lung : Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. Anterior Region : Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) Right inferior pulmonary vein Pulmonary Artery : More superior Eparterial Bronchus : Present above the artery. Hyparterial Bronchus : Present below the artery. - Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. - Anterior Region : Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) Right inferior pulmonary vein - Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) - Right inferior pulmonary vein - Pulmonary Artery : More superior - Eparterial Bronchus : Present above the artery. - Hyparterial Bronchus : Present below the artery. - Left Lung : Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. Anterior Region : Left superior pulmonary vein Left inferior pulmonary vein Pulmonary Artery : More superior in the superior region. - Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. - Anterior Region : Left superior pulmonary vein Left inferior pulmonary vein - Left superior pulmonary vein - Left inferior pulmonary vein - Pulmonary Artery : More superior in the superior region. - Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. - Anterior Region : Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) Right inferior pulmonary vein - Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) - Right inferior pulmonary vein - Pulmonary Artery : More superior - Eparterial Bronchus : Present above the artery. - Hyparterial Bronchus : Present below the artery. - Right superior pulmonary vein (Option A) - Right inferior pulmonary vein - Posterior Region : Bronchus is present. - Anterior Region : Left superior pulmonary vein Left inferior pulmonary vein - Left superior pulmonary vein - Left inferior pulmonary vein - Pulmonary Artery : More superior in the superior region. - Left superior pulmonary vein - Left inferior pulmonary vein Mnemonic (From Superior to Inferior) - Atal Bihari Vajpayee : (Option C) A : Arteries B : Bronchus V : Veins - A : Arteries - B : Bronchus - V : Veins - A : Arteries - B : Bronchus - V : Veins NB: - The eparterial bronchus is unique to the right lung's superior region. - The right bronchus is shorter in length compared to the left (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1177-1178; Solution Pages 1205-1207","sourcePage":1177,"sourcePageEnd":1178},{"id":"anatomy-29-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Identify the labeling correctly:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Superior vena cava, B- Ascending aorta, C- Pulmonary trunk, D- Descending aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Ascending aorta, B- Descending aorta, C- Pulmonary trunk, D- Superior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Ascending aorta, B- Superior vena cava, C- Descending aorta, D- Pulmonary trunk,"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Ascending aorta, B- Superior Vena Cava, C- Pulmonary trunk, D- Descending aorta"},{"letter":"A","text":"Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava is a large vein that carries deoxygenated blood from the upper half of the body to the right atrium of the heart. It is located anterior and slightly to the right of the ascending aorta."},{"letter":"B","text":"Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta arises from the left ventricle of the heart and is the initial portion of the aorta that moves upward before arching."},{"letter":"C","text":"Pulmonary Trunk: The pulmonary trunk is the vessel that arises from the right ventricle and bifurcates into the left and right pulmonary arteries, which carry deoxygenated blood to the lungs. It is positioned anterior to the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava."},{"letter":"D","text":"Descending Aorta: The descending aorta continues from the aortic arch and runs downwards behind the heart, supplying blood to the lower parts of the body. It is located posterior to the heart and more to the left in the thoracic cavity. Incorrect Options: Option B , C, D incorrectly labels the structures"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A- Superior vena cava, B- Ascending aorta, C- Pulmonary trunk, D- Descending aorta","explanation":"Correct Option A - A- , B-Ascending aorta , C- Pulmonary trunk, D- Descending aorta: - A. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava is a large vein that carries deoxygenated blood from the upper half of the body to the right atrium of the heart. It is located anterior and slightly to the right of the ascending aorta. - B. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta arises from the left ventricle of the heart and is the initial portion of the aorta that moves upward before arching. - C. Pulmonary Trunk: The pulmonary trunk is the vessel that arises from the right ventricle and bifurcates into the left and right pulmonary arteries, which carry deoxygenated blood to the lungs. It is positioned anterior to the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava. - D. Descending Aorta: The descending aorta continues from the aortic arch and runs downwards behind the heart, supplying blood to the lower parts of the body. It is located posterior to the heart and more to the left in the thoracic cavity. A. Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava is a large vein that carries deoxygenated blood from the upper half of the body to the right atrium of the heart. It is located anterior and slightly to the right of the ascending aorta. B. Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta arises from the left ventricle of the heart and is the initial portion of the aorta that moves upward before arching. C. Pulmonary Trunk: The pulmonary trunk is the vessel that arises from the right ventricle and bifurcates into the left and right pulmonary arteries, which carry deoxygenated blood to the lungs. It is positioned anterior to the ascending aorta and the superior vena cava. D. Descending Aorta: The descending aorta continues from the aortic arch and runs downwards behind the heart, supplying blood to the lower parts of the body. It is located posterior to the heart and more to the left in the thoracic cavity. Incorrect Options: Option B , C, D incorrectly labels the structures","sourceRef":"Question Page 1208; Solution Pages 1238-1239","sourcePage":1208,"sourcePageEnd":1208},{"id":"anatomy-29-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Identify the structure marked in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bronchus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Arch of aorta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Brachiocephalic trunk"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulmonary trunk"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pulmonary trunk","explanation":"Correct Option D - Pulmonary trunk: - The marked structure in the given image is the pulmonary trunk. The pulmonary trunk starts from the upper part of the right ventricle, near the left 3rd costal cartilage, and runs backward and leftward. It ends beneath the aortic arch, in front of the left principal bronchus, dividing into the right and left pulmonary arteries. Incorrect Options: Option A - Bronchus: - Relation with Pulmonary Trunk: The left main bronchus passes beneath the aortic arch and behind the pulmonary trunk before entering the left lung, forming the bronchopulmonary angle.. Option B - Arch of Aorta: - Relation with Pulmonary Trunk: The pulmonary trunk is positioned anterior-left to the ascending aorta, which partially encircles its superior and posterior aspects as it extends into the aortic arch. Option C - Brachiocephalic Trunk: - Relation with Pulmonary Trunk: The pulmonary trunk is located anteriorly and to the left of the ascending aorta, which is continuous with the aortic arch.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1208-1209; Solution Pages 1239-1240","sourcePage":1208,"sourcePageEnd":1209},{"id":"anatomy-29-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Identify the arrow marked chamber. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right atrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left atrium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right ventricle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left ventricle"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Left atrium","explanation":"Correct options B - The heart is divided into four chambers: two atria (plural of atrium) and two ventricles. The left atrium is one of the upper chambers of the heart. - The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs via the pulmonary veins. It then contracts to push the blood into the left ventricle, which is responsible for pumping the oxygenated blood out to the rest of the body. Incorrect options: Option A: Right atrium: The right atrium is the other upper chamber of the heart. It receives deoxygenated blood from the body through the superior and inferior vena cava. The right atrium then contracts to push the blood into the right ventricle. Option C: Right ventricle: The right ventricle is one of the lower chambers of the heart. It receives deoxygenated blood from the right atrium and pumps it to the lungs for oxygenation. Option D: Left ventricle: The left ventricle is the other lower chamber of the heart. It receives oxygenated blood from the left atrium and pumps it out to the body's various organs and tissues.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1209-1210; Solution Page 1240","sourcePage":1209,"sourcePageEnd":1210},{"id":"anatomy-29-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":4,"question":"At which vertebral level is the anatomical cross-section shown in the image provided? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T10"},{"letter":"B","text":"T12"},{"letter":"C","text":"T11"},{"letter":"D","text":"T9"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. T10","explanation":"Correct Option A - T10: - Visible Structures: Both right and left lungs. Liver Esophagus Spleen - Both right and left lungs. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen - Explanation: At the T10 level, both lungs are visible, indicating the presence of thoracic structures. The liver, esophagus, and spleen are also visible, which is consistent with this level. At T11 and T12, the lungs start to taper off and may not be fully visible. The absence of the colon is also noted at this level, confirming T10 as the correct answer. Visible Structures: - Both right and left lungs. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen Both right and left lungs. Liver Esophagus Spleen Incorrect Options: Option B - T12: - Visible Structures: Lungs are not visible. Liver Esophagus Spleen - Lungs are not visible. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen - Lungs are not visible. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen Lungs are not visible. - Explanation : At T12, the lungs are typically not visible, as they taper off around the lower thoracic levels. The presence of the liver, esophagus, and spleen can be noted, but the key distinguishing feature is the absence of lung visibility, which rules out T12 for the givenimage. At T12, the lungs are typically not visible, as they taper off around the lower thoracic levels. The presence of the liver, esophagus, and spleen can be noted, but the key distinguishing feature is the absence of lung visibility, which rules out T12 for the given image. Option C - T11: - Visible Structures: Lungs start to taper off and may not be fully visible. Liver Esophagus Spleen - Lungs start to taper off and may not be fully visible. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen - Lungs start to taper off and may not be fully visible. - Liver - Esophagus - Spleen Lungs start to taper off and may not be fully visible. - Explanation : Similar to T12, at T11 the lungs are less visible as they begin to taper off. This is inconsistent with the observation that both lungs are visible in the given cross-section, thus ruling out T11. Similar to T12, at T11 the lungs are less visible as they begin to taper off. This is inconsistent with the observation that both lungs are visible in the given cross-section, thus ruling out T11. Option D - T9: - Visible Structures: Both right and left lungs. Liver (partially). Esophagus. Spleen is not typically visible. - Both right and left lungs. - Liver (partially). - Esophagus. - Spleen is not typically visible. - Both right and left lungs. - Liver (partially). - Esophagus. - Spleen is not typically visible. Liver (partially). Esophagus. Spleen is not typically visible. - Explanation : At T9, both lungs are visible, and the superior aspect of the liver starts to appear. However, the spleen is usually not visible at this level, which contradicts the given cross-section details. At T9, both lungs are visible, and the superior aspect of the liver starts to appear. However, the spleen is usually not visible at this level, which contradicts the given cross-section details.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1210-1211; Solution Pages 1240-1243","sourcePage":1210,"sourcePageEnd":1211},{"id":"anatomy-29-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Identify the artery that supplies the anatomical region of the heart shown in the provided image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior interventricular artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"left circumflex artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left marginal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right coronary artery (RCA)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior interventricular artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Anterior Interventricular Artery (Left Anterior Descending Artery): - The anterior interventricular artery, also known as the left anterior descending (LAD) artery, is responsible for supplying the anterior part of the interventricular septum and the anterior walls of both ventricles. - Associated Regions: Anterior wall of the left ventricle. Part of the right ventricle. Anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. - Anterior wall of the left ventricle. - Part of the right ventricle. - Anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. The anterior interventricular artery, also known as the left anterior descending (LAD) artery, is responsible for supplying the anterior part of the interventricular septum and the anterior walls of both ventricles. Associated Regions: - Anterior wall of the left ventricle. - Part of the right ventricle. - Anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. Anterior wall of the left ventricle. Part of the right ventricle. Anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum. Incorrect Options: Option B - Left Circumflex Artery - Supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle and part of the left atrium. - Associated Regions: Lateral wall of the left ventricle. Posterior wall of the left ventricle. Part of the left atrium. - Lateral wall of the left ventricle. - Posterior wall of the left ventricle. - Part of the left atrium. Supplies the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle and part of the left atrium. - Lateral wall of the left ventricle. - Posterior wall of the left ventricle. - Part of the left atrium. Lateral wall of the left ventricle. Posterior wall of the left ventricle. Part of the left atrium. Option C - Left Marginal Artery: - Function: Supplies the left margin (or border) of the heart. - Details: It is a branch of the left circumflex artery and runs along the margin of the left ventricle. - Associated Regions: Lateral wall of the left ventricle, especially along the margin. - Lateral wall of the left ventricle, especially along the margin. Function: Supplies the left margin (or border) of the heart. Details: It is a branch of the left circumflex artery and runs along the margin of the left ventricle. - Lateral wall of the left ventricle, especially along the margin. Lateral wall of the left ventricle, especially along the margin. Option D - Right Coronary Artery (RCA): - Supplies the right atrium, right ventricle, and part of the left ventricle, as well as the sinoatrial (SA) and atrioventricular (AV) nodes. - Associated Regions: Right atrium. Right ventricle. Inferior wall of the left ventricle. SA and AV nodes. - Right atrium. - Right ventricle. - Inferior wall of the left ventricle. - SA and AV nodes. Supplies the right atrium, right ventricle, and part of the left ventricle, as well as the sinoatrial (SA) and atrioventricular (AV) nodes. - Right atrium. - Right ventricle. - Inferior wall of the left ventricle. - SA and AV nodes. Right atrium. Right ventricle. Inferior wall of the left ventricle. SA and AV nodes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1211-1212; Solution Pages 1243-1245","sourcePage":1211,"sourcePageEnd":1212},{"id":"anatomy-29-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements is true regarding the anatomy of the breast?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The superior medial quadrant has more tissue"},{"letter":"B","text":"There are 10 lobules present"},{"letter":"C","text":"The axillary tail of Spence crosses the anterior axillary fold"},{"letter":"D","text":"The nipple is located at the level of the fourth intercostal space in most women"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The axillary tail of Spence crosses the anterior axillary fold","explanation":"Correct Option C - The axillary tail of Spence crosses the anterior axillary fold: - The axillary tail of Spence is an extension of the breast tissue that extends into the axilla, and it does cross the anterior axillary fold. Incorrect Options: Option A - The superior medial quadrant has more tissue: - The superior lateral quadrant actually contains the most glandular tissue and is a common site for breast cancer. Option B - There are 10 lobules present: - The human breast typically has 15-20 lobules, not just 10. Option D - The nipple is located at the level of the fourth intercostal space in most women: - While the nipple is commonly said to be at the level of the fourth intercostal space, this can vary greatly among individuals and is not a definitive anatomical landmark for all women.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1212; Solution Pages 1245-1246","sourcePage":1212,"sourcePageEnd":1212},{"id":"anatomy-29-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Identify the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior end of rib"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior end of rib"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right pulmonary artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Costal cartilage"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anterior end of rib","explanation":"Option A - Anterior end of Rib: - The image shown is a chest X-ray in PA view, as indicated by the rib reaching the midline (vertebrae). Posterior ribs appear more horizontal, while anterior ribs are more oblique. The anterior end of the rib cannot be seen attached to the vertebrae because costal cartilages are not visible in an X-ray. The arrow is pointing to the anterior rib, making Option A the correct answer The image shown is a chest X-ray in PA view, as indicated by the rib reaching the midline (vertebrae). Posterior ribs appear more horizontal, while anterior ribs are more oblique. The anterior end of the rib cannot be seen attached to the vertebrae because costal cartilages are not visible in an X-ray. The arrow is pointing to the anterior rib, making Option A the correct answer Incorrect Options: - Option B, C and D: Refer to explanation of Option A","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1212-1213; Solution Page 1246","sourcePage":1212,"sourcePageEnd":1213},{"id":"anatomy-29-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Identify the vascular structure marked in the given radiology image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aberrant right subclavian artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aberrant left subclavian artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ascending aorta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Main pulmonary artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Aberrant right subclavian artery","explanation":"Correct Option A - Aberrant right subclavian artery: - This is a common vascular anomaly where the right subclavian artery arises abnormally from the aortic arch and travels behind the esophagus to the right arm. This is a common vascular anomaly where the right subclavian artery arises abnormally from the aortic arch and travels behind the esophagus to the right arm. Incorrect Options: Option B - Aberrant left subclavian artery: - An aberrant left subclavian artery is a rare anomaly compared to the right. - It would arise from the right side and travel to the left, but this is not the typical presentation of aberrant subclavian arteries. An aberrant left subclavian artery is a rare anomaly compared to the right. It would arise from the right side and travel to the left, but this is not the typical presentation of aberrant subclavian arteries. Option C - Ascending aorta: - The ascending aorta is the initial section of the aorta arising from the heart and extending upwards. - It would not have the characteristic path seen in an aberrant subclavian artery. The ascending aorta is the initial section of the aorta arising from the heart and extending upwards. It would not have the characteristic path seen in an aberrant subclavian artery. Option D - Main pulmonary artery: - The main pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. - Its course and branching are distinct and do not resemble the path of an aberrant subclavian artery. The main pulmonary artery carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs. Its course and branching are distinct and do not resemble the path of an aberrant subclavian artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1213-1214; Solution Pages 1246-1247","sourcePage":1213,"sourcePageEnd":1214},{"id":"anatomy-29-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":9,"question":"What structure develops from the region labeled as \"A\" in the model of embryological heart development?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Atria"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left ventricle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Infundibulum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Interventricular septum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Infundibulum","explanation":"Correct Option C - Infundibulum - In the embryological development of the heart, the area marked as \"A\" corresponds to the outflow tract of the developing heart. This outflow tract gives rise to several structures, including the infundibulum. The infundibulum is a conical structure located at the outflow of the right ventricle. It connects the right ventricle to the pulmonary artery and directs blood flow from the heart to the lungs. Incorrect Options Option A - Atria: The atria develop from the primitive heart tube, specifically from the regions known as the primitive atrium and sinus venosus. Option B - Left ventricle: The left ventricle develops from the primitive heart tube and forms the main pumping chamber of the heart that sends oxygenated blood to the systemic circulation. Option D - Interventricular septum: The interventricular septum develops from the fusion of several components during heart development.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1214-1215; Solution Pages 1247-1248","sourcePage":1214,"sourcePageEnd":1215},{"id":"anatomy-29-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Identify the level of the cross-section shown in the image below:- **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T1-T2"},{"letter":"B","text":"T2-T3"},{"letter":"C","text":"T3-T4"},{"letter":"D","text":"T4-T5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. T3-T4","explanation":"Correct Option C - T3-T4: - The image shows the arch of the aorta, brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery, we can make an inference about the level of the cross-section. - The cross-section shown in the image corresponds to the level between the T3 and T4 vertebrae. Incorrect Options Option A - T1-T2: This option indicates a cross-section at the level between the T1 and T2 vertebrae, which is different from the structures mentioned in the image description. Option B - T2-T3: This option indicates a cross-section at the level between the T2 and T3 vertebrae, which is also different from the structures mentioned in the image description. Option D - T4-T5: This option indicates a cross-section at the level between the T4 and T5 vertebrae, which is not consistent with the structures seen in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1215-1216; Solution Page 1248","sourcePage":1215,"sourcePageEnd":1216},{"id":"anatomy-29-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Mediastinal surface of the right lung is related to? A. The Azygos vein B. The Right Lymphatic Duct C. The Aorta D. The Trachea","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A only"},{"letter":"B","text":"B & D only"},{"letter":"C","text":"C only"},{"letter":"D","text":"A & D only"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A only","explanation":"Correct Option A - A only : - The Azygos vein: The azygos vein is a major vein that drains blood from the posterior part of the thoracic wall into the superior vena cava. It does run along the mediastinal surface of the right lung, making option A correct. Incorrect Options: Option B - Lymphatic vessel that drains lymph from the upper right quadrant of the body. Option C - While the aortais located in the mediastinum, it is positioned more towards the left side rather than the mediastinal surface of the right lung. Option D - The trachea travels down the center of the thoracic cavity, behind the mediastinal surface of both lungs. Although it's close to the mediastinal surface of the right lung, it's not directly connected to it.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1216; Solution Pages 1248-1249","sourcePage":1216,"sourcePageEnd":1216},{"id":"anatomy-29-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Sternum is attached to scapula via?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Manubrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Clavicle"},{"letter":"C","text":"First rib"},{"letter":"D","text":"Second rib"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Clavicle","explanation":"Correct Option B - Clavicle - Clavicle: The clavicle is a bone that connects the sternum (specifically the manubrium) to the scapula. It forms the acromioclavicular joint with the scapula, providing a direct attachment between the sternum and the scapula. This makes option B correct. Incorrect Options: Option A - Manubrium: The manubrium is the top portion of the sternum and forms the sternoclavicular joint with the clavicle. However, it doesn't directly connect to the scapula. Option C - First rib: The first rib, attaching to the sternum at the sternocostal joint, isn't directly involved in connecting the sternum to the scapula. Option D - Second rib: The second rib, like the first rib, attaches to the sternum at the sternocostal joint, but it isn't directly linked to the scapula.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1216-1217; Solution Page 1249","sourcePage":1216,"sourcePageEnd":1217},{"id":"anatomy-29-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The region marked corresponds to the surface marking for which of the following structures:- **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thoracic duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right atrium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferomedial bronchopulmonary segments"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hilum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Right atrium","explanation":"Correct Option B - Right atrium: The marked region corresponds to the surface marking for the right atrium, which aligns with the right sternal border. Incorrect Option: Option A - The thoracic duct in the posterior mediastinum drains lymph from the lower and left upper body into the venous system but isn't represented by the marked region on the image. Option C - Inferomedial bronchopulmonary segments are specific regions within the lungs. They are not related to the region marked on the image, which is external to the lungs. Option D - The hilum is where blood vessels, nerves, and bronchi enter or exit an organ, like the hila of the lungs. However, the marked region on the image isn't the hilum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1217; Solution Pages 1249-1250","sourcePage":1217,"sourcePageEnd":1217},{"id":"anatomy-29-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Marked structure is derived from the ? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Endocardial cushions"},{"letter":"B","text":"Septum primum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left venous valve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bulbous venosus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Endocardial cushions","explanation":"Correct Option A - Endocardial cushions: The endocardial cushions play a crucial role in the development of the heart's septa and valves. They contribute to the formation of various structures, including the atrial septum, ventricular septum, and the atrioventricular (AV) valves. Therefore, it is correct to say that the marked structure is derived from the endocardial cushions. Incorrect Option: Option B - Septum primum: During embryonic development, the septum primum grows downward from the roof of the primitive atrium but doesn't directly form the marked structure. Option C - Left venous valve: The left venous valve regulates blood flow from the coronary sinus in the right atrium but doesn't contribute to the marked structure's formation. Option D - Bulbous venosus: The bulbous venosus, part of the embryonic heart tube, helps form the right ventricle but doesn't directly create the marked structure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1217-1218; Solution Page 1250","sourcePage":1217,"sourcePageEnd":1218},{"id":"anatomy-29-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following does not supply the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right coronary artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior interventricular artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Marginal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diagonal artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Diagonal artery","explanation":"Correct Option D - Diagonal artery: The diagonal artery does not supply the marked structure (right ventricle). The diagonal artery is a branch of the left anterior descending artery and primarily supplies the left ventricle and the anterior walls of the heart. Incorrect Option: Option A - The right coronary artery supplies blood to the right ventricle, which is the marked structure. It's one of the main arteries responsible for nourishing the right side of the heart.. Option B - The anterior interventricular artery, a branch of the left coronary artery, supplies blood to the interventricular septum and front walls of both ventricles, including the right ventricle. Option C - The marginal artery, a branch of the right coronary artery, supplies blood to the right ventricle. It runs along the heart's right border, providing blood to the right ventricle's lateral wall.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1218-1219; Solution Pages 1250-1251","sourcePage":1218,"sourcePageEnd":1219},{"id":"anatomy-29-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":16,"question":"In the given section of the heart, match the marked (A,B,C&D;) structures. Musculi pectinate Anterior papillary muscle of left ventricle Anterior papillary muscle of right ventricle Anterior leaflet of mitral valve membranous part of ventricular septum **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-5, B-2, C-1, D-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-1, B-2, C-5, D-3"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-5, B-3, C-2, D-1"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-1, B-2, C-4, D-5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. A-5, B-3, C-2, D-1","explanation":"Correct Option C - A-5, B-3, C-2, D-1: A - Membranous part of ventricular septum B - Anterior papillary muscle of right ventricle C - Anterior papillary muscle of left ventricle D - Musculi pectinate Incorrect Options - A, B & D are incorrect answer","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1219-1220; Solution Page 1251","sourcePage":1219,"sourcePageEnd":1220},{"id":"anatomy-29-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":17,"question":"The structure marked in the image drains into: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Junction of superior vena cava and left brachiocephalic veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thoracic Duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Directly into the coronary sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Azygos vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Thoracic Duct","explanation":"Correct Option B - Thoracic Duct - The marked structure is the thoracic duct. The thoracic duct runs in the thorax and is the continuation of the cisterna chyli. It drains all body parts except the right half above the diaphragm. Left-sided trunks empty into it, and it ultimately drains into the left venous angle. I ncorrect Options: Option A - The superior vena cava forms from the left and right innominate veins, returning deoxygenated blood from the head, neck, upper limbs, and torso to the right atrium. Option C - The coronary sinus is formed by several smaller veins that feed into it. It is located in the heart's posterior (back) surface between the left atrium and left ventricle. Option D - The azygos vein usually originates from the inferior vena cava's posterior aspect at the renal veins' level. It drains into the superior vena cava.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1220-1221; Solution Pages 1251-1252","sourcePage":1220,"sourcePageEnd":1221},{"id":"anatomy-29-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Based on the clinical image below, what is the most probable nerve to be injured? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thoracodorsal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Axillary nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intercostal nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Long thoracic nerve"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Long thoracic nerve","explanation":"Correct Option D: Long thoracic nerve - Injury to the long thoracic nerve results in weakness of the serratus anterior and winging of the scapula, as seen in the image. - The long thoracic nerve, from the upper part of the brachial plexus's superior trunk, usually receives contributions from cervical nerves C5, C6, and C7. It innervates the serratus anterior muscle, weakness of which often causes scapular winging due to inadequate protraction countered by rhomboid and trapezius actions, leading to medial scapular winging. Incorrect Option: Option A - The thoracodorsal nerve, mainly from C7 with contributions from C6 to C8, innervates the Latissimus dorsi muscle. It's often utilized as a nerve graft in surgeries. Option B - The axillary nerve, arising from the brachial plexus's posterior cord, controls the teres minor, deltoid muscles, shoulder skin, and glenohumeral joint, often injured in orthopedic surgeries, shoulder dislocations, and rotator cuff tears. . Option D - The intercostal nerve, from T1 to T11 spinal nerves, provides sensory innervation to the chest wall and abdomen skin and motor innervation to intercostal and abdominal muscles.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1221-1222; Solution Page 1252","sourcePage":1221,"sourcePageEnd":1222},{"id":"anatomy-29-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":19,"question":"The inferior scapular angle is at which vertebrae level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T4"},{"letter":"B","text":"T7"},{"letter":"C","text":"T8"},{"letter":"D","text":"T2"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. T7","explanation":"Correct Option B - T7: - The superior scapular angle is located at T2, while the inferior scapular angle is situated at T7. Incorrect Options: A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1222; Solution Pages 1252-1253","sourcePage":1222,"sourcePageEnd":1222},{"id":"anatomy-29-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Which artery does not provide blood supply to the corresponding area of the dura mater among the following options?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Middle meningeal artery – anterior cranial fossa"},{"letter":"B","text":"Accessory meningeal branch of maxillary artery – middle cranial fossa"},{"letter":"C","text":"Meningeal branch of internal carotid artery – posterior cranial fossa"},{"letter":"D","text":"Meningeal branch of anterior and posterior ethmoid artery – anterior cranial fossa"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Meningeal branch of internal carotid artery – posterior cranial fossa","explanation":"Correct Option C - Meningeal branch of internal carotid artery – posterior cranial fossa: - Meningeal branch of the internal carotid artery – posterior cranial fossa: The internal carotid artery primarily supplies the brain with blood, but it does not directly supply the dura mater in the posterior cranial fossa. Instead, the posterior cranial fossa's dura mater is mainly supplied by branches of the vertebral arteries, including the posterior meningeal arteries. Incorrect Options: Option A - Middle meningeal artery – anterior cranial fossa: The middle meningeal artery is a branch of the maxillary artery and supplies the dura mater in the anterior cranial fossa, including the frontal and parietal regions. Option B - Accessory meningeal branch of the maxillary artery – middle cranial fossa: The accessory meningeal branch of the maxillary artery supplies the dura mater in the middle cranial fossa, which includes the temporal and sphenoid regions. Option D - Meningeal branch of anterior and posterior ethmoid artery – anterior cranial fossa: The meningeal branches of the anterior and posterior ethmoid arteries supply the dura mater in the anterior cranial fossa, specifically the ethmoid and frontal regions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1222-1223; Solution Page 1253","sourcePage":1222,"sourcePageEnd":1223},{"id":"anatomy-29-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Common carotid artery pulse is taken at the level of?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cricoid cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Laryngeal prominence"},{"letter":"C","text":"Suprasternal notch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Angle of mandible"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Cricoid cartilage","explanation":"Correct Option A - Cricoid cartilage: - Common carotid artery bifurcates in the carotid triangle at the superior border of the lamina of the thyroid cartilage. - The common carotid artery pulse is felt at the cricoid cartilage in the carotid triangle. Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1223; Solution Pages 1253-1254","sourcePage":1223,"sourcePageEnd":1223},{"id":"anatomy-29-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":22,"question":"While administering epidural anesthesia, which structure is not punctured before injecting the drug?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supraspinous ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Interspinous ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ligamentum flavum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Arachnoid mater"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Arachnoid mater","explanation":"Correct Option D - Arachnoid mater: - The arachnoid mater is not punctured while administering epidural anesthesia. - The arachnoid mater extends up to the S2 vertebrae. - The arachnoid and the dura matter are punctured while performing lumbar puncture. - Structures puncture in the epidural anesthesia Skin Superficial fascia Supraspinous ligament Interspinous ligament Ligamentum flavum - Skin - Superficial fascia - Supraspinous ligament - Interspinous ligament - Ligamentum flavum - Following the piercing of the above structures the epidural space is reached and the anesthesia is injected. - Skin - Superficial fascia - Supraspinous ligament - Interspinous ligament - Ligamentum flavum Incorrect Options: Option A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1223; Solution Pages 1254-1255","sourcePage":1223,"sourcePageEnd":1223},{"id":"anatomy-29-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Identify the arrow-marked chamber? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right atrium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left atrium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right ventricle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left ventricle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Right ventricle","explanation":"Correct Option C - Right ventricle - The structure marked in the above scan is the right ventricle. Incorrect Options - A,B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1223-1224; Solution Page 1255","sourcePage":1223,"sourcePageEnd":1224},{"id":"anatomy-29-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Identify the incorrect match: -","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-cilia"},{"letter":"B","text":"4-lamina propria"},{"letter":"C","text":"2-goblet cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"3-nuclei of epithelial cell"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 4-lamina propria","explanation":"Correct Option B - 4-lamina propria: - The incorrect match is option b. 4 - lamina propria. The lamina propria is a layer of connective tissue found in various mucous membranes, including the respiratory epithelium. It is situated beneath the epithelium and provides support and nourishment to the overlying epithelial cells. However the labelled layer in the picture is thick basement membrane. Incorrect Options: Option A - 1 - cilia: Cilia are hair-like structures on respiratory epithelial cells, aiding in moving mucus and trapped particles out of the respiratory tract. Option C - 2 - goblet cells: Goblet cells are specialized epithelial cells found in the respiratory epithelium. They are so named because of their distinctive goblet-like shape. Option D - 3 - nuclei of epithelial cell: The nuclei of epithelial cells are present within each respiratory epithelial cell. They are the central organelles that contain the cell's genetic material in the form of DNA.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1224-1225; Solution Pages 1255-1256","sourcePage":1224,"sourcePageEnd":1225},{"id":"anatomy-29-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":25,"question":"After a fall from a height with his hand outstretched, a man is brought to the emergency department. An X-ray reveals a fracture in the clavicle. Which specific region of the clavicle is the most likely to be fractured?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Junction of medial 2/3rd and lateral 1/3rd"},{"letter":"B","text":"Junction of medial 1/3rd and lateral 2/3rd"},{"letter":"C","text":"Junction of middle 1/3rd and medial 1/3rd"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle of the clavicle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Junction of medial 2/3rd and lateral 1/3rd","explanation":"Correct Option A - Junction of medial 2/3rd and lateral 1/3rd - The junction of medial 2/3rd and lateral 1/3rd of the clavicle. This is the most common site of clavicular fracture, accounting for approximately 80% of all clavicular fractures. This area is also known as the \"junction\" or \"transition zone\" because it is where the clavicle changes from a thicker, more rounded bone to a thinner, flatter bone. Incorrect Options: Option B - Junction of medial 1/3rd and lateral 2/3rd: Clavicular fractures at this junction are rare due to its thickness and strength, needing considerable force to cause a fracture Option C - Junction of middle 1/3rd and medial 1/3rd: Fractures at the middle 1/3rd and medial 1/3rd junction of the clavicle are uncommon due to its thinner and more flexible nature. Option D - Middle of the clavicle: Mid-clavicular fractures are uncommon and typically result from direct trauma or a fall onto the shoulder, rather than a fall onto an outstretched hand.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1225; Solution Pages 1256-1257","sourcePage":1225,"sourcePageEnd":1225},{"id":"anatomy-29-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":26,"question":"Which of the following structures passes through the foramen marked by the arrow? (or) Which structure passes through the foramen marked by the arrow? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oesophagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thoracic duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sympathetic trunk"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Thoracic duct","explanation":"Correct Option C - Thoracic Duct: The arrow marks the aortic hiatus, a passage in the diaphragm at the lower T-12 border where the descending thoracic aorta continues as the abdominal aorta. The thoracic duct, a large lymphatic vessel, passes through here, draining lymphatic fluid and immune cells from the lower body and left upper body side. It enters the thoracic cavity at T12, ultimately emptying into the left subclavian vein. The diaphragm aids in keeping the thoracic duct open during inhalation, aiding lymphatic fluid flow. Incorrect Options: - Option A - The esophagus passes through the diaphragm at the level of the T10 vertebra (the tenth thoracic vertebra) and enters the stomach just below the diaphragm. - Option B - Inferior vena cava: The inferior vena cava passes through the diaphragm at the level of the T8 vertebra (the eighth thoracic vertebra) and enters the heart’s right atrium. - Option D - Sympathetic trunk: The sympathetic trunk passes through the diaphragm at multiple levels, with some fibers entering the abdomen and others remaining in the thoracic cavity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1225-1226; Solution Pages 1257-1258","sourcePage":1225,"sourcePageEnd":1226},{"id":"anatomy-29-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":27,"question":"On palpation at the site marked by the red arrow on the image, a bony ridge is felt. What is the vertebral level of the marked arrow? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T3-T4 intervertebral disc"},{"letter":"B","text":"T2-T3 intervertebral disc"},{"letter":"C","text":"T5-T6 intervertebral disc"},{"letter":"D","text":"T4-T5 intervertebral disc"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. T4-T5 intervertebral disc","explanation":"Correct Option D - T4-T5 intervertebral disc - If the bony ridge felt during palpation is located above the suprasternal notch , the marked arrow is to be at the T4-T5 intervertebral disc level, representing angle of louis/sternal angle. Incorrect Options: Option A - T3-T4 intervertebral - The T3-T4 intervertebral disc is usually located above at the level of the angle of Louis. Option B - T2-T3 intervertebral disc - The T2-T3 intervertebral disc is usually located at the level of the sternal angle, which is a palpable bony protrusion located at the level of the second rib and the sternum. Option C - T5-T6 intervertebral disc - The T5-T6 intervertebral disc is located approximately at the level of the inferior angle of the scapula, which is a palpable bony landmark located on the posterior aspect of the scapula.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1226-1227; Solution Page 1258","sourcePage":1226,"sourcePageEnd":1227},{"id":"anatomy-29-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":28,"question":"A defect in which of the following pharyngeal arch artery causes the defect shown in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right 4th arch artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left 4th"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right 6th"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left 6th"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Left 6th","explanation":"Correct Option D - Left 6th: - The diagram shows the Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). - Patent Ductus Arteriosus (PDA) is a congenital heart defect where the ductus arteriosus, linking the pulmonary artery and aorta in fetal development, remains open after birth. Typically, it closes within days, allowing independent functioning of the aorta and pulmonary artery. - The sixth left aortic arch gives rise to the left pulmonary arteries, and the distal part of 6 th arch arteries form ductus arteriosus, which supplies blood to the lungs. During development, the pulmonary arteries initially arise from the sixth aortic arch but later become incorporated into the developing lungs. Incorrect Options: Option A - Fourth right aortic arch: The fourth right aortic arch gives rise to the proximal part of the right subclavian artery, which is an artery that supplies blood to the right arm, head, and neck.. Option B - Fourth left aortic arch: The fourth left aortic arch gives rise to the part of the aortic arch, the left subclavian artery, and the proximal part of the common carotid artery. Option C - Sixth right aortic arch: The sixth right aortic arch typically regresses in embryonic development, forming no adult derivative arteries. However, it gives rise to the right pulmonary artery during development.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1227-1228; Solution Pages 1258-1259","sourcePage":1227,"sourcePageEnd":1228},{"id":"anatomy-29-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":29,"question":"Arrange lung hilar structures from anterior to posterior:- Primary bronchus Bronchial artery Pulmonary vein Pulmonary artery","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3,4"},{"letter":"B","text":"2,3,4,1"},{"letter":"C","text":"3,4,1,2"},{"letter":"D","text":"4,3,2,1"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 3,4,1,2","explanation":"Correct Option C - 3, 4, 1, 2: Arrangement of lung hilar structures from anterior to posterior: Pulmonary veins > Pulmonary artery > Primary bronchus > Bronchial arteries Incorrect Options - A, B & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Page 1228; Solution Pages 1259-1260","sourcePage":1228,"sourcePageEnd":1228},{"id":"anatomy-29-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":30,"question":"In the supine position, which lobe of the lung is impacted when aspiration occurs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lingula"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upper part of right inferior lobe"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right superior lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right middle lobe"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Upper part of right inferior lobe","explanation":"Correct Option B - Upper part of right inferior lobe: - In the supine position, aspirate is more likely to enter the right main bronchus due to its wider and more vertical alignment compared to the left main bronchus. Further down, it would likely enter the upper part of the right inferior lobe because the posterior basal segment of the lower lobe is most dependent in the supine position. Incorrect Options: Option A - Lingula: Aspiration often favors the right bronchus due to its anatomy, making aspiration into the lingula, corresponding to the right middle lobe, less likely. Option C - Right superior lobe: The right superior lobe is not the most dependent region in the supine position, so it's less likely to be affected by aspiration. Option D - Right middle lobe: Aspiration may affect the right middle lobe when upright or seated, but in the supine position, the upper part of the right inferior lobe is more susceptible.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1228-1229; Solution Page 1260","sourcePage":1228,"sourcePageEnd":1229},{"id":"anatomy-29-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding anatomical closure is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anatomical closure of foramen ovale is by 3 to 5 days"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anatomical closure of ductus venosus is by 2 to 3 weeks"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right umbilical vein is absent after birth"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anatomical closure of ductus arteriosus is by 2 to 3 weeks"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anatomical closure of foramen ovale is by 3 to 5 days","explanation":"Correct Option A - Anatomical closure of foramen ovale is by 3 to 5 days: - Anatomical closure of the foramen ovale: The foramen ovale is a natural opening between the two atria in the fetal heart that allows blood to bypass the non-functional lungs. - The closure is a gradual process and may take a few weeks to months to complete. Incorrect Options: Option B - Anatomical closure of the ductus venosus is by 2 to 3 weeks: The ductus venosus undergoes anatomical closure within 2 to 3 weeks and eventually becomes a ligament known as the ligamentum venosum. Option C - Right umbilical vein is absent after birth: After birth, the right umbilical vein closes and becomes a fibrous cord known as the ligamentum teres hepatis. Option D - Anatomical closure of the ductus arteriosus is by 2 to 3 weeks: After birth, the ductus arteriosus closes functionally within hours to days and anatomically within 2 to 3 weeks, forming the ligamentum arteriosum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1229; Solution Pages 1260-1261","sourcePage":1229,"sourcePageEnd":1229},{"id":"anatomy-29-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":32,"question":"The type of joint marked in the image below is: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Syndesmosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Synarthrosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synchondrosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Synovial"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Synovial","explanation":"Correct Option D - Synovial: - The image is referring to the costotransverse joint, which is the joint between the rib and the transverse process of the corresponding vertebra. - The costotransverse joint is a synovial joint characterized by a synovial cavity, articular cartilage, a joint capsule, and synovial fluid. Movement in synovial joints is enabled by the synovial cavity and the presence of hyaline cartilage on the articulating surfaces. Incorrect Options: Option A - Syndesmosis: A syndesmosis is a type of joint where the bones are connected by a fibrous connective tissue, such as the tibiofibular joint. Option B - Synarthrosis: Synarthrosis refers to an immovable joint, such as the sutures between the bones of the skull. Option C - Synchondrosis: Synchondrosis is a type of joint where the bones are connected by hyaline cartilage, such as the joint between the first rib and the sternum (costal cartilage).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1229-1230; Solution Pages 1261-1262","sourcePage":1229,"sourcePageEnd":1230},{"id":"anatomy-29-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":33,"question":"Match the auscultatory areas of the heart from the image given below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-aortic, 2-pulmonary, 3-mitral, 4-tricuspid"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-aortic, 2-pulmonary, 3-tricuspid, 4-mitral"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-mitral, 2-pulmonary, 3-tricuspid, 4-aortic"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-aortic,2-tricuspid, 3-pulmonary, 4-mitral"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 1-aortic, 2-pulmonary, 3-tricuspid, 4-mitral","explanation":"Correct Option B: 1-aortic, 2-pulmonary, 3-tricuspid, 4-mitral 1 – Aortic Valve: Second right costal cartilage near the sternum 2 – Pulmonary Valve: Second left intercostal space near the sternum 3 – Tricuspid Valve: Lower end of the sternum 4 – Mitral Valve: Cardiac apex Incorrect options - A, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Page 1230; Solution Page 1262","sourcePage":1230,"sourcePageEnd":1230},{"id":"anatomy-29-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Third part of the vertebral artery is related to which of the following ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4"},{"letter":"B","text":"3"},{"letter":"C","text":"2"},{"letter":"D","text":"1"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 2","explanation":"Correct Option C - 2: - .The third part of the vertebral artery is related to the lamina of the vertebrae. - The vertebral artery, a key supplier of blood to the brain, travels through the transverse foramina of cervical vertebrae and ascends the cervical spine. Its third segment enters the suboccipital triangle via the foramen magnum and is associated with the posterior arch of the atlas (C1) and the vertebrae's laminae in the suboccipital region. Incorrect Options: Option A. 4 : Anterior arch of vertebra: The anterior arch of the atlas (C1) is not directly linked to the third part of the vertebral artery, despite the artery's passage through the transverse foramina of the cervical vertebrae. Option B. 3 : Superior articular facet:The superior articular facet of a vertebra, found on its posterior aspect, articulates with the vertebra above it, but it doesn't impact the course of the vertebral artery, particularly its third part.. Option D. 1 : Spinous process: The spinous process extends posteriorly from the vertebral arch but isn't directly related to the course of the vertebral artery, especially its third part.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1230-1231; Solution Pages 1262-1263","sourcePage":1230,"sourcePageEnd":1231},{"id":"anatomy-29-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":35,"question":"The image shows the cross-section of the abdominal cavity. Identify the structure marked ‘A.’ **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Descending aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ascending aorta"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Descending aorta","explanation":"Correct Option A - Descending aorta: - The diagram focuses on the posterior mediastinum, and the marked structure is descending aorta. The round structure just in front of the vertebral column is descending aorta, slightly towards the left. The descending aorta is located posterior to the diaphragm, in the midline of the body. It is a continuation of the thoracic aorta and descends along the vertebral column in the posterior mediastinum before entering the abdominal cavity. In the abdominal cavity, it descends along the posterior wall, slightly to the left of the midline, and gives off branches to the abdominal organs. Incorrect Options: Option B - Superior vena cava: The superior vena cava, found in the superior mediastinum, enters the right atrium of the heart. It is not visible in abdominal cavity cross-sections. Option C - Inferior vena cava: The inferior vena cava enters the right atrium from the posterior mediastinum, traveling along the back of the abdominal wall to the right of the midline, collecting blood from the lower body and abdominal organs. Option D - Ascending aorta: The ascending aorta begins at the left ventricle, ascends to the superior mediastinum, and branches into the brachiocephalic trunk, left common carotid artery, and left subclavian artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1231-1232; Solution Page 1263","sourcePage":1231,"sourcePageEnd":1232},{"id":"anatomy-29-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":36,"question":"All of the following are true about the right coronary artery except (or) Which of the following statements is false regarding the right coronary artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It’s diameter is less than left coronary artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"It arises from the right aortic sinus"},{"letter":"C","text":"It gives rise to circumflex coronary branch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right conal artery is it’s first branch"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It gives rise to circumflex coronary branch","explanation":"Correct Option C - It gives rise to the circumflex coronary branch. This statement is false. The right coronary artery does not typically give rise to the circumflex coronary branch. The circumflex branch is a branch of the left coronary artery. It wraps around the left side of the heart and supplies blood to the left atrium and portions of the left ventricle. Incorrect Options: Option A - Its diameter is less than the left coronary artery. This statement is generally true.Left coronary artery (LCA) typically has a slightly larger diameter than the the right coronary artery (RCA) Option B - It arises from the anterior aortic sinus. This statement is true. The right coronary artery originates from the right aortic sinus, which is one of the three aortic sinuses located at the base of the aorta. Option D - Right conal artery is its first branch. This statement is true. The right conal artery is indeed the first branch of the right coronary artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1232-1233; Solution Pages 1263-1264","sourcePage":1232,"sourcePageEnd":1233},{"id":"anatomy-29-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":37,"question":"Which of the following statements is false about thoracolumbar fascia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They contain nociceptive nerve endings"},{"letter":"B","text":"In the lumbar region, it is in 4 layers"},{"letter":"C","text":"In the thoracic region, it is attached medially to the vertebrae"},{"letter":"D","text":"The anterior, posterior and middle join to form the aponeurotic origin of transversus abdominis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. In the lumbar region, it is in 4 layers","explanation":"Correct Option B - In the lumbar region, it is in 4 layers - The lumbar thoracolumbar fascia consists of three layers: the posterior, middle, and anterior layers. These layers provide structural support and stability to the lumbar region, facilitating movement and biomechanical functions. Incorrect Options: Option A - They contain nociceptive nerve endings: This statement is correct. The Thoracolumbar fascia contains nociceptive nerve endings. These nerve endings are responsible for a few types of back pains. Option C - In the thoracic region, it is attached medially to the vertebrae : This statement is correct. The thoracic part of the thoracolumbar fascia is attached medially to the spines of the vertebrae. Option D - The anterior, posterior and middle join to form the aponeurotic origin of transversus abdominis: This statement is correct. The anterior middle and posterior layers of the thoracolumbar fascia form the aponeurosis of the transversus abdominis muscle anteriorly","sourceRef":"Question Page 1233; Solution Page 1264","sourcePage":1233,"sourcePageEnd":1233},{"id":"anatomy-29-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Which of the following is wrongly labeled here?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-superior thoracic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"B – thoracoacromial artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"C-subscapular artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"D-posterior circumflex artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. C-subscapular artery","explanation":"Correct Options C - C-subscapular artery - The artery marked as C in the image is incorrectly marked as a subscapular artery; it is the Brachial artery . - The brachial artery is the continuation of the axillary artery in the arm starting from the lower border of the teres major muscle whereas the subscapular artery is a branch arising from the third part of axillary artery. Incorrect Options: Option A - A-Superior thoracic artery: The structure marked A in the image is the superior thoracic artery, which is a branch of the first part of the axillary artery. Option B - B-Thoracoacromial artery: Structure B in the image represents the thoracoacromial artery, one of the two branches from the second part of the axillary artery. Option D - D-Posterior circumflex artery: Structure D in the image represents the posterior circumflex artery, one of the three branches originating from the third part of the axillary artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1233; Solution Pages 1264-1265","sourcePage":1233,"sourcePageEnd":1233},{"id":"anatomy-29-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":39,"question":"The joint between the attachment of the 8th and 9th rib to the 7th rib is a:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fibrocartilaginous joint"},{"letter":"B","text":"Symphyseal joint"},{"letter":"C","text":"Synovial joint"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibrous joint"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Synovial joint","explanation":"Correct Option C - Synovial joint - The joint between the attachment of the 8th and 9th rib to the 7th rib is classified as a synovial joint. - Synovial joints are characterized by the presence of a joint cavity filled with synovial fluid, which allows for smooth movement between the articulating surfaces. Incorrect Options: Option A - Fibrocartilaginous joint: Fibrocartilaginous joints, like intervertebral discs and the pubic symphysis, provide stability with limited movement due to fibrocartilage between bones. . Option B - Symphyseal joint: Symphyseal joints, featuring hyaline and fibrocartilage, allow limited movement and are seen in the pubic symphysis and intervertebral discs. Option D - Fibrous joint: Fibrous joints, like skull sutures, typically allow minimal movement due to fibrous connective tissue. However, the joint between the 8th and 9th rib attachment to the 7th rib permits movement and isn't solely fibrous.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1233-1234; Solution Pages 1265-1266","sourcePage":1233,"sourcePageEnd":1234},{"id":"anatomy-29-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":40,"question":"Which of the following statements about Sibson's fascia is not correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Attached to the inner border of 2 nd rib"},{"letter":"B","text":"Covers apical part of lung"},{"letter":"C","text":"Not a part of scalenus minimus muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vessels pass above the fascia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Attached to the inner border of 2 nd rib","explanation":"Correct Option A: Attached to the inner border of 2 nd rib - Sibson's fascia attaches to the inner border of the 1st rib and covers the apical part of the lung. - Sibson's fascia, also known as the suprapleural membrane, is a thin layer of connective tissue located at the apex of the lung. Incorrect Options: Option B - Covers apical part of the lung: This statement is correct. Sibson's fascia covers the apical part of the lung and forms a protective layer over the lung apex. Option C - Not a part of scalenus minimus muscle: This statement is correct. The scalenus minimus muscle is a small muscle found in some individuals near the apex of the lung, but it is not directly associated with Sibson's fascia. Option D - Vessels pass above the fascia: This statement is correct. Sibson's fascia shields the lung apex from blood vessels like the subclavian vessels and brachiocephalic veins.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1234; Solution Pages 1266-1267","sourcePage":1234,"sourcePageEnd":1234},{"id":"anatomy-29-41","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":41,"question":"The structure not passing through the aortic opening of the diaphragm is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Azygous vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Thoracic duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vagal trunk"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Vagal trunk","explanation":"Correct Option D - Vagal trunk - The aortic opening of the diaphragm, also known as the aortic hiatus, is an opening in the diaphragm through which structures pass between the thoracic and abdominal cavities. - It is located at the level of the 12th thoracic vertebra. - The vagal trunk comes through an esophageal opening at 10th thoracic vertebra. Incorrect Options Option A - Aorta: The aorta, the largest artery in the body, passes through the aortic opening of the diaphragm.. Option B - Azygous vein: The azygous vein, an important venous channel in the thorax, ascends on the right side of the vertebral column and passes through the aortic opening of the diaphragm. Option C - Thoracic duct: The thoracic duct, the main lymphatic vessel in the body, also passes through the aortic opening of the diaphragm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1234; Solution Page 1267","sourcePage":1234,"sourcePageEnd":1234},{"id":"anatomy-29-42","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":42,"question":"The pre tracheal layer of deep cervical fascia encloses all of the following except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oesophagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sternocleidomastoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sternohyoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thyroid gland"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Sternocleidomastoid","explanation":"Correct Option B - Sternocleidomastoid: The deep cervical fascia is a connective tissue that partitions the neck and supports its structures. The pretracheal layer of the deep cervical fascia surrounds the trachea, esophagus, and thyroid gland in the front of the neck. However, it doesn't cover the sternocleidomastoid muscle, a major neck muscle that sits above it. Incorrect Options: Option A - Oesophagus: The pretracheal layer of the deep cervical fascia envelops the esophagus, providing a protective covering as it traverses the neck. Option C - Sternohyoid:The pretracheal layer of the deep cervical fascia envelops the sternohyoid muscle, situated beneath the sternocleidomastoid muscle in the front of the neck. . Option D - Thyroid gland: The pretracheal layer of the deep cervical fascia encloses the thyroid gland. It forms a sheath around the gland, providing support and protection.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1234-1235; Solution Pages 1267-1268","sourcePage":1234,"sourcePageEnd":1235},{"id":"anatomy-29-43","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":43,"question":"What is the most prominent indication of breast cancer among the options provided?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Peau d’orange"},{"letter":"B","text":"Nipple retraction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Puckering"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both nipple retraction and puckering"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Peau d’orange","explanation":"Correct Option A - Peau d’orange - Peau d'orange refers to the appearance of the breast skin that resembles the texture of an orange peel. It is caused by the infiltration and thickening of the breast skin due to underlying breast cancer. This condition is characterized by dimpling, puckering, or indentation of the skin, giving it a pebbled or orange peel-like appearance. It occurs due to the obstruction or invasion of lymphatic vessels by cancer cells, resulting in lymphatic congestion and edema. Incorrect Options:: Option B - Nipple retraction: Nipple retraction or inversion can be a sign of breast cancer, but it is not the most conspicuous sign. Option C - Puckering: Puckering or dimpling of the breast skin can be a sign of breast cancer, but it is not the most conspicuous sign.. Option D - Both nipple retraction and puckering: Nipple retraction and puckering can signal breast cancer, but the most noticeable sign is often peau d'orange.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1235; Solution Page 1268","sourcePage":1235,"sourcePageEnd":1235},{"id":"anatomy-29-44","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-29","chapter":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 29 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":29,"questionNumber":44,"question":"Which of the following veins drain directly into the brachiocephalic vein?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hemiazygous vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior thyroid veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right superior intercostal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left superior intercostal vein"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Left superior intercostal vein","explanation":"Correct Option D - Left superior intercostal vein - The left superior intercostal vein collects blood from the 2nd and 3rd posterior intercostal veins on the left side of the body and typically directs this blood flow into the left brachiocephalic vein. Incorrect Options: Option A: Hemiazygous vein: Instead of directly connecting to the brachiocephalic vein, it usually joins with the azygous vein, which then empties into the superior vena cava. Option B: Superior thyroid veins: The superior and middle thyroid veins drain the thyroid gland, flowing into the internal jugular vein rather than directly into the brachiocephalic vein. Option C: Right superior intercostal vein: The right superior intercostal vein drains upper right intercostal spaces, usually joining the azygous vein before reaching the superior vena cava, rather than directly connecting to the brachiocephalic vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1235; Solution Pages 1268-1269","sourcePage":1235,"sourcePageEnd":1235},{"id":"anatomy-30-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A newborn presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. An abdominal X-ray shows signs of bowel obstruction. Intraoperatively, the surgeon finds multiple fibrous bands with the cecum on the left side and the small intestine on the right side. What is the most likely underlying anomaly?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Nonrotation of the midgut"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reversed rotation of the midgut"},{"letter":"C","text":"Incomplete midgut rotation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Midgut volvulus with normal rotation"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Nonrotation of the midgut","explanation":"Nonrotation of the midgut refers to a congenital anomaly where the midgut only completes the first 90° of the normal 270° counterclockwise rotation, leaving the midgut improperly positioned within the abdomen. This leads to abnormal positioning of the small intestine on the right and the colon on the left, predisposing the infant to volvulus and obstruction, as seen in this case. The bilious vomiting and abdominal distension are typical presentations of midgut volvulus. Types of malrotation Characteristic Non-Rotation Incomplete Rotation Reverse Rotation Features Failure of normal 270° Incomplete rotation. Large Intestines rotate 90° counterclockwise rotation. intestine passes behind clockwise instead of Only the first 90° rotation the duodenum and the counterclockwise. Large occurs. Small intestine is superior mesenteric artery intestine returns earlier on the right side and large (SMA). SMA compresses and lies behind the intestine on the left. the duodenum. duodenum and SMA. Small intestine misplaced anteriorly. Clinical Presentation Abdominal distension Bilious vomiting Abdominal Bilious vomiting Failure to Bilious vomiting (if volvulus pain and distension thrive Symptoms of bowel occurs) Absent IC junction Features of duodenal obstruction Possible shock in RIF IC junction may be obstruction (due to SMA if volvulus is present. midline, below the pylorus. compression). Complications Volvulus Intestinal obstruction Surgical Management Ladd’s procedure Midgut volvulus with normal rotation (Option D): Midgut volvulus can still occur even with normal rotation, but it’s not the underlying cause here as the infant has abnormal rotation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1270; Solution Pages 1282-1283","sourcePage":1270,"sourcePageEnd":1270},{"id":"anatomy-30-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A newborn presents with bilious vomiting shortly after birth. X-ray abdomen is taken and is shown below. Further evaluation reveals pancreatic tissue encircling and compressing the second part of the duodenum. Which embryological structure is primarily responsible for this congenital abnormality?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal pancreatic bud"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral pancreatic bud"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hepatic diverticulum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitelline duct"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ventral pancreatic bud","explanation":"Bilious vomiting, x-ray showing double bubble sign of duodenal obstruction and evaluation indicating presence of pancreatic tissue encircling and compressing the second part of the duodenum is suggestive of Annular pancreas, occurring due to abnormal rotation of the ventral pancreatic bud . Development of pancreas: Origin of Pancreatic Buds & its derivatives Dorsal Pancreatic Bud : located in the dorsal mesentery, forms the upper part of the head, neck, body, and tail of the pancreas. Ventral Pancreatic Bud : Arises near the hepatic bud in the ventral mesentery. Forms the lower part of the head and the uncinate process. Rotation and Fusion : Rotation of the duodenal loop brings the ventral pancreatic bud to the right side of the duodenum, eventually fusing with the dorsal bud. Except for the tail, the pancreas becomes retroperitoneal after the fusion and rotation. Duct System Formation Main Pancreatic Duct (wirsung) : Formed from the distal part of the dorsal duct and proximal part of the ventral duct. Accessory Pancreatic Duct (santorini) : Derived from the proximal portion of the dorsal duct. Papillae Openings The main pancreatic duct opens into the duodenum at the major duodenal papilla with the bile duct. The accessory duct opens at the minor duodenal papilla . Exocrine and Endocrine Components Exocrine Pancreas : Derived from endodermal cells that reorganize into acini. Endocrine Pancreas : The islets of Langerhans develop from cells separating from the duct system. Developmental anomalies of Pancreas 1. Annular pancreas: The ventral pancreatic bud consists of two components that typically fuse and rotate around the duodenum, positioning below the dorsal bud. (Option B) Sometimes, the right part migrates normally while the left moves in the opposite direction, leading to malformations that can constrict the 2nd part of the duodenum and cause obstruction. 2. Pancreatic divisum: Failure of fusion of parts of pancreas derived from dorsal and ventral pancreatic buds with each other. This leads to most pancreatic secretions draining through the accessory duct (duct of Santorini) into the minor papilla, which is often smaller and can become obstructed. This incomplete drainage leads to recurrent pancreatitis. Accessory pancreatic tissue Accessory pancreatic tissue can be found in various locations, including: Stomach wall Duodenum (most common) Meckel’s diverticulum Greater omentum Hilum of the spleen It appears as yellowish, lobulated nodules, typically single and ranging from 1–6 mm in diameter. Approximately one-third of these nodules contain islets of Langerhans. Dorsal pancreatic bud (Option A): Forms parts of the pancreas (body, neck, and tail), but does not encircle the duodenum and is not involved in annular pancreas formation. Hepatic diverticulum (Option C): Develops into the liver and bile ducts, unrelated to pancreatic development or duodenal obstruction. Vitelline duct (Option D): Related to the yolk sac and conditions like Meckel's diverticulum, not involved in pancreatic or duodenal development.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1270-1271; Solution Pages 1283-1285","sourcePage":1270,"sourcePageEnd":1271},{"id":"anatomy-30-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements is correct regarding the peritoneal folds of abdomen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The greater omentum attaches to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum, providing a barrier to infections in the peritoneal cavity."},{"letter":"B","text":"The lesser omentum connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the diaphragm and provides support to the liver"},{"letter":"C","text":"The greater omentum forms a fold between the liver and the diaphragm, providing structural support to the stomach."},{"letter":"D","text":"The greater omentum is often used as a free flap in reconstructive surgeries based on gastro-omental vessels in gastrointestinal perforations."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The greater omentum is often used as a free flap in reconstructive surgeries based on gastro-omental vessels in gastrointestinal perforations.","explanation":"based on gastro-omental vessels in gastrointestinal perforations . The remarkable capacity of the greater omentum to participate in reparative processes is highlighted by its use as an omental pedicle flap or free flap in reconstructive surgery: it has been used to support closure of gastrointestinal perforations, fill ‘dead space’ after surgical excision of pathology, and cover wound defects. Peritoneum: The peritoneum consists of 2 layers: the superficial parietal layer and the deep visceral layer. The peritoneal cavity contains the omentum, ligaments, and mesentery. - The lesser omentum (or hepatogastric) is attached to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the liver. - The greater omentum (or gastrocolic) hangs from the greater curvature of the stomach and loops down in front of the intestines before curving back upwards to attach to the transverse colon.In effect it is draped in front of the intestines like an apron and may serve as an insulating or protective layer. Omenta : Sources Structure From To Contains Dorsal mesentery Greater omentum Greater curvature of Transverse colon right and left stomach (and gastroepiploic spleen) vessels and fat Gastrosplenic Stomach Spleen Short gastric artery, ligament Left gastroepiploic artery Gastrophrenic Stomach Diaphragm Left inferior phrenic ligament artery Gastrocolic ligament Stomach Transverse colon Right gastroepiploic artery Splenorenal ligament Spleen Kidney Splenic artery, Tail of pancreas Ventral mesentery Lesser omentum Lesser curvature of Liver The right free the stomach (and margin-hepatic duodenum) artery, portal vein, and bile duct,lymph nodes and the lymph vessels,hepatic plexus of nerve,all enclosed in perivascular fibrous sheath. Along the lesser curvature of the stomach-left and right gastric artery,the gastric group of lymph nodes and lymphatic s, branches from the gastric nerve. Hepatogastric Stomach Liver Right and left gastric ligament artery Hepatoduodenal Duodenum Liver Hepatic artery ligament proper, hepatic portal vein, bile duct, autonomic nerves Horizontal disposition of the peritoneum in the upper part of the abdomen Horizontal disposition of the peritoneum in the lower part of the abdomen Mesenteries Sources Structure From To Contains Dorsal mesentery Mesentery proper Small intestine Posterior abdominal Superior mesenteric (jejunum and ileum) wall artery, accompanying veins, autonomic nerve plexuses, lymphatics, 100–200 lymph nodes and connective tissue with fat Transverse mesocolo Transverse colon Posterior abdominal Middle colic n wall Sigmoid mesocolon Sigmoid colon Pelvic wall Sigmoid arteries and superior rectal artery Mesoappendix Mesentery of ileum Appendix Appendicular artery Other ligaments and folds Sources Structure From To Contains Ventral mesentery Falciform ligament Liver Thoracic diaphragm, Round ligament of anterior abdominal liver, paraumbilical wall vein Left umbilical vein Round ligament of Liver Umbilicus liver Ventral mesentery Coronary ligament Liver Thoracic diaphragm Ductus venosus Ligamentum Liver Liver venosum Phrenicocolic Left colic flexure Thoracic diaphragm ligament Ventral mesentery Left triangular Liver ligament, right triangular ligament Umbilical folds Urinary bladder Ileocecal fold Ileum Cecum Broad ligament of Uterus Pelvic wall Mesovarium, the uterus mesosalpinx, mesometrium Round ligament of Uterus Inguinal canal uterus Suspensory ligament Ovary Pelvic wall Ovarian artery of the ovary - The greater omentum attaches to the lesser curvature of the stomach and the duodenum, providing a barrier to infections in the peritoneal cavity. (Option A) : The greater omentum attaches to the greater curvature of the stomach, not the lesser curvature - The lesser omentum connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the diaphragm and provides support to the liver (Option B) : The lesser omentum connects the lesser curvature of the stomach and the first part of the duodenum to the liver (via the hepatic portal area), not the greater curvature or diaphragm. - The greater omentum forms a fold between the liver and the diaphragm, providing structural support to the stomach. (Option C) : The greater omentum does not connect the liver and diaphragm. The falciform ligament is a thin anteroposterior double fold of peritoneum that represents an anterior portion of the mesentery connecting the liver to the posterior aspect of the anterior abdominal wall.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1271; Solution Pages 1285-1289","sourcePage":1271,"sourcePageEnd":1271},{"id":"anatomy-30-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Match the following with their corresponding dermatomal distribution: Thumb 1) L3 Umblicus 2) L5 Knee 3) C6 Dorsum of foot 4) T10 Thumb 1) L3 Umblicus 2) L5 Knee 3) C6 Dorsum of foot 4) T10 **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A-1, B-2, C-3, D-4"},{"letter":"B","text":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1"},{"letter":"C","text":"A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2"},{"letter":"D","text":"A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2","explanation":"Thumb 1) C6 Umblicus 2) T10 Knee 3) L3 Dorsum of foot 4) L5 Dermatomes: - The sensory fibres in individual spinal and cranial nerves have characteristic, but often overlapping, peripheral (cutaneous) distributions. - As far as the innervation of the body surface is concerned, the area of skin that is supplied by a particular spinal or cranial nerve is referred to as a dermatome, and the areas of skin supplied by named peripheral nerves are often referred to as the peripheral nerve field (or cutaneous nerve distribution) of that nerve. Nerve root Dermatomal distribution C2 Temple, Forehead, Occiput C3 Entire Neck, Posterior Cheek, Temporal Area, Prolongation forward under Mandible C4 Shoulder Area, Clavicular Area, Upper Scapular Area C5 Deltoid Area, Anterior aspect of entire arm to base of thumb C6 Anterior Arm, Radial side of hand to thumb and index finger C7 Lateral Arm and Forearm to index, long, and ring fingers C8 Medial Arm and forearm to long, ring, and little fingers T1 Medial side of forearm to base of little finger T2 Medial side of upper arm to medial elbow, pectoral and midscapular areas T3-6 Upper Thorax T5-7 Costal Margin T8-12 Abdomen and Lumbar Region L1 Back, over trochanter and groin L2 Back, front of thigh to knee L3 Back, upper buttock, anterior thigh and knee, medial lower leg L4 Medial buttock, latera thigh, medial leg, dorsum of foot, big toe L5 Buttock, posterior and lateral thigh, lateral aspect of leg, dorsum of foot, medial half of sole, first, second, and third toes S1 Buttock, Thigh, and Leg Posterior S2 Same as S1 S3 Groin, medial thigh to knee S4 Perineum, genitals, lower sacrum Clinical significance: - A dermatome is an area of skin supplied by sensory neurons that arise from a spinal nerve ganglion. Symptoms that follow a dermatome (e.g. like pain or a rash) may indicate a pathology that involves the related nerve root. - In referred pain , sensory nerve fibers such as that from dermatomes may come together at the same spinal cord level as the general visceral afferent fibers such as that from the heart. When the general visceral sensory fiber is stimulated, the central nervous system does not clearly discern whether the pain is coming from the body wall or from the viscera, so it perceives the pain as coming from somewhere on the body wall, e.g. left arm/hand pain, jaw pain. So the pain is \"referred to\" the related dermatomes of the same spinal segment.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1271-1272; Solution Pages 1289-1292","sourcePage":1271,"sourcePageEnd":1272},{"id":"anatomy-30-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 35-year-old male is involved in a high-speed motor vehicle accident and presents to the trauma unit with severe abdominal pain and hypotension. On examination, he has contusion on the left side of his abdomen. FAST reveals free fluid in the abdomen. CT scan shows a retroperitoneal hematoma and signs of injury to a retroperitoneal organ. Which of the following organs is most likely to be injured in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Small intestine"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left kidney"},{"letter":"D","text":"D) Spleen"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Left kidney","explanation":"A retroperitoneal hematoma seen on CT scan, along with the clinical signs of severe abdominal pain, contusion, and hypotension, strongly suggests that the left kidney has been injured in this case. The retroperitoneum can occasionally be a site of significant bleeding, usually after trauma, surgical intervention, or even spontaneously in patients with vascular lesions (e.g., abdominal aortic aneurysm) or those treated with anticoagulation therapy. Presentation varies based on etiology. Symptoms can include hypotension, tachycardia, ecchymoses in the affected areas, fatigue, hematuria, and flank or back pain. Computed tomography of the abdomen is the diagnostic imaging of choice. Management of retroperitoneal hematoma is almost always medical, with resuscitation, blood transfusions, and reversal of anticoagulation as necessary. Retroperitoneal Space: The retroperitoneum is an anatomical space located behind the abdominal or peritoneal cavity. Abdominal organs that are not suspended by the mesentery and lie between the abdominal wall and parietal peritoneum are said to lie within the retroperitoneum. Several individual spaces make up the retroperitoneum. These spaces are the anterior pararenal space, posterior pararenal space, and the perirenal space. The retroperitoneum divides into three main anatomical spaces: - The anterior pararenal space: contains the head, neck, and body of the pancreas (the tail of the pancreas is within the splenorenal ligament), ascending and descending colon, and the duodenum (except for the proximal first segment). - The perirenal space: includes the adrenal gland, kidney, ureters, and renal vessels. - The posterior pararenal space : It is surrounded by the posterior leaf of the renal fascia and muscles of the posterior abdominal wall, contains no major organs and is composed primarily of fat, blood vessels, and lymphatics. - There is also a fourth, less well-defined space known as the great vessel space. It lies anterior to the vertebral bodies and psoas muscles and contains the aorta, inferior vena cava, and surrounding fat. - The Stomach (Option A) is an intraperitoneal organ. Trauma to the stomach would generally present with signs of peritoneal irritation and perforation, not retroperitoneal bleeding. - The Small intestine (Option C) is also an intraperitoneal organ. Injury to the small intestine would typically cause signs of peritonitis, but not a retroperitoneal hematoma. - The Spleen (Option D) is an intraperitoneal organ located in the left upper quadrant. Injury to the spleen would more commonly present with signs of intraperitoneal bleeding.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1272; Solution Pages 1292-1294","sourcePage":1272,"sourcePageEnd":1272},{"id":"anatomy-30-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following planes carries the origin of the hepatic portal vein and the termination of the spinal cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Subcostal plane"},{"letter":"B","text":"Supracristal plane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intertubercular plane"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transpyloric plane"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Transpyloric plane","explanation":"The origin of the hepatic portal vein and the termination of the spinal cord are in the transpyloric plane of the abdomen. Quadrants of the abdomen - The abdomen is divided into 4 quadrants by a horizontal transumbilical plane and a vertical median plane . They are: Right upper quadrant Left upper quadrant Right lower quadrant Left lower quadrant - Right upper quadrant - Left upper quadrant - Right lower quadrant - Left lower quadrant - Right upper quadrant - Left upper quadrant - Right lower quadrant - Left lower quadrant - Midline (Median Plane): Passes through the xiphoid process and pubic symphysis. - Paramedian Lines: Left and right lines from midclavicular points to midway between the anterior superior iliac spine and the pubic symphysis. - Xiphisternal Plane : Aligned with the T9 vertebral body; it often intersects the xiphisternal joint. - Transpyloric Plane : Midway between the jugular notch and the superior pubic symphysis; intersects the L1-L2 intervertebral disc or superior half of L2. Key structures include: Superior mesenteric artery origin Hepatic portal vein origin Hilum of the left kidney Renal arteries Duodenojejunal flexure Termination of the spinal cord (conus medullaris) - Superior mesenteric artery origin - Hepatic portal vein origin - Hilum of the left kidney - Renal arteries - Duodenojejunal flexure - Termination of the spinal cord (conus medullaris) - Subcostal Plane (Option A) : Joins the lowest points of the costal arch; typically intersects the vertebral body of L2. - Supracristal Plane (Tuffier’s Line) (Option B) : Joins the superior points of the iliac crests; corresponds to the aortic bifurcation (L4-L5). - Intertubercular Plane (Option C) : Joins iliac tubercles; commonly at the L5 vertebral body. - Plane of the Pubic Crest : In line with the superior pubic symphysis; intersects the head of the femur and coccyx in supine individuals. - Superior mesenteric artery origin - Hepatic portal vein origin - Hilum of the left kidney - Renal arteries - Duodenojejunal flexure - Termination of the spinal cord (conus medullaris) Nine regions of the abdomen - They are formed by the subcostal and intertubercular planes and left and right midclavicular lines. - The regions and their associated contents are: Region Contents Right hypochondrium Liver Gallbladder Epigastric region Stomach Pancreas Duodenum Left hypochondrium Spleen Left colic flexure Right lumbar region Right kidney Right ureter Ascending colon Umbilical region Loops of small intestine Aorta Inferior vena cava Left lumbar region Left kidney Left ureter Descending colon Right iliac region Caecum Appendix Hypogastric region Coils of small intestine Urinary bladder (if distended) Uterus (if enlarged) Left iliac region Sigmoid colon","sourceRef":"Question Page 1272; Solution Pages 1294-1297","sourcePage":1272,"sourcePageEnd":1272},{"id":"anatomy-30-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A triangular area formed in the bladder is called the trigone. Which of the following is the origin of the epithelium of the trigone?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cranial vesicourethral canal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Mesonephric duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Splanchnic mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Allantois"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Mesonephric duct","explanation":"The epithelium of the trigone is from the endothelium of absorbed mesonephric ducts. Development of the Urinary Bladder: A Step-by-Step Process Formation of the Cloaca - Cloaca: The terminal portion of the hindgut in the embryo. - Urorectal Septum: a partition that divides the cloaca into: Dorsal Part: forms the primitive rectum, the later part of the large intestine. Ventral Part: becomes the primitive urogenital sinus - Dorsal Part: forms the primitive rectum, the later part of the large intestine. - Ventral Part: becomes the primitive urogenital sinus - Dorsal Part: forms the primitive rectum, the later part of the large intestine. - Ventral Part: becomes the primitive urogenital sinus Subdivisions of the Primitive Urogenital Sinus - Cranial Part (Upper): Forms the vesicourethral canal, a precursor to the urinary bladder and urethra. - Caudal Part (Lower): Develops into the definitive urogenital sinus, contributing to structures in both males and females. Development of the Vesicourethral Canal - Regions of the Canal: Wider Upper Part: Expands to become the urinary bladder. Narrower Lower Part: Develops into the primitive urethra. - Wider Upper Part: Expands to become the urinary bladder. - Narrower Lower Part: Develops into the primitive urethra. - Wider Upper Part: Expands to become the urinary bladder. - Narrower Lower Part: Develops into the primitive urethra. Incorporation of the Mesonephric Ducts - Mesonephric Ducts: Gradually absorbed into the posterior wall of the vesicourethral canal. - Trigone Formation: Absorbed portions contribute to the formation of the trigone, a triangular area in the bladder wall. Fate of the Allantois - Allantois (Option D) : Initially extends from the bladder apex and atrophies to form the urachus (median umbilical ligament), connecting the bladder to the umbilicus. Cellular Origins and Layers - Epithelium: The inner lining originates from the endoderm of the cranial vesicourethral canal (Option A) ; trigone epithelium derives from the mesoderm of the absorbed mesonephric ducts. - Muscular and Serous Walls: Develop from splanchnic mesoderm (Option C) surrounding the developing gut.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1272-1273; Solution Pages 1297-1300","sourcePage":1272,"sourcePageEnd":1273},{"id":"anatomy-30-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A newborn baby was found to have hydronephrosis. Which of the following would be the cause of this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Obstruction by the umbilical arteries"},{"letter":"B","text":"Crossed ectopia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Malrotation of kidneys"},{"letter":"D","text":"Aberrant renal arteries"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Aberrant renal arteries","explanation":"Aberrant renal arteries cause compression of the ureters and cause hydronephrosis. Ascent of Kidneys: Early Development and Position - Metanephros: The definitive human kidney arises from the metanephros, initially located in the sacral region of the embryo. - Blood Supply: Receives blood from the lateral sacral arteries at this stage. Ascent Process - Differential Growth: As the embryo develops, significant growth of the abdominal wall causes the kidneys to ascend towards the lumbar region. This ascent results from surrounding structures changing positions, not physical movement of the kidneys. - As the embryo develops, significant growth of the abdominal wall causes the kidneys to ascend towards the lumbar region. - This ascent results from surrounding structures changing positions, not physical movement of the kidneys. - Change in Blood Supply: Ascent leads to a shift in blood supply from lateral sacral arteries to higher branches of the aorta. The definitive renal artery originates from the aorta at the second lumbar segment. - Ascent leads to a shift in blood supply from lateral sacral arteries to higher branches of the aorta. - The definitive renal artery originates from the aorta at the second lumbar segment. - As the embryo develops, significant growth of the abdominal wall causes the kidneys to ascend towards the lumbar region. - This ascent results from surrounding structures changing positions, not physical movement of the kidneys. - Ascent leads to a shift in blood supply from lateral sacral arteries to higher branches of the aorta. - The definitive renal artery originates from the aorta at the second lumbar segment. Factors Influencing Ascent - Abdominal Wall Growth: The primary driver of kidney ascent. - Umbilical Arteries: During ascent, kidneys pass between the right and left umbilical arteries. Obstruction by these arteries can impede ascent, causing kidneys to remain in the pelvic region. (Option A) - During ascent, kidneys pass between the right and left umbilical arteries. - Obstruction by these arteries can impede ascent, causing kidneys to remain in the pelvic region. (Option A) - During ascent, kidneys pass between the right and left umbilical arteries. - Obstruction by these arteries can impede ascent, causing kidneys to remain in the pelvic region. (Option A) Rotation of the Kidney - Hilum Reorientation: Initially, the hilum faces anteriorly; as the kidney ascends, it rotates to face medially (towards the midline). - Initially, the hilum faces anteriorly; as the kidney ascends, it rotates to face medially (towards the midline). - Initially, the hilum faces anteriorly; as the kidney ascends, it rotates to face medially (towards the midline). Clinical Significance of Anomalies - Anomalies of Position: Pelvic Kidney: Kidney remains in the sacral region. Incomplete Ascent: Kidney lies opposite the lower lumbar vertebrae. Thoracic Kidney: Rare ascent into the thoracic cavity. Crossed Ectopia (Option B) : Both kidneys are located on the same side of the midline. - Pelvic Kidney: Kidney remains in the sacral region. - Incomplete Ascent: Kidney lies opposite the lower lumbar vertebrae. - Thoracic Kidney: Rare ascent into the thoracic cavity. - Crossed Ectopia (Option B) : Both kidneys are located on the same side of the midline. - Aberrant Renal Arteries: Abnormal kidney position can lead to unusual blood supply, potentially compressing the ureter and causing hydronephrosis (kidney swelling due to urine buildup). - Abnormal kidney position can lead to unusual blood supply, potentially compressing the ureter and causing hydronephrosis (kidney swelling due to urine buildup). - Malrotation (Option C) Abnormal hilum position due to incomplete rotation - Abnormal hilum position due to incomplete rotation - Pelvic Kidney: Kidney remains in the sacral region. - Incomplete Ascent: Kidney lies opposite the lower lumbar vertebrae. - Thoracic Kidney: Rare ascent into the thoracic cavity. - Crossed Ectopia (Option B) : Both kidneys are located on the same side of the midline. - Abnormal kidney position can lead to unusual blood supply, potentially compressing the ureter and causing hydronephrosis (kidney swelling due to urine buildup). - Abnormal hilum position due to incomplete rotation","sourceRef":"Question Page 1273; Solution Pages 1300-1303","sourcePage":1273,"sourcePageEnd":1273},{"id":"anatomy-30-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A newborn presents with a congenital urinary tract anomaly. During evaluation, the physician explains the stages of kidney development and associated structures. Which of the following statements is true regarding the formation of the definitive kidney in mammals?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pronephros is the functional kidney in humans and contributes to the urinary system."},{"letter":"B","text":"Mesonephros develops in the cervical region and contains excretory tubules."},{"letter":"C","text":"Metanephros arises from the metanephric blastema and the ureteric bud."},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral plate mesoderm forms the foundation of the urinary and genital systems."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Metanephros arises from the metanephric blastema and the ureteric bud.","explanation":"Metanephros is the definitive kidney that arises from both the metanephric blastema (forming nephrons) and the ureteric bud (forming the collecting system). Key Players in Development - Intermediate Mesoderm: Forms the foundation of the urinary and genital systems. (Option D) - Cloaca: Part of the hindgut; divides into the rectum and urogenital sinus . - Urogenital Ridge: Develops from the intermediate mesoderm, giving rise to the genital ridge (gonads) and nephrogenic cord (future kidneys). Stages of Kidney Evolution - Pronephros (Most Primitive): Develops in the cervical region of the nephrogenic cord. Nonfunctional in humans; associated duct persists as the mesonephric duct. (Option A) - Develops in the cervical region of the nephrogenic cord. - Nonfunctional in humans; associated duct persists as the mesonephric duct. (Option A) - Mesonephros (Intermediate): Functional in amphibians; develops in the thoracolumbar region. (Option B) Consists of excretory tubules draining into the mesonephric duct; some tubules contribute to the male reproductive system. - Functional in amphibians; develops in the thoracolumbar region. (Option B) - Consists of excretory tubules draining into the mesonephric duct; some tubules contribute to the male reproductive system. - Metanephros (Definitive Kidney): (Option C) Functional kidney in mammals, birds, and reptiles; begins in the sacral region. Arises from two sources: 1. Excretory Tubules (Nephrons): Develop from the metanephric blastema. 2. Collecting System : Forms from the ureteric bud branching from the mesonephric duct. - Functional kidney in mammals, birds, and reptiles; begins in the sacral region. - Arises from two sources: 1. Excretory Tubules (Nephrons): Develop from the metanephric blastema. 2. Collecting System : Forms from the ureteric bud branching from the mesonephric duct. - Develops in the cervical region of the nephrogenic cord. - Nonfunctional in humans; associated duct persists as the mesonephric duct. (Option A) - Functional in amphibians; develops in the thoracolumbar region. (Option B) - Consists of excretory tubules draining into the mesonephric duct; some tubules contribute to the male reproductive system. - Functional kidney in mammals, birds, and reptiles; begins in the sacral region. - Arises from two sources: 1. Excretory Tubules (Nephrons): Develop from the metanephric blastema. 2. Collecting System : Forms from the ureteric bud branching from the mesonephric duct. Step-by-Step Formation of the Kidney - Ureteric Bud Branching: The ureteric bud grows towards metanephric blastema , forming an ampulla . Ampulla divides to form the renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, and collecting tubules. - The ureteric bud grows towards metanephric blastema , forming an ampulla . - Ampulla divides to form the renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, and collecting tubules. - Nephron Formation: Ampulla induces differentiation in the metanephric blastema, leading to nephron formation. Cells form vesicles, elongate into S-shaped tubules , and develop glomeruli through capillary invasion. - Ampulla induces differentiation in the metanephric blastema, leading to nephron formation. - Cells form vesicles, elongate into S-shaped tubules , and develop glomeruli through capillary invasion. - The ureteric bud grows towards metanephric blastema , forming an ampulla . - Ampulla divides to form the renal pelvis, major and minor calyces, and collecting tubules. - Ampulla induces differentiation in the metanephric blastema, leading to nephron formation. - Cells form vesicles, elongate into S-shaped tubules , and develop glomeruli through capillary invasion. Ascent and Rotation of the Kidney - Ascent: Initially located in the sacral region it ascends to the lumbar region as the abdominal wall grows. Acquires new blood supply from higher aortic branches. - Initially located in the sacral region it ascends to the lumbar region as the abdominal wall grows. - Acquires new blood supply from higher aortic branches. - Rotation: Hilum faces anteriorly at first; and rotates medially during ascent. - Hilum faces anteriorly at first; and rotates medially during ascent. - Initially located in the sacral region it ascends to the lumbar region as the abdominal wall grows. - Acquires new blood supply from higher aortic branches. - Hilum faces anteriorly at first; and rotates medially during ascent.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1273; Solution Pages 1303-1305","sourcePage":1273,"sourcePageEnd":1273},{"id":"anatomy-30-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A newborn presents with bilious vomiting and abdominal distension. Imaging reveals malrotation of the midgut. The pediatric surgeon explains that during normal embryological development, the midgut should rotate around a specific arterial axis to reach its anatomical position. How many degrees does the midgut rotate, and what is the axis of this rotation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"180 degrees, Inferior Mesenteric Artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"270 degrees, Superior Mesenteric Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"90 degrees, Inferior Mesenteric Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"360 degrees, Superior Mesenteric Artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 270 degrees, Superior Mesenteric Artery","explanation":"During normal embryological development, the midgut undergoes a rotation of 270 degrees counterclockwise around the axis of the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). This rotation helps position the intestines correctly within the abdominal cavity. If this rotation does not occur properly, it can lead to malrotation. The midgut loop undergoes a counterclockwise rotation (approximately 270 degrees) around the superior mesenteric artery (SMA). This rotation occurs in two phases: - Initial 90-degree Rotation: As the loop begins to retract from the umbilical cord, it rotates 90 degrees counterclockwise. - Final 180-degree Rotation: Once retracted fully into the abdominal cavity, it completes an additional 180-degree rotation. Final Positioning: - After the complete rotation, the small intestine is positioned predominantly in the upper left quadrant, while the colon occupies the right side of the abdomen. - The duodenum becomes fixed to the posterior abdominal wall, which is important for its stability and function. Formation of Mesenteries: The rotation and positioning of the intestines also result in the development of mesenteries, which are double layers of peritoneum that support the intestines and provide blood supply and nerve connections.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1273-1274; Solution Pages 1305-1307","sourcePage":1273,"sourcePageEnd":1274},{"id":"anatomy-30-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 26-year-old woman with a history of recurrent abortion undergoes an ultrasound at 8 weeks of gestation and is found to have an abnormality in the fetus as shown below (arrow). The doctor reassures her that this condition typically resolves on its own. When should she schedule a follow-up ultrasound to monitor the resolution of the abnormality?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"12 weeks"},{"letter":"B","text":"14 weeks"},{"letter":"C","text":"16 weeks"},{"letter":"D","text":") 18 weeks"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 12 weeks","explanation":"Ultrasound shows physiological umbilical hernia. A physiological umbilical hernia is a normal developmental process where the intestines temporarily protrude into the base of the umbilical cord during early pregnancy. This occurs as the fetal abdomen is too small to accommodate the rapidly growing intestines. By around 12 weeks of gestation, the intestines typically return to the abdominal cavity, and the umbilicalhernia resolves on its own. A follow-up ultrasound around 12 weeks is recommended to ensureproper closure and development. Physiological Umbilical Hernia (PUH): Occurs during early embryogenesis due to rapid growth of the midgut and liver, temporarily exceeding the size of the abdominal cavity. - Midgut Herniation (6th Week): The midgut herniates into the umbilical cord as the abdominal cavity is too small. It undergoes a 90-degree counterclockwise rotation around the superior mesenteric artery. - Midgut Retraction (10th-12th Week): The abdominal cavity expands, allowing the midgut to return. During retraction, an additional 180-degree counterclockwise rotation completes the 270-degree total, positioning the intestines. Failure to Regress: Omphalocele: If the midgut doesn't return, omphalocele occurs, where organs remain herniated through the umbilical ring, covered by a membrane. It differs from Gastroschisis, where the organs protrude without a membrane.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1274; Solution Pages 1307-1308","sourcePage":1274,"sourcePageEnd":1274},{"id":"anatomy-30-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which of the following statements about the ligament of Treitz is not true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It connects the duodenojejunal flexure to the right crus of the diaphragm."},{"letter":"B","text":"It contains striated muscle fibers in its upper part."},{"letter":"C","text":"It primarily serves as a conduit for blood supply to the small intestine."},{"letter":"D","text":"It acts as a landmark in the diagnosis of intestinal malrotation."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It primarily serves as a conduit for blood supply to the small intestine.","explanation":"The ligament of Treitz does not serve as a conduit for blood supply to the small intestine ; it is done by the superior mesenteric arteries. The ligament primarily stabilizes the duodenojejunal flexure and serves as an anatomical landmark. Retention bands are ligaments that hold the gut in place during the primitive gut loop and rotation phase There are 2 types of retention band - Superior retention band: Develops into ligament of Treitz. - Inferior retention band: Undergoes atrophy. Ligament of Treitz It is a fibromuscular band that suspends the duodenojejunal flexure from the right crus of the diaphragm. (Option A) Attachments : - Upper end connects to the right crus of the diaphragm. - Lower end attaches to the posterior surface of the duodenojejunal flexure. Composition : - Striated muscle fibers in the upper part. (Option B) - Elastic fibers in the middle. - Non-striated muscle fibers in the lower part. Function : - Fixes the duodenojejunal flexure in place. - Prevents downward displacement by the weight of the small intestine. Clinical Relevance : Acts as an important landmark in radiological assessments for diagnosing incomplete rotation or malrotation of the small intestine. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1274-1275; Solution Pages 1308-1310","sourcePage":1274,"sourcePageEnd":1275},{"id":"anatomy-30-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A healthcare provider is discussing the anatomy of the umbilical cord with a medical student. How many arteries and veins are typically present in a normal umbilical cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 artery and 1 vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 arteries and 1 vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"2 arteries and 2 veins"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 artery and 2 veins"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2 arteries and 1 vein","explanation":"Umbilical cord has one umbilical vein and two umbilical arteries. Contents of the Umbilical Cord Umbilical Arteries Two arteries transport deoxygenated blood from the fetus to the placenta. Umbilical Vein Initially, two veins are present; The right one disappears. The remaining left umbilical vein carries oxygenated blood from the placenta to the fetus. Wharton's Jelly A gelatinous substance derived from the mucoid degeneration of the mesoderm of the connecting stalk protects umbilical vessels. Allantoic Diverticulum Ventral projection of hindgut into the connecting stalk. It is incorporated into the urinary bladder proximally and forms the urachus distally. Vitellointestinal Duct Connection between the midgut and yolk sac; usually disappears but may leave remnants like cysts or fibrous cords. Intra and Extraembryonic Coelom Communication Exists at the fetal end around the vitellointestinal duct until week 10, allowing for a physiological hernia of the midgut loop. Umbilical Cord Development: - Origin Develops from the yolk sac and carries its remnants as contents. The umbilical cord develops from the connecting stalk, a tubular structure covered by the amniotic membrane. - Develops from the yolk sac and carries its remnants as contents. - The umbilical cord develops from the connecting stalk, a tubular structure covered by the amniotic membrane. - Week 2 The appearance of extraembryonic coelom divides extraembryonic mesoderm into two layers, leaving one area. An unsplit mesoderm forms the connecting stalk, suspending the embryonic disc, amniotic cavity, and yolk sac. - The appearance of extraembryonic coelom divides extraembryonic mesoderm into two layers, leaving one area. - An unsplit mesoderm forms the connecting stalk, suspending the embryonic disc, amniotic cavity, and yolk sac. - Week 3 Allantoic diverticulum, an outpouching of the secondary yolk sac, extends into the connecting stalk's mesoderm. Development of extraembryonic blood vessels in the connecting stalk and chorion forms umbilical vessels . - Allantoic diverticulum, an outpouching of the secondary yolk sac, extends into the connecting stalk's mesoderm. - Development of extraembryonic blood vessels in the connecting stalk and chorion forms umbilical vessels . - Week 4 Embryonic folding repositions the connecting stalk and the allantoic diverticulum to the ventral surface of the embryo. The yolk sac turns into the head and tail folds, giving rise to the foregut and hindgut with the midgut in between. The communication between the midgut and definitive yolk sac (extraembryonic part) is the vitellointestinal duct. The amniotic membrane encases these structures, forming the umbilical cord. - Embryonic folding repositions the connecting stalk and the allantoic diverticulum to the ventral surface of the embryo. - The yolk sac turns into the head and tail folds, giving rise to the foregut and hindgut with the midgut in between. - The communication between the midgut and definitive yolk sac (extraembryonic part) is the vitellointestinal duct. - The amniotic membrane encases these structures, forming the umbilical cord. - Develops from the yolk sac and carries its remnants as contents. - The umbilical cord develops from the connecting stalk, a tubular structure covered by the amniotic membrane. - The appearance of extraembryonic coelom divides extraembryonic mesoderm into two layers, leaving one area. - An unsplit mesoderm forms the connecting stalk, suspending the embryonic disc, amniotic cavity, and yolk sac. - Allantoic diverticulum, an outpouching of the secondary yolk sac, extends into the connecting stalk's mesoderm. - Development of extraembryonic blood vessels in the connecting stalk and chorion forms umbilical vessels . - Embryonic folding repositions the connecting stalk and the allantoic diverticulum to the ventral surface of the embryo. - The yolk sac turns into the head and tail folds, giving rise to the foregut and hindgut with the midgut in between. - The communication between the midgut and definitive yolk sac (extraembryonic part) is the vitellointestinal duct. - The amniotic membrane encases these structures, forming the umbilical cord.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1275; Solution Pages 1310-1313","sourcePage":1275,"sourcePageEnd":1275},{"id":"anatomy-30-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements about the development of the liver and gallbladder is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Liver develops from the mesodermal hepatic bud."},{"letter":"B","text":"Cystic duct forms from the pars hepatica of the hepatic bud."},{"letter":"C","text":"Bile duct initially opens on the dorsal aspect of the developing duodenum."},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitelline and umbilical veins contribute to the formation of hepatic sinusoids."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Vitelline and umbilical veins contribute to the formation of hepatic sinusoids.","explanation":"Vitelline and umbilical veins within septum transversum get absorbed and broken to form hepatic sinusoids. Development of Liver: - Develops from the endodermal hepatic bud.(Option A) - Hepatic bud arises as an outgrowth from the ventral margin of the terminal part of the foregut. - Hepatic bud grows ventrally and cranially into the ventral mesogastrium and through it into the septum transversum. - Hepatic bud elongates and divides into: Pars hepatica: Forms the liver. Pars cystica: Forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. (Option B) - Pars hepatica: Forms the liver. - Pars cystica: Forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. (Option B) - Division of Pars Hepatica: Divides into right and left branches to form right and left hepatic ducts. Two termination of hepatic ducts forms: Right and left lobes of the liver. - Divides into right and left branches to form right and left hepatic ducts. - Two termination of hepatic ducts forms: Right and left lobes of the liver. - Formation of Hepatic Architecture: Hepatic bud (endoderm) forms: Hepatic cells Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). Vitelline and umbilical veins within septum transversum get absorbed and broken to form hepatic sinusoids. (Option C) Septum transversum (intraembryonic mesoderm) contributes to: Fibrous capsule of Glisson Kupffer’s cells Hematopoietic cells Connective tissue cells Blood vessels Absorption and breakdown of vitelline and umbilical veins in septum tranversum forms: Sinusoids - Hepatic bud (endoderm) forms: Hepatic cells Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). - Hepatic cells - Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). - Vitelline and umbilical veins within septum transversum get absorbed and broken to form hepatic sinusoids. (Option C) - Septum transversum (intraembryonic mesoderm) contributes to: Fibrous capsule of Glisson Kupffer’s cells Hematopoietic cells Connective tissue cells Blood vessels - Fibrous capsule of Glisson - Kupffer’s cells - Hematopoietic cells - Connective tissue cells - Blood vessels - Absorption and breakdown of vitelline and umbilical veins in septum tranversum forms: Sinusoids - Pars hepatica: Forms the liver. - Pars cystica: Forms the gallbladder and cystic duct. (Option B) - Divides into right and left branches to form right and left hepatic ducts. - Two termination of hepatic ducts forms: Right and left lobes of the liver. - Hepatic bud (endoderm) forms: Hepatic cells Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). - Hepatic cells - Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). - Vitelline and umbilical veins within septum transversum get absorbed and broken to form hepatic sinusoids. (Option C) - Septum transversum (intraembryonic mesoderm) contributes to: Fibrous capsule of Glisson Kupffer’s cells Hematopoietic cells Connective tissue cells Blood vessels - Fibrous capsule of Glisson - Kupffer’s cells - Hematopoietic cells - Connective tissue cells - Blood vessels - Absorption and breakdown of vitelline and umbilical veins in septum tranversum forms: Sinusoids - Hepatic cells - Intrahepatic biliary apparatus (parenchyma). - Fibrous capsule of Glisson - Kupffer’s cells - Hematopoietic cells - Connective tissue cells - Blood vessels Development of Gallbladder: - Gallbladder and cystic duct develop from the pars cystica of the hepatic bud. - Common bile duct forms from the narrow section of the hepatic bud between the pars cystica and the duodenum. - Initially, the bile duct opens on the ventral aspect of the developing duodenum. (Option C) - Due to differential growth and rotation of the duodenal loop, the bile duct shifts to open on the dorsomedial aspect, alongside the ventral pancreatic bud.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1275; Solution Pages 1313-1316","sourcePage":1275,"sourcePageEnd":1275},{"id":"anatomy-30-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A research study is conducted to understand the embryological development of gastrointestinal (GI) structures in an embryo. Which of the following statements about the normal embryological development of the GI tract is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Esophagus develops entirely from the foregut."},{"letter":"B","text":"Stomach initially forms as a fusiform dilation of the foregut."},{"letter":"C","text":"Duodenum forms a C-shaped loop during stomach rotation."},{"letter":"D","text":"Midgut-derived parts of the duodenum are supplied by the celiac artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Midgut-derived parts of the duodenum are supplied by the celiac artery","explanation":"Midgut-derived parts of the duodenum are actually supplied by the superior mesenteric artery , not the celiac artery. Development of Esophagus: - Develops from the foregut part between the pharynx and the stomach. (Option A) - Initially short, it elongates with neck development, diaphragm descent, and pleural cavity expansion. - Muscular coat of the esophagus is derived from the surrounding visceral mesenchyme. - Upper two-thirds of the esophagus contains striated muscle, innervated by the vagus nerve. - Lower one-third of the esophagus contains smooth muscle, innervated by the splanchnic plexus. Development of Stomach: - Begins as a fusiform dilatation of the foregut , distal to the esophagus. (Option B) - Dorsally attached to the posterior abdominal wall by the dorsal mesogastrium. - Ventrally attached to the septum transversum by the ventral mesogastrium. - Stomach Rotation: First rotation: 90■clockwise along the longitudinal axis, reorienting the stomach's surfaces and shifting the position of the vagus nerves. Second rotation: Along the anteroposterior axis, altering the position of the fundus, duodenum, and ends of the stomach. - First rotation: 90■clockwise along the longitudinal axis, reorienting the stomach's surfaces and shifting the position of the vagus nerves. - Second rotation: Along the anteroposterior axis, altering the position of the fundus, duodenum, and ends of the stomach. - First rotation: 90■clockwise along the longitudinal axis, reorienting the stomach's surfaces and shifting the position of the vagus nerves. - Second rotation: Along the anteroposterior axis, altering the position of the fundus, duodenum, and ends of the stomach. Development of Duodenum: - Duodenum above the entry of the bile duct (first part and upper half of the second part) is derived from the foregut. - Part below the entry of the bile duct (remaining portion of second part along with third and fourth part) is derived from the midgut. - Rotation of stomach: Duodenum forms a C-shaped loop and rotates to the right. (Option C) - Developing duodenum is initially attached to the posterior abdominal wall by the mesoduodenum, which later fuses with the peritoneum, making most of the duodenum retroperitoneal. - Blood Supply of Duodenum: Foregut derived parts: Celiac artery Midgut derived parts: Superior mesenteric artery (Option D) - Foregut derived parts: Celiac artery - Midgut derived parts: Superior mesenteric artery (Option D) - Foregut derived parts: Celiac artery - Midgut derived parts: Superior mesenteric artery (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1275-1276; Solution Pages 1316-1318","sourcePage":1275,"sourcePageEnd":1276},{"id":"anatomy-30-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following statements about the development of mesentery is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal mesentery is derived from the septum transversum."},{"letter":"B","text":"Ventral mesentery gives rise to the greater omentum."},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal mesentery extends from the lower esophagus to the rectum."},{"letter":"D","text":"Ventral mesentery is not continuous with the dorsal mesentery."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Ventral mesentery is not continuous with the dorsal mesentery.","explanation":"Dorsal Mesentery is a collection of connective tissue that maintains the gut tube; it extends from the lower esophagus to the rectum, Mesentery: - Initially, the foregut, midgut, and hindgut are in contact with the mesenchyme of the posterior abdominal wall. - By the fifth week of development, the gut is suspended by the dorsal mesentery from the abdominal wall. - Dorsal Mesentery: Collection of connective tissue that maintains the gut tube and its derivatives in anatomical position. It extends from the lower esophagus to the rectum, acting as a pathway for blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves to the gut tubes and their derivatives. (Option C) Its name varies based on the gut tube segment it attaches to: Dorsal mesogastrium Greater omentum (Option B) Mesoduodenum Mesentery proper (small intestine) Mesocolon Mesoappendix Mesosigmoid Mesorectum - Collection of connective tissue that maintains the gut tube and its derivatives in anatomical position. - It extends from the lower esophagus to the rectum, acting as a pathway for blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves to the gut tubes and their derivatives. (Option C) - Its name varies based on the gut tube segment it attaches to: Dorsal mesogastrium Greater omentum (Option B) Mesoduodenum Mesentery proper (small intestine) Mesocolon Mesoappendix Mesosigmoid Mesorectum - Dorsal mesogastrium - Greater omentum (Option B) - Mesoduodenum - Mesentery proper (small intestine) - Mesocolon - Mesoappendix - Mesosigmoid - Mesorectum - Collection of connective tissue that maintains the gut tube and its derivatives in anatomical position. - It extends from the lower esophagus to the rectum, acting as a pathway for blood vessels, lymphatics and nerves to the gut tubes and their derivatives. (Option C) - Its name varies based on the gut tube segment it attaches to: Dorsal mesogastrium Greater omentum (Option B) Mesoduodenum Mesentery proper (small intestine) Mesocolon Mesoappendix Mesosigmoid Mesorectum - Dorsal mesogastrium - Greater omentum (Option B) - Mesoduodenum - Mesentery proper (small intestine) - Mesocolon - Mesoappendix - Mesosigmoid - Mesorectum - Dorsal mesogastrium - Greater omentum (Option B) - Mesoduodenum - Mesentery proper (small intestine) - Mesocolon - Mesoappendix - Mesosigmoid - Mesorectum - Ventral Mesentery: Derived from the septum transversum. (Option A) Growth of liver into septum divides it into: Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. Ventral mesentery is continuous with the dorsal mesentery. (Option D) Clinical Relevance: Continuity of the dorsal and ventral mesentery is significant for surgeries, especially when resecting tumors or organs in the abdominal cavity. - Derived from the septum transversum. (Option A) - Growth of liver into septum divides it into: Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. - Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. - Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. - Ventral mesentery is continuous with the dorsal mesentery. (Option D) - Clinical Relevance: Continuity of the dorsal and ventral mesentery is significant for surgeries, especially when resecting tumors or organs in the abdominal cavity. - Derived from the septum transversum. (Option A) - Growth of liver into septum divides it into: Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. - Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. - Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. - Ventral mesentery is continuous with the dorsal mesentery. (Option D) - Clinical Relevance: Continuity of the dorsal and ventral mesentery is significant for surgeries, especially when resecting tumors or organs in the abdominal cavity. - Ventral mesogastrium (lesser omentum): Extending from the stomach and most parts of the duodenum to the liver. - Falciform ligament: Extending from the liver to the ventral body wall. - Derivatives of Ventral and Dorsal Mesogastrium: Peritoneum: - Continuous serous membrane lining the inner surface of the abdominal cavity. - It reflects onto the viscera (internal organs). - Visceral peritoneum: Layer in direct contact with the viscera (organs). - Parietal peritoneum: Layer attached to the abdominal wall. - Peritoneal Reflections: Occur where the peritoneum bridges the space between an organ and the posterior abdominal wall. E.g.: Coronary and triangular reflections: Found on the surface of the liver; Lienorenal reflection: Connects the spleen and the left kidney. Blood Supply in Primitive Gut: - During the formation of the primitive gut, the dorsal aorta gives off ventral branches to the gut. - Branches in the region of the midgut extend to the yolk sac and are called vitelline arteries. - Most of the ventral branches of the dorsal aorta disappear over time, except for three major arteries: Celiac artery: Supplies the foregut Superior mesenteric artery: Supplies the midgut Inferior mesenteric artery: Supplies the hindgut - Celiac artery: Supplies the foregut - Superior mesenteric artery: Supplies the midgut - Inferior mesenteric artery: Supplies the hindgut - Celiac artery: Supplies the foregut - Superior mesenteric artery: Supplies the midgut - Inferior mesenteric artery: Supplies the hindgut","sourceRef":"Question Page 1276; Solution Pages 1318-1322","sourcePage":1276,"sourcePageEnd":1276},{"id":"anatomy-30-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A patient on examination and radiological imaging was found to have an absent gallbladder. Which gut tube structure is associated with this defect, and what is the primitive artery that supplies it?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Midgut, superior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Foregut, celiac trunk"},{"letter":"C","text":"Midgut, celiac trunk"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hindgut, inferior mesenteric artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Foregut, celiac trunk","explanation":"- The gallbladder is derived from the foregut. - The gallbladder develops as an outpouching from the hepatic diverticulum of the foregut during embryonic development. The foregut is primarily supplied by the celiac trunk, a major branch of the abdominal aorta. Gut Tube: The gut tube is a primitive structure that forms during early embryonic development and eventually gives rise to the organs of the digestive system and associated structures. - It is derived from the endoderm, one of the three primary germ layers, and extends from the future mouth (oropharyngeal membrane) to the anus (cloacal membrane). - The gut tube is typically divided into three main regions. Foregut Midgut Hindgut Extends from: Oropharyngeal Extends from: Distal part of the Extends from : Distal third of the membrane to the first part of the duodenum (after the bile duct transverse colon to the cloacal duodenum (distal to the bile duct) entrance) to the proximal two-thirds membrane of the transverse colon Derivatives: Pharynx Esophagus Derivatives: Distal half of the Derivatives: Distal third of the Stomach Proximal half of the duodenum Jejunum Ileum Cecum transverse colon Descending colon duodenum (up to the bile duct) Liver and appendix Ascending colon Sigmoid colon Rectum Upper anal Gallbladder Pancreas Respiratory Proximal two-thirds of the transverse canal (above pectinate line) Urinary system colon bladder and urethra (urogenital sinus) Arterial supply: Celiac trunk Arterial supply: Superior mesenteric Arterial supply: Inferior mesenteric artery artery","sourceRef":"Question Page 1276; Solution Pages 1322-1325","sourcePage":1276,"sourcePageEnd":1276},{"id":"anatomy-30-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A 2-day-old infant is brought to the emergency department with severe respiratory distress. The physical examination reveals scaphoid abdomen, decreased breath sounds on the left side, and a chest X-ray shows a mediastinal shift to the right. Which type of hernia is most likely responsible for this presentation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Morgagni hernia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bochdalek hernia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hiatal hernia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Umbilical hernia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Bochdalek hernia","explanation":"Given the age of the infant and the clinical presentation of severe respiratory distress, decreased breath sounds, and mediastinal shift, the most likely diagnosis is a Bochdalek hernia . It directly leads to lung compression and impaired respiratory function. Bochdalek hernia Morgagni hernia More common congenital diaphragmatic hernia (CDH) Less common CDH Often left sided Often right sided M/c site: Postero lateral M/c side: Anteromedial (retrosternal) Spleen and intestines enter the thoracic cavity. The transverse colon enters the thoracic cavity. Intestine passes through the left pleuroperitoneal The transverse colon passes through the right retrosternal membrane and makes the left lung small. space (space of Morgagni) behind the sternum but in front of the heart.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1276-1277; Solution Page 1325","sourcePage":1276,"sourcePageEnd":1277},{"id":"anatomy-30-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":19,"question":"What structures are transmitted through the marked opening of the diaphragm in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oesophagus and gastric nerves"},{"letter":"B","text":"Aorta and thoracic duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior vena cava and left phrenic nerve"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve","explanation":"The marked opening in the above image is vena caval hiatus which contains inferior vena cava and right phrenic nerve . Major openings in the diaphragm Level of opening T8 T10 T12 Location In the central tendon In the muscular part In crus of the diaphragm Structures passing through Inferior v ena cava Right O esophagus (Option A) A orta (Option B) Thoracic the opening phrenic nerve (Option C) Right and left vagus nerve duct Azygous vein (Option D) Hemiazygous vein (Mnemonic: VOA (Voice Of America)) Small openings in the diaphragm - Larry’s Space (Foramen of Morgagni) - Superior epigastric vessels pass between diaphragm origins at xiphoid process and 7th costal cartilage. - Median arcuate ligament - Sympathetic chain - Lateral arcuate ligament - Subcostal nerves and vessels - Splanchnic nerve passes through the right and left crus of the diaphragm. - The azygous vein may pass through the left crus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1277; Solution Pages 1325-1328","sourcePage":1277,"sourcePageEnd":1277},{"id":"anatomy-30-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":20,"question":"All of the following are the derivatives of the structure labeled A in the image below, except: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Falciform ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Coronary ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ligamentum teres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lesser omentum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ligamentum teres","explanation":"Structure labeled A is septum transversum. Ligamentum teres (round ligament of the liver) is not derived from the septum transversum. It is a remnant of the left umbilical vein. Septum Transversum: The septum transversum is an embryological structure that plays a vital role in early development, serving as a partial barrier between the thoracic and abdominal cavities. It contributes to the formation of several structures Location and Function - Initial Position : The septum transversum forms between the developing heart and liver, creating a partition between the thoracic and abdominal cavities. - Early Role : In early embryonic development, it functions as a partial diaphragm, separating the coelomic cavity into the thoracic (above) and abdominal (below) compartments. This separation is not complete, as gaps remain, which are later closed by other structures. Derivatives of the Septum Transversum 1. Central Tendon of Diaphragm (Diaphragm) 2.Portions of Liver Mesenchyme (Liver): Connective tissue stroma of the liver including Glisson’s capsule (fibrous capsule of the liver). Peritoneal coverings of liver Kupffer cells, Hemopoietic cells, sinusoids, etc 3. Ventral mesentery of the foregut. Derivatives of Ventral mesentery: Falciform ligament, Lesser omentum, Coronary ligament. (Options A, B and D) 4. Fibrous pericardium Associated Membranes and Cavities: - The septum transversum is sometimes referred to as the pericardioperitoneal membrane, as it separates the developing pericardial cavity (containing the heart) from the peritoneal cavity (containing abdominal organs like the liver and stomach). - It also contributes to the separation of the right and left pleuroperitoneal membranes, which are involved in the development of the pleural cavities surrounding the lungs.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1277-1278; Solution Pages 1328-1330","sourcePage":1277,"sourcePageEnd":1278},{"id":"anatomy-30-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Which of the following structures contributes to the formation of the marked part of the diaphragm (inferior view)? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Septum transversum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pleuro-peritoneal membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal mesentery of esophagus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mesoderm of body wall"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Dorsal mesentery of esophagus","explanation":"The marked part of the diaphragm corresponds to the crura of the diaphragm, which is primarily derived from the dorsal mesentery of the esophagus. Part of Diaphragm Source of Development 1. Central Tendon of Diaphragm Septum transversum 2. Small Peripheral Part Pleuroperitoneal Membrane 3. Crura of Diaphragm Dorsal Mesentery of Esophagus 4. Large Peripheral Part Mesoderm of Body Wall","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1278-1279; Solution Pages 1330-1331","sourcePage":1278,"sourcePageEnd":1279},{"id":"anatomy-30-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following is true about gastroschisis as opposed to an omphalocele?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Has a covering of amnion around the herniated bowels"},{"letter":"B","text":"Is associated with chromosomal anomalies and cardiac anomalies"},{"letter":"C","text":"Due to the failure of the gut tube back to return into the abdomen"},{"letter":"D","text":"The herniated bowel loops are damaged due to amniotic fluid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The herniated bowel loops are damaged due to amniotic fluid","explanation":"The herniated loops of the bowel in gastroschisis are exposed as they lack any covering as in omphalocele and hence can be affected by the corrosive effect of amniotic fluid. Gastroschisis Omphalocele Definition A congenital defect where the A congenital ventral body wall defect abdominal wall fails to close, leading where portions of the midgut fail to to herniation of intestinal loops into return to the abdominal cavity after the amniotic cavity. M/c in physiological herniation. (Option C) premature/SGA babies Location of defect Right paraumbilical Central Sac Absent, bowel is exposed. Present (if not ruptured). Sac is made of amnion, Wharton's jelly and peritoneum (Option A) Description Free intestinal loops. (Liver is not Firm mass including liver, intestine usually herniated) and/ other organs (like stomach, spleen) Umbilical cord Adjacent to the defect Attached to the sac Associated anomalies Associated anatomic defects are Associated abnormalities are uncommon common and include: Neural tube defect Cardiac abnormalities (Option B) Chromosomal defect Detection Identified through fetal ultrasound. Elevated alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) Elevated alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) concentrations in maternal serum levels in maternal serum and and amniotic fluid, similar to amniotic fluid. gastroschisis. Complication Affected bowel loops may be Feeding difficulties Failure to thrive damaged due to exposure to Inguinal hernias Gastroesophageal corrosive amniotic fluid . Risk of reflux Occasionally esophagitis volvulus , where the bowel twists around itself, compromising blood supply. Prognosis Favorable High mortality rates Image Omphalocele","sourceRef":"Question Page 1279; Solution Pages 1331-1332","sourcePage":1279,"sourcePageEnd":1279},{"id":"anatomy-30-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A neonate presents with dribbling of urine from the umbilicus. Which of the following statements is true regarding the affected structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Persistence of a small area of allantois"},{"letter":"B","text":"The lumen of the upper part of the urachus persists"},{"letter":"C","text":"Forms a cystic dilation due to secretions from the lining"},{"letter":"D","text":"Persistence of intraembryonic part of allantois"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Persistence of intraembryonic part of allantois","explanation":"Dribbling of urine from the umbilicus is a finding in cases of urachal fistula. It is caused by the persistence of the intraembryonic portion of the allantois. Urachus is formed by fibrosis of the distal part of the allantoic diverticulum. Its proximal part joins the apex of the urinary bladder. Urachal anomalies lead to bladder defects like: - Urachal fistula Caused by the persistence of the intraembryonic portion of allantois. Causes urine to drain from the umbilicus. - Caused by the persistence of the intraembryonic portion of allantois. - Causes urine to drain from the umbilicus. - Caused by the persistence of the intraembryonic portion of allantois. - Causes urine to drain from the umbilicus. - Urachal cyst When only a small area of the allantois persists. (Option A) The secretory action from the lining results in a cystic dilation. (Option C) - When only a small area of the allantois persists. (Option A) - The secretory action from the lining results in a cystic dilation. (Option C) - When only a small area of the allantois persists. (Option A) - The secretory action from the lining results in a cystic dilation. (Option C) - Urachal sinus When the lumen of the upper part persists and forms a sinus. (Option B) Usually continuous with the urinary bladder. - When the lumen of the upper part persists and forms a sinus. (Option B) - Usually continuous with the urinary bladder. - When the lumen of the upper part persists and forms a sinus. (Option B) - Usually continuous with the urinary bladder.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1279-1280; Solution Pages 1332-1333","sourcePage":1279,"sourcePageEnd":1280},{"id":"anatomy-30-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-30","chapter":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 30 - Embryology of Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":30,"questionNumber":24,"question":"A newborn is scheduled for surgery after presenting with fecal discharge from the umbilicus. An intraoperative image is shown below. What is the most likely diagnosis? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Meckel’s diverticulum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vitelline fistula"},{"letter":"C","text":"Vitelline sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitelline cyst"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Vitelline fistula","explanation":"Newborn exhibiting fecal discharge from the umbilicus , along with intraoperative finding of vitelline duct joining umbilicus to small intestine point towards diagnosis of vitelline fistula or umbilical fistula . Vitelline duct - The midgut during the early embryonic period communicates with the extraembryonic part of the yolk sac via the vitellointestinal duct or omphalomesenteric duct . - It's involved in nutrient transfer and hematopoiesis - It disappears by the 5th week of development , and if it persists, it leads to the following anomalies: Vitelline duct anomalies Meckel’s diverticulum (Option A) It is the persistent proximal part of the embryonic Vitello intestinal duct. Key features: It occurs in about 2% of the population. Its length is around 2 inches (5 cm). It is located 2 feet (60 cm) proximal to the ileocecal junction . It is more common in males . Its wall structure and caliber resemble that of the ileum . It is a true diverticulum containing all the layers of ileum. The apex may be free or connected to the umbilicus by a fibrous band. It attaches to the antimesenteric border of the ileum. It can contain ectopic gastric mucosa and pancreatic tissue . Generally asymptomatic, but it can cause: Intestinal obstruction. Ulceration and bleeding if ectopic gastric mucosa or pancreatic tissue is present, mimicking acute abdomen. Symptoms resembling acute appendicitis when inflamed. Vitelline fistula (Option B) If the vitelline duct remains patent, it creates a direct connection between the umbilicus and the intestinal tract . This condition is called an umbilical fistula or vitelline fistula. It can lead to fecal discharge from the umbilicus. Vitelline sinus (Option C) If the distal segment of the vitelline duct remains patent, it can lead to the development of an umbilical sinus Vitelline cyst (Option D) When both ends of the vitelline duct become fibrous cords and the middle portion forms a large cyst, known as an enterocystoma or vitelline cyst. Fibrous cords can cause intestinal obstruction, strangulation, or volvulus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1280; Solution Pages 1333-1335","sourcePage":1280,"sourcePageEnd":1280},{"id":"anatomy-31-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 6-month-old boy is brought to the clinic by his parents due to a mass noticed in his right groin. An ultrasound confirms that it is an undescended testis, located just at the superficial inguinal ring Based on the normal developmental timeline of testicular descent, at which intrauterine period should the testis have normally reached the deep inguinal ring?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"8th week of gestation"},{"letter":"B","text":"3rd month of gestation"},{"letter":"C","text":"5th month of gestation"},{"letter":"D","text":"7th month of gestation"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 5th month of gestation","explanation":"The testis should have reached the deep inguinal ring by 5 months of gestation . Descent of testis: The normal timeline of testicular descent: - 2nd month of gestation: The testis starts its descent from the posterior abdominal wall. - 3rd month of gestation: The testis reaches the iliac fossa . (Option B) - 4th to 6th month of gestation: The testis descends further and rests at the deep inguinal ring during this period. - 7th month of gestation: The testis traverses the inguinal canal. (Option D) - 8th month of gestation: The testis reaches the superficial inguinal ring. (Option A) - 9th month of gestation: The testis enters the scrotum - At or just after birth: reaches the base of the scrotum. Fig. Descent of testis","sourceRef":"Question Page 1336; Solution Pages 1342-1344","sourcePage":1336,"sourcePageEnd":1336},{"id":"anatomy-31-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following statements is FALSE regarding the peritoneal cavity?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The abdominal cavity is divided into supracolic and infracolic compartments."},{"letter":"B","text":"The supracolic compartment is further divided into subphrenic spaces."},{"letter":"C","text":"The infracolic compartment is divided into right and left parts by the falciform ligament."},{"letter":"D","text":"The right paracolic gutter lies along the lateral side of the ascending colon."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The infracolic compartment is divided into right and left parts by the falciform ligament.","explanation":"ligament. The infracolic compartment is divided into right and left parts by the mesentery, not the falciform ligament. Peritoneal Cavity - Peritoneal cavity is divided into two parts (Option A ruled out) Abdominal cavity Pelvic cavity - Abdominal cavity - Pelvic cavity - Abdominal Cavity Divisions: Divided by the transverse colon and transverse mesocolon into: Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. Paracolic Gutters: Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Divided by the transverse colon and transverse mesocolon into: Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. - Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) - Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. - Paracolic Gutters: Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) - Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Abdominal cavity - Pelvic cavity - Divided by the transverse colon and transverse mesocolon into: Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. - Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) - Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. - Paracolic Gutters: Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) - Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Supracolic Compartment: Subdivided into subphrenic spaces by peritoneal reflection around the liver. (Option B ruled out) - Infracolic Compartment: Subdivided into right and left parts by the mesentery. - Right Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the ascending colon. (Option D ruled out) - Left Paracolic Gutter: Lateral side of the descending colon. - Pelvic Cavity divisions: - True Pelvis (Lesser Pelvis): The lower part that lies below the pelvic brim and contains the pelvic organs. Contents: Urinary bladder Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) Rectum and anal canal - Contents: Urinary bladder Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) Rectum and anal canal - Urinary bladder - Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) - Rectum and anal canal - False Pelvis (Greater Pelvis): The upper part, technically part of the abdominal cavity but supports the intestines and provides some protection to pelvic organs. - Contents: Urinary bladder Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) Rectum and anal canal - Urinary bladder - Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) - Rectum and anal canal - Urinary bladder - Reproductive organs (e.g., uterus, ovaries in females; prostate, seminal vesicles in males) - Rectum and anal canal","sourceRef":"Question Page 1336; Solution Pages 1344-1346","sourcePage":1336,"sourcePageEnd":1336},{"id":"anatomy-31-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 14-year-old girl presents with a mass in the right groin that is suspected to be an ovary. Imaging confirms that her ovary has been drawn into the inguinal canal. Which structure is most likely responsible for the abnormal descent of the ovary into the inguinal canal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Round ligament of the uterus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Suspensory ligament of the ovary"},{"letter":"C","text":"Broad ligament of the uterus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Uterosacral ligament"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Round ligament of the uterus","explanation":"The ovary develops in the abdominal cavity from a genital ridge (formed from thickened coelomic epithelium) on the posterior abdominal wall, covering the mesonephros. - It receives primordial germ cells from the yolk sac and then descends into the pelvis. - During normal development, the descent is influenced by The gubernaculum (which later forms the round ligament of the ovary and the uterus ) and The processus vaginalis , an evagination of the peritoneum. - The gubernaculum (which later forms the round ligament of the ovary and the uterus ) and - The processus vaginalis , an evagination of the peritoneum. - The gubernaculum (which later forms the round ligament of the ovary and the uterus ) and - The processus vaginalis , an evagination of the peritoneum. - In rare cases, the ovary may follow the round ligament and be drawn into the inguinal canal. This condition, known as imperfect descent of the ovary , can present clinically as a groin mass. Suspensory ligament of the ovary (Option B): The suspensory ligament attaches the ovary to the lateral pelvic wall and contains the ovarian vessels. It is not involved in guiding the descent of the ovary into the inguinal canal. Broad ligament of the uterus (Option C): The broad ligament is a peritoneal fold that supports the uterus, fallopian tubes, and ovaries, but it is not responsible for pulling the ovary into the inguinal canal. Uterosacral ligament (Option D): This ligament connects the uterus to the sacrum and helps maintain the position of the uterus. It plays no role in the descent of the ovary.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1336-1337; Solution Pages 1346-1347","sourcePage":1336,"sourcePageEnd":1337},{"id":"anatomy-31-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following structures is NOT found in the spermatic cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vas deferens"},{"letter":"B","text":"Testicular artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epididymis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pampiniform plexus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Epididymis","explanation":"Epididymis is the structure where sperm mature and are stored but is not part of the spermatic cord; it is located on the surface of the testis. The spermatic cord is a collection of structures that pass to and fro from the testis through the inguinal canal. It extends from the deep inguinal ring to the posterior border of the testis and is covered by three fascial layers. Contents of the spermatic cord: - Ductus deferens, in the posterior part (Option A) - Three arteries: - The pampiniform venous plexus (Option D) - Lymphatics, especially from testis draining into pre- and para-aortic nodes, and some from the coverings draining into external iliac nodes. - Nerves: Genital branch of the genitofemoral nerve, and sympathetic fibers accompany the arteries. - Remains of processus vaginalis Fig: Transverse section of the spermatic cord","sourceRef":"Question Page 1337; Solution Pages 1347-1348","sourcePage":1337,"sourcePageEnd":1337},{"id":"anatomy-31-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":5,"question":"What is the correct order of the layers of the anterior abdominal wall from superficial to deep?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Skin → Scarpa fascia → Internal oblique → External oblique → Transversalis fascia → Transversus abdominis → Peritoneum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skin → Transversalis fascia → Internal oblique → External oblique → Transversus abdominis → Scarpa fascia → Peritoneum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Skin → Scarpa fascia → External oblique → Internal oblique → Transversus abdominis → Transversalis fascia → Peritoneum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Skin → External oblique → Scarpa fascia → Internal oblique → Transversus abdominis → Transversalis fascia → Peritoneum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Skin → Scarpa fascia → External oblique → Internal oblique → Transversus abdominis → Transversalis fascia → Peritoneum","explanation":"abdominis → Transversalis fascia → Peritoneum - The most superficial layer is the skin. - Deep to the skin is the superficial fascia, which has two layers. a. Camper fascia is called a fatty layer of subcutaneous tissue. b. Scarpa fascia is called the membranous layer of subcutaneous tissue. - Muscles of the anterior abdominal wall a. External oblique muscle. b. Internal oblique muscle c. Transversus abdominis muscle - TransversaIis fascia - Extraperitoneal fat - Parietal peritoneum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1337; Solution Pages 1348-1349","sourcePage":1337,"sourcePageEnd":1337},{"id":"anatomy-31-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":6,"question":"At which level does the rectus sheath become entirely posterior to the rectus abdominis muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the level of the xiphoid process"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the level of umbilicus"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the arcuate line"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. At the arcuate line","explanation":"Below the arcuate line, the posterior layer of the rectus sheath is absent, leaving the rectus abdominis muscle in direct contact with the transversalis fascia. Fig: of Rectus sheath at different levels The formation of rectus sheath differs from the above downward as follows: 1. Above the level of costal margin: - The anterior wall is formed by the aponeurosis of the external oblique only. - The posterior wall is deficient and muscle lies directly on the 5th, 6th, and 7th costal cartilage. 2. Between costal margin and arcuate line: - Anterior wall is formed by the fusion of aponeurosis of external oblique with the anterior lamina of aponeurosis of internal oblique. - The posterior wall is formed by the fusion of aponeurosis of the transversus abdominis with the posterior lamina of the aponeurosis of the internal oblique. 3. Below the level of the arcuate line: At the level of the xiphoid process (Option A) : Above this level, the rectus sheath is made up of both the anterior and posterior layers formed by the aponeuroses of the abdominal muscles. At the level of the umbilicus (Option B) : Here, the rectus sheath still has anterior and posterior layers, but the arrangement starts to change as you move downward.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1337-1338; Solution Pages 1349-1351","sourcePage":1337,"sourcePageEnd":1338},{"id":"anatomy-31-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements about the inguinal canal is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the transversalis fascia."},{"letter":"B","text":"The inguinal canal contains the ilioinguinal nerve."},{"letter":"C","text":"The aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle forms the roof of the inguinal canal."},{"letter":"D","text":"In males, the inguinal canal contains the spermatic cord."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle forms the roof of the inguinal canal.","explanation":"canal. The roof of the inguinal canal is formed by the arching fibers of the internal oblique and the transversus abdominis muscles , not the external oblique aponeurosis. The inguinal canal is a passageway in the lower abdominal wall. Contents of inguinal canal: - In males: Spermatic cord and ilioinguinal nerve (Options B and D) - In females: Round ligament of the uterus and ilioinguinal nerve (Option B) The boundaries of the inguinal canal are: - Anterior wall: It is formed from superficial to deep by: - Posterior wall: It is formed from deep to superficial by: - Roof: It is formed by the lower arched fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles. - Floor: It is formed by:","sourceRef":"Question Page 1338; Solution Pages 1351-1352","sourcePage":1338,"sourcePageEnd":1338},{"id":"anatomy-31-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents with a swelling in the right inguinal region that increases in size on coughing or standing. Upon examination, a diagnosis of indirect inguinal hernia is made, and surgical repair is planned. While preparing for the surgery, the anatomical relations of the inguinal canal are reviewed. Which of the following correctly describes the anatomical relations of the inguinal canal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the transversalis fascia."},{"letter":"B","text":"The inguinal canal contains the inferior epigastric vessels."},{"letter":"C","text":"The deep inguinal ring is located medial to the inferior epigastric vessels."},{"letter":"D","text":"D) The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by the conjoint tendon"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the transversalis fascia.","explanation":"The inguinal canal runs between these structures, with the inguinal ligament forming the inferior boundary and the iliopsoas muscle lying posterior. Relations of the inguinal canal: Structure Description Parietal peritoneum Most posterior structure. Transversalis fascia Has a defect called the deep inguinal ring, through which the testes and spermatic cord pass. Site for indirect inguinal hernias. Anterior to parietal peritoneum. Forms the posterior in the whole extent. (Option A) Lateral umbilical fold Formed by the inferior epigastric vessels. Median umbilical ligament Remnant of the urachus (from the allantois). Medial umbilical ligament Remnant of the obliterated umbilical artery. Pyramidalis muscle Small muscle attaching to the pubic bone. Rectus abdominis lies above it. Covered by the rectus sheath and supplied by the subcostal nerve (T12). Rectus abdominis muscle Receives segmental innervation from T6–T12. Transversus abdominis muscle Does not form the anterior wall of the inguinal canal. Contributes to the roof and posterior wall. Combines with internal oblique to form the conjoint tendon. Internal oblique muscle Forms parts of the anterior, roof, and posterior walls of the inguinal canal. Contributes to cremasteric muscle and fascia. External oblique muscle Aponeurosis forms the anterior wall of the inguinal canal and the superficial inguinal ring. Forms external spermatic fascia. Internal spermatic fascia Derived from the transversalis fascia. The inguinal canal does not contain the inferior epigastric vessels. The vessels are located medial to the deep inguinal ring, and the canal contains structures such as the spermatic cord (in males) or the round ligament (in females). (Option B) The deep inguinal ring is located lateral (not medial) to the inferior epigastric vessels. (Option C) The floor of the inguinal canal is not formed by the conjoint tendon. The floor is primarily formed by the inguinal ligament. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1338; Solution Pages 1352-1355","sourcePage":1338,"sourcePageEnd":1338},{"id":"anatomy-31-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements about the femoral triangle is FALSE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The medial boundary of the femoral triangle is formed by the adductor longus muscle."},{"letter":"B","text":"The lateral boundary of the femoral triangle is formed by the iliacus and psoas major muscles"},{"letter":"C","text":"The femoral nerve is one of the contents of the femoral triangle."},{"letter":"D","text":"The anterior boundary of the femoral ring is the inguinal ligament."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The lateral boundary of the femoral triangle is formed by the iliacus and psoas major muscles","explanation":"psoas major muscles. - The femoral triangle is an important anatomical region in the upper thigh, where key neurovascular structures pass, and it's clinically relevant for procedures like catheterization and palpating the femoral pulse. - The lateral boundary of the femoral triangle is formed by the sartorius muscle . - The iliacus and psoas major muscles form a part of the floor of the femoral triangle, not its lateral boundary. Femoral Triangle Boundaries Contents Lateral Boundary: Medial margin of the sartorius muscle. The structures are arranged from medial to lateral in the Medial Boundary: Medial margin of the adductor longus following order (VAN): Femoral vein (most medial) and Its muscle. Apex: The point where the sartorius overlaps the Tributaries Femoral artery and Its Branches Femoral adductor longus. Floor: Laterally: Iliacus and psoas major nerve (most lateral) Femoral Sheath Nerves: Femoral muscles. Medially: Pectineus and adductor longus Nerve Nerve to Pectineus Femoral Branch of muscles. Roof: Formed by the overlying fascia lata. Genitofemoral Nerve Lateral Cutaneous Nerve of the Thigh Deep Inguinal Lymph Nodes Femoral Ring- Base (upper end) of Femoral Canal Boundaries Contents Anterior: Inguinal ligament Posterior: Pectineus and its Lymph node of Cloquet (Rosenmüller) Lymphatics covering fascia Medial: Concave margin of lacunar Areolar tissue ligament Lateral: Femoral vein The femoral ring , surrounded by the inguinal ligament, lacunar ligament, pectineal ligament, and femoral vein , creates a narrow space. This makes femoral hernias highly prone to strangulation , as the herniated tissue can easily get trapped, leading to compromised blood supply and potential ischemia.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1338-1339; Solution Pages 1355-1356","sourcePage":1338,"sourcePageEnd":1339},{"id":"anatomy-31-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the marked structures is formed directly by the rotation of the stomach and duodenum? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"both a and b"},{"letter":"B","text":"both b and c"},{"letter":"C","text":"b only"},{"letter":"D","text":"d only"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. b only","explanation":"The rotation of the stomach and duodenum incorporates the upper right part of the peritoneal cavity into the lesser sac . In the given image, a- epiploic foramen b- lesser sac c- greater sac d- greater omentum. Formation of the Lesser Sac - The growth of the right lobe of the liver pulls the ventral mesentery to the right, causing the rotation of the stomach and duodenum. - This rotation incorporates the upper right part of the peritoneal cavity into the lesser sac. - The right border of the ventral mesentery becomes the right border of the lesser omentum and forms the anterior boundary of the entrance into the lesser sac. Formation of the Greater Sac - The remaining part of the peritoneal cavity, not included in the lesser sac, is called the greater sac. (Option C ruled out) - The greater sac and lesser sac communicate through the epiploic foramen. (Option A ruled out) Formation of the Greater Omentum - The spleen develops in the upper part of the dorsal mesentery, and the greater omentum forms from the rapid growth of the dorsal mesentery below the spleen. (Option D ruled out) - Initially, the greater omentum extends from the greater curvature of the stomach to the posterior abdominal wall above the transverse mesocolon. - As it continues to grow, the greater omentum descends as an apron-like double layer of peritoneum in front of the transverse colon. - Later, the posterior layer of the greater omentum fuses with the transverse mesocolon, attaching it to the anterior surface of the transverse colon. - Over time, the greater omentum becomes fat-laden, and the inferior recess of the lesser sac extends between its layers.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1339; Solution Pages 1356-1358","sourcePage":1339,"sourcePageEnd":1339},{"id":"anatomy-31-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 50-year-old male undergoes an emergency laparotomy for severe upper abdominal bleeding after blunt abdominal trauma. During the procedure, the surgeon performs the Pringle maneuver by clamping the hepatoduodenal ligament to control hemorrhage. The clamp is applied to temporarily occlude the portal triad and assess the source of bleeding. Which structure forms the superior boundary of the anatomical space where the Pringle maneuver is performed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"B","text":"Caudate lobe of the liver"},{"letter":"C","text":"First part of the duodenum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Proper hepatic artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Caudate lobe of the liver","explanation":"The Pringle maneuver involves clamping the hepatoduodenal ligament, which contains the portal triad, to control bleeding in the liver or surrounding structures. - This maneuver is performed at the epiploic foramen (Foramen of Winslow) . - The caudate lobe of the liver forms the superior boundary of the Epiploic Foramen. Epiploic Foramen / Omental Foramen / Foramen of Winslow: A vertical slit-like opening connecting the lesser sac with the greater sac, located behind the right free margin of the lesser omentum at the level of the 12th thoracic vertebra. Boundaries: - Anterior: Right free margin of the lesser omentum (portal vein, proper hepatic artery, bile duct) (Option D ruled out) - Posterior: Inferior vena cava, right suprarenal gland, T12 vertebra (Option A ruled out) - Superior: Caudate process of the liver - Inferior: First part of the duodenum, horizontal part of the hepatic artery (Option C ruled out) Clinical Relevance: - Pringle Maneuver: A technique to control bleeding from the liver by compressing the free edge of the lesser omentum. - Fluid Collections: The apposed walls of the foramen can lead to isolated fluid collections in the greater sac or omental bursa.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1339-1340; Solution Pages 1358-1359","sourcePage":1339,"sourcePageEnd":1340},{"id":"anatomy-31-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 35-year-old female presents with lower abdominal pain, high fever, and an elevated heart rate. She appears pale and diaphoretic on examination, with hypotension and a leukocytosis noted on laboratory tests. Imaging reveals a pelvic abscess. The surgeon suspects that pus has spread from an upper abdominal source. Which of the following sequences represents the correct flow of pus between important peritoneal spaces, leading to a pelvic abscess?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rectouterine pouch → Hepatorenal pouch → Paracolic gutter → Lesser sac → Pelvic abscess"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser sac → Foramen of Winslow → Greater sac → Hepatorenal pouch → Paracolic gutter → Rectouterine pouch → Pelvic abscess"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater sac → Foramen of Winslow → Hepatorenal pouch → Paracolic gutter → Lesser sac → Pelvic abscess"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hepatorenal pouch → Paracolic gutter → Lesser sac → Foramen of Winslow → Pelvic abscess"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lesser sac → Foramen of Winslow → Greater sac → Hepatorenal pouch → Paracolic gutter → Rectouterine pouch → Pelvic abscess","explanation":"Paracolic gutter → Rectouterine pouch → Pelvic abscess Pus moves from the lesser sac through the foramen of Winslow into the greater sac, enters the hepatorenal pouch of Morrison, continues through the paracolic gutter, then reaches the rectouterine pouch of Douglas, where a pelvic abscess may form. - Greater Sac: Largest compartment of the peritoneal cavity, spanning the entire abdomen. - Largest compartment of the peritoneal cavity, spanning the entire abdomen. - Lesser Sac: Smaller compartment, located behind the stomach, liver, and lesser omentum, as a diverticulum from the greater sac. Both the sacs can be connected through the foramen of Winslow. - Smaller compartment, located behind the stomach, liver, and lesser omentum, as a diverticulum from the greater sac. - Both the sacs can be connected through the foramen of Winslow. - Paracolic Gutters: Right Paracolic Gutter: Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. Left Paracolic Gutter:Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, andcontinuous with the pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Right Paracolic Gutter: Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. - Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. - Left Paracolic Gutter: Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, and continuous with the pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, and continuous with the pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Subphrenic Spaces: Six spaces near the liver, important for surgeons due to frequent pus collection (subphrenic abscesses). Right side: Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Right extraperitoneal compartment. Left side: Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Six spaces near the liver, important for surgeons due to frequent pus collection (subphrenic abscesses). - Right side: Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Right extraperitoneal compartment. - Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). - Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Right extraperitoneal compartment. - Left side: Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Hepatorenal Pouch (Morison’s Pouch): Located between the liver and right kidney, communicates with the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen. Most dependent part in the supine position , often collecting pus after upper abdominal surgery (e.g., cholecystectomy). Communicates with the rectouterine pouch via the right paracolic gutter, serving as a route for infection from the abdomen to the pelvis. - Located between the liver and right kidney, communicates with the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen. - Most dependent part in the supine position , often collecting pus after upper abdominal surgery (e.g., cholecystectomy). - Communicates with the rectouterine pouch via the right paracolic gutter, serving as a route for infection from the abdomen to the pelvis. - Largest compartment of the peritoneal cavity, spanning the entire abdomen. - Smaller compartment, located behind the stomach, liver, and lesser omentum, as a diverticulum from the greater sac. - Both the sacs can be connected through the foramen of Winslow. - Right Paracolic Gutter: Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. - Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. - Left Paracolic Gutter: Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, and continuous with the pelvic cavity below. Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, and continuous with the pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Located lateral to the ascending colon, continuous with the hepatorenal pouch (Morrison’s pouch) above and pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread from the appendix or hepatorenal pouch. - Located lateral to the descending colon, limited by the phrenicocolic ligament above, and continuous with the pelvic cavity below. - Infections may spread upward from the pelvis. - Six spaces near the liver, important for surgeons due to frequent pus collection (subphrenic abscesses). - Right side: Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Right extraperitoneal compartment. - Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). - Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Right extraperitoneal compartment. - Left side: Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Right posterior intraperitoneal compartment (Hepatorenal pouch or Morrison's pouch). - Right anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Right extraperitoneal compartment. - Left posterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left anterior intraperitoneal compartment. - Left extraperitoneal compartment. - Located between the liver and right kidney, communicates with the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen. - Most dependent part in the supine position , often collecting pus after upper abdominal surgery (e.g., cholecystectomy). - Communicates with the rectouterine pouch via the right paracolic gutter, serving as a route for infection from the abdomen to the pelvis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1340; Solution Pages 1359-1363","sourcePage":1340,"sourcePageEnd":1340},{"id":"anatomy-31-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which muscle is primarily involved in a midline abdominal incision?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Rectus Abdominis"},{"letter":"B","text":"External Oblique"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal Oblique"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transverse Abdominis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Rectus Abdominis","explanation":"The rectus abdominis is the primary muscle involved in a midline abdominal incision. The incision typically passes through the rectus sheath that encloses the rectus abdominis muscle. It may separate the muscle or retract it, but this muscle is directly related to the incision. The external oblique, Internal oblique, and Transverse abdominis muscles are located laterally and are not directly involved in the midline incision (Option B, C, and D).","sourceRef":"Question Page 1340; Solution Page 1363","sourcePage":1340,"sourcePageEnd":1340},{"id":"anatomy-31-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-31","chapter":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topic":"Lesson 31 - Inguinal and Femoral Anatomy and Hernia; Abdominal Wall Layers; Peritoneal Cavity","topicIndex":31,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements about the conjoint tendon is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The conjoint tendon is formed by the fusion of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles."},{"letter":"B","text":"The conjoint tendon contributes to the posterior wall of the inguinal canal."},{"letter":"C","text":"The conjoint tendon is located in the anterior wall of the inguinal canal."},{"letter":"D","text":"A weakness in the conjoint tendon can lead to a direct inguinal hernia."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The conjoint tendon is located in the anterior wall of the inguinal canal.","explanation":"- The conjoint tendon, also known as the inguinal aponeurotic falx or Henle's ligament, is a condensation of tissue that runs through the lateral edge of the lower rectus sheath. - It is located in the inferior abdomen and is formed from the common aponeurosis of the internal oblique muscle and transverse abdominis muscle .(Option A) - The conjoint tendon makes up the main part of the medial portion of the posterior wall of the inguinal canal. (Option B) A weakness in the conjoint tendon can lead to a direct inguinal hernia, as it is an important structure in maintaining the integrity of the inguinal canal. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1340-1341; Solution Pages 1363-1365","sourcePage":1340,"sourcePageEnd":1341},{"id":"anatomy-32-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following is NOT a common collateral pathway for venous return in cases of IVC obstruction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Vertebral venous plexus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal thoracic veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pulmonary veins"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pulmonary veins","explanation":"Pulmonary veins do not serve as a collateral pathway in cases of IVC obstruction. Vertebral venous plexus (Option A): True statement, the vertebral venous plexus is a common collateral pathway for venous return in cases of IVC obstruction. Internal thoracic veins (Option B): True statement, the internal thoracic veins (also called the internal mammary veins) are another common collateral route during IVC obstruction. They connect to the subclavian veins and can provide an alternative route for venous return to the heart. Superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall (Option C): True statement, the superficial veins of the anterior abdominal wall, particularly the paraumbilical veins, form collateral pathways through the epigastric veins. In the case of IVC obstruction, these veins become prominent and can provide venous drainage. Inferior vena cava obstruction: - Collaterals develop due to obstruction of the inferior vena cava. Etiology: - Thrombosis - Embolism - Extrinsic compression like tumor, pregnancy Pathology: - Ultimately blood is connected to the superior vena cava through extensive venous collateral systems. Collateral systems: - Azygos–hemiazygos venous system, vertebral venous plexuses. - Superficial veins of the thoracic and abdominal walls- thoraco-epigastric anastomosis. - Note: Lumbar veins contribute significantly to these pathways . Lumbar veins: Thoraco-epigastric collateral: - The superior epigastric vein forms anastomose with lateral thoracic vein and the inferior epigastric vein forms anastomose with internal thoracic vein. - The anastomosis formed is known as thoraco-epigastric anastomosis. - Lateral thoracic vein drains into the axillary vein. - The axillary vein continues as the subclavian vein. - The subclavian vein receives the internal thoracic vein. - The subclavian vein drains into the brachiocephalic vein. - Brachiocephalic vein drains into superior vena cava. - Superior vena cava drains into the right atrium.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1366; Solution Pages 1373-1375","sourcePage":1366,"sourcePageEnd":1366},{"id":"anatomy-32-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":2,"question":"How many Houston's valves are typically present in the human rectum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3"},{"letter":"B","text":"4"},{"letter":"C","text":"5"},{"letter":"D","text":"6"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 4","explanation":"There are typically 4 Houston's valves in the human rectum. - Houston's valves (or permanent folds) are semilunar (crescentic) transverse folds in the rectum. - They are located against the concavities of the lateral curvatures of the rectum. - They are formed by the reduplication of the mucous membrane containing submucous tissue and thickening of circular smooth muscle of the rectal wall. - Houston's valves are permanent and become more prominent when the rectum is distended. - According to Houston, there are four valves numbered from above downward: First valve: Located near the upper end, close to the rectosigmoid junction, projecting from the right or left wall. Second valve: About 1 inch (2.5 cm) above the third fold, projecting from the left rectal wall along the concavity of the upper lateral curvature. Third valve: The largest, most constant, and most important valve, located on the anterior and right wall of the rectum along the concavity of the middle lateral curvature, at the level of the upper end of the ampulla. Fourth valve: Located on the left wall of the rectum along the concavity of the lower lateralcurvature, about 2.5 cm below the third valve. - First valve: Located near the upper end, close to the rectosigmoid junction, projecting from the right or left wall. - Second valve: About 1 inch (2.5 cm) above the third fold, projecting from the left rectal wall along the concavity of the upper lateral curvature. - Third valve: The largest, most constant, and most important valve, located on the anterior and right wall of the rectum along the concavity of the middle lateral curvature, at the level of the upper end of the ampulla. - Fourth valve: Located on the left wall of the rectum along the concavity of the lower lateral curvature, about 2.5 cm below the third valve. - First valve: Located near the upper end, close to the rectosigmoid junction, projecting from the right or left wall. - Second valve: About 1 inch (2.5 cm) above the third fold, projecting from the left rectal wall along the concavity of the upper lateral curvature. - Third valve: The largest, most constant, and most important valve, located on the anterior and right wall of the rectum along the concavity of the middle lateral curvature, at the level of the upper end of the ampulla. - Fourth valve: Located on the left wall of the rectum along the concavity of the lower lateral curvature, about 2.5 cm below the third valve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1366; Solution Pages 1375-1377","sourcePage":1366,"sourcePageEnd":1366},{"id":"anatomy-32-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following is not involved in the formation of the Marginal Artery of Drummond?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ileocolic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right colic artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left colic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior epigastric artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Inferior epigastric artery","explanation":"- Marginal Artery of Drummond is a circumferential anastomotic arterial channel. - It extends from the ileocaecal junction to the rectosigmoid junction. - It is located approximately 3 cm from the inner margin of the colon. - It is formed by the anastomoses between: Colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery (i.e., ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic). Colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery (i.e., left colic and sigmoidal). - Colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery (i.e., ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic). - Colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery (i.e., left colic and sigmoidal). - The vasa recta (small vessels) arise from the Marginal Artery of Drummond and supply the colon. - Colic branches of the superior mesenteric artery (i.e., ileocolic, right colic, and middle colic). - Colic branches of the inferior mesenteric artery (i.e., left colic and sigmoidal). Critical point of the colon: It is located at the splenic flexure. - At this level, the ascending branch of the left colic artery may fail to anastomose with the left branch of the middle colic artery. - However, an anastomotic channel called the \" arc of Riolan \" exists between: The main trunk of the middle colic artery. The ascending branch of the left colic artery. - The main trunk of the middle colic artery. - The ascending branch of the left colic artery. - If the arc of Riolan is poorly developed, the arterial supply to the splenic flexure is compromised. - As a result, the splenic flexure may undergo ischemic changes. - The main trunk of the middle colic artery. - The ascending branch of the left colic artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1366-1367; Solution Pages 1377-1379","sourcePage":1366,"sourcePageEnd":1367},{"id":"anatomy-32-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Examine the image below showing a cross-section of the small intestine. Which part of the small intestine does this section belong to? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Jejunum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ileum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cecum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Jejunum","explanation":"Plicae circulares are large, prominent circular folds found in the jejunum. They are designed to increase surface area for nutrient absorption. In contrast, the ileum has smaller, less prominent folds, and the duodenum has fewer plicae circulares. - Plicae circulares are circular folds of the mucous membrane found in the small intestine, particularly in the jejunum. - These folds are more prominent, closely set, and contribute to the thicker feel of the jejunum when compared to the ileum, which has less prominent and more sparsely arranged folds. - The plicae circulares increase the surface area, are more numerous in the jejunum, where the diameter of the intestine is slightly wider. - In contrast, the ileum contains many more aggregated lymph follicles (Peyer's patches) but fewer plicae circulares. Differences between the Jejunum and the Ileum: Feature Jejunum Ileum Walls Thicker and more vascular Thinner and less vascular Lumen Wider, often found empty Narrower, often found full Circular folds/plicae circulares Longer and closely set Smaller and sparsely set (valves of Kerckring) Villi More, larger, thicker, and leaf-like Fewer, shorter, thinner, and finger-like Aggregated lymph follicles (Peyer's Small, circular, and few in number, Large, oval, more in number, found patches) found only in the distal part of the throughout the ileum, maximum in jejunum the distal part Mesentery Contains less fat, becomes Contains more fat, no peritoneal semitranslucent between the vasa windows between the vasa recta recta, called peritoneal windows Arterial arcades One or two rows with long vasa recta Four or five rows with short vasa recta","sourceRef":"Question Page 1367; Solution Pages 1379-1380","sourcePage":1367,"sourcePageEnd":1367},{"id":"anatomy-32-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Where is the paraduodenal recess located, and which structure is found in its fold?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"To the right of the duodenum, contains the superior mesenteric artery."},{"letter":"B","text":"To the left of the fourth part of the duodenum, contains the inferior mesenteric vein."},{"letter":"C","text":"To the left of the fourth part of the duodenum, contains the superior mesenteric vein."},{"letter":"D","text":"In front of the pancreas, contains the splenic vein."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. To the left of the fourth part of the duodenum, contains the inferior mesenteric vein.","explanation":"mesenteric vein. The paraduodenal recess is an anatomical feature located to the left of the fourth part of the duodenum. It is situated behind the paraduodenal fold of the peritoneum. The key feature of this recess is that it contains the inferior mesenteric vein (IMV) in the free border of the paraduodenal fold. Duodenal Recesses: At the duodenojejunal junction, small pocket-like pouches of peritoneum, called duodenal recesses, may occur. These recesses can sometimes lead to strangulated retroperitoneal hernias. The key recesses around the duodenojejunal flexure are: - Superior Duodenal Recess : Located to the left of the upper end of the fourth part of the duodenum, behind the superior duodenojejunal fold, with its orifice facing downward. - Inferior Duodenal Recess : Found just below the superior recess, behind the inferior duodenojejunal fold, with its orifice facing upward. - Paraduodenal Recess : The lowest recess, located to the left of the fourth part of the duodenum behind the paraduodenal fold, with its orifice facing medially. The fold contains the inferior mesenteric vein. Retroduodenal Recess : The largest, but rarely present; located behind the third and fourth parts of the duodenum, with its orifice facing downward and left.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1367-1368; Solution Pages 1380-1382","sourcePage":1367,"sourcePageEnd":1368},{"id":"anatomy-32-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements accurately describes the anatomical extent of the attached border of the following structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pylorus of the stomach to the transverse colon"},{"letter":"C","text":"Duodenojejunal flexure to the right iliac fossa"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stomach to the appendix"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction","explanation":"The given image represents the mesentery. The attached border, or the root of mesentery, is attached to an oblique line across the posterior abdominal wall, extending from the duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction. The mesentery presents two borders: Root of mesentery (attached border): Extends from the duodenojejunal flexure to the ileocecal junction. Structures crossed by root of mesentery: Free border (intestinal border): - Its length permits the free mobility of the loops of jejunum and ileum in the abdominal cavity. - Near the intestinal border it presents fat-free oval/circular windows (translucent area) of peritoneum. The amount of fat is greater in the distal part of the mesentery. The contents of the mesentery are:","sourceRef":"Question Page 1368; Solution Pages 1382-1383","sourcePage":1368,"sourcePageEnd":1368},{"id":"anatomy-32-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A 55-year-old man with a history of chronic liver disease presents with increasing abdominal swelling, difficulty swallowing, and hematemesis. On examination, he has a distended abdomen, and upon palpation, his spleen is enlarged. Which anatomical feature is primarily responsible for his hematemesis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paraumbilical to epigastric vein connections"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left gastric to esophageal vein connections"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior to middle rectal vein connections"},{"letter":"D","text":"Splenic to inferior mesenteric vein connections"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Left gastric to esophageal vein connections","explanation":"- This case is indicative of portal hypertension, a common consequence of chronic liver disease. - left gastric to esophageal vein connections are primarily responsible for the hematemesis. Paraumbilical to epigastric vein connections (Option A): This refers to the formation of caput medusae, which is a characteristic sign of portal hypertension. While this is a visible finding in the abdominal wall, it is not directly responsible for hematemesis. Superior to middle rectal vein connections (Option C): This describes collateral circulation between the superior rectal vein (from the portal system) and the middle rectal vein (from the systemic circulation). While this can contribute to rectal varices, it is less likely to cause hematemesis compared to esophageal varices. Splenic to inferior mesenteric vein connection (Option D): Does not directly lead to hematemesis. Portal Hypertension : Reversal of blood flow away from the portal vein due to high pressure. Clinical Features: - Splenomegaly - Ascites - Esophageal varices - Rectal varices - Caput medusae Site of anastomosis Veins forming portocaval Clinical signs anastomosis Lower third of the esophagus Left gastric vein ↑↓ Esophageal Esophageal varices veins draining into azygos vein Umbilicus Paraumbilical veins ↑↓ Superficial Caput medusae veins of anterior abdominal wall Mid-anal canal Superior rectal vein ↑↓ Middle and Hemorrhoids inferior rectal veins","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1368-1369; Solution Pages 1383-1384","sourcePage":1368,"sourcePageEnd":1369},{"id":"anatomy-32-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":8,"question":"In cases of a strangulated internal hernia into the lesser sac through the epiploic foramen, the surgical approach is often made through the greater omentum as the epiploic foramen cannot be safely widened. Which of the following structures around the epiploic foramen restricts its enlargement during surgery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hepatic artery, bile duct, portal vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Celiac trunk and splenic artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Portal vein, duodenum, hepatic vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior mesenteric artery, cystic duct, portal vein"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Hepatic artery, bile duct, portal vein","explanation":"- The epiploic foramen (of Winslow) is bordered anteriorly by the right free margin of lesser omentum which contains portal vein, hepatic artery, and common bile duct within the hepatoduodenal ligament. - This makes it dangerous to enlarge the foramen during surgery. Therefore, surgeons typically access the lesser sac through the greater omentum to avoid damaging these structures. Celiac trunk and splenic artery (Option B): The celiac trunk and splenic artery are located more superiorly and posteriorly to the epiploic foramen. Portal vein, duodenum, hepatic vein (Option C): While the duodenum and hepatic vein are located near the epiploic foramen, they are not as directly involved in restricting its size during surgery. Superior mesenteric artery, cystic duct, portal vein (Option D): The superior mesenteric artery and cystic duct are located more anteriorly or inferiorly and are not directly involved in the restriction of the epiploic foramen. Epiploic foramen (of Winslow) / Omental foramen: - It is a vertical slit-like opening through which the lesser sac communicates with the greater sac. - Situated at the level of the 12th thoracic vertebra. Boundaries: Anteriorly: - Right free margin of the lesser omentum - Portal vein, hepatic artery, and common bile duct are located within the hepatoduodenal ligament Posteriorly: - Inferior vena cava - Right suprarenal gland - T12 vertebra Superiorly: - Caudate process of the liver Inferiorly: - First part of the duodenum","sourceRef":"Question Page 1369; Solution Pages 1384-1385","sourcePage":1369,"sourcePageEnd":1369},{"id":"anatomy-32-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following anatomical relationships is incorrect regarding portal venous circulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The portal vein is formed posterior to the neck of the pancreas"},{"letter":"B","text":"The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"The portal vein ends at the porta hepatis"},{"letter":"D","text":"The portal vein is located posterior to the Inferior vena cava"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The portal vein is located posterior to the Inferior vena cava","explanation":"The portal vein is always located anterior to the Inferior vena cava throughout its course from the origin to the termination. The portal vein is formed posterior to the neck of the pancreas (Option A): True statement, the portal vein is formed by the junction of the superior mesenteric vein (SMV) and the splenic vein. This junction occurs posterior to the neck of the pancreas, which is a correct anatomical description. The splenic vein joins the superior mesenteric vein to form the portal vein (Option B): True statement, the splenic vein and superior mesenteric vein join together to form the portal vein behind the neck of the pancreas, which is an accurate description of the portal venous system. The portal vein ends at the porta hepatis (Option C): True statement, the portal vein travels to the porta hepatis (the hilum of the liver), where it divides into right and left branches that supply the liver. This is the correct location for the termination of the portal vein. Portal venous circulation Formation: - Formed by the union of the superior mesenteric and splenic veins behind the neck of the pancreas at the level of the second lumbar vertebra - Inferior mesenteric vein drains into the splenic vein Course: - It runs upwards - First behind the neck of the pancreas - Next, behind the first part of the duodenum - Lastly, in the right free margin of the lesser omentum Termination: - Ends at porta hepatis by dividing into right and left branches - Branches enter the liver - Branches open up into the liver sinusoids Relation Part Anteriorly Posteriorly Infraduodenal part Neck of pancreas Inferior vena cava Retroduodenal part First part of duodenum, bile duct, Inferior vena cava gastroduodenal artery Supraduodenal part Hepatic artery, bile duct (free margin Inferior vena cava of the lesser omentum). Tributaries: - Left gastric - Right gastric - Superior pancreaticoduodenal - Cystic vein in its right branch - Paraumbilical veins in its left branch","sourceRef":"Question Page 1369; Solution Pages 1385-1388","sourcePage":1369,"sourcePageEnd":1369},{"id":"anatomy-32-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents with abdominal pain and constipation. A CT scan reveals a lesion affecting the distal third of the transverse colon and the descending colon. Which of the following arteries is most likely involved in the blood supply to this region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Celiac trunk"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Common hepatic artery"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior mesenteric artery","explanation":"The inferior mesenteric artery supplies the distal third of the transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, and rectum. Celiac trunk (Option A): Supplies the stomach, liver, spleen, but not the colon, making it incorrect. Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) (Option B): Supplies the small intestine, ascending colon, and proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon, not the distal third or descending colon. Common hepatic artery (Option D): A branch of the celiac trunk that supplies the liver and parts of the stomach, not the colon. Main Arteries of the Abdomen The abdomen's three primary arteries- celiac trunk, superior mesenteric artery, and inferior mesenteric artery- branch from the abdominal aorta and supply blood to abdominal organs. - Celiac Trunk Origin: Twelfth thoracic vertebra Branches: Left gastric, common hepatic, splenic arteries Supplies: Foregut organs (stomach, liver, pancreas, spleen, parts of the duodenum) - Origin: Twelfth thoracic vertebra - Branches: Left gastric, common hepatic, splenic arteries - Supplies: Foregut organs (stomach, liver, pancreas, spleen, parts of the duodenum) - Superior Mesenteric Artery Origin: First lumbar vertebra Supplies: Midgut organs (jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, proximal 2/3rd of transverse colon) - Origin: First lumbar vertebra - Supplies: Midgut organs (jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, proximal 2/3rd of transverse colon) - Inferior Mesenteric Artery Origin: Third lumbar vertebra Supplies: Hindgut organs ( distal third of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, proximal anal canal) - Origin: Third lumbar vertebra - Supplies: Hindgut organs ( distal third of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, proximal anal canal) - Origin: Twelfth thoracic vertebra - Branches: Left gastric, common hepatic, splenic arteries - Supplies: Foregut organs (stomach, liver, pancreas, spleen, parts of the duodenum) - Origin: First lumbar vertebra - Supplies: Midgut organs (jejunum, ileum, cecum, ascending colon, proximal 2/3rd of transverse colon) - Origin: Third lumbar vertebra - Supplies: Hindgut organs ( distal third of transverse colon, descending colon, sigmoid colon, rectum, proximal anal canal)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1369-1370; Solution Pages 1388-1390","sourcePage":1369,"sourcePageEnd":1370},{"id":"anatomy-32-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":11,"question":"During abdominal surgery, the surgeon encounters a paired venous structure running vertically along the posterior abdominal wall continuing into the thoracic cavity, just lateral to the vertebral column as shown in the image below. What is this structure most likely? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hemiazygos vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ascending lumbar vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Renal vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ascending lumbar vein","explanation":"The paired ascending lumbar veins run vertically along the posterior abdominal wall medial to the psoas major. Ascending lumbar veins: - The paired ascending lumbar veins run medial to psoas major and connect the ipsilateral common iliac, iliolumbar, and lumbar veins. - They join the subcostal vein to form the azygos vein on the right and the hemiazygos on the left. - Ascending lumbar veins pass through the right and left crura of the respiratory diaphragm to continue into the thoracic cavity. - Blood can flow in either direction in the ascending lumbar veins. Inferior vena cava (Option A): It runs vertically and not a paired vein. Hence, not the correct option. Hemiazygos vein (Option B): The paired ascending lumbar veins join the subcostal vein to form the azygos vein on the right and the hemiazygos on the left. Renal vein (Option D): It is a paired venous structure that courses horizontally opening into the inferior vena cava almost at right angles. Hence, not the right option.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1370; Solution Pages 1390-1391","sourcePage":1370,"sourcePageEnd":1370},{"id":"anatomy-32-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A surgeon must be careful with the inferior vena cava and its associated vessels during laparoscopic nephrectomy. Which of the following is true about IVC?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left gonadal vein drains directly into IVC"},{"letter":"B","text":"Common iliac veins join to form the inferior vena cava at L3 vertebra level"},{"letter":"C","text":"Three large hepatic veins drain the liver into inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right and left 5th lumbar veins drain into IVC"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Three large hepatic veins drain the liver into inferior vena cava","explanation":"Three large and many small hepatic veins drain directly into the anterior surface of the inferior vena cava just before it pierces the diaphragm. Left gonadal vein drains directly into IVC (Option A): False. The left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which then drains into the IVC, not directly into the IVC. Common iliac veins join to form the inferior vena cava at L3 vertebra level (Option B): False. The common iliac veins join to form the IVC at the L5 vertebral level, not L3. Right and left 5th lumbar veins drain into IVC (Option D): False. The lumbar veins are numbered from L1 to L4, and they drain into the IVC. The 5th lumbar segment does not have corresponding veins that drain into the IVC. Inferior vena cava: - Origin: Formed by the union of the right and left common iliac veins at the level of L5 vertebra. - Termination: Opens into the lower and posterior part of the right atrium. - Course: It ascends along the front of the vertebral column, positioned to the right of the aorta, creates a groove on the posterior surface of the liver, and passes through the central tendon of the diaphragm at the T8 vertebral level. Tributaries of IVC Key features Right and left 3rd and 4th lumbar veins It drains the posterior, lateral, and anterior abdominal walls into IVC. Right gonadal vein Left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which drains into the inferior vena cava. Right suprarenal vein Left suprarenal vein drains into the left renal vein, which drains into the inferior vena cava. Right inferior phrenic vein Left inferior phrenic vein drains into the left suprarenal, or left renal vein. Right and left renal veins Left renal vein (7.5cm) is three times longer than right renal vein (2.5cm) Hepatic veins Three large and many small veins open directly into the anterior surface of the inferior vena cava just before it pierces the diaphragm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1370-1371; Solution Pages 1391-1393","sourcePage":1370,"sourcePageEnd":1371},{"id":"anatomy-32-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following is/are NOT a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery? Superior vesical Obturator Middle rectal Iliolumbar Inferior gluteal","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 2, and 5"},{"letter":"B","text":"2, 4, and 5"},{"letter":"C","text":"Only 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"1, and 2"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Only 4","explanation":"The iliolumbar artery is a branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery. The others are branches of the anterior division. Internal iliac artery - It is a branch of the common iliac artery. - It supplies the pelvic viscera. Mnemonic: Posterior Division: - S: Superior gluteal artery - I: Iliolumbar artery - L: Lateral sacral artery Note: - Anterior division gives six branches in males and seven branches in females. - The uterine artery is the 7th branch. - In females inferior vesical artery is replaced by the vaginal artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1371; Solution Pages 1393-1394","sourcePage":1371,"sourcePageEnd":1371},{"id":"anatomy-32-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A medical student is reviewing the anatomy of the inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) and its branches. Which of the following statements about the IMA is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The IMA arises from the abdominal aorta at the level of the third lumbar vertebra (L3)."},{"letter":"B","text":"The IMA supplies the proximal segments of the small intestine."},{"letter":"C","text":"The IMA gives rise to the superior rectal artery, which supplies the rectum."},{"letter":"D","text":"IMA provides collateral circulation if SMA is stenosed"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The IMA supplies the proximal segments of the small intestine.","explanation":"IMA does not supply the proximal segments of the small intestine. The proximal small intestine is primarily supplied by branches of the celiac trunk and SMA. The IMA arises from the abdominal aorta at the level of the third lumbar vertebra (L3) (Option A): This is a correct statement. The IMA typically arises from the abdominal aorta at L3, 3-4 cm above aortic bifurcation. The IMA gives rise to the superior rectal artery, which supplies the rectum (Option C): The IMA gives rise to the superior rectal artery, which supplies the rectum and proximal anal canal. IMA provides collateral circulation if SMA is stenosed (Option D): IMA provides collateral circulation if SMA is stenosed, preventing ischemia of the right side. Inferior Mesenteric Artery (IMA): Origin: - Arises from the abdominal aorta at L3, 3-4 cm above aortic bifurcation. - Located behind the inferior border of the horizontal part of the duodenum. - Smaller than celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery (SMA). Course and Branches of IMA Branch Course & Anastomosis Supplied Regions Left Colic Artery Ascends within left colic mesentery; Distal transverse colon, descending forms part of the marginal artery of colon Drummond Sigmoid Arteries Descend obliquely in sigmoid Distal descending colon, sigmoid mesocolon; anastomose with left colon colic and superior rectal arteries Superior Rectal Artery Crosses left common iliac vessels; Rectum, proximal anal canal descends to rectum, divides into two branches Clinical Significance - Colonic Ischemia: IMA provides collateral circulation if SMA is stenosed, preventing ischemia of the right side. - Colonic Surgery: IMA and marginal artery are crucial for planning resections, ensuring blood supply post-surgery. - Abdominal Aortic Aneurysm (AAA) Surgery: IMA identification is important to prevent colonic ischemia during AAA repairs when IMA may be temporarily occluded.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1371; Solution Pages 1394-1395","sourcePage":1371,"sourcePageEnd":1371},{"id":"anatomy-32-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 50-year-old male presents with chronic postprandial abdominal pain and weight loss. Imaging reveals compression of the third part of the duodenum between the superior mesenteric artery and the aorta. Which of the following best explains the patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesenteric Arterial Thrombosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Aortic Aneurysm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome","explanation":"This condition is caused by compression of the third part of the duodenum by the SMA, often due to weight loss leading to a loss of perivascular fat, which narrows the angle between the SMA and the aorta. Superior Mesenteric Artery (SMA): - Originates from the anterior surface of the abdominal aorta, ~1 cm below the celiac trunk at L1 level. - Passes behind the splenic vein, body of the pancreas, and left renal vein, separating it from the aorta. - Runs anterior to the uncinate process of the pancreas and third part of the duodenum, entering the mesentery. - Supplies the small intestine and parts of the large intestine. Branches Branch Supplies Inferior pancreaticoduodenal Head of pancreas, duodenum Jejunal and Ileal arteries Jejunum, ileum (via arcades and straight arteries) Ileocolic artery Ileum, cecum, appendix (gives rise to cecal & appendicular arteries) Right colic artery Ascending colon Middle colic artery Transverse colon Anatomical Variations - Middle colic artery may originate from various sources (dorsal pancreatic, hepatic, inferior mesenteric). - Right hepatic artery arises from the SMA in ~15% of individuals. - Accessory/replaced right hepatic artery may course behind the portal vein. Clinical Significance Condition Description Surgical Importance Key for procedures like bypass or embolectomy in intestinal ischemia. Superior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome Compression of the duodenum by the SMA due to weight loss, causing obstruction. Collateral Circulation SMA anastomoses with celiac trunk and inferior mesenteric artery for backup blood flow. Inferior Mesenteric Artery Syndrome (Option B) : This is not a recognized condition; the inferior mesenteric artery does not compress the duodenum. Mesenteric Arterial Thrombosis (Option C) : This would present with acute ischemic symptoms, not chronic postprandial pain and duodenal obstruction. Aortic Aneurysm (Option D) : This would more likely cause back pain or pulsatile abdominal mass, not duodenal compression.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1371-1372; Solution Pages 1395-1397","sourcePage":1371,"sourcePageEnd":1372},{"id":"anatomy-32-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A surgical procedure is planned to remove a tumor from the head of the pancreas. Which of the following arterial arcades is primarily responsible for supplying the posterior aspect of the head of the pancreas?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade"},{"letter":"C","text":"Kirk's arcade"},{"letter":"D","text":"Communicating artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade","explanation":"Posterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade is formed by the anastomosis of the posterior superior and posterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries, which supply the posterior aspect of the head of the pancreas. Arterial Supply to the Head of the Pancreas - The head of the pancreas is supplied by branches from the celiac trunk and superior mesenteric artery . - Key arteries supplying the head of the pancreas and adjacent duodenum include: Artery Type Origin Anterior Superior Pancreaticoduodenal Branches from the gastroduodenal artery Posterior Superior Pancreaticoduodenal Branches from the gastroduodenal artery Anterior Inferior Pancreaticoduodenal Branches from the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery Posterior Inferior Pancreaticoduodenal Branches from the inferior pancreaticoduodenal artery Arterial arcades encircle the head of the pancreas: Arcade Type Formed By Supplies Anterior Pancreaticoduodenal Anastomosis of anterior superior and Anterior duodenum, head of (Option A ruled out) anterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal pancreas, uncinate process arteries Posterior Pancreaticoduodenal Anastomosis of posterior superior Posterior head of pancreas, adjacent and posterior inferior duodenum, distal bile duct pancreaticoduodenal arteries Anterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade (Option A): - Formed by anastomosis of anterior superior and anterior inferior pancreaticoduodenal arteries - Supplies the anterior part of the head of the pancreas. Kirk's arcade (Option C): - Formed by a right branch of the dorsal pancreatic artery anastomosing with a branch from the gastroduodenal artery or anterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade. - Supplies the anterior surface of the head and neck of the pancreas. Communicating artery (Middle Pancreaticoduodenal Arcade) (Option D): - Connects anterior pancreaticoduodenal arcade and posterior superior pancreaticoduodenal artery. - Supplies blood to the major duodenal papilla. The arterial supply of the pancreas The anterior and posterior pancreaticoduodenal arterial arcades and a typical communicating artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1372; Solution Pages 1397-1399","sourcePage":1372,"sourcePageEnd":1372},{"id":"anatomy-32-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-32","chapter":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topic":"Lesson 32 - Abdomen Arterial Supply, Venous Drainage of Thorax and Abdomen","topicIndex":32,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with chronic upper abdominal pain and is diagnosed with compression of the celiac trunk by the median arcuate ligament. Which of the following arteries is not a direct branch of the celiac trunk?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left gastric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Splenic artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Common hepatic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Inferior mesenteric artery","explanation":"Inferior mesenteric artery arises independently from the abdominal aorta and supplies the hindgut, not the foregut. Left gastric artery (Option A): This is a direct branch of the celiac trunk and supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach and the lower esophagus. Splenic artery (Option B): This is also a direct branch of the celiac trunk and supplies the spleen, parts of the stomach, and pancreas. Common hepatic artery (Option C): This is another direct branch of the celiac trunk and supplies the liver, gallbladder, duodenum, and parts of the stomach. Celiac Trunk: Origin : First major artery from the abdominal aorta, arises below the aortic hiatus at T12 level. - Length: 1–3 cm long. Branches of the Celiac Trunk - Normal Trifurcation: Left gastric artery. Common hepatic artery. Splenic artery. - Left gastric artery. - Common hepatic artery. - Splenic artery. - Left gastric artery. - Common hepatic artery. - Splenic artery. Anatomical Variations - Separate left gastric artery origin from the abdominal aorta. - Inferior phrenic arteries originating from the celiac trunk. - Common origin shared with the superior mesenteric artery or its branches. Compression (Dunbar Syndrome) - Compression by the median arcuate ligament can cause visceral ischemia and abdominal pain. Function - Supplies blood to foregut-derived organs: Distal esophagus to the middle of the duodenum. Liver, spleen, and pancreas. - Distal esophagus to the middle of the duodenum. - Liver, spleen, and pancreas. - Distal esophagus to the middle of the duodenum. - Liver, spleen, and pancreas.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1372; Solution Pages 1399-1401","sourcePage":1372,"sourcePageEnd":1372},{"id":"anatomy-33-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35-year-old man presents with abdominal pain and ascites. Imaging reveals obstruction of hepatic venous outflow consistent with Budd-Chiari syndrome. Which of the following statements best explains the preservation of the caudate lobe in this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The caudate lobe has a separate arterial supply that protects it from hepatic venous obstruction."},{"letter":"B","text":"The caudate lobe drains directly into the inferior vena cava via emissary veins, bypassing the obstructed hepatic veins."},{"letter":"C","text":"The caudate lobe is less affected by portal hypertension due to its anatomical position."},{"letter":"D","text":"The caudate lobe undergoes necrosis less frequently than other liver segments in Budd-Chiari syndrome."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The caudate lobe drains directly into the inferior vena cava via emissary veins, bypassing the obstructed hepatic veins.","explanation":"veins, bypassing the obstructed hepatic veins. The direct drainage of the caudate lobe into the IVC via emissary veins is key to its preservation during hepatic venous obstruction. - Definition : Characterized by obstruction of hepatic venous outflow, leading to increased hepatic sinusoidal pressure. - Causes portal hypertension and liver congestion due to impaired venous drainage. - Most often associated with hypercoagulable or prothrombotic states. - The caudate lobe shows increased contrast enhancement compared to the rest of the liver. - Separate Drainage : The caudate lobe is drained directly by emissary veins into the inferior vena cava (IVC), bypassing the obstructed hepatic veins. - Caudate Hypertrophy : Due to its preserved drainage, the caudate lobe may undergo hypertrophy, compensating for the affected areas. The caudate lobe has a separate arterial supply that protects it from hepatic venous obstruction (Option A): The caudate lobe does not have a separate arterial supply; liver segments I-IV are all supplied by the left branch of the hepatic artery its preservation is due to its unique venous drainage. The caudate lobe is less affected by portal hypertension due to its anatomical position (Option C) : The caudate lobe can still be affected by portal hypertension; its preservation is due to drainage patterns, not position. The caudate lobe undergoes necrosis less frequently than other liver segments in Budd-Chiari syndrome (Option D) : Necrosis can occur in the liver, but the caudate lobe's preservation is related to its drainage, not necrosis rates.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1402; Solution Pages 1407-1408","sourcePage":1402,"sourcePageEnd":1402},{"id":"anatomy-33-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":2,"question":"During a surgical procedure involving the stomach, the surgeon needs to ensure adequate blood supply. Which artery should the surgeon be most concerned about when addressing the lesser curvature of the stomach?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left gastroepiploic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right gastric artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Short gastric arteries"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right gastroepiploic artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Right gastric artery","explanation":"The right gastric artery, a branch of the common hepatic artery, supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach, making it crucial during surgical interventions in that area. Left gastroepiploic artery (Option A): This artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach, not the lesser curvature. It arises from the splenic artery . Short gastric arteries (Option C): These arteries also supply the greater curvature of the stomach and the fundus, and they arise from the splenic artery . They do not contribute significantly to the lesser curvature. Right gastroepiploic artery (Option D): This artery supplies the greater curvature of the stomach and arises from the gastroduodenal artery . It does not supply the lesser curvature. Arterial supply of stomach - Lesser Curvature : Supplied by: Left gastric artery (branch of the coeliac trunk). Right gastric artery (branch of the common hepatic artery). - Left gastric artery (branch of the coeliac trunk). - Right gastric artery (branch of the common hepatic artery). - Greater Curvature : Supplied by: Right gastroepiploic artery (branch of gastroduodenal artery). Left gastroepiploic artery (branch of splenic artery). - Right gastroepiploic artery (branch of gastroduodenal artery). - Left gastroepiploic artery (branch of splenic artery). - Fundus : Supplied by: 5 to 7 short gastric arteries (branches of the splenic artery). - 5 to 7 short gastric arteries (branches of the splenic artery). - Left gastric artery (branch of the coeliac trunk). - Right gastric artery (branch of the common hepatic artery). - Right gastroepiploic artery (branch of gastroduodenal artery). - Left gastroepiploic artery (branch of splenic artery). - 5 to 7 short gastric arteries (branches of the splenic artery). Venous drainage of the stomach - Lesser Curvature : Right and left gastric veins drain directly into the portal vein. - Right and left gastric veins drain directly into the portal vein. - Greater Curvature : The right gastroepiploic vein drains into the superior mesenteric vein. Left gastroepiploic and short gastric veins drain into the splenic vein. - The right gastroepiploic vein drains into the superior mesenteric vein. - Left gastroepiploic and short gastric veins drain into the splenic vein. - Right and left gastric veins drain directly into the portal vein. - The right gastroepiploic vein drains into the superior mesenteric vein. - Left gastroepiploic and short gastric veins drain into the splenic vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1402; Solution Pages 1408-1410","sourcePage":1402,"sourcePageEnd":1402},{"id":"anatomy-33-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":3,"question":"During a cholecystectomy, a surgeon encounters a variation in the biliary anatomy where the cystic duct is unusually short and close to the hepatic duct. Which of the following structures must the surgeon identify to avoid injury and ensure proper bile drainage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Common bile duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sphincter of Oddi"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right hepatic duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pancreatic duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Common bile duct","explanation":"The common bile duct is formed by the union of the common hepatic duct and the cystic duct, draining bile into the duodenum. Proper identification during surgery prevents injury and ensures bile drainage. The Biliary Apparatus: Function: Transports bile from the liver to the duodenum to aid fat digestion. Bile Flow: From Liver to Duodenum - Hepatocytes : Liver cells responsible for bile production. - Intricate Duct System : Bile Canaliculi : Tiny channels between hepatocytes where bile transport begins. Canals of Hering : Short ducts collecting bile from canaliculi, lined by hepatocytes and cholangiocytes. Bile Ductules & Interlobular Ducts : Bile moves through ductules (lined by cholangiocytes) into interlobular ducts within liver portal tracts. Segmental & Hepatic Ducts : Interlobular ducts form segmental ducts, eventually merging into right and left hepatic ducts, which exit the liver. - Bile Canaliculi : Tiny channels between hepatocytes where bile transport begins. - Canals of Hering : Short ducts collecting bile from canaliculi, lined by hepatocytes and cholangiocytes. - Bile Ductules & Interlobular Ducts : Bile moves through ductules (lined by cholangiocytes) into interlobular ducts within liver portal tracts. - Segmental & Hepatic Ducts : Interlobular ducts form segmental ducts, eventually merging into right and left hepatic ducts, which exit the liver. - Extrahepatic Ducts : The right and left hepatic ducts unite to form the common hepatic duct, which joins the cystic duct from the gallbladder, creating the bile duct. - Bile Canaliculi : Tiny channels between hepatocytes where bile transport begins. - Canals of Hering : Short ducts collecting bile from canaliculi, lined by hepatocytes and cholangiocytes. - Bile Ductules & Interlobular Ducts : Bile moves through ductules (lined by cholangiocytes) into interlobular ducts within liver portal tracts. - Segmental & Hepatic Ducts : Interlobular ducts form segmental ducts, eventually merging into right and left hepatic ducts, which exit the liver. Duodenum : Bile is released into the descending duodenum for digestion. Gallbladder: - It is the reservoir of bile - Structure : Pear-shaped pouch connected to the bile duct by the cystic duct. - Function : Stores and concentrates bile. - Anatomical Variability : Variations in size, shape, and position affect surgical procedures (e.g., cholecystectomy). - Clinical Significance: Gallstones & Obstructions (Causing pain/fever/jaundice) Sphincter of Oddi (Option B): Regulates the flow of bile into the duodenum but is not the focus during the identification of biliary anatomy in this scenario. The right hepatic duct (Option C) is higher up in the biliary tree, and although important, the common bile duct is more critical during this surgery. Pancreatic duct (Option D): Carries pancreatic secretions to the duodenum but is not directly involved in bile flow.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1402-1403; Solution Pages 1410-1413","sourcePage":1402,"sourcePageEnd":1403},{"id":"anatomy-33-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following structures is not a component of the sphincter of Oddi?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sphincter of the bile duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sphincter of the pancreatic duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sphincter of the ampulla"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sphincter of Rosenmuller"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Sphincter of Rosenmuller","explanation":"The sphincter of Rosenmuller is located in the pharynx and is responsible for closing the nasopharynx during swallowing. It is not a part of the Sphincter of Oddi. Sphincter of the bile duct (Option A): This is a part of the sphincter of Oddi, controlling the flow of bile from the common bile duct into the duodenum. Sphincter of the pancreatic duct (Option B): This part of the sphincter controls the flow of pancreatic enzymes and other secretions from the pancreatic duct into the duodenum. Sphincter of the ampulla (Option C): Also known as the sphincter of Oddi , it regulates the flow of bile and pancreatic secretions into the duodenum at the ampulla of Vater. Sphincter of Oddi - The sphincter of Oddi is a smooth muscle complex located at the distal bile duct and pancreatic duct junction in the duodenum. - Its function is to regulate bile and pancreatic secretion flow into the duodenum and prevent reflux from the duodenum into the ducts. - In adults, it is 15–20 mm long, primarily situated within the duodenal wall. - It comprises three distinct components: Sphincter of the bile duct Sphincter of the ampulla Sphincter of the pancreatic duct - Sphincter of the bile duct - Sphincter of the ampulla - Sphincter of the pancreatic duct - Sphincter of the bile duct - Sphincter of the ampulla - Sphincter of the pancreatic duct","sourceRef":"Question Page 1403; Solution Pages 1413-1414","sourcePage":1403,"sourcePageEnd":1403},{"id":"anatomy-33-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 45-year-old patient is scheduled for a liver imaging study. The radiologist explains that dividing the liver into physiological lobes is important for understanding liver anatomy and function. Which of the following is primarily responsible for dividing the liver into physiological lobes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Falciform ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cantlie's line"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ligamentum teres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Porta hepatis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Cantlie's line","explanation":"Cantlie's line is the imaginary line that divides the liver into right and left physiological lobes. Physiological Lobes of the Liver - The liver is divided into right and left physiological lobes based on the intrahepatic distribution of the bile ducts, hepatic artery, and portal vein. This division follows an imaginary sagittal plane called Cantlie’s line (Main portal fissure) . Posteroinferior Surface: The plane runs through the fossa for the gallbladder to the groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC). Anterosuperior Surface: It extends from the IVC to the cystic notch, located slightly to the right of the falciform ligament. - Posteroinferior Surface: The plane runs through the fossa for the gallbladder to the groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC). - Anterosuperior Surface: It extends from the IVC to the cystic notch, located slightly to the right of the falciform ligament. - The physiological right and left lobes are approximately equal in size. The caudate lobe is shared equally between the right and left lobes. - Each true lobe has its own primary branch of the hepatic artery and portal vein and is drained by its own hepatic duct. - Segments V and VIII lie immediately to the right, and segment IV immediately to the left, of the main portal fissure. - Posteroinferior Surface: The plane runs through the fossa for the gallbladder to the groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC). - Anterosuperior Surface: It extends from the IVC to the cystic notch, located slightly to the right of the falciform ligament. Segments of the liver- radiological view Anatomical Lobes of the Liver - Falciform ligament (Option A): It is incorrect because the liver is divided into the right and left lobes on the diaphragmatic surface by the attachment of the falciform ligament, with the right lobe being approximately six times larger than the left. - Ligamentum teres (Option C) and Porta hepatis (Option D): These options are incorrect because on the visceral surface , the liver consists of four lobes: right lobe, left lobe, quadrate lobe, and caudate lobe, separated by fissures and fossae, including the ligamentum teres, ligamentum venosum, porta hepatis, groove for the inferior vena cava (IVC), and fossa for the gallbladder.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1403; Solution Pages 1414-1416","sourcePage":1403,"sourcePageEnd":1403},{"id":"anatomy-33-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Match the following Sectors of the liver with their respective segments. 1. Right Posterior Sector a) Segments IV 2. Right Anterior Sector b) Segments III 3. Left Medial Sector c) Segment VII 4. Left Lateral Sector d) Segments V 1. Right Posterior Sector a) Segments IV 2. Right Anterior Sector b) Segments III 3. Left Medial Sector c) Segment VII 4. Left Lateral Sector d) Segments V **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-d, 3-b, 4-c"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-d, 2-c, 3-b, 4-a"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b","explanation":"- The liver is divided into four portal sectors—right anterior, right posterior, left medial, and left lateral—by the main branches of the hepatic portal vein. - The three main hepatic veins lie between these sectors, referred to as intersectoral veins, or portal fissures. - Each sector is typically subdivided into segments based on the supply from tertiary divisions of the perivascular fibrous capsules (Glissonian sheaths). Fissure Type Location Structures it divides Major Fissures Main Portal Fissure Liver parenchyma Contains the Middle hepatic vein Divides the liver into right and left hemilivers. Left Portal Fissure Liver parenchyma Contains the left hepatic vein Divides the left hemiliver into medial and lateral sectors Right Portal Fissure Liver parenchyma Contains the right hepatic vein Divides the right hemiliver into anterior and posterior sectors Minor Fissures Umbilical Fissure Surface of the liver Divides segments II and III from segment IV Venous Fissure Surface of the liver Contains the ligamentum venosum Separates the caudate lobe from segment II. Fissure of Gans Surface of the liver Found on the undersurface of the right lobe Surface landmark Sector Segments Right Posterior Sector Segments VI and VII Right Anterior Sector Segments V and VIII Left Medial Sector Segment IV (and part of I) Left Lateral Sector Segments II and III","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1403-1404; Solution Pages 1416-1418","sourcePage":1403,"sourcePageEnd":1404},{"id":"anatomy-33-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A patient is undergoing imaging for liver evaluation. The radiologist describes a segment located posterior to the right hepatic vein and in the superior part of the right posterior sector. Which segment of the liver is being described?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Segment V"},{"letter":"B","text":"Segment III"},{"letter":"C","text":"Segment VII"},{"letter":"D","text":"Segment II"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Segment VII","explanation":"Segment VII is the segment located in the superior part of the right posterior sector and posterior to the right hepatic vein. Segment V (Option A): Incorrect because this segment is located in the right anterior inferior sector of the liver, anterior to the right hepatic vein, so it does not match the description provided. Segment III (Option B): Incorrect because segment III is located in the left liver, specifically in the inferior part of the left lateral sector, which is far from the described location. Segment II (Option D): Incorrect because this segment is also located in the left liver, in the superior part of the left lateral sector, and does not correspond to the posterior location described. - Segments I to IV (Left Lobe): Supply: Left branch of the hepatic artery and left branch of the portal vein. Drainage: Drained by the left hepatic duct. - Supply: Left branch of the hepatic artery and left branch of the portal vein. - Drainage: Drained by the left hepatic duct. - Segments V to VIII (Right Lobe): Supply: Right hepatic artery and right branch of the portal vein. Drainage: Drained by the right hepatic duct. - Supply: Right hepatic artery and right branch of the portal vein. - Drainage: Drained by the right hepatic duct. - Supply: Left branch of the hepatic artery and left branch of the portal vein. - Drainage: Drained by the left hepatic duct. - Supply: Right hepatic artery and right branch of the portal vein. - Drainage: Drained by the right hepatic duct. Segments of the liver Segment VIII: The superior part of Segment I (Caudate Lobe): Posterior Segment II: Posterolateral to the left the right anterior sector. Drainage: to segment IV, straddles the inferior portal fissure. Drainage: Drains into Drains into the right and middle vena cava. Drainage: Multiple small the left hepatic vein; rarely directly hepatic veins. veins drain directly into the inferior into the inferior vena cava. vena cava. Bile Ducts: Related to the confluence of the right and left hepatic ducts. Segment VII: The superior part of the Segment III: Between the umbilical right posterior sector, posterior to the and left portal fissures. Drainage: right hepatic vein. Drainage: Primarily into the left hepatic vein; Primarily into the right hepatic vein; alternative drainage via falciform occasionally directly into the inferior ligament veins. vena cava. Segment VI: Inferior part of the right Segment V: Inferior segment of the Segment IV: Between the umbilical posterior sector, posterior to the right right anterior sector. Drainage: fissure and main portal fissure, portal fissure. Drainage: Usually into Typically into the right and middle anterior to segment I. Drainage: the right hepatic vein; may drain hepatic veins; can drain directly into Usually into the middle hepatic vein; directly into the inferior vena cava. the inferior vena cava. can also drain into the left hepatic vein.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1404; Solution Pages 1418-1419","sourcePage":1404,"sourcePageEnd":1404},{"id":"anatomy-33-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45 y/o man presents with symptoms of gastric dysmotility and increased acid secretion. Which structures are primarily involved in the parasympathetic innervation of the stomach?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Greater splanchnic nerves and coeliac plexus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vagus nerves and their branches"},{"letter":"C","text":"T6 to T10 spinal segments"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lumbar sympathetic ganglia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Vagus nerves and their branches","explanation":"The parasympathetic innervation of the stomach is primarily provided by the vagus nerves and their branches. Nerve supply of stomach Sympathetic Innervation Parasympathetic Innervation Derived from T6 to T10 spinal segments via greater Derived from vagus nerves . Anterior vagal trunk : Hepatic splanchnic nerves . Fibers run along arteries via coeliac branch : Supplies liver, gallbladder, and pyloric antrum. and hepatic plexuses . Functions: Vasomotor (regulates Coeliac branch : Follows left gastric artery to celiac blood flow). Motor to pyloric sphincter . Inhibits the plexus. Gastric branch -Nerve of Latarjet: The largest remaining gastric musculature. Pathway for pain branch, follows the lesser curvature to supply the anterior sensations from the stomach gastric surface down to the pylorus. Posterior vagal trunk : Smaller gastric branches : For posterior fundus, body, and pyloric antrum. Larger coeliac branches : For coeliac plexus. The nerve of Grassi : One or more branches of the posterior vagal trunk originate at the level of the gastroesophageal junction and supply the gastric fundus. Functions : Motor and secretomotor to stomach. Stimulates gastric motility and secretion of pepsin and HCl . Inhibits pyloric sphincter. Greater splanchnic nerves and coeliac plexus (Option A) : These are involved in sympathetic innervation. T6 to T10 spinal segments (Option C) : These are associated with sympathetic, not parasympathetic innervation. Lumbar sympathetic ganglia (Option D) : These ganglia are involved in sympathetic innervation, not parasympathetic.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1404-1405; Solution Pages 1419-1421","sourcePage":1404,"sourcePageEnd":1405},{"id":"anatomy-33-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following structures is not part of the stomach bed?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left kidney"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreas"},{"letter":"C","text":"Diaphragm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right kidney"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Right kidney","explanation":"The right kidney is not part of the stomach bed, as it is located on the right side of the body, far from the stomach. Stomach Bed: The stomach bed refers to the group of structures located behind the posterior surface of the stomach. These structures form the support for the stomach and are separated from it by the cavity of the lesser sac. Left kidney (Option A): The left kidney lies posterior to the stomach and is part of the stomach bed. Pancreas (Option B): The pancreas lies posterior to the stomach, and is part of the stomach bed. Diaphragm (Option C): The diaphragm forms the superior aspect of the stomach bed and is part of the stomach bed. Structures located in the stomach bed: Note: The spleen is sometimes considered part of the stomach bed but is separated from the stomach by the greater sac.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1405; Solution Pages 1421-1422","sourcePage":1405,"sourcePageEnd":1405},{"id":"anatomy-33-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 60 y/o woman presents with abdominal pain localized to the upper right quadrant. Imaging reveals a mass adjacent to the uncinate process of the pancreas. Which part of the duodenum is most likely in direct relation to this mass?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First (superior) part"},{"letter":"B","text":"Second (descending) part"},{"letter":"C","text":"Third (horizontal) part"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fourth (ascending) part"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Third (horizontal) part","explanation":"The third (horizontal) part is positioned horizontally and is more likely to be in contact with structures adjacent to the pancreas. Duodenum - Shortest, widest, and most fixed part of the small intestine. - Extends from pylorus to duodenojejunal flexure . - Shaped like a C around the head of the pancreas. - Located above the level of the umbilicus , opposite the L1, L2, and L3 vertebrae . - Length : 25 cm (10 inches). - Shape : C-shaped loop. - Divided into four parts : First (superior) part : 5 cm (2 inches) Second (descending) part : 7.5 cm (3 inches) Third (horizontal) part : 10 cm (4 inches) Fourth (ascending) part : 2.5 cm (1 inch) - First (superior) part : 5 cm (2 inches) - Second (descending) part : 7.5 cm (3 inches) - Third (horizontal) part : 10 cm (4 inches) - Fourth (ascending) part : 2.5 cm (1 inch) - Mostly retroperitoneal , except proximal 2.5 cm. First Part Second Part Third Part Fourth part Anteriorly : Quadrate lobe Anteriorly : Liver, Anteriorly : Superior Anteriorly : Transverse of liver, gallbladder. transverse colon, small mesenteric vessels, the colon, transverse Posteriorly : Portal vein, intestine. Posteriorly : root of the mesentery. mesocolon, stomach. gastroduodenal artery, bile Right kidney, renal Posteriorly : IVC, Posteriorly : Left psoas duct. Superiorly : Epiploic vessels, IVC, psoas major. abdominal aorta, right major, left sympathetic foramen. Inferiorly : Head Medially : Head of the ureter, gonadal vessels. chain, left renal artery. and neck of the pancreas. pancreas, bile duct. Superiorly : Head of the Superiorly : Body of Laterally : Right colic pancreas with the uncinate pancreas. To the left : Left flexure. process. Inferiorly : Coils kidney, left ureter. of jejunum. First (superior) part (Option A) : While it is anteriorly related to the liver, it is not in direct contact with the pancreas. Second (descending) part (Option B): It is adjacent to the head of the pancreas but not directly to the uncinate process. Fourth (ascending) part (Option D) : It is more posterior and further from the pancreas","sourceRef":"Question Page 1405; Solution Pages 1422-1424","sourcePage":1405,"sourcePageEnd":1405},{"id":"anatomy-33-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of the ileum compared to the jejunum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thicker and more vascular wall"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fewer Peyer’s patches"},{"letter":"C","text":"More numerous straight arteries"},{"letter":"D","text":"Larger and more prominent circular folds"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. More numerous straight arteries","explanation":"The ileum has more numerous but shorter straight arteries compared to the jejunum. Comparison of Jejunum and Ileum: Feature Jejunum Ileum Diameter Larger (4 cm external, 3 cm internal) Smaller (3 cm external, 2.5 cm internal) Wall Thickness Thicker, more vascular Thinner, less vascular (Option A) Circular Folds Deeper, more numerous Flatter, less numerous distally (Option D) Mesentery Shorter Longer Arteries Larger, with 1-3 tiers of arcades Smaller, with 2-6 tiers of arcades Straight Arteries Fewer, longer More numerous, shorter (Option C) Lymphoid Tissue Fewer Peyer's patches More Peyer’s patches, especially in terminal 25 cm (Option B) Villi Taller, more numerous Shorter, fewer towards the distal end","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1405-1406; Solution Pages 1424-1425","sourcePage":1405,"sourcePageEnd":1406},{"id":"anatomy-33-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-33","chapter":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topic":"Lesson 33 - Anatomy Abdominal Organs","topicIndex":33,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 45 y/o male patient presents with abdominal pain and distension. A CT scan reveals multiple appendices epiploicae, particularly prominent in the sigmoid colon. Which of the following statements regarding the anatomy of the large intestine is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The caecum is typically 8-10 cm long and is a common site for appendices epiploicae."},{"letter":"B","text":"The sigmoid colon, which is S-shaped, leads directly into the rectum."},{"letter":"C","text":"The total length of the large intestine in adults ranges from 1 to 1.5 meters."},{"letter":"D","text":"Appendices epiploicae are more common in the distal colon."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. The caecum is typically 8-10 cm long and is a common site for appendices epiploicae.","explanation":"epiploicae. The caecum is not typically 8-10 cm long, and appendices epiploicae are more prevalent in the sigmoid colon and distal colon. Length of the Large Intestine and Its Components: Total Length of the Large intestine length in a living adult: 1 to 1.5 metres (Option C ruled out) Components of the Large Intestine: Component Description Caecum (Option A) Blind pouch at the beginning of the large intestine and is approximately 6 cm long Vermiform Appendix Narrow tube, 6-10 cm long, attached to the caecum Ascending Colon Travels upward from the caecum (20-25cm long) Right Colic Flexure Bend between ascending and transverse colon Transverse Colon Crosses the abdomen horizontally Left Colic Flexure Bend between transverse and descending colon Descending Colon Travels downward from the left colic flexure (10-15 cm long) Sigmoid Colon S-shaped segment leading to the rectum (Option B ruled out) Rectum Final straight portion, ~15 cm above the anal canal Appendices Epiploicae: Feature Details Description Small, fat-filled pouches of peritoneum on the colon's surface. Location Few or absent on caecum and ascending colon (Option A). More common on the distal colon (Option D ruled out). Numerous on the sigmoid colon; can enlarge with obesity. Absent on rectum. Taeniae Coli - They are are three distinct longitudinal bands of smooth muscle found in the colon. - They help in peristalsis in the colon. - The arrangement of Taenia coli allows for the characteristic haustra or pouches of the colon, contributing to its segmented appearance. - There are 3 types: Type of Taeniae Coli Position Free Taenia Located on the antimesenteric aspect, opposite the mesentery. Omental Taenia Positioned posterolaterally. Mesocolic Taenia Positioned posteromedially, between the free taenia and the mesentery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1406; Solution Pages 1425-1428","sourcePage":1406,"sourcePageEnd":1406},{"id":"anatomy-34-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":1,"question":"What structure crosses the ureter anteriorly on both sides during its abdominal course?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gonadal vessels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Genitofemoral nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Duodenum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gonadal vessels","explanation":"In both males and females, the ureter is crossed anteriorly by the gonadal vessels during its abdominal course. - These vessels are the testicular artery and vein in males and the ovarian artery and vein in females. Relations of the abdominal part of the Ureter Anterior Relation Posterior Relation Right Ureter The second part of the Duodenum Right Psoas Major Bifurcation of Right Colic vessels Ileocolic vessels Right Common Iliac artery Right testicular and ovarian vessels Root of mesentery Left Ureter Left Colic vessels Sigmoidal vessels Left Psoas Major Bifurcation of Left Left testicular and ovarian vessels Common Iliac artery Sigmoid mesocolon Inferior mesenteric artery (Option A): The inferior mesenteric artery is a medial relation to the left ureter but does not cross it anteriorly. Genitofemoral nerve (Option C): The ureter crosses anterior to the genitofemoral nerve as it travels down the abdomen, making it a posterior relation. Duodenum (Option D): The descending part of the duodenum only crosses the right ureter anteriorly at its origin near the renal pelvis, but it does not cross the ureter on both sides. - The left ureter is not related to the duodenum at all.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1429; Solution Pages 1433-1434","sourcePage":1429,"sourcePageEnd":1429},{"id":"anatomy-34-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which vessels primarily supply the middle part of the ureter?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Renal artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vesical vessels"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aorta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Uterine vessels"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Aorta","explanation":"- The middle part of the ureter is primarily supplied by branches from the aorta. - It can also receive blood from gonadal or iliac vessels, but the aorta is the key vessel for this segment. Blood Supply of the Ureter - Arteries are closely associated with the peritoneum. - They form a plexus on the ureter's surface with ascending and descending branches. - The upper and middle parts receive blood from the medial side, while the pelvic part is supplied from the lateral side. - Upper Part: Supplied by branches from the renal artery. (Option A ruled out) May also receive branches from gonadal or colic vessels. - Supplied by branches from the renal artery. (Option A ruled out) - May also receive branches from gonadal or colic vessels. - Middle Part: Receives branches from the aorta. Can also receive branches from gonadal or iliac vessels. - Receives branches from the aorta. - Can also receive branches from gonadal or iliac vessels. - Pelvic Part: Supplied by branches from vesical, middle rectal, or uterine vessels. (Options B & C ruled out) - Supplied by branches from vesical, middle rectal, or uterine vessels. (Options B & C ruled out) - Supplied by branches from the renal artery. (Option A ruled out) - May also receive branches from gonadal or colic vessels. - Receives branches from the aorta. - Can also receive branches from gonadal or iliac vessels. - Supplied by branches from vesical, middle rectal, or uterine vessels. (Options B & C ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1429; Solution Pages 1434-1435","sourcePage":1429,"sourcePageEnd":1429},{"id":"anatomy-34-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 9 y/o child presents with abdominal pain and hematuria. Imaging reveals that the left renal vein is compressed between the abdominal aorta and the superior mesenteric artery. What is a key anatomical feature contributing to this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The left renal vein passes behind the abdominal aorta."},{"letter":"B","text":"The pancreas lies directly posterior to the superior mesenteric artery."},{"letter":"C","text":"The left renal vein travels behind the pancreas before crossing the aorta."},{"letter":"D","text":"The pancreas is situated inferior to the left renal vein."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The left renal vein travels behind the pancreas before crossing the aorta.","explanation":"The left renal vein's route behind the pancreas contributes to its susceptibility to compression. Relationship of the Pancreas, Veins, and Aorta in Nutcracker Syndrome Nutcracker Syndrome: - A condition caused by compression of the left renal vein leading to the left renal vein hypertension. - Highlights the anatomical relationship between the pancreas, veins, and aorta. Anatomical Features Description Left Renal Vein Originates from the hilum of the left kidney. Travels behind the pancreas and splenic vein. (Option C) (Option D ruled out) Crosses the abdominal aorta anteriorly below the superior mesenteric artery. (Option A ruled out) Nutcracker Analogy The left renal vein is compressed between the aorta and superior mesenteric artery, resembling a nutcracker. Symptoms Can lead to left renal vein hypertension. Symptoms include hematuria and varicocele, especially in children. Pancreas' Role in Nutcracker Syndrome: - Positioned directly in front of the compression site, anterior to the SMA. (Option B ruled out) - Proximity to vascular structures suggests the potential to worsen the condition. - The uncinate process being close to the superior mesenteric artery and vein further emphasises the pancreas's proximity to major vessels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1429-1430; Solution Pages 1435-1437","sourcePage":1429,"sourcePageEnd":1430},{"id":"anatomy-34-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which vein receives drainage from both the left gonadal and left adrenal veins?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right renal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left renal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right adrenal vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Left renal vein","explanation":"Left gonadal and left adrenal veins drain into the left renal vein , emptying into the inferior vena cava. - This is a key anatomical difference from the right side, where the veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava. Inferior vena cava (Option A): While the right gonadal and adrenal veins drain directly into the inferior vena cava, the left gonadal and adrenal veins drain into the left renal vein instead. Right renal vein (Option B): The right renal vein handles drainage from structures on the right side of the body, such as the right kidney. - It does not receive blood from the left gonadal or adrenal veins. Right adrenal vein (Option D): The right adrenal vein is responsible for draining blood from the right adrenal gland into the inferior vena cava. - It does not interact with the left gonadal or adrenal veins.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1430; Solution Pages 1437-1438","sourcePage":1430,"sourcePageEnd":1430},{"id":"anatomy-34-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following layers immediately surrounds the kidney and adheres closely to its surface?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pararenal fat"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fibrous capsule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Renal fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Perirenal fat"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Fibrous capsule","explanation":"The fibrous capsule, also known as the true capsule, is a thin, fibrous membrane that tightly adheres to the surface of the kidney. - It is the kidney's immediate protective layer, safeguarding it from minor injuries. Internal Structure - Cortex: Outer region, extends between renal pyramids as renal columns. - Medulla : Contains 5-11 renal pyramids, whose apices (renal papillae) project into minor calyces. - Renal sinus: Houses renal pelvis, calyces, vessels, and fat. - Calyces : Minor calyces collect urine, merging into major calyces that form the renal pelvis. Microscopic Structure - Nephron: Functional unit responsible for filtering blood and producing urine, consisting of the renal corpuscle and tubule. - Collecting ducts : Drain urine from nephrons into papillary ducts, which lead to the minor calyces. Coverings of the kidney (From inside Description Function out) Renal Capsule (Option B) A thin, fibrous layer directly covering Protection, shape maintenance, stem the kidney. Contains mesenchymal cell niche for repair stem cells (MSCs). Perirenal Fat (Option D ruled out) Adipose tissue surrounds the renal Cushions and insulates the kidney capsule (also called perinephric fat). Renal Fascia (Option C ruled out) Connective tissue (Gerota's fascia) Secures kidney position and around perirenal fat, anchoring the separates from adjacent organs kidney. Pararenal Fat (Option A ruled out) Outer adipose layer beyond the renal Provides additional cushioning and fascia. support","sourceRef":"Question Page 1430; Solution Pages 1438-1439","sourcePage":1430,"sourcePageEnd":1430},{"id":"anatomy-34-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which structure is in direct contact with the upper pole of the right kidney?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right lobe of the liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right suprarenal gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right colic flexure"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Right suprarenal gland","explanation":"The right suprarenal gland lies directly on the upper pole of the right kidney, potentially overlapping the kidney's medial border. - There is no intervening peritoneum, making the gland and kidney directly connected. Anterior Relations of Kidney Right Kidney Left Kidney The right lobe of the Liver Right suprarenal gland The Left suprarenal gland Stomach Spleen Jejunum Left colic second part of the duodenum Jejunum Hepatic (Right flexure Pancreas and splenic vessels colic) flexure The right lobe of the liver (Option A): While the right lobe of the liver covers a large portion of the anterior surface of the right kidney, they are separated by a layer of peritoneum . Duodenum (Option B): The descending part of the duodenum lies against the medial side of the right kidney. - However, it contacts the central portion of the kidney rather than the upper pole. Right colic flexure (Option D): The right colic flexure, the junction of the ascending and transverse colon, is located inferiorly and laterally to the right kidney.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1430; Solution Pages 1439-1441","sourcePage":1430,"sourcePageEnd":1430},{"id":"anatomy-34-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which rib is related to the posterior surface of the left kidney but not the right kidney?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"7th rib"},{"letter":"B","text":"8th rib"},{"letter":"C","text":"12th rib"},{"letter":"D","text":"11th rib"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 11th rib","explanation":"The 11th rib is a posterior relation of the left kidney but not the right kidney . - The right kidney is related to the 12th rib, while the left kidney is related to both the 11th and 12th ribs. Posterior Relations of Kidney Right Kidney Left Kidney Only 12th Rib 11th Rib 12th Rib ( Common to both Right and Left Kidney) Muscles : Diaphragm and in mediolateral order - Psoas Major, Quadratus Lumborum, Transversus Abdominis Ligaments : Medial and Lateral Arcuate Nerves : Subcostal, Iliohypogastric, Ilioinguinal Vessels : Subcostal 7th rib (Option A): The 7th rib is located higher in the thoracic region and does not have any direct relation to the kidneys. 8th rib (Option B): The 8th rib is positioned too high to be a posterior relation to either kidney. 12th rib (Option C): The 12th rib is related to both the right and left kidneys, not just the left kidney. It lies posteriorly to both kidneys.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1430-1431; Solution Pages 1441-1442","sourcePage":1430,"sourcePageEnd":1431},{"id":"anatomy-34-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following is not a typical site of ureteric constriction?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Junction with the renal pelvis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Crossing by ductus deferens"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the pelvic outlet"},{"letter":"D","text":"Opening at the trigone"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. At the pelvic outlet","explanation":"The opening of the pelvic outlet is not typically recognized as a site of ureteric constriction. - Ureteric constriction occurs at the pelvic inlet ( pelvic brim) where it crosses the common iliac artery. Ureteric constrictions The ureters have several points of constriction that are clinically significant, particularly for renal stone impaction. The typical sites of constriction are: 1. Junction with the renal pelvis : This is where the ureter narrows as it transitions from the renal pelvis. (Option A ruled out) 2. At the linea terminalis : Near the medial border of the psoas major, where the ureter is kinked as it crosses the pelvic brim to enter the pelvis at the pelvic inlet. 3. Intramural segment : As the ureter pierces the bladder wall, it runs obliquely through the wall, which is the narrowest part . 4. Crossing by ductus deferens or broad ligament: The ureter crosses underneath the ductus deferens in males and lies behind the broad ligament in females. (Option B ruled out) 5. Opening at the trigone: The ureter opens at the lateral angle of the trigone area within the urinary bladder. (Option D ruled out)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1431; Solution Pages 1442-1443","sourcePage":1431,"sourcePageEnd":1431},{"id":"anatomy-34-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which part of the pancreas is covered by the peritoneum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Head of the pancreas"},{"letter":"B","text":"Body of the pancreas"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tail of the pancreas"},{"letter":"D","text":"Entire pancreas"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Tail of the pancreas","explanation":"The tail of the pancreas is the only part of the pancreas that is covered by the peritoneum. - It extends towards the spleen and is enclosed by the peritoneum, making it intraperitoneal. Retroperitoneal organs: - Kidneys - Duodenum - Ascending and descending colon - Rectum (lower ■ ) - Adrenal glands - Ureters - Head and body of Pancreas (Options A, B) - Aorta and Inferior venacava","sourceRef":"Question Page 1431; Solution Page 1443","sourcePage":1431,"sourcePageEnd":1431},{"id":"anatomy-34-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-34","chapter":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topic":"Lesson 34 - Anatomy Pancreas, Kidney, and Ureter","topicIndex":34,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents with complaints of a progressively enlarged abdomen and discomfort on the left side. Imaging studies reveal splenomegaly. Which structure is primarily responsible for preventing the downward displacement of the spleen in this case?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Phrenicocolic Ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrosplenic Ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Splenorenal Ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diaphragm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Phrenicocolic Ligament","explanation":"- Downward displacement of the enlarged spleen is prevented by th e phrenicolic ligament. - The phrenicolic ligament connects the splenic flexure of the colon to the diaphragm. It is not attached to the spleen. - However, it lies below the spleen and supports its lower pole. - When the spleen gets enlarged, phrenicolic ligaments along with the splenic flexure of the colon restrict its downward displacement. - Hence, it is also called Sustentaculum lienis. Ligaments of the spleen:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1431-1432; Solution Pages 1443-1444","sourcePage":1431,"sourcePageEnd":1432},{"id":"anatomy-35-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements about the autonomic innervation of the gastrointestinal tract is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The vagus nerve provides parasympathetic innervation to the entire length of the gut."},{"letter":"B","text":"The pelvic splanchnic nerves supply sympathetic innervation to the hindgut."},{"letter":"C","text":"The greater splanchnic nerves provide sympathetic innervation primarily to the midgut."},{"letter":"D","text":"The parasympathetic supply to the hindgut originates from the sacral spinal cord."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The parasympathetic supply to the hindgut originates from the sacral spinal cord.","explanation":"cord. The parasympathetic supply to the hindgut originates from the sacral spinal cord , specifically from segments S2-S4 . These fibers form the pelvic splanchnic nerves. Foregut, midgut and hindgut- Innervation Feature Foregut Midgut Hindgut Sympathetic (Preganglionic) Preganglionic fibers arise Preganglionic fibers arise Preganglionic fibers arise from the thoracic spinal from the thoracic spinal from the lumbar spinal cord (T5–T9)- and form the cord (T10–T11) - and form cord (L1–L2) - and form greater splanchnic nerves , the lesser splanchnic the lumbar splanchnic which travel to the celiac nerves , which travel to the nerves , which travel to the ganglia . superior mesenteric inferior mesenteric ganglion. ganglion. Sympathetic (Postganglioni Postganglionic fibers from Postganglionic fibers from Postganglionic fibers from c) the celiac ganglion supply the superior mesenteric the inferior mesenteric the foregut organs. These ganglion supply the midgut ganglion supply the fibers follow branches of organs. These fibers follow hindgut organs. These the celiac trunk to reach the superior mesenteric fibers follow the inferior the target organs. artery and its branches. mesenteric artery and its branches. Parasympathetic Preganglionic parasympath Preganglionic parasympath Preganglionic parasympath etic fibers arise from the etic fibers also arise from etic fibers arise from the vagus nerve (CN X) . They the vagus nerve (CN X) . pelvic splanchnic nerves pass through the celiac They pass through the (S2–S4) . These fibers plexus and directly superior mesenteric plexus pass through the inferior innervate the organs of the and innervate the midgut hypogastric plexus and foregut. organs. innervate the hindgut organs. - While the vagus nerve does provide parasympathetic innervation to a large portion of the gut, it does not supply the entire length. - It innervates the foregut and midgut, extending approximately to the proximal two-thirds of the transverse colon. (Option A) - The pelvic splanchnic nerves provide parasympathetic, not sympathetic, innervation to the hindgut. (Option B) - The greater splanchnic nerves primarily provide sympathetic innervation to the foregut structures, not the midgut. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1445; Solution Pages 1448-1449","sourcePage":1445,"sourcePageEnd":1445},{"id":"anatomy-35-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 45-year-old male presents with severe epigastric pain radiating to the back. He reports that the pain worsens after eating and is associated with nausea. On examination, there is epigastric tenderness, but no rebound or guarding. The patient's past medical history is significant for chronic pancreatitis. You suspect autonomic involvement in his pain pattern. Which of the following nerves is most likely involved in transmitting the visceral pain signals from the pancreas?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Greater splanchnic nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser splanchnic nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pelvic splanchnic nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vagus nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Greater splanchnic nerve","explanation":"Epigastric pain radiating to the back in case of chronic pancreatitis is due to autonomic innervation via the greater splanchnic nerve. Sympathetic Innervation: The nerve supply of the abdomen is primarily through the sympathetic and parasympathetic systems, with the sympathetic innervation arising from the thoracic sympathetic chain. - The sympathetic fibers that supply the abdomen primarily come from the lower thoracic splanchnic nerves , which are part of the thoracic sympathetic trunk. - These nerves include the greater, lesser, and least splanchnic nerves , which originate from the T5 to T12 vertebral levels . These nerves carry presynaptic sympathetic fibers that travel through the sympathetic trunk without synapsing and enter the abdominopelvic splanchnic nerves . - The greater splanchnic nerve (T5–T9) synapses in the celiac ganglion , providing sympathetic innervation to foregut derivatives, including the stomach, liver, pancreas, and parts of the small intestine. Chronic pancreatitis can cause epigastric pain, often referred to the back due to autonomic innervation via the greater splanchnic nerve. (Option A) - Chronic pancreatitis can cause epigastric pain, often referred to the back due to autonomic innervation via the greater splanchnic nerve. (Option A) - The lesser splanchnic nerve (T10–T11) synapses in the superior mesenteric ganglion , innervating the midgut derivatives, including the small intestine and parts of the colon. (Option B) - The least splanchnic nerve (T12) innervates the kidneys via the aorticorenal ganglion . - Chronic pancreatitis can cause epigastric pain, often referred to the back due to autonomic innervation via the greater splanchnic nerve. (Option A) After synapsing in the prevertebral ganglia (like the celiac, superior mesenteric, and aorticorenal ganglia), the postsynaptic fibers follow the arterial branches in peri-arterial plexuses to their target organs. - Sympathetic stimulation in the abdomen primarily causes vasoconstriction and inhibits peristalsis . - This sympathetic supply is important for regulating blood flow and slowing down gastrointestinal activity, particularly during stress responses (fight or flight). Pelvic splanchnic nerve (Option C) : Involved in parasympathetic supply to the hindgut, not the foregut. - Pain from the pancreas would not be transmitted via this nerve. Vagus nerve (Option D) : Primarily parasympathetic, focusing on motility and secretions. - It does not carry the sympathetic visceral pain signals from the pancreas.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1445; Solution Pages 1449-1451","sourcePage":1445,"sourcePageEnd":1445},{"id":"anatomy-35-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 45-year-old man presents with dull, poorly localized abdominal pain that began 12 hours ago. Over time, the pain becomes sharper and more localized in the right lower quadrant. The patient also reports nausea and loss of appetite. On physical examination, tenderness is noted at McBurney's point. Which of the following best explains the initial presentation of the patient's pain?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Irritation of the peritoneum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stimulation of visceral afferent fibers accompanying sympathetic nerves"},{"letter":"C","text":"Direct irritation of somatic nerves in the abdominal wall"},{"letter":"D","text":"Referred pain via the phrenic nerve (C3-C5)"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Stimulation of visceral afferent fibers accompanying sympathetic nerves","explanation":"Referred pain occurs when visceral afferent fibers transmit pain signals to the spinal cord, which misinterprets these signals as originating from a somatic region innervated by the same spinal segment (same dermatome). - Initially, the pain in appendicitis is referred to the periumbilical region due to irritation of visceral afferent fibers. - These fibers travel with the sympathetic nerves (thoracolumbar flow), which innervate the small intestine. - As the inflammation progresses and the parietal peritoneum becomes involved, the pain localizes to the right lower quadrant. - Unlike visceral pain, pain from structures like the parietal peritoneum is more localized and sharp. - This type of pain is mediated by somatic nerves, which can pinpoint the exact area of irritation. Referred pain and associated dermatomes: Region Dermatome Shoulder tip C4 Nipples T4 Epigastrium (Foregut pain) T7 Periumbilical (Umbilical pain) T10 Suprapubic region (Inguinal region) L1 Little toe S1 The lower limb will begin in L1 and S1 region Irritation of the peritoneum (Option A) : This explains the late phase of appendicitis when the pain becomes sharp and localized due to somatic innervation of the parietal peritoneum. - However, it does not account for the initial referred pain. Direct irritation of somatic nerves in the abdominal wall (Option C) : Somatic nerves cause sharp, well-localized pain, as seen in later-stage appendicitis, but they do not explain the initial vague, dull pain. Referred pain via the phrenic nerve (C3-C5) (Option D) : The phrenic nerve innervates the diaphragm and causes referred pain to the shoulder (e.g., from diaphragmatic irritation). - It is unrelated to appendicitis or abdominal pain.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1445-1446; Solution Pages 1451-1453","sourcePage":1445,"sourcePageEnd":1446},{"id":"anatomy-35-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":4,"question":"At which vertebral level does the abdominal aorta typically bifurcate into the common iliac arteries?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T12"},{"letter":"B","text":"L2"},{"letter":"C","text":"L4"},{"letter":"D","text":"L5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. L4","explanation":"The abdominal aorta typically bifurcates into the common iliac arteries at the level of the L4 vertebra. - This bifurcation point is an important anatomical landmark in the abdomen. Abdominal Aorta and Its Branches Vertebral Level Major Events and Branches Details T12 (Option A) Aortic hiatus in the diaphragm Opening of the diaphragm for Beginning of abdominal aorta passage of the aorta Transition from thoracic to abdominal aorta T12-L1 Celiac artery Supplies the foregut L1 Superior mesenteric artery (SMA) Superior to the renal artery (originate at the same level) At transpyloric plane Supplies midgut L1-L2 (Option B) Renal arteries Inferior suprarenal Supply kidneys Gonadal artery arteries Gonadal arteries (Testicular or ovarian arteries) L3 Inferior mesenteric artery (IMA) Supplies hindgut L4 Bifurcation Aorta divides into right and left common iliac arteries L4-L5 (Option D) Common iliac arteries Further divide into internal and external iliac arteries","sourceRef":"Question Page 1446; Solution Pages 1453-1454","sourcePage":1446,"sourcePageEnd":1446},{"id":"anatomy-35-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with severe abdominal pain and bloody diarrhea. CT angiography reveals thrombosis of a major vein draining the distal transverse colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon. Which of the following veins is most likely to be affected in this patient's condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hepatic portal vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior mesenteric vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior mesenteric vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Celiac vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Inferior mesenteric vein","explanation":"The patient's symptoms and the affected area (distal transverse colon, descending colon, and sigmoid colon) indicate an issue with the hindgut and its blood supply . - Hindgut is drained by inferior mesenteric vein. Feature Foregut Midgut Hindgut Embryological Origin Derived from the cranial Derived from the middle Derived from the caudal part of the primitive gut part of the primitive gut part of the primitive gut tube tube tube Main Organs/Structures Esophagus, stomach, liver, Second, third, and fourth Distal 1/3 of transverse gallbladder, pancreas, and parts of the duodenum, colon, descending colon, first and second parts of jejunum, ileum, cecum, sigmoid colon, rectum, the duodenum appendix, ascending upper part of the anal canal colon, proximal 2/3 of transverse colon Arterial Supply Celiac trunk (Celiac artery) Superior mesenteric artery Inferior mesenteric artery Venous Drainage Drains into the portal vein Drains into the portal vein Drains into the portal vein via the splenic vein and its via the superior mesenteric via the inferior mesenteric tributaries vein vein Lymphatic Drainage Celiac lymph nodes Superior mesenteric lymph Inferior mesenteric lymph nodes nodes Nerve Supply Sympathetic: Greater Sympathetic: Lesser Sympathetic: Lumbar splanchnic nerves splanchnic nerves splanchnic nerves (L1–L2); (T5–T9); Parasympathetic: (T10–T11); Parasympatheti Parasympathetic: Pelvic Vagus nerve c: Vagus nerve splanchnic nerves (S2–S4)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1446; Solution Pages 1454-1455","sourcePage":1446,"sourcePageEnd":1446},{"id":"anatomy-35-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following statements about the innervation and vascular supply of the uterus and vagina is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The parasympathetic supply to the entire uterus and vagina comes from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4)"},{"letter":"B","text":"The uterine artery is a branch of the external iliac artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"The vaginal venous plexus drains primarily into the internal pudendal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"The superior part of the vagina receives its arterial supply from the vaginal artery, while the inferior part is supplied by branches of the internal pudendal artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. The superior part of the vagina receives its arterial supply from the vaginal artery, while the inferior part is supplied by branches of the internal pudendal artery","explanation":"orrect Answer: D) The superior part of the vagina receives its arterial supply from the vaginal artery, while the inferior part is supplied by branches of the internal pudendal artery The superior part of the vagina receives its arterial supply from the vaginal artery , while branches of the internal pudendal artery supply the inferior part . - The neurovascular supply of the female reproductive system is given in the table below: Neurovascular supply of the female reproductive system Structure Arterial Supply Venous Drainage Sympathetic Parasympathetic Innervation Innervation Uterus Uterine artery Uterine venous T12-L2 via superior Pelvic splanchnic (branch of internal plexus to internal hypogastric plexus nerves (S2-S4) iliac artery) iliac veins Upper 2/3 of Vagina Vaginal artery Vaginal venous T12-L2 via inferior Pelvic splanchnic (branch of internal plexus to internal hypogastric plexus nerves (S2-S4) iliac artery) iliac veins Lower 1/3 of Vagina Branches of internal Vaginal venous Pudendal nerve Pudendal nerve pudendal artery plexus to internal (S2-S4) (S2-S4) iliac veins - While the parasympathetic supply to the uterus and upper 2/3 of the vagina comes from the pelvic splanchnic nerves (S2-S4), the lower 1/3 of the vagina receives parasympathetic innervation via the pudendal nerve. (Option A) - The uterine artery is a branch of the internal iliac artery, not the external iliac artery. (Option B) - The vaginal venous plexus drains primarily into the internal iliac veins, not the internal pudendal vein. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1446-1447; Solution Pages 1455-1457","sourcePage":1446,"sourcePageEnd":1447},{"id":"anatomy-35-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements about pre-aortic and para-aortic lymph nodes is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pre-aortic nodes primarily drain lymph from the lower limbs"},{"letter":"B","text":"Para-aortic nodes are located posterior to the abdominal aorta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Coeliac nodes are a subset of pre-aortic nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral aortic nodes drain lymph from the stomach and pancreas"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Coeliac nodes are a subset of pre-aortic nodes","explanation":"Coeliac nodes are a constituent of the pre-aortic group of lymph nodes. - Pre-aortic nodes can be subdivided into coeliac, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric nodes. Pre-aortic and para-aortic lymph nodes Lymph Node Group Location Primary Drainage Subgroups Pre-aortic nodes Anterior to the abdominal Gastrointestinal viscera Coeliac, superior aorta including pancreas, liver, mesenteric, inferior spleen mesenteric nodes Para-aortic nodes (Lateral On either side of the Deep layers of body wall, Left and right lumbar (of aortic) abdominal aorta retroperitoneal pelvic the kidney), aortocaval, viscera, gonads (testes retrocaval, precaval groups and ovaries) - Pre-aortic nodes primarily drain lymph from gastrointestinal viscera, not the lower limbs. (Option A) - Para-aortic nodes (also called lateral aortic nodes) are located on either side of the abdominal aorta, not posterior to it. (Option B) - Lateral aortic nodes (para-aortic) primarily drain lymph from the deep layers of the body wall, retroperitoneal pelvic viscera, and gonads. (Option D). The stomach and pancreas are primarily drained by pre-aortic nodes (specifically coeliac and superior mesenteric nodes). - The stomach and pancreas are primarily drained by pre-aortic nodes (specifically coeliac and superior mesenteric nodes). - The stomach and pancreas are primarily drained by pre-aortic nodes (specifically coeliac and superior mesenteric nodes).","sourceRef":"Question Page 1447; Solution Pages 1457-1458","sourcePage":1447,"sourcePageEnd":1447},{"id":"anatomy-35-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-35","chapter":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topic":"Lesson 35 - Abdominal Neurovascular Bundles","topicIndex":35,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 36-year-old male presents with pain in his right lower quadrant. Imaging reveals no abnormalities in the appendix, yet the patient experiences referred pain that radiates to the periumbilical region. Which of the following best explains this phenomenon?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Convergence of somatic and visceral afferent fibers in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"B","text":"Specific nerve injury affecting the parietal peritoneum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Direct stimulation of visceral pain fibers in the appendix"},{"letter":"D","text":"Local irritation of the somatic sensory nerves in the abdominal wall"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Convergence of somatic and visceral afferent fibers in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord","explanation":"spinal cord The Convergence Projection Hypothesis explains how visceral pain is often misinterpreted as somatic pain. - Visceral organs are innervated by visceral afferent fibers, which enter the spinal cord at the same segmental level as somatic afferents. - When these visceral fibers converge with somatic fibers in the dorsal horn of the spinal cord, the brain interprets the signal as originating from the somatic region, leading to referred pain. - In the case of appendicitis, visceral afferents from the appendix converge with somatic afferents from the T10 dermatome, resulting in referred pain around the umbilicus. Specific nerve injury affecting the parietal peritoneum (Option B): While irritation of the parietal peritoneum can cause sharp, localized pain, this does not explain referred pain to a distant somatic area. Direct stimulation of visceral pain fibers in the appendix (Option C) would result in poorly localized, dull, and cramping pain, typically felt in the midline. Local irritation of the somatic sensory nerves in the abdominal wall (Option D): Irritation of somatic sensory nerves would lead to localized pain directly at the site of irritation, not referred pain. References:","sourceRef":"Question Page 1447; Solution Pages 1458-1460","sourcePage":1447,"sourcePageEnd":1447},{"id":"anatomy-36-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following is a drain into IVC?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left gonadal vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Left adrenal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left renal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right ascending lumbar"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Left renal vein","explanation":"Correct Option B - Left renal vein: - Left Renal Vein directly drains into the Inferior Vena Cava (IVC). This vein receives blood from the left kidney and left gonadal vein. Incorrect Options: Option A - Left Gonadal Vein: - The left gonadal vein (left testicular vein in males or left ovarian vein in females) drains into the left renal vein, and then the blood flows into the IVC. Option B - Left adrenal vein: - The left adrenal vein also drains into the left renal vein, which eventually connects to the IVC. Option D - Right ascending lumbar vein: - Right ascending lumbar vein drain into the azygous vein - Azygous vein is on right side - Azygous will drain in Superior Vena Cava (SVC) at T4 level","sourceRef":"Question Page 1461; Solution Pages 1485-1486","sourcePage":1461,"sourcePageEnd":1461},{"id":"anatomy-36-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Testicular artery is a branch of:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abdominal aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Common iliac artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"External iliac artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal iliac artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Abdominal aorta","explanation":"Correct Option A - Explanation: The testicular artery is a branch of the abdominal aorta . - It arises from the anterior aspect of the abdominal aorta just below the origin of the renal arteries. - It travels inferiorly to reach the testes and provides the arterial blood supply to the testicles. Incorrect options : Option B . Common iliac artery: The common iliac artery is a vessel that arises from the abdominal aorta and bifurcates into the internal and external iliac arteries. It does not directly supply the testicular artery. Option C . External iliac artery: The external iliac artery is a branch of the common iliac artery and supplies the lower limb. It does not supply the testicular artery. Option D . Internal iliac artery: The internal iliac artery is a branch of the common iliac artery and supplies the pelvis and pelvic organs. It does not supply the testicular artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1461; Solution Pages 1486-1487","sourcePage":1461,"sourcePageEnd":1461},{"id":"anatomy-36-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Deep inguinal ring is formed in:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transversus abdominis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transversalis fascia"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Transversalis fascia","explanation":"Correct option: D. - The deep inguinal ring is an opening in the transversalis fascia, which is the innermost layer of the abdominal wall. - It is located above and medial to the midpoint of the inguinal ligament. - The deep inguinal ring is an important anatomical landmark in the inguinal region and serves as the starting point of the inguinal canal. Incorrect options: Option A . External oblique aponeurosis: The external oblique aponeurosis contributes to the formation of the superficial inguinal ring, which is a separate structure from the deep inguinal ring. Option B . Internal oblique aponeurosis: The internal oblique aponeurosis also contributes to the formation of the superficial inguinal ring, not the deep inguinal ring. Option C . Transversus abdominis: The transversus abdominis muscle lies deep to the internal oblique muscle and its aponeurosis. It does not directly contribute to the formation of the deep inguinal ring.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1461; Solution Page 1487","sourcePage":1461,"sourcePageEnd":1461},{"id":"anatomy-36-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Inguinal ligament is formed due to","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cremaster muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"External oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transversus abdominis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. External oblique aponeurosis","explanation":"Correct option B. - The inguinal ligament is formed due to the external oblique aponeurosis. - The external oblique muscle of the abdominal wall gives rise to a broad flat tendon called the external oblique aponeurosis. - The lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis forms the inguinal ligament, which extends from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle. - The inguinal ligament plays a role in supporting the structures of the inguinal region. Incorrect options : Option A . Cremaster muscle: The cremaster muscle is a thin muscle that covers the spermatic cord. It is not involved in the formation of the inguinal ligament. Option C . Internal oblique aponeurosis: The internal oblique muscle contributes to the formation of the conjoint tendon, which is a thickening of the transversalis fascia. It does not directly form the inguinal ligament. Option D . Transversus abdominis: The transversus abdominis muscle is the deepest muscle of the abdominal wall. It does not contribute to the formation of the inguinal ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1461-1462; Solution Pages 1487-1488","sourcePage":1461,"sourcePageEnd":1462},{"id":"anatomy-36-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":5,"question":"During embryological development, the midgut undergoes rotation. In a 6-month-old infant presenting with bilious emesis, it is discovered that the cecum is fixed in the right upper quadrant due to peritoneal bands, leading to a diagnosis of intestinal malrotation. Which of the following statements accurately describes the midgut rotation during embryological development?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"270-degree clockwise"},{"letter":"B","text":"270-degree anticlockwise"},{"letter":"C","text":"360-degree clockwise"},{"letter":"D","text":"360-degree anticlockwise"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 270-degree anticlockwise","explanation":"Correct option B - During normal embryological development, the midgut rotates 270 degrees anticlockwise (counterclockwise) around the axis of the superior mesenteric artery. - This rotation occurs in three stages: 90 degrees during herniation into the umbilical cord, an additional 180 degrees during retraction back into the abdominal cavity. - This process positions the cecum in the lower right quadrant of the abdomen and the small intestines in a way that avoids complications such as malrotation. Incorrect options: Option A: 270-degree clockwise: This option suggests a clockwise rotation of 270 degrees. However, the midgut rotation occurs in a counterclockwise direction rather than clockwise. Therefore, option A is incorrect. Option C: 360-degree clockwise: This option suggests a clockwise rotation of 360 degrees. However, the midgut rotation does not involve a full 360-degree rotation in either direction. Therefore, option C is incorrect. Option D: 360-degree anticlockwise: This option implies a counterclockwise rotation of 360 degrees. While the counterclockwise rotation is correct, the midgut rotation does not involve a full 360-degree rotation. Hence, option D is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1462; Solution Pages 1488-1489","sourcePage":1462,"sourcePageEnd":1462},{"id":"anatomy-36-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":6,"question":"In relation to the anatomy of vasa brevia, a senior surgeon asks a first-year surgery resident a question that suggests vasa brevia is another term for which artery runs through the gastrosplenic ligament. This scenario is based on a research article published about the branches and course of vasa brevia.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Long gastric arteries"},{"letter":"B","text":"Short gastric arteries"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iliea vasa recta"},{"letter":"D","text":"Jejunal vasa recta"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Short gastric arteries","explanation":"Correct option: B - Short gastric arteries: The short gastric arteries are branches of the splenic artery that supply the fundus and upper part of the greater curvature of the stomach. They pass through the gastrosplenic ligament, which is a fold of the peritoneum connecting the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen. Incorrect options: Option A: Long gastric arteries: The long gastric arteries are branches of the splenic artery that supply the fundus and upper part of the greater curvature of the stomach. However, they do not pass through the gastrosplenic ligament. Option C: Ileal vasa recta: The ileal vasa recta are small arteries that arise from the superior mesenteric artery and supply the terminal part of the ileum in the small intestine. They are not associated with the gastrosplenic ligament. Option D: Jejunal vasa recta: The jejunal vasa recta are small arteries that arise from the superior mesenteric artery and supply the jejunum in the small intestine. Similar to the ileal vasa recta, they are not related to the gastrosplenic ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1462; Solution Page 1489","sourcePage":1462,"sourcePageEnd":1462},{"id":"anatomy-36-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Identify the marked artery originating from celiac trunk territory? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Splenic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastroepiploic Artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gastroduodenal Artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Left gastric artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gastroepiploic Artery","explanation":"Correct Option: B - Gastroepiploic artery: The gastroepiploic artery, also known as the gastro-omental artery, is a branch of the celiac trunk territory. It arises from the splenic artery, which is one of the main branches of the celiac trunk. The gastroepiploic artery runs along the greater curvature of the stomach and supplies blood to the stomach and the greater omentum. Incorrect Options: Option A: Splenic artery: The splenic artery is a branch of the celiac trunk, but it does not originate from the celiac trunk territory. Instead, it arises directly from the celiac trunk itself. Option C: Gastroduodenal artery: The gastroduodenal artery is another branch of the celiac trunk, but it does not originate from the celiac trunk territory. It arises directly from the common hepatic artery, which is a separate branch of the celiac trunk. Option D: Left gastric artery: The left gastric artery is also a branch of the celiac trunk, but it does not originate from the celiac trunk territory. It arises directly from the common hepatic artery, just before it gives rise to the proper hepatic artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1462-1463; Solution Pages 1489-1490","sourcePage":1462,"sourcePageEnd":1463},{"id":"anatomy-36-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following ligaments is another name for the lienorenal ligament derived from the peritoneum, where the tail of the pancreas is located in a healthy adult female?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hapato-gastric"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hepato-duodenal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gastro-splenic"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spleno-renal"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Spleno-renal","explanation":"Correct option D - Spleno-renal: The lienorenal ligament, also known as the splenorenal ligament, is a double-layered fold of peritoneum that connects the spleen to the posterior abdominal wall and the left kidney. It plays a crucial role in supporting and positioning the spleen within the abdominal cavity. - Functions: Splenorenal Support: The primary function of the lienorenal ligament is to support the spleen in its anatomical position. The spleen is a relatively mobile organ, and the lienorenal ligament helps anchor it to the posterior abdominal wall and the left kidney, preventing excessive movement and rotation. This support ensures that the spleen remains in its usual location in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. Vascular and Neural Contents: Within the lienorenal ligament, you can find several important structures: Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. Potential Pathway for Disease Spread: Due to its connections with the spleen, stomach, and surrounding structures, the lienorenal ligament can serve as a pathway for the spread of disease or infections between these organs. - Splenorenal Support: The primary function of the lienorenal ligament is to support the spleen in its anatomical position. The spleen is a relatively mobile organ, and the lienorenal ligament helps anchor it to the posterior abdominal wall and the left kidney, preventing excessive movement and rotation. This support ensures that the spleen remains in its usual location in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. - Vascular and Neural Contents: Within the lienorenal ligament, you can find several important structures: Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. - Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. - Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. - Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. - Potential Pathway for Disease Spread: Due to its connections with the spleen, stomach, and surrounding structures, the lienorenal ligament can serve as a pathway for the spread of disease or infections between these organs. Functions: - Splenorenal Support: The primary function of the lienorenal ligament is to support the spleen in its anatomical position. The spleen is a relatively mobile organ, and the lienorenal ligament helps anchor it to the posterior abdominal wall and the left kidney, preventing excessive movement and rotation. This support ensures that the spleen remains in its usual location in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. - Vascular and Neural Contents: Within the lienorenal ligament, you can find several important structures: Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. - Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. - Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. - Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. - Potential Pathway for Disease Spread: Due to its connections with the spleen, stomach, and surrounding structures, the lienorenal ligament can serve as a pathway for the spread of disease or infections between these organs. Splenorenal Support: The primary function of the lienorenal ligament is to support the spleen in its anatomical position. The spleen is a relatively mobile organ, and the lienorenal ligament helps anchor it to the posterior abdominal wall and the left kidney, preventing excessive movement and rotation. This support ensures that the spleen remains in its usual location in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen. Vascular and Neural Contents: Within the lienorenal ligament, you can find several important structures: - Splenic Artery and Vein: The lienorenal ligament contains the splenic artery and vein, which are the main blood vessels supplying and draining the spleen. - Lymphatics: Lymphatic vessels from the spleen also pass through the lienorenal ligament. - Autonomic Nerves: Nerve fibers related to the autonomic nervous system may also pass through this ligament. Potential Pathway for Disease Spread: Due to its connections with the spleen, stomach, and surrounding structures, the lienorenal ligament can serve as a pathway for the spread of disease or infections between these organs. Incorrect options: Option A: Hapato-gastric: The hepatogastric ligament, also known as the lesser omentum, connects the liver to the lesser curvature of the stomach Option B: Hepato-duodenal: The hepatoduodenal ligament, also known as the lesser omentum, connects the liver to the duodenum. Like the hepatogastric ligament, it does not include the tail of the pancreas. Option C: Gastro-splenic: The gastro-splenic ligament, also known as the lienorenal ligament, is the correct ligament in question. It is derived from the peritoneum and connects the greater curvature of the stomach to the spleen. The tail of the pancreas lies within this ligament","sourceRef":"Question Page 1463; Solution Pages 1490-1492","sourcePage":1463,"sourcePageEnd":1463},{"id":"anatomy-36-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Identify the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stomach"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spleen"},{"letter":"C","text":"2 nd part of duodenum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pancreas"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Stomach","explanation":"Correct option: Option A. - Stomach: The correct option is option A. The stomach is located in the upper left quadrant of the abdomen, near the left kidney. It is anatomically adjacent to the left kidney. Incorrect options: Option B. Spleen: The spleen is an incorrect option. While the spleen is also located in the upper left quadrant of the abdomen, it is positioned more laterally and superiorly compared to the left kidney. Option C. 2nd part of duodenum: The second part of the duodenum is an incorrect option. The duodenum is a part of the small intestine, and it is located in the central and upper abdomen. It is positioned more medially and inferiorly compared to the left kidney. Option D. Pancreas: Option D is not explained in the initial question. However, the pancreas is an incorrect option in relation to the marked area. The pancreas is located behind the stomach and extends horizontally across the abdomen.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1463-1464; Solution Pages 1492-1493","sourcePage":1463,"sourcePageEnd":1464},{"id":"anatomy-36-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is true about nerve supply of adrenal gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Release of catecholamines is not affected by nerve supply to adrenals"},{"letter":"B","text":"Preganglionic fibres from lower thoracic and lumbar vertebra come via sympathetic chain to supply adrenals"},{"letter":"C","text":"Adrenal cortex doesn't have a direct nerve supply"},{"letter":"D","text":"Adrenal medulla doesn’t have nerve supply"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Preganglionic fibres from lower thoracic and lumbar vertebra come via sympathetic chain to supply adrenals","explanation":"Correct Option : B - The preganglionic fibers from the lower thoracic and lumbar segments of the spinal cord come via the sympathetic chain to supply the adrenal medulla. - The adrenal gland consists of two main parts: the outer adrenal cortex and the inner adrenal medulla. The adrenal cortex is responsible for the production and secretion of steroid hormones, while the adrenal medulla produces and releases catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine), which are involved in the body's response to stress. - The adrenal medulla is innervated by sympathetic preganglionic fibers. These preganglionic fibers arise from the lower thoracic (T10-T12) and lumbar (L1-L2) segments of the spinal cord. They pass through the sympathetic chain ganglia without synapsing and continue as splanchnic nerves to reach the adrenal medulla. Upon reaching the adrenal medulla, these fibers synapse with chromaffin cells, which are specialized cells responsible for the production and release of catecholamines. Incorrect Option : Option A . The release of catecholamines from the adrenal medulla is indeed affected by the nerve supply. Stimulation of the sympathetic preganglionic fibers leads to the release of catecholamines from the adrenal medulla into the bloodstream. Option C . It primarily functions as an endocrine organ, producing hormones such as cortisol, aldosterone, and sex hormones. The adrenal medulla, on the other hand, is innervated by sympathetic nerve fibers and plays a role in the release of catecholamines (epinephrine and norepinephrine) in response to sympathetic stimulation. Option D . As mentioned earlier, the adrenal medulla does have nerve supply through the sympathetic preganglionic fibers that synapse with chromaffin cells, leading to the release of catecholamines.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1464; Solution Pages 1493-1494","sourcePage":1464,"sourcePageEnd":1464},{"id":"anatomy-36-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which statement is incorrect regarding the Adrenal gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Yellow colored structures"},{"letter":"B","text":"The left adrenal gland is present below the spleen."},{"letter":"C","text":"The right adrenal gland is crescent in shape."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is located retroperitoneally"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The right adrenal gland is crescent in shape.","explanation":"Correct Option C - The right adrenal gland is crescent in shape. : - The right adrenal gland is triangular or pyramidal, not crescent-shaped. Incorrect Options: Option A - Yellow-colored structures: The adrenal cortex tends to exhibit a yellowish hue due to its higher fat content, while the adrenal medulla appears more reddish-brown in color. Option B - The left adrenal gland is present below the spleen : Left adrenal present below the spleen. Option D - It is located retroperitoneally : The adrenal glands are situated on top of each kidney in the retroperitoneal space.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1464-1465; Solution Page 1494","sourcePage":1464,"sourcePageEnd":1465},{"id":"anatomy-36-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":12,"question":"The fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis contribute to the wall of the inguinal canal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Roof"},{"letter":"D","text":"Floor"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Roof","explanation":"Correct Option - Roof: - The roof of the inguinal canal is formed by the fibers of the internal oblique and transversus abdominis muscles. These muscles contribute to the structure that covers the canal from above. Anterior wall External oblique aponeurosis Roof Internal oblique muscles Transversus abdominis muscle Posterior wall Transversalis fascia Parietal peritoneum Floor Inguinal ligament Incorrect Options: Option A - Anterior: The anterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle. Option B - Posterior: The posterior wall of the inguinal canal is formed by the transversalis fascia and the parietal peritoneum. Option D - Floor: The floor of the inguinal canal is formed by the inguinal ligament, which is a thickened lower border of the external oblique aponeurosis. It runs from the anterior superior iliac spine to the pubic tubercle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1465; Solution Pages 1494-1495","sourcePage":1465,"sourcePageEnd":1465},{"id":"anatomy-36-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The structure that divides the liver in Couinaud’s classification system is:-","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Portal vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hepatic artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bile duct"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cystic duct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Portal vein","explanation":"Correct Option: Option A: The structure that divides the liver in Couinaud's classification system is the portal vein. In Couinaud's classification system, the liver is divided into functional units called liver segments. These segments are based on the distribution of the portal vein, hepatic artery, and bile ducts within the liver. Incorrect Option: Option B: The hepatic artery is not the structure that divides the liver in Couinaud's classification system. While the hepatic artery is an important blood vessel that supplies oxygenated blood to the liver, it does not serve as the dividing structure for liver segments. Option C: The bile ducts, which include the common hepatic duct and its branches, transport bile from the liver to the gallbladder and small intestine. While bile ducts play a significant role in liver function, they are not the dividing structure in Couinaud's classification system. Option D: The cystic duct is a small duct that connects the gallbladder to the common bile duct. It is not involved in the division of the liver according to Couinaud's classification system.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1465; Solution Pages 1495-1496","sourcePage":1465,"sourcePageEnd":1465},{"id":"anatomy-36-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":14,"question":"What is the most common type of hernia in females?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Obturatorhernia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Direct inguinalhernia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Indirect inguinalhernia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Femoralhernia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Indirect inguinalhernia","explanation":"Correct Option C - Indirect inguinal hernia: - Indirect inguinal hernias are the most common type of hernia overall, including in females. - This type of hernia occurs when abdominal contents protrude through the deep inguinal ring and into the inguinal canal, often due to a congenital defect in the abdominal wall. - Despite the anatomical predisposition in females for femoral hernias, indirect inguinal hernias still occur more frequently in both sexes. Indirect inguinal hernias are the most common type of hernia overall, including in females. This type of hernia occurs when abdominal contents protrude through the deep inguinal ring and into the inguinal canal, often due to a congenital defect in the abdominal wall. Despite the anatomical predisposition in females for femoral hernias, indirect inguinal hernias still occur more frequently in both sexes. Incorrect Options: Option A - Obturator hernia: - These are quite rare and occur through the obturator foramen. They are typically found in older, multiparous women and are not the most common type of hernia in females. These are quite rare and occur through the obturator foramen. They are typically found in older, multiparous women and are not the most common type of hernia in females. Option B - Direct inguinal hernia: - Direct inguinal hernias occur due to weakness in the abdominal wall in the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle. These are more common in men and are not the most common type in females. Direct inguinal hernias occur due to weakness in the abdominal wall in the inguinal (Hesselbach's) triangle. These are more common in men and are not the most common type in females. Option D - Femoral hernia: - While femoral hernias are more common in females compared to males due to the wider female pelvis, they are still less common than indirect inguinal hernias in females. While femoral hernias are more common in females compared to males due to the wider female pelvis, they are still less common than indirect inguinal hernias in females.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1465-1466; Solution Pages 1496-1497","sourcePage":1465,"sourcePageEnd":1466},{"id":"anatomy-36-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Identify the marked structure: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Splenogastric ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lienorenal ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater omentum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lesser omentum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Lienorenal ligament","explanation":"Correct Option B - Lienorenal ligament: - The marked structure is the Lienorenal ligament or the splenorenal ligament. - It connects the spleen to the left kidney as the spleen has come to the left side due to the rotation of the stomach. - It also carries the splenic artery. Incorrect Options: - Options A, C & D: These do not indicate the marked structure given in the question","sourceRef":"Question Page 1466; Solution Page 1497","sourcePage":1466,"sourcePageEnd":1466},{"id":"anatomy-36-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which structure is located approximately 25 cm from the central incisor?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cricopharynx"},{"letter":"B","text":"Arch of Aorta"},{"letter":"C","text":"Left principal bronchus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diaphragm"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Arch of Aorta","explanation":"Correct Option B - Arch of aorta: - The arch of the aorta is positioned around the level of the T4 vertebra, which is roughly 23 cm from the central incisor. Incorrect Options: Option A - Cricopharynx: The narrowing due to cricopharyngeal sphincter is located 15 cm from the upper incisor. Option C - Left principal bronchus: The left principal bronchus is located 28 cm from the upper incisor. Option D - Esophageal hiatus: The esophageal hiatus is located 40 cm from the upper incisor.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1466-1467; Solution Pages 1497-1498","sourcePage":1466,"sourcePageEnd":1467},{"id":"anatomy-36-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Most common site of Congenital diaphragmatic hernia is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Central tendon"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterolateral"},{"letter":"C","text":"Crural"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterolateral"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterolateral","explanation":"Correct Option B – Posterolateral: - The most common site for congenital diaphragmatic hernia is the posterolateral region of the diaphragm, specifically the left side. This area is where the foramen of Bochdalek is located, which is a common site for herniation of abdominal contents into the thoracic cavity Incorrect Options: Option A - Central Tendon : Hernias in this area are less common. The central tendon is a structure where the muscle fibers of the diaphragm converge, but it is not typically associated with congenital diaphragmatic hernias. Option C - Crural : This area refers to the portion of the diaphragm attaching to the lumbar vertebrae. Hernias in this region are not as common as those in the posterolateral area. Option D - Anterolateral : Hernias in the anterolateral region are rare compared to those in the posterolateral region. This region is less associated with congenital diaphragmatic hernias.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1467; Solution Page 1498","sourcePage":1467,"sourcePageEnd":1467},{"id":"anatomy-36-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":18,"question":"A patient presents with fecal discharge from the umbilicus. What's the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Urachal fistula"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ileal diverticulum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Omphalocele"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gastroschisis"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ileal diverticulum","explanation":"Correct Option B - Ileal diverticulum: - Ileal diverticulum (Meckel's diverticulum) : This is a congenital anomaly resulting from incomplete obliteration of the vitelline duct. A vitelline fistula can form, leading to fecal discharge from the umbilicus, which is a characteristic sign. Incorrect Options: Option A - Urachal fistula : Involves a connection between the bladder and the umbilicus, typically resulting in urine discharge, not fecal material. Option C - Omphalocele : A congenital defect in the abdominal wall where abdominal contents herniate into the umbilical cord. There is no fecal discharge involved. Option D - Gastroschisis : A defect in the abdominal wall, usually to the right of the umbilicus, where the intestines protrude without a protective sac. It is not associated with fecal discharge from the umbilicus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1467; Solution Pages 1498-1499","sourcePage":1467,"sourcePageEnd":1467},{"id":"anatomy-36-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Where are the Ito cells in the liver located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Space of disse"},{"letter":"B","text":"Space of moll"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sinusoids"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bile canaliculi"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Space of disse","explanation":"Correct Option A: Ito cells, or hepatic stellate cells, reside in the space of Disse within the liver, situated between sinusoidal endothelial cells and hepatocytes in liver lobules. This space holds extracellular matrix components, growth factors, and signaling molecules, creating a crucial microenvironment. Ito cells play a vital role in liver function and pathology. Incorrection Options: Option B. Space of Moll: The space of Moll is a small space located between the posterior surface of the cornea and the anterior surface of the iris. It is not related to the location of Ito cells in the liver. Option C. Sinusoids: While Ito cells are located in close proximity to the sinusoids, they are specifically found within the space of Disse, which is a distinct anatomical space within the liver. Option D. Bile canaliculi: Bile canaliculi are tiny channels between adjacent hepatocytes that transport bile towards the bile ducts. Ito cells are not located within the bile canaliculi.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1467; Solution Page 1499","sourcePage":1467,"sourcePageEnd":1467},{"id":"anatomy-36-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":20,"question":"The subcostal line corresponds to which vertebrae level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T12"},{"letter":"B","text":"L1"},{"letter":"C","text":"L2"},{"letter":"D","text":"L3"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. L3","explanation":"Correct Option D - L3: - The subcostal line corresponds to the L3 vertebrae. - The subcostal plane refers to a horizontal (or transverse) plane which lies just below the level of the costal margin, i.e., connecting the 10th costal cartilages of right and left sides with each other. - Posteriorly, the position of subcostal plane equates to the body of the third lumbar (L3) vertebrae. Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1467-1468; Solution Pages 1499-1500","sourcePage":1467,"sourcePageEnd":1468},{"id":"anatomy-36-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":21,"question":"What structures are contained within the anterior pararenal space?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Head, neck, and body of the pancreas"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adrenal gland, kidney, ureters"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aorta, inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ascending and descending colon"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Head, neck, and body of the pancreas","explanation":"Correct Option A - Head, neck, and body of the pancreas: - They are contained within the anterior pararenal space They are contained within the anterior pararenal space Incorrect Options: Option B - Adrenal gland, kidney, ureters: These structures are contained within the perirenal space, not the anterior pararenal space. The perirenal space is adjacent to the kidneys and contains the adrenal glands, kidneys, ureters, and renal vessels. Option C - Aorta, inferior vena cava: These major blood vessels are not contained within the anterior pararenal space. They are located within the fourth space mentioned in the paragraph, known as the great vessel space, which lies anterior to the vertebral bodies and psoas muscles. Option D - Ascending and descending colon: The ascending and descending colon are also not located within the anterior pararenal space. They are part of the abdominal digestive tract and are not specifically mentioned as being within the anterior pararenal space.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1468; Solution Page 1500","sourcePage":1468,"sourcePageEnd":1468},{"id":"anatomy-36-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A condition requires the surgery depicted in the image shown below. All the following are usually seen associated with this condition except? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Absent kidney"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hepatic artery anomalies"},{"letter":"C","text":"Malrotation of gut"},{"letter":"D","text":"Polysplenia"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Absent kidney","explanation":"Correct Option A - Absent kidney: - Although renal anomalies can occur in Heterotaxy polysplenia syndrome, the absence of a kidney (unilateral renal agenesis) is not typically associated with this condition. Incorrect Options: Option B - Hepatic artery anomalies: Anomalies or variations in the hepatic artery can be seen in Heterotaxy polysplenia syndrome, as the condition affects the normal development and arrangement of abdominal organs. Option C - Malrotation of the gut: Malrotation of the gut, where the intestines are not properly positioned during development, is a common finding in Heterotaxy polysplenia syndrome. Option D - Polysplenia: Polysplenia is commonly associated with the condition depicted in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1468-1469; Solution Pages 1500-1501","sourcePage":1468,"sourcePageEnd":1469},{"id":"anatomy-36-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":23,"question":"A 35-year-old patient underwent an endoscopic examination of the gastrointestinal tract due to recurrent abdominal discomfort and altered bowel habits. Identify the arrow-marked structure in the given image. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bruner gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intestinal cells of kajal"},{"letter":"C","text":"Crypts of lieberkuhn"},{"letter":"D","text":"Peyer's patch"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Bruner gland","explanation":"Correct Option A - Brunner's Gland: - The given histology slide is of duodenum. - Identification points: Wall made up of four layers. Mucosa – presence of numerous broad villi lined by simple columnar epithelium with microvilli and goblet cells. Presence of crypts of lieberkuhn Presence of submucous Brunner’s gland. - Wall made up of four layers. - Mucosa – presence of numerous broad villi lined by simple columnar epithelium with microvilli and goblet cells. - Presence of crypts of lieberkuhn - Presence of submucous Brunner’s gland. - The marked structure by the arrow is of Brunner's gland. - Wall made up of four layers. - Mucosa – presence of numerous broad villi lined by simple columnar epithelium with microvilli and goblet cells. - Presence of crypts of lieberkuhn - Presence of submucous Brunner’s gland. Incorrect Options: - Options B, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to Option A for an explanation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1469; Solution Pages 1501-1502","sourcePage":1469,"sourcePageEnd":1469},{"id":"anatomy-36-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":24,"question":"At which vertebral level can the celiac vessel be observed in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T9"},{"letter":"B","text":"T10"},{"letter":"C","text":"T12"},{"letter":"D","text":"L2"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. T12","explanation":"Correct Option C:T12 The celiac trunk emerges from the abdominal aorta just below the diaphragm's aortic opening, around the T12-L1 intervertebral disc level. It courses forward to the right and divides into three branches. Incorrect options: Option A . Coeliac vessel does not arise from T9 vertebral level. Option B . Coeliac vessel does not arise from T10 vertebral level. Option D . Coeliac vessel does not arise from L2 vertebral level.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1469-1470; Solution Pages 1502-1503","sourcePage":1469,"sourcePageEnd":1470},{"id":"anatomy-36-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":25,"question":"What is the vertebral level of the abdominal plane labelled 2? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower border of L1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Upper border of L1"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper border of L3"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lower border of L4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Upper border of L3","explanation":"Correct Option C: Upper border of L3 - The abdominal plane labeled as 2 is the Subcostal plane, also known as the \"plane of the superior mesenteric artery.\" It's located just below the costal margin, connecting the 10th costal cartilages bilaterally. - In the posterior aspect, it corresponds to the upper border of the third lumbar (L3) vertebra. This plane intersects with the aorta, inferior vena cava, and the third part of the duodenum. Incorrect Options: Option A - lower border of L1 - At the lower border of L1 is the location of the transpyloric plane, which is also known as the \"plane of Addison.\" Option B - Upper border of L1 - The upper border of L1 does not correspond to the subcostal plane. Option D - Lower border of L4 - The lower border of L4 is the location of the intertubercular plane, which is also known as the \"plane of the iliac crests.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1470; Solution Page 1503","sourcePage":1470,"sourcePageEnd":1470},{"id":"anatomy-36-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":26,"question":"To what level of vertebrae does the given structure correspond?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the junction of L1 & L2"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the junction of L2 & L3"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the junction of T12 & L1"},{"letter":"D","text":"At the junction of L3 & L4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. At the junction of L3 & L4","explanation":"Correct Option D: - The marked structure is the umbilicus which lies at the junction of L3 & L4. The umbilicus is located at the level of the intervertebral disc between the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4). Associated structures at this level include the inferior vena cava, the renal veins, the gonadal arteries and veins, the ureters, and the descending colon. Incorrect Options: Option A - At the junction of L1 & L2: Associated structures at this level include the spinal cord, the aorta, the inferior vena cava, the renal arteries and veins, and the ureters. Option B - At the junction of L2 & L3: Associated structures at this level include the inferior vena cava, the renal veins, the gonadal arteries and veins, and the ascending colon. Option C - At the junction of T12 & L1: Associated structures at this level include the aorta, the inferior vena cava, the celiac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery, and the renal arteries.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1470-1471; Solution Pages 1503-1504","sourcePage":1470,"sourcePageEnd":1471},{"id":"anatomy-36-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":27,"question":"Identify Pulmonary trunk","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. B","explanation":"Correct Option-B Incorrect Options: Options A, C & D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1471-1472; Solution Page 1504","sourcePage":1471,"sourcePageEnd":1472},{"id":"anatomy-36-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":28,"question":"Which of the following structures marked in the image contains the splenic artery? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2"},{"letter":"B","text":"1"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 4","explanation":"Correct Option: D - The splenic artery is a major branch of the coeliac artery and supplies blood to the spleen . The splenic artery is carried by the splenorenal ligament. It runs along the upper border of the pancreas and is usually identified as a curved vessel in imaging studies. On MRI and CT scans, the splenic artery can be visualized as a tubular structure with a characteristic branching pattern. It is also often identified by its location near the spleen and pancreas. Incorrect Options: - Option A. points to the lesser momentum. The pancreatic branches, arising from the splenic artery, supply blood to the pancreas, often running alongside the pancreatic ducts. - Option b. The portal vein forms behind the neck of the pancreas where the superior mesenteric vein and splenic vein unite, typically at the L2 level. - Option c . The gastroepiploic ligament contains the short gastric vessels.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1472-1473; Solution Pages 1504-1505","sourcePage":1472,"sourcePageEnd":1473},{"id":"anatomy-36-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":29,"question":"A patient with a medical history of liver cirrhosis now presents with abdominal pain, jaundice, and portal hypertension. Anastomosis between which of the following veins will be seen?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Left colic vein and middle colic veins"},{"letter":"B","text":"Esophageal veins and left gastric veins"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superior and phrenic veins"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sigmoid and superior rectal veins"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Esophageal veins and left gastric veins","explanation":"Correct Option B - Esophageal veins and left gastric veins: - In patients with liver cirrhosis, there is increased portal hypertension, which then develops collateral circulation through the anastomoses between the portal and systemic veins to drain the blood. - Among the options given, the left gastric vein (portal vein) draining into esophageal veins (systemic vein) is the only combination of portosystemic anastomoses. Incorrect Options: Option A - Left colic vein and middle colic veins: The left colic vein drains the descending colon, and the middle colic vein drains the transverse colon. Both are part of the portal circulation and don't drain into systemic veins. Thus, anastomoses between them aren't observed in portal hypertension. Option C - Superior and inferior phrenic veins: The superior and inferior phrenic veins, part of the systemic circulation, drain blood from the diaphragm. Since they don't experience backflow due to portal hypertension, anastomoses aren't observed. Option D - Sigmoid and superior rectal veins: The sigmoid vein drains the sigmoid colon, and the superior rectal vein drains the rectum, both part of the portal circulation but don't drain into the systemic circulation thus anastomoses between them aren't seen in portal hypertension.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1473; Solution Pages 1505-1506","sourcePage":1473,"sourcePageEnd":1473},{"id":"anatomy-36-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Identify the liver segment marked in the image below: - **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"V"},{"letter":"B","text":"IVa"},{"letter":"C","text":"VII"},{"letter":"D","text":"III"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. V","explanation":"Correct Option A - V: - The image shows segment V of the liver, one of its eight anatomical segments. Located in the left lobe, it's situated between the left hepatic vein and the left portal vein. Segment V is part of the left lateral sector and sits between segments IV and VI. Incorrect Options: Option B - Iva: Segment Iva is a different segment located in the left lobe of the liver, closer to the middle hepatic vein. It is distinct from segment V. Option C - VII: Segment VII is located in the right lobe of the liver, and it is positioned between the middle hepatic vein and the right intersegmental fissure. It is distinct from segment V. Option D - III: Segment III is located in the left lobe of the liver, and it lies between the left hepatic vein and the left intersegmental fissure. It is distinct from segment V.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1473-1474; Solution Pages 1506-1507","sourcePage":1473,"sourcePageEnd":1474},{"id":"anatomy-36-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":31,"question":"Please identify the position of the appendix in the image provided. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A -Retrocecal; B - Preileal, C - Postileal, D - Pelvic; E- Subcecal"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Retrocecal; B- Postileal; C - Preileal; D - Pelvic; E - Paracecal"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Retrocecal; B- Postileal; C - Preileal; D - Subcecal; E - Pelvic"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Retrocecal; B- Postileal; C - Preileal; D - Pelvic; E - Subcecal"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. A- Retrocecal; B- Postileal; C - Preileal; D - Pelvic; E - Subcecal","explanation":"Correct Option D: A- Retrocecal; B- Postileal; C - Preileal; C - Preileal, D - Pelvic; E - Subcecal Incorrect Options: Options A, B & C are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1474-1475; Solution Page 1507","sourcePage":1474,"sourcePageEnd":1475},{"id":"anatomy-36-32","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":32,"question":"During the examination of a patient with a hernia, a surgeon forcefully pushes the hernial sac back into the abdominal cavity without directly pushing back the contents first. What type of hernia has occurred as a result of this, which is depicted in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Strangulated hernia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reduction en masse"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sliding hernia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Maydl's hernia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Reduction en masse","explanation":"Correct Option Option B: Reduction en masse: - Reduction en masse refers to a situation where the hernia sac is forcibly pushed back into the abdominal cavity without properly reducing or returning the herniated contents to their normal position. - This can occur when the hernia sac gets stuck or incarcerated within the abdominal wall opening. Incorrect Options: Option A: Strangulated Hernia: A strangulated hernia results from compromised blood supply to hernia contents, often due to a small opening in the muscle and significant content volume. Option C: Sliding hernia: A sliding hernia occurs when an organ, usually the stomach, protrudes from the abdominal cavity, sharing a wall with the hernia sac. If an organ doesn't slide into the sac, it's not a sliding hernia. Option D: Maydl's hernia: Maydl's hernia, known for its zigzag appearance due to multiple constrictions along the sac, carries a higher risk of complications like bowel issues. However, it doesn't match the scenario described.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1475-1476; Solution Pages 1507-1508","sourcePage":1475,"sourcePageEnd":1476},{"id":"anatomy-36-33","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":33,"question":"All are anterior to the third part of the duodenum except?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Root of mesentery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Jejunum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fundus of gallbladder"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fundus of gallbladder","explanation":"Correct Option D: Fundus of gallbladder - The anterior relations of the third part of the duodenum include the superior mesenteric artery and vein, root of mesentery, and coils of jejunum whereas the fundus of the gallbladder is related to the first and second part. It is not related to the third part. Incorrect Options: Option A: Superior mesenteric artery: It is related to the anterior surface of the third part of the duodenum. Hence, this choice is incorrect. Option B: Root of mesentery: It is related to the anterior surface of the third part of the duodenum. Hence, this choice is incorrect. Option C: Jejunum: It is related to the anterior surface of the third part of the duodenum. Hence, this choice is incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1476; Solution Page 1508","sourcePage":1476,"sourcePageEnd":1476},{"id":"anatomy-36-34","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":34,"question":"Ligation of common hepatic artery will impair blood supply in:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right gastric and right gastroepiploic artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right gastric and left gastric artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right gastroepiploic and short gastric vessels"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right gastric and short gastric vessels"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Right gastric and right gastroepiploic artery","explanation":"Correct Option A: Right gastric and right gastroepiploic artery - Ligation or occlusion of the common hepatic artery will interrupt the blood supply to these branches, resulting in impaired blood flow to the right gastric artery and right gastroepiploic artery. Incorrect Options: Option B: Right gastric and left gastric artery: The left gastric artery is not directly related to the common hepatic artery. It arises from the celiac trunk separately and supplies the lesser curvature of the stomach. Option C: Right gastroepiploic and short gastric vessels: The short gastric vessels come from the splenic artery, while the right gastroepiploic artery stems from the common hepatic artery, running alongside the greater curvature of the stomach. Option D: Right gastric and short gastric vessels: The short gastric vessels are branches of the splenic artery, not the common hepatic artery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1476-1477; Solution Pages 1508-1509","sourcePage":1476,"sourcePageEnd":1477},{"id":"anatomy-36-35","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":35,"question":"A tumor of the uncinate process of the pancreas affects which of the following vessels?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Portal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Common hepatic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superior mesenteric artery","explanation":"Correct Option A: Superior mesenteric artery - The superior mesenteric artery is located in close proximity to the uncinate process of the pancreas. - If a tumor develops in this area, it can encase or compress the superior mesenteric artery, obstructing blood flow. - This can result in various complications, including ischemia of the intestine. Incorrect Options: Option B: Portal vein: A tumor in the pancreas's uncinate process usually doesn't directly affect the portal vein. But if a tumor in the pancreatic head spreads to nearby structures, it may indirectly involve or obstruct the portal vein. Option C: Common hepatic artery: A tumor in the pancreas's uncinate process usually doesn't directly affect the common hepatic artery. But if it spreads to nearby structures, it might involve the common hepatic artery. Option D: Inferior mesenteric artery: The tumor in the pancreas's uncinate process, situated in the pancreatic head, doesn't directly impact the inferior mesenteric artery due to its distance.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1477; Solution Pages 1509-1510","sourcePage":1477,"sourcePageEnd":1477},{"id":"anatomy-36-36","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":36,"question":"The structure marked A in the image below gives rise to which of the following structures? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gastrosplenic ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lienorenal ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Falciform ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gastrophrenic ligament"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Falciform ligament","explanation":"Correct Option C: - The structure marked A in the image is a falciform ligament. - This broad, lean, mesentery-like structure connects the liver to the anterior abdominal wall and separates the right and left lobes of the liver. Incorrect Options: Option A . Gastrosplenic ligament: - The gastrosplenic tendon overlaps the peritoneum that interfaces the stomach to the spleen but isn't related to structure A in the image. Option B . Lienorenal ligament The lienorenal ligament connects the spleen to the left kidney. It's unrelated to the structure labeled A in the image. Option D . Gastrophrenic Ligament : The gastrophrenic tendon connects the stomach to the diaphragm but isn't related to structure A in the image.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1477-1478; Solution Pages 1510-1511","sourcePage":1477,"sourcePageEnd":1478},{"id":"anatomy-36-37","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":37,"question":"below the rectus abdominis muscle and above the transversalis fascia, containing the neurovascular structures supplying the abdominal wall.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Between the internal oblique and external oblique"},{"letter":"B","text":"Deep to transversus abdominis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Between the internal oblique and rectus abdominis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis","explanation":"Correct Option D: Between the internal oblique and transversus abdominis The neurovascular plane of the abdominal wall lies between the internal oblique and transversus muscles. This plane is continuous with the neurovascular plane of the thoracic wall. Various nerves and vessels run in this plane. Incorrect Options : Option A: Neurovascular plane doesn’t lie here. Option B: Deep to this is arcuate line. Option C: Internal oblique ridge shells the rectus abdominis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1478; Solution Page 1511","sourcePage":1478,"sourcePageEnd":1478},{"id":"anatomy-36-38","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":38,"question":"Which of the following branches of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery? Superior gluteal artery Inferior gluteal artery Superior vesical artery Middle rectal artery Inferior vesical artery Vaginal artery","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3,5"},{"letter":"B","text":"2,3,4,6"},{"letter":"C","text":"2,3,4,5,6"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,4,5,6"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 2,3,4,5,6","explanation":"Correct Option C: (2,3,4,5,6): This option includes all the correct branches of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery. The inferior gluteal artery (2), superior vesical artery (3), middle rectal artery (4), inferior vesical artery (5), and vaginal artery (6) are all branches of the anterior division. Incorrect Options: Option A: (1,2,3,5): This option is incorrect because it includes the superior gluteal artery (1) which are branches of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery. Option B: (2,3,4,6): This option is incorrect because it does not include inferior vesical artery(5) which is also a branch of the anterior division. Option D: (1,4,5,6): This option is incorrect because it includes the superior gluteal artery (1) which are branches of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1478; Solution Pages 1511-1512","sourcePage":1478,"sourcePageEnd":1478},{"id":"anatomy-36-39","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":39,"question":"The marked structure develops from which of the following structures? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Common cardinal vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior cardinal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Supracardinal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subcardinal vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Supracardinal vein","explanation":"Correct Option: C - The azygous vein, which drains deoxygenated blood from the posterior chest wall, develops from the supracardinal vein during embryonic growth. Initially spanning the embryo's spine, the supracardinal vein transforms to form different segments of the venous system. On the right side, it becomes the azygous vein, while on the left, it forms the hemiazygos and accessory hemiazygos veins, responsible for draining the posterior chest wall and abdomen. Incorrect Options: Option A . Common cardinal vein: The common cardinal vein forms the systemic venous system, such as the superior vena cava and the right atrium, but not the azygous vein. Option B . Anterior cardinal vein: The anterior cardinal vein contributes to the internal jugular veins and brachiocephalic veins' development but not the azygous vein. Option D . Subcardinal vein: The subcardinal vein participates in forming the renal and gonadal veins but not the azygous vein.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1478-1479; Solution Page 1512","sourcePage":1478,"sourcePageEnd":1479},{"id":"anatomy-36-40","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":40,"question":"The para-sagittal section of the abdomen is given below. What is the superior border of the marked structure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Caudate lobe of liver"},{"letter":"B","text":"Duodenum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lesser omentum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inferior vena cava"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Caudate lobe of liver","explanation":"Correct Option A. In the para-sagittal abdomen section, the caudate lobe of the liver forms the superior border of the foramen of Winslow. This foramen, positioned behind the lesser omentum's free edge and in front of the inferior vena cava, serves as a passage between the lesser sac and the greater peritoneal cavity. Incorrect Options: Option B . Duodenum: The duodenum, although close to the foramen of Winslow, doesn't form its superior border. Instead, it runs below the foramen without directly contributing to its upper boundary. Option C . Lesser omentum: The lesser omentum connects the liver and the lesser curvature of the stomach but doesn't form the superior border of the foramen of Winslow. It's positioned in front of the foramen instead of above it. Option D . Inferior vena cava: The IVC, though nearby, doesn't constitute the foramen of Winslow's superior border. It passes behind the foramen without being directly part of its upper boundary.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1479-1480; Solution Pages 1512-1513","sourcePage":1479,"sourcePageEnd":1480},{"id":"anatomy-36-41","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":41,"question":"What is the basis for the formation of omphaloceles?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Excessive length of the intestine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Physiological hernia failing to go back"},{"letter":"C","text":"Herniation of liver"},{"letter":"D","text":"Herniation of umbilicus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Physiological hernia failing to go back","explanation":"Correct Option: B) Physiological hernia failing to go back: Omphalocele is believed to result from a physiological herniation of abdominal organs into the base of the umbilical cord during early fetal development. Normally, the abdominal contents herniate into the umbilical cord at around 6 weeks of gestation but then return back into the abdominal cavity. However, in the case of omphalocele, this physiological herniation fails to go back, resulting in the persistence of the herniated organs outside the abdominal cavity. Incorrect Options : Option A) Excessive length of the intestine: Omphalocele occurs due to a defect in abdominal muscle development, leading to organ herniation through the umbilical ring, not because of excessive intestinal length.. Option C) Herniation of liver: Omphalocele involves abdominal organs, like the liver, herniating through the umbilical ring. However, it's not only about liver herniation; other abdominal organs are also involved. Option D) Herniation of umbilicus: Omphalocele does not involve the herniation of the umbilicus itself but rather the herniation of abdominal organs through the umbilical ring.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1480; Solution Page 1513","sourcePage":1480,"sourcePageEnd":1480},{"id":"anatomy-36-42","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":42,"question":"Which of the following structures does not form the boundaries of the Calot triangle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Common hepatic duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cystic duct"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cystic artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gall bladder"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Gall bladder","explanation":"Correct Option-D: Gallbladder: - The gallbladder is not considered one of the boundaries of the Calot triangle. - The Calot triangle is an anatomical space located between the cystic duct, common hepatic duct, and cystic artery. Incorrect Options: Option A: Common hepatic duct: The common hepatic duct is one of the boundaries of the Calot triangle. It carries bile from the liver and joins with the cystic duct to form the common bile duct. Option B: Cystic duct: The cystic duct is another boundary of the Calot triangle. It connects the gallbladder to the common hepatic duct and forms the neck of the gallbladder. Option C: Cystic artery: The cystic artery is also part of the Calot triangle. It arises from the right hepatic artery or its branches and supplies blood to the gallbladder.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1480-1481; Solution Pages 1513-1514","sourcePage":1480,"sourcePageEnd":1481},{"id":"anatomy-36-43","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":43,"question":"Area marked in the given image supplied by dermatome number? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T8"},{"letter":"B","text":"T9"},{"letter":"C","text":"T10"},{"letter":"D","text":"T11"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. T10","explanation":"Correct Option - C: T10: The area marked in the given image corresponds to the T10 dermatome. Each dermatome represents a specific segment of spinal nerve innervation. The T10 dermatome covers the region at the level of the tenth thoracic vertebra, which is consistent with the location shown in the image. Incorrect Options: Option A: T8: The T8 dermatome, positioned slightly higher on the trunk than the marked level in the image, provides sensory innervation to the skin at the eighth thoracic vertebra level. Option B: T9: The T9 dermatome, situated slightly above the marked level in the image, provides sensory innervation to the skin at the ninth thoracic vertebra level. Option D: T11: The T11 dermatome, found slightly below the marked level in the image, provides sensory innervation to the skin at the level of the eleventh thoracic vertebra.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1481; Solution Pages 1514-1515","sourcePage":1481,"sourcePageEnd":1481},{"id":"anatomy-36-44","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":44,"question":"Which of the following was separated by the cholecysto-vena caval line?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Gallbladder and inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"B","text":"Porta hepatis and inferior vena cava"},{"letter":"C","text":"Caudate lobe and quadrangular lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right and left lobe of liver"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Right and left lobe of liver","explanation":"Correct Option: Option D: Right and left lobe of liver: The cholecysto-vena caval line is an imaginary line drawn on the visceral surface of the liver. It separates the right lobe of the liver from the left lobe of the liver. The right lobe is larger and encompasses most of the liver's volume, while the left lobe is smaller. Incorrect Options: Option A: Gallbladder and inferior vena cava: The cholecysto-vena caval line doesn't separate the gallbladder and the inferior vena cava. The gallbladder is in the right lobe of the liver, while the inferior vena cava runs alongside the liver. Option B: Porta hepatis and inferior vena cava: The cholecysto-vena caval line doesn't separate the porta hepatis and the inferior vena cava. The porta hepatis is where the hepatic artery, portal vein, and bile ducts connect with the liver. Option C: Caudate lobe and quadrangular lobe: The cholecysto-vena caval line doesn't divide the caudate lobe and quadrangular lobe. The caudate lobe is posterior on the liver, while the quadrangular lobe is in the left lobe.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1481-1482; Solution Page 1515","sourcePage":1481,"sourcePageEnd":1482},{"id":"anatomy-36-45","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":45,"question":"Which of the following structures is not derived from the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pectineal ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inguinal ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lacunar ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Linea semilunaris"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Linea semilunaris","explanation":"Correct Option D - Linea semilunaris: The linea semilunaris, also known as the semilunar line, is a curved, fibrous line on the lateral abdominal wall that represents the transition zone between the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle and the rectus sheath. Incorrect Options : Option A - Pectineal ligament: - The pectineal ligament, or Cooper's ligament, arises from the external oblique's aponeurosis and provides support to the inguinal region. Option B - Inguinal ligament: - The inguinal ligament, also known as Poupart's ligament, is derived from the lower border of the aponeurosis of the external oblique muscle. Option C - Lacunar ligament: - The lacunar ligament, known as Gimbernat's ligament, is derived from the lower border of the external oblique muscle's aponeurosis and contributes to the formation of the femoral ring and the inguinal ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1482; Solution Pages 1515-1516","sourcePage":1482,"sourcePageEnd":1482},{"id":"anatomy-36-46","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-36","chapter":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 36 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":36,"questionNumber":46,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about the iliopubic tract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is a thickening of the lumbar fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"It lies anterior to the Poupart’s ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"It inserts into t he pubic tubercle in front of the conjoint tendon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Its fibers fan out with fascia iliaca laterally"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Its fibers fan out with fascia iliaca laterally","explanation":"Correct Option D: Its fibers fan out with fascia iliaca laterally - The fibers of the iliopubic tract fan out with the fascia iliaca laterally. - The fascia iliaca is a broad, fibrous sheet that covers the iliopsoas muscle and provides attachment for various muscles and structures in the hip and thigh region. Incorrect Options: Option A - It is a thickening of the lumbar fascia: This statement is incorrect. The iliopubic tract is not a thickening of the lumbar fascia. It is a thickened band of fascia that runs parallel to the inguinal ligament. Option B - It lies anterior to the Poupart's ligament: This statement is incorrect. The iliopubic tract, situated deeper in the anatomical layers, runs parallel to the inguinal ligament (Poupart's ligament) and lies posterior to it. Option C - It inserts into the pubic tubercle in front of the conjoint tendon: This statement is incorrect. The iliopubic tract does not directly insert into the pubic tubercle. It blends with the transversalis fascia and forms the posterior wall of the inguinal canal.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1482; Solution Page 1516","sourcePage":1482,"sourcePageEnd":1482},{"id":"anatomy-37-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35-year-old female presents with persistent flank pain. An abdominal ultrasound reveals that the lower poles of both kidneys are fused. What is the most likely developmental cause of this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Abnormal migration of the metanephros derivative"},{"letter":"B","text":"Failure of the ureteric bud to differentiate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Complete degeneration of the pronephros"},{"letter":"D","text":"Improper development of the urogenital sinus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Abnormal migration of the metanephros derivative","explanation":"- This presentation is indicative of a horseshoe kidney. - A condition where the lower poles of the kidney fuse during development. - Normally, the Kidney ascends from the pelvis to its final position in the abdomen. - Abnormal migration can prevent this ascent and lead to fusion. - The ascent of the horseshoe kidney is blocked at the level of the inferior mesenteric artery. Development of the Renal System: - Urogenital Sinus: (Option D) Forms the bladder. Forms the urethra. Contributes to the lower 2/3rd of the vagina. - Forms the bladder. - Forms the urethra. - Contributes to the lower 2/3rd of the vagina. - Forms the bladder. - Forms the urethra. - Contributes to the lower 2/3rd of the vagina. Renal System Development Excretory Part Metanephrons Bowman's capsule, proximal convoluted tubules, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubules. Collecting Part Ureteric bud Collecting duct, minor calyces, major calyces, ureter. Note: - Metanephric Mesenchyme: Essential for kidney development. - The definitive kidney becomes functional by the 12th week of gestation , i.e., it begins excreting urine. Failure of the ureteric bud to differentiate (Option B) would lead to malformations with the collecting system of the kidney and does not cause horseshoe kidney. Complete degeneration of the pronephros (Option C) is a normal developmental process and does not cause a horseshoe kidney.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1517; Solution Pages 1521-1523","sourcePage":1517,"sourcePageEnd":1517},{"id":"anatomy-37-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 17-year-old girl with primary amenorrhea is diagnosed with Mullerian agenesis. Which all structures are likely to be affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina"},{"letter":"B","text":"Uterus, epoophoron, and lower 2/3rd of the vagina"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fallopian tube, trigone of bladder, and clitoris"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ovaries, clitoris, and labia majora"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina","explanation":"In females, the paramesonephric ducts , also known as Müllerian ducts give origin to the uterine tubes ( fallopian tubes ), the uterus , and the upper one-third of the vagina. Derivatives Wolffian Ducts (Mesonephric Ducts) Müllerian Ducts (Paramesonephric Ducts) In males Ureteric buds develop into ureters, Remains rudimentary and forms an pelvis, calyces, and collecting appendix of testis tubules of the kidneys. Trigone of the urinary bladder Posterior wall of the part of the prostatic urethra Epididymis Ductus deferens Seminal vesicles Ejaculatory ducts Mesodermal part of the prostate In females Ureteric buds develop into ureters, Uterine tubes Uterus Upper one-third pelvis, and calyces, and they collect of the vagina tubules of the kidneys. Trigone of the urinary bladder Posterior wall of the urethra Epoophoron Paroophoron - Gartner’s duct","sourceRef":"Question Page 1517; Solution Pages 1523-1524","sourcePage":1517,"sourcePageEnd":1517},{"id":"anatomy-37-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 26-year-old female patient presents to the emergency department in active labour. The obstetrician is concerned about the size of the pelvic outlet and its implications for the delivery of the baby. Which of the following structures is NOT considered a boundary of the pelvic outlet?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower border of the pubic symphysis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ischial tuberosities"},{"letter":"C","text":"Tip of the coccyx"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sacral promontory"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Sacral promontory","explanation":"Sacral promontory is a part of the pelvic inlet , not the outlet. Pelvis Components: - Structures from anterior to posterior are: Urinary bladder Uterus (in females) Rectum - Urinary bladder - Uterus (in females) - Rectum - Urinary bladder - Uterus (in females) - Rectum Note: These components are supported by the pelvic diaphragm (levator ani and coccygeus). Boundaries of the Perineum (same as pelvic outlet) : - Anteriorly: Lower border of the pubic symphysis (Option A) Arcuate or inferior pubic ligament - Lower border of the pubic symphysis (Option A) - Arcuate or inferior pubic ligament - Posteriorly: Tip of the coccyx (Option C) - Tip of the coccyx (Option C) - On each side from anterior to posterior: Conjoined ischiopubic rami Ischial tuberosity (Option B) Sacrotuberous ligament - Conjoined ischiopubic rami - Ischial tuberosity (Option B) - Sacrotuberous ligament - Lower border of the pubic symphysis (Option A) - Arcuate or inferior pubic ligament - Tip of the coccyx (Option C) - Conjoined ischiopubic rami - Ischial tuberosity (Option B) - Sacrotuberous ligament Openings in Perineum Males Females Urethra Anal canal Urethra Vagina Anal canal","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1517-1518; Solution Pages 1524-1526","sourcePage":1517,"sourcePageEnd":1518},{"id":"anatomy-37-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 35 yr old male with injury to the perineum, presented with blood collection in the perineal area extending to the mid-thigh. Which of the following correctly describes the location and characteristics of the perineal space involved in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Obturator fascia and inferior pelvic diaphragm fascia meet to form its apex"},{"letter":"B","text":"Located below the perineal membrane, open anteriorly, and continuous with spaces in the scrotum and penis"},{"letter":"C","text":"It contains the urethral sphincter within the wall of the urethra"},{"letter":"D","text":"Wedge-shaped spaces located below the pelvic diaphragm on either side of the anal canal"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Located below the perineal membrane, open anteriorly, and continuous with spaces in the scrotum and penis","explanation":"spaces in the scrotum and penis. - Superficial perineal space is involved in this patient. - Location: Superficial perineal space is situated below the perineal membrane, which separates it from the deep perineal space. - Continuity: It is open anteriorly, allowing for continuity with other anatomical spaces. In males, this includes the spaces in the scrotum and penis, while in females, it connects to the clitoral region. The Colles' fascia fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh, which typically prevents blood from spreading further into the thigh beyond this point. - It is open anteriorly, allowing for continuity with other anatomical spaces. - In males, this includes the spaces in the scrotum and penis, while in females, it connects to the clitoral region. - The Colles' fascia fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh, which typically prevents blood from spreading further into the thigh beyond this point. - It is open anteriorly, allowing for continuity with other anatomical spaces. - In males, this includes the spaces in the scrotum and penis, while in females, it connects to the clitoral region. - The Colles' fascia fuses with the fascia lata of the thigh, which typically prevents blood from spreading further into the thigh beyond this point. Perineal Regions: - The perineum, located between the thighs, contains the external genitalia and is divided into two triangular regions: Anal and urogenital. Region Description Anal Region Contains the anal canal and ischioanal fossa Urogenital Region Contains external urogenital organs Perineal Spaces: Space Description Superficial Perineal Space Located below the perineal membrane, open anteriorly, it connects with spaces in the scrotum/penis (males) or clitoris (females), enclosed posteriorly by Colles' fascia. (Option B) Deep Perineal Space Situated above the perineal membrane, previously thought to contain the urethral sphincter (now known to be within the urethra), it is thin and open above. (Option C) Ischioanal Fossa Wedge-shaped spaces in the anal triangle, located below the pelvic diaphragm, base formed by perineal skin, apex where obturator fascia and inferior pelvic diaphragm fascia meet. (Options A & D) Clinical Significance: - Trauma : Straddle injuries may lead to hematoma or confined urine extravasation. - Infections : Common site for Bartholin's abscess in females. - Surgery : Involves structures like the penile/clitoral bulb for reconstruction. - Urethral Injuries : Membranous urethra rupture can cause urine leakage, often in pelvic fractures. - Surgery : Relevant in urethral repairs and sphincter interventions. - Incontinence : Damage to the external urethral sphincter may result in urinary issues. - Abscesses : Frequent site for ischioanal abscesses, which may cross the midline. - Pudendal Nerve Block : Targeted here for regional anesthesia. - Surgery : Key area for drainage of abscesses and treatment of anal fistulas.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1518; Solution Pages 1526-1528","sourcePage":1518,"sourcePageEnd":1518},{"id":"anatomy-37-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In a newborn presenting with ambiguous genitalia at birth, USG reveals the presence of a uterus. The karyotype was found to be XY. What is the most common cause?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Excessive production of DHT."},{"letter":"B","text":"Mutation in the SRY gene."},{"letter":"C","text":"Inadequate expression of WNT4."},{"letter":"D","text":"Increased levels of estrogen."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Mutation in the SRY gene.","explanation":"- Mutation in the SRY gene prevents the formation of testes - Results in a lack of testosterone and Müllerian Inhibiting Substance ( MIS ). - The absence of MIS leads to the proliferation of mullerian ducts ( uterus present ) and the lack of testosterone leads to degeneration of the wolfian duct. Genital System Development: - Indifferent Stage: Both male and female structures can develop from common precursors. - Both male and female structures can develop from common precursors. - Intermediate Mesoderm: A major portion of the genitourinary system develops from the intermediate mesoderm. Forms genitourinary ridges and ducts. - A major portion of the genitourinary system develops from the intermediate mesoderm. - Forms genitourinary ridges and ducts. - Genitourinary Ridges: Appears in 5th week of development Give rise to: Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). - Appears in 5th week of development - Give rise to: Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). - Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development - Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development - Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). - Both male and female structures can develop from common precursors. - A major portion of the genitourinary system develops from the intermediate mesoderm. - Forms genitourinary ridges and ducts. - Appears in 5th week of development - Give rise to: Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). - Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development - Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development - Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). - Testis (in males): Appears in the 7th week of development - Ovary (in females): Appears in the 8th week of development - Kidneys (although the kidneys develop from the nephrogenic cord, which is part of the intermediate mesoderm, they are closely related to the genital structures during development). Duct Types: - Müllerian (Paramesonephric) Duct: Functional in females, develops into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and the upper 1/3rd of the vagina . Inhibited in males by Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS) (also known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone or AMH), produced by Sertoli cells of the testes. - Functional in females, develops into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and the upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - Inhibited in males by Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS) (also known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone or AMH), produced by Sertoli cells of the testes. - Wolffian (Mesonephric) Duct: Functional in males, it develops into epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles , and ejaculatory ducts. ( MNEMONIC- SEED : Seminal vesicle, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens) Regresses in females in the absence of testosterone. - Functional in males, it develops into epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles , and ejaculatory ducts. ( MNEMONIC- SEED : Seminal vesicle, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens) - Regresses in females in the absence of testosterone. - Functional in females, develops into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and the upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - Inhibited in males by Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS) (also known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone or AMH), produced by Sertoli cells of the testes. - Functional in males, it develops into epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles , and ejaculatory ducts. ( MNEMONIC- SEED : Seminal vesicle, epididymis, ejaculatory duct, ductus deferens) - Regresses in females in the absence of testosterone. External Genital Development by Stage: - Arises from the cloacal folds and genital tubercle (not the dorsal somatic lateral plate mesoderm). - Male and female external genitalia can be differentiated by 12-14 weeks of intrauterine life. Structure Male Development Female Development Genital Tubercle Becomes the phallus (penis) Becomes the clitoris Urethral Folds Fuse to form the penile urethra Become the labia minora Genital Swellings Form the scrotum Form the labia majora Sex Differentiation: - Male Development: SRY Gene: Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. Testosterone Effects: Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). Dihydrotestosterone (DHT): Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS): Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - SRY Gene: Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. - Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. - Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. - Testosterone Effects: Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). - Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). - Dihydrotestosterone (DHT): Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). - Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). - Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS): Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) - Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - Female Development: Absence of SRY Gene: Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . Paramesonephric (Müllerian) Ducts: Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . External Genitalia: Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . - Absence of SRY Gene: Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . - Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . - Paramesonephric (Müllerian) Ducts: Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - External Genitalia: Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . - Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . - SRY Gene: Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. - Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. - Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. - Testosterone Effects: Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). - Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). - Dihydrotestosterone (DHT): Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). - Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). - Müllerian Inhibiting Substance (MIS): Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) - Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - Located on the Y chromosome , it initiates the development of testes. - Activates downstream genes like SOX9 and SF1 , which are essential for Sertoli and Leydig cell differentiation. - Testosterone, secreted by Leydig cells, stimulates the mesonephric (Wolffian) ducts to form male reproductive structures (e.g., epididymis, vas deferens, seminal vesicles, and ejaculatory ducts). - Promotes the development of external genitalia (penis and scrotum). - Known as Anti-Müllerian Hormone (AMH) - Secreted by Sertoli cells, it causes the regression of the paramesonephric (Müllerian) ducts , preventing the development of female reproductive structures. - Absence of SRY Gene: Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . - Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . - Paramesonephric (Müllerian) Ducts: Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - External Genitalia: Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . - Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . - Without the SRY gene, the gonads develop into ovaries . - Develop into the fallopian tubes, uterus, and upper 1/3rd of the vagina . - Develop in the absence of testosterone, forming the clitoris, labia minora, and labia majora . Excessive production of DHT (Option A) promotes the development of male external genitalia. Inadequate expression of WNT4 (Option C) : No role in male development. Increased levels of estrogen (Option D) : Do not present with functional Müllerian ducts alongside regressed Wolffian ducts in a genetically male infant.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1518; Solution Pages 1528-1533","sourcePage":1518,"sourcePageEnd":1518},{"id":"anatomy-37-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 16-year-old adolescent girl developed secondary sexual characteristics at 12 but has never menstruated. Ultrasound indicates no uterus. Karyotyping and hormone assays are normal. Which structure is most likely NOT affected in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ovaries"},{"letter":"B","text":"Fallopian Tubes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper part of the Vagina"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cervix"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ovaries","explanation":"- The presentation is consistent with Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser (MRKH) syndrome , characterised by Müllerian agenesis. - In Mullerian agenesis, Ovaries are not affected. - Ovaries develop from the genito-urinary ridge. Development of the Female Genitourinary System: - Müllerian (Paramesonephric) Ducts : Origin : Mesodermal derivative. Developmental Fate : Uterus Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) Cervix (Options D) Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Origin : Mesodermal derivative. - Developmental Fate : Uterus Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) Cervix (Options D) Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Uterus - Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) - Cervix (Options D) - Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Vaginal Development : Upper one-third : Derived from the fused Müllerian ducts. Lower two-thirds : Derived from the endoderm of the urogenital sinus. - Upper one-third : Derived from the fused Müllerian ducts. - Lower two-thirds : Derived from the endoderm of the urogenital sinus. - Urogenital Sinus : Origin : Endodermal derivative. Forms : Urinary bladder Urethra Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Origin : Endodermal derivative. - Forms : Urinary bladder Urethra Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Urinary bladder - Urethra - Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Mesonephros and Mesonephric Ducts : Mesonephros leaves two mesonephric ducts that open into the urogenital sinus. Mesonephric duct gives rise to the ureteric bud , which penetrates the metanephros, leading to the formation of the collecting system of the kidney. - Mesonephros leaves two mesonephric ducts that open into the urogenital sinus. - Mesonephric duct gives rise to the ureteric bud , which penetrates the metanephros, leading to the formation of the collecting system of the kidney. - Wolffian (Mesonephric) Duct : Vestigial Remnants (in females): Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. - Vestigial Remnants (in females): Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. - Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. - Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. - Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. - Origin : Mesodermal derivative. - Developmental Fate : Uterus Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) Cervix (Options D) Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Uterus - Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) - Cervix (Options D) - Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Uterus - Uterine ( Fallopian ) tubes (Options B) - Cervix (Options D) - Upper one-third of the vagina (Options C) - Upper one-third : Derived from the fused Müllerian ducts. - Lower two-thirds : Derived from the endoderm of the urogenital sinus. - Origin : Endodermal derivative. - Forms : Urinary bladder Urethra Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Urinary bladder - Urethra - Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Urinary bladder - Urethra - Lower two-thirds of the vagina - Mesonephros leaves two mesonephric ducts that open into the urogenital sinus. - Mesonephric duct gives rise to the ureteric bud , which penetrates the metanephros, leading to the formation of the collecting system of the kidney. - Vestigial Remnants (in females): Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. - Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. - Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. - Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. - Epoophoron : Located above the ovary. - Paroophoron : Found near the ovary. - Gartner’s duct : A remnant in the lateral pelvic area. Note: Remnants of the Mullerian duct ( Paramesonephric duct ) in males are the appendix of the testis.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1518-1519; Solution Pages 1533-1535","sourcePage":1518,"sourcePageEnd":1519},{"id":"anatomy-37-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":7,"question":"An 8-year-old male child presents with a history of recurrent urinary tract infections. Imaging studies reveal a significant dilation of the ureters and an abnormal configuration of the bladder trigone. The patient is diagnosed with vesicoureteral reflux (VUR). Urologists suspect that the anomaly is linked to the embryological development of the trigone of the bladder. Which of the following statements about the trigone of the urinary bladder is FALSE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The trigone is formed from the mesonephric duct."},{"letter":"B","text":"The trigone is composed of endodermal tissue."},{"letter":"C","text":"The proximal part of the mesonephric duct is the Wolffian duct. Mucosal rugosities are absent in trigone region."},{"letter":"D","text":"The trigone is formed on the anterior wall of the bladder."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The trigone is composed of endodermal tissue.","explanation":"The trigone of the urinary bladder is derived from mesodermal tissue. Development of the Trigone of the Urinary Bladder: - Origin: The trigone is formed from the absorption of the tip of the mesonephric (Wolffian) duct into the posterior wall of the urogenital sinus . (Options A & D) - Tissue Derivation: The trigone is composed of mesodermal tissue. (Option B) The remainder of the urinary bladder is derived from endoderm (urogenital sinus). - The trigone is composed of mesodermal tissue. (Option B) - The remainder of the urinary bladder is derived from endoderm (urogenital sinus). - Mesonephric Duct: After the formation of the trigone, the proximal part of the mesonephric duct is referred to as the Wolffian duct. - After the formation of the trigone, the proximal part of the mesonephric duct is referred to as the Wolffian duct. - Clinical Significance: Anomalies in the development of the trigone can lead to urinary tract abnormalities. - Anomalies in the development of the trigone can lead to urinary tract abnormalities. - Anatomical Significance: The trigone is an important landmark within the bladder , delineating the area between the ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice. - The trigone is an important landmark within the bladder , delineating the area between the ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice. - The trigone is composed of mesodermal tissue. (Option B) - The remainder of the urinary bladder is derived from endoderm (urogenital sinus). - After the formation of the trigone, the proximal part of the mesonephric duct is referred to as the Wolffian duct. - Anomalies in the development of the trigone can lead to urinary tract abnormalities. - The trigone is an important landmark within the bladder , delineating the area between the ureteric orifices and the internal urethral orifice.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1519; Solution Pages 1535-1536","sourcePage":1519,"sourcePageEnd":1519},{"id":"anatomy-37-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A one-day-old newborn is admitted to the neonatal intensive care unit (NICU) due to severe respiratory distress. Prenatal ultrasounds had revealed bilateral renal agenesis and severe oligohydramnios. The neonatologist explains that this malformation occurred early in gestation. Which of the following developmental processes is most likely responsible for the bilateral renal agenesis seen in this newborn?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Failure of the ureteric bud formation and induce the metanephric mesenchyme."},{"letter":"B","text":"Failure of the pronephros development"},{"letter":"C","text":"Failure of the mesonephric duct to fuse with the urogenital sinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Failure of the mesonephric duct to differentiate into kidney"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Failure of the ureteric bud formation and induce the metanephric mesenchyme.","explanation":"mesenchyme - The ureteric bud is an outgrowth from the mesonephric duct and induces the metanephric mesenchyme to differentiate into nephrons (the functional units of the kidney). - Hence, failure of the ureteric bud formation or not properly inducing the metanephric mesenchyme leads to bilateral renal agenesis. 1. Collecting System (from Ureteric Bud): - Forms: Ureter Renal pelvis Major calyces Minor calyces Collecting ducts (tubules) - Ureter - Renal pelvis - Major calyces - Minor calyces - Collecting ducts (tubules) - Potential Issues: If the ureteric bud splits too early, it may result in: Bifid ureter (two ureters) - If the ureteric bud splits too early, it may result in: Bifid ureter (two ureters) - Ureter - Renal pelvis - Major calyces - Minor calyces - Collecting ducts (tubules) - If the ureteric bud splits too early, it may result in: Bifid ureter (two ureters) 2. Excretory System (from Metanephric Blastema): - Each collecting tubule is surrounded by a metanephric tissue cap , which forms the excretory tubules. Formation of Nephrons (functional units of the kidney): - Proximal end: Forms Bowman's capsule, which surrounds a glomerulus. - Distal end: Elongates to form the: Proximal convoluted tubule Loop of Henle Distal convoluted tubule - Proximal convoluted tubule - Loop of Henle - Distal convoluted tubule - Proximal convoluted tubule - Loop of Henle - Distal convoluted tubule Renal System Development Excretory Part Metanephrons Bowman's capsule, proximal convoluted tubules, loop of Henle, distal convoluted tubules. Collecting Part Ureteric bud Collecting duct, minor calyces, major calyces, ureter. Failure of the pronephros development (Option B): The pronephros is the first, most primitive form of the kidney in early embryonic life, but it is non-functional in humans and regresses by the 4th week of gestation. Development or involution of pronephros has no significant role in the formation of the permanent kidneys. Failure of the mesonephric duct to fuse with the urogenital sinus (Option C): A failure in this fusion would lead to malformations of the bladder or urethra, not bilateral renal agenesis. Ureteric bud and metanephric blastema interactions happen independently of this fusion process. Failure of the mesonephric duct to differentiate into kidney (Option D): The mesonephric duct does not directly differentiate into the kidney. It gives rise to the ureteric bud, which then induces the metanephric mesenchyme to form the functional kidney.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1519-1520; Solution Pages 1536-1539","sourcePage":1519,"sourcePageEnd":1520},{"id":"anatomy-37-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following pairs correctly matches the embryonic structure with its corresponding differentiated structure in males and females during the development of external genitalia?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Genital tubercle → Clitoris (Female), Glans penis (Male)"},{"letter":"B","text":"Genital fold → Labia majora (Female), Penoscrotal raphe (Male)"},{"letter":"C","text":"Genital swelling → Labia majora (Female), Glans penis (Male)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Genital fold → Labia minora (Female), Scrotum (Male)"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Genital tubercle → Clitoris (Female), Glans penis (Male)","explanation":"The urethral folds (often referred to as genital folds) develop into the labia minora in females and contribute to the ventral urethra in males. Development of External Genitalia: Structure Female Male Genital tubercle (phallus) Clitoris Glans penis Genital fold (urethral fold) Labia minora Penoscrotal raphe Part of the ventral urethra Genital swelling (labioscrotal swelling) Labia majora Scrotum","sourceRef":"Question Page 1520; Solution Pages 1539-1540","sourcePage":1520,"sourcePageEnd":1520},{"id":"anatomy-37-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-37","chapter":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topic":"Lesson 37 - Development of GUT, Pelvis & Perineum","topicIndex":37,"questionNumber":10,"question":"During a pelvic exam, a gynaecologist is teaching the medical students about the structures of the vestibule of the vagina. Which of the following structures is NOT located within the vestibule?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External urethral orifice"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vaginal orifice"},{"letter":"C","text":"Clitoral glans"},{"letter":"D","text":"Openings of the Bartholin glands"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Clitoral glans","explanation":"- The vestibule begins just below the clitoris and ends inferiorly at the posterior commissure of the labia minora. - It contains the vaginal and external urethral orifices and the openings of the two greater vestibular glands ( Bartholin glands ) and numerous lesser vestibular glands. - The clitoral glans is not located within the vestibule. Vestibule of Vagina: - It is the area between the labia minora. - It is a smooth area that starts just below the clitoris at the top and extends downward to the posterior junction of the labia minora. - Contents: External urethral orifice (Option A) Vaginal orifice (Option B) Openings of the two greater vestibular glands ( Bartholin gland ) (Option D) Openings of numerous lesser vestibular glands ( Skene's Glands ) - mucous-secreting glands. - External urethral orifice (Option A) - Vaginal orifice (Option B) - Openings of the two greater vestibular glands ( Bartholin gland ) (Option D) - Openings of numerous lesser vestibular glands ( Skene's Glands ) - mucous-secreting glands. - External urethral orifice (Option A) - Vaginal orifice (Option B) - Openings of the two greater vestibular glands ( Bartholin gland ) (Option D) - Openings of numerous lesser vestibular glands ( Skene's Glands ) - mucous-secreting glands.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1520; Solution Pages 1540-1541","sourcePage":1520,"sourcePageEnd":1520},{"id":"anatomy-38-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 58-year-old male patient presents with difficulty in urination and reports a sensation of incomplete bladder emptying. Upon examination, the neurologist suspects an issue with autonomic innervation to the urinary bladder. What are the root values for the sympathetic and parasympathetic innervation of the urinary bladder?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sympathetic: S1-S2; Parasympathetic: T10-L2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sympathetic: T12-L1; Parasympathetic: S3-S5"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sympathetic: L1-L3; Parasympathetic: S1-S3"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sympathetic: T10-L2; Parasympathetic: S2-S4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Sympathetic: T10-L2; Parasympathetic: S2-S4","explanation":"- Urinary bladder is innervated by the vesical plexus, which comprises both sympathetic and parasympathetic fibers, involving both motor and sensory functions. - Sympathetic innervation controls bladder filling , whereas the parasympathetic innervation controls bladder emptying. - Somatic innervation is responsible for the voluntary control of urination. Type of Innervation Nerves Function Parasympathetic Nerve Fibers (S2, Pelvic splanchnic nerves Excitatory effect on Detrusor muscle S3, S4) Inhibitory to the vesical sphincter. Sympathetic Fibers (T10-L2) Inferior mesenteric plexus Inhibitory effect on Detrusor muscle Hypogastric plexus Motor action on vesical sphincter Somatic Nerve (S1, S2, S3) Pudendal nerve Supplies external urethral sphincter, which is under voluntary control","sourceRef":"Question Page 1542; Solution Pages 1548-1549","sourcePage":1542,"sourcePageEnd":1542},{"id":"anatomy-38-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 45-year-old female presents with sharp pain and swelling in her right buttock and thigh after falling on her side, leading to difficulty sitting and walking. Examination shows swelling, tenderness, and bruising over the right buttock, with no deformity, dislocation, or neurological deficits, indicating a possible hematoma. What is the origin of the affected artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior division of the internal iliac artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior division of the internal iliac artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior division of the obturator artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior division of the obturator artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Posterior division of the internal iliac artery","explanation":"The scenario describes a hematoma resulting from trauma to the Superior gluteal artery , which is a branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery . Superior Gluteal Artery Origin Branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery. Course Supply Superficial Branch: Gluteus maximus Deep Branch: Gluteus medius Gluteus minimus - Obturator artery : Branch of the internal iliac artery Divides (at obturator foramen) into: Anterior (medial) and posterior (lateral) branches. Acetabular branch (posterior division) passes through the acetabular notch (supplying the fat in the acetabular fossa) and sends a twig to the head of the femur along the round ligament. - Branch of the internal iliac artery - Divides (at obturator foramen) into: Anterior (medial) and posterior (lateral) branches. - Acetabular branch (posterior division) passes through the acetabular notch (supplying the fat in the acetabular fossa) and sends a twig to the head of the femur along the round ligament. - Branch of the internal iliac artery - Divides (at obturator foramen) into: Anterior (medial) and posterior (lateral) branches. - Acetabular branch (posterior division) passes through the acetabular notch (supplying the fat in the acetabular fossa) and sends a twig to the head of the femur along the round ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1542; Solution Pages 1549-1551","sourcePage":1542,"sourcePageEnd":1542},{"id":"anatomy-38-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 55-year-old male presents with rectal bleeding and changes in bowel habits. The physician evaluates the rectal anatomy to determine the best surgical approach. Which part of the rectum is covered by peritoneum only on the anterior aspect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Upper 1/3rd"},{"letter":"B","text":"Middle 1/3rd"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lower 1/3rd"},{"letter":"D","text":"Whole of the rectum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Middle 1/3rd","explanation":"Middle third of the Rectum is covered by the peritoneum only on the anterior aspect. Part of Rectum Peritoneal Relation Upper 1/3rd (Option A) Covered with peritoneum on the anterior and lateral aspects. Middle 1/3rd (Option B) Covered only on the anterior aspect. Lower 1/3rd (Option C) Devoid of peritoneum Visceral Relations of Rectum: - Anterior Relations: Part of Rectum Male Female Upper 2/3rd Rectovesical pouch (coils of intestine Rectouterine pouch (coils of intestine and sigmoid colon) and sigmoid colon) Lower 1/3rd Base of the urinary bladder Ureters Vagina (lower part) (terminal parts) Seminal vesicles Vas deferens (ampullae) Prostate - Posterior Relations (Same in both the sexes) : In the Midline On each side of the Midline Lower three pieces of the sacrum Coccyx Anococcygeal Pyriformis Coccygeus Levator ani Sympathetic chains raphe Ganglion impar Median sacral vessels Superior Anterior primary rami of S3, S4, S5, and coccygeal nerve rectal artery Fascia of Waldeyer Pelvic splanchnic nerves Lateral sacral vessels","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1542-1543; Solution Pages 1551-1552","sourcePage":1542,"sourcePageEnd":1543},{"id":"anatomy-38-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":4,"question":"A 58-year-old woman presents with faecal incontinence, particularly during activities that increase intra-abdominal pressure, such as coughing or lifting heavy objects. MRI reveals atrophy in a muscle responsible for maintaining the anorectal angle. What’s the likely muscle involved in this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External anal sphincter"},{"letter":"B","text":"Conjoint longitudinal muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Puborectalis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal anal sphincter"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Puborectalis","explanation":"- Puborectalis muscle , part of the levator ani muscle, forms a sling around the anorectal junction and is crucial for maintaining the anorectal angle. - Dysfunction or atrophy of this muscle can lead to faecal incontinence , especially during increases in intra-abdominal pressure. Resting Tone is mainly provided by Relaxed External anal sphincter Weakness of External anal Internal Anal Sphincter (IAS) (EAS) and Puborectalis muscle Sphincter, Internal anal Sphincter, Puborectalis muscle. Anorectal Ring Muscles Muscle Description Function Significance Internal Anal Sphincter Thickened, terminal Provides continuous Dysfunction can lead to (Option D) portion of the inner circular resting tone to the anal faecal incontinence. smooth muscle layer of the canal through sympathetic large intestine. innervation. External Anal Sphincter Main bulk of the anal Allows voluntary contro l Important for voluntary (Option A) sphincter complex and is over defecation; allows faecal control primarily made of striated sustained contraction. muscle. Conjoint Longitudinal Layer between internal Contracts during Plays a role in defecation; Muscle (Option B) and external anal defecation , shortening age-related decline in sphincters; continuous with and widening the anal muscle fibres can impact the outer longitudinal canal, facilitating faecal function. smooth muscle of the passage. rectum. Puborectalis (Option C) Sling muscle part of the Maintains the anorectal Critical for faecal levator ani muscle , angle. continence surrounding the anorectal junction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1543; Solution Pages 1552-1553","sourcePage":1543,"sourcePageEnd":1543},{"id":"anatomy-38-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 46-year-old female reports intermittent rectal bleeding and a feeling of fullness during bowel movements. She has a history of constipation and obesity. Examination reveals internal haemorrhoids. Which of the following options correctly describes their characteristics concerning location and nerve supply? Presence of a visible thrombus in the anal region Ability to manually reduce the mass. Located below the dentate line, covered by anoderm. Innervated by pelvic splanchnic nerves; generally painless.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,2,3"},{"letter":"B","text":"2,3"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,2"},{"letter":"D","text":"2,4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 2,4","explanation":"- Hemorrhoids are variceal enlargement of the submucosal anal and perianal venous plexuses. - They are divided into internal and external piles. - Internal Hemorrhoids or True piles are usually painless and occur above the dentate line which bleed on straining during defecation. Type of piles Internal Hemorrhoids External Hemorrhoids Location Above pectinate line Below pectinate line Contributing Vein Main tributaries and radicals of Tributaries of Inferior rectal vein Superior rectal vein Common Site 3, 7 and 11 o’clock positions of the No such common site. anal wall, in the lithotomy position Pain Painless (as innervated by Painful (innervated by somatic splanchnic nerves ) (Statement 4) nerves) Bleeding Bleeds on straining at stool Do not bleed on straining Points to know Initially, they are contained within the A thrombosed external haemorrhoid anal canal (1st degree) , later, they appears as a painful, swollen, bluish expand and protrude during lump at the anal verge resulting from defecation (2nd degree), and finally, the rupture of a subcutaneous vein. they stay protruded (3rd degree). (Statement 1) These are located 2nd-degree piles can be manually below the dentate line and present reduced if they are prolapsed and not as tense hematomas at the anal thrombosed. (Statement 2) margin (perianal hematomas). (Statement 3)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1543-1544; Solution Pages 1553-1554","sourcePage":1543,"sourcePageEnd":1544},{"id":"anatomy-38-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 33-year-old woman in labour is experiencing discomfort and perianal pain and is scheduled to receive a pudendal nerve block. Which anatomical landmarks should the clinician identify to locate the pudendal nerve accurately?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lesser sciatic notch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pubic symphysis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Greater sciatic notch"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ischial spine"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Ischial spine","explanation":"- In the pudendal nerve block , the nerve is infiltrated with a local anaesthetic at the point where it crosses the ischial spine using a needle inserted through the vaginal wall, directed by a finger. - It aids in achieving almost painless vaginal or forceps deliveries and is also helpful for minor surgeries involving the vagina and perineum. - When a bilateral pudendal block is administered, it results in a loss of the anal reflex (saddle anaesthesia), relaxation of the pelvic floor muscles , and diminished sensation in the vulva and lower third of the vagina. Lesser sciatic notch (Option A) is the area where the pudendal nerve exits the gluteal region and enters the pudendal canal. It's not easily accessible for the nerve block. Pubic symphysis (Option B): Administering the block here would not accurately target the pudendal nerve and is unlikely to deliver the intended analgesic effect for perineal pain. Greater sciatic notch (Option C) is the point from which the pudendal nerve enters the gluteal region; it is not the preferred site for nerve block.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1544; Solution Pages 1554-1556","sourcePage":1544,"sourcePageEnd":1544},{"id":"anatomy-38-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following accurately describes the X, Y, and Z listed below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Posterior rami of S1, S2, S3"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior rami of S1, S2, S3"},{"letter":"C","text":"Posterior rami of S2, S3, S4"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior rami of S2, S3, S4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior rami of S2, S3, S4","explanation":"The given image depicts the origin of the pudendal nerve , which is formed in the pelvis by the anterior rami of sacral plexus S2, S3 and S4. - Sacral plexus is formed by the anterior portion of the L4 nerve , the entire L5 nerve, and the S1, S2, and S3 nerves. - These nerves then divide into anterior and posterior rami. - Anterior rami: Nerve Root & Nerve To Supplies (L4, L5, S1) Nerve to Quadratus Femoris Quadratus femoris Inferior gemellus Hip joint (L5, S1, S2) Nerve to Obturator Internus Obturator internus Superior gemellus (S2, S3, S4) Pudendal nerve Genital skin, External genital organs Muscles of the perineum (S3, S4) Perforating cutaneous nerve Small area of skin in the gluteal area (L4, L5, S1, S2, S3) Tibial part of the sciatic nerve. Hamstrings All muscles of the calf and the sole (S1, S2) Posterior cutaneous nerve of thigh Skin at the back of the thigh - Posterior rami : Nerve Root & Nerve To Supplies (L4, L5, S1) Superior Gluteal Nerve Gluteus medius, Gluteus minimus Tensor fascia lata (L5, S1, S2) Inferior Gluteal Nerve Gluteus maximus (L4, L5, S1, S2) Common Peroneal Part of the Sciatic Evertors of foot Dorsiflexors of ankle joint and extensor Nerve digitorum brevis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1544-1545; Solution Pages 1556-1557","sourcePage":1544,"sourcePageEnd":1545},{"id":"anatomy-38-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with chronic perineal pain and numbness following a difficult vaginal delivery. The clinician suspects entrapment of the nerve supplying sensation to the perineum. Through which foramina does this nerve exit and re-enter the pelvis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Greater sciatic foramen → Obturator foramen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lesser sciatic foramen → Greater sciatic foramen,"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator foramen → Lesser sciatic foramen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Greater sciatic foramen → Lesser sciatic foramen"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Greater sciatic foramen → Lesser sciatic foramen","explanation":"- Pudendal nerve originates in the pelvis from the anterior rami of the S2, S3 and S4 nerves of the sacral plexus. - It enters the gluteal region through the greater sciatic notch, curves behind the ischial spine for a brief distance, and exits the gluteal area by passing into the lesser sciatic foramen.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1545; Solution Pages 1557-1558","sourcePage":1545,"sourcePageEnd":1545},{"id":"anatomy-38-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 62-year-old female presents with persistent pelvic pain and urinary incontinence. Imaging reveals a mass in the pelvic region compressing nearby blood vessels, raising suspicion of internal iliac artery involvement. Which of the following is not a branch of the internal iliac artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral sacral artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Uterine artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal pudendal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Deep circumflex iliac artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Deep circumflex iliac artery","explanation":"Deep circumflex iliac artery originates from the external iliac artery, not the internal iliac artery. - Lateral sacral artery (Option A) is a branch of the posterior division of the internal iliac artery , supplying the sacral region. - Uterine artery (Option B) branches from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery and supplies the uterus. - Internal pudendal artery (Option C) also arises from the anterior division of the internal iliac artery, it supplies the perineum and external genitalia. - Common iliac artery is formed by the bifurcation of the abdominal aorta at the L4 vertebra and divides into the external and internal iliac arteries. - External iliac artery, after giving off its branches, continues as the femoral artery, while the internal iliac artery splits into anterior and posterior divisions . Artery Branches Supplies External Iliac Artery Deep circumflex iliac artery Muscles attached to the iliac crest Inferior epigastric artery Muscles and overlying skin of rectus sheath Internal Iliac Artery (Anterior Division) Superior vesical artery Urinary bladder’s superior surface Muscular wall of ductus deferens Obturator artery Medial thigh Middle rectal artery Muscle coats of rectum Prostate Seminal vesicles Inferior Vesical artery Urinary bladder Prostate seminal vesicle The lower part of the ureter Inferior gluteal artery Gluteal region Internal Pudendal artery Perineum region Vaginal artery Vagina and base of the urinary bladder Uterine artery Vagina and uterus Internal Iliac Artery (Posterior S uperior Gluteal artery Muscles of the gluteal region Division) (Mnemonic - SIL) I liolumbar artery Iliacus Muscles of back Cauda equina L ateral sacral artery Cauda equina Back muscles of the sacrum","sourceRef":"Question Page 1545; Solution Pages 1558-1561","sourcePage":1545,"sourcePageEnd":1545},{"id":"anatomy-38-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A 65-year-old male presents with urinary retention and pelvic discomfort. A digital rectal examination reveals an enlarged prostate gland. During surgery to relieve the obstruction, the urologist must take care to avoid damaging the structures that separate the prostate from the rectum. Which of the following structures separates the prostate gland from the rectum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Waldeyer’s fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Scarpa’s fascia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Denonvilliers’ fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Camper’s fascia"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Denonvilliers’ fascia","explanation":"- The structure that separates the prostate gland from the rectum is the Rectovesical Fascia (also known as Denonvilliers' Fascia ). - It extends from the floor of the rectovesical pouch to the perineal body. - Waldeyer’s fascia (Option A) is a connective tissue by which the rectum (ampulla) is attached to the sacrum and coccyx. - The superficial fascia of the anterior abdominal wall is divided into: A superficial fatty layer, Camper’s fascia (Option D) A deep membranous layer, Scarpa’s fascia (Option B) - A superficial fatty layer, Camper’s fascia (Option D) - A deep membranous layer, Scarpa’s fascia (Option B) - A superficial fatty layer, Camper’s fascia (Option D) - A deep membranous layer, Scarpa’s fascia (Option B)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1545-1546; Solution Pages 1561-1564","sourcePage":1545,"sourcePageEnd":1546},{"id":"anatomy-38-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 55-year-old male with intermittent abdominal pain, changes in bowel habits, and occasional rectal bleeding is scheduled for a sigmoidoscopy. The cannula is inserted in the left lateral position to avoid injuring the rectal folds that are present against the concavities of the rectum. Which of the following statements regarding these folds is inaccurate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"First fold is situated near the rectosigmoid junction."},{"letter":"B","text":"These folds are permanent."},{"letter":"C","text":"Third fold is located at the upper end of the ampulla."},{"letter":"D","text":"They are flattened in a distended rectum."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. They are flattened in a distended rectum.","explanation":"In this case, the cannula is inserted laterally during the sigmoidoscopy to prevent injury to the permanent, transverse folds referred to as Houston's valves , which are more prominent during distension. Two Types of Folds in the Rectum: Longitudinal Houston’s valve (Transverse or horizontal folds or plicae transversales) Temporary folds Situated in the lower part of an empty Permanent folds (Option B) Situated against the rectum. Obliterated by distension concavities of the lateral curvatures of the rectum. More prominent by distension (Option D) Types of Houston’s Valves: Types First Transverse Fold Second Transverse Third Transverse Fourth Transverse Fold Fold Fold Site Near the upper end, Above the third fold At the level of the Below the third valve close to the (2.5cm) upper end of the (2.5cm) rectosigmoid ampulla. (Option C) junction. (Option A) Wall of Projection Right or left wall Left wall (upper Anterior and right Left wall (lower lateral curvature) wall (middle lateral lateral curvature) curvature)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1546; Solution Pages 1564-1565","sourcePage":1546,"sourcePageEnd":1546},{"id":"anatomy-38-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 50-year-old man presents with perianal pain and bleeding during bowel movements. The region affected is supplied by a vessel that arises from the internal iliac system and supplies the lower anal canal. Which of the following arteries is the direct source of this vessel?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inferior mesenteric artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal pudendal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior gluteal artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Internal pudendal artery","explanation":"Internal pudendal artery is a branch of the anterior division of the internal iliac artery , and it gives rise to the inferior rectal artery that supplies the lower third of the rectum and the anal canal. Arterial Supply of the Rectum: Artery Source Supplies Superior Rectal Artery Branch of the inferior mesenteric Upper two-thirds of the rectum artery (Option A) Middle Rectal Artery Branch of the internal iliac artery Middle third of the rectum Inferior Rectal Artery Branch of the internal pudendal Distal rectum & anal canal artery (Option B) Venous Drainage of the Rectum: Vein Drains Into Region Drained Superior Rectal Vein Inferior mesenteric vein Upper two-thirds of the rectum Middle Rectal Vein Internal iliac vein Middle third of the rectum Inferior Rectal Vein Internal pudendal vein Anal canal & distal rectum Superior gluteal artery (Option D) is a branch of the internal iliac artery, supplying the gluteal muscles, but it does not directly contribute to the rectal blood supply.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1546; Solution Pages 1565-1566","sourcePage":1546,"sourcePageEnd":1546},{"id":"anatomy-38-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 50-year-old male presents with painful anal swelling and bleeding. External haemorrhoids are diagnosed, and the physician explains the pain is due to somatic innervation below a specific anatomical landmark in the anal canal. Which of the following separates the areas of somatic and visceral nerve supply in the anal canal?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"White line of Hilton"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anorectal junction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iliopectineal line"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pectinate line"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pectinate line","explanation":"- Anal canal is divided into upper and lower parts by pectinate line or dentate line (teeth shaped), which forms embryological, neurovascular and histological boundaries. - Upper part is supplied by the autonomic nervous system (painless) and the lower by somatic nerves (painful). - Lower ends of the anal column are joined by short transverse folds of mucous membranes known as anal valves . - Mucosal depressions above these valves are called anal sinuses. - Together, the anal valves form a transverse line encircling the anal canal, known as the pectinate line. Features Upper Anal Canal Lower Anal Canal Development Endodermal Ectodermal Epithelium Simple columnar Stratified squamous Nerve supply Autonomic nerves Painless Somatic nerves Painful Sympathetic Sympathetic (L1, L2) (S2, S, S4) Parasympathetic (S2, S3, S4) Arterial supply Superior rectal artery Inferior rectal artery Venous drainage Superior rectal vein draining into the Inferior rectal vein draining into caval portal system system Lymphatic system Internal iliac nodes Superficial inguinal nodes Common Type of Hemorrhoids Internal hemorrhoids External hemorrhoids White line of Hilton (Option A) is located at the junction between the subcutaneous portion of the external anal sphincter and the lower border of the internal anal sphincter. It marks the boundary of the mucocutaneous junction. Anorectal junction (Option B) is defined by the forward convexity of the rectal perineal flexure, located just below the tip of the coccyx. Here, the rectal ampulla narrows as it passes through the pelvic diaphragm, aligning with the apex of the prostate in males. Iliopectineal line (Option C) is a ridge located on the inner surface of the pelvis, created by the fusion of the ilium and pubis, extending from the pubic symphysis to the arcuate line of the ilium. This anatomical landmark is significant in clinical practice for directing pelvic surgeries and evaluating conditions that impact the pelvic organs.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1546-1547; Solution Pages 1566-1568","sourcePage":1546,"sourcePageEnd":1547},{"id":"anatomy-38-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-38","chapter":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topic":"Lesson 38 - Neurovascular Anatomy of Pelvis, Perineum, Rectum, and Anal Canal","topicIndex":38,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A 73-year-old male presents with rectal pain, and there is concern for rectal metastasis, given his history of prostate cancer. Which lymph nodes are most likely involved in the lymphatic drainage of the region marked ‘X’ in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Para-aortic lymph nodes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inferior mesenteric lymph nodes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial inguinal lymph nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal iliac lymph nodes"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Internal iliac lymph nodes","explanation":"The ‘X’ marked region is the lower part of the rectum . Internal iliac lymph nodes are primarily involved in the lymphatic drainage of the lower part of the rectum. Lymphatic Drainage of the Rectum and Anal Canal: Region Lymphatic Drainage Upper half of the rectum Inferior mesenteric nodes via pararectal and sigmoid nodes (Option B) Lower half of the rectum & upper anal canal Internal iliac nodes Anal Canal (below the pectinate line) Superficial inguinal lymph nodes (Option C) Lymphatic drainage of anal canal\" data-author=\"NA\" data-hash=\"7679\" data-license=\"NA\" data-source=\"NA\" data-tags=\"April2025\" height=\"500\" src=\"https://image.prepladder.com/notes/q4TLKhg3WfXJ9WXFVQtX1743852721.png\" width=\"500\" /> Para-aortic lymph nodes (Option A) are situated along the abdominal aorta, mainly at the L1 and L2 vertebral levels. They are responsible for draining lymph from the pelvic organs, lower extremities, and abdominal viscera, including the kidneys, ureters, and sections of the gastrointestinal tract.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1547; Solution Pages 1568-1570","sourcePage":1547,"sourcePageEnd":1547},{"id":"anatomy-39-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following best describes the boundaries of the ischiorectal fossa?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medially by the obturator internus, laterally by the levator ani."},{"letter":"B","text":"Medially by the external anal sphincter, laterally by the ischial tuberosity."},{"letter":"C","text":"Medially by the coccyx, laterally by the sacrotuberous ligament."},{"letter":"D","text":"Medially by the pubic symphysis, laterally by the ischial spine."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Medially by the external anal sphincter, laterally by the ischial tuberosity.","explanation":"Ischiorectal Fossa/Ischioanal Fossa: - Is a space filled with fat lateral to the anal canal and just below the pelvic diaphragm. - Has a shape of a triangular pyramid , with the apex at the boundary of the anal canal and the obturator fascia and the base directed towards the perineal surface. - Divided into the perianal space and the ischiorectal space by the perineal fascia. - Anatomy and Boundaries: Location : Situated on either side of the anal canal and extends from the skin of the perineum to the pelvic diaphragm. Boundaries : - Location : Situated on either side of the anal canal and extends from the skin of the perineum to the pelvic diaphragm. - Boundaries : - Location : Situated on either side of the anal canal and extends from the skin of the perineum to the pelvic diaphragm. - Boundaries : Medially External anal sphincter Levator ani (muscles of the pelvic floor) Laterally Obturator internus muscle Ischial tuberosity Anteriorly Pubic symphysis and puborectalis muscle. Posteriorly Sacrotuberous ligament and gluteus maximus muscle. - Contents: Internal pudendal artery, vein, and nerve. Inferior rectal artery and vein Posterior scrotal vessels and nerves Perineal branch of S4 and perforating cutaneous nerve Lymphatics Inferior rectal nerve Subcutaneous fat is abundant on both sides of the anal canal, which permits distension of the anal canal during defecation. - Internal pudendal artery, vein, and nerve. - Inferior rectal artery and vein - Posterior scrotal vessels and nerves - Perineal branch of S4 and perforating cutaneous nerve - Lymphatics - Inferior rectal nerve - Subcutaneous fat is abundant on both sides of the anal canal, which permits distension of the anal canal during defecation. - Clinical Significance: Abscess Formation: Ischioanal abscesses can occur due to infection, leading to significant pain and swelling in the perineal area. Surgical Access: Knowledge of the ischiorectal fossa is important for surgical procedures in the anal region, such as drainage of abscesses or repair of fistulas. Fecal Incontinence : Injury to the neurovascular structures in this area can lead to issues with anal control. - Abscess Formation: Ischioanal abscesses can occur due to infection, leading to significant pain and swelling in the perineal area. - Surgical Access: Knowledge of the ischiorectal fossa is important for surgical procedures in the anal region, such as drainage of abscesses or repair of fistulas. - Fecal Incontinence : Injury to the neurovascular structures in this area can lead to issues with anal control. - Internal pudendal artery, vein, and nerve. - Inferior rectal artery and vein - Posterior scrotal vessels and nerves - Perineal branch of S4 and perforating cutaneous nerve - Lymphatics - Inferior rectal nerve - Subcutaneous fat is abundant on both sides of the anal canal, which permits distension of the anal canal during defecation. - Abscess Formation: Ischioanal abscesses can occur due to infection, leading to significant pain and swelling in the perineal area. - Surgical Access: Knowledge of the ischiorectal fossa is important for surgical procedures in the anal region, such as drainage of abscesses or repair of fistulas. - Fecal Incontinence : Injury to the neurovascular structures in this area can lead to issues with anal control.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1571; Solution Pages 1574-1576","sourcePage":1571,"sourcePageEnd":1571},{"id":"anatomy-39-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following structures encloses all three masses of erectile tissue but does not extend into the glans penis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superficial fascia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tunica albuginea"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bulbospongiosus muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Buck’s fascia"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Buck’s fascia","explanation":"Deep fascia (Buck’s fascia) is a membranous fascia that encloses all three masses of erectile tissue (the corpora cavernosa and corpus spongiosum) but does not extend into the glans penis. Structure of Penis Root of the Penis Located in the superficial perineal pouch. Contains two crura and one bulb. Crura attach to the pubic arch, covered by ischiocavernosus muscle. Bulb attaches to the perineal membrane between the crura, covered by bulbospongiosus muscle. Urethra pierces the bulb to enter the corpus spongiosum. Body of the Penis Continues from the root and is covered by skin. Fills with blood during erection. Contains two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum. Corpora Cavernosa: Continuations of the crura. Run alongside each other, terminating beneath the glans penis. Encased in tunica albuginea , which has superficial fibers enclosing both corpora and deeper fibers enclosing each separately with a median septum. Corpus Spongiosum: Continues from the bulb of the penis. Expands to form the glans penis. Urethra runs through the corpus spongiosum. Surrounded by a fibrous sheath. Glans Penis Expanded terminal part of the corpus spongiosum. Base has a projecting edge (corona glandis), overhanging the neck of the penis. Urethra forms the navicular fossa within the glans. Skin and Fasciae Skin is thin, dark, loosely attached. Prepuce (foreskin) covers the glans and can be retracted. Frenulum is a median fold of skin on the underside of the glans. Preputial sac holds smegma from the preputial glands. Superficial fascia is loose areolar tissue without fat. Deep fascia (Buck's fascia) encloses all erectile tissues but doesn’t extend into the glans. Supporting Ligaments Fundiform ligament: Extends from the linea alba, splitting to enclose the penis. Suspensory ligament: Lies beneath the fundiform ligament, extends from the pubic symphysis, and merges with the penile fascia. Superficial fascia (Option A): Composed of loose areolar tissue, it covers the penis but lacks fat and is more superficial compared to Buck's fascia. Tunica albuginea (Option B): Encases only the corpora cavernosa, not the entire penis or the corpus spongiosum. Bulbospongiosus muscle (Option C): Covers the bulb of the penis, contributing to the function of the corpus spongiosum but does not enclose all three masses of erectile tissue.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1571; Solution Pages 1576-1578","sourcePage":1571,"sourcePageEnd":1571},{"id":"anatomy-39-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 32-year-old man presents to the ER after falling onto a sharp object while riding a bicycle. He complains of difficulty urinating and swelling around his genital area. Examination reveals urine extravasation into the scrotum and anterior abdominal wall. What is the most likely site of urethral injury in this patient?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Membranous urethra"},{"letter":"B","text":"Prostatic urethra"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bulb of the penis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Can be any of the above"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Bulb of the penis","explanation":"- The most common site of urethral rupture is at the bulb of the penis , typically caused by trauma such as a fall onto a sharp object. - In this case, the urethra is crushed against the pubic bones. Urine then leaks into the superficial perineal pouch and spreads over the scrotum, penis, and anterior abdominal wall beneath the membranous layer of the superficial fascia ( superficial extravasation ). Urethral Injury Causes Anterior Urethra Meatus Instrumentation, iatrogenic, hypospadias, skin disorders (lichen sclerosus) Pendulous Urethra Instrumentation, iatrogenic, hypospadias, skin disorders (lichen sclerosus), sexually transmitted infections, crush injury Bulbar Urethra Instrumentation, iatrogenic, skin disorders (lichen sclerosus), sexually transmitted infections, crush injury, straddle-type injury Membranous urethra (Option A): Located above the urogenital diaphragm, an injury here would cause urine to leak into the deep perineal pouch, not into the superficial perineal structures. Prostatic urethra (Option B): An injury to the prostatic urethra would involve the deep perineal structures and extraperitoneal space, not the scrotum or anterior abdominal wall.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1571-1572; Solution Pages 1578-1579","sourcePage":1571,"sourcePageEnd":1572},{"id":"anatomy-39-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following muscles is NOT found in the superficial perineal pouch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bulbospongiosus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ischiocavernosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Superficial transverse perineal muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"External urethral sphincter"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. External urethral sphincter","explanation":"Superficial Perineal Pouch: - Perineum is a diamond-shaped space below the pelvic diaphragm and refers to the lowest region of the trunk. - An imaginary line between the anterior ends of the ischial tuberosity divides the perineum into 2 triangular divisions. - The anterior smaller triangle is known as the urogenital triangle , and the posterior larger division is called the anal triangle of the perineum. - Urogenital triangle is further divided by a quadrangular fascial layer called the perineal membrane into a deep perineal pouch above the perineal membrane and a superficial perineal space below the membrane. - Superficial perineal space is further divided into the subcutaneous and superficial perineal pouch by the 3 fascia layers: Superficial fascia of the perineum, deep perineal fascia, and the perineal membrane. - Boundaries of the Superficial Perineal Pouch: Superior boundary (roof) Perineal membrane. Inferior boundary (floor) Colles' fascia (superficial fascia of the perineum) Lateral boundaries Ischiopubic rami Posterior boundary Fusion with the perineal body and the posterior edge of the perineal membrane. Anterior boundary The space is open and communicates with the potential space between the fascia of the anterior abdominal wall and the perineum. - Contents of the Superficial Perineal Pouch: Muscles (Option A, B & C) Bulbospongiosus Ischiocavernosus Superficial transverse perineal muscles Erectile Tissues Crura of the penis (corpora cavernosa) Bulb of the penis (corpus spongiosum) Crura of the clitoris Vestibular bulbs Vessels and Nerves Branches of the internal pudendal vessels and pudendal nerve. Glands Greater vestibular (Bartholin’s) glands : Located at the posterior aspect of the vaginal opening, secreting mucus to lubricate the vagina. - Functions : Provides a space for muscles that aid in the support of the pelvic floor and control of the external genitalia. Houses erectile tissues that play an important role in sexual function. It contains neurovascular structures that supply the external genitalia. - Provides a space for muscles that aid in the support of the pelvic floor and control of the external genitalia. - Houses erectile tissues that play an important role in sexual function. - It contains neurovascular structures that supply the external genitalia. - Provides a space for muscles that aid in the support of the pelvic floor and control of the external genitalia. - Houses erectile tissues that play an important role in sexual function. - It contains neurovascular structures that supply the external genitalia.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1572; Solution Pages 1579-1582","sourcePage":1572,"sourcePageEnd":1572},{"id":"anatomy-39-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 40-year-old woman presents with stress urinary incontinence following childbirth. She reports leakage of urine during physical activity. Physical examination reveals no signs of infection or injury, but pelvic floor weakness is noted. The pelvic ultrasound shows damage to the muscles of the urogenital diaphragm. Which of the following muscles is most likely involved in this patient’s condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ischiocavernosus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superficial transverse perineal muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"External urethral sphincter"},{"letter":"D","text":"Bulbospongiosus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. External urethral sphincter","explanation":"External urethral sphincter is located in the deep perineal pouch and plays a critical role in controlling urinary continence. - Deep perineal pouch is in the urogenital triangle of the perineum below the pelvic diaphragm. - It presents as a triangular, trilaminar space with sphincter urethrae [external urethral sphincter], deep transverse perineal, and compressor urethrae [in females]. - Boundaries of the Deep Perineal Pouch: Superior boundary (roof): Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani). Inferior boundary (floor): Perineal membrane (inferior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm). Lateral boundary: Inferior portions of the obturator internus muscle and the ischiopubic rami. - Superior boundary (roof): Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani). - Inferior boundary (floor): Perineal membrane (inferior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm). - Lateral boundary: Inferior portions of the obturator internus muscle and the ischiopubic rami. - Superior boundary (roof): Pelvic diaphragm (levator ani). - Inferior boundary (floor): Perineal membrane (inferior fascia of the urogenital diaphragm). - Lateral boundary: Inferior portions of the obturator internus muscle and the ischiopubic rami. - Contents of the Deep Perineal Pouch: In Males In Females Muscles and Glands External urethral sphincter: External urethral sphincter: Surrounds the membranous part of Surrounds the urethra and controls the urethra and controls the voluntary voluntary urination. Deep transverse release of urine. Deep transverse perineal muscle: Provides support to perineal muscle: Provides structural the perineum and helps maintain the support to the perineum. integrity of the pelvic floor. Bulbourethral (Cowper's) glands: Compressor urethrae muscle: Aids in Small glands that secrete urinary continence by compressing pre-ejaculate fluid into the urethra. the urethra. Urethrovaginal sphincter: Membranous urethra: Part of the Wraps around the urethra and urethra that passes through the deep vagina, assisting in control over the perineal pouch. urethra. Nerves and Vessels Branches of the dorsal nerve and Branches of the internal pudendal artery of the penis, along with the vessels and pudendal nerve pudendal nerve branches. supplying the clitoris and surrounding regions. - Functions: Urinary continence: The external urethral sphincter plays a key role in controlling urination, helping to retain urine voluntarily. Support of pelvic structures: The muscles of the deep perineal pouch help support the perineum and pelvic organs. Sexual function: In males, the bulbourethral glands secrete fluid that lubricates the urethra during sexual arousal. - Urinary continence: The external urethral sphincter plays a key role in controlling urination, helping to retain urine voluntarily. - Support of pelvic structures: The muscles of the deep perineal pouch help support the perineum and pelvic organs. - Sexual function: In males, the bulbourethral glands secrete fluid that lubricates the urethra during sexual arousal. - Urinary continence: The external urethral sphincter plays a key role in controlling urination, helping to retain urine voluntarily. - Support of pelvic structures: The muscles of the deep perineal pouch help support the perineum and pelvic organs. - Sexual function: In males, the bulbourethral glands secrete fluid that lubricates the urethra during sexual arousal.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1572; Solution Pages 1582-1584","sourcePage":1572,"sourcePageEnd":1572},{"id":"anatomy-39-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 45-year-old woman is undergoing a gynecological procedure in the lithotomy position. The surgeon is identifying the bony landmarks for access to the pelvic organs. During the examination, the surgeon palpates a bony structure near the midline at the anterior aspect of the perineum. Which of the following bony landmarks is the surgeon most likely palpating?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ischial tuberosity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sacrum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pubic symphysis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pubic tubercle"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pubic symphysis","explanation":"Pubic symphysis is located at the anterior aspect of the perineum and is easily palpated when the patient is in the lithotomy position. View from Lithotomy Position: Bones and Anatomical Landmarks: - Pubic Symphysis: Located at the anterior aspect of the pelvis and acts as the anterior boundary of the perineum. - Ischiopubic Rami: They form the lateral borders of the urogenital triangle and are important as attachment points for muscles like the ischiocavernosus and the deep and superficial perineal muscles. - Ischial Tuberosities: They serve as the lateral borders of the anal triangle. In the lithotomy position, the ischial tuberosities can be palpated as they provide attachment points for the superficial transverse perineal muscles and are important in identifying the perineal body. - Coccyx : The small, triangular bone at the base of the spine, forming the posterior border of the pelvic outlet and provides an attachment point for the coccygeus muscle. - Sacrum : Lies posteriorly and superiorly, forming the posterior wall of the pelvic cavity. - Obturator Foramen: A large opening formed by the pubis and ischium which allows the passage of the obturator nerve and vessels. Perineal Regions Related to Bones: From the lithotomy position, the perineal area can be divided into the urogenital triangle and the anal triangle. Muscles Attached to Bony Landmarks (Visible from Lithotomy Position) : - Levator Ani (Pubococcygeus, Iliococcygeus): Support the pelvic organs and form part of the pelvic diaphragm. - Ischiocavernosus and Bulbospongiosus: Attach to the ischiopubic rami and help in erection (males) and clitoral function (females). - Superficial and Deep Transverse Perineal Muscles: Attach to the ischial tuberosities and the perineal body, providing support to the pelvic floor.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1572-1573; Solution Pages 1584-1586","sourcePage":1572,"sourcePageEnd":1573},{"id":"anatomy-39-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following muscles is composed of the pelvic diaphragm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Puborectalis, pubococcygeus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Obturator internus, piriformis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Levator ani, ischiococcygeus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ischiocavernosus, bulbospongiosus"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Levator ani, ischiococcygeus","explanation":"Pelvic Viscera Support: - Pelvic diaphragm : The primary structure supporting the pelvic viscera consists mainly of the levator ani and ischiococcygeus muscles. Provides structural support for organs such as the bladder, uterus, rectum, and small bowel. It helps maintain continence by supporting the urethra and rectum. Levator ani muscles (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus) form the bulk of the pelvic diaphragm and are essential for maintaining pelvic organ support. Puborectalis muscle forms a sling around the rectum and is crucial in maintaining fecal continence by controlling the anorectal angle. - The primary structure supporting the pelvic viscera consists mainly of the levator ani and ischiococcygeus muscles. - Provides structural support for organs such as the bladder, uterus, rectum, and small bowel. - It helps maintain continence by supporting the urethra and rectum. - Levator ani muscles (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus) form the bulk of the pelvic diaphragm and are essential for maintaining pelvic organ support. - Puborectalis muscle forms a sling around the rectum and is crucial in maintaining fecal continence by controlling the anorectal angle. - The primary structure supporting the pelvic viscera consists mainly of the levator ani and ischiococcygeus muscles. - Provides structural support for organs such as the bladder, uterus, rectum, and small bowel. - It helps maintain continence by supporting the urethra and rectum. - Levator ani muscles (puborectalis, pubococcygeus, and iliococcygeus) form the bulk of the pelvic diaphragm and are essential for maintaining pelvic organ support. - Puborectalis muscle forms a sling around the rectum and is crucial in maintaining fecal continence by controlling the anorectal angle. - Perineal Body: Perineal body is a fibromuscular mass located between the vagina and anus (or scrotum and anus in males). It serves as an anchoring point for several pelvic floor muscles, such as the external anal sphincter, bulbospongiosus, and transverse perineal muscles. It plays a critical role in maintaining the integrity of the pelvic floor , particularly during childbirth. - Perineal body is a fibromuscular mass located between the vagina and anus (or scrotum and anus in males). - It serves as an anchoring point for several pelvic floor muscles, such as the external anal sphincter, bulbospongiosus, and transverse perineal muscles. - It plays a critical role in maintaining the integrity of the pelvic floor , particularly during childbirth. - Perineal body is a fibromuscular mass located between the vagina and anus (or scrotum and anus in males). - It serves as an anchoring point for several pelvic floor muscles, such as the external anal sphincter, bulbospongiosus, and transverse perineal muscles. - It plays a critical role in maintaining the integrity of the pelvic floor , particularly during childbirth. - Urogenital Diaphragm: Urogenital diaphragm consists of a layer of muscle and connective tissue located below the pelvic diaphragm, providing support to the urethra and vagina. It helps in maintaining continence and offers additional support to pelvic organs , though it plays a less prominent role compared to the pelvic diaphragm. - Urogenital diaphragm consists of a layer of muscle and connective tissue located below the pelvic diaphragm, providing support to the urethra and vagina. - It helps in maintaining continence and offers additional support to pelvic organs , though it plays a less prominent role compared to the pelvic diaphragm. - Urogenital diaphragm consists of a layer of muscle and connective tissue located below the pelvic diaphragm, providing support to the urethra and vagina. - It helps in maintaining continence and offers additional support to pelvic organs , though it plays a less prominent role compared to the pelvic diaphragm. - Endopelvic Fascia: This connective tissue surrounds the pelvic organs and connects them to the pelvic walls, helping to stabilize and support these organs. Damage or weakening of the endopelvic fascia, often due to childbirth or aging, can lead to pelvic organ prolapse. - This connective tissue surrounds the pelvic organs and connects them to the pelvic walls, helping to stabilize and support these organs. - Damage or weakening of the endopelvic fascia, often due to childbirth or aging, can lead to pelvic organ prolapse. - This connective tissue surrounds the pelvic organs and connects them to the pelvic walls, helping to stabilize and support these organs. - Damage or weakening of the endopelvic fascia, often due to childbirth or aging, can lead to pelvic organ prolapse.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1573; Solution Pages 1586-1588","sourcePage":1573,"sourcePageEnd":1573},{"id":"anatomy-39-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-39","chapter":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topic":"Lesson 39 - Perineal Pouches and Ischiorectal Fossa, Extravasation of Urine","topicIndex":39,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 40-year-old male presents to the emergency department after a motor vehicle accident. He has an inability to urinate and perineal pain. Physical examination reveals a high-riding prostate on digital rectal examination, and imaging confirms extravasation of urine around the bladder and prostate. What is the most likely site of urethral injury?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Membranous urethra"},{"letter":"B","text":"Penile urethra"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bulb of the penis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Can be any of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Membranous urethra","explanation":"- If the rupture occurs above the urogenital diaphragm, urine escapes into the deep perineal pouch and may spread upward around the prostate and bladder into the extraperitoneal space ( deep extravasation ). - Here, the membranous urethra is located above the urogenital diaphragm. - A high-riding prostate could be further suggestive of posterior urethral injury , though not a reliable indicator. Urethral Injury Causes Posterior Urethra Membranous Urethra Instrumentation, pelvic fracture with urethral distraction defects Prostatic Urethra Instrumentation, radiation therapy for prostate cancer (external beam radiation therapy, brachytherapy) Bladder Neck Instrumentation, radiation therapy for prostate cancer (external beam radiation therapy, brachytherapy) Penile urethra (Option B) and Bulb of the penis (Option C) (Bulbar urethra) lie below the urogenital diaphragm, and thus, urine leaks into the superficial perineal pouch and spreads over the scrotum, penis, and anterior abdominal wall beneath the membranous layer of the superficial fascia (superficial extravasation).","sourceRef":"Question Page 1573; Solution Pages 1588-1589","sourcePage":1573,"sourcePageEnd":1573},{"id":"anatomy-40-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 35 year old male presents with perineal pain and swelling after a traumatic injury during a sporting event. On examination, there is significant bruising over the scrotum and penis. Imaging confirms fluid collection in the superficial perineal space. Which of the following structures is most likely affected by the injury?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sphincter urethrae muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Bulb of the penis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal nerve of the penis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prostate gland"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Bulb of the penis","explanation":"The bulb of the penis is located in the superficial perineal space, making it vulnerable to injury in this region. The bulb is covered by the bulbospongiosus muscle and contributes to the corpus spongiosum. Pouches in Perineum Superficial Perineal Space Deep Perineal Space Location Below the perineal membrane. Above the perineal membrane. Boundaries Superiorly: Perineal membrane Superiorly: Open Inferiorly: Perineal Inferiorly: Colles' fascia membrane Laterally: Ischiopubic (membranous layer of superficial rami Anteriorly: Gap between the fascia) Laterally: Ischiopubic rami perineal membrane and inferior pubic Posteriorly: Fusion of the perineal ligament. membrane with Colles' fascia Anteriorly: Continuous with the spaces of the scrotum and penis in males, and the clitoris and anterior abdominal wall in females. Contents In males : Root of the penis (corpora In males: Part of the urethra Muscles cavernosa and corpus spongiosum) ( sphincter urethrae in the urethral Muscles (ischiocavernosus, wall, deep transversus perinei) bulbospongiosus, superficial (Option A) Dorsal nerve of the penis transverse perinei) Branches of the (Option C) Muscular branches from perineal nerve and artery Ducts of the perineal nerve Artery of the penis the bulbourethral glands In females: Bulbourethral glands In females: Body of the clitoris (corpora Parts of the urethra and vagina cavernosa) Bulbs of the vestibule Muscles (sphincter urethrae in the Muscles (ischiocavernosus, urethral wall, deep transversus bulbospongiosus, superficial perinei, compressor urethrae, transverse perinei) Branches of the sphincter urethrovaginalis), Dorsal perineal nerve and artery The greater nerve of the clitoris, Muscular vestibular glands and their ducts branches from the perineal nerve, Artery of the clitoris, and no glands. Contents of Superficial and Deep Perineal Pouches: Prostate gland (Option D) is located much higher, in the pelvic region, and is not part of the perineal spaces.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1590; Solution Pages 1595-1597","sourcePage":1590,"sourcePageEnd":1590},{"id":"anatomy-40-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":2,"question":"From the muscles given below, choose the muscles forming the pelvic diaphragm. Levator ani Coccygeus Bulbospongiosus Ischiocavernosus Superficial transverse perineal muscle Deep transverse perineal muscle","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 6"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 and 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 and 5"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1 and 2","explanation":"Levator ani and coccygeus muscles on both sides and their covering fasciae forms the pelvic diaphragm. Pelvic Diaphragm: Definition Muscular partition between the true pelvis and the perineum Components Levator ani and coccygeus muscles on both sides and their covering fasciae Functions Support to the pelvic viscera Sphincteric action on the rectum and vagina Increasing intra-abdominal pressure during defecation, micturition, and parturition Openings 1. Hiatus Urogenitalis: Triangular gap between anterior fibers of the two levator ani muscles. Transmits the urethra in males, and the urethra and vagina in females. 2. Hiatus Rectalis: Round opening between the perineal body and the anococcygeal raphe. Provides passage to the anorectal junction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1590; Solution Pages 1597-1598","sourcePage":1590,"sourcePageEnd":1590},{"id":"anatomy-40-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Choose the wrong pairing of fibers of pubococcygeus with its name.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior fibers-levator prostatae"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior fibers-sphincter urethrovaginalis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle fibers-puborectalis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior fibers-puborectalis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Posterior fibers-puborectalis","explanation":"The middle fibers of pubococcygeus are called puborectalis , not the posterior fibers","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1590-1591; Solution Page 1598","sourcePage":1590,"sourcePageEnd":1591},{"id":"anatomy-40-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following lobes of the prostate is responsible for producing uvula vesicae?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior lobe"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior lobe"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median lobe"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral lobe"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Median lobe","explanation":"The median lobe of the prostate forms uvula vesicae. Lobe Location Key Features Anterior Lobe In front of the urethra, connecting the Devoid of glandular tissue Also two lateral lobes called isthmus Posterior Lobe Behind the ejaculatory ducts and Primary site of carcinoma of the median lobe prostate Median Lobe Behind the upper part of the urethra Wedge-shaped Common site of and in front of the ejaculatory ducts adenoma Base produces an elevation at the apex of trigonum vesicae called uvula vesicae - A slight elevation in the mucous membrane of urinary bladder immediately above and behind the internal urethral orifice Most commonly enlarged in BPH Lateral Lobes One on each side of the urethra Separated by posterior median sulcus Contains glandular tissue Possible site of adenoma in old age","sourceRef":"Question Page 1591; Solution Pages 1598-1600","sourcePage":1591,"sourcePageEnd":1591},{"id":"anatomy-40-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":5,"question":"A 72-year-old man visits the clinic for evaluation of urinary symptoms, including nocturia, increased urgency, and a sensation of incomplete bladder emptying. During the examination, a digital rectal exam reveals a smooth, enlarged prostate without any palpable nodules. The patient's PSA level is slightly elevated at 5.5 ng/mL. In which zone of the prostate does this condition primarily occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Peripheral zone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Central zone"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transitional zone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior fibromuscular stroma"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Transitional zone","explanation":"The above mentioned condition is benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) and the transitional zone is primarily responsible for it. Prostate Zones Description Peripheral Zone Extends posterolaterally around the gland from the apex to the base. Represents the most common site in the prostate for developing prostate carcinomas. Transitional Zone Constitutes two small lobules around the prostatic urethra. Represent the region where benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) primarily originates. Carcinomas that originate in the transition zone have been suggested to be of lower malignant potential. Central Zone Surrounds the ejaculatory duct apparatus and makes up the majority of the prostatic base. Less frequently involved in BPH or malignancy. Anterior Fibromuscular Stroma Rarely associated with disease","sourceRef":"Question Page 1591; Solution Pages 1600-1601","sourcePage":1591,"sourcePageEnd":1591},{"id":"anatomy-40-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A 65-year-old male presents to the clinic with complaints of frequent urination, especially at night, and difficulty initiating urination. He reports that he feels an urge to urinate but often has to wait before the stream begins. During the digital rectal exam, the physician notes a smooth, enlarged prostate. Patient has a medical history of diabetes and hyperlipidemia. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prostate cancer"},{"letter":"B","text":"Prostatitis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Urinary tract infection (UTI)"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH)","explanation":"Patient’s symptoms of increased urinary frequency, nocturia, and difficulty initiating urination, along with the finding of an enlarged prostate, are classic signs of benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH). Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): - Non-malignant enlargement or hyperplasia of the prostate tissue. - Common in older men , particularly those over 50 and prevalence increases with age. - Hyperplasia results from impaired cell death , resulting in the accumulation of senescent cells in the prostate. Pathophysiology Hormonal changes (increased dihydrotestosterone, DHT) contribute to prostate growth. Proliferation of stromal and epithelial cells, and delayed epithelial cell death in the transitional zone. Risk factors Diabetes mellitus Obesity Metabolic syndrome Heavy caffeine consumption High dose supplemental vitamin-C 1st degree relatives Symptoms Storage Symptoms: Urinary frequency Urgency Nocturia Incontinence (Urgency, stress) Voiding Symptoms: Hesitancy Small stream Intermittency Terminal dribbling Urinary retention Diagnosis Digital rectal exam (DRE) reveals enlarged prostate. Urinalysis to rule out infection. Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) test to assess for prostate cancer. Urinary flow rate tests can evaluate the severity of obstruction. 24 hour voiding diary Urodynamics Complications Acute urinary retention Urinary tract infections Bladder stones Kidney damage (if untreated) Treatment Alpha-blockers ( tamsulosin ), 5-alpha reductase inhibitors ( finasteride ). Transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP) Transurethral incision of the prostate Transurethral electrovaporization Holmium and Thulium laser enucleation of the prostate Prostate cancer (Option A) presents similarly but would typically require further evaluation and would more likely present with nodularity or firmness rather than a smooth enlarged prostate. Prostatitis (Option B) causes urinary symptoms but usually associated with pain, fever or dysuria. Urinary tract infection (Option D) causes urinary frequency and urgency but would usually be associated with other symptoms like dysuria or fever.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1591-1592; Solution Pages 1601-1602","sourcePage":1591,"sourcePageEnd":1592},{"id":"anatomy-40-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following statements about the prostatic urethra is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is primarily lined by stratified squamous epithelium."},{"letter":"B","text":"It is the shortest segment of the male urethra."},{"letter":"C","text":"It connects the bladder to the membranous urethra and receives the ejaculatory ducts."},{"letter":"D","text":"It is solely responsible for the passage of urine, with no role in the reproductive system."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It connects the bladder to the membranous urethra and receives the ejaculatory ducts.","explanation":"ejaculatory ducts. Male Urethra is Divided into 4 Parts: - Pre-prostatic urethra - Prostatic urethra - Membranous urethra - Spongy urethra Prostatic Urethra: Segment of the male urethra that passes through the prostate gland. Location Widest part of male urethra. (Option B) Contains urethral crest, seminal colliculus, prostatic utricle and orifices of prostatic duct. Extends from the bladder neck to the beginning of the membranous urethra which is approximately 3-4 cm in length . (Option C) Histology Urothelium immediately surrounded by glandular and strong tissue of the periurethral zone of the prostate. (Option A) Functions Conducts urine from the bladder to the external urethral orifice and serves as a passage for semen during ejaculation. (Option D) Associated Structures Receives the ejaculatory ducts , which deliver semen from the seminal vesicles and the vas deferens and contains the prostatic urethral orifices, which allow for the secretion of prostatic fluid. Clinical Significance Can be affected by conditions such as benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) and prostate cancer , leading to urinary obstruction. Digital rectal examination (DRE) can assess the prostate’s condition via the prostatic urethra.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1592; Solution Pages 1602-1604","sourcePage":1592,"sourcePageEnd":1592},{"id":"anatomy-40-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following correctly lists the segments of the male urethra in the order they occur from the bladder to the external urethral orifice?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prostatic urethra → Membranous urethra → Spongy urethra"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spongy urethra → Membranous urethra → Prostatic urethra"},{"letter":"C","text":"Membranous urethra → Spongy urethra → Prostatic urethra"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prostatic urethra → Spongy urethra → Membranous urethra"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Prostatic urethra → Membranous urethra → Spongy urethra","explanation":"The correct order of the segments of the male urethra is Prostatic urethra → Membranous urethra → Spongy urethra Male Urethra: - Urethra is a dynamic fibromuscular tube which serves as the terminal region of the male urinary and reproductive systems. - Average male urethra is 20 cm long and begins within the bladder wall and ends in the distal glans of the penis. - Receives blood supply from the inferior vesical artery , bulbourethral artery , and the internal pudendal artery. - The sympathetic, parasympathetic and visceral innervation to the male urethra gets delivered through the prostatic plexus. - Littre’s glands are small mucous-secreting glands located in the submucosa, along the length of male urethra, primarily in the spongy urethra. - Divided into 4 parts: Pre-Prostatic Urethra Location : Extends inferiorly from urinary bladder and ends prior to entering the prostate gland. Gross Features : It is embedded within the walls of the urinary bladder and is typically 0.5 to 1.5 cm in length. Histological Features : Transitional epithelium (urothelium) Prostatic Urethra Location : Completely encompassed within the prostate gland. Gross Features : This is generally the widest part of the male urethra, and contains the urethral crest, seminal colliculus, prostatic utricle, and the orifices of the prostatic ducts - typically 3.0 to 4.0 cm in length. Histological Features : Urothelium immediately surrounded by the glandular and stromal tissue of the periurethral zone of the prostate. Membranous Urethra Location : Begins immediately outside of the prostate and ends just prior to entering the bulb of the penis. Gross Features : Passes through the deep perineal pouch, the external urethral sphincter, and the perineal membrane.Typically 1 to 1.5 cm in length. Histological Features: Pseudostratified columnar epithelium. Spongy Urethra/Penile Urethra Location : Travels the entire length of the penis via the corpus spongiosum. Gross Features: Widens initially as it enters the bulb of the penis and widens again in the glans of the penis, forming the navicular fossa.Typically around 15 cm in length. Histological Features : Pseudostratified columnar epithelium except for the terminal portion which is stratified squamous epithelium.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1592; Solution Pages 1604-1605","sourcePage":1592,"sourcePageEnd":1592},{"id":"anatomy-40-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Gynaecologist is planning to do uterine artery ligation. She must be careful while performing it due to the risk of injuring which structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ureter, as it lies anterior to uterine artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ureter, as it lies posterior to uterine artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bladder, as it lies superior to uterine artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Urethra, as it lies inferior to uterine artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ureter, as it lies posterior to uterine artery","explanation":"- While performing uterine artery ligation, ‘ water under bridge ’ can occur due to injury to Ureter . - ‘Water’ refers to urine, due to leak from the ureter and ‘bridge’ is formed by the uterine artery. - Ureter lies posteroinferior to the uterine artery. Relations of Arteries with Ureter: - Uterine artery is a branch of anterior division of internal iliac artery and ovarian artery is a branch of abdominal aorta , both of which anastomose. - Uterine artery is anterosuperior to the ureter by 2.5 cm and then crosses the ureter to ascend along the lateral side of the uterus. - Ureter passes forward, just above the lateral fornix of the vagina, and lies about 2 cm to the side of the supravaginal portion of the cervix. It then curves inward toward the urinary bladder, coming into close contact with the vagina. - While performing hysterectomy, careful ligation of the uterine artery should be done to prevent this. - Presence of peristaltic movements and the pale glistening appearance with firm cord feel on palpation will help in identifying the ureter. Mesenteries Supporting Female Reproductive System: Broad ligament Fold of peritoneum attaching uterus to the lateral pelvic wall Mesovarium Attaches ovary to the posterior layer of broad ligament Mesometrium Part of broad ligament lying between uterine tube and ovarian ligament ( Ovarian ligament: passes from lower pole of ovary to lateral angle of uterus) Suspensory Ligament of the Ovary Part of the broad ligament that stretches from the upper pole of the ovary and the infundibulum of the uterine tube to the lateral pelvic wall Also known as infundibulopelvic ligament","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1592-1593; Solution Pages 1605-1607","sourcePage":1592,"sourcePageEnd":1593},{"id":"anatomy-40-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Match the branches of uterine arteries with their names based on the image given: 1 a) Basal artery 2 b) Spiral artery 3 c) Arcuate artery 4 d) Radial artery 1 a) Basal artery 2 b) Spiral artery 3 c) Arcuate artery 4 d) Radial artery **Type:** Match the Following **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-c, 2-a, 3-d, 4-b"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-a, 2-d, 3-c, 4-b"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1-c, 2-d, 3-a, 4-b","explanation":"Sequence of Uterine Artery: A Arcuate Artery Supplies the myometrium(outer ■) and gives Radial artery The arcuate artery around the cervix is also called the circular artery of the cervix R Radial Artery Supplies the myometrium ( inner ■) Gives Basal artery B Basal Artery Present in the basal endometrium, does not get sloughed off during menstruation Forms vascular layers, known as stratum vasculare S Spiral Artery Present in the innermost layer of endometrium to get sloughed off during menstruation. Spirally arranged to prevent excess bleed during menses","sourceRef":"Question Page 1593; Solution Pages 1607-1608","sourcePage":1593,"sourcePageEnd":1593},{"id":"anatomy-40-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 30 year old female presented with post coital bleeding. Hysterectomy with lymph node dissection was done. Gross specimen of the uterus is shown below. Which of the following nodes group is least likely to be positive for malignancy?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superficial inguinal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal iliac"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sacral"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Superficial inguinal","explanation":"Clinical scenario and the gross specimen is diagnostic of Carcinoma cervix . Cervix belongs to the lower lymphatics of the uterus that drains into the Internal iliac, obturator & sacral group (never into the inguinal) . Lymphatic Drainage of Uterus: Lymphatics Region Draining Lymph Node Upper Fundus and upper half of uterine Aortic group (Pre- & Lateral aortic) body including ovary and fallopian tube Round ligament and uterine isthmus Superficial inguinal Middle Lower half of uterine body External iliac Lower Cervix Internal iliac, Sacral & Obturator group (never inguinal) Vagina Upper 1/3rd External iliac Middle 1/3rd Internal iliac Lower 1/3rd Superficial inguinal","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1593-1594; Solution Pages 1608-1609","sourcePage":1593,"sourcePageEnd":1594},{"id":"anatomy-40-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-40","chapter":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topic":"Lesson 40 - Prostate, Male Urethra, and Female Reproductive System","topicIndex":40,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Angle shown in the image below in a non-gravid uterus is maintained by? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Round ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Uterosacral ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both (A) and (B)"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pubocervical ligament"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Both (A) and (B)","explanation":"- Angle shown in the image between the cervix and vagina is known as the Angle of Anteversion. - Angle between the uterus and cervix is known as the Angle of Anteflexion . - Uterus is maintained in an anteverted state mainly by Round ligament with minor contribution from uterosacral ligament in non-pregnant female. (Hence, Option C would be most appropriate) Angle of Anteversion & Anteflexion: Angle of Anteversion Description Angle between long axis of cervix and long axis of vagina Angle Normal: 90° Maintained by Non-pregnant female: Round ligament (main) + uterosacral ligament (Option C) Pregnant female: Round ligament only Angle of Anteflexion Description Angle between long axis of uterus and long axis of cervix Angle Normal: 120°-170° Maintained by Uterosacral ligament Pelvic Fascia: - The pelvic fascia is categorized into sections covering the pelvic wall, floor, and viscera. Fascia of the Pelvic Wall: Tough and membranous, covering the obturator internus and piriformis muscles and attaching to the bone margins. Fascia of the Pelvic Floor: Looser and less dense, covered by the parietal layer of pelvic fascia. Fascia Surrounding the Pelvic Viscera: Loosely structured, often containing areolar tissue, allowing for the distension of pelvic organs. - Fascia of the Pelvic Wall: Tough and membranous, covering the obturator internus and piriformis muscles and attaching to the bone margins. - Fascia of the Pelvic Floor: Looser and less dense, covered by the parietal layer of pelvic fascia. - Fascia Surrounding the Pelvic Viscera: Loosely structured, often containing areolar tissue, allowing for the distension of pelvic organs. - Fascia of the Pelvic Wall: Tough and membranous, covering the obturator internus and piriformis muscles and attaching to the bone margins. - Fascia of the Pelvic Floor: Looser and less dense, covered by the parietal layer of pelvic fascia. - Fascia Surrounding the Pelvic Viscera: Loosely structured, often containing areolar tissue, allowing for the distension of pelvic organs. Deep Endopelvic Connective Tissue Condenses to Form the Following Ligaments: Mackenrodt’s Ligaments Formed by condensation of parietal fascia covering the obturator internus. Distal part of the ureter passes under the uterine artery within the upper part of the cardinal ligament. Function: Lateral stabilization to the cervix at the level of ischial spine. Uterosacral Ligaments Formed by condensation of peritoneum. They hold the cervix posteriorly at the level of the ischial spines and the uterus in Anteflexed state. Pubocervical Fascia (Bladder Pillar) Serve mainly as vascular conduit. Extensions: Rectovaginal septum: known as Fascia of Denonvilliers’ Pubocervical ring: encircles supravaginal part of cervix Vesicovaginal septum: supports bladder and anterior vaginal wall Round Ligament Runs anterior to obturator artery and lateral to the inferior epigastric artery. Main ligament responsible for maintaining the uterus in Anteverted state. Embryologically, it corresponds with the gubernaculum of testis. In the fetus, the peritoneum continuing with the round ligament in the inguinal region is known as Canal of Nuck. Ovarian Ligament Morphologically continuous with the round ligament and is homologous to the gubernaculum of testis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1594; Solution Pages 1609-1612","sourcePage":1594,"sourcePageEnd":1594},{"id":"anatomy-41-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following statements regarding the levator ani muscles is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Supports pelvic viscera"},{"letter":"B","text":"Converge downwards and medially"},{"letter":"C","text":"Consists of iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Attached to pelvic brim"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Attached to pelvic brim","explanation":"Correct Option: Option D: The wrong statement regarding the levator ani muscles is that they are attached to the pelvic brim. The levator ani muscles are not attached to the pelvic brim. Instead, they originate from various bony structures in the pelvis, including the pubis, ischial spine, and tendinous arch of the pelvic fascia. The muscles then converge and attach to the coccyx and the anococcygeal raphe. Incorrect options: Option A: The statement that the levator ani muscles support pelvic viscera is correct. The levator ani muscles form a muscular sling within the pelvis and provide support to the pelvic organs, including the bladder, uterus, and rectum. Option B: The statement that the levator ani muscles converge downwards and medially is correct. The muscle fibers of the levator ani muscles come together and run in a downward and medial direction towards the midline of the pelvis. Option C: The statement that the levator ani muscles consist of the iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus muscles is correct. The levator ani muscles are composed of three main parts: the pubococcygeus, iliococcygeus, and puborectalis muscles. The iliococcygeus and pubococcygeus muscles are two of the components of the levator ani muscle group.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1613; Solution Page 1625","sourcePage":1613,"sourcePageEnd":1613},{"id":"anatomy-41-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Identify the marked area in the image given. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Morrison's pouch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Vesicouterine pouch"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pouch of Douglas"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ischioanal Fossa"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pouch of Douglas","explanation":"- The marked area in the image is the Pouch of Douglas. - The Pouch of Douglas, also known as the rectouterine pouch, is the lowest point of the peritoneal cavity in females. - It is located between the posterior aspect of the uterus and the anterior aspect of the rectum. - It is a common site for the accumulation of fluids, such as in the case of peritoneal infections or blood after pelvic surgeries. Rectouterine pouch (Pouch of Douglas): - Pouch between rectum posteriorly and uterus anteriorly - Most dependent part of the abdominal cavity in standing - Most dependent part of the abdomen in the lying down position is the hepatorenal pouch of Morrison Morrison's pouch (Option A): This is the space between the liver and the right kidney. It is not located in the pelvic area but in the upper abdomen. Vesicouterine pouch (Option B): This is located between the uterus and the bladder, higher in the pelvic cavity, and is not the same as the Pouch of Douglas. Ischioanal Fossa (Option D) is a fat-filled space situated on either side of the anus, located just below the pelvic diaphragm, essentially acting as a cushion around the anal canal; it is considered part of the anal region and is sometimes referred to as the ischiorectal fossa.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1613-1614; Solution Pages 1625-1626","sourcePage":1613,"sourcePageEnd":1614},{"id":"anatomy-41-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The ischial spine corresponds to which level?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S3-S5"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tip of the coccyx"},{"letter":"C","text":"L4"},{"letter":"D","text":"S2"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Tip of the coccyx","explanation":"Correct Option B - Tip of the coccyx: - The ischial spine refers to the sharp bony prominence which protrudes from the posterior margin of the ischium. It separates the greater sciatic notch above from the lesser sciatic notch below. - Despite commonly being referenced in relation to the S3-S5 vertebral level in some anatomical texts, in certain contexts or specific anatomical references, it may be aligned or associated with the tip of the coccyx. Incorrect Options: - Option A, C and D: Refer to explanation of Option B","sourceRef":"Question Page 1614; Solution Pages 1626-1627","sourcePage":1614,"sourcePageEnd":1614},{"id":"anatomy-41-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Inguinal ligament is formed due to:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transversalis fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inguinal muscle"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. External oblique aponeurosis","explanation":"Correct Option A - External oblique aponeurosis: - The inguinal ligament is indeed formed by the lower free border of the external oblique aponeurosis. It runs from the anterior superior iliac spine (ASIS) to the pubic tubercle, providing support to the inguinal region. Incorrect Options : Option B - Internal oblique aponeurosis: While the internal oblique contributes to the inguinal canal, it's not directly responsible for the formation of the inguinal ligament. Option C - Transversalis fascia: The transversalis fascia is involved in the formation of the deep inguinal ring, but it does not contribute to the inguinal ligament. Option D - Inguinal muscle: There isn't a specific muscle referred to as the \"inguinal muscle\" that forms the inguinal ligament.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1614; Solution Page 1627","sourcePage":1614,"sourcePageEnd":1614},{"id":"anatomy-41-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Deep inguinal ring is formed in:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"External oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transversalis fascia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transversus abdominis"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Transversalis fascia","explanation":"Correct Option C - Transversalis fascia: - The deep inguinal ring is formed by an opening in the transversalis fascia. It's a passage through which structures like the spermatic cord (in males) or the round ligament of the uterus (in females) pass. Incorrect Options: Option A - Internal oblique aponeurosis: This contributes to the formation of the superficial inguinal ring, not the deep inguinal ring. Option B - External oblique aponeurosis: Similar to the internal oblique, the external oblique contributes to the formation of the superficial inguinal ring, not the deep inguinal ring. Option D - Transversus abdominis: The fibers of the transversus abdominis contribute to the formation of the transversalis fascia, indirectly playing a role in the creation of the deep inguinal ring.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1614; Solution Pages 1627-1628","sourcePage":1614,"sourcePageEnd":1614},{"id":"anatomy-41-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What is the vertebral level shown by the blue line in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L2-L3"},{"letter":"B","text":"L4-L5"},{"letter":"C","text":"L5-S1"},{"letter":"D","text":"L3-L4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. L4-L5","explanation":"Correct Option B - L4-L5 - If the blue line is at the L4-L5 level, then this option is correct. L4-L5 refers to the intervertebral space between the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4) and the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5). This is a common area for vertebral numbering and is often used as a reference point in medical and anatomical contexts. Incorrect Options: Option A - L2-L3: This option is incorrect as the blue line is at the L4-L5 level. The L2-L3 level would be higher up on the spine, and if the blue line is visibly at L4-L5, then this option is not the correct answer. Option C - L5-S1: This option is incorrect as the blue line is at the L4-L5 level. L5-S1 refers to the intervertebral space between the fifth lumbar vertebra (L5) and the first sacral vertebra (S1), which is lower down the spine. If the blue line is at L4-L5, then this option is not the correct answer. Option D - L3-L4: This option is incorrect as the blue line is at the L4-L5 level. L3-L4 refers to the intervertebral space between the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4). If the blue line is visible at the L4-L5 level, then this option is not the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1614-1615; Solution Page 1628","sourcePage":1614,"sourcePageEnd":1615},{"id":"anatomy-41-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A person who had a road traffic accident presented to ER with injury to bulbar urethra. What can be the location of collection of the urine due to this injury?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inguinal pouch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Peritoneal cavity"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ischiorectal fossa"},{"letter":"D","text":"Anterior abdominal wall"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Anterior abdominal wall","explanation":"Correct Option D - Anterior abdominal wall - The correct answer. The bulbar urethra is a part of the male urethra located just before the penile urethra. An injury to the bulbar urethra can lead to a urethral rupture, allowing urine to leak into the surrounding tissues. In the case of bulbar urethral rupture, the urine extravasates to Superficial perineal pouch Scrotum Penile region Anterior abdominal wall - Superficial perineal pouch - Scrotum - Penile region - Anterior abdominal wall - Superficial perineal pouch - Scrotum - Penile region - Anterior abdominal wall Incorrect Options: Option A - Inguinal pouch: The inguinal pouch is not a typical location for urine collection. It is an area in the groin region, and urine collection there wouldn't be directly related to a bulbar urethral injury. Option B - Peritoneal cavity: The peritoneal cavity is the abdominal cavity where various organs are located. It is not a common location for urine collection due to a bulbar urethral injury. Leakage of urine into the peritoneal cavity could result in a condition known as urinoma, but this is not the primary location of urine collection in cases of bulbar urethral injury. Option C - Ischiorectal fossa: The ischiorectal fossa is situated in the pelvic region, but it is not a usual site for urine collection due to a bulbar urethral injury. This fossa contains fat and connective tissue and is not directly connected to the urinary system.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1615; Solution Pages 1628-1629","sourcePage":1615,"sourcePageEnd":1615},{"id":"anatomy-41-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 55-Year-old woman presents to the clinic complaining of blood in her stools. Physical exam reveals firm, enlarged inguinal lymph nodes. A contrast CT scan is performed and a possibly cancerous mass is visualized in the lower part of the gastrointestinal tract. In which location is the mass most likely located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anal canal inferior to the pectinate line"},{"letter":"B","text":"Distal rectum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sigmoid colon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Proximal rectum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Anal canal inferior to the pectinate line","explanation":"Correct Option -A- Anal canal inferior to the pectinate line: - The lymph from the inferior part of the anal canal drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. - The lymph from the distal rectum drains to the sacral nodes. - The lymph from the sigmoid colon drains to the inferior mesenteric nodes and then to the internal iliac nodes. - The lymph from the proximal rectum drains to the sacral nodes, and the lymph from the anal canal above the pectinate line drains to the internal iliac nodes. Incorrect Options: Option B- Distal rectum: This option is incorrect as lymph from the inferior part of the anal canal drains to the superficial inguinal nodes. Option C- Sigmoid colon: This option is incorrect as sigmoid colon lymph nodes drain to the inferior mesenteric nodes. Option D- Proximal rectum: This option is incorrect as proximal rectum drains to the sacral nodes.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1615-1616; Solution Pages 1629-1630","sourcePage":1615,"sourcePageEnd":1616},{"id":"anatomy-41-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":9,"question":"What type of joint is seen in the labeled structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Syndesmosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Primary cartilaginous joints"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fibrous joint"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary cartilaginous joint"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Secondary cartilaginous joint","explanation":"Correct Option D: Secondary cartilaginous joint The pubic symphysis is a joint sandwiched between your left pelvic bone and right pelvic bone. It is a secondary cartilaginous joint. The secondary cartilaginous joints are permanent joints, which are slightly movable and their movements depend upon the sufficient amount of white-fibro cartilage tissues. Examples of secondary cartilaginous joints are: 1. Intervertebral disc Incorrect Option: Option A: Syndesmosis is a fibrous joint in which two adjacent bones are linked by a strong membrane or ligaments. Examples of this joint include the distal tibiofibular syndesmosis, which is a syndesmotic joint formed by two bones and four ligaments. Option B: Primary cartilaginous joints, also called synchondrosis (“joined by cartilage”), is a cartilaginous joint where bones are joined together by hyaline cartilage or bone is united to hyaline cartilage. These joints are mainly seen in developing appendicular bones. Option C: The manubriosternal joint is a cartilaginous joint (symphysis) where only a thin layer of hyaline cartilage covers the articular surfaces with an intervening fibrocartilage disc.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1616; Solution Page 1630","sourcePage":1616,"sourcePageEnd":1616},{"id":"anatomy-41-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":10,"question":"All of the following form the marked structure except: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2 nd dorsal interossei"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lumbricals extensor digitorum"},{"letter":"C","text":"Extensor digitorum"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 nd palmar interossei"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 2 nd palmar interossei","explanation":"Correct option: Option D. - This is the correct answer. The 2nd palmar interossei muscle does not form the marked structure. The palmar interossei muscles are located in the palm of the hand and are responsible for adduction of the fingers toward the midline of the hand. They do not directly contribute to the formation of the dorsal digital expansion on the dorsal side of the fingers. Incorrect Option: Option A . The 2nd dorsal interossei muscle is involved in forming the marked structure. The dorsal digital expansion is formed by the dorsal interossei muscles, which are located between the metacarpal bones of the hand. They extend from the metacarpal bones to the extensor expansions on the dorsal side of the fingers. Option B. The lumbricals extensor digitorum muscles are involved in forming the marked structure. The lumbrical muscles arise from the tendons of the flexor digitorum profundus and pass through the dorsal digital expansion. They contribute to the extension of the fingers at the metacarpophalangeal joints and flexion at the interphalangeal joints. Option C. The extensor digitorum muscle is involved in forming the marked structure. The extensor digitorum muscle is located on the dorsal side of the forearm and gives rise to the tendons that insert into the dorsal digital expansions. It is responsible for the extension of the fingers at the metacarpophalangeal joints, proximal interphalangeal joints, and distal interphalangeal joints.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1616-1617; Solution Pages 1630-1631","sourcePage":1616,"sourcePageEnd":1617},{"id":"anatomy-41-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following structures given below is related to the structure highlighted (red) in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pudendal nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sciatic nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator nerve"},{"letter":"D","text":"Femoral nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Pudendal nerve","explanation":"Option A: a. Pudendal nerve: The pudendal nerve is the correct option. The pudendal nerve is a nerve that arises from the sacral plexus and provides sensory and motor innervation to the perineum, including the external genitalia and the anus. The ischial spine is an important landmark in the pelvis, and the pudendal nerve passes posterior to the ischial spine during itscourse. Incorrect Options: Option B: Sciatic nerve: The sciatic nerve is an incorrect option. The sciatic nerve is the largest nerve in the body and arises from the sacral plexus. It runs down the posterior thigh and does not directly pass through or relate to the ischial spine. Option C: Obturator nerve: The obturator nerve is an incorrect option. The obturator nerve arises from the lumbar plexus and provides sensory and motor innervation to the medial compartment of the thigh. It does not have a direct relationship with the ischial spine. Option D: Femoral nerve: Option D is not explained in the initial question. However, the femoral nerve is an incorrect option. The femoral nerve arises from the lumbar plexus and provides sensory and motor innervation to the anterior thigh. It does not have a direct relationship with the ischial spine.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1617-1618; Solution Pages 1631-1632","sourcePage":1617,"sourcePageEnd":1618},{"id":"anatomy-41-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":12,"question":"In a 55-year-old male, who has been diagnosed with left testicular carcinoma, which lymph node is typically affected first?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Inguinal lymph nodes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Para-aortic lymph nodes"},{"letter":"C","text":"Iliac lymph nodes"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pre-caval lymph nodes"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Para-aortic lymph nodes","explanation":"Correct Option B: - The para-aortic lymph nodes are located near the aorta, and drain lymphatic fluids from the testicles. - In most cases, the para-aortic lymph nodes are the first lymph nodes to be involved in testicular carcinoma. - Spread to these nodes can occur even with small tumors. Incorrect Options: Option A: Inguinal lymph nodes: The inguinal lymph nodes are located in the groin region and drain lymphatic fluids from the lower extremities, external genitalia, and perineum. Testicular carcinoma can spread to the inguinal lymph nodes, but it is not the first node to be involved in most cases. Option C: Iliac lymph nodes: The iliac lymph nodes involved in testicular carcinoma the para-aortic lymph nodes. Option D: Pre-caval lymph nodes:. The pre-caval lymph nodes can become involved in testicular carcinoma after the para-aortic lymph nodes.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1618; Solution Page 1632","sourcePage":1618,"sourcePageEnd":1618},{"id":"anatomy-41-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Bleeding vessel in hemorrhoids is?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superior rectal artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior rectal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Middle rectal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Middle rectal vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Superior rectal vein","explanation":"Correct option B - Superior rectal vein: Hemorrhoids are swollen and inflamed blood vessels in the rectum and anus. The bleeding associated with hemorrhoids usually occurs from the engorged veins. The superior rectal vein, which drains blood from the rectum, is often involved in hemorrhoidal bleeding. Thus, the superior rectal vein (option B) is the correct answer. Incorrect options: Option A: Superior rectal artery: The superior rectal artery is responsible for supplying arterial blood to the rectum. However, in the context of hemorrhoids, the bleeding vessel is typically a vein rather than an artery. Therefore, the superior rectal artery (option A) is not the correct answer. Option C: Middle rectal artery: The middle rectal artery is responsible for supplying blood to the middle portion of the rectum. While it plays a role in rectal blood supply, it is not typically associated with bleeding in hemorrhoids. Therefore, the middle rectal artery (option C) is not the correct choice. Option D; Middle rectal vein: The middle rectal vein drains blood from the middle portion of the rectum. Similar to the middle rectal artery, it is not directly associated with hemorrhoidal bleeding. Hence, the middle rectal vein (option D) is not the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1618-1619; Solution Pages 1632-1633","sourcePage":1618,"sourcePageEnd":1619},{"id":"anatomy-41-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":14,"question":"A woman delivered a healthy baby and to control her postpartum hemorrhage an artery was ligated. Then later she developed pain under her gluteus muscle. Which was the artery ligated ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Uterine artery"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior gluteal artery"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior gluteal artery"},{"letter":"D","text":"Internal iliac artery"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Internal iliac artery","explanation":"CORRECT ANSWER : D - The artery that was likely ligated in the case of a woman who developed pain under her gluteus muscle after controlling postpartum hemorrhage is the Internal Iliac Artery. - The internal iliac artery is a major branch of the common iliac artery that supplies blood to various structures in the pelvis, including the gluteal muscles. Ligating the internal iliac artery can lead to compromised blood flow to these muscles, potentially resulting in pain under thegluteus muscle. Incorrect options: Option A. Uterine artery: The uterine artery is commonly ligated during procedures to control postpartum hemorrhage. Ligating the uterine artery would not typically cause pain under the gluteus muscle. The uterine artery primarily supplies blood to the uterus and does not directly affect the gluteal region. Option B. Superior gluteal artery: The superior gluteal artery is responsible for supplying blood to the gluteal muscles, including the gluteus medius and gluteus minimus. Ligating the superior gluteal artery would compromise blood flow to these muscles, but it is unlikely to be the source of pain specifically under the gluteus muscle. Option C. Inferior gluteal artery: The inferior gluteal artery supplies blood to the gluteus maximus muscle. Ligating the inferior gluteal artery may result in reduced blood flow to the gluteus maximus, but it is unlikely to be the primary cause of pain under the gluteus muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1619; Solution Pages 1633-1634","sourcePage":1619,"sourcePageEnd":1619},{"id":"anatomy-41-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":15,"question":"In the scenario of a man who fell into a manhole and sustained perineal injury, leading to difficulty in urination and presence of blood at the urethral tip, a diagnosis of bulbar urethral injury was determined. In this particular case, where would the urine accumulation be the least expected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Perineum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior abdominal wall"},{"letter":"C","text":"The medial portion of thigh"},{"letter":"D","text":"Root of scrotum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The medial portion of thigh","explanation":"Correct Choice: C. Medial portion of thigh - Explanation: In the case of Bulbar urethral injury extravasation of urine can be seen. - In the rupture of bulbous spongy urethra below the perineal membrane, the urine may drain at 4 places: - Superficial perineal pouch below the perineal membrane, - Scrotum, - Penis, Anterior abdominal wall under the scarpa's fascia and anterior to the pubis bone. - The urine cannot go to three places; - Above the perineal membrane, deep perineal pouch. - Ischiorectal fossa prevented by colles fascia. - Fascia lata, the deep fascia of the thigh prevented by urine entering the thigh. Incorrect choices: Option A. Perineum: The extravasated urine spreads deep to the membranous layer of the superficial fascia and first fills the superficial perineal space. Hence, urine collection in this region is likely. Option B. Anterior Abdominal wall: Apart from the perineum the urine also fills the lower part of the anterior abdominal wall. Hence, urine collection in this region is likely. Option D. The Scrotum: Since the attachment of the membranous layer in this region is not firm hence, filling of urine is likely in the scrotum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1619; Solution Pages 1634-1635","sourcePage":1619,"sourcePageEnd":1619},{"id":"anatomy-41-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Deep inguinal ring is a defect in","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"External oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal oblique aponeurosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Fascia transversalis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rectus sheath"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Fascia transversalis","explanation":"The correct answer for the defect in the deep inguinal ring is the fascia transversalis. The inguinal region is an area in the lower abdomen where the spermatic cord passes through in males or the round ligament of the uterus in females. The deep inguinal ring is an opening or defect in the transversalis fascia, which is a layer of connective tissue that lines the abdominal cavity. Let's go through the provided options and their explanations: To summarize, the correct answer is the fascia transversalis. The deep inguinal ring is a defect or opening in this layer of connective tissue, allowing structures to pass through the inguinal canal.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1619-1620; Solution Page 1635","sourcePage":1619,"sourcePageEnd":1620},{"id":"anatomy-41-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which lobe of the prostate is responsible for the formation of the uvula vesicae?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Median"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lateral"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Median","explanation":"Correct Option: Option C: Median lobe: The median lobe of the prostate is the correct answer. It is a small, middle portion of the prostate that projects into the bladder and forms the uvula vesicae. The uvula vesicae is a smooth, rounded elevation located in the internal base of the bladder. Incorrect Option: Option A: Anterior lobe: The anterior lobe of the prostate does not specifically form the uvula vesicae. The anterior lobe is a small part of the prostate that lies in front of the urethra, but it does not contribute to the formation of the uvula vesicae. Option B: Posterior lobe: The posterior lobe of the prostate also does not form the uvula vesicae. The posterior lobe is the largest part of the prostate and it surrounds the ejaculatory ducts, but it is not directly involved in the formation of the uvula vesicae. Option D: Lateral lobes: The lateral lobes of the prostate do not form the uvula vesicae. They are the largest and most prominent parts of the prostate gland that are located on either side of the urethra, but they do not contribute to the formation of the uvula vesicae.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1620; Solution Pages 1635-1636","sourcePage":1620,"sourcePageEnd":1620},{"id":"anatomy-41-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":18,"question":"The patient exhibited an enlarged lymph node in the inguinal area. The primary cause that is most probable is","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Testicular cancer"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anal cancer"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sigmoid colon cancer"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prostate cancer"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Anal cancer","explanation":"Correct Option B: Anal cancer - Anal cancer: Anal cancer can spread to the inguinal lymph nodes, particularly if the cancer is located near the anal canal. - Inguinal lymphadenopathy is a common presentation in anal cancer, making it a likely primary cause of an enlarged inguinal lymph node. Incorrect options: Option A: Testicular cancer: Testicular cancer typically spreads to the retroperitoneal lymph nodes located near the back of the abdomen, rather than the inguinal lymph nodes in the groin area. While it is possible for testicular cancer to involve the inguinal lymph nodes in advanced stages, it is not the most likely primary cause of inguinal lymph node enlargement. Option C: Sigmoid colon cancer: Sigmoid colon cancer is less likely to directly involve the inguinal lymph nodes. It typically spreads to the regional mesenteric lymph nodes before involving distant lymph nodes. Therefore, it is less likely to be the primary cause of inguinal lymph node enlargement. Option D: Prostate cancer: Prostate cancer can metastasize to the regional pelvic lymph nodes, including the internal iliac and obturator lymph nodes. While it is possible for prostate cancer to involve the inguinal lymph nodes, it is relatively uncommon and usually occurs in advanced stages.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1620; Solution Page 1636","sourcePage":1620,"sourcePageEnd":1620},{"id":"anatomy-41-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which muscles are responsible for constituting the pelvic diaphragm? A. Iliococcygeus B. Pubococcygeus C. Obturator internus D. Deep transverse perinei","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A and B"},{"letter":"B","text":"A, B, C and D"},{"letter":"C","text":"A, B and D"},{"letter":"D","text":"A and D"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A and B","explanation":"Correct Option A - A and B: - The pelvic diaphragm is primarily composed of the levator ani and the coccygeus muscles. The levator ani itself is made up of three parts: the puborectalis, the pubococcygeus, and the iliococcygeus. Thus, both the iliococcygeus (option A) and the pubococcygeus (option B) muscles form parts of the pelvic diaphragm. Ischiococcygeus is also a part of pelvic diaphragm. Incorrect Options: Options B, C and D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1620-1621; Solution Pages 1636-1637","sourcePage":1620,"sourcePageEnd":1621},{"id":"anatomy-41-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":20,"question":"The rupture of the penile urethra does not cause extravasation of blood into which of the following spaces?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Superficial perineal pouch"},{"letter":"B","text":"Anterior abdominal wall"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ischiorectal fossa"},{"letter":"D","text":"Skin around the scrotum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ischiorectal fossa","explanation":"Correct Option C - Ischiorectal fossa: - The penile urethra is confined within the corpus spongiosum of the penis, and any rupture or injury to this structure would lead to extravasation of blood or urine. - Ischiorectal fossa - This space, filled with fat, lies on either side of the anal canal. - The boundaries of the ischiorectal fossa, particularly the fascial layers and the pelvic diaphragm, prevent the extravasation of blood from a ruptured penile urethra into this space. Therefore, option c is the correct answer. Incorrect Options: Option A . Superficial perineal pouch: This is a potential space between the membranous layer of the superficial fascia (Colles' fascia) and the perineal membrane. A rupture of the penile urethra can cause blood to extravasate into this space, which could then spread into the scrotum and lower abdominal wall. Option B . Anterior abdominal wall: If the penile urethra ruptures below the perineal membrane, urine may drain into the anterior abdominal wall under Scarpa's fascia, anterior to the pubis bone. Option D . Skin around the scrotum: Blood from a ruptured penile urethra can track along the Colles' fascia into the scrotum, leading to penile hematoma.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1621; Solution Page 1637","sourcePage":1621,"sourcePageEnd":1621},{"id":"anatomy-41-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":21,"question":"What is the volume of the prostate in healthy adult males?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"20cc"},{"letter":"B","text":"50cc"},{"letter":"C","text":"75cc"},{"letter":"D","text":"100cc"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 20cc","explanation":"Correct Option A - 20 cc: - The normal volume of the prostate in adult males typically ranges between 15 to 30 cubic centimeters (cc), with an average volume of around 20cc. - This measure, however, can be influenced by various factors, including age, hormonal status, and overall health. Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1621; Solution Pages 1637-1638","sourcePage":1621,"sourcePageEnd":1621},{"id":"anatomy-41-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":22,"question":"A defect in which of the following forms the structure marked below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Internal oblique"},{"letter":"B","text":"External oblique"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parietal peritoneum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fascia transversalis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Fascia transversalis","explanation":"Correct Option D: - A very thin layer of connective tissue called the fascia transversalis lines most of the abdominal cavity between the posterior side of the transversus abdominis and superficial to the extraperitoneal fat and peritoneum. - A defect in the fascia transversalis can also result in a direct inguinal hernia. - Other conditions that can affect the fascia transversalis include hernias of the linea alba (a fibrous band that runs down the center of the abdominal wall) and abdominal wall defects such as gastroschisis (a congenital defect in which the abdominal contents protrude through a hole in the abdominal wall). Incorrect Options: Option A . Internal oblique: A defect in the internal oblique muscle can result in a hernia known as a direct inguinal hernia. Other conditions affecting the internal oblique muscle include muscle strains, tears, and ruptures. Option B . External oblique: A defect in the external oblique muscle can result in a hernia known as an indirect inguinal hernia. Other conditions affecting the external oblique muscle include muscle strains, tears, and ruptures. Option C . The parietal peritoneum: A defect in the parietal peritoneum can result in a hernia known as an incisional hernia. This occurs when the abdominal cavity’s lining protrudes through a surgical incision. Other conditions that can affect the parietal peritoneum include peritonitis (inflammation of the peritoneum) and ascites (accumulation of fluid within the abdominal cavity).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1621-1622; Solution Pages 1638-1639","sourcePage":1621,"sourcePageEnd":1622},{"id":"anatomy-41-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-41","chapter":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 41 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":41,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Muscle inserted on lesser trochanter?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Psoas major muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Psoas minor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Obturator internus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Piriformis"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Psoas major muscle","explanation":"Correct Option A- Psoas Major Muscle: Psoas Major - Long muscle coming from the Lumbar vertebra - Inserts into lesser trochanter - femur bone - Innervated by anterior rami of L1, 2, 3 - Flexes the thigh Incorrect Options: Option B- Psoas minor: - Inserted into the illiopectinal eminence - Innervated by the anterior rami of L1, 2 - Flexes the thigh Option C- Obturator internus: - Inserted into greater trochanter of the femur - Innervated by the nerve to obturator internus (L5, S1) - Laterally rotates extended thigh and abducts flexed hip Option D- Piriformis: - Inserts superiorly into the tip of the greater trochanter. - Innervated by the branches of anterior rami (S1, S2) - Laterally rotates extended thigh and abducts flexed hip","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1622-1623; Solution Pages 1639-1640","sourcePage":1622,"sourcePageEnd":1623},{"id":"anatomy-42-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which phase of the cell cycle does DNA replication occur in?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"S phase"},{"letter":"B","text":"G1 phase"},{"letter":"C","text":"G2 phase"},{"letter":"D","text":"M phase"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. S phase","explanation":"- DNA replication occurs in S phase - The cell cycle is divided into two basic parts: Mitosis (M phase) and interphase. Interphase: - The phase of cell growth - Includes G1, S, and G2 phases - 95% of the cell cycle is spent in interphase - In S phase (synthesis) - DNA replication occurs - In a total cycle time of 24 hours, the G1 phase might last about 11 hours, the S phase about 8 hours, G2 about 4 hours, and M about 1 hour.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1641; Solution Pages 1646-1647","sourcePage":1641,"sourcePageEnd":1641},{"id":"anatomy-42-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Which of the following is wrongly paired?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mitosis - no crossing over"},{"letter":"B","text":"Meiosis - 4 daughter cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mitosis - 1 division"},{"letter":"D","text":"Meiosis - daughter cells are diploid"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Meiosis - daughter cells are diploid","explanation":"In meiosis, the daughter cells are haploid, not diploid. Mitosis Meiosis Purpose Growth, repair, and replacement of Production of gametes cells Number of Divisions One Two successive divisions (first and second meiotic division) Stages Prophase Metaphase Anaphase First meiotic division and second Telophase meiotic division have- Prophase, Metaphase, Anaphase, and Telophase Prophase of the first meiotic division- Prophase-1 is subdivided into further stages: Leptotene Zygotene Pachytene Diplotene Diakinesis Interphase DNA replication occurs in the No DNA replication in the interphase interphase between 2 mitotic divisions between the first and second meiotic division Resulting Daughter Cells Two daughter cells, genetically Four daughter cells, genetically identical to the parent cell diverse and haploid Chromosome Number Maintains diploid number (46 Reduces chromosome number from chromosomes) diploid (46) to haploid (23) Chromosome Behavior Chromatids separate during Homologous chromosomes separate anaphase in anaphase I Chromatids separate in anaphase II Crossing Over Does not occur Occurs during prophase I (pachytene), leading to genetic variation","sourceRef":"Question Page 1641; Solution Pages 1647-1649","sourcePage":1641,"sourcePageEnd":1641},{"id":"anatomy-42-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":3,"question":"23 bivalents are formed in which stage of prophase 1?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Leptotene"},{"letter":"B","text":"Zygotene"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pachytene"},{"letter":"D","text":"Diplotene"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Zygotene","explanation":"Prophase 1 is the first stage of the first meiotic division Prophase 1 is subdivided into stages as given below: - Leptotene: Chromosomes become visible - Zygotene: 46 chromosomes form 23 pairs = 23 bivalents - Pachytene: The two chromatids of each chromosome become distinct. Thus, a bivalent consists of four chromatids - tetrad (Crossing over occurs where the two central chromatids coil and cross at several points called chiasmata). - The two chromatids of each chromosome become distinct. - Thus, a bivalent consists of four chromatids - tetrad (Crossing over occurs where the two central chromatids coil and cross at several points called chiasmata). - The two chromatids of each chromosome become distinct. - Thus, a bivalent consists of four chromatids - tetrad (Crossing over occurs where the two central chromatids coil and cross at several points called chiasmata). - Diplotene: The two chromosomes of the bivalent begin to separate. Chromatids involved in crossing over break at the chiasmata, and the \"loose\" pieces attach to the opposite chromatid. - The two chromosomes of the bivalent begin to separate. - Chromatids involved in crossing over break at the chiasmata, and the \"loose\" pieces attach to the opposite chromatid. - The two chromosomes of the bivalent begin to separate. - Chromatids involved in crossing over break at the chiasmata, and the \"loose\" pieces attach to the opposite chromatid. - Diakinesis: Final stage of prophase 1 Chromosomes continue to condense, and the nuclear envelope breaks down. Homologous chromosomes are still attached at the chiasmata, which begin to move towards the ends of the chromosomes (terminalization). Spindle fibers start to form in preparation for metaphase I. - Final stage of prophase 1 - Chromosomes continue to condense, and the nuclear envelope breaks down. - Homologous chromosomes are still attached at the chiasmata, which begin to move towards the ends of the chromosomes (terminalization). - Spindle fibers start to form in preparation for metaphase I. - Final stage of prophase 1 - Chromosomes continue to condense, and the nuclear envelope breaks down. - Homologous chromosomes are still attached at the chiasmata, which begin to move towards the ends of the chromosomes (terminalization). - Spindle fibers start to form in preparation for metaphase I.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1641; Solution Pages 1649-1652","sourcePage":1641,"sourcePageEnd":1641},{"id":"anatomy-42-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":4,"question":"In which of the following stages of the first meiotic division do the chromosomes get attached to the spindle fibers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Prophase 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Metaphase 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anaphase 1"},{"letter":"D","text":"Telophase 1"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Metaphase 1","explanation":"Metaphase 1: The second stage of first meiotic division. - Chromosomes get arranged as a metaphase plate in the equator of the cells - midway between the 2 centrioles → Chromosome’s centromeres get attached to microtubules of the spindle via kinetochores. - Kinetochore fibers pull the chromosomes toward the poles, creating tension through the cohesin complex. - The tension indicates that the sister chromatids have achieved proper biorientation. - The cell monitors the attachment of microtubules to chromosomes and possibly the tension generated by the microtubules.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1641-1642; Solution Pages 1652-1654","sourcePage":1641,"sourcePageEnd":1642},{"id":"anatomy-42-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following statements on Anaphase 1 is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Centromere divides into 2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Each chromatid becomes a chromosome"},{"letter":"C","text":"DNA replicates"},{"letter":"D","text":"Each chromosome from the pair migrates to opposite poles of the spindle"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Each chromosome from the pair migrates to opposite poles of the spindle","explanation":"Anaphase 1: The third stage of first meiotic division. - Each chromosome from the pair migrates to opposite poles of the spindle. - No division of centromere.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1642; Solution Pages 1654-1655","sourcePage":1642,"sourcePageEnd":1642},{"id":"anatomy-42-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following events occurs during telophase of meiosis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Chromatids condense into chromosomes."},{"letter":"B","text":"Nuclear envelope disintegrates."},{"letter":"C","text":"Chromatids are pulled towards the spindle poles."},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. None of the above","explanation":"In telophase, chromosomes unwind and return to a less condensed chromatin state. - Telophase is the concluding stage of both mitosis and meiosis, following the movement of chromatids to the spindle poles. - It is essential for ensuring that each daughter cell inherits an identical chromosome set, preserving genetic continuity in cell divisions. - Key events in this phase include: Chromosome Relaxation : The chromatids start to unwind and revert to chromatin, becoming less distinct under a microscope. Nuclear Envelope Reassembly: A new nuclear membrane forms around each set of separated chromatids, resulting in two distinct nuclei. Spindle Breakdown: The spindle fibers, responsible for chromosome movement, are disassembled. Cytokinesis Begins: While telophase focuses on nuclear changes, it often coincides with cytokinesis, where the cytoplasm splits, producing two daughter cells. - Chromosome Relaxation : The chromatids start to unwind and revert to chromatin, becoming less distinct under a microscope. - Nuclear Envelope Reassembly: A new nuclear membrane forms around each set of separated chromatids, resulting in two distinct nuclei. - Spindle Breakdown: The spindle fibers, responsible for chromosome movement, are disassembled. - Cytokinesis Begins: While telophase focuses on nuclear changes, it often coincides with cytokinesis, where the cytoplasm splits, producing two daughter cells. - Chromosome Relaxation : The chromatids start to unwind and revert to chromatin, becoming less distinct under a microscope. - Nuclear Envelope Reassembly: A new nuclear membrane forms around each set of separated chromatids, resulting in two distinct nuclei. - Spindle Breakdown: The spindle fibers, responsible for chromosome movement, are disassembled. - Cytokinesis Begins: While telophase focuses on nuclear changes, it often coincides with cytokinesis, where the cytoplasm splits, producing two daughter cells. Chromatids condense into chromosomes (Option A) : - This event occurs in prophase , not telophase. - During prophase, chromatin condenses to form visible chromosomes. Nuclear envelope disintegrates (Option B) : - This event happens in prophase , where the nuclear membrane breaks down to allow spindle fibers access to the chromosomes. - In telophase, the nuclear envelope re-forms. Chromatids are pulled towards the spindle poles (Option C) : - This occurs in anaphase when the chromatids are separated and moved to opposite ends of the cell by the spindle fibers.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1642; Solution Pages 1655-1656","sourcePage":1642,"sourcePageEnd":1642},{"id":"anatomy-42-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following best describes the stage at which the primary oocyte is arrested during oogenesis before puberty?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Metaphase of meiosis I"},{"letter":"B","text":"Prophase of meiosis II"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anaphase of meiosis I"},{"letter":"D","text":"Prophase of meiosis I"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Prophase of meiosis I","explanation":"The primary oocyte arrests in Prophase of meiosis I during fetal development and remains in this stage until puberty. PGCs → Oogonia (mitotic divisions) → Primary Oocytes (arrest in prophase I) → Primordial Follicles Before Birth: Oogonia peak at 7 million → Degeneration → Remaining oocytes enter prophase I ↓ At Birth: Oocyte maturation inhibitor (OMI) causes arrest in prophase I (diplotene stage) ↓ Puberty : Pool of growing follicles established → 15–20 follicles mature monthly Formation of antral (vesicular) follicles with fluid-filled spaces → Primary Follicle ↓ Follicular Development: Follicular cells → Cuboidal (granulosa cells) → Secrete zona pellucida Theca folliculi forms → Theca interna and externa layers around granulosa cells Antrum enlarges, forming Graafian Follicle (25 mm) ↓ Ovulation: LH surge induces completion of meiosis I Formation of secondary oocyte and polar body Secondary oocyte arrests in metaphase II ↓ Fertilization Yes: Meiosis II completed No: Oocyte degenerates 24 hours post-ovulation","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1642-1643; Solution Pages 1656-1657","sourcePage":1642,"sourcePageEnd":1643},{"id":"anatomy-42-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the role of Sertoli cells during spermatogenesis?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They induce spermatogonial stem cells to initiate spermatogenesis."},{"letter":"B","text":"They stimulate Leydig cells to produce testosterone."},{"letter":"C","text":"They secrete androgen-binding proteins and support germ cells."},{"letter":"D","text":"They transform type A spermatogonia into spermatids."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. They secrete androgen-binding proteins and support germ cells.","explanation":"Sertoli cells play a crucial role in spermatogenesis by secreting androgen-binding proteins supporting, protecting, and nourishing the developing germ cells. Primordial germ cells (PGCs) arrive in the gonad of a genetic male around the end of the fifth week of development. ↓ At Birth Germ cells in sex cords (Surrounded by supporting cells-Sertoli cells) ↓ Puberty Sex cords → Seminiferous tubules PGCs → Spermatogonial stem cells Type A spermatogonia form → Undergo mitosis → Type B spermatogonia ↓ Meiosis I Type B spermatogonia → Primary spermatocytes Primary spermatocytes → Prolonged prophase I (22 days) → Completion of meiosis I → Secondary spermatocytes ↓ Meiosis II Secondary spermatocytes → Meiosis II → Haploid spermatids ↓ Cytoplasmic Bridges Successive germ cells connected by cytoplasmic bridges Spermatogonia and spermatids remain embedded in Sertoli cells ↓ Hormonal Regulation LH → Leydig cells → Testosterone → Sertoli cells → Promotes spermatogenesis FSH → Sertoli cells → Testicular fluid and androgen receptor protein synthesis ↓ Spermiogenesis Spermatids → Spermatozoa Acrosome formation Nuclear condensation Formation of neck, middle piece, and tail Cytoplasm shedding (phagocytized by Sertoli cells) ↓ Final Maturation Spermatozoa → Lumen of seminiferous tubules → Epididymis (gain full motility)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1643; Solution Pages 1657-1658","sourcePage":1643,"sourcePageEnd":1643},{"id":"anatomy-42-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following statements is most accurate regarding the relationship between the luteinizing hormone (LH) surge and ovulation? A) The LH surge typically begins 24 hours before ovulation. B) The LH surge stimulates the release of the second polar body from the oocyte. C) The LH surge is initiated by a rise in progesterone levels from the corpus luteum. D) The peak of the LH surge occurs approximately 10-12 hours before ovulation. E) A smaller follicle size is always associated with a higher LH peak.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"D only"},{"letter":"B","text":"Only E"},{"letter":"C","text":"Both D and E"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. D only","explanation":"- The luteinizing hormone (LH) surge plays a crucial role in the menstrual cycle, preceding ovulation. - The LH surge is essential for triggering ovulation, with its peak closely preceding the release of the egg. - The surge is triggered by a significant rise in estradiol , produced by the dominant follicle, typically larger than 15mm on ultrasound. (Statement C) - While the LH surge happens about 36 hours before ovulation, the LH surge peaks 10-12 hours before ovulation , making it a useful marker for timing ovulation (Statement A) - It stimulates luteinization of granulosa cells , promotes progesterone production, and prompts the oocyte to resume meiosis, leading to the release of the first polar body. (Statement B) - Surges with multiple peaks are linked to smaller follicle sizes before rupture and lower LH levels on the day of ovulation. (Statement E)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1643; Solution Pages 1658-1659","sourcePage":1643,"sourcePageEnd":1643},{"id":"anatomy-42-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is most accurate regarding in vitro fertilization (IVF) for women with bilateral tubal blockage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"IVF is ineffective for women with bilateral tubal blockage."},{"letter":"B","text":"Bilateral salpingectomy is always required before IVF in cases of tubal blockage."},{"letter":"C","text":"ICSI is used to fertilize the egg in all IVF cases, regardless of sperm quality."},{"letter":"D","text":"Preimplantation genetic testing (PGT) is necessary for women of advanced maternal age."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Preimplantation genetic testing (PGT) is necessary for women of advanced maternal age.","explanation":"maternal age. IVF provides a solution for women with bilateral tubal blockage, a condition in which both fallopian tubes are blocked, preventing natural conception. - Mechanism : IVF bypasses the fallopian tubes by directly retrieving eggs from the ovaries, fertilizing them in a lab, and transferring the embryos into the uterus. - Surgical Considerations : Women with severe tubal damage may undergo bilateral salpingectomy (removal of both fallopian tubes) to improve IVF success rates by avoiding complications from dysfunctional tubes. (Option B ruled out) - Success Rates: IVF success in cases of bilateral tubal blockage depends on factors like age, overall reproductive health, and egg/embryo quality. Despite these variables, IVF remains a highly effective treatment. (Option A ruled out) - Despite these variables, IVF remains a highly effective treatment. (Option A ruled out) - Despite these variables, IVF remains a highly effective treatment. (Option A ruled out) - The main steps of IVF include: - Most often done using injectable gonadotropins. - After about 36 hours from a trigger shot or HCG injection, egg retrieval is performed using transvaginal ultrasound-guided aspiration. - Retrieved eggs are placed in a special medium, and normal sperm is added for insemination. - If sperm abnormalities are present, intracytoplasmic sperm injection (ICSI) is used, where a single sperm is injected directly into the egg's cytoplasm. (Option C ruled out) - After fertilization, embryos are assessed and graded. - Transfer usually occurs on Day 3 or Day 5. - It’s a procedure used to detect genetic mutations, chromosomal translocations, or aneuploidy (abnormal number of chromosomes). - It’s especially useful in cases of advanced maternal age, recurrent IVF failures, recurrent pregnancy loss, or unexplained infertility. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1643-1644; Solution Pages 1659-1660","sourcePage":1643,"sourcePageEnd":1644},{"id":"anatomy-42-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":11,"question":"What is the correct sequence of stem cell potency based on their ability to differentiate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pluripotent > Totipotent > Multipotent > Oligopotent > Unipotent"},{"letter":"B","text":"Totipotent > Pluripotent > Multipotent > Oligopotent > Unipotent"},{"letter":"C","text":"Totipotent > Pluripotent > Oligopotent > Multipotent > Unipotent"},{"letter":"D","text":"Totipotent > Pluripotent > Oligopotent > Multipotent > Unipotent"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Totipotent > Pluripotent > Multipotent > Oligopotent > Unipotent","explanation":"The correct order of arrangement of stem cells based on their potency to differentiate into different cells from (highest to lowest) is Totipotent > Pluripotent > Multipotent > Oligopotent > Unipotent","sourceRef":"Question Page 1644; Solution Page 1660","sourcePage":1644,"sourcePageEnd":1644},{"id":"anatomy-42-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which stage of spermatogenesis is characterised by an independent assortment of maternal and paternal chromosomes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spermatogonia to primary spermatocyte"},{"letter":"B","text":"Primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte"},{"letter":"C","text":"Secondary spermatocyte to spermatid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spermatid to spermatozoa"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte","explanation":"- Independent assortment is the random distribution of chromosomes - It occurs at Meiosis 1 where there is division from primary spermatocyte to secondary spermatocyte. - It states that genes located on different chromosomes are inherited independently of each other. In other words, the allele a parent passes on for one trait does not affect the allele passed on for another trait. - During Metaphase I of meiosis, the homologous chromosomes line up in pairs at the cell's equator. The orientation of each chromosome pair is random, meaning the maternal or paternal chromosome can go into either of the two daughter cells.This random alignment is the basis of independent assortment . - Independent assortment is a key factor in genetic variation. This prevents all chromosomes from the same source ending up in the same gamete.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1644; Solution Pages 1660-1662","sourcePage":1644,"sourcePageEnd":1644},{"id":"anatomy-42-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":13,"question":"What is the part of the embryo that exerts morphogenetic stimulus on its adjacent part called?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Differentiator"},{"letter":"B","text":"Organiser"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stem cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"Inducer"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Organiser","explanation":"- Any part of the embryo that exerts a morphogenetic stimulus on adjacent part is known as the organiser. - In morphogenetic development, organizers can be classified into different levels depending on their roles in inducing specific developmental patterns in the embryo. - First level is the organiser that induces the development of new tissues. - This newly developed tissue can then induce another tissue to become secondary organisers. - Therefore , there is a sequence or chain of organiser called primary , secondary and tertiary organisers. Examples of three level of organisers:","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1644-1645; Solution Pages 1662-1663","sourcePage":1644,"sourcePageEnd":1645},{"id":"anatomy-42-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":14,"question":"During fertilisation , all of the following barriers in the ovum are penetrated by the sperm except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Corona radiata"},{"letter":"B","text":"Zona pellucida"},{"letter":"C","text":"Oocyte cell membrane"},{"letter":"D","text":"Nuclear membrane"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Nuclear membrane","explanation":"- The sperm does not penetrate the nuclear membrane of oocyte - Sperm must overcome three barriers to fertilize an egg: - After the fusion of sperm and oocyte cell membranes , the nucleus of ovum is converted to the female pronucleus. - The nucleus of sperm become male pronucleus.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1645; Solution Page 1663","sourcePage":1645,"sourcePageEnd":1645},{"id":"anatomy-42-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-42","chapter":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topic":"Lesson 42 - Gametogenesis and IVF","topicIndex":42,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following is not an acrosomal enzymes?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acrosin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Hyaluronidase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Acid phosphatase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Alkaline phosphatase"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Alkaline phosphatase","explanation":"- Alkaline phosphatase is not an acrosomal enzyme. - The three main acrosomal enzymes are: acrosin , hyaluronidase and acid phosphatase. - The sperm binds to the zona pellucida through the glycoprotein ligands ZP3 and ZP2(zona proteins). - The binding causes the release of the acrosomal enzymes that allow the sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida. - This is called an acrosomal reaction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1645; Solution Pages 1663-1664","sourcePage":1645,"sourcePageEnd":1645},{"id":"anatomy-43-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which day during Week 1 of embryonic development matches with the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Day 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Day 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Day 4"},{"letter":"D","text":"Day 5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Day 4","explanation":"- The first week of development begins with fertilization and ends with the initial stages of implantation. - The image given shows Morula formation where the zygote becomes a solid ball of 16-32 cells. Cleavage: Once the zygote has reached the two-cell stage, it undergoes a series of mitotic divisions, which become smaller with each cleavage division known as blastomeres. DAY EVENT Day 0 Fertilization: The sperm fertilizes the egg in the fallopian tube, forming a zygote (single diploid cell) Day 1 Two cell stage Day 2 Four cell stage Day 3 Twelve cell stage Day 4 Morula Formation: The zygote becomes a solid ball of 16-32 cells. Day 5 Blastocyst Formation: The morula continues to divide and develops a fluid-filled cavity (blastocoel), which consists of the outer trophoblast (future placenta) and the inner cell mass (future embryo). Day 6&7 Implantation begins: Blastocyst attaches to the endometrial lining of the uterus and trophoblast begins to invade the uterine wall to start implantation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1665; Solution Pages 1670-1671","sourcePage":1665,"sourcePageEnd":1665},{"id":"anatomy-43-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":2,"question":"How many cells are typically present in the morula stage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"4-8 cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"16-32 cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"64-128 cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"2 cells"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 16-32 cells","explanation":"Morula - Early stage in embryo development that occurs around day 3 or day 4 after fertilization. - Formed by cleavage of compacted blastomeres. - By the time the morula forms, the embryo typically consists of 16-32 blastomeres. - The cells in the morula are tightly packed, forming a compact structure that resembles a mulberry. - Even though the number of cells increases, the overall size of the morula remains the same as the zygote, because cleavage divisions occur without increasing the embryo's volume. - The group of cells present in the centre of morula gives rise to the inner cell mass and embryo proper and cells at the periphery (outer cell mass) are critical in the cavitation of the morula as it transitions into a blastocyst.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1665-1666; Solution Pages 1671-1672","sourcePage":1665,"sourcePageEnd":1666},{"id":"anatomy-43-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":3,"question":"What is the function of cortical reaction in fertilization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Facilitate the entry of additional sperm into the oocyte."},{"letter":"B","text":"Prevent polyspermy by modifying the egg's outer membrane."},{"letter":"C","text":"Help the sperm to penetrate the zona pellucida."},{"letter":"D","text":"Release digestive enzymes that help break down the oocyte's nucleus."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Prevent polyspermy by modifying the egg's outer membrane.","explanation":"Process Mechanism Purpose Acrosome Reaction The acrosome (cap-like structure on Allows sperm to penetrate the zona the sperm head) releases hydrolytic pellucida and fertilize the oocyte. enzymes (e.g., hyaluronidase, acrosin). These enzymes digest the zona pellucida , allowing the sperm to penetrate and reach the oocyte’s plasma membrane. Cortical Reaction The fusion of the sperm with the egg Prevents polyspermy (entry of more membrane triggers the release of than one sperm), ensuring viable cortical granules (vesicles) into the embryo development. perivitelline space. The contents of these granules alter the zona pellucida and egg membrane, preventing additional sperm from binding and penetrating. Zona Reaction Occurs due to the cortical reaction. Prevents further sperm from binding The enzymes released modify the or penetrating the oocyte, ensuring structure of the zona pellucida, only one fertilizes it. making it impenetrable to other sperm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1666; Solution Pages 1672-1673","sourcePage":1666,"sourcePageEnd":1666},{"id":"anatomy-43-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":4,"question":"During which phase of the menstrual cycle does implantation typically occur?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Menstrual phase"},{"letter":"B","text":"Follicular phase"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ovulatory phase"},{"letter":"D","text":"Luteal phase"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Luteal phase","explanation":"Implantation is a process by which a fertilized egg, or blastocyst, attaches to and embeds itself into the lining of the endometrium. - Implantation is the result of mutual trophoblastic and endometrial action. - Timing : Begins about 2-4 days after the morula enters the uterine cavity (morula is formed 3-4 days after fertilisation). - Blastocyst Stage: The fertilized egg develops into a blastocyst, which is a structure with an inner cell mass, a trophoblast layer, and a blastocoel (fluid-filled cavity). - Trophoblast Cells: The outer layer of the blastocyst, called the trophoblast, plays a key role in implantation. These cells adhere to and invade the uterine lining (endometrium). - These cells adhere to and invade the uterine lining (endometrium). - Uterine Lining: Implantation occurs in the endometrium, which is the thickened lining of the uterus, during the luteal phase of the menstrual cycle. - Process : The blastocyst attaches to the endometrial lining and begins to burrow into it. Trophoblast cells secrete enzymes that help in embedding the blastocyst and establish a connection with the maternal blood supply. - The blastocyst attaches to the endometrial lining and begins to burrow into it. - Trophoblast cells secrete enzymes that help in embedding the blastocyst and establish a connection with the maternal blood supply. - Hormonal Changes: The implantation process triggers the release of hormones, such as human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG), which helps maintain the corpus luteum and prevents menstruation. - These cells adhere to and invade the uterine lining (endometrium). - The blastocyst attaches to the endometrial lining and begins to burrow into it. - Trophoblast cells secrete enzymes that help in embedding the blastocyst and establish a connection with the maternal blood supply.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1666; Solution Pages 1673-1674","sourcePage":1666,"sourcePageEnd":1666},{"id":"anatomy-43-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which substance helps in the attachment of the blastocyst to the uterus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L-selectin"},{"letter":"B","text":"Integrin"},{"letter":"C","text":"E-cadherin"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibronectin"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. L-selectin","explanation":"Blastocyst: - About the time the morula enters the uterine cavity, fluid begins to penetrate through the zona pellucida into the intercellular spaces of the inner cell mass and finally, a single cavity blastocoele formed with blastocyst. - Occurs approximately 5 days after fertilization. - Inner cell mass/Embryoblast: This will develop into the embryo and the associated extra-embryonic tissues. - Outer cell mass/Trophoblast: Forms part of the placenta and facilitate implantation into the uterine wall. - Blastocoel: A fluid-filled cavity within the blastocyst that helps the embryo to expand and implant. - L-Selectin on trophoblast cells and its carbohydrate receptors on the uterine epithelium mediate the initial attachment of the blastocyst to the uterus. - Implantation is the result of mutual trophoblastic and endometrial action. - The embryoblast differentiates into epiblast cells dorsally and hypoblast cells ventrally . - Some of the hypoblast are specified to form the anterior visceral endoderm (AVE) and become the cranial end of the embryo.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1666; Solution Pages 1674-1676","sourcePage":1666,"sourcePageEnd":1666},{"id":"anatomy-43-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":6,"question":"What is the most characteristic event of the 3rd week of development?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Formation of blastocyst"},{"letter":"B","text":"Gastrulation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Implantation"},{"letter":"D","text":"Formation of morula"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Gastrulation","explanation":"Gastrulation: - The most characteristic event occurring during the third week of gestation. - The Process of the establishment of all three germ layers (ectoderm, endoderm, mesoderm) from one dimensional layer of epithelial cells, a blastula. - Begins with formation of primitive streak on the surface of epiblast and becomes clearly visible as a narrow groove with slightly bulging regions in 15-16 day embryo. - The cephalic end of the streak is called the primitive node consisting of a slightly elevated area surrounding the small primitive pit. - The migration of the cells of epiblast towards the primitive streak is called invagination . - Cell migration and specification are controlled by fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8) which is synthesized by streak cells. - FGF8 controls cell specification into the mesoderm by regulating BRACHYURY (T) expression. - After invagination of cells, some displace the hypoblast creating the embryonic endoderm while others come to lie between the epiblast, a newly created endoderm to form mesoderm. - Cells remaining in the epiblast form an ectoderm. - The epiblast through the process of gastrulation is the source of all the germ layers. Formation of blastocyst (Option A): The blastocyst forms around the 5th day, much earlier than the 3rd week. Implantation (Option C): Happens around the end of the first week or beginning of the second week. Formation of morula (Option D): The morula is formed on day 3 or 4 after fertilization, before the blastocyst stage, so it’s much earlier than the 3rd week.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1666-1667; Solution Pages 1676-1677","sourcePage":1666,"sourcePageEnd":1667},{"id":"anatomy-43-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What structure forms at the surface of the epiblast to initiate gastrulation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2"},{"letter":"B","text":"3"},{"letter":"C","text":"4"},{"letter":"D","text":"1"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 4","explanation":"Primitive streak/groove (structure-4) forms at the surface of epiblast to initiate gastrulation. Markings in image: 1 - Prechordal plate 2 - Primitive pit 3 - Primitive node 4 - Primitive streak/ groove Gastrulation: Description Gastrulation, occurring in the third week of gestation, establishes the three germ layers: ectoderm, mesoderm, and endoderm. Structures and migration Formation of the primitive streak on the surface of the epiblast . (Option C) Initially vague, it becomes a distinct groove by days 15-16. The primitive node forms at the cephalic end of epiblast, slightly elevated with a small surrounding primitive pit. Epiblast cells migrate towards the primitive streak, become flask-shaped, detach, and move inward (invagination). Regulation Fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8) regulates cell migration Mesoderm specification by downregulating E-cadherin and affecting BRACHYURY (T) expression. Formation of Germ Layers Invaginated cells displace the hypoblast to form the endoderm, some lie between epiblast and endoderm to form mesoderm, and remaining epiblast cells form ectoderm. Prechordal Plate Forms between the notochord tip and oropharyngeal membrane from the first migrating cells, crucial for forebrain induction. Oropharyngeal Membrane At the cranial end, it is a region of tightly adhered ectoderm and endoderm cells that will become the oral cavity opening.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1667; Solution Pages 1677-1679","sourcePage":1667,"sourcePageEnd":1667},{"id":"anatomy-43-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A stillborn baby was noted to have the following findings. The above pathology is derived from which among the following structure shown in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2","explanation":"The pathology shown is Oropharyngeal teratoma , arises from Primordial germ cells that fail to migrate to the gonadal ridge and Primordial germ cells (PGC) in the image corresponds to “2”. Markings in the image are: 1 - Allantois 2 - Primordial Germ cells (Option B) 3 - Yolk sac 4 - Heart Primordial Germ Cells (PGC): Origin and Migration of Primordial Germ Cells (PGC) 2nd week: PGC originate in the epiblast and migrate through the primitive streak. 3rd week: They are located among endoderm cells in the wall of the yolk sac, close to the allantois. 4th week: The PGC migrate by ameboid movement along the dorsal mesentery of the hindgut. 5th week: They arrive at the primitive gonads. 6th week: Invade the genital ridges. Gonads Shortly before and during the arrival of primordial germ cells, the epithelium of the genital ridge starts to proliferate. Epithelial cells penetrate the underlying mesenchyme, forming primitive sex cords, which are irregularly shaped. In both male and female embryos, the primitive sex cords are connected to the surface epithelium. At this stage, the gonad is called the indifferent gonad, as there is no distinction between male and female gonads yet. Pathologies associated: due to failure of migration of PGC Teratoma (oropharyngeal/sacrococcygeal) Non development of gonads","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1667-1669; Solution Pages 1679-1681","sourcePage":1667,"sourcePageEnd":1669},{"id":"anatomy-43-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A patient with a history of 6 weeks of amenorrhea and a positive urinary pregnancy test underwent an ultrasonography, which showed the following The marked structure in the image communicates via a duct with which of the following parts of the gastrointestinal system? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Foregut"},{"letter":"B","text":"Midgut"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hindgut"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cloacal membrane"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Midgut","explanation":"- The marked structure in the image is the Yolk sac. - The Midgut of the embryo communicates with the yolk sac via a stalk called the Vitelline duct. Yolk sac: Description A small membranous structure outside the embryo. The yolk sac connects to the developing embryo at midgut via the vitelline duct. (Option B) The primitive yolk sac is lined by Hypoblast cells, which is derived from Endoderm (forms the first lining). Functions Primitive hematopoiesis Germ cell production. Nutritive role (before blood vessels form) Development The primary yolk sac forms from proliferating hypoblast cells after implantation, while the epiblast forms the amnion . Both structures develop between days 8-14 of embryogenesis. Hypoblast cells are continuous with the exocoelomic membrane. By the end of the second week, extraembryonic mesoderm fills the space between the trophoblast, amnion, and exocoelomic membrane. As vacuoles form within this tissue, the extraembryonic coelom or chorionic cavity develops. The extraembryonic mesoderm lining the cytotrophoblast and amnion is called extraembryonic somatic mesoderm , while the lining around the yolk sac is the extraembryonic splanchnic mesoderm . The primary yolk sac enlarges, part of it regresses, and the remaining portion forms the secondary yolk sac. Portions of the secondary yolk sac not taken up inside the embryo forms the tertiary yolk sac. Visualization Timeline Although the yolk sac forms in the second week, it is visible on ultrasound by 5 weeks post-fertilization (or 7 gestational weeks). Growth is linear between 5-10 weeks post-fertilization. Not visible after the first trimester. Foregut (Option A): - The foregut is initially bounded by the oropharyngeal membrane, which separates the stomodeum (primitive oral cavity) from the pharynx. - The membrane ruptures in the 4th week, establishing an open connection between the oral cavity and primitive gut. Hindgut (Option C) and Cloacal membrane (Option D): - The hindgut terminates at the cloacal membrane , which separates the upper anal canal (endodermal) from the lower proctodeum (ectodermal) . - The membrane breaks down in the 7th week to create the anal opening. - Cephalocaudal growth and lateral folding incorporate part of the allantois into the embryo, forming the cloaca.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1669; Solution Pages 1681-1683","sourcePage":1669,"sourcePageEnd":1669},{"id":"anatomy-43-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-43","chapter":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topic":"Lesson 43 - Development Period","topicIndex":43,"questionNumber":10,"question":"The floor of the amniotic cavity is formed by which among the following cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trophoblast cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endodermal cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ectodermal cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Mesodermal cells"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ectodermal cells","explanation":"Amnion: - It is the fetal membrane that covers the embryo and forms the amniotic sac that is filled with amniotic fluid. It appears in the 2nd week of development. - Formation and expansion of amniotic cavity: The amniotic cavity is lined by extension of cells of ectoderm from the inner cell mass and amniogenic cells. - The roof, lateral wall and its floor is formed by ectodermal cells. (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1669; Solution Pages 1683-1684","sourcePage":1669,"sourcePageEnd":1669},{"id":"anatomy-44-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 10-week-old fetus is found to have a developmental anomaly affecting the iris muscles and the posterior pituitary. Which of the following is directly involved in the formation of these structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Neural crest cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Surface ectoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Neuroectoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Epiblast cells"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Neuroectoderm","explanation":"- Neural plate ectoderm derivatives include various structures of the central nervous system and associated tissues, specifically: Iris muscles (Dilator pupillae and sphincter pupillae) are derived from the neuroectoderm. Neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) is also derived from the neuroectoderm, specifically from an extension of the diencephalon. Retina, optic nerve, ciliaris Membranous labyrinth - Iris muscles (Dilator pupillae and sphincter pupillae) are derived from the neuroectoderm. - Neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) is also derived from the neuroectoderm, specifically from an extension of the diencephalon. - Retina, optic nerve, ciliaris - Membranous labyrinth - Neural plate is a cohesive structure whose cells are linked together by junctional complexes. - There are structural changes that occur within neuroepithelial cells of the neural plate that contribute to the formation of the neural groove and then the neural tube. - Iris muscles (Dilator pupillae and sphincter pupillae) are derived from the neuroectoderm. - Neurohypophysis (posterior pituitary) is also derived from the neuroectoderm, specifically from an extension of the diencephalon. - Retina, optic nerve, ciliaris - Membranous labyrinth Surface ectoderm Neuroectoderm Neural crest cell Epidermis of the skin Hair Nails Central nervous system (Brain and Sensory ganglia of the spinal nerves( Sweat & Sebaceous glands spinal cord) Peripheral nervous dorsal root ganglia) Sensory ganglia Mammary glands Enamel of the system Retina Sensory epithelia of of the cranial nerves Autonomic teeth Lens of eye Epithelium of nose & ear Pineal gland Posterior ganglia Meninges (Pia mater & sensory organs in the inner ear & lobe of the pituitary gland Arachnoid mater) of the brain & nose Anterior lobe of the pituitary spinal cord Schwann cells gland Melanoblasts of the skin Suprarenal medulla (chromaffin cells) Several skeletal & muscular components in the head (derived from pharyngeal arches) Parafollicular C cells Embryonic tissue Eye tissue Ectoderm Neuroectoderm Retina Pigmented epithelium of iris Retinal pigment epithelium Dilator & sphincter muscles of iris Optic nerve Neural crest cell Corneal stroma & endothelium Trabecular meshwork Ciliary muscles Melanocytes Schwann cells Meningeal sheath of optic nerve Vitreous Surface Ectoderm Conjunctiva Surface epithelium of cornea Lens Lacrimal gland Mesoderm Eye muscles(EOM) Endothelium of all eye structures Neural crest cells (Option A) originate from the edge of the neural tube and contribute to various peripheral structures, including parts of the PNS and some facial bones . Surface ectoderm (Option B) primarily forms the outer skin layers, sweat glands, sebaceous glands, and other structures . Epiblast cells (Option D) are early embryonic cells that give rise to the three germ layers .","sourceRef":"Question Page 1685; Solution Pages 1696-1697","sourcePage":1685,"sourcePageEnd":1685},{"id":"anatomy-44-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":2,"question":"A 3-week-old infant shows signs of a developmental abnormality involving the formation of tongue muscles. Which group of somites is responsible for this condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cervical somites"},{"letter":"B","text":"Occipital somites"},{"letter":"C","text":"Preoccipital somites"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thoracic somites"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Occipital somites","explanation":"Occipital somites contribute to the formation of the muscles of the tongue. Paraxial Mesoderm: - Extends as a longitudinal column on either side of the notochord and the developing neural tube. - Developing otic capsules (neuroectodermal thickenings that form the membranous labyrinth of the internal ear) divide the paraxial mesoderm into preotic and postotic parts. - Preotic Paraxial Mesoderm: Comprises unsegmented head mesoderm, known as somitomeres. - Postotic Paraxial Mesoderm: Forms somites Somites: - Paraxial mesoderm becomes segmented to form 42-44 pairs of somites develop on either side of the neural tube and notochord. - Appear between the 20th and 30th day, marking the 4th week as the somite period. - Somite Structure: Sclerotome: Ventromedial part; forms vertebral column and ribs. Dermatome: Lateral part; forms dermis of skin and subcutaneous tissue. Myotome: Intermediate part; forms striated muscle. - Sclerotome: Ventromedial part; forms vertebral column and ribs. - Dermatome: Lateral part; forms dermis of skin and subcutaneous tissue. - Myotome: Intermediate part; forms striated muscle. - Sclerotome: Ventromedial part; forms vertebral column and ribs. - Dermatome: Lateral part; forms dermis of skin and subcutaneous tissue. - Myotome: Intermediate part; forms striated muscle. - Innervation: Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions: One spinal nerve innervates each myotome (no. of somites = no. of spinal nerves) . Coccygeal region: Somites exceed spinal nerves and many degenerate. - Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions: One spinal nerve innervates each myotome (no. of somites = no. of spinal nerves) . - Coccygeal region: Somites exceed spinal nerves and many degenerate. - Cervical, thoracic, lumbar, and sacral regions: One spinal nerve innervates each myotome (no. of somites = no. of spinal nerves) . - Coccygeal region: Somites exceed spinal nerves and many degenerate. Somites Number of Pairs Skeletal Elements Musculature Preoccipital (Option C) 3 Base of skull Extraocular muscles of eyeball Occipital (Option B) 4-5 Tongue musculature except palatoglossus Cervical (Option A) 8 Vertebra Striated muscles of trunk, diaphragm, limbs Thoracic (Option D) 12 Vertebra and ribs Lumbar 5 Vertebra Sacral 5 Coccygeal 8 - 10","sourceRef":"Question Page 1685; Solution Pages 1697-1700","sourcePage":1685,"sourcePageEnd":1685},{"id":"anatomy-44-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":3,"question":"If the development of the endoderm is negatively affected and fails to normally differentiate, which of the following glands would not be affected by this?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Follicular cells of thyroid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Parenchyma of parathyroid glands"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior pituitary"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pancreas"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Anterior pituitary","explanation":"The pituitary gland develops from the ectoderm, and hence, an improper differentiation of the endoderm would not affect it. Glands developed from ectoderm and endoderm: Ectoderm Endoderm Neural ectoderm: Posterior pituitary Connective tissue of Thymus glands (parenchyma) Parathyroid glands thymus Connective tissue of thyroid and parathyroids (parenchyma) (Option B) Liver Pancreas (Option D) Adrenal medulla Connective tissue of lacrimal, nasal, Follicular cells of thyroid (Option A) C cells of thyroid labial, palatine, oral, and salivary glands Prostate Urethral glands Surface ectoderm: Rathke’s pouch gives rise to the anterior pituitary (Option C) Sweat glands and sebaceous glands Parotid, sublingual, submandibular salivary glands Mammary glands Lacrimal glands","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1685-1686; Solution Pages 1700-1701","sourcePage":1685,"sourcePageEnd":1686},{"id":"anatomy-44-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements about yolk sac development is correct?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary yolk sac is lined by cuboidal cells."},{"letter":"B","text":"Secondary yolk sac is lined by flattened cells."},{"letter":"C","text":"Primary yolk sac forms by the end of the first week"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tertiary yolk sac connects to the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct."}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Tertiary yolk sac connects to the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct.","explanation":"Tertiary yolk sac communicates with the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct. Development of Yolk Sac: - Yolk sac is an endodermal sac lying ventral to the embryonic disc. - Considered a vestigial structure in humans with hardly any nutritive yolk inside it. - Develops from the cavity of blastocyst (blastocele). - 3 stages of development: Primary yolk sac: Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Primary yolk sac: Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) - Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. - Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Primary yolk sac: Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) - Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. - Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Blastocyst cavity (blastocele) becomes the primary yolk sac when it is lined by Heuser’s membrane (flattened cells from endoderm of embryoblast). (Option A) - Heuser's membrane attaches to the undersurface of the embryonic disc. - Formed by the end of the second week. (Option C) - Secondary yolk sac: With the formation of extraembryonic coelom, the primary yolk sac becomes smaller and is termed as secondary yolk sac. Cells lining the yolk sac become cuboidal. (Option B) - With the formation of extraembryonic coelom, the primary yolk sac becomes smaller and is termed as secondary yolk sac. - Cells lining the yolk sac become cuboidal. (Option B) - With the formation of extraembryonic coelom, the primary yolk sac becomes smaller and is termed as secondary yolk sac. - Cells lining the yolk sac become cuboidal. (Option B) - Tertiary (definitive) yolk sac: Remnant of the secondary yolk sac. As the embryo folds, most of the yolk sac is incorporated into the embryo to form the primitive gut. Remaining portion not absorbed into the embryo is termed as tertiary/definitive yolk sac. It communicates with the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct. (Option D) - Remnant of the secondary yolk sac. - As the embryo folds, most of the yolk sac is incorporated into the embryo to form the primitive gut. - Remaining portion not absorbed into the embryo is termed as tertiary/definitive yolk sac. - It communicates with the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct. (Option D) - Remnant of the secondary yolk sac. - As the embryo folds, most of the yolk sac is incorporated into the embryo to form the primitive gut. - Remaining portion not absorbed into the embryo is termed as tertiary/definitive yolk sac. - It communicates with the midgut via the vitellointestinal duct. (Option D) - Functions of Yolk Sac:","sourceRef":"Question Page 1686; Solution Pages 1701-1704","sourcePage":1686,"sourcePageEnd":1686},{"id":"anatomy-44-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following statements about the lamina terminalis is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It forms the anterior wall of the third ventricle."},{"letter":"B","text":"It gives rise to the septum pellucidum."},{"letter":"C","text":"The lamina terminalis is located at the caudal end of the prosencephalon."},{"letter":"D","text":"The first commissural fibers to develop pass through the lamina terminalis."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The lamina terminalis is located at the caudal end of the prosencephalon.","explanation":"- The lamina terminalis is the part of the neural tube wall that closes the cranial end of the prosencephalon . (Option C) - It lies in the anterior wall of the third ventricle after the appearance of the telencephalic vesicles. (Option A) - The lamina terminalis forms the commissural plate , which allows neural fibers to pass between hemispheres. - Commissural fibers : Anterior commissure (first to develop). (Option D) Hippocampal commissure/fornix (second). Corpus callosum (largest, forms later and extends backward, roofing over the diencephalon). The septum pellucidum persists from the portion of the lamina terminalis between the corpus callosum and fornix. (Option B) Other commissures include the optic chiasma, habenular commissure, and posterior commissure. - Anterior commissure (first to develop). (Option D) - Hippocampal commissure/fornix (second). - Corpus callosum (largest, forms later and extends backward, roofing over the diencephalon). - The septum pellucidum persists from the portion of the lamina terminalis between the corpus callosum and fornix. (Option B) - Other commissures include the optic chiasma, habenular commissure, and posterior commissure. - Anterior commissure (first to develop). (Option D) - Hippocampal commissure/fornix (second). - Corpus callosum (largest, forms later and extends backward, roofing over the diencephalon). - The septum pellucidum persists from the portion of the lamina terminalis between the corpus callosum and fornix. (Option B) - Other commissures include the optic chiasma, habenular commissure, and posterior commissure.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1686; Solution Pages 1704-1705","sourcePage":1686,"sourcePageEnd":1686},{"id":"anatomy-44-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":6,"question":"The cecal bed arises from which part of the embryonic gut?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Post-arterial segment of Midgut"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pre-arterial segment of Midgut"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pre-allantoic part of Hindgut"},{"letter":"D","text":"Post-allantoic part of Hindgut"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Post-arterial segment of Midgut","explanation":"- The postarterial segment of the midgut loop gives off a cecal bud. - The cecum and appendix are formed by enlargement of this bud. Derivatives of Midgut: - Prearterial segment: Duodenum: Descending (second) part distal to the major papilla; horizontal (third) and ascending (fourth) parts Jejunum Ileum except the terminal part. - Duodenum: Descending (second) part distal to the major papilla; horizontal (third) and ascending (fourth) parts - Jejunum - Ileum except the terminal part. - Postarterial segment: Terminal ileum Cecum and appendix Ascending colon Right two-thirds of the transverse colon. - Terminal ileum - Cecum and appendix - Ascending colon - Right two-thirds of the transverse colon. - Duodenum: Descending (second) part distal to the major papilla; horizontal (third) and ascending (fourth) parts - Jejunum - Ileum except the terminal part. - Terminal ileum - Cecum and appendix - Ascending colon - Right two-thirds of the transverse colon.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1686; Solution Pages 1705-1706","sourcePage":1686,"sourcePageEnd":1686},{"id":"anatomy-44-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":7,"question":"The stomatodeum is derived from which of the following?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ectoderm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Neural crest cells"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ectoderm","explanation":"- The stomatodeum (future mouth) is a depression bounded cranially by a bulging produced by the brain, and caudally by a bulging produced by the pericardial cavity. - It is derived from the ectoderm . - It forms the oral epithelium , including the lips, teeth, and part of the palate. Endoderm (Option B) contributes to the internal lining of the gastrointestinal tract but is not responsible for the stomatodeum. Mesoderm (Option C) contributes to structures such as muscles and connective tissues but not to the stomatodeum directly. Neural Crest Cells (Option D) contribute to structures like the face and skull but do not form the stomatodeum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1686-1687; Solution Pages 1706-1707","sourcePage":1686,"sourcePageEnd":1687},{"id":"anatomy-44-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":8,"question":"What is the correct sequence of events in the maturation of the lungs?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Canalicular phase → Pseudoglandular phase → Alveolar phase → Saccular phase."},{"letter":"B","text":"Pseudoglandular phase → Canalicular phase → Saccular phase → Alveolar phase."},{"letter":"C","text":"Saccular phase → Alveolar phase → Pseudoglandular phase → Canalicular phase."},{"letter":"D","text":"Alveolar phase → Canalicular phase → Saccular phase → Pseudoglandular phase."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pseudoglandular phase → Canalicular phase → Saccular phase → Alveolar phase.","explanation":"phase. The development of the lungs follows a well-defined sequence: - Pseudoglandular phase (5–16 weeks): Formation of terminal bronchioles. - Canalicular phase (16–26 weeks): Formation of respiratory bronchioles and capillary networks. - Saccular phase (26–36 weeks): Formation of primitive alveoli and and capillaries establish close contact. - Alveolar phase (36 weeks–postnatal): Formation of mature alveoli and blood-air barrier. Premature Birth and RDS: - The onset of surfactant production in the canalicular phase and its increase during the saccular phase is essential for preventing alveolar collapse. - Premature infants born before 34 weeks are at significant risk for Respiratory Distress Syndrome (RDS) due to insufficient surfactant production and immature alveolar development. - Treatment includes surfactant replacement therapy and supportive ventilation .","sourceRef":"Question Page 1687; Solution Pages 1707-1708","sourcePage":1687,"sourcePageEnd":1687},{"id":"anatomy-44-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following structures is derived from the endodermal lining of the laryngotracheal tube?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cartilaginous framework of the trachea"},{"letter":"B","text":"Alveolar epithelial cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Muscular components of the respiratory tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parietal pleura covering the lungs"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Alveolar epithelial cells","explanation":"The alveolar epithelial cells (Type I and Type II) are derived from the endoderm of the laryngotracheal tube. Derivatives of the Laryngotracheal Tube: - The laryngotracheal tube develops from the foregut and gives rise to critical structures in the respiratory system. - Endodermal Derivatives : Endoderm : Forms the lining of the larynx , trachea , bronchi , and alveoli , including the Type I and Type II alveolar cells . - Endoderm : Forms the lining of the larynx , trachea , bronchi , and alveoli , including the Type I and Type II alveolar cells . - Mesodermal Derivatives : Splanchnic Mesoderm : Forms the cartilage , smooth muscle , and visceral pleura of the respiratory tract. Somatic Mesoderm : Forms the parietal pleura lining the thoracic cavity. - Splanchnic Mesoderm : Forms the cartilage , smooth muscle , and visceral pleura of the respiratory tract. - Somatic Mesoderm : Forms the parietal pleura lining the thoracic cavity. - Endoderm : Forms the lining of the larynx , trachea , bronchi , and alveoli , including the Type I and Type II alveolar cells . - Splanchnic Mesoderm : Forms the cartilage , smooth muscle , and visceral pleura of the respiratory tract. - Somatic Mesoderm : Forms the parietal pleura lining the thoracic cavity. Developmental Stages: - 4th week : The lung bud appears as an outgrowth from the foregut. - 4-5th weeks : The tracheoesophageal septum forms, separating the foregut into the trachea and esophagus . - Subsequent weeks : Primary, secondary, and tertiary bronchi form, leading to the adult bronchopulmonary segments.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1687; Solution Pages 1708-1709","sourcePage":1687,"sourcePageEnd":1687},{"id":"anatomy-44-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is the first sign of respiratory development?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Formation of primary bronchi"},{"letter":"B","text":"Appearance of the lung bud"},{"letter":"C","text":"Development of type I alveolar cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Formation of the tracheoesophageal septum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Appearance of the lung bud","explanation":"- The first sign of respiratory development is the appearance of the lung bud (also called the respiratory diverticulum ) - It appears as an outgrowth from the ventral wall of the foregut around the 4th week of embryonic development. - Development depends on retinoic acid (RA) produced by adjacent mesoderm, which upregulates the transcription factor TBX4 . - This marks the initiation of lung formation and the beginning of the respiratory system's development. Formation of primary bronchi (Option A) occurs later in development, around the 5th week . Development of type I alveolar cells (Option C) : Type I alveolar cells, which are involved in the formation of the blood-air barrier, begin to form later during the canalicular phase (16–26 weeks). Formation of the tracheoesophageal septum (Option D) occurs shortly after the appearance of the lung bud and serves to separate the respiratory tract from the esophagus. This process occurs during the 4th to 5th week .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1687-1688; Solution Pages 1709-1710","sourcePage":1687,"sourcePageEnd":1688},{"id":"anatomy-44-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following embryological structures contributes to the development of the post-renal segment of the inferior vena cava?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Right vitelline vein"},{"letter":"B","text":"Right subcardinal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Right supracardinal vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Right posterior cardinal vein"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Right supracardinal vein","explanation":"The Post-renal (Infra-renal) segment of the Inferior vena cava (IVC) develops primarily from the right supracardinal vein. Formation of IVC: - The beginning of the IVC is called the Posterior cardinal vein. - Posterior cardinal vein contributes to Sacro cardinal vein. - Kidneys drain into the inferior vena cava with the help of renal veins. - Right supracardinal vein contributes the Post-renal (infra-renal) segment of the IVC. - Right subcardinal vein contributes the Renal and Pre-renal (suprarenal) segment of IVC. - Terminal portion of the IVC is contributed by the right sided vitelline veins. - The right sided vitelline veins will contribute to the right Hepatiocardiac channel. - All this happen in the right side whereas Left sided veins will disappear. - There is Anastomosis between: Supracardinal and Subcardinal vein Subcardinal and hepatocardiac channel - Supracardinal and Subcardinal vein - Subcardinal and hepatocardiac channel - Supracardinal and Subcardinal vein - Subcardinal and hepatocardiac channel","sourceRef":"Question Page 1688; Solution Pages 1710-1711","sourcePage":1688,"sourcePageEnd":1688},{"id":"anatomy-44-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 25-year-old male is found to have a persistent left-sided superior vena cava (SVC) during routine imaging. What is the most likely cause of this anomaly?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Failure of right-sided SVC to develop"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abnormal development of the left anterior cardinal vein"},{"letter":"C","text":"Persistence of the left umbilical vein"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abnormal development of the right posterior cardinal vein"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Abnormal development of the left anterior cardinal vein","explanation":"A persistent left-sided SVC arises from the left anterior cardinal vein , which typically regresses during fetal development. - Normal Development : The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . The left anterior cardinal vein regresses, with its remnant forming the left brachiocephalic vein , which connects the left and right venous systems. - The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . - The left anterior cardinal vein regresses, with its remnant forming the left brachiocephalic vein , which connects the left and right venous systems. - The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . - The left anterior cardinal vein regresses, with its remnant forming the left brachiocephalic vein , which connects the left and right venous systems. - Persistent Left-Sided SVC : Occurs due to the failure of the left anterior cardinal vein to regress . This results in the formation of a left-sided SVC , which drains into the right atrium through the coronary sinus . The right SVC typically develops normally. - Occurs due to the failure of the left anterior cardinal vein to regress . - This results in the formation of a left-sided SVC , which drains into the right atrium through the coronary sinus . - The right SVC typically develops normally. - Occurs due to the failure of the left anterior cardinal vein to regress . - This results in the formation of a left-sided SVC , which drains into the right atrium through the coronary sinus . - The right SVC typically develops normally. - Double SVC : If the left brachiocephalic vein fails to form , the left anterior cardinal vein persists as a separate venous channel. In this condition: The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . - If the left brachiocephalic vein fails to form , the left anterior cardinal vein persists as a separate venous channel. - In this condition: The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . - The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . - The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . - If the left brachiocephalic vein fails to form , the left anterior cardinal vein persists as a separate venous channel. - In this condition: The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . - The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . - The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . - The right anterior cardinal vein forms the right SVC . - The left anterior cardinal vein persists as the left SVC . A . Left superior vena cava draining into the right atrium by way of the coronary sinus B . Double superior vena cava. The communicating [brachiocephalic] vein between the two anterior cardinals has failed to develop","sourceRef":"Question Page 1688; Solution Pages 1711-1713","sourcePage":1688,"sourcePageEnd":1688},{"id":"anatomy-44-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The structure “b” fuses with structure “c” during the 3rd month of pregnancy. Identify the structure “c”.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Decidua capsularis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Decidua basalis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Myometrium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Decidua parietalis"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Decidua parietalis","explanation":"The structure “c” is decidua parietalis that fuses with the growing decidua capsularis around the embryo. Three types of decidua: Decidua basalis/Serotina Decidua capsularis/Reflexa Decidua parietalis/Vera This forms the maternal component Separates the embryo from the Lines the rest of the uterine cavity of the placenta The maternal spiral uterine cavity Covers the embryo like arteries grow and dilate to form a capsule sinusoids with blood Contains large decidual cells rich in lipids and glycogen (may be a source of nutrition for the embryo) Firmly attached to the chorion With the progression of the pregnancy, the decidua capsularis grows with the fetus and finally fuses with the parietalis during the 3rd month and obliterates the uterine cavity. The decidua is eventually shed off with the placenta and membranes after delivery.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1688-1689; Solution Pages 1713-1714","sourcePage":1688,"sourcePageEnd":1689},{"id":"anatomy-44-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about villi?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The secondary villi have a central core of cytotrophoblast"},{"letter":"B","text":"The uteroplacental circulation begins only after the formation of tertiary villi"},{"letter":"C","text":"The tertiary villi have a mesoderm core that carries the fetal blood"},{"letter":"D","text":"The primary villi have a core of extraembryonic mesoderm"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The tertiary villi have a mesoderm core that carries the fetal blood","explanation":"- The tertiary villi are formed with the extraembryonic mesoderm core filled with fetal blood, which establishes the fetoplacental circulation. - The chorion is formed by the extraembryonic somatic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast, and syncytiotrophoblast. - The chorionic villi formed from the chorion cover the chorionic sac (extraembryonic coelom) until the beginning of the 8th week. According to the structure of the chorionic villi at different periods of embryonic development they are divided into: Primary villi Secondary villi Tertiary villi A central core of cytotrophoblast Has 3 layers: Outer syncytiotrophobla Similar to secondary villi but have (Option D) covered by a layer of st Intermediate cytotrophoblast Inner fetal blood capillaries in the syncytiotrophoblast. Intervillous extraembryonic mesoderm (Option A) mesoderm space separates the villus from each other. Formation of the villi: - Appearance of lacunae and trabeculae (around the 12th day of gestation): Small cavities appear in the rapidly growing and thick syncytiotrophoblast. These cavities are called lacunae and they increase in size and arrange themselves from irregular to a radial design around the blastocyst. The syncytium separates them from one another, forming partitions called trabeculae, which in time get surrounded by lacunae as they communicate with each other and one large space is formed. - Small cavities appear in the rapidly growing and thick syncytiotrophoblast. - These cavities are called lacunae and they increase in size and arrange themselves from irregular to a radial design around the blastocyst. - The syncytium separates them from one another, forming partitions called trabeculae, which in time get surrounded by lacunae as they communicate with each other and one large space is formed. - Small cavities appear in the rapidly growing and thick syncytiotrophoblast. - These cavities are called lacunae and they increase in size and arrange themselves from irregular to a radial design around the blastocyst. - The syncytium separates them from one another, forming partitions called trabeculae, which in time get surrounded by lacunae as they communicate with each other and one large space is formed. - Erosion of maternal endometrium: The endometrium of the maternal uterus is eroded by the growing syncytiotrophoblasts, which leads to the opening of some blood vessels and the leaking of blood into the lacunar space (uteroplacental circulation). (Option B) - The endometrium of the maternal uterus is eroded by the growing syncytiotrophoblasts, which leads to the opening of some blood vessels and the leaking of blood into the lacunar space (uteroplacental circulation). (Option B) - Formation of chorionic villi: Once the cytotrophoblast grows into the trabeculae, it becomes a two-layered structure with a core of cytotrophoblast covered by a layer of syncytiotrophoblast and surrounded by maternal blood in the lacunar space. The trabeculae have now become the primary villi and the lacunar space, the intervillous space . When the extraembryonic mesoderm invades this primary villus and forms the core, this structure becomes the secondary villi, with the mesoderm core covered by cytotrophoblast and then the syncytiotrophoblast. Once the blood vessels fill the mesoderm, the tertiary villi are formed. The maternal blood runs in the intervillous space, and the fetal blood in the villi. - Once the cytotrophoblast grows into the trabeculae, it becomes a two-layered structure with a core of cytotrophoblast covered by a layer of syncytiotrophoblast and surrounded by maternal blood in the lacunar space. - The trabeculae have now become the primary villi and the lacunar space, the intervillous space . - When the extraembryonic mesoderm invades this primary villus and forms the core, this structure becomes the secondary villi, with the mesoderm core covered by cytotrophoblast and then the syncytiotrophoblast. - Once the blood vessels fill the mesoderm, the tertiary villi are formed. - The maternal blood runs in the intervillous space, and the fetal blood in the villi. - The endometrium of the maternal uterus is eroded by the growing syncytiotrophoblasts, which leads to the opening of some blood vessels and the leaking of blood into the lacunar space (uteroplacental circulation). (Option B) - Once the cytotrophoblast grows into the trabeculae, it becomes a two-layered structure with a core of cytotrophoblast covered by a layer of syncytiotrophoblast and surrounded by maternal blood in the lacunar space. - The trabeculae have now become the primary villi and the lacunar space, the intervillous space . - When the extraembryonic mesoderm invades this primary villus and forms the core, this structure becomes the secondary villi, with the mesoderm core covered by cytotrophoblast and then the syncytiotrophoblast. - Once the blood vessels fill the mesoderm, the tertiary villi are formed. - The maternal blood runs in the intervillous space, and the fetal blood in the villi.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1689; Solution Pages 1714-1718","sourcePage":1689,"sourcePageEnd":1689},{"id":"anatomy-44-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A woman who is Rh-negative is pregnant with a Rh-positive baby and is advised to take anti-D immunoglobulin for safe future pregnancies. Which of the following disappears from the placental membrane, thus thinning it out later in the pregnancy?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Syncytiotrophoblast"},{"letter":"B","text":"Endothelium of fetal capillaries"},{"letter":"C","text":"The basement membrane of fetal capillaries"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cytotrophoblasts"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Cytotrophoblasts","explanation":"The placental membrane or barrier separates the maternal and fetal blood in the intrauterine period but thins out towards the end of pregnancy for many reasons, one of which is the disappearance of the cytotrophoblast. Placenta has 2 parts (maternal and fetal), 2 surfaces (maternal and fetal), 2 types of cotyledons, and a peripheral margin. - Maternal part is formed by the decidua basalis or decidual plate of the endometrium. - Fetal part is from the chorion frondosum or chorionic plate. - Maternal surface is rough and irregular having maternal cotyledons. - Fetal surface is smooth and covered by amnion. - Peripheral margin of the placenta presents the fetal membrane formed from decidua capsularis and pareitalis, chorion laeve and amnion. Placental Membrane: - This separates the fetal blood in the villi from the maternal blood in the intervillous space running side by side but opposite in direction. - The exchange of oxygen, nutrition, and waste products occurs through this membrane. - The following are the structures that form the placental membrane early in pregnancy : Endothelium of maternal blood vessels and its basement membrane Syncytiotrophoblast Cytotrophoblast Extraembryonic mesoderm (EEM) Endothelium of fetal blood vessels and its basement membrane - Endothelium of maternal blood vessels and its basement membrane - Syncytiotrophoblast - Cytotrophoblast - Extraembryonic mesoderm (EEM) - Endothelium of fetal blood vessels and its basement membrane - Structures that form the placental membrane Later in pregnancy: Endothelium of fetal capillaries and their basement membrane (Option B & C) Syncytiotrophoblast (Option A) - Endothelium of fetal capillaries and their basement membrane (Option B & C) - Syncytiotrophoblast (Option A) - The placental membrane thins later in pregnancy due to : Disappearance of cytotrophoblast Thinning of syncytiotrophoblast Edematous stroma Peripheral migration of fetal blood vessels - Disappearance of cytotrophoblast - Thinning of syncytiotrophoblast - Edematous stroma - Peripheral migration of fetal blood vessels - The total area of this membrane varies from 4 meter-squared to 14 meter-squared and is 0.025mm thick at first and later 0.002mm. - Endothelium of maternal blood vessels and its basement membrane - Syncytiotrophoblast - Cytotrophoblast - Extraembryonic mesoderm (EEM) - Endothelium of fetal blood vessels and its basement membrane - Endothelium of fetal capillaries and their basement membrane (Option B & C) - Syncytiotrophoblast (Option A) - Disappearance of cytotrophoblast - Thinning of syncytiotrophoblast - Edematous stroma - Peripheral migration of fetal blood vessels","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1689-1690; Solution Pages 1718-1720","sourcePage":1689,"sourcePageEnd":1690},{"id":"anatomy-44-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":16,"question":"What is the structure marked “C” formed of? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trophoblast + Somatopleuric EEM"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trophoblast+ Splanchnopleuric EEM"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ectoderm + EEM"},{"letter":"D","text":"Amniogenic cells + Somatopleuric EEM"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Trophoblast + Somatopleuric EEM","explanation":"The structure marked “C” is Chorion , which is formed by the parietal (or somatopleuric) extraembryonic mesoderm and the trophoblast. Formation of extraembryonic mesoderm: - Extra embryonic mesoderm, also called the primary mesoderm, is a mass of cells formed from the cells of the yolk sac cells (Hueser’s membrane) and lies outside the embryonic disc. - This separates the endodermal layer covering the yolk sac and the trophoblast, as well as the wall of the amniotic cavity from the trophoblast. - It does not form any tissues of the embryo. Formation of extraembryonic coelom: - The extraembryonic mesoderm develops small cavities that later coalesce into one large cavity called the extraembryonic coelom (chorionic cavity), which divides the extraembryonic mesoderm into two layers. - The layer between the amniotic cavity and the inside of the trophoblast is the parietal or somatopleuric extraembryonic mesoderm, and the layer outside the yolk sac is called the visceral or splanchnopleuric extraembryonic mesoderm. Formation of connecting stalk: - The part of the extraembryonic mesoderm that keeps the amniotic sac, embryo, and yolk sac attached to the trophoblast, develops a structure called the connecting stalk . Formation of chorion and amnion: - The parietal (or somatopleuric) extraembryonic mesoderm, cytotrophoblast and syncytiotrophoblast form a membrane called the chorion . - The amniogenic cells of the walls of the amniotic cavity and the parietal extraembryonic mesoderm form the amnion . - The amnion and chorion play a major role in childbirth .","sourceRef":"Question Page 1690; Solution Pages 1720-1723","sourcePage":1690,"sourcePageEnd":1690},{"id":"anatomy-44-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":17,"question":"During the third week of gestation, the formation of the primitive streak signifies the start of gastrulation. Which statement about the primitive streak accurately describes this process?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It forms from hypoblast cells and establishes the embryonic central axis."},{"letter":"B","text":"It appears dorsally, becoming linear and marking the transition to a trilaminar disc."},{"letter":"C","text":"It develops into the prechordal plate, defining the head-tail axis."},{"letter":"D","text":"It originates from trophoblast cells, contributing to the amniotic cavity and yolk sac formation."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. It appears dorsally, becoming linear and marking the transition to a trilaminar disc.","explanation":"disc. - The primitive streak is a key structure that forms on the dorsal aspect of the embryonic disc and becomes a linear structure as the disc elongates. - This marks the beginning of gastrulation and the transition from a bilaminar to a trilaminar disc , establishing the embryonic axis . - The primitive streak is an early, temporary structure that emerges in the blastula during the initial stages of embryonic development. - Around the 15th day of development, following the formation of the prechordal plate, epiblast cells along the central axis near the tail end of the embryo start to proliferate, creating an elevation that protrudes into the amniotic cavity known as the primitive streak . (Option A, B and D) - Its cranial end forms the primitive node. - A groove called a primitive groove appears in the primitive streak, which continues with a small depression, a primitive pit , in the primitive node.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1690-1691; Solution Pages 1723-1724","sourcePage":1690,"sourcePageEnd":1691},{"id":"anatomy-44-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":18,"question":"During embryonic development, different structures are derived from the lateral plate mesoderm. Which of the following structures are not derived from the lateral plate mesoderm?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Shoulder and hip girdle bones"},{"letter":"B","text":"Smooth muscle layers of the gut"},{"letter":"C","text":"Smooth muscle of the iris"},{"letter":"D","text":"Costal cartilages and limb muscles"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Smooth muscle of the iris","explanation":"Smooth muscle of the iris (sphincter and dilator pupillae) is derived from mesenchymal cells originating from the ectoderm , not the lateral plate mesoderm. Lateral Plate Mesoderm: - Located in the lateral part of the embryonic disc. - Splits into two layers due to the formation of the intraembryonic coelom. Somatopleuric (Parietal) Layer Splanchnopleuric (Visceral) Layer In contact with the ectoderm. In contact with the endoderm. Continuous with the somatopleuric extraembryonic Continuous with the splanchnopleuric extraembryonic mesoderm over the amnion. mesoderm covering the yolk sac. Forms the dermis of the skin in the body wall and limbs. Forms connective tissue and smooth muscle layers of the Contributes to bones and connective tissues of the limbs gut , cardiac muscle, and the circulatory system. (Option and sternum. Sclerotome and muscle precursor cells that B) Form a visceral layer around peritoneal, pleural, and migrate into the parietal layer of lateral plate mesoderm pericardial cavities. form the costal cartilages , l imb muscles , and most of the body wall muscles. (Option D) Gives rise to shoulder and hip girdle bones. (Option A) Forms a parietal layer around peritoneal, pleural, and pericardial cavities.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1691; Solution Pages 1724-1725","sourcePage":1691,"sourcePageEnd":1691},{"id":"anatomy-44-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A newborn is diagnosed with congenital abnormalities of both the kidneys and reproductive organs, including hypoplasia of the kidneys and abnormal development of the gonads. Which embryological structure is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paraxial mesoderm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Intermediate mesoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral plate mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extraembryonic mesoderm"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Intermediate mesoderm","explanation":"- Intermediate mesoderm is crucial for the development of the nephrogenic cord (which forms the urinary system) and the genital ridge (which forms the genital system). - Abnormalities in this structure can result in both renal and gonadal defects. Intermediate Mesoderm: - Lies between the paraxial mesoderm and the coelomic cavity in the lateral plate mesoderm before head folding. - After the folding of the embryonic disc and formation of the peritoneal cavity, it forms a bulge on the posterior abdominal wall, known as the urogenital ridge. - Urogenital Ridge: Covered by coelomic epithelium. Division: Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Covered by coelomic epithelium. - Division: Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. - Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Nephrogenic Cord: Extends from the cervical to the sacral region. Structures Formed: - Extends from the cervical to the sacral region. - Structures Formed: - Covered by coelomic epithelium. - Division: Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. - Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Genital Ridge: Medial part; gives rise to the genital system. - Nephrogenic Cord: Lateral part; forms the urinary system and the adrenal cortex. - Extends from the cervical to the sacral region. - Structures Formed: Paraxial mesoderm (Option A) forms somites that give rise to the axial skeleton, skeletal muscles, and dermis of the skin. It does not primarily contribute to kidney or gonadal development. Lateral plate mesoderm (Option C) contributes to the connective tissue of the body wall, limbs, and the visceral layers of the gut and circulatory system but does not directly form the kidneys or gonads. Extraembryonic mesoderm (Option D) supports embryonic development by forming structures like the yolk sac, allantois, and chorionic villi, which are essential for placental and early embryonic support but do not directly form the kidneys or reproductive organs.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1691; Solution Pages 1725-1727","sourcePage":1691,"sourcePageEnd":1691},{"id":"anatomy-44-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":20,"question":"An embryo is observed to have 12 pairs of somites on ultrasound. Based on this, what is the most likely developmental age of the embryo?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"21 days"},{"letter":"B","text":"23 days"},{"letter":"C","text":"26 days"},{"letter":"D","text":"30 days"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 23 days","explanation":"- Somites begin forming around day 20 , and new somites form at a rate of approximately three pairs per day. - By 23 days, an embryo typically has about 12 pairs of somites, making this the most likely developmental age. Somite Formation: - First pair of somites arises in the occipital region at approximately the 20th day of development. - Somites appear in a craniocaudal sequence at a rate of 3 pairs/day. - At the end of the fifth week: 42 to 44 pairs of somites are present. - Distribution: Occipital - 4, cervical - 8, thoracic - 12, lumbar - 5, sacral - 5, coccygeal - 8 to 10 - The first occipital and last 5-7 coccygeal somites eventually disappear. - Remaining Somites: Contribute to the formation of the axial skeleton. - Clinical Application (Age Estimation): The age of an embryo can be accurately determined during this period by counting the number of somites. Number of Somites Correlated to Approximate Age in Days Approximate Age (days) Number of Somites 20 1-4 21 (Option A) 4-7 22 7 - 10 23 (Option B) 10 - 13 24 13 - 17 25 17 - 20 26 (Option C) 20 - 23 27 23 - 26 28 26 - 29 30 (Option D) 34 - 35","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1691-1692; Solution Pages 1727-1728","sourcePage":1691,"sourcePageEnd":1692},{"id":"anatomy-44-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A newborn presents with scoliosis and underdeveloped axial muscles. Imaging reveals defects in the vertebral column. Which embryological structure is primarily responsible for the development of these affected tissues?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Paraxial mesoderm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral plate mesoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Intermediate mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Extraembryonic mesoderm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Paraxial mesoderm","explanation":"- Paraxial mesoderm gives rise to somites , which further differentiate into the sclerotome (forming the vertebral column), myotome (forming skeletal muscles), and dermatome (forming dermis). - Defects in this structure can result in abnormalities in the axial skeleton (such as scoliosis) and skeletal muscles. Intraembryonic Mesoderm/ Secondary Mesoderm: - Formed by the proliferation of cells from the primitive streak. - Separates the ectoderm and endoderm except in specific regions that remain bilaminar throughout life: Prochordal plate (future oral cavity) Cloacal membrane (future anal and urogenital openings) Midline caudal to the prochordal plate - Prochordal plate (future oral cavity) - Cloacal membrane (future anal and urogenital openings) - Midline caudal to the prochordal plate - Prochordal plate (future oral cavity) - Cloacal membrane (future anal and urogenital openings) - Midline caudal to the prochordal plate - Subdivisions: 1. Paraxial Mesoderm (Option A) 2. Lateral Plate Mesoderm (Option B) 3. Intermediate Mesoderm (Option C) Located on either side of the It forms a thinner layer located A longitudinal strip found between notochord and becomes thick. laterally. the paraxial and lateral plate mesoderm. It forms: Somitomeres: Form It forms: Somatopleuric (parietal) It forms: Urinary and genital systems. mesoderm and muscles of the head layer: Forms connective tissue of the and jaw. Somites: Form midline axial body wall and limbs, such as skeleton, skeletal muscles and appendicular skeleton bones , dermis of back. muscles and dermis of periphery. Splanchnopleuric (visceral) layers: Forms connective tissue and smooth muscle layers of the gut, cardiac muscle and the circulatory system. Extraembryonic Mesoderm/ Primary Mesoderm: (Option D) - Arises from the cells of the trophoblast. - Lies between the trophoblast and the endodermal cells lining the yolk sac. - Also separates the wall of the amniotic cavity from the trophoblast. - Classified as \"extraembryonic\" because it is located outside the embryonic disc. - Crucial in supporting embryonic development by forming tissues such as the yolk sac, allantois, and chorionic villi.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1692; Solution Pages 1728-1730","sourcePage":1692,"sourcePageEnd":1692},{"id":"anatomy-44-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":22,"question":"Which of the following is a correct pairing of a structure and its corresponding embryological origin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sweat glands – Neural crest cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Sebaceous glands – Mesoderm"},{"letter":"C","text":"Parotid gland – Surface ectoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lens – Mesoderm"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Parotid gland – Surface ectoderm","explanation":"The surface ectoderm gives rise to the Parotid gland. Derivatives of Surface Ectoderm: Structures Derivatives Epithelium & Glands Skin epithelium Sweat glands (Option A) Sebaceous glands (Option B) Myoepithelioctyes of skin Eyes Conjunctiva Surface epithelium of cornea Lens (Option D) Salivary Glands Parotid gland (Option C) Submandibular gland Sublingual gland Opening Structures Oral opening Stomodeum Proctodeum Rathke pouch (Anterior pituitary)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1692; Solution Pages 1730-1731","sourcePage":1692,"sourcePageEnd":1692},{"id":"anatomy-44-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":23,"question":"Which of the following components is responsible for the formation of sensory ganglia during embryonic development in the given figure? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. B","explanation":"- Neural crest cells are responsible for forming sensory ganglia , among other structures, during embryonic development. - In the above image, B denotes the neural crest cell. Components of Ectoderm: - Neural Plate (Option D) : Forms above the notochord and paraxial mesoderm; Develops into the central nervous system. - Neural Crest cells: Present dorsal to the neural tube at the edges of the neural plate. Cells move throughout the embryo and contribute to various structures such as Dorsal root ganglia, autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla, skin melanocytes, craniofacial skeleton, teeth odontoblasts etc. - Cells move throughout the embryo and contribute to various structures such as Dorsal root ganglia, autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla, skin melanocytes, craniofacial skeleton, teeth odontoblasts etc. - Surface Ectoderm (Option C) : Forms the epidermis, hair, glands, and other structures. - Placodal Ectoderm (Option A) : Specialized ectodermal patches contributing to sensory structures (e.g., Otic, nasal, and lens placodes) and endocrine structures. - Cells move throughout the embryo and contribute to various structures such as Dorsal root ganglia, autonomic ganglia, adrenal medulla, skin melanocytes, craniofacial skeleton, teeth odontoblasts etc. Derivatives of Ectoderm: Surface Ectoderm Neuroectoderm Epidermis of the skin Hair Nails Sweat & Sebaceous Central nervous system (Brain and spinal cord) Peripheral glands Mammary glands Enamel of the teeth Lens of eye nervous system Retina Sensory epithelia of nose & ear Epithelium of sensory organs in the inner ear & nose Pineal gland Posterior lobe of the pituitary gland Nasolacrimal duct (NLD) Anterior lobe of the pituitary gland","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1692-1693; Solution Pages 1731-1733","sourcePage":1692,"sourcePageEnd":1693},{"id":"anatomy-44-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Which of the following statements about the prechordal plate is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Derived from hypoblast cells"},{"letter":"B","text":"Formed before primitive streak formation"},{"letter":"C","text":"Forms the cephalocaudal axis of the embryo"},{"letter":"D","text":"Gives rise to the notochord"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Derived from hypoblast cells","explanation":"- The prechordal plate is derived from epiblast cells, not hypoblast cells . - The epiblast is a layer of cells in the early embryo that gives rise to various important structures, including the prechordal plate. Formation : - Lies between the tip of the notochord and the oropharyngeal membrane. - This is a small aggregation of mesodermal cells closely opposed to the endoderm. - It is derived from some of the first cells (epiblast cells) (Option A) that migrate from the primitive node at day 14. Function and Role : - Establishes the central axis of the embryo, dividing it into right and left halves. - Helps distinguish the future head ( cranial ) and tail ( caudal ) ends of the embryo. (Option C) - Plays an essential role in inducing the development of the forebrain. - The primordium of the oropharyngeal membrane, marking the future site of the oral cavity (mouth). - There is no mesoderm in this region; direct contact occurs between the ectoderm and endoderm. - Notochord Formation : (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1693; Solution Pages 1733-1734","sourcePage":1693,"sourcePageEnd":1693},{"id":"anatomy-44-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-44","chapter":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topic":"Lesson 44 - General Embryology","topicIndex":44,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of events during gastrulation in the third week of gestation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Epiblast cells form the primitive streak, leading to the formation of the three germ layers."},{"letter":"B","text":"The primitive streak forms at the cephalic end, initiating neural tube, gut, and cardiovascular development."},{"letter":"C","text":"The prechordal plate forms after the notochord, and epiblast cells migrate to create the three germ layers."},{"letter":"D","text":"The Endoderm forms from mesodermal cells, with ectoderm and mesoderm forming later."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Epiblast cells form the primitive streak, leading to the formation of the three germ layers.","explanation":"germ layers. - During gastrulation, epiblast cells migrate to form the primitive streak. - This migration leads to the differentiation of epiblast cells into the three germ layers: endoderm, mesoderm, and ectoderm. Start of Process Formation of the primitive streak on the surface of the epiblast. Primitive Streak Initially indistinct, it becomes a narrow groove with bulging regions by days 15-16. Primitive Node Located at the cephalic end of the streak, a slightly raised area surrounding a small pit. Cell Migration Epiblast cells migrate toward the primitive streak and undergo invagination. Invagination Cells transform into flask-shaped forms, detach from the epiblast, and move beneath it. Regulation Fibroblast growth factor 8 (FGF8) regulates cell movement and mesoderm formation. FGF8 decreases E-cadherin levels, which helps in migration Cell Spread Invaginated cells replace the hypoblast and spread laterally and cranially. Contact with Extraembryonic Mesoderm Cells establish contact with the extraembryonic mesoderm covering the yolk sac and amnion. Prechordal Plate Forms between the notochord tip and the oropharyngeal membrane. Important for forebrain induction. Oropharyngeal Membrane Composed of tightly joined ectoderm and endoderm cells. Will become the future oral cavity opening. Formation of Germ Layers Endoderm : Displaces the hypoblast. Mesoderm : Forms between the epiblast and endoderm. Ectoderm : Remains in the epiblast. Outcome The epiblast gives rise to all embryonic tissues and organs through the process of gastrulation.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1693-1694; Solution Pages 1734-1736","sourcePage":1693,"sourcePageEnd":1694},{"id":"anatomy-45-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Implantation occurs on which day post fertilization?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2"},{"letter":"B","text":"5"},{"letter":"C","text":"8"},{"letter":"D","text":"15"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 5","explanation":"Correct Option B - 5: - Implantation typically occurs about 5 to 7 days after fertilization. After fertilization, the embryo travels down the fallopian tube and reaches the uterine cavity where it begins the process of implantation into the endometrial lining. Incorrect Options: Option A - 2: - Implantation does not occur as early as 2 days post-fertilization. At this stage, the embryo is still in the blastocyst stage and is traveling towards the uterus. Option C - 8: - While implantation can indeed occur around 8 days post-fertilization, the process generally begins between days 5 and 7. Day 8 is within the range but is a bit later than the typical onset. Option D - 15: - By day 15, the implantation process should already be completed. The embryo has already been implanted and is in the early stages of developing into a more complex structure.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1737; Solution Pages 1752-1753","sourcePage":1737,"sourcePageEnd":1737},{"id":"anatomy-45-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Post ovulation the oocyte is:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary oocyte arrested in prophase- 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Primary oocyte arrested in prophase- 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Secondary oocyte arrested in prophase- 2"},{"letter":"D","text":"Secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase- 2"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase- 2","explanation":"Correct Option D - Secondary oocyte arrested in metaphase-2: - After ovulation, the oocyte is a secondary oocyte that is arrested in metaphase of the second meiotic division. It completes the second meiotic division only if fertilization occurs. If fertilization does not take place, the oocyte will degenerate. Incorrect Options: Option A - Primary oocyte arrested in prophase-1: - The primary oocyte is arrested in prophase of the first meiotic division. This arrest occurs before ovulation. During ovulation, the oocyte has already completed the first meiotic division to become a secondary oocyte. Option B - Primary oocyte arrested in prophase-2: - The primary oocyte is not arrested in prophase-2; it is arrested in prophase-1. Prophase-2 arrest does not occur until after the first meiotic division is completed. Option C - Secondary oocyte arrested in prophase-2: - The secondary oocyte is arrested in metaphase-2, not prophase-2. Prophase-2 arrest is not a correct description for the secondary oocyte's state after ovulation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1737; Solution Page 1753","sourcePage":1737,"sourcePageEnd":1737},{"id":"anatomy-45-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The covering of an omphalocele is derived from which layer?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Amnion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Chorion"},{"letter":"C","text":"Mesoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Endoderm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Amnion","explanation":"Correct Option A - Amnion: - Amnion : The covering of an omphalocele is derived from the amnion. An omphalocele is a congenital defect where abdominal organs herniate through the umbilical ring into the base of the umbilical cord, and these herniated organs are covered by a membrane composed of amnion on the outside and peritoneum on the inside. Amnion : The covering of an omphalocele is derived from the amnion. An omphalocele is a congenital defect where abdominal organs herniate through the umbilical ring into the base of the umbilical cord, and these herniated organs are covered by a membrane composed of amnion on the outside and peritoneum on the inside. Incorrect Options: Option B - Chorion : The chorion is another fetal membrane, but it does not contribute to the covering of an omphalocele. Option C - Mesoderm : The mesoderm is a germ layer involved in the formation of various tissues and organs but not in the direct covering of an omphalocele. Option D - Endoderm : The endoderm forms the lining of the gastrointestinal tract and other internal structures but does not contribute to the covering of an omphalocele.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1737; Solution Pages 1753-1754","sourcePage":1737,"sourcePageEnd":1737},{"id":"anatomy-45-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following prevents polyspermy?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Acrosome reaction"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cortical reaction"},{"letter":"C","text":"Zona reaction"},{"letter":"D","text":"Capacitation"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Cortical reaction","explanation":"Correct Option B - Cortical reaction: - This is a key mechanism to prevent polyspermy. Upon sperm penetration, cortical granules in the oocyte release their contents, which modify the zona pellucida (the layer surrounding the oocyte). This change creates a barrier that prevents additional sperm from binding and entering the oocyte, ensuring that only one sperm fertilizes the egg. Incorrect Options: Option A - Acrosome reaction: - Incorrect: The acrosome reaction is the process by which the sperm releases enzymes to penetrate the zona pellucida of the oocyte. It is crucial for sperm to access the egg but does not prevent polyspermy. Option C - Zona reaction: - Incorrect: The zona reaction involves changes in the zona pellucida but is not the primary mechanism preventing polyspermy. It refers to the structural changes in the zona pellucida that occur after the cortical reaction. Option D - Capacitation: - Incorrect: Capacitation is a physiological process that sperm undergo to gain the ability to fertilize an egg. It involves changes to the sperm's membrane and is necessary for fertilization but does not prevent polyspermy.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1737-1738; Solution Pages 1754-1755","sourcePage":1737,"sourcePageEnd":1738},{"id":"anatomy-45-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following structures develop from the 6th pharyngeal arch artery on the left side?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Arch of Aorta"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ductus arteriosus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Carotid arteries"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subclavian artery"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Ductus arteriosus","explanation":"Correct Option B - Ductus arteriosus - The 6 th pharyngeal arch artery on the left side specifically develops into the ductus arteriosus and a portion of the right and left pulmonary arteries. Incorrect Options: Option A - Arch of Aorta: The arch of the aorta primarily develops from the 4th pharyngeal arch artery on the left side. Option C - Carotid arteries: The common carotid arteries and the initial portion of the internal carotid arteries develop from the 3rd pharyngeal arch artery. Option D - Subclavian artery: The right subclavian artery develops from the 4th pharyngeal arch artery on the right side.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1738; Solution Page 1755","sourcePage":1738,"sourcePageEnd":1738},{"id":"anatomy-45-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which of the following cartilage do not calcify with age?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroid"},{"letter":"B","text":"Arytenoid"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epiglottis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cricoid"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Epiglottis","explanation":"Correct Option C - Epiglottis : - The epiglottis is a flexible flap of cartilage located at the base of the tongue, above the larynx. It remains cartilaginous throughout life and does not calcify with age. Its main function is to prevent food and liquids from entering the airway during swallowing. Incorrect Options: Option A - Thyroid cartilage: The thyroid cartilage, also known as the Adam's apple, doesn't calcify with age, but it may ossify, particularly in males, due to some changes over time. Option B - Arytenoid cartilage: The arytenoid cartilages usually don't calcify with age. However, they can degenerate or ossify, particularly in individuals with specific medical conditions. Option D - Cricoid cartilage: While the cricoid cartilagedoes not typically calcify with age, it can undergo degenerative changes or ossification in certain cases.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1738-1739; Solution Pages 1755-1756","sourcePage":1738,"sourcePageEnd":1739},{"id":"anatomy-45-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following arises from 6th pharyngeal arch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial pterygoids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral pterygoids"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ductus arteriosus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tensor veli palatini"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ductus arteriosus","explanation":"Correct Option C- Ductus arteriosus The ductus arteriosus arises from the sixth pharyngeal arch , also known as the pulmonary arch or the pulmonary arch of the aorta . The ductus arteriosus is a fetal blood vessel that connects the pulmonary trunk to the descending aorta, allowing a portion of oxygenated blood to bypass the developing fetal lungs. This shunt helps direct oxygenated blood towards the systemic circulation while the lungs are not yet fully functional in the prenatal period. Incorrect Options: Option A - Medial pterygoids: These are muscles of mastication that arise from the first pharyngeal arch , not the sixth. Option B - Lateral pterygoids: These are also muscles of mastication that arise from the first pharyngeal arch , not the sixth. Option D - Tensor veli palatini: This muscle arises from the first pharyngeal arch , not the sixth. It is involved in the opening of the auditory tube during swallowing.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1739; Solution Page 1756","sourcePage":1739,"sourcePageEnd":1739},{"id":"anatomy-45-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":8,"question":"The following structure develops in which pharyngeal arch?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1: - The above image shows the lateral section of the middle ear cavity, and the marked structure is the malleus. - Malleus develops from the 1st pharyngeal arch Incorrect Options - B, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1739-1740; Solution Pages 1756-1757","sourcePage":1739,"sourcePageEnd":1740},{"id":"anatomy-45-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Which of the following structures is derived from the marked point in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Ribs"},{"letter":"B","text":"Kidneys"},{"letter":"C","text":"Dorsal root ganglion"},{"letter":"D","text":"Parietal pleura"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Ribs","explanation":"Correct Option A - Ribs: - Structures derived from the paraxial mesoderm include the somites . - Somites are bilaterally paired blocks of mesoderm that form on either side of the neural tube during early embryonic development. The somites give rise to various structures in the embryo, including the axial skeleton (such as vertebrae), skeletal muscles, and the dermis of the skin. Incorrect Options: Option B - Kidneys: - Kidneys are the derivatives of the intermediate mesoderm. Option C - Dorsal root ganglion: - Dorsal root ganglia are derived from the neural crest cells. . Option D - Parietal pleura: - The pericardial, pleural, and peritoneal cavities, housing vital organs, originate from the lateral plate mesoderm during embryonic development.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1740; Solution Pages 1757-1758","sourcePage":1740,"sourcePageEnd":1740},{"id":"anatomy-45-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following statement regarding bones is false?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Osteocytes are the major cell type in mature bone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Collagen is synthesized in bone osteoblasts"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bone mainly contains type 2 collagen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Calcium phosphate is one of the major inorganic mineral"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Bone mainly contains type 2 collagen","explanation":"Correct Option C - Bone mainly contains type 2 collagen: - Bone predominantly contains non-branching type 1 collagen. Incorrect Options - A, B and d : - Osteocytes are the major cell type in mature bone: Osteocytes are the primary cell type in mature bone tissue, derived from osteoblasts responsible for synthesizing and depositing the bone matrix. - Collagen is synthesized in bone osteoblasts: Osteoblasts synthesize and secrete collagen, forming the bone's organic framework, providing strength and flexibility. - Calcium phosphate is one of the major inorganic minerals: Bone's inorganic mineral component is mainly calcium phosphate, predominantly in hydroxyapatite crystal form, offering hardness and rigidity.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1740-1741; Solution Page 1758","sourcePage":1740,"sourcePageEnd":1741},{"id":"anatomy-45-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Meissner corpuscles are present in:-","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Papillary dermis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Reticular dermis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Basal epidermis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stratum lucidum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Papillary dermis","explanation":"Correct Option A - Papillary dermis - Meissner corpuscles, also known as tactile corpuscles, are specialized sensory receptors involved in the perception of light touch and vibration. They are encapsulated nerve endings located within the dermis, specifically in the papillary dermis. The papillary dermis is the upper layer of the dermis, directly beneath the epidermis. It is characterized by its uneven surface due to the presence of dermal papillae. Incorrect Options: Option B - Reticular dermis: The reticular dermis, the deeper layer, lacks Meissner corpuscles but houses sensory receptors like Pacinian corpuscles, crucial for detecting deep pressure and vibration. Option C - Basal epidermis: The basal epidermis, or stratum basale primarily consists of basal cells responsible for renewal and attaching to the basement membrane. Meissner corpuscles aren't present in the epidermis. Option D - Stratum lucidum:. The stratum lucidum is a thin, translucent layer of the epidermis that is typically found in thick skin areas, such as the palms and soles. It does not contain Meissner corpuscles.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1741; Solution Pages 1758-1759","sourcePage":1741,"sourcePageEnd":1741},{"id":"anatomy-45-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Type I collagen is not seen in –","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Bone"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Aponeurosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Elastic cartilage"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Elastic cartilage","explanation":"Correct Option D - Elastic cartilage - Elastic cartilage is a specialized type of cartilage found in structures such as the external ear, epiglottis, and eustachian tube. Unlike other types of cartilage, such as hyaline cartilage, elastic cartilage contains a significant amount of elastic fibers in addition to collagen. Type II collagen is the predominant collagen type found in elastic cartilage, providing structural support along with the elastic fibers. Incorrect Options: Option A - Bone: Type I collagen is the most abundant collagen type in bone tissue. It provides strength and support to the bone structure. Option B - Ligament: Type I collagen is the primary collagen type found in ligaments, contributing to their tensile strength. Option C - Aponeurosis: Similar to ligaments, aponeurosis predominantly consists of Type I collagen, which gives it its strength and resilience.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1741; Solution Page 1759","sourcePage":1741,"sourcePageEnd":1741},{"id":"anatomy-45-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":13,"question":"The structure marked with the asterisk in the electron micrograph indicates **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mitochondria"},{"letter":"B","text":"Golgi bodies"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pre synaptic Secretory vesicles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ribosomes"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Pre synaptic Secretory vesicles","explanation":"Correct Option C - Pre synaptic Secretory vesicles - In the given image , the structure marked with an asterisk in the electron micrograph represents pre-synaptic secretory vesicles. Secretory vesicles are membrane-bound organelles found within the pre-synaptic terminal of neurons. They play a crucial role in the process of neurotransmission. - Secretory vesicles store and transport neurotransmitters, which are chemical messengers that transmit signals between neurons or from neurons to target cells. Incorrect Options: Option A - Mitochondria: Mitochondria are responsible for energy production in the form of ATP and are not specifically involved in neurotransmission. Option B - Golgi bodies: Golgi bodies modify, sort, and package proteins for secretion or delivery within cells. Although crucial for cellular transport, they aren't directly linked to neurotransmission. Option C - Ribosomes: Ribosomes are cellular structures involved in protein synthesis and are not directly related to neurotransmission.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1741-1742; Solution Pages 1759-1760","sourcePage":1741,"sourcePageEnd":1742},{"id":"anatomy-45-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":14,"question":"During which developmental week does the heartbeat initiate in the cardiac region?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"2 nd week"},{"letter":"B","text":"4 th week"},{"letter":"C","text":"6 th week"},{"letter":"D","text":"10 th week"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 4 th week","explanation":"Correct Option B - 4 th week - The heart begins to beat in the 4th week of development. At this stage, the heart has formed and starts contracting, initiating blood circulation within the developing embryo Incorrect Options Option A - 2nd week: During the 2nd week of development, the heart is undergoing early stages of formation. However, it does not start beating during this time. Option C - 6th week: By the 6th week of development, the heart is already beating. This option is incorrect because the heart starts beating earlier, in the 4th week. Option D - 10th week: By the 10th week of development, the heart has been beating for several weeks. This option is incorrect because it suggests a later timeframe for the initiation of heartbeats.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1742; Solution Page 1760","sourcePage":1742,"sourcePageEnd":1742},{"id":"anatomy-45-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Ureteric bud develops from:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mesonephric duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Metanephros"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pronephros"},{"letter":"D","text":"Genital sinus"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Mesonephric duct","explanation":"Correct Option A - Mesonephric duct: - The ureteric bud is a structure that arises from the mesonephric duct, also known as Wolffian duct. - During embryonic development, the ureteric bud grows out from the mesonephric duct and gives rise to the ureter, renal pelvis, calyces, and collecting ducts. - The ureteric bud interacts with the metanephric mesenchyme to form the kidney. Incorrect Options: Option B - Metanephros: The definitive kidney (metanephros) develops from the interaction of the ureteric bud and metanephric mesenchyme. But the ureteric bud doesn't originate from the metanephros. Option C - Pronephros: The pronephros, the most anterior and non-functional part of the embryonic kidney system, regresses early and doesn't form the ureteric bud. Option D - Genital sinus: The genital sinus contributes to the urogenital system's development, forming the bladder and urethra, but it isn't directly linked to the development of the ureteric bud.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1742-1743; Solution Pages 1760-1761","sourcePage":1742,"sourcePageEnd":1743},{"id":"anatomy-45-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following develops from the dorsal mesogastrium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Falciform ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Spleen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lesser omentum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Liver"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Spleen","explanation":"Correct Option B - Spleen: The spleen develops from the dorsal mesogastrium. During embryonic development, the spleen forms from mesenchymal cells within the mesoderm, specifically in the dorsal mesogastrium. Incorrect Options: Option A - Falciformligament : The falciform ligament arises from the ventral mesogastrium during embryonic development. Option C - Lesser omentum: The lesser omentum does not develop from the dorsal mesogastrium but rather from the ventral mesogastrium. Option D - Liver : The liver does not develop directly from the dorsal mesogastrium. It forms in association with the ventral mesogastrium.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1743; Solution Pages 1761-1762","sourcePage":1743,"sourcePageEnd":1743},{"id":"anatomy-45-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A newborn had hypoplasia of the mandibles and zygomatic bones, malformed external ears, and slanted palpebral fissures. Abnormality in the development of which of the following pharyngeal arches will most likely lead to the given condition?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pharyngeal arch 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pharyngeal arch 2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Pharyngeal arch 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pharyngeal arch 4"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Pharyngeal arch 1","explanation":"Correct Option A - Pharyngeal arch 1 - The first pharyngeal arch, also known as the mandibular arch, is a crucial structure in embryonic development, forming part of the head and neck region. It gives rise to various structures that are essential for the formation of the face and neck. - Defects in the development of this arch can result in a condition called mandibulofacial dysostosis or Treacher-Collins syndrome, which is characterized by the same features described in the scenario. Incorrect Options: Option B - Pharyngeal arch 2: . Abnormal development of this arch can result in abnormalities of the ear and hearing, but not the features described in the scenario. Option C - Pharyngeal arch 3: Abnormal development of this arch can result in problems with swallowing and speech, but not the features described in the scenario. Option D - Pharyngeal arch 4: Abnormal development of these arches can cause problems with speech and breathing, but not the features described in the scenario.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1743; Solution Page 1762","sourcePage":1743,"sourcePageEnd":1743},{"id":"anatomy-45-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":18,"question":"1st pharyngeal arch gives rise to all except:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial pterygoids"},{"letter":"B","text":"Levator veli palatin"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lateral pterygoid"},{"letter":"D","text":"Tensor veli palatini"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Levator veli palatin","explanation":"Correct Option B - Levator veli palatini: This structure is not derived from the 1st pharyngeal arch. The levator veli palatini muscle is actually derived from the 4th pharyngeal arch. It forms part of the soft palate and helps elevate the soft palate during swallowing. Incorrect Options: Option A - Medial pterygoids: The medial pterygoid muscles, from the 1st pharyngeal arch, aid in jaw movement during chewing, situated in the back of the oral cavity. Option C - Lateral pterygoids: The lateral pterygoid muscles, from the 1st pharyngeal arch, assist in jaw movement, mainly in opening the mouth, situated in the oral cavity's lateral walls. Option D - Tensor veli palatini: The tensor veli palatini muscle, from the 1st pharyngeal arch, is in the soft palate, helping control the Eustachian tube during swallowing and yawning.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1743-1744; Solution Pages 1762-1763","sourcePage":1743,"sourcePageEnd":1744},{"id":"anatomy-45-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following is false about skin?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dermis is derived from ectoderm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skin accounts for 15% of total body weight"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epidermis is derived from ectoderm"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dermis is made up of type 1 and type 3 collagen"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Dermis is derived from ectoderm","explanation":"Correct Option - A: Dermis is derived from ectoderm: This statement is false. The dermis is not derived from ectoderm but rather from mesoderm. During embryonic development, the dermis is formed from mesodermal cells that migrate into the developing skin. Incorrect Option: Option B - Skin accounts for 15% of total body weight: Skin weight varies based on age, sex, and body composition, usually constituting around 7% to 15% of total body weight in adults. Option C - Epidermis is derived from ectoderm: The epidermis, which is the outermost layer of the skin, is derived from the ectodermal germ layer during embryonic development. Option D - Dermis is made up of type 1 and type 3 collagen: The dermis contains collagen fibers, primarily type 1 and type 3, providing strength, support, and elasticity to the skin.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1744; Solution Page 1763","sourcePage":1744,"sourcePageEnd":1744},{"id":"anatomy-45-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Identify the structure marked in the diagram showing the development of ovarian follicles. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary follicle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Antral follicle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Primordial follicle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Corpus luteum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Antral follicle","explanation":"Correct Option B - Antral follicle - The given structure marked in the image is a SECONDARY FOLLICLE also known as the ANTRAL FOLLICLE. - As meiosis progresses within the oocyte, primary follicles mature into secondary follicles, which then develop into antral follicles. Antral follicles eventually transform into mature follicles, also referred to as Graafian or tertiary follicles. Incorrect Options: Option A - When the primordial follicle is stimulated, it becomes a primary follicle. Option C - Primordial germ cells migrate into the developing gonad early in embryogenesis, and differentiate into oogonia. These oogonia proliferate by mitosis. Option D - After ovulation, the ruptured follicle collapses and fills with a blood clot (corpus haemorrhagicum) which then forms the corpus luteum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1744-1745; Solution Pages 1763-1764","sourcePage":1744,"sourcePageEnd":1745},{"id":"anatomy-45-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":21,"question":"A new born presented to the NICU with respiratory distress and meconium discharge from the umbilicus. The condition occurs due to the patency of the which structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Mesonephric duct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Allantois"},{"letter":"C","text":"Urachus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vitelline Fistula"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Vitelline Fistula","explanation":"Correct option D - Vitelline Fistula - By the 7th week of developement the vitelline duct is completely obliterated and regresses into the umbilical cord. - Remnants of the vitellointestinal duct may form a tumour at the umbilicus (raspberry red tumour; or cherry red tumour). Persistence of a patent vitellointestinal duct results in a faecal fistula at the umbilicus. Incorrect Options: Option A - The mesonephric duct connects the primitive kidney, the mesonephros, to the cloaca. Option B - It helps the embryo exchange gases and handle liquid waste. Option C - The urachus is a tube between the bladder and the belly button that is present before birth.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1745; Solution Page 1764","sourcePage":1745,"sourcePageEnd":1745},{"id":"anatomy-45-22","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":22,"question":"What is the embryological basis of the developmental defects shown in the image below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Failure in closure of anterior neuropore of neural tube"},{"letter":"B","text":"Failure in closure of posterior neuropore of neural tube"},{"letter":"C","text":"Failure in closure of complete neural tube"},{"letter":"D","text":"Failure in closure of caudal neuropore of neural tube"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Failure in closure of anterior neuropore of neural tube","explanation":"Correct Option A - Failure in closure of anterior neuropore of neural tube - The given image shows Anencephaly due to non-closure of anterior/cranial neuropore with an absent skull cap and a small, degenerated brain. Incorrect Options: Option B - Defect in the lumbo-sacral region due to non closure of posterior/caudal neuropore is known as Rachischisis Option C - Cranio-rachischisis is when the complete neural tube fails to close, resulting in both anencephaly and rachischisis occurring together in a baby. Option D - Defect in the lumbo-sacral region due to non closure of caudal/posterior neuropore is known as Rachischisis","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1745-1746; Solution Pages 1764-1765","sourcePage":1745,"sourcePageEnd":1746},{"id":"anatomy-45-23","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":23,"question":"The long left recurrent laryngeal nerve is due to the persistence of which arch artery?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"3 rd arch"},{"letter":"B","text":"4 th arch"},{"letter":"C","text":"5 th arch"},{"letter":"D","text":"6 th arch"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 6 th arch","explanation":"Correct Option D - 6 th arch: - In the case of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve, it loops around the aortic arch, running in close proximity to the ligamentum arteriosum (a remnant of the fetal ductus arteriosus). - This unique course is due to the persistence of the 6 th pharyngeal arch artery, also known as the pulmonary artery. - During normal development, the 6 th arch artery regresses, but if it persists, it leads to the characteristic course of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. Incorrect Options: Option A - 3 rd arch: The 3 rd arch artery forms the common carotid artery and proximal part of the internal carotid artery but doesn't affect the path of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve. Option B - 4 th arch: The 4 th arch artery forms the aortic arch on the left and the subclavian artery on the right, but it doesn't impact the left recurrent laryngeal nerve's path. Option C - 5 th arch: The 5 th arch artery is an embryonic structure that does not persist in its original form. It does not contribute to the course of the left recurrent laryngeal nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1746; Solution Page 1765","sourcePage":1746,"sourcePageEnd":1746},{"id":"anatomy-45-24","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":24,"question":"Meiosis occurs in:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Adrenal"},{"letter":"B","text":"Adult ovary"},{"letter":"C","text":"Prepubertal testis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Hypothalamus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Adult ovary","explanation":"Correct Option B - Adult ovary: - Option B, correctly identifies the adult ovary as the site where meiosis occurs in the female reproductive system. Meiosis in the ovary produces mature eggs for fertilization. It is a specialized cell division process for making gametes, involving two divisions to yield four haploid cells. Incorrect Options: Option A - Adrenal glands are not involved in the process of meiosis. They primarily produce hormones such as cortisol and adrenaline. Option C - Meiosis starts in the testes during puberty when sperm production begins.Therefore, prepubertal testes are not actively involved in meiosis. Option D - The hypothalamus regulates many body functions but is not directly involved in meiosis.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1746; Solution Pages 1765-1766","sourcePage":1746,"sourcePageEnd":1746},{"id":"anatomy-45-25","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":25,"question":"Absence or mutation of SRY gene results in ?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Agenesis of testis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Undescended testis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hydrocele testis"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Agenesis of testis","explanation":"Correct Option A - Agenesis of testis - The absence or mutation of the SRY gene causes the promotion of the differentiation of female gonads . The primary germ cells get converted into oogonia. - The absence of the SRY gene inhibits the differentiation to the male gonads . - Hence the absence or mutation of the SRY gene will cause agenesis of the testis. Incorrect Options: Option B - Undescended testis: Undescended testis is a developmental disorder involving the failure of the gubernaculum testis to shorten, halting the descent of the testis from the abdomen to the scrotal sacs. This isn't caused by the SRY gene. Option C - Hydrocele testis: Hydrocele testis is the accumulation of fluid between the two layers of tunica vaginalis. It is not caused by the SRY gene mutation. Option D - None of the above: since option a is correct this option is not applicable.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1746; Solution Page 1766","sourcePage":1746,"sourcePageEnd":1746},{"id":"anatomy-45-26","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":26,"question":"On what is the SRY gene located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Y chromosome short arm"},{"letter":"B","text":"Y chromosome long arm"},{"letter":"C","text":"X chromosome short arm"},{"letter":"D","text":"X chromosome long arm"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Y chromosome short arm","explanation":"Correct Option A - Y chromosome short arm - The SRY gene (Sex determining region on the Y chromosome) is a gene present on the short arm of the Y chromosome. - Responsible for transcription of SRY protein - SRY protein causes the differentiation of male sex organs and Primary germ cells (PGCs) into Spermatogonia. Incorrect Options - B,C,D are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1746-1747; Solution Pages 1766-1767","sourcePage":1746,"sourcePageEnd":1747},{"id":"anatomy-45-27","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":27,"question":"What are the derivatives of the marked cells? Odontoblasts Sympathetic chain Melanocytes Nephrogenic cord Bulbus cordis Conotruncal septum **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,3,5,6"},{"letter":"B","text":"2,3,4,6"},{"letter":"C","text":"1,2,3,6"},{"letter":"D","text":"1,2,3,4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 1,2,3,6","explanation":"Correct Option C - 1,2,3,6: - The marked cells in the diagram are NEURAL CREST CELLS (NCC). Neural crest cells are multipotent and migrate from the neural tube during embryonic development. They give rise to a wide variety of cell types. The derivatives include odontoblasts (cells that produce dentin in teeth), cells in the sympathetic chain, and melanocytes (cells that produce melanin, the pigment responsible for skin color). They also contribute to the formation of the conotruncal septum, part of the developing heart. Hence, option c is the correct answer. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1747; Solution Page 1767","sourcePage":1747,"sourcePageEnd":1747},{"id":"anatomy-45-28","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":28,"question":"Arrange the cells according to their positions from the basal layer towards the lumen in the seminiferous tubules:- Spermatogonia Primary spermatocyte Spermatid Spermatozoa Myoepithelial cell","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1,5,4,2,3"},{"letter":"B","text":"1,2,3,4,5"},{"letter":"C","text":"5,4,3,2,1"},{"letter":"D","text":"5,1,2,3,4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 5,1,2,3,4","explanation":"Correct Option D - 5,1,2,3,4: - In the seminiferous tubules, the arrangement of cells from the basal layer towards the lumen is: Myoepithelial cells, Spermatogonia, Primary spermatocytes, Spermatids, and finally Spermatozoa. This outlines the process of spermatogenesis in seminiferous tubules: starting with myoepithelial cells, then spermatogonia near the tubule's base. Spermatogonia divide to form primary spermatocytes, which undergo two rounds of meiosis to become spermatids. Finally, spermiogenesis transforms spermatids into spermatozoa. Incorrect Options - A, B, and C are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1747-1748; Solution Pages 1767-1768","sourcePage":1747,"sourcePageEnd":1748},{"id":"anatomy-45-29","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":29,"question":"A child lacks thymus and inferior parathyroid glands. Defective development of which of the following structures is likely to be the cause?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 3","explanation":"Correct Option C - 3: The thymus and inferior parathyroid glands develop from the third pharyngeal pouch, part of the branchial apparatus. The thymus is crucial for T cell maturation, while parathyroid glands regulate calcium levels. The inferior parathyroid glands arise from the third pouch, while the superior ones come from the fourth. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1748; Solution Page 1768","sourcePage":1748,"sourcePageEnd":1748},{"id":"anatomy-45-30","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":30,"question":"Which of the following structures develops from the structure marked? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Medial umbilical ligament"},{"letter":"B","text":"Median umbilical ligament"},{"letter":"C","text":"Meckel's diverticulum"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ligamentum teres"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Median umbilical ligament","explanation":"Correct Option B - Median umbilical ligament: - The marked structure is the allantois, a hindgut diverticulum. It forms the urachus during fetal development, connecting the bladder apex to the umbilicus, which later degenerates into the median umbilical ligament. This ligament runs along the midline of abdominal wall. Incorrect Options: Option A - Medial umbilical ligament: The medial umbilical ligament is a remnant of an obliterated umbilical artery. Option C - Meckel's diverticulum: Meckel's diverticulum, a pouch in the small intestine wall, arises from yolk sac remnants and affects about 2% of people. Option D - Ligamentum teres: After birth, the umbilical vein typically degenerates and forms the ligamentum teres, which run from the liver to the umbilicus (belly button).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1748-1749; Solution Pages 1768-1769","sourcePage":1748,"sourcePageEnd":1749},{"id":"anatomy-45-31","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-45","chapter":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 45 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":45,"questionNumber":31,"question":"The following condition is seen in the image results from the failure of fusion of which prominence **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lateral nasal prominence and maxillary prominence"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lateral nasal prominence and mandibular prominence"},{"letter":"C","text":"Medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence"},{"letter":"D","text":"Medial nasal prominence and mandibular prominence"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence","explanation":"Correct Options C - Medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence: - The failure of fusion between the medial nasal prominence and maxillary prominence can result in cleft lip and cleft palate, as well as other facial malformations. - A cleft lip is when there is a gap or split in the upper lip, while a cleft palate is when there is a gap or split in the roof of the mouth. - These malformations can occur on one or both sides of the face and can range in severity. Incorrect Options: - Option A - Lateral nasal prominence and maxillary prominence: The failure of fusion between themcan result in various congenital malformations, including oblique facial cleft. - Option B - Lateral nasal prominence and mandibular prominence: The lateral nasal prominence doesn't fuse with the mandibular prominence during embryonic development, leading to possible facial malformations. - Option D - Medial nasal prominence and mandibular prominence: The failure of fusion between the themcan result in various malformations","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1749-1750; Solution Pages 1769-1770","sourcePage":1749,"sourcePageEnd":1750},{"id":"anatomy-46-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following is an apocrine gland?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Sweat gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreas"},{"letter":"C","text":"Sebaceous gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"None of the above"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Sweat gland","explanation":"The sweat gland is a type of apocrine gland. They are found in the axilla, perigenital area, and as ceruminous glands in the ear. Apocrine Glands Merocrine Glands (Option B) Holocrine Glands (Option C) The secretions are released along Also called eccrine glands. The The whole cell membrane is lost. with the apical part of the cell secretion is released by exocytosis Becomes the sebum. Entire cell membrane. Blebs are formed. without losing a part of the secretory organelles break down and become Mammary gland: Modified sweat cell Secretions are released without secretions. Peripheral cells are gland. E.g: Breast, Sweat gland the loss of the cell membrane. E.g: intact. E.g: Sebaceous gland Salivary glands, Pancreas","sourceRef":"Question Page 1771; Solution Page 1782","sourcePage":1771,"sourcePageEnd":1771},{"id":"anatomy-46-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Match the following layer of skin with the cells present in it. 1. Stratum Corneum a) Melanocytes 2. Stratum Granulosum b) Keratin Squames 3. Stratum Spinosum c) Keratohyalin granules 4. Stratum Basale d) Langerhan cells 1. Stratum Corneum a) Melanocytes 2. Stratum Granulosum b) Keratin Squames 3. Stratum Spinosum c) Keratohyalin granules 4. Stratum Basale d) Langerhan cells **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-c, 2-d, 3-b, 4-a"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-d, 2-a, 3-b, 4-c"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-a, 2-b, 3-c, 4-d"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. 1-b, 2-c, 3-d, 4-a","explanation":"There are 5 layers of skin epithelium (epidermis). The cells present in these layers are: - Stratum Corneum: Keratin Squames: Flattened, water-repellent cells composed of keratin. - Keratin Squames: Flattened, water-repellent cells composed of keratin. - Stratum Lucidum: Only seen in thick skin like palm and sole. - Stratum Granulosum: Keratinocytes: Contain keratohyaline granules for keratin production. - Keratinocytes: Contain keratohyaline granules for keratin production. - Stratum Spinosum: Prickle cell layer Keratinocytes: Multilayered cells with desmosomes; involved in keratin synthesis. Langerhan cells: Act as antigen presenting cells. - Prickle cell layer - Keratinocytes: Multilayered cells with desmosomes; involved in keratin synthesis. - Langerhan cells: Act as antigen presenting cells. - Stratum Basale: Basal Keratinocytes: Cuboidal cells that proliferate and replenish upper layers. Melanocytes: Produce melanin. Merkel Cells: Touch receptors. - Basal Keratinocytes: Cuboidal cells that proliferate and replenish upper layers. - Melanocytes: Produce melanin. - Merkel Cells: Touch receptors. - Dermis: Fibroblasts: Produce collagen and elastin. Sensory Nerve Endings: Detect stimuli. - Fibroblasts: Produce collagen and elastin. - Sensory Nerve Endings: Detect stimuli. - Keratin Squames: Flattened, water-repellent cells composed of keratin. - Keratinocytes: Contain keratohyaline granules for keratin production. - Prickle cell layer - Keratinocytes: Multilayered cells with desmosomes; involved in keratin synthesis. - Langerhan cells: Act as antigen presenting cells. - Basal Keratinocytes: Cuboidal cells that proliferate and replenish upper layers. - Melanocytes: Produce melanin. - Merkel Cells: Touch receptors. - Fibroblasts: Produce collagen and elastin. - Sensory Nerve Endings: Detect stimuli.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1771; Solution Pages 1782-1783","sourcePage":1771,"sourcePageEnd":1771},{"id":"anatomy-46-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements is true about the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"GALT tissues are part of the Waldeyer ring."},{"letter":"B","text":"Tonsils primarily produce secretory IgA to protect mucosal surfaces."},{"letter":"C","text":"Tonsils are covered by stratified squamous epithelium that forms deep crypts."},{"letter":"D","text":"Tonsils contain diffuse lymphoid tissue scattered in the lamina propria."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Tonsils are covered by stratified squamous epithelium that forms deep crypts.","explanation":"The Tonsils are covered by stratified squamous epithelium that forms crypts, a key feature of tonsils. Mucosa Associated Lymphoid Tissue (MALT) Tonsils Includes diffuse lymphoid cells and organized aggregates The palatine tonsils are part of Waldeyer's ring, along with (e.g., tonsils, Peyer’s patches). (Option D) The the lingual, pharyngeal, and tubal tonsils. They are aggregations consist of lymphatic follicles (nodules) covered by stratified squamous epithelium , which forms resembling lymph node structure, with possible germinal deep tonsillar crypts. (Option C) Beneath the tonsil is a centers . Between the nodules, diffuse lymphoid tissue, collagenous hemicapsule separating it from muscle. The known as the parafollicular zone , is present. Parts: tonsillar tissue contains lymphoid follicles with germinal GALT: Found in the gastrointestinal tract (e.g., Peyer’s centers, similar to lymph nodes. Bacteria and particles patches). (Option A) BALT: Present in the bronchial tree. entering the crypts are passed to follicles, triggering Waldeyer Ring: Lymphoid tissue in the oropharynx. immune responses. Efferent lymphatics drain to deep Consists of T & B cells, Plasma cells and mast cells. cervical lymph nodes. In the tubal tonsils, respiratory Antibody Production: IgA: MALT produces IgA, which is epithelium covers the surface instead of squamous the main antibody in mucosal areas, protects mucosal epithelium. surfaces. (Option B) IgG & IgM: Present in lamina propria. IgE: Involved in allergic reactions. Function: Epithelium samples luminal antigens.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1771-1772; Solution Pages 1783-1784","sourcePage":1771,"sourcePageEnd":1772},{"id":"anatomy-46-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements about the spleen is TRUE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"White pulp occupies most of the spleen and contains a higher number of RBCs."},{"letter":"B","text":"The red pulp parenchyma is supported by reticular fibers and contains sheathed capillaries."},{"letter":"C","text":"B cells form an eccentric cylindrical sheath around a central artery, known as PALS."},{"letter":"D","text":"The Perilymphoid zone contains numerous sinuses and abundant reticulin meshwork."}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. The red pulp parenchyma is supported by reticular fibers and contains sheathed capillaries.","explanation":"sheathed capillaries. Red pulp is supported by reticular fibers, and it contains capillaries that terminate as sheathed capillaries. Spleen - The capsule is composed of collagen fibers. - Trabeculae function as partitions. - Red pulp occupies most of the area. Red pulp contains a higher number of RBCs and also WBCs. Red pulp features splenic sinusoids surrounded by splenic cords. The red pulp parenchyma is made up of loose tissue supported by reticular fibers, interspersed with capillaries, which end as sheathed capillaries. (Option B) The perilymphoid (Perifollicular) zone of red pulp, surrounding the white pulp, lacks sinuses, has a sparse reticulin meshwork, and contains red and white blood cells in proportions similar to those in blood. (Option D) - Red pulp contains a higher number of RBCs and also WBCs. - Red pulp features splenic sinusoids surrounded by splenic cords. - The red pulp parenchyma is made up of loose tissue supported by reticular fibers, interspersed with capillaries, which end as sheathed capillaries. (Option B) - The perilymphoid (Perifollicular) zone of red pulp, surrounding the white pulp, lacks sinuses, has a sparse reticulin meshwork, and contains red and white blood cells in proportions similar to those in blood. (Option D) - White pulp constitutes 5% - 20% of the total spleen mass. (Option A) White pulp contains both T and B cells. T cells form an eccentric cylindrical sheath around a central artery, mainly containing T helper cells, like PALS. (Option C) B cells form follicles at the edge of the PALS, often showing germinal centers in younger individuals. - White pulp contains both T and B cells. - T cells form an eccentric cylindrical sheath around a central artery, mainly containing T helper cells, like PALS. (Option C) - B cells form follicles at the edge of the PALS, often showing germinal centers in younger individuals. - Splenic cords contain lymphoid macrophages, lymphocytes, and plasma cells (LMP cells). - Red pulp contains a higher number of RBCs and also WBCs. - Red pulp features splenic sinusoids surrounded by splenic cords. - The red pulp parenchyma is made up of loose tissue supported by reticular fibers, interspersed with capillaries, which end as sheathed capillaries. (Option B) - The perilymphoid (Perifollicular) zone of red pulp, surrounding the white pulp, lacks sinuses, has a sparse reticulin meshwork, and contains red and white blood cells in proportions similar to those in blood. (Option D) - White pulp contains both T and B cells. - T cells form an eccentric cylindrical sheath around a central artery, mainly containing T helper cells, like PALS. (Option C) - B cells form follicles at the edge of the PALS, often showing germinal centers in younger individuals.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1772; Solution Pages 1784-1786","sourcePage":1772,"sourcePageEnd":1772},{"id":"anatomy-46-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":5,"question":"In a histological image of the thymus, which cell type is not present in the medulla? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thymocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Epithelial cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hassall's corpuscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Dendritic cells"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Thymocytes","explanation":"Thymocytes are primarily located in the cortex of the thymus and are not present in the medulla. - Cortex : The outer, more densely packed region of the thymus. Filled with immature T cells (thymocytes) , undergoing differentiation and maturation as they move towards the medulla. The cortex contains thymic nurse cells that support the maturing thymocytes. T cells express surface markers (like TCR, CD3, CD4, and CD8) during maturation. Macrophages are present to phagocytose apoptotic T cells, which fail to mature properly. - The outer, more densely packed region of the thymus. - Filled with immature T cells (thymocytes) , undergoing differentiation and maturation as they move towards the medulla. - The cortex contains thymic nurse cells that support the maturing thymocytes. - T cells express surface markers (like TCR, CD3, CD4, and CD8) during maturation. - Macrophages are present to phagocytose apoptotic T cells, which fail to mature properly. - Medulla : The inner, less densely populated region. Dominated by epithelial cells with pale-staining nuclei and large cytoplasm. (Option B) Contains Hassall's corpuscles , concentric structures made of keratinized epithelial cells, involved in T cell regulation. (Option C) Dendritic cells and macrophages in the medulla present self-antigens to maturing T cells, ensuring the elimination of self-reactive cells to prevent autoimmunity. (Option D) - The inner, less densely populated region. - Dominated by epithelial cells with pale-staining nuclei and large cytoplasm. (Option B) - Contains Hassall's corpuscles , concentric structures made of keratinized epithelial cells, involved in T cell regulation. (Option C) - Dendritic cells and macrophages in the medulla present self-antigens to maturing T cells, ensuring the elimination of self-reactive cells to prevent autoimmunity. (Option D) - The outer, more densely packed region of the thymus. - Filled with immature T cells (thymocytes) , undergoing differentiation and maturation as they move towards the medulla. - The cortex contains thymic nurse cells that support the maturing thymocytes. - T cells express surface markers (like TCR, CD3, CD4, and CD8) during maturation. - Macrophages are present to phagocytose apoptotic T cells, which fail to mature properly. - The inner, less densely populated region. - Dominated by epithelial cells with pale-staining nuclei and large cytoplasm. (Option B) - Contains Hassall's corpuscles , concentric structures made of keratinized epithelial cells, involved in T cell regulation. (Option C) - Dendritic cells and macrophages in the medulla present self-antigens to maturing T cells, ensuring the elimination of self-reactive cells to prevent autoimmunity. (Option D) - Epithelial Framework : Throughout the thymus, epithelial cells provide a structural scaffold for lymphocyte development and produce hormones like thymopoietin, which regulate T cell maturation and proliferation. - It is divided into lobules by connective tissue septa. - A protective blood-thymus barrier prevents antigens from entering, safeguarding T cell maturation. - With age, the thymus undergoes involution , where lymphoid tissue is replaced by fat, but it continues to produce mature T cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1772-1773; Solution Pages 1786-1788","sourcePage":1772,"sourcePageEnd":1773},{"id":"anatomy-46-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":6,"question":"A pathologist is examining a lymph node biopsy. Which of the following correctly identifies these structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"C- Capsule, E- Medulla, D- Cortex, A- Germinal centers"},{"letter":"B","text":"D- Capsule, E- Medulla, C- Cortex, A- Germinal centers"},{"letter":"C","text":"C- Capsule, A- Medulla, D- Cortex, E- Germinal centers"},{"letter":"D","text":"D- Capsule, A- Medulla, C- Cortex, E- Germinal centers"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. C- Capsule, A- Medulla, D- Cortex, E- Germinal centers","explanation":"Parts of the Lymph Node Structure Description Function Capsule - Outer covering of the lymph node - Provides structural support and Consists mainly of collagen fibers - contains the lymph node contents May contain some elastic fibers and smooth muscle Subcapsular sinus - Located just beneath the capsule - Initial site for incoming lymph filtration Receives afferent lymph vessels Cortex - Outer zone with densely packed - Site of B-lymphocyte activity lymphocytes - Contains lymphatic (superficial) - T-lymphocyte area follicles/nodules - Divided into (deep/paracortex) superficial (nodular) and deep (paracortex) regions Lymphatic follicles - Rounded areas within the cortex - Sites of B-lymphocyte proliferation Contain germinal centers when and differentiation activated Germinal centers - Lightly stained central region of - Site of B-cell proliferation and activated follicles - Contain selection - Indicates an active proliferating B-lymphocytes immune response (centroblasts and centrocytes) Medulla - Inner, lighter staining region - - Houses plasma cells and Contains medullary cords and macrophages - Site of antibody sinuses production and lymph filtration Hilum - Concave region where blood Provides vascular and lymphatic vessels enter/exit - Site where connections efferent lymph vessel leaves","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1773-1774; Solution Pages 1788-1791","sourcePage":1773,"sourcePageEnd":1774},{"id":"anatomy-46-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":7,"question":"A histologist is examining three different cartilage samples. Sample A shows a homogeneous basophilic matrix with groups of chondrocytes in cell nests. Sample B contains numerous branching and anastomosing fibers forming a network. Sample C lacks perichondrium and has chondrocytes arranged in single rows or groups with numerous collagen bundles. Which of the following correctly identifies these cartilage types?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A - Elastic, B - Hyaline, C - Fibrocartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"A - Hyaline, B - Elastic, C - Fibrocartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"A - Fibrocartilage, B - Hyaline, C - Elastic"},{"letter":"D","text":"A - Hyaline, B - Fibrocartilage, C - Elastic"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. A - Hyaline, B - Elastic, C - Fibrocartilage","explanation":"The table mentions characteristics of various type of cartilage: Types of Cartilage Characteristic Hyaline Cartilage (Most Elastic Cartilage Fibrocartilage Common) Matrix appearance Homogeneous, basophilic Contains elastic fiber Numerous collagen network bundles Chondrocyte arrangement Groups (cell nests/isogeno Similar to hyaline Single rows or isogenous us groups) groups Perichondrium Present (except in articular Present Absent cartilage) Main fiber type Type II collagen Elastic fibers Type I (primarily) and Type II collagen Main functions Structural support, forms Provides flexibility and Resists compression and articular surfaces resilience shearing, shock absorption Examples Costal cartilage, tracheal Auricle, epiglottis, auditory Intervertebral discs, rings, articular cartilage tube menisci","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1774-1775; Solution Pages 1791-1792","sourcePage":1774,"sourcePageEnd":1775},{"id":"anatomy-46-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Match the following types of collagen with their primary location: 1. Found in cartilage (Hyaline and Elastic Cartilage) a. Type I collagen 2. Major component of basement membranes b. Type II collagen 3. Predominant in bowel and blood vessels c. Type III collagen 4. Primary collagen in bones, tendons, and dermis d. Type IV collagen 1. Found in cartilage (Hyaline and Elastica.Cartilage) Type I collagen 2. Major component of basement membranes b. Type II collagen 3. Predominant in bowel and blood vessels c. Type III collagen 4. Primary collagen in bones, tendons, and d. Type dermis IV collagen **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-b, 2-d, 3-a, 4-c"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-d, 2-b, 3-c, 4-a"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 1-b, 2-d, 3-c, 4-a","explanation":"Types of Collagen Types of Collagen Locations Type I Skin, tendon, sclera, bone, fascia, dermis Type II Vitreous humor of eye, intervertebral disc, cartilage (Hyaline and Elastic Cartilage) Type III Skin, blood vessels, bowel Type IV Basement membrane","sourceRef":"Question Page 1775; Solution Pages 1792-1793","sourcePage":1775,"sourcePageEnd":1775},{"id":"anatomy-46-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A histologist is examining three different tissue samples. Sample A shows loosely arranged fibers with numerous cells and abundant ground substance. Sample B exhibits densely packed, parallel collagen bundles with elongated fibroblast nuclei. Sample C contains interwoven collagen fibers without a specific orientation. Which of the following correctly matches the samples to their connective tissue types?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A - Dense irregular, B - Loose connective, C - Dense regular"},{"letter":"B","text":"A - Loose connective, B - Dense regular, C - Dense irregular"},{"letter":"C","text":"A - Dense regular, B - Dense irregular, C - Loose connective"},{"letter":"D","text":"A - Loose connective, B - Dense irregular, C - Dense regular"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. A - Loose connective, B - Dense regular, C - Dense irregular","explanation":"The characteristics of various types of connective tissue is mentioned in the table below: Types of Connective Tissue Characteristic Loose Connective Tissue Dense Regular Connective Dense Irregular Tissue Connective Tissue Fiber arrangement Loosely arranged Densely packed, parallel Densely packed, arrangement interwoven without specific orientation Cell content Numerous fibroblasts and Few cells, mainly Few cells (fibroblasts) macrophages fibroblasts Ground substance Abundant Less in amount Less in amount Main function Bind different tissues Provide strength and Provide strength in together, allow tissue resistance to tensile forces multiple directions movement Examples Endomysium, subperiosteal Tendons, ligaments, deep Dermis, CT sheath of tissue, lamina propria of fascia, aponeuroses, muscle and nerves, GIT, hypodermis, central tendon of capsules of glands, sclera, mesentery diaphragm, dura mater periosteum","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1775-1776; Solution Pages 1793-1794","sourcePage":1775,"sourcePageEnd":1776},{"id":"anatomy-46-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A pathologist is analyzing a biopsy sample from a patient with suspected connective tissue disorder. Upon staining, she observes fibers that appear black with silver impregnation and form a delicate network throughout the tissue. Which of the following best describes these fibers?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type I collagen fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elastic fibers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Reticular fibers"},{"letter":"D","text":"Smooth muscle fibers"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Reticular fibers","explanation":"impregnation , which is a unique feature among connective tissue fibers. Also, reticular fibers anastomose and form networks. Type of Connective Tissue Fibers Characteristic Collagen Fibers Reticular Fibers Elastic Fibers Appearance under light - Wavy bundles - White on - Fine & anastomose to - Thin, branching fibers - microscope naked eye form network Single Staining characteristics - Pink with H&E; - Blue - Black with silver - Not visible with H&E; - with Masson's trichrome - impregnation (specific) - Black with Verhoeff's stain Red with Van Gieson - PAS positive - Purple with orcein stain Brown with Silver impregnation (Option A) Functions - Tensile strength - Structural support in - Elasticity and resilience lymphoid organs - Component of basement membrane Locations Widespread (e.g., dermis, Lymphoid organs, liver, Blood vessels, skin, lungs, tendons, ligaments) kidney, bone marrow elastic cartilage Elastic fibers (Option B) are not typically stained with silver. Smooth muscle fibers (Option D) are not relevant in this context, as they are not connective tissue fibers and do not have the characteristics described.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1776; Solution Pages 1794-1796","sourcePage":1776,"sourcePageEnd":1776},{"id":"anatomy-46-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following pairs correctly identifies the locations of Meissner's and Auerbach's plexus in the gastrointestinal tract, respectively?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers; Mucosa"},{"letter":"B","text":"Submucosa; Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers"},{"letter":"C","text":"Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers; Muscularis propri"},{"letter":"D","text":"Between serosa and longitudinal muscle layer; Between the submucosa and mucosa"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Submucosa; Between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers","explanation":"Meissner's plexus (submucosal plexus) is located in the submucosa, while Auerbach's plexus (myenteric plexus) is located between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. - The GI tract has four main layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis propria, and adventitia (serosa in peritoneal cavity). - Mucosa consists of: Epithelium Lamina propria Muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle for local movements). - Epithelium - Lamina propria - Muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle for local movements). - Submucosa is a layer of loose connective tissue with blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves. - Muscularis propria has smooth muscle arranged in: Inner circular layer Outer longitudinal layer Only stomach has an additional oblique layer. - Inner circular layer - Outer longitudinal layer - Only stomach has an additional oblique layer. - Adventitia/Serosa is an outer layer of loose tissue, containing major vessels, nerves, and fat. The serosa is the visceral peritoneum present on the entire GIT except esophagus. Adventitia is present over the esophagus. - The enteric nervous system controls gut function with the Meissner (submucosal) plexus and Auerbach (myenteric) plexus. Meissner's Plexus (Submucosal Plexus): Located in the submucosa. Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. Auerbach's Plexus (Myenteric Plexus): Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. - Meissner's Plexus (Submucosal Plexus): Located in the submucosa. Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. - Located in the submucosa. - Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. - Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. - Auerbach's Plexus (Myenteric Plexus): Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. - Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. - Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. - Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. - Epithelium - Lamina propria - Muscularis mucosae (smooth muscle for local movements). - Inner circular layer - Outer longitudinal layer - Only stomach has an additional oblique layer. - Meissner's Plexus (Submucosal Plexus): Located in the submucosa. Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. - Located in the submucosa. - Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. - Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. - Auerbach's Plexus (Myenteric Plexus): Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. - Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. - Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. - Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. - Located in the submucosa. - Contains parasympathetic ganglion cells and postganglionic sympathetic fibres. - Regulates mucosal glands and the smooth muscle of the muscularis mucosae. - Found between the circular and longitudinal muscle layers of the muscularis propria. - Contains larger clusters of parasympathetic ganglion cells. - Primarily controls smooth muscle contractions for peristalsis. Fig. Auerbach plexus between two sets of muscles","sourceRef":"Question Page 1776; Solution Pages 1796-1799","sourcePage":1776,"sourcePageEnd":1776},{"id":"anatomy-46-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Which two of the following statements about serous, mucinous, and mixed glands are correct? Serous glands produce a proteinaceous, enzyme-rich watery fluid, while mucinous glands secrete mucopolysaccharides that form a thick, viscous mucus. Cells of serous acini are triangular with centrally placed rounded nuclei, whereas cells of mucous acini are tall with flat nuclei at their bases. The pancreas is an example of a mixed gland, containing both serous and mucous secreting acini. The lumen of mucous acini is typically smaller than that of serous acini.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1 and 2 are correct"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 3 are correct"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 and 4 are correct"},{"letter":"D","text":"1 and 4 are correct"},{"letter":"E","text":"g. goblet cells, and minor salivary glands of the tongue and palate. Fig: Histology of serous and mucinous glands Fig: Histology of mixed glands The other options B, C, and D are incorrect as they either misrepresent the functions or locations of these glands."}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1 and 2 are correct","explanation":"Serous gland Mucous gland Mixed gland The secretions of serous glands are In mucous glands, the secretion The gland contains acini that secrete proteinaceous in nature. Cells contains mucopolysaccharides . both mucus and serous secretions secrete an enzyme-rich watery fluid. Cells secrete mucus which is a thick, E.g. submandibular and sublingual Cells of serous acini are triangular viscous, and jelly-like protective glands. with a rounded nucleus. Their nuclei lubricant known as mucin. Cells of are centrally placed. E.g. pancreas. mucous acini are tall with flat nuclei at their bases. The lumen of these acini is larger than the serous acini E. g. goblet cells, and minor salivary glands of the tongue and palate. Fig: Histology of serous and mucinous glands Fig: Histology of mixed glands The other options B, C, and D are incorrect as they either misrepresent the functions or locations of these glands.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1776-1777; Solution Pages 1799-1800","sourcePage":1776,"sourcePageEnd":1777},{"id":"anatomy-46-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which of the following histological features is characteristic of Barrett's esophagus?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Squamous cell hyperplasia"},{"letter":"B","text":"Columnar epithelium with intestinal metaplasia"},{"letter":"C","text":"Columnar epithelium with gastric metaplasia"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glandular adenomatous hyperplasia"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Columnar epithelium with intestinal metaplasia","explanation":"This is the hallmark of Barrett's esophagus, indicating a transformation from squamous to columnar epithelium due to chronic irritation. - Barrett's esophagus occurs when chronic reflux esophagitis causes the stratified squamous epithelium of the lower esophagus to be replaced by columnar epithelium . - Intestinal metaplasia is present, with columnar epithelium containing goblet cells at the lower esophagus. - The Z-line (where squamous transitions to columnar epithelium) is normally at the gastroesophageal junction but may shift up to 5 cm proximally , increasing the risk of esophageal cancer . - Parietal cells may be found in stomach biopsies, but goblet cells are not present in the stomach. Fig: Histology picture of Barrett’s esophagus Squamous cell hyperplasia (Option A): This is typically not a feature of Barrett's esophagus; rather, it refers to an increase in the number of squamous cells, which occurs in response to irritation. Columnar epithelium with gastric metaplasia (Option C): Barrett’s esophagus specifically involves intestinal metaplasia, not gastric metaplasia. Intestinal metaplasia is defined by the presence of goblet cells. Glandular adenomatous hyperplasia (Option D): While Barrett's esophagus can predispose to dysplasia and adenocarcinoma, the primary change is intestinal metaplasia, not hyperplasia of glandular structures.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1777; Solution Pages 1800-1802","sourcePage":1777,"sourcePageEnd":1777},{"id":"anatomy-46-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following structures in the human body is primarily lined by stratified squamous epithelium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lumen of the trachea"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skin surface"},{"letter":"C","text":"Gastric lumen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Alveoli of lungs"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Skin surface","explanation":"- The outer layer of the skin (epidermis) is composed of keratinized stratified squamous epithelium, providing a barrier against environmental damage. - It is characterized by multiple layers of cells, providing protection against abrasion, pathogens, and chemical exposure. It is particularly well-suited for areas subject to friction and wear. There are seven openings that are divided into two categories which are lined by stratified squamous epithelium: - Four on the face: Nasal cavity Oral cavity Eye Ear - Nasal cavity - Oral cavity - Eye - Ear - Three on the perineum: Urethra Vagina Anal canal - Urethra - Vagina - Anal canal - Nasal cavity - Oral cavity - Eye - Ear - Urethra - Vagina - Anal canal Openings on the face Description Ear The external ear cavity is lined by Stratified squamous epithelium Middle ear cavity, lined by stratified cuboidal epithelium The middle ear cavity and external ear cavity are separated by the tympanic membrane. This tympanic membrane has two epitheliums: Inner layer: Simple cuboidal epithelium Outer layer: Stratified squamous epithelium Eye The first layer of the cornea is lined by stratified squamous epithelium Nasal cavity Lined by Stratified squamous epithelium. It is lined with skin The nasal vestibule is lined by the skin with hair: Stratified squamous epithelium Oral cavity It is discussed in 2 parts: Tongue, hard palate Tonsils, epiglottis Openings of perineum Description Urethra It is lined by stratified squamous epithelium Only the tip of the male urethra is lined by stratified squamous epithelium The rest of the male urethra is mostly lined by stratified columnar epithelium. Vagina The transition from stratified squamous epithelium occurs at the cervix , where the vaginal epithelium meets the simple columnar epithelium of the endocervix at the squamocolumnar junction . Anal canal Simple columnar epithelium above the pectinate line. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium below the pectinate line. Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium at the anal verge, continuous with the skin. Lumen of the trachea (Option A): Lined by ciliated pseudostratified columnar epithelium, which helps trap and move particles. Gastric lumen (Option C): Lined by simple columnar epithelium, which is specialized for secretion and absorption in the stomach. Alveoli in the lungs (Option D) Lined by simple squamous epithelium, allowing for efficient gas exchange.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1777; Solution Pages 1802-1804","sourcePage":1777,"sourcePageEnd":1777},{"id":"anatomy-46-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":15,"question":"Which of the following best describes transitional epithelium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is specialized for absorption and secretion and consists of a single layer of cuboidal cells."},{"letter":"B","text":"It is found in the skin and is composed of multiple layers of flat cells that provide a protective barrier."},{"letter":"C","text":"It is found in the urinary bladder and ureters, consisting of multiple layers of cells that can stretch and change shape."},{"letter":"D","text":"It lines the digestive tract and is characterized by a single layer of columnar cells that facilitate nutrient absorption."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. It is found in the urinary bladder and ureters, consisting of multiple layers of cells that can stretch and change shape.","explanation":"cells that can stretch and change shape. - Transitional epithelium is found in the renal pelvis and calyces, the ureter, the urinary bladder, and prostatic urethra. - Because of this distribution, it is also called urothelium. This is a multilayered epithelium with four to six thick cells that can contract and expand as needed (stretchable epithelium). Features of transitional epithelium: - Cells towards the lumen are very large and called umbrella-shaped cells (typical for transitional epithelium). - Each cell is attached to the basement membrane by a cytoplasmic extension. The basement membrane is straight. - It is exclusively located in the urinary system and is named as urothelium. - It is called transitional because it is transitional between stratified squamous and stratified columnar epithelia. It differs from stratified squamous epithelium in that the cells at the surface are not squamous. - The deepest cells are columnar or cuboidal. The middle layers are made up of polyhedral or pear-shaped cells. - The superficial cell layer, which lines the lumen, is the only fully differentiated layer of the epithelium. It provides an impenetrable barrier between the lumen and the bloodstream. The epithelium most specialized for absorption and secretion would be simple columnar epithelium (Option A) , which lines organs involved in these processes, such as the intestines and glands. Transitional epithelium is not found in the skin; the skin is lined by stratified squamous epithelium (Option B). The digestive tract (Option D) is lined by different types of epithelium depending on the region. Transitional epithelium is not found in the digestive tract; it is found in the urinary system .","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1777-1778; Solution Pages 1804-1805","sourcePage":1777,"sourcePageEnd":1778},{"id":"anatomy-46-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which of the following locations contains pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oral cavity"},{"letter":"B","text":"Esophagus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Respiratory tract"},{"letter":"D","text":"Uterine cervix"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Respiratory tract","explanation":"The respiratory tract, including the trachea and bronchi, is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium, which helps move mucus. Stratified squamous epithelium Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium Multiple layers of cells undergo maturation from a True simple epithelium. All cells rest on the basement cuboidal basal layer to a flattened surface layer. Basal membrane, but the nuclei are at varying levels, creating cells continuously divide and migrate to the surface where an illusion of multiple layers. Cells exhibit polarity with they become anucleate squames. It is present in the nuclei mainly in the basal two-thirds. regions which need protection It is of two types Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium Stratified squamous keratinized epithelium Cilia are never present on true stratified epithelia. Lines the oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, anal canal, Respiratory epithelium in the respiratory tract. (Option C) uterine cervix and vagina. (Options A, B, & D ruled out) Cilia is present to move the mucus","sourceRef":"Question Page 1778; Solution Pages 1805-1806","sourcePage":1778,"sourcePageEnd":1778},{"id":"anatomy-46-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Match the following: 1. Simple squamous epithelium a) Proximal convoluted tubule 2. Simple cuboidal epithelium b) Gallbladder 3. Simple columnar epithelium c) Femoral Endothelium 1. Simple squamous epithelium a) Proximal convoluted tubule 2. Simple cuboidal epithelium b) Gallbladder 3. Simple columnar epithelium c) Femoral Endothelium **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1- a, 2-b, 3-c"},{"letter":"B","text":"1- c, 2-a, 3-b"},{"letter":"C","text":"1- b, 2-c, 3-a"},{"letter":"D","text":"1- b, 2-a, 3-c"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 1- c, 2-a, 3-b","explanation":"Simple epithelia consist of a single layer of cells and are primarily found at interfaces where selective diffusion, absorption, and secretion occur. They are of three types. Types of Simple epithelium Description Simple squamous epithelium Pavemented epithelium Flattened, irregularly shaped cells forming a continuous surface. Lining surfaces of gas and fluid exchange Lining pleural, pericardial, and peritoneal cavities to enable fluid exchange and reduce friction. Peritoneum (mesothelium) Vascular endothelium Simple cuboidal epithelium The cells are polygonal and nucleus is round located in the center of the cell. Usually lines small ducts and tubules that have excretory, secretory or absorptive functions Collecting tubules of the kidney Small excretory ducts of the salivary glands and pancreas. Microvilli- PCT of kidney Simple columnar epithelium Tall columnar cells with the nuclei being located in the middle of the cells. Non-ciliated cells have secretory function whereas ciliated cells help in transport. Non-ciliated- Gallbladder lining. Microvilli- Small intestine Surface cilia- Fallopian tube Pseudostratification- Respiratory tract Goblet cells- Small and large bowel Stereocilia- Vas deferens","sourceRef":"Question Page 1778; Solution Pages 1806-1807","sourcePage":1778,"sourcePageEnd":1778},{"id":"anatomy-46-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Given below are two histological images 1 and 2, which of the following pairs accurately represents the tissues depicted?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1- Stomach; 2- Lymph node"},{"letter":"B","text":"1- Urinary Bladder; 2- Trachea"},{"letter":"C","text":"1- Lymph node; 2- Stomach"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-Trachea; 2- Urinary Bladder"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 1- Urinary Bladder; 2- Trachea","explanation":"Trachea Urinary Bladder Epithelium : Lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar Epithelium: Lined with transitional epithelium , which epithelium, goblet cells, serous cells, and basal cells forms many folds when the bladder is in a relaxed state. (neuroendocrine and stem cells). Ciliated columnar cells The bladder wall consists of three smooth muscle layers – are more abundant in the lower trachea, while goblet and inner longitudinal, outer circular, and outermost basal cells are more common in the upper trachea. longitudinal, known collectively as the detrusor muscle. Lamina Propria : Loose, highly vascular connective tissue These layers of smooth muscle and elastic fibers contract beneath the epithelium, becoming more condensed at during micturition . Muscularis Mucosa : Separate the deeper levels with fibroelastic tissue. Submucosa : lamina propria from the submucosa, but it is often Contains seromucinous glands that decrease in number incomplete and not always visible. Adventitia: The outer toward the lower trachea; serous cells stain strongly, adventitial coat contains arteries, veins, and lymphatic mucous cells stain poorly with H&E.; Hyaline cartilage vessels. rings support the trachea, with the submucosa merging into the perichondrium or fibroelastic tissue between the rings. Stomach (Option A & C): The stomach is lined by simple columnar epithelium with gastric pits and glands, including surface mucous cells, mucous neck cells, parietal cells, and chief cells. It would not typically show the features of the urinary bladder or trachea . Fig. Histology of stomach Lymph node (Option A & C): A lymph node features a capsule, cortex with lymphatic follicles, and a medulla with medullary cords and sinuses. It does not match the typical histological features of the trachea or urinary bladder. Fig. Histology of lymph node","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1778-1780; Solution Pages 1807-1808","sourcePage":1778,"sourcePageEnd":1780},{"id":"anatomy-46-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-46","chapter":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topic":"Lesson 46 - Tissue Histology- I","topicIndex":46,"questionNumber":19,"question":"Which of the following structures is characterized by the presence of compound tubuloalveolar mucous glands in the submucosa, and a muscular layer that transitions from skeletal to smooth muscle?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Esophagus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Stomach"},{"letter":"C","text":"Small intestine"},{"letter":"D","text":"Both A & C are correct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Esophagus","explanation":"The esophagus is lined with nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium and contains mucous glands (esophageal glands proper) in its submucosa for lubrication. Its muscular layer transitions from skeletal muscle (upper third) to smooth muscle (lower third), characteristic of the process of swallowing. Structure Description Esophagus Has four layers: mucosa, submucosa, muscularis externa, and adventitia. Lumen lined with nonkeratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Submucosa - compound tubuloalveolar mucous glands, known as esophageal glands proper, which aid in lubrication. The upper third of the esophagus has predominantly skeletal muscle due to voluntary control during swallowing. In the middle third, there is a gradual transition to smooth muscle, and the lower third consists entirely of smooth muscle. Lacks a serous covering, except for a short section near its lower end. Stomach (Option B ruled out) The epithelium-forming glands are supported by Lamina Propria, a connective tissue. It has a peritoneum called serosa, which is missing from the oesophagus. Lined by Simple columnar epithelium and forms some gastric glands Mucosa contains gastric pits or gastric foveolae. The gastric glands contain: Surface mucous cells- Bicarbonate ions Mucous neck cells - mucus Parietal (Oxyntic) cells- HCL & Intrinsic Factor. Chief, peptic or zymogenic cells- Pepsin Neuroendocrine cells- Serotonin & Gastrin hormone. Small Intestine (Option C & D ruled out) Small intestine has columnar epithelium, which has villi, and microvilli leading to formation of brush border epithelium. The intestinal villi are lined by a simple columnar epithelium which is continuous with the crypts. Plicae circulares (Valves of Kerckring) are circular folds given by the mucosa and submucosa. Microvilli are finger-like projections to increase the surface area for absorption. Lymphoid clusters called Peyer’s patches are characteristically found in the lamina propria of the small intestine. Cells in the mucosa are: Enterocytes Paneth cells- defence Goblet cells- Mucin Neuroendocrine cells- hormones","sourceRef":"Question Page 1780; Solution Pages 1808-1810","sourcePage":1780,"sourcePageEnd":1780},{"id":"anatomy-47-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":1,"question":"A 28-year-old patient with recurrent abdominal pain undergoes an endoscopic biopsy of the small intestine. The pathologist observes cells with prominent eosinophilic secretory granules located in the deeper parts of the intestinal crypts. These cells are known to produce substances that destroy bacteria. Which of the following cell types is the pathologist most likely observing as shown below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Enterocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"M cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Paneth cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Enterochromaffin cells"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Paneth cells","explanation":"The cells located in the deeper parts of the intestinal crypts having prominent eosinophilic secretory granules are Paneth cells , which produce lysozyme that destroys bacteria and other microorganisms in the small intestine. Cells in Small Intestine Cell Type Location Function Characteristics Absorptive Columnar Cells Luminal surface (villi) Absorption of amino acids, - Oval nucleus in lower (Enterocytes) (Option A) carbohydrates, and lipids part - Striated border (microvilli) - Junctional complexes and desmosomes Goblet Cells Scattered among Mucus secretion - Expanded upper part columnar cells, increasing with mucin granules - in number towards ileum Flattened nucleus near base - Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum Undifferentiated Cells Walls of intestinal crypts - Proliferate and migrate - Columnar cells to villi - Differentiate into absorptive or goblet cells Paneth Cells (Zymogen Deeper parts of intestinal - Produce lysozyme - Prominent eosinophilic Cells) crypts (destroys bacteria) - May secretory granules - produce other enzymes Considerable Golgi apparatus - Rough endoplasmic reticulum Endocrine Cells (Argentaffin/ Epithelial lining, most Secrete endocrine - Membrane-bound Enterochromaffin Cells) numerous near lower substances vesicles with neuroactive (Option D) ends of crypts substances - Granules stain with silver salts M Cells (Option B) Overlying Peyer's patches - Engulf and transport - Microfolds instead of microbes to basolateral microvilli surface - Stimulate gut-associated lymphoid tissue (GALT) -Antigen-pre senting function","sourceRef":"Question Page 1811; Solution Pages 1822-1823","sourcePage":1811,"sourcePageEnd":1811},{"id":"anatomy-47-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Match the following: 1. Cervix a) 2. Fallopian tube b) 3. Ovary c) 4. Uterus d) 5. Vagina e) 1. Cervix a) 2. Fallopian tube b) 3. Ovary c) 4. Uterus d) 5. Vagina e) **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1-b, 2-c, 3-e, 4-a, 5-d"},{"letter":"B","text":"1-d, 2-e, 3-a, 4-c, 5-b"},{"letter":"C","text":"1-c, 2-b, 3-e, 4-d, 5-a"},{"letter":"D","text":"1-b, 2-e, 3-a, 4-c, 5-d"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 1-d, 2-e, 3-a, 4-c, 5-b","explanation":"Structures of the Female Reproductive Tract Ovary The ovary's cortex contains numerous follicles at different developmental stages, along with postovulatory structures like corpora lutea and degenerated follicles . The superficial cortex is fibrous, known as the tunica albuginea, and is covered by germinal epithelium, a continuation of the peritoneum. The highly vascular medulla supports the ovarian structure. Fallopian Tube Mucosal lining with branching longitudinal folds , especially prominent in the ampulla, the usual site of fertilization. The wall consists of an inner circular and outer longitudinal smooth muscle layer, while the serosa, continuous with the broad ligament, has a mesothelial surface. The epithelium is composed of ciliated, non-ciliated secretory , and intercalated cells. Ciliated cells help propel the ovum with their wave-like motion, while non-ciliated cells secrete a fluid that nourishes and protects the ovum. The ratio of ciliated to non-ciliated cells varies with ovarian hormones, and intraepithelial lymphocytes are present. Uterus The uterus is a pear-shaped organ about 7 cm long, with a thick myometrium that provides support and expels the fetus during birth. The endometrium, measuring 1 to 5 mm, varies in thickness throughout the menstrual cycle and supports fetal development. The endometrium has three distinct layers: the stratum basalis (deepest, stable layer), the stratum spongiosum (intermediate, spongy appearance), and the stratum compactum (superficial, compact layer). The stratum functionalis , comprising the compact and spongy layers, undergoes significant changes and is shed during menstruation. Cervix The cervix consists of the endocervical canal lined by tall columnar mucus-secreting cells and the ectocervix lined by thic k stratified squamous epithelium , adapted to different environments. The squamocolumnar junction between the ectocervix and endocervical epithelium is located at the external os and marks an abrupt transition. The cervical stroma is primarily composed of collagenous tissue with some smooth muscle and is infiltrated by leukocytes for defense against infection. Hormonal changes during the menstrual cycle affect mucus secretion, with thinner mucus facilitating sperm passage around ovulation and thicker mucus forming a protective plug post-ovulation. Vagina The vaginal wall consists of a mucosal layer lined by stratified squamous epithelium , a sm ooth muscle layer , and an outer adventitial layer. The lamina propria is rich in elastin and small veins but lacks glands. Lubrication comes from cervical mucus and secretions from the labia minora. The muscle layer has ill-defined inner circular and outer longitudinal arrangements, allowing for distension during childbirth. The epithelium undergoes cyclical changes in glycogen levels, which is metabolized by bacteria to produce lactic acid, inhibiting pathogenic growth.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1811-1813; Solution Pages 1823-1824","sourcePage":1811,"sourcePageEnd":1813},{"id":"anatomy-47-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Which of the following statements about the structure of sperm is true?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The head of sperm contains a central core of axoneme."},{"letter":"B","text":"The principal piece of the tail contains hyaluronidase."},{"letter":"C","text":"The neck of the sperm contains centrioles."},{"letter":"D","text":"The tail contains the nucleus of the sperm."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The neck of the sperm contains centrioles.","explanation":"The neck of the sperm contains centrioles, which play a crucial role in cell division and in forming the axoneme of the flagellum. Structure of Sperm Head Approximately 7 µm long with a flattened pear shape. Nucleus: Contains very condensed chromatin with areas of dispersed chromatin known as nuclear vacuoles. (Option D) Acrosomal Cap: Surrounds the anterior two-thirds of the nucleus; a membrane-bound vesicle containing glycoproteins and hydrolytic enzymes (e.g., hyaluronidase) that assist in fertilization by dissolving the zona pellucida. (Option B) Neck This is a short segment linking the head to the tail. Contain remnants of centrioles , one of which forms the axoneme of the flagellum. Often retains some cytoplasmic material in this region. Tail Divided into three parts: Middle piece, principal piece, and end piece. Middle Piece: Contains the flagellar axoneme surrounded by nine outer dense fibres and tightly packed elongated mitochondria arranged in a helix for energy. Principal Piece: Constitutes most of the tail's length; has a central core with axoneme and coarse fibres, surrounded by fibrous ribs arranged circularly. Features dorsal and ventral columns for potential propulsion during movement. (Option A) End Piece: Short tapering portion containing only the axoneme.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1813-1814; Solution Pages 1824-1825","sourcePage":1813,"sourcePageEnd":1814},{"id":"anatomy-47-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Which of the following statements about the structure of the testis is FALSE?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The testis is surrounded by the tunica vaginalis."},{"letter":"B","text":"The seminiferous tubules produce sperm."},{"letter":"C","text":"The ductuli efferentes carry sperm from the epididymis to the rete testis."},{"letter":"D","text":"The blood-testis barrier is formed by sertoli cells."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. The ductuli efferentes carry sperm from the epididymis to the rete testis.","explanation":"The ductuli efferentes carry sperm from the rete testis to the epididymis , not the other way around. - Testis descends from the posterior peritoneal cavity wall to the scrotum during embryological development. - Tunica Vaginalis: Testis is surrounded by a double layer of mesothelium (visceral and parietal layers), enclosing a potential space filled with serous fluid for lubrication. (Option A) - Tunica Albuginea: Capsule of the testis, forms incomplete collagenous septa dividing the testis into ~250 lobules. - Seminiferous Tubules: 1-4 convoluted tubes in each lobule, responsible for sperm production. (Option B) - Rete Testis: Convergence point for seminiferous tubules. - Ductuli Efferentes: 15-20 small ducts carry spermatozoa from the rete testis to the epididymis. Blood-Testis Barrier: - The blood-testis barrier is formed by Sertoli cells, which are connected by junctional complexes, including extensive tight junctions located towards the basal layer of the spermatogenic epithelium. (Option D) - This barrier divides the seminiferous tubule into two compartments: the basal and the adluminal compartments. The adluminal compartment contains spermatids, isolating them from the systemic circulation. - The primary function of the blood-testis barrier is to protect developing gametes, which are antigenically different from somatic cells, from exposure to the immune system, thereby preventing an autoimmune response. - Sertoli cells mediate all metabolic exchanges with the systemic compartment, ensuring the proper development of sperm.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1814; Solution Pages 1825-1827","sourcePage":1814,"sourcePageEnd":1814},{"id":"anatomy-47-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following correctly describes the epithelial lining at the prostatic urethra?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Keratinized stratified squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Transitional epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Stratified columnar epithelium"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium","explanation":"male urethra is lined by non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium. Region of Male Urinary Tract Epithelial Lining Urinary bladder/Ureter Transitional epithelium Prostatic urethra Transitional epithelium Membranous urethra Stratified columnar epithelium Spongy/penile urethra Stratified columnar interspersed with patches of pseudostratified columnar/stratified squamous nonkeratinized epithelium","sourceRef":"Question Page 1814; Solution Pages 1827-1828","sourcePage":1814,"sourcePageEnd":1814},{"id":"anatomy-47-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Which part of the nephron is characterized by cuboidal epithelial cells with a prominent brush border?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Renal corpuscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Proximal convoluted tubule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Loop of Henle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Distal convoluted tubule"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Proximal convoluted tubule","explanation":"The PCT is characterized by cuboidal epithelial cells with a prominent brush border and is responsible for reabsorbing about 65-80% of the filtered solutes and water. Part of Nephron Epithelial Lining Key Feature Main Function Renal Corpuscle Podocytes and simple Contains glomerulus Blood filtration squamous Proximal Convoluted Cuboidal with brush border Prominent brush border Major solute and water Tubule reabsorption Loop of Henle Simple squamous U-shaped structure Urine concentration (descending) to cuboidal (ascending) Distal Convoluted Tubule Cuboidal without brush Basal striations Electrolyte balance border Collecting Duct Cuboidal (principal and Responsive to ADH Final urine concentration intercalated cells) Option A: The renal corpuscle contains the glomerulus and Bowman's capsule, responsible for blood filtration. It does not have cuboidal cells with a brush border. Option C: The loop of Henle has varying epithelial types (simple squamous to cuboidal) depending on the segment, but does not have a prominent brush border. Option D: The DCT has cuboidal epithelial cells but lacks a brush border.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1814; Solution Page 1828","sourcePage":1814,"sourcePageEnd":1814},{"id":"anatomy-47-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which of the following liver units is considered the most relevant functional unit for understanding patterns of toxic liver injury?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Classic lobule"},{"letter":"B","text":"Portal lobule"},{"letter":"C","text":"Portal acinus"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sinusoidal unit"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Portal acinus","explanation":"units: Classic Lobule: (Option A) - Hexagonal shape with central vein and portal triads at corners - Represents the basic structural unit of the liver Portal Lobule: (Option B) - Triangular shape with portal triad at center and central veins at corners - Emphasizes bile drainage Portal Acinus: - Diamond-shaped unit between two portal triads - Divided into three zones based on oxygen and nutrient supply: Zone 1 (periportal): Most oxygenated, more affected by toxins, least susceptible to ischaemia Zone 2 (midlobular): Intermediate oxygenation, Intermediate affected by toxins, Intermediate susceptible to ischaemia Zone 3 (centrilobular): Farthest from blood supply, least susceptible to toxicity, more susceptible to ischaemia - Zone 1 (periportal): Most oxygenated, more affected by toxins, least susceptible to ischaemia - Zone 2 (midlobular): Intermediate oxygenation, Intermediate affected by toxins, Intermediate susceptible to ischaemia - Zone 3 (centrilobular): Farthest from blood supply, least susceptible to toxicity, more susceptible to ischaemia - Most relevant for understanding toxic liver injury due to its correlation with metabolic zonation - Zone 1 (periportal): Most oxygenated, more affected by toxins, least susceptible to ischaemia - Zone 2 (midlobular): Intermediate oxygenation, Intermediate affected by toxins, Intermediate susceptible to ischaemia - Zone 3 (centrilobular): Farthest from blood supply, least susceptible to toxicity, more susceptible to ischaemia Sinusoidal unit ( Option D) is not a type of recognized liver unit.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1814-1815; Solution Pages 1828-1830","sourcePage":1814,"sourcePageEnd":1815},{"id":"anatomy-47-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 35-year-old patient with chronic hepatitis C undergoes a liver biopsy. The pathologist observes the classical hexagonal structure of liver lobules. Which of the following statements correctly regarding the blood flow and architecture within a liver lobule?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Blood flows from the central vein to the portal triad"},{"letter":"B","text":"The hepatic artery provides 70% of the liver's blood supply"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bile flows in the same direction as blood within the lobule"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sinusoids drain blood into the central vein"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Sinusoids drain blood into the central vein","explanation":"is in the direction as given below: Hepatic artery/Portal vein → Hepatic Sinusoids → Central vein → Hepatic vein→ Inferior vena cava → Heart Liver architecture and hepatic sinusoids Liver blood supply Dual blood supply: Portal vein (70%) and hepatic artery (30%) O2 supply for Liver : Hepatic artery ( 60 % ) > Portal vein ( 40 % ) Portal vein carries nutrient-rich blood from the gut Hepatic artery provides oxygenated blood Liver lobule structure Hexagonal shape with central vein in the middle Portal triads at the corners, containing branches of: a) Portal vein b) Hepatic artery c) Bile duct Blood flow within the lobule Blood enters the lobule from branches of the portal vein and hepatic artery in the portal triad Flows through sinusoids between plates of hepatocytes Sinusoids drain into the central vein (centripetal flow) Central veins unite to form hepatic veins, which ultimately drain into the inferior vena cava Components of Hepatic sinusoids Endothelial cells: Form the discontinuous lining Kupffer cells: Resident macrophages attached to the endothelium Space of Disse: Perisinusoidal space between endothelium and hepatocytes Hepatic stellate cells (Ito cells): Located in the space of Disse, primary storage site of Vitamin A. Blood flows from the portal triad towards the central vein. ( vs Option A) The portal vein provides about 70% of the blood supply ( vs Option B) , while the hepatic artery provides the remaining 30%. Bile flows in the opposite direction to blood ( vs Option C) , moving from hepatocytes towards the bile ducts in the portal triad.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1815; Solution Pages 1830-1831","sourcePage":1815,"sourcePageEnd":1815},{"id":"anatomy-47-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":9,"question":"A 55-year-old patient presents with rectal bleeding. During the examination, the proctologist identifies a lesion at the junction between the columnar epithelium and the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium in the anal canal. At which of the following anatomical landmarks is this lesion most likely located?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"White line of Hilton"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pectinate line"},{"letter":"C","text":"Internal anal sphincter"},{"letter":"D","text":"External anal sphincter"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pectinate line","explanation":"The lesion described is most likely located at the pectinate line (also known as the dentate line ). This is the junction between the columnar epithelium of the upper anal canal and the non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium of the middle anal canal ( pecten ). Region Length Epithelium Features Upper anal canal 15 mm Columnar epithelium 6-12 longitudinal folds called anal columns Between columns there are anal sinuses in which anal glands open Internal hemorrhoidal plexus in submucosa Pectinate (dentate) line - Transition zone Junction between columnar and non-keratiniz ed stratified squamous epithelium Formed by anal valves (lower ends of anal columns) Middle anal canal (Pecten) 15 mm Non-keratinized stratified Bluish appearance due to squamous epithelium dense venous plexus White line of Hilton (Option - Transition zone Lower limit of the pecten A) Junction between non-keratinized and keratinized epithelium Lower anal canal 8-10 mm Keratinized stratified Contains hair follicles, squamous epithelium (true sebaceous glands, and skin) sweat glands External hemorrhoidal plexus in submucosa Internal anal sphincter - - Formed by smooth (Option C) muscles present in muscularis externa External anal sphincter - - Formed by skeletal (Option D) muscles present in muscularis externa","sourceRef":"Question Page 1815; Solution Pages 1831-1833","sourcePage":1815,"sourcePageEnd":1815},{"id":"anatomy-47-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":10,"question":"A pathologist is examining a biopsy sample from a patient's large intestine. The sample shows a mucosa without villi, numerous goblet cells, and an abundance of lymphoid tissue in the submucosa. The muscularis externa lacks taenia coli. Which part of the large intestine is this sample most likely from?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cecum"},{"letter":"B","text":"Appendix"},{"letter":"C","text":"Colon"},{"letter":"D","text":"Rectum"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Appendix","explanation":"villi, numerous goblet cells, absence of taenia coli and an abundance of lymphoid tissue in the submucosa is most likely from the appendix. Features Cecum (Option A) Appendix Colon (Option C) Rectum (Option D) Villi Absent Absent Absent Absent Goblet cells Numerous Numerous Numerous Numerous Taenia coli Present Absent Present Absent Epithelium Simple columnar Simple columnar Simple columnar Simple columnar Distinctive features Widest part of large Abundant lymphoid Sacculations, Temporary and intestine tissue appendices epiploica permanent folds e","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1815-1816; Solution Page 1833","sourcePage":1815,"sourcePageEnd":1816},{"id":"anatomy-47-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Which of the following skin receptors is a rapidly adapting cell?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Merkel cell"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ruffini receptor"},{"letter":"C","text":"Free nerve endings"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pacinian corpuscle"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pacinian corpuscle","explanation":"Pacinian corpuscle is a rapidly adapting mechanoreceptor that detects vibration and deep pressure. Skin Receptors - Merkel cell - At basal layer for light touch sensation. - Meissner's corpuscle - Two-point discrimination. - Pacinian corpuscle - Carry pressure and vibration. - Ruffini receptor - Dermal stretch. - Free nerve endings - Conducts pain, crude touch and temperature. Rapidly adapting cells Slowly adapting cells Meissner corpuscle Pacinian corpuscle (Option D) Merkel cell (Option A) Ruffini receptor (Option B) Free nerve endings (Option C)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1816; Solution Pages 1833-1834","sourcePage":1816,"sourcePageEnd":1816},{"id":"anatomy-47-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A pathologist is examining biopsy samples from different parts of the small intestine. Which of the following histological features would most likely indicate that the sample is from the ileum?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Presence of numerous tall villi"},{"letter":"B","text":"Abundant Brunner's glands in the submucosa"},{"letter":"C","text":"Numerous large Peyer's patches"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thick circular folds in the lumen"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Numerous large Peyer's patches","explanation":"Numerous large Peyer's patches are a characteristic feature of the ileum . The table below discusses various histological features of small intestine: Feature Duodenum Jejunum Ileum Lumen and Wall Wider lumen, thicker wall Wider lumen, thicker wall Narrow lumen, thinner wall Circular Folds Large, thick circular folds Large, thick circular folds Small, few circular folds Villi Numerous broad villi with Numerous tall villi with Few, short and slender villi goblet cells goblet cells with goblet cells Brunner's Glands Present in submucosa Absent Absent Peyer's Patches Absent Very few, only in distal part Numerous, extending into mucosa Histology image Presence of numerous tall villi (Option A) : This is more characteristic of the jejunum, not the ileum. The ileum has fewer, shorter, and more slender villi. Brunner's glands (Option B) are absent in both the jejunum and ileum. Large, thick circular folds (Option D) are more prominent in the duodenum and jejunum. The ileum has smaller and fewer circular folds.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1816; Solution Pages 1834-1835","sourcePage":1816,"sourcePageEnd":1816},{"id":"anatomy-47-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A 45-year-old patient undergoes an endoscopic biopsy of the stomach due to persistent dyspepsia. Histological examination of the glandular tissue reveals cells that are columnar in shape, located in the lower half of the gland, and stain blue with hematoxylin. Which of the following statements is most likely true about these cells?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"They secrete hydrochloric acid and intrinsic factor"},{"letter":"B","text":"They produce mucus"},{"letter":"C","text":"They secrete pepsinogen"},{"letter":"D","text":"They release the hormone gastrin"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. They secrete pepsinogen","explanation":"The histological features describe chief cells of the gastric glands. - Chief cell/peptic cell/ zymogen cells stain blue with hematoxylin due to basophilic cytoplasm, are columnar in shape and located in the basal (lower) part of the gastric pit. Types of epithelium in stomach Region Epithelium Type Gastric Pits Characteristics Cardia Simple columnar Shallow Relatively thin mucosa Confined to small area near esophageal opening Fundus and Body Simple columnar Deep (occupying Thicker mucosa Contains superficial one-fourth or majority of gastric glands less of the mucosa) Pylorus Simple columnar Deep (occupying Thicker mucosa than two-thirds of the depth of cardia Contains the mucosa) specialized pyloric glands Gastric glands and their cells Cell Type Location Characteristics Function Chief cells (Peptic cells) Base of glands in fundus - Cuboidal or low columnar Secrete pepsinogen and body - Basophilic cytoplasm - (precursor of pepsin) Secretory granules in apical cytoplasm - Abundant rough endoplasmic reticulum Parietal cells (Oxyntic cells) Mostly in fundus and body - Large, ovoid or - Secrete hydrochloric acid polyhedral - Eosinophilic - Produce intrinsic factor cytoplasm - Narrow apical (Option A) part reaching lumen - Intracellular canaliculi - Numerous mitochondria Mucous neck cells Neck region of glands in - Large cells with clear Secrete mucus (Option B) fundus and body cytoplasm - Flattened nucleus pushed to base of cell G cells Mainly in pyloric glands - Endocrine cells - Secrete gastrin hormone Contains secretory (Option D) granules Endocrine cells (aka Scattered throughout - Near basal parts of - Secrete various argentaffin cells) gastric glands, more in gastric glands - Contain hormones including gastrin pylorus neurosecretory granules - - Some contain serotonin Flattened, don't reach (5HT) lumen Stem cells Isthmus of gastric glands Undifferentiated cells Replace other cell types, important for healing","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1816-1817; Solution Pages 1835-1837","sourcePage":1816,"sourcePageEnd":1817},{"id":"anatomy-47-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Which of the following statements with respect to histology of alveoli is INCORRECT? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"The most abundant cell type lines form 70-90% of the alveolar surface and are of simple squamous type"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type II alveolar epithelial cells are microvilli containing cells which secrete surfactant similar to that of Clara cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"In some alveolar area, basement membrane of the endothelium fuses with that of the alveolar epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Type I alveolar epithelial cells are involved in replacing the damaged type II alveolar cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Type I alveolar epithelial cells are involved in replacing the damaged type II alveolar cells","explanation":"alveolar cells Explanation: The statement is incorrect as type II alveolar epithelial cells are involved in replacing the damaged type I alveolar cells , not vice versa. Histological features of alveoli: General histological features Alveolar Wall Very thin, lined by simple squamous Allows for efficient gas exchange epithelium Connective Tissue Contains collagen and elastic fibers Provides structural support Capillary Endothelium Extremely thin cells lining alveolar Allows for efficient gas exchange capillaries ; Capillary basement membrane can fuse with alveolar basement membrane at certain places (Option C) Cells in Alveoli Type I Alveolar Epithelial Cells Simple squamous cells, cover 90% Main site of gas exchange of alveolar surface (Option A) Type II Alveolar Epithelial Cells Rounded secretory cells with Secrete pulmonary surfactant, can microvilli replace Type I cells (Option B) Type III Alveolar Cells (Brush Cells) Present in alveolar lining Function not fully understood Alveolar Macrophages (Dust Cells) Present in alveolar space Phagocytose particles, remove excess surfactant","sourceRef":"Question Page 1817; Solution Pages 1837-1838","sourcePage":1817,"sourcePageEnd":1817},{"id":"anatomy-47-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 45-year-old patient presents with chronic cough and shortness of breath. Bronchoscopy is performed, and a biopsy of the bronchial wall is taken. Histological examination reveals an epithelium with tall columnar cells bearing cilia on their apical surface, interspersed with goblet cells. Which part of the respiratory tract is most likely represented by this biopsy?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trachea"},{"letter":"B","text":"Terminal bronchioles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Respiratory bronchioles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Alveoli"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Trachea","explanation":"cells with cilia and interspersed goblet cells- is characteristic of the trachea and principal bronchi. Epithelium in Respiratory Tract Location Type of Epithelium Description Trachea and Main Bronchi Pseudostratified ciliated columnar Tall columnar cells with cilia, goblet cells present Bronchi and Bronchioles Simple ciliated columnar Single layer of ciliated columnar cells, fewer goblet cells Terminal Bronchioles (Option B) Simple ciliated columnar/cuboidal Single layer of cuboidal cells, some ciliated cells, no goblet cells, Clara cells Respiratory Bronchioles (Option C) Simple ciliated cuboidal to simple Single layer of ciliated cuboidal cells squamous or single layer of simple squamous, no goblet cells, Clara cells Alveoli (Option D) Simple squamous Thin, flat cells for efficient gas exchange","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1817-1818; Solution Pages 1838-1839","sourcePage":1817,"sourcePageEnd":1818},{"id":"anatomy-47-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":16,"question":"Which histological feature most accurately describes the epithelium responsible for mucociliary clearance in the upper respiratory tract?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinized"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Simple cuboidal epithelium with Clara cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Simple squamous epithelium with alveolar macrophages"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with goblet cells","explanation":"The epithelium responsible for mucociliary clearance in the upper respiratory tract is the respiratory epithelium. Respiratory epithelium - The respiratory epithelium is predominantly composed of pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium with numerous goblet cells in the trachea and larger bronchi, which is essential for mucociliary clearance. - This type of epithelium has the appearance of multiple layers due to the varying positions of nuclei, but all cells are in contact with the basement membrane, making it pseudostratified . - Goblet cells : These cells are responsible for producing mucus, which traps particles, a critical component of the mucociliary clearance system. - Cilia : Ciliated cells beat in a coordinated manner to propel mucus and trapped particles toward the pharynx, preventing the accumulation of foreign materials in the lower airways. The epithelial lining changes along the respiratory tract, becoming simpler (cuboidal or squamous) in the smaller airways and alveoli to facilitate gas exchange. Stratified squamous epithelium, non-keratinized (Option A): Found in areas subjected to mechanical stress, like the oropharynx, but lacks the cilia and goblet cells needed for mucociliary clearance. Simple cuboidal epithelium with Clara cells (Option C): Found in terminal bronchioles, where Clara cells secrete surfactant-like substances, but this epithelium is not responsible for the primary mucociliary clearance in the larger airways. Simple squamous epithelium with alveolar macrophages (Option D): Found in the alveoli, this epithelium is thin to facilitate gas exchange, while alveolar macrophages perform phagocytosis of debris. It does not participate in mucociliary clearance.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1818; Solution Pages 1839-1840","sourcePage":1818,"sourcePageEnd":1818},{"id":"anatomy-47-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":17,"question":"A 30-year-old man presents with difficulty swallowing and a mild hoarse voice. Examination reveals no significant findings, and imaging shows no abnormal masses in the oropharynx. A biopsy is taken from a structure in the oropharynx that histologically shows a mixture of stratified squamous epithelium and pseudostratified columnar epithelium. This structure also contains seromucous glands and an elastic cartilage core. Which of the following structures is most likely biopsied?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hard palate"},{"letter":"B","text":"Soft palate"},{"letter":"C","text":"Epiglottis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Vocal cords"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Epiglottis","explanation":"The structure biopsied in this scenario is the epiglottis. Histology of the Epiglottis: - The epiglottis is a leaf-shaped structure that serves as a flap during swallowing, closing the laryngeal inlet to prevent food from entering the airway. - Its upper surface is covered by stratified squamous epithelium (in the area exposed to mechanical wear from food), while the lower surface (facing the airway) has pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium (respiratory epithelium). - The core of the epiglottis contains elastic cartilage , which provides flexibility and support. - There are seromucous glands present, especially in the lamina propria, which help keep the surface moist. Epiglottis Histology of the Palate: - The hard palate is lined by keratinized stratified squamous epithelium on its oral side, with a bony core. It lacks elastic cartilage and respiratory epithelium. (Option A) - The soft palate has a surface of non-keratinized stratified squamous epithelium on the oral side, and pseudostratified columnar ciliated epithelium on the nasal side. Unlike the epiglottis, the core of the soft palate consists of skeletal muscle rather than cartilage. (Option B) Palate Vocal cords (Option D) : The true vocal cords are lined by stratified squamous epithelium , but they lack pseudostratified columnar epithelium and elastic cartilage. Instead, they have a ligamentous core (vocal ligament) and skeletal muscle (vocalis muscle).","sourceRef":"Question Page 1818; Solution Pages 1840-1842","sourcePage":1818,"sourcePageEnd":1818},{"id":"anatomy-47-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Identify the type of tissue shown in the image below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Smooth muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Skeletal muscle"},{"letter":"C","text":"Cardiac muscle"},{"letter":"D","text":"Purkinje fibers"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Cardiac muscle","explanation":"Skeletal Muscle Cardiac Muscle Smooth Muscle Longitudinal Section : Fibers are Composed of individual cells with Longitudinal Section : Fibers are long, parallel, and non-branching . centrally placed nuclei . Displays spindle-shaped with tapering ends . Characteristic transverse striations transverse striations. Clear Centrally located elongated nucleus . are visible. Multiple flat nuclei located perinuclear halo around the nucleus. No visible striations. Transverse at the periphery . Fibers are Cells are separated by intercalated Section : Spindle-shaped cells are separated by connective tissue discs (transverse lines). Fibers cut at varying lengths, producing (Epimysium, perimysium, exhibit branching. Blood vessels are different shapes and sizes. Nuclei endomysium). Transverse Section : present. Loose connective tissue is are visible in cells cut through the Fibers appear as irregularly round seen above and below muscle layers. center. Cells cut away from the structures with peripheral nuclei. center do not show nuclei. Muscle fibers are grouped into numerous fascicles. Myofibrils appear as small dots within fibers at higher magnification.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1818-1819; Solution Pages 1842-1843","sourcePage":1818,"sourcePageEnd":1819},{"id":"anatomy-47-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":19,"question":"A 45-year-old woman presents with signs of progressive muscle weakness and fatigue. She is diagnosed with a rare genetic disorder affecting intercellular communication in her skeletal and cardiac muscles. Genetic testing reveals a mutation in a connexin protein. Which of the following best describes the physiological consequence of this mutation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Impaired electrical signal propagation between cardiac myocytes"},{"letter":"B","text":"Disruption of epithelial barrier function"},{"letter":"C","text":"Reduced mechanical stability between cells"},{"letter":"D","text":"Abnormal attachment of muscle fibers to the extracellular matrix"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Impaired electrical signal propagation between cardiac myocytes","explanation":"Gap junctions, also called macula communicans, are specialized cell structures that facilitate direct communication between adjacent cells. They consist of channels formed by proteins called connexins, which allow ions, metabolites, and small molecules to pass between cells. Features of gap junctions: - Connexons : Each channel is made of six connexin proteins that align between two cells to create a passage for substances like sodium, potassium, calcium, and small molecules. - Electrical Communication : In tissues like cardiac and nerve cells, gap junctions enable rapid transmission of electrical impulses for synchronized function. - Regulation : The channels can close in response to changes in pH or calcium levels, controlling the communication between cells. Distribution : Gap junctions are widely found in the body, playing a key role in lowering electrical resistance and supporting functions like electrical synapses in neurons Disruption of epithelial barrier function (Option B) : This is characteristic of defects in tight junctions (zonula occludens), which prevent paracellular leakage between epithelial cells. Tight junctions are unrelated to connexin proteins. Reduced mechanical stability between cells (Option C) : This describes the role of desmosomes , which provide structural integrity by linking intermediate filaments between cells. Desmosomes are crucial for mechanical stress resistance but have no role in direct cell communication. Abnormal attachment of muscle fibers to the extracellular matrix (Option D) : This refers to hemidesmosomes or integrins , which anchor cells to the basement membrane or extracellular matrix. These structures are involved in cell adhesion rather than communication through gap junctions.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1819-1820; Solution Pages 1843-1844","sourcePage":1819,"sourcePageEnd":1820},{"id":"anatomy-47-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-47","chapter":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topic":"Lesson 47 - Tissue Histology- II","topicIndex":47,"questionNumber":20,"question":"In the provided image of epithelial cell junctions, identify the correct sequence of junctions from the apical to the basal surface: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Adherens junction, B- Tight junction, C- Desmosome, D- Hemidesmosome, E- Gap junction"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Tight junction, B- Adherens junction, C- Desmosome, D- Gap junction, E- Hemidesmosome"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Desmosome, B- Tight junction, C- Hemidesmosome, D- Adherens junction, E- Gap junction"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Hemidesmosome, B- Gap junction, C- Adherens junction, D- Tight junction, E- Desmosome"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. A- Tight junction, B- Adherens junction, C- Desmosome, D- Gap junction, E- Hemidesmosome","explanation":"Hemidesmosome Cell junctions are specialized membrane structures that connect adjacent cells or attach cells to the extracellular matrix. In epithelial cells, these junctions are organized in a specific pattern from the apical to the basal surface. - At the apical region, the cell membranes fuse to form a tight junction (zonula occludens) , which encircles the cells. - Below this is the zonula adherens , where proteins link to the cytoskeleton of both cells, separated by a small intercellular space with adhesion proteins. - Further down is a desmosome , a spotlike structure with attachment proteins connecting the cells. The sequence of junctions from apical to basal is: - Tight junctions (Zonula occludens) - Adherens junctions (Zonula adherens) - Desmosomes (Macula adherens) - Gap junctions - Hemidesmosomes Functions: - Tight junctions (zonula occludens) : Found in epithelia of the stomach, intestines, and bladder, these prevent the passage of substances between cells by fusing membranes with claudin proteins. - Zonula adherens : Links adjacent cells through cytoskeleton attachments, reinforced by actin filaments. - Desmosomes : Seen in skin and cardiac muscle, they prevent cell separation under mechanical stress by anchoring to intermediate filaments. - Hemidesmosomes : At the base of epithelial cells, anchoring them to the basement membrane and connective tissue. - Gap junctions : Channels (connexons) between cells allowing molecular and electrical communication, crucial for cardiac muscle and nerve function.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1820; Solution Pages 1844-1846","sourcePage":1820,"sourcePageEnd":1820},{"id":"anatomy-48-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Reticular fibers are not present in which of the following structures?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thymus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lymph node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bone marrow"},{"letter":"D","text":"Spleen"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Thymus","explanation":"Correct Option A - Thymus: - Reticular fibers are a type of connective tissue fibers composed of collagen type III. - They form a delicate network of fibers that provide structural support to various organs and tissues. - Reticular fibers are particularly abundant in lymphoid organs, where they form the framework for immune cell localization and function. - Unlike other lymphoid organs, the thymus does not contain reticular fibers. - Instead, it has a unique structural framework composed of epithelial cells called thymic epithelial cells. Incorrect Options - Reticular fibers are seen in options B, C, and D.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1847; Solution Page 1864","sourcePage":1847,"sourcePageEnd":1847},{"id":"anatomy-48-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":2,"question":"Histological section is given below. Identify the marked cell. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Stellate cell"},{"letter":"B","text":"Basket cell"},{"letter":"C","text":"Purkinje cell"},{"letter":"D","text":"Granular cell"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Purkinje cell","explanation":"The marked area in the image is a Purkinje cell. The cerebellar cortex consists of the following three distinct layers: Layers of the cerebellar cortex Molecular (plexiform) layer (Options Purkinje cell layer Granular layer (Option D) A, B) It primarily contains numerous It consists of a single row of large The inner granular layer is made up dendritic branches from Purkinje flask-shaped cells, the Purkinje cells . of many closely packed small cells, with relatively few widely granule cells , along with a few larger spaced nerve cells. Golgi cells. These nerve cells are of two types: The axons of Purkinje cells are the Golgi cells are inhibitory while the (a) Basket cells (b) Stellate cells. only output from the cerebellar cortex granular cells are excitatory. and have an inhibitory effect on the intracerebellar nuclei. Among the five types of intrinsic neurons (Basket cells, Stellate cells, Purkinje cells, Granule cells and Golgi cells)- All are inhibitory except granule cells.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1847-1848; Solution Pages 1864-1865","sourcePage":1847,"sourcePageEnd":1848},{"id":"anatomy-48-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":3,"question":"Identify the lymphatic structure from the histology section given below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Tonsil"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lymph node"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spleen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Thymus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Thymus","explanation":"The image shows a distinct lobulated structure with a darker outer cortex and a lighter inner medulla. This is characteristic of the thymus. - The outer, more densely packed region of the thymus. - Filled with immature T cells (thymocytes) , undergoing differentiation and maturation as they move towards the medulla. - The cortex contains thymic nurse cells that support the maturing thymocytes. - T cells express surface markers (like TCR, CD3, CD4, and CD8) during maturation. - Macrophages are present to phagocytose apoptotic T cells, which fail to mature properly. - The inner, less densely populated region. - Dominated by epithelial cells with pale-staining nuclei and large cytoplasm. - Contains Hassall's corpuscles , concentric structures made of keratinized epithelial cells, involved in T cell regulation. - Dendritic cells and macrophages in the medulla present self-antigens to maturing T cells, ensuring the elimination of self-reactive cells to prevent autoimmunity. - Epithelial Framework : Throughout the thymus, epithelial cells provide a structural scaffold for lymphocyte development and produce hormones like thymopoietin, which regulate T cell maturation and proliferation. - It is divided into lobules by connective tissue septa. - A protective blood-thymus barrier prevents antigens from entering, safeguarding T cell maturation. With age, the thymus undergoes involution , where lymphoid tissue is replaced by fat, but it continues to produce mature T cells. Tonsil (Option A): Tonsils contain lymphoid follicles with germinal centers. The histology of tonsils features these follicles, unlike the thymus. Lymph node (Option B): Lymph nodes also have lymphoid follicles but have a more defined capsule, cortex, paracortex, and medulla. Spleen (Option C): The spleen has a characteristic red pulp (containing blood) and white pulp (containing lymphocytes)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1848; Solution Pages 1865-1869","sourcePage":1848,"sourcePageEnd":1848},{"id":"anatomy-48-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":4,"question":"What is the type of cartilage present in the histology section given below? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hyaline cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elastic cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Articular cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibrocartilage"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Hyaline cartilage","explanation":"- The image shows a glassy, bluish-white matrix with chondrocytes in lacunae - Hyaline cartilage is the most common type. It has a glassy, translucent matrix due to the fine collagen fibrils. Chondrocytes (cartilage cells) are scattered throughout the matrix, often appearing in small groups (isogenous groups) within lacunae (spaces). Types of Cartilage Characteristic Hyaline Cartilage (Most Elastic Cartilage (Yellow Fibrocartilage (Option D) Common) cartilage) (Option B) Matrix appearance Homogeneous, basophilic Contains elastic fiber Numerous collagen network bundles Chondrocyte arrangement Groups (cell nests/isogeno Similar to hyaline Single rows or isogenous us groups) groups Perichondrium Present (except in articular Present Absent cartilage) Main fiber type Type II collagen Elastic fibers Type I (primarily) and Type II collagen Main functions Structural support, forms Provides flexibility and Resists compression and articular surfaces resilience shearing, shock absorption Examples Costal cartilage, tracheal Auricle, epiglottis, auditory Intervertebral discs, rings, articular cartilage tube menisci Articular cartilage (Option C): Articular cartilage is a specific type of hyaline cartilage covering the bones' surfaces in synovial joints. While it is a form of hyaline cartilage, the term \"articular\" refers to its location rather than a different type of cartilage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1848-1849; Solution Pages 1869-1873","sourcePage":1848,"sourcePageEnd":1849},{"id":"anatomy-48-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Match the receptors with their correct function: 1. Ruffini corpuscle A. Vibration 2. Merkel cells B. Sustained pressure 3. Pacinian corpuscle C. Stretching 4. Meissner’s corpuscle D. Fine touch 1. Ruffini corpuscle A. Vibration 2. Merkel cells B. Sustained pressure 3. Pacinian corpuscle C. Stretching 4. Meissner’s corpuscle D. Fine touch **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1C, 2B, 3A, 4D"},{"letter":"B","text":"1D, 2C, 3B, 4A"},{"letter":"C","text":"1B, 2A, 3D, 4C"},{"letter":"D","text":"1C, 2D, 3B, 4A"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. 1C, 2B, 3A, 4D","explanation":"Correct Option A - 1C, 2B, 3A, 4D: - Ruffini corpuscle (1C) : These are mechanoreceptors that respond to the stretching of the skin, which is crucial for the perception of the direction and amount of stretch. - Merkel cells (2B) : These are slowly adapting receptors that are sensitive to sustained pressure and texture, providing fine details about objects touching the skin. - Pacinian corpuscle (3A) : These are rapidly adapting receptors that detect vibration and deep pressure, essential for sensing rapid changes in the environment. - Meissner’s corpuscle (4D) : These receptors are sensitive to fine touch and low-frequency vibration, allowing the detection of textures and slight pressure changes. Incorrect Options: - Options B, C and D are marked incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1849-1850; Solution Pages 1873-1874","sourcePage":1849,"sourcePageEnd":1850},{"id":"anatomy-48-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Identify the markers in the given slide: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A- Eccrine sweat gland, B- Arrector pilorum, C- Sebaceous gland, D- Apocrine gland"},{"letter":"B","text":"A- Arrector pilorum, B- Eccrine sweat gland, C- Sebaceous gland, D- Apocrine gland"},{"letter":"C","text":"A- Eccrine sweat gland, B- Arrector pilorum, C- Apocrine gland, D-Sebaceous gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"A- Sebaceous gland, B- Apocrine gland, C- Eccrine sweat gland, D- Arrector pilorum"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. A- Eccrine sweat gland, B- Arrector pilorum, C- Sebaceous gland, D- Apocrine gland","explanation":"Correct Option A - A- Eccrine sweat gland, B- Arrector pilorum, C- Sebaceous gland, D- Apocrine gland: A - Eccrine Sweat Gland: Eccrine sweat glands are responsible for thermoregulation and are found throughout the body, primarily in the palms, soles, and forehead. They are characterized by their tubular structure and are often found deep in the dermis. B - Arrector Pilorum: These are small muscles attached to hair follicles. When they contract, they cause the hair to stand up, commonly known as piloerection. They are typically found alongside hair follicles. C - Sebaceous Gland: Sebaceous glands are responsible for producing sebum, an oily substance that lubricates the skin and hair. They are usually associated with hair follicles and appear as small clusters of cells in the dermis. D - Apocrine Gland: Apocrine sweat glands are primarily found in the axillary and genital regions. They secrete a thicker, milky fluid into hair follicles and are involved in scent production. These glands are larger than eccrine glands and have a more complex structure. Incorrect Options: Options B, C, and D: These options are incorrectly matched.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1850; Solution Pages 1874-1875","sourcePage":1850,"sourcePageEnd":1850},{"id":"anatomy-48-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":7,"question":"Which hormone is produced from the layer marked in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Androgen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Cortisol"},{"letter":"C","text":"Corticosterone"},{"letter":"D","text":"Epinephrine"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Epinephrine","explanation":"Correct Option D - Epinephrine: - The marked area is the adrenal medulla, which is responsible for producing catecholamines like norepinephrine and epinephrine. Incorrect Options: - Options A, B, and C are not produced in the adrenal medulla.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1850-1851; Solution Page 1875","sourcePage":1850,"sourcePageEnd":1851},{"id":"anatomy-48-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Which of the following is the lining epithelium of the trachea?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Simple squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transitional epithelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pseudostratified ciliated columnar","explanation":"Correct Option D - Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium: - The trachea is lined by pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium. Incorrect Options: Option A - Simple squamous epithelium: - Simple squamous epithelium is present in Mesothelium lining ventral body cavities Endothelial lining of the ventral heart and blood vessels Portion of kidney tubules (thin sections of nephron Henle loops) The inner lining of the cornea Alveoli of lungs - Mesothelium lining ventral body cavities - Endothelial lining of the ventral heart and blood vessels - Portion of kidney tubules (thin sections of nephron Henle loops) - The inner lining of the cornea - Alveoli of lungs - Mesothelium lining ventral body cavities - Endothelial lining of the ventral heart and blood vessels - Portion of kidney tubules (thin sections of nephron Henle loops) - The inner lining of the cornea - Alveoli of lungs Option B - Transitional epithelium: - Transitional epithelium is found in the urinary bladder. Option C - Stratified squamous epithelium: - Skin and esophagus are lined by stratified squamous epithelium.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1851; Solution Pages 1875-1876","sourcePage":1851,"sourcePageEnd":1851},{"id":"anatomy-48-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":9,"question":"The structures marked in the image below are found in the epithelial lining of all of the following sites, except: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Oviduct"},{"letter":"B","text":"Epididymis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Bronchi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Ependyma"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Epididymis","explanation":"Correct Option B - Epididymis: - The marked structures in the histological image are cilia. - Cilia are slender, hair-like structures found on the surface of many epithelial cells. - They play a vital role in the movement of fluids and particles along various passageways in the body. - While the epithelial lining of the epididymis does have stereocilia (long microvilli), it does not typically have cilia. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D have cilia in their epithelial lining.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1851-1852; Solution Pages 1876-1877","sourcePage":1851,"sourcePageEnd":1852},{"id":"anatomy-48-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which type of collagen is present in cartilage?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type 1 collagen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type 2 collagen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Type 3 collagen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Type 4 collagen"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Type 2 collagen","explanation":"Correct Option B - Type 2 collagen: - The cartilage tissue in our body is predominantly made of type 2 collagen fibre s. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1852; Solution Page 1877","sourcePage":1852,"sourcePageEnd":1852},{"id":"anatomy-48-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":11,"question":"Identify the given structure?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lymph node"},{"letter":"B","text":"Glomerulus"},{"letter":"C","text":"Spleen"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pancreatic islet cells"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Pancreatic islet cells","explanation":"Correct Option D - Pancreatic islet cells: - Pancreatic islet cells are clusters of endocrine cells within the pancreas that secrete hormones such as insulin and glucagon. - Pancreatic islet cells are also known as islets of Langerhans. - Each islet comprises a few distinctive sorts of cells, including alpha, beta, delta, and PP cells that work together to direct blood glucose levels. - The islet cells are organized in a round or unpredictably molded cluster and are encompassed by a thin layer of connective tissue. Incorrect Options - A, B, and C are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1852-1853; Solution Pages 1877-1878","sourcePage":1852,"sourcePageEnd":1853},{"id":"anatomy-48-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":12,"question":"Given below is a histological section of the thyroid gland. Which of the following is secreted by the cell marked ‘X’ in the image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Thyroxine"},{"letter":"B","text":"Calcitonin"},{"letter":"C","text":"PTH"},{"letter":"D","text":"Serotonin"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Calcitonin","explanation":"Correct Option B - Calcitonin: - The cell identified on the histological image of thyroid tissue is parafollicular cells, also known as C cells. - They can be found within the basal lamina of the thyroid follicles without extending into the follicular lumen or between thyroid follicles in the interfollicular space, either singly or in the form of groups. - Parafollicular cells produce calcitonin. - This hormone aids in the regulation of blood calcium levels by downregulating bone resorption and limiting calcium reuptake in the kidneys. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1853-1854; Solution Page 1878","sourcePage":1853,"sourcePageEnd":1854},{"id":"anatomy-48-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":13,"question":"Which layer is indicated in the provided histological section?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Outer plexiform layer"},{"letter":"B","text":"Inner plexiform layer"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ganglion cell layer"},{"letter":"D","text":"Photoreceptor layer"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Inner plexiform layer","explanation":"Correct Option B - Inner plexiform layer: - The inner plexiform layer is the layer in the retina where synapses occur between bipolar cells, amacrine cells, and ganglion cells. - It is responsible for processing and integrating visual signals before transmitting them to the ganglion cell layer. Incorrect Options - A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1854-1855; Solution Pages 1878-1879","sourcePage":1854,"sourcePageEnd":1855},{"id":"anatomy-48-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":14,"question":"Identify the structure marked in the given image? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Respiratory bronchus"},{"letter":"B","text":"Terminal bronchioles"},{"letter":"C","text":"Alveolar sac"},{"letter":"D","text":"Intrapulmonary bronchus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Terminal bronchioles","explanation":"Correct Option B - Terminal bronchioles: - Terminal bronchioles differ from larger bronchi as they are devoid of cartilage in their walls. - The ciliated cells are still present in terminal bronchioles, but their abundance is reduced. - Additionally, terminal bronchioles exhibit alveolar outgrowths, marking the transition to structures involved in gas exchange. Incorrect Options: Option A - Respiratory bronchus: - Respiratory bronchioles have alveoli budding directly from their walls. - This is a distinctive feature that indicates their role in gas exchange. - Epithelium may become more cuboidal, eventually transitioning to simple squamous epithelium in the alveoli. - Similar to terminal bronchioles, respiratory bronchioles lack cartilage in their walls. Option C - Alveolar sac: - Alveolar sacs are composed of clusters of alveoli, which are small, balloon-like structures. - Alveolar walls are extremely thin to facilitate efficient gas exchange. Option D - Intrapulmonary bronchus: - Intrapulmonary bronchi have smooth muscle in their walls, which helps regulate airflow. - Goblet cells, found in the epithelium of intrapulmonary bronchi, secrete mucus to aid in capturing particles and facilitating their expulsion from the respiratory tract. - Cartilage found in the intrapulmonary bronchus helps maintain the shape of the bronchi, preserving the integrity of the airway structure.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1855-1856; Solution Pages 1879-1880","sourcePage":1855,"sourcePageEnd":1856},{"id":"anatomy-48-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":15,"question":"The inner layer of the fallopian tube is covered by:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Simple columnar epithelium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Ciliated columnar epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Cuboidal epithelium with microvilli"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Ciliated columnar epithelium","explanation":"Correct option C - Ciliated columnar epithelium: - The fallopian tube is lined by ciliated columnar epithelium. - It is present in the inner hollow surface of the fallopian tube. Incorrect Options - A, B, and D are incorrect.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1856; Solution Pages 1880-1881","sourcePage":1856,"sourcePageEnd":1856},{"id":"anatomy-48-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":16,"question":"The image below shows the cross-section of the trachea. Identify the structure marked. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trachealis muscle"},{"letter":"B","text":"Tracheal cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Esophageal submucosal gland"},{"letter":"D","text":"Perichondrium"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Trachealis muscle","explanation":"Correct Option A - Trachealis muscle: - The trachealis muscle is a thick band of smooth muscle that bridges the ends of each of the horseshoe-shaped tracheal rings that help to stabilize the entire length of the windpipe. Incorrect Options: Option B - Tracheal cartilage: Tracheal cartilages also known as tracheal rings. Option C - Esophageal submucosal gland: Esophageal submucosal gland is located underneath the esophageal muscularis mucosa. Option D - Perichondrium: Perichondrium is mainly found on the surfaces of elastic and hyaline cartilage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1856-1857; Solution Page 1881","sourcePage":1856,"sourcePageEnd":1857},{"id":"anatomy-48-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Identify the type of cartilage shown in the image below: **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Hyaline cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Elastic cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Articular cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"Fibrocartilage"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Elastic cartilage","explanation":"Correct Option B -: Elastic cartilage - The histological image provided in the question shows elastic cartilage due to the presence of numerous branching elastic fibers. - Elastic cartilage contains abundant elastic fibers within its extracellular matrix. - These elastic fibers give the tissue a yellowish appearance. Incorrect Option Option A - Hyaline cartilage: - Hyaline cartilage appears as a translucent, bluish-white tissue under a microscope. - It consists of a smooth, homogenous extracellular matrix with dispersed chondrocytes (cartilage cells) in lacunae. - It lacks prominent fibers and has a glassy appearance. Option C - Articular cartilage: - Articular cartilage is a specialized type of hyaline cartilage that covers the ends of bones in synovial joints. - It has a smooth surface and contains chondrocytes in a matrix of collagen fibers. - Under a microscope, articular cartilage resembles hyaline cartilage but is typically thicker due to the mechanical stresses it undergoes. Option D - Fibrocartilage: - Fibrocartilage has a dense arrangement of collagen fibers within its extracellular matrix. It appears as a tough and fibrous tissue. - Under a microscope, fibrocartilage exhibits thick bundles of collagen fibers arranged in parallel, with chondrocytes scattered in between. - It has a more cellular appearance compared to hyaline or elastic cartilage.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1857-1858; Solution Pages 1881-1882","sourcePage":1857,"sourcePageEnd":1858},{"id":"anatomy-48-18","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":18,"question":"Identify which of the following structure is a merocrine gland:","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1"},{"letter":"B","text":"2"},{"letter":"C","text":"3"},{"letter":"D","text":"4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. 2","explanation":"Correct Option B - 2: - Merocrine glands, also known as eccrine glands, are those where the secretory cells secrete their substances directly through the cell membrane without any loss of cell material. - The secretion is by exocytosis. - These glands are the most common type of sweat gland, and they are abundant on the surface of the palms, soles, and forehead. - They play an important role in thermoregulation by releasing sweat that evaporates from the skin surface, reducing body temperature. Incorrect Options: Options A, C, and D are incorrect. Refer to the explanation of the correct answer.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1858-1859; Solution Pages 1882-1883","sourcePage":1858,"sourcePageEnd":1859},{"id":"anatomy-48-19","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":19,"question":"The given histology image is of?The given histology image is of? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Leydig cells of testis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Pancreatic islet cells"},{"letter":"C","text":"Hassall's corpuscles"},{"letter":"D","text":"Glomerulus"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Glomerulus","explanation":"Correct Option D - Glomerulus: - The structure of the histological image given is of the kidney showing a glomerulus by proximal convoluted tubules (PCT), and distal convoluted tubules (DCT). Incorrect Options: Option A - Leydig cells of the testis: - Leydig cells are found between the seminiferous tubules in the interstitial tissue of the testis. - They are larger than the neighboring cells and have a distinct morphology. - Their cytoplasm is often eosinophilic. - The cells typically have a single, large, central nucleus. Option B - Pancreatic islet cells: - Islet cells have a large, round nucleus and a lightly-stained cytoplasm. - Islet cells are typically arranged in groups, with different cell types clustered together. Option C - Hassall's corpuscles: - These structures are located in the medulla of the thymus gland. - They are composed of reticular epithelial cells arranged in a concentric fashion with central degenerating eosinophilic matter. - The cells in each layer are tightly packed, forming a flattened, disc-like shape. - The blue dots are lymphocytes with large nucleus (basophilic).","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1859-1860; Solution Pages 1883-1885","sourcePage":1859,"sourcePageEnd":1860},{"id":"anatomy-48-20","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":20,"question":"Identify the histological image below. **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Pseudostratified columnar epithelium"},{"letter":"B","text":"Non-keratinized squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"C","text":"Stratified squamous epithelium"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transitional epithelium"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Transitional epithelium","explanation":"Correct Option D - Transitional epithelium - Cells towards the lumen are umbrella shaped (Globular). - Basement membrane is straight. - Transitional epithelium is found in the bladder and ureters. - The cells may also have multiple nuclei and a scalloped appearance at the free surface. Incorrect Options - A: Pseudostratified columnar epithelium: - This tissue appears layered, but all cells are attached to the basement membrane. - Look for nuclei located at different levels and the presence of cilia on the apical surface. Option B - Non-keratinized squamous epithelium: - This tissue comprises layers of flat cells. - Look for nuclei located towards the basal surface and the absence of keratin Option C - Stratified squamous epithelium: - This tissue comprises multiple layers of flat cells, but the outermost layer is dead and keratinized. - Look for flattened cells at the surface that lack nuclei and contain keratin.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1860-1861; Solution Pages 1885-1886","sourcePage":1860,"sourcePageEnd":1861},{"id":"anatomy-48-21","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-48","chapter":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 48 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":48,"questionNumber":21,"question":"Identify the cartilage given below?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Non-articular hyaline cartilage"},{"letter":"B","text":"Articular hyaline cartilage"},{"letter":"C","text":"Yellow cartilage"},{"letter":"D","text":"White fibrocartilage"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. White fibrocartilage","explanation":"Correct Option D - White fibrocartilage: - It is a cartilage with collagen fibers arranged in parallel bundles, giving it its characteristic strength and unbending nature. - White fibrocartilage is found in zones of the body where strength and support are required, such as the intervertebral discs, pubic symphysis, and menisci of the knee. Incorrect Options: Option A: Non-articular hyaline cartilage: - It is found in areas of the body that do not directly participate in joint development, such as the respiratory system and the development plates of long bones. Option B: Articular hyaline cartilage: - It is a type of cartilage that covers the articular surfaces of bones in synovial joints, permitting smooth and frictionless movement and never get ossified. Option C: Yellow cartilage: - It is another name for elastic cartilage, which contains elastic fibers and is found in zones of the body that require both flexibility and strength, such as the ear and the epiglottis. -","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1861-1862; Solution Pages 1886-1887","sourcePage":1861,"sourcePageEnd":1862},{"id":"anatomy-49-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":1,"question":"Which of the following muscles contributes to forming both Petit’s triangle and the triangle of auscultation?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Latissimus dorsi"},{"letter":"B","text":"Internal oblique"},{"letter":"C","text":"Rhomboid major"},{"letter":"D","text":"Trapezius"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Latissimus dorsi","explanation":"The latissimus dorsi muscle contributes to the inferior border of the triangle of auscultation and posteriorly in Petit’s triangle. Triangle of auscultation Petit’s triangle Description The triangle of auscultation is a small Also known as the inferior lumbar gap in the musculature on the back triangle . of the thorax present near the inferior angle of the scapula. Borders and contents Superiorly by trapezius muscle Posteriorly by latissimus dorsi (Option D) Inferiorly by latissimus Anteriorly by the posterior border of dorsi Laterally by the medial border the external oblique The iliac crest of scapula The floor has the 6th and inferiorly forms the base Floor has 7th ribs and the sixth intercostal internal oblique muscle (Option B) space Part of the rhomboid major muscle is exposed in the triangle (Option C) Significance The apex of the lower lobe of the A collection of pus from the abdomen lung is found deep to it. When the may show up here. Site of lumbar scapulae move anteriorly (arms hernia. M/c site for lumbar hernia: folded and trunk flexed), the triangle Superior lumbar triangle (Grynfeltt) > expands, allowing clear auscultation Inferior lumbar triangle (Petit) On the left side, the cardiac orifice of Detaching the fascia from the iliac the stomach lies deep, previously crest enables access to the used to detect esophageal tumors by retroperitoneal space. auscultating swallowed liquids.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1888; Solution Pages 1897-1898","sourcePage":1888,"sourcePageEnd":1888},{"id":"anatomy-49-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":2,"question":"All of the following statements about the atlantooccipital joint is/are true except: 1. Allows for flexion and extension of the head 2. It is a synovial condyloid joint 3. Primarily responsible for lateral bending of the head 4. Formed by the articulation between the occipital condyles and the superior articular facets of the atlas 5. It facilitates rotational movement of the head","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"1, 2, and 4"},{"letter":"B","text":"2 and 3"},{"letter":"C","text":"3 and 5"},{"letter":"D","text":"3, 4, and 5"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. 3 and 5","explanation":"- While the atlanto-occipital joint allows some lateral bending , it is not the primary joint responsible for this movement. - Lateral bending of the head is more significantly facilitated by the atlantoaxial joint (between the atlas and axis, C1 and C2) and the cervical spine below. - The atlantoaxial joint allows for rotational movements and contributes to lateral flexion. Atlanto-Occipital Joint The atlanto-occipital joint is a synovial joint formed between the atlas (C1) and the occipital bone of the skull. Here are key features: Structure: This joint consists of the superior articular facets of the atlas, which articulate with the occipital condyles of the skull. Function: It primarily allows for nodding movements (flexion and extension), often described as \" yes\" movements. Anatomy: - The odontoid process (or dens) is a peg-like structure that projects from the body of the axis (C2). - It is crucial for the stability and functionality of the cervical spine. - The odontoid process is held in place by the transverse ligament of the atlas, which prevents excessive backward movement of the atlas relative to the dens. - In the case of fusion, it can be assumed that the body of C1 and C2 may appear fused if there are pathological changes or congenital anomalies. Atlanto-Axial Joint The atlantoaxial joint is another synovial joint located between the atlas (C1) and axis (C2), and it has specific characteristics: Structure: This joint consists of the dens of the axis fitting into the anterior arch of the atlas. Function : It primarily facilitates rotational movements of the head, allowing for the \"no\" movement. - The atlantoaxial joint is classified as a pivot or trochoid joint, enabling rotational movement around the central axis formed by the dens. - The transverse ligament again plays a key role by stabilizing the odontoid process and allowing for controlled rotation of the skull and atlas around the dens.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1888; Solution Pages 1898-1902","sourcePage":1888,"sourcePageEnd":1888},{"id":"anatomy-49-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":3,"question":"A 5-month-old infant with suspected meningitis is scheduled for a lumbar puncture. Which of the following statements about lumbar puncture in infants is incorrect?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Procedure is typically done at the L3-L4 intervertebral space."},{"letter":"B","text":"Procedure is preferably performed under ultrasound guidance."},{"letter":"C","text":"Supracristal plane corresponds to the L4-L5 vertebral level."},{"letter":"D","text":"Depth to the subarachnoid space is approximately 1.4 cm in neonates."}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Supracristal plane corresponds to the L4-L5 vertebral level.","explanation":"In infants , the supracristal plane intersects the vertebral column at L3-L4, not L4-L5. Lumbar Puncture: Aspect Adults Neonates and infants Spinal Cord Termination Terminates at the level of L1-L2. Terminates between L1 and L3 in neonates. Conus Medullaris Location Around the L1 and L2 lumbar Commonly at the lower third of L1 vertebra and can sometimes be vertebral body (range: lower third of lower. T12 to L2/L3 intervertebral disc). Supracristal Plane A line between the highest points of Intersects the vertebral column at the iliac crests intersects the L3-L4 level in children. It may vary vertebral column around L4 or the from L2/L3 to L5/S1 interspinous L4/L5 intervertebral disc; this may space. (Option C) vary from the L2/L3 to L5/S1 level. In most adults, the plane intersects the vertebral column inferior to the termination of the spinal cord. Ultrasound Guidance Not routinely used. May be preferable to avoid overlap between the supracristal plane and the termination of the spinal cord. (Option B) Positioning Patients can be seated or lying on Infants should be positioned to their side (lateral decubitus) on a maximize convex curvature to the firm, flat surface. Flex the lumbar lumbar spine, either lying or sitting. spine to separate the vertebral {{caption_text}} spines maximally and expose the ligamentum flavum. {{caption_text}} Needle Insertion Insert the needle between the spines Performed between spinous of L3 and L4 or L4 and L5, and processes of either L3 and L4 or L4 anesthetize the skin and soft tissues. and L5, using a trocar needle and into subarachnoid space below the level of conus medullaris. (Option A) Depth to Subarachnoid Space: Approximately 1.4 cm in neonates, increasing with age. (Option D) Needle Puncturing {{caption_text}} Needle Path Technique Lumbar puncture needle inserted in the midline or just to one side. Angle the needle in the horizontal and sagittal planes to pierce the ligamentum flavum in or near the midline. Slight loss of resistance as the needle enters the epidural space. Careful advancement to pierce the dura and arachnoid to release CSF. {{caption_text}}","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1888-1889; Solution Pages 1902-1903","sourcePage":1888,"sourcePageEnd":1889},{"id":"anatomy-49-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":4,"question":"{{caption_text}} Based on the image, which of the following structures is primarily responsible for facilitating the described movement of the head? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dens (Odontoid process) of C2"},{"letter":"B","text":"Transverse ligament of C1"},{"letter":"C","text":"Atlanto-occipital joint"},{"letter":"D","text":"Superior Articular Facet of C1"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Dens (Odontoid process) of C2","explanation":"- The dens (odontoid process) of C2 is the key structure facilitating head rotation. - It projects upward from the axis and fits into the atlas (C1), allowing the atlas and skull to rotate around it. - This pivot mechanism enables the characteristic side-to-side movement of the head. C1 Vertebra (Atlas) C2 Vertebra (Axis) {{caption_text}} Body Lacks a traditional vertebral body. Prominent body with a unique Consists of an anterior and posterior projection called the odontoid arch. process (dens). Odontoid process: Bony projection that fits into the atlas ring, allowing for rotational movement of the head and neck. Spine Lacks spine The spine is massive with a bifid tip Superior Articular Facets Articulate with the occipital condyles Articulate with the inferior facets of of the skull (atlantooccipital joint) the atlas (atlantoaxial joint) ; oriented to permit head rotation. Inferior Articular Facets Articulates with C2 (atlantoaxial joint) Articulates with C3 Transverse process Long transverse process. Has Small transverse processes. Lacks anterior and posterior tubercles for anterior tubercle. ligament and muscle attachments. Transverse Foramen Contains a transverse foramen for the passage of the vertebral artery Role and Movement Supports the skull; allows nodding Crucial for head rotation; the dens movements (flexion and extension) serves as the central pivot point. No of the head. {{caption_text}} Yes movement movement Special Note Distinct for lacking a vertebral body Provides stability and acts as the and spinous process. primary pivot for head rotation.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1889; Solution Pages 1903-1904","sourcePage":1889,"sourcePageEnd":1889},{"id":"anatomy-49-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Assertion (A): There are 8 cervical spinal nerves despite there being only 7 cervical vertebrae. Reason (R): The first cervical nerve exits above the first cervical vertebra, and the remaining nerves exit below their corresponding vertebrae. **Type:** Assertion-Reason","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation for the Assertion"},{"letter":"B","text":"Both Assertion and Reason are correct, but the Reason is not the correct explanation for the Assertion"},{"letter":"C","text":"The Assertion is correct, but the Reason is incorrect"},{"letter":"D","text":"The Assertion is incorrect, but the Reason is correct"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Both Assertion and Reason are correct, and the Reason is the correct explanation for the Assertion","explanation":"explanation for the Assertion. - The human cervical spine has 8 cervical spinal nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae. - The first cervical nerve exits above the first cervical vertebra, and each subsequent nerve exits below the corresponding vertebra, which accounts for the presence of 8 cervical nerves despite having only 7 cervical vertebrae. Regions Number of Vertebrae Spinal Nerves (Pairs) Cervical 7 8 Thoracic 12 12 Lumbar 5 5 Sacral 5 5 Coccygeal 4 (varies) 1 Total 33 vertebrae 31 pairs","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1889-1890; Solution Pages 1904-1905","sourcePage":1889,"sourcePageEnd":1890},{"id":"anatomy-49-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":6,"question":"An anatomy professor presents an image of the back region and asks to identify the nerve innervation of muscle marked in green? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Suprascapular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"From the dorsal rami of C1"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subscapular nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Dorsal scapular nerve","explanation":"- The muscle marked in above image is Levator scapulae, which is innervated by dorsal scapular nerve.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1890; Solution Page 1905","sourcePage":1890,"sourcePageEnd":1890},{"id":"anatomy-49-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":7,"question":"By the end of fetal development, at which vertebral level does the spinal cord terminate?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Lower border of L1"},{"letter":"B","text":"Lower border of L2"},{"letter":"C","text":"Upper border of L3"},{"letter":"D","text":"Lower border of L3"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Upper border of L3","explanation":"By the end of fetal development, the spinal cord terminates at the upper border of the L3 vertebra. Fetus : - Initially, the spinal cord and vertebral column are the same length. - The vertebral column grows faster, causing the spinal cord to ascend. - Ascent milestones: Begins around 9 weeks . Reaches the last sacral vertebra by 12 weeks. Attains the lower border of S1 by 15 weeks. Reaches the lower border of L4 at 6 months. Reaches the lower border of L3 at full term . (Option C) - Begins around 9 weeks . - Reaches the last sacral vertebra by 12 weeks. - Attains the lower border of S1 by 15 weeks. - Reaches the lower border of L4 at 6 months. - Reaches the lower border of L3 at full term . (Option C) - Begins around 9 weeks . - Reaches the last sacral vertebra by 12 weeks. - Attains the lower border of S1 by 15 weeks. - Reaches the lower border of L4 at 6 months. - Reaches the lower border of L3 at full term . (Option C) Child : - The spinal cord terminates at the upper border of L3 . Adult : - The spinal cord ends at the lower border of L1 (Option A) known as the conus medullaris. - Below this level, the vertebral canal contains the cauda equina. Clinical significance: - Lumbar puncture in adults must be done below L2 at the level of L3-L4 or L4-L5 laminae as this avoids injury to the spinal cord. - In children, it is done at the lower level of L4.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1890-1891; Solution Pages 1905-1908","sourcePage":1890,"sourcePageEnd":1891},{"id":"anatomy-49-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":8,"question":"A 54-year-old female is a known case of rheumatoid arthritis, underwent MRI which showed tectorial membrane thickening. This structure is derived from which of the following ligaments?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Anterior atlanto occipital membrane"},{"letter":"B","text":"Posterior atlanto occipital membrane"},{"letter":"C","text":"Anterior longitudinal ligament"},{"letter":"D","text":"Posterior longitudinal ligament"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Posterior longitudinal ligament","explanation":"- The membrana tectoria is a structure in the cervical spine that is part of the ligaments of the spinal cord. - It is a continuation of the posterior longitudinal ligament (PLL) in the neck region and plays an important role in stabilizing the cervical spine - It lies in the upper part of the cervical spine on the posterior surface of vertebral body, specifically in the area around the atlas (C1) and axis (C2), just below the level of the foramen magnum in the skull. - Above the level of axis , the posterior longitudinal ligament continues as membrana tectoria. - It extends from the axis to foramen magnum.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1891; Solution Page 1908","sourcePage":1891,"sourcePageEnd":1891},{"id":"anatomy-49-9","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":9,"question":"Match the right options. a Spine of scapula i T2 b Highest point of iliac crest ii T3 c Superior angle of scapula iii T7 d Inferior angle of scapula iv L4 Spine of scapula i T2 Highest point of iliac crest ii T3 Superior angle of scapula iii T7 Inferior angle of scapula iv L4","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv"},{"letter":"B","text":"a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii"},{"letter":"C","text":"a-iv, b-ii, c-iii, d-i"},{"letter":"D","text":"a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. a-ii, b-iv, c-i, d-iii","explanation":"- The scapula extends from rib 2 to rib 7 with its superior angle palpable over the trapezius and its inferior angle over the latissimus dorsi. - These angles are connected to each other by the medial border of the scapula running vertically. - The spine of the scapula is easily palpable as it runs subcutaneously. Parts of scapula Corresponding vertebrae Spine T3 (spinous process) Superior angle T2 Inferior angle T7 Other important landmarks Highest point of iliac crest L4 Posterior-superior iliac spine S2","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1891-1892; Solution Pages 1908-1910","sourcePage":1891,"sourcePageEnd":1892},{"id":"anatomy-49-10","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":10,"question":"Which of the following is true about cauda equina?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is comprised of the terminal end of the spinal cord"},{"letter":"B","text":"Ends at L1-L2 vertebra in adults"},{"letter":"C","text":"Surrounded by CSF in the subarachnoid space"},{"letter":"D","text":"The filum terminale begins from the apex of cauda equina"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Surrounded by CSF in the subarachnoid space","explanation":"The cauda equina is surrounded by the CSF in the subarachnoid space and is a bundle of spinal nerve roots (not the spinal cord's end) that extends into the sacral region surrounded by the filum terminale . Cauda Equina Conus Medullaris Definition A bundle of spinal nerve roots The tapered end of the spinal cord resembling a horse's tail (Option A) Location Below the conus medullaris, in the At the lower end of the spinal cord vertebral canal Structure Composed of dorsal and ventral Comprised of the terminal end of the nerve roots from L2 to S5 and Co1 spinal cord Number of Nerve Roots 40 nerve roots (16 lumbar, 20 sacral, None; terminates without forming 4 coccygeal) new roots Formation of Spinal Nerves Dorsal and ventral roots join at Does not contribute to new spinal intervertebral foramina nerves Filum Terminale Surrounds cauda equina Attached to the apex of conus medullaris (Option D) Clinical Relevance Site for lumbar puncture; potential for Indicates termination of spinal cord; cauda equina syndrome important for anatomical reference Length Extends to the sacral region, around Generally ends at L1-L2 vertebrae in 15-20 cm (Option B) adults Surrounding Structures Surrounded by cerebrospinal fluid in Encased by pia mater; continuous the subarachnoid space with filum terminale Anatomical Changes with Age Remains relatively constant Position shifts upward in younger individuals","sourceRef":"Question Page 1892; Solution Pages 1910-1912","sourcePage":1892,"sourcePageEnd":1892},{"id":"anatomy-49-11","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":11,"question":"A 42-year-old male presents with complaints of lower back pain radiating down his left leg, associated with numbness over the top of his foot and difficulty in dorsiflexing his big toe. On examination, he has weakness in the extension of the great toe and decreased sensation along the dorsum of the foot. Reflexes are normal. Based on the symptoms, which vertebral level is most likely affected?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"L3-L4"},{"letter":"B","text":"L4-L5"},{"letter":"C","text":"L5-S1"},{"letter":"D","text":"T12-L1"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. L4-L5","explanation":"- The patient's symptoms suggest nerve root compression, commonly caused by a herniated disc. - The key findings in this case include lower back pain radiating down the left leg, numbness over the top of the foot, and weakness in dorsiflexion of the great toe (extensor hallucis longus), without reflex abnormalities. - These are classic signs of L5 nerve root involvement. L5 nerve root compression, often due to L4-L5 disc herniation, results in: - Weakness in great toe dorsiflexion (extensor hallucis longus). - Sensory loss over the dorsum of the foot. - Radiating pain down the lateral aspect of leg. Slipped disc: - A herniated disc, also known as a slipped disc, occurs when the soft center (nucleus pulposus) of a spinal disc pushes through a tear in the tougher exterior (annulus fibrosus) and presses on nearby nerves. - Herniated discs most commonly occur in the cervical (neck) and lumbar (lower back) spine. Here's an explanation of herniated discs affecting both the cervical and lumbar regions. Cervical Slip Disc (Upper Limb Involvement): - In the cervical region, the nerves exit above their corresponding vertebrae (except for the C8 nerve). - The affected nerve root, in the case of a herniated disc, is typically the one corresponding to the lower vertebra in the pair. Cervical Slip Discs and Affected Nerve Roots Herniated Disc Between Compressed Nerve Root Symptoms (Upper Limb) C4 and C5 C5 Shoulder pain, weakness in deltoid muscle C5 and C6 C6 Pain in biceps, weakness in wrist extension C6 and C7 C7 Triceps weakness, difficulty extending the elbow C7 and T1 C8 Hand weakness, difficulty with finger movements Lumbar Slip Disc (Lower Limb Involvement) - In the lumbar region, the nerves exit below their corresponding vertebrae. - Therefore, when a disc herniation occurs between two vertebrae, the nerve root corresponding to the lower vertebra is usually compressed. Lumbar Slip Discs and Affected Nerve Roots Herniated Disc Between Compressed Nerve Root Symptoms (Lower Limb) L3 and L4 L4 Weakness in knee extension, reduced patellar reflex L4 and L5 L5 Weakness in foot dorsiflexion, difficulty walking L5 and S1 S1 Pain down the back of the leg, reduced Achilles reflex, foot drop Regional Prevalence: - Cervicothoracic (C6-T1): 10% of cases - Lumbosacral (L4-S1): 90% of cases - Thoracic: Rare L3-L4 (Option A): - Compression at this level typically affects the L4 nerve root, causing weakness in knee extension (quadriceps) and decreased sensation over the medial side of the lower leg. - This is not consistent with the patient's symptoms. L5-S1 (Option C): - Compression here would affect the S1 nerve root, causing weakness in plantarflexion (gastrocnemius), decreased ankle reflex, and sensory loss along the lateral aspect of the foot. - This does not match the patient's findings. T12-L1 (Option D): - This is much higher in the vertebral column and would affect muscles and sensations around the lower abdomen and upper thighs, which are unrelated to the symptoms described in this case.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1892-1893; Solution Pages 1912-1916","sourcePage":1892,"sourcePageEnd":1893},{"id":"anatomy-49-12","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":12,"question":"A 2-year-old child is brought in for a routine check-up. The parents have noticed a pronounced lumbar curvature since the child began walking. Which of the following best describes this lumbar curvature?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Primary curvature"},{"letter":"B","text":"Secondary curvature"},{"letter":"C","text":"Compensatory curve from sacral kyphosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Curvature related to increased abdominal weight"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Secondary curvature","explanation":"Lumbar lordosis is a secondary curvature that becomes evident in infancy as the child begins to assume upright posture, stand and walk, contributing to normal posture development. Vertebral Curvature: - Vertebral column in adults has 4 curvatures: Cervical lordosis Thoracic kyphosis Lumbar lordosis Sacral kyphosis - Cervical lordosis - Thoracic kyphosis - Lumbar lordosis - Sacral kyphosis - Cervical lordosis - Thoracic kyphosis - Lumbar lordosis - Sacral kyphosis - Types of Vertebral Curvature: Primary Curvature Secondary Curvature Developmental. Thoracic and sacral kyphosis (both Acquired in relation to the erect human posture. Cervical concave anteriorly) are primary curvatures. (Option A & and lumbar lordosis (both concave posteriorly) are C) Develop during the fetal period in response to the secondary curvatures. Result from extension from the flexed fetal position. Are in the same direction as the main flexed fetal position. Begin to appear during the late fetal curvatures of the fetal vertebral column. Retained period but do not become obvious until infancy (roughly, throughout life due to differences in height between the 1st year). Secondary curvatures are primarily maintained anterior and posterior parts of the vertebrae. by differences in the thickness between the anterior and posterior parts of the intervertebral (IV) discs. Cervical lordosis becomes evident when an infant begins to raise (extend) the head while prone and to hold the head up while sitting. Lumbar lordosis appears when toddlers (children learning to walk) begin to assume upright posture, standing and walking. It is more pronounced in females. (Option B) - Functional Importance: Curvatures of the vertebral column enhance flexibility (shock-absorbing resilience), augmenting the passive flexibility provided by the intervertebral (IV) discs. - Response to Increased Load: When bearing heavy loads (e.g., carrying a heavy backpack), both the IV discs and curvatures are compressed, increasing the curvature. - Impact of Additional Anterior Weight: Carrying weight in front of the body’s normal gravitational axis (e.g., large breasts, obesity, pregnancy, or carrying a child) tends to increase these curvatures. (Option D)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1893; Solution Pages 1916-1917","sourcePage":1893,"sourcePageEnd":1893},{"id":"anatomy-49-13","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":13,"question":"A patient has restricted spinal rotation and lateral flexion. Which vertebral structure primarily functions as a lever for these movements?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Spinous process"},{"letter":"B","text":"Superior articular process"},{"letter":"C","text":"Inferior articular process"},{"letter":"D","text":"Transverse process"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Transverse process","explanation":"Transverse processes project laterally from the pediculolaminar junctions and serve as levers for muscles and ligaments mainly involved in spinal rotation and lateral flexion. Typical Vertebra Has a ventral body, dorsal arch with processes, and a vertebral foramen. Image {{caption_text}} Vertebral Body Adjacent vertebral bodies are connected by fibrocartilage discs. Form a flexible, weight-supporting central axis. Transmits even greater forces generated from attached muscles. Vertebral Arch Consists of pedicles and a broader laminae. Transverse, superior, and inferior articular processes project from the junction of the pedicle and lamina. Vertebral Foramina Formed by the vertebral arch and body. Contains the spinal cord, meninges and their vessels. Intervertebral Foramina Located between adjoining vertebrae, near junctions of vertebral bodies and arches. Transmit mixed spinal nerves, small recurrent nerves, and blood and lymphatic vessels. Spinous Processes (Option A) Project dorsally from the junction of the lamina. Act as levers for muscles that control posture and active movements (flexion/extension, lateral flexion and rotation) of the vertebral column. Transverse Processes (Option D) Project laterally from the pediculolaminar junctions. Serve as levers for muscles and ligaments, mainly concerned with rotation and lateral flexion. Articular Processes (Option B & C) Superior articular processes project cranially with dorsal facets; inferior articular processes project caudally with ventral facets. They form zygapophysial or facet joints and form part of the posterior boundaries of intervertebral foramina, permitting only limited vertebral movement. Trabecular Structure Vertebrae are internally composed of trabecular bone with an outer shell of compact bone perforated by vascular foramina. Trabecular interior contains red bone marrow and basivertebral veins.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1893; Solution Pages 1917-1918","sourcePage":1893,"sourcePageEnd":1893},{"id":"anatomy-49-14","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":14,"question":"An anatomy professor presents an image of a vertebra in class and asks the students to identify its type. Which type of vertebra is depicted in the image? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Cervical vertebrae"},{"letter":"B","text":"Thoracic vertebrae"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lumbar vertebrae"},{"letter":"D","text":"Sacral vertebrae"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lumbar vertebrae","explanation":"Lumbar vertebrae are characterized by their large size, thick and horizontal spinous processes , and the absence of transverse foramina (which are present in cervical vertebrae) or costal facets (which are found in thoracic vertebrae). Feature Cervical Vertebrae (Option Thoracic Vertebrae Lumbar Vertebrae (Option A) (Option B) C) Image {{caption_text}} Size Small size Intermediate-size Large size Body Small , broad with a Waisted cylinder ; nearly Wider transversely convex anterior surface. equal transverse and AP dimensions. Vertebral Canal Triangular Small and circular Triangular Pedicles and Laminae Pedicles: Attach midway Pedicles: Do not diverge Pedicles: Short Laminae: between the discal surface as the vertebral foramen is Broad and short . of the vertebral body. small and circular Lamina: Lamina: Thin , slightly Short , thick , and broad. curved . Spinous Process Short , bifid with two Slants downward. Almost horizontal, unequal tubercles. quadrangular, and thickened along its posterior and inferior borders. Transverse Process Composite structure Large, club-like. Long, thin. Superior Articular Facets Flat and ovoid, directed Directed posteriorly and Bear vertical concave superoposteriorly . slightly superolaterally . facets facing posteromedial ly . Inferior Articular Facets Directed mainly anteriorly . Directed forwards and Have vertical convex slightly superomedially . articular facets facing anterolaterally. Special Features Foramen Transversarium: Have lateral costal facets Costal facets and Transmits the vertebral and transverse process transverse foramina are artery and vein, and a facets for rib articulation. absent. branch from the cervicothoracic ganglion (vertebral nerve) except in the seventh cervical vertebra. Sacral vertebrae (Option D) are not considered individually because they fuse into a single immobile sacrum during adolescence, typically formed from five segments.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1893-1894; Solution Pages 1918-1920","sourcePage":1893,"sourcePageEnd":1894},{"id":"anatomy-49-15","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":15,"question":"A 35-year-old patient undergoes spinal surgery. Postoperatively, he develops a cerebrospinal fluid leak. Which space is most likely compromised?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"A"},{"letter":"B","text":"B"},{"letter":"C","text":"C"},{"letter":"D","text":"D"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. C","explanation":"The subarachnoid space , which contains cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), is likely compromised after spinal surgery due to potential puncture of the dura or arachnoid. Epidural Space (Option A) Subdural Space Subarachnoid Space Located between the spinal dura Potential space between the dura Wide space between pia mater and mater and the periosteum. Contains: and arachnoid mater. (Option B) arachnoid mater. Filled with Loose areolar tissue Semiliquid fat Contains a thin layer of serous fluid. cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) . (Option C) Spinal arteries supplying deeper Allows movement of the dura over Cushions the brain and spinal cord. structures Internal vertebral venous the arachnoid. Extends onto spinal Wider in the spinal region, especially plexus. Epidural anesthesia is given nerves and connects with lymphatic below the spinal cord where it here spaces. encloses the cauda equina. Lumbar punctures are typically performed between L3 & L4 vertebrae. Periosteal space (Option D) is not a recognized anatomical space for CSF.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1894-1895; Solution Pages 1920-1921","sourcePage":1894,"sourcePageEnd":1895},{"id":"anatomy-49-16","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":16,"question":"A patient undergoes a procedure affecting a delicate, thread-like structure extending from the conus medullaris to the coccyx as marked in the figure. Which of the following statements regarding this structure is correct? {{caption_text}} **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"It is composed mainly of dura mater and contains no nerve fibers"},{"letter":"B","text":"Distal part of it is called the coccygeal ligament."},{"letter":"C","text":"It does not extend beyond the sacral hiatus"},{"letter":"D","text":"It measures about 50 cm in length"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Distal part of it is called the coccygeal ligament.","explanation":"The marked structure in the given image is the Filum terminale and it consists of 2 parts: filum terminale internum and the distal filum terminale externum, also known as the coccygeal ligament . Filum terminale is a thread-like extension of the pia mater (Option A) measuring about 20 cm in length. (Option D) - Location : Extends from the tip of the conus medullaris to the base of the coccyx. Pathway : Pierces the dural tube, traverses the sacral canal and sacral hiatus (Option C) and attaches to the dorsal surface of the first coccygeal vertebra. - Pathway : Pierces the dural tube, traverses the sacral canal and sacral hiatus (Option C) and attaches to the dorsal surface of the first coccygeal vertebra. - Pathway : Pierces the dural tube, traverses the sacral canal and sacral hiatus (Option C) and attaches to the dorsal surface of the first coccygeal vertebra. - Segments Filum Terminale Internum : Approximately 15 cm long, located within the dural sac. Filum Terminale Externum : About 5 cm long, situated outside the dura mater, below the second sacral vertebra . - Filum Terminale Internum : Approximately 15 cm long, located within the dural sac. - Filum Terminale Externum : About 5 cm long, situated outside the dura mater, below the second sacral vertebra . - Composition : Primarily pia mater, with some nerve fibers from the second and third coccygeal nerves on its outer surface. The central canal of the spinal cord extends into it for about 5 mm. - Primarily pia mater, with some nerve fibers from the second and third coccygeal nerves on its outer surface. - The central canal of the spinal cord extends into it for about 5 mm. - Filum Terminale Internum : Approximately 15 cm long, located within the dural sac. - Filum Terminale Externum : About 5 cm long, situated outside the dura mater, below the second sacral vertebra . - Primarily pia mater, with some nerve fibers from the second and third coccygeal nerves on its outer surface. - The central canal of the spinal cord extends into it for about 5 mm.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1895-1896; Solution Pages 1921-1923","sourcePage":1895,"sourcePageEnd":1896},{"id":"anatomy-49-17","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-49","chapter":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topic":"Lesson 49 - Back Region","topicIndex":49,"questionNumber":17,"question":"Which of the following spinal segments is located between the cervical enlargement of the spinal cord?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"T9 to L1"},{"letter":"B","text":"C5 to T1"},{"letter":"C","text":"L2 to S3"},{"letter":"D","text":"C3 to T1"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. C5 to T1","explanation":"The cervical enlargement is specifically found in the C5 to T1 spinal segments, providing nerves that form the brachial plexus . Cervical Enlargement Lumbar Enlargement Extent C5 to T1 spinal segments (Option B) L2 to S3 spinal segments (Option C) Function Gives rise to the brachial plexus Contributes to the lumbar plexus Corresponding vertebral level C3 to T1 vertebrae. (Option D) T9 to L1 vertebrae. (Option A)","sourceRef":"Question Page 1896; Solution Pages 1923-1924","sourcePage":1896,"sourcePageEnd":1896},{"id":"anatomy-50-1","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":1,"question":"To what level of vertebrae does the marked structure correspond? **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"At the junction of L1 & L2"},{"letter":"B","text":"At the junction of L2 & L3"},{"letter":"C","text":"At the junction of T12 & L1"},{"letter":"D","text":"At the junction of L3 & L4"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. At the junction of L3 & L4","explanation":"Correct Option D - At the junction of L3 & L4 - The marked structure is the umbilicus which lies at the junction of L3 & L4. The umbilicus is located at the level of the intervertebral disc between the third lumbar vertebra (L3) and the fourth lumbar vertebra (L4). Associated structures at this level include the inferior vena cava, the renal veins, the gonadal arteries and veins, the ureters, and the descending colon. Incorrect Options: - Option A - At the junction of L1 & L2: Associated structures at this level include the spinal cord, the aorta, the inferior vena cava, the renal arteries and veins, and the ureters. - Option B - At the junction of L2 & L3: Associated structures at this level include the inferior vena cava, the renal veins, the gonadal arteries and veins, and the ascending colon. - Option C - At the junction of T12 & L1: Associated structures at this level include the aorta, the inferior vena cava, the celiac trunk, the superior mesenteric artery, and the renal arteries","sourceRef":"Question Page 1925; Solution Pages 1929-1930","sourcePage":1925,"sourcePageEnd":1925},{"id":"anatomy-50-2","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":2,"question":"The muscle marked by the arrow in the image below is innervated by the : **Visual / Image Reference:** The source question references an image/diagram/histology visual. The extracted text was preserved as-is; where the source exposes only `{{caption_text}}`, the placeholder is retained rather than inventing visual details.","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Dorsal scapular nerve"},{"letter":"B","text":"Suprascapular nerve"},{"letter":"C","text":"From the dorsal rami of C1"},{"letter":"D","text":"Subscapular nerve"}],"correctOption":1,"correctAnswerText":"A. Dorsal scapular nerve","explanation":"Correct Option A - Dorsal scapular nerve: - The muscle marked is levator scapulae muscle and it is innervated by the dorsal scapular nerve (Option A). - The dorsal scapular nerve arises from the ventral rami of the C5 nerve root and runs deep to the levator scapulae muscle. - It provides motor innervation to the levator scapulae muscle and the rhomboid muscles (rhomboid major and rhomboid minor). Incorrect Options: Option B - The suprascapular nerve innervates the supraspinatus and infraspinatus muscles but does not directly innervate the levator scapulae muscle. Option C- The dorsal rami of C1, or suboccipital nerve, innervates the muscles at the back of the head but not the levator scapulae. Option D - The subscapular nerve is a branch of the posterior cord of the brachial plexus and innervates the subscapularis muscle, not the levator scapulae muscle.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1925-1926; Solution Page 1930","sourcePage":1925,"sourcePageEnd":1926},{"id":"anatomy-50-3","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":3,"question":"The movements at the _______ joint permit a person to look to the right and left","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Atlanto-occipital"},{"letter":"B","text":"Atlanto -axial"},{"letter":"C","text":"C2-C3"},{"letter":"D","text":"C3-C4"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Atlanto -axial","explanation":"Correct Option B - Atlanto-axial joint: The Atlanto-axial joint is formed between the atlas (C1 vertebra) and the axis (C2 vertebra) in the spine. This joint is responsible for rotational movements of the head. The axis (C2) has a peg-like structure called the dens or odontoid process, which fits into the atlas (C1), allowing for rotation. Therefore, the Atlanto-axial joint permits the specific movements required for looking to the right and left. Incorrect Options: Option A - Atlanto-occipital joint The Atlanto-occipital joint permits flexion and extension movements of the head, like nodding. It's mainly for tilting the head forward and backward, rather than side-to-side rotation. Option C - C2-C3 intervertebral joint The C2-C3 intervertebral joint permits flexion, extension, lateral bending, and some degree of rotation, but sideways rotation is mainly handled by the Atlanto-axial joint, not C2-C3. Option D - C3-C4 intervertebral joint The C3-C4 joint, similar to C2-C3, allows neck movements like flexion, extension, lateral bending, and some degree of rotation. However, it's not directly involved in sideways rotational movements.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1926; Solution Pages 1930-1931","sourcePage":1926,"sourcePageEnd":1926},{"id":"anatomy-50-4","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":4,"question":"Ligamentum flavum has high levels of:-","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Type 1 collagen"},{"letter":"B","text":"Type 2 collagen"},{"letter":"C","text":"Reticular fibres"},{"letter":"D","text":"Elastin fibres"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. Elastin fibres","explanation":"Correct Option D - Elastin fibres: The ligamentum flavum connects adjacent vertebrae, crucial for spinal stability and flexibility. Elastin fibers within it provide elasticity, enabling stretching and recoiling to maintain stability during movement. Its yellowish color comes from high elastin levels. Incorrect Options: Option A - Type 1 collagen: Type 1 collagen is abundant in tendons, ligaments, and bone, but the ligamentum flavum contains lower levels compared to other connective tissues. Option B - Type 2 collagen: Type 2 collagen is primarily found in hyaline cartilage, like in joint articular cartilage and vertebral discs, but it's not notably present in the ligamentum flavum. Option C - Reticular fibers: Reticular fibers, composed of type 3 collagen, form mesh-like networks in organs like lymph nodes and spleen, but they're not notably present in the ligamentum flavum.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1926-1927; Solution Page 1931","sourcePage":1926,"sourcePageEnd":1927},{"id":"anatomy-50-5","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":5,"question":"Which of the following is normally seen in lumbar spine? (or) What is typically observed in the lumbar spine?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Scoliosis"},{"letter":"B","text":"Kyphosis"},{"letter":"C","text":"Lordosis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Recurvatum"}],"correctOption":3,"correctAnswerText":"C. Lordosis","explanation":"Correct Option C - Lordosis: - Lordosis: Lordosis refers to the inward curvature of the spine, creating a concave shape. In the lumbar spine, lordosis is considered a normal anatomical feature. It helps to maintain the balance and proper alignment of the spine, allowing for efficient weight distribution and movement. Incorrect Options: Option A - Scoliosis: Scoliosis is an abnormal sideways curvature of the spine, typically found in the thoracic or thoracolumbar regions, not the lumbar spine. Option B - Kyphosis: Kyphosis is an excessive forward curvature of the spine, often causing a rounded or hunched-back appearance. While normal in the thoracic spine, it's uncommon in the lumbar spine. Option D - Recurvatum: Recurvatum is hyperextension of a joint, seen notably in the knee but not typically in the lumbar spine.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1927; Solution Pages 1931-1932","sourcePage":1927,"sourcePageEnd":1927},{"id":"anatomy-50-6","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":6,"question":"Retraction of the scapula is primarily done by?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Serratus anterior"},{"letter":"B","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"C","text":"Subscapularis"},{"letter":"D","text":"Supraspinatus"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Trapezius","explanation":"Correct Option B - Trapezius - Trapezius: The trapezius muscle is a large muscle that covers the upper back and neck. It plays a key role in the movement and stabilization of the scapula. The middle and lower fibers of the trapezius muscle primarily perform scapular retraction, which involves pulling the scapula backward toward the spine. Incorrect Options: Option A - Serratus anterior: The serratus anterior muscle protracts and rotates the scapula, stabilizing it against the rib cage. However, it doesn't directly retract the scapula at the sternoclavicular joint. Option C - Subscapularis: The subscapularis muscle, part of the rotator cuff, sits on the front of the scapula. It internally rotates the humerus and stabilizes the shoulder joint but doesn't aid in scapular retraction. Option D - Supraspinatus: The supraspinatus muscle, part of the rotator cuff, is located on the back of the scapula. It helps initiate shoulder abduction but isn't mainly responsible for scapular retraction.","sourceRef":"Question Page 1927; Solution Pages 1932-1933","sourcePage":1927,"sourcePageEnd":1927},{"id":"anatomy-50-7","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":7,"question":"What is the cause of scapular winging?","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"Trapezius"},{"letter":"B","text":"Serratus anterior"},{"letter":"C","text":"Latissimus dorsi"},{"letter":"D","text":"Pectroralis major"}],"correctOption":2,"correctAnswerText":"B. Serratus anterior","explanation":"Correct Option B - Serratus anterior: - Serratus anterior: The serratus anterior muscle is primarily responsible for stabilizing the scapula against the thoracic wall. When the serratus anterior muscle is paralyzed or weakened, it can lead to winging of the scapula. This muscle is especially important for maintaining proper scapular position during movements such as pushing, pulling, and reaching. Hence, option B is correct. Incorrect Options: Option A - Trapezius: The trapezius muscle stabilizes and moves the scapula, but its paralysis typically doesn't cause scapular winging. Option C - Latissimus dorsi: The latissimus dorsi muscle assists in various shoulder movements but is typically not associated with scapular winging, even when weakened or paralyzed. Option D - Pectoralis major: The pectoralis major muscle aids shoulder movements but usually doesn't cause scapular winging if paralyzed.","sourceRef":"Question Pages 1927-1928; Solution Page 1933","sourcePage":1927,"sourcePageEnd":1928},{"id":"anatomy-50-8","subject":"Anatomy","chapterId":"anatomy-50","chapter":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topic":"Lesson 50 - Previous Year Questions","topicIndex":50,"questionNumber":8,"question":"Match the following muscles with their action: a. Pectoralis major b. Supraspinatus c. Infraspinatus d. Latissimus dorsi 1. Extension at shoulder 2. Flexion at shoulder 3. Lateral rotation of the shoulder 4. Abduction at shoulder a. Pectoralis major b. Supraspinatus c. Infraspinatus 1. Extensiond.atLatissimus shoulder 2.dorsi Flexion at shoulder 3. Lateral rotation of **Type:** Match the Following","options":[{"letter":"A","text":"a - 4, b - 3, c - 2, d - 1"},{"letter":"B","text":"a - 4, b - 2, c - 3, d - 1"},{"letter":"C","text":"a - 2, b - 4, c - 1, d - 3"},{"letter":"D","text":"a - 2, b - 4, c - 3, d - 1"}],"correctOption":4,"correctAnswerText":"D. a - 2, b - 4, c - 3, d - 1","explanation":"Correct Option D - a - 2, b - 4, c - 3, d - 1: a. Pectoralis major 2. Flexion at shoulder b. Supraspinatus 4. Abduction at shoulder c. Infraspinatus 3. Lateral rotation of the shoulder d. Latissimus dorsi 1. Extension at shoulder Incorrect Options - A, B & C are incorrect","sourceRef":"Question Page 1928; Solution Pages 1933-1934","sourcePage":1928,"sourcePageEnd":1928}]}]};
